COPY public.quiz_questions (id, quiz_id, question_text, options, correct_option_index, explanation) FROM stdin;
4361	1095	What is the primary architectural difference between Git and GitHub?	["Git is a centralized VCS, while GitHub is a distributed VCS.", "Git is the local version control software, while GitHub is a remote hosting service for Git repositories.", "Git is used for backend code, while GitHub is used for frontend code.", "There is no difference; they are two names for the exact same software."]	1	Git is the actual command-line tool and distributed version control system that runs locally on your machine. GitHub is a cloud-based platform that hosts Git repositories remotely, providing a UI and collaboration features.
4362	1095	Which Git command initializes a new, empty Git repository in the current directory by creating a hidden folder?	["git start", "git create", "git init", "git new"]	2	The `git init` command initializes a repository by generating a hidden `.git` folder in the current directory, which stores all the metadata and version history.
4363	1095	You have modified multiple files but only want to include specific files in your next commit. What is the intermediate holding zone where these files must be placed first?	["The Local Repository", "The Staging Area (Index)", "The Remote Origin", "The Stash"]	1	The Staging Area (or Index) acts as a holding zone. When you run `git add <file>`, you move files to the staging area so you can carefully review and group them before finalizing the commit.
4364	1095	What does the command `git commit -m "Message"` actually do under the hood?	["It pushes all local changes directly to the remote GitHub server.", "It takes all files from the Staging Area and creates a permanent snapshot in the local repository history.", "It saves the current working directory state into a temporary stash.", "It moves modified files from the working directory straight to the remote repository."]	1	A commit takes a snapshot of exactly what is currently in the Staging Area, assigns it a unique SHA-1 hash, and permanently saves it to the local Git timeline.
4365	1095	If you want to view a compressed, single-line history of your previous commits, which command is most appropriate?	["git status --short", "git diff --inline", "git log --oneline", "git show --summary"]	2	`git log --oneline` (or `--oneline` flag variants) compresses the log output, showing just the first few characters of the commit hash and the commit message on a single line.
4366	1095	You discover a bug introduced in a specific commit several days ago. You want to undo those exact changes, but you DO NOT want to rewrite or delete the commit history. Which command should you use?	["git reset --hard <commit-id>", "git revert <commit-id>", "git rebase -i HEAD~3", "git clean -f"]	1	`git revert` generates a brand new commit that mathematically inverses the changes introduced by the target commit. It safely undoes the bug while preserving the linear, collaborative history, unlike `git reset` which destroys history.
4367	1095	What is the primary difference between `git reset --soft` and `git reset --hard`?	["--soft deletes the remote branch, while --hard deletes the local branch.", "--soft moves the HEAD backward but keeps your file changes in the staging area; --hard permanently deletes the commits and wipes the uncommitted file changes.", "--soft is used for rebasing, while --hard is used for merging.", "--soft only affects tracked files, while --hard only affects untracked files."]	1	`git reset --hard` completely obliterates the commit history and resets the actual files on your hard drive to match that older state. `--soft` moves the HEAD pointer back but leaves your files intact and staged.
4368	1095	You are working on a feature branch but need to urgently pull the latest changes from `main`. You have uncommitted changes that you aren't ready to commit yet. Which command safely shelves your work?	["git shelf", "git stash", "git hold", "git pause"]	1	`git stash` temporarily stores your modified, tracked files in a stack, leaving you with a clean working directory. You can later restore them using `git stash pop` or `git stash apply`.
4369	1095	Which command securely links your local Git repository to a remote GitHub repository named 'origin'?	["git remote add origin <URL>", "git link origin <URL>", "git connect remote <URL>", "git push --set-upstream <URL>"]	0	`git remote add origin <URL>` adds a remote reference. 'origin' is simply the conventional alias used for the primary remote repository URL.
4370	1095	What is the primary purpose of setting up an SSH Key (Secure Shell Key) with GitHub?	["To encrypt the source code files before they are pushed to the server.", "To securely authenticate your local machine with the GitHub server without typing a username and password every time you push or pull.", "To grant read-only access to outside collaborators.", "To compress the repository size during remote transfers."]	1	SSH keys provide a secure, password-less authentication handshake between your local machine's private key and GitHub's stored public key.
4371	1095	A team member pushed a crucial hotfix to the remote repository. You want to download these changes and automatically merge them into your current local branch. Which command does this?	["git fetch", "git clone", "git pull", "git sync"]	2	`git pull` is a composite command. It runs `git fetch` (to download the remote commits) and immediately runs `git merge` to integrate them into your current active local branch.
4372	1095	What is the critical difference between `git merge` and `git rebase` when integrating a feature branch into the main branch?	["Merge is used for local branches, while Rebase is used for remote branches.", "Merge creates a new 'merge commit' and preserves the branching history; Rebase rewrites history to place the feature commits linearly on top of the main branch.", "Merge deletes the feature branch automatically, while Rebase keeps it.", "Rebase resolves merge conflicts automatically, while Merge requires manual resolution."]	1	Merging preserves the non-linear, parallel history of branches by tying them together with a new commit. Rebasing physically alters commit hashes to create a clean, single-line linear history.
4373	1095	Why is it considered dangerous (an anti-pattern) to use `git rebase` on a public, shared branch that other developers have already pulled?	["Because it deletes the source code files from the server.", "Because rebasing rewrites the commit SHAs. If pushed, it destroys the shared history, forcing colleagues into catastrophic merge conflicts.", "Because GitHub blocks rebase commands entirely on public repositories.", "Because rebasing bypasses Pull Requests."]	1	The Golden Rule of Rebasing: Never rebase a public branch. Since rebasing generates entirely new cryptographic SHA hashes for commits, other developers' local tracking branches will go entirely out of sync with the rewritten remote branch.
4374	1095	You want to find out exactly which user introduced a specific line of code that caused a bug, and in which commit they did it. Which tool/command is used for this?	["git inspect", "git blame", "git track", "git trace"]	1	`git blame <filename>` annotates each line in a file, showing the SHA hash of the commit, the author's name, and the timestamp of when that exact line was last modified.
4375	1095	What is the most efficient single command to simultaneously create a new branch named 'auth-feature' and immediately switch to it?	["git branch auth-feature && git switch auth-feature", "git checkout -b auth-feature", "git new auth-feature", "git init branch auth-feature"]	1	The command `git checkout -b <branch-name>` (or the newer `git switch -c <branch-name>`) handles both creation and switching in a single atomic step.
4376	1095	You just created a local branch `feature-b` and committed some code. When you run `git push`, Git rejects it and says the branch doesn't exist on the remote. What command fixes this?	["git push origin main", "git remote update", "git push --set-upstream origin feature-b", "git push -f"]	2	Local branches do not automatically exist on GitHub. You must use `--set-upstream` (or `-u`) on the first push to tell Git to create the branch on the remote server and link your local tracking branch to it.
4377	1095	When a developer submits a 'Pull Request' (PR) on GitHub, what is the primary architectural purpose of this feature?	["It forces the Git CLI to automatically resolve all merge conflicts.", "It is a GitHub-specific UI feature that formally requests project maintainers to review, discuss, and approve the code before it is merged into the main branch.", "It creates an SSH tunnel to securely transfer files.", "It compresses the repository to save cloud storage."]	1	Pull Requests are not a core feature of the Git protocol; they are a collaboration tool built by GitHub to enforce code reviews, run automated tests (CI/CD), and facilitate discussion prior to merging.
4378	1095	In Git, what exactly does the `HEAD` pointer signify?	["It always points to the most recent commit on the main branch.", "It is the remote server's master copy of the repository.", "It is a symbolic reference indicating the specific commit or branch your working directory is currently checked out to.", "It is a unique identifier assigned to the first (root) commit of the repository."]	2	`HEAD` acts as a cursor. Whether you checkout a branch, a specific commit hash (detached HEAD), or a tag, `HEAD` simply points to whatever snapshot your local filesystem is currently reflecting.
4379	1095	During a merge, Git halts and warns you of a 'Merge Conflict'. What causes this, and how must it be resolved?	["It happens when the repository exceeds storage limits; you must delete old branches.", "It occurs when Git cannot communicate with GitHub; you must retry the push.", "It happens when two branches modified the exact same lines of code differently. You must manually open the files, edit the conflicting lines, stage them, and commit the resolution.", "It happens when the staging area is full; you must run `git stash` to clear it."]	2	Git is highly intelligent at auto-merging files, but if User A and User B edit the exact same line of the same file, Git pauses. It injects conflict markers (`<<<<<<<`) into the file. The developer must manually choose the correct code, remove the markers, stage the file, and commit.
4380	1095	What is the purpose of the `.gitignore` file?	["It prevents users from deleting files in the remote repository.", "It allows you to specify files and directories (like node_modules, .env secrets, or compiled binaries) that Git should permanently ignore and never stage.", "It hides specific branches from other developers on the team.", "It forces Git to delete files that have not been modified in 30 days."]	1	The `.gitignore` file acts as a filter. By adding file patterns to it, Git will not show them as 'untracked' in `git status` and will prevent them from being accidentally staged and pushed to GitHub (vital for security keys and heavy dependency folders).
\.
