22119	Lecture 1: Introduction to JavaScript — Why JS Exists, ECMAScript, V8 Engine & Browser vs. Node.js Runtime	js-lecture-1-introduction-v8-engine-ecmascript-nodejs-runtime	Understand from first principles why JavaScript was created, how ECMAScript standardizes the language across browser engines, and how embedding V8 inside C++ created Node.js for full-stack MERN development.	2026-10-06 21:14:25.4193+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhile **HTML** defines the structural skeleton of a webpage and **CSS** controls visual styling and transitions, neither can execute logic, handle button click events dynamically, or compute data on the client side. **JavaScript** was created by Brendan Eich in 1995 (in 10 days at Netscape) to serve as the interactive brain of the web.\n\n### 💡 The Browser Wars & ECMAScript Standardization\n* **Why Standardization Was Needed:** Early browsers (Netscape Navigator vs. Microsoft Internet Explorer with JScript) implemented incompatible scripting behaviors, forcing developers to write separate codebases for different browsers.\n* **ECMA International & ECMAScript (ES):** Standardized under **ECMA-262**. **ECMAScript** is the official specification (the rulebook), while **JavaScript** is the concrete implementation of that specification.\n* **Major Milestones:** **ES5 (2009)** standardized strict mode and array methods; **ES6 / ES2015** introduced `let`, `const`, Arrow Functions, Promises, Classes, and ES Modules.\n\n### 💡 JavaScript Engines & Runtime Environments\nA CPU only understands binary machine code, not plain-text JavaScript. Every environment that runs JavaScript relies on a **JavaScript Engine**:\n* **Google V8 Engine:** Written in C++, powers Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge, Brave, and **Node.js**. Uses a **Just-In-Time (JIT)** compilation pipeline (**Ignition** interpreter generates bytecode + **TurboFan** optimizing compiler compiles hot code directly into native machine code).\n* **SpiderMonkey:** Mozilla's engine powering Firefox.\n* **JavaScriptCore (Nitro):** Apple's engine powering Safari.\n* **Why Node.js Unlocked Full-Stack JS:** In 2009, Ryan Dahl extracted Google's open-source C++ **V8 Engine** out of Chrome and embedded it into a standalone C++ runtime (**Node.js**) with file-system, network, and OS bindings—allowing developers to run JavaScript outside the browser via `node index.js`.\n\n### 🛠️ Running JavaScript in Node.js vs. Browser\n```javascript\n// File: first.js\n// Execute in terminal using: node first.js\nconsole.log("Hello Coder Army! JavaScript running outside the browser on V8.");\n\nconst num1 = 10;\nconst num2 = 20;\nconsole.log("Sum:", num1 + num2); // Output: Sum: 30\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Introduction", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Introduction", "title": "MDN Web Docs — JavaScript Guide & Introduction"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=611_04Ml25c", "title": "Javascript Full Course | Introduction to JAVASCRIPT | Javascript Full Course #01", "duration": "49m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-1-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What is the difference between ECMAScript and JavaScript?", "title": "Q1: What is the difference between ECMAScript and JavaScript?", "answer": "ECMAScript (standardized as ECMA-262 by ECMA International) is the official language specification that defines the syntax, types, and behavior rules of the language. JavaScript is the practical implementation of the ECMAScript specification, combined with host environment APIs (like the DOM, fetch, and localStorage in browsers, or fs and http in Node.js).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "ECMAScript (standardized as ECMA-262 by ECMA International) is the official language specification that defines the syntax, types, and behavior rules of the language. JavaScript is the practical implementation of the ECMAScript specification, combined with host environment APIs (like the DOM, fetch, and localStorage in browsers, or fs and http in Node.js)."}, {"id": "js-lec-1-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: How does the Google V8 Engine execute JavaScript code? Is JS interpreted or compiled?", "title": "Q2: How does the Google V8 Engine execute JavaScript code? Is JS interpreted or compiled?", "answer": "Modern JavaScript engines like V8 use Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation, which combines both interpretation and compilation. First, the parser converts JS source code into an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST). Second, the Ignition interpreter converts the AST into bytecode and begins executing it immediately. Third, while running, the profiler monitors \\"hot\\" functions (code executed repeatedly) and passes them to the TurboFan optimizing compiler, which compiles them into highly optimized native machine code.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Modern JavaScript engines like V8 use Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation, which combines both interpretation and compilation. First, the parser converts JS source code into an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST). Second, the Ignition interpreter converts the AST into bytecode and begins executing it immediately. Third, while running, the profiler monitors \\"hot\\" functions (code executed repeatedly) and passes them to the TurboFan optimizing compiler, which compiles them into highly optimized native machine code."}, {"id": "js-lec-1-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is Node.js, and how does it allow JavaScript to run outside the browser?", "title": "Q3: What is Node.js, and how does it allow JavaScript to run outside the browser?", "answer": "Node.js is an open-source, cross-platform C++ runtime environment that embeds Chrome's V8 JavaScript engine alongside libuv (for asynchronous I/O and the event loop). Because V8 compiles JavaScript into native machine instructions independent of HTML/DOM, wrapping V8 with C++ system bindings allows JavaScript to access the file system, network sockets, and OS processes on a server.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Node.js is an open-source, cross-platform C++ runtime environment that embeds Chrome's V8 JavaScript engine alongside libuv (for asynchronous I/O and the event loop). Because V8 compiles JavaScript into native machine instructions independent of HTML/DOM, wrapping V8 with C++ system bindings allows JavaScript to access the file system, network sockets, and OS processes on a server."}, {"id": "js-lec-1-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Are console.log, setTimeout, and document.getElementById part of core JavaScript (ECMAScript)?", "title": "Q4: Are console.log, setTimeout, and document.getElementById part of core JavaScript (ECMAScript)?", "answer": "No. None of them are part of the core ECMAScript specification. They are Host / Environment APIs provided by the runtime environment (Web APIs in the browser, or C++ bindings in Node.js). Note that document.getElementById exists only in the browser DOM environment and throws a ReferenceError in Node.js.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "No. None of them are part of the core ECMAScript specification. They are Host / Environment APIs provided by the runtime environment (Web APIs in the browser, or C++ bindings in Node.js). Note that document.getElementById exists only in the browser DOM environment and throws a ReferenceError in Node.js."}, {"id": "js-lec-1-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Why can a C++ program embed and execute JavaScript files?", "title": "Q5: Why can a C++ program embed and execute JavaScript files?", "answer": "Because the V8 engine itself is a C++ library. Any C++ application can include the V8 header files, initialize a V8 Isolate and Context in RAM, pass a raw JavaScript string or file into V8, and let V8 compile and execute those instructions on the CPU.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q5", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Because the V8 engine itself is a C++ library. Any C++ application can include the V8 header files, initialize a V8 Isolate and Context in RAM, pass a raw JavaScript string or file into V8, and let V8 compile and execute those instructions on the CPU."}, {"id": "js-lec-1-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why did the web standardize on JavaScript instead of running languages like C++ or Java directly in the browser DOM?", "title": "Q6: Why did the web standardize on JavaScript instead of running languages like C++ or Java directly in the browser DOM?", "answer": "Browsers needed a lightweight, memory-safe, dynamically typed scripting language that could run inside a strict security sandbox without manual memory allocation pointers or separate compilation steps before page load. JavaScript was built specifically to manipulate UI elements safely in real time.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Browsers needed a lightweight, memory-safe, dynamically typed scripting language that could run inside a strict security sandbox without manual memory allocation pointers or separate compilation steps before page load. JavaScript was built specifically to manipulate UI elements safely in real time."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhile **HTML** defines the structural skeleton of a webpage and **CSS** controls visual styling and transitions, neither can execute logic, handle button click events dynamically, or compute data on the client side. **JavaScript** was created by Brendan Eich in 1995 (in 10 days at Netscape) to serve as the interactive brain of the web.\n\n### 💡 The Browser Wars & ECMAScript Standardization\n* **Why Standardization Was Needed:** Early browsers (Netscape Navigator vs. Microsoft Internet Explorer with JScript) implemented incompatible scripting behaviors, forcing developers to write separate codebases for different browsers.\n* **ECMA International & ECMAScript (ES):** Standardized under **ECMA-262**. **ECMAScript** is the official specification (the rulebook), while **JavaScript** is the concrete implementation of that specification.\n* **Major Milestones:** **ES5 (2009)** standardized strict mode and array methods; **ES6 / ES2015** introduced `let`, `const`, Arrow Functions, Promises, Classes, and ES Modules.\n\n### 💡 JavaScript Engines & Runtime Environments\nA CPU only understands binary machine code, not plain-text JavaScript. Every environment that runs JavaScript relies on a **JavaScript Engine**:\n* **Google V8 Engine:** Written in C++, powers Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge, Brave, and **Node.js**. Uses a **Just-In-Time (JIT)** compilation pipeline (**Ignition** interpreter generates bytecode + **TurboFan** optimizing compiler compiles hot code directly into native machine code).\n* **SpiderMonkey:** Mozilla's engine powering Firefox.\n* **JavaScriptCore (Nitro):** Apple's engine powering Safari.\n* **Why Node.js Unlocked Full-Stack JS:** In 2009, Ryan Dahl extracted Google's open-source C++ **V8 Engine** out of Chrome and embedded it into a standalone C++ runtime (**Node.js**) with file-system, network, and OS bindings—allowing developers to run JavaScript outside the browser via `node index.js`.\n\n### 🛠️ Running JavaScript in Node.js vs. Browser\n```javascript\n// File: first.js\n// Execute in terminal using: node first.js\nconsole.log("Hello Coder Army! JavaScript running outside the browser on V8.");\n\nconst num1 = 10;\nconst num2 = 20;\nconsole.log("Sum:", num1 + num2); // Output: Sum: 30\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 1: Introduction to JavaScript — Why JS Exists, ECMAScript, V8 Engine & Browser vs. Node.js Runtime	Understand from first principles why JavaScript was created, how ECMAScript standardizes the language across browser engines, and how embedding V8 inside C++ created Node.js for full-stack MERN development.	20	Easy	\N	1	[{"id": "js-lec-1-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What is the difference between ECMAScript and JavaScript?", "title": "Q1: What is the difference between ECMAScript and JavaScript?", "answer": "ECMAScript (standardized as ECMA-262 by ECMA International) is the official language specification that defines the syntax, types, and behavior rules of the language. JavaScript is the practical implementation of the ECMAScript specification, combined with host environment APIs (like the DOM, fetch, and localStorage in browsers, or fs and http in Node.js).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "ECMAScript (standardized as ECMA-262 by ECMA International) is the official language specification that defines the syntax, types, and behavior rules of the language. JavaScript is the practical implementation of the ECMAScript specification, combined with host environment APIs (like the DOM, fetch, and localStorage in browsers, or fs and http in Node.js)."}, {"id": "js-lec-1-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: How does the Google V8 Engine execute JavaScript code? Is JS interpreted or compiled?", "title": "Q2: How does the Google V8 Engine execute JavaScript code? Is JS interpreted or compiled?", "answer": "Modern JavaScript engines like V8 use Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation, which combines both interpretation and compilation. First, the parser converts JS source code into an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST). Second, the Ignition interpreter converts the AST into bytecode and begins executing it immediately. Third, while running, the profiler monitors \\"hot\\" functions (code executed repeatedly) and passes them to the TurboFan optimizing compiler, which compiles them into highly optimized native machine code.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Modern JavaScript engines like V8 use Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation, which combines both interpretation and compilation. First, the parser converts JS source code into an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST). Second, the Ignition interpreter converts the AST into bytecode and begins executing it immediately. Third, while running, the profiler monitors \\"hot\\" functions (code executed repeatedly) and passes them to the TurboFan optimizing compiler, which compiles them into highly optimized native machine code."}, {"id": "js-lec-1-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is Node.js, and how does it allow JavaScript to run outside the browser?", "title": "Q3: What is Node.js, and how does it allow JavaScript to run outside the browser?", "answer": "Node.js is an open-source, cross-platform C++ runtime environment that embeds Chrome's V8 JavaScript engine alongside libuv (for asynchronous I/O and the event loop). Because V8 compiles JavaScript into native machine instructions independent of HTML/DOM, wrapping V8 with C++ system bindings allows JavaScript to access the file system, network sockets, and OS processes on a server.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Node.js is an open-source, cross-platform C++ runtime environment that embeds Chrome's V8 JavaScript engine alongside libuv (for asynchronous I/O and the event loop). Because V8 compiles JavaScript into native machine instructions independent of HTML/DOM, wrapping V8 with C++ system bindings allows JavaScript to access the file system, network sockets, and OS processes on a server."}, {"id": "js-lec-1-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Are console.log, setTimeout, and document.getElementById part of core JavaScript (ECMAScript)?", "title": "Q4: Are console.log, setTimeout, and document.getElementById part of core JavaScript (ECMAScript)?", "answer": "No. None of them are part of the core ECMAScript specification. They are Host / Environment APIs provided by the runtime environment (Web APIs in the browser, or C++ bindings in Node.js). Note that document.getElementById exists only in the browser DOM environment and throws a ReferenceError in Node.js.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "No. None of them are part of the core ECMAScript specification. They are Host / Environment APIs provided by the runtime environment (Web APIs in the browser, or C++ bindings in Node.js). Note that document.getElementById exists only in the browser DOM environment and throws a ReferenceError in Node.js."}, {"id": "js-lec-1-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Why can a C++ program embed and execute JavaScript files?", "title": "Q5: Why can a C++ program embed and execute JavaScript files?", "answer": "Because the V8 engine itself is a C++ library. Any C++ application can include the V8 header files, initialize a V8 Isolate and Context in RAM, pass a raw JavaScript string or file into V8, and let V8 compile and execute those instructions on the CPU.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q5", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Because the V8 engine itself is a C++ library. Any C++ application can include the V8 header files, initialize a V8 Isolate and Context in RAM, pass a raw JavaScript string or file into V8, and let V8 compile and execute those instructions on the CPU."}, {"id": "js-lec-1-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why did the web standardize on JavaScript instead of running languages like C++ or Java directly in the browser DOM?", "title": "Q6: Why did the web standardize on JavaScript instead of running languages like C++ or Java directly in the browser DOM?", "answer": "Browsers needed a lightweight, memory-safe, dynamically typed scripting language that could run inside a strict security sandbox without manual memory allocation pointers or separate compilation steps before page load. JavaScript was built specifically to manipulate UI elements safely in real time.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Browsers needed a lightweight, memory-safe, dynamically typed scripting language that could run inside a strict security sandbox without manual memory allocation pointers or separate compilation steps before page load. JavaScript was built specifically to manipulate UI elements safely in real time."}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Introduction	\N	\N	\N
22075	Lecture 17: LEFT JOIN vs. RIGHT JOIN — Unmatched Rows, Ghost Hunter Anti-Joins & Perspective Flipping	pg-lecture-17-left-join-vs-right-join-anti-joins	Preserve all rows from the primary table with LEFT JOIN, find unmatched records using IS NULL anti-joins, and rewrite RIGHT JOINs cleanly as LEFT JOINs.	2026-10-05 19:34:44.247876+05:30	### 🎯 Computer Science Fundamentals: Lecture 17: LEFT JOIN vs. RIGHT JOIN — Unmatched Rows, Ghost Hunter Anti-Joins & Perspective Flipping\nCore systems concepts frequently tested in technical screening rounds and engineering interviews.\n\n- **System Architecture**: How modern operating systems, networks, and processors coordinate resources.\n- **Key Concepts to Master**: Concurrency primitives, resource isolation, latency vs throughput tradeoffs, and fault tolerance.\n- **Interview Key Takeaways**:\n  - Be prepared to draw diagrams explaining data flow and component lifecycles.\n  - Explain practical real-world applications (e.g. why databases use WAL, why HTTP/2 uses multiplexing).	[{"url": "https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/", "title": "OS Three Easy Pieces Guide"}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/operating-systems/", "title": "GeeksforGeeks CS Fundamentals"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LyAnUeYd2Cg", "title": "PostgreSQL LEFT JOIN vs RIGHT JOIN Explained | Real Examples, Interview Questions & Tricks", "duration": "19m", "creator_name": "Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical"}]	[{"id": "pg-17-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/", "name": "Combine Two Tables (Classic LEFT JOIN)", "title": "Combine Two Tables (Classic LEFT JOIN)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Combine Two Tables (Classic LEFT JOIN)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "name": "Customers Who Never Order (LEFT JOIN + IS NULL Anti-Join)", "title": "Customers Who Never Order (LEFT JOIN + IS NULL Anti-Join)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Customers Who Never Order (LEFT JOIN + IS NULL Anti-Join)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/replace-employee-id-with-the-unique-identifier/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/replace-employee-id-with-the-unique-identifier/", "name": "Replace Employee ID With The Unique Identifier", "title": "Replace Employee ID With The Unique Identifier", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Replace Employee ID With The Unique Identifier\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/replace-employee-id-with-the-unique-identifier/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employee-bonus/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employee-bonus/", "name": "Employee Bonus (LEFT JOIN + NULL Handling)", "title": "Employee Bonus (LEFT JOIN + NULL Handling)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Employee Bonus (LEFT JOIN + NULL Handling)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employee-bonus/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customer-who-visited-but-did-not-make-any-transactions/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customer-who-visited-but-did-not-make-any-transactions/", "name": "Customer Who Visited but Did Not Make Any Transactions (Ghost Hunter Query)", "title": "Customer Who Visited but Did Not Make Any Transactions (Ghost Hunter Query)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Customer Who Visited but Did Not Make Any Transactions (Ghost Hunter Query)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customer-who-visited-but-did-not-make-any-transactions/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/students-and-examinations/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/students-and-examinations/", "name": "Students and Examinations (LEFT JOIN Preservation)", "title": "Students and Examinations (LEFT JOIN Preservation)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Students and Examinations (LEFT JOIN Preservation)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/students-and-examinations/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/average-selling-price/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/average-selling-price/", "name": "Average Selling Price (LEFT JOIN with Zero Units Sold)", "title": "Average Selling Price (LEFT JOIN with Zero Units Sold)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_7", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Average Selling Price (LEFT JOIN with Zero Units Sold)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/average-selling-price/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/project-employees-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/project-employees-i/", "name": "Project Employees I", "title": "Project Employees I", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_8", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Project Employees I\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/project-employees-i/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/confirmation-rate/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/confirmation-rate/", "name": "Confirmation Rate (LEFT JOIN + Conditional Aggregation)", "title": "Confirmation Rate (LEFT JOIN + Conditional Aggregation)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_9", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Confirmation Rate (LEFT JOIN + Conditional Aggregation)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/confirmation-rate/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/", "name": "Monthly Transactions I", "title": "Monthly Transactions I", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_10", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Monthly Transactions I\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nUnlike `INNER JOIN` (which drops rows that do not match in both tables), outer joins (**`LEFT JOIN`** and **`RIGHT JOIN`**) preserve **100% of the rows** from one side of the join and fill in `NULL` wherever the other table has no matching record.\n\n### ⚖️ `LEFT JOIN` vs. `RIGHT JOIN` Mechanics\n\n* **`LEFT JOIN`:** The **First (Left)** table written after `FROM`. — Columns from the Right table are populated with `NULL`. — **Standard in 99% of production queries**.\n* **`RIGHT JOIN`:** The **Second (Right)** table written after `RIGHT JOIN`. — Columns from the Left table are populated with `NULL`. — Rarely used — can always be rewritten as a `LEFT JOIN` by swapping table order.\n\n### 🛠️ 3 Essential Production Query Patterns\n\n#### 1. Standard `LEFT JOIN` (All Students + Their Enrollments, If Any)\n```sql\n-- Returns ALL students, even those who have enrolled in 0 courses\nSELECT \n    u.name AS student,\n    e.course_id\nFROM users u\nLEFT JOIN enrollments e\n    ON u.id = e.student_id\nWHERE u.role = 'student';\n```\n\n#### 2. The "Ghost Hunter" Anti-Join Pattern (`LEFT JOIN` + `IS NULL`)\nFind records in Table A that have **zero matching records** in Table B (e.g., students who registered an account but never enrolled in any course):\n```sql\nSELECT \n    u.name,\n    u.email\nFROM users u\nLEFT JOIN enrollments e\n    ON u.id = e.student_id\nWHERE u.role = 'student'\n  AND e.course_id IS NULL; -- Never write = NULL!\n```\n\n#### 3. Flipping Perspectives (`RIGHT JOIN` Rewritten as `LEFT JOIN`)\nBoth queries below return every course (including courses with 0 enrolled students), proving why you only ever need `LEFT JOIN`:\n```sql\n-- Using RIGHT JOIN\nSELECT e.student_id, c.title AS course_name\nFROM enrollments e\nRIGHT JOIN courses c ON e.course_id = c.id;\n\n-- ✅ Cleaner Equivalent Using LEFT JOIN (Simply swap table order!)\nSELECT e.student_id, c.title AS course_name\nFROM courses c\nLEFT JOIN enrollments e ON c.id = e.course_id;\n```	Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)	https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical	654	Lecture 17: LEFT JOIN vs. RIGHT JOIN — Unmatched Rows, Ghost Hunter Anti-Joins & Perspective Flipping	Preserve all rows from the primary table with LEFT JOIN, find unmatched records using IS NULL anti-joins, and rewrite RIGHT JOINs cleanly as LEFT JOINs.	19	Medium	Module 5: Relational Joins & Multi-Table Queries	17	[{"id": "pg-17-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/", "name": "Combine Two Tables (Classic LEFT JOIN)", "title": "Combine Two Tables (Classic LEFT JOIN)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Combine Two Tables (Classic LEFT JOIN)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "name": "Customers Who Never Order (LEFT JOIN + IS NULL Anti-Join)", "title": "Customers Who Never Order (LEFT JOIN + IS NULL Anti-Join)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Customers Who Never Order (LEFT JOIN + IS NULL Anti-Join)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/replace-employee-id-with-the-unique-identifier/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/replace-employee-id-with-the-unique-identifier/", "name": "Replace Employee ID With The Unique Identifier", "title": "Replace Employee ID With The Unique Identifier", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Replace Employee ID With The Unique Identifier\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/replace-employee-id-with-the-unique-identifier/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employee-bonus/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employee-bonus/", "name": "Employee Bonus (LEFT JOIN + NULL Handling)", "title": "Employee Bonus (LEFT JOIN + NULL Handling)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Employee Bonus (LEFT JOIN + NULL Handling)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employee-bonus/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customer-who-visited-but-did-not-make-any-transactions/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customer-who-visited-but-did-not-make-any-transactions/", "name": "Customer Who Visited but Did Not Make Any Transactions (Ghost Hunter Query)", "title": "Customer Who Visited but Did Not Make Any Transactions (Ghost Hunter Query)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Customer Who Visited but Did Not Make Any Transactions (Ghost Hunter Query)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customer-who-visited-but-did-not-make-any-transactions/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/students-and-examinations/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/students-and-examinations/", "name": "Students and Examinations (LEFT JOIN Preservation)", "title": "Students and Examinations (LEFT JOIN Preservation)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Students and Examinations (LEFT JOIN Preservation)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/students-and-examinations/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/average-selling-price/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/average-selling-price/", "name": "Average Selling Price (LEFT JOIN with Zero Units Sold)", "title": "Average Selling Price (LEFT JOIN with Zero Units Sold)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_7", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Average Selling Price (LEFT JOIN with Zero Units Sold)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/average-selling-price/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/project-employees-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/project-employees-i/", "name": "Project Employees I", "title": "Project Employees I", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_8", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Project Employees I\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/project-employees-i/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/confirmation-rate/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/confirmation-rate/", "name": "Confirmation Rate (LEFT JOIN + Conditional Aggregation)", "title": "Confirmation Rate (LEFT JOIN + Conditional Aggregation)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_9", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Confirmation Rate (LEFT JOIN + Conditional Aggregation)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/confirmation-rate/"}, {"id": "pg-17-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/", "name": "Monthly Transactions I", "title": "Monthly Transactions I", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "17_10", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Monthly Transactions I\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/"}]	https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-join.html	\N	\N	\N
22120	Lecture 2: Variables & Data Types — var, let, const, 7 Primitives vs. Reference Types & Mutability	js-lecture-2-variables-data-types-var-let-const-mutability	Master variable declarations (let, const, var), all 7 primitive data types vs. non-primitive reference types, typeof quirks, and why primitives are strictly immutable while objects are mutable.	2026-10-06 21:14:25.4193+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nJavaScript is a **dynamically typed** language—you do not declare data types ahead of time (let x = 10 can later hold "Rohit"). Every value in JavaScript falls into one of two categories: **Primitive Data Types (7 types)** or **Non-Primitive / Reference Data Types (`Object`)**.\n\n### 💡 Variable Declarations: `let`, `const`, and Legacy `var`\n* **`let`:** Block-scoped variable whose binding can be reassigned later (`let age = 20; age = 30;`), but cannot be redeclared in the same scope.\n* **`const`:** Block-scoped constant binding that **must be initialized at declaration** and cannot be reassigned to a new value or reference.\n* **`var` (Legacy — Avoid in Modern Code):** Function-scoped (ignores `if` and `for` block curly braces), allows accidental duplicate redeclarations (`var a = 10; var a = 20;`), and initializes to `undefined` during hoisting.\n\n### 💡 The 7 Primitive Data Types (Immutable Values)\n1. **`number`:** 64-bit IEEE-754 floating-point values (handles both integers and decimals up to `2^53 - 1`, i.e., `9007199254740991`).\n2. **`bigint`:** Arbitrary-precision integers beyond `2^53 - 1`, created by appending `n` (e.g., `90071992547409912345n`).\n3. **`string`:** Immutable sequence of UTF-16 characters enclosed in `""`, `''`, or ````.\n4. **`boolean`:** Logical `true` or `false`.\n5. **`undefined`:** Default value automatically assigned by JS to a declared variable that has not yet been initialized.\n6. **`null`:** Intentional absence of any object/value assigned explicitly by the developer.\n7. **`symbol`:** Guaranteed unique and immutable primitive identifier (e.g., `Symbol("id")`).\n\n### 💡 Non-Primitive Data Types & Mutability Philosophy\n* **Non-Primitives (`Object`, `Array`, `Function`):** Stored by **reference**.\n* **Why Primitives Are Immutable:** If you run `let str = "Rohit"; str[0 = "M";`, the string remains `"Rohit"`. Reassigning `let a = 10; a = 20;` does not mutate the value `10` in memory—it creates a new value `20` and points `a` to it.\n* **Why Non-Primitives Are Mutable:** Copying an object (`let obj2 = obj1`) copies the **memory reference**, not the object itself. Mutating `obj2.age = 30` directly mutates `obj1.age` because both variables point to the same underlying object.\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Primitives, `typeof` Quirks & Reference Mutation\n```javascript\n// 1. All 7 Primitive Data Types & typeof operator\nlet num = 42;                         // typeof -> "number"\nlet big = 9007199254740999n;          // typeof -> "bigint"\nlet str = "Coder Army";               // typeof -> "string"\nlet isPlaced = true;                  // typeof -> "boolean"\nlet uninit;                           // typeof -> "undefined"\nlet emptyVal = null;                  // typeof -> "object" (Famous 1995 JS bug!)\nlet sym = Symbol("unique_id");        // typeof -> "symbol"\n\n// 2. Primitive Immutability (Pass by Value behavior)\nlet a = 10;\nlet b = a;\nb = 20;\nconsole.log(a, b); // 10, 20 (a is completely unaffected)\n\nlet name = "Rohit";\nname[0 = "M";     // Ignored silently in non-strict mode\nconsole.log(name); // "Rohit" (Strings are immutable)\n\n// 3. Non-Primitive Mutability (Shared Reference)\nconst user1 = { name: "Rohit", age: 20 };\nconst user2 = user1; // Copies reference address, NOT a new object!\nuser2.age = 25;\nconsole.log(user1.age); // 25 (Both point to the exact same object)\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Data_structures", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Data_structures", "title": "MDN Web Docs — JavaScript Data Types and Data Structures"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UXYJGHhuGA8", "title": "JavaScript Variables & Data Types Explained(var, let, const, mutable) | Javascript Full Course #02", "duration": "69m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-2-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What are the exact differences between var, let, and const?", "title": "Q1: What are the exact differences between var, let, and const?", "answer": "1) Scope: var is function-scoped (leaks out of if/for blocks), whereas let and const are strictly block-scoped ({ ... }). 2) Redeclaration: var allows redeclaring the same identifier in the same scope; let and const throw a SyntaxError. 3) Reassignment: var and let can be reassigned; const cannot be reassigned after initialization. 4) Hoisting: var is initialized to undefined during the memory phase, while let and const remain uninitialized in the Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ) until their declaration line.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) Scope: var is function-scoped (leaks out of if/for blocks), whereas let and const are strictly block-scoped ({ ... }). 2) Redeclaration: var allows redeclaring the same identifier in the same scope; let and const throw a SyntaxError. 3) Reassignment: var and let can be reassigned; const cannot be reassigned after initialization. 4) Hoisting: var is initialized to undefined during the memory phase, while let and const remain uninitialized in the Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ) until their declaration line."}, {"id": "js-lec-2-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why does typeof null return \\"object\\" in JavaScript?", "title": "Q2: Why does typeof null return \\"object\\" in JavaScript?", "answer": "This is a historical bug from the very first 1995 implementation of JavaScript. In the original 32-bit engine, values were represented with type tags in the lowest bits, where 000 denoted an Object. The null pointer was represented as all zero bits (0x00), causing the engine to misread its type tag as 000 (\\"object\\"). The committee later rejected fixing it because changing typeof null to \\"null\\" would break millions of existing production websites.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "This is a historical bug from the very first 1995 implementation of JavaScript. In the original 32-bit engine, values were represented with type tags in the lowest bits, where 000 denoted an Object. The null pointer was represented as all zero bits (0x00), causing the engine to misread its type tag as 000 (\\"object\\"). The committee later rejected fixing it because changing typeof null to \\"null\\" would break millions of existing production websites."}, {"id": "js-lec-2-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: If a variable is declared with const (e.g., const user = { name: \\"Rohit\\" }), why can we still modify user.name = \\"Mohit\\"?", "title": "Q3: If a variable is declared with const (e.g., const user = { name: \\"Rohit\\" }), why can we still modify user.name = \\"Mohit\\"?", "answer": "Because const makes the variable binding (the memory reference stored in user) constant, NOT the underlying object value itself. Mutating a property inside the object (user.name = \\"Mohit\\") does not change the memory address that user points to. However, attempting to reassign user = { name: \\"Mohit\\" } throws a TypeError: Assignment to constant variable because that tries to point user to a brand-new memory address.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Because const makes the variable binding (the memory reference stored in user) constant, NOT the underlying object value itself. Mutating a property inside the object (user.name = \\"Mohit\\") does not change the memory address that user points to. However, attempting to reassign user = { name: \\"Mohit\\" } throws a TypeError: Assignment to constant variable because that tries to point user to a brand-new memory address."}, {"id": "js-lec-2-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What does it mean that Primitive Data Types are immutable in JavaScript?", "title": "Q4: What does it mean that Primitive Data Types are immutable in JavaScript?", "answer": "Immutability means a primitive value itself can never be altered in place once created in memory. For example, if let str = \\"hello\\", trying to change str[0] = \\"H\\" has no effect, and methods like str.toUpperCase() return a brand-new string (\\"HELLO\\") without modifying the original string. When you write let x = 10; x = 20;, you are not mutating the number 10 into 20; you are pointing the variable x to a new memory location holding 20.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Immutability means a primitive value itself can never be altered in place once created in memory. For example, if let str = \\"hello\\", trying to change str[0] = \\"H\\" has no effect, and methods like str.toUpperCase() return a brand-new string (\\"HELLO\\") without modifying the original string. When you write let x = 10; x = 20;, you are not mutating the number 10 into 20; you are pointing the variable x to a new memory location holding 20."}, {"id": "js-lec-2-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is the difference between null and undefined?", "title": "Q5: What is the difference between null and undefined?", "answer": "undefined means a variable has been declared (or an object property/function return was accessed) but no value has been assigned to it yet—it is the engine's default placeholder. null is an explicit assignment value used by developers to represent \\"no value\\" or an intentionally empty object reference. Also, typeof undefined is \\"undefined\\", while typeof null is \\"object\\".", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "undefined means a variable has been declared (or an object property/function return was accessed) but no value has been assigned to it yet—it is the engine's default placeholder. null is an explicit assignment value used by developers to represent \\"no value\\" or an intentionally empty object reference. Also, typeof undefined is \\"undefined\\", while typeof null is \\"object\\"."}, {"id": "js-lec-2-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: When do you need BigInt in JavaScript, and what is Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER?", "title": "Q6: When do you need BigInt in JavaScript, and what is Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER?", "answer": "Standard JavaScript Numbers use 64-bit IEEE-754 double-precision floats, which allocate 53 bits for the significand (mantissa). Therefore, the largest exact integer JS can safely represent without losing precision is 2^53 - 1 (9,007,199,254,740,991, or Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER). Beyond this limit, expressions like 9007199254740992 + 1 === 9007199254740992 evaluate to true due to precision loss! BigInt (created by appending n, like 9007199254740993n) stores arbitrarily large integers accurately.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Standard JavaScript Numbers use 64-bit IEEE-754 double-precision floats, which allocate 53 bits for the significand (mantissa). Therefore, the largest exact integer JS can safely represent without losing precision is 2^53 - 1 (9,007,199,254,740,991, or Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER). Beyond this limit, expressions like 9007199254740992 + 1 === 9007199254740992 evaluate to true due to precision loss! BigInt (created by appending n, like 9007199254740993n) stores arbitrarily large integers accurately."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nJavaScript is a **dynamically typed** language—you do not declare data types ahead of time (let x = 10 can later hold "Rohit"). Every value in JavaScript fall