` directly mutates `obj1.age` because both variables point to the same underlying object.\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Primitives, `typeof` Quirks & Reference Mutation\n```javascript\n// 1. All 7 Primitive Data Types & typeof operator\nlet num = 42;                         // typeof -> "number"\nlet big = 9007199254740999n;          // typeof -> "bigint"\nlet str = "Coder Army";               // typeof -> "string"\nlet isPlaced = true;                  // typeof -> "boolean"\nlet uninit;                           // typeof -> "undefined"\nlet emptyVal = null;                  // typeof -> "object" (Famous 1995 JS bug!)\nlet sym = Symbol("unique_id");        // typeof -> "symbol"\n\n// 2. Primitive Immutability (Pass by Value behavior)\nlet a = 10;\nlet b = a;\nb = 20;\nconsole.log(a, b); // 10, 20 (a is completely unaffected)\n\nlet name = "Rohit";\nname[0 = "M";     // Ignored silently in non-strict mode\nconsole.log(name); // "Rohit" (Strings are immutable)\n\n// 3. Non-Primitive Mutability (Shared Reference)\nconst user1 = { name: "Rohit", age: 20 };\nconst user2 = user1; // Copies reference address, NOT a new object!\nuser2.age = 25;\nconsole.log(user1.age); // 25 (Both point to the exact same object)\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 2: Variables & Data Types — var, let, const, 7 Primitives vs. Reference Types & Mutability	Master variable declarations (let, const, var), all 7 primitive data types vs. non-primitive reference types, typeof quirks, and why primitives are strictly immutable while objects are mutable.	20	Easy	\N	2	[{"id": "js-lec-2-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What are the exact differences between var, let, and const?", "title": "Q1: What are the exact differences between var, let, and const?", "answer": "1) Scope: var is function-scoped (leaks out of if/for blocks), whereas let and const are strictly block-scoped ({ ... }). 2) Redeclaration: var allows redeclaring the same identifier in the same scope; let