10789	Segment Isolation & Inter-Group Stitching: Reverse Nodes in K-Group Size of a Linked List	dsa-reverse-nodes-in-k-group-linked-list	Reverse linked list nodes k at a time by isolating k-node segments, reversing them in-place, and stitching previous tails to new segment heads in O(N) time.	2026-10-03 15:07:44.961093+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Segment Isolation & Inter-Group Stitching: Reverse Nodes in K-Group Size of a Linked List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lIar1skcQYI", "title": "Lecture 257: Reverse Nodes in k-Group Size of Linked List", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/reverse-nodes-in-k-group/", "title": "Reverse Nodes in k-Group (LC 25)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Given the head of a linked list, reverse the nodes of the list k at a time, leaving leftover nodes (< k) as-is."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/swap-nodes-in-pairs/", "title": "Swap Nodes in Pairs (LC 24)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Special case of Reverse Nodes in k-Group where k = 2."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/reverse-nodes-in-even-length-groups/", "title": "Reverse Nodes in Even Length Groups (LC 2074)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Apply segment isolation and reversal to groups of increasing lengths 1, 2, 3, ... when group length is even."}]	Module 38: Doubly Linked List Medium Problems & Hard Linked List Re-Wiring (K-Group Reversal, Rotation & Multi-Level Flattening)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the `head` of a singly linked list and an integer `k`, reverse the nodes of the list **`k` at a time** in-place and return the modified list. If the number of remaining nodes at the end is **less than `k`**, leave them in their original order without reversing.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (The Modular `getKthNode` + Isolate + Reverse Pattern)\n- **Why We Isolate Each `k`-Group (`kThNode->next = nullptr`):**\n  - Our standard `reverseLinkedList(temp)` reverses until it hits `nullptr`.\n  - So for each group starting at `temp`:\n    1. **Find `kThNode`:** Walk `k - 1` steps from `temp` using `getKthNode(temp, k)`.\n    2. **Incomplete Group Check (`kThNode == nullptr`):** If fewer than `k` nodes remain, attach `if (prevLast != nullptr) prevLast->next = temp;` and **`break`**!\n    3. **Save `nextNode` & Sever the Group:** Save `Node* nextNode = kThNode->next;`, then set **`kThNode->next = nullptr;`** so `[temp ... kThNode]` is an isolated list!\n    4. **Reverse the Isolated Group:** Call `reverseLinkedList(temp);`. Now `kThNode` is the new front of this group and `temp` is the new tail of this group!\n    5. **Stitch to Previous Group (or Update `head`):**\n       - If `temp == head` (first group): update **`head = kThNode;`**.\n       - Else (subsequent groups): link **`prevLast->next = kThNode;`**.\n    6. **Advance Pointers:** Save **`prevLast = temp;`** and move **`temp = nextNode;`**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(2N)` Time (`O(N)` to find `k`-th nodes + `O(N)` to reverse segments) and `O(1)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Reverse Nodes in K-Group Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nNode* reverseLinkedList(Node* head) {\n    Node* temp = head;\n    Node* prev = nullptr;\n    while (temp != nullptr) {\n        Node* front = temp->next;\n        temp->next = prev;\n        prev = temp;\n        temp = front;\n    }\n    return prev;\n}\n\nNode* getKthNode(Node* temp, int k) {\n    k -= 1;\n    while (temp != nullptr && k > 0) {\n        k--;\n        temp = temp->next;\n    }\n    return temp;\n}\n\nNode* kReverse(Node* head, int k) {\n    Node* temp = head;\n    Node* prevLast = nullptr;\n\n    while (temp != nullptr) {\n        Node* kThNode = getKthNode(temp, k);\n        // If remaining nodes are fewer than k, link them as-is and break!\n        if (kThNode == nullptr) {\n            if (prevLast != nullptr) prevLast->next = temp;\n            break;\n        }\n\n        Node* nextNode = kThNode->next; // Preserve start of next group\n        kThNode->next = nullptr;        // Isolate current k-group\n\n        reverseLinkedList(temp);\n\n        if (temp == head) {\n            head = kThNode;             // First group updates overall head\n        } else {\n            prevLast->next = kThNode;   // Stitch previous group's tail to current group's new head\n        }\n\n        prevLast = temp;                // 'temp' is now the tail of the reversed group!\n        temp = nextNode;\n    }\n    return head;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting `if (prevLast != nullptr) prevLast->next = temp;` When `kThNode == nullptr`:** Because the previous group's `kThNode->next` was set to `nullptr` to isolate it, its new tail `prevLast` currently points to `nullptr`! You **must** re-attach `prevLast->next = temp` before breaking so the leftover `< k` tail nodes aren't lost!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22121	Lecture 3: Memory Management Internals — Stack vs. Heap (First Principles), V8 Pointer Tagging & Smi	js-lecture-3-memory-management-stack-vs-heap-v8-pointer-tagging	Deconstruct from first principles how RAM is organized into Stack and Heap, debunk the myth that all primitives live on the Stack, and understand V8 Smi (Small Integers) and Pointer Tagging.	2026-10-06 21:14:25.4193+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMost superficial tutorials claim *"all primitives are stored on the Stack and all objects are stored on the Heap."* In a dynamically typed engine like **Google V8**, that statement is architecturally inaccurate. Understanding how V8 actually manages **Stack**, **Heap**, **Small Integers (`Smi`)**, and **Tagged Pointers** from first principles reveals why JavaScript handles mutability and references the way it does.\n\n### 💡 Why Can't Everything Live Inside a Contiguous Stack?\n* **How the Call Stack Works:** The Stack is a fast, fixed-size, contiguous region of RAM managed in strict LIFO (Last-In, First-Out) order using a CPU stack pointer.\n* **The Dynamic Typing Problem:** In C++, `int a = 10` is guaranteed to always be 4 bytes on the stack. In JavaScript, `let a = 10` can be reassigned on the very next line to a 50-character string or a 10,000-element array! If large strings or dynamic objects were stored directly inline on the contiguous Stack, growing them would require shifting every single variable below them in RAM—destroying performance.\n* **The Solution (Stack Pointers + Dynamic Heap):** Variable bindings (execution frame slots) live on the **Stack**, holding compact memory addresses (pointers) that point to actual values allocated dynamically in the **Heap**.\n\n### 💡 V8 Engine Optimization: `Smi` (Small Integers) & Pointer Tagging\nAllocating a Heap object for every tiny loop counter (`i = 0, 1, 2...`) would be too slow. V8 solves this using **Pointer Tagging** (inspection of the Least Significant Bit, LSB):\n* **Memory Alignment Fact:** On 32-bit/64-bit architectures, word-aligned Heap memory addresses are multiples of 4 or 8, meaning their binary representation always ends in `0`!\n* **Tag Bit (`0` vs. `1`):** V8 uses the last bit of a slot on the Stack as a tag:\n  * **LSB = `0` (`Smi` — Small Integer):** The value is stored **directly inline on the Stack** as a fast unboxed integer (31-bit signed integer on 32-bit systems), avoiding any Heap lookup!\n  * **LSB = `1` (`HeapObject` Pointer):** Indicates that the slot holds a memory address pointing to a **Heap Object** (which includes Strings, Floating-Point `HeapNumber`s, `BigInt`s, `Boolean`/`null`/`undefined` singletons, Arrays, and Objects).\n\n### 🛠️ Visualizing V8 Memory Allocation & Immutability\n```javascript\n// 1. Smi (Small Integer): Stored inline in the Stack frame (Tag bit = 0)\nlet count = 10; \n\n// 2. Heap allocations (Tag bit = 1 -> Points to V8 Heap):\n// Strings, Floats (HeapNumber), and BigInts are allocated on the Heap,\n// but they are IMMUTABLE. Reassigning creates a new Heap entry and updates the Stack pointer:\nlet name = "Rohit";\nlet copyName = name; // Both Stack slots point to the same Heap string "Rohit"\nname = "Mohit";      // Allocates "Mohit" in Heap and updates 'name' pointer; 'copyName' still points to "Rohit"\n\n// 3. Mutable Heap Objects (Arrays & Objects):\n// Mutating a property modifies the Heap block in place WITHOUT changing the Stack pointer!\nlet user1 = { balance: 500 };\nlet user2 = user1;   // Both Stack slots hold the exact same Heap pointer\nuser2.balance = 900; // Modifies the Heap block in place -> user1.balance is now 900!\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Memory_management", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Memory_management", "title": "MDN Web Docs — JavaScript Memory Management & Garbage Collection"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WRBFDQR0oM4", "title": "JavaScript Memory Management Explained: Stack vs Heap (First Principle) | Javascript Full Course #03", "duration": "86m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-3-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Is it true that all Primitive data types are stored on the Stack in V8? How does V8 actually store primitives vs. objects?", "title": "Q1: Is it true that all Primitive data types are stored on the Stack in V8? How does V8 actually store primitives vs. objects?", "answer": "No, that is a common misconception from statically typed languages like C++. Because JavaScript variables are dynamically typed and strings/BigInts can be arbitrarily large, V8 only stores Small Integers (Smi) directly inline inside the Stack frame. Strings, floating-point numbers (HeapNumber), BigInts, and oddballs (null, undefined, true, false singletons) are allocated in the V8 Heap, while the Stack frame holds tagged pointers referencing them. The difference between primitives and objects is not where they live, but that primitives are immutable (reassignment points the Stack slot to a new Heap address) whereas objects are mutable in place.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q1", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "No, that is a common misconception from statically typed languages like C++. Because JavaScript variables are dynamically typed and strings/BigInts can be arbitrarily large, V8 only stores Small Integers (Smi) directly inline inside the Stack frame. Strings, floating-point numbers (HeapNumber), BigInts, and oddballs (null, undefined, true, false singletons) are allocated in the V8 Heap, while the Stack frame holds tagged pointers referencing them. The difference between primitives and objects is not where they live, but that primitives are immutable (reassignment points the Stack slot to a new Heap address) whereas objects are mutable in place."}, {"id": "js-lec-3-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: What is Pointer Tagging in the V8 Engine, and how does V8 distinguish a Small Integer (Smi) from a Heap Pointer?", "title": "Q2: What is Pointer Tagging in the V8 Engine, and how does V8 distinguish a Small Integer (Smi) from a Heap Pointer?", "answer": "Because aligned memory addresses in RAM are multiples of 4 (or 8), their lowest binary bit (Least Significant Bit) is naturally 0. V8 exploits this by using the lowest bit of every value slot as a tag: if the last bit is 0, V8 treats the remaining bits directly as an inline Small Integer (Smi) without dereferencing memory. If the last bit is 1, V8 treats the value as a pointer to an object allocated on the Heap.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q2", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Because aligned memory addresses in RAM are multiples of 4 (or 8), their lowest binary bit (Least Significant Bit) is naturally 0. V8 exploits this by using the lowest bit of every value slot as a tag: if the last bit is 0, V8 treats the remaining bits directly as an inline Small Integer (Smi) without dereferencing memory. If the last bit is 1, V8 treats the value as a pointer to an object allocated on the Heap."}, {"id": "js-lec-3-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: Why can't dynamic objects or variable-length strings be stored directly inline on the Call Stack?", "title": "Q3: Why can't dynamic objects or variable-length strings be stored directly inline on the Call Stack?", "answer": "The Call Stack relies on contiguous memory blocks and fixed offsets so the CPU can push and pop stack frames in O(1) time. If an object or string stored inline in the middle of the Stack grew in size (e.g., adding keys to an object or concatenating a string), it would overwrite adjacent stack frames or require copying and shifting the entire Stack in RAM.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "The Call Stack relies on contiguous memory blocks and fixed offsets so the CPU can push and pop stack frames in O(1) time. If an object or string stored inline in the middle of the Stack grew in size (e.g., adding keys to an object or concatenating a string), it would overwrite adjacent stack frames or require copying and shifting the entire Stack in RAM."}, {"id": "js-lec-3-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: If two variables hold let a = \\"Rohit\\" and let b = a, do they duplicate the string in RAM?", "title": "Q4: If two variables hold let a = \\"Rohit\\" and let b = a, do they duplicate the string in RAM?", "answer": "No. Because strings are immutable, V8 uses string interning and shared references. Both a and b on the Stack point to the exact same \\"Rohit\\" string address in the Heap. Because strings cannot be mutated in place, sharing the address is 100% safe—if b is later reassigned to \\"Mohit\\", b's Stack pointer simply updates to point to a new Heap address without affecting a.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "No. Because strings are immutable, V8 uses string interning and shared references. Both a and b on the Stack point to the exact same \\"Rohit\\" string address in the Heap. Because strings cannot be mutated in place, sharing the address is 100% safe—if b is later reassigned to \\"Mohit\\", b's Stack pointer simply updates to point to a new Heap address without affecting a."}, {"id": "js-lec-3-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: How does Garbage Collection (GC) work in JavaScript's Heap?", "title": "Q5: How does Garbage Collection (GC) work in JavaScript's Heap?", "answer": "V8 uses a Generational Garbage Collector (Orinoco) based on the Mark-and-Sweep algorithm. It divides the Heap into a Young Generation (Nursery/Intermediate for newly allocated short-lived objects, cleaned frequently by the Scavenger) and an Old Generation (for long-lived objects). Starting from \\"roots\\" (active Call Stack variables and global references), the GC marks all reachable Heap objects and sweeps (frees) any unreachable memory blocks.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "V8 uses a Generational Garbage Collector (Orinoco) based on the Mark-and-Sweep algorithm. It divides the Heap into a Young Generation (Nursery/Intermediate for newly allocated short-lived objects, cleaned frequently by the Scavenger) and an Old Generation (for long-lived objects). Starting from \\"roots\\" (active Call Stack variables and global references), the GC marks all reachable Heap objects and sweeps (frees) any unreachable memory blocks."}, {"id": "js-lec-3-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why are null, undefined, true, and false never duplicated in memory no matter how many variables use them?", "title": "Q6: Why are null, undefined, true, and false never duplicated in memory no matter how many variables use them?", "answer": "V8 pre-allocates singletons (called \\"Oddballs\\") in a read-only root section of the Heap for null, undefined, true, and false when the engine boots. Every variable assigned undefined or true simply points to that single read-only address.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "V8 pre-allocates singletons (called \\"Oddballs\\") in a read-only root section of the Heap for null, undefined, true, and false when the engine boots. Every variable assigned undefined or true simply points to that single read-only address."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMost superficial tutorials claim *"all primitives are stored on the Stack and all objects are stored on the Heap."* In a dynamically typed engine like **Google V8**, that statement is architecturally inaccurate. Understanding how V8 actually manages **Stack**, **Heap**, **Small Integers (`Smi`)**, and **Tagged Pointers** from first principles reveals why JavaScript handles mutability and references the way it does.\n\n### 💡 Why Can't Everything Live Inside a Contiguous Stack?\n* **How the Call Stack Works:** The Stack is a fast, fixed-size, contiguous region of RAM managed in strict LIFO (Last-In, First-Out) order using a CPU stack pointer.\n* **The Dynamic Typing Problem:** In C++, `int a = 10` is guaranteed to always be 4 bytes on the stack. In JavaScript, `let a = 10` can be reassigned on the very next line to a 50-character string or a 10,000-element array! If large strings or dynamic objects were stored directly inline on the contiguous Stack, growing them would require shifting every single variable below them in RAM—destroying performance.\n* **The Solution (Stack Pointers + Dynamic Heap):** Variable bindings (execution frame slots) live on the **Stack**, holding compact memory addresses (pointers) that point to actual values allocated dynamically in the **Heap**.\n\n### 💡 V8 Engine Optimization: `Smi` (Small Integers) & Pointer Tagging\nAllocating a Heap object for every tiny loop counter (`i = 0, 1, 2...`) would be too slow. V8 solves this using **Pointer Tagging** (inspection of the Least Significant Bit, LSB):\n* **Memory Alignment Fact:** On 32-bit/64-bit architectures, word-aligned Heap memory addresses are multiples of 4 or 8, meaning their binary representation always ends in `0`!\n* **Tag Bit (`0` vs. `1`):** V8 uses the last bit of a slot on the Stack as a tag:\n  * **LSB = `0` (`Smi` — Small Integer):** The value is stored **directly inline on the Stack** as a fast unboxed integer (31-bit signed integer on 32-bit systems), avoiding any Heap lookup!\n  * **LSB = `1` (`HeapObject` Pointer):** Indicates that the slot holds a memory address pointing to a **Heap Object** (which includes Strings, Floating-Point `HeapNumber`s, `BigInt`s, `Boolean`/`null`/`undefined` singletons, Arrays, and Objects).\n\n### 🛠️ Visualizing V8 Memory Allocation & Immutability\n```javascript\n// 1. Smi (Small Integer): Stored inline in the Stack frame (Tag bit = 0)\nlet count = 10; \n\n// 2. Heap allocations (Tag bit = 1 -> Points to V8 Heap):\n// Strings, Floats (HeapNumber), and BigInts are allocated on the Heap,\n// but they are IMMUTABLE. Reassigning creates a new Heap entry and updates the Stack pointer:\nlet name = "Rohit";\nlet copyName = name; // Both Stack slots point to the same Heap string "Rohit"\nname = "Mohit";      // Allocates "Mohit" in Heap and updates 'name' pointer; 'copyName' still points to "Rohit"\n\n// 3. Mutable Heap Objects (Arrays & Objects):\n// Mutating a property modifies the Heap block in place WITHOUT changing the Stack pointer!\nlet user1 = { balance: 500 };\nlet user2 = user1;   // Both Stack slots hold the exact same Heap pointer\nuser2.balance = 900; // Modifies the Heap block in place -> user1.balance is now 900!\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 3: Memory Management Internals — Stack vs. Heap (First Principles), V8 Pointer Tagging & Smi	Deconstruct from first principles how RAM is organized into Stack and Heap, debunk the myth that all primitives live on the Stack, and understand V8 Smi (Small Integers) and Pointer Tagging.	20	Easy	\N	3	[{"id": "js-lec-3-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Is it true that all Primitive data types are stored on the Stack in V8? How does V8 actually store primitives vs. objects?", "title": "Q1: Is it true that all Primitive data types are stored on the Stack in V8? How does V8 actually store primitives vs. objects?", "answer": "No, that is a common misconception from statically typed languages like C++. Because JavaScript variables are dynamically typed and strings/BigInts can be arbitrarily large, V8 only stores Small Integers (Smi) directly inline inside the Stack frame. Strings, floating-point numbers (HeapNumber), BigInts, and oddballs (null, undefined, true, false singletons) are allocated in the V8 Heap, while the Stack frame holds tagged pointers referencing them. The difference between primitives and objects is not where they live, but that primitives are immutable (reassignment points the Stack slot to a new Heap address) whereas objects are mutable in place.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q1", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "No, that is a common misconception from statically typed languages like C++. Because JavaScript variables are dynamically typed and strings/BigInts can be arbitrarily large, V8 only stores Small Integers (Smi) directly inline inside the Stack frame. Strings, floating-point numbers (HeapNumber), BigInts, and oddballs (null, undefined, true, false singletons) are allocated in the V8 Heap, while the Stack frame holds tagged pointers referencing them. The difference between primitives and objects is not where they live, but that primitives are immutable (reassignment points the Stack slot to a new Heap address) whereas objects are mutable in place."}, {"id": "js-lec-3-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: What is Pointer Tagging in the V8 Engine, and how does V8 distinguish a Small Integer (Smi) from a Heap Pointer?", "title": "Q2: What is Pointer Tagging in the V8 Engine, and how does V8 distinguish a Small Integer (Smi) from a Heap Pointer?", "answer": "Because aligned memory addresses in RAM are multiples of 4 (or 8), their lowest binary bit (Least Significant Bit) is naturally 0. V8 exploits this by using the lowest bit of every value slot as a tag: if the last bit is 0, V8 treats the remaining bits directly as an inline Small Integer (Smi) without dereferencing memory. If the last bit is 1, V8 treats the value as a pointer to an object allocated on the Heap.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q2", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Because aligned memory addresses in RAM are multiples of 4 (or 8), their lowest binary bit (Least Significant Bit) is naturally 0. V8 exploits this by using the lowest bit of every value slot as a tag: if the last bit is 0, V8 treats the remaining bits directly as an inline Small Integer (Smi) without dereferencing memory. If the last bit is 