d shared references. Both a and b on the Stack point to the exact same \\"Rohit\\" string address in the Heap. Because strings cannot be mutated in place, sharing the address is 100% safe—if b is later reassigned to \\"Mohit\\", b's Stack pointer simply updates to point to a new Heap address without affecting a."}, {"id": "js-lec-3-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: How does Garbage Collection (GC) work in JavaScript's Heap?", "title": "Q5: How does Garbage Collection (GC) work in JavaScript's Heap?", "answer": "V8 uses a Generational Garbage Collector (Orinoco) based on the Mark-and-Sweep algorithm. It divides the Heap into a Young Generation (Nursery/Intermediate for newly allocated short-lived objects, cleaned frequently by the Scavenger) and an Old Generation (for long-lived objects). Starting from \\"roots\\" (active Call Stack variables and global references), the GC marks all reachable Heap objects and sweeps (frees) any unreachable memory blocks.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "V8 uses a Generational Garbage Collector (Orinoco) based on the Mark-and-Sweep algorithm. It divides the Heap into a Young Generation (Nursery/Intermediate for newly allocated short-lived objects, cleaned frequently by the Scavenger) and an Old Generation (for long-lived objects). Starting from \\"roots\\" (active Call Stack variables and global references), the GC marks all reachable Heap objects and sweeps (frees) any unreachable memory blocks."}, {"id": "js-lec-3-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why are null, undefined, true, and false never duplicated in memory no matter how many variables use them?", "title": "Q6: Why are null, undefined, true, and false never duplicated in memory no matter how many variables use them?", "answer": "V8 pre-allocates singletons (called \\"Oddballs\\") in a read-only root section of the Heap for null, undefined, true, and false when the engine boots. Every variable assigned undefined or true simply points to that single read-only address.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "V8 pre-allocates singletons (called \\"Oddballs\\") in a read-only root section of the Heap for null, undefined, true, and false when the engine boots. Every variable assigned undefined or true simply points to that single read-only address."}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Memory_management	\N	\N	\N
11588	Square-Root Primality Testing: Check if a Number Is Prime or Not	dsa-check-if-a-number-is-prime-or-not	Master Square-Root Primality Testing: Check if a Number Is Prime or Not with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:12:24.304063+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Square-Root Primality Testing: Check if a Number Is Prime or Not\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MJcckSfoYdI", "title": "Square-Root Primality Testing: Check if a Number Is Prime or Not - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/prime-number2314/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Prime Number", "difficulty": "Basic", "description": "For a given number N, check if it is prime or not in O(sqrt(N)) time."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/three-divisors/", "title": "LeetCode 1952: Three Divisors", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Count the exact number of divisors of n using the sqrt(n) loop and check if the count equals 3."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/prime-in-diagonal/", "title": "LeetCode 2614: Prime In Diagonal", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Apply O(sqrt(N)) primality testing to find the largest prime number on the diagonals of a 2D matrix."}]	Module 39: Mathematical & Number Theory Algorithms: Divisors, Primes, Binary Exponentiation & Sieve Precomputation	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a positive integer `N`, determine whether `N` is a **Prime Number** in **`O(sqrt(N))` time** and **`O(1)` space**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Exact Mathematical Definition of a Prime Number:**\n  - Why is *"a number divisible by 1 and itself"* an incomplete definition? Because `N = 1` is divisible by `1` and itself (`1`), yet **`1` is NOT a prime number**!\n  - **Exact Definition:** A prime number is a natural number greater than `1` that has **EXACTLY `2` distinct positive divisors** (`1` and `N`). Since `1` has only `1` divisor, `1` is not prime!\n- **Counting Divisors in `O(sqrt(N))` Time:**\n  - Initialize `cnt = 0` and loop `i` from `1` while `i * i <= N`.\n  - Whenever `N % i == 0`:\n    - Increment `cnt++` (for divisor `i`).\n    - If `(N / i) != i`, increment `cnt++` (for complementary divisor `N / i`).\n  - After the loop (or breaking early as soon as `cnt > 2`), return **`cnt == 2`**!\n\n### 💻 Check if a Number Is Prime Blueprint\n```cpp\nbool isPrime(int n) {\n    int cnt = 0;\n\n    for (int i = 1; i * i <= n; i++) {\n        if (n % i == 0) {\n            cnt++;\n            if ((n / i) != i) {\n                cnt++;\n            }\n            // Early exit optimization: more than 2 divisors means composite!\n            if (cnt > 2) return false;\n        }\n    }\n    // Returns true ONLY if n has exactly 2 divisors (automatically false for n = 1!)\n    return cnt == 2;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Returning `true` for `n = 1`:** If you loop from `i = 2` to `i * i <= n` and return `true` at the end without an explicit `if (n <= 1) return false;` check, your function will falsely claim `1` is prime! Using `cnt == 2` (or checking `if (n <= 1) return false;` upfront) handles `n = 1` cleanly.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
