4717	Shortest-Path DP Counting: Number of Ways to Arrive at Destination	dsa-number-of-ways-to-arrive-at-destination	Count the number of distinct shortest paths from source to destination modulo 10^9 + 7 by combining Dijkstra with dynamic programming.	2026-10-03 11:07:15.301021+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Shortest-Path DP Counting: Number of Ways to Arrive at Destination\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_-0mx0SmYxA", "title": "Lecture 165: Number of Ways to Arrive at Destination", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-ways-to-arrive-at-destination/", "title": "Number of Ways to Arrive at Destination (LC 1976)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the number of ways you can arrive at intersection n - 1 from 0 in the shortest amount of time modulo 10^9 + 7."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/number-of-ways-to-arrive-at-destination/1", "title": "Number of Ways to Arrive at Destination", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count the total distinct shortest paths from node 0 to node n - 1 in a weighted undirected graph."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/all-paths-from-source-to-target/", "title": "All Paths From Source to Target (LC 797)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Contrast enumerating all paths with counting shortest paths via DP + Dijkstra."}]	Module 24: Shortest Path Algorithms in Graphs: DAG Relaxation, BFS & Dijkstra's Algorithm	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an undirected weighted graph of `n` intersections (`0` to `n - 1`), find the **number of distinct shortest paths** from intersection `0` to intersection `n - 1` modulo `10^9 + 7`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Combining Dijkstra's `dist[]` With a DP `ways[]` Array:**\n  - Maintain `dist[n]` initialized to `LLONG_MAX` and `ways[n]` initialized to `0`.\n  - Base case at source `0`: `dist[0] = 0` and **`ways[0] = 1`**.\n  - When relaxing edge `u → v` with weight `wt`:\n    1. **Strictly Shorter Path Found (`d + wt < dist[v]`):**\n       - Update `dist[v] = d + wt`.\n       - Reset ways to match `u`: **`ways[v] = ways[u]`**!\n       - Push `{dist[v], v}` into the min-heap.\n    2. **Another Equal Shortest Path Found (`d + wt == dist[v]`):**\n       - Do **not** push `v` into the heap again!\n       - Simply add `u`'s ways into `v`: **`ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % MOD`**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(E log V)` Time and `O(V + E)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Number of Ways to Arrive at Destination Blueprint\n```cpp\nint countPaths(int n, vector<vector<int>> &roads) {\n    vector<pair<int, long long>> adj[n];\n    for (const auto &r : roads) {\n        adj[r[0]].push_back({r[1], r[2]});\n        adj[r[1]].push_back({r[0], r[2]});\n    }\n\n    priority_queue<pair<long long, int>,\n                   vector<pair<long long, int>>,\n                   greater<pair<long long, int>>> pq;\n\n    vector<long long> dist(n, LLONG_MAX);\n    vector<long long> ways(n, 0);\n    long long mod = 1e9 + 7;\n\n    dist[0] = 0;\n    ways[0] = 1;\n    pq.push({0, 0});\n\n    while (!pq.empty()) {\n        auto [d, u] = pq.top();\n        pq.pop();\n\n        if (d > dist[u]) continue;\n\n        for (auto [v, wt] : adj[u]) {\n            if (d + wt < dist[v]) {\n                dist[v] = d + wt;\n                pq.push({dist[v], v});\n                ways[v] = ways[u]; // Reset ways to ways[u]\n            } else if (d + wt == dist[v]) {\n                ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % mod; // Accumulate additional shortest paths\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return ways[n - 1] % mod;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **32-Bit Integer Overflow on `dist`:** Edge weights in LeetCode 1976 can be up to `10^9`, so path distances easily exceed `2 * 10^9` (`INT_MAX`). Always use **`long long`** and **`LLONG_MAX`** for `dist` and the priority queue!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
21126	Lecture 13: Relational Modeling — Foreign Keys, Many-to-Many Junction Tables & ON DELETE CASCADE	pg-lecture-13-foreign-keys-junction-tables-on-delete-cascade	Design 1:N and M:N relationships, resolve Many-to-Many associations using Junction (Bridge) Tables, prevent Orphan Records, and choose between ON DELETE CASCADE and SET NULL.	2026-10-05 17:59:36.035454+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Lecture 13: Relational Modeling — Foreign Keys, Many-to-Many Junction Tables & ON DELETE CASCADE\nPart of the **PostgreSQL & Relational SQL Mastery** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xQDEftYpjmw", "title": "PostgreSQL Foreign Key Explained | Junction Tables, Many To many Relationships & ON DELETE CASCADE", "duration": "21m", "creator_name": "Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical"}]	[{"id": "pg-13-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/", "name": "Recyclable and Low Fat Products", "title": "Recyclable and Low Fat Products", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "13_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Recyclable and Low Fat Products\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/"}, {"id": "pg-13-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "name": "Find Customer Referee", "title": "Find Customer Referee", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "13_2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Find Customer Referee\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/"}, {"id": "pg-13-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "name": "Big Countries", "title": "Big Countries", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "13_3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Big Countries\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/"}, {"id": "pg-13-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/", "name": "Article Views I", "title": "Article Views I", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "13_4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Article Views I\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/"}, {"id": "pg-13-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/", "name": "Invalid Tweets", "title": "Invalid Tweets", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "13_5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Invalid Tweets\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/"}, {"id": "pg-13-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/", "name": "Combine Two Tables", "title": "Combine Two Tables", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "13_6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Combine Two Tables\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/"}, {"id": "pg-13-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "name": "Customers Who Never Order", "title": "Customers Who Never Order", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "13_7", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Customers Who Never Order\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/"}, {"id": "pg-13-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "name": "Duplicate Emails", "title": "Duplicate Emails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "13_8", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Duplicate Emails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/"}, {"id": "pg-13-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "name": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "title": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "13_9", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Employees Earning More Than Their Managers\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/"}, {"id": "pg-13-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "name": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "title": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "13_10", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Delete Duplicate Emails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nRelational databases derive their power from connecting separate entity tables cleanly without duplicating data. This lecture covers **Foreign Keys**, **Referential Integrity**, **Orphan Record Prevention**, and resolving **Many-to-Many ($M:N$) Relationships** using **Junction (Bridge) Tables**.\n\n### 🔗 1. Foreign Keys & One-to-Many ($1:N$) Relationships\nA **Foreign Key (FK)** is a column in a child table (e.g., `users.department_id`) that references the **Primary Key (PK)** of a parent table (`departments.id`).\n* **Rule:** Always declare Foreign Key columns as `INT` or `BIGINT` (matching the parent PK's underlying type), **never `SERIAL`**, because the child column stores an existing ID from the parent table rather than auto-incrementing its own sequence.\n\n### 🌉 2. Solving Many-to-Many ($M:N$) with a Junction (Bridge) Table\nConsider `users` (students) and `courses`:\n* Can **1 student** enroll in **many courses**? **Yes.**\n* Can **1 course** have **many students**? **Yes.**\n\nNeither table can store multiple IDs in a single cell (`101, 102, 103` violates First Normal Form). We solve this by creating a third **Junction / Bridge Table** (`enrollments`) holding two Foreign Keys:\n\n```text\nusers (id PK)  1 ───────< enrollments (user_id FK, course_id FK) >─────── 1  courses (id PK)\n```\n\n### 🗑️ 3. Orphan Records & Deletion Policies (`ON DELETE CASCADE` vs. `SET NULL`)\nAn **Orphan Record** occurs when a parent row is deleted, leaving child rows pointing to a non-existent ID. PostgreSQL prevents this via referential actions:\n\n* ****`ON DELETE RESTRICT`** *(Default)*:** Blocks deletion of the parent if any child rows exist. — Preventing accidental deletion of a Department that still has active Users.\n* **`ON DELETE CASCADE`:** Automatically deletes all matching child rows. — `enrollments`: If a student or course is deleted, remove their enrollment link rows.\n* **`ON DELETE SET NULL`:** Keeps the child row but sets its Foreign Key column to `NULL`. — `payments` / `invoices`: Even if a user deletes their account, financial records must be retained for tax/audit compliance.	Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)	https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical	654	Lecture 13: Relational Modeling — Foreign Keys, Many-to-Many Junction Tables & ON DELETE CASCADE	Design 1:N and M:N relationships, resolve Many-to-Many associations using Junction (Bridge) Tables, prevent Orphan Records, and choose between ON DELETE CASCADE and SET NULL.	21	Medium	Module 4: Schema Integrity, Keys & Relationships	13	[{"id": "pg-13-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/", "name": "Recyclable and Low Fat Products", "title": "Recyclable and Low Fat Products", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "13_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Recyclable and Low Fat Products\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/"}, {"id": "pg-13-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "name": "Find Customer Referee", "title": "Find Customer Referee", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "13_2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Find Customer Referee\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/"}, {"id": "pg-13-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "name": "Big Countries", "title": "Big Countries", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "13_3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Big Countries\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/"}, {"id": "pg-13-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/", "name": "Article Views I