s-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "name": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "title": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "13_9", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Employees Earning More Than Their Managers\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/"}, {"id": "pg-13-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "name": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "title": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "13_10", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Delete Duplicate Emails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/"}]	https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-constraints.html#DDL-CONSTRAINTS-FK	\N	\N	\N
22123	Lecture 5: Numbers, Math Object & Random Generation — toFixed, toPrecision, PRNG Seeds & CSPRNG Security	js-lecture-5-numbers-math-object-random-number-generation-prng	Format numbers with toFixed() and toPrecision(), master Math methods, derive the universal bounded random integer formula, and understand why Math.random() is unsafe for OTPs or gambling apps.	2026-10-06 21:14:25.4193+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThis lecture explores JavaScript's **`Number`** methods, the built-in **`Math`** object, how to derive bounded random integer formulas from first principles, and how Pseudo-Random Number Generators (**PRNGs**) work under the hood.\n\n### 💡 Essential `Number` Formatting Methods\n* **`num.toFixed(digits)`:** Formats a number to `digits` decimal places after the dot and **returns a `String`** (e.g., `(10.5678).toFixed(2)` -> `"10.57"`). Convert back using `Number(...)` if further math is needed.\n* **`num.toPrecision(totalDigits)`:** Formats a number to a specified **total number of significant digits** (before + after decimal) and returns a `String` (e.g., `(10.5678).toPrecision(3)` -> `"10.6"`).\n* **`num.toString(radix)`:** Converts a number to a string in base `radix` (e.g., `(10).toString(2)` -> `"1010"` in binary).\n* **Avoid `new Number(10)`:** Creates a Heap wrapper `Object` (`typeof new Number(10) === "object"`), causing `new Number(10) === new Number(10)` to evaluate to `false` because it compares two distinct object memory addresses!\n\n### 💡 Deriving the Universal Random Integer Formula (`min` to `max` Inclusive)\n* **`Math.random()`:** Returns a floating-point number in the half-open interval `[0, 1)` (includes `0`, strictly less than `1`, e.g., `0.0` to `0.999999...`).\n* **0 to `N - 1` (e.g., `0` to `9`):** Multiply by `10` (`[0, 9.999...)`) and apply `Math.floor()`: `Math.floor(Math.random() * 10)`.\n* **1 to `N` (e.g., Dice Roll `1` to `6`):** `Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1`.\n* **Universal Formula for Any Range `[min, max` Inclusive:**\n  * Total number of possible outcomes = `(max - min + 1)`.\n  * Shift starting offset by `+ min`:\n  * **`Math.floor(Math.random() * (max - min + 1)) + min`**\n\n### 💡 Why `Math.random()` Is Predictable (PRNG Internals & Security)\n* Deterministic computers cannot generate true randomness purely from math; `Math.random()` uses a **Pseudo-Random Number Generator (PRNG)** (such as xorshift128+ in V8) initialized with a starting **Seed** value (often derived from system clock/state).\n* Because the next output is computed deterministically from the internal state (`next = (a * seed + c) % m`), an attacker who observes a sequence of outputs can reverse-engineer the seed and predict every future "random" number!\n* **Never use `Math.random()` for OTPs, Password Reset Tokens, or Real-Money Gaming/Poker.** Instead, use a Cryptographically Secure PRNG (**CSPRNG**) like **`crypto.getRandomValues()`** or **`crypto.randomInt()`** (which harvests hardware entropy like CPU thermal noise, mouse jitter, and disk I/O timing).\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Number Methods, Math & Random Range Formula\n```javascript\n// 1. Number formatting methods (Note: they return Strings!)\nconst price = 245.6789;\nconsole.log(price.toFixed(2));      // "245.68" (String)\nconsole.log(price.toPrecision(4));  // "245.7"  (4 total significant digits)\n\n// 2. Core Math methods\nconsole.log(Math.floor(4.9));       // 4\nconsole.log(Math.ceil(4.1));        // 5\nconsole.log(Math.round(4.5));       // 5\nconsole.log(Math.trunc(-4.9));      // -4 (strips decimal, whereas Math.floor(-4.9) is -5)\n\n// 3. Universal Random Integer Generator between [min, max inclusive\nfunction getRandomIntInclusive(min, max) {\n  return Math.floor(Math.random() * (max - min + 1)) + min;\n}\n\n// Dice roll (1 to 6):\nconsole.log(getRandomIntInclusive(1, 6));\n// 4-Digit OTP range (1000 to 9999):\nconsole.log(getRandomIntInclusive(1000, 9999));\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Math", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Math", "title": "MDN Web Docs — Math Built-In Object Reference"}, {"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Number", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Number", "title": "MDN Web Docs — Number Built-In Object Reference"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HTcT-TuraHM", "title": "JavaScript Numbers & Math Object Explained | Random Number Generation | Javascript Full Course #05", "duration": "58m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-5-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Derive the formula to generate a random integer between min and max (both inclusive) using Math.random(). Why do we use Math.floor() instead of Math.round()?", "title": "Q1: Derive the formula to generate a random integer between min and max (both inclusive) using Math.random(). Why do we use Math.floor() instead of Math.round()?", "answer": "The formula is Math.floor(Math.random() * (max - min + 1)) + min. Since Math.random() returns [0, 1), multiplying by total outcomes (max - min + 1) scales the range to [0, max - min + 0.9999...). Applying Math.floor() maps every integer bucket to an equal width of 1.0 (0 to max - min), and adding min shifts the range to [min, max]. Using Math.round() instead of Math.floor() would produce an uneven distribution where the boundary numbers (min and max) have only half the probability (0.5 bucket width) of the middle numbers!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "The formula is Math.floor(Math.random() * (max - min + 1)) + min. Since Math.random() returns [0, 1), multiplying by total outcomes (max - min + 1) scales the range to [0, max - min + 0.9999...). Applying Math.floor() maps every integer bucket to an equal width of 1.0 (0 to max - min), and adding min shifts the range to [min, max]. Using Math.round() instead of Math.floor() would produce an uneven distribution where the boundary numbers (min and max) have only half the probability (0.5 bucket width) of the middle numbers!"}, {"id": "js-lec-5-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why should you NEVER use Math.random() to generate OTPs, session tokens, or card shuffles in a real-money game?", "title": "Q2: Why should you NEVER use Math.random() to generate OTPs, session tokens, or card shuffles in a real-money game?", "answer": "Because Math.random() is a Pseudo-Random Number Generator (PRNG, specifically xorshift128+ in V8) optimized for speed, not cryptographic unpredictability. Given a small sample of consecutive outputs from Math.random(), an attacker can mathematically reconstruct the internal state/seed of the PRNG and predict 100% of future outputs. For security-sensitive tasks, always use Web Crypto API (crypto.getRandomValues() or Node's crypto.randomInt()), which uses OS-level hardware entropy (mouse movements, thermal sensors, interrupt timings).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q2", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Because Math.random() is a Pseudo-Random Number Generator (PRNG, specifically xorshift128+ in V8) optimized for speed, not cryptographic unpredictability. Given a small sample of consecutive outputs from Math.random(), an attacker can mathematically reconstruct the internal state/seed of the PRNG and predict 100% of future outputs. For security-sensitive tasks, always use Web Crypto API (crypto.getRandomValues() or Node's crypto.randomInt()), which uses OS-level hardware entropy (mouse movements, thermal sensors, interrupt timings)."}, {"id": "js-lec-5-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is the return type of (123.456).toFixed(2), and what is the difference between toFixed() and toPrecision()?", "title": "Q3: What is the return type of (123.456).toFixed(2), and what is the difference between toFixed() and toPrecision()?", "answer": "Both toFixed() and toPrecision() return a String, not a Number! toFixed(n) specifies the number of digits strictly after the decimal point (e.g., (123.456).toFixed(2) -> \\"123.46\\"). toPrecision(n) specifies the total number of significant digits across the entire number before and after the decimal point (e.g., (123.456).toPrecision(4) -> \\"123.5\\").", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Both toFixed() and toPrecision() return a String, not a Number! toFixed(n) specifies the number of digits strictly after the decimal point (e.g., (123.456).toFixed(2) -> \\"123.46\\"). toPrecision(n) specifies the total number of significant digits across the entire number before and after the decimal point (e.g., (123.456).toPrecision(4) -> \\"123.5\\")."}, {"id": "js-lec-5-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why does new Number(20) == new Number(20) return false, while Number(\\"20\\") === 20 returns true?", "title": "Q4: Why does new Number(20) == new Number(20) return false, while Number(\\"20\\") === 20 returns true?", "answer": "Calling Number(\\"20\\") without the new keyword acts as a type-conversion function and returns a primitive number 20. Calling new Number(20) with the new keyword constructs a heap-allocated Number wrapper Object. When comparing two objects with == or ===, JavaScript compares their memory references, and two distinct new Number(20) allocations have different memory addresses.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Calling Number(\\"20\\") without the new keyword acts as a type-conversion function and returns a primitive number 20. Calling new Number(20) with the new keyword constructs a heap-allocated Number wrapper Object. When comparing two objects with == or ===, JavaScript compares their memory references, and two distinct new Number(20) allocations have different memory addresses."}, {"id": "js-lec-5-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is the difference between Math.floor() and Math.trunc() on negative numbers like -4.7?", "title": "Q5: What is the difference between Math.floor() and Math.trunc() on negative numbers like -4.7?", "answer": "Math.floor(-4.7) rounds down toward negative infinity, returning -5. Math.trunc(-4.7) simply strips the fractional decimal digits toward zero, returning -4.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Math.floor(-4.7) rounds down toward negative infinity, returning -5. Math.trunc(-4.7) simply strips the fractional decimal digits toward zero, returning -4."}, {"id": "js-lec-5-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why does calling a method on a number literal like 42.toFixed(2) throw a SyntaxError, whereas (42).toFixed(2) or 42..toFixed(2) works?", "title": "Q6: Why does calling a method on a number literal like 42.toFixed(2) throw a SyntaxError, whereas (42).toFixed(2) or 42..toFixed(2) works?", "answer": "In JavaScript grammar, the first dot after an integer literal (42.) is parsed as a decimal point for a floating-point number, leaving no dot for property access. Wrapping the number in parentheses (42).toFixed(2) or using two dots 42..toFixed(2) disambiguates the decimal point from the member-access dot operator, allowing JS to auto-box the primitive into a temporary Number object.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "In JavaScript grammar, the first dot after an integer literal (42.) is parsed as a decimal point for a floating-point number, leaving no dot for property access. Wrapping the number in parentheses (42).toFixed(2) or using two dots 42..toFixed(2) disambiguates the decimal point from the member-access dot operator, allowing JS to auto-box the primitive into a temporary Number object."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThis lecture explores JavaScript's **`Number`** methods, the built-in **`Math`** object, how to derive bounded random integer formulas from first principles, and how Pseudo-Random Number Generators (**PRNGs**) work under the hood.\n\n### 💡 Essential `Number` Formatting Methods\n* **`num.toFixed(digits)`:** Formats a number to `digits` decimal places after the dot and **returns a `String`** (e.g., `(10.5678).toFixed(2)` -> `"10.57"`). Convert back using `Number(...)` if further math is needed.\n* **`num.toPrecision(totalDigits)`:** Formats a number to a specified **total number of significant digits** (before + after decimal) and returns a `String` (e.g., `(10.5678).toPrecision(3)` -> `"10.6"`).\n* **`num.toString(radix)`:** Converts a number to a string in base `radix` (e.g., `(10).toString(2)` -> `"1010"` in binary).\n* **Avoid `new Number(10)`:** Creates a Heap wrapper `Object` (`typeof new Number(10) === "object"`), causing `new Number(10) === new Number(10)` to evaluate to `false` because it compares two distinct object memory addresses!\n\n### 💡 Deriving the Universal Random Integer Formula (`min` to `max` Inclusive)\n* **`Math.random()`:** Returns a floating-point number in the half-open interval `[0, 1)` (includes `0`, strictly less than `1`, e.g., `0.0` to `0.999999...`).\n* **0 to `N - 1` (e.g., `0` to `9`):** Multiply by `10` (`[0, 9.999...)`) and apply `Math.floor()`: `Math.floor(Math.random() * 10)`.\n* **1 to `N` (e.g., Dice Roll `1` to `6`):** `Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1`.\n* **Universal Formula for Any Range `[min, max` Inclusive:**\n  * Total number of possible outcomes = `(max - min + 1)`.\n  * Shift starting offset by `+ min`:\n  * **`Math.floor(Math.random() * (max - min + 1)) + min`**\n\n### 💡 Why `Math.random()` Is Predictable (PRNG Internals & Security)\n* Deterministic computers cannot generate true randomness purely from math; `Math.random()` uses a **Pseudo-Random Number Generator (PRNG)** (such as xorshift128+ in V8) initialized with a starting **Seed** value (often derived from system clock/state).\n* Because the next output is computed deterministically from the internal state (`next = (a * seed + c) % m`), an attacker who observes a sequence of outputs can reverse-engineer the seed and predict every future "random" number!\n* **Never use `Math.random()` for OTPs, Password Reset Tokens, or Real-Money Gaming/Poker.** Instead, use a Cryptographically Secure PRNG (**CSPRNG**) like **`crypto.getRandomValues()`** or **`crypto.randomInt()`** (which harvests hardware entropy like CPU thermal noise, mouse jitter, and disk I/O timing).\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Number Methods, Math & Random Range Formula\n```javascript\n// 1. Number formatting methods (Note: they return Strings!)\nconst price = 245.6789;\nconsole.log(price.toFixed(2));      // "245.68" (String)\nconsole.log(price.toPrecision(4));  // "245.7"  (4 total significant digits)\n\n// 2. Core Math methods\nconsole.log(Math.floor(4.9));       // 4\nconsole.log(Math.ceil(4.1));        // 5\nconsole.log(Math.round(4.5));       // 5\nconsole.log(Math.trunc(-4.9));      // -4 (strips decimal, whereas Math.floor(-4.9) is -5)\n\n// 3. Universal Random Integer Generator between [min, max inclusive\nfunction getRandomIntInclusive(min, max) {\n  return Math.floor(Math.random() * (max - min + 1)) + min;\n}\n\n// Dice roll (1 to 6):\nconsole.log(getRandomIntInclusive(1, 6));\n// 4-Digit OTP range (1000 to 9999):\nconsole.log(getRandomIntInclusive(1000, 9999));\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 5: Numbers, Math Object & Random Generation — toFixed, toPrecision, PRNG Seeds & CSPRNG Security	Format numbers with toFixed() and toPrecision(), master Math methods, derive the universal bounded random integer formula, and understand why Math.random() is unsafe for OTPs or gambling apps.	20	Easy	\N	5	[{"id": "js-lec-5-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Derive the formula to generate a random integer between min 