fter the decimal point (e.g., (123.456).toPrecision(4) -> \\"123.5\\")."}, {"id": "js-lec-5-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why does new Number(20) == new Number(20) return false, while Number(\\"20\\") === 20 returns true?", "title": "Q4: Why does new Number(20) == new Number(20) return false, while Number(\\"20\\") === 20 returns true?", "answer": "Calling Number(\\"20\\") without the new keyword acts as a type-conversion function and returns a primitive number 20. Calling new Number(20) with the new keyword constructs a heap-allocated Number wrapper Object. When comparing two objects with == or ===, JavaScript compares their memory references, and two distinct new Number(20) allocations have different memory addresses.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Calling Number(\\"20\\") without the new keyword acts as a type-conversion function and returns a primitive number 20. Calling new Number(20) with the new keyword constructs a heap-allocated Number wrapper Object. When comparing two objects with == or ===, JavaScript compares their memory references, and two distinct new Number(20) allocations have different memory addresses."}, {"id": "js-lec-5-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is the difference between Math.floor() and Math.trunc() on negative numbers like -4.7?", "title": "Q5: What is the difference between Math.floor() and Math.trunc() on negative numbers like -4.7?", "answer": "Math.floor(-4.7) rounds down toward negative infinity, returning -5. Math.trunc(-4.7) simply strips the fractional decimal digits toward zero, returning -4.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Math.floor(-4.7) rounds down toward negative infinity, returning -5. Math.trunc(-4.7) simply strips the fractional decimal digits toward zero, returning -4."}, {"id": "js-lec-5-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why does calling a method on a number literal like 42.toFixed(2) throw a SyntaxError, whereas (42).toFixed(2) or 42..toFixed(2) works?", "title": "Q6: Why does calling a method on a number literal like 42.toFixed(2) throw a SyntaxError, whereas (42).toFixed(2) or 42..toFixed(2) works?", "answer": "In JavaScript grammar, the first dot after an integer literal (42.) is parsed as a decimal point for a floating-point number, leaving no dot for property access. Wrapping the number in parentheses (42).toFixed(2) or using two dots 42..toFixed(2) disambiguates the decimal point from the member-access dot operator, allowing JS to auto-box the primitive into a temporary Number object.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "In JavaScript grammar, the first dot after an integer literal (42.) is parsed as a decimal point for a floating-point number, leaving no dot for property access. Wrapping the number in parentheses (42).toFixed(2) or using two dots 42..toFixed(2) disambiguates the decimal point from the member-access dot operator, allowing JS to auto-box the primitive into a temporary Number object."}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Math	\N	\N	\N
22124	Lecture 6: Strings & Date Object In-Depth — Template Literals, String Methods, Unix Epoch & UTC vs. Local Time	js-lecture-6-strings-and-date-object-utc-epoch-methods	Master ES6 template literals, essential string manipulation methods (slice, substring, replaceAll, split, trim), and JavaScript Date internals (Unix Epoch milliseconds, 0-indexed months, and UTC vs. IST local time).	2026-10-06 21:14:25.4193+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nStrings and Dates are two of the most heavily used data structures in web development. This lecture covers modern **ES6 Template Literals**, non-mutating **String methods**, and how JavaScript's **`Date`** object represents time internally using **Unix Epoch milliseconds** and **UTC (Coordinated Universal Time)**.\n\n### 💡 Modern Strings & Essential String Methods\nBecause strings are **immutable primitives**, every string method returns a **brand-new string** without modifying the original string:\n* **Template Literals (` `...` `):** Backtick strings support multi-line text without syntax errors and inline expression interpolation via `${expression}`.\n* **Searching:** `str.includes("sub")`, `str.indexOf("sub")`, `str.lastIndexOf("sub")`, `str.startsWith()`, `str.endsWith()`.\n* **Extracting Substrings:**\n  * **`str.slice(start, end)`:** Extracts from `start` up to (but not including) `end`. **Supports negative indices** (e.g., `str.slice(-4)` extracts the last 4 characters).\n  * **`str.substring(start, end)`:** Similar to `slice`, but **treats negative indices as `0`** and automatically swaps `start` and `end` if `start > end`.\n* **Transforming:** `str.toUpperCase()`, `str.toLowerCase()`, `str.trim()` / `trimStart()` / `trimEnd()`, `str.replace(old, new)`, `str.replaceAll(old, new)`, and `str.split(delimiter)` (converts a string into an Array).\n\n### 💡 JavaScript `Date` Internals: Unix Epoch & UTC vs. Local Time\n* **Unix Epoch Milliseconds:** Internally, a JavaScript `Date` is a single integer counting milliseconds elapsed since **January 1, 1970, 00:00:00 UTC**. Calling `Date.now()` or `new Date().getTime()` returns this exact millisecond timestamp, which is completely timezone-independent (ideal for countdown timers and database timestamps).\n* **Quirk — Months Are 0-Indexed in Numeric Constructors:**\n  * When passing numbers `new Date(2025, 9, 18)`, month `0` is January and month `9` is **October**!\n  * However, when passing an ISO string `new Date("2025-10-18")`, month `10` is **October** (1-indexed).\n* **UTC vs. Local Time (e.g., IST = UTC + 5:30):** Servers and databases always communicate in **UTC** (ending in `Z`, e.g., `2025-10-01T12:00:00.000Z`). When a browser renders that UTC date using `.toString()` or `.toLocaleString()`, it automatically converts it to the user's local OS timezone (adding `+5 hours 30 minutes` in India).\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: String Manipulation & Date/Timezone Handling\n```javascript\n// 1. Template Literals & String Extraction (slice vs. substring)\nconst course = "Strike is coming soon";\nconsole.log(`Announcement: ${course.toUpperCase()}!`);\n\nconsole.log(course.slice(0, 6));      // "Strike"\nconsole.log(course.slice(-4));        // "soon" (negative index counts from end)\nconsole.log(course.substring(-4, 6)); // "Strike" (negative treated as 0)\n\n// 2. Replace, Split, and Trim\nconst rawInput = "   rohit,mohit,aman   ";\nconst namesArray = rawInput.trim().split(",");\nconsole.log(namesArray); // ["rohit", "mohit", "aman"\n\n// 3. Date Object, Unix Epoch Milliseconds & 0-Indexed Months\nconst nowMs = Date.now(); // Milliseconds since Jan 1, 1970 UTC\nconst octDateNumeric = new Date(2025, 9, 18); // Month 9 = October!\nconst octDateString = new Date("2025-10-18T00:00:00Z"); // String = 1-indexed October\n\nconsole.log(octDateString.toISOString());  // Always UTC ("...Z")\nconsole.log(octDateString.toString());     // Converted to local timezone (e.g., IST +05:30)\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/String", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/String", "title": "MDN Web Docs — String Built-In Object Reference"}, {"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Date", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Date", "title": "MDN Web Docs — Date Built-In Object Reference"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8dgsUGEgPXo", "title": "JavaScript Strings & Dates Explained In-Depth | Javascript Full Course #06", "duration": "66m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-6-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What is the difference between String.prototype.slice() and String.prototype.substring()?", "title": "Q1: What is the difference between String.prototype.slice() and String.prototype.substring()?", "answer": "Both extract characters from startIndex up to (but excluding) endIndex, but they differ in two ways: 1) Negative arguments: slice() accepts negative indices and counts backward from the end of the string (e.g., \\"Rohit\\".slice(-2) -> \\"it\\"), whereas substring() clamps any negative number or NaN to 0. 2) Swapped arguments: if start > end (e.g., (4, 1)), substring(4, 1) automatically swaps them to substring(1, 4), whereas slice(4, 1) returns an empty string \\"\\".", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Both extract characters from startIndex up to (but excluding) endIndex, but they differ in two ways: 1) Negative arguments: slice() accepts negative indices and counts backward from the end of the string (e.g., \\"Rohit\\".slice(-2) -> \\"it\\"), whereas substring() clamps any negative number or NaN to 0. 2) Swapped arguments: if start > end (e.g., (4, 1)), substring(4, 1) automatically swaps them to substring(1, 4), whereas slice(4, 1) returns an empty string \\"\\"."}, {"id": "js-lec-6-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: How can primitives like strings have methods like \\"rohit\\".toUpperCase() if primitives are not objects?", "title": "Q2: How can primitives like strings have methods like \\"rohit\\".toUpperCase() if primitives are not objects?", "answer": "JavaScript uses a mechanism called Auto-Boxing (Primitive Wrapper Objects). Whenever you access a property or method on a string, number, or boolean primitive, the JS engine momentarily wraps the primitive inside a temporary new String(str) object in memory, executes the method from String.prototype, returns the new result, and immediately discards the temporary wrapper object.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "JavaScript uses a mechanism called Auto-Boxing (Primitive Wrapper Objects). Whenever you access a property or method on a string, number, or boolean primitive, the JS engine momentarily wraps the primitive inside a temporary new String(str) object in memory, executes the method from String.prototype, returns the new result, and immediately discards the temporary wrapper object."}, {"id": "js-lec-6-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: Why does new Date(2025, 10, 1) create a date in November instead of October, while new Date(\\"2025-10-01\\") creates a date in October?", "title": "Q3: Why does new Date(2025, 10, 1) create a date in November instead of October, while new Date(\\"2025-10-01\\") creates a date in October?", "answer": "JavaScript's Date constructor inherited C/Java's legacy struct tm convention where numeric month arguments are 0-indexed (0 = January, 9 = October, 10 = November, 11 = December), while days of the month are 1-indexed (1–31). However, ISO-8601 date strings (\\"2025-10-01\\") follow standard human calendar numbering (01 = January, 10 = October).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "JavaScript's Date constructor inherited C/Java's legacy struct tm convention where numeric month arguments are 0-indexed (0 = January, 9 = October, 10 = November, 11 = December), while days of the month are 1-indexed (1–31). However, ISO-8601 date strings (\\"2025-10-01\\") follow standard human calendar numbering (01 = January, 10 = October)."}, {"id": "js-lec-6-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why should backend APIs always store and transmit timestamps in UTC (ISO-8601) or Unix Epoch milliseconds?", "title": "Q4: Why should backend APIs always store and transmit timestamps in UTC (ISO-8601) or Unix Epoch milliseconds?", "answer": "Users and servers are distributed across different timezones (e.g., a server in US-East serving users in India at UTC+5:30 and London at UTC+0). Storing UTC timestamps (like 2025-10-18T14:00:00Z or Date.now() epoch milliseconds) provides a single unambiguous point in time. When sent to the browser, the client's Date object automatically renders that UTC timestamp in the user's local OS timezone.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Users and servers are distributed across different timezones (e.g., a server in US-East serving users in India at UTC+5:30 and London at UTC+0). Storing UTC timestamps (like 2025-10-18T14:00:00Z or Date.now() epoch milliseconds) provides a single unambiguous point in time. When sent to the browser, the client's Date object automatically renders that UTC timestamp in the user's local OS timezone."}, {"id": "js-lec-6-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is the difference between str.replace() and str.replaceAll()?", "title": "Q5: What is the difference between str.replace() and str.replaceAll()?", "answer": "When passed a string pattern, str.replace(\\"a\\", \\"b\\") replaces ONLY the very first occurrence of \\"a\\" (unless a global regex /a/g is used). str.replaceAll(\\"a\\", \\"b\\") (introduced in ES2021) replaces every occurrence of the substring across the entire string without requiring a regular expression.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "When passed a string pattern, str.replace(\\"a\\", \\"b\\") replaces ONLY the very first occurrence of \\"a\\" (unless a global regex /a/g is used). str.replaceAll(\\"a\\", \\"b\\") (introduced in ES2021) replaces every occurrence of the substring across the entire string without requiring a regular expression."}, {"id": "js-lec-6-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: How do you calculate the exact number of days, hours, minutes, and seconds remaining for a countdown timer between now and a target future date?", "title": "Q6: How do you calculate the exact number of days, hours, minutes, and seconds remaining for a countdown timer between now and a target future date?", "answer": "Subtract the current millisecond timestamp from the target millisecond timestamp: const diffMs = targetDate.getTime() - Date.now(). Then compute: days = Math.floor(diffMs / (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24)), hours = Math.floor((diffMs / (1000 * 60 * 60)) % 24), minutes = Math.floor((diffMs / (1000 * 60)) % 60), and seconds = Math.floor((diffMs / 1000) % 60).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Subtract the current millisecond timestamp from the target millisecond timestamp: const diffMs = targetDate.getTime() - Date.now(). Then compute: days = Math.floor(diffMs / (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24)), hours = Math.floor((diffMs / (1000 * 60 * 60)) % 24), minutes = Math.floor((diffMs / (1000 * 60)) % 60), and seconds = Math.floor((diffMs / 1000) % 60)."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nStrings and Dates are two of the most heavily used data structures in web development. This lecture covers modern **ES6 Template Literals**, non-mutating **String methods**, and how JavaScript's **`Date`** object represents time internally using **Unix Epoch milliseconds** and **UTC (Co