it automatically converts it to the user's local OS timezone (adding `+5 hours 30 minutes` in India).\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: String Manipulation & Date/Timezone Handling\n```javascript\n// 1. Template Literals & String Extraction (slice vs. substring)\nconst course = "Strike is coming soon";\nconsole.log(`Announcement: ${course.toUpperCase()}!`);\n\nconsole.log(course.slice(0, 6));      // "Strike"\nconsole.log(course.slice(-4));        // "soon" (negative index counts from end)\nconsole.log(course.substring(-4, 6)); // "Strike" (negative treated as 0)\n\n// 2. Replace, Split, and Trim\nconst rawInput = "   rohit,mohit,aman   ";\nconst namesArray = rawInput.trim().split(",");\nconsole.log(namesArray); // ["rohit", "mohit", "aman"\n\n// 3. Date Object, Unix Epoch Milliseconds & 0-Indexed Months\nconst nowMs = Date.now(); // Milliseconds since Jan 1, 1970 UTC\nconst octDateNumeric = new Date(2025, 9, 18); // Month 9 = October!\nconst octDateString = new Date("2025-10-18T00:00:00Z"); // String = 1-indexed October\n\nconsole.log(octDateString.toISOString());  // Always UTC ("...Z")\nconsole.log(octDateString.toString());     // Converted to local timezone (e.g., IST +05:30)\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 6: Strings & Date Object In-Depth — Template Literals, String Methods, Unix Epoch & UTC vs. Local Time	Master ES6 template literals, essential string manipulation methods (slice, substring, replaceAll, split, trim), and JavaScript Date internals (Unix Epoch milliseconds, 0-indexed months, and UTC vs. IST local time).	20	Easy	\N	6	[{"id": "js-lec-6-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What is the difference between String.prototype.slice() and String.prototype.substring()?", "title": "Q1: What is the difference between String.prototype.slice() and String.prototype.substring()?", "answer": "Both extract characters from startIndex up to (but excluding) endIndex, but they differ in two ways: 1) Negative arguments: slice() accepts negative indices and counts backward from the end of the string (e.g., \\"Rohit\\".slice(-2) -> \\"it\\"), whereas substring() clamps any negative number or NaN to 0. 2) Swapped arguments: if start > end (e.g., (4, 1)), substring(4, 1) automatically swaps them to substring(1, 4), whereas slice(4, 1) returns an empty string \\"\\".", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Both extract characters from startIndex up to (but excluding) endIndex, but they differ in two ways: 1) Negative arguments: slice() accepts negative indices and counts backward from the end of the string (e.g., \\"Rohit\\".slice(-2) -> \\"it\\"), whereas substring() clamps any negative number or NaN to 0. 2) Swapped arguments: if start > end (e.g., (4, 1)), substring(4, 1) automatically swaps them to substring(1, 4), whereas slice(4, 1) returns an empty string \\"\\"."}, {"id": "js-lec-6-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: How can primitives like strings have methods like \\"rohit\\".toUpperCase() if primitives are not objects?", "title": "Q2: How can primitives like strings have methods like \\"rohit\\".toUpperCase() if primitives are not objects?", "answer": "JavaScript uses a mechanism called Auto-Boxing (Primitive Wrapper Objects). Whenever you access a property or method on a string, number, or boolean primitive, the JS engine momentarily wraps the primitive inside a temporary new String(str) object in memory, executes the method from String.prototype, returns the new result, and immediately discards the temporary wrapper object.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "JavaScript uses a mechanism called Auto-Boxing (Primitive Wrapper Objects). Whenever you access a property or method on a string, number, or boolean primitive, the JS engine momentarily wraps the primitive inside a temporary new String(str) object in memory, executes the method from String.prototype, re