s://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "name": "Find Customer Referee", "title": "Find Customer Referee", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Find Customer Referee\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/"}, {"id": "pg-2-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "name": "Big Countries", "title": "Big Countries", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Big Countries\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/"}, {"id": "pg-2-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/", "name": "Article Views I", "title": "Article Views I", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Article Views I\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/"}, {"id": "pg-2-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/", "name": "Invalid Tweets", "title": "Invalid Tweets", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Invalid Tweets\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/"}, {"id": "pg-2-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/", "name": "Combine Two Tables", "title": "Combine Two Tables", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Combine Two Tables\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/"}, {"id": "pg-2-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "name": "Customers Who Never Order", "title": "Customers Who Never Order", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_7", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Customers Who Never Order\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/"}, {"id": "pg-2-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "name": "Duplicate Emails", "title": "Duplicate Emails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_8", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Duplicate Emails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/"}, {"id": "pg-2-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "name": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "title": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_9", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Employees Earning More Than Their Managers\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/"}, {"id": "pg-2-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "name": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "title": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_10", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Delete Duplicate Emails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nSetting up a production-parity local PostgreSQL environment requires installing the database server engine, the graphical administration studio (**pgAdmin 4**), and the interactive command-line client (**`psql`**), followed by registering the binary directory in your OS `PATH`.\n\n### ⚙ Critical Installation Parameters\nWhen running the EnterpriseDB (EDB) PostgreSQL installer, pay strict attention to four core configurations:\n\n1. **Selected Components:**\n   * **PostgreSQL Server:** The core relational database engine.\n   * **pgAdmin 4:** Graphical User Interface (GUI) for visual database management.\n   * **Command Line Tools:** Installs `psql`, `pg_dump`, and `pg_restore`.\n   * *(Stack Builder can be unchecked as it is optional).*\n2. **Superuser Account & Password:**\n   * Default Superuser username: `postgres`\n   * Set a memorable password during setup — you will need it for every CLI, pgAdmin, and Node.js connection pool.\n3. **Default Port:** `5432` (Standard TCP port for PostgreSQL).\n4. **Locale:** Keep set to `[Default locale]` so character encoding and alphabetical sorting match your OS.\n\n### 🛠️ Fixing "psql is not recognized" (Windows PATH Setup)\nIf typing `psql` in PowerShell or CMD throws a recognition error, Windows does not know where the PostgreSQL binaries live:\n\n1. Copy your installation binary path:\n   ```text\n   C:\\Program Files\\PostgreSQL\\18\\bin\n   ```\n2. Open **Edit the system environment variables** -> **Environment Variables** -> **System Variables** -> Select **Path** -> **Edit** -> **New** -> Paste the path and save.\n3. Restart your terminal and authenticate as the superuser:\n   ```bash\n   psql -U postgres\n   ```\n   * `psql`: Launches the PostgreSQL interactive CLI.\n   * `-U postgres`: Specifies the database username (`postgres`).\n\n### 🔍 Verifying Your Installation & Hierarchy\nOnce inside the `postgres=#` prompt, always end SQL statements with a semicolon (`;`):\n\n```sql\n-- Check installed PostgreSQL engine version\nSELECT version();\n```\n\n#### Why PostgreSQL Has a Deeper Tree in pgAdmin 4 than MySQL:\nUnlike MySQL (where tables sit directly inside a database), PostgreSQL organizes objects using **Schemas** for namespace isolation:\n```text\nServer (PostgreSQL 18)\n └── Databases (e.g., my_app)\n      └── Schemas\n           └── public (Default schema namespace)\n                └── Tables\n```	Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)	https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical	654	Lecture 2: PostgreSQL 18 Installation, Environment PATH, psql CLI & pgAdmin 4 Setup	Step-by-step installation of PostgreSQL Server, configuring Windows System PATH variables, authenticating via psql CLI, and navigating pgAdmin 4.	19	Easy	Module 1: PostgreSQL Foundations & Setup	2	[{"id": "pg-2-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/", "name": "Recyclable and Low Fat Products", "title": "Recyclable and Low Fat Products", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Recyclable and Low Fat Products\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/"}, {"id": "pg-2-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "name": "Find Customer Referee", "title": "Find Customer Referee", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Find Customer Referee\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/"}, {"id": "pg-2-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "name": "Big Countries", "title": "Big Countries", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Big Countries\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/"}, {"id": "pg-2-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/", "name": "Article Views I", "title": "Article Views I", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Article Views I\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/"}, {"id": "pg-2-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/", "name": "Invalid Tweets", "title": "Invalid Tweets", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Invalid Tweets\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/"}, {"id": "pg-2-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/", "name": "Combine Two Tables", "title": "Combine Two Tables", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Combine Two Tables\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/"}, {"id": "pg-2-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "name": "Customers Who Never Order", "title": "Customers Who Never Order", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_7", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Customers Who Never Order\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/"}, {"id": "pg-2-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "name": "Duplicate Emails", "title": "Duplicate Emails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_8", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Duplicate Emails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/"}, {"id": "pg-2-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "name": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "title": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_9", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Employees Earning More Than Their Managers\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/"}, {"id": "pg-2-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "name": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "title": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "2_10", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Delete Duplicate Emails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/"}]	https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-psql.html	\N	\N	\N
22125	Lecture 7: JavaScript Arrays In-Depth — Mutation vs. Non-Mutation, splice, flat, .sort() & Why Arrays Are Objects	js-lecture-7-arrays-in-depth-methods-sorting-internal-object-structure	Master array manipulation (push/pop, shift/unshift, slice vs. splice, spread, flat, lexicographical vs. numeric sort) and understand why JavaScript arrays are actually specialized Objects.	2026-10-06 21:14:25.4193+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn C++ or Java, an array is a fixed-size, contiguous block of homogeneous memory where `arr[i` is computed via `base_address + i * sizeof(type)`. In JavaScript, an **Array** can hold mixed types (`[10, "Rohit", true, { a: 1 }`), grow dynamically, and even accept custom string keys (`arr.name = "test"`)—because **a JavaScript Array is actually a specialized Object under the hood** (`typeof [ === "object"`).\n\n### 💡 Mutating vs. Non-Mutating Array Methods\n* **End of Array (Fast O(1) — Mutates Original):**\n  * **`arr.push(val)`:** Appends to the end.\n  * **`arr.pop()`:** Removes and returns the last element.\n* **Start of Array (Slow O(N) — Mutates Original):**\n  * **`arr.unshift(val)`:** Inserts at index `0` (shifts all existing elements right).\n  * **`arr.shift()`:** Removes index `0` (shifts all existing elements left).\n* **`slice(start, end)` (Non-Mutating) vs. `splice(start, deleteCount, ...items)` (Mutating):**\n  * **`slice`:** Returns a shallow copy of a portion of the array without modifying the original array.\n  * **`splice`:** Modifies the original array in place by deleting `deleteCount` elements starting at `start` and optionally inserting new `items`.\n* **Combining & Flattening Arrays (Non-Mutating):**\n  * **Spread Operator (`[...arr1, ...arr2`):** Cleaner modern alternative to `arr1.concat(arr2)`.\n  * **`arr.flat(depth)`:** Flattens nested sub-arrays up to `depth` levels (use `arr.flat(Infinity)` to flatten arbitrarily deep nesting).\n\n### 💡 The `.sort()` Trap: Lexicographical (UTF-16) vs. Numeric Sorting\n* By default, `arr.sort()` converts every element into a **String** and sorts them **lexicographically (dictionary order by UTF-16 code units)**!\n* As a result, `[10, 100, 25, 5.sort()` outputs `[10, 100, 25, 5` because `"100"` comes before `"25"` alphabetically (`"1" < "2"`)!\n* **Fix:** Always pass a numeric comparator callback:\n  * **Ascending:** `arr.sort((a, b) => a - b)`\n  * **Descending:** `arr.sort((a, b) => b - a)`\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Array Operations, Sorting & Object Proof\n```javascript\n// 1. slice (non-mutating) vs. splice (mutating in-place)\nconst nums = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50;\nconst sliced = nums.slice(1, 4);      // [20, 30, 40, nums is unchanged\nconst spliced = nums.splice(1, 2, 99); // Removes [20, 30 and inserts 99 in place\nconsole.log(nums);                    // [10, 99, 40, 50\n\n// 2. Fixing the Default Lexicographical .sort() Trap\nconst scores = [100, 25, 5, 42, 10;\nconsole.log([...scores.sort());               // [10, 100, 25, 42, 5 (Dictionary order!)\nconsole.log([...scores.sort((a, b) => a - b)); // [5, 10, 25, 42, 100 (Numeric Ascending)\nconsole.log([...scores.sort((a, b) => b - a)); // [100, 42, 25, 10, 5 (Numeric Descending)\n\n// 3. Flattening Nested Arrays & Checking Array Type\nconst nested = [1, [2, [3, [4;\nconsole.log(nested.flat(Infinity));   // [1, 2, 3, 4\nconsole.log(typeof nested);           // "object"\nconsole.log(Array.isArray(nested));   // true (Always use Array.isArray!)\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array", "title": "MDN Web Docs — Array Built-In Object Reference"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fuA8BQkQO_E", "title": "JavaScript Arrays Explained In-Depth  | Javascript Full Course #07", "duration": "70m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-7-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Why is a JavaScript Array not a true traditional array like in C++, and why does typeof [] return \\"object\\"?", "title": "Q1: Why is a JavaScript Array not a true traditional array like in C++, and why does typeof [] return \\"object\\"?", "answer": "A traditional C++ array is a fixed-length, contiguous block of RAM storing elements of a single fixed byte-size so index lookup is base_address + index * size. In JavaScript, an array can hold heterogeneous data types (numbers, strings, objects) whose sizes vary, can grow/shrink dynamically, can have sparse holes, and can even hold arbitrary key-value properties (arr.foo = \\"bar\\"). Internally, a JS Array is an Object whose keys are stringified indices (\\"0\\", \\"1\\", \\"2\\") with an auto-updating length property and Array.prototype methods.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "7-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "A traditional C++ array is a fixed-length, contiguous block of RAM storing elements of a single fixed byte-size so index lookup is base_address + index * size. In JavaScript, an array can hold heterogeneous data types (numbers, strings, objects) whose sizes vary, can grow/shrink dynamically, can have sparse holes, and can even hold arbitrary key-value properties (arr.foo = \\"bar\\"). Internally, a JS Array is an Object whose keys are stringified indices (\\"0\\", \\"1\\", \\"2\\") with an auto-updating length property and Array.prototype methods."}, {"id": "js-lec-7-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why does [10, 5, 100, 25].sort() output [10, 100, 25, 5], and how does the comparator (a, b) => a - b work?", "title": "Q2: Why does [10, 5, 100, 25].sort() output [10, 100, 25, 5], and how does the comparator (a, b) => a - b work?", "answer": "When called without arguments, Array.prototype.sort() converts each element to a string (\\"10\\", \\"5\\", \\"100\\", \\"25\\") and compares their UTF-16 character codes left-to-right. Since \\"1\\" (code 49) is less than \\"2\\" (code 50) and \\"5\\" (code 53), \\"100\\" is placed before \\"25\\" and \\"5\\". Passing (a, b) => a - b instructs sort() to check the sign of the return value: if negative (a < b), a comes first; if positive (a > b), b comes first; if 0, their relative order is preserved.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "7-q2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "When called without arguments, Array.prototype.sort() converts each element to a string (\\"10\\", \\"5\\", \\"100\\", \\"25\\") and compares their UTF-16 character codes left-to-right. Since \\"1\\" (code 49) is less than \\"2\\" (code 50) and \\"5\\" (code 53), \\"100\\" is placed before \\"25\\" and \\"5\\". Passing (a, b) => a - b instructs sort() to check the sign of the return value: if negative (a < b), a comes first; if positive (a > b), b comes first; if 0, their relative order is preserved."}, {"id": "js-lec-7-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is the exact difference between Array.prototype.slice() and Array.prototype.splice()?", "title": "Q3: What is the exact difference between Array.prototype.slice() and Array.prototype.splice()?", "answer": "1) Mutability: slice(start, end) does NOT mutate the original array—it returns a new shallow-copied sub-array. splice(start, deleteCount, ...items) directly mutates the original array in place and returns an array of the deleted elements. 2) Capabilities: slice can only extract elements, whereas splice can delete, insert, or replace elements anywhere inside the array.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "7-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) Mutability: slice(start, end) does NOT mutate the original array—it returns a new shallow-copied sub-array. splice(start, deleteCount, ...items) directly mutates the original array in place and returns an array of the deleted elements. 2) Capabilities: slice can only extract elements, whereas splice can delete, insert, or replace elements anywhere inside the array."}, {"id": "js-lec-7-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: How do you reliably check if a variable is an Array in JavaScript if typeof returns \\"object\\"?", "title": "Q4: How do you reliably check if a variable is an Array in JavaScript if typeof returns \\"object\\"?", "answer": "Always use Array.isArray(value). Unlike typeof (which returns \\"object\\" for arrays, null, and plain objects), Array.isArray([]) returns true and even works reliably across different iframes/realms where instanceof Array might fail.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "7-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Always use Array.isArray(value). Unlike typeof (which returns \\"object\\" for arrays, null, and plain objects), Array.isArray([]) returns true and even works reliably across different iframes/realms where instanceof Array might fail."}, {"id": "js-lec-7-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Why are push() and pop() much faster than unshift() and shift() on large arrays?", "title": "Q5: Why are push() and pop() much faster than unshift() and shift() on large arrays?", "answer": "push() and pop() add or remove elements at the end of the array in O(1) amortized time because no existing indices change. unshift() and shift() add or remove at index 0, which forces the engine to re-index and shift every single remaining element in the array by 1 position, taking O(N) linear time.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "7-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "push() and pop() add or remove elements at the end of the array in O(1) amortized time because no existing indices change. unshift() and shift() add or remove at index 0, which forces the engine to re-index and shift every single remaining element in the array by 1 position, taking O(N) linear time."}, {"id": "js-lec-7-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: What happens if you assign arr[100] = \\"hello\\" on an array const arr = [1, 2]?", "title": "Q6: What happens if you assign arr[100] = \\"hello\\" on an array const arr = [1, 2]?", "answer": "JavaScript creates a \\"sparse array\\" (an array with empty slots). arr.length immediately becomes 101, and indices 2 through 99 become empty slots (which return undefined if accessed, and downgrade V8's internal representation from a fast packed C-like backing store to a slower dictionary hash-map mode).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "7-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "JavaScript creates a \\"sparse array\\" (an array with empty slots). arr.length immediately becomes 101, and indices 2 through 99 become empty slots (which return undefined if accessed, and downgrade V8's internal representation from a fast packed C-like backing store to a slower dictionary hash-map mode)."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn C++ or Java, an array is a fixed-size, contiguous block of homogeneous memory where `arr[i` is computed via `base_address + i * sizeof(type)`. In JavaScript, an **Array** can hold mixed types (`[10, "Rohit", true, { a: 1 }`), grow dynamically, and even accept custom string keys (`arr.name = "test"`)—because **a JavaScript Array is actually a specialized Object under the hood** (`typeof [ === "object"`).\n\n### 💡 Mutating vs. Non-Mutating Array Methods\n* **End of Array (Fast O(1) — Mutates Original):**\n  * **`arr.push(val)`:** Appends to the end.\n  * **`arr.pop()`:** Removes and returns the last element.\n* **Start of Array (Slow O(N) — Mutates Original):**\n  * **`arr.unshift(val)`:** Inserts at index `0` (shifts all existing elements right).\n  * **`arr.shift()`:** Removes index `0` (shifts all existing elements left).\n* **`slice(start, end)` (Non-Mutating) vs. `splice(start, deleteCount, ...items)` (Mutating):**\n  * **`slice`:** Returns a shallow copy of a portion of the array without modifying the original array.\n  * **`splice`:** Modifies the original array in place by deleting `deleteCount` elements starting at `start` and optionally inserting new `items`.\n* **Combining & Flattening Arrays (Non-Mutating):**\n  * **Spread Operator (`[...arr1, ...arr2`):** Cleaner modern alternative to `arr1.concat(arr2)`.\n  * **`arr.flat(depth)`:** Flattens nested sub-arrays up to `depth` levels (use `arr.flat(Infinity)` to flatten arbitrarily deep nesting).\n\n### 💡 The `.sort()` Trap: Lexicographical (UTF-16) vs. Numeric Sorting\n* By default, `arr.sort()` converts every element into a **String** and sorts them **lexicographically (dictionary order by UTF-16 code units)**!\n* As a result, `[10, 100, 25, 5.sort()` outputs `[10, 100, 25, 5` because `"100"` comes before `"25"` alphabetically (`"1" < "2"`)!\n* **Fix:** Always pass a numeric comparator callback:\n  * **Ascending:** `arr.sort((a, b) => a - b)`\n  * **Descending:** `arr.sort((a, b) => b - a)`\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Array Operations, Sorting & Object Proof\n```javascript\n// 1. slice (non-mutating) vs. splice (mutating in-place)\nconst nums = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50;\nconst sliced = nums.slice(1, 4);      // [20, 30, 40, nums is unchanged\nconst spliced = nums.splice(1, 2, 99); // Removes [20, 30 and inserts 99 in place\nconsole.log(nums);                    // [10, 99, 40, 50\n\n// 2. Fixing the Default Lexicographical .sort() Trap\nconst scores = [100, 25, 5, 42, 10;\nconsole.log([...scores.sort());               // [10, 100, 25, 42, 5 (Dictionary order!)\nconsole.log([...scores.sort((a, b) => a - b)); // [5, 10, 25, 42, 100 (Numeric Ascending)\nconsole.log([...scores.sort((a, b) => b - a)); // [100, 42, 25, 10, 5 (Numeric Descending)\n\n// 3. Flattening Nested Arrays & Checking Array Type\nconst nested = [1, [2, [3, [4;\nconsole.log(nested.flat(Infinity));   // [1, 2, 3, 4\nconsole.log(typeof nested);           // "object"\nconsole.log(Array.isArray(nested));   // true (Always use Array.isArray!)\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 7: JavaScript Arrays In-Depth — Mutation vs. Non-Mutation, splice, flat, .sort() & Why Arrays Are Objects	Master array manipulation (push/pop, shift/unshift, slice vs. splice, spread, flat, lexicographical vs. numeric sort) and understand why JavaScript arrays are actually specialized Objects.	20	Easy	\N	7	[{"id": "js-lec-7-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Why is a JavaScript Array not a true traditional array like in C++, and why does typeof [] return \\"object\\"?", "title": "Q1: Why is a JavaScript Array not a true traditional array like in C++, and why does typeof [] return \\"object\\"?", "answer": "A traditional C++ array is a fixed-length, contiguous block of RAM storing elements of a single fixed byte-size so index lookup is base_address + index * size. In JavaScript, an array can hold heterogeneous data types (numbers, strings, objects) whose sizes vary, can grow/shrink dynamically, can have sparse holes, and can even hold arbitrary key-value properties (arr.foo = \\"bar\\"). Internally, a JS Array is an Object whose keys are stringified indices (\\"0\\", \\"1\\", \\"2\\") with an auto-updating length property and Array.prototype methods.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "7-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "A traditional C++ array is a fixed-length, contiguous block of RAM storing elements of a single fixed byte-size so index lookup is base_address + index * size. In JavaScript, an array can hold heterogeneous data types (numbers, strings, objects) whose sizes vary, can grow/shrink dynamically, can have sparse holes, and can even hold arbitrary key-value properties (arr.foo = \\"bar\\"). Internally, a JS Array is an Object whose keys are stringified indices (\\"0\\", \\"1\\", \\"2\\") with an auto-updating length property and Array.prototype methods."}, {"id": "js-lec-7-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why does [10, 5, 100, 25].sort() output [10, 100, 25, 5], and how does the comparator (a, b) => a - b work?", "title": "Q2: Why does [10, 5, 100, 25].sort() output [10, 100, 25, 5], and how does the comparator (a, b) => a - b work?", "answer": "When called without arguments, Array.prototype.sort() converts each element to a string (\\"10\\", \\"5\\", \\"100\\", \\"25\\") and compares their UTF-16 character codes left-to-right. Since \\"1\\" (code 49) is less than \\"2\\" (code 50) and \\"5\\" (code 53), \\"100\\" is placed before \\"25\\" and \\"5\\". Passing (a, b) => a - b instructs sort() to check the sign of the return value: if negative (a < b), a comes first; if positive (a > b), b comes first; if 0, their relative order is preserved.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "7-q2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "When called without arguments, Array.prototype.sort() converts each element to a string (\\"10\\", \\"5\\", \\"100\\", \\"25\\") and compares their UTF-16 character codes left-to-right. Since \\"1\\" (code 49) is less than \\"2\\" (code 50) and \\"5\\" (code 53), \\"100\\" is placed before \\"25\\" and \\"5\\". Passing (a, b) => a - b instructs sort() to check the sign of the return value: if negative (a < b), a comes first; if positive (a > b), b comes first; if 0, their relative order is preserved."}, {"id": "js-lec-7-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is the exact difference between Array.prototype.slice() and Array.prototype.splice()?", "title": "Q3: What is the exact difference between Array.prototype.slice() and Array.prototype.splice()?", "answer": "1) Mutability: slice(start, end) does NOT mutate the original array—it returns a new shallow-copied sub-array. splice(start, deleteCount, ...items) directly mutates the original array in place and returns an array of the deleted elements. 2) Capabilities: slice can only extract elements, whereas splice can delete, insert, or replace elements anywhere inside the array.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "7-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) Mutability: slice(start, end) does NOT mutate the original array—it returns a new shallow-copied sub-array. splice(start, deleteCount, ...items) directly mutates the original array in place and returns an array of the deleted elements. 2) Capabilities: slice can only extract elements, whereas splice can delete, insert, or replace elements anywhere inside the array."}, {"id": "js-lec-7-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: How do you reliably check if a variable is an Array in JavaScript if typeof returns \\"object\\"?", "title": "Q4: How do you reliably check if a variable is an Array in JavaScript if typeof returns \\"object\\"?", "answer": "Always use Array.isArray(value). Unlike typeof (which returns \\"object\\" for arrays, null, and plain objects), Array.isArray([]) returns true and even works reliably across different iframes/realms where instanceof Array might fail.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "7-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Always use Array.isArray(value). Unlike typeof (which returns \\"object\\" for arrays, null, and plain objects), Array.isArray([]) returns true and even works reliably across different iframes/realms where instanceof Array might fail."}, {"id": "js-lec-7-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Why are push() and pop() much faster than unshift() and shift() on large arrays?", "title": "Q5: Why are push() and pop() much faster than unshift() and shift() on large arrays?", "answer": "push() and pop() add or remove elements at the end of the array in O(1) amortized time because no existing indices change. unshift() and shift() add or remove at index 0, which forces the engine to re-index and shift every single remaining element in the array by 1 position, taking O(N) linear time.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "7-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "push() and pop() add or remove elements at the end of the array in O(1) amortized time because no existing indices change. unshift() and shift() add or remove at index 0, which forces the engine to re-index and shift every single remaining element in the array by 1 position, taking O(N) linear time."}, {"id": "js-lec-7-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: What happens if you assign arr[100] = \\"hello\\" on an array const arr = [1, 2]?", "title": "Q6: What happens if you assign arr[100] = \\"hello\\" on an array const arr = [1, 2]?", "answer": "JavaScript creates a \\"sparse array\\" (an array with empty slots). arr.length immediately becomes 101, and indices 2 through 99 become empty slots (which return undefined if accessed, and downgrade V8's internal representation from a fast packed C-like backing store to a slower dictionary hash-map mode).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "7-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "JavaScript creates a \\"sparse array\\" (an array with empty slots). arr.length immediately becomes 101, and indices 2 through 99 become empty slots (which return undefined if accessed, and downgrade V8's internal representation from a fast packed C-like backing store to a slower dictionary hash-map mode)."}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array	\N	\N	\N
22234	Command Design Pattern	lld-command-design-pattern	Encapsulate requests as objects to decouple sender and receiver. Implement scalable Smart Home Remotes and Undo/Redo mechanisms.	2026-10-07 17:12:04.258699+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Command Design Pattern\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/cnQZsN0jxEY", "title": "Command Design Pattern | Real-world use case + Code", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v15-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Core Purpose of the Command Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe Command Pattern fundamentally decouples the **Invoker** (the object initiating the request, like a UI Button) from the **Receiver** (the object performing the work, like a Database or Smart Bulb).\\n\\nIt achieves this by wrapping the request into a standalone `Command` object. The Invoker only possesses a pointer to an `ICommand` interface and calls `execute()`. It possesses absolutely zero knowledge of what the command does, who the Receiver is, or how the work is accomplished. This allows you to dynamically swap the action a button performs at runtime.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the fundamental decoupling achieved by the Command Design Pattern. Who is being decoupled from whom, and how does encapsulating a request as an object achieve this?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v15-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Implementing Undo/Redo Functionality", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **The Interface Extension:** Expand the `ICommand` interface to include an `undo()` method alongside `execute()`.\\n2. **Concrete Implementation:** A `BoldTextCommand` saves the previous text formatting state before it executes. Its `undo()` method restores that saved state.\\n3. **History Management:** The application maintains a `std::stack<ICommand*>` called the Undo Stack.\\n4. **Execution Flow:** Whenever a command is executed, it is pushed onto the Undo Stack. When the user presses `Ctrl+Z`, the application pops the top command from the stack and invokes its `undo()` method. (A secondary Redo Stack can be maintained if the user presses `Ctrl+Y`).", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Command Pattern natively support \\"Undo\\" functionality in a Text Editor application? Describe the data structures and method calls required.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v15-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Command Pattern vs Strategy Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Strategy Pattern (How):** Encapsulates different *algorithms* to achieve the exact same goal. For example, `BubbleSort` and `QuickSort` are strategies to sort an array. The client cares about the end result (a sorted array), but delegates *how* it gets sorted.\\n* **Command Pattern (What/When):** Encapsulates a specific *action* or *request* to be executed. For example, `TurnOnLightCommand` and `StartFanCommand` achieve completely different goals. The invoker doesn't care about the result; it just cares about dispatching the request at the correct time (e.g., when a button is clicked or delayed via a queue).", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Both the Command Pattern and Strategy Pattern encapsulate behaviors into objects. What is the fundamental difference in their architectural intent?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v15-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Macro Commands (Batch Processing)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nYou implement a **Macro Command** (often utilizing the Composite Pattern).\\n\\n1. Create a `MacroCommand` class that implements `ICommand`.\\n2. Internally, it holds a list/array of other commands: `std::vector<ICommand*> commands;`\\n3. The constructor accepts multiple concrete commands: `new DimLightsCommand()`, `new TurnOnStereoCommand()`, etc.\\n4. Its `execute()` method simply iterates through the list, calling `execute()` on every stored command sequentially.\\n5. The Invoker (the \\"Party Mode\\" button) simply executes the `MacroCommand` exactly as it would a single command.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How would you use the Command Pattern to implement a \\"Party Mode\\" button that simultaneously dims the lights, turns on the stereo, and starts the disco ball?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v15-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Queuing Requests via Commands", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nBecause the Command Pattern turns a method call into a standalone, physical object (`ICommand`), that object can be serialized, passed across network boundaries, or stored in a data structure.\\n\\nIn a Thread Pool, you cannot easily enqueue a raw function call with variable parameters. Instead, producers create `Command` objects and push them onto a queue (`std::queue<ICommand*>`). Background worker threads continuously pop commands off the queue and blindly call `command->execute()`. The thread pool remains completely decoupled from the actual business logic of the jobs it processes.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is the Command Pattern uniquely suited for implementing Job Queues or Thread Pools?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Tight Coupling Problem\n* Imagine a Universal Remote Control with physical buttons. If button #1 directly calls `Light.turnOn()`, the remote is permanently hardcoded to the `Light` object.\n* If you want button #1 to suddenly control a `Fan`, you must rewrite the Remote's codebase, violating the Open/Closed Principle.\n\n### 💡 2. Encapsulating Requests as Objects\n* **The Command Pattern** solves this by turning a request into a standalone object containing all information about the request.\n* **The Actors:**\n  * **Receiver:** The actual device doing the work (e.g., `Light`, `Fan`).\n  * **Command:** An interface with an `execute()` method. A concrete `LightOnCommand` holds a reference to the `Light` receiver.\n  * **Invoker:** The remote control button. It holds a reference to a `Command` object and blindly calls `command.execute()`.\n* The Remote (Invoker) doesn't know what the Receiver is. It only knows how to trigger a Command.\n\n### 💡 3. The Power of Undo\n* Because the command is an object, you can store it in a Stack data structure.\n* By adding an `undo()` method to the Command interface, a text editor can seamlessly implement `Ctrl+Z` by popping the last executed Command off the stack and invoking its `undo()` method.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	Command Design Pattern	Encapsulate requests as objects to decouple sender and receiver. Implement scalable Smart Home Remotes and Undo/Redo mechanisms.	30	Hard	Low Level Design	29	[{"id": "lld-v15-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Core Purpose of the Command Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe Command Pattern fundamentally decouples the **Invoker** (the object initiating the request, like a UI Button) from the **Receiver** (the object performing the work, like a Database or Smart Bulb).\\n\\nIt achieves this by wrapping the request into a standalone `Command` object. The Invoker only possesses a pointer to an `ICommand` interface and calls `execute()`. It possesses absolutely zero knowledge of what the command does, who the Receiver is, or how the work is accomplished. This allows you to dynamically swap the action a button performs at runtime.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the fundamental decoupling achieved by the Command Design Pattern. Who is being decoupled from whom, and how does encapsulating a request as an object achieve this?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v15-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Implementing Undo/Redo Functionality", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **The Interface Extension:** Expand the `ICommand` interface to include an `undo()` method alongside `execute()`.\\n2. **Concrete Implementation:** A `BoldTextCommand` saves the previous text formatting state before it executes. Its `undo()` method restores that saved state.\\n3. **History Management:** The application maintains a `std::stack<ICommand*>` called the Undo Stack.\\n4. **Execution Flow:** Whenever a command is executed, it is pushed onto the Undo Stack. When the user presses `Ctrl+Z`, the application pops the top command from the stack and invokes its `undo()` method. (A secondary Redo Stack can be maintained if the user presses `Ctrl+Y`).", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Command Pattern natively support \\"Undo\\" functionality in a Text Editor application? Describe the data structures and method calls required.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v15-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Command Pattern vs Strategy Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Strategy Pattern (How):** Encapsulates different *algorithms* to achieve the exact same goal. For example, `BubbleSort` and `QuickSort` are strategies to sort an array. The client cares about the end result (a sorted array), but delegates *how* it gets sorted.\\n* **Command Pattern (What/When):** Encapsulates a specific *action* or *request* to be executed. For example, `TurnOnLightCommand` and `StartFanCommand` achieve completely different goals. The invoker doesn't care about the result; it just cares about dispatching the request at the correct time (e.g., when a button is clicked or delayed via a queue).", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Both the Command Pattern and Strategy Pattern encapsulate behaviors into objects. What is the fundamental difference in their architectural intent?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v15-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Macro Commands (Batch Processing)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nYou implement a **Macro Command** (often utilizing the Composite Pattern).\\n\\n1. Create a `MacroCommand` class that implements `ICommand`.\\n2. Internally, it holds a list/array of other commands: `std::vector<ICommand*> commands;`\\n3. The constructor accepts multiple concrete commands: `new DimLightsCommand()`, `new TurnOnStereoCommand()`, etc.\\n4. Its `execute()` method simply iterates through the list, calling `execute()` on every stored command sequentially.\\n5. The Invoker (the \\"Party Mode\\" button) simply executes the `MacroCommand` exactly as it would a single command.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How would you use the Command Pattern to implement a \\"Party Mode\\" button that simultaneously dims the lights, turns on the stereo, and starts the disco ball?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v15-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Queuing Requests via Commands", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nBecause the Command Pattern turns a method call into a standalone, physical object (`ICommand`), that object can be serialized, passed across network boundaries, or stored in a data structure.\\n\\nIn a Thread Pool, you cannot easily enqueue a raw function call with variable parameters. Instead, producers create `Command` objects and push them onto a queue (`std::queue<ICommand*>`). Background worker threads continuously pop commands off the queue and blindly call `command->execute()`. The thread pool remains completely decoupled from the actual business logic of the jobs it processes.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is the Command Pattern uniquely suited for implementing Job Queues or Thread Pools?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22284	Document Metadata, Viewport Scaling & Technical SEO	seo-and-head-tag-meta-tags	The head element lifecycle, mobile viewport configurations, favicon integration, and meta tags for search engine crawlers.	2026-10-12 00:13:48.931515+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Document Metadata, Viewport Scaling & Technical SEO\nPart of the **HTML5 Foundations** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/0ZGAKHoVFs0", "title": "Document Metadata, Viewport Scaling & Technical SEO", "creator_name": "CodeHelp - by Babbar", "duration_minutes": 20, "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CodeHelp"}]	[{"type": "assignment", "title": "Build & Test a Working Implementation of Document Metadata, Viewport Scaling & Technical SEO", "checklist": ["Implement core logic with validation and boundary checks", "Write tests verifying expected output and edge cases"], "description": "Implement a production-grade module demonstrating the core principles of Document Metadata, Viewport Scaling & Technical SEO.", "starterCodeUrl": ""}]	\N	> **Notice:** Practical evaluation questions are not required for this foundational track.\n\n### 📌 Core Topics\n- The role of the `<head>` element and search engine crawlers.\n- Meta tags: `charset="UTF-8"`, `viewport`, description, keywords, author.\n- Linking external assets: Favicons (`<link rel="icon">`) and CSS stylesheets.	Code