10791	In-Place Dummy-Head Two-Pointer Zipper: Merge Two Sorted Linked Lists	dsa-merge-two-sorted-linked-lists	Merge two sorted linked lists in-place in O(N1 + N2) time and O(1) space by re-wiring next pointers using a dummy head zipper.	2026-10-03 15:07:44.966126+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: In-Place Dummy-Head Two-Pointer Zipper: Merge Two Sorted Linked Lists\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jXu-H7XuClE", "title": "Lecture 259: Merge Two Sorted Linked Lists", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-two-sorted-lists/", "title": "Merge Two Sorted Lists (LC 21)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Merge the two sorted lists into one sorted list by splicing together the nodes of the first two lists."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-k-sorted-lists/", "title": "Merge k Sorted Lists (LC 23)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Extend merging 2 sorted lists to merging k sorted lists."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/merge-two-sorted-linked-lists/1", "title": "Merge two sorted linked lists", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Merge two sorted linked lists in-place in O(1) auxiliary space."}]	Module 38: Doubly Linked List Medium Problems & Hard Linked List Re-Wiring (K-Group Reversal, Rotation & Multi-Level Flattening)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the heads `list1` and `list2` of two sorted singly linked lists, merge them into a **single sorted linked list in-place** (by reusing and re-linking the existing nodes, without allocating `N1 + N2` new nodes!) and return the merged head.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Optimal)\n- **1. Brute Force Array + Sort (`O((N1 + N2) log(N1 + N2))` Time, `O(N1 + N2)` Space):**\n  - Copy all values into a vector, sort it, and build a new linked list. Wastes both time and memory!\n- **2. Optimal In-Place Dummy-Node Two-Pointer Zipper (`O(N1 + N2)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Allocate a single **`Node* dummyNode = new Node(-1);`** and set `Node* temp = dummyNode;`.\n  - Maintain `t1 = list1` and `t2 = list2`.\n  - Loop **`while (t1 != nullptr && t2 != nullptr)`**:\n    - **If `t1->data < t2->data`:** Point `temp->next = t1;`, move `temp = t1;`, and advance `t1 = t1->next;`.\n    - **Else:** Point `temp->next = t2;`, move `temp = t2;`, and advance `t2 = t2->next;`.\n  - **Attach the Remaining Non-Empty Tail in `O(1)`:**\n    - Once one list finishes, attach the entire remaining chain of the other list in a single assignment:\n      **`if (t1 != nullptr) temp->next = t1; else temp->next = t2;`**!\n  - Return **`dummyNode->next`**.\n\n### 💻 Merge Two Sorted Linked Lists (In-Place O(1) Space) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nNode* sortTwoLists(Node* list1, Node* list2) {\n    Node* t1 = list1;\n    Node* t2 = list2;\n    Node* dummyNode = new Node(-1);\n    Node* temp = dummyNode;\n\n    while (t1 != nullptr && t2 != nullptr) {\n        if (t1->data < t2->data) {\n            temp->next = t1;\n            temp = t1;\n            t1 = t1->next;\n        } else {\n            temp->next = t2;\n            temp = t2;\n            t2 = t2->next;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Attach whichever list still has remaining nodes in O(1)\n    if (t1 != nullptr) temp->next = t1;\n    else temp->next = t2;\n\n    Node* mergedHead = dummyNode->next;\n    delete dummyNode;\n    return mergedHead;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Using a `while` Loop to Append Leftover Nodes:** Unlike merging arrays (where you must copy remaining elements one by one), in a linked list all remaining nodes are **already linked together**! A single `if (t1 != nullptr) temp->next = t1; else temp->next = t2;` attaches the entire tail in `O(1)` time.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
