 `useMemo`?", "explanation": "Using useMemo adds initial overhead. If used on trivial calculations, the cost of memoizing outweighs the calculation itself, slowing down the app.", "correctAnswer": "A", "correct_index": 0, "correct_answer": "Memory leaks and degraded performance due to overhead"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["It converts objects to primitives", "It prevents objects or arrays created inside components from getting a new memory address on every render", "It automatically deep-clones nested objects", "It binds the `this` keyword properly"], "question": "How does `useMemo` help with referential equality in React?", "explanation": "By memoizing an object or array, the memory reference remains the same across renders, preventing unnecessary child component re-renders.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "It prevents objects or arrays created inside components from getting a new memory address on every render"}]	\N	> **Architectural Deep Dive:** This lecture covers the strict technical nuances, reconciliation behavior, and performance implications of using useMemo in production environments. Pay special attention to concurrent rendering behaviors in React 18+.	Web Dev Simplified	https://www.youtube.com/@WebDevSimplified	\N	Learn useMemo In 10 Minutes	Deep dive architectural lecture focusing on the mechanics, use cases, and anti-patterns of the useMemo hook.	10	Easy	Core Hooks & API	3	[]	\N	10	https://youtu.be/THL1OPn72vo	Learn useMemo In 10 Minutes
1122	Breadth-First Queue Traversal: Level-Order Traversal of a Binary Tree	dsa-level-order-traversal-bfs-binary-tree	Traverse a binary tree level by level using a FIFO queue and level-snapshot loop in O(N) time and space.	2026-10-03 00:35:47.13509+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Breadth-First Queue Traversal: Level-Order Traversal of a Binary Tree\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EoAsWbO7sqg", "title": "Lecture 80: Level Order Traversal of Binary Tree", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-level-order-traversal/", "title": "Binary Tree Level Order Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the level-by-level node values of a binary tree from top to bottom."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/average-of-levels-in-binary-tree/", "title": "Average of Levels in Binary Tree", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Use queue-based BFS level snapshots to compute the average value on each level."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/level-order-traversal/1", "title": "Level order traversal", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Traverse a binary tree level by level using a FIFO queue."}]	Module 15: Binary Trees: Hierarchical Representation & Fundamental Traversals	### 📌 Topic Overview\n**Level-Order Traversal (BFS)** visits a binary tree level by level from top to bottom, and from left to right within each level, using a **FIFO Queue (`queue<TreeNode*>`)**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Queue Snapshot Technique:**\n  1. If `root == nullptr`, return an empty 2D vector `{}`.\n  2. Push `root` into a `queue<TreeNode*> q`.\n  3. While `!q.empty()`:\n     - Capture the current level's node count: **`int size = q.size();`**.\n     - Create a `vector<int> level` and loop `size` times:\n       - Pop the front node from `q`.\n       - If `node->left != nullptr`, push `node->left` into `q`.\n       - If `node->right != nullptr`, push `node->right` into `q`.\n       - Append `node->val` to `level`.\n     - Push `level` into `ans`.\n- **Complexity:** `O(N)` Time (each node is pushed and popped once) and `O(N)` Space (queue holds at most one level of nodes).\n\n### 💻 Level-Order BFS Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<vector<int>> levelOrder(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<vector<int>> ans;\n    if (root == nullptr) return ans;\n\n    queue<TreeNode*> q;\n    q.push(root);\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int size = q.size(); // Snapshot of nodes in current level\n        vector<int> level;\n        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n            TreeNode* node = q.front();\n            q.pop();\n            if (node->left != nullptr) q.push(node->left);\n            if (node->right != nullptr) q.push(node->right);\n            level.push_back(node->val);\n        }\n        ans.push_back(level);\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
