 "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "name": "Find Customer Referee", "title": "Find Customer Referee", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Find Customer Referee\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/"}, {"id": "pg-4-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "name": "Big Countries", "title": "Big Countries", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Big Countries\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/"}, {"id": "pg-4-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/", "name": "Article Views I", "title": "Article Views I", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Article Views I\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/"}, {"id": "pg-4-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/", "name": "Invalid Tweets", "title": "Invalid Tweets", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Invalid Tweets\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/"}, {"id": "pg-4-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/", "name": "Combine Two Tables", "title": "Combine Two Tables", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Combine Two Tables\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/"}, {"id": "pg-4-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "name": "Customers Who Never Order", "title": "Customers Who Never Order", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_7", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Customers Who Never Order\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/"}, {"id": "pg-4-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "name": "Duplicate Emails", "title": "Duplicate Emails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_8", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Duplicate Emails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/"}, {"id": "pg-4-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "name": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "title": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_9", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Employees Earning More Than Their Managers\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/"}, {"id": "pg-4-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "name": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "title": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_10", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Delete Duplicate Emails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nLearn how to populate relational tables in PostgreSQL ranging from single-record inserts to multi-row batch inserts, conflict-tolerant upserts (`ON CONFLICT`), and bulk CSV ingestion using pgAdmin 4.\n\n### 🏗️ Project Schema Setup (`departments` & `users`)\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE departments (\n    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    name VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE NOT NULL\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE users (\n    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    email VARCHAR(150) UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n    role VARCHAR(20) CHECK (role IN ('student', 'professor', 'admin')),\n    department_id INTEGER REFERENCES departments(id),\n    metadata JSONB,\n    is_active BOOLEAN DEFAULT true,\n    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\n);\n```\n\n### 🚀 4 Ways to Insert Data in PostgreSQL\n\n#### 1. Single-Row Insert\nOmit `SERIAL` or `DEFAULT` columns so PostgreSQL auto-generates them:\n```sql\nINSERT INTO departments (name)\nVALUES ('Computer Science');\n```\n\n#### 2. Multi-Row Batch Insert\nInsert multiple tuples in a single network round-trip by separating value groups with commas:\n```sql\nINSERT INTO departments (id, name)\nVALUES \n    (2, 'Mechanical Engineering'),\n    (3, 'Electrical Engineering'),\n    (4, 'Business Administration'),\n    (5, 'Basic Sciences');\n```\n\n#### 3. `RETURNING` Clause (Backend API Essential)\nInstead of running a second `SELECT` query to get the auto-generated `id` or `created_at` timestamp, append `RETURNING`:\n```sql\nINSERT INTO users (name, email, role, department_id, is_active)\nVALUES ('Vinod Thapa', 'vinod@college.com', 'admin', 1, true)\nRETURNING id, created_at;\n```\n\n#### 4. Graceful Duplicate Handling with `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`\nWhen ingesting batches that may contain existing unique values, a single duplicate normally aborts the entire transaction. Use `ON CONFLICT` to skip duplicates and insert only fresh records:\n```sql\nINSERT INTO departments (id, name)\nVALUES \n    (6, 'Computer Science'), -- Already exists!\n    (7, 'Civil Engineering'),\n    (8, 'Biotechnology')\nON CONFLICT (name) DO NOTHING;\n```\n\n### 📂 Bulk CSV Import Checklist in pgAdmin 4\n1. Right-click target table (`users`) -> **Import/Export Data** -> Toggle to **Import**.\n2. Select your `.csv` file and enable the **Header** toggle under Options.\n3. Under **Columns**, uncheck auto-generated columns not present in the CSV (`id`, `metadata`, `created_at`).\n4. **Debugging Trailing Blank Lines:** Ensure your CSV file has no empty trailing lines at the bottom, or PostgreSQL `COPY` will throw `ERROR: missing data for column`.	Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)	https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical	654	Lecture 4: Data Ingestion — Single & Batch INSERT, RETURNING, ON CONFLICT & Bulk CSV Import	Insert single and multiple rows, capture generated fields with RETURNING, handle duplicate keys gracefully via ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING, and bulk-import CSV files.	29	Easy	Module 2: CRUD Operations & Data Manipulation	4	[{"id": "pg-4-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/", "name": "Recyclable and Low Fat Products", "title": "Recyclable and Low Fat Products", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Recyclable and Low Fat Products\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/"}, {"id": "pg-4-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "name": "Find Customer Referee", "title": "Find Customer Referee", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Find Customer Referee\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/"}, {"id": "pg-4-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "name": "Big Countries", "title": "Big Countries", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Big Countries\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/"}, {"id": "pg-4-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/", "name": "Article Views I", "title": "Article Views I", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Article Views I\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/"}, {"id": "pg-4-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/", "name": "Invalid Tweets", "title": "Invalid Tweets", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Invalid Tweets\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/"}, {"id": "pg-4-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/", "name": "Combine Two Tables", "title": "Combine Two Tables", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Combine Two Tables\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/"}, {"id": "pg-4-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "name": "Customers Who Never Order", "title": "Customers Who Never Order", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_7", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Customers Who Never Order\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/"}, {"id": "pg-4-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "name": "Duplicate Emails", "title": "Duplicate Emails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_8", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Duplicate Emails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/"}, {"id": "pg-4-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "name": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "title": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_9", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Employees Earning More Than Their Managers\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/"}, {"id": "pg-4-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "name": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "title": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "4_10", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Delete Duplicate Emails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/"}]	https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-insert.html	\N	\N	\N
22223	UML Diagrams: Class & Sequence Diagrams	lld-uml-class-sequence-diagrams	Visualizing system design: Class Diagrams (attributes, methods, associations, aggregation, composition) and Sequence Diagrams (lifelines, sync/async messages).	2026-10-07 16:56:30.769208+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: UML Diagrams: Class & Sequence Diagrams\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/nPJyyO9pb5s", "title": "What is UML Diagrams | Class & Sequence Diagrams with Real Examples", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v4-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Aggregation vs Composition in UML", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Aggregation (Weak \\"Has-A\\" - Hollow Diamond $\\\\diamondsuit$):**\\n   * The contained object has an independent lifecycle outside the container.\\n   * *Example:* A `Room` contains `Chair` and `Bed`. If the `Room` object is destroyed, the `Chair` still exists and can be moved to another room.\\n   * *Code:* The container holds pointers or references passed from outside (`Room(Chair* c)`), and its destructor does NOT delete the chair.\\n\\n2. **Composition (Strong \\"Has-A\\" - Filled Diamond $\\\\blacklozenge$):**\\n   * The contained object cannot exist without the owner; its lifecycle is strictly bound to the owner.\\n   * *Example:* A `Room` has `Walls`. If the `Room` is destroyed, the `Walls` are destroyed with it.\\n   * *Code:* The container directly owns the value or creates and destroys the pointer inside its own constructor and destructor.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Differentiate between Aggregation (hollow diamond) and Composition (filled diamond) using Room, Furniture, and Wall entities. How are their lifecycles bound in code?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v4-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Mapping UML Class Diagrams to C++ Code", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n```cpp\\n#include <string>\\n\\nclass BankAccount {\\nprivate:\\n    std::string accountNumber;\\n    double balance;\\n\\nprotected:\\n    std::string accountHolder;\\n\\npublic:\\n    virtual ~BankAccount() = default;\\n\\n    bool deposit(double amount) {\\n        if (amount <= 0) return false;\\n        balance += amount;\\n        return true;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Pure virtual method makes BankAccount an abstract class\\n    virtual bool withdraw(double amount) = 0;\\n\\n    // Encapsulated getter\\n    double getBalance() const { return balance; }\\n};\\n```", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Translate the following UML specification into a clean C++ class declaration:\\n- Class: BankAccount\\n- Private attributes: accountNumber (string), balance (double)\\n- Protected attribute: accountHolder (string)\\n- Public methods: deposit(double amount) returning bool, withdraw(double amount) pure virtual returning bool", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v4-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Trace an ATM Cash Withdrawal in a Sequence Diagram", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Card Insertion:** `User` sends synchronous call `insertCard(card)` to `ATM`. `ATM` activates.\\n2. **PIN Entry:** `ATM` prompts `User` for PIN; `User` calls `enterPin(1234)`.\\n3. **Validation Call:** `ATM` sends sync message `validateCard(cardId, pin)` to `BankServer`. `BankServer` lifeline activates and returns `true` (dashed return arrow).\\n4. **Withdrawal Request:** `User` calls `requestCash(amount)` on `ATM`.\\n5. **Balance Check & Debit:** `ATM` calls `debitAccount(amount)` on `BankServer`. `BankServer` verifies balance, executes database transaction, and returns `success`.\\n6. **Hardware Dispense:** `ATM` calls `dispense(amount)` on `CashDispenser`. `CashDispenser` activates, mechanically delivers physical bills, and returns `cashDispensed`.\\n7. **Completion & Eject:** `ATM` returns `cashReady` to `User` and ejects card. Lifelines deactivate.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Describe the sequential message flow in an ATM withdrawal sequence diagram involving User, ATM, CardReader, BankServer, and CashDispenser.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v4-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Multiplicity Notations in UML Relationships", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Multiplicity Notations:**\\n* **`1`:** Exactly one instance.\\n* **`0..1`:** Zero or one instance (optional relationship / nullable pointer).\\n* **`*` or `0..*`:** Zero or many instances (unbounded list/collection).\\n* **`1..*`:** At least one or many instances (non-empty collection).\\n\\n**Customer-to-Order Example:**\\n* At the `Customer` end: `1` (Each order belongs to exactly one customer).\\n* At the `Order` end: `0..*` (A customer can have zero orders initially, or accumulate many orders over time).\\n* *Code Representation:* `class Customer { std::vector<Order*> orders; };` and `class Order { Customer* customer; };`.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain UML Multiplicity notations (1, 0..1, 1..*, *) in class relationships. Provide an example of a Customer-to-Order relationship.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v4-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Synchronous vs Asynchronous Messages in Sequence Diagrams", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Synchronous Messages (Solid line with filled triangle arrowhead $\\\\longrightarrow$):**\\n   * The calling object halts its own execution and blocks waiting for the called object to complete processing and return a result.\\n   * *Software Reality:* Standard blocking in-memory function calls or synchronous HTTP/REST request-response round-trips.\\n\\n2. **Asynchronous Messages (Solid line with open stick arrowhead $\\\\longrightarrow$):**\\n   * The calling object dispatches the message and immediately resumes its own work without waiting for a reply.\\n   * *Software Reality:* Fire-and-forget events, message queues (Kafka/RabbitMQ), background worker job enqueuing, or non-blocking threads.\\n\\n3. **Reply / Return Messages (Dashed line with open arrowhead $\\\\dashrightarrow$):**\\n   * Explicitly denotes returning data and transferring control back to the caller.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How are Synchronous and Asynchronous messages visually distinguished in UML Sequence Diagrams, and what do they represent in software architectures?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. Structural vs. Behavioral Diagrams\n* **Structural Diagrams (Class Diagrams):** Depict the static architecture, classes, data members, methods, and relationships.\n* **Behavioral Diagrams (Sequence Diagrams):** Depict the dynamic chronological execution flow, message transfers, and lifecycle interactions between objects.\n\n### 💡 2. The Anatomy of a Class Diagram\n* **3 Compartments:** Class Name (top), Attributes/Variables (middle), Methods/Behaviors (bottom).\n* **Visibility Notation:** `+` Public, `-` Private, `#` Protected.\n* **Relationship Arrows:**\n  * **Inheritance (Is-A):** Solid line with a closed hollow triangle arrow pointing to base class.\n  * **Simple Association:** Open arrow line pointing to the referenced class.\n  * **Aggregation (Weak Has-A):** Hollow diamond at the container end (e.g., `Room` has `Chair`; chair can exist independently).\n  * **Composition (Strong Has-A):** Filled solid diamond at the owner end (e.g., `Chair` has `Legs`; legs have no independent lifecycle).\n\n### 💡 3. Sequence Diagram Components\n* **Lifelines:** Vertical dashed lines showing the chronological timeline of an object from top to bottom.\n* **Activation Bars:** Thin vertical rectangles denoting when an object is actively executing code.\n* **Messages:** Solid line with solid arrowhead (Synchronous call), dashed line with open arrowhead (Reply/Return), open arrowhead (Asynchronous call).	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	UML Diagrams: Class & Sequence Diagrams	Visualizing system design: Class Diagrams (attributes, methods, associations, aggregation, composition) and Sequence Diagrams (lifelines, sync/async messages).	72	Hard	Low Level Design	18	[{"id": "lld-v4-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Aggregation vs Composition in UML", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Aggregation (Weak \\"Has-A\\" - Hollow Diamond $\\\\diamondsuit$):**\\n   * The contained object has an independent lifecycle outside the container.\\n   * *Example:* A `Room` contains `Chair` and `Bed`. If the `Room` object is destroyed, the `Chair` still exists and can be moved to another room.\\n   * *Code:* The container holds pointers or references passed from outside (`Room(Chair* c)`), and its destructor does NOT delete the chair.\\n\\n2. **Composition (Strong \\"Has-A\\" - Filled Diamond $\\\\blacklozenge$):**\\n   * The contained object cannot exist without the owner; its lifecycle is strictly bound to the owner.\\n   * *Example:* A `Room` has `Walls`. If the `Room` is destroyed, the `Walls` are destroyed with it.\\n   * *Code:* The container directly owns the value or creates and destroys the pointer inside its own constructor and destructor.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Differentiate between Aggregation (hollow diamond) and Composition (filled diamond) using Room, Furniture, and Wall entities. How are their lifecycles bound in code?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v4-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Mapping UML Class Diagrams to C++ Code", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n```cpp\\n#include <string>\\n\\nclass BankAccount {\\nprivate:\\n    std::string accountNumber;\\n    double balance;\\n\\nprotected:\\n    std::string accountHolder;\\n\\npublic:\\n    virtual ~BankAccount() = default;\\n\\n    bool deposit(double amount) {\\n        if (amount <= 0) return false;\\n        balance += amount;\\n        return true;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Pure virtual method makes BankAccount an abstract class\\n    virtual bool withdraw(double amount) = 0;\\n\\n    // Encapsulated getter\\n    double getBalance() const { return balance; }\\n};\\n```", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Translate the following UML specification into a clean C++ class declaration:\\n- Class: BankAccount\\n- Private attributes: accountNumber (string), balance (double)\\n- Protected attribute: accountHolder (string)\\n- Public methods: deposit(double amount) returning bool, withdraw(double amount) pure virtual returning bool", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v4-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Trace an ATM Cash Withdrawal in a Sequence Diagram", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Card Insertion:** `User` sends synchronous call `insertCard(card)` to `ATM`. `ATM` activates.\\n2. **PIN Entry:** `ATM` prompts `User` for PIN; `User` calls `enterPin(1234)`.\\n3. **Validation Call:** `ATM` sends sync message `validateCard(cardId, pin)` to `BankServer`. `BankServer` lifeline activates and returns `true` (dashed return arrow).\\n4. **Withdrawal Request:** `User` calls `requestCash(amount)` on `ATM`.\\n5. **Balance Check & Debit:** `ATM` calls `debitAccount(amount)` on `BankServer`. `BankServer` verifies balance, executes database transaction, and returns `success`.\\n6. **Hardware Dispense:** `ATM` calls `dispense(amount)` on `CashDispenser`. `CashDispenser` activates, mechanically delivers physical bills, and returns `cashDispensed`.\\n7. **Completion & Eject:** `ATM` returns `cashReady` to `User` and ejects card. Lifelines deactivate.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Describe the sequential message flow in an ATM withdrawal sequence diagram involving User, ATM, CardReader, BankServer, and CashDispenser.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v4-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Multiplicity Notations in UML Relationships", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Multiplicity Notations:**\\n* **`1`:** Exactly one instance.\\n* **`0..1`:** Zero or one instance (optional relationship / nullable pointer).\\n* **`*` or `0..*`:** Zero or many instances (unbounded list/collection).\\n* **`1..*`:** At least one or many instances (non-empty collection).\\n\\n**Customer-to-Order Example:**\\n* At the `Customer` end: `1` (Each order belongs to exactly one customer).\\n* At the `Order` end: `0..*` (A customer can have zero orders initially, or accumulate many orders over time).\\n* *Code Representation:* `class Customer { std::vector<Order*> orders; };` and `class Order { Customer* customer; };`.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain UML Multiplicity notations (1, 0..1, 1..*, *) in class relationships. Provide an example of a Customer-to-Order relationship.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v4-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Synchronous vs Asynchronous Messages in Sequence Diagrams", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Synchronous Messages (Solid line with filled triangle arrowhead $\\\\longrightarrow$):**\\n   * The calling object halts its own execution and blocks waiting for the called object to complete processing and return a result.\\n   * *Software Reality:* Standard blocking in-memory function calls or synchronous HTTP/REST request-response round-trips.\\n\\n2. **Asynchronous Messages (Solid line with open stick arrowhead $\\\\longrightarrow$):**\\n   * The calling object dispatches the message and immediately resumes its own work without waiting for a reply.\\n   * *Software Reality:* Fire-and-forget events, message queues (Kafka/RabbitMQ), background worker job enqueuing, or non-blocking threads.\\n\\n3. **Reply / Return Messages (Dashed line with open arrowhead $\\\\dashrightarrow$):**\\n   * Explicitly denotes returning data and transferring control back to the caller.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How are Synchronous and Asynchronous messages visually distinguished in UML Sequence Diagrams, and what do they represent in software architectures?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22179	Discount	aptitude-quant-discount	Master Marked Price (MRP) mechanics, equivalent successive discounts, false-weight dealer margin mapping, and nullifying absolute losses via percentage markups.	2026-10-07 11:23:07.969127+05:30	### 📌 1. Fundamentals of Discount & Marked Price\n* **Definition:** Discount is ALWAYS calculated on the **Marked Price (MP)**, List Price, or Advertised Price, NEVER on Cost Price (CP).\n  * **`Discount Amount = MP - SP`**\n  * **`Discount % = (Discount Amount / MP) × 100`**\n  * **`SP = MP × (100 - Discount%) / 100`**\n* **The Magic Formula linking CP, MP, Profit, and Discount:**\n  * **`CP / MP = (100 - Discount%) / (100 + Profit%)`**\n  * *(Use this to instantly find markup percentages without calculating intermediate Selling Prices!)*\n\n### 💡 2. Successive Discounts\n* If two successive discounts of `a%` and `b%` are given, the **Single Equivalent Discount** is:\n  **`Equivalent Discount = a + b - (ab / 100) %`**\n* For three successive discounts of `a%`, `b%`, and `c%`, first find the equivalent of `a` and `b` (call it `x`), then find the equivalent of `x` and `c` using the same formula:\n  **`Final Equivalent = x + c - (xc / 100) %`**\n* **Important Property:** A single discount of `X%` is ALWAYS strictly greater than two successive discounts that sum to `X%` (e.g., a flat 20% discount is better for the buyer than successive 10% and 10% discounts, which equal 19%).\n\n### 💡 3. High-Yield Placement Hacks\n* **Buy X Get Y Free:**\n  * Discount % = **`[ Y / (X + Y) ] × 100`**\n  * *(You get Y items free out of the TOTAL X+Y items you carry home!)*\n* **Loss Nullification:**\n  * If a fraction `1/N` of goods is sold at a loss of `L%`, to achieve "No Profit, No Loss" overall, the remaining `(N - 1)/N` goods must be sold at a profit of:\n  * **`Profit % = L / (N - 1) %`**	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FW97hRrHcSw", "type": "youtube", "title": "Discount - Shortcuts & Tricks for 2026 Placement Tests, Job Interviews & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-discount-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Successive vs Single Discount] Find the exact difference between a single discount of 30% and two successive discounts of 25% and 5% given on shopping of ₹2,000.", "options": ["A) ₹15", "B) ₹20", "C) ₹25", "D) ₹30"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Sonali could not decide between a single flat discount of 30% or two successive discounts of 25% and 5%, both given on a shopping bill of ₹2,000. What is the exact difference in Rupees between the two discount schemes?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Flat 30% discount on ₹2000 = ₹600 saved. 2) Two successive discounts of 25% and 5%: Equivalent discount = 25 + 5 - (25×5)/100 = 30 - 1.25 = 28.75%. 3) Discount amount = 28.75% of ₹2000 = ₹575 saved. 4) Difference = 600 - 575 = ₹25. (Shortcut: The difference in percentage is simply (a×b)/100 % = 1.25%. 1.25% of 2000 = ₹25).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-discount-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Three Successive Discounts] If successive discounts are 10%, 20%, and 30%, what is the single equivalent discount percentage?", "options": ["A) 49.6%", "B) 50.4%", "C) 52.6%", "D) 60.0%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A store offers three successive discounts of 10%, 20%, and 30% on a laptop. What is the single equivalent net discount percentage?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Step 1: Find the equivalent of 10% and 20%: 10 + 20 - (10×20)/100 = 30 - 2 = 28%. Step 2: Combine 28% with the third discount 30%: 28 + 30 - (28×30)/100 = 58 - 840/100 = 58 - 8.4 = 49.6%.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-discount-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Finding Second Unknown Discount] Two successive discounts, with the first being 20%, were given on a table having a marked price of ₹6,400. If it was finally sold for ₹4,608, find the second discount.", "options": ["A) 8%", "B) 10%", "C) 12%", "D) 15%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two successive discounts, with the first being 20%, were given on a table having a marked price of ₹6,400. If it was finally sold for ₹4,608, what was the exact percentage of the second discount?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Price after 1st discount (20% off): 80% of 6400 = 0.8 × 6400 = 5120. 2) The 2nd discount reduces the price from ₹5120 to ₹4608. 3) Second discount amount = 5120 - 4608 = ₹512. 4) Second discount % = (512 / 5120) × 100 = 10%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-discount-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Comparing Discount Options] Which of the following yields the maximum discount on ₹6,896: (1) Two successive of 5% and 5%, (2) Single 10%, (3) Two successive of 8% and 2%?", "options": ["A) Scheme 1", "B) Scheme 2", "C) Scheme 3", "D) All three yield the exact same discount"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Which of the following discount schemes yields the MAXIMUM discount for a customer on an item marked at ₹6,896?\\n1. Two successive discounts of 5% and 5%\\n2. A single flat discount of 10%\\n3. Two successive discounts of 8% and 2%", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Evaluate the equivalent discount for each: 1) 5 + 5 - 25/100 = 9.75%. 2) Flat 10%. 3) 8 + 2 - 16/100 = 9.84%. Since the sum of all pairs is 10%, the single flat discount of 10% is mathematically ALWAYS greater than any successive sequence that adds up to 10. (The base price 6896 is irrelevant decoy info).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-discount-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Discount Linked to Profit via Mark Price] Raj decides to sell his watch at a 5% discount, but his brother buys it from him at an 8% discount. Due to this, Raj gets ₹45 less in profit. Find the Marked Price.", "options": ["A) ₹1,200", "B) ₹1,500", "C) ₹1,800", "D) ₹2,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Raj decides to sell his watch at a 5% discount on the marked price. However, his brother buys it from him at an 8% discount instead. Due to giving this extra discount, Raj's overall profit decreases by exactly ₹45. What was the marked price of the watch?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The extra discount given is 8% - 5% = 3% of the Marked Price. This 3% extra discount directly corresponds to the ₹45 loss in profit! So, 3% of MP = ₹45 => (3/100) × MP = 45 => MP = 4500 / 3 = ₹1,500.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-discount-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Nullifying Loss over Remaining Goods] Simran bought pet food worth ₹56,000. She sold 1/3 of it incurring a loss of 40%. At what profit percent must she sell the rest to nullify this loss?", "options": ["A) 15%", "B) 20%", "C) 30%", "D) 40%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Simran bought pet food worth ₹56,000. She then sold 1/3 of it, incurring a loss of 40% on that portion. At what exact profit percentage must she sell the remaining supplies to completely nullify this loss (resulting in no profit, no loss overall)?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "She sold 1 part (out of 3) at a 40% loss. To break even, this 40% loss must be recovered as profit evenly spread across the remaining 2 parts. Profit needed per remaining part = 40% / 2 = 20% profit.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-discount-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Double Discount Back-Calculation] There was 25% off on handbags. Madhu got an additional 10% discount for paying cash. If she paid ₹405, what was the price tag?", "options": ["A) ₹540", "B) ₹600", "C) ₹640", "D) ₹675"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A store offered a flat 25% off on handbags. Madhu bought a handbag and received an additional 10% successive discount on the billed amount for paying in cash. If she finally paid ₹405, what was the original price tag on the handbag?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Equivalent discount of 25% and 10% = 25 + 10 - 2.5 = 32.5%. 2) She pays (100 - 32.5)% = 67.5% of the Marked Price. 3) 67.5% of MP = 405 => (67.5/100) × MP = 405 => MP = 40500 / 67.5 = 405000 / 675 = ₹600.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-discount-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Markup and Break-Even Discount] Chandrika raised the price of their products by 40%. How much discount should they give so as to sell the products on a no-profit no-loss basis?", "options": ["A) 25.0%", "B) 28.57%", "C) 30.0%", "D) 40.0%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A company raised the price of its products by 40% above the cost price. How much discount percentage must they now offer on this new marked price in order to sell the products on a \\"no profit, no loss\\" basis?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Original CP = 100. New Marked Price = 140. To break even, they must sell it back at SP = 100. The discount required in rupees = 140 - 100 = 40. The discount percentage is calculated on the NEW marked price: (40 / 140) × 100 = (2/7) × 100 ≈ 28.57%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-discount-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Number of Items Bought After Discount] There is a 10% discount on a dozen pairs of trousers marked at ₹8,000. How many pairs of trousers can be bought with ₹2,400?", "options": ["A) 3 pairs", "B) 4 pairs", "C) 5 pairs", "D) 6 pairs"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "There is a flat 10% discount on a bundle of one dozen (12) pairs of trousers marked collectively at ₹8,000. Under this discount scheme, exactly how many pairs of trousers can a customer buy with ₹2,400?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Marked price for 12 pairs = 8000. After 10% discount, Selling Price for 12 pairs = 8000 - 800 = ₹7,200. 2) Thus, the discounted price for 1 pair = 7200 / 12 = ₹600. 3) With ₹2400, the customer can buy 2400 / 600 = 4 pairs of trousers.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-discount-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Revenue Change Analysis] How much does the saree sales revenue increase if Udai announces a 25% discount, causing the sales volume to increase by 40%?", "options": ["A) 5% Increase", "B) 10% Increase", "C) 5% Decrease", "D) 15% Increase"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Udai announces a 25% discount on sarees, which consequently causes the total sales volume (number of sarees sold) to increase by 40%. By what exact percentage does his total sales revenue increase or decrease?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Assume he originally sells 10 sarees at ₹100 each. Original Revenue = 1000. New Price = ₹75 (25% off). New Volume = 14 sarees (40% up). New Revenue = 75 × 14 = 1050. Change in revenue = 1050 - 1000 = +50. Percentage increase = (50 / 1000) × 100 = 5% increase.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-discount-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Finding Target Savings Amount] A brand announced 25% discount on trousers. Vijay wanted to save ₹400 in discount. How many trousers should he buy if each trouser costs ₹320?", "options": ["A) 3", "B) 4", "C) 5", "D) 6"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A brand announced a 25% discount on their trousers. Vijay went to the shop wanting to save exactly ₹400 via the discount. How many trousers must he buy to achieve this savings if the original marked price of each trouser is ₹320?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Discount per trouser = 25% of 320 = (1/4) × 320 = ₹80. To save a total of ₹400 in discounts, he must buy Total Savings / Discount per Item = 400 / 80 = 5 trousers.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-discount-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Reverse Calculation of Advertised Price] If a book costs ₹900 and is sold with a profit of 10% and discount of 12%, then find the advertised price.", "options": ["A) ₹1,080", "B) ₹1,125", "C) ₹1,200", "D) ₹1,250"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A retailer buys a book at a cost price of ₹900. He wishes to offer a 12% discount to his customers while still earning a 10% profit on his cost. What must be the advertised (marked) price of the book?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Using the CP/MP formula: CP / MP = (100 - Discount%) / (100 + Profit%). 900 / MP = (100 - 12) / (100 + 10) = 88 / 110. Simplify 88/110 by dividing by 22: 4/5. So 900 / MP = 4 / 5 => 4 × MP = 4500 => MP = 4500 / 4 = ₹1,125.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-discount-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Profit Value to Marked Price] Profit of Raj while selling a book to Simran is ₹63. Despite a discount of 23% on the book, Raj manages to get 10% profit. Find the list price of the book.", "options": ["A) ₹800", "B) ₹900", "C) ₹1,000", "D) ₹1,200"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Raj earns a profit of exactly ₹63 by selling a book to Simran. Even after allowing a 23% discount on the marked price, Raj still manages to make a 10% profit on his cost price. What is the marked (list) price of the book?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Profit = 10% of CP = ₹63. Thus, CP = 630. 2) Selling Price SP = CP + Profit = 630 + 63 = 693. 3) SP is also the price after a 23% discount on Marked Price (MP). So, 77% of MP = 693 => (77/100) × MP = 693 => MP = 69300 / 77. Since 77 × 9 = 693, MP = ₹900.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-discount-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Buy X Get Y Free + Discount] A shopkeeper offers \\"Buy 3 get 2 free\\" and an additional 20% discount. What is the total equivalent discount?", "options": ["A) 48%", "B) 52%", "C) 56%", "D) 60%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A shopkeeper offers a promotional scheme: \\"Buy 3 items, get 2 items free\\", and additionally provides a 20% flat discount on the final bill. What is the total equivalent discount percentage the customer receives?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) \\"Buy 3 get 2 free\\" means getting 5 items for the price of 3. Discount 1 = (Free items / Total items) × 100 = (2 / 5) × 100 = 40%. 2) The additional discount is 20%. 3) Successive discounts of 40% and 20%: Equivalent = 40 + 20 - (40×20)/100 = 60 - 8 = 52%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-discount-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Markup Nullified by Double Discount] A trader marks up his goods by 50%. He then offers two successive discounts of 20% and 10%. What is his overall profit or loss percentage?", "options": ["A) 8% Profit", "B) 8% Loss", "C) 12% Profit", "D) 12% Loss"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A trader marks up the price of his goods by 50% above the cost price. He then offers his customers two successive discounts of 20% and 10% on the marked price. What is his overall net profit or loss percentage?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let CP = 100. Marked Price MP = 150. Successive discounts of 20% and 10% equal a single discount of 20 + 10 - 2 = 28%. So SP = 150 - 28% of 150 = 150 - (0.28 × 150) = 150 - 42 = 108. Since CP = 100 and SP = 108, the trader makes an 8% profit.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-discount-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Finding True Weight in Dishonest Dealing] A dishonest dealer marks up his goods by 20% and gives a discount of 10%. He also uses a false weight, earning an overall profit of 35%. What weight does he use for 1 kg?", "options": ["A) 800 grams", "B) 850 grams", "C) 900 grams", "D) 950 grams"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A dishonest dealer marks up his goods by 20% above the cost price and gives a discount of 10% on the marked price. Additionally, he uses a false weight for 1 kg. If his overall net profit is 35%, what false weight does he actually use for 1 kg?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let true CP for 1000g = ₹1000 (₹1/g). 1) He marks up by 20% -> MP = 1200. 2) Gives 10% discount -> SP = 1200 - 120 = ₹1080. 3) He makes an overall profit of 35% on his actual cost. So SP = 1.35 × Actual CP => 1080 = 1.35 × Actual CP => Actual CP = 1080 / 1.35 = 800. Since 1g costs ₹1, an actual CP of ₹800 means he is only handing over 800 grams to the customer!", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-discount-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Ratio of MP to CP Given Profit and Discount] An article is sold at a 15% discount on the marked price, generating a profit of 19%. What is the ratio of the Marked Price to the Cost Price?", "options": ["A) 5 : 4", "B) 6 : 5", "C) 7 : 5", "D) 8 : 7"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An article is sold at a 15% discount on the marked price, generating a profit of exactly 19%. What is the ratio of the Marked Price to the Cost Price?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Using the formula CP / MP = (100 - Discount%) / (100 + Profit%) = (100 - 15) / (100 + 19) = 85 / 119. Divide numerator and denominator by 17 (since 17×5=85 and 17×7=119): CP / MP = 5 / 7. Therefore, the ratio of Marked Price to Cost Price (MP : CP) is 7 : 5.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-discount-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Unknown Third Discount in Successive Chain] The marked price of an article is ₹5,000. It is sold for ₹3,060 after three successive discounts of 20%, 15%, and x%. Find the value of x.", "options": ["A) 10%", "B) 12%", "C) 15%", "D) 18%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The marked price of an article is ₹5,000. A customer buys it for ₹3,060 after receiving three successive discounts of 20%, 15%, and x%. What is the exact value of the third discount x%?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Apply the known discounts sequentially: 1) First discount of 20% on 5000: Price = 5000 × 0.80 = 4000. 2) Second discount of 15% on 4000: 15% of 4000 = 600. Price = 4000 - 600 = 3400. 3) The third discount reduces the price from 3400 to 3060. Discount amount = 3400 - 3060 = 340. 4) Third discount percentage x = (340 / 3400) × 100 = 10%.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-discount-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Discount and Tax Combination] The marked price of a TV is ₹20,000. A discount of 10% is given, but an 8% sales tax is charged on the discounted price. What is the final selling price?", "options": ["A) ₹19,440", "B) ₹19,600", "C) ₹19,800", "D) ₹20,160"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The marked price of a television is ₹20,000. The store offers a discount of 10% on the marked price, but the government charges an 8% sales tax on the discounted price. What is the final amount the customer must pay?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Apply the discount first: 10% off 20,000 = Price drops to 18,000. 2) Apply sales tax on the discounted price: 8% of 18,000 = (8/100) × 18000 = 1440. 3) Final price = 18,000 + 1,440 = ₹19,440. (Note: Sales tax is an addition, discount is a subtraction, applied sequentially).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-discount-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Equalizing Selling Prices of Two Items] A merchant marks up item A by 20% and item B by 30%. He gives a 10% discount on A and a 20% discount on B. If both sell for the same price, find the ratio of their cost prices.", "options": ["A) 26 : 27", "B) 27 : 26", "C) 13 : 14", "D) 14 : 13"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A merchant marks up the cost price of item A by 20% and item B by 30%. He then offers a 10% discount on the marked price of A, and a 20% discount on the marked price of B. If the final selling prices of both items are identical, what is the ratio of the cost price of A to the cost price of B?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let CP of A be C_a and CP of B be C_b. 1) SP of A = C_a × 1.20 × 0.90 = 1.08 × C_a. 2) SP of B = C_b × 1.30 × 0.80 = 1.04 × C_b. 3) Since SP_A = SP_B => 1.08 C_a = 1.04 C_b => C_a / C_b = 1.04 / 1.08 = 104 / 108. Dividing by 4 gives 26 / 27.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. Fundamentals of Discount & Marked Price\n* **Definition:** Discount is ALWAYS calculated on the **Marked Price (MP)**, List Price, or Advertised Price, NEVER on Cost Price (CP).\n  * **`Discount Amount = MP - SP`**\n  * **`Discount % = (Discount Amount / MP) × 100`**\n  * **`SP = MP × (100 - Discount%) / 100`**\n* **The Magic Formula linking CP, MP, Profit, and Discount:**\n  * **`CP / MP = (100 - Discount%) / (100 + Profit%)`**\n  * *(Use this to instantly find markup percentages without calculating intermediate Selling Prices!)*\n\n### 💡 2. Successive Discounts\n* If two successive discounts of `a%` and `b%` are given, the **Single Equivalent Discount** is:\n  **`Equivalent Discount = a + b - (ab / 100) %`**\n* For three successive discounts of `a%`, `b%`, and `c%`, first find the equivalent of `a` and `b` (call it `x`), then find the equivalent of `x` and `c` using the same formula:\n  **`Final Equivalent = x + c - (xc / 100) %`**\n* **Important Property:** A single discount of `X%` is ALWAYS strictly greater than two successive discounts that sum to `X%` (e.g., a flat 20% discount is better for the buyer than successive 10% and 10% discounts, which equal 19%).\n\n### 💡 3. High-Yield Placement Hacks\n* **Buy X Get Y Free:**\n  * Discount % = **`[ Y / (X + Y) ] × 100`**\n  * *(You get Y items free out of the TOTAL X+Y items you carry home!)*\n* **Loss Nullification:**\n  * If a fraction `1/N` of goods is sold at a loss of `L%`, to achieve "No Profit, No Loss" overall, the remaining `(N - 1)/N` goods must be sold at a profit of:\n  * **`Profit % = L / (N - 1) %`**	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Discount	Master Marked Price (MRP) mechanics, equivalent successive discounts, false-weight dealer margin mapping, and nullifying absolute losses via percentage markups.	52	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	13	[{"id": "apt-discount-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Successive vs Single Discount] Find the exact difference between a single discount of 30% and two successive discounts of 25% and 5% given on shopping of ₹2,000.", "options": ["A) ₹15", "B) ₹20", "C) ₹25", "D) ₹30"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Sonali could not decide between a single flat discount of 30% or two successive discounts of 25% and 5%, both given on a shopping bill of ₹2,000. What is the exact difference in Rupees between the two discount schemes?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Flat 30% discount on ₹2000 = ₹600 saved. 2) Two successive discounts of 25% and 5%: Equivalent discount = 25 + 5 - (25×5)/100 = 30 - 1.25 = 28.75%. 3) Discount amount = 28.75% of ₹2000 = ₹575 saved. 4) Difference = 600 - 575 = ₹25. (Shortcut: The difference in percentage is simply (a×b)/100 % = 1.25%. 1.25% of 2000 = ₹25).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-discount-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Three Successive Discounts] If successive discounts are 10%, 20%, and 30%, what is the single equivalent discount percentage?", "options": ["A) 49.6%", "B) 50.4%", "C) 52.6%", "D) 60.0%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A store offers three successive discounts of 10%, 20%, and 30% on a laptop. What is the single equivalent net discount percentage?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Step 1: Find the equivalent of 10% and 20%: 10 + 20 - (10×20)/100 = 30 - 2 = 28%. Step 2: Combine 28% with the third discount 30%: 28 + 30 - (28×30)/100 = 58 - 840/100 = 58 - 8.4 = 49.6%.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-discount-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Finding Second Unknown Discount] Two successive discounts, with the first being 20%, were given on a table having a marked price of ₹6,400. If it was finally sold for ₹4,608, find the second discount.", "options": ["A) 8%", "B) 10%", "C) 12%", "D) 15%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two successive discounts, with the first being 20%, were given on a table having a marked price of ₹6,400. If it was finally sold for ₹4,608, what was the exact percentage of the second discount?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Price after 1st discount (20% off): 80% of 6400 = 0.8 × 6400 = 5120. 2) The 2nd discount reduces the price from ₹5120 to ₹4608. 3) Second discount amount = 5120 - 4608 = ₹512. 4) Second discount % = (512 / 5120) × 100 = 10%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-discount-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Comparing Discount Options] Which of the following yields the maximum discount on ₹6,896: (1) Two successive of 5% and 5%, (2) Single 10%, (3) Two successive of 8% and 2%?", "options": ["A) Scheme 1", "B) Scheme 2", "C) Scheme 3", "D) All three yield the exact same discount"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Which of the following discount schemes yields the MAXIMUM discount for a customer on an item marked at ₹6,896?\\n1. Two successive discounts of 5% and 5%\\n2. A single flat discount of 10%\\n3. Two successive discounts of 8% and 2%", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Evaluate the equivalent discount for each: 1) 5 + 5 - 25/100 = 9.75%. 2) Flat 10%. 3) 8 + 2 - 16/100 = 9.84%. Since the sum of all pairs is 10%, the single flat discount of 10% is mathematically ALWAYS greater than any successive sequence that adds up to 10. (The base price 6896 is irrelevant decoy info).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-discount-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Discount Linked to Profit via Mark Price] Raj decides to sell his watch at a 5% discount, but his brother buys it from him at an 8% discount. Due to this, Raj gets ₹45 less in profit. Find the Marked Price.", "options": ["A) ₹1,200", "B) ₹1,500", "C) ₹1,800", "D) ₹2,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Raj decides to sell his watch at a 5% discount on the marked price. However, his brother buys it from him at an 8% discount instead. Due to giving this extra discount, Raj's overall profit decreases by exactly ₹45. What was the marked price of the watch?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The extra discount given is 8% - 5% = 3% of the Marked Price. This 3% extra discount directly corresponds to the ₹45 loss in profit! So, 3% of MP = ₹45 => (3/100) × MP = 45 => MP = 4500 / 3 = ₹1,500.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-discount-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Nullifying Loss over Remaining Goods] Simran bought pet food worth ₹56,000. She sold 1/3 of it incurring a loss of 40%. At what profit percent must she sell the rest to nullify this loss?", "options": ["A) 15%", "B) 20%", "C) 30%", "D) 40%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Simran bought pet food worth ₹56,000. She then sold 1/3 of it, incurring a loss of 40% on that portion. At what exact profit percentage must she sell the remaining supplies to completely nullify this loss (resulting in no profit, no loss overall)?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "She sold 1 part (out of 3) at a 40% loss. To break even, this 40% loss must be recovered as profit evenly spread across the remaining 2 parts. Profit needed per remaining part = 40% / 2 = 20% profit.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-discount-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Double Discount Back-Calculation] There was 25% off on handbags. Madhu got an additional 10% discount for paying cash. If she paid ₹405, what was the price tag?", "options": ["A) ₹540", "B) ₹600", "C) ₹640", "D) ₹675"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A store offered a flat 25% off on handbags. Madhu bought a handbag and received an additional 10% successive discount on the billed amount for paying in cash. If she finally paid ₹405, what was the original price tag on the handbag?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Equivalent discount of 25% and 10% = 25 + 10 - 2.5 = 32.5%. 2) She pays (100 - 32.5)% = 67.5% of the Marked Price. 3) 67.5% of MP = 405 => (67.5/100) × MP = 405 => MP = 40500 / 67.5 = 405000 / 675 = ₹600.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-discount-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Markup and Break-Even Discount] Chandrika raised the price of their products by 40%. How much discount should they give so as to sell the products on a no-profit no-loss basis?", "options": ["A) 25.0%", "B) 28.57%", "C) 30.0%", "D) 40.0%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A company raised the price of its products by 40% above the cost price. How much discount percentage must they now offer on this new marked price in order to sell the products on a \\"no profit, no loss\\" basis?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Original CP = 100. New Marked Price = 140. To break even, they must sell it back at SP = 100. The discount required in rupees = 140 - 100 = 40. The discount percentage is calculated on the NEW marked price: (40 / 140) × 100 = (2/7) × 100 ≈ 28.57%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-discount-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Number of Items Bought After Discount] There is a 10% discount on a dozen pairs of trousers marked at ₹8,000. How many pairs of trousers can be bought with ₹2,400?", "options": ["A) 3 pairs", "B) 4 pairs", "C) 5 pairs", "D) 6 pairs"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "There is a flat 10% discount on a bundle of one dozen (12) pairs of trousers marked collectively at ₹8,000. Under this discount scheme, exactly how many pairs of trousers can a customer buy with ₹2,400?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Marked price for 12 pairs = 8000. After 10% discount, Selling Price for 12 pairs = 8000 - 800 = ₹7,200. 2) Thus, the discounted price for 1 pair = 7200 / 12 = ₹600. 3) With ₹2400, the customer can buy 2400 / 600 = 4 pairs of trousers.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-discount-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Revenue Change Analysis] How much does the saree sales revenue increase if Udai announces a 25% discount, causing the sales volume to increase by 40%?", "options": ["A) 5% Increase", "B) 10% Increase", "C) 5% Decrease", "D) 15% Increase"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Udai announces a 25% discount on sarees, which consequently causes the total sales volume (number of sarees sold) to increase by 40%. By what exact percentage does his total sales revenue increase or decrease?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Assume he originally sells 10 sarees at ₹100 each. Original Revenue = 1000. New Price = ₹75 (25% off). New Volume = 14 sarees (40% up). New Revenue = 75 × 14 = 1050. Change in revenue = 1050 - 1000 = +50. Percentage increase = (50 / 1000) × 100 = 5% increase.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-discount-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Finding Target Savings Amount] A brand announced 25% discount on trousers. Vijay wanted to save ₹400 in discount. How many trousers should he buy if each trouser costs ₹320?", "options": ["A) 3", "B) 4", "C) 5", "D) 6"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A brand announced a 25% discount on their trousers. Vijay went to the shop wanting to save exactly ₹400 via the discount. How many trousers must he buy to achieve this savings if the original marked price of each trouser is ₹320?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Discount per trouser = 25% of 320 = (1/4) × 320 = ₹80. To save a total of ₹400 in discounts, he must buy Total Savings / Discount per Item = 400 / 80 = 5 trousers.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-discount-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Reverse Calculation of Advertised Price] If a book costs ₹900 and is sold with a profit of 10% and discount of 12%, then find the advertised price.", "options": ["A) ₹1,080", "B) ₹1,125", "C) ₹1,200", "D) ₹1,250"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A retailer buys a book at a cost price of ₹900. He wishes to offer a 12% discount to his customers while still earning a 10% profit on his cost. What must be the advertised (marked) price of the book?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Using the CP/MP formula: CP / MP = (100 - Discount%) / (100 + Profit%). 900 / MP = (100 - 12) / (100 + 10) = 88 / 110. Simplify 88/110 by dividing by 22: 4/5. So 900 / MP = 4 / 5 => 4 × MP = 4500 => MP = 4500 / 4 = ₹1,125.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-discount-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Profit Value to Marked Price] Profit of Raj while selling a book to Simran is ₹63. Despite a discount of 23% on the book, Raj manages to get 10% profit. Find the list price of the book.", "options": ["A) ₹800", "B) ₹900", "C) ₹1,000", "D) ₹1,200"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Raj earns a profit of exactly ₹63 by selling a book to Simran. Even after allowing a 23% discount on the marked price, Raj still manages to make a 10% profit on his cost price. What is the marked (list) price of the book?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Profit = 10% of CP = ₹63. Thus, CP = 630. 2) Selling Price SP = CP + Profit = 630 + 63 = 693. 3) SP is also the price after a 23% discount on Marked Price (MP). So, 77% of MP = 693 => (77/100) × MP = 693 => MP = 69300 / 77. Since 77 × 9 = 693, MP = ₹900.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-discount-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Buy X Get Y Free + Discount] A shopkeeper offers \\"Buy 3 get 2 free\\" and an additional 20% discount. What is the total equivalent discount?", "options": ["A) 48%", "B) 52%", "C) 56%", "D) 60%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A shopkeeper offers a promotional scheme: \\"Buy 3 items, get 2 items free\\", and additionally provides a 20% flat discount on the final bill. What is the total equivalent discount percentage the customer receives?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) \\"Buy 3 get 2 free\\" means getting 5 items for the price of 3. Discount 1 = (Free items / Total items) × 100 = (2 / 5) × 100 = 40%. 2) The additional discount is 20%. 3) Successive discounts of 40% and 20%: Equivalent = 40 + 20 - (40×20)/100 = 60 - 8 = 52%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-discount-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Markup Nullified by Double Discount] A trader marks up his goods by 50%. He then offers two successive discounts of 20% and 10%. What is his overall profit or loss percentage?", "options": ["A) 8% Profit", "B) 8% Loss", "C) 12% Profit", "D) 12% Loss"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A trader marks up the price of his goods by 50% above the cost price. He then offers his customers two successive discounts of 20% and 10% on the marked price. What is his overall net profit or loss percentage?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let CP = 100. Marked Price MP = 150. Successive discounts of 20% and 10% equal a single discount of 20 + 10 - 2 = 28%. So SP = 150 - 28% of 150 = 150 - (0.28 × 150) = 150 - 42 = 108. Since CP = 100 and SP = 108, the trader makes an 8% profit.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-discount-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Finding True Weight in Dishonest Dealing] A dishonest dealer marks up his goods by 20% and gives a discount of 10%. He also uses a false weight, earning an overall profit of 35%. What weight does he use for 1 kg?", "options": ["A) 800 grams", "B) 850 grams", "C) 900 grams", "D) 950 grams"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A dishonest dealer marks up his goods by 20% above the cost price and gives a discount of 10% on the marked price. Additionally, he uses a false weight for 1 kg. If his overall net profit is 35%, what false weight does he actually use for 1 kg?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let true CP for 1000g = ₹1000 (₹1/g). 1) He marks up by 20% -> MP = 1200. 2) Gives 10% discount -> SP = 1200 - 120 = ₹1080. 3) He makes an overall profit of 35% on his actual cost. So SP = 1.35 × Actual CP => 1080 = 1.35 × Actual CP => Actual CP = 1080 / 1.35 = 800. Since 1g costs ₹1, an actual CP of ₹800 means he is only handing over 800 grams to the customer!", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-discount-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Ratio of MP to CP Given Profit and Discount] An article is sold at a 15% discount on the marked price, generating a profit of 19%. What is the ratio of the Marked Price to the Cost Price?", "options": ["A) 5 : 4", "B) 6 : 5", "C) 7 : 5", "D) 8 : 7"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An article is sold at a 15% discount on the marked price, generating a profit of exactly 19%. What is the ratio of the Marked Price to the Cost Price?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Using the formula CP / MP = (100 - Discount%) / (100 + Profit%) = (100 - 15) / (100 + 19) = 85 / 119. Divide numerator and denominator by 17 (since 17×5=85 and 17×7=119): CP / MP = 5 / 7. Therefore, the ratio of Marked Price to Cost Price (MP : CP) is 7 : 5.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-discount-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Unknown Third Discount in Successive Chain] The marked price of an article is ₹5,000. It is sold for ₹3,060 after three successive discounts of 20%, 15%, and x%. Find the value of x.", "options": ["A) 10%", "B) 12%", "C) 15%", "D) 18%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The marked price of an article is ₹5,000. A customer buys it for ₹3,060 after receiving three successive discounts of 20%, 15%, and x%. What is the exact value of the third discount x%?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Apply the known discounts sequentially: 1) First discount of 20% on 5000: Price = 5000 × 0.80 = 4000. 2) Second discount of 15% on 4000: 15% of 4000 = 600. Price = 4000 - 600 = 3400. 3) The third discount reduces the price from 3400 to 3060. Discount amount = 3400 - 3060 = 340. 4) Third discount percentage x = (340 / 3400) × 100 = 10%.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-discount-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Discount and Tax Combination] The marked price of a TV is ₹20,000. A discount of 10% is given, but an 8% sales tax is charged on the discounted price. What is the final selling price?", "options": ["A) ₹19,440", "B) ₹19,600", "C) ₹19,800", "D) ₹20,160"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The marked price of a television is ₹20,000. The store offers a discount of 10% on the marked price, but the government charges an 8% sales tax on the discounted price. What is the final amount the customer must pay?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Apply the discount first: 10% off 20,000 = Price drops to 18,000. 2) Apply sales tax on the discounted price: 8% of 18,000 = (8/100) × 18000 = 1440. 3) Final price = 18,000 + 1,440 = ₹19,440. (Note: Sales tax is an addition, discount is a subtraction, applied sequentially).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-discount-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Equalizing Selling Prices of Two Items] A merchant marks up item A by 20% and item B by 30%. He gives a 10% discount on A and a 20% discount on B. If both sell for the same price, find the ratio of their cost prices.", "options": ["A) 26 : 27", "B) 27 : 26", "C) 13 : 14", "D) 14 : 13"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A merchant marks up the cost price of item A by 20% and item B by 30%. He then offers a 10% discount on the marked price of A, and a 20% discount on the marked price of B. If the final selling prices of both items are identical, what is the ratio of the cost price of A to the cost price of B?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let CP of A be C_a and CP of B be C_b. 1) SP of A = C_a × 1.20 × 0.90 = 1.08 × C_a. 2) SP of B = C_b × 1.30 × 0.80 = 1.04 × C_b. 3) Since SP_A = SP_B => 1.08 C_a = 1.04 C_b => C_a / C_b = 1.04 / 1.08 = 104 / 108. Dividing by 4 gives 26 / 27.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22086	Lecture 1: MongoDB & NoSQL Foundations — Collections, Documents & BSON vs. JSON	mongo-lecture-1-nosql-foundations-bson-vs-json	Understand how NoSQL document databases differ from relational SQL tables, how BSON extends JSON with binary types, and why MongoDB excels at horizontal scaling (Sharding) and high-availability Replication.	2026-10-06 19:11:34.825286+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMongoDB is a leading open-source **NoSQL (Non-Relational) Document Database** that stores data as flexible, JSON-like documents encoded in **BSON (Binary JSON)** rather than rigid rows and columns.\n\n### 💡 SQL (RDBMS) vs. NoSQL (MongoDB) Architecture\n* **Storage Terminology:** Relational databases store data in **Tables** made of **Rows** (records) and **Columns** (fields). MongoDB stores data inside **Collections** made of individual **Documents** containing **Key-Value Fields**.\n* **Schema Flexibility:** SQL databases enforce a **fixed schema**—adding a new attribute (such as `married: true`) for a single user requires altering the entire table. MongoDB provides a **flexible schema** where each document in a collection can hold different fields or nested structures dynamically.\n* **Relationships vs. Document Locality:** RDBMS platforms (PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle) split entities across normalized tables and require multi-table `JOIN` queries. MongoDB co-locates related data inside rich nested documents and arrays for high-speed single-query reads.\n\n### 🔍 JSON vs. BSON (Binary JSON)\nWhile developers write queries and documents in standard human-readable **JSON**, MongoDB's storage engine persists data on disk in **BSON (Binary JSON)**:\n* **Encoding Format:** JSON is plain text (UTF-8 strings), whereas BSON is machine-optimized **binary encoding**.\n* **Storage Size & Speed:** Text-based JSON has parsing overhead; BSON is lightweight, compact, and significantly faster for the database engine to traverse and index.\n* **Extended Data Types:** Standard JSON only supports `String`, `Number`, `Boolean`, `Array`, `Object`, and `null`. BSON replaces generic numbers with explicit **32-bit Integer (`int`)**, **64-bit Integer (`long`)**, and **64-bit Floating Point (`double`)**, and adds native **`ObjectId`**, **`Date` (`ISODate`)**, **`Timestamp`**, and **`Regular Expression`** types.\n\n### 🛠️ Relational Row vs. MongoDB BSON Document\n```javascript\n// Equivalent MongoDB Document inside the "users" Collection\n{\n  "_id": ObjectId("6712a8f9c0ffee1234567890"),\n  "name": "Salman Khan",\n  "age": 25,\n  "gender": "Male",\n  "married": false,\n  "skills": ["Node.js", "MongoDB", "React"\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Key Architectural Benefits of MongoDB\n* **Horizontal Scaling via Sharding:** When a single database server reaches capacity, MongoDB automatically partitions data across multiple parallel servers (**Shards**) to handle massive write/read throughput.\n* **High Availability via Replication:** MongoDB maintains synchronized **Replica Sets** (cloned nodes across servers or regions) so that if a primary node fails, a secondary node automatically takes over with zero data loss.\n* **Built-In Geospatial & Real-Time Analytics:** Native support for location coordinates (e.g., mapping apps) and high-frequency binary writes (e.g., stock market tick streams).	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/introduction/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/introduction/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 1: MongoDB & NoSQL Foundations"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q1V1A9ZcpKo", "title": "MongoDB Introduction Tutorial in Hindi/Urdu", "duration": "16m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMongoDB is a leading open-source **NoSQL (Non-Relational) Document Database** that stores data as flexible, JSON-like documents encoded in **BSON (Binary JSON)** rather than rigid rows and columns.\n\n### 💡 SQL (RDBMS) vs. NoSQL (MongoDB) Architecture\n* **Storage Terminology:** Relational databases store data in **Tables** made of **Rows** (records) and **Columns** (fields). MongoDB stores data inside **Collections** made of individual **Documents** containing **Key-Value Fields**.\n* **Schema Flexibility:** SQL databases enforce a **fixed schema**—adding a new attribute (such as `married: true`) for a single user requires altering the entire table. MongoDB provides a **flexible schema** where each document in a collection can hold different fields or nested structures dynamically.\n* **Relationships vs. Document Locality:** RDBMS platforms (PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle) split entities across normalized tables and require multi-table `JOIN` queries. MongoDB co-locates related data inside rich nested documents and arrays for high-speed single-query reads.\n\n### 🔍 JSON vs. BSON (Binary JSON)\nWhile developers write queries and documents in standard human-readable **JSON**, MongoDB's storage engine persists data on disk in **BSON (Binary JSON)**:\n* **Encoding Format:** JSON is plain text (UTF-8 strings), whereas BSON is machine-optimized **binary encoding**.\n* **Storage Size & Speed:** Text-based JSON has parsing overhead; BSON is lightweight, compact, and significantly faster for the database engine to traverse and index.\n* **Extended Data Types:** Standard JSON only supports `String`, `Number`, `Boolean`, `Array`, `Object`, and `null`. BSON replaces generic numbers with explicit **32-bit Integer (`int`)**, **64-bit Integer (`long`)**, and **64-bit Floating Point (`double`)**, and adds native **`ObjectId`**, **`Date` (`ISODate`)**, **`Timestamp`**, and **`Regular Expression`** types.\n\n### 🛠️ Relational Row vs. MongoDB BSON Document\n```javascript\n// Equivalent MongoDB Document inside the "users" Collection\n{\n  "_id": ObjectId("6712a8f9c0ffee1234567890"),\n  "name": "Salman Khan",\n  "age": 25,\n  "gender": "Male",\n  "married": false,\n  "skills": ["Node.js", "MongoDB", "React"\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Key Architectural Benefits of MongoDB\n* **Horizontal Scaling via Sharding:** When a single database server reaches capacity, MongoDB automatically partitions data across multiple parallel servers (**Shards**) to handle massive write/read throughput.\n* **High Availability via Replication:** MongoDB maintains synchronized **Replica Sets** (cloned nodes across servers or regions) so that if a primary node fails, a secondary node automatically takes over with zero data loss.\n* **Built-In Geospatial & Real-Time Analytics:** Native support for location coordinates (e.g., mapping apps) and high-frequency binary writes (e.g., stock market tick streams).	Yahoo Baba	https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba	655	Lecture 1: MongoDB & NoSQL Foundations — Collections, Documents & BSON vs. JSON	Understand how NoSQL document databases differ from relational SQL tables, how BSON extends JSON with binary types, and why MongoDB excels at horizontal scaling (Sharding) and high-availability Replication.	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/introduction/	\N	\N	\N
22180	Speed, Distance & Time	aptitude-quant-speed-distance-time	Solve early/late arrival problems with the clock-difference shortcut, average speed multi-leg journeys, catching thieves, and inverse speed/time proportionalities.	2026-10-07 11:23:07.969127+05:30	### 📌 1. Core Formulas & Unit Conversions\n* **The DST Triangle:** `Distance = Speed × Time`.\n* **Unit Conversions:**\n  * To convert **km/hr to m/s**: Multiply by **`5 / 18`**.\n  * To convert **m/s to km/hr**: Multiply by **`18 / 5`**.\n\n### 💡 2. Average Speed Concepts\n* **Average Speed** is ALWAYS `Total Distance / Total Time`. Do NOT simply average the speeds!\n* **Shortcut for Two Equal Distances:** If a person travels from A to B at speed `x` and returns from B to A at speed `y`, the average speed for the round trip is:\n  **`Average Speed = (2xy) / (x + y)`**\n* **Shortcut for Three Equal Distances:**\n  **`Average Speed = (3xyz) / (xy + yz + zx)`**\n\n### 💡 3. High-Yield Placement Shortcuts\n* **Early/Late Arrival (Clock Difference Trick):**\n  * If a person travels at speed `S1` and is late by `T1` mins, and at speed `S2` is early by `T2` mins.\n  * `Time Difference (ΔT) = T1 + T2` (if one is late and one is early). If both are late, subtract them.\n  * **`Distance = (S1 × S2) / (S2 - S1) × (ΔT / 60)`**.\n* **Two Bodies Starting at the Same Time (Police vs Thief):**\n  * When two bodies start moving at the same time and eventually meet, the **Time** traveled by both is exactly the same!\n  * Thus, `Distance of A / Speed of A = Distance of B / Speed of B`.\n* **Meeting at an Intermediate Point (Cross-Timing Shortcut):**\n  * If A and B start simultaneously from opposite ends, meet at an intermediate point, and then take `t_a` and `t_b` hours to reach their respective destinations:\n  * **`Speed of A / Speed of B = √(t_b / t_a)`**.	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jzNxXm5twx4", "type": "youtube", "title": "Speed, Distance & Time - Shortcuts & Tricks for Placement Tests, Job Interviews & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-sdt-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Speed Multiplier & Time Difference] A boy increases his speed to 9/5 times his original speed. By doing this, he reaches his school 30 minutes before his usual time. How much time does he usually take?", "options": ["A) 45.0 minutes", "B) 50.5 minutes", "C) 67.5 minutes", "D) 72.0 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A boy increases his speed to 9/5 times of his original speed. By doing this, he reaches his school 30 minutes before the usual time. How much time does he usually take to reach school?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Distance is constant, so Time is inversely proportional to Speed. New Speed = 9/5 of Old Speed. Therefore, New Time = 5/9 of Old Time (T). The difference in time is T - (5/9)T = (4/9)T. We are given this difference is 30 minutes. (4/9)T = 30 => 4T = 270 => T = 270 / 4 = 67.5 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Police and Thief Relative Distance] Ramesh sees a thief at a distance of 80 m. The thief runs at 5 m/s and Ramesh chases at 7 m/s. How much distance does the thief cover before being caught?", "options": ["A) 150 meters", "B) 200 meters", "C) 280 meters", "D) 350 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Ramesh spots a thief at a distance of 80 meters. The thief starts running at a speed of 5 m/s and Ramesh immediately chases him at 7 m/s. Exactly how much distance does the thief cover before Ramesh catches him?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Both run for the same Time (T). Distance = Speed × Time. Ramesh travels (80 + D) meters and Thief travels D meters. (80 + D) / 7 = D / 5 => 5(80 + D) = 7D => 400 + 5D = 7D => 2D = 400 => D = 200 meters.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Proportional Cycle Race Distances] P cycles twice as fast as Q. R cycles 1/3 as fast as Q. If R completes the 4500m race in 45 mins, where was Q from the finishing line when P finished the race?", "options": ["A) 1,500 meters from finish", "B) 2,250 meters from finish", "C) 3,000 meters from finish", "D) Q had already finished"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "P, Q, and R are in a 4,500m cycle race. P cycles twice as fast as Q, and R cycles 1/3 as fast as Q. If R completes the race in 45 minutes, where was Q exactly from the finishing line when P finished the race?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Since P cycles twice as fast as Q, P will cover twice the distance of Q in the exact same time. When P reaches the finish line (covers 4,500m), Q covers exactly half that distance (2,250m). Therefore, Q is 4500 - 2250 = 2,250 meters away from the finishing line. (Note: R's speed and time are irrelevant decoy info!).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Rebound Meeting Point] A and B travel from X to Y (72 km apart) at 17 km/hr and 19 km/hr. B reaches Y, returns immediately, and meets A at point C. Find the distance from Y to C.", "options": ["A) 2 km", "B) 4 km", "C) 6 km", "D) 8 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Ajay and Vijay travel from town A to town B (72 km apart) at 17 km/hr and 19 km/hr respectively. Vijay reaches B first, returns immediately, and meets Ajay at point C. What is the distance from B to C?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Both travel for the same Time. Let distance from B to C be x. Ajay travels (72 - x) km. Vijay travels 72 km to B, plus x km back = (72 + x) km. Since Time is equal: (72 - x) / 17 = (72 + x) / 19. Cross-multiply: 19(72 - x) = 17(72 + x) => 1368 - 19x = 1224 + 17x => 36x = 144 => x = 4 km.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Early and Late Arrival Equation] Traveling at 50 km/hr, a man is 30 mins late. At 60 km/hr, he is 10 mins early. Find the distance.", "options": ["A) 150 km", "B) 180 km", "C) 200 km", "D) 240 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Surendra travels from home to office by car at 50 km/hr and is late by 30 minutes. When he travels at 60 km/hr, he reaches 10 minutes earlier than the usual time. How far is his office from his home?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Time difference = 30 mins late + 10 mins early = 40 minutes = 40/60 = 2/3 hours. Using the shortcut D = [S1 × S2 / (S2 - S1)] × ΔT = [50 × 60 / (60 - 50)] × (2/3) = [3000 / 10] × 2/3 = 300 × 2/3 = 200 km.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Two \\"Late\\" Arrivals Difference] Rohit drives at 30 km/hr and reaches bank 20 mins late. At 45 km/hr, he is still 8 mins late. Find distance.", "options": ["A) 15 km", "B) 18 km", "C) 24 km", "D) 30 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Rohit drives from home at 30 km/hr and reaches his bank 20 minutes late. Next day, he increases his speed to 45 km/hr, but still reaches 8 minutes late. How far is the bank from his home?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Both times he is late, so subtract the times to find the real difference: 20 mins - 8 mins = 12 minutes = 12/60 = 1/5 hours. Using D = [S1 × S2 / (S2 - S1)] × ΔT = [30 × 45 / (45 - 30)] × 1/5 = [1350 / 15] × 1/5 = 90 / 5 = 18 km.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Multi-Leg Average Speed] Pratik travels 96 km at 16 km/hr, 124 km at 31 km/hr, and 105 km at 7 km/hr. Find his average speed.", "options": ["A) 12 km/hr", "B) 13 km/hr", "C) 14 km/hr", "D) 15 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pratik travels 96 km at a speed of 16 km/hr by bike, 124 km at 31 km/hr by car, and another 105 km at 7 km/hr in a horse cart. What is his exact average speed for the entire journey?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Average Speed = Total Distance / Total Time. 1) Total Distance = 96 + 124 + 105 = 325 km. 2) Times for each leg: T1 = 96/16 = 6 hours; T2 = 124/31 = 4 hours; T3 = 105/7 = 15 hours. Total Time = 6 + 4 + 15 = 25 hours. 3) Avg Speed = 325 / 25 = 13 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Fractional Distance Average Speed] Rohit covers 1/4 distance at 20 km/hr, 1/4 at 10 km/hr, and rest at 80 km/hr. Find average speed.", "options": ["A) 22.85 km/hr", "B) 25.00 km/hr", "C) 36.66 km/hr", "D) 40.00 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Rohit covers 1/4 of his total journey distance at 20 km/hr, another 1/4 of the distance at 10 km/hr, and the remaining half (1/2) of the distance at 80 km/hr. What is his exact average speed for the whole distance?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let total distance = D. Avg Speed = Total D / Total T. T1 = (D/4)/20 = D/80. T2 = (D/4)/10 = D/40. The remaining distance is 1/2. T3 = (D/2)/80 = D/160. Total Time = D/80 + D/40 + D/160. LCM is 160. (2D + 4D + 1D) / 160 = 7D / 160. Avg Speed = D / (7D / 160) = 160 / 7 ≈ 22.85 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Cross-Timing Square Root Shortcut] A walks from Jammu to Delhi, B from Delhi to Jammu. After passing each other, they take 361 hours and 289 hours respectively to finish. Find ratio of speeds.", "options": ["A) 17 : 19", "B) 19 : 17", "C) 289 : 361", "D) 361 : 289"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A walks from Jammu to Delhi, and simultaneously B starts walking from Delhi to Jammu. After crossing each other on the way, they complete their remaining journeys in 361 hours and 289 hours respectively. What is the ratio of the speed of A to the speed of B?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "When two travelers start simultaneously from opposite ends, cross paths, and take times t_a and t_b to finish the remaining journey, the ratio of their speeds is S_a / S_b = √(t_b / t_a). Here t_a = 361, t_b = 289. S_a / S_b = √(289 / 361) = 17 / 19. Ratio is 17 : 19.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Time Conversion & Reverse Engineering Speed] A car traveling at 5/7 of its actual speed covers 42 km in 1 hr 40 mins 48 secs. Find actual speed.", "options": ["A) 25 km/hr", "B) 30 km/hr", "C) 35 km/hr", "D) 42 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A car traveling at 5/7 of its actual original speed covers 42 km in exactly 1 hour, 40 minutes, and 48 seconds. What is the actual original speed of the car in km/hr?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Convert time entirely to seconds: 1 hr = 3600s; 40 mins = 2400s; plus 48s = 6048 seconds. 2) Distance = 42,000 meters. 3) New Speed = D / T = 42000 / 6048 m/s. Convert m/s to km/hr by multiplying by 18/5: (42000 / 6048) × (18 / 5) = 25 km/hr. 4) We know New Speed = (5/7) × Actual Speed. So 25 = (5/7) × Actual Speed => Actual Speed = 25 × 7 / 5 = 35 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Two Speeds with Equal Distance Formula] A man covers a certain distance by car driving at 70 km/hr and returns back to the starting point riding on a scooter at 55 km/hr. Find his average speed.", "options": ["A) 61.6 km/hr", "B) 62.5 km/hr", "C) 63.2 km/hr", "D) 64.0 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man covers a certain distance from city X to city Y driving a car at 70 km/hr and returns back along the exact same route riding a scooter at 55 km/hr. What is his overall average speed for the whole journey?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For a round trip of identical distance, Average Speed = (2xy) / (x + y). x = 70, y = 55. Avg Speed = (2 × 70 × 55) / (70 + 55) = 7700 / 125. 7700 / 125 = 61.6 km/hr. (Never simply average 70 and 55!).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Overtaking Moving Target] Two trains A and B start from stations X and Y towards each other at speeds 80 km/hr and 95 km/hr. At the time of their meeting, the second train had traveled 180 km more than the first. Find distance XY.", "options": ["A) 1,900 km", "B) 2,100 km", "C) 2,400 km", "D) 2,800 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains start simultaneously from stations X and Y towards each other at speeds of 80 km/hr and 95 km/hr respectively. At the exact moment they pass each other, it is found that the second train has traveled 180 km more than the first train. What is the total distance between X and Y?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Since they start simultaneously, the Time (T) traveled by both until they meet is exactly the same! Distance difference = 180 km. D_B - D_A = 180 => (95 × T) - (80 × T) = 180 => 15T = 180 => T = 12 hours. Total distance = D_A + D_B = (80 + 95) × T = 175 × 12 = 2,100 km.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Linear Chase with Head Start] A thief steals a car at 1:30 PM and drives it at 40 km/hr. The theft is discovered at 2:00 PM and the owner sets off in another car at 50 km/hr. When will he overtake the thief?", "options": ["A) 3:30 PM", "B) 4:00 PM", "C) 4:30 PM", "D) 5:00 PM"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A thief steals a car at 1:30 PM and drives it away at 40 km/hr. The theft is discovered 30 minutes later at 2:00 PM, and the owner sets off immediately in another car at 50 km/hr to chase him. At what exact time will the owner overtake the thief?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Head start distance: From 1:30 PM to 2:00 PM (0.5 hours), the thief travels 40 × 0.5 = 20 km. 2) Relative speed of owner to catch up = 50 - 40 = 10 km/hr. 3) Time to catch up = Distance Gap / Relative Speed = 20 km / 10 km/hr = 2 hours. 4) The chase started at 2:00 PM, so the owner catches the thief at 2:00 PM + 2 hours = 4:00 PM.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Time Gained by Avoiding Stops] Excluding stoppages, the speed of a bus is 54 km/hr and including stoppages, it is 45 km/hr. For how many minutes does the bus stop per hour?", "options": ["A) 9 minutes", "B) 10 minutes", "C) 12 minutes", "D) 15 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Excluding stoppages, the average speed of a bus is 54 km/hr. Including stoppages, its average speed drops to 45 km/hr. For exactly how many minutes does the bus stop per hour?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Difference in distance covered in 1 hour = 54 - 45 = 9 km. This 9 km is the distance the bus COULD have traveled during the time it spent stopped. At its non-stopping speed of 54 km/hr, the time taken to cover 9 km is (9 / 54) hours = 1/6 hours. Convert to minutes: (1/6) × 60 = 10 minutes. So, the bus stops for 10 minutes per hour.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Walking Opposite Directions on Circular Track] A, B, and C walk around a circular track 12 km long at speeds 3, 7, and 13 km/hr respectively. If they start at the same time and point, when will they meet again?", "options": ["A) 12 hours", "B) 24 hours", "C) 36 hours", "D) 84 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A, B, and C start walking simultaneously from the exact same point around a circular track 12 km long at speeds of 3 km/hr, 7 km/hr, and 13 km/hr respectively. If A and B walk in the same direction, but C walks in the opposite direction, after how many hours will all three meet again at the starting point?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Regardless of the direction they walk, a person returns to the STARTING POINT exactly after covering multiples of the entire circumference! Time taken by A for one round = 12/3 = 4 hours. By B = 12/7 hours. By C = 12/13 hours. They will meet at the starting point simultaneously at the LCM of their individual round times. LCM of fractions (4/1, 12/7, 12/13) = LCM of Numerators (4, 12, 12) / HCF of Denominators (1, 7, 13) = 12 / 1 = 12 hours.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Passing Trains of Different Lengths] Two trains 130 m and 110 m long are moving in opposite directions on parallel tracks at 40 km/hr and 32 km/hr. In what time will they pass each other completely?", "options": ["A) 10 seconds", "B) 12 seconds", "C) 15 seconds", "D) 18 seconds"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains, 130 meters and 110 meters long, are moving in opposite directions on parallel tracks at speeds of 40 km/hr and 32 km/hr respectively. In how many exact seconds will they completely cross each other?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Total distance to clear each other = Sum of their lengths = 130 + 110 = 240 m. 2) Since they travel in opposite directions, relative speed = Sum of speeds = 40 + 32 = 72 km/hr. 3) Convert 72 km/hr to m/s: 72 × (5/18) = 20 m/s. 4) Time = Distance / Relative Speed = 240 / 20 = 12 seconds.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Train Crossing a Platform] A train traveling at 72 km/hr crosses a 250 m long platform in 26 seconds. What is the length of the train?", "options": ["A) 250 meters", "B) 270 meters", "C) 320 meters", "D) 350 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A train traveling at a speed of 72 km/hr completely crosses a 250-meter long station platform in exactly 26 seconds. What is the length of the train?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Convert speed to m/s: 72 × (5/18) = 20 m/s. 2) Total distance covered in 26 seconds = Speed × Time = 20 × 26 = 520 meters. 3) Total distance covered when crossing a platform = Length of Train + Length of Platform. Thus, L + 250 = 520 => L = 520 - 250 = 270 meters.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Boats and Streams Relative Speed] A boat can travel with a speed of 13 km/hr in still water. If the speed of the stream is 4 km/hr, find the time taken by the boat to go 68 km downstream.", "options": ["A) 3 hours", "B) 4 hours", "C) 5 hours", "D) 6 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A motorboat has a speed of 13 km/hr in completely still water. If the speed of the river current (stream) is 4 km/hr, how much time will the boat take to travel 68 km downstream?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Downstream speed = Speed of boat in still water + Speed of stream = 13 + 4 = 17 km/hr. Time = Distance / Downstream Speed = 68 / 17 = 4 hours. (If it were upstream, speed would be 13 - 4 = 9 km/hr).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Rowing Up and Down Average] A man rows downstream 32 km and 14 km upstream. If he takes 6 hours to cover each distance, what is the speed of the current?", "options": ["A) 1.5 km/hr", "B) 2.0 km/hr", "C) 2.5 km/hr", "D) 3.0 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man rows a boat 32 km downstream and 14 km upstream. If he takes exactly 6 hours to cover each of these two respective distances, what is the speed of the river current?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Downstream speed (D) = Distance / Time = 32 / 6 = 16/3 km/hr. 2) Upstream speed (U) = Distance / Time = 14 / 6 = 7/3 km/hr. 3) The speed of the current (Stream Speed) is half the difference between downstream and upstream speeds: V_stream = (D - U) / 2 = (16/3 - 7/3) / 2 = (9/3) / 2 = 3 / 2 = 1.5 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Bullet Sounds & Approaching Train] Two guns were fired from the same place at an interval of 13 minutes. A person in an approaching train hears the second shot 12 minutes 30 seconds after the first. If the speed of sound is 330 m/s, find the speed of the train.", "options": ["A) 47.52 km/hr", "B) 50.25 km/hr", "C) 52.80 km/hr", "D) 55.44 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two guns are fired from the same location at an exact interval of 13 minutes. A person sitting in a train approaching that location hears the second shot exactly 12 minutes and 30 seconds after hearing the first. If the speed of sound is 330 m/s, what is the speed of the approaching train?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The difference in time between the shots fired (13 min) and heard (12.5 min) is 0.5 mins (30 seconds). This 30-second time gap corresponds to the distance the train traveled in 12.5 minutes! So, Distance traveled by Train in 12.5 mins = Distance sound would have traveled in 30 seconds. Distance = Speed_sound × 30s = 330 × 30 = 9900 meters. The train covers 9900 meters in 12.5 minutes (750 seconds). Speed of train = 9900 / 750 = 13.2 m/s. Convert to km/hr: 13.2 × (18/5) = 47.52 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. Core Formulas & Unit Conversions\n* **The DST Triangle:** `Distance = Speed × Time`.\n* **Unit Conversions:**\n  * To convert **km/hr to m/s**: Multiply by **`5 / 18`**.\n  * To convert **m/s to km/hr**: Multiply by **`18 / 5`**.\n\n### 💡 2. Average Speed Concepts\n* **Average Speed** is ALWAYS `Total Distance / Total Time`. Do NOT simply average the speeds!\n* **Shortcut for Two Equal Distances:** If a person travels from A to B at speed `x` and returns from B to A at speed `y`, the average speed for the round trip is:\n  **`Average Speed = (2xy) / (x + y)`**\n* **Shortcut for Three Equal Distances:**\n  **`Average Speed = (3xyz) / (xy + yz + zx)`**\n\n### 💡 3. High-Yield Placement Shortcuts\n* **Early/Late Arrival (Clock Difference Trick):**\n  * If a person travels at speed `S1` and is late by `T1` mins, and at speed `S2` is early by `T2` mins.\n  * `Time Difference (ΔT) = T1 + T2` (if one is late and one is early). If both are late, subtract them.\n  * **`Distance = (S1 × S2) / (S2 - S1) × (ΔT / 60)`**.\n* **Two Bodies Starting at the Same Time (Police vs Thief):**\n  * When two bodies start moving at the same time and eventually meet, the **Time** traveled by both is exactly the same!\n  * Thus, `Distance of A / Speed of A = Distance of B / Speed of B`.\n* **Meeting at an Intermediate Point (Cross-Timing Shortcut):**\n  * If A and B start simultaneously from opposite ends, meet at an intermediate point, and then take `t_a` and `t_b` hours to reach their respective destinations:\n  * **`Speed of A / Speed of B = √(t_b / t_a)`**.	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Speed, Distance & Time	Solve early/late arrival problems with the clock-difference shortcut, average speed multi-leg journeys, catching thieves, and inverse speed/time proportionalities.	41	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	14	[{"id": "apt-sdt-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Speed Multiplier & Time Difference] A boy increases his speed to 9/5 times his original speed. By doing this, he reaches his school 30 minutes before his usual time. How much time does he usually take?", "options": ["A) 45.0 minutes", "B) 50.5 minutes", "C) 67.5 minutes", "D) 72.0 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A boy increases his speed to 9/5 times of his original speed. By doing this, he reaches his school 30 minutes before the usual time. How much time does he usually take to reach school?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Distance is constant, so Time is inversely proportional to Speed. New Speed = 9/5 of Old Speed. Therefore, New Time = 5/9 of Old Time (T). The difference in time is T - (5/9)T = (4/9)T. We are given this difference is 30 minutes. (4/9)T = 30 => 4T = 270 => T = 270 / 4 = 67.5 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Police and Thief Relative Distance] Ramesh sees a thief at a distance of 80 m. The thief runs at 5 m/s and Ramesh chases at 7 m/s. How much distance does the thief cover before being caught?", "options": ["A) 150 meters", "B) 200 meters", "C) 280 meters", "D) 350 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Ramesh spots a thief at a distance of 80 meters. The thief starts running at a speed of 5 m/s and Ramesh immediately chases him at 7 m/s. Exactly how much distance does the thief cover before Ramesh catches him?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Both run for the same Time (T). Distance = Speed × Time. Ramesh travels (80 + D) meters and Thief travels D meters. (80 + D) / 7 = D / 5 => 5(80 + D) = 7D => 400 + 5D = 7D => 2D = 400 => D = 200 meters.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Proportional Cycle Race Distances] P cycles twice as fast as Q. R cycles 1/3 as fast as Q. If R completes the 4500m race in 45 mins, where was Q from the finishing line when P finished the race?", "options": ["A) 1,500 meters from finish", "B) 2,250 meters from finish", "C) 3,000 meters from finish", "D) Q had already finished"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "P, Q, and R are in a 4,500m cycle race. P cycles twice as fast as Q, and R cycles 1/3 as fast as Q. If R completes the race in 45 minutes, where was Q exactly from the finishing line when P finished the race?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Since P cycles twice as fast as Q, P will cover twice the distance of Q in the exact same time. When P reaches the finish line (covers 4,500m), Q covers exactly half that distance (2,250m). Therefore, Q is 4500 - 2250 = 2,250 meters away from the finishing line. (Note: R's speed and time are irrelevant decoy info!).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Rebound Meeting Point] A and B travel from X to Y (72 km apart) at 17 km/hr and 19 km/hr. B reaches Y, returns immediately, and meets A at point C. Find the distance from Y to C.", "options": ["A) 2 km", "B) 4 km", "C) 6 km", "D) 8 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Ajay and Vijay travel from town A to town B (72 km apart) at 17 km/hr and 19 km/hr respectively. Vijay reaches B first, returns immediately, and meets Ajay at point C. What is the distance from B to C?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Both travel for the same Time. Let distance from B to C be x. Ajay travels (72 - x) km. Vijay travels 72 km to B, plus x km back = (72 + x) km. Since Time is equal: (72 - x) / 17 = (72 + x) / 19. Cross-multiply: 19(72 - x) = 17(72 + x) => 1368 - 19x = 1224 + 17x => 36x = 144 => x = 4 km.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Early and Late Arrival Equation] Traveling at 50 km/hr, a man is 30 mins late. At 60 km/hr, he is 10 mins early. Find the distance.", "options": ["A) 150 km", "B) 180 km", "C) 200 km", "D) 240 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Surendra travels from home to office by car at 50 km/hr and is late by 30 minutes. When he travels at 60 km/hr, he reaches 10 minutes earlier than the usual time. How far is his office from his home?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Time difference = 30 mins late + 10 mins early = 40 minutes = 40/60 = 2/3 hours. Using the shortcut D = [S1 × S2 / (S2 - S1)] × ΔT = [50 × 60 / (60 - 50)] × (2/3) = [3000 / 10] × 2/3 = 300 × 2/3 = 200 km.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Two \\"Late\\" Arrivals Difference] Rohit drives at 30 km/hr and reaches bank 20 mins late. At 45 km/hr, he is still 8 mins late. Find distance.", "options": ["A) 15 km", "B) 18 km", "C) 24 km", "D) 30 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Rohit drives from home at 30 km/hr and reaches his bank 20 minutes late. Next day, he increases his speed to 45 km/hr, but still reaches 8 minutes late. How far is the bank from his home?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Both times he is late, so subtract the times to find the real difference: 20 mins - 8 mins = 12 minutes = 12/60 = 1/5 hours. Using D = [S1 × S2 / (S2 - S1)] × ΔT = [30 × 45 / (45 - 30)] × 1/5 = [1350 / 15] × 1/5 = 90 / 5 = 18 km.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Multi-Leg Average Speed] Pratik travels 96 km at 16 km/hr, 124 km at 31 km/hr, and 105 km at 7 km/hr. Find his average speed.", "options": ["A) 12 km/hr", "B) 13 km/hr", "C) 14 km/hr", "D) 15 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pratik travels 96 km at a speed of 16 km/hr by bike, 124 km at 31 km/hr by car, and another 105 km at 7 km/hr in a horse cart. What is his exact average speed for the entire journey?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Average Speed = Total Distance / Total Time. 1) Total Distance = 96 + 124 + 105 = 325 km. 2) Times for each leg: T1 = 96/16 = 6 hours; T2 = 124/31 = 4 hours; T3 = 105/7 = 15 hours. Total Time = 6 + 4 + 15 = 25 hours. 3) Avg Speed = 325 / 25 = 13 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Fractional Distance Average Speed] Rohit covers 1/4 distance at 20 km/hr, 1/4 at 10 km/hr, and rest at 80 km/hr. Find average speed.", "options": ["A) 22.85 km/hr", "B) 25.00 km/hr", "C) 36.66 km/hr", "D) 40.00 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Rohit covers 1/4 of his total journey distance at 20 km/hr, another 1/4 of the distance at 10 km/hr, and the remaining half (1/2) of the distance at 80 km/hr. What is his exact average speed for the whole distance?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let total distance = D. Avg Speed = Total D / Total T. T1 = (D/4)/20 = D/80. T2 = (D/4)/10 = D/40. The remaining distance is 1/2. T3 = (D/2)/80 = D/160. Total Time = D/80 + D/40 + D/160. LCM is 160. (2D + 4D + 1D) / 160 = 7D / 160. Avg Speed = D / (7D / 160) = 160 / 7 ≈ 22.85 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Cross-Timing Square Root Shortcut] A walks from Jammu to Delhi, B from Delhi to Jammu. After passing each other, they take 361 hours and 289 hours respectively to finish. Find ratio of speeds.", "options": ["A) 17 : 19", "B) 19 : 17", "C) 289 : 361", "D) 361 : 289"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A walks from Jammu to Delhi, and simultaneously B starts walking from Delhi to Jammu. After crossing each other on the way, they complete their remaining journeys in 361 hours and 289 hours respectively. What is the ratio of the speed of A to the speed of B?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "When two travelers start simultaneously from opposite ends, cross paths, and take times t_a and t_b to finish the remaining journey, the ratio of their speeds is S_a / S_b = √(t_b / t_a). Here t_a = 361, t_b = 289. S_a / S_b = √(289 / 361) = 17 / 19. Ratio is 17 : 19.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Time Conversion & Reverse Engineering Speed] A car traveling at 5/7 of its actual speed covers 42 km in 1 hr 40 mins 48 secs. Find actual speed.", "options": ["A) 25 km/hr", "B) 30 km/hr", "C) 35 km/hr", "D) 42 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A car traveling at 5/7 of its actual original speed covers 42 km in exactly 1 hour, 40 minutes, and 48 seconds. What is the actual original speed of the car in km/hr?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Convert time entirely to seconds: 1 hr = 3600s; 40 mins = 2400s; plus 48s = 6048 seconds. 2) Distance = 42,000 meters. 3) New Speed = D / T = 42000 / 6048 m/s. Convert m/s to km/hr by multiplying by 18/5: (42000 / 6048) × (18 / 5) = 25 km/hr. 4) We know New Speed = (5/7) × Actual Speed. So 25 = (5/7) × Actual Speed => Actual Speed = 25 × 7 / 5 = 35 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Two Speeds with Equal Distance Formula] A man covers a certain distance by car driving at 70 km/hr and returns back to the starting point riding on a scooter at 55 km/hr. Find his average speed.", "options": ["A) 61.6 km/hr", "B) 62.5 km/hr", "C) 63.2 km/hr", "D) 64.0 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man covers a certain distance from city X to city Y driving a car at 70 km/hr and returns back along the exact same route riding a scooter at 55 km/hr. What is his overall average speed for the whole journey?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For a round trip of identical distance, Average Speed = (2xy) / (x + y). x = 70, y = 55. Avg Speed = (2 × 70 × 55) / (70 + 55) = 7700 / 125. 7700 / 125 = 61.6 km/hr. (Never simply average 70 and 55!).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Overtaking Moving Target] Two trains A and B start from stations X and Y towards each other at speeds 80 km/hr and 95 km/hr. At the time of their meeting, the second train had traveled 180 km more than the first. Find distance XY.", "options": ["A) 1,900 km", "B) 2,100 km", "C) 2,400 km", "D) 2,800 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains start simultaneously from stations X and Y towards each other at speeds of 80 km/hr and 95 km/hr respectively. At the exact moment they pass each other, it is found that the second train has traveled 180 km more than the first train. What is the total distance between X and Y?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Since they start simultaneously, the Time (T) traveled by both until they meet is exactly the same! Distance difference = 180 km. D_B - D_A = 180 => (95 × T) - (80 × T) = 180 => 15T = 180 => T = 12 hours. Total distance = D_A + D_B = (80 + 95) × T = 175 × 12 = 2,100 km.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Linear Chase with Head Start] A thief steals a car at 1:30 PM and drives it at 40 km/hr. The theft is discovered at 2:00 PM and the owner sets off in another car at 50 km/hr. When will he overtake the thief?", "options": ["A) 3:30 PM", "B) 4:00 PM", "C) 4:30 PM", "D) 5:00 PM"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A thief steals a car at 1:30 PM and drives it away at 40 km/hr. The theft is discovered 30 minutes later at 2:00 PM, and the owner sets off immediately in another car at 50 km/hr to chase him. At what exact time will the owner overtake the thief?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Head start distance: From 1:30 PM to 2:00 PM (0.5 hours), the thief travels 40 × 0.5 = 20 km. 2) Relative speed of owner to catch up = 50 - 40 = 10 km/hr. 3) Time to catch up = Distance Gap / Relative Speed = 20 km / 10 km/hr = 2 hours. 4) The chase started at 2:00 PM, so the owner catches the thief at 2:00 PM + 2 hours = 4:00 PM.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Time Gained by Avoiding Stops] Excluding stoppages, the speed of a bus is 54 km/hr and including stoppages, it is 45 km/hr. For how many minutes does the bus stop per hour?", "options": ["A) 9 minutes", "B) 10 minutes", "C) 12 minutes", "D) 15 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Excluding stoppages, the average speed of a bus is 54 km/hr. Including stoppages, its average speed drops to 45 km/hr. For exactly how many minutes does the bus stop per hour?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Difference in distance covered in 1 hour = 54 - 45 = 9 km. This 9 km is the distance the bus COULD have traveled during the time it spent stopped. At its non-stopping speed of 54 km/hr, the time taken to cover 9 km is (9 / 54) hours = 1/6 hours. Convert to minutes: (1/6) × 60 = 10 minutes. So, the bus stops for 10 minutes per hour.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Walking Opposite Directions on Circular Track] A, B, and C walk around a circular track 12 km long at speeds 3, 7, and 13 km/hr respectively. If they start at the same time and point, when will they meet again?", "options": ["A) 12 hours", "B) 24 hours", "C) 36 hours", "D) 84 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A, B, and C start walking simultaneously from the exact same point around a circular track 12 km long at speeds of 3 km/hr, 7 km/hr, and 13 km/hr respectively. If A and B walk in the same direction, but C walks in the opposite direction, after how many hours will all three meet again at the starting point?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Regardless of the direction they walk, a person returns to the STARTING POINT exactly after covering multiples of the entire circumference! Time taken by A for one round = 12/3 = 4 hours. By B = 12/7 hours. By C = 12/13 hours. They will meet at the starting point simultaneously at the LCM of their individual round times. LCM of fractions (4/1, 12/7, 12/13) = LCM of Numerators (4, 12, 12) / HCF of Denominators (1, 7, 13) = 12 / 1 = 12 hours.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Passing Trains of Different Lengths] Two trains 130 m and 110 m long are moving in opposite directions on parallel tracks at 40 km/hr and 32 km/hr. In what time will they pass each other completely?", "options": ["A) 10 seconds", "B) 12 seconds", "C) 15 seconds", "D) 18 seconds"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains, 130 meters and 110 meters long, are moving in opposite directions on parallel tracks at speeds of 40 km/hr and 32 km/hr respectively. In how many exact seconds will they completely cross each other?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Total distance to clear each other = Sum of their lengths = 130 + 110 = 240 m. 2) Since they travel in opposite directions, relative speed = Sum of speeds = 40 + 32 = 72 km/hr. 3) Convert 72 km/hr to m/s: 72 × (5/18) = 20 m/s. 4) Time = Distance / Relative Speed = 240 / 20 = 12 seconds.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Train Crossing a Platform] A train traveling at 72 km/hr crosses a 250 m long platform in 26 seconds. What is the length of the train?", "options": ["A) 250 meters", "B) 270 meters", "C) 320 meters", "D) 350 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A train traveling at a speed of 72 km/hr completely crosses a 250-meter long station platform in exactly 26 seconds. What is the length of the train?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Convert speed to m/s: 72 × (5/18) = 20 m/s. 2) Total distance covered in 26 seconds = Speed × Time = 20 × 26 = 520 meters. 3) Total distance covered when crossing a platform = Length of Train + Length of Platform. Thus, L + 250 = 520 => L = 520 - 250 = 270 meters.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Boats and Streams Relative Speed] A boat can travel with a speed of 13 km/hr in still water. If the speed of the stream is 4 km/hr, find the time taken by the boat to go 68 km downstream.", "options": ["A) 3 hours", "B) 4 hours", "C) 5 hours", "D) 6 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A motorboat has a speed of 13 km/hr in completely still water. If the speed of the river current (stream) is 4 km/hr, how much time will the boat take to travel 68 km downstream?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Downstream speed = Speed of boat in still water + Speed of stream = 13 + 4 = 17 km/hr. Time = Distance / Downstream Speed = 68 / 17 = 4 hours. (If it were upstream, speed would be 13 - 4 = 9 km/hr).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Rowing Up and Down Average] A man rows downstream 32 km and 14 km upstream. If he takes 6 hours to cover each distance, what is the speed of the current?", "options": ["A) 1.5 km/hr", "B) 2.0 km/hr", "C) 2.5 km/hr", "D) 3.0 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man rows a boat 32 km downstream and 14 km upstream. If he takes exactly 6 hours to cover each of these two respective distances, what is the speed of the river current?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Downstream speed (D) = Distance / Time = 32 / 6 = 16/3 km/hr. 2) Upstream speed (U) = Distance / Time = 14 / 6 = 7/3 km/hr. 3) The speed of the current (Stream Speed) is half the difference between downstream and upstream speeds: V_stream = (D - U) / 2 = (16/3 - 7/3) / 2 = (9/3) / 2 = 3 / 2 = 1.5 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-sdt-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Bullet Sounds & Approaching Train] Two guns were fired from the same place at an interval of 13 minutes. A person in an approaching train hears the second shot 12 minutes 30 seconds after the first. If the speed of sound is 330 m/s, find the speed of the train.", "options": ["A) 47.52 km/hr", "B) 50.25 km/hr", "C) 52.80 km/hr", "D) 55.44 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two guns are fired from the same location at an exact interval of 13 minutes. A person sitting in a train approaching that location hears the second shot exactly 12 minutes and 30 seconds after hearing the first. If the speed of sound is 330 m/s, what is the speed of the approaching train?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The difference in time between the shots fired (13 min) and heard (12.5 min) is 0.5 mins (30 seconds). This 30-second time gap corresponds to the distance the train traveled in 12.5 minutes! So, Distance traveled by Train in 12.5 mins = Distance sound would have traveled in 30 seconds. Distance = Speed_sound × 30s = 330 × 30 = 9900 meters. The train covers 9900 meters in 12.5 minutes (750 seconds). Speed of train = 9900 / 750 = 13.2 m/s. Convert to km/hr: 13.2 × (18/5) = 47.52 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22128	Lecture 10: How JavaScript Code Runs & Hoisting — Execution Context (GEC), Memory vs. Execution Phase & TDZ	js-lecture-10-execution-context-hoisting-temporal-dead-zone-call-stack	Trace step-by-step how JavaScript creates the Global Execution Context (GEC), runs the Memory Allocation Phase and Code Execution Phase on the Call Stack, and enforces Hoisting and the Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ).	2026-10-06 21:14:25.4193+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nJavaScript does **not** execute code in a single blind top-to-bottom pass. Whenever a script runs, the engine creates a **Global Execution Context (`GEC`)** on the **Call Stack** and processes the code in **two distinct phases**:\n1. **Phase 1: Memory Allocation (Creation) Phase**\n2. **Phase 2: Code Execution Phase**\n\n### 💡 How the 2 Phases Work (The Real Mechanism Behind Hoisting)\n* **Phase 1 — Memory Allocation Phase (Before Any Line Executes):**\n  * **`var` variables:** Allocated memory and initialized to **`undefined`**.\n  * **`function` declarations:** Allocated memory in the Heap and the identifier is bound to the **actual function object/bytecode pointer**.\n  * **`let` and `const` variables:** Allocated memory, but marked as **`<uninitialized>`** inside the **Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ)**.\n* **Phase 2 — Code Execution Phase:**\n  * The engine walks through the code line-by-line, assigning actual values (`10`, `20`) to variables and pushing a **new Function Execution Context (`FEC`)** onto the Call Stack whenever a function is invoked. Once a function returns, its Execution Context is popped off the Call Stack and garbage-collected.\n\n### 💡 Debunking the "Hoisting Moves Code to the Top" Myth\n* **The Myth:** Many tutorials claim *"JavaScript physically moves variable and function declarations to the top of the file."*\n* **The Reality:** Not a single line of code is moved! **Hoisting** is simply the consequence of **Phase 1 (Memory Allocation Phase)** scanning declarations before **Phase 2 (Execution Phase)** begins.\n* **Why Function Expressions & Arrow Functions Cannot Be Called Before Definition:**\n  * If you write `const add = (a, b) => a + b;` (or `var add = function() {}`), `add` is treated as a **variable** during Phase 1 (`<uninitialized>` in TDZ for `const`/`let`, or `undefined` for `var`), NOT a function declaration! Calling `add()` before that line throws a `ReferenceError` (for `let`/`const`) or `TypeError: add is not a function` (for `var`).\n\n### 🛠️ Tracing Hoisting & Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ)\n```javascript\n// 1. Function Declarations are bound in Phase 1 -> Can be called early!\nconsole.log(sum(10, 20)); // Outputs: 30\n\n// 2. 'var' is initialized to 'undefined' in Phase 1\nconsole.log(a);           // Outputs: undefined\nvar a = 10;\n\n// 3. 'let' and 'const' are <uninitialized> in TDZ during Phase 1\n// console.log(b);        // Throws ReferenceError: Cannot access 'b' before initialization\nlet b = 20;\n\nfunction sum(num1, num2) {\n  return num1 + num2;\n}\n\n// 4. Function Expression stored in const/var cannot be called before its line:\n// greet();               // ReferenceError (if const/let) or TypeError (if var)\nconst greet = function() {\n  console.log("Hello!");\n};\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Hoisting", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Hoisting", "title": "MDN Web Docs — Hoisting & Execution Context Glossary"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ijPq-u3y9cs", "title": "How JavaScript Code Runs | Hoisting in Javascript | Javascript Full Course #10", "duration": "48m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-10-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Walk through the two phases of an Execution Context in JavaScript. What happens to var, let, const, and function declarations in Phase 1?", "title": "Q1: Walk through the two phases of an Execution Context in JavaScript. What happens to var, let, const, and function declarations in Phase 1?", "answer": "Every Execution Context (Global or Function) executes in two phases: 1) Memory Allocation (Creation) Phase: The engine scans the scope for declarations. var variables are allocated and initialized to undefined; function declarations are allocated in the Heap and bound completely to their identifier; let and const declarations are allocated in the scope environment but left <uninitialized> (in the Temporal Dead Zone). 2) Code Execution Phase: The engine executes statements line-by-line, assigning actual values to variables and pushing new Function Execution Contexts onto the Call Stack for each function call.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "10-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Every Execution Context (Global or Function) executes in two phases: 1) Memory Allocation (Creation) Phase: The engine scans the scope for declarations. var variables are allocated and initialized to undefined; function declarations are allocated in the Heap and bound completely to their identifier; let and const declarations are allocated in the scope environment but left <uninitialized> (in the Temporal Dead Zone). 2) Code Execution Phase: The engine executes statements line-by-line, assigning actual values to variables and pushing new Function Execution Contexts onto the Call Stack for each function call."}, {"id": "js-lec-10-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Are let and const hoisted in JavaScript? What is the Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ)?", "title": "Q2: Are let and const hoisted in JavaScript? What is the Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ)?", "answer": "Yes, let and const ARE hoisted—the engine knows they exist in the scope during Phase 1 (Memory Allocation). However, unlike var (which is initialized to undefined), let and const remain <uninitialized>. The Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ) is the time window between entering the scope (Phase 1) and executing the actual line where the let or const variable is initialized in Phase 2. Accessing the variable anywhere inside the TDZ throws a ReferenceError: Cannot access variable before initialization.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "10-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Yes, let and const ARE hoisted—the engine knows they exist in the scope during Phase 1 (Memory Allocation). However, unlike var (which is initialized to undefined), let and const remain <uninitialized>. The Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ) is the time window between entering the scope (Phase 1) and executing the actual line where the let or const variable is initialized in Phase 2. Accessing the variable anywhere inside the TDZ throws a ReferenceError: Cannot access variable before initialization."}, {"id": "js-lec-10-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is the difference in error when calling var fn = () => {} vs. const fn = () => {} before its declaration line?", "title": "Q3: What is the difference in error when calling var fn = () => {} vs. const fn = () => {} before its declaration line?", "answer": "With var fn = () => {}, during Phase 1 fn is initialized to undefined. When fn() is called before the assignment line, JS tries to invoke undefined as a function and throws a TypeError: fn is not a function. With const fn = () => {}, fn is <uninitialized> inside the Temporal Dead Zone during Phase 1, so referencing fn before its declaration line throws a ReferenceError: Cannot access 'fn' before initialization.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "10-q3", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "With var fn = () => {}, during Phase 1 fn is initialized to undefined. When fn() is called before the assignment line, JS tries to invoke undefined as a function and throws a TypeError: fn is not a function. With const fn = () => {}, fn is <uninitialized> inside the Temporal Dead Zone during Phase 1, so referencing fn before its declaration line throws a ReferenceError: Cannot access 'fn' before initialization."}, {"id": "js-lec-10-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why is the popular definition \\"Hoisting physically moves declarations to the top of the file\\" technically incorrect?", "title": "Q4: Why is the popular definition \\"Hoisting physically moves declarations to the top of the file\\" technically incorrect?", "answer": "The JavaScript engine never rewrites or moves source code lines upward. Instead, hoisting is a byproduct of the engine's two-pass architecture: the compiler/interpreter registers variable and function identifiers in the Lexical Environment memory table during the Creation Phase before executing the first line of code in the Execution Phase.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "10-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "The JavaScript engine never rewrites or moves source code lines upward. Instead, hoisting is a byproduct of the engine's two-pass architecture: the compiler/interpreter registers variable and function identifiers in the Lexical Environment memory table during the Creation Phase before executing the first line of code in the Execution Phase."}, {"id": "js-lec-10-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What happens to a Function Execution Context (FEC) on the Call Stack once the function executes its return statement?", "title": "Q5: What happens to a Function Execution Context (FEC) on the Call Stack once the function executes its return statement?", "answer": "As soon as a function finishes executing and returns its value to the caller, its Function Execution Context is immediately popped off the Call Stack, and its local stack variables are destroyed (unless referenced by an inner closure in the Heap).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "10-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "As soon as a function finishes executing and returns its value to the caller, its Function Execution Context is immediately popped off the Call Stack, and its local stack variables are destroyed (unless referenced by an inner closure in the Heap)."}, {"id": "js-lec-10-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Where is a function's executable code actually stored in V8 when its metadata object is allocated in the Heap?", "title": "Q6: Where is a function's executable code actually stored in V8 when its metadata object is allocated in the Heap?", "answer": "In V8, when a function is created, a JSFunction object (storing properties like name and length: number of parameters) is allocated in the Heap. That object holds an internal pointer to V8's dedicated Code Space in RAM, where the compiled Ignition bytecode (and TurboFan machine code) for that function body resides.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "10-q6", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "In V8, when a function is created, a JSFunction object (storing properties like name and length: number of parameters) is allocated in the Heap. That object holds an internal pointer to V8's dedicated Code Space in RAM, where the compiled Ignition bytecode (and TurboFan machine code) for that function body resides."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nJavaScript does **not** execute code in a single blind top-to-bottom pass. Whenever a script runs, the engine creates a **Global Execution Context (`GEC`)** on the **Call Stack** and processes the code in **two distinct phases**:\n1. **Phase 1: Memory Allocation (Creation) Phase**\n2. **Phase 2: Code Execution Phase**\n\n### 💡 How the 2 Phases Work (The Real Mechanism Behind Hoisting)\n* **Phase 1 — Memory Allocation Phase (Before Any Line Executes):**\n  * **`var` variables:** Allocated memory and initialized to **`undefined`**.\n  * **`function` declarations:** Allocated memory in the Heap and the identifier is bound to the **actual function object/bytecode pointer**.\n  * **`let` and `const` variables:** Allocated memory, but marked as **`<uninitialized>`** inside the **Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ)**.\n* **Phase 2 — Code Execution Phase:**\n  * The engine walks through the code line-by-line, assigning actual values (`10`, `20`) to variables and pushing a **new Function Execution Context (`FEC`)** onto the Call Stack whenever a function is invoked. Once a function returns, its Execution Context is popped off the Call Stack and garbage-collected.\n\n### 💡 Debunking the "Hoisting Moves Code to the Top" Myth\n* **The Myth:** Many tutorials claim *"JavaScript physically moves variable and function declarations to the top of the file."*\n* **The Reality:** Not a single line of code is moved! **Hoisting** is simply the consequence of **Phase 1 (Memory Allocation Phase)** scanning declarations before **Phase 2 (Execution Phase)** begins.\n* **Why Function Expressions & Arrow Functions Cannot Be Called Before Definition:**\n  * If you write `const add = (a, b) => a + b;` (or `var add = function() {}`), `add` is treated as a **variable** during Phase 1 (`<uninitialized>` in TDZ for `const`/`let`, or `undefined` for `var`), NOT a function declaration! Calling `add()` before that line throws a `ReferenceError` (for `let`/`const`) or `TypeError: add is not a function` (for `var`).\n\n### 🛠️ Tracing Hoisting & Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ)\n```javascript\n// 1. Function Declarations are bound in Phase 1 -> Can be called early!\nconsole.log(sum(10, 20)); // Outputs: 30\n\n// 2. 'var' is initialized to 'undefined' in Phase 1\nconsole.log(a);           // Outputs: undefined\nvar a = 10;\n\n// 3. 'let' and 'const' are <uninitialized> in TDZ during Phase 1\n// console.log(b);        // Throws ReferenceError: Cannot access 'b' before initialization\nlet b = 20;\n\nfunction sum(num1, num2) {\n  return num1 + num2;\n}\n\n// 4. Function Expression stored in const/var cannot be called before its line:\n// greet();               // ReferenceError (if const/let) or TypeError (if var)\nconst greet = function() {\n  console.log("Hello!");\n};\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 10: How JavaScript Code Runs & Hoisting — Execution Context (GEC), Memory vs. Execution Phase & TDZ	Trace step-by-step how JavaScript creates the Global Execution Context (GEC), runs the Memory Allocation Phase and Code Execution Phase on the Call Stack, and enforces Hoisting and the Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ).	20	Easy	\N	10	[{"id": "js-lec-10-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Walk through the two phases of an Execution Context in JavaScript. What happens to var, let, const, and function declarations in Phase 1?", "title": "Q1: Walk through the two phases of an Execution Context in JavaScript. What happens to var, let, const, and function declarations in Phase 1?", "answer": "Every Execution Context (Global or Function) executes in two phases: 1) Memory Allocation (Creation) Phase: The engine scans the scope for declarations. var variables are allocated and initialized to undefined; function declarations are allocated in the Heap and bound completely to their identifier; let and const declarations are allocated in the scope environment but left <uninitialized> (in the Temporal Dead Zone). 2) Code Execution Phase: The engine executes statements line-by-line, assigning actual values to variables and pushing new Function Execution Contexts onto the Call Stack for each function call.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "10-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Every Execution Context (Global or Function) executes in two phases: 1) Memory Allocation (Creation) Phase: The engine scans the scope for declarations. var variables are allocated and initialized to undefined; function declarations are allocated in the Heap and bound completely to their identifier; let and const declarations are allocated in the scope environment but left <uninitialized> (in the Temporal Dead Zone). 2) Code Execution Phase: The engine executes statements line-by-line, assigning actual values to variables and pushing new Function Execution Contexts onto the Call Stack for each function call."}, {"id": "js-lec-10-q2", "url": null