eError: Cannot access 'fn' before initialization.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "10-q3", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "With var fn = () => {}, during Phase 1 fn is initialized to undefined. When fn() is called before the assignment line, JS tries to invoke undefined as a function and throws a TypeError: fn is not a function. With const fn = () => {}, fn is <uninitialized> inside the Temporal Dead Zone during Phase 1, so referencing fn before its declaration line throws a ReferenceError: Cannot access 'fn' before initialization."}, {"id": "js-lec-10-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why is the popular definition \\"Hoisting physically moves declarations to the top of the file\\" technically incorrect?", "title": "Q4: Why is the popular definition \\"Hoisting physically moves declarations to the top of the file\\" technically incorrect?", "answer": "The JavaScript engine never rewrites or moves source code lines upward. Instead, hoisting is a byproduct of the engine's two-pass architecture: the compiler/interpreter registers variable and function identifiers in the Lexical Environment memory table during the Creation Phase before executing the first line of code in the Execution Phase.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "10-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "The JavaScript engine never rewrites or moves source code lines upward. Instead, hoisting is a byproduct of the engine's two-pass architecture: the compiler/interpreter registers variable and function identifiers in the Lexical Environment memory table during the Creation Phase before executing the first line of code in the Execution Phase."}, {"id": "js-lec-10-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What happens to a Function Execution Context (FEC) on the Call Stack once the function executes its return statement?", "title": "Q5: What happens to a Function Execution Context (FEC) on the Call Stack once the function executes its return statement?", "answer": "As soon as a function finishes executing and returns its value to the caller, its Function Execution Context is immediately popped off the Call Stack, and its local stack variables are destroyed (unless referenced by an inner closure in the Heap).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "10-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "As soon as a function finishes executing and returns its value to the caller, its Function Execution Context is immediately popped off the Call Stack, and its local stack variables are destroyed (unless referenced by an inner closure in the Heap)."}, {"id": "js-lec-10-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Where is a function's executable code actually stored in V8 when its metadata object is allocated in the Heap?", "title": "Q6: Where is a function's executable code actually stored in V8 when its metadata object is allocated in the Heap?", "answer": "In V8, when a function is created, a JSFunction object (storing properties like name and length: number of parameters) is allocated in the Heap. That object holds an internal pointer to V8's dedicated Code Space in RAM, where the compiled Ignition bytecode (and TurboFan machine code) for that function body resides.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "10-q6", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "In V8, when a function is created, a JSFunction object (storing properties like name and length: number of parameters) is allocated in the Heap. That object holds an internal pointer to V8's dedicated Code Space in RAM, where the compiled Ignition bytecode (and TurboFan machine code) for that function body resides."}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Hoisting	\N	\N	\N
19	Asynchronous JavaScript: Promises, Async/Await & Event Loop	async-js-promises-event-loop	Microtask queue, event loop phases, Promise.all, Promise.allSettled, and error handling.	2026-09-27 22:28:22.357026+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Asynchronous JavaScript: Promises, Async/Await & Event Loop\nPart of the **Modern JavaScript & TypeScript Foundations** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Asynchronous%20JavaScript%3A%20Promises%2C%20Async%2FAwait%20%26%20Event%20Loop%20tutorial", "title": "Asynchronous JavaScript: Promises, Async/Await & Event Loop Full Guide", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"type": "assignment", "title": "Build & Test a Working Implementation of Asynchronous JavaScript: Promises, Async/Await & Event Loop", "checklist": ["Implement core logic with validation and boundary checks", "Write tests verifying expected output and edge cases"], "description": "Implement a production-grade module demonstrating the core principles of Asynchronous JavaScript: Promises, Async/Await & Event Loop.", "starterCodeUrl": ""}]	\N	\N	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	10	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22241	Chain of Responsibility Pattern	lld-chain-of-responsibility	Pass requests along a dynamic chain of handlers. Master recursive delegation to decouple request senders from receivers using the ATM Cash Dispenser model.	2026-10-07 17:16:35.515424+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Chain of Responsibility Pattern\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/LXVKB6deQMo", "title": "Chain of Responsibility Pattern | Build Cash Dispenser(UML & Code)", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v22-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Core Intent of Chain of Responsibility", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn a central `switch(requestType)` block, the sender is tightly coupled to every possible handler. If a new handler is added, the central router must be modified and recompiled.\\n\\nCoR decouples them by having the sender hold only a single reference to the \\"head\\" of the chain (an abstract `IHandler`). The sender pushes the request to the head. The request automatically bubbles down the chain until a handler resolves it. The sender never knows which concrete class handled the request, and handlers can be dynamically reordered, added, or removed at runtime without touching the sender's code.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Chain of Responsibility pattern decouple the sender of a request from its receiver? Why is this preferable to a central Switch/Case routing block?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v22-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: CoR vs Linked List Data Structure", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Intent:** A Linked List is a *Data Structure* designed to store and traverse static data in sequential memory. CoR is a *Behavioral Design Pattern* designed to evaluate and execute business logic against an incoming request payload.\\n2. **Payload Execution:** In a Linked List, traversal typically continues until the tail is reached. In CoR, traversal often short-circuits: the moment a handler successfully processes the request, the chain halts and returns. \\n3. **Node Independence:** A Linked List node is a dumb container. A CoR node is an autonomous Domain Object encapsulating distinct business rules (e.g., `AuthenticationHandler` vs `RateLimitHandler`).", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Structurally, the Chain of Responsibility pattern looks identical to a Linked List. Conceptually and architecturally, how do they differ in intent and payload execution?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v22-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: The Unhandled Request Fallback", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf the request reaches the end of the chain (`nextHandler == null`) and is still unresolved, it simply drops off, returning a silent failure or partial completion.\\n*Example:* An ATM user requests ₹150, but the lowest denominator handler is ₹100. The ₹50 remainder hits the end of the chain.\\n\\n**Mitigation:** The final handler (or the base abstract class logic) must catch this unhandled state and explicitly throw a domain exception (e.g., `InsufficientDenominationException`) so the client is alerted that the request failed rather than silently losing the data.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What happens in a Chain of Responsibility if a request reaches the end of the chain without being fully handled? How should this be mitigated?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v22-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Open/Closed Principle in CoR", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf using a monolithic `Dispenser` class, you would have to open the file and inject `if (amount >= 200)` logic into the existing code.\\n\\nWith CoR:\\n1. You create a brand-new class: `class Handler200 : public IHandler` and implement the `dispense()` logic.\\n2. In the setup/configuration code, you wire it into the chain: `handler500->setNext(handler200); handler200->setNext(handler100);`.\\nThe existing `Handler500` and `Handler100` classes remain completely **closed to modification**, while the system is **open to extension**.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Demonstrate how the Open/Closed Principle (OCP) is preserved when the Reserve Bank introduces a new ₹200 currency note into the ATM software.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v22-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Real-World Use Case: Logging Systems", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nLoggers maintain a chain of appenders or handlers based on severity.\\nIf the application triggers `logger.info(\\"User logged in\\")`:\\n1. The `ErrorLogger` node receives it. Because its threshold is `ERROR` and the request is `INFO`, it ignores the payload and passes it to `next`.\\n2. The `DebugLogger` node ignores it.\\n3. The `InfoLogger` node matches the threshold, formats the string, writes it to `info.log`, and stops the chain.\\nThis allows granular control over where different severity logs are routed (e.g., sending `ERROR` logs to PagerDuty while `INFO` logs just go to disk) dynamically.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How do widespread logging frameworks (like Log4j or Python Logger) utilize the Chain of Responsibility for handling log levels (INFO, DEBUG, ERROR)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Dynamic Routing Problem\n* If a system needs to route a request through multiple potential handlers (e.g., dispensing cash, routing customer support tickets, or approving a corporate leave request), using nested `if-else` statements permanently hardcodes the routing logic.\n* Adding a new handler requires opening and modifying the core router, violating the Open/Closed Principle.\n\n### 💡 2. The Chain of Responsibility (CoR) Solution\n* **Definition:** Passes requests along a chain of handlers. Upon receiving a request, each handler decides either to process it (partially or fully) or to pass it to the next handler in the chain.\n* **The Structure:** Every handler implements an `IHandler` interface. Every handler possesses a pointer to the `next` handler in the chain (`IHandler* nextHandler`).\n\n### 💡 3. The ATM Cash Dispenser Example\n* **The Setup:** An ATM needs to dispense ₹3,500. Handlers exist for ₹2000, ₹500, and ₹100 notes.\n* **Execution:**\n  1. The request hits the `2000Handler`. It calculates `3500 / 2000 = 1`. It dispenses one ₹2000 note. The remainder is ₹1,500. It delegates the remaining ₹1,500 to `nextHandler->dispense(1500)`.\n  2. The `500Handler` dispenses three ₹500 notes. The remainder is ₹0.\n  3. The chain halts early. The sender (ATM software) never knew exactly *which* handlers executed the request.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	Chain of Responsibility Pattern	Pass requests along a dynamic chain of handlers. Master recursive delegation to decouple request senders from receivers using the ATM Cash Dispenser model.	30	Hard	Low Level Design	36	[{"id": "lld-v22-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Core Intent of Chain of Responsibility", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn a central `switch(requestType)` block, the sender is tightly coupled to every possible handler. If a new handler is added, the central router must be modified and recompiled.\\n\\nCoR decouples them by having the sender hold only a single reference to the \\"head\\" of the chain (an abstract `IHandler`). The sender pushes the request to the head. The request automatically bubbles down the chain until a handler resolves it. The sender never knows which concrete class handled the request, and handlers can be dynamically reordered, added, or removed at runtime without touching the sender's code.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Chain of Responsibility pattern decouple the sender of a request from its receiver? Why is this preferable to a central Switch/Case routing block?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v22-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: CoR vs Linked List Data Structure", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Intent:** A Linked List is a *Data Structure* designed to store and traverse static data in sequential memory. CoR is a *Behavioral Design Pattern* designed to evaluate and execute business logic against an incoming request payload.\\n2. **Payload Execution:** In a Linked List, traversal typically continues until the tail is reached. In CoR, traversal often short-circuits: the moment a handler successfully processes the request, the chain halts and returns. \\n3. **Node Independence:** A Linked List node is a dumb container. A CoR node is an autonomous Domain Object encapsulating distinct business rules (e.g., `AuthenticationHandler` vs `RateLimitHandler`).", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Structurally, the Chain of Responsibility pattern looks identical to a Linked List. Conceptually and architecturally, how do they differ in intent and payload execution?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v22-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: The Unhandled Request Fallback", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf the request reaches the end of the chain (`nextHandler == null`) and is still unresolved, it simply drops off, returning a silent failure or partial completion.\\n*Example:* An ATM user requests ₹150, but the lowest denominator handler is ₹100. The ₹50 remainder hits the end of the chain.\\n\\n**Mitigation:** The final handler (or the base abstract class logic) must catch this unhandled state and explicitly throw a domain exception (e.g., `InsufficientDenominationException`) so the client is alerted that the request failed rather than silently losing the data.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What happens in a Chain of Responsibility if a request reaches the end of the chain without being fully handled? How should this be mitigated?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v22-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Open/Closed Principle in CoR", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf using a monolithic `Dispenser` class, you would have to open the file and inject `if (amount >= 200)` logic into the existing code.\\n\\nWith CoR:\\n1. You create a brand-new class: `class Handler200 : public IHandler` and implement the `dispense()` logic.\\n2. In the setup/configuration code, you wire it into the chain: `handler500->setNext(handler200); handler200->setNext(handler100);`.\\nThe existing `Handler500` and `Handler100` classes remain completely **closed to modification**, while the system is **open to extension**.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Demonstrate how the Open/Closed Principle (OCP) is preserved when the Reserve Bank introduces a new ₹200 currency note into the ATM software.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v22-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Real-World Use Case: Logging Systems", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nLoggers maintain a chain of appenders or handlers based on severity.\\nIf the application triggers `logger.info(\\"User logged in\\")`:\\n1. The `ErrorLogger` node receives it. Because its threshold is `ERROR` and the request is `INFO`, it ignores the payload and passes it to `next`.\\n2. The `DebugLogger` node ignores it.\\n3. The `InfoLogger` node matches the threshold, formats the string, writes it to `info.log`, and stops the chain.\\nThis allows granular control over where different severity logs are routed (e.g., sending `ERROR` logs to PagerDuty while `INFO` logs just go to disk) dynamically.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How do widespread logging frameworks (like Log4j or Python Logger) utilize the Chain of Responsibility for handling log levels (INFO, DEBUG, ERROR)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22087	Lecture 2: MongoDB Environment Setup on Windows — Community Server 8.0, Compass GUI & mongosh	mongo-lecture-2-windows-installation-compass-mongosh	Install MongoDB Community Server 8.0 (MSI), MongoDB Compass GUI, and MongoDB Shell (mongosh) on 64-bit Windows, configure System and User PATH variables, and verify local connections on port 27017.	2026-10-06 19:11:34.825286+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nRunning MongoDB locally on a 64-bit Windows 10/11 machine requires three core components: **MongoDB Community Server** (the background database engine), **MongoDB Compass** (the official visual GUI), and **MongoDB Shell (`mongosh`)** (the interactive command-line interface).\n\n### 💡 Core Components & Installation Workflow\n* **64-Bit OS Requirement:** MongoDB 8.0+ strictly requires a 64-bit Windows operating system. Always select the **`.msi` installer package** (rather than `.zip`) for automated service registration.\n* **Run MongoDB as a Windows Service:** During the "Complete" installation wizard, keep **"Install MongoDB as a Service"** checked so the `mongod` server starts automatically on system boot.\n* **MongoDB Compass GUI:** Installed alongside the server; connects by default to `mongodb://localhost:27017` and displays the three built-in system databases (`admin`, `config`, and `local`).\n* **MongoDB Shell (`mongosh`):** Downloaded separately from the MongoDB Tools page and installed into a clean directory such as `C:\\mongosh\\`.\n\n### 🛠️ Configuring Windows Environment Variables (`PATH`)\nTo run `mongod` and `mongosh` from any Command Prompt window without typing full file paths:\n* **System Variables `Path`:** Add the MongoDB Server binary folder:\n  `C:\\Program Files\\MongoDB\\Server\\8.0\\bin`\n* **User Variables `Path`:** Add the MongoDB Shell directory:\n  `C:\\mongosh`\n\n### 🛠️ Verifying the Installation in Command Prompt\n```bash\n# 1. Start the interactive MongoDB Shell (connects to mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017)\nmongosh\n\n# 2. List all default system databases (admin, config, local)\nshow dbs\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Setup Pitfalls\n* **Command Not Recognized:** If `mongosh` throws *"is not recognized as an internal or external command"*, restart your Command Prompt after saving the Environment Variables so Windows reloads the updated `PATH`.	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/install-mongodb-on-windows/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/install-mongodb-on-windows/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 2: MongoDB Environment Setup on Windows"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=skBh4ft-IjI", "title": "MongoDB Installation on Windows Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "14m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nRunning MongoDB locally on a 64-bit Windows 10/11 machine requires three core components: **MongoDB Community Server** (the background database engine), **MongoDB Compass** (the official visual GUI), and **MongoDB Shell (`mongosh`)** (the interactive command-line interface).\n\n### 💡 Core Components & Installation Workflow\n* **64-Bit OS Requirement:** MongoDB 8.0+ strictly requires a 64-bit Windows operating system. Always select the **`.msi` installer package** (rather than `.zip`) for automated service registration.\n* **Run MongoDB as a Windows Service:** During the "Complete" installation wizard, keep **"Install MongoDB as a Service"** checked so the `mongod` server starts automatically on system boot.\n* **MongoDB Compass GUI:** Installed alongside the server; connects by default to `mongodb://localhost:27017` and displays the three built-in system databases (`admin`, `config`, and `local`).\n* **MongoDB Shell (`mongosh`):** Downloaded separately from the MongoDB Tools page and installed into a clean directory such as `C:\\mongosh\\`.\n\n### 🛠️ Configuring Windows Environment Variables (`PATH`)\nTo run `mongod` and `mongosh` from any Command Prompt window without typing full file paths:\n* **System Variables `Path`:** Add the MongoDB Server binary folder:\n  `C:\\Program Files\\MongoDB\\Server\\8.0\\bin`\n* **User Variables `Path`:** Add the MongoDB Shell directory:\n  `C:\\mongosh`\n\n### 🛠️ Verifying the Installation in Command Prompt\n```bash\n# 1. Start the interactive MongoDB Shell (connects to mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017)\nmongosh\n\n# 2. List all default system databases (admin, config, local)\nshow dbs\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Setup Pitfalls\n* **Command Not Recognized:** If `mongosh` throws *"is not recognized as an internal or external command"*, restart your Command Prompt after saving the Environment Variables so Windows reloads the updated `PATH`.	Yahoo Baba	https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba	655	Lecture 2: MongoDB Environment Setup on Windows — Community Server 8.0, Compass GUI & mongosh	Install MongoDB Community Server 8.0 (MSI), MongoDB Compass GUI, and MongoDB Shell (mongosh) on 64-bit Windows, configure System and User PATH variables, and verify local connections on port 27017.	20	Easy	\N	2	[]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/install-mongodb-on-windows/	\N	\N	\N
22129	Lecture 11: Scope, Lexical Environment, Closures & Higher-Order Functions — Data Encapsulation & Factory Patterns	js-lecture-11-scope-lexical-environment-closures-higher-order-functions	Master Global, Function, and Block scope, the Lexical Scope Chain, how Closures preserve outer variables in the Heap for private state encapsulation, and Higher-Order Functions.	2026-10-06 21:14:25.4193+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\n**Closures** and **Lexical Scoping** are among the most frequently asked topics in JavaScript interviews. They explain how inner functions remember variables from their outer parent function **even after the outer function has finished executing and been popped off the Call Stack**.\n\n### 💡 Scope & The "One-Way Glass" Lexical Scope Chain\n* **3 Types of Scope in JS:**\n  1. **Global Scope:** Accessible everywhere in the file.\n  2. **Function (Local) Scope:** Variables declared inside a function (`var`, `let`, `const`) are accessible only inside that function.\n  3. **Block Scope (`{ ... }`):** `let` and `const` declared inside an `if`, `for`, or `while` block cannot be accessed outside that block. **`var` ignores block scope** and leaks out to the enclosing function or global scope!\n* **Lexical Scoping & Scope Chain:** Scope is determined by **where a function is physically written in the code** (at author time), not where it is called. If a variable is not found in the current function's local memory, JS walks up the outer **Lexical Environment** chain until it reaches the Global Scope.\n\n### 💡 What Is a Closure & How Does It Work in Memory?\n* **Definition:** A **Closure** is a function bundled together with references to its surrounding **Lexical Environment** (the "backpack" of outer variables it depends on).\n* **Memory Mechanics:** Normally, when `outer()` returns, its local variables are destroyed. However, if `outer()` returns an `inner` function that references `count`, V8 moves `count` into a **Context object in the Heap** so `inner()` retains a live reference to `count` (not a frozen copy, but the actual reference!).\n* **Real-World Use Case (Data Privacy / Encapsulation):** Closures allow creating **private variables** (like a bank account `balance`) that external code cannot tamper with directly (`account.balance = 999999` is impossible), exposing only controlled methods (`deposit()`, `withdraw()`, `getBalance()`).\n\n### 💡 Higher-Order Functions (HOFs)\nA **Higher-Order Function** is any function that does at least one of the following:\n1. Accepts another function as an argument (e.g., `Array.prototype.map`, `filter`, `setTimeout`).\n2. Returns a new function as its result (e.g., function factories / multiplier generators).\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Closures, Private Bank Account & Higher-Order Functions\n```javascript\n// 1. Classic Closure Counter (Preserves 'count' reference in Heap after createCounter returns)\nfunction createCounter() {\n  let count = 0;\n  return function increment() {\n    count++;\n    return count;\n  };\n}\nconst counter = createCounter();\nconsole.log(counter()); // 1\nconsole.log(counter()); // 2\nconsole.log(counter()); // 3\n\n// 2. Real-World Closure: Private Bank Account Balance (Data Encapsulation)\nfunction createBankAccount(initialBalance) {\n  let balance = initialBalance; // Private variable! Cannot be accessed directly outside.\n\n  return {\n    deposit(amount) {\n      if (amount > 0 && typeof amount === "number") {\n        balance += amount;\n        return balance;\n      }\n    },\n    withdraw(amount) {\n      if (amount > 0 && amount <= balance) {\n        balance -= amount;\n        return balance;\n      }\n      return "Insufficient funds";\n    },\n    getBalance() {\n      return balance;\n    }\n  };\n}\n\nconst rohitAccount = createBankAccount(500);\nconsole.log(rohitAccount.deposit(200));    // 700\nconsole.log(rohitAccount.withdraw(100));   // 600\nconsole.log(rohitAccount.balance);         // undefined (Protected from direct mutation!)\n\n// 3. Higher-Order Function (Returns a customized multiplier function)\nfunction createMultiplier(factor) {\n  return (number) => number * factor;\n}\nconst double = createMultiplier(2);\nconst triple = createMultiplier(3);\nconsole.log(double(10), triple(10)); // 20, 30\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Closures", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Closures", "title": "MDN Web Docs — Closures & Lexical Scoping Guide"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UBCPtXXMAWs", "title": "Closure in Javascript | Scope and High order function in javascript | Javascript Full Course #11", "duration": "49m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-11-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What is a Closure in JavaScript, and how can an inner function still access outer variables after the outer function has popped off the Call Stack?", "title": "Q1: What is a Closure in JavaScript, and how can an inner function still access outer variables after the outer function has popped off the Call Stack?", "answer": "A Closure is the combination of a function and the lexical environment within which that function was declared. When the JS engine sees that an inner function references variables from its parent function, V8 allocates those captured variables in a Context object on the Heap rather than destroying them when the parent's stack frame pops off the Call Stack. The returned inner function holds a live reference pointer to that Heap Context.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "11-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "A Closure is the combination of a function and the lexical environment within which that function was declared. When the JS engine sees that an inner function references variables from its parent function, V8 allocates those captured variables in a Context object on the Heap rather than destroying them when the parent's stack frame pops off the Call Stack. The returned inner function holds a live reference pointer to that Heap Context."}, {"id": "js-lec-11-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: How do you use Closures to implement Private Variables (Data Encapsulation) in JavaScript? Give a Bank Account example.", "title": "Q2: How do you use Closures to implement Private Variables (Data Encapsulation) in JavaScript? Give a Bank Account example.", "answer": "If balance is stored as a normal object property (user.balance = 500), any external script can accidentally set user.balance = \\"hacked\\" or -9999. By declaring let balance = 500 inside a factory function createBankAccount() and returning an object containing deposit(), withdraw(), and getBalance() methods, balance is hidden inside the closure scope. External code cannot access balance directly and can only interact with it through the validated methods.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "11-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "If balance is stored as a normal object property (user.balance = 500), any external script can accidentally set user.balance = \\"hacked\\" or -9999. By declaring let balance = 500 inside a factory function createBankAccount() and returning an object containing deposit(), withdraw(), and getBalance() methods, balance is hidden inside the closure scope. External code cannot access balance directly and can only interact with it through the validated methods."}, {"id": "js-lec-11-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: Classic Interview Output Question: What is the difference between using var i vs. let i inside a for loop with setTimeout?", "title": "Q3: Classic Interview Output Question: What is the difference between using var i vs. let i inside a for loop with setTimeout?", "answer": "In for (var i = 1; i <= 3; i++) { setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 1000); }, var is function/global-scoped, so there is only ONE shared i variable in memory. By the time the 3 callbacks run after 1 second, the loop has finished and i is 4, so it prints \\"4, 4, 4\\". When using for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++), let is block-scoped and JavaScript creates a brand-new lexical binding of i for each loop iteration, so each closure captures its own i and prints \\"1, 2, 3\\"!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "11-q3", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "In for (var i = 1; i <= 3; i++) { setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 1000); }, var is function/global-scoped, so there is only ONE shared i variable in memory. By the time the 3 callbacks run after 1 second, the loop has finished and i is 4, so it prints \\"4, 4, 4\\". When using for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++), let is block-scoped and JavaScript creates a brand-new lexical binding of i for each loop iteration, so each closure captures its own i and prints \\"1, 2, 3\\"!"}, {"id": "js-lec-11-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Does a Closure capture a snapshot copy of the outer variable's value, or a live reference to the variable?", "title": "Q4: Does a Closure capture a snapshot copy of the outer variable's value, or a live reference to the variable?", "answer": "A Closure captures a live reference to the variable binding in the outer Lexical Environment, NOT a frozen copy! If the outer function (or a sibling inner function sharing the same closure) modifies the variable before or after the closure is returned, the closure sees the updated value.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "11-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "A Closure captures a live reference to the variable binding in the outer Lexical Environment, NOT a frozen copy! If the outer function (or a sibling inner function sharing the same closure) modifies the variable before or after the closure is returned, the closure sees the updated value."}, {"id": "js-lec-11-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is a Higher-Order Function (HOF)? How does it relate to First-Class Functions?", "title": "Q5: What is a Higher-Order Function (HOF)? How does it relate to First-Class Functions?", "answer": "\\"First-Class Functions\\" is a language property meaning functions are treated like any other value (can be assigned to variables, passed as arguments, and returned). A \\"Higher-Order Function\\" is a specific function that takes advantage of this property by either accepting one or more functions as arguments (callbacks, like map/filter) or returning a function as its output.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "11-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "\\"First-Class Functions\\" is a language property meaning functions are treated like any other value (can be assigned to variables, passed as arguments, and returned). A \\"Higher-Order Function\\" is a specific function that takes advantage of this property by either accepting one or more functions as arguments (callbacks, like map/filter) or returning a function as its output."}, {"id": "js-lec-11-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Can Closures cause Memory Leaks in production applications? How do you prevent them?", "title": "Q6: Can Closures cause Memory Leaks in production applications? How do you prevent them?", "answer": "Yes. Because a closure keeps its referenced outer variables alive in the Heap for as long as the inner function itself is reachable, holding onto a closure (e.g., in an unremoved DOM event listener or global timer) prevents the Garbage Collector from freeing large objects captured in that scope. To prevent leaks, remove event listeners/timers on cleanup and set unused references to null.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "11-q6", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Yes. Because a closure keeps its referenced outer variables alive in the Heap for as long as the inner function itself is reachable, holding onto a closure (e.g., in an unremoved DOM event listener or global timer) prevents the Garbage Collector from freeing large objects captured in that scope. To prevent leaks, remove event listeners/timers on cleanup and set unused references to null."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\n**Closures** and **Lexical Scoping** are among the most frequently asked topics in JavaScript interviews. They explain how inner functions remember variables from their outer parent function **even after the outer function has finished executing and been popped off the Call Stack**.\n\n### 💡 Scope & The "One-Way Glass" Lexical Scope Chain\n* **3 Types of Scope in JS:**\n  1. **Global Scope:** Accessible everywhere in the file.\n  2. **Function (Local) Scope:** Variables declared inside a function (`var`, `let`, `const`) are accessible only inside that function.\n  3. **Block Scope (`{ ... }`):** `let` and `const` declared inside an `if`, `for`, or `while` block cannot be accessed outside that block. **`var` ignores block scope** and leaks out to the enclosing function or global scope!\n* **Lexical Scoping & Scope Chain:** Scope is determined by **where a function is physically written in the code** (at author time), not where it is called. If a variable is not found in the current function's local memory, JS walks up the outer **Lexical Environment** chain until it reaches the Global Scope.\n\n### 💡 What Is a Closure & How Does It Work in Memory?\n* **Definition:** A **Closure** is a function bundled together with references to its surrounding **Lexical Environment** (the "backpack" of outer variables it depends on).\n* **Memory Mechanics:** Normally, when `outer()` returns, its local variables are destroyed. However, if `outer()` returns an `inner` function that references `count`, V8 moves `count` into a **Context object in the Heap** so `inner()` retains a live reference to `count` (not a frozen copy, but the actual reference!).\n* **Real-World Use Case (Data Privacy / Encapsulation):** Closures allow creating **private variables** (like a bank account `balance`) that external code cannot tamper with directly (`account.balance = 999999` is impossible), exposing only controlled methods (`deposit()`, `withdraw()`, `getBalance()`).\n\n### 💡 Higher-Order Functions (HOFs)\nA **Higher-Order Function** is any function that does at least one of the following:\n1. Accepts another function as an argument (e.g., `Array.prototype.map`, `filter`, `setTimeout`).\n2. Returns a new function as its result (e.g., function factories / multiplier generators).\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Closures, Private Bank Account & Higher-Order Functions\n```javascript\n// 1. Classic Closure Counter (Preserves 'count' reference in Heap after createCounter returns)\nfunction createCounter() {\n  let count = 0;\n  return function increment() {\n    count++;\n    return count;\n  };\n}\nconst counter = createCounter();\nconsole.log(counter()); // 1\nconsole.log(counter()); // 2\nconsole.log(counter()); // 3\n\n// 2. Real-World Closure: Private Bank Account Balance (Data Encapsulation)\nfunction createBankAccount(initialBalance) {\n  let balance = initialBalance; // Private variable! Cannot be accessed directly outside.\n\n  return {\n    deposit(amount) {\n      if (amount > 0 && typeof amount === "number") {\n        balance += amount;\n        return balance;\n      }\n    },\n    withdraw(amount) {\n      if (amount > 0 && amount <= balance) {\n        balance -= amount;\n        return balance;\n      }\n      return "Insufficient funds";\n    },\n    getBalance() {\n      return balance;\n    }\n  };\n}\n\nconst rohitAccount = createBankAccount(500);\nconsole.log(rohitAccount.deposit(200));    // 700\nconsole.log(rohitAccount.withdraw(100));   // 600\nconsole.log(rohitAccount.balance);         // undefined (Protected from direct mutation!)\n\n// 3. Higher-Order Function (Returns a customized multiplier function)\nfunction createMultiplier(factor) {\n  return (number) => number * factor;\n}\nconst double = createMultiplier(2);\nconst triple = createMultiplier(3);\nconsole.log(double(10), triple(10)); // 20, 30\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 11: Scope, Lexical Environment, Closures & Higher-Order Functions — Data Encapsulation & Factory Patterns	Master Global, Function, and Block scope, the Lexical Scope Chain, how Closures preserve outer variables in the Heap for private state encapsulation, and Higher-Order Functions.	20	Easy	\N	11	[{"id": "js-lec-11-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What is a Closure in JavaScript, and how can an inner function still access outer variables after the outer function has popped off the Call Stack?", "title": "Q1: What is a Closure in JavaScript, and how can an inner function still access outer variables after the outer function has popped off the Call Stack?", "answer": "A Closure is the combination of a function and the lexical environment within which that function was declared. When the JS engine sees that an inner function references variables from its parent function, V8 allocates those captured variables in a Context object on the Heap rather than destroying them when the parent's stack frame pops off the Call Stack. The returned inner function holds a live reference pointer to that Heap Context.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "11-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "A Closure is the combination of a function and the lexical environment within which that function was declared. When the JS engine sees that an inner function references variables from its parent function, V8 allocates those captured variables in a Context object on the Heap rather than destroying them when the parent's stack frame pops off the Call Stack. The returned inner function holds a live reference pointer to that Heap Context."}, {"id": "js-lec-11-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: How do you use Closures to implement Private Variables (Data Encapsulation) in JavaScript? Give a Bank Account example.", "title": "Q2: How do you use Closures to implement Private Variables (Data Encapsulation) in JavaScript? Give a Bank Account example.", "answer": "If balance is stored as a normal object property (user.balance = 500), any external script can accidentally set user.balance = \\"hacked\\" or -9999. By declaring let balance = 500 inside a factory function createBankAccount() and returning an object containing deposit(), withdraw(), and getBalance() methods, balance is hidden inside the closure scope. External code cannot access balance directly and can only interact with it through the validated methods.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "11-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "If balance is stored as a normal object property (user.balance = 500), any external script can accidentally set user.balance = \\"hacked\\" or -9999. By declaring let balance = 500 inside a factory function createBankAccount() and returning an object containing deposit(), withdraw(), and getBalance() methods, balance is hidden inside the closure scope. External code cannot access balance directly and can only interact with it through the validated methods."}, {"id": "js-lec-11-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: Classic Interview Output Question: What is the difference between using var i vs. let i inside a for loop with setTimeout?", "title": "Q3: Classic Interview Output Question: What is the difference between using var i vs. let i inside a for loop with setTimeout?", "answer": "In for (var i = 1; i <= 3; i++) { setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 1000); }, var is function/global-scoped, so there is only ONE shared i variable in memory. By the time the 3 callbacks run after 1 second, the loop has finished and i is 4, so it prints \\"4, 4, 4\\". When using for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++), let is block-scoped and JavaScript creates a brand-new lexical binding of i for each loop iteration, so each closure captures its own i and prints \\"1, 2, 3\\"!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "11-q3", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "In for (var i = 1; i <= 3; i++) { setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 1000); }, var is function/global-scoped, so there is only ONE shared i variable in memory. By the time the 3 callbacks run after 1 second, the loop has finished and i is 4, so it prints \\"4, 4, 4\\". When using for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++), let is block-scoped and JavaScript creates a brand-new lexical binding of i for each loop iteration, so each closure captures its own i and prints \\"1, 2, 3\\"!"}, {"id": "js-lec-11-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Does a Closure capture a snapshot copy of the outer variable's value, or a live reference to the variable?", "title": "Q4: Does a Closure capture a snapshot copy of the outer variable's value, or a live reference to the variable?", "answer": "A Closure captures a live reference to the variable binding in the outer Lexical Environment, NOT a frozen copy! If the outer function (or a sibling inner function sharing the same closure) modifies the variable before or after the closure is returned, the closure sees the updated value.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "11-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "A Closure captures a live reference to the variable binding in the outer Lexical Environment, NOT a frozen copy! If the outer function (or a sibling inner function sharing the same closure) modifies the variable before or after the closure is returned, the closure sees the updated value."}, {"id": "js-lec-11-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is a Higher-Order Function (HOF)? How does it relate to First-Class Functions?", "title": "Q5: What is a Higher-Order Function (HOF)? How does it relate to First-Class Functions?", "answer": "\\"First-Class Functions\\" is a language property meaning functions are treated like any other value (can be assigned to variables, passed as arguments, and returned). A \\"Higher-Order Function\\" is a specific function that takes advantage of this property by either accepting one or more functions as arguments (callbacks, like map/filter) or returning a function as its output.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "11-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "\\"First-Class Functions\\" is a language property meaning functions are treated like any other value (can be assigned to variables, passed as arguments, and returned). A \\"Higher-Order Function\\" is a specific function that takes advantage of this property by either accepting one or more functions as arguments (callbacks, like map/filter) or returning a function as its output."}, {"id": "js-lec-11-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Can Closures cause Memory Leaks in production applications? How do you prevent them?", "title": "Q6: Can Closures cause Memory Leaks in production applications? How do you prevent them?", "answer": "Yes. Because a closure keeps its referenced outer variables alive in the Heap for as long as the inner function itself is reachable, holding onto a closure (e.g., in an unremoved DOM event listener or global timer) prevents the Garbage Collector from freeing large objects captured in that scope. To prevent leaks, remove event listeners/timers on cleanup and set unused references to null.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "11-q6", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Yes. Because a closure keeps its referenced outer variables alive in the Heap for as long as the inner function itself is reachable, holding onto a closure (e.g., in an unremoved DOM event listener or global timer) prevents the Garbage Collector from freeing large objects captured in that scope. To prevent leaks, remove event listeners/timers on cleanup and set unused references to null."}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Closures	\N	\N	\N
23	Writing Custom Reusable Hooks	custom-reusable-hooks	Extracting stateful component logic into clean, composable custom hooks.	2026-09-27 22:28:22.364596+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Writing Custom Reusable Hooks\nPart of the **React 19 Core & Hooks Mastery** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Writing%20Custom%20Reusable%20Hooks%20tutorial", "title": "Writing Custom Reusable Hooks Full Guide", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"type": "assignment", "title": "Build & Test a Working Implementation of Writing Custom Reusable Hooks", "checklist": ["Implement core logic with validation and boundary checks", "Write tests verifying expected output and edge cases"], "description": "Implement a production-grade module demonstrating the core principles of Writing Custom Reusable Hooks.", "starterCodeUrl": ""}]	\N	\N	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	11	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22181	Percentage	aptitude-quant-percentage	Master fast decimal shifting for percentages, comparison ratios (less than/more than), constant expenditure scaling, election voting math, and Venn diagram intersection traps.	2026-10-07 11:23:07.969127+05:30	### 📌 1. Fundamentals of Percentages\n* **Definition:** Percentage means "per 100". To convert a fraction to a percentage, multiply by 100.\n* **Fast Percentage Calculations (Decimal Shifting):**\n  * **10%:** Shift the decimal 1 place to the left (e.g., 10% of 260 = 26.0).\n  * **1%:** Shift the decimal 2 places to the left (e.g., 1% of 260 = 2.60).\n  * **To find 39%:** Find 40% (4 × 10%) and subtract 1%.\n* **Essential Fractions to Percentages:**\n  * `1/2 = 50%`, `1/3 = 33.33%`, `1/4 = 25%`, `1/5 = 20%`\n  * `1/6 = 16.66%`, `1/8 = 12.5%`, `1/9 = 11.11%`, `1/11 = 9.09%`\n\n### 💡 2. High-Yield Percentage Comparison\n* **"X is what % of Y?"** $\rightarrow$ `(X / Y) × 100`\n* **"X is what % MORE/LESS than Y?"** $\rightarrow$ `(Difference / Base) × 100`\n  * *Crucial Rule:* The "Base" is ALWAYS the value immediately following the word "than" or "of".\n\n### 💡 3. Common Exam Scenarios\n* **Constant Expenditure (Price & Consumption):**\n  * If the price of sugar increases by `R%`, to keep expenditure the same, consumption must decrease by:\n    **`[ R / (100 + R) ] × 100 %`**\n  * If price decreases by `R%`, consumption can increase by:\n    **`[ R / (100 - R) ] × 100 %`**\n* **Venn Diagram Traps:**\n  * If `A%` fail in subject 1, `B%` fail in subject 2, and `C%` fail in BOTH, the total percentage of students who failed in at least one subject is **`A + B - C`**. The percentage who PASSED in both is **`100 - (A + B - C)`**.\n* **Successive Population/Depreciation:**\n  * Same as compound interest: `P_final = P_initial × (1 ± R_1/100) × (1 ± R_2/100)`.	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RWdNhJWwzSs", "type": "youtube", "title": "Percentage - Shortcuts & Tricks for Placement Tests, Job Interviews & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-perc-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Missing Percentage Value] If 15% of y is same as 21% of z, then 12.5% of y is equal to what % of z?", "options": ["A) 15.5%", "B) 17.5%", "C) 19.5%", "D) 21.0%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 15% of y is exactly the same as 21% of z, then 12.5% of y is equal to what exact percentage of z?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Given: 15% of y = 21% of z => (15/100)y = (21/100)z => 15y = 21z => y = (21/15)z = 1.4z. We need 12.5% of y: 12.5% of (1.4z) = (12.5/100) × 1.4z = (17.5/100)z = 17.5% of z.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-perc-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Percentage Less Than / More Than] If price of rice is 30% less than wheat, then wheat's price is how much % more than rice?", "options": ["A) 30.00%", "B) 42.85%", "C) 33.33%", "D) 45.45%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the price of rice is 30% less than the price of wheat, then the price of wheat is exactly how much percent MORE than the price of rice?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let price of wheat = 100. Then price of rice = 100 - 30 = 70. How much MORE is wheat than rice? Difference = 100 - 70 = 30. The \\"base\\" is the price of rice (since it asks \\"more than rice\\"). Percentage = (Difference / Rice) × 100 = (30 / 70) × 100 = 3/7 × 100 = 42.85%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-perc-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Constant Expenditure Price Shift] By 20% decrease in price of rice, people buy 10 kg more in ₹100. Find the original price per kg.", "options": ["A) ₹2.00", "B) ₹2.50", "C) ₹3.00", "D) ₹4.00"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Due to a 20% decrease in the price of rice, a family is able to buy 10 kg more rice for exactly ₹100. What was the ORIGINAL price of rice per kg before the decrease?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let original price be P. New price = 0.8P. The difference in quantities bought with ₹100 is 10 kg. Equation: 100 / 0.8P - 100 / P = 10. Factor out 100/P: (100/P) × (1/0.8 - 1) = 10 => (100/P) × (1.25 - 1) = 10 => (100/P) × 0.25 = 10 => 25/P = 10 => P = 25 / 10 = ₹2.50 per kg.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-perc-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Venn Diagram Intersection Trap] 15% failed in Science, 25% failed in Maths, 10% failed in both. Find % passed in both.", "options": ["A) 50%", "B) 60%", "C) 70%", "D) 80%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a class examination, 15% of the total students failed in Science, 25% failed in Maths, and 10% failed in BOTH subjects. What exact percentage of students PASSED in both subjects?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Use the Union formula for Sets: Total Failed in at least one subject = n(Science) + n(Maths) - n(Both) = 15% + 25% - 10% = 30%. Since 30% of the students failed in at least one subject, the remaining students passed in BOTH subjects. Passed = 100% - 30% = 70%.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-perc-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Equating Un-knowns in Elections] In an election between two candidates, one got 40% votes and lost by 1,000 votes. Find total votes.", "options": ["A) 4,000", "B) 5,000", "C) 6,000", "D) 10,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In an election contested by exactly two candidates, the losing candidate secured 40% of the total votes cast and lost by a margin of 1,000 votes. What was the total number of votes casted in the election?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Candidate 1 got 40%. Candidate 2 got the remaining 60%. The difference in their vote shares is 60% - 40% = 20%. This 20% margin is equal to the 1,000 votes! So, 20% of Total Votes = 1000 => (20/100) × T = 1000 => T = 1000 × 5 = 5,000 total votes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-perc-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Successive Multiplicative Decrease] 10% of a village died of cholera. Panic set in, and 25% of the remaining left. Population reduced to 4,050. Find original.", "options": ["A) 5,500", "B) 6,000", "C) 6,500", "D) 7,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "During an epidemic, 10% of the inhabitants of a village died of cholera. Following this, panic set in and 25% of the REMAINING inhabitants left the village. If the population was then reduced to 4,050, what was the original number of inhabitants?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let original population = P. After 10% die, 90% remain: 0.9P. Out of these, 25% leave, so 75% of the 90% remain in the village. Final population = 0.75 × 0.90 × P = 0.675P. We know 0.675P = 4050 => P = 4050 / 0.675 = 4050000 / 675 = 6,000.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-perc-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Deducing Total from Passing Marks] A scores 10% and fails by 30 marks. B scores 40% and gets 30 marks more than minimum passing. Find maximum marks.", "options": ["A) 150", "B) 200", "C) 250", "D) 300"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In an exam, Student A scores 10% of the maximum marks and fails by 30 marks. Student B scores 40% of the maximum marks and gets 30 marks more than the minimum passing marks. What are the maximum possible marks for the exam?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let Maximum marks = M. Passing marks = A's score + 30 = 10% of M + 30. Also, Passing marks = B's score - 30 = 40% of M - 30. Equating them: 10% of M + 30 = 40% of M - 30 => 60 = 30% of M => 60 = 0.3M => M = 60 / 0.3 = 200.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-perc-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Successive Increase and Decrease] Price is first increased by 10% and then decreased by 10%. Find change in price.", "options": ["A) 0% (No change)", "B) 1% Increase", "C) 1% Decrease", "D) 2% Decrease"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The price of an apple is first increased by 10% and later decreased by 10%. What is the net overall percentage change in the price of the apple?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let original price = 100. Increase by 10% -> 110. Decrease by 10% of 110 (which is 11) -> 110 - 11 = 99. The net change from 100 to 99 is a 1% Decrease. (Shortcut: x% increase then x% decrease ALWAYS results in a loss of x^2 / 100 % = 10^2 / 100 = 1% decrease).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-perc-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Comparison of Three Percentages] A's salary is 50% more than B's. How much % is B's salary less than A's?", "options": ["A) 25.00%", "B) 33.33%", "C) 50.00%", "D) 66.66%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Person A's salary is exactly 50% MORE than Person B's salary. By what exact percentage is B's salary LESS than A's salary?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let B's salary = 100. A's salary = 100 + 50 = 150. How much is B less than A? Difference = 150 - 100 = 50. The \\"base\\" is A's salary (since it asks \\"less than A\\"). Percentage = (Difference / A) × 100 = (50 / 150) × 100 = 1/3 × 100 = 33.33%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-perc-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Population Literacy and Gender Ratios] 55% of population is female. 80% of males are literate. Total literacy is 58%. Find female literacy rate.", "options": ["A) 36%", "B) 40%", "C) 45%", "D) 50%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a country, 55% of the total population is female. 80% of the male population is literate. If the total overall literacy rate of the country is 58%, what percentage of the FEMALE population is literate?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Assume total population = 100. Females = 55, Males = 45. Literate Males = 80% of 45 = (80/100) × 45 = 36. Total literate people = 58. Since Total Literate = Literate Males + Literate Females => 58 = 36 + Literate Females => Literate Females = 22. The literacy rate OF FEMALES = (Literate Females / Total Females) × 100 = (22 / 55) × 100 = (2/5) × 100 = 40%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-perc-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Two Numbers Relative to Third] Two numbers are less than a third by 30% and 37%. How much % is the second less than the first?", "options": ["A) 7%", "B) 10%", "C) 12%", "D) 15%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two numbers are strictly less than a third number by 30% and 37% respectively. By what exact percentage is the second number less than the first number?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the third number be 100. First number = 100 - 30 = 70. Second number = 100 - 37 = 63. How much is the second less than the first? Difference = 70 - 63 = 7. Base is the first number (70). Percentage = (7 / 70) × 100 = 10%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-perc-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Price vs Consumption Equality] If price of sugar is raised by 25%, by how much % should a person reduce consumption so expenditure remains same?", "options": ["A) 15%", "B) 20%", "C) 25%", "D) 33.33%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the market price of sugar is suddenly raised by 25%, by what exact percentage must a household reduce its consumption of sugar so that its overall monthly expenditure remains absolutely unchanged?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Shortcut formula for constant expenditure: Reduction % = [R / (100 + R)] × 100. Here R = 25. Reduction = [25 / (100 + 25)] × 100 = (25 / 125) × 100 = 1/5 × 100 = 20%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-perc-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Deduction on Partial Bill] If 20% of an electricity bill is deducted, ₹100 is still to be paid. Find the original bill.", "options": ["A) ₹120", "B) ₹125", "C) ₹140", "D) ₹150"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a 20% deduction is applied to an electricity bill, an amount of ₹100 is still left to be paid. What was the original amount of the electricity bill?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let original bill be B. A 20% deduction means 80% is still owed. So 80% of B = 100 => 0.8B = 100 => B = 100 / 0.8 = 1000 / 8 = ₹125.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-perc-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Error Percentage in Calculation] A student multiplied a number by 3/5 instead of 5/3. What is the percentage error in the calculation?", "options": ["A) 36%", "B) 44%", "C) 54%", "D) 64%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A student carelessly multiplied a positive number by 3/5 instead of multiplying it by 5/3. What is the exact percentage error in the calculated result?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the number be 15 (LCM of 3 and 5). Correct result = 15 × (5/3) = 25. Erroneous result = 15 × (3/5) = 9. Error amount = 25 - 9 = 16. Percentage error = (Error / Correct Result) × 100 = (16 / 25) × 100 = 16 × 4 = 64%.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-perc-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Finding Original Number from Increment] If 15% is added to a number, it becomes 460. What is the number?", "options": ["A) 360", "B) 380", "C) 400", "D) 420"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "When 15% of a number is added to the number itself, the result becomes 460. What is the original number?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the number be N. Adding 15% to it means it becomes 115% of N. So, 115% of N = 460 => 1.15N = 460 => N = 460 / 1.15 = 46000 / 115 = 400.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-perc-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Voting Percentage with Invalid Votes] In an election, 8% of voters didn't cast their votes. The winner got 48% of TOTAL votes and beat his rival by 1,100 votes. Find total voters.", "options": ["A) 25,000", "B) 26,500", "C) 27,500", "D) 30,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a local election involving only two candidates, 8% of the registered voters did not cast their votes. The winning candidate secured exactly 48% of the TOTAL registered votes and defeated his rival by a margin of 1,100 votes. What was the total number of registered voters?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let Total Registered Votes = 100%. Did not vote = 8%. Votes cast = 92%. Winner got 48% of TOTAL votes (100%), so Winner = 48%. Loser = Votes cast - Winner = 92% - 48% = 44%. Margin = Winner - Loser = 48% - 44% = 4%. We are given 4% of Total = 1100 => Total = 1100 / 0.04 = 110000 / 4 = 27,500.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-perc-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Price Reduction and Extra Quantity Formula] A reduction of 10% in price of sugar enables a housewife to buy 6.2 kg more for ₹1,116. Find the reduced price per kg.", "options": ["A) ₹15", "B) ₹18", "C) ₹20", "D) ₹22"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A 10% reduction in the market price of sugar enables a housewife to buy exactly 6.2 kg more sugar for ₹1,116. What is the newly reduced price of sugar per kg?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The extra 6.2 kg is purchased exclusively using the money saved from the 10% price drop! Savings = 10% of ₹1116 = ₹111.60. She buys 6.2 kg for this ₹111.60. Thus, Reduced Price per kg = 111.60 / 6.2 = 1116 / 62 = ₹18.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-perc-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Numerator and Denominator Percentage Changes] If numerator of a fraction is increased by 200% and denominator increased by 350%, the resultant is 5/12. Find original fraction.", "options": ["A) 5 / 8", "B) 5 / 9", "C) 7 / 12", "D) 3 / 8"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the numerator of a positive fraction is increased by 200% and its denominator is increased by 350%, the resultant fraction is 5/12. What was the original fraction?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let original fraction be x/y. Increasing numerator by 200% makes it x + 2x = 3x (or 300% of x). Increasing denominator by 350% makes it y + 3.5y = 4.5y (or 450% of y). So, 3x / 4.5y = 5 / 12 => (x/y) × (3 / 4.5) = 5/12 => (x/y) × (2/3) = 5/12 => x/y = (5/12) × (3/2) = 15/24 = 5/8.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-perc-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Fresh and Dry Fruit Moisture Content] Fresh fruit contains 68% water and dry fruit contains 20% water. How much dry fruit can be obtained from 100 kg of fresh fruit?", "options": ["A) 32 kg", "B) 40 kg", "C) 50 kg", "D) 60 kg"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Fresh fruit contains 68% water by weight, whereas dry fruit contains exactly 20% water by weight. How many kilograms of dry fruit can be obtained from exactly 100 kg of fresh fruit?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "The weight of the solid fruit PULP remains 100% constant during the drying process! In 100 kg of fresh fruit (68% water), the pulp is 100% - 68% = 32% of 100 kg = 32 kg. In dry fruit (20% water), the pulp makes up 100% - 20% = 80% of the total dry weight W. So, 80% of W = 32 kg => 0.8W = 32 => W = 32 / 0.8 = 40 kg.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-perc-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Successive Taxation / Multiplier Chain] The value of a machine depreciates by 10% every year. If it was purchased 3 years ago and its present value is ₹8,748, find its purchase price.", "options": ["A) ₹10,000", "B) ₹11,500", "C) ₹12,000", "D) ₹13,500"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The market value of a machine depreciates by 10% every single year. If it was purchased exactly 3 years ago and its present market value is ₹8,748, what was its original purchase price?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Original Price = P. Present Value = P × (1 - 10/100)^3 = P × (0.9)^3 = P × 0.729. We know Present Value = 8748. So, 0.729 P = 8748 => P = 8748 / 0.729 = 8748000 / 729 = 12,000.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. Fundamentals of Percentages\n* **Definition:** Percentage means "per 100". To convert a fraction to a percentage, multiply by 100.\n* **Fast Percentage Calculations (Decimal Shifting):**\n  * **10%:** Shift the decimal 1 place to the left (e.g., 10% of 260 = 26.0).\n  * **1%:** Shift the decimal 2 places to the left (e.g., 1% of 260 = 2.60).\n  * **To find 39%:** Find 40% (4 × 10%) and subtract 1%.\n* **Essential Fractions to Percentages:**\n  * `1/2 = 50%`, `1/3 = 33.33%`, `1/4 = 25%`, `1/5 = 20%`\n  * `1/6 = 16.66%`, `1/8 = 12.5%`, `1/9 = 11.11%`, `1/11 = 9.09%`\n\n### 💡 2. High-Yield Percentage Comparison\n* **"X is what % of Y?"** $\rightarrow$ `(X / Y) × 100`\n* **"X is what % MORE/LESS than Y?"** $\rightarrow$ `(Difference / Base) × 100`\n  * *Crucial Rule:* The "Base" is ALWAYS the value immediately following the word "than" or "of".\n\n### 💡 3. Common Exam Scenarios\n* **Constant Expenditure (Price & Consumption):**\n  * If the price of sugar increases by `R%`, to keep expenditure the same, consumption must decrease by:\n    **`[ R / (100 + R) ] × 100 %`**\n  * If price decreases by `R%`, consumption can increase by:\n    **`[ R / (100 - R) ] × 100 %`**\n* **Venn Diagram Traps:**\n  * If `A%` fail in subject 1, `B%` fail in subject 2, and `C%` fail in BOTH, the total percentage of students who failed in at least one subject is **`A + B - C`**. The percentage who PASSED in both is **`100 - (A + B - C)`**.\n* **Successive Population/Depreciation:**\n  * Same as compound interest: `P_final = P_initial × (1 ± R_1/100) × (1 ± R_2/100)`.	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Percentage	Master fast decimal shifting for percentages, comparison ratios (less than/more than), constant expenditure scaling, election voting math, and Venn diagram intersection traps.	51	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	15	[{"id": "apt-perc-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Missing Percentage Value] If 15% of y is same as 21% of z, then 12.5% of y is equal to what % of z?", "options": ["A) 15.5%", "B) 17.5%", "C) 19.5%", "D) 21.0%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 15% of y is exactly the same as 21% of z, then 12.5% of y is equal to what exact percentage of z?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Given: 15% of y = 21% of z => (15/100)y = (21/100)z => 15y = 21z => y = (21/15)z = 1.4z. We need 12.5% of y: 12.5% of (1.4z) = (12.5/100) × 1.4z = (17.5/100)z = 17.5% of z.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-perc-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Percentage Less Than / More Than] If price of rice is 30% less than wheat, then wheat's price is how much % more than rice?", "options": ["A) 30.00%", "B) 42.85%", "C) 33.33%", "D) 45.45%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the price of rice is 30% less than the price of wheat, then the price of wheat is exactly how much percent MORE than the price of rice?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let price of wheat = 100. Then price of rice = 100 - 30 = 70. How much MORE is wheat than rice? Difference = 100 - 70 = 30. The \\"base\\" is the price of rice (since it asks \\"more than rice\\"). Percentage = (Difference / Rice) × 100 = (30 / 70) × 100 = 3/7 × 100 = 42.85%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-perc-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Constant Expenditure Price Shift] By 20% decrease in price of rice, people buy 10 kg more in ₹100. Find the original price per kg.", "options": ["A) ₹2.00", "B) ₹2.50", "C) ₹3.00", "D) ₹4.00"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Due to a 20% decrease in the price of rice, a family is able to buy 10 kg more rice for exactly ₹100. What was the ORIGINAL price of rice per kg before the decrease?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let original price be P. New price = 0.8P. The difference in quantities bought with ₹100 is 10 kg. Equation: 100 / 0.8P - 100 / P = 10. Factor out 100/P: (100/P) × (1/0.8 - 1) = 10 => (100/P) × (1.25 - 1) = 10 => (100/P) × 0.25 = 10 => 25/P = 10 => P = 25 / 10 = ₹2.50 per kg.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-perc-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Venn Diagram Intersection Trap] 15% failed in Science, 25% failed in Maths, 10% failed in both. Find % passed in both.", "options": ["A) 50%", "B) 60%", "C) 70%", "D) 80%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a class examination, 15% of the total students failed in Science, 25% failed in Maths, and 10% failed in BOTH subjects. What exact percentage of students PASSED in both subjects?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Use the Union formula for Sets: Total Failed in at least one subject = n(Science) + n(Maths) - n(Both) = 15% + 25% - 10% = 30%. Since 30% of the students failed in at least one subject, the remaining students passed in BOTH subjects. Passed = 100% - 30% = 70%.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-perc-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Equating Un-knowns in Elections] In an election between two candidates, one got 40% votes and lost by 1,000 votes. Find total votes.", "options": ["A) 4,000", "B) 5,000", "C) 6,000", "D) 10,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In an election contested by exactly two candidates, the losing candidate secured 40% of the total votes cast and lost by a margin of 1,000 votes. What was the total number of votes casted in the election?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Candidate 1 got 40%. Candidate 2 got the remaining 60%. The difference in their vote shares is 60% - 40% = 20%. This 20% margin is equal to the 1,000 votes! So, 20% of Total Votes = 1000 => (20/100) × T = 1000 => T = 1000 × 5 = 5,000 total votes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-perc-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Successive Multiplicative Decrease] 10% of a village died of cholera. Panic set in, and 25% of the remaining left. Population reduced to 4,050. Find original.", "options": ["A) 5,500", "B) 6,000", "C) 6,500", "D) 7,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "During an epidemic, 10% of the inhabitants of a village died of cholera. Following this, panic set in and 25% of the REMAINING inhabitants left the village. If the population was then reduced to 4,050, what was the original number of inhabitants?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let original population = P. After 10% die, 90% remain: 0.9P. Out of these, 25% leave, so 75% of the 90% remain in the village. Final population = 0.75 × 0.90 × P = 0.675P. We know 0.675P = 4050 => P = 4050 / 0.675 = 4050000 / 675 = 6,000.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-perc-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Deducing Total from Passing Marks] A scores 10% and fails by 30 marks. B scores 40% and gets 30 marks more than minimum passing. Find maximum marks.", "options": ["A) 150", "B) 200", "C) 250", "D) 300"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In an exam, Student A scores 10% of the maximum marks and fails by 30 marks. Student B scores 40% of the maximum marks and gets 30 marks more than the minimum passing marks. What are the maximum possible marks for the exam?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let Maximum marks = M. Passing marks = A's score + 30 = 10% of M + 30. Also, Passing marks = B's score - 30 = 40% of M - 30. Equating them: 10% of M + 30 = 40% of M - 30 => 60 = 30% of M => 60 = 0.3M => M = 60 / 0.3 = 200.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-perc-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Successive Increase and Decrease] Price is first increased by 10% and then decreased by 10%. Find change in price.", "options": ["A) 0% (No change)", "B) 1% Increase", "C) 1% Decrease", "D) 2% Decrease"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The price of an apple is first increased by 10% and later decreased by 10%. What is the net overall percentage change in the price of the apple?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let original price = 100. Increase by 10% -> 110. Decrease by 10% of 110 (which is 11) -> 110 - 11 = 99. The net change from 100 to 99 is a 1% Decrease. (Shortcut: x% increase then x% decrease ALWAYS results in a loss of x^2 / 100 % = 10^2 / 100 = 1% decrease).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-perc-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Comparison of Three Percentages] A's salary is 50% more than B's. How much % is B's salary less than A's?", "options": ["A) 25.00%", "B) 33.33%", "C) 50.00%", "D) 66.66%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Person A's salary is exactly 50% MORE than Person B's salary. By what exact percentage is B's salary LESS than A's salary?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let B's salary = 100. A's salary = 100 + 50 = 150. How much is B less than A? Difference = 150 - 100 = 50. The \\"base\\" is A's salary (since it asks \\"less than A\\"). Percentage = (Difference / A) × 100 = (50 / 150) × 100 = 1/3 × 100 = 33.33%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-perc-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Population Literacy and Gender Ratios] 55% of population is female. 80% of males are literate. Total literacy is 58%. Find female literacy rate.", "options": ["A) 36%", "B) 40%", "C) 45%", "D) 50%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a country, 55% of the total population is female. 80% of the male population is literate. If the total overall literacy rate of the country is 58%, what percentage of the FEMALE population is literate?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Assume total population = 100. Females = 55, Males = 45. Literate Males = 80% of 45 = (80/100) × 45 = 36. Total literate people = 58. Since Total Literate = Literate Males + Literate Females => 58 = 36 + Literate Females => Literate Females = 22. The literacy rate OF FEMALES = (Literate Females / Total Females) × 100 = (22 / 55) × 100 = (2/5) × 100 = 40%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-perc-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Two Numbers Relative to Third] Two numbers are less than a third by 30% and 37%. How much % is the second less than the first?", "options": ["A) 7%", "B) 10%", "C) 12%", "D) 15%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two numbers are strictly less than a third number by 30% and 37% respectively. By what exact percentage is the second number less than the first number?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the third number be 100. First number = 100 - 30 = 70. Second number = 100 - 37 = 63. How much is the second less than the first? Difference = 70 - 63 = 7. Base is the first number (70). Percentage = (7 / 70) × 100 = 10%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-perc-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Price vs Consumption Equality] If price of sugar is raised by 25%, by how much % should a person reduce consumption so expenditure remains same?", "options": ["A) 15%", "B) 20%", "C) 25%", "D) 33.33%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the market price of sugar is suddenly raised by 25%, by what exact percentage must a household reduce its consumption of sugar so that its overall monthly expenditure remains absolutely unchanged?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Shortcut formula for constant expenditure: Reduction % = [R / (100 + R)] × 100. Here R = 25. Reduction = [25 / (100 + 25)] × 100 = (25 / 125) × 100 = 1/5 × 100 = 20%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-perc-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Deduction on Partial Bill] If 20% of an electricity bill is deducted, ₹100 is still to be paid. Find the original bill.", "options": ["A) ₹120", "B) ₹125", "C) ₹140", "D) ₹150"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a 20% deduction is applied to an electricity bill, an amount of ₹100 is still left to be paid. What was the original amount of the electricity bill?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let original bill be B. A 20% deduction means 80% is still owed. So 80% of B = 100 => 0.8B = 100 => B = 100 / 0.8 = 1000 / 8 = ₹125.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-perc-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Error Percentage in Calculation] A student multiplied a number by 3/5 instead of 5/3. What is the percentage error in the calculation?", "options": ["A) 36%", "B) 44%", "C) 54%", "D) 64%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A student carelessly multiplied a positive number by 3/5 instead of multiplying it by 5/3. What is the exact percentage error in the calculated result?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the number be 15 (LCM of 3 and 5). Correct result = 15 × (5/3) = 25. Erroneous result = 15 × (3/5) = 9. Error amount = 25 - 9 = 16. Percentage error = (Error / Correct Result) × 100 = (16 / 25) × 100 = 16 × 4 = 64%.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-perc-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Finding Original Number from Increment] If 15% is added to a number, it becomes 460. What is the number?", "options": ["A) 360", "B) 380", "C) 400", "D) 420"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "When 15% of a number is added to the number itself, the result becomes 460. What is the original number?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the number be N. Adding 15% to it means it becomes 115% of N. So, 115% of N = 460 => 1.15N = 460 => N = 460 / 1.15 = 46000 / 115 = 400.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-perc-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Voting Percentage with Invalid Votes] In an election, 8% of voters didn't cast their votes. The winner got 48% of TOTAL votes and beat his rival by 1,100 votes. Find total voters.", "options": ["A) 25,000", "B) 26,500", "C) 27,500", "D) 30,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a local election involving only two candidates, 8% of the registered voters did not cast their votes. The winning candidate secured exactly 48% of the TOTAL registered votes and defeated his rival by a margin of 1,100 votes. What was the total number of registered voters?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let Total Registered Votes = 100%. Did not vote = 8%. Votes cast = 92%. Winner got 48% of TOTAL votes (100%), so Winner = 48%. Loser = Votes cast - Winner = 92% - 48% = 44%. Margin = Winner - Loser = 48% - 44% = 4%. We are given 4% of Total = 1100 => Total = 1100 / 0.04 = 110000 / 4 = 27,500.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-perc-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Price Reduction and Extra Quantity Formula] A reduction of 10% in price of sugar enables a housewife to buy 6.2 kg more for ₹1,116. Find the reduced price per kg.", "options": ["A) ₹15", "B) ₹18", "C) ₹20", "D) ₹22"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A 10% reduction in the market price of sugar enables a housewife to buy exactly 6.2 kg more sugar for ₹1,116. What is the newly reduced price of sugar per kg?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The extra 6.2 kg is purchased exclusively using the money saved from the 10% price drop! Savings = 10% of ₹1116 = ₹111.60. She buys 6.2 kg for this ₹111.60. Thus, Reduced Price per kg = 111.60 / 6.2 = 1116 / 62 = ₹18.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-perc-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Numerator and Denominator Percentage Changes] If numerator of a fraction is increased by 200% and denominator increased by 350%, the resultant is 5/12. Find original fraction.", "options": ["A) 5 / 8", "B) 5 / 9", "C) 7 / 12", "D) 3 / 8"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the numerator of a positive fraction is increased by 200% and its denominator is increased by 350%, the resultant fraction is 5/12. What was the original fraction?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let original fraction be x/y. Increasing numerator by 200% makes it x + 2x = 3x (or 300% of x). Increasing denominator by 350% makes it y + 3.5y = 4.5y (or 450% of y). So, 3x / 4.5y = 5 / 12 => (x/y) × (3 / 4.5) = 5/12 => (x/y) × (2/3) = 5/12 => x/y = (5/12) × (3/2) = 15/24 = 5/8.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-perc-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Fresh and Dry Fruit Moisture Content] Fresh fruit contains 68% water and dry fruit contains 20% water. How much dry fruit can be obtained from 100 kg of fresh fruit?", "options": ["A) 32 kg", "B) 40 kg", "C) 50 kg", "D) 60 kg"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Fresh fruit contains 68% water by weight, whereas dry fruit contains exactly 20% water by weight. How many kilograms of dry fruit can be obtained from exactly 100 kg of fresh fruit?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "The weight of the solid fruit PULP remains 100% constant during the drying process! In 100 kg of fresh fruit (68% water), the pulp is 100% - 68% = 32% of 100 kg = 32 kg. In dry fruit (20% water), the pulp makes up 100% - 20% = 80% of the total dry weight W. So, 80% of W = 32 kg => 0.8W = 32 => W = 32 / 0.8 = 40 kg.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-perc-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Successive Taxation / Multiplier Chain] The value of a machine depreciates by 10% every year. If it was purchased 3 years ago and its present value is ₹8,748, find its purchase price.", "options": ["A) ₹10,000", "B) ₹11,500", "C) ₹12,000", "D) ₹13,500"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The market value of a machine depreciates by 10% every single year. If it was purchased exactly 3 years ago and its present market value is ₹8,748, what was its original purchase price?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Original Price = P. Present Value = P × (1 - 10/100)^3 = P × (0.9)^3 = P × 0.729. We know Present Value = 8748. So, 0.729 P = 8748 => P = 8748 / 0.729 = 8748000 / 729 = 12,000.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22226	LLD Project: Document Editor (Google Docs)	lld-project-document-editor	Real-world LLD implementation: Document elements (Text, Image, NewLine), hierarchical composition, persistence delegation, and the Principle of Least Knowledge.	2026-10-07 16:56:30.769208+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: LLD Project: Document Editor (Google Docs)\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/MT9qZFGQXOU", "title": "Build Google Docs | A Real-World LLD Project | Document Editor LLD |", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v7-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Document Element Polymorphic Hierarchy", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n```cpp\\n#include <string>\\n\\nclass IDocumentElement {\\npublic:\\n    virtual ~IDocumentElement() = default;\\n    virtual std::string render() const = 0;\\n};\\n\\nclass TextElement : public IDocumentElement {\\n    std::string text;\\npublic:\\n    TextElement(std::string t) : text(std::move(t)) {}\\n    std::string render() const override { return text; }\\n};\\n\\nclass ImageElement : public IDocumentElement {\\n    std::string imagePath;\\npublic:\\n    ImageElement(std::string p) : imagePath(std::move(p)) {}\\n    std::string render() const override { return \\"[Image: \\" + imagePath + \\"]\\"; }\\n};\\n\\nclass NewLineElement : public IDocumentElement {\\npublic:\\n    std::string render() const override { return \\"\\\\n\\"; }\\n};\\n```\\nThe document simply iterates through `std::vector<IDocumentElement*>` and concatenates `elem->render()`. Adding `TableElement` requires zero changes to the rendering loop.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Design the class hierarchy for document elements (Text, Image, NewLine) in a Google Docs clone so new element types can be added without modifying the core document renderer.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v7-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Persistence Layer Abstraction in Document Editor", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. Define an abstract persistence interface:\\n```cpp\\nclass IPersistence {\\npublic:\\n    virtual ~IPersistence() = default;\\n    virtual void save(const std::string& renderedContent, const std::string& destination) = 0;\\n};\\n```\\n2. Concrete implementations:\\n```cpp\\nclass FileStorage : public IPersistence {\\npublic:\\n    void save(const std::string& content, const std::string& filepath) override {\\n        std::ofstream out(filepath);\\n        out << content;\\n    }\\n};\\nclass DatabaseStorage : public IPersistence { /* SQL/S3 insert logic */ };\\n```\\n3. `DocumentEditor` receives `IPersistence*` via its constructor. When `editor.saveDocument(\\"doc.txt\\")` is invoked, it delegates to `persistence->save(document.render(), destination)` without knowing whether storage is local disk or AWS S3.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How do you decouple file saving (Disk vs Cloud Database) from the DocumentEditor class using the Dependency Inversion Principle?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v7-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: The Principle of Least Knowledge (Law of Demeter) in LLD", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Principle of Least Knowledge (Law of Demeter):**\\n\\"Talk only to your immediate friends; do not talk to strangers.\\" An object should invoke methods only on:\\n1. Itself\\n2. Its own member fields\\n3. Parameters passed into the method\\n4. Objects it directly instantiated\\n\\n**The Violation:**\\n`editor.getDocument().getElements()[0].render();`\\n* `editor` is reaching through `Document` into its internal collection `Elements` to extract an element and invoke `render()`.\\n* This exposes internal storage structures. If `Document` changes its internal storage from a vector to a B-tree or rope data structure, the caller breaks.\\n\\n**The Resolution:**\\n`editor` should simply call `document.render()`, allowing `Document` to internally coordinate its own elements without exposing internal collections.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is the Principle of Least Knowledge (Law of Demeter)? Explain how a method chain like editor.getDocument().getElements()[0].render() violates it.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v7-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Delegation vs Inheritance in DocumentEditor", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Conceptual Relationship:** An `Editor` is NOT a `Document` (Is-A fails). An `Editor` has a `Document` and manages user interaction around it (Has-A succeeds).\\n2. **Encapsulation:** If `DocumentEditor` inherited from `Document`, all internal collection manipulation methods of `Document` would leak into the public interface of `DocumentEditor`.\\n3. **Dynamic Flexibility:** Composition allows a single `DocumentEditor` instance to swap active documents dynamically at runtime (`editor.openDocument(newDoc)`), which is strictly impossible with compile-time inheritance.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the Document Editor architecture, why does DocumentEditor utilize Composition and Delegation with Document, rather than inheriting from Document?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v7-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Memory Management for Polymorphic Elements in C++", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nUsing raw pointers (`std::vector<IDocumentElement*>`) requires manual loops inside destructors calling `delete`, which frequently causes memory leaks if exceptions occur.\\n\\n**The Modern C++ Solution:**\\nUse `std::vector<std::unique_ptr<IDocumentElement>>`:\\n```cpp\\nclass Document {\\n    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<IDocumentElement>> elements;\\npublic:\\n    void addElement(std::unique_ptr<IDocumentElement> elem) {\\n        elements.push_back(std::move(elem));\\n    }\\n    std::string render() const {\\n        std::string result;\\n        for (const auto& elem : elements) {\\n            result += elem->render();\\n        }\\n        return result;\\n    }\\n};\\n```\\n`std::unique_ptr` guarantees exclusive ownership: when `Document` is destroyed or elements are removed, the concrete element instances are automatically and safely deleted with zero memory leaks.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In modern C++, managing raw pointers (IDocumentElement*) in a document vector risks memory leaks. How should std::unique_ptr be utilized to enforce automatic lifecycle management?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Monolithic Anti-Pattern\n* Combining element storage, rendering logic, format parsing, and file I/O inside a single monolithic `DocumentEditor` class violates SRP and OCP.\n\n### 💡 2. Hierarchical Element Architecture\n* **Abstract Base:** `IDocumentElement` defining pure virtual `render()` returning a string.\n* **Specialized Subclasses:** `TextElement`, `ImageElement`, `NewLineElement`, `TabSpaceElement`.\n* **Open for Extension:** Adding bold text, tables, or video embeds only requires creating a new subclass without editing existing classes.\n\n### 💡 3. Delegation & Separation of Concerns\n* **`Document`:** Manages the collection of `IDocumentElement` pointers (adding, ordering, deleting).\n* **`IPersistence`:** Abstract interface for saving documents (`FileStorage`, `DatabaseStorage`).\n* **`DocumentEditor`:** High-level facade that coordinates user actions and delegates responsibilities to `Document` and `IPersistence`.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	LLD Project: Document Editor (Google Docs)	Real-world LLD implementation: Document elements (Text, Image, NewLine), hierarchical composition, persistence delegation, and the Principle of Least Knowledge.	45	Hard	Low Level Design	21	[{"id": "lld-v7-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Document Element Polymorphic Hierarchy", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n```cpp\\n#include <string>\\n\\nclass IDocumentElement {\\npublic:\\n    virtual ~IDocumentElement() = default;\\n    virtual std::string render() const = 0;\\n};\\n\\nclass TextElement : public IDocumentElement {\\n    std::string text;\\npublic:\\n    TextElement(std::string t) : text(std::move(t)) {}\\n    std::string render() const override { return text; }\\n};\\n\\nclass ImageElement : public IDocumentElement {\\n    std::string imagePath;\\npublic:\\n    ImageElement(std::string p) : imagePath(std::move(p)) {}\\n    std::string render() const override { return \\"[Image: \\" + imagePath + \\"]\\"; }\\n};\\n\\nclass NewLineElement : public IDocumentElement {\\npublic:\\n    std::string render() const override { return \\"\\\\n\\"; }\\n};\\n```\\nThe document simply iterates through `std::vector<IDocumentElement*>` and concatenates `elem->render()`. Adding `TableElement` requires zero changes to the rendering loop.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Design the class hierarchy for document elements (Text, Image, NewLine) in a Google Docs clone so new element types can be added without modifying the core document renderer.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v7-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Persistence Layer Abstraction in Document Editor", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. Define an abstract persistence interface:\\n```cpp\\nclass IPersistence {\\npublic:\\n    virtual ~IPersistence() = default;\\n    virtual void save(const std::string& renderedContent, const std::string& destination) = 0;\\n};\\n```\\n2. Concrete implementations:\\n```cpp\\nclass FileStorage : public IPersistence {\\npublic:\\n    void save(const std::string& content, const std::string& filepath) override {\\n        std::ofstream out(filepath);\\n        out << content;\\n    }\\n};\\nclass DatabaseStorage : public IPersistence { /* SQL/S3 insert logic */ };\\n```\\n3. `DocumentEditor` receives `IPersistence*` via its constructor. When `editor.saveDocument(\\"doc.txt\\")` is invoked, it delegates to `persistence->save(document.render(), destination)` without knowing whether storage is local disk or AWS S3.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How do you decouple file saving (Disk vs Cloud Database) from the DocumentEditor class using the Dependency Inversion Principle?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v7-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: The Principle of Least Knowledge (Law of Demeter) in LLD", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Principle of Least Knowledge (Law of Demeter):**\\n\\"Talk only to your immediate friends; do not talk to strangers.\\" An object should invoke methods only on:\\n1. Itself\\n2. Its own member fields\\n3. Parameters passed into the method\\n4. Objects it directly instantiated\\n\\n**The Violation:**\\n`editor.getDocument().getElements()[0].render();`\\n* `editor` is reaching through `Document` into its internal collection `Elements` to extract an element and invoke `render()`.\\n* This exposes internal storage structures. If `Document` changes its internal storage from a vector to a B-tree or rope data structure, the caller breaks.\\n\\n**The Resolution:**\\n`editor` should simply call `document.render()`, allowing `Document` to internally coordinate its own elements without exposing internal collections.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is the Principle of Least Knowledge (Law of Demeter)? Explain how a method chain like editor.getDocument().getElements()[0].render() violates it.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v7-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Delegation vs Inheritance in DocumentEditor", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Conceptual Relationship:** An `Editor` is NOT a `Document` (Is-A fails). An `Editor` has a `Document` and manages user interaction around it (Has-A succeeds).\\n2. **Encapsulation:** If `DocumentEditor` inherited from `Document`, all internal collection manipulation methods of `Document` would leak into the public interface of `DocumentEditor`.\\n3. **Dynamic Flexibility:** Composition allows a single `DocumentEditor` instance to swap active documents dynamically at runtime (`editor.openDocument(newDoc)`), which is strictly impossible with compile-time inheritance.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the Document Editor architecture, why does DocumentEditor utilize Composition and Delegation with Document, rather than inheriting from Document?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v7-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Memory Management for Polymorphic Elements in C++", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nUsing raw pointers (`std::vector<IDocumentElement*>`) requires manual loops inside destructors calling `delete`, which frequently causes memory leaks if exceptions occur.\\n\\n**The Modern C++ Solution:**\\nUse `std::vector<std::unique_ptr<IDocumentElement>>`:\\n```cpp\\nclass Document {\\n    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<IDocumentElement>> elements;\\npublic:\\n    void addElement(std::unique_ptr<IDocumentElement> elem) {\\n        elements.push_back(std::move(elem));\\n    }\\n    std::string render() const {\\n        std::string result;\\n        for (const auto& elem : elements) {\\n            result += elem->render();\\n        }\\n        return result;\\n    }\\n};\\n```\\n`std::unique_ptr` guarantees exclusive ownership: when `Document` is destroyed or elements are removed, the concrete element instances are automatically and safely deleted with zero memory leaks.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In modern C++, managing raw pointers (IDocumentElement*) in a document vector risks memory leaks. How should std::unique_ptr be utilized to enforce automatic lifecycle management?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22091	Lecture 6: MongoDB BSON Data Types — Int32/Int64, Double, ISODate, Arrays, Objects & ObjectId	mongo-lecture-6-bson-data-types-isodate-arrays-objects	Explore BSON data types in depth including 32-bit vs. 64-bit Integers, Doubles, Booleans, ISODate() & new Date() with UTC/timezone offsets, Null, Arrays, and nested Objects.	2026-10-06 19:11:34.825286+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMongoDB supports rich **BSON data types** that let you store numbers with exact bit precision, timezone-aware dates, lists, and deeply nested sub-documents inside a single record.\n\n### 💡 Complete BSON Data Types Breakdown\n* **`ObjectId`:** 12-byte unique identifier automatically assigned to `_id`.\n* **`String`:** UTF-8 text enclosed in single or double quotes (e.g., `"Yahoo Baba"`). Warning: quoting a number like `"25"` stores it as a String and prevents mathematical operations.\n* **`32-bit Integer (int)`:** Whole numbers between `-2,147,483,648` and `+2,147,483,647` (~214 Crore). Consumes **4 bytes** of storage.\n* **`64-bit Integer (long)`:** Whole numbers exceeding the 32-bit range (up to `±9.22 × 10^18`). Consumes **8 bytes** of storage. MongoDB automatically promotes integers exceeding the 32-bit limit to 64-bit.\n* **`Double`:** Floating-point decimal values (e.g., `weight: 68.55`).\n* **`Boolean (bool)`:** Unquoted `true` or `false`.\n* **`Null`:** Unquoted `null` representing an absent or empty value (e.g., `kids: null`).\n* **`Array`:** Ordered list of values enclosed in `[ ... ` (e.g., `hobbies: ["Music", "Sports"`).\n* **`Object (Embedded Document)`:** Nested key-value document enclosed in `{ ... }` (e.g., `address: { street: "MG Road", city: "Delhi", zip: 110001 }`).\n* **`Date` (`ISODate` / `new Date`):** Stores date and time as a 64-bit integer representing milliseconds since the Unix epoch, enabling date range queries and date extraction functions.\n\n### 🛠️ Date & Time Formats in MongoDB (`ISODate` & `new Date`)\nNever store dates as plain strings (`"20-10-2024"`), or you will lose MongoDB's native date comparison and extraction methods.\n* **UTC Time (`Z`):** `ISODate("2024-10-18T18:30:00Z")` — Coordinated Universal Time.\n* **Positive Offset (e.g., CET):** `ISODate("2024-10-18T18:30:00+02:00")` — 2 hours ahead of UTC.\n* **Negative Offset (e.g., EST):** `ISODate("2024-10-18T18:30:00-05:00")` — 5 hours behind UTC.\n* **Current Server Timestamp:** `new Date()` (with empty parentheses) automatically captures the exact current date and time at insertion.\n\n### 🛠️ Inserting a Document Using All Core BSON Types\n```javascript\ndb.personal.insertOne({\n  name: "Mohan Kumar",\n  age: 28,                                  // 32-bit Integer (4 bytes)\n  married: true,                            // Boolean\n  dob: ISODate("2000-10-25T08:00:00Z"),     // UTC Date\n  createdAt: new Date(),                    // Current Timestamp\n  weight: 72.5,                             // Double\n  kids: null,                               // Null\n  hobbies: ["Travel", "Books", "Music",    // Array of Strings\n  address: {                                // Embedded Object\n    street: "75 Park Avenue",\n    city: "Mumbai",\n    zip: 400001\n  }\n})\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/bson-types/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/bson-types/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 6: MongoDB BSON Data Types"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z3pnpP90K7c", "title": "MongoDB Data Types Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "22m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[{"id": "mongo-6-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c27a3fe74e0db37736", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c27a3fe74e0db37736", "name": "Problem #31: Reviews sorted by createdAt descending", "title": "Problem #31: Reviews sorted by createdAt descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "