58	C++ STL Architecture: Dynamic Containers, Iterators, Hashing & Custom Comparators	cpp-stl-containers-iterators-comparators	Master std::vector, pair, stack, queue, priority_queue, set, unordered_map, and built-in sorting algorithms.	2026-10-02 17:05:20.021318+05:30	### 🎯 Computer Science Fundamentals: C++ STL Architecture: Dynamic Containers, Iterators, Hashing & Custom Comparators\nCore systems concepts frequently tested in technical screening rounds and engineering interviews.\n\n- **System Architecture**: How modern operating systems, networks, and processors coordinate resources.\n- **Key Concepts to Master**: Concurrency primitives, resource isolation, latency vs throughput tradeoffs, and fault tolerance.\n- **Interview Key Takeaways**:\n  - Be prepared to draw diagrams explaining data flow and component lifecycles.\n  - Explain practical real-world applications (e.g. why databases use WAL, why HTTP/2 uses multiplexing).	[{"url": "https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/", "title": "OS Three Easy Pieces Guide"}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/operating-systems/", "title": "GeeksforGeeks CS Fundamentals"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RRVYpIET_RU", "title": "Lecture 6: C++ STL Full Course — Vectors, Pairs, Stacks, Queues, Sets, Maps & Algorithms", "duration": "1h 15m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/jewels-and-stones/", "title": "Jewels and Stones", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "771", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Use std::unordered_set to perform constant time O(1) character membership queries."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/c-stl-set-2-pair/1", "title": "C++ STL | Set 2 (pair)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "problemId": "gfg-stl-pair", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Instantiate, compare, and unpack std::pair containers across structured coordinate pairs."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/c-stl-set-1-vector/1", "title": "C++ STL | Set 1 (vector)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "problemId": "gfg-stl-vector", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Execute dynamic capacity operations: push_back, pop_back, sort, and iterator traversals."}]	Module 3: Standard Template Library (STL) & Containers	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThe C++ Standard Template Library (STL) gives you ready-to-use data structures (like dynamic arrays, stacks, queues, sets, and hash maps) and built-in algorithms (like sorting and binary search) so you don't have to code them from scratch during interviews.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Sequence Containers:**\n  - `pair<int, int>`: Groups two values together (`p.first` and `p.second`).\n  - `vector<int>`: A dynamic array that grows automatically via `push_back()`.\n  - `list<int>` & `deque<int>`: Allow fast insertions at both the front (`push_front()`) and back (`push_back()`).\n- **Adapter Containers:**\n  - `stack<int>`: **LIFO** (Last-In, First-Out) using `push()`, `pop()`, and `top()` in `O(1)` time.\n  - `queue<int>`: **FIFO** (First-In, First-Out) using `push()`, `pop()`, and `front()` in `O(1)` time.\n  - `priority_queue<int>`: Keeps the largest element on top by default (**Max-Heap**). Push and pop take `O(log N)` time.\n- **Set & Map Containers:**\n  - `set<int>` vs `unordered_set<int>`: Both store **unique** elements only. `set` keeps them sorted in `O(log N)` time; `unordered_set` is unsorted but faster at `O(1)` average time.\n  - `map<K, V>` vs `unordered_map<K, V>`: Store **key-value pairs** with unique keys. `map` is sorted by key (`O(log N)`); `unordered_map` is hash-based (`O(1)` average).\n- **Essential Built-in Algorithms:**\n  - `sort(v.begin(), v.end())`: Sorts in `O(N log N)` time. You can pass a custom `bool comp(a, b)` function to sort in your own order.\n  - `*max_element(v.begin(), v.end())`: Finds the maximum value in a vector or array.\n\n### 💻 Quick STL Cheat Sheet\n```cpp\nvector<int> nums = {5, 1, 4, 2};\nsort(nums.begin(), nums.end()); // Sorts to: {1, 2, 4, 5}\n\nunordered_map<int, int> freq;\nfor (int x : nums) {\n    freq[x]++; // Counts frequency of each number in O(1) average time\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
11589	Square-Root Trial Division: Print All Prime Factors of a Number	dsa-print-all-prime-factors-of-a-number	Master Square-Root Trial Division: Print All Prime Factors of a Number with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:12:24.305843+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Square-Root Trial Division: Print All Prime Factors of a Number\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LT7XhVdeRyg", "title": "Square-Root Trial Division: Print All Prime Factors of a Number - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/prime-factors5052/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Prime Factors", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Given a number N, find all its unique prime factors in increasing order."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/distinct-prime-factors-of-product-of-array/", "title": "LeetCode 2521: Distinct Prime Factors of Product of Array", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Extract all distinct prime factors of each number in an array using O(sqrt(N)) trial division."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/smallest-value-after-replacing-with-sum-of-prime-factors/", "title": "LeetCode 2507: Smallest Value After Replacing With Sum of Prime Factors", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Repeatedly replace n with the sum of its prime factors using trial division."}]	Module 39: Mathematical & Number Theory Algorithms: Divisors, Primes, Binary Exponentiation & Sieve Precomputation	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an integer `N`, return a list of all **unique prime factors** of `N` (divisors of `N` that are prime numbers). For example, for `N = 780`, the unique prime factors are `[2, 3, 5, 13]`.\n\n### 💡 Progression of Approaches (Brute → Optimal School Division up to sqrt(N))\n- **1. Brute/Better Divisor + Prime Check (`O(sqrt(N) * 2 * sqrt(N))`):**\n  - Find every divisor `(i, N / i)` and run `isPrime()` on each.\n- **2. Optimal Repeated Division up to `sqrt(N)` (`O(sqrt(N) * log N)` Time):**\n  - Start `i` from `2` (the first prime) and loop while **`i * i <= N`**:\n    - If **`N % i == 0`**:\n      1. Append `i` to `list` (why is `i` guaranteed to be prime without calling `isPrime(i)`? Because we already completely divided out all smaller prime factors `2, 3, ...` before reaching `i`, so no composite multiple like `4` or `6` can ever divide the reduced `N`!).\n      2. **Exhaust `i` completely:** Run **`while (N % i == 0) N = N / i;`**!\n  - **The Crucial Post-Loop Check (`if (N != 1) list.push_back(N);`):**\n    - What if `N` itself is a prime (like `37`), or after dividing out `2, 3, 5` from `780` we are left with `N = 13` (a prime factor larger than `sqrt(13)`)?\n    - When the loop `i * i <= N` finishes, if **`N != 1`**, the remaining `N` is guaranteed to be a prime factor—append `N` to `list`!\n\n### 💻 Print All Unique Prime Factors Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> AllPrimeFactors(int N) {\n    vector<int> primeFactors;\n\n    // 1. Trial division up to sqrt(N)\n    for (int i = 2; i * i <= N; i++) {\n        if (N % i == 0) {\n            primeFactors.push_back(i);\n            // Divide N by i as many times as possible to strip all powers of i\n            while (N % i == 0) {\n                N = N / i;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // 2. If N is still > 1, the remaining N is itself a prime factor!\n    if (N != 1) {\n        primeFactors.push_back(N);\n    }\n\n    return primeFactors;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting `if (N != 1) primeFactors.push_back(N);` After the Loop:** Any number `N` can have at most one prime factor greater than `sqrt(N)` (e.g., `13` in `780`, or `37` in `37`). Without `if (N != 1)`, that largest prime factor is completely missed!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
588	Monotonic Boundary Search: Lower Bound, Upper Bound, Search Insert Position & Floor/Ceil	dsa-lower-bound-upper-bound-floor-ceil	Implement Lower Bound (smallest index with arr[i] >= x) and Upper Bound (arr[i] > x) in O(log N) time to solve Search Insert Position and Floor/Ceil queries.	2026-10-02 23:24:14.972905+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Monotonic Boundary Search: Lower Bound, Upper Bound, Search Insert Position & Floor/Ceil\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6zhGS79oQ4k", "title": "Lecture 46: Implement Lower Bound and Upper Bound", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-smallest-letter-greater-than-target/", "title": "Find Smallest Letter Greater Than Target", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Apply Upper Bound binary search on a sorted character array with wrap-around."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-the-distance-value-between-two-arrays/", "title": "Find the Distance Value Between Two Arrays", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Use lower_bound on a sorted array to check if any element falls within [x - d, x + d]."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/counting-elements-in-two-arrays/1", "title": "Counting elements in two arrays", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use Upper Bound to count how many elements in arr2 are <= each element in arr1."}]	Module 12: 1D Binary Search, Bounds & Rotated Array Invariants	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMastering **Lower Bound** and **Upper Bound** in `O(log2 N)` time unlocks dozens of binary search interview questions—including **Search Insert Position** and **Floor & Ceil in a Sorted Array**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **1. Lower Bound (`arr[ind] >= x`):**\n  - The **smallest index `ind`** such that `arr[ind] >= x`. (If all elements are smaller than `x`, return `n`).\n  - Whenever `arr[mid] >= x`, record `ans = mid` as a possible answer and search left (`high = mid - 1`) for an even smaller index. Otherwise, search right (`low = mid + 1`).\n  - *C++ STL Shortcut:* `lower_bound(arr.begin(), arr.end(), x) - arr.begin()`.\n- **2. Upper Bound (`arr[ind] > x`):**\n  - The **smallest index `ind`** such that `arr[ind] > x` (strictly greater).\n  - Identical code to Lower Bound, except the condition changes from `>=` to **`>`** (`if (arr[mid] > x)`)!\n  - *C++ STL Shortcut:* `upper_bound(arr.begin(), arr.end(), x) - arr.begin()`.\n- **3. Search Insert Position:**\n  - Finding the index where `x` is present (or where it would be inserted in sorted order) is **100% identical to Lower Bound**!\n- **4. Floor and Ceil in a Sorted Array:**\n  - **Ceil of `x`:** Smallest element in the array `>= x` (the value at the Lower Bound index).\n  - **Floor of `x`:** Largest element in the array `<= x` (if `arr[mid] <= x`, store `ans = arr[mid]` and move right `low = mid + 1`).\n\n### 💻 Lower Bound & Upper Bound Blueprint\n```cpp\nint lowerBound(vector<int> &arr, int n, int x) {\n    int low = 0, high = n - 1, ans = n;\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (arr[mid] >= x) { // Change >= to > for upperBound!\n            ans = mid;\n            high = mid - 1;  // Look for a smaller valid index on the left\n        } else {\n            low = mid + 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22094	Lecture 9: Deleting Documents — deleteOne(), deleteMany() & Collection Truncation	mongo-lecture-9-delete-documents-deleteone-deletemany	Remove single matching documents by _id or filter using deleteOne(), bulk-delete matching records with deleteMany(), and empty entire collections safely using deleteMany({}).	2026-10-06 19:11:34.825286+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMongoDB provides two targeted methods for deleting documents from a collection: **`deleteOne(filter)`** and **`deleteMany(filter)`**.\n\n### 💡 Single vs. Bulk Deletion Behavior\n* **`db.<collection>.deleteOne({ field: value })`:** Searches the collection and deletes **only the very first document** that matches the filter criteria, even if multiple documents match. Returns `{ acknowledged: true, deletedCount: 1 }`.\n* **Deleting by `_id` (Best Practice):** Because multiple documents can share the same `name` or `class`, always pass `{ _id: ObjectId("...") }` to `deleteOne()` when deleting a specific user record in production.\n* **`db.<collection>.deleteMany({ field: value })`:** Deletes **all documents** in the collection that match the filter criteria and returns the total `deletedCount`.\n* **Truncating a Collection (`deleteMany({})`):** Passing an empty filter object `{}` to `deleteMany({})` deletes every document in the collection while keeping the collection itself (and its indexes/validators) intact.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical Deletion Examples\n```javascript\n// 1. Delete a specific student safely using their unique _id\ndb.students.deleteOne({\n  _id: ObjectId("67164551290b41d4bc701c11")\n})\n// Returns: { acknowledged: true, deletedCount: 1 }\n\n// 2. Delete only the FIRST document where class is "BCA"\ndb.students.deleteOne({ class: "BCA" })\n\n// 3. Delete ALL documents where class is "BCA"\ndb.students.deleteMany({ class: "BCA" })\n// Returns: { acknowledged: true, deletedCount: 2 }\n\n// 4. Delete all documents in the collection (empty filter object {} is mandatory)\ndb.students.deleteMany({})\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes\n* **Calling `deleteMany()` Without `{}`:** Calling `db.students.deleteMany()` with empty parentheses throws a missing filter error; you must explicitly pass `{}` (`db.students.deleteMany({})`) to confirm deleting all documents.	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/remove-documents/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/remove-documents/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 9: Deleting Documents"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cUq0upSof34", "title": "MongoDB Delete Document Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "9m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[{"id": "mongo-9-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37627", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37627", "name": "Problem #120: Logs with level not debug", "title": "Problem #120: Logs with level not debug", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "120", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return logs where **level** is not \\"debug\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37627"}, {"id": "mongo-9-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374c0", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374c0", "name": "Problem #239: Products with all ratings >= 3", "title": "Problem #239: Products with all ratings >= 3", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "239", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Return products where no element in **ratings** is less than 3. Use **$not** and **$lt**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374c0"}, {"id": "mongo-9-prob-3", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37333", "link": "https://mongopractic