960	Row-Level Binary Search: Find the Row With Maximum Number of 1s in a Binary Matrix	dsa-row-with-maximum-number-of-1s	Identify the row with the maximum number of 1s in an N x M row-sorted binary matrix in O(N * log M) time using lower bound.	2026-10-03 00:19:12.458907+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Row-Level Binary Search: Find the Row With Maximum Number of 1s in a Binary Matrix\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SCz-1TtYxDI", "title": "Lecture 68: Find the row with maximum number of 1's", "duration": "24m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/row-with-maximum-ones/", "title": "Row With Maximum Ones", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Count ones in each row and return the smallest row index with the maximum count."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/count-negative-numbers-in-a-sorted-matrix/", "title": "Count Negative Numbers in a Sorted Matrix", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Count negative numbers in a row-and-column sorted matrix in O(n + m) or O(n log m) time."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/row-with-max-1s0023/1", "title": "Row with max 1s", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the 0-based index of the first row that has the maximum number of 1s."}]	Module 14: Multi-Array Partitioning & 2D Matrix Binary Search	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an `N x M` binary matrix where every row is sorted in non-decreasing order (`0`s followed by `1`s), find the row index with the **maximum number of 1s**. If multiple rows tie, return the smallest index.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Row-Wise Lower Bound (O(N * log2 M) Time):**\n  - Because each row is sorted, all `1`s appear consecutively at the end of the row.\n  - The number of `1`s in row `i` is: `m - firstOccurrenceOfOne(row[i])` (where `firstOccurrence` is found using `lower_bound` for `1`).\n- **Iteration:**\n  - Loop `i` from `0` to `n - 1`.\n  - Calculate `countOnes = m - lowerBound(matrix[i], 1)`.\n  - If `countOnes > maxCount`, update `maxCount = countOnes` and `index = i`.\n  - If `maxCount == 0`, return `-1`.\n\n### 💻 Row With Maximum 1s Blueprint\n```cpp\nint rowWithMax1s(vector<vector<int>> &matrix, int n, int m) {\n    int maxCnt = 0, index = -1;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        // lower_bound gives first occurrence of 1 in the sorted row\n        int firstOne = lower_bound(matrix[i].begin(), matrix[i].end(), 1) - matrix[i].begin();\n        int countOnes = m - firstOne;\n        if (countOnes > maxCnt) {\n            maxCnt = countOnes;\n            index = i;\n        }\n    }\n    return index;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22228	Factory Design Pattern	lld-factory-design-pattern	Abstract object creation logic away from business logic. Master Simple Factory, Factory Method, and Abstract Factory implementations for scalable architectures.	2026-10-07 17:07:23.574728+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Factory Design Pattern\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/dMK4TbG29fk", "title": "Factory Design Pattern | Simple, Factory Method & Abstract Factory with Real-Life Examples", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v9-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: The Problem with the \\"new\\" Keyword", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nUsing the `new` keyword hardcodes a dependency on a concrete class. \\nIf a `CheckoutService` contains `IPayment p = new CreditCardPayment();`, it is permanently fused to `CreditCardPayment`. \\n\\n1. **Breaks OCP:** If you want to introduce `UPIPayment`, you must edit `CheckoutService` to add conditional logic.\\n2. **Pollutes Business Logic:** Constructing complex objects (fetching DB credentials, setting up HTTP clients) clutters the core domain logic.\\n3. **Breaks Testability:** You cannot easily inject a `MockPayment` during unit testing if the service hardcodes the instantiation.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is embedding object creation logic (using the \\"new\\" keyword) directly inside business logic considered an architectural anti-pattern?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v9-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Simple Factory vs Factory Method", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Simple Factory:** A single class with a method like `createVehicle(String type)` that uses a massive `switch` statement to return `new Car()`, `new Bike()`, etc.\\n* *Violation:* It violates OCP because every time a new vehicle type is added, you must physically open the `SimpleFactory` class and modify the `switch` statement.\\n\\n**Factory Method:** Defines an abstract `Creator` interface with a pure virtual `createVehicle()` method. You implement specific creators: `CarCreator : public Creator` and `BikeCreator : public Creator`.\\n* *OCP Compliant:* To add a `Truck`, you simply create a `TruckCreator` class without touching existing code. Subclasses decide which concrete product to instantiate.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Differentiate between a \\"Simple Factory\\" and the formal \\"Factory Method\\" design pattern. Why does a Simple Factory technically violate the Open/Closed Principle?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v9-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: The Abstract Factory Pattern for Families of Objects", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Abstract Factory** goes beyond creating a single product; it creates whole *suites* of related or dependent objects that must be used together to prevent compatibility mismatches.\\n\\n**OS UI Example:**\\nSuppose you are building a cross-platform UI. You need Buttons and Checkboxes.\\n* Abstract Factory: `IGUIFactory` (methods: `createButton()`, `createCheckbox()`)\\n* Concrete Factory 1: `WindowsFactory` returns `WindowsButton` and `WindowsCheckbox`.\\n* Concrete Factory 2: `MacFactory` returns `MacButton` and `MacCheckbox`.\\n\\nBy passing a `MacFactory` to the client, you guarantee the client will exclusively receive Mac-themed UI components. It prevents the fatal error of rendering a Windows button inside a Mac dialogue box.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is the Abstract Factory Pattern, and how does it relate to managing \\"families\\" of dependent objects? Provide an OS UI example.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v9-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Factory Pattern and Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nDIP dictates that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions.\\n\\nWithout a Factory, a high-level `App` module depends directly on low-level `MySQLDatabase`. \\nWith a Factory, the `App` asks an `IDatabaseFactory` for an `IDatabaseConnection`. The `App` only knows about abstract interfaces. The low-level implementations (`MySQLDatabase`, `MongoDatabase`) implement those interfaces. The dependency flow is inverted: both high-level and low-level modules now point toward the abstractions, completely isolating the high-level policy from low-level details.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Factory Pattern inherently enforce the Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) in application architecture?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v9-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Real-World Use Cases for Factory Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Database Connection Pooling:** A generic `DatabaseService` requests a connection. Based on environment variables or tenant IDs, a `ConnectionFactory` generates either a PostgreSQL connection string object or an SQLite connection object for local testing.\\n2. **Notification Dispatchers:** An event-driven architecture triggers an alert. A `NotificationFactory` looks at the user's preferences and instantiates the correct strategy object (`SMSNotifier`, `EmailNotifier`, `PushNotifier`), ensuring the main event bus logic is completely isolated from third-party vendor SDK integrations.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Identify and explain two common real-world backend scenarios where the Factory Method pattern is strictly required.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Flaw of the `new` Keyword\n* Instantiating objects directly inside high-level business logic (`Burger b = new PremiumBurger();`) tightly couples the client to concrete implementations.\n* **The Goal:** Separate *object creation* logic from *core business* logic. The client should request an object without caring how it is instantiated.\n\n### 💡 2. Factory Design Pattern Tiers\n* **Simple Factory:** Not a formal "Gang of Four" pattern, but a principle. A single centralized class (e.g., `BurgerFactory`) contains an `if-else` or `switch` statement returning different object types.\n* **Factory Method Pattern:** Defines an interface for creating an object, but lets subclasses decide which class to instantiate. (e.g., `AbstractBurgerFactory` subclassed by `VegBurgerFactory` and `MeatBurgerFactory`).\n* **Abstract Factory Pattern:** Provides an interface for creating *families* of related or dependent objects without specifying their concrete classes (e.g., A `KingBurgerFactory` that builds both `WheatBurger` AND `WheatGarlicBread`).\n\n### 💡 3. Enforcing Dependency Inversion\n* The Factory pattern is the ultimate tool for achieving the Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP). High-level modules no longer depend on low-level modules; both depend on the Factory's abstract interfaces.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	Factory Design Pattern	Abstract object creation logic away from business logic. Master Simple Factory, Factory Method, and Abstract Factory implementations for scalable architectures.	32	Hard	Low Level Design	23	[{"id": "lld-v9-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: The Problem with the \\"new\\" Keyword", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nUsing the `new` keyword hardcodes a dependency on a concrete class. \\nIf a `CheckoutService` contains `IPayment p = new CreditCardPayment();`, it is permanently fused to `CreditCardPayment`. \\n\\n1. **Breaks OCP:** If you want to introduce `UPIPayment`, you must edit `CheckoutService` to add conditional logic.\\n2. **Pollutes Business Logic:** Constructing complex objects (fetching DB credentials, setting up HTTP clients) clutters the core domain logic.\\n3. **Breaks Testability:** You cannot easily inject a `MockPayment` during unit testing if the service hardcodes the instantiation.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is embedding object creation logic (using the \\"new\\" keyword) directly inside business logic considered an architectural anti-pattern?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v9-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Simple Factory vs Factory Method", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Simple Factory:** A single class with a method like `createVehicle(String type)` that uses a massive `switch` statement to return `new Car()`, `new Bike()`, etc.\\n* *Violation:* It violates OCP because every time a new vehicle type is added, you must physically open the `SimpleFactory` class and modify the `switch` statement.\\n\\n**Factory Method:** Defines an abstract `Creator` interface with a pure virtual `createVehicle()` method. You implement specific creators: `CarCreator : public Creator` and `BikeCreator : public Creator`.\\n* *OCP Compliant:* To add a `Truck`, you simply create a `TruckCreator` class without touching existing code. Subclasses decide which concrete product to instantiate.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Differentiate between a \\"Simple Factory\\" and the formal \\"Factory Method\\" design pattern. Why does a Simple Factory technically violate the Open/Closed Principle?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v9-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: The Abstract Factory Pattern for Families of Objects", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Abstract Factory** goes beyond creating a single product; it creates whole *suites* of related or dependent objects that must be used together to prevent compatibility mismatches.\\n\\n**OS UI Example:**\\nSuppose you are building a cross-platform UI. You need Buttons and Checkboxes.\\n* Abstract Factory: `IGUIFactory` (methods: `createButton()`, `createCheckbox()`)\\n* Concrete Factory 1: `WindowsFactory` returns `WindowsButton` and `WindowsCheckbox`.\\n* Concrete Factory 2: `MacFactory` returns `MacButton` and `MacCheckbox`.\\n\\nBy passing a `MacFactory` to the client, you guarantee the client will exclusively receive Mac-themed UI components. It prevents the fatal error of rendering a Windows button inside a Mac dialogue box.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is the Abstract Factory Pattern, and how does it relate to managing \\"families\\" of dependent objects? Provide an OS UI example.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v9-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Factory Pattern and Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nDIP dictates that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions.\\n\\nWithout a Factory, a high-level `App` module depends directly on low-level `MySQLDatabase`. \\nWith a Factory, the `App` asks an `IDatabaseFactory` for an `IDatabaseConnection`. The `App` only knows about abstract interfaces. The low-level implementations (`MySQLDatabase`, `MongoDatabase`) implement those interfaces. The dependency flow is inverted: both high-level and low-level modules now point toward the abstractions, completely isolating the high-level policy from low-level details.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Factory Pattern inherently enforce the Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) in application architecture?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v9-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Real-World Use Cases for Factory Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Database Connection Pooling:** A generic `DatabaseService` requests a connection. Based on environment variables or tenant IDs, a `ConnectionFactory` generates either a PostgreSQL connection string object or an SQLite connection object for local testing.\\n2. **Notification Dispatchers:** An event-driven architecture triggers an alert. A `NotificationFactory` looks at the user's preferences and instantiates the correct strategy object (`SMSNotifier`, `EmailNotifier`, `PushNotifier`), ensuring the main event bus logic is completely isolated from third-party vendor SDK integrations.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Identify and explain two common real-world backend scenarios where the Factory Method pattern is strictly required.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
