57	2D Grid & Nested Loop Mastery: Symmetry, Space-Star Formulas & Distance Matrices	grid-nested-loop-patterns-symmetry-distance	Master the 4-step coordinate rule for patterns: row counts, column formulas, symmetry breakpoints, and pyramid space-star breakdowns.	2026-10-02 17:05:20.017597+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: 2D Grid & Nested Loop Mastery: Symmetry, Space-Star Formulas & Distance Matrices\nPart of the **Problem Solving: DSA & SQL** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tNm_NNSB3_w", "title": "Lecture 5: Master Nested Loops & Pattern Problems in C++ (22 Patterns Blueprint)", "duration": "55m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/pascals-triangle/", "title": "Pascal's Triangle", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "118", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Generate 2D coordinate grid where each cell is computed from the sum of adjacent cells directly above."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/inverted-triangle-of-stars0110/1", "title": "Inverted Triangle of Stars", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "problemId": "gfg-inv-tri", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Formulate spaces and star counts as decreasing mathematical functions of current row index i."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/triangle-pattern-1661718013/1", "title": "Half Diamond Star Pattern", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "problemId": "gfg-half-diamond", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Implement horizontal symmetry inversion breakpoint at row index N."}]	Module 2: Nested Loop Mechanics & Coordinate Patterns	### 📌 Topic Overview\nSolving star and number pattern problems is the best way to master nested `for` loops. Almost every matrix, grid, and dynamic programming problem uses the same row-and-column loop logic practiced here.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The 4-Step Rule for Any Pattern:**\n  1. **Count the Total Rows:** Set your outer loop (`i`) to run for the total number of lines.\n  2. **Relate Columns to the Current Row:** Figure out how many items are printed in row `i` and set that as your inner loop (`j`) condition.\n  3. **Print Inside the Inner Loop:** Print the character or number inside the `j` loop, then print a newline (`\\n`) right after the `j` loop finishes.\n  4. **Spot Symmetry:** If a pattern grows and then shrinks (like a diamond), split the logic at the middle row (`N`) and reverse the formula for the bottom half.\n- **Splitting Rows into `[Spaces + Stars + Spaces]`:** For pyramid patterns, use separate inner loops inside the same row: first print `(N - i - 1)` spaces, then print `(2*i + 1)` stars.\n- **Character Patterns:** Characters can be incremented just like numbers using ASCII values (for example, `'A' + 1` gives `'B'`, and `'A' + 2` gives `'C'`).\n\n### 💻 Pyramid Pattern Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid printPyramid(int n) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        // 1. Leading spaces: n - i - 1\n        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) cout << " ";\n        // 2. Stars: 2*i + 1\n        for (int j = 0; j < 2 * i + 1; j++) cout << "*";\n        // 3. Newline after finishing the row\n        cout << "\n";\n    }\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
11583	Min-Heap K-Way Zipper: Merge K Sorted Linked Lists	dsa-merge-k-sorted-linked-lists-min-heap	Master Min-Heap K-Way Zipper: Merge K Sorted Linked Lists with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:12:24.294669+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Min-Heap K-Way Zipper: Merge K Sorted Linked Lists\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1zktEppsdig", "title": "Min-Heap K-Way Zipper: Merge K Sorted Linked Lists - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-k-sorted-lists/", "title": "LeetCode 23: Merge k Sorted Lists", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "You are given an array of k linked-lists lists, each linked-list is sorted in ascending order. Merge all the linked-lists into one sorted linked-list and return it."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/merge-k-sorted-linked-lists/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Merge K sorted linked lists", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Merge K sorted linked lists of different sizes into a single sorted linked list in-place."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/kth-smallest-element-in-a-sorted-matrix/", "title": "LeetCode 378: Kth Smallest Element in a Sorted Matrix", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use the exact same K-way Min-Heap frontier expansion across sorted rows of a matrix."}]	Module 38: Doubly Linked List Medium Problems & Hard Linked List Re-Wiring (K-Group Reversal, Rotation & Multi-Level Flattening)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array `lists` containing the heads of `K` sorted singly linked lists, merge all `K` lists into a **single sorted linked list** and return its head.\n\n### 💡 Progression of 3 Approaches (Brute → Better → Optimal)\n- **1. Brute Force Array Extraction (`O(M log M)` Time, `O(M)` Space, where `M = N * K`):**\n  - Collect all node values from all `K` lists into a vector, sort it, and build a brand-new linked list. Wastes both time and `O(N * K)` memory!\n- **2. Better Sequential Pairwise Merging (`O(N * K * (K + 1) / 2)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Merge `list[0]` and `list[1]`, then merge that result with `list[2]`, up to `list[K - 1]`. Repeatedly scanning the growing prefix list degrades time to `O(N * K²)`.\n- **3. Optimal Min-Heap Priority Queue (`O(K log K + N * K * log K)` Time, `O(K)` Space):**\n  - Use a **Min-Heap** storing **`pair<int, Node*>`** (`{node->data, node}`) so the smallest value across the `K` active list heads is always at `pq.top()`.\n  - **Step 1:** Push `{list[i]->data, list[i]}` for all non-empty heads (`i` from `0` to `K - 1`).\n  - **Step 2:** Create a `dummyNode = new Node(-1)` and `temp = dummyNode`.\n  - **Step 3:** While `!pq.empty()`:\n    - Pop `{val, currNode} = pq.top()`.\n    - Attach `temp->next = currNode;` and advance `temp = temp->next;`.\n    - If **`currNode->next != nullptr`**, push **`{currNode->next->data, currNode->next}`** into the min-heap!\n  - Return `dummyNode->next`.\n\n### 💻 Merge K Sorted Lists (Min-Heap) Blueprint\n```cpp\nNode* mergeKLists(vector<Node*> &listArray) {\n    // Min-heap storing {node->data, node}\n    priority_queue<pair<int, Node*>,\n                   vector<pair<int, Node*>>,\n                   greater<pair<int, Node*>>> pq;\n\n    // 1. Push the head of each non-empty list into the Min-Heap (O(K log K))\n    for (int i = 0; i < listArray.size(); i++) {\n        if (listArray[i] != nullptr) {\n            pq.push({listArray[i]->data, listArray[i]});\n        }\n    }\n\n    Node* dummyNode = new Node(-1);\n    Node* temp = dummyNode;\n\n    // 2. Extract minimum node and push its next neighbor into the heap\n    while (!pq.empty()) {\n        auto it = pq.top();\n        pq.pop();\n\n        Node* currNode = it.second;\n        if (currNode->next != nullptr) {\n            pq.push({currNode->next->data, currNode->next});\n        }\n\n        temp->next = currNode;\n        temp = temp->next;\n    }\n\n    Node* mergedHead = dummyNode->next;\n    delete dummyNode;\n    return mergedHead;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Pushing Null Heads Into the Priority Queue:** The input `listArray` can contain empty lists (`nullptr`)! Always guard `if (listArray[i] != nullptr)` before dereferencing `listArray[i]->data`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22095	Lecture 10: Querying Documents — find(), findOne(), Projection, count(), sort(), limit() & skip()	mongo-lecture-10-find-projection-sort-limit-skip-count	Query collections with find() and findOne(), shape output fields using inclusion/exclusion Projections, and chain cursor methods (.count(), .sort(), .limit(), .skip()) for pagination.	2026-10-06 19:11:34.825286+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nReading data in MongoDB relies on **`find(query, projection)`** and **`findOne(query, projection)`**, combined with chainable cursor methods (`.projection()`, `.count()`, `.sort()`, `.limit()`, `.skip()`) to shape, order, and paginate results.\n\n### 💡 Querying & Projection Mechanics\n* **`db.<collection>.find(query)`:** Returns a cursor containing **all** documents matching the query filter. Calling `find()` or `find({})` returns every document in the collection.\n* **`db.<collection>.findOne(query)`:** Returns only the **first** document matching the query filter (or `null` if none match).\n* **Projection (Shaping Output Fields):** Passed as the second argument `find(query, { field: 1 })` or chained via `.projection({ field: 1 })`:\n  * **`1` (Inclusion):** Includes the specified field in the returned documents. All other fields (except `_id`) are automatically hidden.\n  * **`0` (Exclusion):** Excludes the specified field. Note that `_id` is always returned by default unless you explicitly specify `_id: 0`.\n\n### 💡 Cursor Chaining: Count, Sort, Limit & Skip (Pagination)\n* **`.count()`:** Returns the integer count of documents matching the `find()` filter.\n* **`.sort({ field: 1 | -1 })`:** Orders results by `field` in **ascending order (`1`)** (A-Z, smallest to largest) or **descending order (`-1`)** (Z-A, largest to smallest).\n* **`.limit(n)`:** Restricts the result set to at most `n` documents.\n* **`.skip(n)`:** Skips the first `n` documents before returning results. Combining `.limit(pageSize).skip((page - 1) * pageSize)` powers backend API pagination.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical Query, Projection & Pagination Examples\n```javascript\n// 1. Find all BCA students vs. only the first BCA student\ndb.students.find({ class: "BCA" })\ndb.students.findOne({ class: "BCA" })\n\n// 2. Projection: Show ONLY name and class, hiding _id\ndb.students.find(\n  { class: "BCA" },\n  { name: 1, class: 1, _id: 0 }\n)\n// Equivalent chained method:\ndb.students.find({ class: "BCA" }).projection({ name: 1, class: 1, _id: 0 })\n\n// 3. Count matching records\ndb.students.find({ class: "BCA" }).count()\n\n// 4. Sort students by age descending (-1) and project name + age\ndb.students.find({}, { name: 1, age: 1, _id: 0 }).sort({ age: -1 })\n\n// 5. Pagination with limit() and skip() (3 records per page)\ndb.students.find().limit(3).skip(0) // Page 1: Records 1, 2, 3\ndb.students.find().limit(3).skip(3) // Page 2: Records 4, 5, 6\ndb.students.find().limit(3).skip(6) // Page 3: Record 7\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/query-documents/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/query-documents/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 10: Querying Documents"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=THQP1EhvTDk", "title": "MongoDB Find Document Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "25m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[{"id": "mongo-10-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db3771e", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db3771e", "name": "Problem #39: Match inventory not discontinued then project", "title": "Problem #39: Match inventory not discontinued then project", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "39", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: **$match** discontinued false, **$project** sku name quantity.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db3771e"}, {"id": "mongo-10-prob-2", "url": 