69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375a6", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375a6", "name": "Problem #163: Match articles with tag js then project title", "title": "Problem #163: Match articles with tag js then project title", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "163", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: **$match** articles with tag \\"js\\", **$project** title only.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375a6"}, {"id": "mongo-10-prob-10", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db3759a", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db3759a", "name": "Problem #167: Top 5 articles by views", "title": "Problem #167: Top 5 articles by views", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "167", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the 5 articles with highest **views**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db3759a"}, {"id": "mongo-10-prob-11", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db37597", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db37597", "name": "Problem #168: Skip 10 articles limit 15", "title": "Problem #168: Skip 10 articles limit 15", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "168", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return articles, skip 10, limit 15.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db37597"}, {"id": "mongo-10-prob-12", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db374f9", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db374f9", "name": "Problem #220: Project log level and message", "title": "Problem #220: Project log level and message", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "220", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return logs with only **level** and **message**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db374f9"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nReading data in MongoDB relies on **`find(query, projection)`** and **`findOne(query, projection)`**, combined with chainable cursor methods (`.projection()`, `.count()`, `.sort()`, `.limit()`, `.skip()`) to shape, order, and paginate results.\n\n### 💡 Querying & Projection Mechanics\n* **`db.<collection>.find(query)`:** Returns a cursor containing **all** documents matching the query filter. Calling `find()` or `find({})` returns every document in the collection.\n* **`db.<collection>.findOne(query)`:** Returns only the **first** document matching the query filter (or `null` if none match).\n* **Projection (Shaping Output Fields):** Passed as the second argument `find(query, { field: 1 })` or chained via `.projection({ field: 1 })`:\n  * **`1` (Inclusion):** Includes the specified field in the returned documents. All other fields (except `_id`) are automatically hidden.\n  * **`0` (Exclusion):** Excludes the specified field. Note that `_id` is always returned by default unless you explicitly specify `_id: 0`.\n\n### 💡 Cursor Chaining: Count, Sort, Limit & Skip (Pagination)\n* **`.count()`:** Returns the integer count of documents matching the `find()` filter.\n* **`.sort({ field: 1 | -1 })`:** Orders results by `field` in **ascending order (`1`)** (A-Z, smallest to largest) or **descending order (`-1`)** (Z-A, largest to smallest).\n* **`.limit(n)`:** Restricts the result set to at most `n` documents.\n* **`.skip(n)`:** Skips the first `n` documents before returning results. Combining `.limit(pageSize).skip((page - 1) * pageSize)` powers backend API pagination.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical Query, Projection & Pagination Examples\n```javascript\n// 1. Find all BCA students vs. only the first BCA student\ndb.students.find({ class: "BCA" })\ndb.students.findOne({ class: "BCA" })\n\n// 2. Projection: Show ONLY name and class, hiding _id\ndb.students.find(\n  { class: "BCA" },\n  { name: 1, class: 1, _id: 0 }\n)\n// Equivalent chained method:\ndb.students.find({ class: "BCA" }).projection({ name: 1, class: 1, _id: 0 })\n\n// 3. Count matching records\ndb.students.find({ class: "BCA" }).count()\n\n// 4. Sort students by age descending (-1) and project name + age\ndb.students.find({}, { name: 1, age: 1, _id: 0 }).sort({ age: -1 })\n\n// 5. Pagination with limit() and skip() (3 records per page)\ndb.students.find().limit(3).skip(0) // Page 1: Records 1, 2, 3\ndb.students.find().limit(3).skip(3) // Page 2: Records 4, 5, 6\ndb.students.find().limit(3).skip(6) // Page 3: Record 7\n```	Yahoo Baba	https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba	655	Lecture 10: Querying Documents — find(), findOne(), Projection, count(), sort(), limit() & skip()	Query collections with find() and findOne(), shape output fields using inclusion/exclusion Projections, and chain cursor methods (.count(), .sort(), .limit(), .skip()) for pagination.	20	Easy	\N	10	[{"id": "mongo-10-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db3771e", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db3771e", "name": "Problem #39: Match inventory not discontinued then project", "title": "Problem #39: Match inventory not discontinued then project", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "39", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: **$match** discontinued false, **$project** sku name quantity.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db3771e"}, {"id": "mongo-10-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37672", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37672", "name": "Problem #95: Project review productId userId rating", "title": "Problem #95: Project review productId userId rating", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "95", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return reviews with only **productId**, **userId**, **rating**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37672"}, {"id": "mongo-10-prob-3", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3766f", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3766f", "name": "Problem #96: Project inventory sku name quantity", "title": "Problem #96: Project inventory sku name quantity", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "96", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return inventory with only **sku**, **name**, **quantity**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3766f"}, {"id": "mongo-10-prob-4", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3766c", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3766c", "name": "Problem #97: Limit reviews to 25", "title": "Problem #97: Limit reviews to 25", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "97", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return only 25 reviews.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3766c"}, {"id": "mongo-10-prob-5", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37669", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37669", "name": "Problem #98: Limit inventory to 15", "title": "Problem #98: Limit inventory to 15", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "98", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return only 15 inventory documents.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37669"}, {"id": "mongo-10-prob-6", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37666", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37666", "name": "Problem #99: Reviews sorted by helpfulVotes descending", "title": "Problem #99: Reviews sorted by helpfulVotes descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "99", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return reviews sorted by **helpfulVotes** descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37666"}, {"id": "mongo-10-prob-7", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37663", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37663", "name": "Problem #100: Reviews sorted by rating descending", "title": "Problem #100: Reviews sorted by rating descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "100", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return reviews sorted by **rating** descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37663"}, {"id": "mongo-10-prob-8", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37660", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37660", "name": "Problem #101: Inventory sorted by quantity descending", "title": "Problem #101: Inventory sorted by quantity descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "101", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return inventory sorted by **quantity** descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37660"}, {"id": "mongo-10-prob-9", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375a6", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375a6", "name": "Problem #163: Match articles with tag js then project title", "title": "Problem #163: Match articles with tag js then project title", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "163", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: **$match** articles with tag \\"js\\", **$project** title only.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375a6"}, {"id": "mongo-10-prob-10", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db3759a", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db3759a", "name": "Problem #167: Top 5 articles by views", "title": "Problem #167: Top 5 articles by views", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "167", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the 5 articles with highest **views**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db3759a"}, {"id": "mongo-10-prob-11", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db37597", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db37597", "name": "Problem #168: Skip 10 articles limit 15", "title": "Problem #168: Skip 10 articles limit 15", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "168", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return articles, skip 10, limit 15.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db37597"}, {"id": "mongo-10-prob-12", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db374f9", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db374f9", "name": "Problem #220: Project log level and message", "title": "Problem #220: Project log level and message", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "220", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return logs with only **level** and **message**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db374f9"}]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/query-documents/	\N	\N	\N
9	Relational Schema Design & Normalization	schema-design-normalization	Entity relationships (1:1, 1:N, N:M), foreign keys, cascading constraints, and 3NF.	2026-09-27 22:28:22.325889+05:30	### 🎯 Core Concepts & Architecture Breakdown\nRelational Database Management Systems (RDBMS) model data into tables (relations) with strict schemas, foreign keys, and referential integrity constraints.\n\n- **Entity-Relationship Modeling**: Identifying entities, attributes, and relationships:\n  - **1:1 (One-to-One)**: User and User Profile (often combined or separated for privacy/performance).\n  - **1:N (One-to-Many)**: User and Orders (foreign key `user_id` placed on the 'many' table).\n  - **N:M (Many-to-Many)**: Students and Courses (requires a junction/pivot table `student_courses`).\n\n### ⚡ Normalization Forms (1NF, 2NF, 3NF, BCNF)\n1. **1NF (First Normal Form)**: Each column contains atomic (indivisible) values. No repeating groups or comma-separated lists.\n2. **2NF (Second Normal Form)**: Meets 1NF, and all non-key attributes are fully functionally dependent on the entire primary key (eliminates partial dependency in composite keys).\n3. **3NF (Third Normal Form)**: Meets 2NF, and no non-key attribute is transitively dependent on the primary key (no `A -> B -> C`).\n\n### 💡 Placement & Interview Key Takeaways\n1. **3NF vs Denormalization Tradeoff**: Highly normalized schemas eliminate data anomalies and save storage, but require multi-table JOINs. Read-heavy analytics systems frequently denormalize data to optimize query performance.\n2. **Foreign Key Constraints**: Understand `ON DELETE CASCADE`, `ON DELETE SET NULL`, and `ON DELETE RESTRICT`.\n3. **Surrogate Keys vs Natural Keys**: Surrogate keys (auto-incrementing integers or UUIDs) decouple domain logic from row identity.\n\n### ⚠️ Common Traps & Production Gotchas\n- Storing JSON or arrays instead of properly normalized child tables for relational queries: Avoid anti-pattern of storing `tag1,tag2,tag3` in text columns.	[{"url": "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl.html", "title": "PostgreSQL DDL & Schema Architecture"}, {"url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Database_normalization", "title": "Database Normalization Principles: 1NF to 3NF"}, {"url": "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-constraints.html", "title": "PostgreSQL Constraints & Foreign Keys"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UrYLYV7WSHM", "title": "Database Normalization Explained in Simple Terms", "duration": "16m"}, {"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=remG3xZ4g0U", "title": "PostgreSQL Schema Design Best Practices", "duration": "32m"}]	[{"type": "assignment", "title": "Design an E-Commerce Placement Schema in 3NF", "checklist": ["Create products table with DECIMAL(10,2) price and positive CHECK constraint", "Create orders table with status enum ('pending', 'paid', 'shipped')", "Create order_items junction table capturing price_at_purchase to preserve historical integrity", "Add composite index on (order_id, product_id)"], "description": "Write DDL SQL statements creating tables for Users, Categories, Products, Orders, and OrderItems. Enforce primary keys, unique constraints, foreign keys with ON DELETE CASCADE where appropriate, and check constraints (e.g. price > 0).", "starterCodeUrl": "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-sql.html"}, {"type": "quiz", "title": "SQL Practice Challenge: Detect Anomalies in Un-normalized Schema", "checklist": ["Decompose into Students and Colleges tables", "Establish foreign key relationship student.college_id -> college.id"], "description": "Given a flat table containing Student, College, CollegeAddress, and HODName, identify update, insertion, and deletion anomalies, and normalize to 3NF.", "starterCodeUrl": ""}]	\N	\N	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	7	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
1117	Object-Reference Node Modeling: Binary Tree Representation in Java	dsa-binary-tree-representation-java	Model binary tree nodes in Java using class references and instantiate heap-allocated tree structures.	2026-10-03 00:35:47.116905+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Object-Reference Node Modeling: Binary Tree Representation in Java\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hyLyW7rP24I", "title": "Lecture 75: Binary Tree Representation in Java", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/same-tree/", "title": "Same Tree", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Compare two binary tree node structures recursively for structural and value equality."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/size-of-binary-tree/1", "title": "Size of Binary Tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Basic", "description": "Count the total number of nodes in a binary tree."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/sum-of-binary-tree/1", "title": "Sum of Binary Tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Basic", "description": "Compute the sum of all node data values across a binary tree."}]	Module 15: Binary Trees: Hierarchical Representation & Fundamental Traversals	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn Java, Binary Trees are built using object references instead of raw memory pointers. Each `Node` class instance contains a `data` field and two object references (`Node left` and `Node right`) that default to `null`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Java `Node` Class Design:**\n  - `int data`: Stores the key/value of the node.\n  - `Node left, right`: Reference variables pointing to the left and right child objects.\n  - Constructor `public Node(int key)` assigns `data = key` (and `left` and `right` are `null` by default).\n- **Building a Tree in Java:**\n  - Instantiate the root with `Node root = new Node(1);` and link children via dot notation: `root.left = new Node(2);` and `root.right = new Node(3);`.\n\n### 💻 Java Binary Tree Node Blueprint\n```java\nclass Node {\n    int data;\n    Node left;\n    Node right;\n\n    public Node(int key) {\n        this.data = key;\n        this.left = null;\n        this.right = null;\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Node root = new Node(1);\n        root.left = new Node(2);\n        root.right = new Node(3);\n        root.right.left = new Node(5);\n    }\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22237	LLD Project: Spotify / Music Player App	lld-project-spotify-music-player	Architect a Music Player LLD integrating Strategy (Shuffle/Sequential), Adapter (Bluetooth/Wired), Singleton (Managers), and Facade patterns.	2026-10-07 17:12:04.258699+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: LLD Project: Spotify / Music Player App\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/DkLwFqbCsu8", "title": "Build Spotify, Music Player App | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v18-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Integrating the Strategy Pattern for Playback Modes", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nInstead of a monolithic `AudioEngine` determining the next song via `if (mode == SHUFFLE)`, the logic is abstracted into an `IPlaybackStrategy` interface with `getNextSong()`, `getPreviousSong()`, and `hasNext()` methods.\\n\\nConcrete implementations:\\n1. `SequentialStrategy`: Maintains an `index` integer, returning `playlist[index++]`.\\n2. `ShuffleStrategy`: Copies the playlist to a temporary list, picks a `rand() % remaining`, removes it, and pushes it to a History Stack (for previous song capability).\\n3. `CustomQueueStrategy`: Maintains a user-defined `std::queue<Song>`.\\nThe Facade requests the next song via `strategy->getNextSong()`, completely decoupling the core engine from algorithmic sequencing.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the Music Player LLD, explain how the Strategy Pattern is applied to support Sequential, Shuffle, and Custom Queue playback without writing massive switch statements inside the AudioEngine.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v18-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: The Adapter Pattern for Audio Outputs", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `AudioEngine` strictly depends on an abstract `IAudioOutputDevice` interface featuring a `playAudio(Song s)` method.\\n\\nWe build specific Adapters:\\n* `BluetoothAdapter : public IAudioOutputDevice` (internally holds a reference to `BluetoothSpeakerAPI`).\\nWhen `AudioEngine->play()` is called, it executes `outputDevice->playAudio(song)`. The Adapter translates this generic command into `vendorApi->streamViaBluetooth(song.getBytes())`.\\nIf a new Wi-Fi casting protocol is introduced, the `AudioEngine` codebase remains untouched; we simply write a new Adapter.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Different hardware APIs handle sound differently (e.g., BluetoothSpeakerAPI vs AppleAirpodsAPI). How does the Adapter pattern prevent the AudioEngine from becoming tightly coupled to these hardware vendors?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v18-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Playlist Manager Data Structure Optimization", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `PlaylistManager` is the central repository holding thousands of user playlists.\\nIf a `std::vector` was used, when a user clicks \\"Play My Work Playlist\\", the system would have to perform a linear scan $O(N)$ across all playlists to find the matching name.\\n\\nBy using `std::unordered_map<std::string, Playlist>`, the Manager uses the Playlist Name (or unique ID) as the key. Fetching the playlist via `map[\\"My Work Playlist\\"]` resolves in $O(1)$ constant time, providing instant playback performance regardless of how many playlists exist.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why does the PlaylistManager in the Spotify LLD utilize a Hash Map (std::map or std::unordered_map) instead of a Vector to store playlists?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v18-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Interface Segregation in Custom Queues", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf we force `addNextToQueue()` into the base `IPlaybackStrategy` interface, Sequential and Shuffle strategies are forced to implement dummy methods or throw errors, strictly violating ISP.\\n\\n**The Solution:**\\n1. Do not place `addNextToQueue()` in the base interface.\\n2. Keep it specific to the concrete `CustomQueueStrategy` class.\\n3. The Facade or Client handles the specific capability via dynamic typing or dedicated endpoints. When a user clicks \\"Add to Queue\\", the system checks if the current strategy is a `CustomQueueStrategy` (via `dynamic_cast`). If not, it instantiates one, copies the remaining sequential songs into the queue, and seamlessly transitions the active strategy mid-playback without violating interfaces.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "The Custom Queue strategy requires an \\"addNextToQueue(Song)\\" method, but Sequential and Shuffle do not. How do you handle this without violating the Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) on the base Strategy interface?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v18-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: The Role of the Facade in the Music Player App", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWithout the Facade, the Frontend UI code (e.g., React Native buttons) would be forced to orchestrate the backend manually.\\nWhen a user clicks \\"Play Playlist via Bluetooth\\", the UI would have to:\\n1. Query the `DeviceManager` to boot the `BluetoothAdapter`.\\n2. Query the `PlaylistManager` to fetch the songs.\\n3. Instantiate the `ShuffleStrategy`.\\n4. Wire the strategy to the `AudioEngine`.\\n\\nThis creates massive, brittle coupling. By introducing the `MusicPlayerFacade`, the UI simply calls `facade.playAll(\\"Rock Mix\\", SHUFFLE, BLUETOOTH)`. The Facade encapsulates and hides the entire orchestration process.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is the MusicPlayerFacade class necessary? What chaotic coupling would occur in the frontend UI if the Facade was omitted?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. Identifying the Patterns\n* A real-world application like Spotify is never solved by a single design pattern. It requires an orchestrated ecosystem of patterns.\n* **Adapter:** Connect the core `AudioEngine` to diverse output endpoints (`BluetoothSpeakerAPI`, `WiredHeadphonesAPI`).\n* **Strategy:** Swap algorithms for playlist execution (`SequentialPlay`, `RandomShuffle`, `CustomQueue`).\n* **Singleton:** Ensure Single Source of Truth for `PlaylistManager` and `DeviceManager`.\n* **Facade:** A central `MusicPlayerApp` shielding the frontend UI from backend complexity.\n\n### 💡 2. Bottom-Up Architectural Flow\n* **Models:** Start with atomic entities: `Song` (name, artist, filepath).\n* **Collections:** Build `Playlist` containing a `std::vector<Song>`.\n* **Managers:** Build `PlaylistManager` holding a `Map<String, Playlist>` for $O(1)$ lookups.\n* **Playback Logic:** The `AudioEngine` maintains the `currentSong` state and executes `play()` via the provided Adapter.\n\n### 💡 3. The Orchestration\n* The Client calls `app.playAll(playlistName, SHUFFLE)` on the Facade.\n* The Facade requests the `Playlist` from the `PlaylistManager`.\n* The Facade dynamically instantiates the `RandomShuffle` Strategy and passes the Playlist to it.\n* A `while (strategy.hasNext())` loop extracts the next song and pushes it to the `AudioEngine` over the connected Adapter.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	LLD Project: Spotify / Music Player App	Architect a Music Player LLD integrating Strategy (Shuffle/Sequential), Adapter (Bluetooth/Wired), Singleton (Managers), and Facade patterns.	75	Hard	Low Level Design	32	[{"id": "lld-v18-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Integrating the Strategy Pattern for Playback Modes", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nInstead of a monolithic `AudioEngine` determining the next song via `if (mode == SHUFFLE)`, the logic is abstracted into an `IPlaybackStrategy` interface with `getNextSong()`, `getPreviousSong()`, and `hasNext()` methods.\\n\\nConcrete implementations:\\n1. `SequentialStrategy`: Maintains an `index` integer, returning `playlist[index++]`.\\n2. `ShuffleStrategy`: Copies the playlist to a temporary list, picks a `rand() % remaining`, removes it, and pushes it to a History Stack (for previous song capability).\\n3. `CustomQueueStrategy`: Maintains a user-defined `std::queue<Song>`.\\nThe Facade requests the next song via `strategy->getNextSong()`, completely decoupling the core engine from algorithmic sequencing.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the Music Player LLD, explain how the Strategy Pattern is applied to support Sequential, Shuffle, and Custom Queue playback without writing massive switch statements inside the AudioEngine.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v18-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: The Adapter Pattern for Audio Outputs", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `AudioEngine` strictly depends on an abstract `IAudioOutputDevice` interface featuring a `playAudio(Song s)` method.\\n\\nWe build specific Adapters:\\n* `BluetoothAdapter : public IAudioOutputDevice` (internally holds a reference to `BluetoothSpeakerAPI`).\\nWhen `AudioEngine->play()` is called, it executes `outputDevice->playAudio(song)`. The Adapter translates this generic command into `vendorApi->streamViaBluetooth(song.getBytes())`.\\nIf a new Wi-Fi casting protocol is introduced, the `AudioEngine` codebase remains untouched; we simply write a new Adapter.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Different hardware APIs handle sound differently (e.g., BluetoothSpeakerAPI vs AppleAirpodsAPI). How does the Adapter pattern prevent the AudioEngine from becoming tightly coupled to these hardware vendors?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v18-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Playlist Manager Data Structure Optimization", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `PlaylistManager` is the central repository holding thousands of user playlists.\\nIf a `std::vector` was used, when a user clicks \\"Play My Work Playlist\\", the system would have to perform a linear scan $O(N)$ across all playlists to find the matching name.\\n\\nBy using `std::unordered_map<std::string, Playlist>`, the Manager uses the Playlist Name (or unique ID) as the key. Fetching the playlist via `map[\\"My Work Playlist\\"]` resolves in $O(1)$ constant time, providing instant playback performance regardless of how many playlists exist.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why does the PlaylistManager in the Spotify LLD utilize a Hash Map (std::map or std::unordered_map) instead of a Vector to store playlists?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v18-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Interface Segregation in Custom Queues", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf we force `addNextToQueue()` into the base `IPlaybackStrategy` interface, Sequential and Shuffle strategies are forced to implement dummy methods or throw errors, strictly violating ISP.\\n\\n**The Solution:**\\n1. Do not place `addNextToQueue()` in the base interface.\\n2. Keep it specific to the concrete `CustomQueueStrategy` class.\\n3. The Facade or Client handles the specific capability via dynamic typing or dedicated endpoints. When a user clicks \\"Add to Queue\\", the system checks if the current strategy is a `CustomQueueStrategy` (via `dynamic_cast`). If not, it instantiates one, copies the remaining sequential songs into the queue, and seamlessly transitions the active strategy mid-playback without violating interfaces.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "The Custom Queue strategy requires an \\"addNextToQueue(Song)\\" method, but Sequential and Shuffle do not. How do you handle this without violating the Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) on the base Strategy interface?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v18-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: The Role of the Facade in the Music Player App", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWithout the Facade, the Frontend UI code (e.g., React Native buttons) would be forced to orchestrate the backend manually.\\nWhen a user clicks \\"Play Playlist via Bluetooth\\", the UI would have to:\\n1. Query the `DeviceManager` to boot the `BluetoothAdapter`.\\n2. Query the `PlaylistManager` to fetch the songs.\\n3. Instantiate the `ShuffleStrategy`.\\n4. Wire the strategy to the `AudioEngine`.\\n\\nThis creates massive, brittle coupling. By introducing the `MusicPlayerFacade`, the UI simply calls `facade.playAll(\\"Rock Mix\\", SHUFFLE, BLUETOOTH)`. The Facade encapsulates and hides the entire orchestration process.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is the MusicPlayerFacade class necessary? What chaotic coupling would occur in the frontend UI if the Facade was omitted?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22096	Lecture 11: Comparison Query Operators — $eq, $ne, $gt, $gte, $lt, $lte, $in & $nin	mongo-lecture-11-comparison-query-operators	Write expressive filter queries across find(), updateMany(), and deleteMany() using MongoDB comparison operators: $eq, $ne, $gt, $gte, $lt, $lte, $in, and $nin.	2026-10-06 19:11:34.825286+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMongoDB **Comparison Query Operators** allow you to filter documents by numeric ranges, inequalities, and set memberships inside `find()`, `updateOne()` / `updateMany()`, and `deleteOne()` / `deleteMany()`.\n\n### 💡 All 8 Comparison Operators\nEvery comparison operator is placed inside a nested object attached to the target field: `{ <field>: { <$operator>: <value> } }`.\n* **`$eq` (Equal To):** Matches values strictly equal to the target value (`{ age: { $eq: 20 } }` is equivalent to `{ age: 20 }`).\n* **`$ne` (Not Equal To):** Matches all documents where the field value does NOT equal the target value (`{ class: { $ne: "BCA" } }`).\n* **`$gt` (Greater Than):** Matches numeric or date values strictly greater than the target (`{ age: { $gt: 20 } }` -> 21, 22, 23...).\n* **`$gte` (Greater Than or Equal To):** Matches values greater than or equal to the target (`{ age: { $gte: 20 } }` -> 20, 21, 22...).\n* **`$lt` (Less Than):** Matches values strictly less than the target (`{ age: { $lt: 20 } }` -> 19, 18...).\n* **`$lte` (Less Than or Equal To):** Matches values less than or equal to the target (`{ age: { $lte: 20 } }` -> 20, 19...).\n* **`$in` (In Array):** Matches documents where the field value equals **any** value in the provided array (`{ class: { $in: ["BCA", "BSc" } }` — cleaner alternative to multiple OR conditions).\n* **`$nin` (Not In Array):** Matches documents where the field value does **not** match any value in the array (`{ age: { $nin: [20, 24 } }`).\n\n### 🛠️ Practical Comparison Queries in find(), updateMany() & deleteMany()\n```javascript\n// 1. Range & Inequality Queries in find()\ndb.students.find({ age: { $gt: 20 } })\ndb.students.find({ age: { $lte: 20 } })\ndb.students.find({ class: { $ne: "BCA" } })\n\n// 2. Matching Multiple Values with $in and $nin\ndb.students.find({ age: { $in: [20, 24 } })\ndb.students.find({ class: { $in: ["BCA", "BSc" } })\ndb.students.find({ class: { $nin: ["BCA", "BSc" } })\n\n// 3. Using Comparison Operators inside updateMany()\n// Promote all students younger than 20 to class "12th"\ndb.students.updateMany(\n  { age: { $lt: 20 } },\n  { $set: { class: "12th" } }\n)\n\n// 4. Using Comparison Operators inside deleteMany()\n// Delete all students younger than 20\ndb.students.deleteMany({ age: { $lt: 20 } })\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/query-comparison/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/query-comparison/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 11: Comparison Query Operators"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3HHyQOV-9LU", "title": "MongoDB Comparison Query Operators Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "16m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[{"id": "mongo-11-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db3771b", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db3771b", "name": "Problem #40: Reviews with rating lt 3", "title": "Problem #40: Reviews with rating lt 3", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "40", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return reviews where rating < 3.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db3771b"}, {"id": "mongo-11-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db37718", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db37718", "name": "Problem #41: Reviews with rating gte 4", "title": "Problem #41: Reviews with rating gte 4", "platform": "MongoPrac