s where **views** < 500.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375a3"}, {"id": "mongo-11-prob-11", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375a0", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375a0", "name": "Problem #165: Find articles with views gt 2000", "title": "Problem #165: Find articles with views gt 2000", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "165", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return articles where **views** > 2000.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375a0"}, {"id": "mongo-11-prob-12", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db3759d", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db3759d", "name": "Problem #166: Find logs with level in info warn", "title": "Problem #166: Find logs with level in info warn", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "166", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return logs where **level** is info or warn.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db3759d"}]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/query-comparison/	\N	\N	\N
1953	Bottom-Up Tree Modification: Children Sum Property in a Binary Tree	dsa-children-sum-property-binary-tree	Convert a binary tree in-place to satisfy the children sum property in O(N) time by pushing values downward and summing them back up.	2026-10-03 01:18:07.147265+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Bottom-Up Tree Modification: Children Sum Property in a Binary Tree\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fnmisPM6cVo", "title": "Lecture 101: Check for Children Sum Property in a Binary Tree", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/children-sum-parent/1", "title": "Check for Children Sum Property in a Binary Tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Verify whether every non-leaf node satisfies node->data == left->data + right->data."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/root-equals-sum-of-children/", "title": "Root Equals Sum of Children", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Check the base 3-node children sum identity."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/flatten-binary-tree-to-linked-list/", "title": "Flatten Binary Tree to Linked List", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Practice in-place pointer updates across binary tree nodes."}]	Module 18: Binary Trees: Node Distances, Tree Modifications & Reconstruction	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn a binary tree, the **Children Sum Property** states that for every non-leaf node, its value must equal the sum of its left and right children: `node->val == (node->left ? node->left->val : 0) + (node->right ? node->right->val : 0)`. You are allowed only to increment node values by any amount (`val += 1` as many times as needed), without modifying tree structure.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Top-Down Forward Push:**\n  - Before recurring down, compare `node->val` with `childSum = (left ? left->val : 0) + (right ? right->val : 0)`.\n  - If `node->val > childSum`, update both children to match `node->val` (`if (node->left) node->left->val = node->val; if (node->right) node->right->val = node->val;`). This guarantees children will never fall short when summing back up!\n  - If `childSum >= node->val`, update `node->val = childSum`.\n- **The Bottom-Up Backtracking Sum:**\n  - Recursively process `reorder(node->left)` and `reorder(node->right)`.\n  - On the way back up, set `node->val` to the exact sum of its updated children.\n- **Complexity:** `O(N)` Time (single traversal) and `O(H)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Children Sum Conversion Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid changeTree(TreeNode* root) {\n    if (root == nullptr) return;\n\n    int childSum = 0;\n    if (root->left) childSum += root->left->val;\n    if (root->right) childSum += root->right->val;\n\n    // Push parent value downward if children sum is smaller\n    if (childSum >= root->val) {\n        root->val = childSum;\n    } else {\n        if (root->left) root->left->val = root->val;\n        if (root->right) root->right->val = root->val;\n    }\n\n    changeTree(root->left);\n    changeTree(root->right);\n\n    // Sum back up on return\n    int total = 0;\n    if (root->left) total += root->left->val;\n    if (root->right) total += root->right->val;\n    if (root->left || root->right) root->val = total;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22244	Bridge Design Pattern	lld-bridge-design-pattern	Decouple abstractions from their implementations so both can vary independently. Flatten m*n class explosions into m+n scalable components.	2026-10-07 17:16:35.515424+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Bridge Design Pattern\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/KVf8dwgTbiM", "title": "Bridge Pattern | Real-world use case + Code | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v25-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Solving the Class Explosion Problem", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWhen using pure inheritance to combine two independent orthogonal traits (e.g., 4 Shapes and 3 Colors), you must hardcode every permutation as a unique subclass ($4 \\\\times 3 = 12$ classes like `RedCircle`, `BlueSquare`).\\n\\nThe Bridge Pattern splits the traits into two independent class hierarchies. You author 4 `Shape` classes and 3 `IColor` classes ($4 + 3 = 7$ classes). The `Shape` base class holds a pointer to `IColor` (Composition). At runtime, you combine them via instantiation: `new Circle(new Red())`. The permutation matrix is flattened into two parallel hierarchies.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the mathematical transition from $O(M \\\\times N)$ classes to $O(M + N)$ classes achieved by the Bridge Pattern.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v25-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Abstraction vs Implementation (In Bridge Terms)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Confusion:** Junior developers assume \\"Abstraction\\" means an abstract class/interface and \\"Implementation\\" means a concrete subclass. This is incorrect in the context of the Bridge pattern.\\n\\n**The Bridge Meaning:**\\n* **Abstraction:** The *High-Level Policy or Control Layer* (e.g., The GUI Window, the Remote Control, the Car). It delegates raw work.\\n* **Implementation:** The *Low-Level Mechanics or Platform Layer* (e.g., The Operating System API, the TV Hardware, the Engine). It performs the raw, granular work.\\nThe pattern bridges the high-level policy to the low-level platform execution, allowing both layers to evolve independently.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the formal definition of the Bridge Pattern, what do the terms \\"Abstraction\\" and \\"Implementation\\" specifically refer to, and why are they confusing?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v25-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Bridge Pattern vs Strategy Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Strategy Pattern (Algorithmic Substitution):** The intent is to swap out *how* a specific behavior is calculated dynamically at runtime (e.g., swapping MergeSort for QuickSort). The \\"Context\\" (Client) typically remains a single class; only the strategy hierarchy varies.\\n* **Bridge Pattern (Structural Independence):** The intent is to allow *both* the high-level control layer and the low-level execution layer to vary through deep subclassing. You have a massive hierarchy on the left (`Shapes`: Circle, Square) bridging to a massive hierarchy on the right (`RenderAPI`: OpenGL, DirectX). It is a structural scaffolding pattern, not a runtime algorithm-swapping pattern.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Structurally, the Bridge Pattern and Strategy Pattern look nearly identical (Composition + Interface Delegation). What is the fundamental difference in their design intent?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v25-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Cross-Platform UI Development via Bridge", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nGUI frameworks must render buttons on Windows, macOS, and Linux, which have totally incompatible underlying graphics APIs.\\n\\n1. **Abstraction (High-Level):** The framework defines a `Button` class containing generic logic (`onClick`, dimensions, text).\\n2. **Implementation (Low-Level):** Defines an `IWidgetAPI` interface. Concrete classes: `WindowsWidgetAPI`, `MacWidgetAPI`.\\n3. **The Bridge:** The `Button` delegates rendering by bridging to the API: `widgetApi->drawButton(x, y)`.\\nThis prevents creating massive `WindowsButton`, `MacButton`, `LinuxButton` class explosions for every single UI element.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How is the Bridge Pattern heavily utilized in cross-platform GUI frameworks (like Java AWT or React Native) to render a Button component?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v25-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Refining the Abstraction Layer", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe \\"Refined Abstraction\\" is a specialized subclass of the main Abstraction hierarchy (e.g., `Sedan` inheriting from `Car`, or `AdvancedRemoteControl` inheriting from `RemoteControl`).\\n\\nThe refined abstraction takes the granular, primitive methods provided by the Implementation layer and combines them into complex, user-friendly routines. \\n*Example:* An `AdvancedRemoteControl` (Refined Abstraction) might implement a `mute()` method. It achieves this by bridging to the `TVHardwareAPI` (Implementation) and calling `setVolume(0)`. The abstraction layer handles the domain logic; the implementation layer handles the raw execution.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the Bridge Pattern, what is a \\"Refined Abstraction\\", and how does it utilize the Bridge to execute complex operations?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The M * N Class Explosion\n* If you have 3 Car Types (Sedan, SUV, Hatchback) and 3 Engine Types (Petrol, Diesel, EV), tying them via pure inheritance forces you to create $3 \\times 3 = 9$ distinct subclasses (`SedanPetrol`, `SuvEV`, etc.).\n* Adding 1 new Engine type (e.g., Hybrid) instantly requires authoring 3 new subclasses.\n\n### 💡 2. The Bridge Pattern Solution\n* **Definition:** Decouples an abstraction from its implementation so that the two can vary independently.\n* **Separation of Concerns:** \n  * The **Abstraction (High-Level)**: The `Car` hierarchy (`Sedan`, `SUV`).\n  * The **Implementation (Low-Level)**: The `IEngine` hierarchy (`PetrolEngine`, `EVEngine`).\n* The `Car` holds a pointer/reference to the `IEngine` interface (Composition). The `Car` bridges across to the `Engine` to execute operations like `start()`.\n\n### 💡 3. The Math: $M \\times N \\rightarrow M + N$\n* Instead of creating 9 subclasses, you create 3 Car classes and 3 Engine classes (Total = 6 classes).\n* Adding the `HybridEngine` requires exactly **1 new class**. It can immediately be injected into any Car dynamically at runtime via dependency injection.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	Bridge Design Pattern	Decouple abstractions from their implementations so both can vary independently. Flatten m*n class explosions into m+n scalable components.	31	Hard	Low Level Design	39	[{"id": "lld-v25-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Solving the Class Explosion Problem", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWhen using pure inheritance to combine two independent orthogonal traits (e.g., 4 Shapes and 3 Colors), you must hardcode every permutation as a unique subclass ($4 \\\\times 3 = 12$ classes like `RedCircle`, `BlueSquare`).\\n\\nThe Bridge Pattern splits the traits into two independent class hierarchies. You author 4 `Shape` classes and 3 `IColor` classes ($4 + 3 = 7$ classes). The `Shape` base class holds a pointer to `IColor` (Composition). At runtime, you combine them via instantiation: `new Circle(new Red())`. The permutation matrix is flattened into two parallel hierarchies.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the mathematical transition from $O(M \\\\times N)$ classes to $O(M + N)$ classes achieved by the Bridge Pattern.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v25-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Abstraction vs Implementation (In Bridge Terms)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Confusion:** Junior developers assume \\"Abstraction\\" means an abstract class/interface and \\"Implementation\\" means a concrete subclass. This is incorrect in the context of the Bridge pattern.\\n\\n**The Bridge Meaning:**\\n* **Abstraction:** The *High-Level Policy or Control Layer* (e.g., The GUI Window, the Remote Control, the Car). It delegates raw work.\\n* **Implementation:** The *Low-Level Mechanics or Platform Layer* (e.g., The Operating System API, the TV Hardware, the Engine). It performs the raw, granular work.\\nThe pattern bridges the high-level policy to the low-level platform execution, allowing both layers to evolve independently.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the formal definition of the Bridge Pattern, what do the terms \\"Abstraction\\" and \\"Implementation\\" specifically refer to, and why are they confusing?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v25-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Bridge Pattern vs Strategy Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Strategy Pattern (Algorithmic Substitution):** The intent is to swap out *how* a specific behavior is calculated dynamically at runtime (e.g., swapping MergeSort for QuickSort). The \\"Context\\" (Client) typically remains a single class; only the strategy hierarchy varies.\\n* **Bridge Pattern (Structural Independence):** The intent is to allow *both* the high-level control layer and the low-level execution layer to vary through deep subclassing. You have a massive hierarchy on the left (`Shapes`: Circle, Square) bridging to a massive hierarchy on the right (`RenderAPI`: OpenGL, DirectX). It is a structural scaffolding pattern, not a runtime algorithm-swapping pattern.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Structurally, the Bridge Pattern and Strategy Pattern look nearly identical (Composition + Interface Delegation). What is the fundamental difference in their design intent?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v25-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Cross-Platform UI Development via Bridge", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nGUI frameworks must render buttons on Windows, macOS, and Linux, which have totally incompatible underlying graphics APIs.\\n\\n1. **Abstraction (High-Level):** The framework defines a `Button` class containing generic logic (`onClick`, dimensions, text).\\n2. **Implementation (Low-Level):** Defines an `IWidgetAPI` interface. Concrete classes: `WindowsWidgetAPI`, `MacWidgetAPI`.\\n3. **The Bridge:** The `Button` delegates rendering by bridging to the API: `widgetApi->drawButton(x, y)`.\\nThis prevents creating massive `WindowsButton`, `MacButton`, `LinuxButton` class explosions for every single UI element.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How is the Bridge Pattern heavily utilized in cross-platform GUI frameworks (like Java AWT or React Native) to render a Button component?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v25-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Refining the Abstraction Layer", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe \\"Refined Abstraction\\" is a specialized subclass of the main Abstraction hierarchy (e.g., `Sedan` inheriting from `Car`, or `AdvancedRemoteControl` inheriting from `RemoteControl`).\\n\\nThe refined abstraction takes the granular, primitive methods provided by the Implementation layer and combines them into complex, user-friendly routines. \\n*Example:* An `AdvancedRemoteControl` (Refined Abstraction) might implement a `mute()` method. It achieves this by bridging to the `TVHardwareAPI` (Implementation) and calling `setVolume(0)`. The abstraction layer handles the domain logic; the implementation layer handles the raw execution.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the Bridge Pattern, what is a \\"Refined Abstraction\\", and how does it utilize the Bridge to execute complex operations?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
11	Connection Pooling & ACID Transactions	connection-pooling-transactions	Configuring pg.Pool, BEGIN/COMMIT/ROLLBACK, isolation levels, and deadlock prevention.	2026-09-27 22:28:22.331714+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Connection Pooling & ACID Transactions\nPart of the **PostgreSQL & Database Engineering** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Connection%20Pooling%20%26%20ACID%20Transactions%20tutorial", "title": "Connection Pooling & ACID Transactions Full Guide", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"type": "assignment", "title": "Build & Test a Working Implementation of Connection Pooling & ACID Transactions", "checklist": ["Implement core logic with validation and boundary checks", "Write tests verifying expected output and edge cases"], "description": "Implement a production-grade module demonstrating the core principles of Connection Pooling & ACID Transactions.", "starterCodeUrl": ""}]	\N	\N	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	7	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
447	Multi-Candidate Voting Elimination: Majority Element II (> N/3 Occurrences)	dsa-majority-element-ii-extended-moore-voting	Find all elements appearing strictly more than N/3 times using Extended Moore's Voting Algorithm with 2 candidate slots and an essential verification pass.	2026-10-02 23:12:19.366926+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Multi-Candidate Voting Elimination: Majority Element II (> N/3 Occurrences)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vwZj1K0e9U8", "title": "Lecture 35: Majority Elements (> N/3 times) — Find the elements that appears more than N/3 times in the array", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/majority-element/", "title": "Majority Element", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Contrast the N/3 algorithm with the single-candidate N/2 baseline."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sort-characters-by-frequency/", "title": "Sort Characters By Frequency", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use frequency mapping and bucket sorting to group elements by occurrence counts."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/majority-vote/1", "title": "Majority Vote", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find all elements appearing more than N/3 times in an array."}]	Module 11: Advanced Array Patterns & Hard Interview Problems	### 📌 Topic Overview\nFind all elements that appear **strictly more than `floor(N / 3)` times** in an array of size `N`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Mathematical Bound:** In any array of size `N`, there can be **at most 2 elements** that appear more than `N / 3` times (since `3 * (floor(N / 3) + 1) > N`).\n- **Extended Moore's Voting Algorithm (O(N) Time, O(1) Space):**\n  - Maintain two candidate variables (`el1`, `el2`) and two counters (`cnt1 = 0`, `cnt2 = 0`).\n  - Scan the array:\n    1. If `cnt1 == 0 && arr[i] != el2`: Assign `el1 = arr[i]`, `cnt1 = 1`.\n    2. Else if `cnt2 == 0 && arr[i] != el1`: Assign `el2 = arr[i]`, `cnt2 = 1`.\n    3. Else if `arr[i] == el1`: Increment `cnt1++`.\n    4. Else if `arr[i] == el2`: Increment `cnt2++`.\n    5. Else: A distinct third number is seen—decrement **both** `cnt1--` and `cnt2--`!\n- **Mandatory Verification Pass:**\n  - Candidates found are not guaranteed to have frequency `> N / 3`.\n  - Count actual occurrences of `el1` and `el2` in a second pass before adding them to the result.\n\n### 💻 Extended Moore's Voting Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> majorityElementTwo(vector<int> &nums) {\n    int cnt1 = 0, cnt2 = 0, el1 = INT_MIN, el2 = INT_MIN;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        if (cnt1 == 0 && x != el2) { el1 = x; cnt1 = 1; }\n        else if (cnt2 == 0 && x != el1) { el2 = x; cnt2 = 1; }\n        else if (x == el1) cnt1++;\n        else if (x == el2) cnt2++;\n        else { cnt1--; cnt2--; }\n    }\n    vector<int> ans;\n    int c1 = 0, c2 = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        if (x == el1) c1++;\n        if (x == el2) c2++;\n    }\n    int mini = nums.size() / 3 + 1;\n    if (c1 >= mini) ans.push_back(el1);\n    if (c2 >= mini) ans.push_back(el2);\n    return ans;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22249	Flyweight Design Pattern	lld-flyweight-design-pattern	Conserve RAM by sharing a large number of fine-grained objects efficiently. Separate immutable Intrinsic state from mutable Extrinsic state.	2026-10-07 22:38:19.438928+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Flyweight Design Pattern\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/vNSRcegCO8E", "title": "Flyweight Design Pattern | UML + Code | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v30-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Intrinsic vs Extrinsic State", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Intrinsic State:** The internal, immutable data that can be safely shared across thousands of instances. In a word processor, the intrinsic state is the `CharType` (the graphical glyph matrix for the letter 'A', its font family, and base font size). There are only ~26 of these.\\n2. **Extrinsic State:** The external, highly volatile data unique to a specific instance that cannot be shared. In a word processor, this is the exact $(x, y)$ screen coordinate where that specific letter 'A' is drawn on page 4, paragraph 2. \\nThe Flyweight pattern stores the Intrinsic state in RAM once, and passes the Extrinsic state in as parameters during rendering.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the Flyweight Pattern, clearly define the difference between Intrinsic and Extrinsic state using the example of a word processor rendering 100,000 characters on screen.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v30-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Immutability Requirement in Flyweights", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA single Flyweight object is referenced by thousands of Context objects simultaneously. For example, 50,000 Tree Contexts all point to a single `PineTreeFlyweight` containing the green texture.\\n\\nIf the Flyweight object exposed a public setter like `setTexture(\\"Brown\\")`, changing it for *one* dying tree would instantly mutate the texture for all 50,000 trees across the entire map. By making the Flyweight strictly immutable (setting its state exclusively through the constructor and providing zero setters), it guarantees thread-safe sharing without catastrophic side effects.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is it architecturally critical that a Flyweight object (the Intrinsic state holder) be strictly immutable once instantiated by the Factory?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v30-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: The Role of the Flyweight Factory", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `FlyweightFactory` guarantees that identical Flyweights are never instantiated twice.\\nIt maintains an internal cache, typically a Hash Map (`std::unordered_map<std::string, Flyweight*>`).\\n\\nWhen a client requests an object (`factory.getFlyweight(\\"Oak_Green\\")`), the factory generates a composite string key. It checks the HashMap. If the key exists, it returns the existing pointer in $O(1)$ time. Only if the key does not exist does it instantiate `new OakTree()`, store it in the map, and then return the pointer. This converts millions of instantiation requests into a few mapped references.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Flyweight Factory prevent redundant object creation? Explain the underlying data structure used for caching.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v30-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Flyweight vs Singleton", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Singleton:** Restricts instantiation of a class to **exactly one** globally unique instance. It is used to govern a central resource (e.g., a Database Connection Pool or a global Logger). There is only one state.\\n* **Flyweight:** A factory that manages a **pool of many different shared instances**. Its goal is purely RAM optimization through structural deduplication. You don't have one tree; you have a cached pool of specific tree types (Pine, Oak, Birch), and you share those specific instances to minimize the memory footprint of fine-grained, repetitive elements.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Both Flyweight and Singleton limit object instantiation and share objects globally. Contrast their architectural goals and cardinality constraints.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v30-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Memory Reduction Mathematics", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Without Flyweight:**\\nEach asteroid is a monolithic object: `1MB + 16B` $\\\\approx 1\\\\text{MB}$.\\n1,000,000 asteroids $\\\\times 1\\\\text{MB}$ = **~1,000,000 MB (1 Terabyte of RAM)**. The app crashes instantly.\\n\\n**With Flyweight:**\\n1. **Intrinsic Pool:** We create exactly 3 Flyweight objects (the 3 distinct meshes). $3 \\\\times 1\\\\text{MB}$ = **3 MB**.\\n2. **Extrinsic Contexts:** We create 1,000,000 Context objects holding $(x, y)$ plus an 8-byte pointer to the Flyweight ($16\\\\text{B} + 8\\\\text{B} = 24\\\\text{B}$). \\n1,000,000 $\\\\times 24$ Bytes = **~24 MB**.\\n*Total RAM:* $3\\\\text{MB} + 24\\\\text{MB}$ = **27 MB**.\\nBy extracting the 1MB Intrinsic property, we reduced RAM consumption from 1,000 GB to 27 MB.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "If an Asteroid object consists of a 1MB 3D Mesh and 16 Bytes of (x, y) coordinates, mathematically prove the RAM savings of using Flyweight to render 1,000,000 asteroids of 3 distinct types.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The RAM Exhaustion Problem\n* Imagine designing an Asteroid shooter game or rendering a forest with 1,000,000 trees. \n* If every single tree object holds arrays of integers and strings for 3D meshes, leaf textures, coordinates $(x, y)$, and health, instantiating 1,000,000 unique objects will consume gigabytes of RAM and crash the application.\n\n### 💡 2. The Flyweight Solution: State Splitting\n* The Flyweight pattern prevents memory exhaustion by dividing object properties into two categories:\n  1. **Intrinsic State:** Properties that are constant, shared, and immutable across many objects (e.g., the 3D mesh model, the green leaf texture data).\n  2. **Extrinsic State:** Properties that are unique to each individual object and vary constantly (e.g., its specific $(x, y)$ coordinate on the map, its current velocity).\n\n### 💡 3. Caching and Injection\n* **The Flyweight Object:** Holds *only* the Intrinsic state. Instead of 1,000,000 meshes, you only create 3 unique mesh objects (e.g., Pine, Oak, Birch) and cache them in a Flyweight Factory.\n* **The Context Object:** Holds the Extrinsic state $(x, y)$ and a pointer to the shared Flyweight. \n* To render, the system loops through the 1,000,000 lightweight Context objects and passes their unique $(x, y)$ coordinates as parameters into the shared Flyweight's `render(x, y)` method.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	Flyweight Design Pattern	Conserve RAM by sharing a large number of fine-grained objects efficiently. Separate immutable Intrinsic state from mutable Extrinsic state.	55	Hard	Low Level Design	44	[{"id": "lld-v30-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Intrinsic vs Extrinsic State", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Intrinsic State:** The internal, immutable data that can be safely shared across thousands of instances. In a word processor, the intrinsic state is the `CharType` (the graphical glyph matrix for the letter 'A', its font family, and base font size). There are only ~26 of these.\\n2. **Extrinsic State:** The external, highly volatile data unique to a specific instance that cannot be shared. In a word processor, this is the exact $(x, y)$ screen coordinate where that specific letter 'A' is drawn on page 4, paragraph 2. \\nThe Flyweight pattern stores the Intrinsic state in RAM once, and passes the Extrinsic state in as parameters during rendering.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the Flyweight Pattern, clearly define the difference between Intrinsic and Extrinsic state using the example of a word processor rendering 100,000 characters on screen.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v30-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Immutability Requirement in Flyweights", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA single Flyweight object is referenced by thousands of Context objects simultaneously. For example, 50,000 Tree Contexts all point to a single `PineTreeFlyweight` containing the green texture.\\n\\nIf the Flyweight object exposed a public setter like `setTexture(\\"Brown\\")`, changing it for *one* dying tree would instantly mutate the texture for all 50,000 trees across the entire map. By making the Flyweight strictly immutable (setting its state exclusively through the constructor and providing zero setters), it guarantees thread-safe sharing without catastrophic side effects.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is it architecturally critical that a Flyweight object (the Intrinsic state holder) be strictly immutable once instantiated by the Factory?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v30-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: The Role of the Flyweight Factory", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `FlyweightFactory` guarantees that identical Flyweights are never instantiated twice.\\nIt maintains an internal cache, typically a Hash Map (`std::unordered_map<std::string, Flyweight*>`).\\n\\nWhen a client requests an object (`factory.getFlyweight(\\"Oak_Green\\")`), the factory generates a composite string key. It checks the HashMap. If the key exists, it returns the existing pointer in $O(1)$ time. Only if the key does not exist does it instantiate `new OakTree()`, store it in the map, and then return the pointer. This converts millions of instantiation requests into a few mapped references.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Flyweight Factory prevent redundant object creation? Explain the underlying data structure used for caching.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v30-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Flyweight vs Singleton", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Singleton:** Restricts instantiation of a class to **exactly one** globally unique instance. It is used to govern a central resource (e.g., a Database Connection Pool or a global Logger). There is only one state.\\n* **Flyweight:** A factory that manages a **pool of many different shared instances**. Its goal is purely RAM optimization through structural deduplication. You don't have one tree; you have a cached pool of specific tree types (Pine, Oak, Birch), and you share those specific instances to minimize the memory footprint of fine-grained, repetitive elements.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Both Flyweight and Singleton limit object instantiation and share objects globally. Contrast their architectural goals and cardinality constraints.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v30-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Memory Reduction Mathematics", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Without Flyweight:**\\nEach asteroid is a monolithic object: `1MB + 16B` $\\\\approx 1\\\\text{MB}$.\\n1,000,000 asteroids $\\\\times 1\\\\text{MB}$ = **~1,000,000 MB (1 Terabyte of RAM)**. The app crashes instantly.\\n\\n**With Flyweight:**\\n1. **Intrinsic Pool:** We create exactly 3 Flyweight objects (the 3 distinct meshes). $3 \\\\times 1\\\\text{MB}$ = **3 MB**.\\n2. **Extrinsic Contexts:** We create 1,000,000 Context objects holding $(x, y)$ plus an 8-byte pointer to the Flyweight ($16\\\\text{B} + 8\\\\text{B} = 24\\\\text{B}$). \\n1,000,000 $\\\\times 24$ Bytes = **~24 MB**.\\n*Total RAM:* $3\\\\text{MB} + 24\\\\text{MB}$ = **27 MB**.\\nBy extracting the 1MB Intrinsic property, we reduced RAM consumption from 1,000 GB to 27 MB.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "If an Asteroid object consists of a 1MB 3D Mesh and 16 Bytes of (x, y) coordinates, mathematically prove the RAM savings of using Flyweight to render 1,000,000 asteroids of 3 distinct types.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
4715	Stop-Prioritized Queue Relaxation: Cheapest Flights Within K Stops	dsa-cheapest-flights-within-k-stops	Find the cheapest flight price from source to destination constrained by at most K intermediate stops using queue-based BFS.	2026-10-03 11:07:15.292233+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Stop-Prioritized Queue Relaxation: Cheapest Flights Within K Stops\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9XybHVqTHcQ", "title": "Lecture 163: Cheapest Flights Within K Stops", "duration": "24m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/cheapest-flights-within-k-stops/", "title": "Cheapest Flights Within K Stops (LC 787)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the cheapest price from src to dst with at most k stops, or -1 if no such route exists."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-cost-to-reach-destination-in-time/", "title": "Minimum Cost to Reach Destination in Time (LC 1928)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find the minimum passing fee to reach city n - 1 within maxTime minutes using constrained Dijkstra."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/cheapest-flights-within-k-stops/1", "title": "Cheapest Flights Within K Stops", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the cheapest flight cost from source to destination using at most K intermediate stops."}]	Module 24: Shortest Path Algorithms in Graphs: DAG Relaxation, BFS & Dijkstra's Algorithm	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven `n` cities and weighted directed flights `[from, to, price]`, find the **cheapest price** from `src` to `dst` with **at most `K` intermediate stops** (or `-1` if impossible).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Prioritizing by Cost (`dist`) Fails Here:**\n  - Suppose path A reaches city `X` with a cheap cost of `5` but uses `3` stops, while path B reaches city `X` with a slightly higher cost of `7` but uses only `1` stop.\n  - If we prioritize by minimum cost first, path A overwrites `dist[X] = 5`, blocking path B (`7 > 5`). Later, path A runs out of allowed stops (`> K`) before reaching `dst`, whereas path B could have reached `dst` within `K` stops!\n- **The Solution: Prioritize by `stops` Using a Plain Queue (`{stops, {node, cost}}`):**\n  - Because `stops` increases by `+1` at every step, a plain FIFO `queue` processes states in strictly increasing order of `stops` (`0` stops, then `1` stop, ..., up to `K` stops)!\n  - If `stops > K`, `continue` without expanding further.\n- **Complexity:** `O(E)` Time (where `E = flights.size()`) and `O(V + E)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Cheapest Flights Within K Stops Blueprint\n```cpp\nint findCheapestPrice(int n, vector<vector<int>> &flights, int src, int dst, int K) {\n    vector<pair<int, int>> adj[n];\n    for (const auto &f : flights) {\n        adj[f[0]].push_back({f[1], f[2]});\n    }\n\n    // Plain FIFO queue ordered naturally by stops: {stops, {node, cost}}\n    queue<pair<int, pair<int, int>>> q;\n    q.push({0, {src, 0}});\n    vector<int> dist(n, 1e9);\n    dist[src] = 0;\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        auto [stops, state] = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        int u = state.first, cost = state.second;\n\n        if (stops > K) continue;\n\n        for (auto [v, edgePrice] : adj[u]) {\n            if (cost + edgePrice < dist[v] && stops <= K) {\n                dist[v] = cost + edgePrice;\n                q.push({stops + 1, {v, cost + edgePrice}});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    return (dist[dst] == 1e9) ? -1 : dist[dst];\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Using `dist[u]` Instead of `cost` in `cost + edgePrice < dist[v]`:** Because multiple paths with different stop counts can update `dist[u]` while earlier states are still in the queue, always add `edgePrice` to the popped state's `cost`, **not** `dist[u]`!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
7670	Generalized K-Transaction State Compression: Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock IV	dsa-best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-iv-k-transactions	Generalize transaction caps to K transactions using a 2*K transaction index state to achieve O(N * K) time and O(K) space.	2026-10-03 11:39:06.549616+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Generalized K-Transaction State Compression: Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock IV\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IV1dHbk5CDc", "title": "Lecture 218: Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock IV (DP 38)", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-iv/", "title": "Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock IV (LC 188)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find the maximum profit you can achieve when you may complete at most k transactions."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/maximum-profit4657/1", "title": "Maximum Profit (At Most K Transactions)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Compute the maximum profit achievable by buying and selling a share at most K times."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-iii/", "title": "Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock III (LC 123)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Verify that setting k = 2 in Stock IV solves Stock III identically."}]	Module 32: Dynamic Programming on Stocks: State Machines, Transaction Caps, Cooldowns & Fees	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an integer `K` and an array `prices` of length `N`, find the maximum profit you can achieve by completing **at most `K` transactions**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **1. Direct Generalization of Stock III (`cap` up to `K`):**\n  - Replace `cap = 2` from Stock III with `cap = K`!\n- **2. The `2 * K` Transaction-Number (`tranNo`) Optimization (`O(2 * K)` Space):**\n  - Since `K` transactions consist of **`2 * K` alternating actions**:\n    - `tranNo = 0` (Buy 1), `1` (Sell 1), `2` (Buy 2), `3` (Sell 2), ..., `2*K - 1` (Sell K).\n  - Whenever **`tranNo % 2 == 0`**, we are in a **Buy** state:\n    `cur[tranNo] = max(-prices[ind] + after[tranNo + 1], 0 + after[tranNo])`\n  - Whenever **`tranNo % 2 != 0`**, we are in a **Sell** state:\n    `cur[tranNo] = max(+prices[ind] + after[tranNo + 1], 0 + after[tranNo])`\n  - Base case: `tranNo == 2 * K` is `0`.\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * 2 * K)` Time and **`O(2 * K)` Space** (two 1D vectors of size `2 * K + 1`).\n\n### 💻 Buy and Sell Stock IV (1D `2*K` Transaction State) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint maxProfitStockIV(int k, vector<int> &prices) {\n    int n = prices.size();\n    vector<int> after(2 * k + 1, 0), cur(2 * k + 1, 0);\n\n    for (int ind = n - 1; ind >= 0; ind--) {\n        for (int tranNo = 2 * k - 1; tranNo >= 0; tranNo--) {\n            if (tranNo % 2 == 0) {\n                // Even transaction number -> ALLOWED TO BUY\n                cur[tranNo] = max(-prices[ind] + after[tranNo + 1],\n                                   0 + after[tranNo]);\n            } else {\n                // Odd transaction number -> ALLOWED TO SELL\n                cur[tranNo] = max(prices[ind] + after[tranNo + 1],\n                                  0 + after[tranNo]);\n            }\n        }\n        after = cur;\n    }\n    return after[0];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Allocating `vector<int> after(2 * k, 0)` Instead of `2 * k + 1`:** When `tranNo == 2 * k - 1` (the final sell), the transition reads `after[tranNo + 1] = after[2 * k]`. Always allocate size **`2 * k + 1`** so `after[2 * k] == 0` is safely in bounds!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
