.com/problems/combine-two-tables/"}, {"id": "pg-26-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "name": "Customers Who Never Order", "title": "Customers Who Never Order", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "26_7", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Customers Who Never Order\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/"}, {"id": "pg-26-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "name": "Duplicate Emails", "title": "Duplicate Emails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "26_8", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Duplicate Emails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/"}, {"id": "pg-26-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "name": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "title": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "26_9", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Employees Earning More Than Their Managers\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/"}, {"id": "pg-26-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "name": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "title": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "26_10", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Delete Duplicate Emails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhether you are migrating a database to a production cloud server, taking a snapshot before a risky migration, or sharing a complete project database with teammates, you need a reliable **Backup & Restore** workflow.\n\n### 🏗️ The 3 Anatomical Phases of a PostgreSQL Backup\nWhen PostgreSQL exports a database (via `pg_dump` or pgAdmin 4), it structures the backup in three sequential stages so constraints never fail during restoration:\n\n1. **Pre-Data:** Empty table definitions (`CREATE TABLE`), types, and schemas—no rows or foreign keys yet.\n2. **Data:** The actual row records (`COPY` / `INSERT` statements).\n3. **Post-Data:** Constraints, Primary Keys, Foreign Keys, Indexes, Triggers, and Rules applied *after* all data is safely loaded.\n\n### 💾 Step-by-Step: Creating a Full Backup in pgAdmin 4\n\n1. Right-click your target database (e.g., `college_management`) -> Select **Backup...**.\n2. **Filename:** Click the folder icon, choose your destination folder, and name your file ending in `.sql` (e.g., `college_management_backup.sql`).\n3. **Format:**\n   * **Plain:** Generates a human-readable, portable `.sql` script containing all Pre-data, Data, and Post-data commands.\n   * **Custom / Tar / Directory:** Generates compressed binary archives intended for `pg_restore`.\n4. Click **Backup** and verify the process notification reports **Finished / Successfully completed**.\n\n### 🔄 Step-by-Step: Restoring a Backup into a New Database\n\n1. Right-click **Databases** -> **Create** -> **Database...** -> Enter a new database name (e.g., `thapa_college_management`) and click **Save**.\n2. Right-click the newly created database -> Select **Restore...** (or open the Query Tool for Plain SQL scripts).\n3. Set **Format** to match your backup format (e.g., **Plain** or **Custom**), switch the file picker filter to **All Files** / **`.sql`**, select your backup file, and click **Restore**.\n4. Right-click **Tables** under `Schemas -> public` and click **Refresh** to see all restored tables, rows, and constraints.	Thapa Techni