756	Binary Search on Answer Space: Floor Square Root of an Integer in O(log N) Time	dsa-square-root-of-number-binary-search	Find the floor square root of an integer in O(log N) time using binary search on the answer range [1, N] with opposite polarity convergence.	2026-10-02 23:53:37.092171+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Binary Search on Answer Space: Floor Square Root of an Integer in O(log N) Time\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bsv3FPUX_BA", "title": "Lecture 54: Finding Sqrt of a number using Binary Search", "duration": "24m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/valid-perfect-square/", "title": "Valid Perfect Square", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Use binary search on the answer range [1, num] to check if num is a perfect square without built-in sqrt."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sum-of-square-numbers/", "title": "Sum of Square Numbers", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Determine whether two integers a and b exist such that a^2 + b^2 = c."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/count-squares3649/1", "title": "Count Squares", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Basic", "description": "Count the number of perfect squares strictly less than a given integer N."}]	Module 13: Binary Search on Answer Space & Monotonic Predicate Functions	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a positive integer `n`, find the **floor of its square root** (the largest integer `x` such that `x * x <= n`) in `O(log2 N)` time without using built-in `sqrt()`. This problem introduces one of the most important patterns in interviews: **Binary Search on Answers**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **When to Use "Binary Search on Answers":**\n  - Whenever a question asks for the **minimum** or **maximum** possible integer that satisfies a condition, and you know the **range `[low, high]`** where the answer must lie.\n  - Notice the monotonic pattern for `n = 28`: `1, 2, 3, 4, 5` are **possible** (`x * x <= 28`), while `6, 7, 8...` are **not possible** (`x * x > 28`).\n- **Optimal Binary Search (O(log2 N) Time, O(1) Space):**\n  - Set `low = 1` and `high = n`.\n  - Check `mid = low + (high - low) / 2`:\n    - If `(long long)mid * mid <= n`: `mid` is a possible answer, but we want the **largest** possible value, so eliminate the left half (`low = mid + 1`).\n    - Otherwise (`mid * mid > n`): `mid` is too large, so eliminate the right half (`high = mid - 1`).\n  - **Opposite Polarity Property:** `low` starts in the *possible* zone and ends at the first *impossible* value, while `high` starts in the *impossible* zone and ends at the **last possible value**—so `high` holds the exact answer!\n\n### 💻 Floor Square Root Blueprint\n```cpp\nlong long floorSqrt(long long n) {\n    long long low = 1, high = n;\n    while (low <= high) {\n        long long mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        long long val = mid * mid;\n        if (val <= n) {\n            low = mid + 1;  // Possible answer; try to find a larger valid integer\n        } else {\n            high = mid - 1; // Exceeded n; move left to smaller numbers\n        }\n    }\n    return high; // Ends at the largest valid integer\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
4215	Unit-Weight BFS Relaxation: Shortest Path in an Undirected Graph With Unit Weights	dsa-shortest-path-in-undirected-graph-unit-weights	Compute single-source shortest paths in undirected graphs with unit weights using queue-based BFS in O(V + 2E) time.	2026-10-03 11:01:59.90372+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Unit-Weight BFS Relaxation: Shortest Path in an Undirected Graph With Unit Weights\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=C4gxoTaI71U", "title": "Lecture 153: Shortest Path in Undirected Graph with Unit Weights", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/shortest-path-in-undirected-graph-having-unit-distance/1", "title": "Shortest Path in Undirected Graph", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the shortest distance from a given source vertex to all other vertices in an undirected unit-weight graph."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/shortest-path-in-binary-matrix/", "title": "Shortest Path in Binary Matrix (LC 1091)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use unit-weight BFS to find the shortest 8-directional clear path from top-left to bottom-right."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/shortest-path-with-alternating-colors/", "title": "Shortest Path with Alternating Colors (LC 1129)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Apply unit-weight BFS with edge-color state tracking to compute shortest alternating paths."}]	Module 24: Shortest Path Algorithms in Graphs: DAG Relaxation, BFS & Dijkstra's Algorithm	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an **Undirected Graph** with `N` vertices and `M` edges where **every edge has a unit weight of `1`**, find the shortest path distance from a given `src` vertex to every vertex in the graph (or `-1` if unreachable).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Standard BFS Guarantees Shortest Paths for Unit Weights:**\n  - In a graph where every edge costs `1`, a FIFO Queue (`queue<int>`) naturally explores vertices in strictly non-decreasing order of distance (`dist = 0`, then `dist = 1`, then `dist = 2`, etc.).\n  - Therefore, we do not need a Priority Queue or sorting—a plain `queue<int>` achieves optimal **`O(V + 2E)`** time!\n- **Algorithm Steps (`O(V + 2E)` Time, `O(V)` Space):**\n  1. Build the undirected adjacency list `adj`.\n  2. Initialize `dist[N]` with `1e9`, set `dist[src] = 0`, and push `src` into `queue<int> q`.\n  3. While `!q.empty()`:\n     - Pop `u = q.front()`.\n     - For each neighbor `v` in `adj[u]`:\n       - If **`dist[u] + 1 < dist[v]`**: update `dist[v] = dist[u] + 1` and push `v` into `q`.\n  4. Convert any remaining `1e9` values in `dist` to `-1`.\n\n### 💻 Shortest Path in Unit-Weight Undirected Graph Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> shortestPathUnitWeights(vector<vector<int>> &edges, int N, int M, int src) {\n    vector<vector<int>> adj(N);\n    for (const auto &e : edges) {\n        adj[e[0]].push_back(e[1]);\n        adj[e[1]].push_back(e[0]);\n    }\n\n    vector<int> dist(N, 1e9);\n    dist[src] = 0;\n    queue<int> q;\n    q.push(src);\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int u = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n\n        for (int v : adj[u]) {\n            if (dist[u] + 1 < dist[v]) {\n                dist[v] = dist[u] + 1;\n                q.push(v);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {\n        if (dist[i] == 1e9) dist[i] = -1;\n    }\n    return dist;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Hardcoding `src = 0`:** Always read the problem signature carefully—many unit-weight shortest path problems pass an arbitrary `src` parameter rather than starting at vertex `0`!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
306	Maximum Subarray Sum & Subarray Reconstruction: Kadane's Algorithm	dsa-kadanes-algorithm-maximum-subarray-sum	Solve the maximum contiguous subarray sum in O(N) time using Kadane's Algorithm and track start/end indices to reconstruct the subarray.	2026-10-02 22:33:52.484647+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Maximum Subarray Sum & Subarray Reconstruction: Kadane's Algorithm\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AHZpyENo7k4", "title": "Lecture 24: Kadane's Algorithm — Maximum Subarray Sum in an Array", "duration": "32m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-sum-circular-subarray/", "title": "Maximum Sum Circular Subarray", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Apply Kadane's Algorithm to find the maximum subarray sum in a circular array."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-absolute-sum-of-any-subarray/", "title": "Maximum Absolute Sum of Any Subarray", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Track both maximum positive and minimum negative running sums using Kadane's intuition."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/smallest-sum-contiguous-subarray/1", "title": "Smallest sum contiguous subarray", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Invert Kadane's Algorithm to find the contiguous subarray with the minimum sum."}]	Module 9: Medium Array Patterns & Classic Named Algorithms	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array with both positive and negative numbers, how do we find the contiguous subarray with the **largest sum**? Checking all subarrays takes `O(N²)` time, whereas **Kadane's Algorithm** solves it in a single **O(N) pass** and **O(1) space**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Core Intuition of Kadane's Algorithm:**\n  - Carry a running `sum` as you move from left to right.\n  - Even if you hit a negative number, keep carrying `sum` forward **as long as `sum` remains positive**, because a positive sum still adds value to future elements.\n  - The moment `sum` drops below zero (`sum < 0`), carrying a negative sum forward will only shrink future subarrays—so **reset `sum = 0`** and start fresh from the next element!\n- **Follow-Up: Printing the Maximum Subarray Indices:**\n  - Whenever `sum == 0`, record `start = i` as the potential start of a new subarray.\n  - Whenever `sum > maxi`, update `maxi = sum` and save `ansStart = start` and `ansEnd = i`.\n\n### 💻 Kadane's Algorithm (With Subarray Index Tracking)\n```cpp\nlong long maxSubarraySum(vector<int> &arr) {\n    long long sum = 0, maxi = LLONG_MIN;\n    int start = 0, ansStart = -1, ansEnd = -1;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {\n        if (sum == 0) start = i; // Potential start of a new subarray\n        sum += arr[i];\n\n        if (sum > maxi) {\n            maxi = sum;\n            ansStart = start;\n            ansEnd = i;\n        }\n        if (sum < 0) sum = 0;    // Drop negative prefix sum\n    }\n    return maxi;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
