7663	LCS Preservation Formula: Minimum Insertions and Deletions to Convert String A to String B	dsa-minimum-insertions-deletions-to-convert-string-a-to-b	Minimize total insertions and deletions to transform string A into string B by preserving their Longest Common Subsequence intact.	2026-10-03 11:39:06.522032+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: LCS Preservation Formula: Minimum Insertions and Deletions to Convert String A to String B\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMnH0jrir0Q", "title": "Lecture 211: Minimum Insertions/Deletions to Convert String (DP 30)", "duration": "15m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-operation-for-two-strings/", "title": "Delete Operation for Two Strings (LC 583)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the minimum number of steps required to make word1 and word2 the same, where in each step you can delete one character in either string."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/minimum-number-of-deletions-and-insertions0209/1", "title": "Minimum number of deletions and insertions", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the minimum number of deletions and insertions required to transform string str1 into str2."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-ascii-delete-sum-for-two-strings/", "title": "Minimum ASCII Delete Sum for Two Strings (LC 712)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Generalize character deletion counts to minimizing the sum of ASCII values of deleted characters."}]	Module 31: Dynamic Programming on Strings: LCS, Substrings & Palindromic Subsequences	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven two strings `s1` (length `N`) and `s2` (length `M`), you are allowed to **delete** any character from `s1` and **insert** any character anywhere into `s1`. Find the **minimum total operations (deletions + insertions)** required to transform `s1` into `s2`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Keep the Longest Common Subsequence Untouched:**\n  - To minimize operations, what is the largest part of `s1` that already appears in the exact same order inside `s2`? Their **Longest Common Subsequence (`LCS(s1, s2)`)**!\n  - Let `k = LCS(s1, s2)`.\n  - **Step 1 (Deletions from `s1`):** Every character in `s1` that is *not* part of the LCS must be deleted:\n    **`Deletions = N - k`**.\n  - **Step 2 (Insertions into `s1`):** To turn the remaining `k`-character LCS into `s2` (length `M`), we must insert all characters of `s2` that are *not* in the LCS:\n    **`Insertions = M - k`**.\n  - **Total Minimum Operations:** **`(N - k) + (M - k) = N + M - 2 * LCS(s1, s2)`**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * M)` Time and `O(M)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Minimum Insertions/Deletions to Convert String Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint longestCommonSubsequence(string &s1, string &s2) {\n    int n = s1.size(), m = s2.size();\n    vector<int> prev(m + 1, 0), cur(m + 1, 0);\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {\n            if (s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]) {\n                cur[j] = 1 + prev[j - 1];\n            } else {\n                cur[j] = max(prev[j], cur[j - 1]);\n            }\n        }\n        prev = cur;\n    }\n    return prev[m];\n}\n\nint canYouMake(string &s1, string &s2) {\n    int n = s1.size(), m = s2.size();\n    int lcsLen = longestCommonSubsequence(s1, s2);\n    int deletions = n - lcsLen;\n    int insertions = m - lcsLen;\n    return deletions + insertions;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Confusing This With Edit Distance (DP 33):** This problem **only** allows Insertions and Deletions (no Replace operation!). Because Replace is not allowed, the problem reduces directly to `N + M - 2 * LCS(s1, s2)`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
7014	Sign-Assignment Partition Reduction: Target Sum	dsa-target-sum-dp-on-subsequences	Map + and - sign assignments directly to partition difference S1 - S2 = target and count valid subsets for target (totSum - target) / 2.	2026-10-03 11:31:13.729392+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Sign-Assignment Partition Reduction: Target Sum\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b3GD8263-PQ", "title": "Lecture 202: Target Sum (DP 21)", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/target-sum/", "title": "Target Sum (LC 494)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the number of different expressions that you can build by adding '+' or '-' before each integer so they evaluate to target."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/target-sum-1626326450/1", "title": "Target Sum", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count the number of ways to assign + and - signs to array elements to achieve the target sum."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/partitions-with-given-difference/1", "title": "Partitions with Given Difference", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Verify how Target Sum maps directly to Count Partitions With Given Difference (S1 - S2 == target)."}]	Module 30: Dynamic Programming on Subsequences, Subset Sums & 0/1 Knapsack	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an integer array `arr` of size `N` and an integer `target`, you must place either a `'+'` or a `'-'` sign in front of every element in `arr`. Count how many different sign assignments evaluate to `target`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Shifting Negative Sums in Memoization Is Suboptimal:**\n  - If you track `(ind, currentSum)` directly, `currentSum` can become negative, requiring an offset table or hash map.\n- **The Exact Reduction to DP 18 (`Count Partitions With Given Difference`):**\n  - Group all elements assigned `'+'` into subset **`S1`**, and all elements assigned `'-'` into subset **`S2`**.\n  - Then we want **`S1 - S2 == target`**!\n  - Since `S1 + S2 = totSum`, substituting `S1 = totSum - S2` gives:\n    `(totSum - S2) - S2 = target`  ⇒  **`S2 = (totSum - target) / 2`**!\n  - Thus, **Target Sum is 100% identical to counting subsets with sum `k = (totSum - target) / 2`**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * ((totSum - target) / 2))` Time and `O((totSum - target) / 2)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Target Sum Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint findTargetSumWays(vector<int> &nums, int target) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    int totSum = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) totSum += x;\n\n    // (totSum - target) must be non-negative and even!\n    if (totSum - target < 0 || (totSum - target) % 2 != 0) return 0;\n\n    int k = (totSum - target) / 2;\n    vector<int> prev(k + 1, 0), cur(k + 1, 0);\n\n    if (nums[0] == 0) prev[0] = 2;\n    else prev[0] = 1;\n\n    if (nums[0] != 0 && nums[0] <= k) prev[nums[0]] = 1;\n\n    for (int ind = 1; ind < n; ind++) {\n        for (int sum = 0; sum <= k; sum++) {\n            int notTake = prev[sum];\n            int take = 0;\n            if (nums[ind] <= sum) take = prev[sum - nums[ind]];\n            cur[sum] = take + notTake;\n        }\n        prev = cur;\n    }\n    return prev[k];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting When `target` Is Negative (`totSum - target`):** Even if `target` is negative (e.g., `target = -5`), `totSum - target` becomes `totSum + 5`. However, if `abs(target) > totSum`, `totSum - abs(target) < 0`, so checking `totSum - target < 0` and `totSum + target < 0` (or checking `k <= totSum`) keeps bounds safe!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
15087	Element-Contribution Technique & Duplicate Asymmetry (NSE vs PSEE): Sum of Subarray Minimums	dsa-sum-of-subarray-minimums-monotonic-stack	Master Element-Contribution Technique & Duplicate Asymmetry (NSE vs PSEE): Sum of Subarray Minimums with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:30:52.969389+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Element-Contribution Technique & Duplicate Asymmetry (NSE vs PSEE): Sum of Subarray Minimums\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v0e8p9JCgRc", "title": "Element-Contribution Technique & Duplicate Asymmetry (NSE vs PSEE): Sum of Subarray Minimums - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sum-of-subarray-minimums/", "title": "LeetCode 907: Sum of Subarray Minimums", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given an array of integers arr, find the sum of min(b), where b ranges over every (contiguous) subarray of arr, modulo 10^9 + 7."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sum-of-subarray-ranges/", "title": "LeetCode 2104: Sum of Subarray Ranges", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Extend Sum of Subarray Minimums to compute Sum of Subarray Maximums - Sum of Subarray Minimums."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-subarray-min-product/", "title": "LeetCode 1856: Maximum Subarray Min-Product", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use PSE and NSE boundaries for each element to maximize (element * sum of subarray where element is minimum)."}]	Module 43: Monotonic Stack & Contribution Algorithms: Next/Previous Greater/Smaller, Rainwater & Subarray Aggregations	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array `arr` of `N` integers, find the **sum of the minimum elements of all possible contiguous subarrays** of `arr`, modulo `10^9 + 7`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute O(N²) → Optimal Contribution Technique O(5N))\n- **Counting How Many Subarrays Have `arr[i]` as Their Minimum:**\n  - Suppose for index `i`, the **Previous Smaller Element** is at index `pse[i]` (or `-1` if none) and the **Next Smaller Element** is at index `nse[i]` (or `N` if none).\n  - Then `arr[i]` is the minimum element for any subarray that starts in `[pse[i] + 1 ... i]` and ends in `[i ... nse[i] - 1]`.\n  - Number of left choices: **`left = i - pse[i]`**.\n  - Number of right choices: **`right = nse[i] - i`**.\n  - Total subarrays where `arr[i]` is minimum = **`left * right`**, contributing **`(left * right * arr[i]) % MOD`** to the total sum!\n- **The Critical Duplicate Edge Case (NSE vs. PSEE — Why One Side Must Include =!):**\n  - What if `arr = [1, 1]`? If both `PSE` and `NSE` look for *strictly* smaller elements, then for index `0`, `[1, 1]` is counted, AND for index `1`, `[1, 1]` is counted **again**!\n  - **The Fix:** Break ties asymmetrically!\n    - **`findNSE` (Next Smaller Element):** Stop at **strictly smaller** elements (pop `>=`, so equals are included on the right).\n    - **`findPSEE` (Previous Smaller OR EQUAL Element):** Stop at **smaller OR equal** elements (pop only strictly `>`, so equals are **not** extended to the left!).\n\n### 💻 Sum of Subarray Minimums Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> findNSE(const vector<int> &arr) {\n    int n = arr.size();\n    vector<int> nse(n);\n    stack<int> st;\n    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n        // Pop >= so we stop only at a STRICTLY smaller element on the right\n        while (!st.empty() && arr[st.top()] >= arr[i]) {\n            st.pop();\n        }\n        nse[i] = st.empty() ? n : st.top();\n        st.push(i);\n    }\n    return nse;\n}\n\nvector<int> findPSEE(const vector<int> &arr) {\n    int n = arr.size();\n    vector<int> psee(n);\n    stack<int> st;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        // Pop strictly > so we stop at a smaller OR EQUAL element on the left!\n        while (!st.empty() && arr[st.top()] > arr[i]) {\n            st.pop();\n        }\n        psee[i] = st.empty() ? -1 : st.top();\n        st.push(i);\n    }\n    return psee;\n}\n\nint sumSubarrayMins(vector<int> &arr) {\n    int n = arr.size();\n    vector<int> nse = findNSE(arr);\n    vector<int> psee = findPSEE(arr);\n    long long total = 0;\n    long long mod = 1e9 + 7;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        long long left = i - psee[i];\n        long long right = nse[i] - i;\n        total = (total + (left * right % mod) * arr[i] % mod) % mod;\n    }\n    return (int)total;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Using >= in Both findNSE and findPSEE (or > in Both):**\n  - Using `>=` in both **misses** duplicate subarrays like `[1, 1]`.\n  - Using `>` in both **double-counts** duplicate subarrays like `[1, 1]`.\n  - Always use **`>=` in `findNSE`** and **`>` in `findPSEE`**!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
