22227	Strategy Design Pattern	lld-strategy-design-pattern	Solve the Class Explosion problem by favoring Composition over Inheritance. Extract algorithms into interchangeable Strategy classes evaluated dynamically at runtime.	2026-10-07 17:07:23.574728+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Strategy Design Pattern\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/PpKvPrl_gRg", "title": "Strategy Design Pattern Explained with Real-World Example | Design Patterns in LLD", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v8-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: The \\"Class Explosion\\" Problem", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Problem:** When using deep inheritance to model combinations of features, you must create a subclass for every possible permutation. If a vehicle can have 3 engine types and 4 tire types, inheritance requires $3 \\\\times 4 = 12$ distinct subclasses. Adding one new engine type forces the creation of 4 more subclasses, rapidly causing an unmaintainable \\"Class Explosion\\".\\n\\n**The Solution:** The Strategy Pattern flattens the hierarchy using Composition. Instead of inheriting behaviors, the `Vehicle` class \\"has\\" an `IEngineStrategy` and an `ITireStrategy`. You only define the 3 engine classes and 4 tire classes independently ($3 + 4 = 7$ classes). The exact combination is wired together at runtime.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is the \\"Class Explosion\\" problem in object-oriented design, and how does the Strategy Pattern solve it?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v8-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Favor Composition Over Inheritance", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nInheritance (`is-a`) tightly couples the child class to the parent class at compile-time. If the parent's behavior changes, all children break (The Fragile Base Class problem).\\n\\nComposition (`has-a`) treats behaviors as plug-and-play components. By giving a class a pointer to an interface (`IBehavior*`), it delegates work to that component. The Strategy Pattern is the ultimate manifestation of this principle: it rips volatile algorithmic behavior out of the inheritance tree, places it into independent classes, and injects them via composition, achieving total runtime flexibility.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the principle \\"Favor Composition over Inheritance\\" in the context of the Strategy Pattern.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v8-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Applying the Open/Closed Principle (OCP)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWithout the Strategy Pattern, a `SortManager` might contain an `if (type == \\"QUICK\\") ... else if (type == \\"MERGE\\") ...` block. Adding Heap Sort requires opening the `SortManager` class and modifying existing code (violating OCP).\\n\\nWith the Strategy Pattern:\\n1. The `SortManager` only depends on an abstract `ISortStrategy` interface.\\n2. To add Heap Sort, you simply author a brand-new class `HeapSortStrategy : public ISortStrategy`.\\n3. You inject this new class into `SortManager` at runtime.\\nExisting code remains strictly **closed for modification**, while the system is **open for extension**.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Strategy Pattern inherently enforce the Open/Closed Principle (OCP) when a new sorting algorithm needs to be added to an application?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v8-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Payment System Design using Strategy", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **The Strategy Interface:**\\n```cpp\\nclass IPaymentStrategy {\\npublic:\\n    virtual void pay(double amount) = 0;\\n};\\n```\\n2. **Concrete Strategies:**\\n* `class UPIPayment : public IPaymentStrategy { ... void pay(double amount) override { /* UPI API logic */ } }`\\n* `class CreditCardPayment : public IPaymentStrategy { ... }`\\n\\n3. **The Context (Client):**\\n```cpp\\nclass CheckoutProcessor {\\n    IPaymentStrategy* paymentStrategy;\\npublic:\\n    void setPaymentStrategy(IPaymentStrategy* strategy) { paymentStrategy = strategy; }\\n    void processOrder(double total) { \\n        paymentStrategy->pay(total); // Delegated execution\\n    }\\n};\\n```\\nThe `CheckoutProcessor` doesn't know *how* the payment happens; it just delegates to the injected strategy.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In a payment processing system, design the class and interface structure using the Strategy Pattern to support CreditCard, UPI, and NetBanking payments.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v8-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Dependency Injection & Strategy Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nDependency Injection (DI) is the delivery mechanism for the Strategy Pattern. \\n\\nIf the Context class instantiates the strategy itself (`this->strategy = new UPIPayment();`), it remains tightly coupled to a concrete implementation, defeating the purpose of the pattern. \\n\\nThrough Dependency Injection (via the constructor or a setter method), the Context receives an externally created instance of the `IStrategy` interface. This shifts the responsibility of choosing the correct algorithm away from the Context and up to the application's orchestrator or DI container, ensuring the Context remains purely abstracted.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What role does Dependency Injection play in successfully implementing the Strategy Pattern?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Class Explosion Problem\n* **The Trap of Inheritance:** Imagine a `Robot` base class. If robots vary by Flying, Walking, and Talking abilities, using inheritance requires creating a subclass for every combination (`FlyWalkTalkRobot`, `NoFlyWalkTalkRobot`, `FlyNoWalkTalkRobot`, etc.).\n* Adding a single new capability (e.g., `Swimming`) doubles the number of required subclasses, creating an unmanageable **Class Explosion**.\n\n### 💡 2. Favor Composition Over Inheritance\n* Instead of defining *what a robot is* via inheritance ("Is-A"), define *what a robot has* via composition ("Has-A").\n* Extract the volatile behaviors (Fly, Walk, Talk) into independent interfaces (`IFlyStrategy`, `IWalkStrategy`).\n* The `Robot` class holds references to these interfaces.\n\n### 💡 3. The Strategy Pattern Mechanics\n* **Definition:** Defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one, and makes them interchangeable. Strategy lets the algorithm vary independently from clients that use it.\n* **Dynamic Runtime Switching:** Because the robot delegates behavior to its injected strategies, you can swap a `WalkStrategy` with a `RunStrategy` at runtime without altering the `Robot` class codebase.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	Strategy Design Pattern	Solve the Class Explosion problem by favoring Composition over Inheritance. Extract algorithms into interchangeable Strategy classes evaluated dynamically at runtime.	32	Hard	Low Level Design	22	[{"id": "lld-v8-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: The \\"Class Explosion\\" Problem", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Problem:** When using deep inheritance to model combinations of features, you must create a subclass for every possible permutation. If a vehicle can have 3 engine types and 4 tire types, inheritance requires $3 \\\\times 4 = 12$ distinct subclasses. Adding one new engine type forces the creation of 4 more subclasses, rapidly causing an unmaintainable \\"Class Explosion\\".\\n\\n**The Solution:** The Strategy Pattern flattens the hierarchy using Composition. Instead of inheriting behaviors, the `Vehicle` class \\"has\\" an `IEngineStrategy` and an `ITireStrategy`. You only define the 3 engine classes and 4 tire classes independently ($3 + 4 = 7$ classes). The exact combination is wired together at runtime.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is the \\"Class Explosion\\" problem in object-oriented design, and how does the Strategy Pattern solve it?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v8-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Favor Composition Over Inheritance", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nInheritance (`is-a`) tightly couples the child class to the parent class at compile-time. If the parent's behavior changes, all children break (The Fragile Base Class problem).\\n\\nComposition (`has-a`) treats behaviors as plug-and-play components. By giving a class a pointer to an interface (`IBehavior*`), it delegates work to that component. The Strategy Pattern is the ultimate manifestation of this principle: it rips volatile algorithmic behavior out of the inheritance tree, places it into independent classes, and injects them via composition, achieving total runtime flexibility.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the principle \\"Favor Composition over Inheritance\\" in the context of the Strategy Pattern.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v8-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Applying the Open/Closed Principle (OCP)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWithout the Strategy Pattern, a `SortManager` might contain an `if (type == \\"QUICK\\") ... else if (type == \\"MERGE\\") ...` block. Adding Heap Sort requires opening the `SortManager` class and modifying existing code (violating OCP).\\n\\nWith the Strategy Pattern:\\n1. The `SortManager` only depends on an abstract `ISortStrategy` interface.\\n2. To add Heap Sort, you simply author a brand-new class `HeapSortStrategy : public ISortStrategy`.\\n3. You inject this new class into `SortManager` at runtime.\\nExisting code remains strictly **closed for modification**, while the system is **open for extension**.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Strategy Pattern inherently enforce the Open/Closed Principle (OCP) when a new sorting algorithm needs to be added to an application?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v8-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Payment System Design using Strategy", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **The Strategy Interface:**\\n```cpp\\nclass IPaymentStrategy {\\npublic:\\n    virtual void pay(double amount) = 0;\\n};\\n```\\n2. **Concrete Strategies:**\\n* `class UPIPayment : public IPaymentStrategy { ... void pay(double amount) override { /* UPI API logic */ } }`\\n* `class CreditCardPayment : public IPaymentStrategy { ... }`\\n\\n3. **The Context (Client):**\\n```cpp\\nclass CheckoutProcessor {\\n    IPaymentStrategy* paymentStrategy;\\npublic:\\n    void setPaymentStrategy(IPaymentStrategy* strategy) { paymentStrategy = strategy; }\\n    void processOrder(double total) { \\n        paymentStrategy->pay(total); // Delegated execution\\n    }\\n};\\n```\\nThe `CheckoutProcessor` doesn't know *how* the payment happens; it just delegates to the injected strategy.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In a payment processing system, design the class and interface structure using the Strategy Pattern to support CreditCard, UPI, and NetBanking payments.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v8-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Dependency Injection & Strategy Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nDependency Injection (DI) is the delivery mechanism for the Strategy Pattern. \\n\\nIf the Context class instantiates the strategy itself (`this->strategy = new UPIPayment();`), it remains tightly coupled to a concrete implementation, defeating the purpose of the pattern. \\n\\nThrough Dependency Injection (via the constructor or a setter method), the Context receives an externally created instance of the `IStrategy` interface. This shifts the responsibility of choosing the correct algorithm away from the Context and up to the application's orchestrator or DI container, ensuring the Context remains purely abstracted.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What role does Dependency Injection play in successfully implementing the Strategy Pattern?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
12419	Generalized K-Distinct Sliding Window: Longest Substring With At Most K Distinct Characters	dsa-longest-substring-with-at-most-k-distinct-characters	Master Generalized K-Distinct Sliding Window: Longest Substring With At Most K Distinct Characters with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:15:39.084306+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Generalized K-Distinct Sliding Window: Longest Substring With At Most K Distinct Characters\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=teM9ZsVRQyc", "title": "Generalized K-Distinct Sliding Window: Longest Substring With At Most K Distinct Characters - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-substring-with-at-most-k-distinct-characters/", "title": "LeetCode 340: Longest Substring with At Most K Distinct Characters", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given a string s and an integer k, return the length of the longest substring of s that contains at most k distinct characters."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/longest-k-unique-characters-substring0853/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Longest K unique characters substring", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the length of the longest possible substring that has exactly K unique characters (or -1 if none exists)."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/subarrays-with-k-different-integers/", "title": "LeetCode 992: Subarrays with K Different Integers", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Combine At Most K Distinct with Pattern 3 (f(k) - f(k - 1)) to count subarrays with exactly K distinct integers."}]	Module 40: Sliding Window & Two-Pointer Algorithms: Fixed Windows, Variable Windows & Exact-Count Reductions	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a string `s` and an integer `k`, find the length of the **longest substring** that contains **at most `k` distinct characters**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute O(N²) → Better O(2N) → Optimal O(N))\n- **Direct Generalization of Fruit Into Baskets (`2` → `k`):**\n  - Maintain `unordered_map<char, int> mpp` tracking character frequencies inside `[l ... r]`.\n  - **1. Better Approach (`while (mpp.size() > k)`):**\n    - Shrinks `l++` completely until `mpp.size() <= k`. Takes `O(2N)` time.\n  - **2. Optimal Approach (`if (mpp.size() > k)`):**\n    - Shrinks `l++` by **only 1 character** whenever `mpp.size() > k`.\n    - Keeps the sliding window locked at the maximum valid size `maxLen` found so far, running in **pure `O(N)` time**!\n- **Complexity:** **`O(N)`** Time and **`O(min(N, 256))`** Space.\n\n### 💻 Longest Substring With At Most K Distinct Characters Blueprint\n```cpp\nint kDistinctChars(int k, string &s) {\n    int l = 0, r = 0, maxLen = 0;\n    unordered_map<char, int> mpp;\n\n    while (r < s.size()) {\n        mpp[s[r]]++;\n\n        // Optimal O(N) single-step shrink when distinct characters exceed k\n        if (mpp.size() > k) {\n            mpp[s[l]]--;\n            if (mpp[s[l]] == 0) {\n                mpp.erase(s[l]);\n            }\n            l++;\n        }\n\n        if (mpp.size() <= k) {\n            maxLen = max(maxLen, r - l + 1);\n        }\n        r++;\n    }\n    return maxLen;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Confusing "At Most K Distinct" With "Exactly K Distinct" (GFG Variant):** If a problem asks for **exactly `K`** unique characters (returning `-1` if fewer than `K` exist), initialize `maxLen = -1` and change the update condition from `if (mpp.size() <= k)` to **`if (mpp.size() == k)`** (using the `while (mpp.size() > k)` shrink loop)!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22092	Lecture 7: JSON Schema Validation — $jsonSchema, bsonType, enum, Nested Rules & collMod	mongo-lecture-7-json-schema-validation-collmod	Enforce strict document structure and data integrity in MongoDB using validator and $jsonSchema during createCollection() or on existing collections via db.runCommand({ collMod }).	2026-10-06 19:11:34.825286+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhile MongoDB is schema-flexible by default, production applications require **JSON Schema Validation** (`$jsonSchema`) so users cannot accidentally insert strings into numeric fields (like `age: "hello"`) or omit mandatory fields.\n\n### 💡 Core `$jsonSchema` Properties & Constraints\n* **`validator: { $jsonSchema: { ... } }`:** Passed as the second argument to `db.createCollection()` or applied to an existing collection via `db.runCommand({ collMod: "<collection>", validator: ... })`.\n* **`required: ["field1", "field2"`:** Array specifying which fields must be present in every document. Fields omitted from `required` remain optional.\n* **`bsonType`:** Enforces the exact data type for a property:\n  * `"string"`, `"int"` (for Integer), `"double"`, `"bool"` (for Boolean), `"date"`, `"array"`, `"object"`.\n* **`minimum` & `maximum`:** Numeric range bounds for `"int"` and `"double"` fields (e.g., `minimum: 5, maximum: 20`).\n* **`enum`:** Restricts a field to a predefined whitelist of values (e.g., `enum: ["BCA", "BTech", "BSc"`).\n* **`items` (Array Validation):** Validates the data type of elements inside an `"array"` (e.g., `items: { bsonType: "string" }`).\n* **Nested `properties` (Object Validation):** Validates required fields and `bsonType` rules inside nested embedded objects (such as `address.street`, `address.city`, `address.zip`).\n\n### 🛠️ 1. Creating a New Collection with JSON Schema Validation\n```javascript\ndb.createCollection("students", {\n  validator: {\n    $jsonSchema: {\n      title: "Student Object Validation",\n      required: ["name", "age", "course",\n      properties: {\n        name: {\n          bsonType: "string",\n          description: "Name must be a string and is required"\n        },\n        age: {\n          bsonType: "int",\n          minimum: 5,\n          maximum: 20,\n          description: "Age must be an integer between 5 and 20"\n        },\n        course: {\n          bsonType: "string",\n          enum: ["BCA", "BTech", "BSc",\n          description: "Course must be one of: BCA, BTech, BSc"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n})\n```\n\n### 🛠️ 2. Applying Validation to an Existing Collection (`collMod`)\n```javascript\ndb.runCommand({\n  collMod: "personal",\n  validator: {\n    $jsonSchema: {\n      required: ["name", "age", "married", "address",\n      properties: {\n        name: { bsonType: "string" },\n        age: { bsonType: "int" },\n        married: { bsonType: "bool" },\n        hobbies: {\n          bsonType: "array",\n          items: { bsonType: "string", description: "Each hobby must be a string" }\n        },\n        address: {\n          bsonType: "object",\n          required: ["street", "city", "zip",\n          properties: {\n            street: { bsonType: "string" },\n            city: { bsonType: "string" },\n            zip: { bsonType: "int" }\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n})\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Critical Syntax Rules\n* **Shorthand Type Names:** Always use `"int"` (not `"integer"`) and `"bool"` (not `"boolean"`) inside `bsonType`, and capitalize the `S` in `$jsonSchema` and `T` in `bsonType`.	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/schema-validation/specify-json-schema/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/schema-validation/specify-json-schema/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 7: JSON Schema Validation"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mO81XZAmFr8", "title": "MongoDB JSON Schema Validation Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "31m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhile MongoDB is schema-flexible by default, production applications require **JSON Schema Validation** (`$jsonSchema`) so users cannot accidentally insert strings into numeric fields (like `age: "hello"`) or omit mandatory fields.\n\n### 💡 Core `$jsonSchema` Properties & Constraints\n* **`validator: { $jsonSchema: { ... } }`:** Passed as the second argument to `db.createCollection()` or applied to an existing collection via `db.runCommand({ collMod: "<collection>", validator: ... })`.\n* **`required: ["field1", "field2"`:** Array specifying which fields must be present in every document. Fields omitted from `required` remain optional.\n* **`bsonType`:** Enforces the exact data type for a property:\n  * `"string"`, `"int"` (for Integer), `"double"`, `"bool"` (for Boolean), `"date"`, `"array"`, `"object"`.\n* **`minimum` & `maximum`:** Numeric range bounds for `"int"` and `"double"` fields (e.g., `minimum: 5, maximum: 20`).\n* **`enum`:** Restricts a field to a predefined whitelist of values (e.g., `enum: ["BCA", "BTech", "BSc"`).\n* **`items` (Array Validation):** Validates the data type of elements inside an `"array"` (e.g., `items: { bsonType: "string" }`).\n* **Nested `properties` (Object Validation):** Validates required fields and `bsonType` rules inside nested embedded objects (such as `address.street`, `address.city`, `address.zip`).\n\n### 🛠️ 1. Creating a New Collection with JSON Schema Validation\n```javascript\ndb.createCollection("students", {\n  validator: {\n    $jsonSchema: {\n      title: "Student Object Validation",\n      required: ["name", "age", "course",\n      properties: {\n        name: {\n          bsonType: "string",\n          description: "Name must be a string and is required"\n        },\n        age: {\n          bsonType: "int",\n          minimum: 5,\n          maximum: 20,\n          description: "Age must be an integer between 5 and 20"\n        },\n        course: {\n          bsonType: "string",\n          enum: ["BCA", "BTech", "BSc",\n          description: "Course must be one of: BCA, BTech, BSc"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n})\n```\n\n### 🛠️ 2. Applying Validation to an Existing Collection (`collMod`)\n```javascript\ndb.runCommand({\n  collMod: "personal",\n  validator: {\n    $jsonSchema: {\n      required: ["name", "age", "married", "address",\n      properties: {\n        name: { bsonType: "string" },\n        age: { bsonType: "int" },\n        married: { bsonType: "bool" },\n        hobbies: {\n          bsonType: "array",\n          items: { bsonType: "string", description: "Each hobby must be a string" }\n        },\n        address: {\n          bsonType: "object",\n          required: ["street", "city", "zip",\n          properties: {\n            street: { bsonType: "string" },\n            city: { bsonType: "string" },\n            zip: { bsonType: "int" }\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n})\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Critical Syntax Rules\n* **Shorthand Type Names:** Always use `"int"` (not `"integer"`) and `"bool"` (not `"boolean"`) inside `bsonType`, and capitalize the `S` in `$jsonSchema` and `T` in `bsonType`.	Yahoo Baba	https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba	655	Lecture 7: JSON Schema Validation — $jsonSchema, bsonType, enum, Nested Rules & collMod	Enforce strict document structure and data integrity in MongoDB using validator and $jsonSchema during createCollection() or on existing collections via db.runCommand({ collMod }).	20	Easy	\N	7	[]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/schema-validation/specify-json-schema/	\N	\N	\N
443	In-Place Matrix Transformation: Rotate Matrix / Image by 90 Degrees Clockwise	dsa-rotate-matrix-image-90-degrees	Rotate an N x N matrix 90 degrees clockwise in-place using a 2-step diagonal transposition followed by horizontal row reversal.	2026-10-02 23:12:19.354492+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: In-Place Matrix Transformation: Rotate Matrix / Image by 90 Degrees Clockwise\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z0R2u6gd3GU", "title": "Lecture 31: Rotate Image by 90 Degrees Clockwise", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/determine-whether-matrix-can-be-obtained-by-rotation/", "title": "Determine Whether Matrix Can Be Obtained By Rotation", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Apply 90-degree matrix rotations repeatedly to check if one grid matches another."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/transpose-matrix/", "title": "Transpose Matrix", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Practice swapping row and column indices across rectangular N x M matrices."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/rotate-by-90-degree-1587115621/1", "title": "Rotate by 90 degree (Counter-Clockwise)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Rotate a square matrix 90 degrees anti-clockwise in O(1) extra space."}]	Module 10: Array Permutations, Sequence Hashing & In-Place Matrix Manipulation	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an `N x N` 2D square matrix representing an image, rotate the matrix by 90 degrees clockwise **in-place** (without allocating another 2D grid).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Optimal)\n- **1. Brute Force (O(N²) Time, O(N²) Extra Space):**\n  - Create an auxiliary `ans[N][N]` grid.\n  - Notice that row `i` of the original matrix becomes column `N - 1 - i` in the rotated matrix.\n  - Mapping formula: `ans[j][N - 1 - i] = matrix[i][j]`.\n- **2. Optimal In-Place 2-Step Transformation (O(N²) Time, O(1) Extra Space):**\n  - **Step 1: Transpose the Matrix:**\n    - Swap rows and columns across the main diagonal: `swap(matrix[i][j], matrix[j][i])`.\n    - Loop `i` from `0` to `n - 1`, and `j` from `i + 1` to `n - 1` (only traverse above the diagonal so elements aren't swapped twice).\n  - **Step 2: Reverse Every Row:**\n    - Reverse each horizontal row using a two-pointer swap: `reverse(matrix[i].begin(), matrix[i].end())`.\n    - Rotating clockwise by 90 degrees is simply: `Transpose → Reverse Each Row`!\n\n### 💻 Optimal In-Place Rotation Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid rotateMatrix(vector<vector<int>> &matrix) {\n    int n = matrix.size();\n    // 1. Transpose in-place\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n            swap(matrix[i][j], matrix[j][i]);\n        }\n    }\n    // 2. Reverse each row\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        reverse(matrix[i].begin(), matrix[i].end());\n    }\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
10017	3-Pointer Iterative & Recursive Link Inversion: Reverse a Singly Linked List	dsa-reverse-singly-linked-list-iterative-and-recursive	Invert pointer directions in a singly linked list in-place using the 3-pointer iterative pattern in O(1) space and the recursive leap-of-faith approach.	2026-10-03 15:04:07.038447+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: 3-Pointer Iterative & Recursive Link Inversion: Reverse a Singly Linked List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D2vI2DNJGd8", "title": "Lecture 245: Reverse a Singly Linked List | Iterative and Recursive", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/reverse-linked-list/", "title": "Reverse Linked List (LC 206)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Given the head of a singly linked list, reverse the list iteratively and recursively."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/reverse-linked-list-ii/", "title": "Reverse Linked List II (LC 92)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Reverse the nodes of a linked list from position left to position right in a single pass."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/reverse-a-linked-list/1", "title": "Reverse a linked list", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Reverse a singly linked list in-place and return the new head."}]	Module 37: Linked List Medium Problems: Dummy Nodes, In-Place Re-Linking & Tortoise-Hare Pointers	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the `head` of a singly linked list, reverse the direction of all links in-place and return the `newHead`. You must know **both** the **Iterative (`O(1)` Space)** and **Recursive (`O(N)` Stack Space)** implementations for interviews!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **1. Iterative 3-Pointer Reversal (`O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Maintain `temp = head` and `prev = nullptr`.\n  - While `temp != nullptr`:\n    1. Save the next node before breaking the link: **`Node* front = temp->next;`**\n    2. Reverse the link backward: **`temp->next = prev;`**\n    3. Advance `prev = temp;` and `temp = front;`.\n  - Return **`prev`** (which lands on the old tail / new head).\n- **2. Recursive Subproblem Reversal (`O(N)` Time, `O(N)` Stack Space):**\n  - **Base Case:** A list of 0 or 1 node is already reversed: `if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr) return head;`.\n  - **Recursive Leap:** Reverse the remaining `N - 1` nodes: `Node* newHead = reverseRecursive(head->next);`.\n  - Now `head->next` is the **tail of the reversed sublist** (`Node* front = head->next;`)!\n  - Make `front` point back to `head`: **`front->next = head;`**, set **`head->next = nullptr;`**, and return `newHead`!\n\n### 💻 Reverse Singly Linked List (Iterative & Recursive) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\n// 1. Optimal Iterative Approach: O(N) Time, O(1) Space\nNode* reverseIterative(Node* head) {\n    Node* temp = head;\n    Node* prev = nullptr;\n\n    while (temp != nullptr) {\n        Node* front = temp->next; // Save next node\n        temp->next = prev;        // Reverse link backward\n        prev = temp;              // Move prev forward\n        temp = front;             // Move temp forward\n    }\n    return prev;\n}\n\n// 2. Recursive Approach: O(N) Time, O(N) Recursion Stack Space\nNode* reverseRecursive(Node* head) {\n    if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr) {\n        return head;\n    }\n\n    Node* newHead = reverseRecursive(head->next);\n    Node* front = head->next;\n    front->next = head;\n    head->next = nullptr;\n    return newHead;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting `head->next = nullptr;` in Recursive Reversal:** Without setting `head->next = nullptr`, the original first and second nodes point to each other (`1 <-> 2`), creating an infinite cycle at the tail of the reversed list!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
