10788	Contiguous Duplicate Skipping: Remove Duplicates From a Sorted Doubly Linked List	dsa-remove-duplicates-from-sorted-doubly-linked-list	Eliminate duplicate values from a sorted doubly linked list in O(N) single-pass time by skipping adjacent identical nodes and re-linking next/prev pointers.	2026-10-03 15:07:44.956942+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Contiguous Duplicate Skipping: Remove Duplicates From a Sorted Doubly Linked List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YJKVTnOJXSY", "title": "Lecture 256: Remove Duplicates from Sorted Doubly Linked List", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/remove-duplicates-from-a-sorted-doubly-linked-list/1", "title": "Remove duplicates from a sorted doubly linked list", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Given a doubly linked list of n nodes sorted by values, remove duplicate nodes so each value appears once."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/remove-duplicates-from-sorted-list/", "title": "Remove Duplicates from Sorted List (LC 83)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Delete all duplicates such that each element appears only once in a sorted singly linked list."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/remove-duplicates-from-sorted-list-ii/", "title": "Remove Duplicates from Sorted List II (LC 82)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Delete all nodes that have duplicate numbers, leaving only distinct numbers from the original list."}]	Module 38: Doubly Linked List Medium Problems & Hard Linked List Re-Wiring (K-Group Reversal, Rotation & Multi-Level Flattening)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the `head` of a **sorted Doubly Linked List**, remove all duplicate nodes in-place so that each distinct value appears **only once**, and return the `head`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Two Nested `while` Loops Still Run in `O(N)` Linear Time (Not `O(N²)`!):**\n  - Because the DLL is sorted, all duplicates of `temp->data` sit **contiguously** right after `temp`.\n  - For each unique node `temp`, start `nextNode = temp->next`.\n  - Inner loop: **`while (nextNode != nullptr && nextNode->data == temp->data)`**:\n    - Save `Node* duplicate = nextNode;`, advance `nextNode = nextNode->next;`, and **`delete duplicate;`**.\n  - Once `nextNode` lands on the **first node whose value differs from `temp->data`** (or `nullptr`):\n    1. Wire **`temp->next = nextNode;`**\n    2. If `nextNode != nullptr`, wire **`nextNode->prev = temp;`**\n    3. Advance **`temp = temp->next;`** (which jumps directly over all the deleted duplicates to `nextNode`!).\n  - **Complexity Analysis:** The inner loop only visits duplicate nodes, and the outer loop only visits unique nodes—so **every node in the DLL is visited at most once (`O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Space)**!\n\n### 💻 Remove Duplicates From Sorted DLL Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nNode* removeDuplicates(Node* head) {\n    Node* temp = head;\n\n    while (temp != nullptr && temp->next != nullptr) {\n        Node* nextNode = temp->next;\n\n        // Skip and free all contiguous duplicate nodes matching temp->data\n        while (nextNode != nullptr && nextNode->data == temp->data) {\n            Node* duplicate = nextNode;\n            nextNode = nextNode->next;\n            delete duplicate;\n        }\n\n        // Link temp directly to the first distinct nextNode\n        temp->next = nextNode;\n        if (nextNode != nullptr) {\n            nextNode->prev = temp;\n        }\n\n        // Advance temp to the next distinct node\n        temp = temp->next;\n    }\n    return head;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Setting `nextNode->prev = temp` Without Checking `if (nextNode != nullptr)`:** If the tail of the DLL consists of duplicates (e.g., `... <-> 4 <-> 4`), `nextNode` reaches `nullptr` at the end of the inner loop! Always guard `if (nextNode != nullptr)` before dereferencing `nextNode->prev`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
