8568	Bidirectional Prefix/Suffix LIS: Longest Bitonic Subsequence	dsa-longest-bitonic-subsequence-lis	Compute forward LIS and backward LIS tables to find the maximum combined peak bitonic subsequence length dp1[i] + dp2[i] - 1.	2026-10-03 11:44:14.778881+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Bidirectional Prefix/Suffix LIS: Longest Bitonic Subsequence\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4vN0WNdrlg", "title": "Lecture 226: Longest Bitonic Subsequence | LIS (DP 46)", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-number-of-removals-to-make-mountain-array/", "title": "Minimum Number of Removals to Make Mountain Array (LC 1671)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find the minimum deletions to form a mountain array (N - Longest Bitonic Subsequence with dp1[i] > 1 and dp2[i] > 1)."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/longest-bitonic-subsequence0824/1", "title": "Longest Bitonic subsequence", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the length of the longest subsequence that is first strictly increasing and then strictly decreasing."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-mountain-in-array/", "title": "Longest Mountain in Array (LC 845)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Contrast contiguous mountain subarrays with non-contiguous bitonic subsequences."}]	Module 33: Dynamic Programming on Longest Increasing Subsequences (LIS) & Chain Variants	### 📌 Topic Overview\nA **Bitonic Subsequence** is a subsequence that first **strictly increases** and then **strictly decreases** (note: in classic definitions, a purely increasing or purely decreasing subsequence is also considered bitonic; on LeetCode 1671 "Mountain Array", both slopes must be non-empty).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Viewing a Decreasing Suffix from Right-to-Left as an Increasing Sequence:**\n  - Suppose peak element `arr[i]` is the turning point of the bitonic subsequence.\n  - From **`0` to `i` (Left to Right)**, the elements must be **strictly increasing** (`dp1[i]`).\n  - From **`i` to `n - 1` (Left to Right)**, the elements must be **strictly decreasing**—which is **100% identical to being strictly increasing when read backward from `n - 1` down to `i` (`dp2[i]`)**!\n- **Combining `dp1[i]` and `dp2[i]` Without Double-Counting Peak `i`:**\n  - Run standard 1D LIS from left to right (`0` to `n - 1`) to fill `dp1[i]`.\n  - Run standard 1D LIS from right to left (`n - 1` down to `0`) to fill `dp2[i]`.\n  - Because peak `arr[i]` is counted once in `dp1[i]` and once in `dp2[i]`, subtract `1`:\n    **`maxi = max(maxi, dp1[i] + dp2[i] - 1)`**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N²)` Time and `O(N)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Longest Bitonic Subsequence Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint longestBitonicSequence(vector<int> &arr, int n) {\n    vector<int> dp1(n, 1); // LIS from Left to Right (0 .. n-1)\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        for (int prev = 0; prev < i; prev++) {\n            if (arr[prev] < arr[i] && 1 + dp1[prev] > dp1[i]) {\n                dp1[i] = 1 + dp1[prev];\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    vector<int> dp2(n, 1); // LIS from Right to Left (n-1 .. 0)\n    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n        for (int prev = n - 1; prev > i; prev--) {\n            if (arr[prev] < arr[i] && 1 + dp2[prev] > dp2[i]) {\n                dp2[i] = 1 + dp2[prev];\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    int maxi = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        // Subtract 1 because peak element arr[i] is included in both dp1[i] and dp2[i]\n        maxi = max(maxi, dp1[i] + dp2[i] - 1);\n    }\n    return maxi;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Strict Mountain Requirement (LeetCode 1671 & Updated GFG):** If a problem requires **both** an increasing part and a decreasing part to be non-empty, only update `maxi` when **`dp1[i] > 1 && dp2[i] > 1`**!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
