y independent state instance", "Yes, if the hook doesn't accept arguments", "No, but they share the same DOM elements"], "question": "Do multiple components utilizing the exact same custom hook share state?", "explanation": "A custom hook is just a regular function that calls other hooks. State inside them is locally bound to the specific component invoking the hook.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "No, custom hooks reuse stateful logic, not the state itself; each call creates a completely independent state instance"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["It increases rendering speed by 50%", "It allows you to extract and reuse complex component logic cleanly across multiple components", "It allows you to bypass React's lifecycle rules", "It replaces the need for a backend"], "question": "What is the primary benefit of creating a Custom Hook?", "explanation": "Custom hooks enable logic abstraction, allowing developers to cleanly reuse complicated logic like API fetching or local storage management.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "It allows you to extract and reuse complex component logic cleanly across multiple components"}]	\N	> **Architectural Deep Dive:** This lecture covers the strict technical nuances, reconciliation behavior, and performance implications of using Custom Hooks in production environments. Pay special attention to concurrent rendering behaviors in React 18+.	Web Dev Simplified	https://www.youtube.com/@WebDevSimplified	\N	Learn Custom Hooks In 10 Minutes	Deep dive architectural lecture focusing on the mechanics, use cases, and anti-patterns of the Custom Hooks hook.	10	Easy	Core Hooks & API	8	[]	\N	10	https://youtu.be/6ThXsUwLWvc	Learn Custom Hooks In 10 Minutes
173	Functional Recursion on Arrays & Strings: Two-Pointer Reversal & Palindrome Checks	dsa-functional-recursion-arrays-palindromes	Apply functional recursion to arrays and strings, utilizing single index mirror formulas (n - i - 1) for palindrome verification and in-place reversal.	2026-10-02 22:22:31.448364+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Functional Recursion on Arrays & Strings: Two-Pointer Reversal & Palindrome Checks\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=twuC1F6gLI8", "title": "Lecture 11: Problems on Functional Recursion — Reverse Array & Check Palindrome", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/reverse-string/", "title": "Reverse String", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "344", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Apply two-pointer symmetry to reverse a character array in-place."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/valid-palindrome-ii/", "title": "Valid Palindrome II", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "680", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Check whether a string can become a palindrome after deleting at most one character."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/sum-of-array2326/1", "title": "Sum of Array Elements", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "problemId": "gfg-sum-array", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Practice traversing an array recursively using index parameters."}]	Module 5: Recursion & Call Stack Mechanics	### 📌 Topic Overview\nAny iterative two-pointer operation on an array or string—such as reversing an array or checking if a word is a palindrome—can also be written using recursion. Instead of using two separate variables `left` and `right`, we can use a single index `i` and compare it with its mirror index `n - i - 1`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Mirror Index Formula:** For any index `i` from the left, its matching element from the right is at index `n - i - 1`.\n- **Stopping at the Middle (`i >= n / 2`):** When reversing or checking a palindrome, you only need to process the first half of the array. Once `i` reaches `n / 2`, all pairs have been checked or swapped.\n- **Void vs. Boolean Recursion:**\n  - *Reversing an Array:* Uses a `void` function that swaps `arr[i]` with `arr[n - i - 1]` and then calls `reverse(i + 1)`.\n  - *Checking a Palindrome:* Uses a `bool` function that immediately returns `false` if `s[i] != s[n - i - 1]`, or keeps checking `isPalindrome(i + 1)` until the middle is reached.\n- **Time & Space Complexity:** Both operations run in `O(N / 2)` steps—simplified to `O(N)` Time and `O(N)` Auxiliary Stack Space.\n\n### 💻 Quick Code Blueprint\n```cpp\n// Functional Recursion: Check if a string is a Palindrome\nbool isPalindrome(int i, string &s) {\n    int n = s.size();\n    if (i >= n / 2) return true;            // Base case: reached the middle\n    if (s[i] != s[n - i - 1]) return false; // Mismatch found\n    return isPalindrome(i + 1, s);          // Check next inward pair\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
3029	Memory-Efficient Graph Modeling in C++: Adjacency Matrix vs. Adjacency List	dsa-graph-representation-in-cpp	Compare Adjacency Matrix (O(N^2) space) with Adjacency List (O(2E) space) in C++ for unweighted and weighted undirected/directed graphs.	2026-10-03 01:35:42.040146+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Memory-Efficient Graph Modeling in C++: Adjacency Matrix vs. Adjacency List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3oI-34aPMWM", "title": "Lecture 127: Graph Representation in C++", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/print-adjacency-list-1587115620/1", "title": "Print adjacency list", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Construct and return the adjacency list representation of an undirected graph."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-number-of-vertices-to-reach-all-nodes/", "title": "Minimum Number of Vertices to Reach All Nodes (LC 1557)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Process a directed edge list to find all vertices with zero incoming edges."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-total-importance-of-roads/", "title": "Maximum Total Importance of Roads (LC 2285)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count vertex degrees from an edge list to maximize road importance."}]	Module 21: Graph Theory Foundations, Representations & Traversals	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGraph problems typically provide `N` (nodes) and `M` (edges), followed by `M` pairs `(u, v)`. To run graph algorithms, we must store these edges in either an **Adjacency Matrix** or an **Adjacency List**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **1. Adjacency Matrix (`int adj[N + 1][N + 1]`):**\n  - Mark `adj[u][v] = 1` and `adj[v][u] = 1` (for weighted graphs, store `adj[u][v] = wt`).\n  - **Space Complexity:** **`O(N²)`** — wastes massive memory when `N = 10^5`! Only use for small, dense graphs (`N <= 1000`).\n- **2. Adjacency List (`vector<int> adj[N + 1]`):**\n  - An array of vectors where `adj[u]` stores all direct neighbors of node `u`.\n  - **Undirected Graph:** Push both ways (`adj[u].push_back(v)` and `adj[v].push_back(u)`) → **`O(2E)` Space**.\n  - **Directed Graph (`u → v`):** Push only one way (`adj[u].push_back(v)`) → **`O(E)` Space**.\n  - **Weighted Graph:** Store pairs of `{neighbor, weight}` using **`vector<pair<int, int>> adj[N + 1]`**.\n\n### 💻 C++ Adjacency List Blueprint (Unweighted & Weighted)\n```cpp\n// 1. Unweighted Undirected Graph: O(2E) Space\nvoid buildUnweightedGraph(int n, int m, vector<pair<int, int>> &edges) {\n    vector<int> adj[n + 1];\n    for (auto &edge : edges) {\n        int u = edge.first, v = edge.second;\n        adj[u].push_back(v);\n        adj[v].push_back(u); // Omit this line if Directed Graph (u -> v)\n    }\n}\n\n// 2. Weighted Undirected Graph: Stores {neighbor, edgeWeight}\nvoid buildWeightedGraph(int n, vector<tuple<int, int, int>> &edges) {\n    vector<pair<int, int>> adj[n + 1];\n    for (auto &[u, v, wt] : edges) {\n        adj[u].push_back({v, wt});\n        adj[v].push_back({u, wt});\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **0-Based vs. 1-Based Indexing:** If node labels run from `1` to `N`, always allocate size `N + 1` (`vector<int> adj[n + 1]`) so accessing index `n` does not go out of bounds!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
