id": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["It causes infinite loops automatically", "It blocks the main thread and delays visual updates, causing the app to feel sluggish", "It wipes out the local storage", "It crashes the Virtual DOM tree"], "question": "What is a major risk of overusing `useLayoutEffect`?", "explanation": "Because it is completely synchronous, the user will see a frozen screen until useLayoutEffect finishes its calculations.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "It blocks the main thread and delays visual updates, causing the app to feel sluggish"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["For data fetching from an API", "For setting up intervals", "When you need to measure a DOM element (like width or scroll position) and synchronously mutate the DOM before the user sees it", "For triggering Redux actions"], "question": "When is it appropriate to use `useLayoutEffect`?", "explanation": "It prevents visual flickering by performing DOM measurements and applying new styles instantly before the browser repaints the layout.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "When you need to measure a DOM element (like width or scroll position) and synchronously mutate the DOM before the user sees it"}]	\N	> **Architectural Deep Dive:** This lecture covers the strict technical nuances, reconciliation behavior, and performance implications of using useLayoutEffect in production environments. Pay special attention to concurrent rendering behaviors in React 18+.	Web Dev Simplified	https://www.youtube.com/@WebDevSimplified	\N	Learn useLayoutEffect In 5 Minutes	Deep dive architectural lecture focusing on the mechanics, use cases, and anti-patterns of the useLayoutEffect hook.	5	Easy	Core Hooks & API	9	[]	\N	5	https://youtu.be/wU57kvYOxT4	Learn useLayoutEffect In 5 Minutes
6399	DP Table Last-Row Inspection: Partition a Set Into Two Subsets With Minimum Absolute Sum Difference	dsa-partition-set-into-two-subsets-minimum-absolute-sum-difference	Inspect the final row of the subset sum DP table to evaluate all achievable sums s1 <= totSum / 2 and minimize |s1 - s2|.	2026-10-03 11:28:16.962788+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: DP Table Last-Row Inspection: Partition a Set Into Two Subsets With Minimum Absolute Sum Difference\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GS_OqZb2CWc", "title": "Lecture 197: Partition Set With Minimum Absolute Sum Difference (DP 16)", "duration": "21m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/minimum-sum-partition3317/1", "title": "Minimum sum partition", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Given an array of non-negative integers, divide it into two sets S1 and S2 such that the absolute difference between their sums is minimum."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/last-stone-weight-ii/", "title": "Last Stone Weight II (LC 1049)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Model pairwise stone smashing as partitioning stones into two groups with minimum absolute sum difference."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/partition-array-into-two-arrays-to-minimize-sum-difference/", "title": "Partition Array Into Two Arrays to Minimize Sum Difference (LC 2035)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Extend minimum sum difference partitioning to equal-sized halves with negative numbers via Meet-in-the-Middle."}]	Module 30: Dynamic Programming on Subsequences, Subset Sums & 0/1 Knapsack	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array `arr` of `N` non-negative integers, partition the array into two subsets `S1` and `S2` such that the **absolute difference between their sums `|sum(S1) - sum(S2)|` is minimized**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Last-Row Power of Subset Sum Tabulation:**\n  - When we run the bottom-up **Subset Sum** tabulation up to `k = totSum`, the final row `dp[N - 1][s1]` (or `prev[s1]`) tells us in `O(1)` time whether **every possible subset sum `s1` from `0` to `totSum`** is achievable!\n- **Scanning Achieved Sums up to `totSum / 2`:**\n  - For any achievable subset sum `s1` (`prev[s1] == true`), the second subset automatically has sum **`s2 = totSum - s1`**.\n  - Their absolute difference is **`abs(s1 - (totSum - s1)) = abs(totSum - 2 * s1)`**.\n  - Loop `s1` from `0` to `totSum / 2`: whenever `prev[s1] == true`, update **`mini = min(mini, totSum - 2 * s1)`**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * totSum)` Time and `O(totSum)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Minimum Subset Sum Difference Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint minSubsetSumDifference(vector<int> &arr, int n) {\n    int totSum = 0;\n    for (int x : arr) totSum += x;\n\n    int k = totSum;\n    vector<bool> prev(k + 1, false), cur(k + 1, false);\n    prev[0] = cur[0] = true;\n    if (arr[0] <= k) prev[arr[0]] = true;\n\n    for (int ind = 1; ind < n; ind++) {\n        for (int target = 1; target <= k; target++) {\n            bool notTake = prev[target];\n            bool take = false;\n            if (arr[ind] <= target) take = prev[target - arr[ind]];\n            cur[target] = take || notTake;\n        }\n        prev = cur;\n    }\n\n    // Inspect the final DP row for all achievable sums s1 in [0 .. totSum / 2]\n    int mini = 1e9;\n    for (int s1 = 0; s1 <= totSum / 2; s1++) {\n        if (prev[s1] == true) {\n            int s2 = totSum - s1;\n            mini = min(mini, abs(s2 - s1));\n        }\n    }\n    return mini;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Using Top-Down Memoization Instead of Tabulation Here:** Top-down memoization `f(N - 1, totSum)` only populates the specific states needed for `totSum`, leaving many intermediate sums `s1` uncomputed (`-1`). Bottom-up **Tabulation** computes all `0 .. totSum` columns in one sweep!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
6402	Single-Array Right-to-Left Space Optimization: 0/1 Knapsack Problem	dsa-0-1-knapsack-problem-single-array-space-optimization	Solve classical 0/1 Knapsack capacity allocation and optimize space from 2D table to a single 1D array by iterating weights right-to-left.	2026-10-03 11:28:16.974028+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Single-Array Right-to-Left Space Optimization: 0/1 Knapsack Problem\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GqOmJHQZivw", "title": "Lecture 200: 0/1 Knapsack Problem (DP 19)", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/0-1-knapsack-problem0945/1", "title": "0 - 1 Knapsack Problem", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given weights and values of N items, put these items in a knapsack of capacity W to get the maximum total value."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/ones-and-zeroes/", "title": "Ones and Zeroes (LC 474)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Extend 0/1 Knapsack single-array reverse iteration to a 2D capacity constraint (at most m 0s and n 1s)."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/last-stone-weight-ii/", "title": "Last Stone Weight II (LC 1049)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use 0/1 Knapsack up to capacity totalSum / 2 to maximize the weight of one stone group."}]	Module 30: Dynamic Programming on Subsequences, Subset Sums & 0/1 Knapsack	### 📌 Topic Overview\nA thief has a knapsack with maximum weight capacity `W` and `N` items, where item `i` has weight `wt[i]` and value `val[i]`. Each item can be picked **at most once (`0` or `1` time)**. Maximize the total value of items placed in the knapsack without exceeding capacity `W`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **2D Recurrence (`f(ind, W)`):**\n  - **Base Case (`ind == 0`):** If `wt[0] <= W`, we can steal item `0` and gain `val[0]`; otherwise return `0`.\n  - **Transitions:**\n    - `notTake = 0 + f(ind - 1, W)`\n    - `take = INT_MIN; if (wt[ind] <= W) take = val[ind] + f(ind - 1, W - wt[ind]);`\n    - Return **`max(take, notTake)`**.\n- **The Single-Array (`O(W)`) Right-to-Left Trick:**\n  - Notice that `cur[w]` only depends on `prev[w]` and `prev[w - wt[ind]]` (values to the **left** of `w` in the previous row!).\n  - If we iterate capacity `w` **from Right to Left (`W` down to `0`)** on a **single array `prev`**, when we are at `prev[w]`, all values to the left (`prev[w - wt[ind]]`) still hold the untouched `ind - 1` row values!\n  - Thus we can eliminate the `cur` array completely and solve 0/1 Knapsack using **just ONE array of size `W + 1`**!\n\n### 💻 0/1 Knapsack (Single-Array Space Optimized) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint knapsack01(vector<int> &wt, vector<int> &val, int n, int maxWeight) {\n    // Single 1D array of size maxWeight + 1!\n    vector<int> prev(maxWeight + 1, 0);\n\n    // 1. Base case for item 0: any capacity W >= wt[0] can take item 0\n    for (int W = wt[0]; W <= maxWeight; W++) {\n        prev[W] = val[0];\n    }\n\n    // 2. Process items 1 to n - 1 iterating W from RIGHT TO LEFT!\n    for (int ind = 1; ind < n; ind++) {\n        for (int W = maxWeight; W >= 0; W--) {\n            int notTake = 0 + prev[W];\n            int take = INT_MIN;\n            if (wt[ind] <= W) {\n                take = val[ind] + prev[W - wt[ind]];\n            }\n            prev[W] = max(take, notTake);\n        }\n    }\n    return prev[maxWeight];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Iterating `W` From Left to Right (`0` to `maxWeight`) on a Single Array:** If you overwrite `prev[W]` from left to right, then later when a larger capacity reads `prev[W - wt[ind]]`, it will read the **already-updated current row value**, effectively picking the same item multiple times (turning 0/1 Knapsack into Unbounded Knapsack)! For 0/1 Knapsack on a single array, **always iterate `W` from `maxWeight` down to `0`**.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
