10016	Fixed-Gap Two-Pointer Lead: Remove N-th Node From the End of a Linked List	dsa-remove-nth-node-from-end-of-linked-list	Delete the N-th node from the end of a linked list in a single traversal by advancing a fast pointer N steps ahead before moving slow and fast together.	2026-10-03 15:04:07.035239+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Fixed-Gap Two-Pointer Lead: Remove N-th Node From the End of a Linked List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3kMKYQ2wNIU", "title": "Lecture 244: Remove Nth Node from End of Linked List", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/remove-nth-node-from-end-of-list/", "title": "Remove Nth Node From End of List (LC 19)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given the head of a linked list, remove the nth node from the end of the list in one pass and return its head."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/swapping-nodes-in-a-linked-list/", "title": "Swapping Nodes in a Linked List (LC 1721)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use the N-step pointer lead technique to locate both the k-th node from the start and the k-th node from the end in one pass."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/nth-node-from-end-of-linked-list/1", "title": "Kth from End of Linked List", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Find the data of the k-th node from the end of a linked list in a single traversal."}]	Module 37: Linked List Medium Problems: Dummy Nodes, In-Place Re-Linking & Tortoise-Hare Pointers	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the `head` of a linked list of length `L` and an integer `N` (`1 <= N <= L`), delete the **`N`-th node from the end** of the linked list and return the updated head.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Optimal)\n- **1. Brute Force Two-Pass (`O(2L)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Pass 1: Count total nodes `L`. If `L == N`, delete the head (`return head->next`).\n  - Pass 2: Walk `res = L - N` steps to stop at the **`(L - N)`-th node from the start** (just before the target node), and re-link `temp->next = temp->next->next`.\n- **2. Optimal Single-Pass Fixed-Gap Two Pointers (`O(L)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - How do we reach the `(L - N)`-th node without knowing `L` in advance?\n  - Start `fast = head` and advance `fast` by **exactly `N` steps** first!\n  - **Head Deletion Edge Case (`fast == nullptr`):** If `fast` is already `nullptr` after `N` steps, then `N == L` (we need to delete the head node!) → delete `head` and return `head->next`.\n  - Otherwise, start `slow = head` and advance **both `fast` and `slow` by 1 step simultaneously** **`while (fast->next != nullptr)`**!\n  - When `fast` lands on the last node (`fast->next == nullptr`), `slow` is guaranteed to sit **immediately before** the `N`-th node from the end!\n\n### 💻 Remove N-th Node From End (Single-Pass) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nNode* removeNthFromEnd(Node* head, int n) {\n    Node* fast = head;\n    Node* slow = head;\n\n    // 1. Advance 'fast' by N steps ahead\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        fast = fast->next;\n    }\n\n    // 2. Edge Case: If fast reached nullptr, N == length of list (delete head!)\n    if (fast == nullptr) {\n        Node* newHead = head->next;\n        delete head;\n        return newHead;\n    }\n\n    // 3. Move both pointers 1 step at a time until fast reaches the tail node\n    while (fast->next != nullptr) {\n        fast = fast->next;\n        slow = slow->next;\n    }\n\n    // 4. 'slow' is now right before the target node to delete\n    Node* delNode = slow->next;\n    slow->next = slow->next->next;\n    delete delNode;\n\n    return head;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Looping `while (fast != nullptr)` Instead of `while (fast->next != nullptr)`:** If you stop when `fast == nullptr`, `slow` lands **on** the node to be deleted instead of **one node before** it! Stopping at `fast->next == nullptr` stops `slow` at the predecessor node.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
10783	Collision-Point Loop Traversal: Find the Length of a Loop in a Linked List	dsa-find-length-of-loop-in-linked-list	Detect cycle collision using Tortoise and Hare, then hold slow stationary and advance fast by 1 step to count the loop length in O(N) time and O(1) space.	2026-10-03 15:07:44.945788+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Collision-Point Loop Traversal: Find the Length of a Loop in a Linked List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=I4g1qbkTPus", "title": "Lecture 251: Find Length of Loop in Linked List", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/find-length-of-loop/1", "title": "Find length of Loop", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Given the head of a linked list, determine whether a loop exists and return the number of nodes in the loop (or 0 if no loop exists)."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/linked-list-cycle/", "title": "Linked List Cycle (LC 141)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Use Floyd's Tortoise and Hare pointers to locate the collision point inside a linked list cycle."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-cycle-in-a-graph/", "title": "Longest Cycle in a Graph (LC 2360)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Extend timestamp-difference cycle length measurement from linked lists to directed graphs."}]	Module 37: Linked List Medium Problems: Dummy Nodes, In-Place Re-Linking & Tortoise-Hare Pointers	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the `head` of a singly linked list, determine if the list contains a loop, and if so, return the **exact number of nodes in the loop** (or `0` if there is no loop).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Optimal)\n- **1. Brute Force Using a Hash Map With a Step Timer (`O(N)` Time, `O(N)` Space):**\n  - Maintain `unordered_map<Node*, int> visited` and an integer `timer = 1`.\n  - As you traverse `temp` from `head`, if `temp` is already in `visited`, the loop length is simply **`timer - visited[temp]`**!\n- **2. Optimal Tortoise & Hare Collision Walk (`O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - **Step 1 (Detect Collision):** Move `slow` by `1` step and `fast` by `2` steps. If `fast == nullptr || fast->next == nullptr`, return `0` (no loop).\n  - **Step 2 (Count Loop Length from Collision Node):**\n    - Once `slow == fast`, both pointers are standing on the **exact same node inside the loop**!\n    - Keep `slow` stationary as an anchor, initialize `cnt = 1`, and advance `fast = fast->next` (by `1` step at a time!).\n    - Loop **`while (slow != fast)`**: increment `cnt++` and move `fast = fast->next;`.\n    - The moment `fast` laps the cycle and reaches `slow` again, return `cnt`!\n\n### 💻 Find Length of Loop in Linked List Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint findLoopLength(Node* slow, Node* fast) {\n    int cnt = 1;\n    fast = fast->next;\n    while (slow != fast) {\n        cnt++;\n        fast = fast->next;\n    }\n    return cnt;\n}\n\nint countNodesinLoop(Node* head) {\n    Node* slow = head;\n    Node* fast = head;\n\n    while (fast != nullptr && fast->next != nullptr) {\n        slow = slow->next;\n        fast = fast->next->next;\n\n        if (slow == fast) {\n            return findLoopLength(slow, fast);\n        }\n    }\n    return 0; // Linear list with no loop\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting to Advance `fast = fast->next` Before `while (slow != fast)`:** At the moment of collision, `slow == fast` is already true! You must step `fast = fast->next` with `cnt = 1` **before** entering `while (slow != fast)` so the loop actually executes!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
4218	Two-Phase BFS + Backwards DFS: Word Ladder II (Optimized Approach for Competitive Constraints)	dsa-word-ladder-ii-optimized-bfs-backtracking	Scale Word Ladder II to competitive constraints using forward step-distance BFS and backwards DFS backtracking.	2026-10-03 11:01:59.922647+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Two-Phase BFS + Backwards DFS: Word Ladder II (Optimized Approach for Competitive Constraints)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AD4SFl7tu7I", "title": "Lecture 156: Word Ladder 2 | Optimised Approach for Leetcode", "duration": "26m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/word-ladder-ii/", "title": "Word Ladder II (LC 126)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Implement the 2-phase BFS + reverse DFS backtracking solution to pass strict LeetCode memory/time limits."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/bus-routes/", "title": "Bus Routes (LC 815)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Use BFS over route transitions to find the minimum number of buses to reach target."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/word-ladder/", "title": "Word Ladder (LC 127)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Use Phase 1 of the optimized approach to map minimum step distances for each word."}]	Module 24: Shortest Path Algorithms in Graphs: DAG Relaxation, BFS & Dijkstra's Algorithm	### 📌 Topic Overview\nOn platforms with strict time and memory limits (like LeetCode 126), storing full `vector<string>` paths inside a BFS queue causes **Memory Limit Exceeded (MLE) / Time Limit Exceeded (TLE)** because the number of partial paths explodes exponentially. We solve this using a **Two-Phase Approach**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Phase 1: Forward Word-Only BFS (`unordered_map<string, int> mpp`):**\n  - Run standard Word Ladder I BFS storing **only strings** in the queue (`queue<string>`), and record the exact minimum step level of every reached word in `mpp[word] = steps`.\n  - Stop as soon as `endWord` is popped.\n- **Phase 2: Backwards DFS from `endWord` to `beginWord`:**\n  - Why backtrack from `endWord` to `beginWord` instead of forward? Because many forward paths hit dead ends, whereas walking backwards from `endWord` along edges where **`mpp[prevWord] + 1 == mpp[word]`** explores **only valid shortest paths** with zero wasted dead-end branches!\n  - When `word == beginWord`, reverse the current sequence and add it to `ans`.\n\n### 💻 Word Ladder II (Optimized Two-Phase) Blueprint\n```cpp\nclass Solution {\n    unordered_map<string, int> mpp;\n    vector<vector<string>> ans;\n    string bWord;\n\n    void dfsBacktrack(string word, vector<string> &seq) {\n        if (word == bWord) {\n            reverse(seq.begin(), seq.end());\n            ans.push_back(seq);\n            reverse(seq.begin(), seq.end()); // Restore for backtracking\n            return;\n        }\n        int steps = mpp[word];\n        for (int i = 0; i < word.size(); i++) {\n            char original = word[i];\n            for (char ch = 'a'; ch <= 'z'; ch++) {\n                word[i] = ch;\n                if (mpp.find(word) != mpp.end() && mpp[word] + 1 == steps) {\n                    seq.push_back(word);\n                    dfsBacktrack(word, seq);\n                    seq.pop_back();\n                }\n            }\n            word[i] = original;\n        }\n    }\n\npublic:\n    vector<vector<string>> findLadders(string beginWord, string endWord, vector<string> &wordList) {\n        unordered_set<string> st(wordList.begin(), wordList.end());\n        queue<string> q;\n        bWord = beginWord;\n        q.push(beginWord);\n        mpp[beginWord] = 1;\n        st.erase(beginWord);\n\n        // Phase 1: Forward BFS to record minimum step depths\n        while (!q.empty()) {\n            string word = q.front();\n            int steps = mpp[word];\n            q.pop();\n            if (word == endWord) break;\n\n            for (int i = 0; i < word.size(); i++) {\n                char original = word[i];\n                for (char ch = 'a'; ch <= 'z'; ch++) {\n                    word[i] = ch;\n                    if (st.count(word)) {\n                        q.push(word);\n                        st.erase(word);\n                        mpp[word] = steps + 1;\n                    }\n                }\n                word[i] = original;\n            }\n        }\n\n        // Phase 2: Backwards DFS from endWord to beginWord\n        if (mpp.find(endWord) != mpp.end()) {\n            vector<string> seq = {endWord};\n            dfsBacktrack(endWord, seq);\n        }\n        return ans;\n    }\n};\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting to Re-Reverse `seq` After Pushing to `ans`:** In `dfsBacktrack`, `seq` is passed by reference! After reversing `seq` to push the forward-ordered path into `ans`, you **must reverse `seq` back** before returning so caller stack frames aren't corrupted!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
5808	Pick / Non-Pick Subsequence Pattern: Maximum Sum of Non-Adjacent Elements (House Robber I)	dsa-maximum-sum-of-non-adjacent-elements-house-robber	Apply the pick/not-pick DP pattern to maximize subsequence sums without taking adjacent elements in O(N) time and O(1) space.	2026-10-03 11:17:17.099909+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Pick / Non-Pick Subsequence Pattern: Maximum Sum of Non-Adjacent Elements (House Robber I)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GrMBfJNk_NY", "title": "Lecture 186: Maximum Sum of Non-Adjacent Elements (House Robber)", "duration": "23m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/house-robber/", "title": "House Robber (LC 198)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the maximum amount of money you can rob tonight without alerting the police (no two adjacent houses)."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/stickler-theif-1587115621/1", "title": "Stickler Thief (Max Sum without Adjacents)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the maximum sum of a subsequence with no two adjacent elements."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-and-earn/", "title": "Delete and Earn (LC 740)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Bucket element sums by value to reduce the problem directly to House Robber."}]	Module 28: 1D Dynamic Programming: Recurrences, Memoization, Tabulation & Space Optimization	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array `nums` of `N` integers, return the **maximum sum of any subsequence** such that **no two picked elements are adjacent** in the original array.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Pick / Not-Pick Recurrence (`f(ind)`):**\n  - At any index `ind`, we have two mutually exclusive choices:\n    1. **Pick `nums[ind]`:** We gain `nums[ind]`, and because we **cannot** pick the adjacent element `ind - 1`, we must jump to **`ind - 2`**:\n       **`pick = nums[ind] + f(ind - 2)`**.\n    2. **Not Pick `nums[ind]`:** We gain `0`, and we are free to consider the adjacent element **`ind - 1`**:\n       **`notPick = 0 + f(ind - 1)`**.\n  - Return **`max(pick, notPick)`**.\n- **Base Cases:**\n  - `ind == 0`: Return `nums[0]` (if we reached `0`, we hadn't picked `1`, so picking `nums[0]` is always optimal).\n  - `ind < 0`: Return `0` (handles `ind - 2` when `ind == 1`).\n- **Space Optimization (`O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Since `dp[i]` only requires `dp[i - 1]` (`prev`) and `dp[i - 2]` (`prev2`), we can compute the answer in **`O(1)` auxiliary space**!\n\n### 💻 Maximum Non-Adjacent Sum (O(1) Space Optimized) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint maximumNonAdjacentSum(vector<int> &nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    if (n == 0) return 0;\n\n    int prev = nums[0]; // Represents dp[i - 1]\n    int prev2 = 0;      // Represents dp[i - 2]\n\n    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n        int take = nums[i];\n        if (i > 1) take += prev2;\n\n        int notTake = 0 + prev;\n\n        int curi = max(take, notTake);\n        prev2 = prev;\n        prev = curi;\n    }\n    return prev;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting to Add `nums[i]` When `i == 1`:** When `i == 1`, `i - 2` is `-1` (out of bounds), **but you still get `nums[1]` if you pick index `1`**! Always initialize `int take = nums[i];` first, and then add `if (i > 1) take += prev2;`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
