e-smallest-number-of-neighbors-at-a-threshold-distance/1", "title": "City With the Smallest Number of Neighbors at a Threshold Distance", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Apply Floyd-Warshall or V runs of Dijkstra to find the least-connected city within a distance threshold."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-possible-sets-of-closing-branches/", "title": "Number of Possible Sets of Closing Branches (LC 2959)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Use Floyd-Warshall on subsets of nodes to verify maximum distance thresholds."}]	Module 25: Negative-Weight Shortest Paths, All-Pairs Shortest Paths & Minimum Spanning Trees	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven `n` cities (`0` to `n - 1`), undirected weighted `edges`, and a `distanceThreshold`, find the city that can reach the **smallest number of other cities** with a shortest-path distance **`<= distanceThreshold`**. If multiple cities tie, return the city with the **greatest number (largest index)**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Step 1: Compute All-Pairs Shortest Paths (`dist[n][n]`):**\n  - Build the `n x n` distance matrix (`dist[i][i] = 0`, edges filled symmetrically) and run the **Floyd-Warshall Algorithm** (or run Dijkstra `n` times, once from each city).\n- **Step 2: Count Reachable Cities & Break Ties with `<=`:**\n  - For each city `city` from `0` to `n - 1`, count how many cities `adjCity` satisfy `dist[city][adjCity] <= distanceThreshold`.\n  - If `cnt <= cntMax`, update `cntMax = cnt` and `cityNo = city`.\n  - Using **`<=`** (instead of `<`) while iterating `city` from `0` to `n - 1` automatically breaks ties in favor of the **larger city index**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N³)` Time and `O(N²)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Find the City (Floyd-Warshall) Blueprint\n```cpp\nint findTheCity(int n, vector<vector<int>> &edges, int distanceThreshold) {\n    vector<vector<int>> dist(n, vector<int>(n, 1e9));\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) dist[i][i] = 0;\n\n    for (const auto &e : edges) {\n        int u = e[0], v = e[1], wt = e[2];\n        dist[u][v] = wt;\n        dist[v][u] = wt;\n    }\n\n    // Floyd-Warshall all-pairs shortest paths\n    for (int via = 0; via < n; via++) {\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n                if (dist[i][via] != 1e9 && dist[via][j] != 1e9) {\n                    dist[i][j] = min(dist[i][j], dist[i][via] + dist[via][j]);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    int minReachable = n + 1;\n    int bestCity = -1;\n\n    for (int city = 0; city < n; city++) {\n        int cnt = 0;\n        for (int adjCity = 0; adjCity < n; adjCity++) {\n            if (dist[city][adjCity] <= distanceThreshold) {\n                cnt++;\n            }\n        }\n        // Using <= ensures ties are broken by the largest city index!\n        if (cnt <= minReachable) {\n            minReachable = cnt;\n            bestCity = city;\n        }\n    }\n    return bestCity;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Using `<` Instead of `<=` When Updating `bestCity`:** The problem requires returning the city with the **greatest index** if there is a tie. Using `cnt <= minReachable` ensures later (larger) indices overwrite earlier ones!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
8566	Transitive Divisibility Sorting: Largest Divisible Subset	dsa-largest-divisible-subset-lis-variant	Sort the array to establish transitive divisibility, then apply 1D LIS tabulation with backtracking to extract the largest divisible subset.	2026-10-03 11:44:14.770348+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Transitive Divisibility Sorting: Largest Divisible Subset\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gDuZwBW9VvM", "title": "Lecture 224: Largest Divisible Subset | Longest Increasing Subsequence (DP 44)", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/largest-divisible-subset/", "title": "Largest Divisible Subset (LC 368)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given a set of distinct positive integers nums, return the largest subset answer such that every pair (answer[i], answer[j]) satisfies answer[i] % answer[j] == 0 or answer[j] % answer[i] == 0."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/largest-divisible-subset--170643/1", "title": "Largest Divisible Subset", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find and print the largest subset where every pair of elements is mutually divisible."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-increasing-subsequence/", "title": "Longest Increasing Subsequence (LC 300)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Compare how sorting transforms pairwise divisibility into a single-check LIS recurrence."}]	Module 33: Dynamic Programming on Longest Increasing Subsequences (LIS) & Chain Variants	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array `arr` of `N` distinct positive integers, find and return the **Largest Divisible Subset**—the largest subset in which for **every pair `(x, y)`** in the subset, either `x % y == 0` or `y % x == 0`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Sorting Unlocked the Transitive LIS Property:**\n  - Unlike a subsequence, a **subset** has no order constraint, so **we are free to sort `arr` in ascending order first**!\n  - Suppose `arr` is sorted and we have picked `[1, 4, 8]` (`4 % 1 == 0` and `8 % 4 == 0`).\n  - Now we consider `16`: if **`16 % 8 == 0`** (divisible by the **last element `8`**), because `8` is already a multiple of `4` and `1`, by **transitivity of divisibility**, `16` is automatically divisible by `4` and `1` too!\n  - Therefore, **after sorting `arr`, Largest Divisible Subset is 100% identical to Printing LIS (DP 42)**, replacing `arr[i] > arr[prev]` with **`arr[i] % arr[prev] == 0`**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N²)` Time (plus `O(N log N)` for sorting) and `O(N)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Largest Divisible Subset Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nvector<int> largestDivisibleSubset(vector<int> &arr) {\n    int n = arr.size();\n    // 1. Sort the array so divisibility becomes transitive!\n    sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());\n\n    vector<int> dp(n, 1), hash(n);\n    int maxi = 1, lastIndex = 0;\n\n    // 2. Run 1D LIS Tabulation with (arr[i] % arr[prev] == 0)\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        hash[i] = i;\n        for (int prev = 0; prev < i; prev++) {\n            if (arr[i] % arr[prev] == 0 && 1 + dp[prev] > dp[i]) {\n                dp[i] = 1 + dp[prev];\n                hash[i] = prev;\n            }\n        }\n        if (dp[i] > maxi) {\n            maxi = dp[i];\n            lastIndex = i;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // 3. Reconstruct the subset using hash[]\n    vector<int> temp;\n    temp.push_back(arr[lastIndex]);\n    while (hash[lastIndex] != lastIndex) {\n        lastIndex = hash[lastIndex];\n        temp.push_back(arr[lastIndex]);\n    }\n    reverse(temp.begin(), temp.end());\n    return temp;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting `sort(arr.begin(), arr.end())` Before Running DP:** Without sorting, `[8, 4, 16]` fails `4 % 8 == 0`, breaking the transitive divisibility chain!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
