22222	OOPs Foundations: Inheritance & Polymorphism	lld-oops-inheritance-polymorphism	Master Object-Oriented reuse and behavior: Is-A hierarchies, access inheritance rules, Method Overloading vs Overriding, and Dynamic Virtual Tables.	2026-10-07 16:56:30.769208+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: OOPs Foundations: Inheritance & Polymorphism\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/KGOEK0-XBIg", "title": "Inheritance & Polymorphism in OOPs", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v3-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Static Polymorphism vs Dynamic Polymorphism", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Method Overloading (Static / Compile-Time):**\\n   * Multiple methods in the same class share the same name but differ in parameter count or parameter types.\\n   * *Resolution:* The compiler uses **Name Mangling** at compile time to encode parameter types into distinct internal symbol names. Resolution has zero runtime performance overhead.\\n\\n2. **Method Overriding (Dynamic / Run-Time):**\\n   * A derived class re-defines a `virtual` method inherited from a base class with the exact same name, return type, and parameter signature.\\n   * *Resolution:* Resolved at runtime via the **Virtual Table (vtable)**. When a method is invoked on a base pointer (`Car* ptr = new ElectricCar(); ptr->accelerate();`), the runtime dereferences `ptr`'s internal `vptr` to find and execute the concrete derived method.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Contrast Method Overloading (Compile-Time) and Method Overriding (Run-Time). How does the compiler resolve each during compilation and execution?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v3-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: How the Virtual Table (vtable) and vptr Function", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **vtable Construction:** For every class containing at least one `virtual` function, the compiler generates a static table of function pointers called the **vtable**.\\n2. **vptr Injection:** For every instance of that class, the compiler injects a hidden pointer called **`vptr`** (typically at byte offset 0 of the object).\\n3. **Initialization:** When the constructor executes (`new ElectricCar()`), the object's `vptr` is set to point to `ElectricCar`'s vtable.\\n4. **Runtime Dispatch:** When `carPtr->accelerate()` executes:\\n   * Step 1: Dereference `carPtr` to read the `vptr`.\\n   * Step 2: Index into the vtable at the fixed slot offset for `accelerate`.\\n   * Step 3: Jump to and execute the function pointer found at that slot.\\nThis indirection provides seamless runtime polymorphism at the cost of one pointer dereference.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the internal memory layout and lookup mechanism of the Virtual Method Table (vtable) and Virtual Table Pointer (vptr) in C++ during polymorphic method invocation.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v3-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Why Base Classes Must Have Virtual Destructors", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Memory Leak Bug:**\\nConsider polymorphic cleanup:\\n```cpp\\nCar* myCar = new ElectricCar();\\ndelete myCar;\\n```\\nIf `Car` has a standard non-virtual destructor:\\n1. The compiler binds the deletion statically at compile-time to `Car::~Car()`.\\n2. Only the base class destructor executes.\\n3. The derived class destructor `ElectricCar::~ElectricCar()` is **NEVER called**.\\n4. Any heap memory, file descriptors, or network sockets allocated specifically by `ElectricCar` (e.g., battery telemetry buffers) are permanently leaked.\\n\\nDeclaring `virtual ~Car() = default;` ensures dynamic dispatch routes the deletion through `ElectricCar`'s destructor first before chaining up to `Car`'s destructor.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is it mandatory to declare a Virtual Destructor in a base class intended for polymorphic deletion (e.g., \\"delete basePtr;\\")? What catastrophic memory bug occurs otherwise?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v3-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Public vs Protected vs Private Inheritance in C++", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n| Base Member | Inherited via Public | Inherited via Protected | Inherited via Private |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **public** | `public` | `protected` | `private` |\\n| **protected** | `protected` | `protected` | `private` |\\n| **private** | Inaccessible | Inaccessible | Inaccessible |\\n\\n**Why Public Inheritance is the LLD Standard:**\\nPublic inheritance models a true **\\"Is-A\\"** relationship (a `Dog` is an `Animal`). It preserves the outward interface contract, allowing derived objects to be substituted wherever base objects are expected (Liskov Substitution Principle). Protected and private inheritance model implementation reuse (\\"implemented-in-terms-of\\") rather than true subtyping, which is almost always better served via Composition.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In C++, how do public, protected, and private inheritance modes alter the accessibility of inherited base members in derived classes? Why is public inheritance the standard for LLD?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v3-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: The Fragile Base Class Problem", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Fragile Base Class Problem:**\\nOccurs when derived classes depend intimately on the internal implementation mechanics of base classes.\\n\\n*Example:* Base class `CustomList` has methods `add()` and `addAll()`, where `addAll()` internally loops and calls `add()`. A child class `CountingList` overrides both `add()` and `addAll()` to increment an internal counter. When `addAll([1, 2])` is called, `CountingList` adds 2 to counter and calls `super.addAll()`, which in turn calls the overridden `add()` twice—double-counting every element to 4.\\n\\nA minor performance tweak in the base class (e.g., optimizing `addAll` to bypass `add`) silently breaks child class invariants without compile-time errors.\\nDeep inheritance hierarchies create high fragility; Composition avoids this by treating dependencies as opaque black boxes.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is the \\"Fragile Base Class Problem\\" in deep inheritance hierarchies, and how does it illustrate why design patterns favor Composition over Inheritance?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. Inheritance (The "Is-A" Relationship)\n* Models real-world hierarchical relationships where derived classes inherit state and behavior from a base class.\n* **Code Reuse & Specialization:** Base `Car` defines common properties (`brand`, `model`, `speed`). `ManualCar` specializes with `shiftGear()`; `ElectricCar` specializes with `chargeBattery()`.\n* **C++ Inheritance Modes:** Public (`class B : public A`), Protected, and Private inheritance dictate how base members are exposed in the derived class.\n\n### 💡 2. Polymorphism (Many Forms)\n* **Static Polymorphism (Compile-Time):**\n  * **Method Overloading:** Same function name, different parameter count or types (e.g., `accelerate()` vs `accelerate(int speed)`). Resolved by compiler name-mangling.\n* **Dynamic Polymorphism (Run-Time):**\n  * **Method Overriding:** Base class declares a `virtual` method; derived class re-implements it with identical signature.\n  * Resolved at runtime via the **vtable (Virtual Method Table)** and **vptr (Virtual Table Pointer)**.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	OOPs Foundations: Inheritance & Polymorphism	Master Object-Oriented reuse and behavior: Is-A hierarchies, access inheritance rules, Method Overloading vs Overriding, and Dynamic Virtual Tables.	45	Hard	Low Level Design	17	[{"id": "lld-v3-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Static Polymorphism vs Dynamic Polymorphism", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Method Overloading (Static / Compile-Time):**\\n   * Multiple methods in the same class share the same name but differ in parameter count or parameter types.\\n   * *Resolution:* The compiler uses **Name Mangling** at compile time to encode parameter types into distinct internal symbol names. Resolution has zero runtime performance overhead.\\n\\n2. **Method Overriding (Dynamic / Run-Time):**\\n   * A derived class re-defines a `virtual` method inherited from a base class with the exact same name, return type, and parameter signature.\\n   * *Resolution:* Resolved at runtime via the **Virtual Table (vtable)**. When a method is invoked on a base pointer (`Car* ptr = new ElectricCar(); ptr->accelerate();`), the runtime dereferences `ptr`'s internal `vptr` to find and execute the concrete derived method.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Contrast Method Overloading (Compile-Time) and Method Overriding (Run-Time). How does the compiler resolve each during compilation and execution?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v3-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: How the Virtual Table (vtable) and vptr Function", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **vtable Construction:** For every class containing at least one `virtual` function, the compiler generates a static table of function pointers called the **vtable**.\\n2. **vptr Injection:** For every instance of that class, the compiler injects a hidden pointer called **`vptr`** (typically at byte offset 0 of the object).\\n3. **Initialization:** When the constructor executes (`new ElectricCar()`), the object's `vptr` is set to point to `ElectricCar`'s vtable.\\n4. **Runtime Dispatch:** When `carPtr->accelerate()` executes:\\n   * Step 1: Dereference `carPtr` to read the `vptr`.\\n   * Step 2: Index into the vtable at the fixed slot offset for `accelerate`.\\n   * Step 3: Jump to and execute the function pointer found at that slot.\\nThis indirection provides seamless runtime polymorphism at the cost of one pointer dereference.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the internal memory layout and lookup mechanism of the Virtual Method Table (vtable) and Virtual Table Pointer (vptr) in C++ during polymorphic method invocation.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v3-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Why Base Classes Must Have Virtual Destructors", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Memory Leak Bug:**\\nConsider polymorphic cleanup:\\n```cpp\\nCar* myCar = new ElectricCar();\\ndelete myCar;\\n```\\nIf `Car` has a standard non-virtual destructor:\\n1. The compiler binds the deletion statically at compile-time to `Car::~Car()`.\\n2. Only the base class destructor executes.\\n3. The derived class destructor `ElectricCar::~ElectricCar()` is **NEVER called**.\\n4. Any heap memory, file descriptors, or network sockets allocated specifically by `ElectricCar` (e.g., battery telemetry buffers) are permanently leaked.\\n\\nDeclaring `virtual ~Car() = default;` ensures dynamic dispatch routes the deletion through `ElectricCar`'s destructor first before chaining up to `Car`'s destructor.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is it mandatory to declare a Virtual Destructor in a base class intended for polymorphic deletion (e.g., \\"delete basePtr;\\")? What catastrophic memory bug occurs otherwise?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v3-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Public vs Protected vs Private Inheritance in C++", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n| Base Member | Inherited via Public | Inherited via Protected | Inherited via Private |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **public** | `public` | `protected` | `private` |\\n| **protected** | `protected` | `protected` | `private` |\\n| **private** | Inaccessible | Inaccessible | Inaccessible |\\n\\n**Why Public Inheritance is the LLD Standard:**\\nPublic inheritance models a true **\\"Is-A\\"** relationship (a `Dog` is an `Animal`). It preserves the outward interface contract, allowing derived objects to be substituted wherever base objects are expected (Liskov Substitution Principle). Protected and private inheritance model implementation reuse (\\"implemented-in-terms-of\\") rather than true subtyping, which is almost always better served via Composition.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In C++, how do public, protected, and private inheritance modes alter the accessibility of inherited base members in derived classes? Why is public inheritance the standard for LLD?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v3-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: The Fragile Base Class Problem", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Fragile Base Class Problem:**\\nOccurs when derived classes depend intimately on the internal implementation mechanics of base classes.\\n\\n*Example:* Base class `CustomList` has methods `add()` and `addAll()`, where `addAll()` internally loops and calls `add()`. A child class `CountingList` overrides both `add()` and `addAll()` to increment an internal counter. When `addAll([1, 2])` is called, `CountingList` adds 2 to counter and calls `super.addAll()`, which in turn calls the overridden `add()` twice—double-counting every element to 4.\\n\\nA minor performance tweak in the base class (e.g., optimizing `addAll` to bypass `add`) silently breaks child class invariants without compile-time errors.\\nDeep inheritance hierarchies create high fragility; Composition avoids this by treating dependencies as opaque black boxes.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is the \\"Fragile Base Class Problem\\" in deep inheritance hierarchies, and how does it illustrate why design patterns favor Composition over Inheritance?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
12417	Bounded-Defect Window Locking: Max Consecutive Ones III	dsa-max-consecutive-ones-iii-sliding-window	Master Bounded-Defect Window Locking: Max Consecutive Ones III with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:15:39.080519+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Bounded-Defect Window Locking: Max Consecutive Ones III\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3E4JBHSLpYk", "title": "Bounded-Defect Window Locking: Max Consecutive Ones III - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/max-consecutive-ones-iii/", "title": "LeetCode 1004: Max Consecutive Ones III", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given a binary array nums and an integer k, return the maximum number of consecutive 1's in the array if you can flip at most k 0's."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-subarray-of-1s-after-deleting-one-element/", "title": "LeetCode 1493: Longest Subarray of 1's After Deleting One Element", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Special case of Max Consecutive Ones III where k = 1 and the deleted element is not counted in the final length."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximize-the-confusion-of-an-exam/", "title": "LeetCode 2024: Maximize the Confusion of an Exam", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Apply Max Consecutive Ones III to a string of 'T' and 'F' characters with at most k flips."}]	Module 40: Sliding Window & Two-Pointer Algorithms: Fixed Windows, Variable Windows & Exact-Count Reductions	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a binary array `nums` (`0`s and `1`s) and an integer `k`, return the maximum number of consecutive `1`s in the array if you are allowed to **flip at most `k` `0`s**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute O(N²) → Better O(2N) → Optimal O(N))\n- **Reframing the Problem as Pattern 2:**\n  - *"Flipping at most `k` `0`s to get the longest streak of `1`s"* is 100% equivalent to:\n    **"Find the longest subarray containing AT MOST `k` zeros!"**\n- **1. Better Approach (`while (zeros > k)` — `O(2N)` Time):**\n  - Expand `r++` and increment `zeros++` whenever `nums[r] == 0`.\n  - While `zeros > k`, shrink from the left: if `nums[l] == 0`, decrement `zeros--`; then `l++`.\n- **2. Optimal Approach (`if (zeros > k)` — Pure `O(N)` Time!):**\n  - Replace `while (zeros > k)` with **`if (zeros > k)`**!\n  - Whenever `zeros > k`, we only move `l` forward by **1 step** (`if (nums[l] == 0) zeros--; l++;`).\n  - Why? Because this locks the window width at `maxLen` (`r` moves `+1` and `l` moves `+1`), refusing to let the window grow until `zeros` drops back to `<= k`, while avoiding the extra `O(N)` inner loop!\n\n### 💻 Max Consecutive Ones III (Optimal O(N) Single-Pass) Blueprint\n```cpp\nint longestOnes(vector<int> &nums, int k) {\n    int l = 0, r = 0, maxLen = 0, zeros = 0;\n    int n = nums.size();\n\n    while (r < n) {\n        if (nums[r] == 0) zeros++;\n\n        // Optimal O(N) trick: use 'if' instead of 'while' to lock window size at maxLen!\n        if (zeros > k) {\n            if (nums[l] == 0) zeros--;\n            l++;\n        }\n\n        if (zeros <= k) {\n            int len = r - l + 1;\n            maxLen = max(maxLen, len);\n        }\n        r++;\n    }\n    return maxLen;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Updating `maxLen` Without Checking `if (zeros <= k)` in the Optimal `if` Version:** Because a single `if (zeros > k)` step only shifts `l` by 1 position, `zeros` might still be `> k` on that iteration! Always wrap `maxLen = max(maxLen, r - l + 1)` inside **`if (zeros <= k)`**!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
954	Contiguous Page Allocation: Book Allocation Problem (Minimize Maximum Pages)	dsa-book-allocation-problem-binary-search	Allocate books contiguously to M students to minimize the maximum pages assigned to any student in O(N * log(sum - max)) time.	2026-10-03 00:19:12.425559+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Contiguous Page Allocation: Book Allocation Problem (Minimize Maximum Pages)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z0hwjftStI4", "title": "Lecture 62: Book Allocation Problem", "duration": "32m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/split-array-largest-sum/", "title": "Split Array Largest Sum", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Split an array into k non-empty contiguous subarrays to minimize the largest subarray sum."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/capacity-to-ship-packages-within-d-days/", "title": "Capacity To Ship Packages Within D Days", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Determine the least ship weight capacity required to ship packages within D days."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/allocate-minimum-number-of-pages0937/1", "title": "Allocate Minimum Pages", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Allocate books to students so that the maximum pages assigned to any student is minimized."}]	Module 13: Binary Search on Answer Space & Monotonic Predicate Functions	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven `n` books with `arr[i]` pages and `m` students, allocate books contiguously such that every student gets at least one book and the **maximum pages assigned to any student is minimized** (Min-of-Max pattern).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Impossible Case:** If `m > n` (more students than books), it is impossible to give each student at least one book—return `-1`.\n- **Search Range (`[max(arr), sum(arr)]`):**\n  - `low = max(arr)`: A student assigned the thickest book must be able to hold at least its page count.\n  - `high = sum(arr)`: If only 1 student existed, they would read all pages.\n- **Feasibility Simulation (`countStudents(arr, maxPages)`):**\n  - Traverse the books, adding pages to the current student.\n  - If adding the next book exceeds `maxPages`, increment student count (`students++`) and assign the new book to the next student.\n- **Binary Search Elimination:**\n  - If `countStudents(arr, mid) > m`: The page barrier `mid` is too tight (requires too many students), so increase barrier (`low = mid + 1`).\n  - Otherwise: `mid` works with `<= m` students—try minimizing pages further (`high = mid - 1`).\n  - `low` ends at the optimal minimum maximum page allocation!\n\n### 💻 Book Allocation Blueprint\n```cpp\nint countStudents(vector<int> &arr, int pages) {\n    int students = 1, pagesStudent = 0;\n    for (int p : arr) {\n        if (pagesStudent + p <= pages) {\n            pagesStudent += p;\n        } else {\n            students++;\n            pagesStudent = p;\n        }\n    }\n    return students;\n}\n\nint findPages(vector<int> &arr, int n, int m) {\n    if (m > n) return -1;\n    int low = *max_element(arr.begin(), arr.end());\n    int high = accumulate(arr.begin(), arr.end(), 0);\n\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (countStudents(arr, mid) > m) {\n            low = mid + 1;\n        } else {\n            high = mid - 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return low;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
