7018	Two-String Index-Shifted Recurrence: Longest Common Subsequence (LCS)	dsa-longest-common-subsequence-dp-on-strings	Compute the length of the longest common subsequence across two strings using match/not-match transitions and 1-based index shifting.	2026-10-03 11:31:13.744054+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Two-String Index-Shifted Recurrence: Longest Common Subsequence (LCS)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NPZn9jBrX8U", "title": "Lecture 206: Longest Common Subsequence (DP on Strings - DP 25)", "duration": "26m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-common-subsequence/", "title": "Longest Common Subsequence (LC 1143)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given two strings text1 and text2, return the length of their longest common subsequence."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/uncrossed-lines/", "title": "Uncrossed Lines (LC 1035)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the maximum number of non-intersecting connecting lines between two arrays (identical to LCS!)."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/longest-common-subsequence-1587115620/1", "title": "Longest Common Subsequence", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the length of the longest common subsequence in two given strings."}]	Module 31: Dynamic Programming on Strings: LCS, Substrings & Palindromic Subsequences	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven two strings `s1` (length `N`) and `s2` (length `M`), find the length of the **Longest Common Subsequence (LCS)**—the longest sequence of characters that appears in the same relative left-to-right order in both strings (not necessarily contiguous).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Match vs. Not-Match Recurrence (`f(i, j)`):**\n  - Maintain index `i` in `s1` and index `j` in `s2`.\n  - **Base Case (`i < 0 || j < 0`):** An empty prefix has `0` common characters → `return 0`.\n  - **1. Characters Match (`s1[i] == s2[j]`):**\n    - Both characters match! Count `1` and move **both** indices back:\n      **`return 1 + f(i - 1, j - 1)`**.\n  - **2. Characters Do NOT Match (`s1[i] != s2[j]`):**\n    - Try shrinking `s1` (`f(i - 1, j)`) OR shrinking `s2` (`f(i, j - 1)`), and take the best of both:\n      **`return max(f(i - 1, j), f(i, j - 1))`**.\n- **The Right-Shifting Index Trick (`1`-Based Indexing for Tabulation):**\n  - In recursion, base cases happen at `i == -1` or `j == -1`, which cannot be indexed in a C++ array `dp[-1]`.\n  - **The Fix:** Shift all indices right by `+1` so index `i` in `dp[N + 1][M + 1]` represents character **`s1[i - 1]`**! Now the base case is cleanly **`i == 0 || j == 0` (`dp[i][0] = 0`, `dp[0][j] = 0`)**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * M)` Time and `O(M)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Longest Common Subsequence (Space Optimized) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint longestCommonSubsequence(string s1, string s2) {\n    int n = s1.size(), m = s2.size();\n    vector<int> prev(m + 1, 0), cur(m + 1, 0);\n\n    // Base case: row 0 (prev[j] = 0) and col 0 (cur[0] = 0) are already 0\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {\n            // Shifted index: i corresponds to s1[i - 1], j corresponds to s2[j - 1]\n            if (s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]) {\n                cur[j] = 1 + prev[j - 1];\n            } else {\n                cur[j] = max(prev[j], cur[j - 1]);\n            }\n        }\n        prev = cur;\n    }\n    return prev[m];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Comparing `s1[i] == s2[j]` Instead of `s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]` in Shifted Tabulation:** When looping `i` from `1` to `n` and `j` from `1` to `m`, always compare **`s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]`** so you don't skip index `0` and read `s1[n]` out of bounds!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22108	Lecture 23: Arithmetic Aggregation Operators — $add, $subtract, $multiply, $divide, $mod, $pow, $sqrt, $abs, $ceil, $floor, $round & $trunc	mongo-lecture-23-arithmetic-aggregation-operators	Perform mathematical calculations and decimal rounding inside $project and $addFields stages using $add, $subtract, $multiply, $divide, $mod, $pow, $sqrt, $abs, $ceil, $floor, $round, and $trunc.	2026-10-06 19:52:12.616986+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMongoDB **Arithmetic Expression Operators** perform mathematical computations on numeric fields (or static numbers) inside aggregation stages such as `$project`, `$addFields`, and `$group`.\n\n### 💡 Core Arithmetic & Exponential Operators\n* **`$add: [ <num1>, <num2>, ... `:** Adds two or more numbers (or field references like `["$price", "$quantity"`) together.\n* **`$subtract: [ <num1>, <num2> `:** Subtracts the second value from the first value (`num1 - num2`).\n* **`$multiply: [ <num1>, <num2>, ... `:** Multiplies two or more numbers together (e.g., computing order line-item totals via `["$price", "$quantity"`).\n* **`$divide: [ <dividend>, <divisor> `:** Divides the first number by the second number and returns the floating-point quotient.\n* **`$mod: [ <dividend>, <divisor> `:** Divides the first number by the second number and returns the **remainder** (e.g., `[10, 3` returns `1`).\n* **`$pow: [ <base>, <exponent> `:** Raises `<base>` to the power of `<exponent>` (e.g., `[2, 3` -> `2 * 2 * 2 = 8`).\n* **`$sqrt: <number>`:** Calculates the square root of a non-negative number (e.g., `{ $sqrt: 16 }` -> `4`).\n\n### 💡 Sign & Decimal Rounding Operators\n* **`$abs: <number>`:** Returns the unsigned absolute value of a number (e.g., `-9.2` -> `9.2`).\n* **`$ceil: <number>`:** Rounds a decimal number **up** to the smallest integer greater than or equal to the value (e.g., `9.2` -> `10`, `9.8` -> `10`).\n* **`$floor: <number>`:** Rounds a decimal number **down** to the largest integer less than or equal to the value (e.g., `9.8` -> `9`, `9.2` -> `9`).\n* **`$round: <number>`:** Rounds to the nearest integer (`.5` and above rounds up like `$ceil`; below `.5` rounds down like `$floor`).\n* **`$trunc: <number>`:** Truncates (strips) the fractional decimal digits without rounding (e.g., `4.8` -> `4`, `4.2` -> `4`).\n\n### 🛠️ Practical Arithmetic Pipeline Examples\n```javascript\n// 1. Compute sum, difference, and total cost ($multiply) on sales documents\ndb.sales.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      product: 1,\n      price: 1,\n      quantity: 1,\n      sumResult: { $add: ["$price", "$quantity" },\n      diffResult: { $subtract: ["$price", "$quantity" },\n      totalCost: { $multiply: ["$price", "$quantity" }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 2. Division, Modulo, Power, and Square Root\ndb.sales.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      quotient: { $divide: [10, 2 },   // 5\n      remainder: { $mod: [10, 3 },     // 1\n      cubed: { $pow: [2, 3 },          // 8\n      root16: { $sqrt: 16 }             // 4\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 3. Decimal Rounding & Sign Handling ($abs, $ceil, $floor, $round, $trunc)\ndb.sales.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      absoluteVal: { $abs: -9.2 },      // 9.2\n      ceilingVal: { $ceil: 9.2 },       // 10\n      floorVal: { $floor: 9.8 },        // 9\n      roundedUp: { $round: 9.8 },       // 10\n      roundedDown: { $round: 9.3 },     // 9\n      truncatedVal: { $trunc: 4.8 }     // 4\n    }\n  }\n)\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/#arithmetic-expression-operators", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/#arithmetic-expression-operators", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 23: Arithmetic Aggregation Operators"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RMKf2AS9r34", "title": "MongoDB Arithmetic Operators Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "20m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[{"id": "mongo-23-prob-1", "url": "