)) or array/object literals ([...arr1, ...arr2]) to unpack an iterable/object into individual elements."}, {"id": "js-lec-9-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why are Rest Parameters (...args) preferred over the legacy arguments object?", "title": "Q4: Why are Rest Parameters (...args) preferred over the legacy arguments object?", "answer": "1) arguments is an \\"array-like\\" object (it has indexed elements and a length property, but lacks Array.prototype methods like map, filter, or reduce), whereas ...args is a genuine Array instance. 2) arguments does not exist inside ES6 Arrow Functions, whereas ...args works in all functions. 3) ...args allows capturing only the remaining parameters after named parameters (e.g., function(first, ...rest)).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "9-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "1) arguments is an \\"array-like\\" object (it has indexed elements and a length property, but lacks Array.prototype methods like map, filter, or reduce), whereas ...args is a genuine Array instance. 2) arguments does not exist inside ES6 Arrow Functions, whereas ...args works in all functions. 3) ...args allows capturing only the remaining parameters after named parameters (e.g., function(first, ...rest))."}, {"id": "js-lec-9-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is an IIFE (Immediately Invoked Function Expression) and why is it used?", "title": "Q5: What is an IIFE (Immediately Invoked Function Expression) and why is it used?", "answer": "An IIFE is a function wrapped in parentheses and invoked immediately after its definition: (() => { ... })(). It is used to execute initialization logic immediately while creating a private scope so internal variables do not leak into or pollute the global scope.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "9-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "An IIFE is a function wrapped in parentheses and invoked immediately after its definition: (() => { ... })(). It is used to execute initialization logic immediately while creating a private scope so internal variables do not leak into or pollute the global scope."}, {"id": "js-lec-9-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: What is the difference between Function Parameters and Function Arguments, and when do Default Parameters trigger?", "title": "Q6: What is the difference between Function Parameters and Function Arguments, and when do Default Parameters trigger?", "answer": "Parameters are the placeholder variable names listed in the function definition (function add(a, b)), while Arguments are the actual values passed when invoking the function (add(10, 20)). A default parameter (function greet(name = \\"Guest\\")) triggers ONLY when the argument is omitted or explicitly passed as undefined—passing null (greet(null)) will NOT trigger the default value; name will remain null!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "9-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Parameters are the placeholder variable names listed in the function definition (function add(a, b)), while Arguments are the actual values passed when invoking the function (add(10, 20)). A default parameter (function greet(name = \\"Guest\\")) triggers ONLY when the argument is omitted or explicitly passed as undefined—passing null (greet(null)) will NOT trigger the default value; name will remain null!"}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Functions	\N	\N	\N
10018	Tortoise-Hare Midpoint & Second-Half Reversal: Check if a Linked List Is a Palindrome	dsa-check-if-linked-list-is-palindrome	Find the middle of a linked list, reverse the second half, compare node values in O(1) space, and restore the original list structure before returning.	2026-10-03 15:04:07.041438+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Tortoise-Hare Midpoint & Second-Half Reversal: Check if a Linked List Is a Palindrome\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lRY_G-u_8jk", "title": "Lecture 246: Check if a Linked List is Palindrome", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/palindrome-linked-list/", "title": "Palindrome Linked List (LC 234)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Given the head of a singly linked list, return true if it is a palindrome in O(N) time and O(1) space."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-twin-sum-of-a-linked-list/", "title": "Maximum Twin Sum of a Linked List (LC 2130)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use the exact same midpoint-finding + second-half reversal pattern to pair twin nodes in O(1) space."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/reorder-list/", "title": "Reorder List (LC 143)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the middle, reverse the second half, and interleave the two halves in-place."}]	Module 37: Linked List Medium Problems: Dummy Nodes, In-Place Re-Linking & Tortoise-Hare Pointers	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the `head` of a singly linked list, determine whether it is a **Palindrome** in **`O(N)` time and `O(1)` extra space**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Stopping `slow` at the First Middle (`m1`) Is Crucial for Even Lengths:**\n  - Start `slow = head, fast = head`.\n  - Loop **`while (fast->next != nullptr && fast->next->next != nullptr)`** (stopping when `fast` reaches the last node for odd length, or the **second-to-last node** for even length!).\n  - This guarantees `slow` lands at the **end of the first half** (`m1`), so `slow->next` is always the exact start of the second half!\n- **The 4-Step `O(1)` Space Algorithm:**\n  1. **Find Middle (`O(N / 2)`):** Stop `slow` at the end of the first half.\n  2. **Reverse Second Half (`O(N / 2)`):** Call `Node* newHead = reverseIterative(slow->next);`.\n  3. **Compare Both Halves (`O(N / 2)`):** Compare `first = head` and `second = newHead` while `second != nullptr`.\n  4. **Restore the Original List (`O(N / 2)` — Mandatory Interview Best Practice!):** Call `reverseIterative(newHead)` again before returning `true` or `false` so the caller's input data is never left mutated!\n\n### 💻 Check Palindrome Linked List (O(1) Space) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nNode* reverseLinkedList(Node* head) {\n    Node* temp = head;\n    Node* prev = nullptr;\n    while (temp != nullptr) {\n        Node* front = temp->next;\n        temp->next = prev;\n        prev = temp;\n        temp = front;\n    }\n    return prev;\n}\n\nbool isPalindrome(Node* head) {\n    if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr) return true;\n\n    // 1. Find the end of the first half (first middle for even-length lists)\n    Node* slow = head;\n    Node* fast = head;\n    while (fast->next != nullptr && fast->next->next != nullptr) {\n        slow = slow->next;\n        fast = fast->next->next;\n    }\n\n    // 2. Reverse the second half starting at slow->next\n    Node* newHead = reverseLinkedList(slow->next);\n\n    // 3. Compare the first half and the reversed second half\n    Node* first = head;\n    Node* second = newHead;\n    while (second != nullptr) {\n        if (first->data != second->data) {\n            reverseLinkedList(newHead); // Restore original list before returning!\n            return false;\n        }\n        first = first->next;\n        second = second->next;\n    }\n\n    // 4. Restore the second half back to its original order\n    reverseLinkedList(newHead);\n    return true;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting to Un-Reverse (`reverseLinkedList(newHead)`) Before Returning:** In production and interviews, mutating an input linked list inside a read-only `isPalindrome()` check is considered bad engineering practice. Always restore the second half before returning!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22191	Chain Rule	aptitude-quant-chain-rule	Master the MDH inversion formula, proportional consumption logic, troop provision exhaustion, and multi-unit worker efficiency conversions.	2026-10-07 11:47:07.968562+05:30	### 📌 1. The Core MDH (Chain Rule) Formula\n* **The Master Equation:** \n  **`(M1 × D1 × H1) / W1 = (M2 × D2 × H2) / W2`**\n  * **`M`** = Number of Men (or machines, pumps, animals)\n  * **`D`** = Number of Days\n  * **`H`** = Hours worked per day\n  * **`W`** = Amount of Work done (or wages earned, items consumed)\n* **Crucial Rule for Unquantifiable Work:** If the work is "building a house", "cleaning a pool", or "finishing a task", treat the total work as **`1`**. Therefore, `W1 = 1` and `W2 = 1`.\n\n### 💡 2. Cross-Multiplying Work States\n* A powerful shortcut derived from the MDH rule is cross-multiplying the work done in the opposite scenarios:\n  **`M1 × D1 × H1 × W2 = M2 × D2 × H2 × W1`**\n* Always put the **Work Done** of the *second* case on the *first* side, and the **Work Done** of the *first* case on the *second* side!\n\n### 💡 3. High-Yield Placement Shortcuts\n* **Men/Women/Boys Equivalency:**\n  If a problem states "3 men OR 4 women can do a work in `X` days", deduce that `3M = 4W`. Convert the target group (e.g., "6 men AND 8 women") entirely into a single unit before applying the MDH formula!\n* **Food Provision & Reinforcements:**\n  If a camp of `M1` men has food for `D` days, and after `d` days, `X` men join:\n  Remaining food originally would last `M1` men for `(D - d)` days.\n  Set up the equation: **`M1 × (D - d) = (M1 + X) × D_new`**. Solve for `D_new`.\n* **Proportional Consumption:**\n  If elements reduce their consumption rate by a percentage (e.g., eating 10% less), modify the "Efficiency" factor in the formula:\n  **`M1 × D1 × E1 = M2 × D2 × E2`** (where `E1 = 100%` and `E2 = 90%`).	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pYL2843EEuQ", "type": "youtube", "title": "Chain Rule - Shortcuts & Tricks for Placement Tests, Job Interviews & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-chain-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Direct MDH Formula Application] 40 boys paint a house in 96 days by working 9 hours per day. How many hours per day do 48 boys need to work to paint the identical house in 45 days?", "options": ["A) 12 hours", "B) 14 hours", "C) 15 hours", "D) 16 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 40 boys can completely paint a house in exactly 96 days by working 9 hours per day, exactly how many hours per day must 48 boys work so they can paint the exact same house in 45 days?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Use the formula M1 × D1 × H1 = M2 × D2 × H2 (since Work W1 = W2 = 1). 40 × 96 × 9 = 48 × 45 × H2 => 34560 = 2160 × H2 => H2 = 34560 / 2160 = 16 hours.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-chain-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Finding Missing Men via Fractional Work] 54 toy makers can prepare 36 toys per day. How many toy makers should be employed to make 416 toys in exactly 16 days?", "options": ["A) 36", "B) 39", "C) 42", "D) 45"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 54 toy makers can prepare exactly 36 toys per day, exactly how many toy makers should an employer hire to get an order of 416 toys completely done in exactly 16 days?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Scenario 1: M1 = 54, D1 = 1, W1 = 36. Scenario 2: M2 = ?, D2 = 16, W2 = 416. Formula: (M1 × D1) / W1 = (M2 × D2) / W2 => (54 × 1) / 36 = (M2 × 16) / 416. Left side = 54 / 36 = 1.5. So 1.5 = M2 × 16 / 416 => M2 = (1.5 × 416) / 16 = 624 / 16 = 39 toy makers.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-chain-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Provisions with Missing Men] 2,500 children had enough chocolates for 40 days. Some children never came, so the chocolates lasted 50 days. How many children never came?", "options": ["A) 400", "B) 450", "C) 500", "D) 600"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A group of 2,500 children in a school had enough chocolates to last for exactly 40 days. However, a certain number of children never came to school, causing the exact same stock of chocolates to last for 50 days. How many children never came to school?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let M2 be the number of children who actually came. The total \\"chocolate-days\\" remains constant. M1 × D1 = M2 × D2 => 2500 × 40 = M2 × 50 => 100,000 = 50 × M2 => M2 = 2000. So 2000 children attended. The number of children who never came = 2500 - 2000 = 500.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-chain-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Translating Equivalencies into MDH] 3 women can paint a house in 18 days and 6 children can do it in 18 days. How many days for 4 women and 4 children?", "options": ["A) 6 days", "B) 8 days", "C) 9 days", "D) 12 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 3 women alone can completely paint a house in exactly 18 days, and 6 children alone can do the same work in exactly 18 days, exactly how many days will 4 women and 4 children working together take to paint the identical house?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Since 3 women take the exact same time as 6 children, 3W = 6C => 1W = 2C. We are asked for 4W + 4C. Convert everything to children: 4W = 8C, so 4W + 4C = 12C. Scenario 1: 6 Children take 18 days. Scenario 2: 12 Children take D2 days. M1 × D1 = M2 × D2 => 6 × 18 = 12 × D2 => 108 = 12 × D2 => D2 = 9 days.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-chain-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Efficiency Deduction across Machines] 12 pumps of type A pump 30 L in 18h/day. 16 pumps of type B pump 40 L in 24h/day. How efficient is type A compared to type B?", "options": ["A) Type A is 1.5 times as efficient as Type B", "B) Type A is 4/3 times as efficient as Type B", "C) Type A is 3/4 times as efficient as Type B", "D) Type A is exactly equal to Type B"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly 12 pumps of one type (Type A) can pump exactly 30 liters of water when each is running for 18 hours per day. However, a set of 16 pumps of another type (Type B) pumps exactly 40 liters of water when each is running for 24 hours per day. How exactly is the pumping efficiency of a Type A pump mathematically related to a Type B pump?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Use MDH formula factoring in Efficiencies E_A and E_B: (M1 × H1 × E_A) / W1 = (M2 × H2 × E_B) / W2 => (12 × 18 × E_A) / 30 = (16 × 24 × E_B) / 40. Simplify left side: (216 / 30) × E_A = 7.2 × E_A. Simplify right side: (384 / 40) × E_B = 9.6 × E_B. So, 7.2 × E_A = 9.6 × E_B => E_A / E_B = 9.6 / 7.2 = 96 / 72 = 4 / 3. Thus, Type A is 4/3 times as efficient as Type B.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-chain-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Fractional Work Remaining] 75 girls complete 1/3 of decoration in 40 hours. With 50 hours left to finish the rest, how many MORE girls are needed?", "options": ["A) 25", "B) 35", "C) 45", "D) 120"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A group of 75 girls complete exactly 1/3 of a building's decoration in 40 hours. They are informed they only have exactly 50 hours left to complete the REST of the decoration. How many MORE girls are strictly necessary to help them completely finish the task on time?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Scenario 1: M1 = 75, H1 = 40, W1 = 1/3. Scenario 2: M2 = ?, H2 = 50, W2 = 2/3 (remaining work). Formula: (M1 × H1) / W1 = (M2 × H2) / W2 => (75 × 40) / (1/3) = (M2 × 50) / (2/3). Cross multiply by denominators: 3000 × 3 = 50 × M2 × (3/2) => 9000 = 75 × M2 => M2 = 9000 / 75 = 120 girls needed total. Since 75 girls are already present, they need 120 - 75 = 45 MORE girls.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-chain-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Speeding up Incomplete Task] 60 workers do 30% of work in 10 days out of a 20 day deadline. How many total workers needed for the remaining 10 days to finish on time?", "options": ["A) 80", "B) 100", "C) 120", "D) 140"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Rohan wants to build a tank in exactly 20 days, for which he initially hires 60 workers. After 10 days, he discovers that only 30% of the work has been completed. To speed up the process and get the tank completely built strictly within the original stipulated 20-day timeframe, how many TOTAL workers should work on the tank for the remaining period?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Scenario 1: M1 = 60, D1 = 10, W1 = 30%. Scenario 2: M2 = ?, D2 = 10 (remaining days), W2 = 70% (remaining work). Formula: (M1 × D1) / W1 = (M2 × D2) / W2 => (60 × 10) / 30 = (M2 × 10) / 70 => 600 / 30 = 10 × M2 / 70 => 20 = M2 / 7 => M2 = 140. He needs 140 total workers (which means adding 80 more).", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-chain-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Proportional Rate Deduction] 72 patients consume a tank of oxygen in 54 hours. If consumption is reduced by 10%, how many hours will the tank last for 90 patients?", "options": ["A) 45 hours", "B) 48 hours", "C) 50 hours", "D) 54 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A hospital tank has enough oxygen to strictly last for exactly 54 hours for 72 patients. If every patient suddenly reduces their average oxygen consumption rate by exactly 10%, for how many exact hours will the identical full tank supply exactly 90 patients?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let initial consumption rate per patient be 100%. Total capacity = 72 patients × 54 hours × 100% = 388,800 units. Scenario 2: Consumption rate is now 90%. So, 90 patients × H hours × 90% = 388,800. 8100 × H = 388800 => H = 388800 / 8100 = 3888 / 81 = 48 hours.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-chain-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Money Distribution in Chain Rule] 15 men working 8 hours a day earn ₹3000 in 10 days. How much will 20 men working 6 hours a day earn in 15 days?", "options": ["A) ₹3,500", "B) ₹4,000", "C) ₹4,500", "D) ₹5,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 15 men working 8 hours a day can earn exactly ₹3,000 in exactly 10 days, what exact amount of money will 20 men earn in exactly 15 days if they work exactly 6 hours a day?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "In the MDH formula, wages earned act identically to \\"Work Done\\". (M1 × D1 × H1) / Wages1 = (M2 × D2 × H2) / Wages2. (15 × 10 × 8) / 3000 = (20 × 15 × 6) / Wages2. Left side = 1200 / 3000 = 0.4. Right side = 1800 / Wages2. So 0.4 = 1800 / Wages2 => Wages2 = 1800 / 0.4 = ₹4,500.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-chain-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Length, Breadth, Depth as Work] 20 men can dig a trench 12m long, 5m broad, and 3m deep in 10 days. How many men are needed to dig a trench 15m long, 4m broad, and 4m deep in 8 days?", "options": ["A) 24 men", "B) 30 men", "C) 33 1/3 men", "D) 36 men"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If exactly 20 men can dig a trench that is 12m long, 5m broad, and 3m deep in exactly 10 days, exactly how many men are required to dig a trench that is 15m long, 4m broad, and 4m deep in exactly 8 days?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Work is the total volume excavated. W1 = 12 × 5 × 3 = 180 cubic meters. W2 = 15 × 4 × 4 = 240 cubic meters. (M1 × D1) / W1 = (M2 × D2) / W2 => (20 × 10) / 180 = (M2 × 8) / 240 => 200 / 180 = 8 × M2 / 240 => 10/9 = M2 / 30 => M2 = 300 / 9 = 33 1/3 men.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-chain-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Running Cost of Engines] If 8 engines consume 24 metric tonnes of coal when running 10 hours a day, how much coal will 6 engines running 14 hours a day consume, assuming 3 engines of former type consume as much as 4 engines of latter type?", "options": ["A) 16.8 tonnes", "B) 18.9 tonnes", "C) 21.0 tonnes", "D) 25.2 tonnes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 8 engines completely consume 24 metric tonnes of coal when each is running 10 hours a day, exactly how many metric tonnes of coal will be consumed by 6 engines running 14 hours a day, given that 3 engines of the former type strictly consume as much as exactly 4 engines of the latter type?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "We have two types of engines. 3 E_A = 4 E_B => E_B = (3/4) E_A. Scenario 1: M1 = 8 E_A, H1 = 10, W1 = 24. Scenario 2: M2 = 6 E_B = 6(3/4) E_A = 4.5 E_A, H2 = 14, W2 = ?. Formula: (M1 × H1) / W1 = (M2 × H2) / W2 => (8 × 10) / 24 = (4.5 × 14) / W2 => 80 / 24 = 63 / W2 => 10/3 = 63 / W2 => W2 = 189 / 10 = 18.9 tonnes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-chain-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Soldiers Leaving Camp with Shifted Provisions] A garrison of 2000 men has provisions for 54 days. After 15 days, some men left the garrison and the remaining provisions lasted for an extra 20 days (i.e. 59 days). How many men left?", "options": ["A) 500", "B) 678", "C) 1322", "D) 1500"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A military garrison initially comprising 2,000 men has provisions safely budgeted to last for exactly 54 days. Exactly 15 days later, a certain number of men leave the garrison. Consequently, the remaining provisions are stretched to last for exactly 59 days. How many men left the garrison?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "After 15 days, the remaining food would normally last the 2,000 men for (54 - 15) = 39 days. So, the total remaining \\"man-days\\" of food = 2000 × 39 = 78,000 man-days. Let M2 be the new number of men. The food lasted for 59 days. M2 × 59 = 78,000 => M2 = 78000 / 59 ≈ 1322 men remaining. Therefore, the number of men who left = 2000 - 1322 = 678.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-chain-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Contractor Penalty Multiplier] A contractor agrees to do a work in 30 days and employs 15 men. After 10 days, he finds only 1/4 of work is done. How many extra men must he hire to finish strictly on time?", "options": ["A) 7.5 (or 8) men", "B) 15 men", "C) 20 men", "D) 22 men"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A contractor agrees to complete a construction work in exactly 30 days and initially employs 15 men for the job. Exactly 10 days later, he inspects the site and finds that only exactly 1/4 of the total work has been done. How many EXTRA men must he urgently employ to ensure the work is finished strictly within the originally agreed timeframe?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Scenario 1: M1 = 15, D1 = 10, W1 = 1/4. Scenario 2: M2 = ?, D2 = 20 (remaining days), W2 = 3/4 (remaining work). Formula: (15 × 10) / (1/4) = (M2 × 20) / (3/4) => 150 × 4 = (M2 × 20) × (4/3) => 600 = 80 × M2 / 3 => 1800 = 80 × M2 => M2 = 1800 / 80 = 22.5 men total. Since he currently has 15 men, he must hire 22.5 - 15 = 7.5 extra men (or practically 8).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-chain-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Cows Eating Grass with Constant Growth] If 10 cows can eat a field of grass in 20 days, and 15 cows can eat it in 10 days, how many cows can eat it in 5 days? (Grass grows uniformly)", "options": ["A) 20", "B) 25", "C) 30", "D) 35"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 10 cows can completely graze a field of grass in exactly 20 days, and 15 cows can graze the identical field in exactly 10 days (assuming the grass is constantly growing at a uniform daily rate), exactly how many cows are required to graze the entire field in exactly 5 days?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let initial grass = G, growth rate per day = r, cow eating rate = c per day. Eq 1: 10c × 20 = G + 20r => 200c = G + 20r. Eq 2: 15c × 10 = G + 10r => 150c = G + 10r. Subtract Eq 2 from Eq 1: 50c = 10r => r = 5c. Substitute r back: 150c = G + 50c => G = 100c. Scenario 3: N cows in 5 days. Nc × 5 = G + 5r => 5Nc = 100c + 5(5c) => 5Nc = 100c + 25c = 125c. Divide by 5c: N = 25 cows.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-chain-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Typists Working Different Page Formats] 4 typists type 400 pages of 20 lines each with 10 words per line in 10 days. How many typists are needed to type 800 pages of 25 lines each with 12 words per line in 12 days?", "options": ["A) 8 typists", "B) 10 typists", "C) 12 typists", "D) 15 typists"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly 4 typists can type exactly 400 pages, each containing 20 lines with 10 words per line, in exactly 10 days. Exactly how many identically skilled typists are required to type exactly 800 pages, each containing 25 lines with 12 words per line, in exactly 12 days?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Work is the total number of words typed! W1 = 400 pages × 20 lines/page × 10 words/line = 80,000 words. W2 = 800 × 25 × 12 = 240,000 words. (M1 × D1) / W1 = (M2 × D2) / W2 => (4 × 10) / 80,000 = (M2 × 12) / 240,000 => 40 / 80 = 12 × M2 / 240 => 1/2 = M2 / 20 => M2 = 10 typists.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-chain-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Two Groups of Animals Equivalency] If 5 horses eat as much as 7 oxen, and 14 oxen eat as much as 20 sheep. How many horses eat as much as 40 sheep?", "options": ["A) 10 horses", "B) 15 horses", "C) 20 horses", "D) 25 horses"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If exactly 5 horses consume as much food as exactly 7 oxen, and exactly 14 oxen consume as much food as exactly 20 sheep, exactly how many horses will mathematically consume as much food as exactly 40 sheep?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "We have 5H = 7O => 1O = 5/7 H. And 14O = 20S => 1S = 14/20 O = 7/10 O. Substitute O: 1S = (7/10) × (5/7) H = 1/2 H. This means 1 sheep eats as much as half a horse (or 2 sheep = 1 horse). Therefore, 40 sheep eat as much as 40 × (1/2) = 20 horses.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-chain-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Inversely Proportional Time with Squared Output] Machines A produce x units in y hours. If their efficiency drops by half, how many hours for 3A machines to produce x^2 units?", "options": ["A) 2xy / 3", "B) 3xy / 2", "C) xy / 6", "D) xy^2 / 3"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A group of exactly A machines can produce exactly x units in exactly y hours. If their operational efficiency suddenly drops by exactly half, exactly how many hours will it mathematically take for a group of 3A machines to produce exactly x^2 units?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Scenario 1: M1 = A, H1 = y, E1 = 1, W1 = x. Scenario 2: M2 = 3A, H2 = ?, E2 = 0.5, W2 = x^2. Formula: (M1 × H1 × E1) / W1 = (M2 × H2 × E2) / W2 => (A × y × 1) / x = (3A × H2 × 0.5) / x^2. Cancel A from both sides: y / x = 1.5 × H2 / x^2. Multiply both sides by x^2: x y = 1.5 × H2 => H2 = xy / 1.5 = xy / (3/2) = 2xy / 3 hours.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-chain-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Fractional Output Over Time] If 9 monkeys eat 9 bananas in 9 minutes, how many minutes will it take 3 monkeys to eat 3 bananas?", "options": ["A) 1 minute", "B) 3 minutes", "C) 9 minutes", "D) 27 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If exactly 9 monkeys can collectively eat exactly 9 bananas in exactly 9 minutes, exactly how many minutes will it take for exactly 3 monkeys to collectively eat exactly 3 bananas?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Scenario 1: M1 = 9, W1 = 9, D1 = 9. Scenario 2: M2 = 3, W2 = 3, D2 = ?. (M1 × D1) / W1 = (M2 × D2) / W2 => (9 × 9) / 9 = (3 × D2) / 3 => 9 = D2. It still takes 9 minutes! (Logically, each individual monkey takes 9 minutes to eat 1 banana).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-chain-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Worker Transfer to Prevent Default] 30 men agree to finish a job in 40 days. After 24 days, they have done only 1/2 of the work. How many more men must be employed to finish on time?", "options": ["A) 15 men", "B) 20 men", "C) 45 men", "D) 50 men"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A contractor employs 30 men who agree to finish a job in exactly 40 days. After exactly 24 days, the contractor observes that only exactly 1/2 (half) of the work has been finished. How many MORE men must be urgently employed to completely finish the work strictly on time?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Scenario 1: M1 = 30, D1 = 24, W1 = 1/2. Scenario 2: M2 = ?, D2 = 16 (40 - 24 remaining days), W2 = 1/2 (remaining work). Formula: (30 × 24) / 0.5 = (M2 × 16) / 0.5 => 30 × 24 = M2 × 16 => 720 = 16 × M2 => M2 = 720 / 16 = 45 total men needed. Since 30 men are already working, he needs 45 - 30 = 15 MORE men.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-chain-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Length of Wall Extension] 8 men can build a wall 120m long in 6 days. How long will a wall built by 16 men in 9 days be?", "options": ["A) 240 meters", "B) 300 meters", "C) 360 meters", "D) 420 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If exactly 8 men can build a straight brick wall that is exactly 120 meters long in exactly 6 days, exactly how many meters long will a similar wall be if it is built by 16 men in exactly 9 days?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Work = Length of the wall. M1 = 8, D1 = 6, W1 = 120. M2 = 16, D2 = 9, W2 = ?. (8 × 6) / 120 = (16 × 9) / W2 => 48 / 120 = 144 / W2 => 1 / 2.5 = 144 / W2 => W2 = 144 × 2.5 = 360 meters.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. The Core MDH (Chain Rule) Formula\n* **The Master Equation:** \n  **`(M1 × D1 × H1) / W1 = (M2 × D2 × H2) / W2`**\n  * **`M`** = Number of Men (or machines, pumps, animals)\n  * **`D`** = Number of Days\n  * **`H`** = Hours worked per day\n  * **`W`** = Amount of Work done (or wages earned, items consumed)\n* **Crucial Rule for Unquantifiable Work:** If the work is "building a house", "cleaning a pool", or "finishing a task", treat the total work as **`1`**. Therefore, `W1 = 1` and `W2 = 1`.\n\n### 💡 2. Cross-Multiplying Work States\n* A powerful shortcut derived from the MDH rule is cross-multiplying the work done in the opposite scenarios:\n  **`M1 × D1 × H1 × W2 = M2 × D2 × H2 × W1`**\n* Always put the **Work Done** of the *second* case on the *first* side, and the **Work Done** of the *first* case on the *second* side!\n\n### 💡 3. High-Yield Placement Shortcuts\n* **Men/Women/Boys Equivalency:**\n  If a problem states "3 men OR 4 women can do a work in `X` days", deduce that `3M = 4W`. Convert the target group (e.g., "6 men AND 8 women") entirely into a single unit before applying the MDH formula!\n* **Food Provision & Reinforcements:**\n  If a camp of `M1` men has food for `D` days, and after `d` days, `X` men join:\n  Remaining food originally would last `M1` men for `(D - d)` days.\n  Set up the equation: **`M1 × (D - d) = (M1 + X) × D_new`**. Solve for `D_new`.\n* **Proportional Consumption:**\n  If elements reduce their consumption rate by a percentage (e.g., eating 10% less), modify the "Efficiency" factor in the formula:\n  **`M1 × D1 × E1 = M2 × D2 × E2`** (where `E1 = 100%` and `E2 = 90%`).	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Chain Rule	Master the MDH inversion formula, proportional consumption logic, troop provision exhaustion, and multi-unit worker efficiency conversions.	30	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	25	[{"id": "apt-chain-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Direct MDH Formula Application] 40 boys paint a house in 96 days by working 9 hours per day. How many hours per day do 48 boys need to work to paint the identical house in 45 days?", "options": ["A) 12 hours", "B) 14 hours", "C) 15 hours", "D) 16 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 40 boys can completely paint a house in exactly 96 days by working 9 hours per day, exactly how many hours per day must 48 boys work so they can paint the exact same house in 45 days?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Use the formula M1 × D1 × H1 = M2 × D2 × H2 (since Work W1 = W2 = 1). 40 × 96 × 9 = 48 × 45 × H2 => 34560 = 2160 × H2 => H2 = 34560 / 2160 = 16 hours.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-chain-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Finding Missing Men via Fractional Work] 54 toy makers can prepare 36 toys per day. How many toy makers should be employed to make 416 toys in exactly 16 days?", "options": ["A) 36", "B) 39", "C) 42", "D) 45"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 54 toy makers can prepare exactly 36 toys per day, exactly how many toy makers should an employer hire to get an order of 416 toys completely done in exactly 16 days?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Scenario 1: M1 = 54, D1 = 1, W1 = 36. Scenario 2: M2 = ?, D2 = 16, W2 = 416. Formula: (M1 × D1) / W1 = (M2 × D2) / W2 => (54 × 1) / 36 = (M2 × 16) / 416. Left side = 54 / 36 = 1.5. So 1.5 = M2 × 16 / 416 => M2 = (1.5 × 416) / 16 = 624 / 16 = 39 toy makers.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-chain-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Provisions with Missing Men] 2,500 children had enough chocolates for 40 days. Some children never came, so the chocolates lasted 50 days. How many children never came?", "options": ["A) 400", "B) 450", "C) 500", "D) 600"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A group of 2,500 children in a school had enough chocolates to last for exactly 40 days. However, a certain number of children never came to school, causing the exact same stock of chocolates to last for 50 days. How many children never came to school?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let M2 be the number of children who actually came. The total \\"chocolate-days\\" remains constant. M1 × D1 = M2 × D2 => 2500 × 40 = M2 × 50 => 100,000 = 50 × M2 => M2 = 2000. So 2000 children attended. The number of children who never came = 2500 - 2000 = 500.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-chain-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Translating Equivalencies into MDH] 3 women can paint a house in 18 days and 6 children can do it in 18 days. How many days for 4 women and 4 children?", "options": ["A) 6 days", "B) 8 days", "C) 9 days", "D) 12 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 3 women alone can completely paint a house in exactly 18 days, and 6 children alone can do the same work in exactly 18 days, exactly how many days will 4 women and 4 children working together take to paint the identical house?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Since 3 women take the exact same time as 6 children, 3W = 6C => 1W = 2C. We are asked for 4W + 4C. Convert everything to children: 4W = 8C, so 4W + 4C = 12C. Scenario 1: 6 Children take 18 days. Scenario 2: 12 Children take D2 days. M1 × D1 = M2 × D2 => 6 × 18 = 12 × D2 => 108 = 12 × D2 => D2 = 9 days.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-chain-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Efficiency Deduction across Machines] 12 pumps of type A pump 30 L in 18h/day. 16 pumps of type B pump 40 L in 24h/day. How efficient is type A compared to type B?", "options": ["A) Type A is 1.5 times as efficient as Type B", "B) Type A is 4/3 times as efficient as Type B", "C) Type A is 3/4 times as efficient as Type B", "D) Type A is exactly equal to Type B"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly 12 pumps of one type (Type A) can pump exactly 30 liters of water when each is running for 18 hours per day. However, a set of 16 pumps of another type (Type B) pumps exactly 40 liters of water when each is running for 24 hours per day. How exactly is the pumping efficiency of a Type A pump mathematically related to a Type B pump?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Use MDH formula factoring in Efficiencies E_A and E_B: (M1 × H1 × E_A) / W1 = (M2 × H2 × E_B) / W2 => (12 × 18 × E_A) / 30 = (16 × 24 × E_B) / 40. Simplify left side: (216 / 30) × E_A = 7.2 × E_A. Simplify right side: (384 / 40) × E_B = 9.6 × E_B. So, 7.2 × E_A = 9.6 × E_B => E_A / E_B = 9.6 / 7.2 = 96 / 72 = 4 / 3. Thus, Type A is 4/3 times as efficient as Type B.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-chain-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Fractional Work Remaining] 75 girls complete 1/3 of decoration in 40 hours. With 50 hours left to finish the rest, how many MORE girls are needed?", "options": ["A) 25", "B) 35", "C) 45", "D) 120"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A group of 75 girls complete exactly 1/3 of a building's decoration in 40 hours. They are informed they only have exactly 50 hours left to complete the REST of the decoration. How many MORE girls are strictly necessary to help them completely finish the task on time?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Scenario 1: M1 = 75, H1 = 40, W1 = 1/3. Scenario 2: M2 = ?, H2 = 50, W2 = 2/3 (remaining work). Formula: (M1 × H1) / W1 = (M2 × H2) / W2 => (75 × 40) / (1/3) = (M2 × 50) / (2/3). Cross multiply by denominators: 3000 × 3 = 50 × M2 × (3/2) => 9000 = 75 × M2 => M2 = 9000 / 75 = 120 girls needed total. Since 75 girls are already present, they need 120 - 75 = 45 MORE girls.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-chain-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Speeding up Incomplete Task] 60 workers do 30% of work in 10 days out of a 20 day deadline. How many total workers needed for the remaining 10 days to finish on time?", "options": ["A) 80", "B) 100", "C) 120", "D) 140"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Rohan wants to build a tank in exactly 20 days, for which he initially hires 60 workers. After 10 days, he discovers that only 30% of the work has been completed. To speed up the process and get the tank completely built strictly within the original stipulated 20-day timeframe, how many TOTAL workers should work on the tank for the remaining period?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Scenario 1: M1 = 60, D1 = 10, W1 = 30%. Scenario 2: M2 = ?, D2 = 10 (remaining days), W2 = 70% (remaining work). Formula: (M1 × D1) / W1 = (M2 × D2) / W2 => (60 × 10) / 30 = (M2 × 10) / 70 => 600 / 30 = 10 × M2 / 70 => 20 = M2 / 7 => M2 = 140. He needs 140 total workers (which means adding 80 more).", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-chain-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Proportional Rate Deduction] 72 patients consume a tank of oxygen in 54 hours. If consumption is reduced by 10%, how many hours will the tank last for 90 patients?", "options": ["A) 45 hours", "B) 48 hours", "C) 50 hours", "D) 54 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A hospital tank has enough oxygen to strictly last for exactly 54 hours for 72 patients. If every patient suddenly reduces their average oxygen consumption rate by exactly 10%, for how many exact hours will the identical full tank supply exactly 90 patients?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let initial consumption rate per patient be 100%. Total capacity = 72 patients × 54 hours × 100% = 388,800 units. Scenario 2: Consumption rate is now 90%. So, 90 patients × H hours × 90% = 388,800. 8100 × H = 388800 => H = 388800 / 8100 = 3888 / 81 = 48 hours.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-chain-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Money Distribution in Chain Rule] 15 men working 8 hours a day earn ₹3000 in 10 days. How much will 20 men working 6 hours a day earn in 15 days?", "options": ["A) ₹3,500", "B) ₹4,000", "C) ₹4,500", "D) ₹5,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 15 men working 8 hours a day can earn exactly ₹3,000 in exactly 10 days, what exact amount of money will 20 men earn in exactly 15 days if they work exactly 6 hours a day?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "In the MDH formula, wages earned act identically to \\"Work Done\\". (M1 × D1 × H1) / Wages1 = (M2 × D2 × H2) / Wages2. (15 × 10 × 8) / 3000 = (20 × 15 × 6) / Wages2. Left side = 1200 / 3000 = 0.4. Right side = 1800 / Wages2. So 0.4 = 1800 / Wages2 => Wages2 = 1800 / 0.4 = ₹4,500.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-chain-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Length, Breadth, Depth as Work] 20 men can dig a trench 12m long, 5m broad, and 3m deep in 10 days. How many men are needed to dig a trench 15m long, 4m broad, and 4m deep in 8 days?", "options": ["A) 24 men", "B) 30 men", "C) 33 1/3 men", "D) 36 men"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If exactly 20 men can dig a trench that is 12m long, 5m broad, and 3m deep in exactly 10 days, exactly how many men are required to dig a trench that is 15m long, 4m broad, and 4m deep in exactly 8 days?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Work is the total volume excavated. W1 = 12 × 5 × 3 = 180 cubic meters. W2 = 15 × 4 × 4 = 240 cubic meters. (M1 × D1) / W1 = (M2 × D2) / W2 => (20 × 10) / 180 = (M2 × 8) / 240 => 200 / 180 = 8 × M2 / 240 => 10/9 = M2 / 30 => M2 = 300 / 9 = 33 1/3 men.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-chain-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Running Cost of Engines] If 8 engines consume 24 metric tonnes of coal when running 10 hours a day, how much coal will 6 engines running 14 hours a day consume, assuming 3 engines of former type consume as much as 4 engines of latter type?", "options": ["A) 16.8 tonnes", "B) 18.9 tonnes", "C) 21.0 tonnes", "D) 25.2 tonnes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 8 engines completely consume 24 metric tonnes of coal when each is running 10 hours a day, exactly how many metric tonnes of coal will be consumed by 6 engines running 14 hours a day, given that 3 engines of the former type strictly consume as much as exactly 4 engines of the latter type?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "We have two types of engines. 3 E_A = 4 E_B => E_B = (3/4) E_A. Scenario 1: M1 = 8 E_A, H1 = 10, W1 = 24. Scenario 2: M2 = 6 E_B = 6(3/4) E_A = 4.5 E_A, H2 = 14, W2 = ?. Formula: (M1 × H1) / W1 = (M2 × H2) / W2 => (8 × 10) / 24 = (4.5 × 14) / W2 => 80 / 24 = 63 / W2 => 10/3 = 63 / W2 => W2 = 189 / 10 = 18.9 tonnes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-chain-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Soldiers Leaving Camp with Shifted Provisions] A garrison of 2000 men has provisions for 54 days. After 15 days, some men left the garrison and the remaining provisions lasted for an extra 20 days (i.e. 59 days). How many men left?", "options": ["A) 500", "B) 678", "C) 1322", "D) 1500"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A military garrison initially comprising 2,000 men has provisions safely budgeted to last for exactly 54 days. Exactly 15 days later, a certain number of men leave the garrison. Consequently, the remaining provisions are stretched to last for exactly 59 days. How many men left the garrison?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "After 15 days, the remaining food would normally last the 2,000 men for (54 - 15) = 39 days. So, the total remaining \\"man-days\\" of food = 2000 × 39 = 78,000 man-days. Let M2 be the new number of men. The food lasted for 59 days. M2 × 59 = 78,000 => M2 = 78000 / 59 ≈ 1322 men remaining. Therefore, the number of men who left = 2000 - 1322 = 678.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-chain-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Contractor Penalty Multiplier] A contractor agrees to do a work in 30 days and employs 15 men. After 10 days, he finds only 1/4 of work is done. How many extra men must he hire to finish strictly on time?", "options": ["A) 7.5 (or 8) men", "B) 15 men", "C) 20 men", "D) 22 men"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A contractor agrees to complete a construction work in exactly 30 days and initially employs 15 men for the job. Exactly 10 days later, he inspects the site and finds that only exactly 1/4 of the total work has been done. How many EXTRA men must he urgently employ to ensure the work is finished strictly within the originally agreed timeframe?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Scenario 1: M1 = 15, D1 = 10, W1 = 1/4. Scenario 2: M2 = ?, D2 = 20 (remaining days), W2 = 3/4 (remaining work). Formula: (15 × 10) / (1/4) = (M2 × 20) / (3/4) => 150 × 4 = (M2 × 20) × (4/3) => 600 = 80 × M2 / 3 => 1800 = 80 × M2 => M2 = 1800 / 80 = 22.5 men total. Since he currently has 15 men, he must hire 22.5 - 15 = 7.5 extra men (or practically 8).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-chain-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Cows Eating Grass with Constant Growth] If 10 cows can eat a field of grass in 20 days, and 15 cows can eat it in 10 days, how many cows can eat it in 5 days? (Grass grows uniformly)", "options": ["A) 20", "B) 25", "C) 30", "D) 35"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 10 cows can completely graze a field of grass in exactly 20 days, and 15 cows can graze the identical field in exactly 10 days (assuming the grass is constantly growing at a uniform daily rate), exactly how many cows are required to graze the entire field in exactly 5 days?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let initial grass = G, growth rate per day = r, cow eating rate = c per day. Eq 1: 10c × 20 = G + 20r => 200c = G + 20r. Eq 2: 15c × 10 = G + 10r => 150c = G + 10r. Subtract Eq 2 from Eq 1: 50c = 10r => r = 5c. Substitute r back: 150c = G + 50c => G = 100c. Scenario 3: N cows in 5 days. Nc × 5 = G + 5r => 5Nc = 100c + 5(5c) => 5Nc = 100c + 25c = 125c. Divide by 5c: N = 25 cows.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-chain-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Typists Working Different Page Formats] 4 typists type 400 pages of 20 lines each with 10 words per line in 10 days. How many typists are needed to type 800 pages of 25 lines each with 12 words per line in 12 days?", "options": ["A) 8 typists", "B) 10 typists", "C) 12 typists", "D) 15 typists"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly 4 typists can type exactly 400 pages, each containing 20 lines with 10 words per line, in exactly 10 days. Exactly how many identically skilled typists are required to type exactly 800 pages, each containing 25 lines with 12 words per line, in exactly 12 days?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Work is the total number of words typed! W1 = 400 pages × 20 lines/page × 10 words/line = 80,000 words. W2 = 800 × 25 × 12 = 240,000 words. (M1 × D1) / W1 = (M2 × D2) / W2 => (4 × 10) / 80,000 = (M2 × 12) / 240,000 => 40 / 80 = 12 × M2 / 240 => 1/2 = M2 / 20 => M2 = 10 typists.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-chain-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Two Groups of Animals Equivalency] If 5 horses eat as much as 7 oxen, and 14 oxen eat as much as 20 sheep. How many horses eat as much as 40 sheep?", "options": ["A) 10 horses", "B) 15 horses", "C) 20 horses", "D) 25 horses"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If exactly 5 horses consume as much food as exactly 7 oxen, and exactly 14 oxen consume as much food as exactly 20 sheep, exactly how many horses will mathematically consume as much food as exactly 40 sheep?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "We have 5H = 7O => 1O = 5/7 H. And 14O = 20S => 1S = 14/20 O = 7/10 O. Substitute O: 1S = (7/10) × (5/7) H = 1/2 H. This means 1 sheep eats as much as half a horse (or 2 sheep = 1 horse). Therefore, 40 sheep eat as much as 40 × (1/2) = 20 horses.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-chain-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Inversely Proportional Time with Squared Output] Machines A produce x units in y hours. If their efficiency drops by half, how many hours for 3A machines to produce x^2 units?", "options": ["A) 2xy / 3", "B) 3xy / 2", "C) xy / 6", "D) xy^2 / 3"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A group of exactly A machines can produce exactly x units in exactly y hours. If their operational efficiency suddenly drops by exactly half, exactly how many hours will it mathematically take for a group of 3A machines to produce exactly x^2 units?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Scenario 1: M1 = A, H1 = y, E1 = 1, W1 = x. Scenario 2: M2 = 3A, H2 = ?, E2 = 0.5, W2 = x^2. Formula: (M1 × H1 × E1) / W1 = (M2 × H2 × E2) / W2 => (A × y × 1) / x = (3A × H2 × 0.5) / x^2. Cancel A from both sides: y / x = 1.5 × H2 / x^2. Multiply both sides by x^2: x y = 1.5 × H2 => H2 = xy / 1.5 = xy / (3/2) = 2xy / 3 hours.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-chain-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Fractional Output Over Time] If 9 monkeys eat 9 bananas in 9 minutes, how many minutes will it take 3 monkeys to eat 3 bananas?", "options": ["A) 1 minute", "B) 3 minutes", "C) 9 minutes", "D) 27 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If exactly 9 monkeys can collectively eat exactly 9 bananas in exactly 9 minutes, exactly how many minutes will it take for exactly 3 monkeys to collectively eat exactly 3 bananas?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Scenario 1: M1 = 9, W1 = 9, D1 = 9. Scenario 2: M2 = 3, W2 = 3, D2 = ?. (M1 × D1) / W1 = (M2 × D2) / W2 => (9 × 9) / 9 = (3 × D2) / 3 => 9 = D2. It still takes 9 minutes! (Logically, each individual monkey takes 9 minutes to eat 1 banana).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-chain-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Worker Transfer to Prevent Default] 30 men agree to finish a job in 40 days. After 24 days, they have done only 1/2 of the work. How many more men must be employed to finish on time?", "options": ["A) 15 men", "B) 20 men", "C) 45 men", "D) 50 men"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A contractor employs 30 men who agree to finish a job in exactly 40 days. After exactly 24 days, the contractor observes that only exactly 1/2 (half) of the work has been finished. How many MORE men must be urgently employed to completely finish the work strictly on time?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Scenario 1: M1 = 30, D1 = 24, W1 = 1/2. Scenario 2: M2 = ?, D2 = 16 (40 - 24 remaining days), W2 = 1/2 (remaining work). Formula: (30 × 24) / 0.5 = (M2 × 16) / 0.5 => 30 × 24 = M2 × 16 => 720 = 16 × M2 => M2 = 720 / 16 = 45 total men needed. Since 30 men are already working, he needs 45 - 30 = 15 MORE men.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-chain-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Length of Wall Extension] 8 men can build a wall 120m long in 6 days. How long will a wall built by 16 men in 9 days be?", "options": ["A) 240 meters", "B) 300 meters", "C) 360 meters", "D) 420 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If exactly 8 men can build a straight brick wall that is exactly 120 meters long in exactly 6 days, exactly how many meters long will a similar wall be if it is built by 16 men in exactly 9 days?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Work = Length of the wall. M1 = 8, D1 = 6, W1 = 120. M2 = 16, D2 = 9, W2 = ?. (8 × 6) / 120 = (16 × 9) / W2 => 48 / 120 = 144 / W2 => 1 / 2.5 = 144 / W2 => W2 = 144 × 2.5 = 360 meters.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
14164	Three-Phase Linear Interval Partitioning: Insert Interval	dsa-insert-interval-greedy-three-phase-scan	Master Three-Phase Linear Interval Partitioning: Insert Interval with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:24:23.645532+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Three-Phase Linear Interval Partitioning: Insert Interval\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xxRE-46OCC8", "title": "Three-Phase Linear Interval Partitioning: Insert Interval - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/insert-interval/", "title": "LeetCode 57: Insert Interval", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Insert newInterval into a sorted list of non-overlapping intervals such that intervals remains sorted and non-overlapping (merging if necessary)."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-intervals/", "title": "LeetCode 56: Merge Intervals", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given an array of intervals, merge all overlapping intervals and return an array of the non-overlapping intervals."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/range-module/", "title": "LeetCode 715: Range Module", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Maintain a dynamic collection of disjoint half-open intervals supporting add, query, and remove operations."}]	Module 41: Greedy Algorithms: Sorting Heuristics, Reachability Horizons & Interval Scheduling	### 📌 Topic Overview\nYou are given an array of non-overlapping `intervals` **already sorted in ascending order by start time**, and a `newInterval = [start, end]`. Insert `newInterval` into `intervals` such that the result remains sorted and non-overlapping (merging any overlapping intervals in-place along the way).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why We Don't Need `O(N log N)` Sorting:**\n  - Because `intervals` is **already sorted and non-overlapping**, inserting `newInterval` splits the array into **3 contiguous zones** that we can process in a single **`O(N)` linear scan** (`i` from `0` to `N - 1`):\n  1. **Phase 1 — Strictly Left Non-Overlapping Zone (`intervals[i][1] < newInterval[0]`):**\n     - Any interval that **ends strictly before `newInterval` starts** cannot overlap with `newInterval`. Push `intervals[i]` directly into `res` and increment `i++`.\n  2. **Phase 2 — Overlapping Middle Zone (`intervals[i][0] <= newInterval[1]`):**\n     - Once Phase 1 finishes, any interval whose **start time is `<= newInterval`'s end time** overlaps with `newInterval`!\n     - Absorb `intervals[i]` into `newInterval` by expanding its bounds:\n       - **`newInterval[0] = min(newInterval[0], intervals[i][0]);`**\n       - **`newInterval[1] = max(newInterval[1], intervals[i][1]);`**\n     - After the `while` loop ends, push the merged `newInterval` into `res`.\n  3. **Phase 3 — Strictly Right Non-Overlapping Zone (`while (i < n)`):**\n     - All remaining intervals start strictly after `newInterval[1]`. Push them directly into `res`!\n- **Complexity:** **`O(N)`** Time and **`O(N)`** Space (to return the result array).\n\n### 💻 Insert Interval (3-Phase O(N) Scan) Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<vector<int>> insertInterval(vector<vector<int>> &intervals, vector<int> &newInterval) {\n    int n = intervals.size();\n    vector<vector<int>> res;\n    int i = 0;\n\n    // Phase 1: Add all intervals that end strictly before newInterval starts\n    while (i < n && intervals[i][1] < newInterval[0]) {\n        res.push_back(intervals[i]);\n        i++;\n    }\n\n    // Phase 2: Merge all overlapping intervals that start <= newInterval's end\n    while (i < n && intervals[i][0] <= newInterval[1]) {\n        newInterval[0] = min(newInterval[0], intervals[i][0]);\n        newInterval[1] = max(newInterval[1], intervals[i][1]);\n        i++;\n    }\n    res.push_back(newInterval);\n\n    // Phase 3: Add all remaining intervals to the right\n    while (i < n) {\n        res.push_back(intervals[i]);\n        i++;\n    }\n\n    return res;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Pushing `newInterval` Inside the Phase 2 `while` Loop:** You must absorb *all* overlapping intervals into `newInterval` first, and push `newInterval` into `res` **once** right after the Phase 2 `while` loop finishes!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
