244: Minimum Rounds to Complete All Tasks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Practice greedy denomination selection (preferring 3s over 2s)."}]	Module 41: Greedy Algorithms: Sorting Heuristics, Reachability Horizons & Interval Scheduling	### 📌 Topic Overview\nAt a lemonade stand, each lemonade costs **`5`**. Customers stand in a queue and pay with a **`5`**, **`10`**, or **`20`** bill. You start with `0` change in hand and must serve customers **in the exact order they arrive**. Return `true` if you can give every customer correct change, or `false` otherwise.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why We Don't Even Need a `twenty` Counter:**\n  - You never give a `20` bill back as change (the maximum change ever owed is `15`), so you only need two integer counters: **`five = 0` and `ten = 0`**!\n- **The Greedy Choice on a `20` Bill (Always Spend `10 + 5` Before `5 + 5 + 5`!):**\n  - When a customer pays with **`5`**: `five++`.\n  - When a customer pays with **`10`**: We owe `5`. If `five > 0`, `five--, ten++`; else `return false`.\n  - When a customer pays with **`20`**: We owe `15`. There are two ways to make `15`:\n    1. **One `10` + One `5` (`if (ten > 0 && five > 0)`)**\n    2. **Three `5`s (`else if (five >= 3)`)**\n  - **Why MUST we try `10 + 5` first?** Because a `5` bill is **more versatile** than a `10` bill (a `5` bill can make change for *both* `10` and `20`, whereas a `10` bill can *only* make change for `20`). Greedily spending the `10` bill first preserves our precious `5` bills for future `10`-bill customers!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N)` Time and `O(1)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Lemonade Change Blueprint\n```cpp\nbool lemonadeChange(vector<int> &bills) {\n    int five = 0, ten = 0;\n\n    for (int bill : bills) {\n        if (bill == 5) {\n            five++;\n        } else if (bill == 10) {\n            if (five > 0) {\n                five--;\n                ten++;\n            } else {\n                return false;\n            }\n        } else { // bill == 20 (owe 15 in change)\n            // Greedily use 10 + 5 first to save 5-dollar bills!\n            if (ten > 0 && five > 0) {\n                ten--;\n                five--;\n            } else if (five >= 3) {\n                five -= 3;\n            } else {\n                return false;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Sorting the `bills` Array:** Unlike Assign Cookies or SJF, customers arrive in a **strict queue order**—you cannot sort `bills`!\n- **Checking `if (five >= 3)` Before `if (ten > 0 && five > 0)`:** Spending three `5`s when you have a `10` available leaves you with no `5`s when the next customer hands you a `10`!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22109	Lecture 24: String Aggregation Operators — $concat, $split, $toUpper, $toLower, $strLenCP, $substrCP, $trim, $replaceAll & $regexFindAll	mongo-lecture-24-string-aggregation-operators	Transform, measure, slice, trim, replace, and regex-search text fields in aggregation pipelines using MongoDB string operators.	2026-10-06 19:52:12.616986+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMongoDB provides a comprehensive suite of **String Aggregation Operators** to format text, normalize casing, extract substrings, strip whitespace, replace tokens, and run regex extractions inside `$project` and `$addFields`.\n\n### 💡 Case, Length, Concatenation & Splitting Operators\n* **`$toString: <expr>`:** Converts a numeric, boolean, or date value into a string.\n* **`$toUpper: <str>` & `$toLower: <str>`:** Converts a string to all uppercase or all lowercase characters.\n* **`$strLenBytes: <str>` & `$strLenCP: <str>`:** Returns the length of a string in UTF-8 bytes (`$strLenBytes`) or Unicode code points/characters (`$strLenCP`).\n* **`$strcasecmp: [<str1>, <str2>`:** Performs a case-insensitive comparison of two strings (returns `0` if equal, `1` if first is greater, `-1` if smaller).\n* **`$concat: [<str1>, <str2>, ...`:** Joins multiple strings into one combined string.\n* **`$split: [<str>, <delimiter>`:** Splits a string around `<delimiter>` (such as `" "` or `"-"`) and returns an array of substrings.\n\n### 💡 Substring, Trimming, Replacement & Regex Operators\n* **`$substrBytes` & `$substrCP: [<str>, <startIndex>, <count>`:** Extracts `<count>` characters starting from 0-based `<startIndex>` (replaces deprecated `$substr`).\n* **`$trim`, `$ltrim`, `$rtrim`:** Removes leading/trailing whitespace (or specified `chars`) from both sides (`$trim`), left side only (`$ltrim`), or right side only (`$rtrim`) via `{ input: "$name", chars: "..." }`.\n* **`$replaceOne` & `$replaceAll`:** Replaces the first occurrence (`$replaceOne`) or every occurrence (`$replaceAll`) of a substring via `{ input: "$name", find: "old", replacement: "new" }`.\n* **`$indexOfBytes` & `$indexOfCP`:** Returns the 0-based index of the first occurrence of a substring (or `-1` if not found).\n* **`$regexMatch`, `$regexFind`, `$regexFindAll`:** Evaluates a regular expression inside an aggregation stage:\n  * `$regexMatch` returns `true` or `false`.\n  * `$regexFind` returns an object `{ match, idx, captures }` for the **first** match.\n  * `$regexFindAll` returns an array of match objects for **all** occurrences in the string.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical String Operator Pipeline Examples\n```javascript\n// 1. Casing, Character Length, Concatenation, and Splitting\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      upperName: { $toUpper: "$name" },\n      lowerName: { $toLower: "$name" },\n      nameLength: { $strLenCP: "$name" },\n      fullLabel: { $concat: ["$name", " - ", "$class" },\n      nameParts: { $split: ["$name", " " }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 2. Substring Extraction, Trimming, and Substring Replacement\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      firstThreeChars: { $substrCP: ["$name", 0, 3 },\n      cleanedName: { $trim: { input: "$name" } },\n      updatedName: {\n        $replaceAll: {\n          input: "$name",\n          find: "Kumar",\n          replacement: "Singh"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 3. Regex Pattern Extraction with $regexFindAll\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      matches: {\n        $regexFindAll: {\n          input: "$name",\n          regex: /K/\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/#string-expression-operators", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/#string-expression-operators", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 24: String Aggregation Operators"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r4xAUGR7m3s", "title": "MongoDB String Operators Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "50m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[{"id": "mongo-24-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3761e", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3761e", "name": "Problem #123: Find log message containing 14", "title": "Problem #123: Find log message containing 14", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "123", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return logs where message contains \\"14\\". Use $regex.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3761e"}, {"id": "mongo-24-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db372b5", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db372b5", "name": "Problem #410: Sort users by name", "title": "Problem #410: Sort users by name", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "410", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Execute: Sort users by name.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db372b5"}, {"id": "mongo-24-prob-3", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db37282", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db37282", "name": "Problem #427: Departments sorted by name", "title": "Problem #427: Departments sorted by name", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "427", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return all departments sorted by **name** ascending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db37282"}, {"id": "mongo-24-prob-4", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db37276", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db37276", "name": "Problem #431: Project department name and budget 