4: Users with name User10", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "434", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the user(s) with **name** \\"User10\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b57a3fe74e0db3726d"}, {"id": "mongo-24-prob-6", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371d4", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371d4", "name": "Problem #475: Users excluding _id from projection", "title": "Problem #475: Users excluding _id from projection", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "475", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return all users with only **name** and **age**, and exclude **_id**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371d4"}, {"id": "mongo-24-prob-7", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371d1", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371d1", "name": "Problem #476: Find departments with name Engineering or Sales", "title": "Problem #476: Find departments with name Engineering or Sales", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "476", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return departments where **name** is \\"Engineering\\" or \\"Sales\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371d1"}, {"id": "mongo-24-prob-8", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371c5", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371c5", "name": "Problem #480: Match users then project name and age", "title": "Problem #480: Match users then project name and age", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "480", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: **$match** active users (active true), then **$project** only **name** and **age**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371c5"}, {"id": "mongo-24-prob-9", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b0", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b0", "name": "Problem #487: Count departments in Engineering", "title": "Problem #487: Count departments in Engineering", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "487", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Use aggregation to count documents in **departments** where **name** is \\"Engineering\\". Return a single doc with count.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b0"}, {"id": "mongo-24-prob-10", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371a1", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371a1", "name": "Problem #492: Project name city and email only", "title": "Problem #492: Project name city and email only", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "492", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return users with only **name**, **city**, and **email** (and _id).", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371a1"}]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/#string-expression-operators	\N	\N	\N
6401	Algebraic Target Reduction: Count Partitions With Given Difference (Target Sum)	dsa-count-partitions-with-given-difference	Transform subset difference constraints S1 - S2 = D into target sum S2 = (totSum - D) / 2 and apply non-negative subset counting.	2026-10-03 11:28:16.969595+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Algebraic Target Reduction: Count Partitions With Given Difference (Target Sum)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zoilQD1kYSg", "title": "Lecture 199: Count Partitions With Given Difference (DP 18)", "duration": "19m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/partitions-with-given-difference/1", "title": "Partitions with Given Difference", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count the number of ways to partition an array into two subsets S1 and S2 such that S1 >= S2 and S1 - S2 == d."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/target-sum/", "title": "Target Sum (LC 494)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Assign + and - signs to each number so the expression evaluates to target (identical to S1 - S2 == target)."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/perfect-sum-problem5633/1", "title": "Perfect Sum Problem", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use the Count Subsets With Sum K engine to solve the reduced target (totSum - d) / 2."}]	Module 30: Dynamic Programming on Subsequences, Subset Sums & 0/1 Knapsack	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array `arr` of `N` non-negative integers and an integer difference `D`, count the number of ways to partition `arr` into two subsets `S1` and `S2` such that **`S1 >= S2`** and **`S1 - S2 == D`**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Algebraic Reduction to "Count Subsets With Sum K" (DP 17):**\n  - We know two equations:\n    1. `S1 + S2 = totSum`  ⇒  `S1 = totSum - S2`\n    2. `S1 - S2 = D`\n  - Substitute `S1` into equation 2:\n    `(totSum - S2) - S2 = D`\n    `totSum - 2 * S2 = D`\n    **`S2 = (totSum - D) / 2`**!\n  - Therefore, counting partitions with `S1 - S2 == D` is **100% equivalent** to counting subsets with sum **`target = (totSum - D) / 2`**!\n- **Two Mandatory Validity Checks Before Running DP:**\n  1. **`totSum - D < 0`:** Target sum cannot be negative (`return 0`).\n  2. **`(totSum - D) % 2 != 0`:** `totSum - D` must be **even** so that `S2` is a whole integer (`return 0`)!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * ((totSum - D) / 2))` Time and `O((totSum - D) / 2)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Count Partitions With Given Difference Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint countPartitions(int n, int d, vector<int> &arr) {\n    int totSum = 0;\n    for (int x : arr) totSum += x;\n\n    // Validity checks: (totSum - d) must be non-negative and even!\n    if (totSum - d < 0 || (totSum - d) % 2 != 0) return 0;\n\n    int k = (totSum - d) / 2;\n    int mod = 1e9 + 7;\n\n    vector<int> prev(k + 1, 0), cur(k + 1, 0);\n    if (arr[0] == 0) prev[0] = 2;\n    else prev[0] = 1;\n\n    if (arr[0] != 0 && arr[0] <= k) prev[arr[0]] = 1;\n\n    for (int ind = 1; ind < n; ind++) {\n        for (int sum = 0; sum <= k; sum++) {\n            int notTake = prev[sum];\n            int take = 0;\n            if (arr[ind] <= sum) take = prev[sum - arr[ind]];\n            cur[sum] = (take + notTake) % mod;\n        }\n        prev = cur;\n    }\n    return prev[k];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting that LeetCode 494 (Target Sum) Is the Exact Same Problem:** Assigning `+` to some elements (group `S1`) and `-` to the rest (group `S2`) so `S1 - S2 = target` is literally **Count Partitions With Given Difference**!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22083	Lecture 25: Common Table Expressions (CTEs) — WITH Clause, Pre-Join Aggregation & Chained CTEs	pg-lecture-25-common-table-expressions-cte-with-clause	Refactor deeply nested subqueries into clean, self-documenting virtual tables using the WITH clause, pre-aggregate data before joining, and chain multiple CTEs.	2026-10-05 19:34:44.247876+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Lecture 25: Common Table Expressions (CTEs) — WITH Clause, Pre-Join Aggregation & Chained CTEs\nPart of the **PostgreSQL & Relational SQL Mastery** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7FcyISrErmQ", "title": "CTE in PostgreSQL Explained | WITH Clause, Syntax, Examples & Multi-Step CTE Query", "duration": "24m", "creator_name": "Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical"}]	[{"id": "pg-25-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/friend-requests-ii-who-has-the-most-friends/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/friend-requests-ii-who-has-the-most-friends/", "name": "Friend Requests II: Who Has the Most Friends (CTE Union & Aggregation)", "title": "Friend Requests II: Who Has the Most Friends (CTE Union & Aggregation)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "25_1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Friend Requests II: Who Has the Most Friends (CTE Union & Aggregation)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/friend-requests-ii-who-has-the-most-friends/"}, {"id": "pg-25-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/restaurant-growth/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/restaurant-growth/", "name": "Restaurant Growth (Multi-Step CTE Pipeline)", "title": "Restaurant Growth (Multi-Step CTE Pipeline)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "25_2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Restaurant Growth (Multi-Step CTE Pipeline)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/restaurant-growth/"}, {"id": "pg-25-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/last-person-to-fit-in-the-bus/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/last-person-to-fit-in-the-bus/", "name": "Last Person to Fit in the Bus (CTE Running Total)", "title": "Last Person to Fit in the Bus (CTE Running Total)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "25_3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Last Person to Fit in the Bus (CTE Running Total)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/last-person-to-fit-in-the-bus/"}, {"id": "pg-25-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/count-salary-categories/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/count-salary-categories/", "name": "Count Salary Categories (Chained CTEs)", "title": "Count Salary Categories (Chained CTEs)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "25_4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Count Salary Categories (Chained CTEs)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/count-salary-categories/"}, {"id": "pg-25-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/product-price-at-a-given-date/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/product-price-at-a-given-date/", "name": "Product Price at a Given Date (CTE Latest State)", "title": "Product Price at a Given Date (CTE Latest State)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "25_5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Product Price at a Given Date (CTE Latest State)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/product-price-at-a-given-date/"}, {"id": "pg-25-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/immediate-food-delivery-ii/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/immediate-food-delivery-ii/", "name": "Immediate Food Delivery II (First Order CTE)", "title": "Immediate Food Delivery II (First Order CTE)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "25_6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Immediate Food Delivery II (First Order CTE)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/immediate-food-delivery-ii/"}, {"id": "pg-25-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/movie-rating/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/movie-rating/", "name": "Movie Rating (Dual CTE Aggregation)", "title": "Movie Rating (Dual CTE Aggregation)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "25_7", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Movie Rating (Dual CTE Aggregation)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/movie-rating/"}, {"id": "pg-25-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/consecutive-numbers/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/consecutive-numbers/", "name": "Consecutive Numbers", "title": "Consecutive Numbers", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "25_8", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Consecutive Numbers\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/consecutive-numbers/"}, {"id": "pg-25-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/department-top-three-salaries/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/department-top-three-salaries/", "name": "Department Top Three Salaries (CTE + Ranking)", "title": "Department Top Three Salaries (CTE + Ranking)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "25_9", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Solve \\"Department Top Three Salaries (CTE + Ranking)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/department-top-three-salaries/"}, {"id": "pg-25-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/trips-and-users/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/trips-and-users/", "name": "Trips and Users (CTE Filtering & Cancellation Rate)", "title": "Trips and Users (CTE Filtering & Cancellation Rate)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "25_10", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Solve \\"Trips and Users (CTE Filtering & Cancellation Rate)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/trips-and-users/"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nA **Common Table Expression (CTE)** is a named, temporary virtual table declared at the top of a query using the **`WITH`** keyword. Think of a CTE like declaring a clean variable or helper function in JavaScript: you define complex logic once at the top, and reference it like a regular table in your main query below.\n\n### 🛠️ 3 Progressive CTE Patterns\n\n#### 1. Basic CTE (Self-Documenting Average Filter)\n```sql\nWITH avg_course_price AS (\n    SELECT AVG(fee) AS avg_fee\n    FROM courses\n)\nSELECT title, fee\nFROM courses\nWHERE fee > (SELECT avg_fee FROM avg_course_price);\n```\n\n#### 2. Pre-Aggregating Data *Before* Joining (Cleaner & Faster Joins)\nInstead of joining a massive `users` table with millions of `payments` rows and grouping afterward, calculate the per-student math inside a CTE first, then join only the summarized rows:\n```sql\nWITH student_spending AS (\n    SELECT \n        student_id,\n        SUM(amount) AS total_spent\n    FROM payments\n    WHERE status = 'completed'\n    GROUP BY student_id\n)\nSELECT \n    u.id,\n    u.name,\n    s.total_spent\nFROM users u\nINNER JOIN student_spending s\n    ON u.id = s.student_id;\n```\n\n#### 3. Chaining Multiple CTEs (The Single-`WITH` Comma Rule)\nWhen defining multiple CTEs, write the **`WITH` keyword only once** at the very top and separate subsequent CTE blocks with a **comma (`,`)**:\n```sql\nWITH active_students AS (\n    SELECT id, name\n    FROM users\n    WHERE role = 'student' AND is_active = true\n),\npremium_courses AS (\n    SELECT id, title\n    FROM courses\n    WHERE fee > 4000\n)\nSELECT \n    a.name AS student_name,\n    p.title AS course_title\nFROM enrollments e\nINNER JOIN active_students a ON e.student_id = a.id\nINNER JOIN premium_courses p ON e.course_id = p.id;\n```\n\n### 💡 Key CTE Engineering Facts\n* **Ephemeral Lifespan:** A CTE exists in memory **only while that single SQL statement is executing** and vanishes immediately after—it does not permanently create a table on disk.\n* **No Trailing Comma Before Main `SELECT`:** Place commas between CTE definitions, but **never** place a comma after the closing parenthesis of the final CTE before the main `SELECT`.	Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)	https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical	654	Lecture 25: Common Table Expressions (CTEs) — WITH Clause, Pre-Join Aggregation & Chained CTEs	Refactor deeply nested subqueries into clean, self-documenting virtual tables using the WITH clause, pre-aggregate data before joining, and chain multiple CTEs.	24	Medium	Module 7: Temporal Queries, Subqueries & CTEs	25	[{"id": "pg-25-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/friend-requests-ii-who-has-the-most-friends/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/friend-requests-ii-who-has-the-most-friends/", "name": "Friend Requests II: Who Has the Most Friends (CTE Union & Aggregation)", "title": "Friend Requests II: Who Has the Most Friends (CTE Union & Aggregation)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "25_1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Friend Requests II: Who Has the Most Friends (CTE Union & Aggregation)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/friend-requests-ii-who-has-the-most-friends/"}, {"id": "pg-25-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/