-price-at-a-given-date/"}, {"id": "pg-25-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/immediate-food-delivery-ii/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/immediate-food-delivery-ii/", "name": "Immediate Food Delivery II (First Order CTE)", "title": "Immediate Food Delivery II (First Order CTE)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "25_6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Immediate Food Delivery II (First Order CTE)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/immediate-food-delivery-ii/"}, {"id": "pg-25-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/movie-rating/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/movie-rating/", "name": "Movie Rating (Dual CTE Aggregation)", "title": "Movie Rating (Dual CTE Aggregation)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "25_7", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Movie Rating (Dual CTE Aggregation)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/movie-rating/"}, {"id": "pg-25-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/consecutive-numbers/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/consecutive-numbers/", "name": "Consecutive Numbers", "title": "Consecutive Numbers", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "25_8", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Consecutive Numbers\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/consecutive-numbers/"}, {"id": "pg-25-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/department-top-three-salaries/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/department-top-three-salaries/", "name": "Department Top Three Salaries (CTE + Ranking)", "title": "Department Top Three Salaries (CTE + Ranking)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "25_9", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Solve \\"Department Top Three Salaries (CTE + Ranking)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/department-top-three-salaries/"}, {"id": "pg-25-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/trips-and-users/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/trips-and-users/", "name": "Trips and Users (CTE Filtering & Cancellation Rate)", "title": "Trips and Users (CTE Filtering & Cancellation Rate)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "25_10", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Solve \\"Trips and Users (CTE Filtering & Cancellation Rate)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/trips-and-users/"}]	https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/queries-with.html	\N	\N	\N
22153	Lecture 13: MVC (Model-View-Controller) Architecture — Separation of Concerns, Models, Controllers & Refactoring Express Apps	node-lecture-13-mvc-architecture-models-views-controllers-separation-of-concerns	Refactor growing Express.js codebases using the MVC (Model-View-Controller) design pattern to cleanly separate data persistence, UI templates, and request-handling business logic.	2026-10-06 22:17:20.380156+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nAs an application grows, placing routing, HTML rendering, array/file storage, and business logic inside the same route files creates unmaintainable spaghetti code. **MVC (Model-View-Controller)** is an architectural pattern that enforces **Separation of Concerns** by dividing the application into three distinct layers.\n\n### 💡 The 3 Pillars of MVC Architecture\n1. **Model (`models/home.js` — Data & Storage Layer):**\n   * Defines the structure (schema/class) of data entities (e.g., `Home` with `houseName`, `price`, `location`, `rating`, `photoUrl`).\n   * Manages all data persistence operations (`save()`, `fetchAll()`, reading/writing to `data/homes.json` or a database).\n   * Does **not** know anything about HTTP `req`/`res` objects or EJS templates.\n2. **View (`views/store/`, `views/host/` — Presentation Layer):**\n   * Responsible solely for how data looks to the user (`home-list.ejs`, `add-home.ejs`, `bookings.ejs`, `404.ejs`).\n   * Organized into actor-specific subfolders (`views/store/` for guests vs. `views/host/` for property hosts).\n3. **Controller (`controllers/storeController.js`, `hostController.js`, `errors.js` — Coordinator Layer):**\n   * Sits between the **Model** and the **View**.\n   * Receives incoming requests from `routes/`, extracts user input (`req.body`), calls methods on the **Model** (`home.save()`, `Home.fetchAll()`), and passes the resulting data to the **View** (`res.render()`).\n\n### 💡 Handling Asynchronous File Reads in `Home.fetchAll(callback)`\nBecause `fs.readFile()` inside `Home.fetchAll()` is **asynchronous**, `Home.fetchAll()` cannot simply `return` the parsed array synchronously! Instead, the Controller passes a **callback function** into `Home.fetchAll(registeredHomes => { res.render(...); })` so the View is rendered only after the JSON file finishes reading from disk.\n\n### 🛠️ Implementing a Model (`models/home.js`) & Controller (`controllers/hostController.js`)\n```javascript\n// File: models/home.js\nconst fs = require("fs");\nconst path = require("path");\nconst rootDir = require("../utils/pathUtil");\nconst homeDataPath = path.join(rootDir, "data", "homes.json");\n\nmodule.exports = class Home {\n  constructor(houseName, price, location, rating, photoUrl) {\n    this.houseName = houseName;\n    this.price = price;\n    this.location = location;\n    this.rating = rating;\n    this.photoUrl = photoUrl;\n  }\n\n  save() {\n    Home.fetchAll((registeredHomes) => {\n      registeredHomes.push(this);\n      fs.writeFile(homeDataPath, JSON.stringify(registeredHomes), (err) => {\n        if (err) console.log("Error writing homes.json:", err);\n      });\n    });\n  }\n\n  static fetchAll(callback) {\n    fs.readFile(homeDataPath, (err, data) => {\n      callback(!err ? JSON.parse(data) : [);\n    });\n  }\n};\n\n// File: controllers/hostController.js\nconst Home = require("../models/home");\n\nexports.postAddHome = (req, res, next) => {\n  const { houseName, price, location, rating, photoUrl } = req.body;\n  const home = new Home(houseName, price, location, rating, photoUrl);\n  home.save();\n  res.render("host/home-added", { pageTitle: "Home Added Successfully" });\n};\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/MVC", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/MVC", "title": "MDN Web Docs — MVC (Model-View-Controller) Architecture Pattern"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3rkdpBjmH8", "title": "Lecture 13: MVC Architecture | NodeJS Complete Course in Hindi", "duration": "102m", "creator_name": "Complete Coding by Prashant Sir", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir"}]	[{"id": "node-lec-13-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Explain the responsibilities of Model, View, and Controller in the MVC architecture pattern, and how Express Routers fit into MVC.", "title": "Q1: Explain the responsibilities of Model, View, and Controller in the MVC architecture pattern, and how Express Routers fit into MVC.", "answer": "1) Model: Manages the data structure, validation rules, and direct persistence logic (reading/writing to files or databases). 2) View: Handles the presentation/UI layer displayed to the user (such as EJS templates or React views). 3) Controller: Acts as the middleman—it receives user input from routes, invokes Model methods to read/mutate data, and selects which View to render. In Express, Routers act as the entry dispatcher that maps URLs and HTTP verbs to the appropriate Controller action.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) Model: Manages the data structure, validation rules, and direct persistence logic (reading/writing to files or databases). 2) View: Handles the presentation/UI layer displayed to the user (such as EJS templates or React views). 3) Controller: Acts as the middleman—it receives user input from routes, invokes Model methods to read/mutate data, and selects which View to render. In Express, Routers act as the entry dispatcher that maps URLs and HTTP verbs to the appropriate Controller action."}, {"id": "node-lec-13-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why did Home.fetchAll() return undefined when we tried to return JSON.parse(data) directly from inside fs.readFile(), and how did passing a callback solve it?", "title": "Q2: Why did Home.fetchAll() return undefined when we tried to return JSON.parse(data) directly from inside fs.readFile(), and how did passing a callback solve it?", "answer": "fs.readFile() is an asynchronous non-blocking operation. When Home.fetchAll() is called, it registers the fs.readFile callback with libuv and immediately finishes executing before the disk read even completes—so fetchAll() itself returns undefined! Any return statement inside the fs.readFile callback only returns to internal libuv callers, not to the Controller. By passing a callback function Home.fetchAll((homes) => res.render(...)), the Controller defers rendering until fs.readFile invokes callback(JSON.parse(data)).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "fs.readFile() is an asynchronous non-blocking operation. When Home.fetchAll() is called, it registers the fs.readFile callback with libuv and immediately finishes executing before the disk read even completes—so fetchAll() itself returns undefined! Any return statement inside the fs.readFile callback only returns to internal libuv callers, not to the Controller. By passing a callback function Home.fetchAll((homes) => res.render(...)), the Controller defers rendering until fs.readFile invokes callback(JSON.parse(data))."}, {"id": "node-lec-13-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: Why is fetchAll() defined as a static method on the Home class while save() is defined as an instance method?", "title": "Q3: Why is fetchAll() defined as a static method on the Home class while save() is defined as an instance method?", "answer": "save() operates on a specific single Home instance that was just instantiated with new Home(houseName, price, ...) (using this to save that specific home). In contrast, fetchAll() retrieves the entire collection of all homes from storage and does not belong to any single home instance—so calling Home.fetchAll() directly on the class without instantiating a dummy new Home() is semantically clean.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "save() operates on a specific single Home instance that was just instantiated with new Home(houseName, price, ...) (using this to save that specific home). In contrast, fetchAll() retrieves the entire collection of all homes from storage and does not belong to any single home instance—so calling Home.fetchAll() directly on the class without instantiating a dummy new Home() is semantically clean."}, {"id": "node-lec-13-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: How does MVC make migrating from JSON file storage to SQL or MongoDB later in a project so easy?", "title": "Q4: How does MVC make migrating from JSON file storage to SQL or MongoDB later in a project so easy?", "answer": "Because of Separation of Concerns! Since all data persistence logic is encapsulated strictly inside the Model layer (models/home.js), your Views (views/*.ejs), Routers (routes/*.js), and almost all Controller logic remain untouched when switching databases. You only rewrite the internal implementation of save(), fetchAll(), and findById() inside the Model.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Because of Separation of Concerns! Since all data persistence logic is encapsulated strictly inside the Model layer (models/home.js), your Views (views/*.ejs), Routers (routes/*.js), and almost all Controller logic remain untouched when switching databases. You only rewrite the internal implementation of save(), fetchAll(), and findById() inside the Model."}, {"id": "node-lec-13-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What happens in a REST API (where React is the frontend) to the \\"V\\" (View) in MVC on the Express backend?", "title": "Q5: What happens in a REST API (where React is the frontend) to the \\"V\\" (View) in MVC on the Express backend?", "answer": "In a decoupled REST API architecture (like MERN), the View layer moves entirely to the client-side React application, while the Express backend follows an MC (Model-Controller / Route-Controller-Service-Model) pattern where Controllers return JSON responses (res.json()) instead of calling res.render().", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In a decoupled REST API architecture (like MERN), the View layer moves entirely to the client-side React application, while the Express backend follows an MC (Model-Controller / Route-Controller-Service-Model) pattern where Controllers return JSON responses (res.json()) instead of calling res.render()."}, {"id": "node-lec-13-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why should you never access req.body or call res.render() directly inside a Model file?", "title": "Q6: Why should you never access req.body or call res.render() directly inside a Model file?", "answer": "Coupling a Model to HTTP req and res objects violates Separation of Concerns and prevents the Model from being reused in non-HTTP contexts (such as background cron jobs, WebSockets, CLI scripts, or unit tests). Models should only accept plain data arguments and return data/Promises.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Coupling a Model to HTTP req and res objects violates Separation of Concerns and prevents the Model from being reused in non-HTTP contexts (such as background cron jobs, WebSockets, CLI scripts, or unit tests). Models should only accept plain data arguments and return data/Promises."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nAs an application grows, placing routing, HTML rendering, array/file storage, and business logic inside the same route files creates unmaintainable spaghetti code. **MVC (Model-View-Controller)** is an architectural pattern that enforces **Separation of Concerns** by dividing the application into three distinct layers.\n\n### 💡 The 3 Pillars of MVC Architecture\n1. **Model (`models/home.js` — Data & Storage Layer):**\n   * Defines the structure (schema/class) of data entities (e.g., `Home` with `houseName`, `price`, `location`, `rating`, `photoUrl`).\n   * Manages all data persistence operations (`save()`, `fetchAll()`, reading/writing to `data/homes.json` or a database).\n   * Does **not** know anything about HTTP `req`/`res` objects or EJS templates.\n2. **View (`views/store/`, `views/host/` — Presentation Layer):**\n   * Responsible solely for how data looks to the user (`home-list.ejs`, `add-home.ejs`, `bookings.ejs`, `404.ejs`).\n   * Organized into actor-specific subfolders (`views/store/` for guests vs. `views/host/` for property hosts).\n3. **Controller (`controllers/storeController.js`, `hostController.js`, `errors.js` — Coordinator Layer):**\n   * Sits between the **Model** and the **View**.\n   * Receives incoming requests from `routes/`, extracts user input (`req.body`), calls methods on the **Model** (`home.save()`, `Home.fetchAll()`), and passes the resulting data to the **View** (`res.render()`).\n\n### 💡 Handling Asynchronous File Reads in `Home.fetchAll(callback)`\nBecause `fs.readFile()` inside `Home.fetchAll()` is **asynchronous**, `Home.fetchAll()` cannot simply `return` the parsed array synchronously! Instead, the Controller passes a **callback function** into `Home.fetchAll(registeredHomes => { res.render(...); })` so the View is rendered only after the JSON file finishes reading from disk.\n\n### 🛠️ Implementing a Model (`models/home.js`) & Controller (`controllers/hostController.js`)\n```javascript\n// File: models/home.js\nconst fs = require("fs");\nconst path = require("path");\nconst rootDir = require("../utils/pathUtil");\nconst homeDataPath = path.join(rootDir, "data", "homes.json");\n\nmodule.exports = class Home {\n  constructor(houseName, price, location, rating, photoUrl) {\n    this.houseName = houseName;\n    this.price = price;\n    this.location = location;\n    this.rating = rating;\n    this.photoUrl = photoUrl;\n  }\n\n  save() {\n    Home.fetchAll((registeredHomes) => {\n      registeredHomes.push(this);\n      fs.writeFile(homeDataPath, JSON.stringify(registeredHomes), (err) => {\n        if (err) console.log("Error writing homes.json:", err);\n      });\n    });\n  }\n\n  static fetchAll(callback) {\n    fs.readFile(homeDataPath, (err, data) => {\n      callback(!err ? JSON.parse(data) : [);\n    });\n  }\n};\n\n// File: controllers/hostController.js\nconst Home = require("../models/home");\n\nexports.postAddHome = (req, res, next) => {\n  const { houseName, price, location, rating, photoUrl } = req.body;\n  const home = new Home(houseName, price, location, rating, photoUrl);\n  home.save();\n  res.render("host/home-added", { pageTitle: "Home Added Successfully" });\n};\n```	Complete Coding by Prashant Sir	https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir	24	Lecture 13: MVC (Model-View-Controller) Architecture — Separation of Concerns, Models, Controllers & Refactoring Express Apps	Refactor growing Express.js codebases using the MVC (Model-View-Controller) design pattern to cleanly separate data persistence, UI templates, and request-handling business logic.	20	Easy	\N	13	[{"id": "node-lec-13-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Explain the responsibilities of Model, View, and Controller in the MVC architecture pattern, and how Express Routers fit into MVC.", "title": "Q1: Explain the responsibilities of Model, View, and Controller in the MVC architecture pattern, and how Express Routers fit into MVC.", "answer": "1) Model: Manages the data structure, validation rules, and direct persistence logic (reading/writing to files or databases). 2) View: Handles the presentation/UI layer displayed to the user (such as EJS templates or React views). 3) Controller: Acts as the middleman—it receives user input from routes, invokes Model methods to read/mutate data, and selects which View to render. In Express, Routers act as the entry dispatcher that maps URLs and HTTP verbs to the appropriate Controller action.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) Model: Manages the data structure, validation rules, and direct persistence logic (reading/writing to files or databases). 2) View: Handles the presentation/UI layer displayed to the user (such as EJS templates or React views). 3) Controller: Acts as the middleman—it receives user input from routes, invokes Model methods to read/mutate data, and selects which View to render. In Express, Routers act as the entry dispatcher that maps URLs and HTTP verbs to the appropriate Controller action."}, {"id": "node-lec-13-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why did Home.fetchAll() return undefined when we tried to return JSON.parse(data) directly from inside fs.readFile(), and how did passing a callback solve it?", "title": "Q2: Why did Home.fetchAll() return undefined when we tried to return JSON.parse(data) directly from inside fs.readFile(), and how did passing a callback solve it?", "answer": "fs.readFile() is an asynchronous non-blocking operation. When Home.fetchAll() is called, it registers the fs.readFile callback with libuv and immediately finishes executing before the disk read even completes—so fetchAll() itself returns undefined! Any return statement inside the fs.readFile callback only returns to internal libuv callers, not to the Controller. By passing a callback function Home.fetchAll((homes) => res.render(...)), the Controller defers rendering until fs.readFile invokes callback(JSON.parse(data)).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "fs.readFile() is an asynchronous non-blocking operation. When Home.fetchAll() is called, it registers the fs.readFile callback with libuv and immediately finishes executing before the disk read even completes—so fetchAll() itself returns undefined! Any return statement inside the fs.readFile callback only returns to internal libuv callers, not to the Controller. By passing a callback function Home.fetchAll((homes) => res.render(...)), the Controller defers rendering until fs.readFile invokes callback(JSON.parse(data))."}, {"id": "node-lec-13-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: Why is fetchAll() defined as a static method on the Home class while save() is defined as an instance method?", "title": "Q3: Why is fetchAll() defined as a static method on the Home class while save() is defined as an instance method?", "answer": "save() operates on a specific single Home instance that was just instantiated with new Home(houseName, price, ...) (using this to save that specific home). In contrast, fetchAll() retrieves the entire collection of all homes from storage and does not belong to any single home instance—so calling Home.fetchAll() directly on the class without instantiating a dummy new Home() is semantically clean.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "save() operates on a specific single Home instance that was just instantiated with new Home(houseName, price, ...) (using this to save that specific home). In contrast, fetchAll() retrieves the entire collection of all homes from storage and does not belong to any single home instance—so calling Home.fetchAll() directly on the class without instantiating a dummy new Home() is semantically clean."}, {"id": "node-lec-13-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: How does MVC make migrating from JSON file storage to SQL or MongoDB later in a project so easy?", "title": "Q4: How does MVC make migrating from JSON file storage to SQL or MongoDB later in a project so easy?", "answer": "Because of Separation of Concerns! Since all data persistence logic is encapsulated strictly inside the Model layer (models/home.js), your Views (views/*.ejs), Routers (routes/*.js), and almost all Controller logic remain untouched when switching databases. You only rewrite the internal implementation of save(), fetchAll(), and findById() inside the Model.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Because of Separation of Concerns! Since all data persistence logic is encapsulated strictly inside the Model layer (models/home.js), your Views (views/*.ejs), Routers (routes/*.js), and almost all Controller logic remain untouched when switching databases. You only rewrite the internal implementation of save(), fetchAll(), and findById() inside the Model."}, {"id": "node-lec-13-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What happens in a REST API (where React is the frontend) to the \\"V\\" (View) in MVC on the Express backend?", "title": "Q5: What happens in a REST API (where React is the frontend) to the \\"V\\" (View) in MVC on the Express backend?", "answer": "In a decoupled REST API architecture (like MERN), the View layer moves entirely to the client-side React application, while the Express backend follows an MC (Model-Controller / Route-Controller-Service-Model) pattern where Controllers return JSON responses (res.json()) instead of calling res.render().", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In a decoupled REST API architecture (like MERN), the View layer moves entirely to the client-side React application, while the Express backend follows an MC (Model-Controller / Route-Controller-Service-Model) pattern where Controllers return JSON responses (res.json()) instead of calling res.render()."}, {"id": "node-lec-13-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why should you never access req.body or call res.render() directly inside a Model file?", "title": "Q6: Why should you never access req.body or call res.render() directly inside a Model file?", "answer": "Coupling a Model to HTTP req and res objects violates Separation of Concerns and prevents the Model from being reused in non-HTTP contexts (such as background cron jobs, WebSockets, CLI scripts, or unit tests). Models should only accept plain data arguments and return data/Promises.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Coupling a Model to HTTP req and res objects violates Separation of Concerns and prevents the Model from being reused in non-HTTP contexts (such as background cron jobs, WebSockets, CLI scripts, or unit tests). Models should only accept plain data arguments and return data/Promises."}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/MVC	\N	\N	\N
22154	Lecture 14: Dynamic Paths (Part 1) — Route Parameters (:homeId, req.params), Detail Pages & Building the Favourite Model	node-lecture-14-dynamic-paths-part-1-route-params-detail-pages-favourites	Define dynamic route segments using Express colon syntax (:homeId), extract IDs via req.params, look up single entities with Home.findById(), and build a Favourite model.	2026-10-06 22:17:20.380156+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn an e-commerce or property booking app like Airbnb, you cannot hardcode a separate static route for every single listing (`/homes/1`, `/homes/2`, ..., `/homes/10000`). **Dynamic Route Parameters** allow a single Express route pattern (`/homes/:homeId`) to capture variable segments from the URL and load the matching entity dynamically.\n\n### 💡 Express Route Parameters (`:paramName` & `req.params`)\n* **Defining a Dynamic Path Segment:** Prefix the variable segment with a colon (`:`) in your router:\n  **`storeRouter.get("/homes/:homeId", storeController.getHomeDetails);`**\n* **Extracting the Value (`req.params`):** When a user clicks `<a href="/homes/<%= home.id %>">Details</a>` (e.g., `/homes/0.84921`), Express populates **`req.params`** as `{ homeId: "0.84921" }`.\n* **Implementing `Home.findById(homeId, callback)`:** Reads all homes via `Home.fetchAll()`, finds the matching object using `homes.find(h => h.id === homeId)`, and passes it to the callback. If `home` is `undefined` (invalid ID), redirect safely to `"/homes"`.\n\n### 💡 Building the `Favourite` Model (`models/favourite.js`)\n* Stores an array of favourited `homeId` strings inside `data/favourite.json` (normalizing data by storing only reference IDs rather than duplicating entire home objects!).\n* **`Favourite.addToFavourite(homeId, callback)`:** Reads existing IDs, checks `if (favourites.includes(homeId))` to prevent duplicate entries, and writes the updated array back to `favourite.json`.\n* **Joining Favourite IDs with Home Objects:** In `getFavouriteList`, call `Favourite.getFavourites(favIds => { Home.fetchAll(homes => { const favHomes = homes.filter(h => favIds.includes(h.id)); ... }); })`.\n\n### 🛠️ Dynamic Route Parameter, `findById()` & `Favourite` Model\n```javascript\n// 1. In routes/storeRouter.js: Dynamic path parameter ':homeId'\nstoreRouter.get("/homes/:homeId", storeController.getHomeDetails);\n\n// 2. In controllers/storeController.js: Extract req.params.homeId\nexports.getHomeDetails = (req, res, next) => {\n  const homeId = req.params.homeId;\n  Home.findById(homeId, (home) => {\n    if (!home) {\n      console.log("Home not found for ID:", homeId);\n      return res.redirect("/homes");\n    }\n    res.render("store/home-detail", {\n      home: home,\n      pageTitle: "Home Detail",\n      currentPage: "Home"\n    });\n  });\n};\n\n// 3. In models/home.js: Static findById lookup\nstatic findById(homeId, callback) {\n  this.fetchAll((homes) => {\n    const homeFound = homes.find((home) => home.id === homeId);\n    callback(homeFound);\n  });\n}\n```	[{"url": "https://expressjs.com/en/guide/routing.html#route-parameters", "link": "https://expressjs.com/en/guide/routing.html#route-parameters", "title": "Express.js Official Docs — Route Parameters (req.params)"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qbHmaHjOKPE", "title": "Lecture 14: Dynamic Path Part - 1 | NodeJS Complete Course in Hindi", "duration": "66m", "creator_name": "Complete Coding by Prashant Sir", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir"}]	[{"id": "node-lec-14-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: How do Route Parameters (:id) work in Express.js, and why does route declaration order matter when combining \\"/homes/favourites\\" and \\"/homes/:homeId\\"?", "title": "Q1: How do Route Parameters (:id) work in Express.js, and why does route declaration order matter when combining \\"/homes/favourites\\" and \\"/homes/:homeId\\"?", "answer": "A route parameter (:homeId) acts as a wildcard placeholder for any non-empty URL segment at that position and stores the captured string in req.params.homeId. Because \\":homeId\\" matches ANY string, if you register router.get(\\"/homes/:homeId\\", ...) BEFORE static sub-routes like router.get(\\"/homes/favourites\\", ...), a request to \\"/homes/favourites\\" will be intercepted by \\"/homes/:homeId\\" with req.params.homeId === \\"favourites\\"! Always register specific static routes above dynamic parameterized routes.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "14-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "A route parameter (:homeId) acts as a wildcard placeholder for any non-empty URL segment at that position and stores the captured string in req.params.homeId. Because \\":homeId\\" matches ANY string, if you register router.get(\\"/homes/:homeId\\", ...) BEFORE static sub-routes like router.get(\\"/homes/favourites\\", ...), a request to \\"/homes/favourites\\" will be intercepted by \\"/homes/:homeId\\" with req.params.homeId === \\"favourites\\"! Always register specific static routes above dynamic parameterized routes."}, {"id": "node-lec-14-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why did our Favourite model store only an array of homeId strings in favourite.json instead of copying the entire Home object?", "title": "Q2: Why did our Favourite model store only an array of homeId strings in favourite.json instead of copying the entire Home object?", "answer": "This follows the database principle of Normalization (Single Source of Truth). If you duplicated the entire Home object inside favourite.json and the host later updated the home's price or photo in homes.json, the copy inside favourite.json would become stale and out-of-sync! Storing only the homeId reference ensures that when we look up favourites against Home.fetchAll(), users always see the latest updated home details.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "14-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "This follows the database principle of Normalization (Single Source of Truth). If you duplicated the entire Home object inside favourite.json and the host later updated the home's price or photo in homes.json, the copy inside favourite.json would become stale and out-of-sync! Storing only the homeId reference ensures that when we look up favourites against Home.fetchAll(), users always see the latest updated home details."}, {"id": "node-lec-14-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is the data type of req.params.homeId when a user visits /homes/42, and what bug can happen if home.id is stored as a Number?", "title": "Q3: What is the data type of req.params.homeId when a user visits /homes/42, and what bug can happen if home.id is stored as a Number?", "answer": "Every value inside req.params is ALWAYS a String (so req.params.homeId is \\"42\\"). If home.id was stored as a numeric 42, strict equality home.id === req.params.homeId (42 === \\"42\\") will evaluate to false! Always ensure consistent types (either store IDs as strings or convert via Number(req.params.homeId) / String(home.id)).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "14-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Every value inside req.params is ALWAYS a String (so req.params.homeId is \\"42\\"). If home.id was stored as a numeric 42, strict equality home.id === req.params.homeId (42 === \\"42\\") will evaluate to false! Always ensure consistent types (either store IDs as strings or convert via Number(req.params.homeId) / String(home.id))."}, {"id": "node-lec-14-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: How do you pass a home.id from an EJS card button when submitting a POST \\"/favourites\\" request without putting the ID in the URL?", "title": "Q4: How do you pass a home.id from an EJS card button when submitting a POST \\"/favourites\\" request without putting the ID in the URL?", "answer": "Place a hidden input field inside the <form action=\\"/favourites\\" method=\\"POST\\">: <input type=\\"hidden\\" name=\\"id\\" value=\\"<%= home.id %>\\" />. When the user clicks the Submit button, the browser sends id in the POST request body (req.body.id) without displaying the input box on the UI.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "14-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Place a hidden input field inside the <form action=\\"/favourites\\" method=\\"POST\\">: <input type=\\"hidden\\" name=\\"id\\" value=\\"<%= home.id %>\\" />. When the user clicks the Submit button, the browser sends id in the POST request body (req.body.id) without displaying the input box on the UI."}, {"id": "node-lec-14-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What should a controller do if Home.findById(req.params.homeId) returns undefined (e.g., a user typed a non-existent or deleted ID in the URL)?", "title": "Q5: What should a controller do if Home.findById(req.params.homeId) returns undefined (e.g., a user typed a non-existent or deleted ID in the URL)?", "answer": "If you pass home: undefined directly into res.render(\\"store/home-detail\\", { home }), the EJS template will crash with TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'houseName'). Always guard with if (!home) return res.redirect(\\"/homes\\") (or render a 404 page) before calling res.render().", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "14-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "If you pass home: undefined directly into res.render(\\"store/home-detail\\", { home }), the EJS template will crash with TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'houseName'). Always guard with if (!home) return res.redirect(\\"/homes\\") (or render a 404 page) before calling res.render()."}, {"id": "node-lec-14-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Can a single Express route have multiple route parameters (e.g., /users/:userId/bookings/:bookingId)?", "title": "Q6: Can a single Express route have multiple route parameters (e.g., /users/:userId/bookings/:bookingId)?", "answer": "Yes! Express supports multiple route parameters in the same path pattern. A request to /users/101/bookings/555 will populate req.params with { userId: \\"101\\", bookingId: \\"555\\" }.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "14-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Yes! Express supports multiple route parameters in the same path pattern. A request to /users/101/bookings/555 will populate req.params with { userId: \\"101\\", bookingId: \\"555\\" }."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn an e-commerce or property booking app like Airbnb, you cannot hardcode a separate static route for every single listing (`/homes/1`, `/homes/2`, ..., `/homes/10000`). **Dynamic Route Parameters** allow a single Express route pattern (`/homes/:homeId`) to capture variable segments from the URL and load the matching entity dynamically.\n\n### 💡 Express Route Parameters (`:paramName` & `req.params`)\n* **Defining a Dynamic Path Segment:** Prefix the variable segment with a colon (`:`) in your router:\n  **`storeRouter.get("/homes/:homeId", storeController.getHomeDetails);`**\n* **Extracting the Value (`req.params`):** When a user clicks `<a href="/homes/<%= home.id %>">Details</a>` (e.g., `/homes/0.84921`), Express populates **`req.params`** as `{ homeId: "0.84921" }`.\n* **Implementing `Home.findById(homeId, callback)`:** Reads all homes via `Home.fetchAll()`, finds the matching object using `homes.find(h => h.id === homeId)`, and passes it to the callback. If `home` is `undefined` (invalid ID), redirect safely to `"/homes"`.\n\n### 💡 Building the `Favourite` Model (`models/favourite.js`)\n* Stores an array of favourited `homeId` strings inside `data/favourite.json` (normalizing data by storing only reference IDs rather than duplicating entire home objects!).\n* **`Favourite.addToFavourite(homeId, callback)`:** Reads existing IDs, checks `if (favourites.includes(homeId))` to prevent duplicate entries, and writes the updated array back to `favourite.json`.\n* **Joining Favourite IDs with Home Objects:** In `getFavouriteList`, call `Favourite.getFavourites(favIds => { Home.fetchAll(homes => { const favHomes = homes.filter(h => favIds.includes(h.id)); ... }); })`.\n\n### 🛠️ Dynamic Route Parameter, `findById()` & `Favourite` Model\n```javascript\n// 1. In routes/storeRouter.js: Dynamic path parameter ':homeId'\nstoreRouter.get("/homes/:homeId", storeController.getHomeDetails);\n\n// 2. In controllers/storeController.js: Extract req.params.homeId\nexports.getHomeDetails = (req, res, next) => {\n  const homeId = req.params.homeId;\n  Home.findById(homeId, (home) => {\n    if (!home) {\n      console.log("Home not found for ID:", homeId);\n      return res.redirect("/homes");\n    }\n    res.render("store/home-detail", {\n      home: home,\n      pageTitle: "Home Detail",\n      currentPage: "Home"\n    });\n  });\n};\n\n// 3. In models/home.js: Static findById lookup\nstatic findById(homeId, callback) {\n  this.fetchAll((homes) => {\n    const homeFound = homes.find((home) => home.id === homeId);\n    callback(homeFound);\n  });\n}\n```	Complete Coding by Prashant Sir	https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir	24	Lecture 14: Dynamic Paths (Part 1) — Route Parameters (:homeId, req.params), Detail Pages & Building the Favourite Model	Define dynamic route segments using Express colon syntax (:homeId), extract IDs via req.params, look up single entities with Home.findById(), and build a Favourite model.	20	Easy	\N	14	[{"id": "node-lec-14-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: How do Route Parameters (:id) work in Express.js, and why does route declaration order matter when combining \\"/homes/favourites\\" and \\"/homes/:homeId\\"?", "title": "Q1: How do Route Parameters (:id) work in Express.js, and why does route declaration order matter when combining \\"/homes/favourites\\" and \\"/homes/:homeId\\"?", "answer": "A route parameter (:homeId) acts as a wildcard placeholder for any non-empty URL segment at that position and stores the captured string