4. If the exact mathematical average for the period is 7.2 Million USD, in exactly how many years was the business strictly above the average?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "The average is 7.2. We simply count the values strictly greater than 7.2: 8.2, 7.5, 8.0, and 8.4. There are exactly 4 years above the average.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-bar-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Stacked Bar Graph Deduction] Total 12,000 units. Odisha=12%. Automised in Odisha = 1000. Find un-automised in Odisha.", "options": ["A) 240", "B) 440", "C) 1,000", "D) 1,440"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A combined chart displays data for a total of 12,000 factories. A pie chart shows exactly 12% of all factories are located in Odisha. A linked bar graph shows exactly 1,000 factories in Odisha are fully automized. How many factories in Odisha are UN-automized?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Total factories in Odisha = 12% of 12,000 = 0.12 × 12000 = 1,440. 2) The bar graph states 1,000 of these are automized. 3) Un-automized factories = Total - Automized = 1440 - 1000 = 440.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-bar-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Bar Graph vs Pie Chart Total Checks] Karnataka = 29% of 12,000. Automized = 1800. Un-automized?", "options": ["A) 1,580", "B) 1,680", "C) 1,780", "D) 1,880"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A pie chart shows that Karnataka holds exactly 29% of a total 12,000 units. A linked bar graph indicates that exactly 1,800 units in Karnataka are automized. Exactly how many units in Karnataka are UN-automized?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Total units in Karnataka = 29% of 12,000 = 0.29 × 12000 = 29 × 120 = 3,480. 2) Un-automized units = Total - Automized = 3480 - 1800 = 1,680.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-bar-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Pie Chart Derived Bar Graph Difference] Telangana = 26% of 12000. Assam = 6% of 12000. Difference in total units?", "options": ["A) 2,000", "B) 2,200", "C) 2,400", "D) 2,600"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "From a base of 12,000 total units, Telangana accounts for exactly 26% and Assam accounts for exactly 6%. Without explicitly calculating the individual totals for both states first, what is the exact absolute difference in the total number of units between the two states?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Shortcut: Find the difference in their percentages first! Difference = 26% - 6% = 20%. The absolute difference in units is simply 20% of the total base. 20% of 12,000 = 0.20 × 12000 = 2,400 units.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-bar-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Reading Stacked Bar Graph Segments] Stacked bar: UPSC 2015=20L, 2016=22L, 2017=18L. Success rate = 0.8%. Total successful?", "options": ["A) 36,000", "B) 42,000", "C) 48,000", "D) 54,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A stacked bar graph displays total examination candidates in Lakhs (L). For the UPSC segment specifically, the extracted values are: 20 Lakhs in 2015, 22 Lakhs in 2016, and 18 Lakhs in 2017. If the ultimate success rate across all three years combined is exactly 0.8%, how many total candidates succeeded in the UPSC exams over these three years?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Total candidates = 20 + 22 + 18 = 60 Lakhs = 6,000,000 candidates. 2) Successful candidates = 0.8% of 6,000,000 = (0.8 / 100) × 6,000,000 = 0.008 × 6,000,000 = 48,000.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-bar-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Zero Growth Identification] Stacked bar: Banking candidates 2015=22L, 2017=22L. Percentage rise or fall?", "options": ["A) 0% (No change)", "B) 1% Rise", "C) 1% Fall", "D) Cannot be determined"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A bar graph records exactly 22 Lakh candidates appearing for Banking exams in 2015. In 2017, the bar graph again records exactly 22 Lakh candidates appearing for the exact same exams. What is the exact percentage rise or fall in the number of candidates from 2015 to 2017?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Since the absolute values are identical (22 Lakhs to 22 Lakhs), the absolute change is 0. Therefore, the percentage rise or fall is exactly 0%.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-bar-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Brand Comparison Higher/Lower Logic] Milk by Brand C: 25, 30, 22, 35, 30. Brand A: 35, 40, 35, 32, 25. Ratio of times C < A to times C > A?", "options": ["A) 2 : 3", "B) 3 : 2", "C) 4 : 1", "D) 1 : 4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A grouped bar graph shows yearly milk production for two brands over 5 years.\\nBrand C: 25, 30, 22, 35, 30\\nBrand A: 35, 40, 35, 32, 25\\nWhat is the exact ratio of the number of years where Brand C's production was STRICTLY LESS than Brand A's to the number of years where Brand C's production was STRICTLY GREATER than Brand A's?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Compare year by year (C vs A): Year 1 (25 < 35, C is Less). Year 2 (30 < 40, C is Less). Year 3 (22 < 35, C is Less). Year 4 (35 > 32, C is Greater). Year 5 (30 > 25, C is Greater). Brand C is strictly Less 3 times. Brand C is strictly Greater 2 times. The required ratio (Less : Greater) is 3 : 2.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-bar-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Ratio of Totals from Income Bar Graph] Income A: 200, 250, 300. B: 150, 200, 250. Ratio of total income?", "options": ["A) 4 : 3", "B) 5 : 4", "C) 6 : 5", "D) 7 : 6"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A bar graph tracks the yearly income of two individuals, A and B, over 3 years.\\nA's incomes: ₹200k, ₹250k, ₹300k\\nB's incomes: ₹150k, ₹200k, ₹250k\\nWhat is the exact ratio of the total 3-year income of A to the total 3-year income of B?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total income of A = 200 + 250 + 300 = 750k. Total income of B = 150 + 200 + 250 = 600k. Ratio A : B = 750 : 600. Divide both by 150: 750/150 = 5, 600/150 = 4. The ratio is 5 : 4.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-bar-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Average Trade Deficit from Import/Export Bars] Exports: 40, 50, 60, 70. Imports: 50, 40, 70, 80. Average trade deficit?", "options": ["A) 0", "B) 5", "C) 10", "D) 15"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A dual bar graph shows a country's Exports and Imports over 4 years.\\nExports: 40, 50, 60, 70\\nImports: 50, 40, 70, 80\\nIf Trade Deficit is defined as (Imports - Exports), what is the exact average trade deficit over these 4 years?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Calculate the deficit (Imports - Exports) for each year: Year 1: 50 - 40 = +10. Year 2: 40 - 50 = -10 (surplus). Year 3: 70 - 60 = +10. Year 4: 80 - 70 = +10. Total deficit over 4 years = 10 - 10 + 10 + 10 = 20. Average deficit = Total / 4 = 20 / 4 = 5.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-bar-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Percentage Share of a Single Bar] Rainfall: M=12, T=15, W=10, Th=18, F=25. What % on Friday?", "options": ["A) 25.00%", "B) 28.50%", "C) 31.25%", "D) 35.00%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A bar graph records daily rainfall in cm for a 5-day work week:\\nMonday: 12 cm, Tuesday: 15 cm, Wednesday: 10 cm, Thursday: 18 cm, Friday: 25 cm.\\nWhat exact percentage of the total weekly rainfall occurred strictly on Friday?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total rainfall = 12 + 15 + 10 + 18 + 25 = 80 cm. Rainfall on Friday = 25 cm. Percentage = (25 / 80) × 100 = (5 / 16) × 100 = 500 / 16 = 31.25%.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-bar-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Difference Between Total Categories] Class X: A=40, B=50, C=30. Class XI: A=30, B=60, C=40. Difference between total Class X and XI?", "options": ["A) 0", "B) 5", "C) 10", "D) 15"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A grouped bar graph shows section-wise student counts for two grade levels.\\nClass X (Sections A, B, C): 40, 50, 30\\nClass XI (Sections A, B, C): 30, 60, 40\\nWhat is the exact absolute difference between the total number of students in Class X and the total number of students in Class XI?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total students in Class X = 40 + 50 + 30 = 120. Total students in Class XI = 30 + 60 + 40 = 130. Absolute difference = 130 - 120 = 10.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-bar-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Deducing Costs from Profit Margin Graph] Margin %: Y1=10%, Y2=15%, Y3=20%. If Revenue in Y2 is 200k, find Cost in Y2.", "options": ["A) $150,000", "B) $170,000", "C) $185,000", "D) $230,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A bar graph displays a company's yearly Profit Margin Percentage (defined as Profit / Sales Revenue × 100). The graph shows: Year 1 = 10%, Year 2 = 15%, Year 3 = 20%. If the absolute Sales Revenue in Year 2 was exactly $200,000, what was the absolute Cost incurred in Year 2? (Note: Cost = Revenue - Profit)", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Profit Margin = 15%. This means Profit is 15% of Sales Revenue. Profit = 0.15 × 200,000 = $30,000. Since Revenue = Cost + Profit, Cost = Revenue - Profit. Cost = 200,000 - 30,000 = $170,000.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-bar-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Year-over-Year Percentage Increase] Sales: 2018=45, 2019=55, 2020=40, 2021=60. % increase from 2020 to 2021?", "options": ["A) 20%", "B) 33.33%", "C) 50%", "D) 150%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A bar graph records car sales (in thousands): 45 (2018), 55 (2019), 40 (2020), and 60 (2021). What is the exact percentage increase in sales specifically from the year 2020 to the year 2021?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Sales in 2020 = 40. Sales in 2021 = 60. Absolute increase = 60 - 40 = 20. The \\"base\\" year for the comparison is 2020 (40). Percentage increase = (Increase / Base) × 100 = (20 / 40) × 100 = 50%.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-bar-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Average Revenue Over Four Quarters] Revenue: Q1=100, Q2=120, Q3=90, Q4=140. Average revenue per quarter?", "options": ["A) 110", "B) 112.5", "C) 115", "D) 120"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A quarterly bar graph shows a startup's revenue (in thousands): Q1 = 100, Q2 = 120, Q3 = 90, Q4 = 140. What is the exact average revenue per quarter for this entire fiscal year?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total revenue for the year = 100 + 120 + 90 + 140 = 450. Since there are 4 quarters, Average = 450 / 4 = 112.5.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-bar-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Multi-Brand Combined Averages] Total milk by A, B, C in 2011 = 35+20+25 = 80. Average = 26.6. How many times was Brand A's production > the average for that year?", "options": ["A) 2", "B) 3", "C) 4", "D) 5"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A complex bar graph displays yearly milk production for three brands. In a specific year (2011), Brand A produced 35, Brand B produced 20, and Brand C produced 25. The mathematical average production across all three brands for that year is 26.6. If a candidate evaluates all 5 years and finds the yearly averages to be 26.6, 33.3, 28.3, 31.6, and 26.6, and Brand A's corresponding productions are 35, 40, 35, 32, and 25. Exactly how many times did Brand A strictly beat the yearly average?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "We compare Brand A's production against the average for each respective year. Year 1: 35 > 26.6 (Yes). Year 2: 40 > 33.3 (Yes). Year 3: 35 > 28.3 (Yes). Year 4: 32 > 31.6 (Yes). Year 5: 25 < 26.6 (No). Brand A strictly beat the yearly average exactly 4 times.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-bar-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Ratio of Aggregate Metrics] Grouped Bar: Pass/Fail counts. Year 1: P=80, F=20. Year 2: P=90, F=30. Ratio of total passed to total failed?", "options": ["A) 17 : 5", "B) 16 : 5", "C) 3 : 1", "D) 4 : 1"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A grouped bar graph shows student pass/fail counts for two consecutive years. \\nYear 1: Passed = 80, Failed = 20\\nYear 2: Passed = 90, Failed = 30\\nWhat is the exact ratio of the total number of passed students to the total number of failed students across both years combined?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total passed = 80 + 90 = 170. Total failed = 20 + 30 = 50. Ratio of total passed to total failed = 170 : 50. Dividing both by 10 yields 17 : 5.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-bar-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Missing Bar Deduction from Average] A bar graph has 5 bars. Average is 60. First four bars are 40, 50, 70, 90. What is the value of the 5th bar?", "options": ["A) $40", "B) $50", "C) $60", "D) $70"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A bar graph displays the monthly expenses for 5 months. The exact overall average expense for the 5 months is explicitly stated as $60. However, the top of the 5th bar is ripped off. The values of the first four visible bars are $40, $50, $70, and $90. What must be the exact value of the 5th bar?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Total sum required for 5 months with an average of 60 = 5 × 60 = 300. The sum of the first four bars = 40 + 50 + 70 + 90 = 250. The value of the 5th bar must perfectly make up the difference: 300 - 250 = 50. Thus, the 5th bar is $50.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22110	Lecture 25: Date Aggregation Operators — $year, $month, $dayOfMonth, $dateAdd, $dateSubtract, $dateDiff, $dateFromParts & $dateToString	mongo-lecture-25-date-aggregation-operators	Extract date components, add or subtract time intervals, compute date differences, construct dates with timezones, and format human-readable date strings with $dateToString.	2026-10-06 19:52:12.616986+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhen dates are stored properly in MongoDB's native **`ISODate`** format, **Date Aggregation Operators** let you extract calendar components, perform date arithmetic, calculate elapsed time between dates, and format human-readable date strings for frontend UIs.\n\n### 💡 Date Component Extraction Operators\n* **Calendar Parts:** **`$year`**, **`$month`** (1–12), **`$week`** (0–53), **`$dayOfMonth`** (1–31), **`$dayOfWeek`** (1=Sunday to 7=Saturday), **`$dayOfYear`** (1–366).\n* **ISO 8601 Calendar Parts (Monday = Day 1):** **`$isoDayOfWeek`** (1=Monday to 7=Sunday), **`$isoWeek`**, **`$isoWeekYear`**.\n* **Time Parts:** **`$hour`** (0–23), **`$minute`** (0–59), **`$second`** (0–59), **`$millisecond`** (0–999).\n\n### 💡 Date Math, Construction & Formatting Operators\n* **`$dateAdd` & `$dateSubtract`:** Adds or subtracts a time interval from a date:\n  `{ startDate: "$dob", unit: "day" | "month" | "year" | "hour", amount: 5 }`.\n* **`$dateDiff`:** Computes the difference between two dates in the specified `unit`:\n  `{ startDate: "$dob", endDate: ISODate("2025-12-31T00:00:00Z"), unit: "year" | "day" }`.\n* **`$dateFromParts` & `$dateToParts`:** Constructs an `ISODate` from numeric `{ year, month, day, hour, minute, timezone }` parts, or decomposes an `ISODate` into an object containing all its individual parts.\n* **`$dateTrunc`:** Truncates a date down to the first day/instant of the specified `unit` (e.g., `unit: "month"` resets `15 Jan 2008` to `01 Jan 2008`).\n* **`$dateToString`:** Formats an `ISODate` into a readable custom string using format specifiers (`%d` = 2-digit day, `%b` = abbreviated month name like `Jan`, `%m` = 2-digit month, `%Y` = 4-digit year):\n  `{ date: "$dob", format: "%d-%b-%Y" }` -> `"15-Jan-2008"`.\n* **`$toDate` & `$dateFromString`:** Converts a date string (like `"20 March 2018"`) or Unix millisecond timestamp into a native `ISODate`.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical Date Operator Pipeline Examples\n```javascript\n// 1. Extract Year, Month, Day, Hour, Minute, and format with $dateToString\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      birthYear: { $year: "$dob" },\n      birthMonth: { $month: "$dob" },\n      dayOfMonth: { $dayOfMonth: "$dob" },\n      dayOfWeek: { $dayOfWeek: "$dob" },\n      isoWeekday: { $isoDayOfWeek: "$dob" },\n      formattedDOB: {\n        $dateToString: {\n          date: "$dob",\n          format: "%d-%b-%Y"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 2. Date Arithmetic: $dateAdd, $dateSubtract, and $dateDiff\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      plusFiveDays: {\n        $dateAdd: { startDate: "$dob", unit: "day", amount: 5 }\n      },\n      minusOneMonth: {\n        $dateSubtract: { startDate: "$dob", unit: "month", amount: 1 }\n      },\n      ageInYears2025: {\n        $dateDiff: {\n          startDate: "$dob",\n          endDate: ISODate("2025-12-31T00:00:00Z"),\n          unit: "year"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 3. Construct Date with Timezone ($dateFromParts) & Deconstruct ($dateToParts)\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      constructedDate: {\n        $dateFromParts: {\n          year: 2025,\n          month: 1,\n          day: 7,\n          hour: 15,\n          minute: 45,\n          timezone: "Europe/London"\n        }\n      },\n      dobParts: {\n        $dateToParts: { date: "$dob", timezone: "UTC" }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/#date-expression-operators", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/#date-expression-operators", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 25: Date Aggregation Operators"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bKIFrtpwAws", "title": "MongoDB Date Operators Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "38m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[{"id": "mongo-25-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374ed", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374ed", "name": "Problem #224: Logs sorted by timestamp descending", "title": "Problem #224: Logs sorted by timestamp descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "224", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return logs sorted by **timestamp** descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374ed"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db3746c", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db3746c", "name": "Problem #265: Find order by orderId ORD-1019", "title": "Problem #265: Find order by orderId ORD-1019", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "265", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the order with orderId \\"ORD-1019\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db3746c"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-3", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b97a3fe74e0db37409", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b97a3fe74e0db37409", "name": "Problem #298: Find order by customerId 14", "title": "Problem #298: Find order by customerId 14", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "298", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return orders where customerId is 14.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b97a3fe74e0db37409"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-4", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37381", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37381", "name": "Problem #342: Orders with status pending", "title": "Problem #342: Orders with status pending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "342", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return orders where status is \\"pending\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37381"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-5", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3735a", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3735a", "name": "Problem #355: Limit orders to 15", "title": "Problem #355: Limit orders to 15", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "355", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return only 15 orders.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3735a"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-6", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37357", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37357", "name": "Problem #356: Orders sorted by createdAt descending", "title": "Problem #356: Orders sorted by createdAt descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "356", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return orders sorted by **createdAt** descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37357"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-7", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3734e", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3734e", "name": "Problem #359: Find order by orderId", "title": "Problem #359: Find order by orderId", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "359", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the order with **orderId** \\"ORD-1001\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3734e"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-8", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37336", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37336", "name": "Problem #367: Orders with status cancelled", "title": "Problem #367: Orders with status cancelled", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "367", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return orders where status is \\"cancelled\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37336"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-9", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37309", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37309", "name": "Problem #382: Customers sorted by createdAt descending", "title": "Problem #382: Customers sorted by createdAt descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "382", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return customers sorted by **createdAt** descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37309"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-10", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db37306", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db37306", "name": "Problem #383: Find orders with status pending or shipped", "title": "Problem #383: Find orders with status pending or shipped", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "383", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return orders where **status** is pending or shipped.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db37306"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhen dates are stored properly in MongoDB's native **`ISODate`** format, **Date Aggregation Operators** let you extract calendar components, perform date arithmetic, calculate elapsed time between dates, and format human-readable date strings for frontend UIs.\n\n### 💡 Date Component Extraction Operators\n* **Calendar Parts:** **`$year`**, **`$month`** (1–12), **`$week`** (0–53), **`$dayOfMonth`** (1–31), **`$dayOfWeek`** (1=Sunday to 7=Saturday), **`$dayOfYear`** (1–366).\n* **ISO 8601 Calendar Parts (Monday = Day 1):** **`$isoDayOfWeek`** (1=Monday to 7=Sunday), **`$isoWeek`**, **`$isoWeekYear`**.\n* **Time Parts:** **`$hour`** (0–23), **`$minute`** (0–59), **`$second`** (0–59), **`$millisecond`** (0–999).\n\n### 💡 Date Math, Construction & Formatting Operators\n* **`$dateAdd` & `$dateSubtract`:** Adds or subtracts a time interval from a date:\n  `{ startDate: "$dob", unit: "day" | "month" | "year" | "hour", amount: 5 }`.\n* **`$dateDiff`:** Computes the difference between two dates in the specified `unit`:\n  `{ startDate: "$dob", endDate: ISODate("2025-12-31T00:00:00Z"), unit: "year" | "day" }`.\n* **`$dateFromParts` & `$dateToParts`:** Constructs an `ISODate` from numeric `{ year, month, day, hour, minute, timezone }` parts, or decomposes an `ISODate` into an object containing all its individual parts.\n* **`$dateTrunc`:** Truncates a date down to the first day/instant of the specified `unit` (e.g., `unit: "month"` resets `15 Jan 2008` to `01 Jan 2008`).\n* **`$dateToString`:** Formats an `ISODate` into a readable custom string using format specifiers (`%d` = 2-digit day, `%b` = abbreviated month name like `Jan`, `%m` = 2-digit month, `%Y` = 4-digit year):\n  `{ date: "$dob", format: "%d-%b-%Y" }` -> `"15-Jan-2008"`.\n* **`$toDate` & `$dateFromString`:** Converts a date string (like `"20 March 2018"`) or Unix millisecond timestamp into a native `ISODate`.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical Date Operator Pipeline Examples\n```javascript\n// 1. Extract Year, Month, Day, Hour, Minute, and format with $dateToString\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      birthYear: { $year: "$dob" },\n      birthMonth: { $month: "$dob" },\n      dayOfMonth: { $dayOfMonth: "$dob" },\n      dayOfWeek: { $dayOfWeek: "$dob" },\n      isoWeekday: { $isoDayOfWeek: "$dob" },\n      formattedDOB: {\n        $dateToString: {\n          date: "$dob",\n          format: "%d-%b-%Y"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 2. Date Arithmetic: $dateAdd, $dateSubtract, and $dateDiff\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      plusFiveDays: {\n        $dateAdd: { startDate: "$dob", unit: "day", amount: 5 }\n      },\n      minusOneMonth: {\n        $dateSubtract: { startDate: "$dob", unit: "month", amount: 1 }\n      },\n      ageInYears2025: {\n        $dateDiff: {\n          startDate: "$dob",\n          endDate: ISODate("2025-12-31T00:00:00Z"),\n          unit: "year"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 3. Construct Date with Timezone ($dateFromParts) & Deconstruct ($dateToParts)\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      constructedDate: {\n        $dateFromParts: {\n          year: 2025,\n          month: 1,\n          day: 7,\n          hour: 15,\n          minute: 45,\n          timezone: "Europe/London"\n        }\n      },\n      dobParts: {\n        $dateToParts: { date: "$dob", timezone: "UTC" }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n```	Yahoo Baba	https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba	655	Lecture 25: Date Aggregation Operators — $year, $month, $dayOfMonth, $dateAdd, $dateSubtract, $dateDiff, $dateFromParts & $dateToString	Extract date components, add or subtract time intervals, compute date differences, construct dates with timezones, and format human-readable date strings with $dateToString.	20	Easy	\N	25	[{"id": "mongo-25-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374ed", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374ed", "name": "Problem #224: Logs sorted by timestamp descending", "title": "Problem #224: Logs sorted by timestamp descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "224", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return logs sorted by **timestamp** descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374ed"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db3746c", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db3746c", "name": "Problem #265: Find order by orderId ORD-1019", "title": "Problem #265: Find order by orderId ORD-1019", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "265", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the order with orderId \\"ORD-1019\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db3746c"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-3", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b97a3fe74e0db37409", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b97a3fe74e0db37409", "name": "Problem #298: Find order by customerId 14", "title": "Problem #298: Find order by customerId 14", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "298", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return orders where customerId is 14.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b97a3fe74e0db37409"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-4", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37381", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37381", "name": "Problem #342: Orders with status pending", "title": "Problem #342: Orders with status pending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "342", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return orders where status is \\"pending\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37381"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-5", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3735a", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3735a", "name": "Problem #355: Limit orders to 15", "title": "Problem #355: Limit orders to 15", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "355", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return only 15 orders.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3735a"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-6", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37357", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37357", "name": "Problem #356: Orders sorted by createdAt descending", "title": "Problem #356: Orders sorted by createdAt descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "356", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return orders sorted by **createdAt** descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37357"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-7", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3734e", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3734e", "name": "Problem #359: Find order by orderId", "title": "Problem #359: Find order by orderId", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "359", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the order with **orderId** \\"ORD-1001\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3734e"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-8", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37336", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37336", "name": "Problem #367: Orders with status cancelled", "title": "Problem #367: Orders with status cancelled", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "367", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return orders where status is \\"cancelled\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37336"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-9", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37309", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37309", "name": "Problem #382: Customers sorted by createdAt descending", "title": "Problem #382: Customers sorted by createdAt descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "382", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return customers sorted by **createdAt** descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37309"}, {"id": "mongo-25-prob-10", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db37306", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db37306", "name": "Problem #383: Find orders with status pending or shipped", "title": "Problem #383: Find orders with status pending or shipped", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "383", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return orders where **status** is pending or shipped.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db37306"}]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/#date-expression-operators	\N	\N	\N
22111	Lecture 26: Array Aggregation Operators — $arrayElemAt, $firstN/$lastN, $maxN/$minN, $slice, $sortArray, $reverseArray, $size, $in, $filter, $map & $objectToArray	mongo-lecture-26-array-aggregation-operators	Index, slice, sort, reverse, search, filter, map, and convert arrays in MongoDB aggregation pipelines using native array expression operators.	2026-10-06 19:52:12.616986+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMongoDB's **Array Aggregation Operators** let you inspect, slice, sort, filter, transform, and convert array fields inside `$project` and `$addFields` without needing to `$unwind` the array first.\n\n### 💡 Element Access, Slicing & Ordering Operators\n* **`$arrayElemAt: [<array>, <index>`:** Returns the element at 0-based `<index>` (negative indices count backward from the end, e.g., `-1` is the last element).\n* **`$firstN` & `$lastN: { input: <array>, n: <count> }`:** Returns the first `n` or last `n` elements of an array.\n* **`$maxN` & `$minN: { input: <array>, n: <count> }`:** Returns the `n` largest or `n` smallest values from a numeric array.\n* **`$slice: [<array>, <position>, <n>`:** Extracts a subset of `n` elements from `<array>` starting at `<position>`.\n* **`$sortArray: { input: <array>, sortBy: 1 | -1 | { field: 1 } }`:** Sorts an array of primitives or sub-documents in ascending (`1`) or descending (`-1`) order.\n* **`$reverseArray: <array>`:** Reverses the order of elements in an array.\n\n### 💡 Array Inspection, Transformation & Conversion Operators\n* **`$size: <array>`:** Returns the total number of elements in an array.\n* **`$isArray: <field>`:** Returns `true` if the operand is an array, or `false` otherwise.\n* **`$in: [<value>, <array>`:** Returns `true` if `<value>` exists inside `<array>`, or `false` otherwise.\n* **`$indexOfArray: [<array>, <searchValue>`:** Returns the 0-based index of `<searchValue>` in `<array>` (or `-1` if not found).\n* **`$concatArrays: [<array1>, <array2>`:** Concatenates two or more arrays into a single array.\n* **`$filter: { input: <array>, as: "<var>", cond: <expr> }`:** Filters array elements matching boolean `cond` (referencing each element via `"$$<var>"`).\n* **`$map: { input: <array>, as: "<var>", in: <expr> }`:** Applies an expression to every element in an array and returns the transformed array.\n* **`$reduce: { input: <array>, initialValue: <val>, in: <expr> }`:** Reduces an array to a single accumulated scalar value using `$$value` and `$$this`.\n* **`$zip` & `$range`:** `$zip` transposes parallel arrays together by index; `$range: [<start>, <end>, <step>` generates a sequence of integers.\n* **`$objectToArray` & `$arrayToObject`:** Converts a key-value object `{ name: "Ram", age: 25 }` into an array of `[ { k: "name", v: "Ram" }, { k: "age", v: 25 } ` pairs (and vice versa).\n\n### 🛠️ Practical Array Operator Pipeline Examples\n```javascript\n// 1. Indexing, Slicing, Sorting, Reversing, and Sizing Arrays\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      secondSkill: { $arrayElemAt: ["$skills", 1 },\n      firstTwoSkills: { $firstN: { input: "$skills", n: 2 } },\n      topTwoMarks: { $maxN: { input: "$marks", n: 2 } },\n      slicedSkills: { $slice: ["$skills", 1, 2 },\n      sortedMarks: { $sortArray: { input: "$marks", sortBy: 1 } },\n      reversedSkills: { $reverseArray: "$skills" },\n      totalSkillsCount: { $size: "$skills" }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 2. Searching ($in, $indexOfArray), Filtering ($filter), and Mapping ($map)\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      knowsPython: { $in: ["Python", "$skills" },\n      pythonIndex: { $indexOfArray: ["$skills", "Python" },\n      highMarks: {\n        $filter: {\n          input: "$marks",\n          as: "m",\n          cond: { $gte: ["$$m", 60 }\n        }\n      },\n      bonusMarks: {\n        $map: {\n          input: "$marks",\n          as: "m",\n          in: { $add: ["$$m", 5 }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 3. Converting an Embedded Object into [{ k, v } Array with $objectToArray\ndb.personal.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      addressArray: { $objectToArray: "$address" }\n    }\n  }\n)\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/#array-expression-operators", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/#array-expression-operators", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 26: Array Aggregation Operators"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZR8P6-gCfME", "title": "MongoDB Array Operators Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "54m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[{"id": "mongo-26-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37330", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37330", "name": "Problem #369: Skip first 20 products", "title": "Problem #369: Skip first 20 products", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "369", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return all products except the first 20.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37330"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db37294", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db37294", "name": "Problem #421: First 3 departments by budget ascending", "title": "Problem #421: First 3 departments by budget ascending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "421", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the 3 departments with smallest budget.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db37294"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-3", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371f8", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371f8", "name": "Problem #463: Skip first 5 in aggregation", "title": "Problem #463: Skip first 5 in aggregation", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "463", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: all users, **$sort** by age ascending, **$skip** 5, **$limit** 10.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371f8"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-4", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371e3", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371e3", "name": "Problem #470: First 20 users sorted by createdAt descending", "title": "Problem #470: First 20 users sorted by createdAt descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "470", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return 20 users with most recent **createdAt** first.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371e3"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-5", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db37162", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db37162", "name": "Problem #513: Skip first 10 users", "title": "Problem #513: Skip first 10 users", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "513", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return all users except the first 10.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db37162"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-6", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c27a3fe74e0db37784", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c27a3fe74e0db37784", "name": "Problem #5: Reviews with rating 2", "title": "Problem #5: Reviews with rating 2", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return reviews where rating is 2.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c27a3fe74e0db37784"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-7", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c27a3fe74e0db3777b", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c27a3fe74e0db3777b", "name": "Problem #8: Find inventory by sku SKU-1023", "title": "Problem #8: Find inventory by sku SKU-1023", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "8", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the inventory with sku \\"SKU-1023\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c27a3fe74e0db3777b"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-8", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db37700", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db37700", "name": "Problem #49: Find inventory by _id 14", "title": "Problem #49: Find inventory by _id 14", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "49", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the inventory document with _id 14.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db37700"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-9", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db376fd", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db376fd", "name": "Problem #50: Find reviews by userId 13", "title": "Problem #50: Find reviews by userId 13", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "50", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return reviews where userId is 13.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db376fd"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-10", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db376fa", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db376fa", "name": "Problem #51: Find reviews by productId 13", "title": "Problem #51: Find reviews by productId 13", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "51", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return reviews where productId is 13.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db376fa"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-11", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37645", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37645", "name": "Problem #110: Find reviews with rating 5", "title": "Problem #110: Find reviews with rating 5", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "110", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return reviews where **rating** is 5.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37645"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-12", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9be7a3fe74e0db375f1", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9be7a3fe74e0db375f1", "name": "Problem #138: Find article by title Article 24", "title": "Problem #138: Find article by title Article 24", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "138", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the article with title \\"Article 24\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9be7a3fe74e0db375f1"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMongoDB's **Array Aggregation Operators** let you inspect, slice, sort, filter, transform, and convert array fields inside `$project` and `$addFields` without needing to `$unwind` the array first.\n\n### 💡 Element Access, Slicing & Ordering Operators\n* **`$arrayElemAt: [<array>, <index>`:** Returns the element at 0-based `<index>` (negative indices count backward from the end, e.g., `-1` is the last element).\n* **`$firstN` & `$lastN: { input: <array>, n: <count> }`:** Returns the first `n` or last `n` elements of an array.\n* **`$maxN` & `$minN: { input: <array>, n: <count> }`:** Returns the `n` largest or `n` smallest values from a numeric array.\n* **`$slice: [<array>, <position>, <n>`:** Extracts a subset of `n` elements from `<array>` starting at `<position>`.\n* **`$sortArray: { input: <array>, sortBy: 1 | -1 | { field: 1 } }`:** Sorts an array of primitives or sub-documents in ascending (`1`) or descending (`-1`) order.\n* **`$reverseArray: <array>`:** Reverses the order of elements in an array.\n\n### 💡 Array Inspection, Transformation & Conversion Operators\n* **`$size: <array>`:** Returns the total number of elements in an array.\n* **`$isArray: <field>`:** Returns `true` if the operand is an array, or `false` otherwise.\n* **`$in: [<value>, <array>`:** Returns `true` if `<value>` exists inside `<array>`, or `false` otherwise.\n* **`$indexOfArray: [<array>, <searchValue>`:** Returns the 0-based index of `<searchValue>` in `<array>` (or `-1` if not found).\n* **`$concatArrays: [<array1>, <array2>`:** Concatenates two or more arrays into a single array.\n* **`$filter: { input: <array>, as: "<var>", cond: <expr> }`:** Filters array elements matching boolean `cond` (referencing each element via `"$$<var>"`).\n* **`$map: { input: <array>, as: "<var>", in: <expr> }`:** Applies an expression to every element in an array and returns the transformed array.\n* **`$reduce: { input: <array>, initialValue: <val>, in: <expr> }`:** Reduces an array to a single accumulated scalar value using `$$value` and `$$this`.\n* **`$zip` & `$range`:** `$zip` transposes parallel arrays together by index; `$range: [<start>, <end>, <step>` generates a sequence of integers.\n* **`$objectToArray` & `$arrayToObject`:** Converts a key-value object `{ name: "Ram", age: 25 }` into an array of `[ { k: "name", v: "Ram" }, { k: "age", v: 25 } ` pairs (and vice versa).\n\n### 🛠️ Practical Array Operator Pipeline Examples\n```javascript\n// 1. Indexing, Slicing, Sorting, Reversing, and Sizing Arrays\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      secondSkill: { $arrayElemAt: ["$skills", 1 },\n      firstTwoSkills: { $firstN: { input: "$skills", n: 2 } },\n      topTwoMarks: { $maxN: { input: "$marks", n: 2 } },\n      slicedSkills: { $slice: ["$skills", 1, 2 },\n      sortedMarks: { $sortArray: { input: "$marks", sortBy: 1 } },\n      reversedSkills: { $reverseArray: "$skills" },\n      totalSkillsCount: { $size: "$skills" }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 2. Searching ($in, $indexOfArray), Filtering ($filter), and Mapping ($map)\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      knowsPython: { $in: ["Python", "$skills" },\n      pythonIndex: { $indexOfArray: ["$skills", "Python" },\n      highMarks: {\n        $filter: {\n          input: "$marks",\n          as: "m",\n          cond: { $gte: ["$$m", 60 }\n        }\n      },\n      bonusMarks: {\n        $map: {\n          input: "$marks",\n          as: "m",\n          in: { $add: ["$$m", 5 }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 3. Converting an Embedded Object into [{ k, v } Array with $objectToArray\ndb.personal.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      addressArray: { $objectToArray: "$address" }\n    }\n  }\n)\n```	Yahoo Baba	https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba	655	Lecture 26: Array Aggregation Operators — $arrayElemAt, $firstN/$lastN, $maxN/$minN, $slice, $sortArray, $reverseArray, $size, $in, $filter, $map & $objectToArray	Index, slice, sort, reverse, search, filter, map, and convert arrays in MongoDB aggregation pipelines using native array expression operators.	20	Easy	\N	26	[{"id": "mongo-26-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37330", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37330", "name": "Problem #369: Skip first 20 products", "title": "Problem #369: Skip first 20 products", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "369", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return all products except the first 20.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37330"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db37294", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db37294", "name": "Problem #421: First 3 departments by budget ascending", "title": "Problem #421: First 3 departments by budget ascending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "421", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the 3 departments with smallest budget.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db37294"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-3", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371f8", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371f8", "name": "Problem #463: Skip first 5 in aggregation", "title": "Problem #463: Skip first 5 in aggregation", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "463", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: all users, **$sort** by age ascending, **$skip** 5, **$limit** 10.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371f8"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-4", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371e3", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371e3", "name": "Problem #470: First 20 users sorted by createdAt descending", "title": "Problem #470: First 20 users sorted by createdAt descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "470", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return 20 users with most recent **createdAt** first.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371e3"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-5", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db37162", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db37162", "name": "Problem #513: Skip first 10 users", "title": "Problem #513: Skip first 10 users", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "513", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return all users except the first 10.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db37162"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-6", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c27a3fe74e0db37784", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c27a3fe74e0db37784", "name": "Problem #5: Reviews with rating 2", "title": "Problem #5: Reviews with rating 2", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return reviews where rating is 2.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c27a3fe74e0db37784"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-7", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c27a3fe74e0db3777b", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c27a3fe74e0db3777b", "name": "Problem #8: Find inventory by sku SKU-1023", "title": "Problem #8: Find inventory by sku SKU-1023", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "8", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the inventory with sku \\"SKU-1023\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c27a3fe74e0db3777b"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-8", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db37700", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db37700", "name": "Problem #49: Find inventory by _id 14", "title": "Problem #49: Find inventory by _id 14", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "49", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the inventory document with _id 14.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db37700"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-9", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db376fd", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db376fd", "name": "Problem #50: Find reviews by userId 13", "title": "Problem #50: Find reviews by userId 13", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "50", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return reviews where userId is 13.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db376fd"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-10", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db376fa", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db376fa", "name": "Problem #51: Find reviews by productId 13", "title": "Problem #51: Find reviews by productId 13", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "51", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return reviews where productId is 13.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c17a3fe74e0db376fa"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-11", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37645", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37645", "name": "Problem #110: Find reviews with rating 5", "title": "Problem #110: Find reviews with rating 5", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "110", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return reviews where **rating** is 5.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37645"}, {"id": "mongo-26-prob-12", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9be7a3fe74e0db375f1", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9be7a3fe74e0db375f1", "name": "Problem #138: Find article by title Article 24", "title": "Problem #138: Find article by title Article 24", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "138", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the article with title \\"Article 24\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9be7a3fe74e0db375f1"}]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/#array-expression-operators	\N	\N	\N
22164	DBMS: Complete Database Management Systems in 1 Shot (Placement & GATE Edition)	cs-dbms-complete-oneshot-mastery	Master Database Management Systems from scratch to advanced system design—3-Schema Architecture, ER Modeling, Relational Algebra, Functional Dependencies, Normalization (1NF to BCNF/4NF), SQL, ACID Transactions, Concurrency Control (2PL, Timestamp Ordering), B/B+ Tree Indexing, Crash Recovery (WAL), and Sharding—followed by a 50-question timed evaluation test.	2026-10-07 00:11:36.831457+05:30	### 📌 1. Why DBMS Over File Systems & The 3-Schema Architecture\nTraditional flat-file systems suffer from **Data Redundancy & Inconsistency**, **Lack of Concurrent Access Control** (race conditions when two processes write simultaneously), **No Atomic Crash Recovery**, **Poor Security Granularity**, and $O(N)$ linear file scans. A **DBMS (Database Management System)** solves these using a layered architecture.\n* **Three-Schema ANSI-SPARC Architecture:**\n  1. **Internal (Physical) Level:** Describes how data is physically stored on disk (byte offsets, B+ Tree indexes, block allocation, compression).\n  2. **Conceptual (Logical) Level:** Describes *what* data is stored across the entire database (tables, columns, data types, constraints, relationships).\n  3. **External (View) Level:** Customized sub-views tailored to specific user roles (e.g., Logistics only sees shipping addresses; Finance sees billing records).\n* **Data Independence:**\n  * **Physical Data Independence:** Ability to modify the Physical Schema (e.g., switching from a hash index to a B+ Tree or moving data files to a new SSD) without altering the Conceptual Schema or application queries.\n  * **Logical Data Independence:** Ability to modify the Conceptual Schema (e.g., adding a new column or splitting a table) without rewriting External Views or application code. (Harder to achieve than Physical Data Independence).\n\n### 💡 2. Entity-Relationship (ER) Modeling & Reduction to Relational Tables\n* **Attributes:** **Simple** vs. **Composite** (e.g., `Name` -> `firstName`, `lastName`), **Single-valued** vs. **Multivalued** (double oval, e.g., `phoneNumbers` — always requires a **separate table**!), **Stored** vs. **Derived** (dashed oval, e.g., `age` computed from `dob`).\n* **Weak Entity Set (Double Rectangle):** Lacks sufficient attributes to form a Primary Key of its own; depends on a **Strong (Owner) Entity** via an **Identifying Relationship** (double diamond) and uses a **Partial Key (Discriminator)** (dashed underline). Always has **Total Participation** (double line) in its identifying relationship.\n* **Minimum Number of Tables Required When Converting an ER Diagram:**\n  * **1:1 Relationship:** Merge into either entity's table (if one side has Total Participation, merge into that side; if both have Total Participation, merge both entities into 1 single table).\n  * **1:N (One-to-Many) Relationship:** Merge the relationship into the **Many ($N$) side's table** by adding the Primary Key of the $1$-side as a Foreign Key.\n  * **M:N (Many-to-Many) Relationship:** Always requires a **separate junction/bridge table** whose Primary Key is the composite of both entities' Primary Keys.\n  * **Multivalued Attribute:** Always requires a **separate table** `(entity_pk, multivalued_attr)`.\n\n### 💡 3. Keys, Relational Algebra & Tuple Relational Calculus\n* **Superkey (SK):** Any set of attributes that uniquely identifies a tuple in a relation. If a relation $R$ has $n$ attributes and a single simple Candidate Key of 1 attribute, the number of Superkeys is $2^{n-1}$.\n* **Candidate Key (CK):** A **minimal Superkey** (no proper subset of a Candidate Key is a Superkey). Attributes that belong to *at least one* Candidate Key are called **Prime Attributes**; all others are **Non-Prime Attributes**.\n* **Primary Key (PK) vs. Unique Key:** A table can have only **one** Primary Key (`NOT NULL` + Unique, Entity Integrity), whereas it can have **multiple** Unique Keys (which allow `NULL` values).\n* **Relational Algebra (Procedural — Specifies *How* to Obtain Results):**\n  * **6 Fundamental Operators:** Selection ($sigma$), Projection ($pi$ — automatically eliminates duplicate rows!), Cartesian Product ($\times$), Set Union ($cup$), Set Difference ($-$), and Rename ($\rho$).\n  * **Derived Operators:** Natural Join ($\bowtie$), Theta/Equi Join ($\bowtie_\\theta$), Set Intersection ($R \\cap S = R - (R - S)$), and Division ($R(A,B) \\div S(B)$ — finds all $A$ values associated with *every* $B$ value in $S$, expressed as $\\pi_A(R) - \\pi_A((\\pi_A(R) \\times S) - R)$).\n* **Tuple Relational Calculus (TRC — Non-Procedural / Declarative):** $\\{ t \\mid P(t) \\}$ specifies *what* tuples to retrieve rather than how.\n\n### 💡 4. Functional Dependencies (FDs), Closures & Normalization (`1NF` to `BCNF` / `4NF`)\n* **Functional Dependency ($X \\rightarrow Y$):** If two tuples agree on attribute set $X$, they must also agree on attribute set $Y$ ($t_1[X] = t_2[X] \\implies t_1[Y] = t_2[Y]$).\n* **Finding Attribute Closure ($X^+$):** Start with $result = X$. Repeatedly add $B$ to $result$ for every FD $A \\rightarrow B$ where $A \\subseteq result$. If $X^+$ contains all attributes of $R$, then $X$ is a **Superkey**.\n* **Normal Forms Hierarchy (Eliminating Update, Insertion, and Deletion Anomalies):**\n  1. **1NF (First Normal Form):** Every attribute must hold **atomic (indivisible)** values (no arrays or comma-separated lists in a cell).\n  2. **2NF (Second Normal Form):** Must be in 1NF + **No Partial Dependency** (no **Non-Prime** attribute can depend on a *proper subset* of any Candidate Key). Note: If every Candidate Key is a single attribute, the relation is **automatically in 2NF**!\n  3. **3NF (Third Normal Form):** Must be in 2NF + **No Transitive Dependency** of a Non-Prime attribute on a Superkey. Formally, for every non-trivial FD $X \\rightarrow Y$: **either $X$ is a Superkey OR $Y$ is a Prime Attribute**.\n  4. **BCNF (Boyce-Codd Normal Form):** Strictly stronger than 3NF. For every non-trivial FD $X \\rightarrow Y$: **$X$ MUST be a Superkey** (even if $Y$ is a Prime Attribute!).\n  5. **4NF (Fourth Normal Form):** Must be in BCNF + contains **no non-trivial Multivalued Dependencies ($X \\twoheadrightarrow Y$)** where $X$ is not a Superkey.\n* **Decomposition Properties (When Splitting $R$ into $R_1$ and $R_2$):**\n  * **Lossless-Join Decomposition (Mandatory):** Natural join $R_1 \\bowtie R_2$ must reconstruct exact original relation $R$ without generating spurious tuples. Condition: $(R_1 \\cap R_2) \\rightarrow R_1$ OR $(R_1 \\cap R_2) \\rightarrow R_2$ (the common attributes must be a Superkey of at least one decomposed table).\n  * **Dependency Preservation:** Every FD in $F^+$ can be checked within a single table without joining tables. **3NF decomposition is ALWAYS both Lossless and Dependency-Preserving**; **BCNF decomposition is ALWAYS Lossless, but NOT always Dependency-Preserving**!\n\n### 💡 5. SQL Execution Order & Query Optimization Essentials\n```sql\n-- Logical Order of SQL Execution by the Query Engine:\n-- 1. FROM & JOIN (Cartesian product + ON filter)\n-- 2. WHERE       (Row-level filter BEFORE grouping; cannot use aggregate functions)\n-- 3. GROUP BY    (Buckets rows into groups)\n-- 4. HAVING      (Group-level filter AFTER grouping; can use COUNT, SUM, AVG)\n-- 5. SELECT      (Evaluates expressions and column aliases)\n-- 6. DISTINCT    (Removes duplicate output rows)\n-- 7. ORDER BY    (Sorts result set; can reference SELECT aliases)\n-- 8. LIMIT / OFFSET\n\nSELECT d.dept_name, COUNT(e.emp_id) AS emp_count, AVG(e.salary) AS avg_sal\nFROM departments d\nLEFT JOIN employees e ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id\nWHERE e.is_active = TRUE\nGROUP BY d.dept_id, d.dept_name\nHAVING COUNT(e.emp_id) >= 5\nORDER BY avg_sal DESC\nLIMIT 10;\n```\n\n### 💡 6. Transactions, ACID Properties, Serializability & Concurrency Control\n* **ACID Properties & Responsible DBMS Subsystems:**\n  * **Atomicity ("All or Nothing"):** Enforced by the **Recovery / Transaction Manager** using **Undo Logs**.\n  * **Consistency (Preserving Invariants):** Enforced by **Integrity Constraints** + **Application Logic**.\n  * **Isolation (Concurrent Execution Independence):** Enforced by the **Concurrency Control Manager** (Locks, Timestamps, MVCC).\n  * **Durability (Surviving Power Loss/Crashes):** Enforced by the **Recovery Manager** using **Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) / Redo Logs**.\n* **Concurrency Anomalies & SQL Isolation Levels:**\n  * **Dirty Read:** Reading uncommitted data written by another transaction that later rolls back.\n  * **Non-Repeatable Read:** Reading the same row twice in a transaction and getting different values because another committed transaction `UPDATE`d it.\n  * **Phantom Read:** Running the same range query (`WHERE salary > 50000`) twice and getting extra rows because another committed transaction `INSERT`ed/`DELETE`d matching rows.\n  * **Isolation Levels:** `READ UNCOMMITTED` (allows all 3) -> `READ COMMITTED` (prevents Dirty Reads; default in PostgreSQL/Oracle) -> `REPEATABLE READ` (prevents Dirty & Non-Repeatable Reads; default in MySQL InnoDB) -> `SERIALIZABLE` (prevents all 3).\n* **Serializability & Precedence Graphs:**\n  * Two operations conflict if they belong to **different transactions**, operate on the **same data item**, and **at least one of them is a `WRITE`** (`R-W`, `W-R`, `W-W`).\n  * A schedule $S$ is **Conflict Serializable** if and only if its **Precedence (Serialization) Graph is a DAG (contains NO cycles)**. Every Conflict Serializable schedule is View Serializable, whereas a View Serializable schedule that is not Conflict Serializable must contain **Blind Writes**.\n* **Locking & Timestamp Protocols:**\n  * **Basic 2PL (Two-Phase Locking):** Growing Phase (acquire locks only) -> Shrinking Phase (release locks only). Guarantees **Conflict Serializability**, but is vulnerable to **Deadlocks** and **Cascading Rollbacks**.\n  * **Strict 2PL:** Holds all **Exclusive (`X` / Write) locks** until `COMMIT`/`ABORT` — guarantees **Strict & Cascadeless schedules**!\n  * **Rigorous 2PL:** Holds **both Shared (`S`) and Exclusive (`X`) locks** until `COMMIT`/`ABORT`.\n  * **Deadlock Prevention (Timestamp-Based, where smaller $TS$ = older transaction):**\n    * **Wait-Die (Non-Preemptive):** Older waits for Younger; Younger dies (rolls back) if it requests a lock held by Older.\n    * **Wound-Wait (Preemptive):** Older wounds (preempts/rolls back) Younger; Younger waits for Older.\n\n### 💡 7. Indexing (`B-Tree` vs. `B+ Tree`), Crash Recovery (WAL) & Scaling\n* **Types of Indexes:**\n  * **Primary Index:** Ordered data file + index on **Primary Key** (Sparse — 1 entry per disk block anchor record).\n  * **Clustering Index:** Ordered data file + index on a **Non-Key** ordering field (Sparse). At most **1 Primary or Clustering index** can exist per table because rows can only be physically sorted in one order on disk!\n  * **Secondary Index:** Unordered field (Candidate Key or Non-Key). **Must be Dense** (1 entry per record) because records with the same value are scattered across different disk blocks.\n* **Why RDBMS Engines Use `B+ Trees` Over `B-Trees`:**\n  1. **Higher Fanout / Lower Tree Height:** `B+ Tree` internal nodes store **only routing keys and child block pointers** (zero data records/record pointers!). More keys fit in a single $4\\text{ KB}$ internal block, keeping tree height at 3–4 levels even for billions of rows.\n  2. **Fast Range Queries ($O(\\log N + K)$):** All keys and data pointers reside in the **Leaf Nodes**, which are linked together in a sorted **doubly linked list**—allowing `WHERE age BETWEEN 20 AND 30` to scan leaves sequentially without climbing back up the tree!\n* **Write-Ahead Logging (WAL):** Log records must be flushed to stable disk **before** the modified data page is written to disk (and all log records of a transaction must be flushed before `COMMIT` returns).	[{"url": "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc.html", "link": "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc.html", "title": "PostgreSQL Official Documentation — Concurrency Control, MVCC & Indexing Reference"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dl00fOOYLOM", "title": "Complete DBMS in 1 Video (With Notes) || For Placement Interviews", "duration": "702m", "creator_name": "CodeHelp - by Babbar", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CodeHelp"}]	[{"id": "dbms-test-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: In the ANSI-SPARC 3-Schema Architecture, a DBA replaces a hash index on the \\"orders\\" table with a B+ Tree index and moves the data file to a new NVMe partition without changing any table definitions or application SQL queries. Which property does this demonstrate?", "options": ["A) Logical Data Independence", "B) Physical Data Independence", "C) View Serializability", "D) Referential Integrity"], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "In the ANSI-SPARC 3-Schema Architecture, a DBA replaces a hash index on the \\"orders\\" table with a B+ Tree index and moves the data file to a new NVMe partition without changing any table definitions or application SQL queries. Which property does this demonstrate?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Physical Data Independence is the capacity to change the Internal (Physical) Schema—such as file organization, storage devices, or index structures—without having to alter the Conceptual (Logical) Schema or external application queries.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "dbms-test-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: Which of the following statements regarding a Weak Entity Set in an Entity-Relationship (ER) diagram is ALWAYS true?", "options": ["A) It can have partial participation in its identifying relationship.", "B) It must have total participation in its identifying relationship, and its primary key in the relational model is formed by combining the primary key of its owner (strong) entity set with its own partial key (discriminator).", "C) Its identifying relationship always requires a separate relational table in the database.", "D) It cannot have any non-key attributes of its own."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which of the following statements regarding a Weak Entity Set in an Entity-Relationship (ER) diagram is ALWAYS true?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "A Weak Entity cannot exist without an owner Strong Entity, so it ALWAYS has Total Participation (double line) in its identifying relationship. When reduced to a relational table, the Weak Entity table automatically absorbs the Identifying Relationship by combining the Owner's Primary Key with its own Partial Key (Discriminator), so no separate table is needed for the identifying relationship.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "dbms-test-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: Table Orders(order_id PK, cust_id FK) references Customers(cust_id PK) with ON DELETE SET NULL. Under what condition will defining this Foreign Key fail or cause a constraint conflict when a Customer is deleted?", "options": ["A) When cust_id in the Orders table is declared with a NOT NULL constraint (or is part of Orders's Primary Key).", "B) When the Customer has 0 orders in the Orders table.", "C) When Orders has more rows than Customers.", "D) ON DELETE SET NULL deletes the entire row from Orders."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Table Orders(order_id PK, cust_id FK) references Customers(cust_id PK) with ON DELETE SET NULL. Under what condition will defining or triggering this rule fail when a Customer is deleted?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "ON DELETE SET NULL updates the foreign key column (Orders.cust_id) to NULL whenever the referenced parent row in Customers is deleted. If Orders.cust_id was defined as NOT NULL (or is part of the Primary Key, which enforces Entity Integrity / NOT NULL), setting it to NULL violates the NOT NULL constraint and fails!", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "dbms-test-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: Which of the following correctly compares Relational Algebra (RA) and Tuple Relational Calculus (TRC)?", "options": ["A) Both RA and TRC are procedural query languages.", "B) RA is a procedural language (specifies step-by-step operations on how to retrieve data), whereas TRC is a non-procedural / declarative language (specifies what predicate the resulting tuples must satisfy); Safe TRC and Basic RA are relationally complete (equally expressive).", "C) TRC is procedural and strictly more expressive than RA.", "D) RA allows infinite result sets while TRC does not."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which of the following correctly compares Relational Algebra (RA) and Tuple Relational Calculus (TRC)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Relational Algebra is procedural because an expression prescribes an ordered sequence of operations (selection, projection, join) to compute the result. Tuple Relational Calculus ({t | P(t)}) is declarative/non-procedural. By Codd's Theorem, Basic Relational Algebra and Safe Tuple Relational Calculus have identical expressive power (Relational Completeness).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "dbms-test-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: In Relational Algebra, the Division operator R(A, B) ÷ S(B) returns all distinct A values that are paired with EVERY B value in S. Which expression using only fundamental operators is equivalent to R ÷ S?", "options": ["A) π_A(R) - π_A((π_A(R) × S) - R)", "B) π_A(R × S) - π_A(R - S)", "C) π_A(R) ∩ π_B(S)", "D) σ_{A=B}(R × S)"], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "In Relational Algebra, the Division operator R(A, B) ÷ S(B) returns all distinct A values that are paired with EVERY B value in S. Which expression using only fundamental operators is equivalent to R ÷ S?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "To compute R(A,B) ÷ S(B): 1) π_A(R) × S generates all possible (A, B) pairs if every A in R were paired with every B in S. 2) Subtracting R from that ((π_A(R) × S) - R) yields the \\"missing/disqualified\\" pairs. 3) Projecting A on those missing pairs gives all A values that failed to match at least one B in S. 4) Subtracting those disqualified A values from π_A(R) leaves only the A values paired with EVERY B in S!", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "dbms-test-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: If a Functional Dependency X -> Y holds on a relation schema R, which statement is guaranteed to be true for any valid instance r(R) and any two tuples t1 and t2?", "options": ["A) If t1[Y] == t2[Y], then t1[X] must equal t2[X].", "B) If t1[X] == t2[X], then t1[Y] must equal t2[Y]; however, if t1[X] != t2[X], t1[Y] and t2[Y] may be either equal or different.", "C) All values in column X must be unique across the entire table.", "D) X and Y must have the same data type."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "If a Functional Dependency X -> Y holds on a relation schema R, which statement is guaranteed to be true for any valid instance r(R) and any two tuples t1 and t2?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "A functional dependency X -> Y is a many-to-one mapping from X to Y: whenever two rows have identical X values (t1[X] == t2[X]), they are strictly required to have identical Y values (t1[Y] == t2[Y]). Only if t1[X] == t2[X] and t1[Y] != t2[Y] is X -> Y violated.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "dbms-test-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: Under what exact condition is a relation R in Third Normal Form (3NF), and how does Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF) tighten that rule?", "options": ["A) 3NF requires every non-trivial FD X -> Y to have X as a Superkey OR Y as a Prime Attribute; BCNF drops the second allowance and requires X to be a Superkey for EVERY non-trivial FD X -> Y.", "B) BCNF allows Partial Dependencies if Y is a Prime Attribute, whereas 3NF does not.", "C) 3NF eliminates Multivalued Dependencies, whereas BCNF eliminates Join Dependencies.", "D) Every 3NF relation is automatically in BCNF, but not every BCNF relation is in 3NF."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Under what exact condition is a relation R in Third Normal Form (3NF), and how does Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF) tighten that rule?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "A relation is in 3NF if for every non-trivial FD X -> Y, either (1) X is a Superkey, OR (2) every attribute in Y - X is a Prime Attribute (part of some Candidate Key). BCNF removes condition (2) and strictly requires that X MUST be a Superkey for every non-trivial FD X -> Y.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "dbms-test-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: Consider relation R(A, B, C) with functional dependencies AB -> C and C -> B. What are the Candidate Keys of R, which Normal Form does R satisfy, and why is decomposing R into BCNF problematic?", "options": ["A) Candidate Keys: AB and AC; R is in 3NF (because B is a prime attribute in C -> B) but violates BCNF (because C is not a superkey); decomposing into BCNF (R1(C,B) and R2(A,C)) loses the functional dependency AB -> C!", "B) Candidate Key: C only; R is in BCNF.", "C) Candidate Keys: AB and BC; R violates 2NF.", "D) R is in 1NF only and can be decomposed into BCNF without losing any dependencies."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Consider relation R(A, B, C) with functional dependencies AB -> C and C -> B. What are the Candidate Keys of R, which Normal Form does R satisfy, and why is decomposing R into BCNF problematic?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "This is the classic textbook example of 3NF vs. BCNF! Since A is never on the RHS of any FD, A must be in every key. AB+ = ABC and AC+ = ACB, so the Candidate Keys are AB and AC (making A, B, and C all Prime Attributes!). In C -> B, C is not a superkey (violating BCNF), but B is a Prime Attribute (satisfying 3NF!). If you decompose R into R1(C, B) and R2(A, C) to achieve BCNF, the decomposition is lossless, but the FD AB -> C is no longer preserved!", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "dbms-test-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: When decomposing a relation R(A, B, C, D) into two relations R1(A, B, C) and R2(C, D), what is the necessary and sufficient condition for the decomposition to be a Lossless-Join Decomposition?", "options": ["A) R1 ∩ R2 must be empty (∅).", "B) The common attribute(s) (R1 ∩ R2 = {C}) must functionally determine all attributes of R1 (C -> ABC) OR functionally determine all attributes of R2 (C -> CD) in F+.", "C) Both R1 and R2 must have the same number of attributes.", "D) C must be a non-prime attribute in R."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "When decomposing a relation R(A, B, C, D) into two relations R1(A, B, C) and R2(C, D), what is the necessary and sufficient condition for the decomposition to be a Lossless-Join Decomposition?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "By Heath's Theorem, decomposing R into R1 and R2 is lossless if and only if the intersection of their attributes (R1 ∩ R2) is a Superkey of R1 ((R1 ∩ R2) -> R1) OR a Superkey of R2 ((R1 ∩ R2) -> R2).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "dbms-test-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: A table EmpSkillsHobbies(emp_id, skill, hobby) has NO non-trivial functional dependencies (its only key is the all-attribute composite key {emp_id, skill, hobby}), yet an employee's skills are completely independent of their hobbies. Which Normal Form is violated and why?", "options": ["A) It violates 2NF due to partial dependency.", "B) It is in BCNF (since there are no non-trivial FDs), but violates Fourth Normal Form (4NF) due to a non-trivial Multivalued Dependency (emp_id ->> skill) where emp_id is not a superkey!", "C) It violates 3NF due to transitive dependency.", "D) It is already in 4NF and 5NF."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "A table EmpSkillsHobbies(emp_id, skill, hobby) has NO non-trivial functional dependencies (its only key is {emp_id, skill, hobby}), yet an employee's skills are independent of their hobbies (emp_id ->> skill and emp_id ->> hobby). Which Normal Form is violated?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Because the only key is {emp_id, skill, hobby} and there are no non-trivial FDs, the table automatically satisfies 1NF, 2NF, 3NF, and BCNF! However, storing two independent 1:N relationships (skills and hobbies) in the same table creates a cross-product redundancy (Multivalued Dependency emp_id ->> skill | hobby) where emp_id is not a superkey—violating 4NF. Decomposing into EmpSkills(emp_id, skill) and EmpHobbies(emp_id, hobby) achieves 4NF.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "dbms-test-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: Why does the SQL query SELECT dept_id, COUNT(*) AS total FROM employees WHERE total > 5 GROUP BY dept_id; fail with a syntax/column error, whereas placing the condition in HAVING works?", "options": ["A) Because the SQL engine executes WHERE (Step 2) BEFORE GROUP BY (Step 3) and SELECT (Step 5), so neither the group aggregates nor the SELECT alias \\"total\\" exist yet when WHERE is evaluated!", "B) Because COUNT(*) can only be used with ORDER BY.", "C) Because WHERE only works on string columns.", "D) Because GROUP BY executes after ORDER BY."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Why does the SQL query SELECT dept_id, COUNT(*) AS total FROM employees WHERE total > 5 GROUP BY dept_id; fail, whereas HAVING COUNT(*) > 5 works?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In the logical query processing order (FROM -> WHERE -> GROUP BY -> HAVING -> SELECT -> ORDER BY), the WHERE clause filters individual rows before grouping occurs and before SELECT aliases are computed. Aggregate functions (COUNT, SUM, AVG) and group filters are evaluated in HAVING (after GROUP BY).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "dbms-test-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: Which statement accurately contrasts the SQL commands DELETE, TRUNCATE, and DROP?", "options": ["A) DELETE is a DML command that removes rows one-by-one, logs each row deletion, fires DELETE triggers, and supports a WHERE clause; TRUNCATE is a DDL operation that deallocates data pages in bulk without firing row-level DELETE triggers; DROP removes both the data and the table schema definition.", "B) TRUNCATE supports a WHERE clause while DELETE does not.", "C) DELETE cannot be rolled back inside a transaction, whereas DROP can never be used on indexed tables.", "D) Both TRUNCATE and DROP delete only matching rows and preserve indexes."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which statement accurately contrasts the SQL commands DELETE, TRUNCATE, and DROP?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "DELETE (DML) scans and deletes matching rows individually, writing undo/redo log entries per row and firing ON DELETE triggers. TRUNCATE (DDL) deallocates whole data pages at once, resets auto-increment counters, does not support a WHERE clause, and does not fire row-level DELETE triggers. DROP (DDL) removes the entire table structure, data, and indexes from the catalog.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "dbms-test-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: Classic SQL Interview Trap: What does SELECT * FROM Emp WHERE dept_id NOT IN (10, 20, NULL); return, and why does NOT EXISTS behave differently?", "options": ["A) All employees whose dept_id is neither 10 nor 20.", "B) Exactly 0 rows (an empty result set)! Because x NOT IN (10, 20, NULL) expands to (x != 10 AND x != 20 AND x != NULL); in SQL 3-valued logic, x != NULL evaluates to UNKNOWN, and TRUE AND UNKNOWN evaluates to UNKNOWN (which WHERE filters out)!", "C) All employees whose dept_id is NULL.", "D) A syntax error because NULL cannot appear inside IN()."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "What does SELECT * FROM Emp WHERE dept_id NOT IN (10, 20, NULL); return in standard SQL?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "This is one of the most famous SQL production bugs! When a subquery inside NOT IN (SELECT mgr_id FROM ...) returns even a single NULL value, x NOT IN (10, 20, NULL) is logically equivalent to x != 10 AND x != 20 AND x != NULL. Because any comparison with NULL using = or != yields UNKNOWN (never TRUE), the entire AND conjunction evaluates to UNKNOWN (or FALSE) for every single row—returning 0 rows! Using NOT EXISTS avoids this trap.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "dbms-test-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: Which internal DBMS components are directly responsible for enforcing Atomicity, Isolation, and Durability in ACID transactions?", "options": ["A) Atomicity: Query Optimizer | Isolation: Disk Controller | Durability: Deadlock Detector", "B) Atomicity: Recovery Manager (Undo Log) | Isolation: Concurrency Control Manager (Locks / MVCC) | Durability: Recovery Manager (Redo Log / Write-Ahead Logging)", "C) Atomicity: B+ Tree Index | Isolation: Foreign Keys | Durability: Buffer Pool", "D) All three are enforced solely by the client application code."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which internal DBMS components are directly responsible for enforcing Atomicity, Isolation, and Durability in ACID transactions?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "1) Atomicity (\\"all or nothing\\") is guaranteed by the Recovery Manager using Undo logs to roll back partial changes of aborted transactions. 2) Isolation is guaranteed by the Concurrency Control Manager using 2PL locking, timestamps, or MVCC snapshots. 3) Durability is guaranteed by the Recovery Manager flushing Redo logs (WAL) to non-volatile disk before acknowledging commit.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "dbms-test-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: Under the SQL standard, which concurrency anomalies are prevented by the REPEATABLE READ isolation level, and which anomaly can still theoretically occur according to the ANSI SQL specification?", "options": ["A) Prevents Dirty Reads and Non-Repeatable Reads, but still allows Phantom Reads.", "B) Prevents only Dirty Reads, and allows Non-Repeatable Reads and Phantom Reads.", "C) Prevents Phantom Reads, but allows Dirty Reads.", "D) Prevents all anomalies only when autocommit is disabled."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Under the ANSI SQL standard, which concurrency anomalies are prevented by the REPEATABLE READ isolation level, and which anomaly can still occur?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In the ANSI SQL standard hierarchy: READ COMMITTED prevents Dirty Reads; REPEATABLE READ prevents both Dirty Reads and Non-Repeatable Reads (by holding read locks or snapshot versions on read rows), but still permits Phantom Reads (new rows inserted by another transaction matching a WHERE range predicate); SERIALIZABLE prevents all three.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "dbms-test-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: Which pair of operations in a concurrent schedule S does NOT constitute a conflict pair when constructing a Precedence (Serialization) Graph?", "options": ["A) R1(A) followed by W2(A)", "B) W1(A) followed by R2(A)", "C) R1(A) followed by R2(A), OR W1(A) followed by W2(B)", "D) W1(A) followed by W2(A)"], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which pair of operations in a concurrent schedule S does NOT constitute a conflict pair when constructing a Precedence (Serialization) Graph?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Two operations conflict if and only if: (1) they belong to different transactions (Ti != Tj), (2) they access the exact same data item (both on A), AND (3) at least one of them is a Write operation (RW, WR, or WW). Two Read operations on the same item (R1(A), R2(A)) commute and never conflict; operations on different data items (W1(A), W2(B)) also never conflict.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "dbms-test-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: Which statement regarding the relationship between Conflict Serializability and View Serializability is TRUE?", "options": ["A) Every View Serializable schedule is Conflict Serializable.", "B) Every Conflict Serializable schedule is View Serializable, and any schedule that is View Serializable but NOT Conflict Serializable MUST contain at least one Blind Write (a Write operation performed without having read the item first).", "C) Checking View Serializability takes O(V + E) polynomial time, whereas checking Conflict Serializability is NP-Complete.", "D) A schedule with a cycle in its Precedence Graph can never be View Serializable."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which statement regarding the relationship between Conflict Serializability and View Serializability is TRUE?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Conflict Serializability is a subset of View Serializability (Conflict Serializable => View Serializable). Testing Conflict Serializability via cycle detection in a Precedence Graph takes polynomial time O(V + E), whereas testing View Serializability is NP-Complete! Crucially, if a schedule has a cycle in its Precedence Graph yet is still View Serializable, it is guaranteed to contain a Blind Write (writing a value without reading it first).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "dbms-test-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: Consider the hierarchy of schedule recoverability: Strict Schedules, Cascadeless (ACA) Schedules, and Recoverable Schedules. What is the exact subset relationship and rule for each?", "options": ["A) Strict ⊂ Cascadeless ⊂ Recoverable: Recoverable requires Tj (which reads from Ti) to commit after Ti commits; Cascadeless requires Tj to read only after Ti commits (no dirty reads); Strict requires Tj to neither read NOR write an item written by Ti until Ti commits or aborts.", "B) Recoverable ⊂ Cascadeless ⊂ Strict.", "C) Cascadeless schedules allow dirty reads as long as commits happen in order.", "D) Every Conflict Serializable schedule is automatically Recoverable."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Consider the hierarchy of schedule recoverability: Strict Schedules, Cascadeless (ACA) Schedules, and Recoverable Schedules. What is the exact subset relationship and rule for each?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Recoverable: If Tj reads an uncommitted value written by Ti, Tj must not commit until Ti commits. 2) Cascadeless (Avoids Cascading Aborts): Tj is not allowed to READ an item written by Ti until Ti has committed (eliminates dirty reads). 3) Strict: Tj is not allowed to READ OR OVERWRITE (Write) an item written by Ti until Ti has committed or aborted. Therefore, Every Strict schedule is Cascadeless, and every Cascadeless schedule is Recoverable (Strict ⊂ Cascadeless ⊂ Recoverable).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "dbms-test-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: How do Basic 2PL, Strict 2PL, Rigorous 2PL, and Conservative (Static) 2PL differ in terms of Cascading Rollbacks and Deadlocks?", "options": ["A) Basic 2PL prevents deadlocks; Strict 2PL allows cascading rollbacks.", "B) Strict 2PL holds all Exclusive (X) locks until commit/abort (preventing cascading rollbacks) and Rigorous 2PL holds both S and X locks until commit/abort, but BOTH can still deadlock; Conservative 2PL pre-declares and acquires all locks before execution starts, making it 100% Deadlock-Free!", "C) Rigorous 2PL is deadlock-free while Conservative 2PL can deadlock.", "D) None of the 2PL variants guarantee Conflict Serializability."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "How do Basic 2PL, Strict 2PL, Rigorous 2PL, and Conservative (Static) 2PL differ in terms of Cascading Rollbacks and Deadlocks?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "All 2PL variants guarantee Conflict Serializability. Basic 2PL can suffer from both cascading rollbacks (because locks are released early in the shrinking phase before commit) and deadlocks. Strict 2PL (holds X locks until commit) and Rigorous 2PL (holds all S and X locks until commit) eliminate cascading rollbacks and guarantee strict schedules, but can still deadlock. Conservative (Static) 2PL requires a transaction to lock all items it needs upfront before starting (or release all and wait), eliminating hold-and-wait and making it Deadlock-Free.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "dbms-test-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: In Timestamp Ordering Concurrency Control, how does Thomas's Write Rule modify the standard Timestamp Ordering protocol when Transaction Ti issues Write(X) with TS(Ti) < W-timestamp(X) (and TS(Ti) >= R-timestamp(X))?", "options": ["A) Standard Timestamp Ordering rolls back Ti, whereas Thomas's Write Rule simply ignores (skips) Ti's obsolete Write(X) and allows Ti to continue without rolling back!", "B) Thomas's Write Rule rolls back the younger transaction that wrote X.", "C) Thomas's Write Rule overwrites X anyway and updates W-timestamp(X) to TS(Ti).", "D) Thomas's Write Rule converts Write(X) into a Read(X)."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "In Timestamp Ordering Concurrency Control, how does Thomas's Write Rule modify the standard protocol when Transaction Ti issues Write(X) with TS(Ti) < W-timestamp(X) (and TS(Ti) >= R-timestamp(X))?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "If TS(Ti) < W-timestamp(X), a younger transaction Tj has already overwritten X with a newer value. Provided no transaction has needed to read Ti's value (TS(Ti) >= R-timestamp(X)), Ti's write is an \\"obsolete write\\" that would have been immediately overwritten by Tj in a serial schedule anyway. Instead of aborting Ti (as Basic Timestamp Ordering does), Thomas's Write Rule ignores the obsolete write and lets Ti proceed—allowing more View Serializable schedules.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 20}, {"id": "dbms-test-q21", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q21: Transaction T1 has timestamp TS(T1) = 10 (Older) and Transaction T2 has timestamp TS(T2) = 20 (Younger). If T1 requests a lock currently held by T2, and separately if T2 requests a lock currently held by T1, what happens under Wait-Die vs. Wound-Wait?", "options": ["A) Wait-Die: Case 1 -> T1 waits, Case 2 -> T2 dies (aborts) | Wound-Wait: Case 1 -> T1 wounds (aborts) T2, Case 2 -> T2 waits.", "B) Wait-Die: Case 1 -> T1 dies, Case 2 -> T2 waits | Wound-Wait: Case 1 -> T1 waits, Case 2 -> T2 dies.", "C) Both schemes abort the older transaction T1 in Case 1.", "D) Wait-Die is preemptive while Wound-Wait is non-preemptive."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Transaction T1 has timestamp TS(T1) = 10 (Older) and Transaction T2 has timestamp TS(T2) = 20 (Younger). Compare what happens when (Case 1) Older T1 requests a lock held by Younger T2, and (Case 2) Younger T2 requests a lock held by Older T1 under Wait-Die vs. Wound-Wait:", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Wait-Die is non-preemptive: an Older transaction is allowed to WAIT for a Younger one (Case 1: T1 waits), while a Younger transaction requesting a lock held by an Older one DIES/rolls back (Case 2: T2 dies). Wound-Wait is preemptive: an Older transaction WOUNDS/preempts a Younger lock holder (Case 1: T2 is rolled back and T1 gets the lock), while a Younger transaction is allowed to WAIT for an Older one (Case 2: T2 waits). In both schemes, a rolled-back transaction keeps its original timestamp so it never starves.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 21}, {"id": "dbms-test-q22", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q22: Why must a Secondary Index (on a non-ordering field) ALWAYS be a Dense Index, whereas a Primary Index (on the ordering Primary Key) can be a Sparse Index?", "options": ["A) Because Secondary Indexes are stored in RAM whereas Primary Indexes are stored on disk.", "B) Because the data file is physically sorted by the Primary Key—so a Primary Index only needs 1 entry per disk block (pointing to the block's anchor record), whereas records ordered by a Secondary Index key are physically scattered across arbitrary disk blocks, requiring an index entry for every single record!", "C) Because Primary Keys cannot be integers.", "D) Because Sparse Indexes cannot use binary search."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Why must a Secondary Index (on a non-ordering field) ALWAYS be a Dense Index, whereas a Primary Index (on the ordering Primary Key) can be a Sparse Index?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "A Sparse Index only works when the underlying data file is physically sorted on disk by the indexed search key (so if you look for key 45 and block 1 starts at 10 and block 2 starts at 50, you know key 45 must be inside block 1). For a Secondary Index, the data file is NOT physically sorted by that secondary column, so consecutive key values live in completely different disk blocks—requiring a Dense Index with a pointer to every individual record (or every unique key's bucket).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 22}, {"id": "dbms-test-q23", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q23: Why do almost all production RDBMS storage engines (PostgreSQL, MySQL InnoDB, SQL Server, Oracle) use B+ Trees instead of B-Trees for table and column indexes?", "options": ["A) B+ Trees are binary trees with at most 2 children per node.", "B) In a B+ Tree, internal nodes store ONLY routing keys and child block pointers (no data records/record pointers), which maximizes node fanout and reduces tree height (fewer disk I/Os), AND all leaf nodes are linked in a sorted doubly linked list for fast O(log N + K) range queries!", "C) B-Trees duplicate keys in leaf nodes whereas B+ Trees never duplicate keys.", "D) B+ Trees do not require rebalancing on insertions or deletions."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Why do almost all production RDBMS storage engines (PostgreSQL, MySQL InnoDB, SQL Server, Oracle) use B+ Trees instead of B-Trees for table and column indexes?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In a B-Tree, data record pointers are stored inside internal nodes alongside keys, consuming valuable space inside each 4KB/8KB disk block and reducing the branching factor (fanout). A B+ Tree strips all data pointers out of internal nodes (putting them exclusively in the leaves), packing 2x–3x more keys per internal node so a 3-level B+ Tree can index hundreds of millions of rows. Plus, the linked leaf nodes make range queries (`BETWEEN`, `>`, `<`) a fast sequential scan instead of an in-order tree traversal.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 23}, {"id": "dbms-test-q24", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q24: In Crash Recovery using Write-Ahead Logging (WAL), what is the difference between Deferred Database Modification and Immediate Database Modification?", "options": ["A) Deferred Modification buffers all database page writes until the transaction commits (so recovery requires ONLY Redo and NO Undo, known as NO-UNDO/REDO); Immediate Modification allows uncommitted dirty pages to be flushed to disk before commit (requiring BOTH Undo for active/aborted transactions and Redo for committed transactions).", "B) Deferred Modification requires only Undo and no Redo.", "C) Immediate Modification does not write log records before modifying disk pages.", "D) Both schemes require identical Undo passes for committed transactions."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "In Crash Recovery using Write-Ahead Logging (WAL), what is the difference between Deferred Database Modification and Immediate Database Modification?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Under Deferred Database Modification (NO-STEAL), uncommitted changes are never written to the database disk files before the transaction commits—so if a crash occurs before commit, the database disk is untouched and needs NO Undo (only Redo for committed transactions). Under Immediate Database Modification (STEAL/NO-FORCE, used by ARIES in real databases), dirty pages can be written to disk before commit as long as the Undo/Redo log record is flushed to disk first (WAL), requiring both Undo (for uncommitted transactions) and Redo (for committed transactions) on crash recovery.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 24}, {"id": "dbms-test-q25", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q25: According to the CAP Theorem for Distributed Databases, why can a distributed system guarantee at most 2 out of 3 properties (Consistency, Availability, Partition Tolerance), and which trade-off must real-world distributed databases actually choose?", "options": ["A) Real-world networks never partition, so distributed databases always choose CA.", "B) Because network partitions (P) are inevitable in distributed networks, a system must choose between CP (Consistency + Partition Tolerance: reject/block requests on the disconnected side to prevent stale data) and AP (Availability + Partition Tolerance: serve requests on both sides and resolve eventual consistency later).", "C) CAP Theorem states that single-node SQL databases cannot have ACID Consistency.", "D) AP systems guarantee strict linearizability at all times."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "According to the CAP Theorem for Distributed Databases, which trade-off must real-world distributed databases actually choose when a network partition occurs between nodes?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In any distributed system across multiple machines/datacenters, network packet drops and partitions (P) are a physical reality. When a network partition splits Node A and Node B, the database must either: (1) choose CP (cancel/error writes on the minority partition so no client ever reads stale inconsistent data), or (2) choose AP (allow both nodes to remain 100% available for reads/writes and reconcile conflicts asynchronously via Eventual Consistency).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 25}, {"id": "dbms-test-q26", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q26: [Candidate Key Count Math] Consider relation R(A, B, C, D, E, F) with the set of Functional Dependencies F = { AB -> C, C -> D, D -> B, E -> F }. How many Candidate Keys does R have, and what are they?", "options": ["A) 1 Candidate Key: { ABE }", "B) 2 Candidate Keys: { ABE, ACE }", "C) 3 Candidate Keys: { ABE, ACE, ADE }", "D) 4 Candidate Keys: { ABE, ACE, ADE, AFE }"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Consider relation R(A, B, C, D, E, F) with the set of Functional Dependencies:\\nF = { AB -> C, C -> D, D -> B, E -> F }\\nHow many Candidate Keys does R have, and what are they?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Step 1: Find essential attributes (attributes that NEVER appear on the right-hand side of any FD): A and E never appear on the RHS, so {A, E} MUST be part of every Candidate Key! Step 2: Compute closure of {A, E}+ = {A, E, F} (missing B, C, D). Step 3: Try adding one attribute from {B, C, D}: (1) {ABE}+ = {A, B, E, F, C, D} (All 6! So ABE is a CK). (2) Since C -> D -> B, replacing B with C gives {ACE}+ = {A, C, E, F, D, B} (All 6! So ACE is a CK). (3) Since D -> B, replacing B with D gives {ADE}+ = {A, D, E, F, B, C} (All 6! So ADE is a CK). Total = 3 Candidate Keys: {ABE, ACE, ADE}.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 26}, {"id": "dbms-test-q27", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q27: [Superkey Combinatorics Math] A relation R(A, B, C, D, E, F) with 6 attributes has exactly two Candidate Keys: {A, B} and {B, C}. What is the exact total number of Superkeys for R?", "options": ["A) 16", "B) 24", "C) 32", "D) 28"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "A relation R(A, B, C, D, E, F) with 6 attributes has exactly two Candidate Keys: {A, B} and {B, C}. What is the exact total number of Superkeys for R?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Use the Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion! A Superkey is any subset of {A,B,C,D,E,F} that contains {A,B} OR contains {B,C}: 1) Number of subsets containing {A, B} (4 remaining attributes C, D, E, F can each be present or absent) = 2^(6-2) = 2^4 = 16. 2) Number of subsets containing {B, C} (4 remaining attributes A, D, E, F) = 2^(6-2) = 2^4 = 16. 3) Subsets containing BOTH {A, B} and {B, C} (i.e., containing {A, B, C}, with 3 remaining attributes D, E, F) were counted twice = 2^(6-3) = 2^3 = 8. Total Superkeys = 16 + 16 - 8 = 24.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 27}, {"id": "dbms-test-q28", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q28: [Highest Normal Form Calculation] Given relation R(A, B, C, D, E) with FDs F = { AB -> C, C -> D, B -> E }, what is the Candidate Key of R and what is the HIGHEST Normal Form satisfied by R?", "options": ["A) Candidate Key: AB | Highest Normal Form: 1NF (violates 2NF because Non-Prime E depends on proper subset B of Candidate Key AB)", "B) Candidate Key: AB | Highest Normal Form: 2NF (violates 3NF due to C -> D)", "C) Candidate Key: ABC | Highest Normal Form: 3NF", "D) Candidate Key: AB | Highest Normal Form: BCNF"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Given relation R(A, B, C, D, E) with Functional Dependencies:\\nF = { AB -> C, C -> D, B -> E }\\nWhat is the Candidate Key of R, and what is the HIGHEST Normal Form satisfied by R?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Step 1: Find Candidate Key: A and B never appear on the RHS of any FD. Check closure {AB}+: AB -> C, C -> D, B -> E, so {AB}+ = {A, B, C, D, E}. Thus, the sole Candidate Key is AB! Prime attributes = {A, B}; Non-Prime attributes = {C, D, E}. Step 2: Check 2NF: In the FD B -> E, the LHS (B) is a proper subset of the Candidate Key AB, and the RHS (E) is a Non-Prime attribute! This is a textbook Partial Dependency, so R violates 2NF! Highest Normal Form satisfied is 1NF.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 28}, {"id": "dbms-test-q29", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q29: [ER-to-Relational Table Count Math] An ER diagram consists of: 3 strong entity sets (E1, E2, E3), 1 weak entity set W (identified by E1 via identifying relationship R_w), a 1:N relationship R1 between E1 and E2, an M:N relationship R2 between E2 and E3, and 1 multivalued attribute on E3. What is the MINIMUM number of relational tables required to represent this database without redundancy?", "options": ["A) 4 tables", "B) 5 tables", "C) 6 tables", "D) 7 tables"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "An ER diagram consists of: 3 strong entity sets (E1, E2, E3), 1 weak entity set W (identified by E1 via identifying relationship R_w), a 1:N relationship R1 between E1 and E2, an M:N relationship R2 between E2 and E3, and 1 multivalued attribute on E3. What is the MINIMUM number of relational tables required?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Let's count each required table: 1) Three strong entity sets E1, E2, E3 -> 3 tables (the 1:N relationship R1 between E1 and E2 is merged into the N-side table E2 as a foreign key, adding 0 extra tables). 2) Weak entity set W + its identifying relationship R_w -> 1 combined table (+1 = 4). 3) M:N relationship R2 between E2 and E3 -> requires 1 separate junction table (+1 = 5). 4) Multivalued attribute on E3 -> requires 1 separate table (+1 = 6). Total = 6 tables.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 29}, {"id": "dbms-test-q30", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q30: [Join Cardinality Bounds Math] Relation R(A, B) has 150 tuples and relation S(B, C) has 60 tuples. Attribute B is the Primary Key of S, and B in R is a Foreign Key referencing S(B) with a NOT NULL constraint. What are the MINIMUM and MAXIMUM number of tuples in the Natural Join R ⋈ S?", "options": ["A) Minimum = 0, Maximum = 9000", "B) Minimum = 60, Maximum = 150", "C) Minimum = 150, Maximum = 150", "D) Minimum = 0, Maximum = 150"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Relation R(A, B) has 150 tuples and relation S(B, C) has 60 tuples. Attribute B is the Primary Key of S, and B in R is a Foreign Key referencing S(B) with a NOT NULL constraint. What are the MINIMUM and MAXIMUM number of tuples in the Natural Join R ⋈ S?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Because R.B is a NOT NULL Foreign Key referencing the Primary Key S.B, every single one of the 150 tuples in R is guaranteed to match AT LEAST one tuple in S (referential integrity + NOT NULL) and AT MOST one tuple in S (since S.B is a unique Primary Key)! Therefore, every tuple in R produces exactly 1 joined tuple, so both the minimum and maximum cardinality of R ⋈ S are 150!", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 30}, {"id": "dbms-test-q31", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q31: [Concurrent vs. Serial Schedule Count Math] Transaction T1 has 3 operations and Transaction T2 has 4 operations. How many total valid interleaved (concurrent + serial) schedules exist, and how many of them are strictly non-serial?", "options": ["A) Total = 35 schedules | Non-serial = 33 schedules", "B) Total = 12 schedules | Non-serial = 10 schedules", "C) Total = 70 schedules | Non-serial = 68 schedules", "D) Total = 5040 schedules | Non-serial = 5038 schedules"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Transaction T1 consists of 3 operations and Transaction T2 consists of 4 operations. How many total valid schedules (preserving the internal operation order of each transaction) exist, and how many of them are strictly non-serial?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "For k transactions with n1, n2, ... operations, the total number of valid schedules that preserve each transaction's internal instruction order is (n1 + n2)! / (n1! * n2!) = (3 + 4)! / (3! * 4!) = 7! / (6 * 24) = (7 * 6 * 5) / 6 = 35 total schedules. Since there are k! = 2! = 2 serial schedules (T1->T2 and T2->T1), the number of strictly non-serial schedules is 35 - 2 = 33.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 31}, {"id": "dbms-test-q32", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q32: [Precedence Graph & Serial Order Math] Consider schedule S: R1(A), R2(B), W1(B), R3(A), W2(C), W3(B), R1(C). By constructing the Precedence Graph for S, determine whether S is Conflict Serializable and its equivalent serial order:", "options": ["A) Conflict Serializable; equivalent to T2 -> T1 -> T3", "B) Conflict Serializable; equivalent to T1 -> T2 -> T3", "C) Conflict Serializable; equivalent to T3 -> T1 -> T2", "D) Not Conflict Serializable because the Precedence Graph contains a cycle between T1 and T2."], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Consider the schedule S across transactions T1, T2, and T3:\\nS: R1(A), R2(B), W1(B), R3(A), W2(C), W3(B), R1(C)\\nBy constructing the Precedence Graph for S, determine whether S is Conflict Serializable:", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let's find all conflicting pairs on A, B, and C: 1) On A: Only R1(A) and R3(A) (both Reads -> 0 conflicts). 2) On B: R2(B) before W1(B) gives edge T2 -> T1; R2(B) before W3(B) gives edge T2 -> T3; W1(B) before W3(B) gives edge T1 -> T3. 3) On C: W2(C) before R1(C) gives edge T2 -> T1. Combined directed edges: T2 -> T1, T2 -> T3, and T1 -> T3. There are NO cycles (DAG)! The unique topological sort is T2 -> T1 -> T3.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 32}, {"id": "dbms-test-q33", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q33: [B+ Tree Internal Node Order Math] A database has a disk block size of 4096 bytes. A search key field is 12 bytes long, and a disk block pointer is 8 bytes long. What is the maximum order p (maximum number of block pointers) of an internal B+ Tree node?", "options": ["A) 136", "B) 186", "C) 205", "D) 256"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "A database uses a disk block size of 4096 bytes. The search key field is 12 bytes long, a disk block pointer is 8 bytes long, and a data record pointer is 10 bytes long. What is the maximum order p (maximum number of child block pointers) of an INTERNAL node in a B+ Tree?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Crucial B+ Tree fact: Internal nodes of a B+ Tree do NOT store data record pointers (the 10-byte record pointer is only in leaf nodes or B-Trees!). An internal node of order p contains at most p block pointers (8B each) and p - 1 search keys (12B each). Setting up the inequality: 8p + 12(p - 1) <= 4096 => 20p - 12 <= 4096 => 20p <= 4108 => p <= 205.4. Flooring to an integer gives p = 205.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 33}, {"id": "dbms-test-q34", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q34: [B-Tree vs. B+ Tree Leaf Order Math] Using the same parameters from Q33 (Block size = 4096B, Search Key = 12B, Block Pointer = 8B, Data Record Pointer = 10B), what is the maximum number of (key, record_pointer) entries that can fit in a B+ Tree LEAF node (which also holds 1 next-leaf block pointer)?", "options": ["A) 136", "B) 185", "C) 204", "D) 205"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Using the parameters from Q33 (Block size = 4096B, Search Key = 12B, Block Pointer = 8B, Data Record Pointer = 10B), what is the maximum number of (key, record_pointer) pairs p_leaf that can fit inside a B+ Tree LEAF node?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "A B+ Tree leaf node holds p_leaf pairs of (Search Key + Data Record Pointer) plus 1 block pointer to the next sibling leaf node. Inequality: p_leaf * (12 + 10) + 8 <= 4096 => 22 * p_leaf <= 4088 => p_leaf <= 185.81 => p_leaf = 185 entries.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 34}, {"id": "dbms-test-q35", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q35: [Primary Sparse Index Disk I/O Math] An ordered file has 30,000 records of size 100 bytes each, stored unspanned in disk blocks of 1024 bytes. A sparse Primary Index is built with key = 9 bytes and block pointer = 6 bytes. How many disk block accesses are needed to locate and fetch a record using binary search on the Primary Index?", "options": ["A) 6 block accesses", "B) 7 block accesses", "C) 12 block accesses", "D) 13 block accesses"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "An ordered file has 30,000 records of size 100 bytes each, stored unspanned in disk blocks of 1024 bytes. A sparse Primary Index is built with search key = 9 bytes and block pointer = 6 bytes. How many total disk block accesses are required in the worst case to locate and fetch a data record using binary search on the Primary Index?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Step 1: Data file blocking factor bfr = floor(1024 / 100) = 10 records/block. Total data blocks B = ceil(30,000 / 10) = 3,000 data blocks (without an index, binary search on the data file would take ceil(log2(3000)) = 12 block accesses). Step 2: Since a Primary Index is SPARSE, it has 1 index entry per data block = 3,000 index entries! Step 3: Index blocking factor bfr_i = floor(1024 / (9 + 6)) = floor(1024 / 15) = 68 index entries/block. Total index blocks B_i = ceil(3,000 / 68) = 45 index blocks. Step 4: Binary search on 45 index blocks takes ceil(log2(45)) = 6 block accesses + 1 block access to read the actual target data block = 7 block accesses!", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 35}, {"id": "dbms-test-q36", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q36: [Timestamp Ordering Rejection Trace] Transactions T1, T2, and T3 have timestamps TS(T1) = 10, TS(T2) = 20, and TS(T3) = 30. Initially R-TS(A) = W-TS(A) = 0. Given the sequence: R1(A), W3(A), R2(A), W2(A), which exact operation is rejected and causes its transaction to roll back under the Basic Timestamp Ordering protocol?", "options": ["A) Step 1: R1(A)", "B) Step 2: W3(A)", "C) Step 3: R2(A) (because TS(T2) = 20 < W-TS(A) = 30)", "D) Step 4: W2(A)"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Transactions T1, T2, and T3 have timestamps TS(T1) = 10, TS(T2) = 20, and TS(T3) = 30. Initially R-TS(A) = 0 and W-TS(A) = 0. Under the Basic Timestamp Ordering protocol, trace the sequence:\\n1. R1(A)   2. W3(A)   3. R2(A)   4. W2(A)\\nWhich operation is the FIRST to be rejected and cause a rollback?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let's trace step-by-step: 1) R1(A): TS(T1)=10 >= W-TS(A)=0 -> Allowed! R-TS(A) becomes max(0, 10) = 10. 2) W3(A): TS(T3)=30 >= R-TS(A)=10 and >= W-TS(A)=0 -> Allowed! W-TS(A) becomes 30. 3) R2(A): T2 tries to read A, so we check if TS(T2) < W-TS(A). Since TS(T2) = 20 < W-TS(A) = 30, a younger transaction (T3) has already overwritten A! Therefore, R2(A) is rejected at Step 3 and T2 is rolled back and restarted with a new timestamp.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 36}, {"id": "dbms-test-q37", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q37: [Undo/Redo Crash Recovery Log Math] A system using Immediate Database Modification crashes. The recovery log on disk contains (in format <Ti, Var, OldVal, NewVal>): <T1, start>, <T1, A, 10, 50>, <T2, start>, <T2, B, 20, 80>, <T1, commit>, <T3, start>, <T3, C, 30, 90>. After recovery completes, what are the guaranteed values of A, B, and C, and which operations were performed?", "options": ["A) Redo T1; Undo T2 and T3 -> Final values: A = 50, B = 20, C = 30", "B) Redo T1, T2, and T3 -> Final values: A = 50, B = 80, C = 90", "C) Undo T1, T2, and T3 -> Final values: A = 10, B = 20, C = 30", "D) Redo T1 and T2; Undo T3 -> Final values: A = 50, B = 80, C = 30"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "A system using Immediate Database Modification crashes. The WAL log on disk shows:\\n<T1, start>\\n<T1, A, 10, 50>\\n<T2, start>\\n<T2, B, 20, 80>\\n<T1, commit>\\n<T3, start>\\n<T3, C, 30, 90>\\n[CRASH]\\nWhat are the recovery actions and final values of A, B, and C after recovery?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "During recovery under Immediate Modification: 1) Transaction T1 has both <T1, start> and <T1, commit> in the log, so T1 is placed in the REDO list (setting A to its new value 50). 2) Transactions T2 and T3 have <start> records but NO <commit> record before the crash, so both T2 and T3 are placed in the UNDO list (restoring B to its old value 20 and C to its old value 30). Final values: A = 50, B = 20, C = 30.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 37}, {"id": "dbms-test-q38", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q38: [SQL NULL Aggregate Math] Table \\"Products\\" has 5 rows with \\"bonus\\" column values: 100, NULL, 200, NULL, 300. What is the exact numerical output of: SELECT COUNT(*), COUNT(bonus), AVG(bonus), SUM(bonus) FROM Products;?", "options": ["A) 5, 5, 120, 600", "B) 5, 3, 200, 600", "C) 3, 3, 200, 600", "D) 5, 3, NULL, NULL"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Table \\"Products\\" has 5 rows with \\"bonus\\" column values: 100, NULL, 200, NULL, 300. What is the exact numerical output of:\\nSELECT COUNT(*), COUNT(bonus), AVG(bonus), SUM(bonus) FROM Products;", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In SQL, all aggregate functions EXCEPT COUNT(*) completely ignore NULL values! 1) COUNT(*) counts all 5 rows = 5. 2) COUNT(bonus) counts only non-null values (100, 200, 300) = 3. 3) SUM(bonus) = 100 + 200 + 300 = 600. 4) AVG(bonus) divides the sum by the count of non-null rows (600 / 3 = 200, NOT 600 / 5 = 120!). Output: 5, 3, 200, 600.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 38}, {"id": "dbms-test-q39", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q39: [Block Nested Loop Join Disk I/O Math] Relation R occupies 600 disk blocks and relation S occupies 100 disk blocks. The buffer pool has 22 memory frames available (20 frames for the outer relation chunk, 1 frame for the inner relation block, 1 frame for output). What is the minimum number of disk block reads required to perform a Block Nested Loop Join of R and S?", "options": ["A) 3,100 block reads", "B) 6,100 block reads", "C) 18,600 block reads", "D) 60,100 block reads"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Relation R occupies 600 disk blocks and relation S occupies 100 disk blocks. The buffer pool has 22 memory frames available (M - 2 = 20 frames for buffering chunks of the outer relation, 1 frame for the inner relation, 1 frame for output). What is the MINIMUM number of disk block I/O reads required for a Block Nested Loop Join?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "In a Block Nested Loop Join with buffer chunk size B_chunk = M - 2 = 20 blocks, the cost formula is: B_outer + ceil(B_outer / 20) * B_inner. Always choose the SMALLER relation (S = 100 blocks) as the outer relation! Cost = 100 + ceil(100 / 20) * 600 = 100 + 5 * 600 = 3,100 disk block reads. (If R were chosen as outer, cost would be 600 + ceil(600/20)*100 = 3,600).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 39}, {"id": "dbms-test-q40", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q40: [Canonical / Minimal Cover Math] Given relation R(A, B, C) and Functional Dependencies F = { A -> BC, B -> C, A -> B, AB -> C }, what is the exact Minimal (Canonical) Cover Fc of F?", "options": ["A) { A -> B, B -> C } (2 FDs)", "B) { A -> BC, B -> C } (2 FDs)", "C) { A -> B, A -> C, B -> C } (3 FDs)", "D) { AB -> C, A -> B } (2 FDs)"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Given relation R(A, B, C) and Functional Dependencies:\\nF = { A -> BC, B -> C, A -> B, AB -> C }\\nWhat is the exact Minimal (Canonical) Cover Fc of F?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Step 1 (Singleton RHS): Split A -> BC into A -> B and A -> C. Set becomes { A -> B, A -> C, B -> C, AB -> C }. Step 2 (Remove extraneous LHS attributes): In AB -> C, since A+ includes B (via A -> B), B is extraneous on the LHS, reducing AB -> C to A -> C. Set becomes { A -> B, A -> C, B -> C }. Step 3 (Remove redundant FDs): Since A -> B and B -> C transitively imply A -> C, the FD A -> C is redundant and removed! The canonical cover is { A -> B, B -> C }.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 40}, {"id": "dbms-test-q41", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q41: [Composite Index Leftmost Prefix Trap] An e-commerce engineering team creates a composite B+ Tree index CREATE INDEX idx_orders ON orders(customer_id, status, created_at);. Queries with WHERE customer_id = 101 AND status = 'PAID' run in 2ms, but the admin dashboard query WHERE status = 'PAID' AND created_at >= '2026-01-01' takes 4,500ms and performs a Full Table Scan! Why?", "options": ["A) B+ Tree indexes cannot index VARCHAR and TIMESTAMP columns together.", "B) Because of the B+ Tree Leftmost Prefix Rule: a composite index on (A, B, C) is sorted first by A, then by B within each A, and then by C. If the leading column (customer_id) is omitted from the WHERE clause, the engine cannot traverse the B+ Tree for (status, created_at)!", "C) Composite indexes only work on JOIN queries, not WHERE clauses.", "D) The query must use HAVING instead of WHERE."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "An engineering team creates a composite B+ Tree index on orders(customer_id, status, created_at). Queries filtering by (customer_id, status) run in 2ms, but queries filtering only by WHERE status = 'PAID' AND created_at >= '2026-01-01' perform a slow Full Table Scan! Why?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "A composite B+ Tree index on (customer_id, status, created_at) is like a phonebook sorted by (LastName, FirstName, City). You can use it to look up (customer_id), (customer_id, status), or (customer_id, status, created_at). However, if you omit the leftmost prefix column (customer_id), the values of status (\\"PAID\\") are scattered across the entire tree under every customer_id—so the query planner cannot do a B+ Tree search and falls back to a scan (or requires a separate index starting with status).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 41}, {"id": "dbms-test-q42", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q42: [Race Condition in Flash-Sale / Ticket Booking] In a train ticket booking API, two users simultaneously click \\"Book\\" on the last remaining seat. Both requests run: SELECT seats_left FROM trains WHERE id = 99; (both read 1), and both run UPDATE trains SET seats_left = seats_left - 1 WHERE id = 99;, resulting in 2 confirmed tickets and seats_left = -1! Which database patterns prevent this Lost Update / Double-Booking bug?", "options": ["A) Using READ UNCOMMITTED isolation level.", "B) Either (1) Pessimistic Locking: SELECT seats_left FROM trains WHERE id = 99 FOR UPDATE (acquires an exclusive row lock so the second transaction waits until the first commits), OR (2) Atomic Conditional Update / Optimistic Locking: UPDATE trains SET seats_left = seats_left - 1 WHERE id = 99 AND seats_left > 0 (and checking affectedRows == 1).", "C) Adding a secondary index on seats_left.", "D) Using LEFT JOIN instead of INNER JOIN."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "Two users simultaneously click \\"Book\\" on the last train seat. Both transactions execute SELECT seats_left (both read 1) and then execute UPDATE trains SET seats_left = 0, double-booking the seat! Which database patterns properly prevent this?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "When reading a value in application memory and then issuing a separate UPDATE based on that read, two concurrent transactions under READ COMMITTED both read seats_left = 1 before either writes. Using SELECT ... FOR UPDATE locks the row on read so the second transaction blocks until the first commits (and then reads 0). Alternatively, a single atomic UPDATE ... WHERE id = 99 AND seats_left > 0 (or Optimistic Concurrency Control with a version column) ensures only 1 transaction gets affectedRows == 1.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 42}, {"id": "dbms-test-q43", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q43: [Deadlock Elimination in Wallet Transfers] In a payment microservice, User 1 transfers ₹100 to User 2 at the exact same millisecond that User 2 transfers ₹50 to User 1. Transaction 1 locks Wallet #1 and waits for Wallet #2; Transaction 2 locks Wallet #2 and waits for Wallet #1—triggering a database Deadlock! How do backend engineers structurally eliminate this deadlock?", "options": ["A) Increase the database connection pool size.", "B) Enforce a deterministic Canonical Lock Ordering: always sort the account IDs and acquire row locks in ascending order of account_id (first lock min(id1, id2), then lock max(id1, id2)), which breaks the Circular Wait condition!", "C) Disable database transactions.", "D) Catch the deadlock error and ignore it without retrying."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "User 1 transfers money to User 2 at the exact same millisecond that User 2 transfers money to User 1. T1 locks Wallet 1 then requests Wallet 2; T2 locks Wallet 2 then requests Wallet 1—causing a Deadlock! How do you structurally prevent this deadlock in code?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "A deadlock requires a Circular Wait cycle in the Wait-For Graph (T1 -> T2 -> T1). By enforcing a strict global lock acquisition order—always locking the smaller wallet_id first (Wallet #1) and the larger wallet_id second (Wallet #2) regardless of who is sender or receiver—both T1 and T2 try to lock Wallet #1 first! One succeeds and completes both updates while the other waits cleanly, making a cycle mathematically impossible.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 43}, {"id": "dbms-test-q44", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q44: [The N+1 Query Problem] A social media feed endpoint fetches the latest 100 posts (SELECT * FROM posts LIMIT 100;) and then loops over those 100 posts in Node.js, executing SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ? for each post's author—sending 101 separate network round-trips to the database! How do you fix this?", "options": ["A) It is called Object Slicing; solved by adding a B-Tree index.", "B) It is the N+1 Query Problem; solved by eager loading via a single SQL JOIN (SELECT p.*, u.name FROM posts p JOIN users u ON p.author_id = u.id) or batching with WHERE u.id IN (id1, id2, ..., id100), reducing 101 queries down to 1 or 2 queries!", "C) It is called Phantom Read; solved by SERIALIZABLE isolation.", "D) It is called Sharding; solved by splitting the table."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "An API endpoint fetches 100 posts (1 query) and then loops over the 100 posts in application code, executing SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ? for each post (100 queries = 101 DB round-trips). What is this anti-pattern called and how is it solved?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "The N+1 Query Problem is the #1 performance killer in backend applications using ORMs/ODMs. Even if each individual query takes 2ms, 101 sequential network round-trips add >200ms of latency. Combining them into a single SQL JOIN (or 2 queries using WHERE id IN (...)) fetches all data in 1–2 round-trips.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 44}, {"id": "dbms-test-q45", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q45: [Unindexed Foreign Key Trap on DELETE] A database has users(id PK) with 100,000 rows and activity_logs(id PK, user_id FK referencing users.id ON DELETE CASCADE) with 50,000,000 rows. Deleting a single user (DELETE FROM users WHERE id = 500) takes 18 seconds and spikes CPU to 100%, even though users.id is the Primary Key! Why?", "options": ["A) Primary Keys are only used for SELECT queries, not DELETE queries.", "B) To enforce the Foreign Key constraint (ON DELETE CASCADE / RESTRICT) when a row in users is deleted, the DBMS must check activity_logs for matching user_id = 500 rows. Because PostgreSQL/SQL Server do NOT automatically create an index on the Foreign Key column (activity_logs.user_id), it performs a Full Table Scan of all 50,000,000 rows in activity_logs!", "C) DELETE cannot use B+ Trees.", "D) The users table needs to be denormalized."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "Table users(id PK) has 100k rows and activity_logs(id PK, user_id FK ON DELETE CASCADE) has 50M rows. Executing DELETE FROM users WHERE id = 500 takes 18 seconds! Since users.id is already indexed as a Primary Key, why is DELETE so slow?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "While RDBMS engines automatically create a unique B+ Tree index on a Primary Key (users.id), many engines (like PostgreSQL, SQL Server, Oracle) do NOT automatically create an index on the child table's Foreign Key column (activity_logs.user_id)! Whenever you DELETE or UPDATE a parent key in users, the database must search the child table activity_logs to check for dependent rows—triggering a 50-million-row sequential scan unless you explicitly create an index on activity_logs(user_id).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 45}, {"id": "dbms-test-q46", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q46: [Over-Indexing Write Amplification] A developer adds 12 separate secondary B+ Tree indexes to a high-velocity telemetry_events table to make every possible analytics filter fast. Immediately, INSERT and UPDATE throughput drops by 75%. Why?", "options": ["A) Because every single INSERT, DELETE, or indexed-column UPDATE must not only write the table row, but also traverse, update, and potentially split/rebalance leaf blocks across all 12 separate B+ Tree structures on disk (Write Amplification)!", "B) Because tables with more than 5 indexes disable the buffer pool.", "C) Because B+ Trees lock the entire database during SELECT queries.", "D) Secondary indexes only slow down DELETE, never INSERT."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "A developer adds 12 secondary B+ Tree indexes to a write-heavy telemetry_events table. Immediately, INSERT and UPDATE throughput drops by 75%. Why do excessive indexes hurt write performance?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Every secondary B+ Tree index is a separate physical data structure on disk. When a new row is inserted into a table with 12 indexes, the storage engine must insert 12 new entries into 12 different B+ Trees, dirtying up to 12 random disk pages, writing WAL log records for all of them, and triggering node splits.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 46}, {"id": "dbms-test-q47", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q47: [Master-Slave Replication Lag — Read-Your-Own-Writes] To scale read traffic, an architecture sends all WRITE queries (INSERT/UPDATE) to the Primary (Master) DB and routes all READ queries (SELECT) to 3 Asynchronous Read Replicas. A user updates their shipping address and immediately gets redirected to the Checkout page, which still shows their OLD address for 2 seconds! Why did this happen and how do you fix it?", "options": ["A) The Primary DB rolled back the transaction.", "B) Asynchronous Replication Lag: the Read Replica had not yet applied the WAL stream from the Primary DB when the redirect GET arrived. Fix it using \\"Read-Your-Own-Writes\\" routing (routing a user's reads to the Primary DB for a short window after they perform a write, or tracking WAL LSN positions).", "C) B+ Tree corruption on the replica.", "D) Missing Foreign Key constraint."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "All writes go to the Primary DB and all reads go to Asynchronous Read Replicas. A user updates their address and is immediately redirected to a page that reads from a Read Replica—showing their OLD address! What caused this and how is it solved?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In Asynchronous Master-Replica replication, the Primary DB commits and responds to the client immediately while shipping WAL logs to Read Replicas asynchronously (which may lag by 50ms–2000ms). If the user's next GET request hits a lagging replica, they see stale state. Routing the mutating user's own reads to the Primary DB for a brief window (e.g., 5 seconds via session/cookie timestamp or LSN check) guarantees Read-Your-Own-Writes consistency.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 47}, {"id": "dbms-test-q48", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q48: [Deep Offset Pagination Bottleneck vs. Keyset Pagination] On a table with 20,000,000 posts, Page 1 (SELECT * FROM posts ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 20 OFFSET 0) takes 1ms, but Page 50,000 (LIMIT 20 OFFSET 1000000) takes 3.2 seconds! Why is large OFFSET slow, and how does Cursor/Keyset Pagination fix it?", "options": ["A) With OFFSET 1000000, the database must still scan, fetch, and count through 1,000,020 rows before discarding the first 1,000,000! Keyset Pagination replaces OFFSET with a B+ Tree range seek (WHERE id < :last_seen_id ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 20), jumping directly to the exact starting row in O(log N) time!", "B) OFFSET disables all indexes when the number exceeds 1,000.", "C) Keyset Pagination loads all 20M rows into Node.js RAM.", "D) Using OFFSET with ASC instead of DESC makes it O(1)."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "On a 20M-row table, LIMIT 20 OFFSET 0 takes 1ms, but LIMIT 20 OFFSET 1000000 takes 3.2 seconds! Why does OFFSET degrade linearly O(N), and how does Keyset (Cursor) Pagination solve it?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "B+ Trees do not store absolute row offsets inside nodes (because every insert/delete would require updating offsets across the entire tree). Thus, OFFSET 1,000,000 forces the engine to walk through 1,000,000 records and throw them away. Keyset/Cursor Pagination passes the last seen indexed value from the previous page (WHERE id < 849201 ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 20), allowing the B+ Tree to seek directly to id 849201 in O(log N) time regardless of how deep the user scrolls!", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 48}, {"id": "dbms-test-q49", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q49: [Database Sharding — Hot Partition Anti-Pattern] A large analytics platform horizontally shards its events table across 16 database servers using range-based sharding on the created_date column (each month mapped to a shard). Despite having 16 servers, 15 servers sit at 2% CPU while 1 server is overloaded at 100% CPU! Why?", "options": ["A) Because monotonically increasing timestamp/date shard keys route 100% of current real-time writes and recent dashboard reads to the single shard holding the current month! Fix it by sharding on a high-cardinality hash key (e.g., hash(tenant_id) or hash(user_id)) to distribute writes uniformly across all 16 shards.", "B) Because 16 is not a prime number.", "C) Because range sharding only works on string columns.", "D) Because B+ Trees cannot be used on sharded tables."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "An analytics platform shards its events table across 16 database servers by created_date (month). 15 shards sit idle at 2% CPU while 1 shard is overwhelmed at 100% CPU! What caused this \\"Hot Shard\\" bottleneck and how do you fix the shard key?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Using a monotonically increasing key (like created_date or an auto-incrementing ID) for range-based sharding directs every single new INSERT and most recent SELECT queries to the latest range shard (the \\"Hot Shard\\"), leaving historical shards idle. Choosing a high-cardinality hash-based or composite shard key (like tenant_id or user_id) spreads concurrent writes evenly across all shards.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 49}, {"id": "dbms-test-q50", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q50: [Denormalization Trade-Off in High-Read Systems] On an e-commerce platform, product pages are viewed 10,000 times per second, while new product reviews are posted 2 times per minute. Computing AVG(rating) and COUNT(*) from a 50M-row reviews table on every page view overloads the DB. The team adds avg_rating and review_count columns directly onto the products table. What is this technique called and what is its trade-off?", "options": ["A) It is called 4NF Normalization; it slows down reads and speeds up writes.", "B) It is Controlled Denormalization (Materializing Aggregates): it introduces controlled redundancy to make read queries O(1) without joins/aggregations, at the cost of requiring transactional updates (or triggers/events) to keep products.avg_rating in sync whenever a review is inserted, updated, or deleted.", "C) It is called Lossy Decomposition; it deletes the reviews table.", "D) It violates 1NF and prevents indexing."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "To avoid running AVG(rating) and COUNT(*) across a 50M-row reviews table on every product page view, the engineering team stores avg_rating and review_count directly on the products table. What is this technique called and what is the trade-off?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "While strict 3NF/BCNF normalization forbids storing derived/redundant aggregates, in read-heavy production systems (10,000 reads/sec vs. 2 writes/min), Controlled Denormalization trades a slightly more complex write path (updating the cached count/average inside the same transaction when a review is added) for massive read performance gains.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 50}]	\N	### 📌 1. Why DBMS Over File Systems & The 3-Schema Architecture\nTraditional flat-file systems suffer from **Data Redundancy & Inconsistency**, **Lack of Concurrent Access Control** (race conditions when two processes write simultaneously), **No Atomic Crash Recovery**, **Poor Security Granularity**, and $O(N)$ linear file scans. A **DBMS (Database Management System)** solves these using a layered architecture.\n* **Three-Schema ANSI-SPARC Architecture:**\n  1. **Internal (Physical) Level:** Describes how data is physically stored on disk (byte offsets, B+ Tree indexes, block allocation, compression).\n  2. **Conceptual (Logical) Level:** Describes *what* data is stored across the entire database (tables, columns, data types, constraints, relationships).\n  3. **External (View) Level:** Customized sub-views tailored to specific user roles (e.g., Logistics only sees shipping addresses; Finance sees billing records).\n* **Data Independence:**\n  * **Physical Data Independence:** Ability to modify the Physical Schema (e.g., switching from a hash index to a B+ Tree or moving data files to a new SSD) without altering the Conceptual Schema or application queries.\n  * **Logical Data Independence:** Ability to modify the Conceptual Schema (e.g., adding a new column or splitting a table) without rewriting External Views or application code. (Harder to achieve than Physical Data Independence).\n\n### 💡 2. Entity-Relationship (ER) Modeling & Reduction to Relational Tables\n* **Attributes:** **Simple** vs. **Composite** (e.g., `Name` -> `firstName`, `lastName`), **Single-valued** vs. **Multivalued** (double oval, e.g., `phoneNumbers` — always requires a **separate table**!), **Stored** vs. **Derived** (dashed oval, e.g., `age` computed from `dob`).\n* **Weak Entity Set (Double Rectangle):** Lacks sufficient attributes to form a Primary Key of its own; depends on a **Strong (Owner) Entity** via an **Identifying Relationship** (double diamond) and uses a **Partial Key (Discriminator)** (dashed underline). Always has **Total Participation** (double line) in its identifying relationship.\n* **Minimum Number of Tables Required When Converting an ER Diagram:**\n  * **1:1 Relationship:** Merge into either entity's table (if one side has Total Participation, merge into that side; if both have Total Participation, merge both entities into 1 single table).\n  * **1:N (One-to-Many) Relationship:** Merge the relationship into the **Many ($N$) side's table** by adding the Primary Key of the $1$-side as a Foreign Key.\n  * **M:N (Many-to-Many) Relationship:** Always requires a **separate junction/bridge table** whose Primary Key is the composite of both entities' Primary Keys.\n  * **Multivalued Attribute:** Always requires a **separate table** `(entity_pk, multivalued_attr)`.\n\n### 💡 3. Keys, Relational Algebra & Tuple Relational Calculus\n* **Superkey (SK):** Any set of attributes that uniquely identifies a tuple in a relation. If a relation $R$ has $n$ attributes and a single simple Candidate Key of 1 attribute, the number of Superkeys is $2^{n-1}$.\n* **Candidate Key (CK):** A **minimal Superkey** (no proper subset of a Candidate Key is a Superkey). Attributes that belong to *at least one* Candidate Key are called **Prime Attributes**; all others are **Non-Prime Attributes**.\n* **Primary Key (PK) vs. Unique Key:** A table can have only **one** Primary Key (`NOT NULL` + Unique, Entity Integrity), whereas it can have **multiple** Unique Keys (which allow `NULL` values).\n* **Relational Algebra (Procedural — Specifies *How* to Obtain Results):**\n  * **6 Fundamental Operators:** Selection ($sigma$), Projection ($pi$ — automatically eliminates duplicate rows!), Cartesian Product ($\times$), Set Union ($cup$), Set Difference ($-$), and Rename ($\rho$).\n  * **Derived Operators:** Natural Join ($\bowtie$), Theta/Equi Join ($\bowtie_\\theta$), Set Intersection ($R \\cap S = R - (R - S)$), and Division ($R(A,B) \\div S(B)$ — finds all $A$ values associated with *every* $B$ value in $S$, expressed as $\\pi_A(R) - \\pi_A((\\pi_A(R) \\times S) - R)$).\n* **Tuple Relational Calculus (TRC — Non-Procedural / Declarative):** $\\{ t \\mid P(t) \\}$ specifies *what* tuples to retrieve rather than how.\n\n### 💡 4. Functional Dependencies (FDs), Closures & Normalization (`1NF` to `BCNF` / `4NF`)\n* **Functional Dependency ($X \\rightarrow Y$):** If two tuples agree on attribute set $X$, they must also agree on attribute set $Y$ ($t_1[X] = t_2[X] \\implies t_1[Y] = t_2[Y]$).\n* **Finding Attribute Closure ($X^+$):** Start with $result = X$. Repeatedly add $B$ to $result$ for every FD $A \\rightarrow B$ where $A \\subseteq result$. If $X^+$ contains all attributes of $R$, then $X$ is a **Superkey**.\n* **Normal Forms Hierarchy (Eliminating Update, Insertion, and Deletion Anomalies):**\n  1. **1NF (First Normal Form):** Every attribute must hold **atomic (indivisible)** values (no arrays or comma-separated lists in a cell).\n  2. **2NF (Second Normal Form):** Must be in 1NF + **No Partial Dependency** (no **Non-Prime** attribute can depend on a *proper subset* of any Candidate Key). Note: If every Candidate Key is a single attribute, the relation is **automatically in 2NF**!\n  3. **3NF (Third Normal Form):** Must be in 2NF + **No Transitive Dependency** of a Non-Prime attribute on a Superkey. Formally, for every non-trivial FD $X \\rightarrow Y$: **either $X$ is a Superkey OR $Y$ is a Prime Attribute**.\n  4. **BCNF (Boyce-Codd Normal Form):** Strictly stronger than 3NF. For every non-trivial FD $X \\rightarrow Y$: **$X$ MUST be a Superkey** (even if $Y$ is a Prime Attribute!).\n  5. **4NF (Fourth Normal Form):** Must be in BCNF + contains **no non-trivial Multivalued Dependencies ($X \\twoheadrightarrow Y$)** where $X$ is not a Superkey.\n* **Decomposition Properties (When Splitting $R$ into $R_1$ and $R_2$):**\n  * **Lossless-Join Decomposition (Mandatory):** Natural join $R_1 \\bowtie R_2$ must reconstruct exact original relation $R$ without generating spurious tuples. Condition: $(R_1 \\cap R_2) \\rightarrow R_1$ OR $(R_1 \\cap R_2) \\rightarrow R_2$ (the common attributes must be a Superkey of at least one decomposed table).\n  * **Dependency Preservation:** Every FD in $F^+$ can be checked within a single table without joining tables. **3NF decomposition is ALWAYS both Lossless and Dependency-Preserving**; **BCNF decomposition is ALWAYS Lossless, but NOT always Dependency-Preserving**!\n\n### 💡 5. SQL Execution Order & Query Optimization Essentials\n```sql\n-- Logical Order of SQL Execution by the Query Engine:\n-- 1. FROM & JOIN (Cartesian product + ON filter)\n-- 2. WHERE       (Row-level filter BEFORE grouping; cannot use aggregate functions)\n-- 3. GROUP BY    (Buckets rows into groups)\n-- 4. HAVING      (Group-level filter AFTER grouping; can use COUNT, SUM, AVG)\n-- 5. SELECT      (Evaluates expressions and column aliases)\n-- 6. DISTINCT    (Removes duplicate output rows)\n-- 7. ORDER BY    (Sorts result set; can reference SELECT aliases)\n-- 8. LIMIT / OFFSET\n\nSELECT d.dept_name, COUNT(e.emp_id) AS emp_count, AVG(e.salary) AS avg_sal\nFROM departments d\nLEFT JOIN employees e ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id\nWHERE e.is_active = TRUE\nGROUP BY d.dept_id, d.dept_name\nHAVING COUNT(e.emp_id) >= 5\nORDER BY avg_sal DESC\nLIMIT 10;\n```\n\n### 💡 6. Transactions, ACID Properties, Serializability & Concurrency Control\n* **ACID Properties & Responsible DBMS Subsystems:**\n  * **Atomicity ("All or Nothing"):** Enforced by the **Recovery / Transaction Manager** using **Undo Logs**.\n  * **Consistency (Preserving Invariants):** Enforced by **Integrity Constraints** + **Application Logic**.\n  * **Isolation (Concurrent Execution Independence):** Enforced by the **Concurrency Control Manager** (Locks, Timestamps, MVCC).\n  * **Durability (Surviving Power Loss/Crashes):** Enforced by the **Recovery Manager** using **Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) / Redo Logs**.\n* **Concurrency Anomalies & SQL Isolation Levels:**\n  * **Dirty Read:** Reading uncommitted data written by another transaction that later rolls back.\n  * **Non-Repeatable Read:** Reading the same row twice in a transaction and getting different values because another committed transaction `UPDATE`d it.\n  * **Phantom Read:** Running the same range query (`WHERE salary > 50000`) twice and getting extra rows because another committed transaction `INSERT`ed/`DELETE`d matching rows.\n  * **Isolation Levels:** `READ UNCOMMITTED` (allows all 3) -> `READ COMMITTED` (prevents Dirty Reads; default in PostgreSQL/Oracle) -> `REPEATABLE READ` (prevents Dirty & Non-Repeatable Reads; default in MySQL InnoDB) -> `SERIALIZABLE` (prevents all 3).\n* **Serializability & Precedence Graphs:**\n  * Two operations conflict if they belong to **different transactions**, operate on the **same data item**, and **at least one of them is a `WRITE`** (`R-W`, `W-R`, `W-W`).\n  * A schedule $S$ is **Conflict Serializable** if and only if its **Precedence (Serialization) Graph is a DAG (contains NO cycles)**. Every Conflict Serializable schedule is View Serializable, whereas a View Serializable schedule that is not Conflict Serializable must contain **Blind Writes**.\n* **Locking & Timestamp Protocols:**\n  * **Basic 2PL (Two-Phase Locking):** Growing Phase (acquire locks only) -> Shrinking Phase (release locks only). Guarantees **Conflict Serializability**, but is vulnerable to **Deadlocks** and **Cascading Rollbacks**.\n  * **Strict 2PL:** Holds all **Exclusive (`X` / Write) locks** until `COMMIT`/`ABORT` — guarantees **Strict & Cascadeless schedules**!\n  * **Rigorous 2PL:** Holds **both Shared (`S`) and Exclusive (`X`) locks** until `COMMIT`/`ABORT`.\n  * **Deadlock Prevention (Timestamp-Based, where smaller $TS$ = older transaction):**\n    * **Wait-Die (Non-Preemptive):** Older waits for Younger; Younger dies (rolls back) if it requests a lock held by Older.\n    * **Wound-Wait (Preemptive):** Older wounds (preempts/rolls back) Younger; Younger waits for Older.\n\n### 💡 7. Indexing (`B-Tree` vs. `B+ Tree`), Crash Recovery (WAL) & Scaling\n* **Types of Indexes:**\n  * **Primary Index:** Ordered data file + index on **Primary Key** (Sparse — 1 entry per disk block anchor record).\n  * **Clustering Index:** Ordered data file + index on a **Non-Key** ordering field (Sparse). At most **1 Primary or Clustering index** can exist per table because rows can only be physically sorted in one order on disk!\n  * **Secondary Index:** Unordered field (Candidate Key or Non-Key). **Must be Dense** (1 entry per record) because records with the same value are scattered across different disk blocks.\n* **Why RDBMS Engines Use `B+ Trees` Over `B-Trees`:**\n  1. **Higher Fanout / Lower Tree Height:** `B+ Tree` internal nodes store **only routing keys and child block pointers** (zero data records/record pointers!). More keys fit in a single $4\\text{ KB}$ internal block, keeping tree height at 3–4 levels even for billions of rows.\n  2. **Fast Range Queries ($O(\\log N + K)$):** All keys and data pointers reside in the **Leaf Nodes**, which are linked together in a sorted **doubly linked list**—allowing `WHERE age BETWEEN 20 AND 30` to scan leaves sequentially without climbing back up the tree!\n* **Write-Ahead Logging (WAL):** Log records must be flushed to stable disk **before** the modified data page is written to disk (and all log records of a transaction must be flushed before `COMMIT` returns).	CodeHelp - by Babbar	https://www.youtube.com/@CodeHelp	22	DBMS: Complete Database Management Systems in 1 Shot (Placement & GATE Edition)	Master Database Management Systems from scratch to advanced system design—3-Schema Architecture, ER Modeling, Relational Algebra, Functional Dependencies, Normalization (1NF to BCNF/4NF), SQL, ACID Transactions, Concurrency Control (2PL, Timestamp Ordering), B/B+ Tree Indexing, Crash Recovery (WAL), and Sharding—followed by a 50-question timed evaluation test.	20	Easy	\N	2	[{"id": "dbms-test-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: In the ANSI-SPARC 3-Schema Architecture, a DBA replaces a hash index on the \\"orders\\" table with a B+ Tree index and moves the data file to a new NVMe partition without changing any table definitions or application SQL queries. Which property does this demonstrate?", "options": ["A) Logical Data Independence", "B) Physical Data Independence", "C) View Serializability", "D) Referential Integrity"], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "In the ANSI-SPARC 3-Schema Architecture, a DBA replaces a hash index on the \\"orders\\" table with a B+ Tree index and moves the data file to a new NVMe partition without changing any table definitions or application SQL queries. Which property does this demonstrate?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Physical Data Independence is the capacity to change the Internal (Physical) Schema—such as file organization, storage devices, or index structures—without having to alter the Conceptual (Logical) Schema or external application queries.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "dbms-test-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: Which of the following statements regarding a Weak Entity Set in an Entity-Relationship (ER) diagram is ALWAYS true?", "options": ["A) It can have partial participation in its identifying relationship.", "B) It must have total participation in its identifying relationship, and its primary key in the relational model is formed by combining the primary key of its owner (strong) entity set with its own partial key (discriminator).", "C) Its identifying relationship always requires a separate relational table in the database.", "D) It cannot have any non-key attributes of its own."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which of the following statements regarding a Weak Entity Set in an Entity-Relationship (ER) diagram is ALWAYS true?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "A Weak Entity cannot exist without an owner Strong Entity, so it ALWAYS has Total Participation (double line) in its identifying relationship. When reduced to a relational table, the Weak Entity table automatically absorbs the Identifying Relationship by combining the Owner's Primary Key with its own Partial Key (Discriminator), so no separate table is needed for the identifying relationship.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "dbms-test-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: Table Orders(order_id PK, cust_id FK) references Customers(cust_id PK) with ON DELETE SET NULL. Under what condition will defining this Foreign Key fail or cause a constraint conflict when a Customer is deleted?", "options": ["A) When cust_id in the Orders table is declared with a NOT NULL constraint (or is part of Orders's Primary Key).", "B) When the Customer has 0 orders in the Orders table.", "C) When Orders has more rows than Customers.", "D) ON DELETE SET NULL deletes the entire row from Orders."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Table Orders(order_id PK, cust_id FK) references Customers(cust_id PK) with ON DELETE SET NULL. Under what condition will defining or triggering this rule fail when a Customer is deleted?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "ON DELETE SET NULL updates the foreign key column (Orders.cust_id) to NULL whenever the referenced parent row in Customers is deleted. If Orders.cust_id was defined as NOT NULL (or is part of the Primary Key, which enforces Entity Integrity / NOT NULL), setting it to NULL violates the NOT NULL constraint and fails!", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "dbms-test-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: Which of the following correctly compares Relational Algebra (RA) and Tuple Relational Calculus (TRC)?", "options": ["A) Both RA and TRC are procedural query languages.", "B) RA is a procedural language (specifies step-by-step operations on how to retrieve data), whereas TRC is a non-procedural / declarative language (specifies what predicate the resulting tuples must satisfy); Safe TRC and Basic RA are relationally complete (equally expressive).", "C) TRC is procedural and strictly more expressive than RA.", "D) RA allows infinite result sets while TRC does not."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which of the following correctly compares Relational Algebra (RA) and Tuple Relational Calculus (TRC)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Relational Algebra is procedural because an expression prescribes an ordered sequence of operations (selection, projection, join) to compute the result. Tuple Relational Calculus ({t | P(t)}) is declarative/non-procedural. By Codd's Theorem, Basic Relational Algebra and Safe Tuple Relational Calculus have identical expressive power (Relational Completeness).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "dbms-test-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: In Relational Algebra, the Division operator R(A, B) ÷ S(B) returns all distinct A values that are paired with EVERY B value in S. Which expression using only fundamental operators is equivalent to R ÷ S?", "options": ["A) π_A(R) - π_A((π_A(R) × S) - R)", "B) π_A(R × S) - π_A(R - S)", "C) π_A(R) ∩ π_B(S)", "D) σ_{A=B}(R × S)"], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "In Relational Algebra, the Division operator R(A, B) ÷ S(B) returns all distinct A values that are paired with EVERY B value in S. Which expression using only fundamental operators is equivalent to R ÷ S?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "To compute R(A,B) ÷ S(B): 1) π_A(R) × S generates all possible (A, B) pairs if every A in R were paired with every B in S. 2) Subtracting R from that ((π_A(R) × S) - R) yields the \\"missing/disqualified\\" pairs. 3) Projecting A on those missing pairs gives all A values that failed to match at least one B in S. 4) Subtracting those disqualified A values from π_A(R) leaves only the A values paired with EVERY B in S!", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "dbms-test-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: If a Functional Dependency X -> Y holds on a relation schema R, which statement is guaranteed to be true for any valid instance r(R) and any two tuples t1 and t2?", "options": ["A) If t1[Y] == t2[Y], then t1[X] must equal t2[X].", "B) If t1[X] == t2[X], then t1[Y] must equal t2[Y]; however, if t1[X] != t2[X], t1[Y] and t2[Y] may be either equal or different.", "C) All values in column X must be unique across the entire table.", "D) X and Y must have the same data type."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "If a Functional Dependency X -> Y holds on a relation schema R, which statement is guaranteed to be true for any valid instance r(R) and any two tuples t1 and t2?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "A functional dependency X -> Y is a many-to-one mapping from X to Y: whenever two rows have identical X values (t1[X] == t2[X]), they are strictly required to have identical Y values (t1[Y] == t2[Y]). Only if t1[X] == t2[X] and t1[Y] != t2[Y] is X -> Y violated.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "dbms-test-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: Under what exact condition is a relation R in Third Normal Form (3NF), and how does Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF) tighten that rule?", "options": ["A) 3NF requires every non-trivial FD X -> Y to have X as a Superkey OR Y as a Prime Attribute; BCNF drops the second allowance and requires X to be a Superkey for EVERY non-trivial FD X -> Y.", "B) BCNF allows Partial Dependencies if Y is a Prime Attribute, whereas 3NF does not.", "C) 3NF eliminates Multivalued Dependencies, whereas BCNF eliminates Join Dependencies.", "D) Every 3NF relation is automatically in BCNF, but not every BCNF relation is in 3NF."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Under what exact condition is a relation R in Third Normal Form (3NF), and how does Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF) tighten that rule?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "A relation is in 3NF if for every non-trivial FD X -> Y, either (1) X is a Superkey, OR (2) every attribute in Y - X is a Prime Attribute (part of some Candidate Key). BCNF removes condition (2) and strictly requires that X MUST be a Superkey for every non-trivial FD X -> Y.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "dbms-test-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: Consider relation R(A, B, C) with functional dependencies AB -> C and C -> B. What are the Candidate Keys of R, which Normal Form does R satisfy, and why is decomposing R into BCNF problematic?", "options": ["A) Candidate Keys: AB and AC; R is in 3NF (because B is a prime attribute in C -> B) but violates BCNF (because C is not a superkey); decomposing into BCNF (R1(C,B) and R2(A,C)) loses the functional dependency AB -> C!", "B) Candidate Key: C only; R is in BCNF.", "C) Candidate Keys: AB and BC; R violates 2NF.", "D) R is in 1NF only and can be decomposed into BCNF without losing any dependencies."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Consider relation R(A, B, C) with functional dependencies AB -> C and C -> B. What are the Candidate Keys of R, which Normal Form does R satisfy, and why is decomposing R into BCNF problematic?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "This is the classic textbook example of 3NF vs. BCNF! Since A is never on the RHS of any FD, A must be in every key. AB+ = ABC and AC+ = ACB, so the Candidate Keys are AB and AC (making A, B, and C all Prime Attributes!). In C -> B, C is not a superkey (violating BCNF), but B is a Prime Attribute (satisfying 3NF!). If you decompose R into R1(C, B) and R2(A, C) to achieve BCNF, the decomposition is lossless, but the FD AB -> C is no longer preserved!", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "dbms-test-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: When decomposing a relation R(A, B, C, D) into two relations R1(A, B, C) and R2(C, D), what is the necessary and sufficient condition for the decomposition to be a Lossless-Join Decomposition?", "options": ["A) R1 ∩ R2 must be empty (∅).", "B) The common attribute(s) (R1 ∩ R2 = {C}) must functionally determine all attributes of R1 (C -> ABC) OR functionally determine all attributes of R2 (C -> CD) in F+.", "C) Both R1 and R2 must have the same number of attributes.", "D) C must be a non-prime attribute in R."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "When decomposing a relation R(A, B, C, D) into two relations R1(A, B, C) and R2(C, D), what is the necessary and sufficient condition for the decomposition to be a Lossless-Join Decomposition?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "By Heath's Theorem, decomposing R into R1 and R2 is lossless if and only if the intersection of their attributes (R1 ∩ R2) is a Superkey of R1 ((R1 ∩ R2) -> R1) OR a Superkey of R2 ((R1 ∩ R2) -> R2).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "dbms-test-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: A table EmpSkillsHobbies(emp_id, skill, hobby) has NO non-trivial functional dependencies (its only key is the all-attribute composite key {emp_id, skill, hobby}), yet an employee's skills are completely independent of their hobbies. Which Normal Form is violated and why?", "options": ["A) It violates 2NF due to partial dependency.", "B) It is in BCNF (since there are no non-trivial FDs), but violates Fourth Normal Form (4NF) due to a non-trivial Multivalued Dependency (emp_id ->> skill) where emp_id is not a superkey!", "C) It violates 3NF due to transitive dependency.", "D) It is already in 4NF and 5NF."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "A table EmpSkillsHobbies(emp_id, skill, hobby) has NO non-trivial functional dependencies (its only key is {emp_id, skill, hobby}), yet an employee's skills are independent of their hobbies (emp_id ->> skill and emp_id ->> hobby). Which Normal Form is violated?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Because the only key is {emp_id, skill, hobby} and there are no non-trivial FDs, the table automatically satisfies 1NF, 2NF, 3NF, and BCNF! However, storing two independent 1:N relationships (skills and hobbies) in the same table creates a cross-product redundancy (Multivalued Dependency emp_id ->> skill | hobby) where emp_id is not a superkey—violating 4NF. Decomposing into EmpSkills(emp_id, skill) and EmpHobbies(emp_id, hobby) achieves 4NF.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "dbms-test-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: Why does the SQL query SELECT dept_id, COUNT(*) AS total FROM employees WHERE total > 5 GROUP BY dept_id; fail with a syntax/column error, whereas placing the condition in HAVING works?", "options": ["A) Because the SQL engine executes WHERE (Step 2) BEFORE GROUP BY (Step 3) and SELECT (Step 5), so neither the group aggregates nor the SELECT alias \\"total\\" exist yet when WHERE is evaluated!", "B) Because COUNT(*) can only be used with ORDER BY.", "C) Because WHERE only works on string columns.", "D) Because GROUP BY executes after ORDER BY."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Why does the SQL query SELECT dept_id, COUNT(*) AS total FROM employees WHERE total > 5 GROUP BY dept_id; fail, whereas HAVING COUNT(*) > 5 works?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In the logical query processing order (FROM -> WHERE -> GROUP BY -> HAVING -> SELECT -> ORDER BY), the WHERE clause filters individual rows before grouping occurs and before SELECT aliases are computed. Aggregate functions (COUNT, SUM, AVG) and group filters are evaluated in HAVING (after GROUP BY).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "dbms-test-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: Which statement accurately contrasts the SQL commands DELETE, TRUNCATE, and DROP?", "options": ["A) DELETE is a DML command that removes rows one-by-one, logs each row deletion, fires DELETE triggers, and supports a WHERE clause; TRUNCATE is a DDL operation that deallocates data pages in bulk without firing row-level DELETE triggers; DROP removes both the data and the table schema definition.", "B) TRUNCATE supports a WHERE clause while DELETE does not.", "C) DELETE cannot be rolled back inside a transaction, whereas DROP can never be used on indexed tables.", "D) Both TRUNCATE and DROP delete only matching rows and preserve indexes."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which statement accurately contrasts the SQL commands DELETE, TRUNCATE, and DROP?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "DELETE (DML) scans and deletes matching rows individually, writing undo/redo log entries per row and firing ON DELETE triggers. TRUNCATE (DDL) deallocates whole data pages at once, resets auto-increment counters, does not support a WHERE clause, and does not fire row-level DELETE triggers. DROP (DDL) removes the entire table structure, data, and indexes from the catalog.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "dbms-test-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: Classic SQL Interview Trap: What does SELECT * FROM Emp WHERE dept_id NOT IN (10, 20, NULL); return, and why does NOT EXISTS behave differently?", "options": ["A) All employees whose dept_id is neither 10 nor 20.", "B) Exactly 0 rows (an empty result set)! Because x NOT IN (10, 20, NULL) expands to (x != 10 AND x != 20 AND x != NULL); in SQL 3-valued logic, x != NULL evaluates to UNKNOWN, and TRUE AND UNKNOWN evaluates to UNKNOWN (which WHERE filters out)!", "C) All employees whose dept_id is NULL.", "D) A syntax error because NULL cannot appear inside IN()."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "What does SELECT * FROM Emp WHERE dept_id NOT IN (10, 20, NULL); return in standard SQL?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "This is one of the most famous SQL production bugs! When a subquery inside NOT IN (SELECT mgr_id FROM ...) returns even a single NULL value, x NOT IN (10, 20, NULL) is logically equivalent to x != 10 AND x != 20 AND x != NULL. Because any comparison with NULL using = or != yields UNKNOWN (never TRUE), the entire AND conjunction evaluates to UNKNOWN (or FALSE) for every single row—returning 0 rows! Using NOT EXISTS avoids this trap.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "dbms-test-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: Which internal DBMS components are directly responsible for enforcing Atomicity, Isolation, and Durability in ACID transactions?", "options": ["A) Atomicity: Query Optimizer | Isolation: Disk Controller | Durability: Deadlock Detector", "B) Atomicity: Recovery Manager (Undo Log) | Isolation: Concurrency Control Manager (Locks / MVCC) | Durability: Recovery Manager (Redo Log / Write-Ahead Logging)", "C) Atomicity: B+ Tree Index | Isolation: Foreign Keys | Durability: Buffer Pool", "D) All three are enforced solely by the client application code."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which internal DBMS components are directly responsible for enforcing Atomicity, Isolation, and Durability in ACID transactions?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "1) Atomicity (\\"all or nothing\\") is guaranteed by the Recovery Manager using Undo logs to roll back partial changes of aborted transactions. 2) Isolation is guaranteed by the Concurrency Control Manager using 2PL locking, timestamps, or MVCC snapshots. 3) Durability is guaranteed by the Recovery Manager flushing Redo logs (WAL) to non-volatile disk before acknowledging commit.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "dbms-test-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: Under the SQL standard, which concurrency anomalies are prevented by the REPEATABLE READ isolation level, and which anomaly can still theoretically occur according to the ANSI SQL specification?", "options": ["A) Prevents Dirty Reads and Non-Repeatable Reads, but still allows Phantom Reads.", "B) Prevents only Dirty Reads, and allows Non-Repeatable Reads and Phantom Reads.", "C) Prevents Phantom Reads, but allows Dirty Reads.", "D) Prevents all anomalies only when autocommit is disabled."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Under the ANSI SQL standard, which concurrency anomalies are prevented by the REPEATABLE READ isolation level, and which anomaly can still occur?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In the ANSI SQL standard hierarchy: READ COMMITTED prevents Dirty Reads; REPEATABLE READ prevents both Dirty Reads and Non-Repeatable Reads (by holding read locks or snapshot versions on read rows), but still permits Phantom Reads (new rows inserted by another transaction matching a WHERE range predicate); SERIALIZABLE prevents all three.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "dbms-test-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: Which pair of operations in a concurrent schedule S does NOT constitute a conflict pair when constructing a Precedence (Serialization) Graph?", "options": ["A) R1(A) followed by W2(A)", "B) W1(A) followed by R2(A)", "C) R1(A) followed by R2(A), OR W1(A) followed by W2(B)", "D) W1(A) followed by W2(A)"], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which pair of operations in a concurrent schedule S does NOT constitute a conflict pair when constructing a Precedence (Serialization) Graph?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Two operations conflict if and only if: (1) they belong to different transactions (Ti != Tj), (2) they access the exact same data item (both on A), AND (3) at least one of them is a Write operation (RW, WR, or WW). Two Read operations on the same item (R1(A), R2(A)) commute and never conflict; operations on different data items (W1(A), W2(B)) also never conflict.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "dbms-test-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: Which statement regarding the relationship between Conflict Serializability and View Serializability is TRUE?", "options": ["A) Every View Serializable schedule is Conflict Serializable.", "B) Every Conflict Serializable schedule is View Serializable, and any schedule that is View Serializable but NOT Conflict Serializable MUST contain at least one Blind Write (a Write operation performed without having read the item first).", "C) Checking View Serializability takes O(V + E) polynomial time, whereas checking Conflict Serializability is NP-Complete.", "D) A schedule with a cycle in its Precedence Graph can never be View Serializable."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which statement regarding the relationship between Conflict Serializability and View Serializability is TRUE?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Conflict Serializability is a subset of View Serializability (Conflict Serializable => View Serializable). Testing Conflict Serializability via cycle detection in a Precedence Graph takes polynomial time O(V + E), whereas testing View Serializability is NP-Complete! Crucially, if a schedule has a cycle in its Precedence Graph yet is still View Serializable, it is guaranteed to contain a Blind Write (writing a value without reading it first).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "dbms-test-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: Consider the hierarchy of schedule recoverability: Strict Schedules, Cascadeless (ACA) Schedules, and Recoverable Schedules. What is the exact subset relationship and rule for each?", "options": ["A) Strict ⊂ Cascadeless ⊂ Recoverable: Recoverable requires Tj (which reads from Ti) to commit after Ti commits; Cascadeless requires Tj to read only after Ti commits (no dirty reads); Strict requires Tj to neither read NOR write an item written by Ti until Ti commits or aborts.", "B) Recoverable ⊂ Cascadeless ⊂ Strict.", "C) Cascadeless schedules allow dirty reads as long as commits happen in order.", "D) Every Conflict Serializable schedule is automatically Recoverable."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Consider the hierarchy of schedule recoverability: Strict Schedules, Cascadeless (ACA) Schedules, and Recoverable Schedules. What is the exact subset relationship and rule for each?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Recoverable: If Tj reads an uncommitted value written by Ti, Tj must not commit until Ti commits. 2) Cascadeless (Avoids Cascading Aborts): Tj is not allowed to READ an item written by Ti until Ti has committed (eliminates dirty reads). 3) Strict: Tj is not allowed to READ OR OVERWRITE (Write) an item written by Ti until Ti has committed or aborted. Therefore, Every Strict schedule is Cascadeless, and every Cascadeless schedule is Recoverable (Strict ⊂ Cascadeless ⊂ Recoverable).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "dbms-test-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: How do Basic 2PL, Strict 2PL, Rigorous 2PL, and Conservative (Static) 2PL differ in terms of Cascading Rollbacks and Deadlocks?", "options": ["A) Basic 2PL prevents deadlocks; Strict 2PL allows cascading rollbacks.", "B) Strict 2PL holds all Exclusive (X) locks until commit/abort (preventing cascading rollbacks) and Rigorous 2PL holds both S and X locks until commit/abort, but BOTH can still deadlock; Conservative 2PL pre-declares and acquires all locks before execution starts, making it 100% Deadlock-Free!", "C) Rigorous 2PL is deadlock-free while Conservative 2PL can deadlock.", "D) None of the 2PL variants guarantee Conflict Serializability."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "How do Basic 2PL, Strict 2PL, Rigorous 2PL, and Conservative (Static) 2PL differ in terms of Cascading Rollbacks and Deadlocks?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "All 2PL variants guarantee Conflict Serializability. Basic 2PL can suffer from both cascading rollbacks (because locks are released early in the shrinking phase before commit) and deadlocks. Strict 2PL (holds X locks until commit) and Rigorous 2PL (holds all S and X locks until commit) eliminate cascading rollbacks and guarantee strict schedules, but can still deadlock. Conservative (Static) 2PL requires a transaction to lock all items it needs upfront before starting (or release all and wait), eliminating hold-and-wait and making it Deadlock-Free.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "dbms-test-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: In Timestamp Ordering Concurrency Control, how does Thomas's Write Rule modify the standard Timestamp Ordering protocol when Transaction Ti issues Write(X) with TS(Ti) < W-timestamp(X) (and TS(Ti) >= R-timestamp(X))?", "options": ["A) Standard Timestamp Ordering rolls back Ti, whereas Thomas's Write Rule simply ignores (skips) Ti's obsolete Write(X) and allows Ti to continue without rolling back!", "B) Thomas's Write Rule rolls back the younger transaction that wrote X.", "C) Thomas's Write Rule overwrites X anyway and updates W-timestamp(X) to TS(Ti).", "D) Thomas's Write Rule converts Write(X) into a Read(X)."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "In Timestamp Ordering Concurrency Control, how does Thomas's Write Rule modify the standard protocol when Transaction Ti issues Write(X) with TS(Ti) < W-timestamp(X) (and TS(Ti) >= R-timestamp(X))?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "If TS(Ti) < W-timestamp(X), a younger transaction Tj has already overwritten X with a newer value. Provided no transaction has needed to read Ti's value (TS(Ti) >= R-timestamp(X)), Ti's write is an \\"obsolete write\\" that would have been immediately overwritten by Tj in a serial schedule anyway. Instead of aborting Ti (as Basic Timestamp Ordering does), Thomas's Write Rule ignores the obsolete write and lets Ti proceed—allowing more View Serializable schedules.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 20}, {"id": "dbms-test-q21", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q21: Transaction T1 has timestamp TS(T1) = 10 (Older) and Transaction T2 has timestamp TS(T2) = 20 (Younger). If T1 requests a lock currently held by T2, and separately if T2 requests a lock currently held by T1, what happens under Wait-Die vs. Wound-Wait?", "options": ["A) Wait-Die: Case 1 -> T1 waits, Case 2 -> T2 dies (aborts) | Wound-Wait: Case 1 -> T1 wounds (aborts) T2, Case 2 -> T2 waits.", "B) Wait-Die: Case 1 -> T1 dies, Case 2 -> T2 waits | Wound-Wait: Case 1 -> T1 waits, Case 2 -> T2 dies.", "C) Both schemes abort the older transaction T1 in Case 1.", "D) Wait-Die is preemptive while Wound-Wait is non-preemptive."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Transaction T1 has timestamp TS(T1) = 10 (Older) and Transaction T2 has timestamp TS(T2) = 20 (Younger). Compare what happens when (Case 1) Older T1 requests a lock held by Younger T2, and (Case 2) Younger T2 requests a lock held by Older T1 under Wait-Die vs. Wound-Wait:", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Wait-Die is non-preemptive: an Older transaction is allowed to WAIT for a Younger one (Case 1: T1 waits), while a Younger transaction requesting a lock held by an Older one DIES/rolls back (Case 2: T2 dies). Wound-Wait is preemptive: an Older transaction WOUNDS/preempts a Younger lock holder (Case 1: T2 is rolled back and T1 gets the lock), while a Younger transaction is allowed to WAIT for an Older one (Case 2: T2 waits). In both schemes, a rolled-back transaction keeps its original timestamp so it never starves.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 21}, {"id": "dbms-test-q22", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q22: Why must a Secondary Index (on a non-ordering field) ALWAYS be a Dense Index, whereas a Primary Index (on the ordering Primary Key) can be a Sparse Index?", "options": ["A) Because Secondary Indexes are stored in RAM whereas Primary Indexes are stored on disk.", "B) Because the data file is physically sorted by the Primary Key—so a Primary Index only needs 1 entry per disk block (pointing to the block's anchor record), whereas records ordered by a Secondary Index key are physically scattered across arbitrary disk blocks, requiring an index entry for every single record!", "C) Because Primary Keys cannot be integers.", "D) Because Sparse Indexes cannot use binary search."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Why must a Secondary Index (on a non-ordering field) ALWAYS be a Dense Index, whereas a Primary Index (on the ordering Primary Key) can be a Sparse Index?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "A Sparse Index only works when the underlying data file is physically sorted on disk by the indexed search key (so if you look for key 45 and block 1 starts at 10 and block 2 starts at 50, you know key 45 must be inside block 1). For a Secondary Index, the data file is NOT physically sorted by that secondary column, so consecutive key values live in completely different disk blocks—requiring a Dense Index with a pointer to every individual record (or every unique key's bucket).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 22}, {"id": "dbms-test-q23", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q23: Why do almost all production RDBMS storage engines (PostgreSQL, MySQL InnoDB, SQL Server, Oracle) use B+ Trees instead of B-Trees for table and column indexes?", "options": ["A) B+ Trees are binary trees with at most 2 children per node.", "B) In a B+ Tree, internal nodes store ONLY routing keys and child block pointers (no data records/record pointers), which maximizes node fanout and reduces tree height (fewer disk I/Os), AND all leaf nodes are linked in a sorted doubly linked list for fast O(log N + K) range queries!", "C) B-Trees duplicate keys in leaf nodes whereas B+ Trees never duplicate keys.", "D) B+ Trees do not require rebalancing on insertions or deletions."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Why do almost all production RDBMS storage engines (PostgreSQL, MySQL InnoDB, SQL Server, Oracle) use B+ Trees instead of B-Trees for table and column indexes?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In a B-Tree, data record pointers are stored inside internal nodes alongside keys, consuming valuable space inside each 4KB/8KB disk block and reducing the branching factor (fanout). A B+ Tree strips all data pointers out of internal nodes (putting them exclusively in the leaves), packing 2x–3x more keys per internal node so a 3-level B+ Tree can index hundreds of millions of rows. Plus, the linked leaf nodes make range queries (`BETWEEN`, `>`, `<`) a fast sequential scan instead of an in-order tree traversal.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 23}, {"id": "dbms-test-q24", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q24: In Crash Recovery using Write-Ahead Logging (WAL), what is the difference between Deferred Database Modification and Immediate Database Modification?", "options": ["A) Deferred Modification buffers all database page writes until the transaction commits (so recovery requires ONLY Redo and NO Undo, known as NO-UNDO/REDO); Immediate Modification allows uncommitted dirty pages to be flushed to disk before commit (requiring BOTH Undo for active/aborted transactions and Redo for committed transactions).", "B) Deferred Modification requires only Undo and no Redo.", "C) Immediate Modification does not write log records before modifying disk pages.", "D) Both schemes require identical Undo passes for committed transactions."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "In Crash Recovery using Write-Ahead Logging (WAL), what is the difference between Deferred Database Modification and Immediate Database Modification?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Under Deferred Database Modification (NO-STEAL), uncommitted changes are never written to the database disk files before the transaction commits—so if a crash occurs before commit, the database disk is untouched and needs NO Undo (only Redo for committed transactions). Under Immediate Database Modification (STEAL/NO-FORCE, used by ARIES in real databases), dirty pages can be written to disk before commit as long as the Undo/Redo log record is flushed to disk first (WAL), requiring both Undo (for uncommitted transactions) and Redo (for committed transactions) on crash recovery.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 24}, {"id": "dbms-test-q25", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q25: According to the CAP Theorem for Distributed Databases, why can a distributed system guarantee at most 2 out of 3 properties (Consistency, Availability, Partition Tolerance), and which trade-off must real-world distributed databases actually choose?", "options": ["A) Real-world networks never partition, so distributed databases always choose CA.", "B) Because network partitions (P) are inevitable in distributed networks, a system must choose between CP (Consistency + Partition Tolerance: reject/block requests on the disconnected side to prevent stale data) and AP (Availability + Partition Tolerance: serve requests on both sides and resolve eventual consistency later).", "C) CAP Theorem states that single-node SQL databases cannot have ACID Consistency.", "D) AP systems guarantee strict linearizability at all times."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "According to the CAP Theorem for Distributed Databases, which trade-off must real-world distributed databases actually choose when a network partition occurs between nodes?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In any distributed system across multiple machines/datacenters, network packet drops and partitions (P) are a physical reality. When a network partition splits Node A and Node B, the database must either: (1) choose CP (cancel/error writes on the minority partition so no client ever reads stale inconsistent data), or (2) choose AP (allow both nodes to remain 100% available for reads/writes and reconcile conflicts asynchronously via Eventual Consistency).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 25}, {"id": "dbms-test-q26", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q26: [Candidate Key Count Math] Consider relation R(A, B, C, D, E, F) with the set of Functional Dependencies F = { AB -> C, C -> D, D -> B, E -> F }. How many Candidate Keys does R have, and what are they?", "options": ["A) 1 Candidate Key: { ABE }", "B) 2 Candidate Keys: { ABE, ACE }", "C) 3 Candidate Keys: { ABE, ACE, ADE }", "D) 4 Candidate Keys: { ABE, ACE, ADE, AFE }"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Consider relation R(A, B, C, D, E, F) with the set of Functional Dependencies:\\nF = { AB -> C, C -> D, D -> B, E -> F }\\nHow many Candidate Keys does R have, and what are they?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Step 1: Find essential attributes (attributes that NEVER appear on the right-hand side of any FD): A and E never appear on the RHS, so {A, E} MUST be part of every Candidate Key! Step 2: Compute closure of {A, E}+ = {A, E, F} (missing B, C, D). Step 3: Try adding one attribute from {B, C, D}: (1) {ABE}+ = {A, B, E, F, C, D} (All 6! So ABE is a CK). (2) Since C -> D -> B, replacing B with C gives {ACE}+ = {A, C, E, F, D, B} (All 6! So ACE is a CK). (3) Since D -> B, replacing B with D gives {ADE}+ = {A, D, E, F, B, C} (All 6! So ADE is a CK). Total = 3 Candidate Keys: {ABE, ACE, ADE}.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 26}, {"id": "dbms-test-q27", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q27: [Superkey Combinatorics Math] A relation R(A, B, C, D, E, F) with 6 attributes has exactly two Candidate Keys: {A, B} and {B, C}. What is the exact total number of Superkeys for R?", "options": ["A) 16", "B) 24", "C) 32", "D) 28"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "A relation R(A, B, C, D, E, F) with 6 attributes has exactly two Candidate Keys: {A, B} and {B, C}. What is the exact total number of Superkeys for R?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Use the Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion! A Superkey is any subset of {A,B,C,D,E,F} that contains {A,B} OR contains {B,C}: 1) Number of subsets containing {A, B} (4 remaining attributes C, D, E, F can each be present or absent) = 2^(6-2) = 2^4 = 16. 2) Number of subsets containing {B, C} (4 remaining attributes A, D, E, F) = 2^(6-2) = 2^4 = 16. 3) Subsets containing BOTH {A, B} and {B, C} (i.e., containing {A, B, C}, with 3 remaining attributes D, E, F) were counted twice = 2^(6-3) = 2^3 = 8. Total Superkeys = 16 + 16 - 8 = 24.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 27}, {"id": "dbms-test-q28", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q28: [Highest Normal Form Calculation] Given relation R(A, B, C, D, E) with FDs F = { AB -> C, C -> D, B -> E }, what is the Candidate Key of R and what is the HIGHEST Normal Form satisfied by R?", "options": ["A) Candidate Key: AB | Highest Normal Form: 1NF (violates 2NF because Non-Prime E depends on proper subset B of Candidate Key AB)", "B) Candidate Key: AB | Highest Normal Form: 2NF (violates 3NF due to C -> D)", "C) Candidate Key: ABC | Highest Normal Form: 3NF", "D) Candidate Key: AB | Highest Normal Form: BCNF"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Given relation R(A, B, C, D, E) with Functional Dependencies:\\nF = { AB -> C, C -> D, B -> E }\\nWhat is the Candidate Key of R, and what is the HIGHEST Normal Form satisfied by R?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Step 1: Find Candidate Key: A and B never appear on the RHS of any FD. Check closure {AB}+: AB -> C, C -> D, B -> E, so {AB}+ = {A, B, C, D, E}. Thus, the sole Candidate Key is AB! Prime attributes = {A, B}; Non-Prime attributes = {C, D, E}. Step 2: Check 2NF: In the FD B -> E, the LHS (B) is a proper subset of the Candidate Key AB, and the RHS (E) is a Non-Prime attribute! This is a textbook Partial Dependency, so R violates 2NF! Highest Normal Form satisfied is 1NF.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 28}, {"id": "dbms-test-q29", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q29: [ER-to-Relational Table Count Math] An ER diagram consists of: 3 strong entity sets (E1, E2, E3), 1 weak entity set W (identified by E1 via identifying relationship R_w), a 1:N relationship R1 between E1 and E2, an M:N relationship R2 between E2 and E3, and 1 multivalued attribute on E3. What is the MINIMUM number of relational tables required to represent this database without redundancy?", "options": ["A) 4 tables", "B) 5 tables", "C) 6 tables", "D) 7 tables"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "An ER diagram consists of: 3 strong entity sets (E1, E2, E3), 1 weak entity set W (identified by E1 via identifying relationship R_w), a 1:N relationship R1 between E1 and E2, an M:N relationship R2 between E2 and E3, and 1 multivalued attribute on E3. What is the MINIMUM number of relational tables required?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Let's count each required table: 1) Three strong entity sets E1, E2, E3 -> 3 tables (the 1:N relationship R1 between E1 and E2 is merged into the N-side table E2 as a foreign key, adding 0 extra tables). 2) Weak entity set W + its identifying relationship R_w -> 1 combined table (+1 = 4). 3) M:N relationship R2 between E2 and E3 -> requires 1 separate junction table (+1 = 5). 4) Multivalued attribute on E3 -> requires 1 separate table (+1 = 6). Total = 6 tables.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 29}, {"id": "dbms-test-q30", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q30: [Join Cardinality Bounds Math] Relation R(A, B) has 150 tuples and relation S(B, C) has 60 tuples. Attribute B is the Primary Key of S, and B in R is a Foreign Key referencing S(B) with a NOT NULL constraint. What are the MINIMUM and MAXIMUM number of tuples in the Natural Join R ⋈ S?", "options": ["A) Minimum = 0, Maximum = 9000", "B) Minimum = 60, Maximum = 150", "C) Minimum = 150, Maximum = 150", "D) Minimum = 0, Maximum = 150"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Relation R(A, B) has 150 tuples and relation S(B, C) has 60 tuples. Attribute B is the Primary Key of S, and B in R is a Foreign Key referencing S(B) with a NOT NULL constraint. What are the MINIMUM and MAXIMUM number of tuples in the Natural Join R ⋈ S?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Because R.B is a NOT NULL Foreign Key referencing the Primary Key S.B, every single one of the 150 tuples in R is guaranteed to match AT LEAST one tuple in S (referential integrity + NOT NULL) and AT MOST one tuple in S (since S.B is a unique Primary Key)! Therefore, every tuple in R produces exactly 1 joined tuple, so both the minimum and maximum cardinality of R ⋈ S are 150!", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 30}, {"id": "dbms-test-q31", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q31: [Concurrent vs. Serial Schedule Count Math] Transaction T1 has 3 operations and Transaction T2 has 4 operations. How many total valid interleaved (concurrent + serial) schedules exist, and how many of them are strictly non-serial?", "options": ["A) Total = 35 schedules | Non-serial = 33 schedules", "B) Total = 12 schedules | Non-serial = 10 schedules", "C) Total = 70 schedules | Non-serial = 68 schedules", "D) Total = 5040 schedules | Non-serial = 5038 schedules"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Transaction T1 consists of 3 operations and Transaction T2 consists of 4 operations. How many total valid schedules (preserving the internal operation order of each transaction) exist, and how many of them are strictly non-serial?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "For k transactions with n1, n2, ... operations, the total number of valid schedules that preserve each transaction's internal instruction order is (n1 + n2)! / (n1! * n2!) = (3 + 4)! / (3! * 4!) = 7! / (6 * 24) = (7 * 6 * 5) / 6 = 35 total schedules. Since there are k! = 2! = 2 serial schedules (T1->T2 and T2->T1), the number of strictly non-serial schedules is 35 - 2 = 33.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 31}, {"id": "dbms-test-q32", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q32: [Precedence Graph & Serial Order Math] Consider schedule S: R1(A), R2(B), W1(B), R3(A), W2(C), W3(B), R1(C). By constructing the Precedence Graph for S, determine whether S is Conflict Serializable and its equivalent serial order:", "options": ["A) Conflict Serializable; equivalent to T2 -> T1 -> T3", "B) Conflict Serializable; equivalent to T1 -> T2 -> T3", "C) Conflict Serializable; equivalent to T3 -> T1 -> T2", "D) Not Conflict Serializable because the Precedence Graph contains a cycle between T1 and T2."], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Consider the schedule S across transactions T1, T2, and T3:\\nS: R1(A), R2(B), W1(B), R3(A), W2(C), W3(B), R1(C)\\nBy constructing the Precedence Graph for S, determine whether S is Conflict Serializable:", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let's find all conflicting pairs on A, B, and C: 1) On A: Only R1(A) and R3(A) (both Reads -> 0 conflicts). 2) On B: R2(B) before W1(B) gives edge T2 -> T1; R2(B) before W3(B) gives edge T2 -> T3; W1(B) before W3(B) gives edge T1 -> T3. 3) On C: W2(C) before R1(C) gives edge T2 -> T1. Combined directed edges: T2 -> T1, T2 -> T3, and T1 -> T3. There are NO cycles (DAG)! The unique topological sort is T2 -> T1 -> T3.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 32}, {"id": "dbms-test-q33", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q33: [B+ Tree Internal Node Order Math] A database has a disk block size of 4096 bytes. A search key field is 12 bytes long, and a disk block pointer is 8 bytes long. What is the maximum order p (maximum number of block pointers) of an internal B+ Tree node?", "options": ["A) 136", "B) 186", "C) 205", "D) 256"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "A database uses a disk block size of 4096 bytes. The search key field is 12 bytes long, a disk block pointer is 8 bytes long, and a data record pointer is 10 bytes long. What is the maximum order p (maximum number of child block pointers) of an INTERNAL node in a B+ Tree?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Crucial B+ Tree fact: Internal nodes of a B+ Tree do NOT store data record pointers (the 10-byte record pointer is only in leaf nodes or B-Trees!). An internal node of order p contains at most p block pointers (8B each) and p - 1 search keys (12B each). Setting up the inequality: 8p + 12(p - 1) <= 4096 => 20p - 12 <= 4096 => 20p <= 4108 => p <= 205.4. Flooring to an integer gives p = 205.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 33}, {"id": "dbms-test-q34", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q34: [B-Tree vs. B+ Tree Leaf Order Math] Using the same parameters from Q33 (Block size = 4096B, Search Key = 12B, Block Pointer = 8B, Data Record Pointer = 10B), what is the maximum number of (key, record_pointer) entries that can fit in a B+ Tree LEAF node (which also holds 1 next-leaf block pointer)?", "options": ["A) 136", "B) 185", "C) 204", "D) 205"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Using the parameters from Q33 (Block size = 4096B, Search Key = 12B, Block Pointer = 8B, Data Record Pointer = 10B), what is the maximum number of (key, record_pointer) pairs p_leaf that can fit inside a B+ Tree LEAF node?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "A B+ Tree leaf node holds p_leaf pairs of (Search Key + Data Record Pointer) plus 1 block pointer to the next sibling leaf node. Inequality: p_leaf * (12 + 10) + 8 <= 4096 => 22 * p_leaf <= 4088 => p_leaf <= 185.81 => p_leaf = 185 entries.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 34}, {"id": "dbms-test-q35", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q35: [Primary Sparse Index Disk I/O Math] An ordered file has 30,000 records of size 100 bytes each, stored unspanned in disk blocks of 1024 bytes. A sparse Primary Index is built with key = 9 bytes and block pointer = 6 bytes. How many disk block accesses are needed to locate and fetch a record using binary search on the Primary Index?", "options": ["A) 6 block accesses", "B) 7 block accesses", "C) 12 block accesses", "D) 13 block accesses"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "An ordered file has 30,000 records of size 100 bytes each, stored unspanned in disk blocks of 1024 bytes. A sparse Primary Index is built with search key = 9 bytes and block pointer = 6 bytes. How many total disk block accesses are required in the worst case to locate and fetch a data record using binary search on the Primary Index?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Step 1: Data file blocking factor bfr = floor(1024 / 100) = 10 records/block. Total data blocks B = ceil(30,000 / 10) = 3,000 data blocks (without an index, binary search on the data file would take ceil(log2(3000)) = 12 block accesses). Step 2: Since a Primary Index is SPARSE, it has 1 index entry per data block = 3,000 index entries! Step 3: Index blocking factor bfr_i = floor(1024 / (9 + 6)) = floor(1024 / 15) = 68 index entries/block. Total index blocks B_i = ceil(3,000 / 68) = 45 index blocks. Step 4: Binary search on 45 index blocks takes ceil(log2(45)) = 6 block accesses + 1 block access to read the actual target data block = 7 block accesses!", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 35}, {"id": "dbms-test-q36", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q36: [Timestamp Ordering Rejection Trace] Transactions T1, T2, and T3 have timestamps TS(T1) = 10, TS(T2) = 20, and TS(T3) = 30. Initially R-TS(A) = W-TS(A) = 0. Given the sequence: R1(A), W3(A), R2(A), W2(A), which exact operation is rejected and causes its transaction to roll back under the Basic Timestamp Ordering protocol?", "options": ["A) Step 1: R1(A)", "B) Step 2: W3(A)", "C) Step 3: R2(A) (because TS(T2) = 20 < W-TS(A) = 30)", "D) Step 4: W2(A)"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Transactions T1, T2, and T3 have timestamps TS(T1) = 10, TS(T2) = 20, and TS(T3) = 30. Initially R-TS(A) = 0 and W-TS(A) = 0. Under the Basic Timestamp Ordering protocol, trace the sequence:\\n1. R1(A)   2. W3(A)   3. R2(A)   4. W2(A)\\nWhich operation is the FIRST to be rejected and cause a rollback?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let's trace step-by-step: 1) R1(A): TS(T1)=10 >= W-TS(A)=0 -> Allowed! R-TS(A) becomes max(0, 10) = 10. 2) W3(A): TS(T3)=30 >= R-TS(A)=10 and >= W-TS(A)=0 -> Allowed! W-TS(A) becomes 30. 3) R2(A): T2 tries to read A, so we check if TS(T2) < W-TS(A). Since TS(T2) = 20 < W-TS(A) = 30, a younger transaction (T3) has already overwritten A! Therefore, R2(A) is rejected at Step 3 and T2 is rolled back and restarted with a new timestamp.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 36}, {"id": "dbms-test-q37", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q37: [Undo/Redo Crash Recovery Log Math] A system using Immediate Database Modification crashes. The recovery log on disk contains (in format <Ti, Var, OldVal, NewVal>): <T1, start>, <T1, A, 10, 50>, <T2, start>, <T2, B, 20, 80>, <T1, commit>, <T3, start>, <T3, C, 30, 90>. After recovery completes, what are the guaranteed values of A, B, and C, and which operations were performed?", "options": ["A) Redo T1; Undo T2 and T3 -> Final values: A = 50, B = 20, C = 30", "B) Redo T1, T2, and T3 -> Final values: A = 50, B = 80, C = 90", "C) Undo T1, T2, and T3 -> Final values: A = 10, B = 20, C = 30", "D) Redo T1 and T2; Undo T3 -> Final values: A = 50, B = 80, C = 30"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "A system using Immediate Database Modification crashes. The WAL log on disk shows:\\n<T1, start>\\n<T1, A, 10, 50>\\n<T2, start>\\n<T2, B, 20, 80>\\n<T1, commit>\\n<T3, start>\\n<T3, C, 30, 90>\\n[CRASH]\\nWhat are the recovery actions and final values of A, B, and C after recovery?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "During recovery under Immediate Modification: 1) Transaction T1 has both <T1, start> and <T1, commit> in the log, so T1 is placed in the REDO list (setting A to its new value 50). 2) Transactions T2 and T3 have <start> records but NO <commit> record before the crash, so both T2 and T3 are placed in the UNDO list (restoring B to its old value 20 and C to its old value 30). Final values: A = 50, B = 20, C = 30.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 37}, {"id": "dbms-test-q38", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q38: [SQL NULL Aggregate Math] Table \\"Products\\" has 5 rows with \\"bonus\\" column values: 100, NULL, 200, NULL, 300. What is the exact numerical output of: SELECT COUNT(*), COUNT(bonus), AVG(bonus), SUM(bonus) FROM Products;?", "options": ["A) 5, 5, 120, 600", "B) 5, 3, 200, 600", "C) 3, 3, 200, 600", "D) 5, 3, NULL, NULL"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Table \\"Products\\" has 5 rows with \\"bonus\\" column values: 100, NULL, 200, NULL, 300. What is the exact numerical output of:\\nSELECT COUNT(*), COUNT(bonus), AVG(bonus), SUM(bonus) FROM Products;", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In SQL, all aggregate functions EXCEPT COUNT(*) completely ignore NULL values! 1) COUNT(*) counts all 5 rows = 5. 2) COUNT(bonus) counts only non-null values (100, 200, 300) = 3. 3) SUM(bonus) = 100 + 200 + 300 = 600. 4) AVG(bonus) divides the sum by the count of non-null rows (600 / 3 = 200, NOT 600 / 5 = 120!). Output: 5, 3, 200, 600.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 38}, {"id": "dbms-test-q39", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q39: [Block Nested Loop Join Disk I/O Math] Relation R occupies 600 disk blocks and relation S occupies 100 disk blocks. The buffer pool has 22 memory frames available (20 frames for the outer relation chunk, 1 frame for the inner relation block, 1 frame for output). What is the minimum number of disk block reads required to perform a Block Nested Loop Join of R and S?", "options": ["A) 3,100 block reads", "B) 6,100 block reads", "C) 18,600 block reads", "D) 60,100 block reads"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Relation R occupies 600 disk blocks and relation S occupies 100 disk blocks. The buffer pool has 22 memory frames available (M - 2 = 20 frames for buffering chunks of the outer relation, 1 frame for the inner relation, 1 frame for output). What is the MINIMUM number of disk block I/O reads required for a Block Nested Loop Join?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "In a Block Nested Loop Join with buffer chunk size B_chunk = M - 2 = 20 blocks, the cost formula is: B_outer + ceil(B_outer / 20) * B_inner. Always choose the SMALLER relation (S = 100 blocks) as the outer relation! Cost = 100 + ceil(100 / 20) * 600 = 100 + 5 * 600 = 3,100 disk block reads. (If R were chosen as outer, cost would be 600 + ceil(600/20)*100 = 3,600).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 39}, {"id": "dbms-test-q40", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q40: [Canonical / Minimal Cover Math] Given relation R(A, B, C) and Functional Dependencies F = { A -> BC, B -> C, A -> B, AB -> C }, what is the exact Minimal (Canonical) Cover Fc of F?", "options": ["A) { A -> B, B -> C } (2 FDs)", "B) { A -> BC, B -> C } (2 FDs)", "C) { A -> B, A -> C, B -> C } (3 FDs)", "D) { AB -> C, A -> B } (2 FDs)"], "category": "Numerical & Calculation", "question": "Given relation R(A, B, C) and Functional Dependencies:\\nF = { A -> BC, B -> C, A -> B, AB -> C }\\nWhat is the exact Minimal (Canonical) Cover Fc of F?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Step 1 (Singleton RHS): Split A -> BC into A -> B and A -> C. Set becomes { A -> B, A -> C, B -> C, AB -> C }. Step 2 (Remove extraneous LHS attributes): In AB -> C, since A+ includes B (via A -> B), B is extraneous on the LHS, reducing AB -> C to A -> C. Set becomes { A -> B, A -> C, B -> C }. Step 3 (Remove redundant FDs): Since A -> B and B -> C transitively imply A -> C, the FD A -> C is redundant and removed! The canonical cover is { A -> B, B -> C }.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 40}, {"id": "dbms-test-q41", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q41: [Composite Index Leftmost Prefix Trap] An e-commerce engineering team creates a composite B+ Tree index CREATE INDEX idx_orders ON orders(customer_id, status, created_at);. Queries with WHERE customer_id = 101 AND status = 'PAID' run in 2ms, but the admin dashboard query WHERE status = 'PAID' AND created_at >= '2026-01-01' takes 4,500ms and performs a Full Table Scan! Why?", "options": ["A) B+ Tree indexes cannot index VARCHAR and TIMESTAMP columns together.", "B) Because of the B+ Tree Leftmost Prefix Rule: a composite index on (A, B, C) is sorted first by A, then by B within each A, and then by C. If the leading column (customer_id) is omitted from the WHERE clause, the engine cannot traverse the B+ Tree for (status, created_at)!", "C) Composite indexes only work on JOIN queries, not WHERE clauses.", "D) The query must use HAVING instead of WHERE."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "An engineering team creates a composite B+ Tree index on orders(customer_id, status, created_at). Queries filtering by (customer_id, status) run in 2ms, but queries filtering only by WHERE status = 'PAID' AND created_at >= '2026-01-01' perform a slow Full Table Scan! Why?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "A composite B+ Tree index on (customer_id, status, created_at) is like a phonebook sorted by (LastName, FirstName, City). You can use it to look up (customer_id), (customer_id, status), or (customer_id, status, created_at). However, if you omit the leftmost prefix column (customer_id), the values of status (\\"PAID\\") are scattered across the entire tree under every customer_id—so the query planner cannot do a B+ Tree search and falls back to a scan (or requires a separate index starting with status).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 41}, {"id": "dbms-test-q42", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q42: [Race Condition in Flash-Sale / Ticket Booking] In a train ticket booking API, two users simultaneously click \\"Book\\" on the last remaining seat. Both requests run: SELECT seats_left FROM trains WHERE id = 99; (both read 1), and both run UPDATE trains SET seats_left = seats_left - 1 WHERE id = 99;, resulting in 2 confirmed tickets and seats_left = -1! Which database patterns prevent this Lost Update / Double-Booking bug?", "options": ["A) Using READ UNCOMMITTED isolation level.", "B) Either (1) Pessimistic Locking: SELECT seats_left FROM trains WHERE id = 99 FOR UPDATE (acquires an exclusive row lock so the second transaction waits until the first commits), OR (2) Atomic Conditional Update / Optimistic Locking: UPDATE trains SET seats_left = seats_left - 1 WHERE id = 99 AND seats_left > 0 (and checking affectedRows == 1).", "C) Adding a secondary index on seats_left.", "D) Using LEFT JOIN instead of INNER JOIN."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "Two users simultaneously click \\"Book\\" on the last train seat. Both transactions execute SELECT seats_left (both read 1) and then execute UPDATE trains SET seats_left = 0, double-booking the seat! Which database patterns properly prevent this?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "When reading a value in application memory and then issuing a separate UPDATE based on that read, two concurrent transactions under READ COMMITTED both read seats_left = 1 before either writes. Using SELECT ... FOR UPDATE locks the row on read so the second transaction blocks until the first commits (and then reads 0). Alternatively, a single atomic UPDATE ... WHERE id = 99 AND seats_left > 0 (or Optimistic Concurrency Control with a version column) ensures only 1 transaction gets affectedRows == 1.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 42}, {"id": "dbms-test-q43", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q43: [Deadlock Elimination in Wallet Transfers] In a payment microservice, User 1 transfers ₹100 to User 2 at the exact same millisecond that User 2 transfers ₹50 to User 1. Transaction 1 locks Wallet #1 and waits for Wallet #2; Transaction 2 locks Wallet #2 and waits for Wallet #1—triggering a database Deadlock! How do backend engineers structurally eliminate this deadlock?", "options": ["A) Increase the database connection pool size.", "B) Enforce a deterministic Canonical Lock Ordering: always sort the account IDs and acquire row locks in ascending order of account_id (first lock min(id1, id2), then lock max(id1, id2)), which breaks the Circular Wait condition!", "C) Disable database transactions.", "D) Catch the deadlock error and ignore it without retrying."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "User 1 transfers money to User 2 at the exact same millisecond that User 2 transfers money to User 1. T1 locks Wallet 1 then requests Wallet 2; T2 locks Wallet 2 then requests Wallet 1—causing a Deadlock! How do you structurally prevent this deadlock in code?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "A deadlock requires a Circular Wait cycle in the Wait-For Graph (T1 -> T2 -> T1). By enforcing a strict global lock acquisition order—always locking the smaller wallet_id first (Wallet #1) and the larger wallet_id second (Wallet #2) regardless of who is sender or receiver—both T1 and T2 try to lock Wallet #1 first! One succeeds and completes both updates while the other waits cleanly, making a cycle mathematically impossible.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 43}, {"id": "dbms-test-q44", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q44: [The N+1 Query Problem] A social media feed endpoint fetches the latest 100 posts (SELECT * FROM posts LIMIT 100;) and then loops over those 100 posts in Node.js, executing SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ? for each post's author—sending 101 separate network round-trips to the database! How do you fix this?", "options": ["A) It is called Object Slicing; solved by adding a B-Tree index.", "B) It is the N+1 Query Problem; solved by eager loading via a single SQL JOIN (SELECT p.*, u.name FROM posts p JOIN users u ON p.author_id = u.id) or batching with WHERE u.id IN (id1, id2, ..., id100), reducing 101 queries down to 1 or 2 queries!", "C) It is called Phantom Read; solved by SERIALIZABLE isolation.", "D) It is called Sharding; solved by splitting the table."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "An API endpoint fetches 100 posts (1 query) and then loops over the 100 posts in application code, executing SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ? for each post (100 queries = 101 DB round-trips). What is this anti-pattern called and how is it solved?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "The N+1 Query Problem is the #1 performance killer in backend applications using ORMs/ODMs. Even if each individual query takes 2ms, 101 sequential network round-trips add >200ms of latency. Combining them into a single SQL JOIN (or 2 queries using WHERE id IN (...)) fetches all data in 1–2 round-trips.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 44}, {"id": "dbms-test-q45", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q45: [Unindexed Foreign Key Trap on DELETE] A database has users(id PK) with 100,000 rows and activity_logs(id PK, user_id FK referencing users.id ON DELETE CASCADE) with 50,000,000 rows. Deleting a single user (DELETE FROM users WHERE id = 500) takes 18 seconds and spikes CPU to 100%, even though users.id is the Primary Key! Why?", "options": ["A) Primary Keys are only used for SELECT queries, not DELETE queries.", "B) To enforce the Foreign Key constraint (ON DELETE CASCADE / RESTRICT) when a row in users is deleted, the DBMS must check activity_logs for matching user_id = 500 rows. Because PostgreSQL/SQL Server do NOT automatically create an index on the Foreign Key column (activity_logs.user_id), it performs a Full Table Scan of all 50,000,000 rows in activity_logs!", "C) DELETE cannot use B+ Trees.", "D) The users table needs to be denormalized."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "Table users(id PK) has 100k rows and activity_logs(id PK, user_id FK ON DELETE CASCADE) has 50M rows. Executing DELETE FROM users WHERE id = 500 takes 18 seconds! Since users.id is already indexed as a Primary Key, why is DELETE so slow?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "While RDBMS engines automatically create a unique B+ Tree index on a Primary Key (users.id), many engines (like PostgreSQL, SQL Server, Oracle) do NOT automatically create an index on the child table's Foreign Key column (activity_logs.user_id)! Whenever you DELETE or UPDATE a parent key in users, the database must search the child table activity_logs to check for dependent rows—triggering a 50-million-row sequential scan unless you explicitly create an index on activity_logs(user_id).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 45}, {"id": "dbms-test-q46", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q46: [Over-Indexing Write Amplification] A developer adds 12 separate secondary B+ Tree indexes to a high-velocity telemetry_events table to make every possible analytics filter fast. Immediately, INSERT and UPDATE throughput drops by 75%. Why?", "options": ["A) Because every single INSERT, DELETE, or indexed-column UPDATE must not only write the table row, but also traverse, update, and potentially split/rebalance leaf blocks across all 12 separate B+ Tree structures on disk (Write Amplification)!", "B) Because tables with more than 5 indexes disable the buffer pool.", "C) Because B+ Trees lock the entire database during SELECT queries.", "D) Secondary indexes only slow down DELETE, never INSERT."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "A developer adds 12 secondary B+ Tree indexes to a write-heavy telemetry_events table. Immediately, INSERT and UPDATE throughput drops by 75%. Why do excessive indexes hurt write performance?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Every secondary B+ Tree index is a separate physical data structure on disk. When a new row is inserted into a table with 12 indexes, the storage engine must insert 12 new entries into 12 different B+ Trees, dirtying up to 12 random disk pages, writing WAL log records for all of them, and triggering node splits.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 46}, {"id": "dbms-test-q47", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q47: [Master-Slave Replication Lag — Read-Your-Own-Writes] To scale read traffic, an architecture sends all WRITE queries (INSERT/UPDATE) to the Primary (Master) DB and routes all READ queries (SELECT) to 3 Asynchronous Read Replicas. A user updates their shipping address and immediately gets redirected to the Checkout page, which still shows their OLD address for 2 seconds! Why did this happen and how do you fix it?", "options": ["A) The Primary DB rolled back the transaction.", "B) Asynchronous Replication Lag: the Read Replica had not yet applied the WAL stream from the Primary DB when the redirect GET arrived. Fix it using \\"Read-Your-Own-Writes\\" routing (routing a user's reads to the Primary DB for a short window after they perform a write, or tracking WAL LSN positions).", "C) B+ Tree corruption on the replica.", "D) Missing Foreign Key constraint."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "All writes go to the Primary DB and all reads go to Asynchronous Read Replicas. A user updates their address and is immediately redirected to a page that reads from a Read Replica—showing their OLD address! What caused this and how is it solved?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In Asynchronous Master-Replica replication, the Primary DB commits and responds to the client immediately while shipping WAL logs to Read Replicas asynchronously (which may lag by 50ms–2000ms). If the user's next GET request hits a lagging replica, they see stale state. Routing the mutating user's own reads to the Primary DB for a brief window (e.g., 5 seconds via session/cookie timestamp or LSN check) guarantees Read-Your-Own-Writes consistency.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 47}, {"id": "dbms-test-q48", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q48: [Deep Offset Pagination Bottleneck vs. Keyset Pagination] On a table with 20,000,000 posts, Page 1 (SELECT * FROM posts ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 20 OFFSET 0) takes 1ms, but Page 50,000 (LIMIT 20 OFFSET 1000000) takes 3.2 seconds! Why is large OFFSET slow, and how does Cursor/Keyset Pagination fix it?", "options": ["A) With OFFSET 1000000, the database must still scan, fetch, and count through 1,000,020 rows before discarding the first 1,000,000! Keyset Pagination replaces OFFSET with a B+ Tree range seek (WHERE id < :last_seen_id ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 20), jumping directly to the exact starting row in O(log N) time!", "B) OFFSET disables all indexes when the number exceeds 1,000.", "C) Keyset Pagination loads all 20M rows into Node.js RAM.", "D) Using OFFSET with ASC instead of DESC makes it O(1)."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "On a 20M-row table, LIMIT 20 OFFSET 0 takes 1ms, but LIMIT 20 OFFSET 1000000 takes 3.2 seconds! Why does OFFSET degrade linearly O(N), and how does Keyset (Cursor) Pagination solve it?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "B+ Trees do not store absolute row offsets inside nodes (because every insert/delete would require updating offsets across the entire tree). Thus, OFFSET 1,000,000 forces the engine to walk through 1,000,000 records and throw them away. Keyset/Cursor Pagination passes the last seen indexed value from the previous page (WHERE id < 849201 ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 20), allowing the B+ Tree to seek directly to id 849201 in O(log N) time regardless of how deep the user scrolls!", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 48}, {"id": "dbms-test-q49", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q49: [Database Sharding — Hot Partition Anti-Pattern] A large analytics platform horizontally shards its events table across 16 database servers using range-based sharding on the created_date column (each month mapped to a shard). Despite having 16 servers, 15 servers sit at 2% CPU while 1 server is overloaded at 100% CPU! Why?", "options": ["A) Because monotonically increasing timestamp/date shard keys route 100% of current real-time writes and recent dashboard reads to the single shard holding the current month! Fix it by sharding on a high-cardinality hash key (e.g., hash(tenant_id) or hash(user_id)) to distribute writes uniformly across all 16 shards.", "B) Because 16 is not a prime number.", "C) Because range sharding only works on string columns.", "D) Because B+ Trees cannot be used on sharded tables."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "An analytics platform shards its events table across 16 database servers by created_date (month). 15 shards sit idle at 2% CPU while 1 shard is overwhelmed at 100% CPU! What caused this \\"Hot Shard\\" bottleneck and how do you fix the shard key?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Using a monotonically increasing key (like created_date or an auto-incrementing ID) for range-based sharding directs every single new INSERT and most recent SELECT queries to the latest range shard (the \\"Hot Shard\\"), leaving historical shards idle. Choosing a high-cardinality hash-based or composite shard key (like tenant_id or user_id) spreads concurrent writes evenly across all shards.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 49}, {"id": "dbms-test-q50", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q50: [Denormalization Trade-Off in High-Read Systems] On an e-commerce platform, product pages are viewed 10,000 times per second, while new product reviews are posted 2 times per minute. Computing AVG(rating) and COUNT(*) from a 50M-row reviews table on every page view overloads the DB. The team adds avg_rating and review_count columns directly onto the products table. What is this technique called and what is its trade-off?", "options": ["A) It is called 4NF Normalization; it slows down reads and speeds up writes.", "B) It is Controlled Denormalization (Materializing Aggregates): it introduces controlled redundancy to make read queries O(1) without joins/aggregations, at the cost of requiring transactional updates (or triggers/events) to keep products.avg_rating in sync whenever a review is inserted, updated, or deleted.", "C) It is called Lossy Decomposition; it deletes the reviews table.", "D) It violates 1NF and prevents indexing."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "To avoid running AVG(rating) and COUNT(*) across a 50M-row reviews table on every product page view, the engineering team stores avg_rating and review_count directly on the products table. What is this technique called and what is the trade-off?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "While strict 3NF/BCNF normalization forbids storing derived/redundant aggregates, in read-heavy production systems (10,000 reads/sec vs. 2 writes/min), Controlled Denormalization trades a slightly more complex write path (updating the cached count/average inside the same transaction when a review is added) for massive read performance gains.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 50}]	https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc.html	\N	\N	\N
22155	Lecture 15: Dynamic Paths (Part 2) — Query Parameters (req.query), Edit/Update Workflows & Cascading Delete Operations	node-lecture-15-dynamic-paths-part-2-query-params-edit-delete-cascade	Extract URL query parameters with req.query, reuse form templates for both Create and Edit workflows, update existing records in place, and implement cascading deletes across related models.	2026-10-06 22:17:20.380156+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nCompleting full CRUD on the Airbnb Host dashboard requires **Editing existing homes** (pre-filling the form with current values and updating in place rather than creating a duplicate entry) and **Deleting homes** (including removing deleted home IDs from the `Favourite` list so no orphaned references remain).\n\n### 💡 Path Parameters (`req.params`) vs. Query Parameters (`req.query`)\n* **Path Parameters (`/host/edit-home/:homeId`):** Mandatory part of the route structure identifying a specific resource. Read via **`req.params.homeId`**.\n* **Query Parameters (`?editing=true&sort=price`):** Optional key-value modifiers appended after `?` and separated by `&`. You do **not** declare query parameters in the router path string! Express parses them automatically into **`req.query`** (e.g., `req.query.editing === "true"`).\n* **Important Quirk:** Just like `req.params`, all values in `req.query` are **Strings** (`"true"`, not boolean `true`), so convert via `const editing = req.query.editing === "true";`.\n\n### 💡 Reusing `edit-home.ejs` for Both Add & Edit + Cascading Delete\n1. **Unified Form Template (`views/host/edit-home.ejs`):**\n   * Form `action` switches dynamically: `action="/host/<%= editing ? 'edit-home' : 'add-home' %>"`.\n   * Inputs pre-fill existing values when `editing` is true: `value="<%= editing ? home.houseName : '' %>"`.\n   * Includes `<input type="hidden" name="id" value="<%= home.id %>" />` when editing.\n2. **Upsert Logic in `home.save()`:**\n   * If `this.id` already exists, update the existing element inside `registeredHomes` using `.map(h => h.id === this.id ? this : h)`.\n   * Otherwise, generate `this.id = Math.random().toString()` and `.push(this)`.\n3. **Cascading Delete (`Home.deleteById` + `Favourite.deleteById`):**\n   * When a Host deletes a property (`POST /host/delete-home/:homeId`), `Home.deleteById(homeId)` removes the home from `homes.json` **AND** invokes `Favourite.deleteById(homeId)` so the deleted home is also purged from `favourite.json`!\n\n### 🛠️ Edit Controller (`req.params` + `req.query`) & Cascading `deleteById`\n```javascript\n// 1. In controllers/hostController.js: Handle GET /host/edit-home/:homeId?editing=true\nexports.getEditHome = (req, res, next) => {\n  const homeId = req.params.homeId;\n  const editing = req.query.editing === "true"; // Convert string "true" to boolean!\n\n  Home.findById(homeId, (home) => {\n    if (!home) return res.redirect("/host/host-home-list");\n    res.render("host/edit-home", {\n      home: home,\n      pageTitle: "Edit your Home",\n      currentPage: "host-homes",\n      editing: editing\n    });\n  });\n};\n\n// 2. In models/home.js: Delete Home AND cascade delete from Favourite model\nstatic deleteById(homeId, callback) {\n  this.fetchAll((homes) => {\n    const updatedHomes = homes.filter((home) => home.id !== homeId);\n    fs.writeFile(homeDataPath, JSON.stringify(updatedHomes), (err) => {\n      if (!err) {\n        Favourite.deleteById(homeId, callback);\n      }\n    });\n  });\n}\n```	[{"url": "https://expressjs.com/en/5x/api.html#req.query", "link": "https://expressjs.com/en/5x/api.html#req.query", "title": "Express.js Official Docs — req.query & Request Object Reference"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=68UFRuVyim8", "title": "Lecture 14: Dynamic Path Part - 2 | NodeJS Complete Course in Hindi", "duration": "53m", "creator_name": "Complete Coding by Prashant Sir", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir"}]	[{"id": "node-lec-15-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Compare req.params, req.query, and req.body in Express.js. When should each be used?", "title": "Q1: Compare req.params, req.query, and req.body in Express.js. When should each be used?", "answer": "1) req.params (Route/Path Parameters, e.g., /homes/:homeId): Used to identify a specific mandatory resource in the URL hierarchy; defined in the route pattern with a colon (:). 2) req.query (Query String Parameters, e.g., ?editing=true&page=2): Used for optional flags, filtering, sorting, or pagination on GET requests; not declared in the route path string. 3) req.body (Request Payload): Used for sending larger or sensitive structured data in POST/PUT/PATCH requests (requires express.urlencoded() or express.json() middleware).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) req.params (Route/Path Parameters, e.g., /homes/:homeId): Used to identify a specific mandatory resource in the URL hierarchy; defined in the route pattern with a colon (:). 2) req.query (Query String Parameters, e.g., ?editing=true&page=2): Used for optional flags, filtering, sorting, or pagination on GET requests; not declared in the route path string. 3) req.body (Request Payload): Used for sending larger or sensitive structured data in POST/PUT/PATCH requests (requires express.urlencoded() or express.json() middleware)."}, {"id": "node-lec-15-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why did we check req.query.editing === \\"true\\" instead of if (req.query.editing) when parsing the query parameter?", "title": "Q2: Why did we check req.query.editing === \\"true\\" instead of if (req.query.editing) when parsing the query parameter?", "answer": "Because URL query values are always parsed as Strings! If someone passed ?editing=false in the URL, the non-empty string \\"false\\" is actually Truthy in JavaScript (so if (\\"false\\") would evaluate to true!). Explicitly comparing req.query.editing === \\"true\\" guarantees a proper boolean true/false result.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Because URL query values are always parsed as Strings! If someone passed ?editing=false in the URL, the non-empty string \\"false\\" is actually Truthy in JavaScript (so if (\\"false\\") would evaluate to true!). Explicitly comparing req.query.editing === \\"true\\" guarantees a proper boolean true/false result."}, {"id": "node-lec-15-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: Why did we use a POST form (<form action=\\"/host/delete-home/<%= home.id %>\\" method=\\"POST\\">) for deleting a home instead of a simple <a href> GET link or DELETE method?", "title": "Q3: Why did we use a POST form (<form action=\\"/host/delete-home/<%= home.id %>\\" method=\\"POST\\">) for deleting a home instead of a simple <a href> GET link or DELETE method?", "answer": "Two reasons: 1) Standard HTML <form> tags in browsers only support GET and POST methods natively (they do not support method=\\"DELETE\\" without JavaScript fetch() or method-override middleware). 2) Destructive actions that mutate or delete server data must NEVER use GET links, because browser prefetchers, web crawlers, or accidental link shares would trigger GET URLs and wipe out database records!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Two reasons: 1) Standard HTML <form> tags in browsers only support GET and POST methods natively (they do not support method=\\"DELETE\\" without JavaScript fetch() or method-override middleware). 2) Destructive actions that mutate or delete server data must NEVER use GET links, because browser prefetchers, web crawlers, or accidental link shares would trigger GET URLs and wipe out database records!"}, {"id": "node-lec-15-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What is a \\"Cascading Delete\\" (Referential Integrity), and why did Home.deleteById() also need to call Favourite.deleteById()?", "title": "Q4: What is a \\"Cascading Delete\\" (Referential Integrity), and why did Home.deleteById() also need to call Favourite.deleteById()?", "answer": "When a parent entity (a Home) is deleted from homes.json, any foreign key references to that homeId still sitting inside favourite.json become \\"orphaned references.\\" Without cascading the delete to Favourite.deleteById(homeId), the Favourites page would continue storing dead IDs (or crash when trying to render deleted homes).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "When a parent entity (a Home) is deleted from homes.json, any foreign key references to that homeId still sitting inside favourite.json become \\"orphaned references.\\" Without cascading the delete to Favourite.deleteById(homeId), the Favourites page would continue storing dead IDs (or crash when trying to render deleted homes)."}, {"id": "node-lec-15-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: In the Edit Home form, why did we need <input type=\\"hidden\\" name=\\"id\\" value=\\"<%= home.id %>\\" /> when submitting POST /host/edit-home?", "title": "Q5: In the Edit Home form, why did we need <input type=\\"hidden\\" name=\\"id\\" value=\\"<%= home.id %>\\" /> when submitting POST /host/edit-home?", "answer": "When the user submits the Edit Home form to POST /host/edit-home, the server needs to know WHICH existing home ID in homes.json should be overwritten with the updated form fields. Since the user shouldn't see or edit the internal ID string, passing it in a hidden input ensures req.body.id is sent along with the updated houseName, price, and location.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "When the user submits the Edit Home form to POST /host/edit-home, the server needs to know WHICH existing home ID in homes.json should be overwritten with the updated form fields. Since the user shouldn't see or edit the internal ID string, passing it in a hidden input ensures req.body.id is sent along with the updated houseName, price, and location."}, {"id": "node-lec-15-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: How does reusing a single edit-home.ejs template with an editing boolean flag compare to maintaining separate add-home.ejs and edit-home.ejs files?", "title": "Q6: How does reusing a single edit-home.ejs template with an editing boolean flag compare to maintaining separate add-home.ejs and edit-home.ejs files?", "answer": "Because Add Home and Edit Home share 95% identical form inputs and Tailwind styling, maintaining two separate files violates DRY (any new field added to Home would have to be manually updated in two places). Using a single template parameterized by editing (true vs. false) keeps the form UI consistent in one file.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Because Add Home and Edit Home share 95% identical form inputs and Tailwind styling, maintaining two separate files violates DRY (any new field added to Home would have to be manually updated in two places). Using a single template parameterized by editing (true vs. false) keeps the form UI consistent in one file."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nCompleting full CRUD on the Airbnb Host dashboard requires **Editing existing homes** (pre-filling the form with current values and updating in place rather than creating a duplicate entry) and **Deleting homes** (including removing deleted home IDs from the `Favourite` list so no orphaned references remain).\n\n### 💡 Path Parameters (`req.params`) vs. Query Parameters (`req.query`)\n* **Path Parameters (`/host/edit-home/:homeId`):** Mandatory part of the route structure identifying a specific resource. Read via **`req.params.homeId`**.\n* **Query Parameters (`?editing=true&sort=price`):** Optional key-value modifiers appended after `?` and separated by `&`. You do **not** declare query parameters in the router path string! Express parses them automatically into **`req.query`** (e.g., `req.query.editing === "true"`).\n* **Important Quirk:** Just like `req.params`, all values in `req.query` are **Strings** (`"true"`, not boolean `true`), so convert via `const editing = req.query.editing === "true";`.\n\n### 💡 Reusing `edit-home.ejs` for Both Add & Edit + Cascading Delete\n1. **Unified Form Template (`views/host/edit-home.ejs`):**\n   * Form `action` switches dynamically: `action="/host/<%= editing ? 'edit-home' : 'add-home' %>"`.\n   * Inputs pre-fill existing values when `editing` is true: `value="<%= editing ? home.houseName : '' %>"`.\n   * Includes `<input type="hidden" name="id" value="<%= home.id %>" />` when editing.\n2. **Upsert Logic in `home.save()`:**\n   * If `this.id` already exists, update the existing element inside `registeredHomes` using `.map(h => h.id === this.id ? this : h)`.\n   * Otherwise, generate `this.id = Math.random().toString()` and `.push(this)`.\n3. **Cascading Delete (`Home.deleteById` + `Favourite.deleteById`):**\n   * When a Host deletes a property (`POST /host/delete-home/:homeId`), `Home.deleteById(homeId)` removes the home from `homes.json` **AND** invokes `Favourite.deleteById(homeId)` so the deleted home is also purged from `favourite.json`!\n\n### 🛠️ Edit Controller (`req.params` + `req.query`) & Cascading `deleteById`\n```javascript\n// 1. In controllers/hostController.js: Handle GET /host/edit-home/:homeId?editing=true\nexports.getEditHome = (req, res, next) => {\n  const homeId = req.params.homeId;\n  const editing = req.query.editing === "true"; // Convert string "true" to boolean!\n\n  Home.findById(homeId, (home) => {\n    if (!home) return res.redirect("/host/host-home-list");\n    res.render("host/edit-home", {\n      home: home,\n      pageTitle: "Edit your Home",\n      currentPage: "host-homes",\n      editing: editing\n    });\n  });\n};\n\n// 2. In models/home.js: Delete Home AND cascade delete from Favourite model\nstatic deleteById(homeId, callback) {\n  this.fetchAll((homes) => {\n    const updatedHomes = homes.filter((home) => home.id !== homeId);\n    fs.writeFile(homeDataPath, JSON.stringify(updatedHomes), (err) => {\n      if (!err) {\n        Favourite.deleteById(homeId, callback);\n      }\n    });\n  });\n}\n```	Complete Coding by Prashant Sir	https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir	24	Lecture 15: Dynamic Paths (Part 2) — Query Parameters (req.query), Edit/Update Workflows & Cascading Delete Operations	Extract URL query parameters with req.query, reuse form templates for both Create and Edit workflows, update existing records in place, and implement cascading deletes across related models.	20	Easy	\N	15	[{"id": "node-lec-15-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Compare req.params, req.query, and req.body in Express.js. When should each be used?", "title": "Q1: Compare req.params, req.query, and req.body in Express.js. When should each be used?", "answer": "1) req.params (Route/Path Parameters, e.g., /homes/:homeId): Used to identify a specific mandatory resource in the URL hierarchy; defined in the route pattern with a colon (:). 2) req.query (Query String Parameters, e.g., ?editing=true&page=2): Used for optional flags, filtering, sorting, or pagination on GET requests; not declared in the route path string. 3) req.body (Request Payload): Used for sending larger or sensitive structured data in POST/PUT/PATCH requests (requires express.urlencoded() or express.json() middleware).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) req.params (Route/Path Parameters, e.g., /homes/:homeId): Used to identify a specific mandatory resource in the URL hierarchy; defined in the route pattern with a colon (:). 2) req.query (Query String Parameters, e.g., ?editing=true&page=2): Used for optional flags, filtering, sorting, or pagination on GET requests; not declared in the route path string. 3) req.body (Request Payload): Used for sending larger or sensitive structured data in POST/PUT/PATCH requests (requires express.urlencoded() or express.json() middleware)."}, {"id": "node-lec-15-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why did we check req.query.editing === \\"true\\" instead of if (req.query.editing) when parsing the query parameter?", "title": "Q2: Why did we check req.query.editing === \\"true\\" instead of if (req.query.editing) when parsing the query parameter?", "answer": "Because URL query values are always parsed as Strings! If someone passed ?editing=false in the URL, the non-empty string \\"false\\" is actually Truthy in JavaScript (so if (\\"false\\") would evaluate to true!). Explicitly comparing req.query.editing === \\"true\\" guarantees a proper boolean true/false result.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Because URL query values are always parsed as Strings! If someone passed ?editing=false in the URL, the non-empty string \\"false\\" is actually Truthy in JavaScript (so if (\\"false\\") would evaluate to true!). Explicitly comparing req.query.editing === \\"true\\" guarantees a proper boolean true/false result."}, {"id": "node-lec-15-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: Why did we use a POST form (<form action=\\"/host/delete-home/<%= home.id %>\\" method=\\"POST\\">) for deleting a home instead of a simple <a href> GET link or DELETE method?", "title": "Q3: Why did we use a POST form (<form action=\\"/host/delete-home/<%= home.id %>\\" method=\\"POST\\">) for deleting a home instead of a simple <a href> GET link or DELETE method?", "answer": "Two reasons: 1) Standard HTML <form> tags in browsers only support GET and POST methods natively (they do not support method=\\"DELETE\\" without JavaScript fetch() or method-override middleware). 2) Destructive actions that mutate or delete server data must NEVER use GET links, because browser prefetchers, web crawlers, or accidental link shares would trigger GET URLs and wipe out database records!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Two reasons: 1) Standard HTML <form> tags in browsers only support GET and POST methods natively (they do not support method=\\"DELETE\\" without JavaScript fetch() or method-override middleware). 2) Destructive actions that mutate or delete server data must NEVER use GET links, because browser prefetchers, web crawlers, or accidental link shares would trigger GET URLs and wipe out database records!"}, {"id": "node-lec-15-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What is a \\"Cascading Delete\\" (Referential Integrity), and why did Home.deleteById() also need to call Favourite.deleteById()?", "title": "Q4: What is a \\"Cascading Delete\\" (Referential Integrity), and why did Home.deleteById() also need to call Favourite.deleteById()?", "answer": "When a parent entity (a Home) is deleted from homes.json, any foreign key references to that homeId still sitting inside favourite.json become \\"orphaned references.\\" Without cascading the delete to Favourite.deleteById(homeId), the Favourites page would continue storing dead IDs (or crash when trying to render deleted homes).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "When a parent entity (a Home) is deleted from homes.json, any foreign key references to that homeId still sitting inside favourite.json become \\"orphaned references.\\" Without cascading the delete to Favourite.deleteById(homeId), the Favourites page would continue storing dead IDs (or crash when trying to render deleted homes)."}, {"id": "node-lec-15-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: In the Edit Home form, why did we need <input type=\\"hidden\\" name=\\"id\\" value=\\"<%= home.id %>\\" /> when submitting POST /host/edit-home?", "title": "Q5: In the Edit Home form, why did we need <input type=\\"hidden\\" name=\\"id\\" value=\\"<%= home.id %>\\" /> when submitting POST /host/edit-home?", "answer": "When the user submits the Edit Home form to POST /host/edit-home, the server needs to know WHICH existing home ID in homes.json should be overwritten with the updated form fields. Since the user shouldn't see or edit the internal ID string, passing it in a hidden input ensures req.body.id is sent along with the updated houseName, price, and location.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "When the user submits the Edit Home form to POST /host/edit-home, the server needs to know WHICH existing home ID in homes.json should be overwritten with the updated form fields. Since the user shouldn't see or edit the internal ID string, passing it in a hidden input ensures req.body.id is sent along with the updated houseName, price, and location."}, {"id": "node-lec-15-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: How does reusing a single edit-home.ejs template with an editing boolean flag compare to maintaining separate add-home.ejs and edit-home.ejs files?", "title": "Q6: How does reusing a single edit-home.ejs template with an editing boolean flag compare to maintaining separate add-home.ejs and edit-home.ejs files?", "answer": "Because Add Home and Edit Home share 95% identical form inputs and Tailwind styling, maintaining two separate files violates DRY (any new field added to Home would have to be manually updated in two places). Using a single template parameterized by editing (true vs. false) keeps the form UI consistent in one file.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Because Add Home and Edit Home share 95% identical form inputs and Tailwind styling, maintaining two separate files violates DRY (any new field added to Home would have to be manually updated in two places). Using a single template parameterized by editing (true vs. false) keeps the form UI consistent in one file."}]	https://expressjs.com/en/5x/api.html#req.query	\N	\N	\N
22112	Lecture 27: Type Conversion & Inspection Operators — $toString, $toInt, $toLong, $toDouble, $toDecimal, $toBool, $toObjectId, $convert, $type & $isNumber	mongo-lecture-27-type-conversion-operators-convert-type-isnumber	Cast field values between BSON types on the fly using $toString, $toInt, $toLong, $toDouble, $toDecimal, $toBool, $toObjectId, and $convert, and inspect types with $type and $isNumber.	2026-10-06 19:52:12.616986+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhen unvalidated data is imported into MongoDB (for example, numeric ages stored as quoted strings `"22"`), mathematical operators fail unless you cast the values first. **Type Aggregation Operators** convert values across BSON types and inspect field types inside aggregation pipelines.\n\n### 💡 Shorthand Type Casting Operators (`$to*`)\n* **`$toString: <expr>`:** Converts numbers, booleans, or `ISODate` values into strings.\n* **`$toInt: <expr>`:** Converts a numeric string (`"22"`), boolean (`true` -> `1`, `false` -> `0`), or floating-point double (`33.58` -> `33`, truncating decimals) into a **32-bit Integer**.\n* **`$toLong: <expr>`:** Converts a value into a **64-bit Integer (`Long`)**.\n* **`$toDouble: <expr>`:** Converts a value into a **64-bit Floating-Point `Double`**.\n* **`$toDecimal: <expr>`:** Converts a value into a high-precision **128-bit `Decimal128`** (ideal for financial/currency calculations). Converts `ISODate` to Unix epoch milliseconds.\n* **`$toBool: <expr>`:** Converts a value to Boolean (`0` -> `false`; any non-zero number, non-empty string, or date -> `true`; `null` -> `null`).\n* **`$toObjectId: <24-hex-char-string>`:** Converts a 24-character hexadecimal string into a native MongoDB `ObjectId`.\n\n### 💡 Universal Conversion & Type Inspection Operators\n* **`$convert: { input: <expr>, to: "<bsonType>" }`:** Universal conversion operator that can replace any `$to*` operator (supports `"int"`, `"long"`, `"double"`, `"decimal"`, `"string"`, `"bool"`, `"date"`, `"objectId"`).\n* **`$type: <field>`:** Returns the BSON data type name of the field as a string (e.g., `"string"`, `"int"`, `"double"`, `"date"`, `"bool"`).\n* **`$isNumber: <field>`:** Returns `true` if the field is any numeric BSON type (`int`, `long`, `double`, `decimal`), or `false` otherwise.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical Type Conversion & Inspection Examples\n```javascript\n// 1. Shorthand Type Conversions ($toInt, $toDouble, $toDecimal, $toBool, $toString)\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      intAge: { $toInt: "$age" },               // "22" -> 22\n      longAge: { $toLong: "$age" },             // "22" -> Long("22")\n      doubleMarks: { $toDouble: "$marks" },     // 64-bit float\n      decimalMarks: { $toDecimal: "$marks" },   // 128-bit Decimal128\n      boolMarks: { $toBool: "$marks" },         // Non-zero -> true\n      stringDOB: { $toString: "$dob" },         // ISODate -> ISO string\n      convertedObjId: {\n        $toObjectId: "6712a8f9c0ffee1234567890"\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 2. Universal $convert + Type Inspection ($type and $isNumber)\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      convertedAge: {\n        $convert: {\n          input: "$age",\n          to: "int"\n        }\n      },\n      ageFieldType: { $type: "$age" },          // e.g. "string"\n      marksFieldType: { $type: "$marks" },      // e.g. "int" or "double"\n      isMarksNumeric: { $isNumber: "$marks" },  // true\n      isDobNumeric: { $isNumber: "$dob" }       // false\n    }\n  }\n)\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/convert/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/convert/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 27: Type Conversion & Inspection Operators"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rQXuHD4wWcg", "title": "MongoDB Type Operators Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "25m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[{"id": "mongo-27-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db3757c", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db3757c", "name": "Problem #177: Find logs with meta.userId 19", "title": "Problem #177: Find logs with meta.userId 19", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "177", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return logs where meta.userId is 19.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db3757c"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db37579", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db37579", "name": "Problem #178: Find articles by authorId 19", "title": "Problem #178: Find articles by authorId 19", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "178", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return articles where authorId is 19.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db37579"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-3", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db374f3", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db374f3", "name": "Problem #222: Limit logs to 30", "title": "Problem #222: Limit logs to 30", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "222", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return only 30 logs.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db374f3"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-4", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374f0", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374f0", "name": "Problem #223: Limit articles to 20", "title": "Problem #223: Limit articles to 20", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "223", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return only 20 articles.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374f0"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-5", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374ea", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374ea", "name": "Problem #225: Articles sorted by publishedAt descending", "title": "Problem #225: Articles sorted by publishedAt descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "225", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return articles sorted by **publishedAt** descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374ea"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-6", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374e7", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374e7", "name": "Problem #226: Articles sorted by views descending", "title": "Problem #226: Articles sorted by views descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "226", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return articles sorted by **views** descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374e7"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-7", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d8", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d8", "name": "Problem #231: Articles with views over 1000", "title": "Problem #231: Articles with views over 1000", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "231", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return articles where **views** is greater than 1000.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d8"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-8", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d5", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d5", "name": "Problem #232: Find logs with level debug", "title": "Problem #232: Find logs with level debug", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "232", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return logs where **level** is \\"debug\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d5"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-9", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d2", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d2", "name": "Problem #233: Find logs with level warn", "title": "Problem #233: Find logs with level warn", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "233", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return logs where **level** is \\"warn\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d2"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-10", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374cf", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374cf", "name": "Problem #234: Find logs with level info", "title": "Problem #234: Find logs with level info", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "234", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return logs where **level** is \\"info\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374cf"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhen unvalidated data is imported into MongoDB (for example, numeric ages stored as quoted strings `"22"`), mathematical operators fail unless you cast the values first. **Type Aggregation Operators** convert values across BSON types and inspect field types inside aggregation pipelines.\n\n### 💡 Shorthand Type Casting Operators (`$to*`)\n* **`$toString: <expr>`:** Converts numbers, booleans, or `ISODate` values into strings.\n* **`$toInt: <expr>`:** Converts a numeric string (`"22"`), boolean (`true` -> `1`, `false` -> `0`), or floating-point double (`33.58` -> `33`, truncating decimals) into a **32-bit Integer**.\n* **`$toLong: <expr>`:** Converts a value into a **64-bit Integer (`Long`)**.\n* **`$toDouble: <expr>`:** Converts a value into a **64-bit Floating-Point `Double`**.\n* **`$toDecimal: <expr>`:** Converts a value into a high-precision **128-bit `Decimal128`** (ideal for financial/currency calculations). Converts `ISODate` to Unix epoch milliseconds.\n* **`$toBool: <expr>`:** Converts a value to Boolean (`0` -> `false`; any non-zero number, non-empty string, or date -> `true`; `null` -> `null`).\n* **`$toObjectId: <24-hex-char-string>`:** Converts a 24-character hexadecimal string into a native MongoDB `ObjectId`.\n\n### 💡 Universal Conversion & Type Inspection Operators\n* **`$convert: { input: <expr>, to: "<bsonType>" }`:** Universal conversion operator that can replace any `$to*` operator (supports `"int"`, `"long"`, `"double"`, `"decimal"`, `"string"`, `"bool"`, `"date"`, `"objectId"`).\n* **`$type: <field>`:** Returns the BSON data type name of the field as a string (e.g., `"string"`, `"int"`, `"double"`, `"date"`, `"bool"`).\n* **`$isNumber: <field>`:** Returns `true` if the field is any numeric BSON type (`int`, `long`, `double`, `decimal`), or `false` otherwise.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical Type Conversion & Inspection Examples\n```javascript\n// 1. Shorthand Type Conversions ($toInt, $toDouble, $toDecimal, $toBool, $toString)\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      intAge: { $toInt: "$age" },               // "22" -> 22\n      longAge: { $toLong: "$age" },             // "22" -> Long("22")\n      doubleMarks: { $toDouble: "$marks" },     // 64-bit float\n      decimalMarks: { $toDecimal: "$marks" },   // 128-bit Decimal128\n      boolMarks: { $toBool: "$marks" },         // Non-zero -> true\n      stringDOB: { $toString: "$dob" },         // ISODate -> ISO string\n      convertedObjId: {\n        $toObjectId: "6712a8f9c0ffee1234567890"\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 2. Universal $convert + Type Inspection ($type and $isNumber)\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      convertedAge: {\n        $convert: {\n          input: "$age",\n          to: "int"\n        }\n      },\n      ageFieldType: { $type: "$age" },          // e.g. "string"\n      marksFieldType: { $type: "$marks" },      // e.g. "int" or "double"\n      isMarksNumeric: { $isNumber: "$marks" },  // true\n      isDobNumeric: { $isNumber: "$dob" }       // false\n    }\n  }\n)\n```	Yahoo Baba	https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba	655	Lecture 27: Type Conversion & Inspection Operators — $toString, $toInt, $toLong, $toDouble, $toDecimal, $toBool, $toObjectId, $convert, $type & $isNumber	Cast field values between BSON types on the fly using $toString, $toInt, $toLong, $toDouble, $toDecimal, $toBool, $toObjectId, and $convert, and inspect types with $type and $isNumber.	20	Easy	\N	27	[{"id": "mongo-27-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db3757c", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db3757c", "name": "Problem #177: Find logs with meta.userId 19", "title": "Problem #177: Find logs with meta.userId 19", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "177", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return logs where meta.userId is 19.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db3757c"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db37579", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db37579", "name": "Problem #178: Find articles by authorId 19", "title": "Problem #178: Find articles by authorId 19", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "178", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return articles where authorId is 19.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db37579"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-3", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db374f3", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db374f3", "name": "Problem #222: Limit logs to 30", "title": "Problem #222: Limit logs to 30", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "222", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return only 30 logs.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db374f3"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-4", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374f0", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374f0", "name": "Problem #223: Limit articles to 20", "title": "Problem #223: Limit articles to 20", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "223", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return only 20 articles.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374f0"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-5", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374ea", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374ea", "name": "Problem #225: Articles sorted by publishedAt descending", "title": "Problem #225: Articles sorted by publishedAt descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "225", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return articles sorted by **publishedAt** descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374ea"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-6", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374e7", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374e7", "name": "Problem #226: Articles sorted by views descending", "title": "Problem #226: Articles sorted by views descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "226", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return articles sorted by **views** descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374e7"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-7", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d8", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d8", "name": "Problem #231: Articles with views over 1000", "title": "Problem #231: Articles with views over 1000", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "231", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return articles where **views** is greater than 1000.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d8"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-8", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d5", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d5", "name": "Problem #232: Find logs with level debug", "title": "Problem #232: Find logs with level debug", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "232", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return logs where **level** is \\"debug\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d5"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-9", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d2", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d2", "name": "Problem #233: Find logs with level warn", "title": "Problem #233: Find logs with level warn", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "233", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return logs where **level** is \\"warn\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374d2"}, {"id": "mongo-27-prob-10", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374cf", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374cf", "name": "Problem #234: Find logs with level info", "title": "Problem #234: Find logs with level info", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "234", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return logs where **level** is \\"info\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374cf"}]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/convert/	\N	\N	\N
22156	Lecture 16: Introduction to SQL in Node.js — Relational Databases, mysql2 Connection Pools & Parameterized Queries	node-lecture-16-sql-in-nodejs-mysql2-connection-pool-parameterized-queries	Replace flat JSON files with a relational SQL database in Node.js using mysql2, manage concurrent queries with Connection Pools (createPool().promise()), and prevent SQL Injection using ? parameterized placeholders.	2026-10-06 22:17:20.380156+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nStoring application data in flat JSON files (`homes.json`) fails in production because every read/write loads and rewrites the entire file in memory, lacks concurrent locking for thousands of users, and cannot index or join large datasets efficiently. This lecture migrates our Node.js backend to a **Relational Database (SQL)** using **`mysql2`**.\n\n### 💡 Relational (SQL) vs. Non-Relational (NoSQL) Databases\n* **SQL (RDBMS — MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle):** Stores data in structured **Tables** with strict **Columns (Schema)** and **Rows (Records/Tuples)**. Enforces strict data types (`INT`, `VARCHAR`, `DECIMAL(10,2)`, `DOUBLE`) and relational integrity across normalized tables.\n* **NoSQL (MongoDB, DynamoDB):** Stores flexible schema-less documents/collections optimized for horizontal scaling and rapid iteration.\n\n### 💡 Connecting Node.js to SQL: Why Connection Pools (`mysql.createPool`):\n* **Single Connection Problem (`createConnection`):** If you open only 1 database connection, simultaneous requests from 500 users must wait in a single-file line for each query to finish, and if the connection drops, the app fails.\n* **Connection Pool (`mysql.createPool({...}).promise()`):** Maintains a pool of reusable, open database connections. Concurrent requests borrow an available connection from the pool, execute their query via Promises, and automatically release the connection back to the pool!\n\n### 💡 Preventing SQL Injection with Parameterized Queries (`?`)\n* **Never concatenate user input into SQL strings!** Instead, pass `?` placeholders into **`db.execute("INSERT INTO homes (...) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?)", [val1, val2, ...)`**.\n* **`db.execute()` Return Structure:** Returns a Promise resolving to a 2-element array **`[rows, fields`** (use array destructuring `const [homes = await db.execute(...)`).\n\n### 🛠️ Configuring `mysql2` Promise Pool & SQL Model Methods\n```javascript\n// File: utils/databaseUtil.js\nconst mysql = require("mysql2");\n\nconst pool = mysql.createPool({\n  host: "localhost",\n  user: "root",\n  password: "your_password",\n  database: "airbnb"\n});\n\nmodule.exports = pool.promise();\n\n// File: models/home.js (SQL Implementation)\nconst db = require("../utils/databaseUtil");\n\nmodule.exports = class Home {\n  constructor(houseName, price, location, rating, photoUrl, description, id) {\n    this.houseName = houseName;\n    this.price = price;\n    this.location = location;\n    this.rating = rating;\n    this.photoUrl = photoUrl;\n    this.description = description;\n    this.id = id;\n  }\n\n  save() {\n    if (this.id) {\n      // Update existing record safely using ? placeholders\n      return db.execute(\n        "UPDATE homes SET houseName=?, price=?, location=?, rating=?, photoUrl=?, description=? WHERE id=?",\n        [this.houseName, this.price, this.location, this.rating, this.photoUrl, this.description, this.id\n      );\n    } else {\n      // Insert new record safely\n      return db.execute(\n        "INSERT INTO homes (houseName, price, location, rating, photoUrl, description) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)",\n        [this.houseName, this.price, this.location, this.rating, this.photoUrl, this.description\n      );\n    }\n  }\n\n  static fetchAll() {\n    return db.execute("SELECT * FROM homes");\n  }\n\n  static findById(homeId) {\n    return db.execute("SELECT * FROM homes WHERE id=?", [homeId);\n  }\n\n  static deleteById(homeId) {\n    return db.execute("DELETE FROM homes WHERE id=?", [homeId);\n  }\n};\n```	[{"url": "https://sidorares.github.io/node-mysql2/docs", "link": "https://sidorares.github.io/node-mysql2/docs", "title": "MySQL2 Official Docs — Using Promise Connection Pools in Node.js"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-6xGHdkwx_M", "title": "Lecture 15: Introduction to SQL | NodeJS Complete Course in Hindi", "duration": "86m", "creator_name": "Complete Coding by Prashant Sir", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir"}]	[{"id": "node-lec-16-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Why do we use a Connection Pool (mysql.createPool) instead of a single Connection (mysql.createConnection) in a Node.js backend?", "title": "Q1: Why do we use a Connection Pool (mysql.createPool) instead of a single Connection (mysql.createConnection) in a Node.js backend?", "answer": "1) Concurrency: A single connection can only execute one query at a time—if 100 users hit your API simultaneously, 99 users are blocked waiting in line. A Connection Pool maintains multiple open connections in parallel so concurrent requests run simultaneously. 2) Latency & Resilience: Establishing a new TCP + authentication handshake with a database on every HTTP request is slow; a pool keeps warm connections ready for reuse and automatically replaces dropped connections.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "1) Concurrency: A single connection can only execute one query at a time—if 100 users hit your API simultaneously, 99 users are blocked waiting in line. A Connection Pool maintains multiple open connections in parallel so concurrent requests run simultaneously. 2) Latency & Resilience: Establishing a new TCP + authentication handshake with a database on every HTTP request is slow; a pool keeps warm connections ready for reuse and automatically replaces dropped connections."}, {"id": "node-lec-16-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: What is SQL Injection, and how does db.execute(\\"SELECT * FROM homes WHERE id = ?\\", [homeId]) prevent it?", "title": "Q2: What is SQL Injection, and how does db.execute(\\"SELECT * FROM homes WHERE id = ?\\", [homeId]) prevent it?", "answer": "SQL Injection occurs when untrusted user input is concatenated directly into a SQL string (e.g., SELECT * FROM homes WHERE id = ${homeId}). An attacker can pass \\"1 OR 1=1; DROP TABLE homes;--\\" to bypass filters or destroy data. Using parameterized queries (? placeholders with db.execute(sql, values)) sends the SQL command structure and the user data parameters separately via a prepared statement protocol—so the database treats the input strictly as literal data, never as executable SQL commands.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "SQL Injection occurs when untrusted user input is concatenated directly into a SQL string (e.g., SELECT * FROM homes WHERE id = ${homeId}). An attacker can pass \\"1 OR 1=1; DROP TABLE homes;--\\" to bypass filters or destroy data. Using parameterized queries (? placeholders with db.execute(sql, values)) sends the SQL command structure and the user data parameters separately via a prepared statement protocol—so the database treats the input strictly as literal data, never as executable SQL commands."}, {"id": "node-lec-16-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: When calling db.execute(\\"SELECT * FROM homes\\").then(([homes, fields]) => ...), why do we destructure [homes, fields] as an array?", "title": "Q3: When calling db.execute(\\"SELECT * FROM homes\\").then(([homes, fields]) => ...), why do we destructure [homes, fields] as an array?", "answer": "The mysql2 promise wrapper resolves db.execute() and db.query() with a 2-element tuple array: index 0 (homes) contains the actual array of matching database row objects (or ResultSetHeader for INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE), and index 1 (fields) contains column metadata definitions.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "The mysql2 promise wrapper resolves db.execute() and db.query() with a 2-element tuple array: index 0 (homes) contains the actual array of matching database row objects (or ResultSetHeader for INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE), and index 1 (fields) contains column metadata definitions."}, {"id": "node-lec-16-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why did flat JSON file storage (homes.json) fail to scale compared to a DBMS?", "title": "Q4: Why did flat JSON file storage (homes.json) fail to scale compared to a DBMS?", "answer": "1) Memory & I/O Overhead: Modifying a single field in a 500 MB JSON file requires reading the entire 500 MB file into RAM, parsing it, and rewriting the entire 500 MB file back to disk. 2) Race Conditions: If two concurrent requests write to homes.json at the same millisecond, one overwrites the other's changes. 3) No Indexing: Finding a record by ID requires an O(N) linear scan, whereas a DBMS uses B-Tree indexes for O(log N) lookups.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) Memory & I/O Overhead: Modifying a single field in a 500 MB JSON file requires reading the entire 500 MB file into RAM, parsing it, and rewriting the entire 500 MB file back to disk. 2) Race Conditions: If two concurrent requests write to homes.json at the same millisecond, one overwrites the other's changes. 3) No Indexing: Finding a record by ID requires an O(N) linear scan, whereas a DBMS uses B-Tree indexes for O(log N) lookups."}, {"id": "node-lec-16-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is the difference between db.query() and db.execute() in mysql2?", "title": "Q5: What is the difference between db.query() and db.execute() in mysql2?", "answer": "db.query() interpolates placeholders on the client side before sending a plain text SQL query to the MySQL server. db.execute() uses MySQL's binary Prepared Statement protocol: the server compiles the SQL statement once and executes it with binary parameters, offering stronger SQL injection protection and faster repeated execution.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "db.query() interpolates placeholders on the client side before sending a plain text SQL query to the MySQL server. db.execute() uses MySQL's binary Prepared Statement protocol: the server compiles the SQL statement once and executes it with binary parameters, offering stronger SQL injection protection and faster repeated execution."}, {"id": "node-lec-16-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why do we configure the id column in our SQL homes table with PRIMARY KEY and AUTO_INCREMENT?", "title": "Q6: Why do we configure the id column in our SQL homes table with PRIMARY KEY and AUTO_INCREMENT?", "answer": "PRIMARY KEY enforces that every row has a unique, non-null identifier and automatically builds a clustered B-Tree index on id for instant lookups. AUTO_INCREMENT delegates unique sequential ID generation to the database engine itself, eliminating any risk of duplicate IDs across concurrent inserts.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "PRIMARY KEY enforces that every row has a unique, non-null identifier and automatically builds a clustered B-Tree index on id for instant lookups. AUTO_INCREMENT delegates unique sequential ID generation to the database engine itself, eliminating any risk of duplicate IDs across concurrent inserts."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nStoring application data in flat JSON files (`homes.json`) fails in production because every read/write loads and rewrites the entire file in memory, lacks concurrent locking for thousands of users, and cannot index or join large datasets efficiently. This lecture migrates our Node.js backend to a **Relational Database (SQL)** using **`mysql2`**.\n\n### 💡 Relational (SQL) vs. Non-Relational (NoSQL) Databases\n* **SQL (RDBMS — MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle):** Stores data in structured **Tables** with strict **Columns (Schema)** and **Rows (Records/Tuples)**. Enforces strict data types (`INT`, `VARCHAR`, `DECIMAL(10,2)`, `DOUBLE`) and relational integrity across normalized tables.\n* **NoSQL (MongoDB, DynamoDB):** Stores flexible schema-less documents/collections optimized for horizontal scaling and rapid iteration.\n\n### 💡 Connecting Node.js to SQL: Why Connection Pools (`mysql.createPool`):\n* **Single Connection Problem (`createConnection`):** If you open only 1 database connection, simultaneous requests from 500 users must wait in a single-file line for each query to finish, and if the connection drops, the app fails.\n* **Connection Pool (`mysql.createPool({...}).promise()`):** Maintains a pool of reusable, open database connections. Concurrent requests borrow an available connection from the pool, execute their query via Promises, and automatically release the connection back to the pool!\n\n### 💡 Preventing SQL Injection with Parameterized Queries (`?`)\n* **Never concatenate user input into SQL strings!** Instead, pass `?` placeholders into **`db.execute("INSERT INTO homes (...) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?)", [val1, val2, ...)`**.\n* **`db.execute()` Return Structure:** Returns a Promise resolving to a 2-element array **`[rows, fields`** (use array destructuring `const [homes = await db.execute(...)`).\n\n### 🛠️ Configuring `mysql2` Promise Pool & SQL Model Methods\n```javascript\n// File: utils/databaseUtil.js\nconst mysql = require("mysql2");\n\nconst pool = mysql.createPool({\n  host: "localhost",\n  user: "root",\n  password: "your_password",\n  database: "airbnb"\n});\n\nmodule.exports = pool.promise();\n\n// File: models/home.js (SQL Implementation)\nconst db = require("../utils/databaseUtil");\n\nmodule.exports = class Home {\n  constructor(houseName, price, location, rating, photoUrl, description, id) {\n    this.houseName = houseName;\n    this.price = price;\n    this.location = location;\n    this.rating = rating;\n    this.photoUrl = photoUrl;\n    this.description = description;\n    this.id = id;\n  }\n\n  save() {\n    if (this.id) {\n      // Update existing record safely using ? placeholders\n      return db.execute(\n        "UPDATE homes SET houseName=?, price=?, location=?, rating=?, photoUrl=?, description=? WHERE id=?",\n        [this.houseName, this.price, this.location, this.rating, this.photoUrl, this.description, this.id\n      );\n    } else {\n      // Insert new record safely\n      return db.execute(\n        "INSERT INTO homes (houseName, price, location, rating, photoUrl, description) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)",\n        [this.houseName, this.price, this.location, this.rating, this.photoUrl, this.description\n      );\n    }\n  }\n\n  static fetchAll() {\n    return db.execute("SELECT * FROM homes");\n  }\n\n  static findById(homeId) {\n    return db.execute("SELECT * FROM homes WHERE id=?", [homeId);\n  }\n\n  static deleteById(homeId) {\n    return db.execute("DELETE FROM homes WHERE id=?", [homeId);\n  }\n};\n```	Complete Coding by Prashant Sir	https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir	24	Lecture 16: Introduction to SQL in Node.js — Relational Databases, mysql2 Connection Pools & Parameterized Queries	Replace flat JSON files with a relational SQL database in Node.js using mysql2, manage concurrent queries with Connection Pools (createPool().promise()), and prevent SQL Injection using ? parameterized placeholders.	20	Easy	\N	16	[{"id": "node-lec-16-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Why do we use a Connection Pool (mysql.createPool) instead of a single Connection (mysql.createConnection) in a Node.js backend?", "title": "Q1: Why do we use a Connection Pool (mysql.createPool) instead of a single Connection (mysql.createConnection) in a Node.js backend?", "answer": "1) Concurrency: A single connection can only execute one query at a time—if 100 users hit your API simultaneously, 99 users are blocked waiting in line. A Connection Pool maintains multiple open connections in parallel so concurrent requests run simultaneously. 2) Latency & Resilience: Establishing a new TCP + authentication handshake with a database on every HTTP request is slow; a pool keeps warm connections ready for reuse and automatically replaces dropped connections.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "1) Concurrency: A single connection can only execute one query at a time—if 100 users hit your API simultaneously, 99 users are blocked waiting in line. A Connection Pool maintains multiple open connections in parallel so concurrent requests run simultaneously. 2) Latency & Resilience: Establishing a new TCP + authentication handshake with a database on every HTTP request is slow; a pool keeps warm connections ready for reuse and automatically replaces dropped connections."}, {"id": "node-lec-16-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: What is SQL Injection, and how does db.execute(\\"SELECT * FROM homes WHERE id = ?\\", [homeId]) prevent it?", "title": "Q2: What is SQL Injection, and how does db.execute(\\"SELECT * FROM homes WHERE id = ?\\", [homeId]) prevent it?", "answer": "SQL Injection occurs when untrusted user input is concatenated directly into a SQL string (e.g., SELECT * FROM homes WHERE id = ${homeId}). An attacker can pass \\"1 OR 1=1; DROP TABLE homes;--\\" to bypass filters or destroy data. Using parameterized queries (? placeholders with db.execute(sql, values)) sends the SQL command structure and the user data parameters separately via a prepared statement protocol—so the database treats the input strictly as literal data, never as executable SQL commands.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "SQL Injection occurs when untrusted user input is concatenated directly into a SQL string (e.g., SELECT * FROM homes WHERE id = ${homeId}). An attacker can pass \\"1 OR 1=1; DROP TABLE homes;--\\" to bypass filters or destroy data. Using parameterized queries (? placeholders with db.execute(sql, values)) sends the SQL command structure and the user data parameters separately via a prepared statement protocol—so the database treats the input strictly as literal data, never as executable SQL commands."}, {"id": "node-lec-16-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: When calling db.execute(\\"SELECT * FROM homes\\").then(([homes, fields]) => ...), why do we destructure [homes, fields] as an array?", "title": "Q3: When calling db.execute(\\"SELECT * FROM homes\\").then(([homes, fields]) => ...), why do we destructure [homes, fields] as an array?", "answer": "The mysql2 promise wrapper resolves db.execute() and db.query() with a 2-element tuple array: index 0 (homes) contains the actual array of matching database row objects (or ResultSetHeader for INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE), and index 1 (fields) contains column metadata definitions.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "The mysql2 promise wrapper resolves db.execute() and db.query() with a 2-element tuple array: index 0 (homes) contains the actual array of matching database row objects (or ResultSetHeader for INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE), and index 1 (fields) contains column metadata definitions."}, {"id": "node-lec-16-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why did flat JSON file storage (homes.json) fail to scale compared to a DBMS?", "title": "Q4: Why did flat JSON file storage (homes.json) fail to scale compared to a DBMS?", "answer": "1) Memory & I/O Overhead: Modifying a single field in a 500 MB JSON file requires reading the entire 500 MB file into RAM, parsing it, and rewriting the entire 500 MB file back to disk. 2) Race Conditions: If two concurrent requests write to homes.json at the same millisecond, one overwrites the other's changes. 3) No Indexing: Finding a record by ID requires an O(N) linear scan, whereas a DBMS uses B-Tree indexes for O(log N) lookups.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) Memory & I/O Overhead: Modifying a single field in a 500 MB JSON file requires reading the entire 500 MB file into RAM, parsing it, and rewriting the entire 500 MB file back to disk. 2) Race Conditions: If two concurrent requests write to homes.json at the same millisecond, one overwrites the other's changes. 3) No Indexing: Finding a record by ID requires an O(N) linear scan, whereas a DBMS uses B-Tree indexes for O(log N) lookups."}, {"id": "node-lec-16-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is the difference between db.query() and db.execute() in mysql2?", "title": "Q5: What is the difference between db.query() and db.execute() in mysql2?", "answer": "db.query() interpolates placeholders on the client side before sending a plain text SQL query to the MySQL server. db.execute() uses MySQL's binary Prepared Statement protocol: the server compiles the SQL statement once and executes it with binary parameters, offering stronger SQL injection protection and faster repeated execution.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "db.query() interpolates placeholders on the client side before sending a plain text SQL query to the MySQL server. db.execute() uses MySQL's binary Prepared Statement protocol: the server compiles the SQL statement once and executes it with binary parameters, offering stronger SQL injection protection and faster repeated execution."}, {"id": "node-lec-16-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why do we configure the id column in our SQL homes table with PRIMARY KEY and AUTO_INCREMENT?", "title": "Q6: Why do we configure the id column in our SQL homes table with PRIMARY KEY and AUTO_INCREMENT?", "answer": "PRIMARY KEY enforces that every row has a unique, non-null identifier and automatically builds a clustered B-Tree index on id for instant lookups. AUTO_INCREMENT delegates unique sequential ID generation to the database engine itself, eliminating any risk of duplicate IDs across concurrent inserts.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "PRIMARY KEY enforces that every row has a unique, non-null identifier and automatically builds a clustered B-Tree index on id for instant lookups. AUTO_INCREMENT delegates unique sequential ID generation to the database engine itself, eliminating any risk of duplicate IDs across concurrent inserts."}]	https://sidorares.github.io/node-mysql2/docs	\N	\N	\N
22157	Lecture 17: MongoDB Integration in Node.js — MongoDB Atlas Cloud, Official mongodb Driver, ObjectId & Document CRUD	node-lecture-17-mongodb-integration-atlas-native-driver-objectid-crud	Provision a cloud database cluster on MongoDB Atlas, connect your Express server using a singleton getDB() utility, and execute native CRUD operations with ObjectId, insertOne, find, updateOne, and deleteOne.	2026-10-06 22:17:20.380156+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn the **MERN Stack**, **MongoDB** is the primary database because it stores data as BSON (Binary JSON) documents that map 1-to-1 to JavaScript objects without requiring rigid relational table migrations or expensive multi-table joins at massive scale.\n\n### 💡 Singleton Database Connection Pattern (`mongoConnect` & `getDB`)\n* **Bad Practice:** Calling `MongoClient.connect()` inside every single model method opens a brand-new TCP/TLS connection to MongoDB Atlas on every request, adding huge latency and exhausting connection limits!\n* **Best Practice (`utils/databaseUtil.js`):**\n  1. Connect to MongoDB Atlas **once** when the Express server boots (`mongoConnect(callback)` inside `app.js` before `app.listen()`).\n  2. Cache the connected database instance in a private module variable `let _db;`.\n  3. Export a synchronous getter **`getDB()`** that returns `_db` (or throws an error if `_db` is not initialized) so any model can immediately run `getDB().collection("homes")`.\n\n### 💡 Native MongoDB Driver CRUD & The `ObjectId` Trap\n* **Create (`insertOne`):** `db.collection("homes").insertOne(this)` — automatically generates a unique `_id: new ObjectId("...")` on the document.\n* **Read All (`find().toArray()`):** `db.collection("homes").find()` returns a **Cursor** (stream pointer), NOT an array! Chain **`.toArray()`** to get the Promise of all documents.\n* **Read Single by `_id` (`new ObjectId(String(homeId))`):**\n  * **Crucial Trap:** In MongoDB, `_id` is stored as a special **`ObjectId`** object, NOT a plain string! Querying `{ _id: homeId }` with a string returns `null`.\n  * **Fix:** Import `{ ObjectId } = require("mongodb")` and query **`{ _id: new ObjectId(String(homeId)) }`** followed by **`.next()`** (or use `findOne()`).\n* **Update (`updateOne` with `$set`):** `db.collection("homes").updateOne({ _id: new ObjectId(String(this._id)) }, { $set: updateFields })`.\n* **Delete (`deleteOne`):** `db.collection("homes").deleteOne({ _id: new ObjectId(String(homeId)) })`.\n\n### 🛠️ Singleton `databaseUtil.js` & Native MongoDB Model CRUD\n```javascript\n// File: utils/databaseUtil.js\nconst mongodb = require("mongodb");\nconst MongoClient = mongodb.MongoClient;\n\nconst MONGO_URL = "mongodb+srv://<username>:<password>@cluster0.mongodb.net/?retryWrites=true&w=majority";\nlet _db;\n\nconst mongoConnect = (callback) => {\n  MongoClient.connect(MONGO_URL)\n    .then((client) => {\n      console.log("Connected to MongoDB Atlas!");\n      _db = client.db("airbnb");\n      callback();\n    })\n    .catch((err) => {\n      console.log("Error while connecting to Mongo:", err);\n    });\n};\n\nconst getDB = () => {\n  if (!_db) {\n    throw new Error("Mongo not connected!");\n  }\n  return _db;\n};\n\nexports.mongoConnect = mongoConnect;\nexports.getDB = getDB;\n\n// File: models/home.js (Native MongoDB Driver)\nconst { ObjectId } = require("mongodb");\nconst { getDB } = require("../utils/databaseUtil");\n\nmodule.exports = class Home {\n  static findById(homeId) {\n    const db = getDB();\n    return db\n      .collection("homes")\n      .find({ _id: new ObjectId(String(homeId)) })\n      .next();\n  }\n};\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/drivers/node/current/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/drivers/node/current/", "title": "MongoDB Official Docs — Node.js Driver Quick Start & CRUD Operations"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfgnVPB4muo", "title": "Lecture 16: Introduction to MongoDB | NodeJS Complete Course in Hindi", "duration": "90m", "creator_name": "Complete Coding by Prashant Sir", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir"}]	[{"id": "node-lec-17-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Why do we initialize MongoClient.connect() once in app.js and cache _db via getDB() instead of connecting inside every model query?", "title": "Q1: Why do we initialize MongoClient.connect() once in app.js and cache _db via getDB() instead of connecting inside every model query?", "answer": "Connecting to a cloud database like MongoDB Atlas involves DNS resolution, TCP handshake, TLS encryption negotiation, and SCRAM authentication—which can take 200–500ms! If you called MongoClient.connect() on every HTTP request, every API call would suffer huge latency and you would quickly hit Atlas's maximum concurrent connection limit. Connecting once at server startup and sharing the pooled _db reference via getDB() allows queries to execute in a few milliseconds.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Connecting to a cloud database like MongoDB Atlas involves DNS resolution, TCP handshake, TLS encryption negotiation, and SCRAM authentication—which can take 200–500ms! If you called MongoClient.connect() on every HTTP request, every API call would suffer huge latency and you would quickly hit Atlas's maximum concurrent connection limit. Connecting once at server startup and sharing the pooled _db reference via getDB() allows queries to execute in a few milliseconds."}, {"id": "node-lec-17-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why does db.collection(\\"homes\\").find({ _id: req.params.homeId }) return null even when a document with that ID exists in MongoDB?", "title": "Q2: Why does db.collection(\\"homes\\").find({ _id: req.params.homeId }) return null even when a document with that ID exists in MongoDB?", "answer": "Because req.params.homeId is a plain String (e.g., \\"6738a...\\"), whereas MongoDB stores document primary keys (_id) as a 12-byte binary BSON ObjectId type! In MongoDB, a String never equals an ObjectId. You must wrap the string using new ObjectId(String(homeId)) from the mongodb package when querying, updating, or deleting by _id.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Because req.params.homeId is a plain String (e.g., \\"6738a...\\"), whereas MongoDB stores document primary keys (_id) as a 12-byte binary BSON ObjectId type! In MongoDB, a String never equals an ObjectId. You must wrap the string using new ObjectId(String(homeId)) from the mongodb package when querying, updating, or deleting by _id."}, {"id": "node-lec-17-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What does db.collection(\\"homes\\").find() return in the native MongoDB driver, and why do we call .toArray() or .next() on it?", "title": "Q3: What does db.collection(\\"homes\\").find() return in the native MongoDB driver, and why do we call .toArray() or .next() on it?", "answer": "collection.find() does NOT immediately return an array of documents—it returns a MongoDB Cursor object (a pointer to the result set on the server). If a collection has 1 million documents, returning a Cursor allows you to iterate or batch-stream results without crashing Node's RAM. Calling .toArray() instructs the cursor to fetch all matching documents into a JS array Promise, while .next() fetches only the first matching document from the cursor.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "collection.find() does NOT immediately return an array of documents—it returns a MongoDB Cursor object (a pointer to the result set on the server). If a collection has 1 million documents, returning a Cursor allows you to iterate or batch-stream results without crashing Node's RAM. Calling .toArray() instructs the cursor to fetch all matching documents into a JS array Promise, while .next() fetches only the first matching document from the cursor."}, {"id": "node-lec-17-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why do we only start app.listen(PORT) inside the callback of mongoConnect(() => { app.listen(PORT); })?", "title": "Q4: Why do we only start app.listen(PORT) inside the callback of mongoConnect(() => { app.listen(PORT); })?", "answer": "If app.listen(PORT) started before MongoClient.connect() finished establishing the database connection, early incoming user requests would call getDB() while _db is still undefined and crash with \\"Error: Mongo not connected!\\". Starting the HTTP server inside the mongoConnect callback guarantees the database is ready before accepting traffic.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "If app.listen(PORT) started before MongoClient.connect() finished establishing the database connection, early incoming user requests would call getDB() while _db is still undefined and crash with \\"Error: Mongo not connected!\\". Starting the HTTP server inside the mongoConnect callback guarantees the database is ready before accepting traffic."}, {"id": "node-lec-17-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: When updating a document with updateOne({ _id: new ObjectId(String(this._id)) }, { $set: updateFields }), why shouldn't _id be included inside the $set object?", "title": "Q5: When updating a document with updateOne({ _id: new ObjectId(String(this._id)) }, { $set: updateFields }), why shouldn't _id be included inside the $set object?", "answer": "In MongoDB, the primary key _id of an existing document is strictly immutable. Attempting to overwrite or modify _id inside a $set update operation (especially if this._id is a string while the stored _id is an ObjectId) throws an immutable field error.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In MongoDB, the primary key _id of an existing document is strictly immutable. Attempting to overwrite or modify _id inside a $set update operation (especially if this._id is a string while the stored _id is an ObjectId) throws an immutable field error."}, {"id": "node-lec-17-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why do NoSQL document databases like MongoDB scale horizontally (Sharding) more easily than traditional normalized SQL databases?", "title": "Q6: Why do NoSQL document databases like MongoDB scale horizontally (Sharding) more easily than traditional normalized SQL databases?", "answer": "In a normalized SQL database, a single entity's data is split across 5–10 related tables, requiring cross-table JOINs. If those tables are split across different physical servers (shards), cross-network joins become slow and complex. MongoDB co-locates related data inside self-contained BSON documents, allowing collections to be partitioned (sharded) across many servers with fast single-document reads.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "In a normalized SQL database, a single entity's data is split across 5–10 related tables, requiring cross-table JOINs. If those tables are split across different physical servers (shards), cross-network joins become slow and complex. MongoDB co-locates related data inside self-contained BSON documents, allowing collections to be partitioned (sharded) across many servers with fast single-document reads."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn the **MERN Stack**, **MongoDB** is the primary database because it stores data as BSON (Binary JSON) documents that map 1-to-1 to JavaScript objects without requiring rigid relational table migrations or expensive multi-table joins at massive scale.\n\n### 💡 Singleton Database Connection Pattern (`mongoConnect` & `getDB`)\n* **Bad Practice:** Calling `MongoClient.connect()` inside every single model method opens a brand-new TCP/TLS connection to MongoDB Atlas on every request, adding huge latency and exhausting connection limits!\n* **Best Practice (`utils/databaseUtil.js`):**\n  1. Connect to MongoDB Atlas **once** when the Express server boots (`mongoConnect(callback)` inside `app.js` before `app.listen()`).\n  2. Cache the connected database instance in a private module variable `let _db;`.\n  3. Export a synchronous getter **`getDB()`** that returns `_db` (or throws an error if `_db` is not initialized) so any model can immediately run `getDB().collection("homes")`.\n\n### 💡 Native MongoDB Driver CRUD & The `ObjectId` Trap\n* **Create (`insertOne`):** `db.collection("homes").insertOne(this)` — automatically generates a unique `_id: new ObjectId("...")` on the document.\n* **Read All (`find().toArray()`):** `db.collection("homes").find()` returns a **Cursor** (stream pointer), NOT an array! Chain **`.toArray()`** to get the Promise of all documents.\n* **Read Single by `_id` (`new ObjectId(String(homeId))`):**\n  * **Crucial Trap:** In MongoDB, `_id` is stored as a special **`ObjectId`** object, NOT a plain string! Querying `{ _id: homeId }` with a string returns `null`.\n  * **Fix:** Import `{ ObjectId } = require("mongodb")` and query **`{ _id: new ObjectId(String(homeId)) }`** followed by **`.next()`** (or use `findOne()`).\n* **Update (`updateOne` with `$set`):** `db.collection("homes").updateOne({ _id: new ObjectId(String(this._id)) }, { $set: updateFields })`.\n* **Delete (`deleteOne`):** `db.collection("homes").deleteOne({ _id: new ObjectId(String(homeId)) })`.\n\n### 🛠️ Singleton `databaseUtil.js` & Native MongoDB Model CRUD\n```javascript\n// File: utils/databaseUtil.js\nconst mongodb = require("mongodb");\nconst MongoClient = mongodb.MongoClient;\n\nconst MONGO_URL = "mongodb+srv://<username>:<password>@cluster0.mongodb.net/?retryWrites=true&w=majority";\nlet _db;\n\nconst mongoConnect = (callback) => {\n  MongoClient.connect(MONGO_URL)\n    .then((client) => {\n      console.log("Connected to MongoDB Atlas!");\n      _db = client.db("airbnb");\n      callback();\n    })\n    .catch((err) => {\n      console.log("Error while connecting to Mongo:", err);\n    });\n};\n\nconst getDB = () => {\n  if (!_db) {\n    throw new Error("Mongo not connected!");\n  }\n  return _db;\n};\n\nexports.mongoConnect = mongoConnect;\nexports.getDB = getDB;\n\n// File: models/home.js (Native MongoDB Driver)\nconst { ObjectId } = require("mongodb");\nconst { getDB } = require("../utils/databaseUtil");\n\nmodule.exports = class Home {\n  static findById(homeId) {\n    const db = getDB();\n    return db\n      .collection("homes")\n      .find({ _id: new ObjectId(String(homeId)) })\n      .next();\n  }\n};\n```	Complete Coding by Prashant Sir	https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir	24	Lecture 17: MongoDB Integration in Node.js — MongoDB Atlas Cloud, Official mongodb Driver, ObjectId & Document CRUD	Provision a cloud database cluster on MongoDB Atlas, connect your Express server using a singleton getDB() utility, and execute native CRUD operations with ObjectId, insertOne, find, updateOne, and deleteOne.	20	Easy	\N	17	[{"id": "node-lec-17-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Why do we initialize MongoClient.connect() once in app.js and cache _db via getDB() instead of connecting inside every model query?", "title": "Q1: Why do we initialize MongoClient.connect() once in app.js and cache _db via getDB() instead of connecting inside every model query?", "answer": "Connecting to a cloud database like MongoDB Atlas involves DNS resolution, TCP handshake, TLS encryption negotiation, and SCRAM authentication—which can take 200–500ms! If you called MongoClient.connect() on every HTTP request, every API call would suffer huge latency and you would quickly hit Atlas's maximum concurrent connection limit. Connecting once at server startup and sharing the pooled _db reference via getDB() allows queries to execute in a few milliseconds.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Connecting to a cloud database like MongoDB Atlas involves DNS resolution, TCP handshake, TLS encryption negotiation, and SCRAM authentication—which can take 200–500ms! If you called MongoClient.connect() on every HTTP request, every API call would suffer huge latency and you would quickly hit Atlas's maximum concurrent connection limit. Connecting once at server startup and sharing the pooled _db reference via getDB() allows queries to execute in a few milliseconds."}, {"id": "node-lec-17-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why does db.collection(\\"homes\\").find({ _id: req.params.homeId }) return null even when a document with that ID exists in MongoDB?", "title": "Q2: Why does db.collection(\\"homes\\").find({ _id: req.params.homeId }) return null even when a document with that ID exists in MongoDB?", "answer": "Because req.params.homeId is a plain String (e.g., \\"6738a...\\"), whereas MongoDB stores document primary keys (_id) as a 12-byte binary BSON ObjectId type! In MongoDB, a String never equals an ObjectId. You must wrap the string using new ObjectId(String(homeId)) from the mongodb package when querying, updating, or deleting by _id.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Because req.params.homeId is a plain String (e.g., \\"6738a...\\"), whereas MongoDB stores document primary keys (_id) as a 12-byte binary BSON ObjectId type! In MongoDB, a String never equals an ObjectId. You must wrap the string using new ObjectId(String(homeId)) from the mongodb package when querying, updating, or deleting by _id."}, {"id": "node-lec-17-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What does db.collection(\\"homes\\").find() return in the native MongoDB driver, and why do we call .toArray() or .next() on it?", "title": "Q3: What does db.collection(\\"homes\\").find() return in the native MongoDB driver, and why do we call .toArray() or .next() on it?", "answer": "collection.find() does NOT immediately return an array of documents—it returns a MongoDB Cursor object (a pointer to the result set on the server). If a collection has 1 million documents, returning a Cursor allows you to iterate or batch-stream results without crashing Node's RAM. Calling .toArray() instructs the cursor to fetch all matching documents into a JS array Promise, while .next() fetches only the first matching document from the cursor.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "collection.find() does NOT immediately return an array of documents—it returns a MongoDB Cursor object (a pointer to the result set on the server). If a collection has 1 million documents, returning a Cursor allows you to iterate or batch-stream results without crashing Node's RAM. Calling .toArray() instructs the cursor to fetch all matching documents into a JS array Promise, while .next() fetches only the first matching document from the cursor."}, {"id": "node-lec-17-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why do we only start app.listen(PORT) inside the callback of mongoConnect(() => { app.listen(PORT); })?", "title": "Q4: Why do we only start app.listen(PORT) inside the callback of mongoConnect(() => { app.listen(PORT); })?", "answer": "If app.listen(PORT) started before MongoClient.connect() finished establishing the database connection, early incoming user requests would call getDB() while _db is still undefined and crash with \\"Error: Mongo not connected!\\". Starting the HTTP server inside the mongoConnect callback guarantees the database is ready before accepting traffic.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "If app.listen(PORT) started before MongoClient.connect() finished establishing the database connection, early incoming user requests would call getDB() while _db is still undefined and crash with \\"Error: Mongo not connected!\\". Starting the HTTP server inside the mongoConnect callback guarantees the database is ready before accepting traffic."}, {"id": "node-lec-17-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: When updating a document with updateOne({ _id: new ObjectId(String(this._id)) }, { $set: updateFields }), why shouldn't _id be included inside the $set object?", "title": "Q5: When updating a document with updateOne({ _id: new ObjectId(String(this._id)) }, { $set: updateFields }), why shouldn't _id be included inside the $set object?", "answer": "In MongoDB, the primary key _id of an existing document is strictly immutable. Attempting to overwrite or modify _id inside a $set update operation (especially if this._id is a string while the stored _id is an ObjectId) throws an immutable field error.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In MongoDB, the primary key _id of an existing document is strictly immutable. Attempting to overwrite or modify _id inside a $set update operation (especially if this._id is a string while the stored _id is an ObjectId) throws an immutable field error."}, {"id": "node-lec-17-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why do NoSQL document databases like MongoDB scale horizontally (Sharding) more easily than traditional normalized SQL databases?", "title": "Q6: Why do NoSQL document databases like MongoDB scale horizontally (Sharding) more easily than traditional normalized SQL databases?", "answer": "In a normalized SQL database, a single entity's data is split across 5–10 related tables, requiring cross-table JOINs. If those tables are split across different physical servers (shards), cross-network joins become slow and complex. MongoDB co-locates related data inside self-contained BSON documents, allowing collections to be partitioned (sharded) across many servers with fast single-document reads.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "In a normalized SQL database, a single entity's data is split across 5–10 related tables, requiring cross-table JOINs. If those tables are split across different physical servers (shards), cross-network joins become slow and complex. MongoDB co-locates related data inside self-contained BSON documents, allowing collections to be partitioned (sharded) across many servers with fast single-document reads."}]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/drivers/node/current/	\N	\N	\N
955	Contiguous Subarray Partitioning: Painter's Partition & Split Array Largest Sum	dsa-painters-partition-and-split-array-largest-sum	Solve Painter's Partition and Split Array Largest Sum using the unified contiguous allocation binary search template.	2026-10-03 00:19:12.433313+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Contiguous Subarray Partitioning: Painter's Partition & Split Array Largest Sum\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=thUd_WJn6wk", "title": "Lecture 63: Painter's Partition & Split Array Largest Sum", "duration": "28m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/split-array-largest-sum/", "title": "Split Array Largest Sum", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Divide array into k subarrays such that the maximum subarray sum is minimized."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-number-of-days-to-make-m-bouquets/", "title": "Minimum Number of Days to Make m Bouquets", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Binary search on day answer space to assemble m bouquets from adjacent flowers."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/the-painters-partition-problem1535/1", "title": "The Painter's Partition Problem-II", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find the minimum time to paint all boards using k painters working continuously."}]	Module 13: Binary Search on Answer Space & Monotonic Predicate Functions	### 📌 Topic Overview\nBoth **Painter's Partition** (distributing `n` contiguous boards among `k` painters) and **Split Array Largest Sum** (splitting an array into `k` contiguous subarrays) share the **exact same underlying invariant as Book Allocation**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Concept Equivalence:**\n  - Books `arr[i]` → Board lengths `boards[i]` → Array elements `nums[i]`.\n  - Students `m` → Painters `k` → Subarrays `k`.\n  - Max pages per student → Max time per painter → Largest subarray sum.\n- **Search Space:**\n  - `low = max(boards)` (slowest single unit that must be processed).\n  - `high = sum(boards)` (all units painted by a single painter).\n- **Complexity:**\n  - `O(N * log2(sum - max + 1))` Time and `O(1)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Unified Partition Blueprint\n```cpp\nint countPartitions(vector<int> &a, int maxSum) {\n    int partitions = 1, currentSum = 0;\n    for (int x : a) {\n        if (currentSum + x <= maxSum) {\n            currentSum += x;\n        } else {\n            partitions++;\n            currentSum = x;\n        }\n    }\n    return partitions;\n}\n\nint splitArray(vector<int> &nums, int k) {\n    int low = *max_element(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n    int high = accumulate(nums.begin(), nums.end(), 0);\n\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (countPartitions(nums, mid) > k) {\n            low = mid + 1;\n        } else {\n            high = mid - 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return low;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22113	Lecture 28: Conditional Aggregation Operators — $cond, $ifNull & Multi-Branch $switch	mongo-lecture-28-conditional-aggregation-operators-cond-ifnull-switch	Build dynamic decision logic, discount calculations, null fallbacks, and multi-tier grading systems inside aggregation pipelines using $cond, $ifNull, and $switch.	2026-10-06 19:52:12.616986+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMongoDB provides three **Conditional Aggregation Operators** to evaluate branching logic inside `$project` and `$addFields`:\n* **`$cond`** for single `if / then / else` conditions.\n* **`$ifNull`** for substituting missing or `null` fields with fallback values.\n* **`$switch`** for multi-branch `case / then / default` grading or tiering logic.\n\n### 💡 Detailed Breakdown of All 3 Conditional Operators\n* **`$cond: { if: <booleanExpr>, then: <trueValue>, else: <falseValue> }`:**\n  * Evaluates `if`. When `true`, returns `then`; when `false`, returns `else`.\n  * Can also take a boolean field directly (e.g., `if: "$discounted"`) and perform math inside `then` (such as applying a 10% discount via `{ $multiply: ["$price", 0.9 }`).\n* **`$ifNull: [ <fieldExpr>, <replacementValueIfNull> `:**\n  * Evaluates `<fieldExpr>`. If the field exists and is not `null`, returns its value. If the field is `null` or completely missing from the document, returns `<replacementValueIfNull>` (e.g., `"No Email"`).\n* **`$switch: { branches: [ { case: <expr1>, then: <val1> }, ... , default: <fallbackVal> }`:**\n  * Evaluates an ordered array of `branches`. Returns the `then` value of the **first** branch whose `case` evaluates to `true`. If no branch matches, returns `default`.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical $cond, $ifNull & $switch Examples\n```javascript\n// 1. $cond: Categorize products & compute 10% discounted Final Price\ndb.products.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      originalPrice: "$price",\n      priceCategory: {\n        $cond: {\n          if: { $gt: ["$price", 1000 },\n          then: "Expensive",\n          else: "Affordable"\n        }\n      },\n      finalPrice: {\n        $cond: {\n          if: "$discounted",\n          then: { $multiply: ["$price", 0.9 },\n          else: "$price"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 2. $ifNull: Provide "No Email" fallback when email is null or missing\ndb.users.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      email: { $ifNull: ["$email", "No Email" }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 3. $switch: Multi-tier student division grading based on percentage\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      percentage: 1,\n      grade: {\n        $switch: {\n          branches: [\n            { case: { $gte: ["$percentage", 80 }, then: "Merit" },\n            { case: { $gte: ["$percentage", 60 }, then: "First Division" },\n            { case: { $gte: ["$percentage", 45 }, then: "Second Division" },\n            { case: { $gte: ["$percentage", 33 }, then: "Third Division" }\n          ,\n          default: "Fail"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/#conditional-expression-operators", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/#conditional-expression-operators", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 28: Conditional Aggregation Operators"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EOE8FNRC6gU", "title": "MongoDb Conditional Operators Tutorial in Hindi Urdu", "duration": "23m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[{"id": "mongo-28-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3737e", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3737e", "name": "Problem #343: Products in category Health", "title": "Problem #343: Products in category Health", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "343", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where category is \\"Health\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3737e"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3737b", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3737b", "name": "Problem #344: Products in category Toys", "title": "Problem #344: Products in category Toys", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "344", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where category is \\"Toys\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3737b"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-3", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37378", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37378", "name": "Problem #345: Products in category Sports", "title": "Problem #345: Products in category Sports", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "345", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where category is \\"Sports\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37378"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-4", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37375", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37375", "name": "Problem #346: Products in category Home", "title": "Problem #346: Products in category Home", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "346", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where category is \\"Home\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37375"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-5", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37372", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37372", "name": "Problem #347: Products in category Books", "title": "Problem #347: Products in category Books", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "347", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where category is \\"Books\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37372"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-6", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3736f", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3736f", "name": "Problem #348: Products in category Clothing", "title": "Problem #348: Products in category Clothing", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "348", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where category is \\"Clothing\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3736f"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-7", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3736c", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3736c", "name": "Problem #349: Products in category Electronics", "title": "Problem #349: Products in category Electronics", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "349", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where category is \\"Electronics\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3736c"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-8", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37366", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37366", "name": "Problem #351: And: products category Electronics and inStock", "title": "Problem #351: And: products category Electronics and inStock", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "351", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return products where **category** is Electronics and **inStock** is true.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37366"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-9", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37324", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37324", "name": "Problem #373: Find customers with tier silver or bronze", "title": "Problem #373: Find customers with tier silver or bronze", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "373", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return customers where **tier** is silver or bronze.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37324"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-10", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db372fa", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db372fa", "name": "Problem #387: Find products with category Clothing or Sports", "title": "Problem #387: Find products with category Clothing or Sports", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "387", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where **category** is Clothing or Sports.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db372fa"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMongoDB provides three **Conditional Aggregation Operators** to evaluate branching logic inside `$project` and `$addFields`:\n* **`$cond`** for single `if / then / else` conditions.\n* **`$ifNull`** for substituting missing or `null` fields with fallback values.\n* **`$switch`** for multi-branch `case / then / default` grading or tiering logic.\n\n### 💡 Detailed Breakdown of All 3 Conditional Operators\n* **`$cond: { if: <booleanExpr>, then: <trueValue>, else: <falseValue> }`:**\n  * Evaluates `if`. When `true`, returns `then`; when `false`, returns `else`.\n  * Can also take a boolean field directly (e.g., `if: "$discounted"`) and perform math inside `then` (such as applying a 10% discount via `{ $multiply: ["$price", 0.9 }`).\n* **`$ifNull: [ <fieldExpr>, <replacementValueIfNull> `:**\n  * Evaluates `<fieldExpr>`. If the field exists and is not `null`, returns its value. If the field is `null` or completely missing from the document, returns `<replacementValueIfNull>` (e.g., `"No Email"`).\n* **`$switch: { branches: [ { case: <expr1>, then: <val1> }, ... , default: <fallbackVal> }`:**\n  * Evaluates an ordered array of `branches`. Returns the `then` value of the **first** branch whose `case` evaluates to `true`. If no branch matches, returns `default`.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical $cond, $ifNull & $switch Examples\n```javascript\n// 1. $cond: Categorize products & compute 10% discounted Final Price\ndb.products.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      originalPrice: "$price",\n      priceCategory: {\n        $cond: {\n          if: { $gt: ["$price", 1000 },\n          then: "Expensive",\n          else: "Affordable"\n        }\n      },\n      finalPrice: {\n        $cond: {\n          if: "$discounted",\n          then: { $multiply: ["$price", 0.9 },\n          else: "$price"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 2. $ifNull: Provide "No Email" fallback when email is null or missing\ndb.users.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      email: { $ifNull: ["$email", "No Email" }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 3. $switch: Multi-tier student division grading based on percentage\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      percentage: 1,\n      grade: {\n        $switch: {\n          branches: [\n            { case: { $gte: ["$percentage", 80 }, then: "Merit" },\n            { case: { $gte: ["$percentage", 60 }, then: "First Division" },\n            { case: { $gte: ["$percentage", 45 }, then: "Second Division" },\n            { case: { $gte: ["$percentage", 33 }, then: "Third Division" }\n          ,\n          default: "Fail"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n```	Yahoo Baba	https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba	655	Lecture 28: Conditional Aggregation Operators — $cond, $ifNull & Multi-Branch $switch	Build dynamic decision logic, discount calculations, null fallbacks, and multi-tier grading systems inside aggregation pipelines using $cond, $ifNull, and $switch.	20	Easy	\N	28	[{"id": "mongo-28-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3737e", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3737e", "name": "Problem #343: Products in category Health", "title": "Problem #343: Products in category Health", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "343", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where category is \\"Health\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3737e"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3737b", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3737b", "name": "Problem #344: Products in category Toys", "title": "Problem #344: Products in category Toys", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "344", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where category is \\"Toys\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3737b"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-3", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37378", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37378", "name": "Problem #345: Products in category Sports", "title": "Problem #345: Products in category Sports", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "345", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where category is \\"Sports\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37378"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-4", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37375", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37375", "name": "Problem #346: Products in category Home", "title": "Problem #346: Products in category Home", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "346", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where category is \\"Home\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37375"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-5", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37372", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37372", "name": "Problem #347: Products in category Books", "title": "Problem #347: Products in category Books", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "347", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where category is \\"Books\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37372"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-6", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3736f", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3736f", "name": "Problem #348: Products in category Clothing", "title": "Problem #348: Products in category Clothing", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "348", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where category is \\"Clothing\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3736f"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-7", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3736c", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3736c", "name": "Problem #349: Products in category Electronics", "title": "Problem #349: Products in category Electronics", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "349", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where category is \\"Electronics\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3736c"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-8", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37366", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37366", "name": "Problem #351: And: products category Electronics and inStock", "title": "Problem #351: And: products category Electronics and inStock", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "351", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return products where **category** is Electronics and **inStock** is true.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37366"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-9", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37324", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37324", "name": "Problem #373: Find customers with tier silver or bronze", "title": "Problem #373: Find customers with tier silver or bronze", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "373", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return customers where **tier** is silver or bronze.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37324"}, {"id": "mongo-28-prob-10", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db372fa", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db372fa", "name": "Problem #387: Find products with category Clothing or Sports", "title": "Problem #387: Find products with category Clothing or Sports", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "387", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where **category** is Clothing or Sports.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db372fa"}]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/ope