uestion": "How do you achieve lazy initialization in `useState` for expensive computations?", "explanation": "Passing an anonymous function ensures the expensive computation is only executed once during the initial render.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "By passing a callback function: `useState(() => computeExpensiveValue())`"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["It renders the component immediately after each call", "It ignores all but the first call", "It batches the state updates and triggers a single re-render", "It throws an infinite loop warning"], "question": "How does React handle multiple `setState` calls in the same synchronous block?", "explanation": "React 18+ automatically batches state updates, combining multiple `setState` calls into a single re-render for better performance.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "It batches the state updates and triggers a single re-render"}]	\N	> **Architectural Deep Dive:** This lecture covers the strict technical nuances, reconciliation behavior, and performance implications of using useState in production environments. Pay special attention to concurrent rendering behaviors in React 18+.	Web Dev Simplified	https://www.youtube.com/@WebDevSimplified	\N	Learn useState In 15 Minutes	Deep dive architectural lecture focusing on the mechanics, use cases, and anti-patterns of the useState hook.	15	Easy	Core Hooks & API	1	[]	\N	15	https://youtu.be/O6P86uwfdR0	Learn useState In 15 Minutes
