 External Stylesheets
22122	Lecture 4: Operators, Type Coercion, Conditionals & Loops — == vs. ===, 0.1 + 0.2, Short-Circuiting & Bitwise	js-lecture-4-operators-type-coercion-conditionals-loops	Master arithmetic, assignment, comparison (== vs. ===), logical short-circuiting (&&, ||), bitwise operators, IEEE-754 floating-point precision (0.1 + 0.2), and control flow loops.	2026-10-06 21:14:25.4193+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nJavaScript operators behave differently from statically typed languages whenever operands have mismatched types due to **Implicit Type Coercion**. Mastering strict vs. loose equality, floating-point binary representation, and logical short-circuit evaluation is essential for technical interviews.\n\n### 💡 Loose Equality (`==`) vs. Strict Equality (`===`)\n* **Loose Equality (`==`):** Performs **Type Coercion** before comparing. If you compare `5 == "5"`, JS converts `"5"` to the number `5` and returns `true`.\n* **Strict Equality (`===`):** Checks **both Data Type and Value** without coercion. `5 === "5"` immediately returns `false`.\n* **Special Equality Rules in Interviews:**\n  * `null == undefined` is **`true`**, but `null === undefined` is **`false`**.\n  * `null` only loosely equals `undefined` and `null` (so `null == 0` is **`false`**!), even though relational operators coerce `null` to `0` (`null >= 0` is **`true`**!).\n  * `NaN === NaN` and `NaN == NaN` are both **`false`**! Always use `Number.isNaN(val)` to check for `NaN`.\n\n### 💡 Why Does `0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3` Evaluate to `false`?\n* Computers store numbers in **base-2 (binary)**. Just as `1/3` is an infinite repeating decimal in base-10 (`0.33333...`), `0.1` (`1/10`) and `0.2` (`1/5`) are **infinite repeating fractions in binary** (`0.0001100110011...`).\n* Because 64-bit IEEE-754 floats truncate at 53 bits of precision, adding the binary approximations produces `0.30000000000000004`.\n* **Production Fix:** Store currency in integer smallest units (paise/cents) or compare with `Math.abs((0.1 + 0.2) - 0.3) < Number.EPSILON`.\n\n### 💡 Truthy/Falsy Values & Logical Short-Circuiting (`&&`, `||`, `!`)\n* **The 8 Falsy Values in JS:** `false`, `0`, `-0`, `0n`, `""` (empty string), `null`, `undefined`, and `NaN`. Everything else (including `"0"`, `[`, and `{}`) is **Truthy**.\n* **`&&` (Logical AND):** Evaluates left-to-right and returns the **first falsy operand**, or the **last operand** if all are truthy (e.g., `"Rohit" && "Mohit"` returns `"Mohit"`; `0 && "Mohit"` returns `0`).\n* **`||` (Logical OR):** Evaluates left-to-right and returns the **first truthy operand**, or the **last operand** if all are falsy (e.g., `"" || "Default"` returns `"Default"`).\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Coercion, Floating-Point Precision & Loops\n```javascript\n// 1. Loose (==) vs. Strict (===) Equality & null/NaN Quirks\nconsole.log(10 == "10");          // true (string "10" coerced to number 10)\nconsole.log(10 === "10");         // false (different types)\nconsole.log(null == undefined);   // true\nconsole.log(null === undefined);  // false\nconsole.log(null == 0);           // false (null only loosely equals undefined)\nconsole.log(null >= 0);           // true  (relational >= coerces null to 0!)\nconsole.log(NaN === NaN);         // false\n\n// 2. IEEE-754 Floating Point Precision Problem\nconsole.log(0.1 + 0.2);           // 0.30000000000000004\nconsole.log(0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3);   // false\nconsole.log(Number((0.1 + 0.2).toFixed(2)) === 0.3); // true\n\n// 3. Logical Short-Circuiting (Returns actual operand values, not just booleans!)\nconsole.log("Coder" && "Army");   // "Army" (first is truthy, returns second)\nconsole.log(0 && "Army");         // 0      (short-circuits on first falsy value)\nconsole.log("" || "Fallback");    // "Fallback" (returns first truthy value)\n\n// 4. Bitwise AND (&) vs. Bitwise OR (|) on binary bits\nconsole.log(2 & 3); // 2 (010 & 011 = 010)\nconsole.log(2 & 5); // 0 (010 & 101 = 000)\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Expressions_and_operators", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Expressions_and_operators", "title": "MDN Web Docs — Expressions and Operators"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dMQr2-T--4", "title": "JavaScript Conditionals, Loops & Operators Explained | Javascript Full Course #04", "duration": "68m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-4-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Why does 0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3 return false in JavaScript, and how do you handle financial calculations safely?", "title": "Q1: Why does 0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3 return false in JavaScript, and how do you handle financial calculations safely?", "answer": "JavaScript numbers follow the 64-bit IEEE-754 binary floating-point standard. In base-2, fractions whose denominators are not powers of 2 (such as 1/10 = 0.1 and 1/5 = 0.2) cannot be represented finitely—they become infinite repeating binary fractions (0.0001100110011...). When truncated to 53 mantissa bits and added together, the rounding error yields 0.30000000000000004. For financial systems, always store and compute amounts as integers in the smallest currency unit (e.g., paise or cents: 10 + 20 === 30) or use Decimal/BigInt libraries.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "JavaScript numbers follow the 64-bit IEEE-754 binary floating-point standard. In base-2, fractions whose denominators are not powers of 2 (such as 1/10 = 0.1 and 1/5 = 0.2) cannot be represented finitely—they become infinite repeating binary fractions (0.0001100110011...). When truncated to 53 mantissa bits and added together, the rounding error yields 0.30000000000000004. For financial systems, always store and compute amounts as integers in the smallest currency unit (e.g., paise or cents: 10 + 20 === 30) or use Decimal/BigInt libraries."}, {"id": "js-lec-4-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Explain why null > 0 is false, null == 0 is false, but null >= 0 is true!", "title": "Q2: Explain why null > 0 is false, null == 0 is false, but null >= 0 is true!", "answer": "Because the Equality check (==) and Relational Comparisons (> < >= <=) follow two completely different ECMAScript specification algorithms! Relational operators convert null to a number (ToNumber(null) -> 0), so null > 0 becomes 0 > 0 (false) and null >= 0 becomes 0 >= 0 (true). However, the Abstract Equality (==) specification explicitly states that null only equals null or undefined and never coerces null to a number, so null == 0 is false.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q2", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Because the Equality check (==) and Relational Comparisons (> < >= <=) follow two completely different ECMAScript specification algorithms! Relational operators convert null to a number (ToNumber(null) -> 0), so null > 0 becomes 0 > 0 (false) and null >= 0 becomes 0 >= 0 (true). However, the Abstract Equality (==) specification explicitly states that null only equals null or undefined and never coerces null to a number, so null == 0 is false."}, {"id": "js-lec-4-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is the output of console.log(\\"Rohit\\" && 42) and console.log(0 || \\"Default\\")? Why don't they return true or false?", "title": "Q3: What is the output of console.log(\\"Rohit\\" && 42) and console.log(0 || \\"Default\\")? Why don't they return true or false?", "answer": "In JavaScript, && and || are value-selector operators that return the actual evaluated operand value rather than coercing to a boolean. Logical AND (&&) returns the first falsy operand it encounters, or the final operand if all are truthy—so \\"Rohit\\" && 42 returns 42. Logical OR (||) returns the first truthy operand it encounters, or the final operand if all are falsy—so 0 || \\"Default\\" returns \\"Default\\".", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In JavaScript, && and || are value-selector operators that return the actual evaluated operand value rather than coercing to a boolean. Logical AND (&&) returns the first falsy operand it encounters, or the final operand if all are truthy—so \\"Rohit\\" && 42 returns 42. Logical OR (||) returns the first truthy operand it encounters, or the final operand if all are falsy—so 0 || \\"Default\\" returns \\"Default\\"."}, {"id": "js-lec-4-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: List all 8 Falsy values in JavaScript. Are empty arrays [] and empty objects {} truthy or falsy?", "title": "Q4: List all 8 Falsy values in JavaScript. Are empty arrays [] and empty objects {} truthy or falsy?", "answer": "The 8 falsy values in JavaScript are: false, 0, -0, 0n (BigInt zero), \\"\\" (empty string), null, undefined, and NaN. Empty arrays [] and empty objects {} are Object references, and every Object in JavaScript is strictly Truthy (so if ([]) executes the if-block). However, [] == false evaluates to true because loose equality coerces [] -> \\"\\" -> 0.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "The 8 falsy values in JavaScript are: false, 0, -0, 0n (BigInt zero), \\"\\" (empty string), null, undefined, and NaN. Empty arrays [] and empty objects {} are Object references, and every Object in JavaScript is strictly Truthy (so if ([]) executes the if-block). However, [] == false evaluates to true because loose equality coerces [] -> \\"\\" -> 0."}, {"id": "js-lec-4-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Why is NaN === NaN false, and what is the difference between isNaN() and Number.isNaN()?", "title": "Q5: Why is NaN === NaN false, and what is the difference between isNaN() and Number.isNaN()?", "answer": "Per the IEEE-754 specification, NaN (\\"Not-a-Number\\") represents an invalid mathematical result (like 0/0 or Number(\\"abc\\")), and two invalid results are never equal to each other, so NaN === NaN is false. The global isNaN(\\"hello\\") coerces its argument to a number first and returns true for strings, whereas Number.isNaN(val) does NOT coerce the value and returns true ONLY if the value is currently of type number and its value is NaN.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Per the IEEE-754 specification, NaN (\\"Not-a-Number\\") represents an invalid mathematical result (like 0/0 or Number(\\"abc\\")), and two invalid results are never equal to each other, so NaN === NaN is false. The global isNaN(\\"hello\\") coerces its argument to a number first and returns true for strings, whereas Number.isNaN(val) does NOT coerce the value and returns true ONLY if the value is currently of type number and its value is NaN."}, {"id": "js-lec-4-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: What is the difference between Logical AND (&&) and Bitwise AND (&)? Why does 2 & 3 equal 2 while 2 & 5 equals 0?", "title": "Q6: What is the difference between Logical AND (&&) and Bitwise AND (&)? Why does 2 & 3 equal 2 while 2 & 5 equals 0?", "answer": "Logical && operates on truthiness of whole expressions and short-circuits. Bitwise & converts both numbers to 32-bit binary integers and compares them bit-by-bit (1 & 1 = 1, otherwise 0). Since 2 is 010 in binary and 3 is 011, 010 & 011 = 010 (2). Since 5 is 101 in binary, 010 & 101 = 000 (0).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Logical && operates on truthiness of whole expressions and short-circuits. Bitwise & converts both numbers to 32-bit binary integers and compares them bit-by-bit (1 & 1 = 1, otherwise 0). Since 2 is 010 in binary and 3 is 011, 010 & 011 = 010 (2). Since 5 is 101 in binary, 010 & 101 = 000 (0)."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nJavaScript operators behave differently from statically typed languages whenever operands have mismatched types due to **Implicit Type Coercion**. Mastering strict vs. loose equality, floating-point binary representation, and logical short-circuit evaluation is essential for technical interviews.\n\n### 💡 Loose Equality (`==`) vs. Strict Equality (`===`)\n* **Loose Equality (`==`):** Performs **Type Coercion** before comparing. If you compare `5 == "5"`, JS converts `"5"` to the number `5` and returns `true`.\n* **Strict Equality (`===`):** Checks **both Data Type and Value** without coercion. `5 === "5"` immediately returns `false`.\n* **Special Equality Rules in Interviews:**\n  * `null == undefined` is **`true`**, but `null === undefined` is **`false`**.\n  * `null` only loosely equals `undefined` and `null` (so `null == 0` is **`false`**!), even though relational operators coerce `null` to `0` (`null >= 0` is **`true`**!).\n  * `NaN === NaN` and `NaN == NaN` are both **`false`**! Always use `Number.isNaN(val)` to check for `NaN`.\n\n### 💡 Why Does `0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3` Evaluate to `false`?\n* Computers store numbers in **base-2 (binary)**. Just as `1/3` is an infinite repeating decimal in base-10 (`0.33333...`), `0.1` (`1/10`) and `0.2` (`1/5`) are **infinite repeating fractions in binary** (`0.0001100110011...`).\n* Because 64-bit IEEE-754 floats truncate at 53 bits of precision, adding the binary approximations produces `0.30000000000000004`.\n* **Production Fix:** Store currency in integer smallest units (paise/cents) or compare with `Math.abs((0.1 + 0.2) - 0.3) < Number.EPSILON`.\n\n### 💡 Truthy/Falsy Values & Logical Short-Circuiting (`&&`, `||`, `!`)\n* **The 8 Falsy Values in JS:** `false`, `0`, `-0`, `0n`, `""` (empty string), `null`, `undefined`, and `NaN`. Everything else (including `"0"`, `[`, and `{}`) is **Truthy**.\n* **`&&` (Logical AND):** Evaluates left-to-right and returns the **first falsy operand**, or the **last operand** if all are truthy (e.g., `"Rohit" && "Mohit"` returns `"Mohit"`; `0 && "Mohit"` returns `0`).\n* **`||` (Logical OR):** Evaluates left-to-right and returns the **first truthy operand**, or the **last operand** if all are falsy (e.g., `"" || "Default"` returns `"Default"`).\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Coercion, Floating-Point Precision & Loops\n```javascript\n// 1. Loose (==) vs. Strict (===) Equality & null/NaN Quirks\nconsole.log(10 == "10");          // true (string "10" coerced to number 10)\nconsole.log(10 === "10");         // false (different types)\nconsole.log(null == undefined);   // true\nconsole.log(null === undefined);  // false\nconsole.log(null == 0);           // false (null only loosely equals undefined)\nconsole.log(null >= 0);           // true  (relational >= coerces null to 0!)\nconsole.log(NaN === NaN);         // false\n\n// 2. IEEE-754 Floating Point Precision Problem\nconsole.log(0.1 + 0.2);           // 0.30000000000000004\nconsole.log(0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3);   // false\nconsole.log(Number((0.1 + 0.2).toFixed(2)) === 0.3); // true\n\n// 3. Logical Short-Circuiting (Returns actual operand values, not just booleans!)\nconsole.log("Coder" && "Army");   // "Army" (first is truthy, returns second)\nconsole.log(0 && "Army");         // 0      (short-circuits on first falsy value)\nconsole.log("" || "Fallback");    // "Fallback" (returns first truthy value)\n\n// 4. Bitwise AND (&) vs. Bitwise OR (|) on binary bits\nconsole.log(2 & 3); // 2 (010 & 011 = 010)\nconsole.log(2 & 5); // 0 (010 & 101 = 000)\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 4: Operators, Type Coercion, Conditionals & Loops — == vs. ===, 0.1 + 0.2, Short-Circuiting & Bitwise	Master arithmetic, assignment, comparison (== vs. ===), logical short-circuiting (&&, ||), bitwise operators, IEEE-754 floating-point precision (0.1 + 0.2), and control flow loops.	20	Easy	\N	4	[{"id": "js-lec-4-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Why does 0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3 return false in JavaScript, and how do you handle financial calculations safely?", "title": "Q1: Why does 0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3 return false in JavaScript, and how do you handle financial calculations safely?", "answer": "JavaScript numbers follow the 64-bit IEEE-754 binary floating-point standard. In base-2, fractions whose denominators are not powers of 2 (such as 1/10 = 0.1 and 1/5 = 0.2) cannot be represented finitely—they become infinite repeating binary fractions (0.0001100110011...). When truncated to 53 mantissa bits and added together, the rounding error yields 0.30000000000000004. For financial systems, always store and compute amounts as integers in the smallest currency unit (e.g., paise or cents: 10 + 20 === 30) or use Decimal/BigInt libraries.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "JavaScript numbers follow the 64-bit IEEE-754 binary floating-point standard. In base-2, fractions whose denominators are not powers of 2 (such as 1/10 = 0.1 and 1/5 = 0.2) cannot be represented finitely—they become infinite repeating binary fractions (0.0001100110011...). When truncated to 53 mantissa bits and added together, the rounding error yields 0.30000000000000004. For financial systems, always store and compute amounts as integers in the smallest currency unit (e.g., paise or cents: 10 + 20 === 30) or use Decimal/BigInt libraries."}, {"id": "js-lec-4-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Explain why null > 0 is false, null == 0 is false, but null >= 0 is true!", "title": "Q2: Explain why null > 0 is false, null == 0 is false, but null >= 0 is true!", "answer": "Because the Equality check (==) and Relational Comparisons (> < >= <=) follow two completely different ECMAScript specification algorithms! Relational operators convert null to a number (ToNumber(null) -> 0), so null > 0 becomes 0 > 0 (false) and null >= 0 becomes 0 >= 0 (true). However, the Abstract Equality (==) specification explicitly states that null only equals null or undefined and never coerces null to a number, so null == 0 is false.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q2", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Because the Equality check (==) and Relational Comparisons (> < >= <=) follow two completely different ECMAScript specification algorithms! Relational operators convert null to a number (ToNumber(null) -> 0), so null > 0 becomes 0 > 0 (false) and null >= 0 becomes 0 >= 0 (true). However, the Abstract Equality (==) specification explicitly states that null only equals null or undefined and never coerces null to a number, so null == 0 is false."}, {"id": "js-lec-4-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is the output of console.log(\\"Rohit\\" && 42) and console.log(0 || \\"Default\\")? Why don't they return true or false?", "title": "Q3: What is the output of console.log(\\"Rohit\\" && 42) and console.log(0 || \\"Default\\")? Why don't they return true or false?", "answer": "In JavaScript, && and || are value-selector operators that return the actual evaluated operand value rather than coercing to a boolean. Logical AND (&&) returns the first falsy operand it encounters, or the final operand if all are truthy—so \\"Rohit\\" && 42 returns 42. Logical OR (||) returns the first truthy operand it encounters, or the final operand if all are falsy—so 0 || \\"Default\\" returns \\"Default\\".", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In JavaScript, && and || are value-selector operators that return the actual evaluated operand value rather than coercing to a boolean. Logical AND (&&) returns the first falsy operand it encounters, or the final operand if all are truthy—so \\"Rohit\\" && 42 returns 42. Logical OR (||) returns the first truthy operand it encounters, or the final operand if all are falsy—so 0 || \\"Default\\" returns \\"Default\\"."}, {"id": "js-lec-4-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: List all 8 Falsy values in JavaScript. Are empty arrays [] and empty objects {} truthy or falsy?", "title": "Q4: List all 8 Falsy values in JavaScript. Are empty arrays [] and empty objects {} truthy or falsy?", "answer": "The 8 falsy values in JavaScript are: false, 0, -0, 0n (BigInt zero), \\"\\" (empty string), null, undefined, and NaN. Empty arrays [] and empty objects {} are Object references, and every Object in JavaScript is strictly Truthy (so if ([]) executes the if-block). However, [] == false evaluates to true because loose equality coerces [] -> \\"\\" -> 0.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "The 8 falsy values in JavaScript are: false, 0, -0, 0n (BigInt zero), \\"\\" (empty string), null, undefined, and NaN. Empty arrays [] and empty objects {} are Object references, and every Object in JavaScript is strictly Truthy (so if ([]) executes the if-block). However, [] == false evaluates to true because loose equality coerces [] -> \\"\\" -> 0."}, {"id": "js-lec-4-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Why is NaN === NaN false, and what is the difference between isNaN() and Number.isNaN()?", "title": "Q5: Why is NaN === NaN false, and what is the difference between isNaN() and Number.isNaN()?", "answer": "Per the IEEE-754 specification, NaN (\\"Not-a-Number\\") represents an invalid mathematical result (like 0/0 or Number(\\"abc\\")), and two invalid results are never equal to each other, so NaN === NaN is false. The global isNaN(\\"hello\\") coerces its argument to a number first and returns true for strings, whereas Number.isNaN(val) does NOT coerce the value and returns true ONLY if the value is currently of type number and its value is NaN.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Per the IEEE-754 specification, NaN (\\"Not-a-Number\\") represents an invalid mathematical result (like 0/0 or Number(\\"abc\\")), and two invalid results are never equal to each other, so NaN === NaN is false. The global isNaN(\\"hello\\") coerces its argument to a number first and returns true for strings, whereas Number.isNaN(val) does NOT coerce the value and returns true ONLY if the value is currently of type number and its value is NaN."}, {"id": "js-lec-4-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: What is the difference between Logical AND (&&) and Bitwise AND (&)? Why does 2 & 3 equal 2 while 2 & 5 equals 0?", "title": "Q6: What is the difference between Logical AND (&&) and Bitwise AND (&)? Why does 2 & 3 equal 2 while 2 & 5 equals 0?", "answer": "Logical && operates on truthiness of whole expressions and short-circuits. Bitwise & converts both numbers to 32-bit binary integers and compares them bit-by-bit (1 & 1 = 1, otherwise 0). Since 2 is 010 in binary and 3 is 011, 010 & 011 = 010 (2). Since 5 is 101 in binary, 010 & 101 = 000 (0).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Logical && operates on truthiness of whole expressions and short-circuits. Bitwise & converts both numbers to 32-bit binary integers and compares them bit-by-bit (1 & 1 = 1, otherwise 0). Since 2 is 010 in binary and 3 is 011, 010 & 011 = 010 (2). Since 5 is 101 in binary, 010 & 101 = 000 (0)."}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Expressions_and_operators	\N	\N	\N
14172	Mathematical Value Encoding (2*val - min): Implement Min Stack in O(1) Time and O(N) Single-Stack Space	dsa-implement-min-stack-encoded-single-stack	Master Mathematical Value Encoding (2*val - min): Implement Min Stack in O(1) Time and O(N) Single-Stack Space with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:24:23.656315+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Mathematical Value Encoding (2*val - min): Implement Min Stack in O(1) Time and O(N) Single-Stack Space\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NdDIaH91P0g", "title": "Mathematical Value Encoding (2*val - min): Implement Min Stack in O(1) Time and O(N) Single-Stack Space - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/min-stack/", "title": "LeetCode 155: Min Stack", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Design a stack that supports push, pop, top, and retrieving the minimum element in constant O(1) time."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/get-minimum-element-from-stack/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Get minimum element from stack", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Implement a stack that supports getMin() in O(1) time and O(1) extra space beyond the single stack."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-frequency-stack/", "title": "LeetCode 895: Maximum Frequency Stack", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Design a stack-like data structure that pops the most frequent element in O(1) time."}]	Module 42: Stack & Queue Foundations: Custom Data Structure Implementations, Expression Conversions & Min Stack	### 📌 Topic Overview\nDesign a stack that supports `push(val)`, `pop()`, `top()`, and **`getMin()`**, all in **`O(1)` constant time**.\n\n### 💡 Progression (O(2N) Space Pair Stack → Optimal O(N) Space Mathematical Encoding)\n- **1. Approach 1 — Storing `pair<int, int>` (`{val, currentMin}` — `O(2N)` Space):**\n  - Push `{val, min(val, st.top().second)}`. `getMin()` simply returns `st.top().second`.\n  - Interviewers will ask: *"Can you eliminate the second element of the pair and use only `O(N)` space (a single `stack<long long>`)?"*\n- **2. Approach 2 — Mathematical Encoding `2 * val - mini` (`O(1)` Time, `O(N)` Space!):**\n  - Maintain `stack<long long> st` and a single variable `long long mini`.\n  - **When `push(val)` is called with a NEW minimum (`val < mini`):**\n    - Instead of pushing `val` directly, we encode the *old* `mini` into the pushed number using:\n      **`encodedVal = 2LL * val - mini`**\n    - Push `encodedVal` onto `st`, and update **`mini = val`**!\n  - **Why Does `encodedVal < mini` Act as a Flag That `val` Was Encoded?**\n    - Because `val < oldMini`  ⇒  `val - oldMini < 0`  ⇒  `2 * val - oldMini < val`  ⇒  **`encodedVal < newMini`**!\n    - Thus, whenever **`st.top() < mini`**, we know 100% that `st.top()` is an **encoded value** and the **true top value is actually `mini`**!\n  - **How `pop()` Restores the Previous Minimum When `st.top() < mini`:**\n    - When popping an encoded value `el = st.top()` where `el < mini`, we recover the previous minimum using the inverse formula:\n      **`previousMini = 2LL * mini - el`**!\n\n### 💻 Min Stack (Optimal Single-Stack Mathematical Encoding) Blueprint\n```cpp\nclass MinStack {\n    stack<long long> st;\n    long long mini;\n\npublic:\n    MinStack() {\n        mini = LLONG_MAX;\n    }\n\n    void push(int value) {\n        long long val = value;\n        if (st.empty()) {\n            mini = val;\n            st.push(val);\n        } else {\n            if (val > mini) {\n                st.push(val);\n            } else {\n                // Encode previous mini into the pushed value: (2 * val - mini) < val\n                st.push(2LL * val - mini);\n                mini = val;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    void pop() {\n        if (st.empty()) return;\n        long long el = st.top();\n        st.pop();\n\n        // If popped element is < mini, it is an encoded marker -> restore previous mini!\n        if (el < mini) {\n            mini = 2LL * mini - el;\n        }\n    }\n\n    int top() {\n        if (st.empty()) return -1;\n        long long el = st.top();\n        // If el < mini, the actual un-encoded value sitting at the top is 'mini'!\n        if (el < mini) return (int)mini;\n        return (int)el;\n    }\n\n    int getMin() {\n        return (int)mini;\n    }\n};\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Using `int` Instead of `long long` for `2 * val - mini`:** On LeetCode 155, `val` can be `INT_MIN` (`-2147483648`) and `mini` can be `INT_MAX` (`+2147483647`), so `2 * val - mini` overflows a 32-bit signed `int`! Always use **`long long`** (`2LL * val - mini`) for the stack and `mini`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
