15084	Virtual Array Doubling & Modulo Indexing: Next Greater Element II (Circular Array)	dsa-next-greater-element-ii-circular-array	Master Virtual Array Doubling & Modulo Indexing: Next Greater Element II (Circular Array) with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:30:52.967093+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Virtual Array Doubling & Modulo Indexing: Next Greater Element II (Circular Array)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7PrncD7v9YQ", "title": "Virtual Array Doubling & Modulo Indexing: Next Greater Element II (Circular Array) - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/next-greater-element-ii/", "title": "LeetCode 503: Next Greater Element II", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given a circular integer array nums, return the next greater number for every element in nums."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/next-greater-element-2/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Next Greater Element in Circular Array", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the next greater element for each element in a circular array in O(N) time."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/next-greater-node-in-linked-list/", "title": "LeetCode 1019: Next Greater Node In Linked List", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Apply monotonic decreasing stack logic to find the next greater node value for each node in a linked list."}]	Module 43: Monotonic Stack & Contribution Algorithms: Next/Previous Greater/Smaller, Rainwater & Subarray Aggregations	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a **circular integer array** `arr` of size `N` (where the element after `arr[N - 1]` wraps around to `arr[0]`), find the **Next Greater Element** for every index `0 .. N - 1`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute O(N²) → Optimal Virtual Doubling O(4N))\n- **How to Handle Circular Wrap-Around Without Allocating a 2N Array:**\n  - Mentally append a copy of `arr` to the end of `arr` so the virtual array has length **`2 * N`** (indices `0` to `2 * N - 1`).\n  - How do we access virtual index `i` (`0 <= i < 2 * N`) in the original array of size `N`? Using the **modulo operator: `arr[i % N]`**!\n- **Algorithm Steps (O(4N) Time, O(2N) Space):**\n  1. Loop `i` backwards from **`2 * N - 1` down to `0`**:\n  2. Pop elements `<= arr[i % N]`: `while (!st.empty() && st.top() <= arr[i % N]) st.pop();`.\n  3. **Only record answers when `i < N`:**\n     - `if (i < N) nge[i] = st.empty() ? -1 : st.top();`\n  4. Push `st.push(arr[i % N]);`.\n- **Why O(4N) Time?** The outer loop runs `2N` times (`2N` pushes) and the `while` loop pops at most `2N` elements total (`2N + 2N = 4N`).\n\n### 💻 Next Greater Element II (Circular Array) Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> nextGreaterElementsCircular(vector<int> &nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    vector<int> nge(n, -1);\n    stack<int> st;\n\n    // Traverse hypothetical doubled array from 2*n - 1 down to 0\n    for (int i = 2 * n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n        while (!st.empty() && st.top() <= nums[i % n]) {\n            st.pop();\n        }\n\n        // Only populate nge[i] when i is in the actual array range [0 .. n - 1]\n        if (i < n) {\n            nge[i] = st.empty() ? -1 : st.top();\n        }\n\n        st.push(nums[i % n]);\n    }\n    return nge;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Writing `nge[i % n] = ...` Without `if (i < n)`:** While overwriting `nge[i % n]` during the first half `2N - 1 .. N` and then overwriting it again during `N - 1 .. 0` happens to produce the same final result, wrapping the assignment in `if (i < n)` avoids `N` wasted array writes!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
