176	Elementary Sorting Algorithms: Selection Sort, Bubble Sort & Insertion Sort	dsa-elementary-sorting-selection-bubble-insertion	Master Selection Sort, Bubble Sort with didSwap flag optimization, and Insertion Sort with inward left-sliding element shifts.	2026-10-02 22:22:31.462415+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Elementary Sorting Algorithms: Selection Sort, Bubble Sort & Insertion Sort\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HGk_ypEuS24", "title": "Lecture 14: Selection Sort, Bubble Sort & Insertion Sort Tutorial and Code", "duration": "40m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sort-colors/", "title": "Sort Colors", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "75", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Sort an array of 0s, 1s, and 2s in-place without using the built-in sort library."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/height-checker/", "title": "Height Checker", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1051", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Compare an array against its non-decreasing sorted order to count misplaced elements."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/insertion-sort/1", "title": "Insertion Sort", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "problemId": "gfg-insert-sort", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Implement the Insertion Sort algorithm from scratch."}]	Module 7: Classical & Divide-and-Conquer Sorting Algorithms	### 📌 Topic Overview\nUnderstanding how basic sorting algorithms work under the hood is a common technical interview requirement. This lecture breaks down the three classic `O(N²)` sorting algorithms: **Selection Sort**, **Bubble Sort**, and **Insertion Sort**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **1. Selection Sort ("Select the Minimum & Swap"):**\n  - In each step `i`, scan the unsorted portion `[i ... n - 1]` to find the index of the minimum element, then swap it with `arr[i]`.\n  - **Complexity:** `O(N²)` in Best, Average, and Worst cases.\n- **2. Bubble Sort ("Push the Maximum to the End"):**\n  - Repeatedly compare adjacent elements (`arr[j]` and `arr[j + 1]`) and swap them if they are in the wrong order. After each pass, the largest remaining element bubbles to the end.\n  - **Optimization:** Track a `didSwap` flag. If a pass makes `0` swaps, the array is already sorted and we can break early!\n  - **Complexity:** `O(N²)` Worst/Average case, `O(N)` Best case (when already sorted).\n- **3. Insertion Sort ("Slide Element Left Into Sorted Position"):**\n  - Pick element `arr[i]` and repeatedly swap it with its left neighbor (`arr[j - 1]`) as long as `arr[j - 1] > arr[j]`.\n  - **Complexity:** `O(N²)` Worst/Average case, `O(N)` Best case (when already sorted).\n\n### 💻 Optimized Bubble Sort & Insertion Sort\n```cpp\n// Insertion Sort: Slide each element left into its sorted spot\nvoid insertionSort(vector<int> &arr, int n) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        int j = i;\n        while (j > 0 && arr[j - 1] > arr[j]) {\n            swap(arr[j - 1], arr[j]);\n            j--;\n        }\n    }\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22114	Lecture 29: Capped Collections — Fixed-Size Circular Buffers (capped, size, max), $natural Sort, isCapped() & collMod	mongo-lecture-29-capped-collections-circular-buffers-collmod	Create and manage fixed-capacity Capped Collections for high-throughput logs and telemetry streams, query in insertion order with $natural, and resize limits via collMod.	2026-10-06 19:52:12.616986+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nA **Capped Collection** is a fixed-size circular collection in MongoDB that automatically overwrites its **oldest documents** (FIFO — First-In, First-Out) once it reaches its allocated byte `size` or maximum document count (`max`), making it ideal for application logs, IoT sensor streams, and audit trails.\n\n### 💡 Core Mechanics of Capped Collections\n* **Dual Capacity Limits (`size` and `max`):**\n  * **`capped: true`:** Enables circular buffer behavior on the collection.\n  * **`size: <bytes>` (Mandatory):** Maximum storage size in bytes (e.g., `51200` bytes = `50 KB`, since `1 KB = 1024 bytes`).\n  * **`max: <count>` (Optional):** Maximum number of documents allowed (e.g., `max: 5`). Whichever limit (`size` or `max`) is reached first triggers automatic eviction of the oldest document on the next insert.\n* **Insertion-Order Retrieval (`$natural`):** Capped collections guarantee insertion order on disk. Use `.sort({ $natural: -1 })` to retrieve the most recently inserted logs first, or `.sort({ $natural: 1 })` (default) for oldest first.\n* **Checking Capped Status (`isCapped()`):** `db.<collection>.isCapped()` returns `true` if the collection is capped, or `false` otherwise.\n* **Converting an Existing Collection (`convertToCapped`):** `db.runCommand({ convertToCapped: "<collection>", size: <bytes> })` converts a standard collection into a capped collection (note: `max` cannot be enforced via `convertToCapped`; only byte `size` applies).\n* **Updating Capped Limits (`collMod`):** Use `db.runCommand({ collMod: "<collection>", cappedMax: <newMax>, cappedSize: <newBytes> })` to resize an existing capped collection on the fly.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical Capped Collection Commands\n```javascript\n// 1. Create a Capped Collection "log" limited to 50 KB (51,200 bytes) and max 5 documents\ndb.createCollection("log", {\n  capped: true,\n  size: 51200,\n  max: 5\n})\n\n// 2. Verify whether collections are capped\ndb.log.isCapped()       // true\ndb.students.isCapped()  // false\n\n// 3. Query logs from newest to oldest using $natural: -1\ndb.log.find().sort({ $natural: -1 })\n\n// 4. Convert an existing standard collection into a Capped Collection (size in bytes)\ndb.runCommand({\n  convertToCapped: "students",\n  size: 51200\n})\n\n// 5. Modify max documents or byte size on an existing Capped Collection via collMod\ndb.runCommand({\n  collMod: "log",\n  cappedMax: 7\n})\n\ndb.runCommand({\n  collMod: "log",\n  cappedSize: 100000\n})\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/capped-collections/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/capped-collections/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 29: Capped Collections"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1WOIh7Apf5A", "title": "MongoDb Capped Collection Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "23m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nA **Capped Collection** is a fixed-size circular collection in MongoDB that automatically overwrites its **oldest documents** (FIFO — First-In, First-Out) once it reaches its allocated byte `size` or maximum document count (`max`), making it ideal for application logs, IoT sensor streams, and audit trails.\n\n### 💡 Core Mechanics of Capped Collections\n* **Dual Capacity Limits (`size` and `max`):**\n  * **`capped: true`:** Enables circular buffer behavior on the collection.\n  * **`size: <bytes>` (Mandatory):** Maximum storage size in bytes (e.g., `51200` bytes = `50 KB`, since `1 KB = 1024 bytes`).\n  * **`max: <count>` (Optional):** Maximum number of documents allowed (e.g., `max: 5`). Whichever limit (`size` or `max`) is reached first triggers automatic eviction of the oldest document on the next insert.\n* **Insertion-Order Retrieval (`$natural`):** Capped collections guarantee insertion order on disk. Use `.sort({ $natural: -1 })` to retrieve the most recently inserted logs first, or `.sort({ $natural: 1 })` (default) for oldest first.\n* **Checking Capped Status (`isCapped()`):** `db.<collection>.isCapped()` returns `true` if the collection is capped, or `false` otherwise.\n* **Converting an Existing Collection (`convertToCapped`):** `db.runCommand({ convertToCapped: "<collection>", size: <bytes> })` converts a standard collection into a capped collection (note: `max` cannot be enforced via `convertToCapped`; only byte `size` applies).\n* **Updating Capped Limits (`collMod`):** Use `db.runCommand({ collMod: "<collection>", cappedMax: <newMax>, cappedSize: <newBytes> })` to resize an existing capped collection on the fly.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical Capped Collection Commands\n```javascript\n// 1. Create a Capped Collection "log" limited to 50 KB (51,200 bytes) and max 5 documents\ndb.createCollection("log", {\n  capped: true,\n  size: 51200,\n  max: 5\n})\n\n// 2. Verify whether collections are capped\ndb.log.isCapped()       // true\ndb.students.isCapped()  // false\n\n// 3. Query logs from newest to oldest using $natural: -1\ndb.log.find().sort({ $natural: -1 })\n\n// 4. Convert an existing standard collection into a Capped Collection (size in bytes)\ndb.runCommand({\n  convertToCapped: "students",\n  size: 51200\n})\n\n// 5. Modify max documents or byte size on an existing Capped Collection via collMod\ndb.runCommand({\n  collMod: "log",\n  cappedMax: 7\n})\n\ndb.runCommand({\n  collMod: "log",\n  cappedSize: 100000\n})\n```	Yahoo Baba	https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba	655	Lecture 29: Capped Collections — Fixed-Size Circular Buffers (capped, size, max), $natural Sort, isCapped() & collMod	Create and manage fixed-capacity Capped Collections for high-throughput logs and telemetry streams, query in insertion order with $natural, and resize limits via collMod.	20	Easy	\N	29	[]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/capped-collections/	\N	\N	\N
34	Hibernate ORM, JPA Annotations & N+1 Problem	hibernate-jpa-n-plus-1	@Entity, @OneToMany, @ManyToMany, FetchType.LAZY, and solving the N+1 select query problem.	2026-09-27 22:28:22.395528+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Hibernate ORM, JPA Annotations & N+1 Problem\nPart of the **Relational Databases with JDBC & Hibernate/JPA** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hibernate%20ORM%2C%20JPA%20Annotations%20%26%20N%2B1%20Problem%20tutorial", "title": "Hibernate ORM, JPA Annotations & N+1 Problem Full Guide", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"type": "assignment", "title": "Build & Test a Working Implementation of Hibernate ORM, JPA Annotations & N+1 Problem", "checklist": ["Implement core logic with validation and boundary checks", "Write tests verifying expected output and edge cases"], "description": "Implement a production-grade module demonstrating the core principles of Hibernate ORM, JPA Annotations & N+1 Problem.", "starterCodeUrl": ""}]	\N	\N	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	16	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22101	Lecture 16: Aggregation Pipeline Foundations — $match, $count, $sort, $sortByCount, $project, $limit, $skip & $sample	mongo-lecture-16-aggregation-pipeline-match-sort-project-sample	Build multi-stage data processing pipelines with db.collection.aggregate([]) using $match, $count, $sort, $sortByCount, $project (with computed fields), $skip, $limit, and $sample.	2026-10-06 19:28:34.895846+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThe **MongoDB Aggregation Pipeline** (`db.<collection>.aggregate([ stage1, stage2, ... )`) processes documents through a sequence of stages. Each stage transforms, filters, sorts, or reshapes the documents and passes its output directly into the next stage.\n\n### 💡 Core Pipeline Stages Covered\n* **`$match`:** Filters documents early in the pipeline using standard comparison (`$gt`, `$lt`) and logical (`$and`, `$or`) operators. Always place `$match` as the first stage to reduce the number of documents processed downstream.\n* **`$count`:** Counts the total number of documents reaching that stage and outputs `{ "<customFieldName>": <count> }`.\n* **`$sort`:** Orders documents by one or more fields (`1` for ascending, `-1` for descending). Supports multi-field tie-breaking (e.g., `{ age: 1, name: 1 }`).\n* **`$project`:** Includes (`1`) or excludes (`0`) fields, and computes **new dynamic fields** using expressions (such as `isValidAge: { $gt: ["$age", 20 }`).\n* **`$sortByCount`:** Groups incoming documents by a specified expression (e.g., `"$class"`), computes the count per distinct value, and automatically sorts the buckets in descending order of `count`.\n* **`$skip` & `$limit`:** Paginates pipeline output. Always place `$skip` **before** `$limit` when building paginated pipelines.\n* **`$sample`:** Randomly selects `n` documents from the collection: `{ $sample: { size: 2 } }`.\n\n### 🛠️ Multi-Stage Aggregation Pipeline Examples\n```javascript\n// 1. Multi-stage pipeline: $match -> $sort -> $project -> $skip -> $limit\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  { $match: { age: { $gt: 20 } } },\n  { $sort: { age: 1, name: 1 } },\n  { $project: { name: 1, class: 1, _id: 0 } },\n  { $skip: 0 },\n  { $limit: 2 }\n)\n\n// 2. $project with a computed boolean field ("isValidAge")\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  { $sort: { age: 1, name: 1 } },\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      class: 1,\n      _id: 0,\n      isValidAge: { $gt: ["$age", 20 }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 3. $match followed by $count\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  { $match: { age: { $gt: 20 } } },\n  { $count: "studentsAbove20" }\n)\n\n// 4. $sortByCount: Group by class, count students, and sort highest to lowest\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  { $sortByCount: "$class" }\n)\n// Output: [ { _id: "BTech", count: 3 }, { _id: "BCA", count: 3 }, { _id: "BSc", count: 2 } \n\n// 5. $sample: Fetch 2 random documents on each execution\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  { $sample: { size: 2 } }\n)\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/aggregation-pipeline/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/aggregation-pipeline/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 16: Aggregation Pipeline Foundations"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3sJbtnfmnYQ", "title": "MongoDB Aggregation Pipeline Operators Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "27m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[{"id": "mongo-16-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c07a3fe74e0db3767e", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c07a3fe74e0db3767e", "name": "Problem #91: Aggregation: sum helpfulVotes by productId", "title": "Problem #91: Aggregation: sum helpfulVotes by productId", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "91", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate: group **reviews** by **productId**, sum **helpfulVotes**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c07a3fe74e0db3767e"}, {"id": "mongo-16-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3767b", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3767b", "name": "Problem #92: Aggregation: average rating by productId", "title": "Problem #92: Aggregation: average rating by productId", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "92", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate: group **reviews** by **productId**, compute average **rating**. Use **$avg**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3767b"}, {"id": "mongo-16-prob-3", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37678", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37678", "name": "Problem #93: Aggregation: inventory count by warehouse", "title": "Problem #93: Aggregation: inventory count by warehouse", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "93", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate: group **inventory** by **warehouse**, count.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37678"}, {"id": "mongo-16-prob-4", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375ac", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375ac", "name": "Problem #161: Count logs", "title": "Problem #161: Count logs", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "161", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: single doc with **count** of logs.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375ac"}, {"id": "mongo-16-prob-5", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375a9", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375a9", "name": "Problem #162: Count articles", "title": "Problem #162: Count articles", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "162", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: single doc with **count** of articles. Use **$count**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375a9"}, {"id": "mongo-16-prob-6", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db3750b", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db3750b", "name": "Problem #214: Unwind article tags then group by tag", "title": "Problem #214: Unwind article tags then group by tag", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "214", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate: **$unwind** articles by **tags**, **$group** by tags, count. Sort by count descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db3750b"}, {"id": "mongo-16-prob-7", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db37508", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db37508", "name": "Problem #215: Aggregation: total views by authorId", "title": "Problem #215: Aggregation: total views by authorId", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "215", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate: group **articles** by **authorId**, sum **views**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db37508"}, {"id": "mongo-16-prob-8", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db37505", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db37505", "name": "Problem #216: Aggregation: logs count by level", "title": "Problem #216: Aggregation: logs count by level", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "216", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate: group **logs** by **level**, count.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db37505"}, {"id": "mongo-16-prob-9", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db37502", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db37502", "name": "Problem #217: Aggregation: articles count by authorId", "title": "Problem #217: Aggregation: articles count by authorId", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "217", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate: group **articles** by **authorId**, count.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bc7a3fe74e0db37502"}, {"id": "mongo-16-prob-10", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db37436", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db37436", "name": "Problem #283: Aggregation products average price by category", "title": "Problem #283: Aggregation products average price by category", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "283", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Group **products** by **category** and compute average **price**. Use **$group** and **$avg**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db37436"}, {"id": "mongo-16-prob-11", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3734b", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3734b", "name": "Problem #360: Group products by category count", "title": "Problem #360: Group products by category count", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "360", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: group **products** by **category**, count. Sort by count descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3734b"}, {"id": "mongo-16-prob-12", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37339", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37339", "name": "Problem #366: Aggregation: sum of all order totals", "title": "Problem #366: Aggregation: sum of all order totals", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "366", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Single document with **grandTotal** as sum of all **orders.total**. Use **$group** _id null.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37339"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThe **MongoDB Aggregation Pipeline** (`db.<collection>.aggregate([ stage1, stage2, ... )`) processes documents through a sequence of stages. Each stage transforms, filters, sorts, or reshapes the documents and passes its output directly into the next stage.\n\n### 💡 Core Pipeline Stages Covered\n* **`$match`:** Filters documents early in the pipeline using standard comparison (`$gt`, `$lt`) and logical (`$and`, `$or`) operators. Always place `$match` as the first stage to reduce the number of documents processed downstream.\n* **`$count`:** Counts the total number of documents reaching that stage and outputs `{ "<customFieldName>": <count> }`.\n* **`$sort`:** Orders documents by one or more fields (`1` for ascending, `-1` for descending). Supports multi-field tie-breaking (e.g., `{ age: 1, name: 1 }`).\n* **`$project`:** Includes (`1`) or excludes (`0`) fields, and computes **new dynamic fields** using expressions (such as `isValidAge: { $gt: ["$age", 20 }`).\n* **`$sortByCount`:** Groups incoming documents by a specified expression (e.g., `"$class"`), computes the count per distinct value, and automatically sorts the buckets in descending order of `count`.\n* **`$skip` & `$limit`:** Paginates pipeline output. Always place `$skip` **before** `$limit` when building paginated pipelines.\n* **`$sample`:** Randomly selects `n` documents from the collection: `{ $sample: { size: 2 } }`.\n\n### 🛠️ Multi-Stage Aggregation Pipeline Examples\n```javascript\n// 1. Multi-stage pipeline: $match -> $sort -> $project -> $skip -> $limit\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  { $match: { age: { $gt: 20 } } },\n  { $sort: { age: 1, name: 1 } },\n  { $project: { name: 1, class: 1, _id: 0 } },\n  { $skip: 0 },\n  { $limit: 2 }\n)\n\n// 2. $project with a computed boolean field ("isValidAge")\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  { $sort: { age: 1, name: 1 } },\n  {\n    $project: {\n      name: 1,\n      class: 1,\n      _id: 0,\n      isValidAge: { $gt: ["$age", 20 }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 3. $match followed by $count\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  { $match: { age: { $gt: 20 } } },\n  { $count: "studentsAbove20" }\n)\n\n// 4. $sortByCount: Group by class, count students, and sort highest to lowest\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  { $sortByCount: "$class" }\n)\n// Output: [ { _id: "BTech", count: 3 }, { _id: "BCA", count: 3 }, { _id: "BSc", count: 2 } \n\n// 5. $sample: Fetch 2 random documents on each execution\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  { $sample: { size: 2 } }\n)\n```	Yahoo Baba	https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba	655	Lecture 16: Aggregation Pipeline Foundations — $match, $count, $sort, $sortByCount, $project, $limit, $skip & $sample	Build multi-stage data processing pipelines with db.collection.aggregate([]) using $match, $count, $sort, $sortByCount, $project (with computed fields), $skip, $limit, and $sample.	20	Easy	\N	16	[{"id": "mongo-16-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c07a3fe74e0db3767e", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c07a3fe74e0db3767e", "name": "Problem #91: Aggregation: sum helpfulVotes by productId", "title": "Problem #91: Aggregation: sum helpfulVotes by productId", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "91", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate: group **reviews** by **productId**, sum **helpfulVotes**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9c07a3fe74e0db3767e"}, {"id": "mongo-16-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3767b", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3767b", "name": "Problem #92: Aggregation: average rating by productId", "title": "Problem #92: Aggregation: average rating by productId", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "92", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate: group **reviews** by **productId**, compute average **rating**. Use **$avg**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3767b"}, {"id": "mongo-16-prob-3", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37678", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37678", "name": "Problem #93: Aggregation: inventory count by warehouse", "title": "Problem #93: Aggregation: inventory count by warehouse", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "93", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate: group **inventory** by **warehouse**, count.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37678"}, {"id": "mongo-16-prob-4", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375ac", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375ac", "name": "Problem #161: Count logs", "title": "Problem #161: Count logs", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "161", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: single doc with **count** of logs.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375ac"}, {"id": "mongo-16-prob-5", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375a9", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bd7a3fe74e0db375a9", "name": "Problem #162: Count articles", "title": "Problem #162: Count articles", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "162", "difficulty": "Hard", "d