 BY)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "20_5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Find Followers Count (GROUP BY + ORDER BY)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-followers-count/"}, {"id": "pg-20-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/daily-leads-and-partners/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/daily-leads-and-partners/", "name": "Daily Leads and Partners (Multi-Column GROUP BY)", "title": "Daily Leads and Partners (Multi-Column GROUP BY)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "20_6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Daily Leads and Partners (Multi-Column GROUP BY)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/daily-leads-and-partners/"}, {"id": "pg-20-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/user-activity-for-the-past-30-days-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/user-activity-for-the-past-30-days-i/", "name": "User Activity for the Past 30 Days I (WHERE + GROUP BY)", "title": "User Activity for the Past 30 Days I (WHERE + GROUP BY)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "20_7", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"User Activity for the Past 30 Days I (WHERE + GROUP BY)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/user-activity-for-the-past-30-days-i/"}, {"id": "pg-20-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/bank-account-summary-ii/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/bank-account-summary-ii/", "name": "Bank Account Summary II (GROUP BY + HAVING SUM)", "title": "Bank Account Summary II (GROUP BY + HAVING SUM)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "20_8", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Bank Account Summary II (GROUP BY + HAVING SUM)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/bank-account-summary-ii/"}, {"id": "pg-20-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/", "name": "Customers Who Bought All Products (GROUP BY + HAVING COUNT DISTINCT)", "title": "Customers Who Bought All Products (GROUP BY + HAVING COUNT DISTINCT)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "20_9", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Customers Who Bought All Products (GROUP BY + HAVING COUNT DISTINCT)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/"}, {"id": "pg-20-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/", "name": "Market Analysis I", "title": "Market Analysis I", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "20_10", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Market Analysis I\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhile aggregate functions collapse an entire table into a single row, **`GROUP BY`** divides rows into distinct buckets (e.g., per role, per payment status, per department) and runs the aggregate function independently on each bucket. **`HAVING`** then filters those aggregated buckets.\n\n### ⚖️ `WHERE` vs. `HAVING` (Classic Interview Question)\n\n* **What It Filters:** Filters **individual raw rows**. — Filters **grouped buckets** created by `GROUP BY`.\n* **When It Runs:** Runs **BEFORE** `GROUP BY` and aggregation. — Runs **AFTER** `GROUP BY` and aggregation.\n* **Can It Use Aggregates (`SUM`, `COUNT`)?:** ❌ **No** (`WHERE COUNT(*) > 5` is a syntax error). — ✅ **Yes** (`HAVING COUNT(id) > 5`).\n\n### 🔄 The Golden Rule & Execution Pipeline\n* **The Golden Rule:** Every column listed in `SELECT` that is **not** wrapped inside an aggregate function (`COUNT`, `SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN`, `MAX`) **MUST** be listed in the `GROUP BY` clause.\n* **Internal SQL Execution Order:**\n  `FROM` $\\rightarrow$ `JOIN` $\\rightarrow$ `WHERE` (filter rows) $\\rightarrow$ `GROUP BY` (form buckets) $\\rightarrow$ **Aggregates** $\\rightarrow$ `HAVING` (filter buckets) $\\rightarrow$ `SELECT` $\\rightarrow$ `ORDER BY` $\\rightarrow$ `LIMIT`.\n\n### 💻 Practical Query Blueprints\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Count how many active users exist in each role ('admin', 'professor', 'student')\nSELECT \n    role,\n    COUNT(id) AS total_users\nFROM users\nWHERE is_active = true\nGROUP BY role;\n\n-- 2. Group payments by status ('completed', 'pending', 'failed') to audit revenue\nSELECT \n    status,\n    SUM(amount) AS "Total Revenue"\nFROM payments\nGROUP BY status;\n\n-- 3. HAVING Clause: Find departments that have strictly more than 5 users\nSELECT \n    department_id,\n    COUNT(id) AS user_count\nFROM users\nGROUP BY department_id\nHAVING COUNT(id) > 5;\n```	Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)	https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical	654	Lecture 20: Grouping Data — GROUP BY, HAVING vs. WHERE & SQL Execution Order	Bucket rows into summary groups with GROUP BY, filter aggregated buckets using HAVING, and master the internal SQL query execution pipeline.	18	Medium	Module 6: Aggregations, Grouping & Analytical Queries	20	[{"id": "pg-20-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/classes-more-than-5-students/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/classes-more-than-5-students/", "name": "Classes More Than 5 Students (GROUP BY + HAVING COUNT)", "title": "Classes More Than 5 Students (GROUP BY + HAVING COUNT)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "20_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Classes More Than 5 Students (GROUP BY + HAVING COUNT)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/classes-more-than-5-students/"}, {"id": "pg-20-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "name": "Duplicate Emails (GROUP BY + HAVING COUNT > 1)", "title": "Duplicate Emails (GROUP BY + HAVING COUNT > 1)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "20_2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Duplicate Emails (GROUP BY + HAVING COUNT > 1)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/"}, {"id": "pg-20-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/actors-and-directors-who-cooperated-at-least-three-times/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/actors-and-directors-who-cooperated-at-least-three-times/", "name": "Actors and Directors Who Cooperated At Least Three Times (Multi-Column GROUP BY)", "title": "Actors and Directors Who Cooperated At Least Three Times (Multi-Column GROUP BY)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "20_3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Actors and Directors Who Cooperated At Least Three Times (Multi-Column GROUP BY)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/actors-and-directors-who-cooperated-at-least-three-times/"}, {"id": "pg-20-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customer-placing-the-largest-number-of-orders/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customer-placing-the-largest-number-of-orders/", "name": "Customer Placing the Largest Number of Orders", "title": "Customer Placing the Largest Number of Orders", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "20_4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Customer Placing the Largest Number of Orders\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customer-placing-the-largest-number-of-orders/"}, {"id": "pg-20-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-followers-count/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-followers-count/", "name": "Find Followers Count (GROUP BY + ORDER BY)", "title": "Find Followers Count (GROUP BY + ORDER BY)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "20_5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Find Followers Count (GROUP BY + ORDER BY)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-followers-count/"}, {"id": "pg-20-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/daily-leads-and-partners/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/daily-leads-and-partners/", "name": "Daily Leads and Partners (Multi-Column GROUP BY)", "title": "Daily Leads and Partners (Multi-Column GROUP BY)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "20_6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Daily Leads and Partners (Multi-Column GROUP BY)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/daily-leads-and-partners/"}, {"id": "pg-20-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/user-activity-for-the-past-30-days-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/user-activity-for-the-past-30-days-i/", "name": "User Activity for the Past 30 Days I (WHERE + GROUP BY)", "title": "User Activity for the Past 30 Days I (WHERE + GROUP BY)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "20_7", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"User Activity for the Past 30 Days I (WHERE + GROUP BY)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/user-activity-for-the-past-30-days-i/"}, {"id": "pg-20-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/bank-account-summary-ii/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/bank-account-summary-ii/", "name": "Bank Account Summary II (GROUP BY + HAVING SUM)", "title": "Bank Account Summary II (GROUP BY + HAVING SUM)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "20_8", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Bank Account Summary II (GROUP BY + HAVING SUM)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/bank-account-summary-ii/"}, {"id": "pg-20-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/", "name": "Customers Who Bought All Products (GROUP BY + HAVING COUNT DISTINCT)", "title": "Customers Who Bought All Products (GROUP BY + HAVING COUNT DISTINCT)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "20_9", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Customers Who Bought All Products (GROUP BY + HAVING COUNT DISTINCT)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/"}, {"id": "pg-20-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/", "name": "Market Analysis I", "title": "Market Analysis I", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "20_10", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Market Analysis I\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/"}]	https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/queries-table-expressions.html#QUERIES-GROUP	\N	\N	\N
22102	Lecture 17: Aggregation $group & Accumulators — $sum, $avg, $min, $max, $median, $push, $addToSet, $first/$last & $top/$bottom	mongo-lecture-17-aggregation-group-and-accumulator-operators	Group documents by keys or across whole collections with $group and compute aggregates using $sum, $count, $avg, $min, $max, $median, $push, $addToSet, $first, $last, $topN, and $bottomN.	2026-10-06 19:28:34.895846+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThe **`$group`** stage partitions incoming documents into groups based on a mandatory **`_id`** key expression (e.g., `_id: "$class"`, or `_id: null` to aggregate the entire collection into a single group) and applies **Accumulator Operators** to compute per-group metrics.\n\n### 💡 Complete Accumulator Operators Reference\n* **`$sum` & `$count`:** Use `{ $sum: 1 }` or `{ $count: {} }` to count documents in each group, or `{ $sum: "$amount" }` to sum a numeric field.\n* **`$avg`, `$min`, `$max`:** Compute the arithmetic mean, minimum value, or maximum value of a numeric field per group.\n* **`$median`:** Computes the middle value of a numeric distribution: `{ $median: { input: "$age", method: "approximate" } }`.\n* **`$push` & `$addToSet`:** Collect field values from each group into an array (`{ $push: "$name" }`), push entire documents (`{ $push: "$$ROOT" }`), or collect only unique values (`{ $addToSet: "$name" }`).\n* **`$first` & `$last`:** Return a field (or `"$$ROOT"`) from the first or last document encountered in each group.\n* **`$top`, `$topN`, `$bottom`, `$bottomN`:** Sort documents **inside each group** via `sortBy` and return the top/bottom `1` or `n` records with selected `output` fields—without needing a separate prior `$sort` stage.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical $group & Accumulator Queries\n```javascript\n// 1. Group by class, count students, and sort by count descending\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $group: {\n      _id: "$class",\n      count: { $sum: 1 },\n      avgAge: { $avg: "$age" },\n      minAge: { $min: "$age" },\n      maxAge: { $max: "$age" }\n    }\n  },\n  { $sort: { count: -1 } }\n)\n\n// 2. Collect student names (or full documents via $$ROOT) into an array per class\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $group: {\n      _id: "$class",\n      studentNames: { $push: "$name" },\n      uniqueNames: { $addToSet: "$name" },\n      allRecords: { $push: "$$ROOT" }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 3. Compute overall collection-wide Average & Median Age using _id: null\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $group: {\n      _id: null,\n      overallAvgAge: { $avg: "$age" },\n      medianAge: {\n        $median: { input: "$age", method: "approximate" }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 4. $topN: Find the 3 youngest students inside each class group\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $group: {\n      _id: "$class",\n      youngestThree: {\n        $topN: {\n          n: 3,\n          output: ["$name", "$class", "$age",\n          sortBy: { age: 1 }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/group/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/group/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 17: Aggregation $group & Accumulators"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2sHcSQNvBm8", "title": "MongoDB Aggregation $group Operator Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "31m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[{"id": "mongo-17-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37321", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37321", "name": "Problem #374: Count orders by status", "title": "Problem #374: Count orders by status", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "374", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: count documents in **orders** per **status**. Use **$group** and **$sum: 1**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37321"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3731b", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3731b", "name": "Problem #376: Unwind orders items then group by productId", "title": "Problem #376: Unwind orders items then group by productId", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "376", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: **$unwind** **orders** by **items**, then **$group** by **items.productId** and sum **items.qty**. Sort by sum descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3731b"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-3", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37312", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37312", "name": "Problem #379: Aggregation: average order total by customer", "title": "Problem #379: Aggregation: average order total by customer", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "379", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate: group **orders** by **customerId**, compute average **total**. Use **$group** and **$avg**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37312"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-4", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3730f", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3730f", "name": "Problem #380: Aggregation: total revenue by order status", "title": "Problem #380: Aggregation: total revenue by order status", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "380", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate **orders**: group by **status**, sum **total** as revenue. Use **$group** and **$sum**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3730f"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-5", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b57a3fe74e0db3726a", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b57a3fe74e0db3726a", "name": "Problem #435: Users count per departmentId", "title": "Problem #435: Users count per departmentId", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "435", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: group users by **departmentId**, count. Sort by count descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b57a3fe74e0db3726a"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-6", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b57a3fe74e0db37267", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b57a3fe74e0db37267", "name": "Problem #436: Min and max budget in departments", "title": "Problem #436: Min and max budget in departments", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "436", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: one document with **minBudget** and **maxBudget** from departments. Use **$group** _id null and **$min** **$max**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b57a3fe74e0db37267"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-7", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371dd", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371dd", "name": "Problem #472: Sum of department budgets", "title": "Problem #472: Sum of department budgets", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "472", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: single document with **totalBudget** as sum of all **budget** in departments. Use **$group** with _id null.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371dd"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-8", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371cb", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371cb", "name": "Problem #478: Limit aggregation to 5", "title": "Problem #478: Limit aggregation to 5", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "478", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate: group users by **role**, count, sort by count descending, **$limit** 5.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371cb"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-9", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371c8", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371c8", "name": "Problem #479: Sort aggregation by count descending", "title": "Problem #479: Sort aggregation by count descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "479", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate: group users by **city**, count, then sort by count descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371c8"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-10", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b9", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b9", "name": "Problem #484: Average age per city", "title": "Problem #484: Average age per city", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "484", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: group **users** by **city** and compute average **age**. Use **$avg**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b9"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-11", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b6", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b6", "name": "Problem #485: Users grouped by role", "title": "Problem #485: Users grouped by role", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "485", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Group **users** by **role** and count. Output _id (role) and count.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b6"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-12", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b3", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b3", "name": "Problem #486: Users grouped by city", "title": "Problem #486: Users grouped by city", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "486", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Use aggregation: group **users** by **city** and count how many per city. Use **$group** and **$sum**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b3"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThe **`$group`** stage partitions incoming documents into groups based on a mandatory **`_id`** key expression (e.g., `_id: "$class"`, or `_id: null` to aggregate the entire collection into a single group) and applies **Accumulator Operators** to compute per-group metrics.\n\n### 💡 Complete Accumulator Operators Reference\n* **`$sum` & `$count`:** Use `{ $sum: 1 }` or `{ $count: {} }` to count documents in each group, or `{ $sum: "$amount" }` to sum a numeric field.\n* **`$avg`, `$min`, `$max`:** Compute the arithmetic mean, minimum value, or maximum value of a numeric field per group.\n* **`$median`:** Computes the middle value of a numeric distribution: `{ $median: { input: "$age", method: "approximate" } }`.\n* **`$push` & `$addToSet`:** Collect field values from each group into an array (`{ $push: "$name" }`), push entire documents (`{ $push: "$$ROOT" }`), or collect only unique values (`{ $addToSet: "$name" }`).\n* **`$first` & `$last`:** Return a field (or `"$$ROOT"`) from the first or last document encountered in each group.\n* **`$top`, `$topN`, `$bottom`, `$bottomN`:** Sort documents **inside each group** via `sortBy` and return the top/bottom `1` or `n` records with selected `output` fields—without needing a separate prior `$sort` stage.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical $group & Accumulator Queries\n```javascript\n// 1. Group by class, count students, and sort by count descending\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $group: {\n      _id: "$class",\n      count: { $sum: 1 },\n      avgAge: { $avg: "$age" },\n      minAge: { $min: "$age" },\n      maxAge: { $max: "$age" }\n    }\n  },\n  { $sort: { count: -1 } }\n)\n\n// 2. Collect student names (or full documents via $$ROOT) into an array per class\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $group: {\n      _id: "$class",\n      studentNames: { $push: "$name" },\n      uniqueNames: { $addToSet: "$name" },\n      allRecords: { $push: "$$ROOT" }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 3. Compute overall collection-wide Average & Median Age using _id: null\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $group: {\n      _id: null,\n      overallAvgAge: { $avg: "$age" },\n      medianAge: {\n        $median: { input: "$age", method: "approximate" }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 4. $topN: Find the 3 youngest students inside each class group\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $group: {\n      _id: "$class",\n      youngestThree: {\n        $topN: {\n          n: 3,\n          output: ["$name", "$class", "$age",\n          sortBy: { age: 1 }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n```	Yahoo Baba	https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba	655	Lecture 17: Aggregation $group & Accumulators — $sum, $avg, $min, $max, $median, $push, $addToSet, $first/$last & $top/$bottom	Group documents by keys or across whole collections with $group and compute aggregates using $sum, $count, $avg, $min, $max, $median, $push, $addToSet, $first, $last, $topN, and $bottomN.	20	Easy	\N	17	[{"id": "mongo-17-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37321", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37321", "name": "Problem #374: Count orders by status", "title": "Problem #374: Count orders by status", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "374", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: count documents in **orders** per **status**. Use **$group** and **$sum: 1**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37321"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3731b", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3731b", "name": "Problem #376: Unwind orders items then group by productId", "title": "Problem #376: Unwind orders items then group by productId", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "376", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: **$unwind** **orders** by **items**, then **$group** by **items.productId** and sum **items.qty**. Sort by sum descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3731b"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-3", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37312", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37312", "name": "Problem #379: Aggregation: average order total by customer", "title": "Problem #379: Aggregation: average order total by customer", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "379", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate: group **orders** by **customerId**, compute average **total**. Use **$group** and **$avg**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37312"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-4", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3730f", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3730f", "name": "Problem #380: Aggregation: total revenue by order status", "title": "Problem #380: Aggregation: total revenue by order status", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "380", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate **orders**: group by **status**, sum **total** as revenue. Use **$group** and **$sum**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db3730f"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-5", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b57a3fe74e0db3726a", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b57a3fe74e0db3726a", "name": "Problem #435: Users count per departmentId", "title": "Problem #435: Users count per departmentId", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "435", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: group users by **departmentId**, count. Sort by count descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b57a3fe74e0db3726a"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-6", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b57a3fe74e0db37267", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b57a3fe74e0db37267", "name": "Problem #436: Min and max budget in departments", "title": "Problem #436: Min and max budget in departments", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "436", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: one document with **minBudget** and **maxBudget** from departments. Use **$group** _id null and **$min** **$max**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b57a3fe74e0db37267"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-7", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371dd", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371dd", "name": "Problem #472: Sum of department budgets", "title": "Problem #472: Sum of department budgets", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "472", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: single document with **totalBudget** as sum of all **budget** in departments. Use **$group** with _id null.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b47a3fe74e0db371dd"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-8", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371cb", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371cb", "name": "Problem #478: Limit aggregation to 5", "title": "Problem #478: Limit aggregation to 5", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "478", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate: group users by **role**, count, sort by count descending, **$limit** 5.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371cb"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-9", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371c8", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371c8", "name": "Problem #479: Sort aggregation by count descending", "title": "Problem #479: Sort aggregation by count descending", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "479", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregate: group users by **city**, count, then sort by count descending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371c8"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-10", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b9", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b9", "name": "Problem #484: Average age per city", "title": "Problem #484: Average age per city", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "484", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Aggregation: group **users** by **city** and compute average **age**. Use **$avg**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b9"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-11", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b6", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b6", "name": "Problem #485: Users grouped by role", "title": "Problem #485: Users grouped by role", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "485", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Group **users** by **role** and count. Output _id (role) and count.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b6"}, {"id": "mongo-17-prob-12", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b3", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b3", "name": "Problem #486: Users grouped by city", "title": "Problem #486: Users grouped by city", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "486", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Use aggregation: group **users** by **city** and count how many per city. Use **$group** and **$sum**.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b37a3fe74e0db371b3"}]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/group/	\N	\N	\N
22192	Area & Perimeter	aptitude-quant-area-and-perimeter	Master geometric mensuration: scaling logic for 2D shapes, internal/external pathway areas, cyclic quadrilaterals, and composite figure optimization.	2026-10-07 11:47:07.968562+05:30	### 📌 1. Core 2D Geometry Formulas\n* **Triangle:** `Area = 1/2 × Base × Height`\n  * Equilateral Triangle: `Area = (√3 / 4) × a^2`, `Height = (√3 / 2) × a`\n  * Heron's Formula: `Area = √(s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c))` where `s = (a+b+c)/2`\n* **Rectangle:** `Area = L × B`, `Perimeter = 2(L + B)`, `Diagonal = √(L^2 + B^2)`\n* **Square:** `Area = a^2`, `Perimeter = 4a`, `Diagonal = a√2`\n* **Circle:** `Area = πr^2`, `Circumference = 2πr`\n* **Parallelogram:** `Area = Base × Height`\n* **Rhombus:** `Area = 1/2 × d1 × d2` (Product of diagonals)\n* **Trapezium:** `Area = 1/2 × (Sum of parallel sides) × Height`\n\n### 💡 2. High-Yield Placement Shortcuts\n* **Pathway Inside/Outside a Rectangle:**\n  * Area of a uniform path of width `w` running **OUTSIDE** a rectangle of dimensions `L×B`: **`2w(L + B + 2w)`**\n  * Area of a uniform path of width `w` running **INSIDE** a rectangle: **`2w(L + B - 2w)`**\n  * Area of two perpendicular cross-paths of width `w` cutting through the center: **`w(L + B - w)`**\n* **Scaling Rules:**\n  * If all linear dimensions of a 2D figure are multiplied by `k`, the **Perimeter** is multiplied by `k`, and the **Area** is multiplied by **`k^2`**.\n  * If the radius of a circle increases by `x%`, its area increases by **`2x + (x^2)/100 %`**.\n\n### 💡 3. Composite and Inscribed Figures\n* **Largest Circle inside a Square:** Radius `r = a / 2` (where `a` is the side of the square). Area = `(π/4)a^2`.\n* **Largest Square inside a Circle:** Diagonal of square = Diameter of circle (`d`). Area = `d^2 / 2 = 2r^2`.\n* **Largest Triangle inside a Semicircle:** The triangle with its base as the diameter and its third vertex on the arc is a right-angled triangle. Its maximum Area = `r^2`.	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xwqUyOT-cmU", "type": "youtube", "title": "Area & Perimeter - Shortcuts & Tricks for Placement Tests, Job Interviews & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-area-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Pathway Outside a Rectangle] A rectangular lawn is 60m long and 40m wide. A uniform path of 5m width surrounds it on the outside. Find the area of the path.", "options": ["A) 1,000 sq meters", "B) 1,100 sq meters", "C) 1,200 sq meters", "D) 1,400 sq meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A rectangular lawn is exactly 60 meters long and exactly 40 meters wide. A uniform walking path exactly 5 meters wide surrounds it completely on the OUTSIDE. What is the total surface area of this path?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Shortcut formula for path outside: Area = 2w(L + B + 2w). Here L = 60, B = 40, w = 5. Area = 2(5) × (60 + 40 + 10) = 10 × 110 = 1,100 sq meters. (Alternative: Outer rectangle is 70×50 = 3500. Inner is 60×40 = 2400. Area = 3500 - 2400 = 1100).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-area-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Percentage Change in Area] If the radius of a circle is increased by 20%, what will be the percentage increase in its area?", "options": ["A) 40%", "B) 44%", "C) 48%", "D) 144%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the radius of a perfect circle is increased by exactly 20%, what will be the exact percentage increase in the total surface area of the circle?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Since Area = πr^2, an increase of x% in radius leads to a successive increase in area. Use the formula: Net % change = x + x + (x^2)/100. Here x = 20. Change = 20 + 20 + (400/100) = 40 + 4 = 44% increase.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-area-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Equilateral Triangle into Circle] A wire is bent in the form of an equilateral triangle enclosing an area of 121√3 sq cm. If it is bent into a circle, find its area.", "options": ["A) 346.5 sq cm", "B) 364.0 sq cm", "C) 385.5 sq cm", "D) 402.5 sq cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A uniform copper wire is bent into the shape of a perfect equilateral triangle, enclosing an area of exactly 121√3 sq cm. If this identical piece of wire is unbent and rebent into the shape of a perfect circle, what will be the total area enclosed by the circle? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Area of equilateral triangle = (√3/4)a^2 = 121√3 => a^2 = 121 × 4 = 484 => a = 22 cm. 2) The perimeter of the triangle (which is the length of the wire) = 3 × 22 = 66 cm. 3) This wire forms a circle: Circumference 2πr = 66 => 2 × (22/7) × r = 66 => 44r / 7 = 66 => r = (66 × 7) / 44 = 10.5 cm. 4) Area of circle = πr^2 = (22/7) × 10.5 × 10.5 = (22/7) × 110.25 = 346.5 sq cm.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-area-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Diagonals of a Rhombus] The area of a rhombus is 216 sq cm and one of its diagonals is 24 cm. Find its perimeter.", "options": ["A) 40 cm", "B) 52 cm", "C) 60 cm", "D) 68 cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The total surface area of a rhombus is exactly 216 sq cm and the length of one of its diagonals is exactly 24 cm. What is the total perimeter of the rhombus?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Area of rhombus = 1/2 × d1 × d2 => 216 = 1/2 × 24 × d2 => 216 = 12 × d2 => d2 = 216 / 12 = 18 cm. 2) The diagonals of a rhombus bisect each other at right angles. The half-diagonals are 12 cm and 9 cm. 3) The side of the rhombus forms a right triangle with legs 12 and 9. Side = √(12^2 + 9^2) = √(144 + 81) = √225 = 15 cm. 4) Perimeter = 4 × side = 4 × 15 = 60 cm.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-area-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Cost of Carpeting a Room] The length of a room is 15m and breadth is 12m. Find the cost of carpeting it with a 75cm wide carpet at ₹20 per meter.", "options": ["A) ₹3,600", "B) ₹4,200", "C) ₹4,800", "D) ₹5,400"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A rectangular room is exactly 15 meters long and 12 meters broad. What is the exact total cost of completely carpeting the floor of the room using a carpet that is exactly 75 cm wide, if the carpet is priced at ₹20 per linear meter?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Area of the floor = Length × Breadth = 15 × 12 = 180 sq meters. 2) The area of the carpet must equal the area of the floor. Area of carpet = Length of carpet × Width of carpet. 180 = Length × 0.75m => Length of carpet = 180 / 0.75 = 240 meters. 3) Total cost = Length in meters × Cost per meter = 240 × 20 = ₹4,800.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-area-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Area of Inscribed Square] A circle is circumscribed around a square of side 10 cm. Find the area of the circle.", "options": ["A) 157.0 sq cm", "B) 314.0 sq cm", "C) 100.0 sq cm", "D) 78.5 sq cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A perfect circle is perfectly circumscribed around (drawn exactly outside, touching the vertices of) a square whose sides are exactly 10 cm long. What is the exact area of the circle? (Take π = 3.14)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "When a circle circumscribes a square, the diagonal of the square acts as the diameter of the circle! 1) Diagonal of square = a√2 = 10√2. 2) Diameter of circle = 10√2 => Radius r = 5√2. 3) Area of circle = πr^2 = 3.14 × (5√2)^2 = 3.14 × (25 × 2) = 3.14 × 50 = 157 sq cm.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-area-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Cross-Path Area Calculation] A rectangular park is 80m × 60m. Two crossroads 5m wide run parallel to the sides. Find the area of the path.", "options": ["A) 675 sq m", "B) 700 sq m", "C) 725 sq m", "D) 750 sq m"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A rectangular park measures exactly 80m by 60m. Two perfectly straight crossroads, each uniformly 5 meters wide, run through the center of the park parallel to its sides and intersect each other at right angles. What is the exact total area of these crossroads?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Area of length-wise path = L × w = 80 × 5 = 400. Area of breadth-wise path = B × w = 60 × 5 = 300. The central 5×5 intersection square is counted twice, so subtract it: 5 × 5 = 25. Total path area = 400 + 300 - 25 = 675 sq m. (Shortcut: w(L + B - w) = 5(80 + 60 - 5) = 5(135) = 675).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-area-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Area of Largest Triangle in Semicircle] What is the area of the largest triangle that can be inscribed in a semicircle of radius r?", "options": ["A) 0.5 × πr^2", "B) r^2", "C) 2r^2", "D) r^2 / 2"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the exact algebraic expression for the maximum possible surface area of a triangle that can be perfectly inscribed inside a semicircle of radius exactly r?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "To maximize the area of a triangle inscribed in a semicircle, the base of the triangle must lie on the diameter of the semicircle (Base = 2r), and the third vertex must touch the highest point on the circular arc, making the Height = r. Max Area = 1/2 × Base × Height = 1/2 × (2r) × r = r^2.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-area-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Number of Tiles for a Floor] How many square tiles of side 20 cm are needed to cover a floor of 4m × 5m?", "options": ["A) 250", "B) 400", "C) 500", "D) 1000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly how many identical square tiles, each having a side length of exactly 20 cm, are required to completely cover a rectangular floor measuring exactly 4 meters by 5 meters?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Convert floor dimensions to cm: L = 400 cm, B = 500 cm. Area of floor = 400 × 500 = 200,000 sq cm. Area of one tile = 20 × 20 = 400 sq cm. Number of tiles = Total Area / Tile Area = 200,000 / 400 = 500 tiles.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-area-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Trapezium Area and Height] The parallel sides of a trapezium are 20m and 30m. Its area is 500 sq m. Find distance between them.", "options": ["A) 10 meters", "B) 15 meters", "C) 20 meters", "D) 25 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The lengths of the two parallel sides of a regular trapezium are exactly 20 meters and 30 meters. If the total surface area of the trapezium is exactly 500 square meters, what is the exact perpendicular distance between its parallel sides?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Area of trapezium = 1/2 × (Sum of parallel sides) × Height. 500 = 1/2 × (20 + 30) × h => 500 = 1/2 × 50 × h => 500 = 25 × h => h = 500 / 25 = 20 meters.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-area-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Square and Rectangle Equivalent Area] The perimeter of a square is 48 cm. The area of a rectangle is 4 sq cm less than the area of the square. If the length of the rectangle is 14 cm, find its perimeter.", "options": ["A) 40 cm", "B) 44 cm", "C) 48 cm", "D) 52 cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The exact perimeter of a perfect square is 48 cm. The total surface area of a rectangle is exactly 4 square cm less than the surface area of the square. If the length of the rectangle is exactly 14 cm, what is the total perimeter of the rectangle?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Perimeter of square = 4a = 48 => a = 12 cm. 2) Area of square = 12^2 = 144 sq cm. 3) Area of rectangle = 144 - 4 = 140 sq cm. 4) Area = L × B => 140 = 14 × B => B = 10 cm. 5) Perimeter of rectangle = 2(L + B) = 2(14 + 10) = 2(24) = 48 cm.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-area-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Wheel Revolutions for Distance] The diameter of a wheel is 1.26 m. How far will it travel in 500 revolutions? (π = 22/7)", "options": ["A) 1,480 meters", "B) 1,860 meters", "C) 1,980 meters", "D) 2,160 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The outer diameter of a circular wheel is exactly 1.26 meters. Exactly how far will the wheel travel in a straight line after completing exactly 500 full revolutions? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Distance in 1 revolution = Circumference = π × d = (22/7) × 1.26 = 22 × 0.18 = 3.96 meters. 2) Distance in 500 revolutions = 500 × 3.96 = 1980 meters.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-area-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Percentage Error in Area] If the length of a rectangle is measured 5% in excess and the breadth is measured 4% in deficit, find the percentage error in the calculated area.", "options": ["A) 0.8% excess", "B) 1.0% deficit", "C) 1.2% excess", "D) 1.0% excess"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Due to a faulty measuring tape, the length of a rectangle is recorded exactly 5% in excess of its true length, and its breadth is recorded exactly 4% in deficit (less) of its true breadth. What is the exact percentage error in the mathematically calculated area?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let original L = 100, B = 100. True Area = 10000. Measured L = 105, Measured B = 96. Measured Area = 105 × 96 = 10080. Error = 10080 - 10000 = +80. % Error = (80 / 10000) × 100 = 0.8% excess. (Shortcut: x + y + xy/100 = 5 - 4 - 20/100 = 1 - 0.2 = 0.8%).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-area-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Circle and Square Equivalent Perimeter] The circumference of a circle equals the perimeter of a square. If the area of the circle is 616 sq cm, find the area of the square.", "options": ["A) 384 sq cm", "B) 484 sq cm", "C) 529 sq cm", "D) 616 sq cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The exact mathematical circumference of a perfect circle is exactly equal to the total perimeter of a perfect square. If the surface area of the circle is exactly 616 sq cm, what is the exact total surface area of the square? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Area of circle = πr^2 = 616 => (22/7)r^2 = 616 => r^2 = 616 × 7 / 22 = 28 × 7 = 196 => r = 14 cm. 2) Circumference = 2πr = 2 × (22/7) × 14 = 88 cm. 3) Since perimeter of square = 4a = 88, the side of the square a = 22 cm. 4) Area of the square = a^2 = 22^2 = 484 sq cm.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-area-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Finding Ratio of Areas of Inscribed and Circumscribed Circles] What is the ratio of the areas of the inscribed and circumscribed circles of an equilateral triangle?", "options": ["A) 1 : 2", "B) 1 : 3", "C) 1 : 4", "D) 1 : √2"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "For a perfect equilateral triangle, what is the exact mathematical ratio of the surface area of its perfectly inscribed circle (incircle) to the surface area of its perfectly circumscribed circle (circumcircle)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For an equilateral triangle of side a: 1) Inradius r = a / (2√3). 2) Circumradius R = a / √3. 3) Notice that R is exactly twice the inradius (R = 2r). 4) Since Area ∝ Radius^2, the ratio of their areas is r^2 : (2r)^2 = r^2 : 4r^2 = 1 : 4.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-area-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Parallelogram Altitude Ratio] A parallelogram has sides 15 cm and 10 cm. The distance between the 15 cm sides is 6 cm. Find the distance between the 10 cm sides.", "options": ["A) 4 cm", "B) 6 cm", "C) 9 cm", "D) 12 cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A regular parallelogram has adjacent side lengths of 15 cm and 10 cm. If the perpendicular distance (altitude) between the two longer parallel sides (15 cm) is exactly 6 cm, what is the exact perpendicular distance between the two shorter parallel sides (10 cm)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The area of a parallelogram is Base × Altitude. 1) Using the longer side as base: Area = 15 × 6 = 90 sq cm. 2) Using the shorter side as base: Area = 10 × h2. Since the area must be identical, 10 × h2 = 90 => h2 = 9 cm.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-area-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Heron's Formula for Triangle Area] The sides of a triangle are 13 cm, 14 cm, and 15 cm. Find its area.", "options": ["A) 78 sq cm", "B) 84 sq cm", "C) 90 sq cm", "D) 96 sq cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A scalene triangle has exact side lengths of 13 cm, 14 cm, and 15 cm. What is the exact total surface area of this triangle?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Use Heron's Formula: s = (a + b + c) / 2 = (13 + 14 + 15) / 2 = 42 / 2 = 21. Area = √(s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)) = √(21 × 8 × 7 × 6). Expand into prime factors: √( (3×7) × (2^3) × 7 × (2×3) ) = √( 2^4 × 3^2 × 7^2 ) = 2^2 × 3 × 7 = 4 × 21 = 84 sq cm.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-area-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Wire Bent from Rectangle to Circle] A wire in the form of a rectangle 18.7 cm by 11 cm is rebent into a circle. Find the radius of the circle.", "options": ["A) 8.50 cm", "B) 9.45 cm", "C) 10.50 cm", "D) 12.00 cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A continuous wire is currently bent in the exact form of a rectangle measuring 18.7 cm long and 11 cm broad. If this entire wire is unbent and flawlessly reshaped into a perfect circle, what will be the exact radius of the newly formed circle? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Length of the wire = Perimeter of rectangle = 2(L + B) = 2(18.7 + 11) = 2(29.7) = 59.4 cm. 2) The circumference of the circle will equal this length: 2πr = 59.4 => 2 × (22/7) × r = 59.4 => 44r / 7 = 59.4 => r = (59.4 × 7) / 44 = 415.8 / 44 = 9.45 cm.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-area-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Circular Park Path Area] A circular park has a radius of 21 m. A 7 m wide path runs outside around it. Find the area of the path.", "options": ["A) 1,078 sq m", "B) 1,120 sq m", "C) 1,232 sq m", "D) 1,540 sq m"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A perfect circular park has an exact radius of 21 meters. A uniform walking path exactly 7 meters wide runs completely completely around the park on the OUTSIDE. What is the exact total surface area of this path? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Inner radius r = 21 m. The path is 7m wide, so the Outer radius R = 21 + 7 = 28 m. Area of the path = Area of Outer Circle - Area of Inner Circle = πR^2 - πr^2 = π(R^2 - r^2) = π(R - r)(R + r) = (22/7) × (28 - 21) × (28 + 21) = (22/7) × 7 × 49 = 22 × 49 = 1,078 sq meters.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-area-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Area of Sector and Arc Length] Find the area of a sector of a circle whose radius is 14 cm and the length of the corresponding arc is 22 cm.", "options": ["A) 154 sq cm", "B) 176 sq cm", "C) 198 sq cm", "D) 214 sq cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the exact mathematical area of a specific sector of a circle, if the circle has an exact radius of 14 cm and the length of the corresponding arc for this sector is exactly 22 cm?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "A fantastic shortcut formula links the area of a sector directly to its arc length (L) and radius (r) without needing to find the central angle: Area of Sector = 1/2 × L × r. Here, L = 22 cm and r = 14 cm. Area = 1/2 × 22 × 14 = 11 × 14 = 154 sq cm.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. Core 2D Geometry Formulas\n* **Triangle:** `Area = 1/2 × Base × Height`\n  * Equilateral Triangle: `Area = (√3 / 4) × a^2`, `Height = (√3 / 2) × a`\n  * Heron's Formula: `Area = √(s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c))` where `s = (a+b+c)/2`\n* **Rectangle:** `Area = L × B`, `Perimeter = 2(L + B)`, `Diagonal = √(L^2 + B^2)`\n* **Square:** `Area = a^2`, `Perimeter = 4a`, `Diagonal = a√2`\n* **Circle:** `Area = πr^2`, `Circumference = 2πr`\n* **Parallelogram:** `Area = Base × Height`\n* **Rhombus:** `Area = 1/2 × d1 × d2` (Product of diagonals)\n* **Trapezium:** `Area = 1/2 × (Sum of parallel sides) × Height`\n\n### 💡 2. High-Yield Placement Shortcuts\n* **Pathway Inside/Outside a Rectangle:**\n  * Area of a uniform path of width `w` running **OUTSIDE** a rectangle of dimensions `L×B`: **`2w(L + B + 2w)`**\n  * Area of a uniform path of width `w` running **INSIDE** a rectangle: **`2w(L + B - 2w)`**\n  * Area of two perpendicular cross-paths of width `w` cutting through the center: **`w(L + B - w)`**\n* **Scaling Rules:**\n  * If all linear dimensions of a 2D figure are multiplied by `k`, the **Perimeter** is multiplied by `k`, and the **Area** is multiplied by **`k^2`**.\n  * If the radius of a circle increases by `x%`, its area increases by **`2x + (x^2)/100 %`**.\n\n### 💡 3. Composite and Inscribed Figures\n* **Largest Circle inside a Square:** Radius `r = a / 2` (where `a` is the side of the square). Area = `(π/4)a^2`.\n* **Largest Square inside a Circle:** Diagonal of square = Diameter of circle (`d`). Area = `d^2 / 2 = 2r^2`.\n* **Largest Triangle inside a Semicircle:** The triangle with its base as the diameter and its third vertex on the arc is a right-angled triangle. Its maximum Area = `r^2`.	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Area & Perimeter	Master geometric mensuration: scaling logic for 2D shapes, internal/external pathway areas, cyclic quadrilaterals, and composite figure optimization.	53	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	26	[{"id": "apt-area-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Pathway Outside a Rectangle] A rectangular lawn is 60m long and 40m wide. A uniform path of 5m width surrounds it on the outside. Find the area of the path.", "options": ["A) 1,000 sq meters", "B) 1,100 sq meters", "C) 1,200 sq meters", "D) 1,400 sq meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A rectangular lawn is exactly 60 meters long and exactly 40 meters wide. A uniform walking path exactly 5 meters wide surrounds it completely on the OUTSIDE. What is the total surface area of this path?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Shortcut formula for path outside: Area = 2w(L + B + 2w). Here L = 60, B = 40, w = 5. Area = 2(5) × (60 + 40 + 10) = 10 × 110 = 1,100 sq meters. (Alternative: Outer rectangle is 70×50 = 3500. Inner is 60×40 = 2400. Area = 3500 - 2400 = 1100).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-area-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Percentage Change in Area] If the radius of a circle is increased by 20%, what will be the percentage increase in its area?", "options": ["A) 40%", "B) 44%", "C) 48%", "D) 144%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the radius of a perfect circle is increased by exactly 20%, what will be the exact percentage increase in the total surface area of the circle?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Since Area = πr^2, an increase of x% in radius leads to a successive increase in area. Use the formula: Net % change = x + x + (x^2)/100. Here x = 20. Change = 20 + 20 + (400/100) = 40 + 4 = 44% increase.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-area-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Equilateral Triangle into Circle] A wire is bent in the form of an equilateral triangle enclosing an area of 121√3 sq cm. If it is bent into a circle, find its area.", "options": ["A) 346.5 sq cm", "B) 364.0 sq cm", "C) 385.5 sq cm", "D) 402.5 sq cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A uniform copper wire is bent into the shape of a perfect equilateral triangle, enclosing an area of exactly 121√3 sq cm. If this identical piece of wire is unbent and rebent into the shape of a perfect circle, what will be the total area enclosed by the circle? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Area of equilateral triangle = (√3/4)a^2 = 121√3 => a^2 = 121 × 4 = 484 => a = 22 cm. 2) The perimeter of the triangle (which is the length of the wire) = 3 × 22 = 66 cm. 3) This wire forms a circle: Circumference 2πr = 66 => 2 × (22/7) × r = 66 => 44r / 7 = 66 => r = (66 × 7) / 44 = 10.5 cm. 4) Area of circle = πr^2 = (22/7) × 10.5 × 10.5 = (22/7) × 110.25 = 346.5 sq cm.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-area-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Diagonals of a Rhombus] The area of a rhombus is 216 sq cm and one of its diagonals is 24 cm. Find its perimeter.", "options": ["A) 40 cm", "B) 52 cm", "C) 60 cm", "D) 68 cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The total surface area of a rhombus is exactly 216 sq cm and the length of one of its diagonals is exactly 24 cm. What is the total perimeter of the rhombus?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Area of rhombus = 1/2 × d1 × d2 => 216 = 1/2 × 24 × d2 => 216 = 12 × d2 => d2 = 216 / 12 = 18 cm. 2) The diagonals of a rhombus bisect each other at right angles. The half-diagonals are 12 cm and 9 cm. 3) The side of the rhombus forms a right triangle with legs 12 and 9. Side = √(12^2 + 9^2) = √(144 + 81) = √225 = 15 cm. 4) Perimeter = 4 × side = 4 × 15 = 60 cm.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-area-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Cost of Carpeting a Room] The length of a room is 15m and breadth is 12m. Find the cost of carpeting it with a 75cm wide carpet at ₹20 per meter.", "options": ["A) ₹3,600", "B) ₹4,200", "C) ₹4,800", "D) ₹5,400"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A rectangular room is exactly 15 meters long and 12 meters broad. What is the exact total cost of completely carpeting the floor of the room using a carpet that is exactly 75 cm wide, if the carpet is priced at ₹20 per linear meter?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Area of the floor = Length × Breadth = 15 × 12 = 180 sq meters. 2) The area of the carpet must equal the area of the floor. Area of carpet = Length of carpet × Width of carpet. 180 = Length × 0.75m => Length of carpet = 180 / 0.75 = 240 meters. 3) Total cost = Length in meters × Cost per meter = 240 × 20 = ₹4,800.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-area-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Area of Inscribed Square] A circle is circumscribed around a square of side 10 cm. Find the area of the circle.", "options": ["A) 157.0 sq cm", "B) 314.0 sq cm", "C) 100.0 sq cm", "D) 78.5 sq cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A perfect circle is perfectly circumscribed around (drawn exactly outside, touching the vertices of) a square whose sides are exactly 10 cm long. What is the exact area of the circle? (Take π = 3.14)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "When a circle circumscribes a square, the diagonal of the square acts as the diameter of the circle! 1) Diagonal of square = a√2 = 10√2. 2) Diameter of circle = 10√2 => Radius r = 5√2. 3) Area of circle = πr^2 = 3.14 × (5√2)^2 = 3.14 × (25 × 2) = 3.14 × 50 = 157 sq cm.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-area-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Cross-Path Area Calculation] A rectangular park is 80m × 60m. Two crossroads 5m wide run parallel to the sides. Find the area of the path.", "options": ["A) 675 sq m", "B) 700 sq m", "C) 725 sq m", "D) 750 sq m"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A rectangular park measures exactly 80m by 60m. Two perfectly straight crossroads, each uniformly 5 meters wide, run through the center of the park parallel to its sides and intersect each other at right angles. What is the exact total area of these crossroads?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Area of length-wise path = L × w = 80 × 5 = 400. Area of breadth-wise path = B × w = 60 × 5 = 300. The central 5×5 intersection square is counted twice, so subtract it: 5 × 5 = 25. Total path area = 400 + 300 - 25 = 675 sq m. (Shortcut: w(L + B - w) = 5(80 + 60 - 5) = 5(135) = 675).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-area-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Area of Largest Triangle in Semicircle] What is the area of the largest triangle that can be inscribed in a semicircle of radius r?", "options": ["A) 0.5 × πr^2", "B) r^2", "C) 2r^2", "D) r^2 / 2"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the exact algebraic expression for the maximum possible surface area of a triangle that can be perfectly inscribed inside a semicircle of radius exactly r?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "To maximize the area of a triangle inscribed in a semicircle, the base of the triangle must lie on the diameter of the semicircle (Base = 2r), and the third vertex must touch the highest point on the circular arc, making the Height = r. Max Area = 1/2 × Base × Height = 1/2 × (2r) × r = r^2.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-area-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Number of Tiles for a Floor] How many square tiles of side 20 cm are needed to cover a floor of 4m × 5m?", "options": ["A) 250", "B) 400", "C) 500", "D) 1000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly how many identical square tiles, each having a side length of exactly 20 cm, are required to completely cover a rectangular floor measuring exactly 4 meters by 5 meters?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Convert floor dimensions to cm: L = 400 cm, B = 500 cm. Area of floor = 400 × 500 = 200,000 sq cm. Area of one tile = 20 × 20 = 400 sq cm. Number of tiles = Total Area / Tile Area = 200,000 / 400 = 500 tiles.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-area-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Trapezium Area and Height] The parallel sides of a trapezium are 20m and 30m. Its area is 500 sq m. Find distance between them.", "options": ["A) 10 meters", "B) 15 meters", "C) 20 meters", "D) 25 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The lengths of the two parallel sides of a regular trapezium are exactly 20 meters and 30 meters. If the total surface area of the trapezium is exactly 500 square meters, what is the exact perpendicular distance between its parallel sides?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Area of trapezium = 1/2 × (Sum of parallel sides) × Height. 500 = 1/2 × (20 + 30) × h => 500 = 1/2 × 50 × h => 500 = 25 × h => h = 500 / 25 = 20 meters.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-area-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Square and Rectangle Equivalent Area] The perimeter of a square is 48 cm. The area of a rectangle is 4 sq cm less than the area of the square. If the length of the rectangle is 14 cm, find its perimeter.", "options": ["A) 40 cm", "B) 44 cm", "C) 48 cm", "D) 52 cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The exact perimeter of a perfect square is 48 cm. The total surface area of a rectangle is exactly 4 square cm less than the surface area of the square. If the length of the rectangle is exactly 14 cm, what is the total perimeter of the rectangle?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Perimeter of square = 4a = 48 => a = 12 cm. 2) Area of square = 12^2 = 144 sq cm. 3) Area of rectangle = 144 - 4 = 140 sq cm. 4) Area = L × B => 140 = 14 × B => B = 10 cm. 5) Perimeter of rectangle = 2(L + B) = 2(14 + 10) = 2(24) = 48 cm.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-area-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Wheel Revolutions for Distance] The diameter of a wheel is 1.26 m. How far will it travel in 500 revolutions? (π = 22/7)", "options": ["A) 1,480 meters", "B) 1,860 meters", "C) 1,980 meters", "D) 2,160 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The outer diameter of a circular wheel is exactly 1.26 meters. Exactly how far will the wheel travel in a straight line after completing exactly 500 full revolutions? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Distance in 1 revolution = Circumference = π × d = (22/7) × 1.26 = 22 × 0.18 = 3.96 meters. 2) Distance in 500 revolutions = 500 × 3.96 = 1980 meters.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-area-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Percentage Error in Area] If the length of a rectangle is measured 5% in excess and the breadth is measured 4% in deficit, find the percentage error in the calculated area.", "options": ["A) 0.8% excess", "B) 1.0% deficit", "C) 1.2% excess", "D) 1.0% excess"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Due to a faulty measuring tape, the length of a rectangle is recorded exactly 5% in excess of its true length, and its breadth is recorded exactly 4% in deficit (less) of its true breadth. What is the exact percentage error in the mathematically calculated area?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let original L = 100, B = 100. True Area = 10000. Measured L = 105, Measured B = 96. Measured Area = 105 × 96 = 10080. Error = 10080 - 10000 = +80. % Error = (80 / 10000) × 100 = 0.8% excess. (Shortcut: x + y + xy/100 = 5 - 4 - 20/100 = 1 - 0.2 = 0.8%).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-area-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Circle and Square Equivalent Perimeter] The circumference of a circle equals the perimeter of a square. If the area of the circle is 616 sq cm, find the area of the square.", "options": ["A) 384 sq cm", "B) 484 sq cm", "C) 529 sq cm", "D) 616 sq cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The exact mathematical circumference of a perfect circle is exactly equal to the total perimeter of a perfect square. If the surface area of the circle is exactly 616 sq cm, what is the exact total surface area of the square? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Area of circle = πr^2 = 616 => (22/7)r^2 = 616 => r^2 = 616 × 7 / 22 = 28 × 7 = 196 => r = 14 cm. 2) Circumference = 2πr = 2 × (22/7) × 14 = 88 cm. 3) Since perimeter of square = 4a = 88, the side of the square a = 22 cm. 4) Area of the square = a^2 = 22^2 = 484 sq cm.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-area-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Finding Ratio of Areas of Inscribed and Circumscribed Circles] What is the ratio of the areas of the inscribed and circumscribed circles of an equilateral triangle?", "options": ["A) 1 : 2", "B) 1 : 3", "C) 1 : 4", "D) 1 : √2"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "For a perfect equilateral triangle, what is the exact mathematical ratio of the surface area of its perfectly inscribed circle (incircle) to the surface area of its perfectly circumscribed circle (circumcircle)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For an equilateral triangle of side a: 1) Inradius r = a / (2√3). 2) Circumradius R = a / √3. 3) Notice that R is exactly twice the inradius (R = 2r). 4) Since Area ∝ Radius^2, the ratio of their areas is r^2 : (2r)^2 = r^2 : 4r^2 = 1 : 4.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-area-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Parallelogram Altitude Ratio] A parallelogram has sides 15 cm and 10 cm. The distance between the 15 cm sides is 6 cm. Find the distance between the 10 cm sides.", "options": ["A) 4 cm", "B) 6 cm", "C) 9 cm", "D) 12 cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A regular parallelogram has adjacent side lengths of 15 cm and 10 cm. If the perpendicular distance (altitude) between the two longer parallel sides (15 cm) is exactly 6 cm, what is the exact perpendicular distance between the two shorter parallel sides (10 cm)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The area of a parallelogram is Base × Altitude. 1) Using the longer side as base: Area = 15 × 6 = 90 sq cm. 2) Using the shorter side as base: Area = 10 × h2. Since the area must be identical, 10 × h2 = 90 => h2 = 9 cm.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-area-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Heron's Formula for Triangle Area] The sides of a triangle are 13 cm, 14 cm, and 15 cm. Find its area.", "options": ["A) 78 sq cm", "B) 84 sq cm", "C) 90 sq cm", "D) 96 sq cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A scalene triangle has exact side lengths of 13 cm, 14 cm, and 15 cm. What is the exact total surface area of this triangle?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Use Heron's Formula: s = (a + b + c) / 2 = (13 + 14 + 15) / 2 = 42 / 2 = 21. Area = √(s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)) = √(21 × 8 × 7 × 6). Expand into prime factors: √( (3×7) × (2^3) × 7 × (2×3) ) = √( 2^4 × 3^2 × 7^2 ) = 2^2 × 3 × 7 = 4 × 21 = 84 sq cm.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-area-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Wire Bent from Rectangle to Circle] A wire in the form of a rectangle 18.7 cm by 11 cm is rebent into a circle. Find the radius of the circle.", "options": ["A) 8.50 cm", "B) 9.45 cm", "C) 10.50 cm", "D) 12.00 cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A continuous wire is currently bent in the exact form of a rectangle measuring 18.7 cm long and 11 cm broad. If this entire wire is unbent and flawlessly reshaped into a perfect circle, what will be the exact radius of the newly formed circle? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Length of the wire = Perimeter of rectangle = 2(L + B) = 2(18.7 + 11) = 2(29.7) = 59.4 cm. 2) The circumference of the circle will equal this length: 2πr = 59.4 => 2 × (22/7) × r = 59.4 => 44r / 7 = 59.4 => r = (59.4 × 7) / 44 = 415.8 / 44 = 9.45 cm.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-area-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Circular Park Path Area] A circular park has a radius of 21 m. A 7 m wide path runs outside around it. Find the area of the path.", "options": ["A) 1,078 sq m", "B) 1,120 sq m", "C) 1,232 sq m", "D) 1,540 sq m"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A perfect circular park has an exact radius of 21 meters. A uniform walking path exactly 7 meters wide runs completely completely around the park on the OUTSIDE. What is the exact total surface area of this path? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Inner radius r = 21 m. The path is 7m wide, so the Outer radius R = 21 + 7 = 28 m. Area of the path = Area of Outer Circle - Area of Inner Circle = πR^2 - πr^2 = π(R^2 - r^2) = π(R - r)(R + r) = (22/7) × (28 - 21) × (28 + 21) = (22/7) × 7 × 49 = 22 × 49 = 1,078 sq meters.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-area-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Area of Sector and Arc Length] Find the area of a sector of a circle whose radius is 14 cm and the length of the corresponding arc is 22 cm.", "options": ["A) 154 sq cm", "B) 176 sq cm", "C) 198 sq cm", "D) 214 sq cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the exact mathematical area of a specific sector of a circle, if the circle has an exact radius of 14 cm and the length of the corresponding arc for this sector is exactly 22 cm?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "A fantastic shortcut formula links the area of a sector directly to its arc length (L) and radius (r) without needing to find the central angle: Area of Sector = 1/2 × L × r. Here, L = 22 cm and r = 14 cm. Area = 1/2 × 22 × 14 = 11 × 14 = 154 sq cm.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22103	Lecture 18: Joining Collections in MongoDB — $lookup, $unwind, $replaceRoot & $mergeObjects	mongo-lecture-18-aggregation-lookup-unwind-replaceroot-joins	Perform Left Outer Joins across MongoDB collections using $lookup and flatten joined sub-documents into clean top-level objects with $unwind, $replaceRoot, $mergeObjects, and $arrayElemAt.	2026-10-06 19:28:34.895846+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhen normalized data is split across multiple collections (such as `library` books referencing `students`, or `customers` linked to `orders`), MongoDB's **`$lookup`** stage performs a **Left Outer Join** between collections in the same database.\n\n### 💡 The 4 Mandatory Parameters of `$lookup`\n* **`from`:** The target foreign collection name to join with (e.g., `"students"` or `"orders"`).\n* **`localField`:** The field in the primary input collection holding the reference key (e.g., `"student_id"` in `library`).\n* **`foreignField`:** The matching field in the `from` collection (e.g., `"_id"` in `students`).\n* **`as`:** The name of the new array field added to each output document containing the matched foreign documents (e.g., `"student"` or `"order_details"`). If no match exists, `as` is an empty array `[`.\n\n### 💡 Flattening Joined Arrays with `$unwind` vs. `$replaceRoot`\n* **`$unwind: "$<arrayField>"`:** Deconstructs the joined array so each matched object becomes a direct sub-object instead of a 1-element array.\n* **`$replaceRoot` + `$mergeObjects` + `$arrayElemAt`:** Extracts the first matched object from the joined array (`{ $arrayElemAt: ["$student", 0 }`), merges its fields directly with the main document (`"$$ROOT"`), and promotes the merged result to the top level so there are no nested arrays or sub-objects.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical $lookup & Root Flattening Pipelines\n```javascript\n// 1. One-to-Many Join: Fetch all customers along with their placed orders array\ndb.customers.aggregate([\n  {\n    $lookup: {\n      from: "orders",\n      localField: "_id",\n      foreignField: "customer_id",\n      as: "order_details"\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 2. Join students with library books & flatten array with $unwind\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $lookup: {\n      from: "library",\n      localField: "_id",\n      foreignField: "student_id",\n      as: "book"\n    }\n  },\n  { $unwind: "$book" }\n)\n\n// 3. Join library with students & promote student fields to the top-level root\ndb.library.aggregate([\n  {\n    $lookup: {\n      from: "students",\n      localField: "student_id",\n      foreignField: "_id",\n      as: "student"\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    $replaceRoot: {\n      newRoot: {\n        $mergeObjects: [\n          { $arrayElemAt: ["$student", 0 },\n          "$$ROOT"\n        \n      }\n    }\n  },\n  { $project: { student: 0 } }\n)\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/lookup/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/lookup/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 18: Joining Collections in MongoDB"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sIIJHVdm7sA", "title": "MongoDB Aggregation $lookUp Operator Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "30m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[{"id": "mongo-18-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db3746f", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db3746f", "name": "Problem #264: Find order by orderId ORD-1020", "title": "Problem #264: Find order by orderId ORD-1020", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "264", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the order with orderId \\"ORD-1020\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db3746f"}, {"id": "mongo-18-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db37433", "link": "https://mongopractice.onlin