difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return orders where status is \\"delivered\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37387"}, {"id": "mongo-18-prob-10", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37384", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37384", "name": "Problem #341: Orders with status shipped", "title": "Problem #341: Orders with status shipped", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "341", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return orders where status is \\"shipped\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37384"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhen normalized data is split across multiple collections (such as `library` books referencing `students`, or `customers` linked to `orders`), MongoDB's **`$lookup`** stage performs a **Left Outer Join** between collections in the same database.\n\n### 💡 The 4 Mandatory Parameters of `$lookup`\n* **`from`:** The target foreign collection name to join with (e.g., `"students"` or `"orders"`).\n* **`localField`:** The field in the primary input collection holding the reference key (e.g., `"student_id"` in `library`).\n* **`foreignField`:** The matching field in the `from` collection (e.g., `"_id"` in `students`).\n* **`as`:** The name of the new array field added to each output document containing the matched foreign documents (e.g., `"student"` or `"order_details"`). If no match exists, `as` is an empty array `[`.\n\n### 💡 Flattening Joined Arrays with `$unwind` vs. `$replaceRoot`\n* **`$unwind: "$<arrayField>"`:** Deconstructs the joined array so each matched object becomes a direct sub-object instead of a 1-element array.\n* **`$replaceRoot` + `$mergeObjects` + `$arrayElemAt`:** Extracts the first matched object from the joined array (`{ $arrayElemAt: ["$student", 0 }`), merges its fields directly with the main document (`"$$ROOT"`), and promotes the merged result to the top level so there are no nested arrays or sub-objects.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical $lookup & Root Flattening Pipelines\n```javascript\n// 1. One-to-Many Join: Fetch all customers along with their placed orders array\ndb.customers.aggregate([\n  {\n    $lookup: {\n      from: "orders",\n      localField: "_id",\n      foreignField: "customer_id",\n      as: "order_details"\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 2. Join students with library books & flatten array with $unwind\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $lookup: {\n      from: "library",\n      localField: "_id",\n      foreignField: "student_id",\n      as: "book"\n    }\n  },\n  { $unwind: "$book" }\n)\n\n// 3. Join library with students & promote student fields to the top-level root\ndb.library.aggregate([\n  {\n    $lookup: {\n      from: "students",\n      localField: "student_id",\n      foreignField: "_id",\n      as: "student"\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    $replaceRoot: {\n      newRoot: {\n        $mergeObjects: [\n          { $arrayElemAt: ["$student", 0 },\n          "$$ROOT"\n        \n      }\n    }\n  },\n  { $project: { student: 0 } }\n)\n```	Yahoo Baba	https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba	655	Lecture 18: Joining Collections in MongoDB — $lookup, $unwind, $replaceRoot & $mergeObjects	Perform Left Outer Joins across MongoDB collections using $lookup and flatten joined sub-documents into clean top-level objects with $unwind, $replaceRoot, $mergeObjects, and $arrayElemAt.	20	Easy	\N	18	[{"id": "mongo-18-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db3746f", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db3746f", "name": "Problem #264: Find order by orderId ORD-1020", "title": "Problem #264: Find order by orderId ORD-1020", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "264", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the order with orderId \\"ORD-1020\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db3746f"}, {"id": "mongo-18-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db37433", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db37433", "name": "Problem #284: Orders with total gte 150", "title": "Problem #284: Orders with total gte 150", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "284", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return orders where total >= 150.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db37433"}, {"id": "mongo-18-prob-3", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db37430", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db37430", "name": "Problem #285: Orders with total lt 100", "title": "Problem #285: Orders with total lt 100", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "285", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return orders where total < 100.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db37430"}, {"id": "mongo-18-prob-4", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db37418", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db37418", "name": "Problem #293: Find customer by _id 15", "title": "Problem #293: Find customer by _id 15", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "293", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the customer with _id 15.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db37418"}, {"id": "mongo-18-prob-5", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db37412", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db37412", "name": "Problem #295: Find order by customerId 15", "title": "Problem #295: Find order by customerId 15", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "295", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return orders where customerId is 15.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9ba7a3fe74e0db37412"}, {"id": "mongo-18-prob-6", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37392", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37392", "name": "Problem #337: Customers with tier gold", "title": "Problem #337: Customers with tier gold", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "337", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return customers where tier is \\"gold\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37392"}, {"id": "mongo-18-prob-7", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3738f", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3738f", "name": "Problem #338: Customers with tier silver", "title": "Problem #338: Customers with tier silver", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "338", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return customers where tier is \\"silver\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3738f"}, {"id": "mongo-18-prob-8", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3738c", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3738c", "name": "Problem #339: Customers with tier bronze", "title": "Problem #339: Customers with tier bronze", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "339", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return customers where tier is \\"bronze\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db3738c"}, {"id": "mongo-18-prob-9", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37387", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37387", "name": "Problem #340: Orders with status delivered", "title": "Problem #340: Orders with status delivered", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "340", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return orders where status is \\"delivered\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37387"}, {"id": "mongo-18-prob-10", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37384", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37384", "name": "Problem #341: Orders with status shipped", "title": "Problem #341: Orders with status shipped", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "341", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return orders where status is \\"shipped\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b87a3fe74e0db37384"}]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/lookup/	\N	\N	\N
22116	Lecture 31: Importing JSON Datasets — MongoDB Database Tools Setup & mongoimport CLI	mongo-lecture-31-mongoimport-json-database-tools	Install MongoDB Command Line Database Tools, configure the Windows System PATH, and import external JSON files or JSON arrays into MongoDB collections using mongoimport.	2026-10-06 19:52:12.616986+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nTo bulk-import external `.json` or `.csv` files exported from MySQL, Oracle, PostgreSQL, or third-party APIs into MongoDB, MongoDB provides the standalone **`mongoimport`** CLI utility inside the **MongoDB Command Line Database Tools** package.\n\n### 💡 Installing MongoDB Database Tools & Setting `PATH`\n* **Download & Install:** Download the **MongoDB Command Line Database Tools (`.msi`)** package from `[mongodb.com/try/download/database-tools(https://mongodb.com/try/download/database-tools)` and complete the installer.\n* **System Environment Variable (`Path`):** Copy the installed tools binary path:\n  `C:\\Program Files\\MongoDB\\Tools\\100\\bin`\n  and add it to your Windows **System Variables -> `Path`** so `mongoimport`, `mongodump`, and `mongorestore` can be run from any terminal.\n* **Run in OS Terminal (NOT inside `mongosh`):** `mongoimport` is an operating-system command-line executable. Always run it directly from Windows Command Prompt / PowerShell / macOS Terminal—outside of `mongosh`.\n\n### 💡 `mongoimport` CLI Flags Breakdown\n* **`<file_path>`:** Path to the source JSON file (e.g., `D:\\test.json`).\n* **`-d <db_name>` (or `--db`):** Target database name (e.g., `-d school`).\n* **`-c <collection_name>` (or `--collection`):** Optional target collection name (e.g., `-c testing`). If omitted, `mongoimport` automatically creates a collection named after the JSON filename (`test`).\n* **`--jsonArray`:** Required when the JSON file contains an **array of objects** `[ { ... }, { ... } ` rather than newline-delimited JSON objects.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical mongoimport Workflow\n```bash\n# 1. Run mongoimport directly in Windows Command Prompt (outside mongosh)\nmongoimport D:\\test.json -d school -c testing --jsonArray\n# Output: 2 document(s) imported successfully. 0 document(s) failed to import.\n\n# 2. Verify imported collection and documents inside mongosh\nmongosh\nuse school\nshow collections\ndb.testing.find()\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/database-tools/mongoimport/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/database-tools/mongoimport/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 31: Importing JSON Datasets"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9svsSHQTpUI", "title": "MongoDB Import JSON Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "10m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nTo bulk-import external `.json` or `.csv` files exported from MySQL, Oracle, PostgreSQL, or third-party APIs into MongoDB, MongoDB provides the standalone **`mongoimport`** CLI utility inside the **MongoDB Command Line Database Tools** package.\n\n### 💡 Installing MongoDB Database Tools & Setting `PATH`\n* **Download & Install:** Download the **MongoDB Command Line Database Tools (`.msi`)** package from `[mongodb.com/try/download/database-tools(https://mongodb.com/try/download/database-tools)` and complete the installer.\n* **System Environment Variable (`Path`):** Copy the installed tools binary path:\n  `C:\\Program Files\\MongoDB\\Tools\\100\\bin`\n  and add it to your Windows **System Variables -> `Path`** so `mongoimport`, `mongodump`, and `mongorestore` can be run from any terminal.\n* **Run in OS Terminal (NOT inside `mongosh`):** `mongoimport` is an operating-system command-line executable. Always run it directly from Windows Command Prompt / PowerShell / macOS Terminal—outside of `mongosh`.\n\n### 💡 `mongoimport` CLI Flags Breakdown\n* **`<file_path>`:** Path to the source JSON file (e.g., `D:\\test.json`).\n* **`-d <db_name>` (or `--db`):** Target database name (e.g., `-d school`).\n* **`-c <collection_name>` (or `--collection`):** Optional target collection name (e.g., `-c testing`). If omitted, `mongoimport` automatically creates a collection named after the JSON filename (`test`).\n* **`--jsonArray`:** Required when the JSON file contains an **array of objects** `[ { ... }, { ... } ` rather than newline-delimited JSON objects.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical mongoimport Workflow\n```bash\n# 1. Run mongoimport directly in Windows Command Prompt (outside mongosh)\nmongoimport D:\\test.json -d school -c testing --jsonArray\n# Output: 2 document(s) imported successfully. 0 document(s) failed to import.\n\n# 2. Verify imported collection and documents inside mongosh\nmongosh\nuse school\nshow collections\ndb.testing.find()\n```	Yahoo Baba	https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba	655	Lecture 31: Importing JSON Datasets — MongoDB Database Tools Setup & mongoimport CLI	Install MongoDB Command Line Database Tools, configure the Windows System PATH, and import external JSON files or JSON arrays into MongoDB collections using mongoimport.	20	Easy	\N	31	[]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/database-tools/mongoimport/	\N	\N	\N
22126	Lecture 8: JavaScript Objects In-Depth — Dot vs. Bracket Notation, Symbol, Shallow vs. Deep Copy, Destructuring & Hidden Classes	js-lecture-8-objects-in-depth-crud-destructuring-shallow-deep-copy-symbols	Master Object CRUD operations, dot vs. dynamic bracket notation, Object.keys/values/entries, destructuring, shallow copy ({...obj}) vs. deep copy (structuredClone), Symbol keys, and V8 Hidden Classes.	2026-10-06 21:14:25.4193+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nObjects are the foundational building blocks of JavaScript. Every object stores data as **Key-Value Pairs** (where keys are automatically coerced to **Strings** or **`Symbol`s**, and values can be any primitive, array, nested object, or function/method).\n\n### 💡 Dot Notation vs. Bracket Notation (`obj.key` vs. `obj[expr`)\n* **Dot Notation (`user.age`):** Clean and fast, but only works for valid identifier names known statically at write-time.\n* **Bracket Notation (`user["home address"` or `user[dynamicVar`):** Mandatory when:\n  1. The key contains **spaces**, hyphens, or starts with a digit (e.g., `user["email id"`).\n  2. You are accessing a property using a **dynamic variable** (e.g., `const key = "age"; user[key` looks up `"age"`, whereas `user.key` looks for a literal property named `"key"`).\n  3. The key is a **`Symbol`** (`const sym = Symbol("id"); user[sym = 101;`).\n\n### 💡 Iteration, Destructuring & `Symbol` Data Type\n* **Static Enumeration Methods:** `Object.keys(user)` (array of keys), `Object.values(user)` (array of values), and `Object.entries(user)` (array of `[key, value` pairs, ideal for `for...of` loops).\n* **Object Destructuring:** Extract properties into variables cleanly: `const { name, age: userAge, address: { city } } = user;`.\n* **Hidden `Symbol` Keys:** Properties keyed by a `Symbol` (`{[sym: "secret"}`) are skipped by `for...in`, `Object.keys()`, and `JSON.stringify()`, preventing accidental key collisions in libraries.\n\n### 💡 Shallow Copy vs. Deep Copy\n* **Reference Assignment (`const u2 = u1`):** Not a copy at all! Both point to the exact same object.\n* **Shallow Copy (`{ ...u1 }` or `Object.assign({}, u1)`):** Copies **top-level primitives** into a new object, but **nested objects/arrays still share the same memory reference**! Modifying `u2.address.city` will still mutate `u1.address.city`.\n* **Deep Copy (`structuredClone(u1)`):** Recursively clones every nested object and array into completely independent memory blocks.\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Dynamic Keys, Destructuring & Shallow vs. Deep Copy\n```javascript\nconst idSym = Symbol("user_id");\nconst user = {\n  name: "Rohit",\n  "account balance": 3400,\n  [idSym: 991,\n  address: { city: "Kotdwar", pin: 246149 }\n};\n\n// 1. Dot vs. Bracket Notation & Deleting Properties\nconsole.log(user.name);               // "Rohit"\nconsole.log(user["account balance"); // 3400\nconsole.log(user[idSym);             // 991\ndelete user["account balance";\n\n// 2. Object Destructuring (with aliasing and nested extraction)\nconst { name: fullName, address: { city } } = user;\nconsole.log(fullName, city); // "Rohit", "Kotdwar"\n\n// 3. Shallow Copy ({ ...user }) vs. Deep Copy (structuredClone)\nconst shallowCopy = { ...user };\nshallowCopy.address.city = "Delhi"; // MUTATES user.address.city too!\n\nconst deepCopy = structuredClone({ name: "Rohit", address: { city: "Kotdwar" } });\ndeepCopy.address.city = "Mumbai";   // Original object remains "Kotdwar"!\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object", "title": "MDN Web Docs — Object Built-In Reference"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iuZkPCBBV5U", "title": "JavaScript Objects Explained In-Depth  | Javascript Full Course #08", "duration": "70m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-8-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What is the difference between a Shallow Copy and a Deep Copy of an object in JavaScript? How do {...obj}, JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj)), and structuredClone(obj) compare?", "title": "Q1: What is the difference between a Shallow Copy and a Deep Copy of an object in JavaScript? How do {...obj}, JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj)), and structuredClone(obj) compare?", "answer": "A Shallow Copy (created via {...obj} or Object.assign({}, obj)) creates a new top-level object and copies top-level primitive values, but copies only the memory references of nested objects/arrays—so mutating a nested object in the copy mutates the original. A Deep Copy recursively clones all nested levels. JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj)) performs a deep copy but fails on Date objects (turns them into strings), undefined, functions, Symbols, BigInts, and circular references. Modern JavaScript's built-in structuredClone(obj) performs a true deep copy that properly preserves Dates, Maps, Sets, nested objects, and circular references.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "8-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "A Shallow Copy (created via {...obj} or Object.assign({}, obj)) creates a new top-level object and copies top-level primitive values, but copies only the memory references of nested objects/arrays—so mutating a nested object in the copy mutates the original. A Deep Copy recursively clones all nested levels. JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj)) performs a deep copy but fails on Date objects (turns them into strings), undefined, functions, Symbols, BigInts, and circular references. Modern JavaScript's built-in structuredClone(obj) performs a true deep copy that properly preserves Dates, Maps, Sets, nested objects, and circular references."}, {"id": "js-lec-8-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: When must you use Bracket Notation obj[key] instead of Dot Notation obj.key?", "title": "Q2: When must you use Bracket Notation obj[key] instead of Dot Notation obj.key?", "answer": "You must use Bracket Notation in three scenarios: 1) When the property name is stored in a dynamic variable or expression (obj[variableName]), because obj.variableName looks for the literal string key \\"variableName\\". 2) When the property key contains spaces, hyphens, or special characters, or starts with a number (e.g., obj[\\"first name\\"]). 3) When the property key is a Symbol (obj[mySymbol]).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "8-q2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "You must use Bracket Notation in three scenarios: 1) When the property name is stored in a dynamic variable or expression (obj[variableName]), because obj.variableName looks for the literal string key \\"variableName\\". 2) When the property key contains spaces, hyphens, or special characters, or starts with a number (e.g., obj[\\"first name\\"]). 3) When the property key is a Symbol (obj[mySymbol])."}, {"id": "js-lec-8-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is the Symbol data type, and why is it used as an object key?", "title": "Q3: What is the Symbol data type, and why is it used as an object key?", "answer": "Every call to Symbol(\\"desc\\") returns a guaranteed unique primitive value (Symbol(\\"id\\") === Symbol(\\"id\\") is false). When used as an object property key ({ [mySym]: 123 }), it prevents property name collisions with third-party code or future object keys, and it remains hidden from standard iteration (for...in, Object.keys(), Object.entries(), JSON.stringify()), only accessible via the Symbol reference itself or Object.getOwnPropertySymbols().", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "8-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Every call to Symbol(\\"desc\\") returns a guaranteed unique primitive value (Symbol(\\"id\\") === Symbol(\\"id\\") is false). When used as an object property key ({ [mySym]: 123 }), it prevents property name collisions with third-party code or future object keys, and it remains hidden from standard iteration (for...in, Object.keys(), Object.entries(), JSON.stringify()), only accessible via the Symbol reference itself or Object.getOwnPropertySymbols()."}, {"id": "js-lec-8-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What is the difference between Object.freeze(obj) and Object.seal(obj)?", "title": "Q4: What is the difference between Object.freeze(obj) and Object.seal(obj)?", "answer": "Object.seal(obj) prevents adding new properties or deleting existing properties (sets configurable: false), but still allows modifying the values of existing writable properties. Object.freeze(obj) goes further: it prevents adding, deleting, AND modifying any existing property values (sets writable: false and configurable: false). Note that both are shallow—nested objects inside a frozen object must be frozen recursively if deep immutability is required.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "8-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Object.seal(obj) prevents adding new properties or deleting existing properties (sets configurable: false), but still allows modifying the values of existing writable properties. Object.freeze(obj) goes further: it prevents adding, deleting, AND modifying any existing property values (sets writable: false and configurable: false). Note that both are shallow—nested objects inside a frozen object must be frozen recursively if deep immutability is required."}, {"id": "js-lec-8-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Why should you use for...of with Object.keys(obj) / Object.entries(obj) instead of a raw for...in loop?", "title": "Q5: Why should you use for...of with Object.keys(obj) / Object.entries(obj) instead of a raw for...in loop?", "answer": "A raw for (let key in obj) loop iterates over all enumerable string properties of the object PLUS any enumerable properties inherited up the prototype chain! In contrast, Object.keys(obj), Object.values(obj), and Object.entries(obj) return only the object's own enumerable properties (ignoring inherited prototype properties).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "8-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "A raw for (let key in obj) loop iterates over all enumerable string properties of the object PLUS any enumerable properties inherited up the prototype chain! In contrast, Object.keys(obj), Object.values(obj), and Object.entries(obj) return only the object's own enumerable properties (ignoring inherited prototype properties)."}, {"id": "js-lec-8-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: How does the V8 Engine optimize object property access internally using Hidden Classes (Shapes) and Inline Caching?", "title": "Q6: How does the V8 Engine optimize object property access internally using Hidden Classes (Shapes) and Inline Caching?", "answer": "Because JS objects can have properties added dynamically, a naive hash-map lookup on every obj.x access would be slow. V8 attaches a \\"Hidden Class\\" (Shape/Map) to every object that records the exact memory offset of each property key, while storing the property values in a contiguous array. When multiple objects share the same property creation order, they share the same Hidden Class, allowing V8 to use Inline Caching to read obj.x in a single O(1) memory offset instruction just like a C++ struct!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "8-q6", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Because JS objects can have properties added dynamically, a naive hash-map lookup on every obj.x access would be slow. V8 attaches a \\"Hidden Class\\" (Shape/Map) to every object that records the exact memory offset of each property key, while storing the property values in a contiguous array. When multiple objects share the same property creation order, they share the same Hidden Class, allowing V8 to use Inline Caching to read obj.x in a single O(1) memory offset instruction just like a C++ struct!"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nObjects are the foundational building blocks of JavaScript. Every object stores data as **Key-Value Pairs** (where keys are automatically coerced to **Strings** or **`Symbol`s**, and values can be any primitive, array, nested object, or function/method).\n\n### 💡 Dot Notation vs. Bracket Notation (`obj.key` vs. `obj[expr`)\n* **Dot Notation (`user.age`):** Clean and fast, but only works for valid identifier names known statically at write-time.\n* **Bracket Notation (`user["home address"` or `user[dynamicVar`):** Mandatory when:\n  1. The key contains **spaces**, hyphens, or starts with a digit (e.g., `user["email id"`).\n  2. You are accessing a property using a **dynamic variable** (e.g., `const key = "age"; user[key` looks up `"age"`, whereas `user.key` looks for a literal property named `"key"`).\n  3. The key is a **`Symbol`** (`const sym = Symbol("id"); user[sym = 101;`).\n\n### 💡 Iteration, Destructuring & `Symbol` Data Type\n* **Static Enumeration Methods:** `Object.keys(user)` (array of keys), `Object.values(user)` (array of values), and `Object.entries(user)` (array of `[key, value` pairs, ideal for `for...of` loops).\n* **Object Destructuring:** Extract properties into variables cleanly: `const { name, age: userAge, address: { city } } = user;`.\n* **Hidden `Symbol` Keys:** Properties keyed by a `Symbol` (`{[sym: "secret"}`) are skipped by `for...in`, `Object.keys()`, and `JSON.stringify()`, preventing accidental key collisions in libraries.\n\n### 💡 Shallow Copy vs. Deep Copy\n* **Reference Assignment (`const u2 = u1`):** Not a copy at all! Both point to the exact same object.\n* **Shallow Copy (`{ ...u1 }` or `Object.assign({}, u1)`):** Copies **top-level primitives** into a new object, but **nested objects/arrays still share the same memory reference**! Modifying `u2.address.city` will still mutate `u1.address.city`.\n* **Deep Copy (`structuredClone(u1)`):** Recursively clones every nested object and array into completely independent memory blocks.\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Dynamic Keys, Destructuring & Shallow vs. Deep Copy\n```javascript\nconst idSym = Symbol("user_id");\nconst user = {\n  name: "Rohit",\n  "account balance": 3400,\n  [idSym: 991,\n  address: { city: "Kotdwar", pin: 246149 }\n};\n\n// 1. Dot vs. Bracket Notation & Deleting Properties\nconsole.log(user.name);               // "Rohit"\nconsole.log(user["account balance"); // 3400\nconsole.log(user[idSym);             // 991\ndelete user["account balance";\n\n// 2. Object Destructuring (with aliasing and nested extraction)\nconst { name: fullName, address: { city } } = user;\nconsole.log(fullName, city); // "Rohit", "Kotdwar"\n\n// 3. Shallow Copy ({ ...user }) vs. Deep Copy (structuredClone)\nconst shallowCopy = { ...user };\nshallowCopy.address.city = "Delhi"; // MUTATES user.address.city too!\n\nconst deepCopy = structuredClone({ name: "Rohit", address: { city: "Kotdwar" } });\ndeepCopy.address.city = "Mumbai";   // Original object remains "Kotdwar"!\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 8: JavaScript Objects In-Depth — Dot vs. Bracket Notation, Symbol, Shallow vs. Deep Copy, Destructuring & Hidden Classes	Master Object CRUD operations, dot vs. dynamic bracket notation, Object.keys/values/entries, destructuring, shallow copy ({...obj}) vs. deep copy (structuredClone), Symbol keys, and V8 Hidden Classes.	20	Easy	\N	8	[{"id": "js-lec-8-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What is the difference between a Shallow Copy and a Deep Copy of an object in JavaScript? How do {...obj}, JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj)), and structuredClone(obj) compare?", "title": "Q1: What is the difference between a Shallow Copy and a Deep Copy of an object in JavaScript? How do {...obj}, JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj)), and structuredClone(obj) compare?", "answer": "A Shallow Copy (created via {...obj} or Object.assign({}, obj)) creates a new top-level object and copies top-level primitive values, but copies only the memory references of nested objects/arrays—so mutating a nested object in the copy mutates the original. A Deep Copy recursively clones all nested levels. JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj)) performs a deep copy but fails on Date objects (turns them into strings), undefined, functions, Symbols, BigInts, and circular references. Modern JavaScript's built-in structuredClone(obj) performs a true deep copy that properly preserves Dates, Maps, Sets, nested objects, and circular references.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "8-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "A Shallow Copy (created via {...obj} or Object.assign({}, obj)) creates a new top-level object and copies top-level primitive values, but copies only the memory references of nested objects/arrays—so mutating a nested object in the copy mutates the original. A Deep Copy recursively clones all nested levels. JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj)) performs a deep copy but fails on Date objects (turns them into strings), undefined, functions, Symbols, BigInts, and circular references. Modern JavaScript's built-in structuredClone(obj) performs a true deep copy that properly preserves Dates, Maps, Sets, nested objects, and circular references."}, {"id": "js-lec-8-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: When must you use Bracket Notation obj[key] instead of Dot Notation obj.key?", "title": "Q2: When must you use Bracket Notation obj[key] instead of Dot Notation obj.key?", "answer": "You must use Bracket Notation in three scenarios: 1) When the property name is stored in a dynamic variable or expression (obj[variableName]), because obj.variableName looks for the literal string key \\"variableName\\". 2) When the property key contains spaces, hyphens, or special characters, or starts with a number (e.g., obj[\\"first name\\"]). 3) When the property key is a Symbol (obj[mySymbol]).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "8-q2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "You must use Bracket Notation in three scenarios: 1) When the property name is stored in a dynamic variable or expression (obj[variableName]), because obj.variableName looks for the literal string key \\"variableName\\". 2) When the property key contains spaces, hyphens, or special characters, or starts with a number (e.g., obj[\\"first name\\"]). 3) When the property key is a Symbol (obj[mySymbol])."}, {"id": "js-lec-8-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is the Symbol data type, and why is it used as an object key?", "title": "Q3: What is the Symbol data type, and why is it used as an object key?", "answer": "Every call to Symbol(\\"desc\\") returns a guaranteed unique primitive value (Symbol(\\"id\\") === Symbol(\\"id\\") is false). When used as an object property key ({ [mySym]: 123 }), it prevents property name collisions with third-party code or future object keys, and it remains hidden from standard iteration (for...in, Object.keys(), Object.entries(), JSON.stringify()), only accessible via the Symbol reference itself or Object.getOwnPropertySymbols().", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "8-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Every call to Symbol(\\"desc\\") returns a guaranteed unique primitive value (Symbol(\\"id\\") === Symbol(\\"id\\") is false). When used as an object property key ({ [mySym]: 123 }), it prevents property name collisions with third-party code or future object keys, and it remains hidden from standard iteration (for...in, Object.keys(), Object.entries(), JSON.stringify()), only accessible via the Symbol reference itself or Object.getOwnPropertySymbols()."}, {"id": "js-lec-8-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What is the difference between Object.freeze(obj) and Object.seal(obj)?", "title": "Q4: What is the difference between Object.freeze(obj) and Object.seal(obj)?", "answer": "Object.seal(obj) prevents adding new properties or deleting existing properties (sets configurable: false), but still allows modifying the values of existing writable properties. Object.freeze(obj) goes further: it prevents adding, deleting, AND modifying any existing property values (sets writable: false and configurable: false). Note that both are shallow—nested objects inside a frozen object must be frozen recursively if deep immutability is required.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "8-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Object.seal(obj) prevents adding new properties or deleting existing properties (sets configurable: false), but still allows modifying the values of existing writable properties. Object.freeze(obj) goes further: it prevents adding, deleting, AND modifying any existing property values (sets writable: false and configurable: false). Note that both are shallow—nested objects inside a frozen object must be frozen recursively if deep immutability is required."}, {"id": "js-lec-8-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Why should you use for...of with Object.keys(obj) / Object.entries(obj) instead of a raw for...in loop?", "title": "Q5: Why should you use for...of with Object.keys(obj) / Object.entries(obj) instead of a raw for...in loop?", "answer": "A raw for (let key in obj) loop iterates over all enumerable string properties of the object PLUS any enumerable properties inherited up the prototype chain! In contrast, Object.keys(obj), Object.values(obj), and Object.entries(obj) return only the object's own enumerable properties (ignoring inherited prototype properties).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "8-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "A raw for (let key in obj) loop iterates over all enumerable string properties of the object PLUS any enumerable properties inherited up the prototype chain! In contrast, Object.keys(obj), Object.values(obj), and Object.entries(obj) return only the object's own enumerable properties (ignoring inherited prototype properties)."}, {"id": "js-lec-8-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: How does the V8 Engine optimize object property access internally using Hidden Classes (Shapes) and Inline Caching?", "title": "Q6: How does the V8 Engine optimize object property access internally using Hidden Classes (Shapes) and Inline Caching?", "answer": "Because JS objects can have properties added dynamically, a naive hash-map lookup on every obj.x access would be slow. V8 attaches a \\"Hidden Class\\" (Shape/Map) to every object that records the exact memory offset of each property key, while storing the property values in a contiguous array. When multiple objects share the same property creation order, they share the same Hidden Class, allowing V8 to use Inline Caching to read obj.x in a single O(1) memory offset instruction just like a C++ struct!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "8-q6", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Because JS objects can have properties added dynamically, a naive hash-map lookup on every obj.x access would be slow. V8 attaches a \\"Hidden Class\\" (Shape/Map) to every object that records the exact memory offset of each property key, while storing the property values in a contiguous array. When multiple objects share the same property creation order, they share the same Hidden Class, allowing V8 to use Inline Caching to read obj.x in a single O(1) memory offset instruction just like a C++ struct!"}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object	\N	\N	\N
755	Slope-Guided Binary Search: Finding a Peak Element in 1D Arrays (Single & Multiple Peaks)	dsa-find-peak-element-binary-search	Find any local peak element in a 1D array in O(log N) time using slope-guided binary search by climbing the increasing slope.	2026-10-02 23:53:37.088435+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Slope-Guided Binary Search: Finding a Peak Element in 1D Arrays (Single & Multiple Peaks)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cXxmbemS6XM", "title": "Lecture 53: Peak element in Array", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/peak-index-in-a-mountain-array/", "title": "Peak Index in a Mountain Array", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Locate the peak index of a strictly unimodal mountain array in O(log N) time."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-a-peak-element-ii/", "title": "Find a Peak Element II", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Extend 1D slope-guided binary search to find a 2D grid peak element."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/maximum-value-in-a-bitonic-array3001/1", "title": "Bitonic Point", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Find the maximum element in an array that first strictly increases and then decreases."}]	Module 12: 1D Binary Search, Bounds & Rotated Array Invariants	### 📌 Topic Overview\nA **Peak Element** is an element that is strictly greater than both its left and right neighbors (`arr[i - 1] < arr[i] > arr[i + 1]`), assuming `-infinity` outside both ends of the array. Even though the array is not globally sorted, we can find any peak in **O(log2 N) time** using slope-guided binary search!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Clean Boundary Handling (`low = 1` to `high = n - 2`):**\n  - Just like in Single Element, check `n == 1`, index `0` (`arr[0] > arr[1]`), and index `n - 1` (`arr[n - 1] > arr[n - 2]`) upfront.\n  - Then run Binary Search safely between `low = 1` and `high = n - 2`.\n- **Visualizing the Slope at `mid`:**\n  1. **At a Peak (`arr[mid] > arr[mid - 1] && arr[mid] > arr[mid + 1]`):** Return `mid`!\n  2. **On an Increasing Slope (`arr[mid] > arr[mid - 1]`):** The curve is rising toward the right, so a peak is guaranteed to exist on the right—eliminate the left half (`low = mid + 1`).\n  3. **On a Decreasing Slope or Valley (`else`):** Otherwise, a peak is guaranteed to exist on the left—eliminate the right half (`high = mid - 1`).\n\n### 💻 Find Peak Element Blueprint\n```cpp\nint findPeakElement(vector<int> &arr) {\n    int n = arr.size();\n    if (n == 1) return 0;\n    if (arr[0] > arr[1]) return 0;\n    if (arr[n - 1] > arr[n - 2]) return n - 1;\n\n    int low = 1, high = n - 2;\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (arr[mid] > arr[mid - 1] && arr[mid] > arr[mid + 1]) {\n            return mid;\n        }\n        if (arr[mid] > arr[mid - 1]) {\n            low = mid + 1;  // Increasing slope -> peak is on the right\n        } else {\n            high = mid - 1; // Decreasing slope or valley -> go left\n        }\n    }\n    return -1;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
1962	In-Place Suffix Threading: Flatten a Binary Tree to a Linked List	dsa-flatten-binary-tree-to-linked-list	Flatten a binary tree into a right-skewed linked list in-place using O(1) space Morris threading and reverse postorder recursion.	2026-10-03 01:18:07.183809+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: In-Place Suffix Threading: Flatten a Binary Tree to a Linked List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sWf7k1x9XR4", "title": "Lecture 110: Flatten a Binary Tree to Linked List", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/flatten-binary-tree-to-linked-list/", "title": "Flatten Binary Tree to Linked List", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Flatten binary tree to a linked list in-place according to preorder sequence."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/flatten-a-multilevel-doubly-linked-list/", "title": "Flatten a Multilevel Doubly Linked List", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Flatten multilevel child pointers into a single doubly linked list."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/flatten-binary-tree-to-linked-list/1", "title": "Flatten binary tree to linked list", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Rearrange tree nodes in-place such that left pointers are NULL and right pointers follow preorder."}]	Module 19: Binary Trees: Threaded Binary Trees & Morris Traversal	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the root of a binary tree, flatten the tree into a "linked list" **in-place**:\n- Every node's `left` child pointer must be set to `nullptr`.\n- Every node's `right` child pointer points to the next node in the tree's **Preorder Traversal**.\n- You must **rearrange the existing nodes** (allocating new nodes is strictly forbidden).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (3 Approaches)\n- **Approach 1: Reverse Postorder Recursion (`Right → Left → Root` — `O(N)` Time, `O(H)` Space):**\n  - Maintain global `TreeNode* prev = nullptr`.\n  - Recurse `flatten(root->right)`, then `flatten(root->left)`.\n  - Set `root->right = prev`, `root->left = nullptr`, and update `prev = root`.\n- **Approach 2: Iterative Stack (`O(N)` Time, `O(N)` Space):**\n  - Push `root` into a stack. While `!st.empty()`, pop `curr`, push `curr->right`, then `curr->left`. Set `curr->right = st.top()`, `curr->left = nullptr`.\n- **Approach 3: Morris In-Place Threading (Optimal — `O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - For current node `curr`:\n    - If `curr->left != nullptr`:\n      - Find the rightmost node of `curr->left` (`prev = curr->left; while (prev->right) prev = prev->right;`).\n      - Connect: `prev->right = curr->right`.\n      - Shift left branch to right: `curr->right = curr->left; curr->left = nullptr`.\n    - Advance `curr = curr->right`.\n\n### 💻 Optimal O(1) Space Morris Flattening Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid flatten(TreeNode* root) {\n    TreeNode* curr = root;\n    while (curr != nullptr) {\n        if (curr->left != nullptr) {\n            TreeNode* prev = curr->left;\n            while (prev->right != nullptr) {\n                prev = prev->right;\n            }\n            // Attach original right subtree to rightmost leaf of left branch\n            prev->right = curr->right;\n            curr->right = curr->left;\n            curr->left = nullptr;\n        }\n        curr = curr->right;\n    }\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22117	Lecture 32: Database Backup & Disaster Recovery — mongodump & mongorestore CLI Utilities	mongo-lecture-32-mongodump-and-mongorestore-backup-recovery	Create binary BSON backups of single collections, specific databases, or entire MongoDB servers using mongodump and restore them with mongorestore.	2026-10-06 19:52:12.616986+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nProduction databases require regular backups for **disaster recovery**, **accidental deletion protection**, **server migration**, and **compliance auditing**. MongoDB provides two paired CLI utilities in the Database Tools suite: **`mongodump`** (exports binary `.bson` data + `.metadata.json` index/schema files) and **`mongorestore`** (restores binary dumps back into MongoDB).\n\n### 💡 Backing Up Data with `mongodump`\nAlways run `mongodump` in your OS Command Prompt (exit `mongosh` first using `exit`):\n* **1. Backup an Entire Database:**\n  `mongodump -d <db_name> -o <output_folder>` (or `--db <db_name> --out <output_folder>`)\n  Exports every collection in `<db_name>` as `<collection>.bson` and `<collection>.metadata.json` inside `<output_folder>/<db_name>/`.\n* **2. Backup a Single Collection Only:**\n  `mongodump -d <db_name> -c <collection_name> -o <output_folder>`\n* **3. Full Server Backup (All Databases at Once):**\n  `mongodump -o <output_folder>` (omitting `-d` and `-c` backs up every database on the server).\n\n### 💡 Restoring Data with `mongorestore`\n* **1. Restore a Single Collection:**\n  `mongorestore -d <target_db> -c <target_collection> <path_to_file.bson>`\n* **2. Restore an Entire Database (Into Original or New Database Name):**\n  `mongorestore -d <target_db> <path_to_db_backup_folder>`\n  For example, you can clone the `school` backup into a brand-new `office` database via `mongorestore -d office C:\\backup\\school`.\n* **3. Restore All Databases & Drop Existing Data First (`--dir` & `--drop`):**\n  `mongorestore --dir <backup_root_folder> --drop`\n  The `--drop` flag drops existing collections before restoring so clean snapshot state is guaranteed.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical Backup & Restore CLI Commands\n```bash\n# --- MONGODUMP (BACKUP) ---\n# 1. Backup the entire "school" database to C:\\backup\nmongodump -d school -o C:\\backup\n# Equivalent long form:\nmongodump --db school --out C:\\backup\n\n# 2. Backup ONLY the "students" collection from "school"\nmongodump -d school -c students -o C:\\backup\n\n# 3. Backup ALL databases on the MongoDB server\nmongodump -o C:\\backup\n\n# --- MONGORESTORE (RECOVERY) ---\n# 4. Restore a single collection from its .bson file\nmongorestore -d school -c students C:\\backup\\school\\students.bson\n\n# 5. Restore the "school" backup into a new database named "office"\nmongorestore -d office C:\\backup\\school\n\n# 6. Restore all databases from C:\\backup, dropping existing collections first\nmongorestore --dir C:\\backup --drop\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/database-tools/mongodump/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/database-tools/mongodump/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 32: Database Backup & Disaster Recovery"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Yr_dPdSPhbw", "title": "MongoDB Database Backup and Restore Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "26m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nProduction databases require regular backups for **disaster recovery**, **accidental deletion protection**, **server migration**, and **compliance auditing**. MongoDB provides two paired CLI utilities in the Database Tools suite: **`mongodump`** (exports binary `.bson` data + `.metadata.json` index/schema files) and **`mongorestore`** (restores binary dumps back into MongoDB).\n\n### 💡 Backing Up Data with `mongodump`\nAlways run `mongodump` in your OS Command Prompt (exit `mongosh` first using `exit`):\n* **1. Backup an Entire Database:**\n  `mongodump -d <db_name> -o <output_folder>` (or `--db <db_name> --out <output_folder>`)\n  Exports every collection in `<db_name>` as `<collection>.bson` and `<collection>.metadata.json` inside `<output_folder>/<db_name>/`.\n* **2. Backup a Single Collection Only:**\n  `mongodump -d <db_name> -c <collection_name> -o <output_folder>`\n* **3. Full Server Backup (All Databases at Once):**\n  `mongodump -o <output_folder>` (omitting `-d` and `-c` backs up every database on the server).\n\n### 💡 Restoring Data with `mongorestore`\n* **1. Restore a Single Collection:**\n  `mongorestore -d <target_db> -c <target_collection> <path_to_file.bson>`\n* **2. Restore an Entire Database (Into Original or New Database Name):**\n  `mongorestore -d <target_db> <path_to_db_backup_folder>`\n  For example, you can clone the `school` backup into a brand-new `office` database via `mongorestore -d office C:\\backup\\school`.\n* **3. Restore All Databases & Drop Existing Data First (`--dir` & `--drop`):**\n  `mongorestore --dir <backup_root_folder> --drop`\n  The `--drop` flag drops existing collections before restoring so clean snapshot state is guaranteed.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical Backup & Restore CLI Commands\n```bash\n# --- MONGODUMP (BACKUP) ---\n# 1. Backup the entire "school" database to C:\\backup\nmongodump -d school -o C:\\backup\n# Equivalent long form:\nmongodump --db school --out C:\\backup\n\n# 2. Backup ONLY the "students" collection from "school"\nmongodump -d school -c students -o C:\\backup\n\n# 3. Backup ALL databases on the MongoDB server\nmongodump -o C:\\backup\n\n# --- MONGORESTORE (RECOVERY) ---\n# 4. Restore a single collection from its .bson file\nmongorestore -d school -c students C:\\backup\\school\\students.bson\n\n# 5. Restore the "school" backup into a new database named "office"\nmongorestore -d office C:\\backup\\school\n\n# 6. Restore all databases from C:\\backup, dropping existing collections first\nmongorestore --dir C:\\backup --drop\n```	Yahoo Baba	https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba	655	Lecture 32: Database Backup & Disaster Recovery — mongodump & mongorestore CLI Utilities	Create binary BSON backups of single collections, specific databases, or entire MongoDB servers using mongodump and restore them with mongorestore.	20	Easy	\N	32	[]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/database-tools/mongodump/	\N	\N	\N
22104	Lecture 19: Range Bucketing in Aggregations — $bucket & $bucketAuto Stages	mongo-lecture-19-aggregation-bucket-and-bucketauto	Categorize numeric documents into custom histogram ranges using $bucket (with boundaries and default fallback) or automatically balanced range buckets using $bucketAuto.	2026-10-06 19:28:34.895846+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhile `$group` groups documents by exact field values, **`$bucket`** and **`$bucketAuto`** group numeric documents into **value ranges (histograms)**—such as grouping students into academic divisions (`33-45%`, `45-60%`, `60-80%`, `80-100%`) or products into price tiers.\n\n### 💡 `$bucket` vs. `$bucketAuto`\n* **`$bucket` (Custom Manual Boundaries):**\n  * **`groupBy`:** The numeric field expression to bucket on (e.g., `"$percentage"`).\n  * **`boundaries`:** An ascending array of numeric range edges (e.g., `[33, 45, 60, 80, 100`). Each bucket has an inclusive lower bound and exclusive upper bound `[lower, upper)`.\n  * **`default`:** Fallback bucket name (e.g., `"Fail Students"` or `"Other"`) that catches any document falling outside the specified `boundaries`.\n  * **`output` (Optional):** Specifies accumulator metrics per bucket (`$sum`, `$avg`, `$push`). If omitted, MongoDB defaults to returning `{ count: <number> }`.\n* **`$bucketAuto` (Automatic Evenly-Distributed Buckets):**\n  * Instead of manual `boundaries`, you specify **`buckets: <integer>`** (e.g., `buckets: 3`).\n  * MongoDB automatically inspects the min/max values of `groupBy` and calculates balanced range boundaries (`_id: { min: ..., max: ... }`) so documents are distributed as evenly as possible across the requested number of buckets.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical $bucket & $bucketAuto Examples\n```javascript\n// 1. $bucket: Group students into custom percentage divisions with a "Fail Students" fallback\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $bucket: {\n      groupBy: "$percentage",\n      boundaries: [33, 45, 60, 80, 100,\n      default: "Fail Students",\n      output: {\n        count: { $sum: 1 }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 2. $bucketAuto: Automatically partition students into 3 balanced percentage tiers\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $bucketAuto: {\n      groupBy: "$percentage",\n      buckets: 3,\n      output: {\n        count: { $sum: 1 },\n        average_percentage: { $avg: "$percentage" },\n        total_percentage: { $sum: "$percentage" }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/bucket/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/bucket/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 19: Range Bucketing in Aggregations"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=i5X3BjvrB9U", "title": "MongoDB $bucket & $bucketAuto Operator Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "20m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[{"id": "mongo-19-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374ba", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374ba", "name": "Problem #241: Products with price over 225", "title": "Problem #241: Products with price over 225", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "241", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where price > 225.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bb7a3fe74e0db374ba"}, {"id": "mongo-19-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37363", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b77a3fe74e0db37363", "name": "Problem #352: Products with price lte 30", "title": "Problem #352: Products with price lte 30", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "352", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return products where **price** is <= 30.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b7