ucts sorted by **price** ascending.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db372f1"}, {"id": "mongo-19-prob-10", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db372eb", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db372eb", "name": "Problem #392: Products with price between 50 and 150", "title": "Problem #392: Products with price between 50 and 150", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "392", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return products where **price** is between 50 and 150 inclusive.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db372eb"}]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/bucket/	\N	\N	\N
22130	Lecture 12: Array Higher-Order Methods (forEach, filter, map, reduce) & ES6 Set and Map Data Structures	js-lecture-12-map-filter-reduce-foreach-set-and-map-data-structures	Master functional array iteration with forEach, filter, map, and reduce, write custom polyfills on Array.prototype, and leverage ES6 Set and Map collections for O(1) lookups.	2026-10-06 21:36:53.504856+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nModern JavaScript applications (especially in **React** and **Node.js**) rely heavily on declarative **Higher-Order Array Methods** (`forEach`, `filter`, `map`, `reduce`) and specialized ES6 collections (**`Set`** and **`Map`**) instead of imperative `for` loops.\n\n### 💡 Declarative Array Iteration: `forEach`, `filter`, `map` & `reduce`\nEvery callback passed to `forEach`, `filter`, and `map` receives up to 3 arguments: **`(element, index, array)`**.\n* **`arr.forEach((el, idx, arr) => { ... })`:** Executes a side-effect callback for each element. **Always returns `undefined`** and cannot be chained or stopped early with `break`.\n* **`arr.filter((el, idx, arr) => boolean)`:** Returns a **new array** containing only the elements for which the callback returns a truthy value. Does not mutate the original array.\n* **`arr.map((el, idx, arr) => newValue)`:** Transforms every element 1-to-1 and returns a **new array** of the exact same length containing the transformed values.\n* **`arr.reduce((accumulator, currentValue, idx, arr) => nextAcc, initialValue)`:** Reduces an entire array down to a single value (a sum, an object frequency map, or a grouped lookup table). Always pass `initialValue` explicitly!\n\n### 💡 ES6 `Set` & `Map` Data Structures\n* **`Set` (Collection of Unique Values):**\n  * Automatically rejects duplicate values in O(1) average time.\n  * Core API: `new Set(iterable)`, `.add(val)`, `.delete(val)`, `.has(val)`, `.clear()`, and `.size` (property, not .length).\n  * **1-Line Array Deduplication:** `const uniqueArr = [...new Set(arr);`.\n* **`Map` (True Key-Value Hash Map):**\n  * Unlike plain Objects `{}` (which coerce all keys to Strings or Symbols), a **`Map` allows keys of ANY data type**—including numbers, booleans, arrays, functions, and object references!\n  * Preserves exact insertion order and provides O(1) lookups via `.set(key, val)`, `.get(key)`, `.has(key)`, `.delete(key)`, and `.size`.\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Chaining, Custom Polyfill, `Set` & `Map`\n```javascript\nconst products = [\n  { id: 1, name: "Laptop", price: 60000, inStock: true },\n  { id: 2, name: "Mouse", price: 800, inStock: false },\n  { id: 3, name: "Keyboard", price: 2500, inStock: true }\n;\n\n// 1. Chaining filter -> map -> reduce\nconst totalInStockPrice = products\n  .filter(p => p.inStock)\n  .map(p => p.price)\n  .reduce((acc, price) => acc + price, 0);\nconsole.log(totalInStockPrice); // 62500\n\n// 2. Writing a Custom Polyfill for filter on Array.prototype\nArray.prototype.myFiltering = function(callback) {\n  const ans = [;\n  for (let i = 0; i < this.length; i++) {\n    if (callback(this[i, i, this)) {\n      ans.push(this[i);\n    }\n  }\n  return ans;\n};\n\n// 3. Deduplicating an Array with Set & Using Non-String Keys in Map\nconst rawIds = [10, 20, 10, 30, 20;\nconst uniqueIds = [...new Set(rawIds); // [10, 20, 30\n\nconst userMap = new Map();\nconst objKey = { role: "admin" };\nuserMap.set(objKey, "Full Access");\nuserMap.set(101, "Rohit");\nconsole.log(userMap.get(objKey), userMap.size); // "Full Access", 2\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/reduce", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/reduce", "title": "MDN Web Docs — Array.prototype.reduce, map & filter"}, {"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Keyed_collections", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Keyed_collections", "title": "MDN Web Docs — ES6 Map & Set Keyed Collections"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UByPRYaMEkI", "title": "Map, Filter, Reduce, Set & Map in Javascript | Javascript Full Course #12", "duration": "50m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-12-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What is the difference between Array.prototype.map() and Array.prototype.forEach()?", "title": "Q1: What is the difference between Array.prototype.map() and Array.prototype.forEach()?", "answer": "1) Return Value: map() allocates and returns a brand-new array of the same length containing the return value of the callback for each element. forEach() always returns undefined and is used purely for side effects (like logging or mutating external state). 2) Chainability: Because map() returns an array, you can chain .filter(), .reduce(), or .sort() directly after it, whereas you cannot chain array methods after forEach().", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) Return Value: map() allocates and returns a brand-new array of the same length containing the return value of the callback for each element. forEach() always returns undefined and is used purely for side effects (like logging or mutating external state). 2) Chainability: Because map() returns an array, you can chain .filter(), .reduce(), or .sort() directly after it, whereas you cannot chain array methods after forEach()."}, {"id": "js-lec-12-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: How would you implement your own custom polyfill for Array.prototype.map() and Array.prototype.filter() in an interview?", "title": "Q2: How would you implement your own custom polyfill for Array.prototype.map() and Array.prototype.filter() in an interview?", "answer": "Attach the method to Array.prototype using a regular function (never an arrow function, so this points to the calling array). Inside, create const result = [], loop from i = 0 to this.length - 1, invoke callback(this[i], i, this), and for map push the returned value into result (or for filter, push this[i] only if callback returns truthy), then return result.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Attach the method to Array.prototype using a regular function (never an arrow function, so this points to the calling array). Inside, create const result = [], loop from i = 0 to this.length - 1, invoke callback(this[i], i, this), and for map push the returned value into result (or for filter, push this[i] only if callback returns truthy), then return result."}, {"id": "js-lec-12-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What happens if you call arr.reduce((acc, curr) => acc + curr) WITHOUT passing an initialValue, especially on an empty array?", "title": "Q3: What happens if you call arr.reduce((acc, curr) => acc + curr) WITHOUT passing an initialValue, especially on an empty array?", "answer": "If initialValue is omitted on a non-empty array, reduce() uses arr[0] as the initial accumulator and starts iterating from index 1. However, if the array is empty [] and no initialValue is provided, calling [].reduce(...) immediately throws a TypeError: Reduce of empty array with no initial value! Always pass an explicit initialValue (like 0, [], or {}) in production code.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "If initialValue is omitted on a non-empty array, reduce() uses arr[0] as the initial accumulator and starts iterating from index 1. However, if the array is empty [] and no initialValue is provided, calling [].reduce(...) immediately throws a TypeError: Reduce of empty array with no initial value! Always pass an explicit initialValue (like 0, [], or {}) in production code."}, {"id": "js-lec-12-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What are the key differences between a JavaScript Object {} and an ES6 Map?", "title": "Q4: What are the key differences between a JavaScript Object {} and an ES6 Map?", "answer": "1) Key Types: Plain Object keys are strictly coerced to Strings (or Symbols)—using an object as a key in {} turns it into \\"[object Object]\\". An ES6 Map accepts ANY value as a key (objects, functions, numbers, booleans) without coercion. 2) Size & Iteration: Map tracks its size in O(1) via map.size and is directly iterable with for...of in exact insertion order; Objects require Object.keys(obj).length. 3) Prototype Pollution: A Map has no default prototype keys colliding with user keys like \\"toString\\" or \\"constructor\\".", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "1) Key Types: Plain Object keys are strictly coerced to Strings (or Symbols)—using an object as a key in {} turns it into \\"[object Object]\\". An ES6 Map accepts ANY value as a key (objects, functions, numbers, booleans) without coercion. 2) Size & Iteration: Map tracks its size in O(1) via map.size and is directly iterable with for...of in exact insertion order; Objects require Object.keys(obj).length. 3) Prototype Pollution: A Map has no default prototype keys colliding with user keys like \\"toString\\" or \\"constructor\\"."}, {"id": "js-lec-12-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: If you add two identical-looking objects to a Set: const s = new Set(); s.add({ a: 1 }); s.add({ a: 1 }); what will s.size be?", "title": "Q5: If you add two identical-looking objects to a Set: const s = new Set(); s.add({ a: 1 }); s.add({ a: 1 }); what will s.size be?", "answer": "s.size will be 2! Set checks uniqueness using the SameValueZero algorithm (similar to ===, except NaN equals NaN). Because the two object literals { a: 1 } and { a: 1 } are two separate allocations on the Heap with different memory addresses, Set treats them as two distinct values. If you stored const obj = { a: 1 } and called s.add(obj) twice, s.size would be 1.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "s.size will be 2! Set checks uniqueness using the SameValueZero algorithm (similar to ===, except NaN equals NaN). Because the two object literals { a: 1 } and { a: 1 } are two separate allocations on the Heap with different memory addresses, Set treats them as two distinct values. If you stored const obj = { a: 1 } and called s.add(obj) twice, s.size would be 1."}, {"id": "js-lec-12-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why does [1, 2, 3].filter(x => x * 2) return [1, 2, 3] instead of [2, 4, 6]?", "title": "Q6: Why does [1, 2, 3].filter(x => x * 2) return [1, 2, 3] instead of [2, 4, 6]?", "answer": "Because filter() does not transform elements—it only coerces the callback's return value to a boolean (Truthy vs. Falsy) to decide whether to keep the original element! Since 1*2 (2), 2*2 (4), and 3*2 (6) are all non-zero truthy numbers, filter() keeps all three original elements [1, 2, 3]. To transform them into [2, 4, 6], you must use .map().", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Because filter() does not transform elements—it only coerces the callback's return value to a boolean (Truthy vs. Falsy) to decide whether to keep the original element! Since 1*2 (2), 2*2 (4), and 3*2 (6) are all non-zero truthy numbers, filter() keeps all three original elements [1, 2, 3]. To transform them into [2, 4, 6], you must use .map()."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nModern JavaScript applications (especially in **React** and **Node.js**) rely heavily on declarative **Higher-Order Array Methods** (`forEach`, `filter`, `map`, `reduce`) and specialized ES6 collections (**`Set`** and **`Map`**) instead of imperative `for` loops.\n\n### 💡 Declarative Array Iteration: `forEach`, `filter`, `map` & `reduce`\nEvery callback passed to `forEach`, `filter`, and `map` receives up to 3 arguments: **`(element, index, array)`**.\n* **`arr.forEach((el, idx, arr) => { ... })`:** Executes a side-effect callback for each element. **Always returns `undefined`** and cannot be chained or stopped early with `break`.\n* **`arr.filter((el, idx, arr) => boolean)`:** Returns a **new array** containing only the elements for which the callback returns a truthy value. Does not mutate the original array.\n* **`arr.map((el, idx, arr) => newValue)`:** Transforms every element 1-to-1 and returns a **new array** of the exact same length containing the transformed values.\n* **`arr.reduce((accumulator, currentValue, idx, arr) => nextAcc, initialValue)`:** Reduces an entire array down to a single value (a sum, an object frequency map, or a grouped lookup table). Always pass `initialValue` explicitly!\n\n### 💡 ES6 `Set` & `Map` Data Structures\n* **`Set` (Collection of Unique Values):**\n  * Automatically rejects duplicate values in O(1) average time.\n  * Core API: `new Set(iterable)`, `.add(val)`, `.delete(val)`, `.has(val)`, `.clear()`, and `.size` (property, not .length).\n  * **1-Line Array Deduplication:** `const uniqueArr = [...new Set(arr);`.\n* **`Map` (True Key-Value Hash Map):**\n  * Unlike plain Objects `{}` (which coerce all keys to Strings or Symbols), a **`Map` allows keys of ANY data type**—including numbers, booleans, arrays, functions, and object references!\n  * Preserves exact insertion order and provides O(1) lookups via `.set(key, val)`, `.get(key)`, `.has(key)`, `.delete(key)`, and `.size`.\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Chaining, Custom Polyfill, `Set` & `Map`\n```javascript\nconst products = [\n  { id: 1, name: "Laptop", price: 60000, inStock: true },\n  { id: 2, name: "Mouse", price: 800, inStock: false },\n  { id: 3, name: "Keyboard", price: 2500, inStock: true }\n;\n\n// 1. Chaining filter -> map -> reduce\nconst totalInStockPrice = products\n  .filter(p => p.inStock)\n  .map(p => p.price)\n  .reduce((acc, price) => acc + price, 0);\nconsole.log(totalInStockPrice); // 62500\n\n// 2. Writing a Custom Polyfill for filter on Array.prototype\nArray.prototype.myFiltering = function(callback) {\n  const ans = [;\n  for (let i = 0; i < this.length; i++) {\n    if (callback(this[i, i, this)) {\n      ans.push(this[i);\n    }\n  }\n  return ans;\n};\n\n// 3. Deduplicating an Array with Set & Using Non-String Keys in Map\nconst rawIds = [10, 20, 10, 30, 20;\nconst uniqueIds = [...new Set(rawIds); // [10, 20, 30\n\nconst userMap = new Map();\nconst objKey = { role: "admin" };\nuserMap.set(objKey, "Full Access");\nuserMap.set(101, "Rohit");\nconsole.log(userMap.get(objKey), userMap.size); // "Full Access", 2\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 12: Array Higher-Order Methods (forEach, filter, map, reduce) & ES6 Set and Map Data Structures	Master functional array iteration with forEach, filter, map, and reduce, write custom polyfills on Array.prototype, and leverage ES6 Set and Map collections for O(1) lookups.	20	Easy	\N	12	[{"id": "js-lec-12-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What is the difference between Array.prototype.map() and Array.prototype.forEach()?", "title": "Q1: What is the difference between Array.prototype.map() and Array.prototype.forEach()?", "answer": "1) Return Value: map() allocates and returns a brand-new array of the same length containing the return value of the callback for each element. forEach() always returns undefined and is used purely for side effects (like logging or mutating external state). 2) Chainability: Because map() returns an array, you can chain .filter(), .reduce(), or .sort() directly after it, whereas you cannot chain array methods after forEach().", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) Return Value: map() allocates and returns a brand-new array of the same length containing the return value of the callback for each element. forEach() always returns undefined and is used purely for side effects (like logging or mutating external state). 2) Chainability: Because map() returns an array, you can chain .filter(), .reduce(), or .sort() directly after it, whereas you cannot chain array methods after forEach()."}, {"id": "js-lec-12-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: How would you implement your own custom polyfill for Array.prototype.map() and Array.prototype.filter() in an interview?", "title": "Q2: How would you implement your own custom polyfill for Array.prototype.map() and Array.prototype.filter() in an interview?", "answer": "Attach the method to Array.prototype using a regular function (never an arrow function, so this points to the calling array). Inside, create const result = [], loop from i = 0 to this.length - 1, invoke callback(this[i], i, this), and for map push the returned value into result (or for filter, push this[i] only if callback returns truthy), then return result.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Attach the method to Array.prototype using a regular function (never an arrow function, so this points to the calling array). Inside, create const result = [], loop from i = 0 to this.length - 1, invoke callback(this[i], i, this), and for map push the returned value into result (or for filter, push this[i] only if callback returns truthy), then return result."}, {"id": "js-lec-12-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What happens if you call arr.reduce((acc, curr) => acc + curr) WITHOUT passing an initialValue, especially on an empty array?", "title": "Q3: What happens if you call arr.reduce((acc, curr) => acc + curr) WITHOUT passing an initialValue, especially on an empty array?", "answer": "If initialValue is omitted on a non-empty array, reduce() uses arr[0] as the initial accumulator and starts iterating from index 1. However, if the array is empty [] and no initialValue is provided, calling [].reduce(...) immediately throws a TypeError: Reduce of empty array with no initial value! Always pass an explicit initialValue (like 0, [], or {}) in production code.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "If initialValue is omitted on a non-empty array, reduce() uses arr[0] as the initial accumulator and starts iterating from index 1. However, if the array is empty [] and no initialValue is provided, calling [].reduce(...) immediately throws a TypeError: Reduce of empty array with no initial value! Always pass an explicit initialValue (like 0, [], or {}) in production code."}, {"id": "js-lec-12-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What are the key differences between a JavaScript Object {} and an ES6 Map?", "title": "Q4: What are the key differences between a JavaScript Object {} and an ES6 Map?", "answer": "1) Key Types: Plain Object keys are strictly coerced to Strings (or Symbols)—using an object as a key in {} turns it into \\"[object Object]\\". An ES6 Map accepts ANY value as a key (objects, functions, numbers, booleans) without coercion. 2) Size & Iteration: Map tracks its size in O(1) via map.size and is directly iterable with for...of in exact insertion order; Objects require Object.keys(obj).length. 3) Prototype Pollution: A Map has no default prototype keys colliding with user keys like \\"toString\\" or \\"constructor\\".", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "1) Key Types: Plain Object keys are strictly coerced to Strings (or Symbols)—using an object as a key in {} turns it into \\"[object Object]\\". An ES6 Map accepts ANY value as a key (objects, functions, numbers, booleans) without coercion. 2) Size & Iteration: Map tracks its size in O(1) via map.size and is directly iterable with for...of in exact insertion order; Objects require Object.keys(obj).length. 3) Prototype Pollution: A Map has no default prototype keys colliding with user keys like \\"toString\\" or \\"constructor\\"."}, {"id": "js-lec-12-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: If you add two identical-looking objects to a Set: const s = new Set(); s.add({ a: 1 }); s.add({ a: 1 }); what will s.size be?", "title": "Q5: If you add two identical-looking objects to a Set: const s = new Set(); s.add({ a: 1 }); s.add({ a: 1 }); what will s.size be?", "answer": "s.size will be 2! Set checks uniqueness using the SameValueZero algorithm (similar to ===, except NaN equals NaN). Because the two object literals { a: 1 } and { a: 1 } are two separate allocations on the Heap with different memory addresses, Set treats them as two distinct values. If you stored const obj = { a: 1 } and called s.add(obj) twice, s.size would be 1.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "s.size will be 2! Set checks uniqueness using the SameValueZero algorithm (similar to ===, except NaN equals NaN). Because the two object literals { a: 1 } and { a: 1 } are two separate allocations on the Heap with different memory addresses, Set treats them as two distinct values. If you stored const obj = { a: 1 } and called s.add(obj) twice, s.size would be 1."}, {"id": "js-lec-12-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why does [1, 2, 3].filter(x => x * 2) return [1, 2, 3] instead of [2, 4, 6]?", "title": "Q6: Why does [1, 2, 3].filter(x => x * 2) return [1, 2, 3] instead of [2, 4, 6]?", "answer": "Because filter() does not transform elements—it only coerces the callback's return value to a boolean (Truthy vs. Falsy) to decide whether to keep the original element! Since 1*2 (2), 2*2 (4), and 3*2 (6) are all non-zero truthy numbers, filter() keeps all three original elements [1, 2, 3]. To transform them into [2, 4, 6], you must use .map().", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Because filter() does not transform elements—it only coerces the callback's return value to a boolean (Truthy vs. Falsy) to decide whether to keep the original element! Since 1*2 (2), 2*2 (4), and 3*2 (6) are all non-zero truthy numbers, filter() keeps all three original elements [1, 2, 3]. To transform them into [2, 4, 6], you must use .map()."}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/reduce	\N	\N	\N
22105	Lecture 20: Transforming Fields & Arrays — $addFields, $unwind, $concat, $concatArrays, $ifNull, $cond & $$REMOVE	mongo-lecture-20-aggregation-addfields-unwind-cond-ifnull	Append computed fields without dropping existing ones using $addFields, conditionally strip fields with $$REMOVE, $ifNull, and $cond, and deconstruct top-level or nested arrays with $unwind.	2026-10-06 19:28:34.895846+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nUnlike `$project` (which drops any field you do not explicitly list), **`$addFields`** preserves all existing fields in the document while adding new computed fields, overwriting existing fields, or conditionally removing unwanted fields via `"$$REMOVE"`. Meanwhile, **`$unwind`** deconstructs array fields into individual documents per array element.\n\n### 💡 Key Operators & Expressions Covered\n* **`$addFields`:** Appends new top-level fields or nested objects (e.g., `"profile.age": 30`) while keeping all original document properties intact.\n* **`$concat`:** Concatenates an array of strings into a single string: `{ $concat: ["$firstName", " ", "$lastName" }`.\n* **`"$$REMOVE"`:** System variable that removes a field from the output document when assigned to it.\n* **`$ifNull`:** Checks if a field is `null` or missing and substitutes a fallback value (or `"$$REMOVE"` to hide null fields completely): `{ $ifNull: ["$city", "$$REMOVE" }`.\n* **`$cond`:** Ternary conditional operator `{ $cond: { if: <booleanExpr>, then: <trueVal>, else: <falseVal> } }`.\n* **`$concatArrays`:** Appends new elements to an existing array field inside an aggregation stage: `{ $concatArrays: ["$marks", [70 }`.\n* **`$unwind`:** Deconstructs an array field (top-level like `"$sizes"` or nested inside an object like `"$items.tags"`) so a document with 4 array elements outputs 4 separate documents.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical $addFields & $unwind Pipelines\n```javascript\n// 1. $addFields: Compute fullName, totalMarks, avgMarks, and hide firstName/lastName + null city\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $addFields: {\n      fullName: { $concat: ["$firstName", " ", "$lastName" },\n      totalMarks: { $sum: "$marks" },\n      averageMarks: { $avg: "$marks" },\n      firstName: "$$REMOVE",\n      lastName: "$$REMOVE",\n      city: { $ifNull: ["$city", "$$REMOVE" }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 2. $addFields with $cond: Hide city field only when city equals "Delhi"\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $addFields: {\n      city: {\n        $cond: {\n          if: { $eq: ["$city", "Delhi" },\n          then: "$$REMOVE",\n          else: "$city"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 3. $addFields with $concatArrays: Append a 4th subject score (70) to student #1's marks array\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  { $match: { _id: 1 } },\n  {\n    $addFields: {\n      marks: { $concatArrays: ["$marks", [70 }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 4. $unwind on a top-level array ("$sizes") vs. a nested object array ("$items.tags")\ndb.inventory.aggregate([\n  { $unwind: "$sizes" }\n)\n\ndb.products.aggregate([\n  { $unwind: "$items.tags" }\n)\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/addFields/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/addFields/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 20: Transforming Fields & Arrays"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xLdOjrX46Kw", "title": "MongoDB $addFields & $unwind Operator Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "34m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[{"id": "mongo-20-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db372b2", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9b67a3fe74e0db372b2", "name": "Problem #411: Find users with tag12", "title": "Problem #411: Find users with tag12", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "411", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Execute: Find users with tag12.", "external_url": "https://mongopractic