22081	Lecture 23: Date & Time Functions — TIMESTAMPTZ, NOW(), INTERVAL, EXTRACT & TO_CHAR	pg-lecture-23-date-time-functions-timestamp-interval-extract-to-char	Perform relative date math with INTERVAL, slice date components for monthly revenue reports using EXTRACT, and format timestamps into strings with TO_CHAR.	2026-10-05 19:34:44.247876+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Lecture 23: Date & Time Functions — TIMESTAMPTZ, NOW(), INTERVAL, EXTRACT & TO_CHAR\nPart of the **PostgreSQL & Relational SQL Mastery** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qM2tokrgpFE", "title": "PostgreSQL Date & Time Functions Explained | Timestamp, Interval, EXTRACT & TO_CHAR with Queries", "duration": "22m", "creator_name": "Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical"}]	[{"id": "pg-23-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/rising-temperature/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/rising-temperature/", "name": "Rising Temperature (Date Interval Comparison)", "title": "Rising Temperature (Date Interval Comparison)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Rising Temperature (Date Interval Comparison)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/rising-temperature/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/user-activity-for-the-past-30-days-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/user-activity-for-the-past-30-days-i/", "name": "User Activity for the Past 30 Days I (30-Day Window Filter)", "title": "User Activity for the Past 30 Days I (30-Day Window Filter)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"User Activity for the Past 30 Days I (30-Day Window Filter)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/user-activity-for-the-past-30-days-i/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/list-the-products-ordered-in-a-period/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/list-the-products-ordered-in-a-period/", "name": "List the Products Ordered in a Period (Month/Year Extraction)", "title": "List the Products Ordered in a Period (Month/Year Extraction)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"List the Products Ordered in a Period (Month/Year Extraction)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/list-the-products-ordered-in-a-period/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/the-latest-login-in-2020/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/the-latest-login-in-2020/", "name": "The Latest Login in 2020 (EXTRACT YEAR Filter)", "title": "The Latest Login in 2020 (EXTRACT YEAR Filter)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"The Latest Login in 2020 (EXTRACT YEAR Filter)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/the-latest-login-in-2020/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/game-play-analysis-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/game-play-analysis-i/", "name": "Game Play Analysis I (First Login Date)", "title": "Game Play Analysis I (First Login Date)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Game Play Analysis I (First Login Date)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/game-play-analysis-i/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sales-analysis-iii/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sales-analysis-iii/", "name": "Sales Analysis III (Date Boundary Filtering)", "title": "Sales Analysis III (Date Boundary Filtering)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Sales Analysis III (Date Boundary Filtering)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sales-analysis-iii/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/", "name": "Monthly Transactions I (TO_CHAR YYYY-MM Grouping)", "title": "Monthly Transactions I (TO_CHAR YYYY-MM Grouping)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_7", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Monthly Transactions I (TO_CHAR YYYY-MM Grouping)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/game-play-analysis-iv/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/game-play-analysis-iv/", "name": "Game Play Analysis IV (Consecutive Day INTERVAL + 1 day)", "title": "Game Play Analysis IV (Consecutive Day INTERVAL + 1 day)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_8", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Game Play Analysis IV (Consecutive Day INTERVAL + 1 day)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/game-play-analysis-iv/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/restaurant-growth/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/restaurant-growth/", "name": "Restaurant Growth (7-Day Moving Window)", "title": "Restaurant Growth (7-Day Moving Window)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_9", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Restaurant Growth (7-Day Moving Window)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/restaurant-growth/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/", "name": "Market Analysis I (Year Extraction in Join)", "title": "Market Analysis I (Year Extraction in Join)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_10", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Market Analysis I (Year Extraction in Join)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nActivity feeds (*"Enrolled in the last 30 days"*), subscription expirations (*"+ 6 months"*), and monthly financial reports (*"Revenue grouped by month"*) all rely on PostgreSQL's built-in temporal data types and functions.\n\n### 🕒 Core Temporal Types & Functions\n\n* **`TIMESTAMP` vs. `TIMESTAMPTZ`:** Stores both date and time. Always prefer **`TIMESTAMPTZ`** (`TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE`) in production to avoid cross-country UTC bugs. — `2026-06-20 14:30:00+05:30`\n* **`NOW()` / `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP`:** Returns the exact server date and time when the query executes. — `SELECT NOW();`\n* **`INTERVAL`:** Adds or subtracts human-readable time durations (automatically handles leap years and 28/30/31-day months). — `NOW() - INTERVAL '30 days'`, `NOW() + INTERVAL '6 months'`\n* **`EXTRACT(part FROM col)`:** Plucks a specific numeric component (`YEAR`, `MONTH`, `DAY`, `HOUR`) out of a timestamp. — `EXTRACT(MONTH FROM paid_at)` $\\rightarrow$ `6`\n* **`TO_CHAR(col, format)`:** Formats a timestamp into a custom display string (respects capitalization of `Month` vs `month`). — `TO_CHAR(NOW(), 'DD Mon YYYY')` $\\rightarrow$ `'20 Jun 2026'`\n\n### 💻 Real-World Query Blueprints\n\n#### 1. Recent Activity Feed (Students Enrolled in the Last 30 Days)\n```sql\nSELECT \n    u.name,\n    e.enrolled_on\nFROM enrollments e\nINNER JOIN users u\n    ON e.student_id = u.id\nWHERE e.enrolled_on >= NOW() - INTERVAL '30 days';\n```\n\n#### 2. Monthly Revenue Report (`EXTRACT` + `GROUP BY`)\n```sql\nSELECT \n    EXTRACT(MONTH FROM paid_at) AS payment_month,\n    SUM(amount) AS monthly_revenue\nFROM payments\nWHERE status = 'completed'\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(MONTH FROM paid_at)\nORDER BY payment_month ASC;\n```\n\n#### 3. Certificate Date Formatting (`TO_CHAR`) & Graduation Deadlines (`INTERVAL`)\n```sql\n-- Format certificate issue date cleanly as '20 June 2026'\nSELECT \n    student_id,\n    TO_CHAR(issued_at, 'DD Month YYYY') AS formatted_issue_date\nFROM certificates;\n\n-- Calculate 6-Month Bootcamp Graduation Date\nSELECT \n    student_id,\n    enrolled_on,\n    enrolled_on + INTERVAL '6 months' AS graduation_date\nFROM enrollments;\n```	Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)	https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical	654	Lecture 23: Date & Time Functions — TIMESTAMPTZ, NOW(), INTERVAL, EXTRACT & TO_CHAR	Perform relative date math with INTERVAL, slice date components for monthly revenue reports using EXTRACT, and format timestamps into strings with TO_CHAR.	22	Medium	Module 7: Temporal Queries, Subqueries & CTEs	23	[{"id": "pg-23-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/rising-temperature/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/rising-temperature/", "name": "Rising Temperature (Date Interval Comparison)", "title": "Rising Temperature (Date Interval Comparison)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Rising Temperature (Date Interval Comparison)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/rising-temperature/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/user-activity-for-the-past-30-days-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/user-activity-for-the-past-30-days-i/", "name": "User Activity for the Past 30 Days I (30-Day Window Filter)", "title": "User Activity for the Past 30 Days I (30-Day Window Filter)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"User Activity for the Past 30 Days I (30-Day Window Filter)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/user-activity-for-the-past-30-days-i/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/list-the-products-ordered-in-a-period/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/list-the-products-ordered-in-a-period/", "name": "List the Products Ordered in a Period (Month/Year Extraction)", "title": "List the Products Ordered in a Period (Month/Year Extraction)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"List the Products Ordered in a Period (Month/Year Extraction)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/list-the-products-ordered-in-a-period/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/the-latest-login-in-2020/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/the-latest-login-in-2020/", "name": "The Latest Login in 2020 (EXTRACT YEAR Filter)", "title": "The Latest Login in 2020 (EXTRACT YEAR Filter)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"The Latest Login in 2020 (EXTRACT YEAR Filter)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/the-latest-login-in-2020/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/game-play-analysis-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/game-play-analysis-i/", "name": "Game Play Analysis I (First Login Date)", "title": "Game Play Analysis I (First Login Date)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Game Play Analysis I (First Login Date)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/game-play-analysis-i/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sales-analysis-iii/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sales-analysis-iii/", "name": "Sales Analysis III (Date Boundary Filtering)", "title": "Sales Analysis III (Date Boundary Filtering)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Sales Analysis III (Date Boundary Filtering)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sales-analysis-iii/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/", "name": "Monthly Transactions I (TO_CHAR YYYY-MM Grouping)", "title": "Monthly Transactions I (TO_CHAR YYYY-MM Grouping)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_7", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Monthly Transactions I (TO_CHAR YYYY-MM Grouping)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/game-play-analysis-iv/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/game-play-analysis-iv/", "name": "Game Play Analysis IV (Consecutive Day INTERVAL + 1 day)", "title": "Game Play Analysis IV (Consecutive Day INTERVAL + 1 day)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_8", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Game Play Analysis IV (Consecutive Day INTERVAL + 1 day)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/game-play-analysis-iv/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/restaurant-growth/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/restaurant-growth/", "name": "Restaurant Growth (7-Day Moving Window)", "title": "Restaurant Growth (7-Day Moving Window)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_9", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Restaurant Growth (7-Day Moving Window)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/restaurant-growth/"}, {"id": "pg-23-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/", "name": "Market Analysis I (Year Extraction in Join)", "title": "Market Analysis I (Year Extraction in Join)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "23_10", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Market Analysis I (Year Extraction in Join)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/"}]	https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/functions-datetime.html	\N	\N	\N
22118	Lecture 33: Authentication & Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) — mongod.cfg Authorization, createUser, db.auth & Built-In Roles	mongo-lecture-33-authentication-rbac-user-management	Secure MongoDB servers by enabling authorization in mongod.cfg, creating administrative and database-scoped users, authenticating via db.auth(), and managing RBAC roles.	2026-10-06 19:52:12.616986+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nBy default, a fresh local MongoDB installation allows anyone connecting on port `27017` to read, modify, or drop any database without logging in. In production, you must enable **Access Control (Authentication)** and assign **Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)** permissions to each user.\n\n### 💡 Built-In MongoDB Roles Hierarchy\n* **Database User & Admin Roles (Scoped to a Specific Database):**\n  * **`read`:** Can only read data (`find`, `aggregate`); cannot insert, update, or delete.\n  * **`readWrite`:** Can read and modify data (`insert`, `update`, `delete`) in the target database.\n  * **`dbAdmin`:** Schema validation, indexing, and collection statistics (no user management).\n  * **`userAdmin`:** Can create and modify users and roles on that database.\n  * **`dbOwner`:** Combines `readWrite`, `dbAdmin`, and `userAdmin` on that specific database.\n* **All-Database & Superuser Roles (Created in `admin` Database):**\n  * **`readAnyDatabase` / `userAdminAnyDatabase` / `dbAdminAnyDatabase`:** Global administrative privileges across all databases.\n  * **`root`:** Full superuser access to all databases, users, and server operations.\n  * **`backup` & `restore`:** Dedicated roles for running `mongodump` and `mongorestore`.\n\n### 💡 Enabling Authorization in `mongod.cfg`\n1. Open `C:\\Program Files\\MongoDB\\Server\\8.0\\bin\\mongod.cfg` as Administrator.\n2. Uncomment `security:` and indent `authorization: enabled` by 2 spaces:\n   ```yaml\n   security:\n     authorization: enabled\n   ```\n3. Open `services.msc` (Win + R -> `services.msc`), right-click **MongoDB Server**, and click **Restart**.\n\n### 🛠️ Complete User & Role Management Lifecycle in `mongosh`\n```javascript\n// 1. Switch to "admin" DB and create the primary "root" superuser\nuse admin\ndb.createUser({\n  user: "admin",\n  pwd: "admin123",\n  roles: [\n    { role: "root", db: "admin" }\n  \n})\n\n// 2. Authenticate as "admin" (returns { ok: 1 })\ndb.auth("admin", "admin123")\n\n// 3. Create a read-only "developer" user for the "school" database\ndb.createUser({\n  user: "developer",\n  pwd: "dev123",\n  roles: [\n    { role: "read", db: "school" }\n  \n})\n\n// 4. Inspect users in the current database\ndb.getUsers()\ndb.getUser("developer")\n\n// 5. Upgrade "developer" role to "readWrite" or grant/revoke additional roles\ndb.updateUser("developer", {\n  roles: [\n    { role: "readWrite", db: "school" }\n  \n})\n\ndb.grantRolesToUser("developer", [\n  { role: "dbOwner", db: "school" }\n)\n\ndb.revokeRolesFromUser("developer", [\n  { role: "read", db: "school" }\n)\n\n// 6. Change password & drop users\ndb.changeUserPassword("developer", "pass123")\ndb.dropUser("developer")\n// Drop all users in current DB with majority write concern:\n// db.dropAllUsers({ w: "majority", wtimeout: 5000 })\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/authentication/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/authentication/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 33: Authentication & Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=90szdRqZTeo", "title": "MongoDB Authentication & User Management Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "39m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nBy default, a fresh local MongoDB installation allows anyone connecting on port `27017` to read, modify, or drop any database without logging in. In production, you must enable **Access Control (Authentication)** and assign **Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)** permissions to each user.\n\n### 💡 Built-In MongoDB Roles Hierarchy\n* **Database User & Admin Roles (Scoped to a Specific Database):**\n  * **`read`:** Can only read data (`find`, `aggregate`); cannot insert, update, or delete.\n  * **`readWrite`:** Can read and modify data (`insert`, `update`, `delete`) in the target database.\n  * **`dbAdmin`:** Schema validation, indexing, and collection statistics (no user management).\n  * **`userAdmin`:** Can create and modify users and roles on that database.\n  * **`dbOwner`:** Combines `readWrite`, `dbAdmin`, and `userAdmin` on that specific database.\n* **All-Database & Superuser Roles (Created in `admin` Database):**\n  * **`readAnyDatabase` / `userAdminAnyDatabase` / `dbAdminAnyDatabase`:** Global administrative privileges across all databases.\n  * **`root`:** Full superuser access to all databases, users, and server operations.\n  * **`backup` & `restore`:** Dedicated roles for running `mongodump` and `mongorestore`.\n\n### 💡 Enabling Authorization in `mongod.cfg`\n1. Open `C:\\Program Files\\MongoDB\\Server\\8.0\\bin\\mongod.cfg` as Administrator.\n2. Uncomment `security:` and indent `authorization: enabled` by 2 spaces:\n   ```yaml\n   security:\n     authorization: enabled\n   ```\n3. Open `services.msc` (Win + R -> `services.msc`), right-click **MongoDB Server**, and click **Restart**.\n\n### 🛠️ Complete User & Role Management Lifecycle in `mongosh`\n```javascript\n// 1. Switch to "admin" DB and create the primary "root" superuser\nuse admin\ndb.createUser({\n  user: "admin",\n  pwd: "admin123",\n  roles: [\n    { role: "root", db: "admin" }\n  \n})\n\n// 2. Authenticate as "admin" (returns { ok: 1 })\ndb.auth("admin", "admin123")\n\n// 3. Create a read-only "developer" user for the "school" database\ndb.createUser({\n  user: "developer",\n  pwd: "dev123",\n  roles: [\n    { role: "read", db: "school" }\n  \n})\n\n// 4. Inspect users in the current database\ndb.getUsers()\ndb.getUser("developer")\n\n// 5. Upgrade "developer" role to "readWrite" or grant/revoke additional roles\ndb.updateUser("developer", {\n  roles: [\n    { role: "readWrite", db: "school" }\n  \n})\n\ndb.grantRolesToUser("developer", [\n  { role: "dbOwner", db: "school" }\n)\n\ndb.revokeRolesFromUser("developer", [\n  { role: "read", db: "school" }\n)\n\n// 6. Change password & drop users\ndb.changeUserPassword("developer", "pass123")\ndb.dropUser("developer")\n// Drop all users in current DB with majority write concern:\n// db.dropAllUsers({ w: "majority", wtimeout: 5000 })\n```	Yahoo Baba	https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba	655	Lecture 33: Authentication & Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) — mongod.cfg Authorization, createUser, db.auth & Built-In Roles	Secure MongoDB servers by enabling authorization in mongod.cfg, creating administrative and database-scoped users, authenticating via db.auth(), and managing RBAC roles.	20	Easy	\N	33	[]	https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/authentication/	\N	\N	\N
22193	Volume & Surface Area	aptitude-quant-volume-and-surface-area	Calculate 3D metrics for cubes, cuboids, cylinders, cones, and spheres. Master volume conservation during melting and structural cost optimizations.	2026-10-07 11:47:07.968562+05:30	### 📌 1. Core 3D Geometry Formulas\n* **Cuboid:**\n  * Volume = `L × B × H`\n  * Total Surface Area (TSA) = `2(LB + BH + HL)`\n  * Longest diagonal = `√(L^2 + B^2 + H^2)`\n* **Cube:**\n  * Volume = `a^3`, TSA = `6a^2`, Diagonal = `a√3`\n* **Cylinder:**\n  * Volume = `πr^2h`\n  * Curved Surface Area (CSA) = `2πrh`\n  * TSA = `2πr(h + r)`\n* **Cone:**\n  * Volume = `1/3 πr^2h`\n  * Slant height `l = √(r^2 + h^2)`\n  * CSA = `πrl`, TSA = `πr(l + r)`\n* **Sphere:**\n  * Volume = `4/3 πr^3`\n  * Surface Area = `4πr^2`\n* **Hemisphere:**\n  * Volume = `2/3 πr^3`\n  * CSA = `2πr^2`, TSA = `3πr^2`\n\n### 💡 2. High-Yield Placement Shortcuts\n* **Melting and Recasting (Conservation of Volume):**\n  * When a solid is melted and formed into a new shape (or multiple small shapes), the **TOTAL VOLUME** remains strictly constant!\n  * Example: Number of small lead spheres obtained by melting a large sphere = `Volume of large / Volume of 1 small`.\n* **Flow Rates through Pipes into Tanks:**\n  * Volume of water flowing per hour = `Cross-sectional Area of Pipe × Speed of water flowing`.\n  * Time to fill tank = `(Volume of Tank) / (Volume flowing per hour)`.\n\n### 💡 3. Proportional Scaling in 3D\n* If the linear dimension (radius, side, height) is multiplied by `k`:\n  * Surface Area is multiplied by **`k^2`**.\n  * Volume is multiplied by **`k^3`**.\n* If a sphere's radius is increased by 10%, its Volume increases by `(1.10)^3 = 1.331`, which is a 33.1% increase.	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2rp_-h6PnFo", "type": "youtube", "title": "Volume & Surface Area - Shortcuts & Tricks 2026 Placement Tests, Job Interviews & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-vol-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Melting Spheres into a Single Sphere] Three solid metallic spheres of radii 3 cm, 4 cm, and 5 cm are melted to form a single solid sphere. Find the radius of the new sphere.", "options": ["A) 6 cm", "B) 7 cm", "C) 8 cm", "D) 12 cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Three completely solid metallic spheres having radii of 3 cm, 4 cm, and 5 cm respectively are melted down and recast together into a single large solid sphere. What is the exact radius of the newly formed sphere?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total Volume is conserved! V1 + V2 + V3 = V_new => (4/3)π(3^3) + (4/3)π(4^3) + (4/3)π(5^3) = (4/3)π(R^3). Cancel out (4/3)π from all terms: 3^3 + 4^3 + 5^3 = R^3 => 27 + 64 + 125 = R^3 => 216 = R^3. Taking the cube root gives R = 6 cm.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-vol-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Cone and Cylinder Ratio] A cone, a hemisphere, and a cylinder stand on equal bases and have the same height. Find the ratio of their volumes.", "options": ["A) 1 : 2 : 3", "B) 1 : 3 : 2", "C) 2 : 3 : 4", "D) 3 : 2 : 1"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A right circular cone, a solid hemisphere, and a right circular cylinder all stand on identically sized circular bases and all possess the exact same vertical height. What is the exact mathematical ratio of their respective volumes (Cone : Hemisphere : Cylinder)?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let the base radius be r. Since the hemisphere has height equal to its radius, the common height h = r for all three objects! 1) Volume of Cone = (1/3)πr^2h = (1/3)πr^3. 2) Volume of Hemisphere = (2/3)πr^3. 3) Volume of Cylinder = πr^2h = πr^3. The ratio is (1/3) : (2/3) : 1 = 1 : 2 : 3.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-vol-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Percentage Increase in Volume of a Cube] If each edge of a cube is increased by 50%, find the percentage increase in its surface area.", "options": ["A) 100%", "B) 125%", "C) 150%", "D) 225%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If every single edge of a perfect solid cube is increased by exactly 50%, what will be the exact overall percentage increase in its total surface area?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let original edge = a. Surface Area = 6a^2. New edge = 1.5a. New Surface Area = 6(1.5a)^2 = 6(2.25a^2) = 2.25 × (6a^2). The new area is 2.25 times the old area, meaning it has strictly increased by 1.25 times, or exactly 125%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-vol-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Longest Pole in a Room] The length, breadth, and height of a room are 12m, 4m, and 3m. Find the length of the longest pole that can be placed in it.", "options": ["A) 13 m", "B) 14 m", "C) 15 m", "D) 16 m"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The internal length, breadth, and height of a rectangular room are exactly 12m, 4m, and 3m respectively. What is the maximum possible length of a single straight pole that can be completely placed inside this room?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The longest pole corresponds to the main diagonal of the cuboid space. Diagonal = √(L^2 + B^2 + H^2) = √(12^2 + 4^2 + 3^2) = √(144 + 16 + 9) = √169 = 13 meters.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-vol-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Water Flowing through a Pipe] Water flows through a cylindrical pipe of 14 cm internal diameter at 3 km/hr into a rectangular tank 50m × 44m. Rise in water level in 2 hours?", "options": ["A) 3.5 cm", "B) 4.2 cm", "C) 5.0 cm", "D) 6.4 cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Liquid water freely flows through a cylindrical pipe of internal diameter exactly 14 cm at a constant rate of 3 km/hr into a rectangular empty tank measuring 50m by 44m. Exactly what will be the rise in the water level of the tank after exactly 2 hours? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Cross-sectional area of pipe: Radius = 7 cm = 0.07 m. Area = πr^2 = (22/7) × (0.07)^2 = 0.0154 sq meters. 2) Volume of water flowing per hour = Area × Speed = 0.0154 × 3000 m/hr = 46.2 cubic meters. 3) Volume flowing in 2 hours = 46.2 × 2 = 92.4 cubic meters. 4) This volume accumulates in the tank: Volume = L × B × Rise => 92.4 = 50 × 44 × Rise => 92.4 = 2200 × Rise => Rise = 92.4 / 2200 = 0.042 meters = 4.2 cm.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-vol-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Surface Area of Combined Cubes] Two cubes, each of volume 64 cubic cm, are joined end to end. Find the surface area of the resulting cuboid.", "options": ["A) 128 sq cm", "B) 160 sq cm", "C) 192 sq cm", "D) 256 sq cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two identical solid cubes, each having an exact volume of 64 cubic cm, are perfectly joined end to end. What is the total surface area of the resulting solid cuboid?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Volume of one cube = a^3 = 64 => side a = 4 cm. 2) When two such cubes are joined end-to-end, the resulting cuboid has Length L = 4 + 4 = 8 cm, Breadth B = 4 cm, and Height H = 4 cm. 3) Total Surface Area = 2(LB + BH + HL) = 2(8×4 + 4×4 + 4×8) = 2(32 + 16 + 32) = 2(80) = 160 sq cm.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-vol-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Melting Cylinder into Cone] A metallic cylinder of radius 8 cm and height 2 cm is melted and converted into a right circular cone of height 6 cm. Radius of the cone?", "options": ["A) 6 cm", "B) 8 cm", "C) 10 cm", "D) 12 cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A solid metallic right circular cylinder possessing a base radius of 8 cm and a height of 2 cm is melted down and completely recast into a solid right circular cone having a height of 6 cm. What is the base radius of the new cone?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Volume is conserved! Volume of Cylinder = Volume of Cone => π(r_cyl)^2(h_cyl) = (1/3)π(r_cone)^2(h_cone). Substitute values: π × 8^2 × 2 = (1/3) × π × (r_cone)^2 × 6. Cancel π from both sides: 64 × 2 = (r_cone)^2 × 2 => 128 = 2 × (r_cone)^2 => (r_cone)^2 = 64. Thus, the radius of the cone is 8 cm.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-vol-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Volume of Largest Cone in a Cube] Find the volume of the largest right circular cone that can be cut out of a cube of edge 14 cm.", "options": ["A) 718.66 cubic cm", "B) 1078.0 cubic cm", "C) 1232.0 cubic cm", "D) 2156.0 cubic cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the exact mathematical volume of the largest possible solid right circular cone that can be perfectly carved out of a solid wooden cube whose edges measure exactly 14 cm? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "To be the largest possible cone, its circular base must perfectly touch the midpoints of the bottom face of the cube, making its diameter equal to the cube's edge (Diameter = 14 cm => Radius r = 7 cm). The height of the cone must extend fully to the top face, so h = 14 cm. Volume of Cone = (1/3)πr^2h = (1/3) × (22/7) × (7^2) × 14 = (1/3) × 22 × 7 × 14 = (1/3) × 2156 = 718.66 cubic cm.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-vol-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Increase in Volume of Cylinder] If the radius of a cylinder is doubled and its height is halved, find the ratio of the new volume to the old volume.", "options": ["A) 1 : 1", "B) 2 : 1", "C) 4 : 1", "D) 1 : 2"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the base radius of a solid cylinder is mathematically doubled and its vertical height is simultaneously halved, what is the exact ratio of its newly formed volume to its original volume?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Original Volume V1 = πr^2h. New parameters: R = 2r, H = h/2. New Volume V2 = π(2r)^2(h/2) = π(4r^2)(h/2) = 2πr^2h. Ratio = V2 / V1 = (2πr^2h) / (πr^2h) = 2 : 1.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-vol-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Surface Area of Hemisphere] Find the Total Surface Area of a solid hemisphere of radius 21 cm.", "options": ["A) 2,772 sq cm", "B) 3,124 sq cm", "C) 4,158 sq cm", "D) 5,544 sq cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the exact Total Surface Area (TSA) of a perfectly solid hemisphere possessing an exact radius of 21 cm? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "For a SOLID hemisphere, Total Surface Area = Curved Surface Area + Area of flat circular base = 2πr^2 + πr^2 = 3πr^2. TSA = 3 × (22/7) × (21)^2 = 3 × (22/7) × 441 = 3 × 22 × 63 = 66 × 63 = 4,158 sq cm.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-vol-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Number of Lead Shots from a Cube] How many lead shots, each 3 mm in diameter, can be made from a cuboid of dimensions 9cm × 11cm × 12cm?", "options": ["A) 84,000", "B) 88,000", "C) 90,000", "D) 92,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly how many identical spherical lead shots, each possessing an outer diameter of exactly 3 mm, can be completely cast by melting down a solid cuboid measuring exactly 9 cm by 11 cm by 12 cm? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Convert all dimensions to cm! Diameter of shot = 3 mm = 0.3 cm => Radius r = 0.15 cm. 1) Volume of cuboid = 9 × 11 × 12 = 1188 cubic cm. 2) Volume of one spherical shot = (4/3)πr^3 = (4/3) × (22/7) × (0.15)^3 = (88/21) × 0.003375 = 0.0141428 cubic cm. 3) Number of shots = Total Volume / Volume of 1 shot = 1188 / [(88/21) × (3.375/1000)] = (1188 × 21 × 1000) / (88 × 3.375) = 24948000 / 297 = 84,000 lead shots.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-vol-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Hollowing out a Cylinder] A hollow cylindrical tube 20 cm long is made of iron and its external and internal diameters are 8 cm and 6 cm. Find volume of iron used.", "options": ["A) 330 cubic cm", "B) 440 cubic cm", "C) 550 cubic cm", "D) 660 cubic cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A hollow cylindrical tube strictly 20 cm long is cast out of iron. Its external outer diameter is exactly 8 cm, and its internal empty diameter is exactly 6 cm. What is the exact total mathematical volume of iron used in fabricating the tube? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Outer radius R = 8/2 = 4 cm. Inner radius r = 6/2 = 3 cm. The volume of material = Volume of outer cylinder - Volume of inner empty cylinder = πR^2h - πr^2h = πh(R^2 - r^2). Volume = (22/7) × 20 × (4^2 - 3^2) = (22/7) × 20 × (16 - 9) = (22/7) × 20 × 7 = 22 × 20 = 440 cubic cm.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-vol-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Percentage Error in Cylinder Volume] If the radius of a cylinder decreases by 10% and height increases by 20%, what is the change in volume?", "options": ["A) 2.8% decrease", "B) 2.8% increase", "C) 1.2% decrease", "D) 1.2% increase"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the base radius of a solid cylinder is decreased by exactly 10%, while its vertical height is simultaneously increased by exactly 20%, what is the exact net percentage change in its overall volume?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Volume V = πr^2h. V ∝ r^2 × h. Let initial V = 100 × 100 × 100 (conceptually). New radius = 90% of r (multiplier 0.9). New height = 120% of h (multiplier 1.2). New Volume factor = (0.9)^2 × 1.2 = 0.81 × 1.2 = 0.972. Thus, the new volume is exactly 97.2% of the original volume. Change = 100 - 97.2 = 2.8% decrease.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-vol-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Ratio of Surface Areas of Spheres] If the ratio of volumes of two spheres is 8:27, find the ratio of their surface areas.", "options": ["A) 2 : 3", "B) 4 : 9", "C) 8 : 27", "D) 16 : 81"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The mathematical volumes of two distinct perfect solid spheres are strictly in the ratio 8 : 27. What is the exact ratio of their respective total surface areas?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Volume ratio = (r1)^3 / (r2)^3 = 8 / 27. Taking the cube root of both sides gives the ratio of their radii: r1 / r2 = 2 / 3. Surface Area ratio = (r1)^2 / (r2)^2 = (2)^2 / (3)^2 = 4 / 9. Ratio is 4 : 9.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-vol-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Earth Dug out of a Well] A well of diameter 3m is dug 14m deep. The earth taken out is spread evenly around it to form an embankment 4m wide. Find the height of the embankment.", "options": ["A) 1.125 meters", "B) 1.250 meters", "C) 1.500 meters", "D) 2.250 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A cylindrical well with an inner diameter of exactly 3m is dug 14m deep into the ground. All the earth (soil) taken out is spread flawlessly and evenly around it to form an elevated circular embankment strictly 4m wide. What is the exact height of this new embankment?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Volume of earth dug out = Volume of well = πr^2h = π × (1.5)^2 × 14 = π × 2.25 × 14 = 31.5π cubic meters. 2) The embankment forms a hollow cylinder completely around the well! Inner radius = 1.5m. Outer radius = 1.5m + 4m = 5.5m. 3) Area of the embankment base = π(R^2 - r^2) = π(5.5^2 - 1.5^2) = π(30.25 - 2.25) = 28π sq meters. 4) Volume of embankment = Base Area × Height => 31.5π = 28π × H => H = 31.5 / 28 = 9/8 = 1.125 meters.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-vol-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Surface Area of a Cuboid Painted] Dimensions of a room are 10m × 8m × 5m. Find the cost of painting its 4 walls and the ceiling at ₹10 per sq m.", "options": ["A) ₹1,800", "B) ₹2,100", "C) ₹2,600", "D) ₹3,400"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The interior dimensions of a room are exactly 10m long, 8m broad, and 5m high. What is the exact total monetary cost of painting its 4 walls and the ceiling (ignoring the floor) at the rate of ₹10 per square meter?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Area of 4 walls = Lateral Surface Area = 2(L + B) × H = 2(10 + 8) × 5 = 2(18) × 5 = 180 sq m. 2) Area of ceiling = L × B = 10 × 8 = 80 sq m. 3) Total area to paint = 180 + 80 = 260 sq m. 4) Total cost = 260 sq m × ₹10 = ₹2,600.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-vol-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Frustum Volume Application] A drinking glass is in the shape of a frustum of a cone of height 14 cm. Diameters of its two circular ends are 4 cm and 2 cm. Find capacity.", "options": ["A) 88 cubic cm", "B) 102.66 cubic cm", "C) 112 cubic cm", "D) 154 cubic cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A solid drinking glass is specifically shaped as a frustum of a right circular cone having a vertical height of exactly 14 cm. If the diameters of its two circular flat ends are 4 cm and 2 cm, what is the total fluid capacity (volume) of the glass? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Formula for Volume of a Frustum of a cone = (1/3)πh(R^2 + r^2 + Rr). Here, Top Radius R = 4/2 = 2 cm, Bottom Radius r = 2/2 = 1 cm, Height h = 14 cm. Volume = (1/3) × (22/7) × 14 × (2^2 + 1^2 + 2×1) = (1/3) × 44 × (4 + 1 + 2) = (1/3) × 44 × 7 = 308 / 3 = 102.66 cubic cm.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-vol-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Cuboid Diagonal vs Edges Sum] Sum of length, breadth, and depth of a cuboid is 19 cm, and its diagonal is 5√5 cm. Surface area?", "options": ["A) 125 sq cm", "B) 176 sq cm", "C) 236 sq cm", "D) 361 sq cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The mathematical sum of the length, breadth, and depth of a solid cuboid is exactly 19 cm, and the length of its longest internal diagonal is exactly 5√5 cm. What is the total outer surface area of the cuboid?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) We are given L + B + H = 19. 2) Diagonal = √(L^2 + B^2 + H^2) = 5√5 => L^2 + B^2 + H^2 = 125. 3) Using algebraic identity: (L + B + H)^2 = L^2 + B^2 + H^2 + 2(LB + BH + HL). Notice that 2(LB + BH + HL) IS the Total Surface Area (TSA)! 4) 19^2 = 125 + TSA => 361 = 125 + TSA => TSA = 361 - 125 = 236 sq cm.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-vol-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Weight of an Iron Pipe] An iron pipe is 21 cm long and its exterior diameter is 8 cm. If the thickness of the pipe is 1 cm and iron weighs 8 g/cm^3, find weight of the pipe.", "options": ["A) 2.456 kg", "B) 3.696 kg", "C) 4.224 kg", "D) 5.120 kg"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A straight iron pipe is exactly 21 cm long, and its exterior diameter is exactly 8 cm. If the structural thickness of the iron pipe is strictly 1 cm and the physical density of iron is exactly 8 grams per cubic centimeter, what is the exact weight of the pipe? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Outer radius R = 8/2 = 4 cm. Since thickness = 1 cm, Inner radius r = 4 - 1 = 3 cm. 2) Volume of iron = πh(R^2 - r^2) = (22/7) × 21 × (4^2 - 3^2) = 66 × (16 - 9) = 66 × 7 = 462 cubic cm. 3) Total weight = Volume × Density = 462 × 8 g = 3696 grams. Convert to kg = 3.696 kg.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-vol-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Water Conserved After Submersion] A rectangular tank is 15m × 11m and contains water to a depth of 5m. 200 solid spherical blocks of radius 21 cm are dropped. Rise in water level?", "options": ["A) 4.12 cm", "B) 4.60 cm", "C) 4.704 cm", "D) 5.25 cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A rectangular tank base measures exactly 15m by 11m, and it presently contains water to a depth of 5m. Exactly 200 solid spherical steel blocks, each having a radius of exactly 21 cm, are completely dropped and submerged into the tank. What is the exact mathematical rise in the water level of the tank? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) The rise in water volume equals the total volume of all submerged blocks! 2) Volume of ONE sphere = (4/3)πr^3 = (4/3) × (22/7) × (0.21 m)^3 = (88/21) × 0.009261 = 0.038808 cubic meters. 3) Volume of 200 spheres = 200 × 0.038808 = 7.7616 cubic meters. 4) The accumulated volume causes the water to rise across the entire tank base. Area of tank base = 15 × 11 = 165 sq m. 5) Rise in level = Total Submerged Volume / Base Area = 7.7616 / 165 = 0.04704 meters = 4.704 cm.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. Core 3D Geometry Formulas\n* **Cuboid:**\n  * Volume = `L × B × H`\n  * Total Surface Area (TSA) = `2(LB + BH + HL)`\n  * Longest diagonal = `√(L^2 + B^2 + H^2)`\n* **Cube:**\n  * Volume = `a^3`, TSA = `6a^2`, Diagonal = `a√3`\n* **Cylinder:**\n  * Volume = `πr^2h`\n  * Curved Surface Area (CSA) = `2πrh`\n  * TSA = `2πr(h + r)`\n* **Cone:**\n  * Volume = `1/3 πr^2h`\n  * Slant height `l = √(r^2 + h^2)`\n  * CSA = `πrl`, TSA = `πr(l + r)`\n* **Sphere:**\n  * Volume = `4/3 πr^3`\n  * Surface Area = `4πr^2`\n* **Hemisphere:**\n  * Volume = `2/3 πr^3`\n  * CSA = `2πr^2`, TSA = `3πr^2`\n\n### 💡 2. High-Yield Placement Shortcuts\n* **Melting and Recasting (Conservation of Volume):**\n  * When a solid is melted and formed into a new shape (or multiple small shapes), the **TOTAL VOLUME** remains strictly constant!\n  * Example: Number of small lead spheres obtained by melting a large sphere = `Volume of large / Volume of 1 small`.\n* **Flow Rates through Pipes into Tanks:**\n  * Volume of water flowing per hour = `Cross-sectional Area of Pipe × Speed of water flowing`.\n  * Time to fill tank = `(Volume of Tank) / (Volume flowing per hour)`.\n\n### 💡 3. Proportional Scaling in 3D\n* If the linear dimension (radius, side, height) is multiplied by `k`:\n  * Surface Area is multiplied by **`k^2`**.\n  * Volume is multiplied by **`k^3`**.\n* If a sphere's radius is increased by 10%, its Volume increases by `(1.10)^3 = 1.331`, which is a 33.1% increase.	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Volume & Surface Area	Calculate 3D metrics for cubes, cuboids, cylinders, cones, and spheres. Master volume conservation during melting and structural cost optimizations.	48	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	27	[{"id": "apt-vol-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Melting Spheres into a Single Sphere] Three solid metallic spheres of radii 3 cm, 4 cm, and 5 cm are melted to form a single solid sphere. Find the radius of the new sphere.", "options": ["A) 6 cm", "B) 7 cm", "C) 8 cm", "D) 12 cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Three completely solid metallic spheres having radii of 3 cm, 4 cm, and 5 cm respectively are melted down and recast together into a single large solid sphere. What is the exact radius of the newly formed sphere?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total Volume is conserved! V1 + V2 + V3 = V_new => (4/3)π(3^3) + (4/3)π(4^3) + (4/3)π(5^3) = (4/3)π(R^3). Cancel out (4/3)π from all terms: 3^3 + 4^3 + 5^3 = R^3 => 27 + 64 + 125 = R^3 => 216 = R^3. Taking the cube root gives R = 6 cm.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-vol-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Cone and Cylinder Ratio] A cone, a hemisphere, and a cylinder stand on equal bases and have the same height. Find the ratio of their volumes.", "options": ["A) 1 : 2 : 3", "B) 1 : 3 : 2", "C) 2 : 3 : 4", "D) 3 : 2 : 1"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A right circular cone, a solid hemisphere, and a right circular cylinder all stand on identically sized circular bases and all possess the exact same vertical height. What is the exact mathematical ratio of their respective volumes (Cone : Hemisphere : Cylinder)?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let the base radius be r. Since the hemisphere has height equal to its radius, the common height h = r for all three objects! 1) Volume of Cone = (1/3)πr^2h = (1/3)πr^3. 2) Volume of Hemisphere = (2/3)πr^3. 3) Volume of Cylinder = πr^2h = πr^3. The ratio is (1/3) : (2/3) : 1 = 1 : 2 : 3.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-vol-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Percentage Increase in Volume of a Cube] If each edge of a cube is increased by 50%, find the percentage increase in its surface area.", "options": ["A) 100%", "B) 125%", "C) 150%", "D) 225%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If every single edge of a perfect solid cube is increased by exactly 50%, what will be the exact overall percentage increase in its total surface area?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let original edge = a. Surface Area = 6a^2. New edge = 1.5a. New Surface Area = 6(1.5a)^2 = 6(2.25a^2) = 2.25 × (6a^2). The new area is 2.25 times the old area, meaning it has strictly increased by 1.25 times, or exactly 125%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-vol-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Longest Pole in a Room] The length, breadth, and height of a room are 12m, 4m, and 3m. Find the length of the longest pole that can be placed in it.", "options": ["A) 13 m", "B) 14 m", "C) 15 m", "D) 16 m"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The internal length, breadth, and height of a rectangular room are exactly 12m, 4m, and 3m respectively. What is the maximum possible length of a single straight pole that can be completely placed inside this room?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The longest pole corresponds to the main diagonal of the cuboid space. Diagonal = √(L^2 + B^2 + H^2) = √(12^2 + 4^2 + 3^2) = √(144 + 16 + 9) = √169 = 13 meters.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-vol-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Water Flowing through a Pipe] Water flows through a cylindrical pipe of 14 cm internal diameter at 3 km/hr into a rectangular tank 50m × 44m. Rise in water level in 2 hours?", "options": ["A) 3.5 cm", "B) 4.2 cm", "C) 5.0 cm", "D) 6.4 cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Liquid water freely flows through a cylindrical pipe of internal diameter exactly 14 cm at a constant rate of 3 km/hr into a rectangular empty tank measuring 50m by 44m. Exactly what will be the rise in the water level of the tank after exactly 2 hours? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Cross-sectional area of pipe: Radius = 7 cm = 0.07 m. Area = πr^2 = (22/7) × (0.07)^2 = 0.0154 sq meters. 2) Volume of water flowing per hour = Area × Speed = 0.0154 × 3000 m/hr = 46.2 cubic meters. 3) Volume flowing in 2 hours = 46.2 × 2 = 92.4 cubic meters. 4) This volume accumulates in the tank: Volume = L × B × Rise => 92.4 = 50 × 44 × Rise => 92.4 = 2200 × Rise => Rise = 92.4 / 2200 = 0.042 meters = 4.2 cm.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-vol-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Surface Area of Combined Cubes] Two cubes, each of volume 64 cubic cm, are joined end to end. Find the surface area of the resulting cuboid.", "options": ["A) 128 sq cm", "B) 160 sq cm", "C) 192 sq cm", "D) 256 sq cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two identical solid cubes, each having an exact volume of 64 cubic cm, are perfectly joined end to end. What is the total surface area of the resulting solid cuboid?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Volume of one cube = a^3 = 64 => side a = 4 cm. 2) When two such cubes are joined end-to-end, the resulting cuboid has Length L = 4 + 4 = 8 cm, Breadth B = 4 cm, and Height H = 4 cm. 3) Total Surface Area = 2(LB + BH + HL) = 2(8×4 + 4×4 + 4×8) = 2(32 + 16 + 32) = 2(80) = 160 sq cm.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-vol-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Melting Cylinder into Cone] A metallic cylinder of radius 8 cm and height 2 cm is melted and converted into a right circular cone of height 6 cm. Radius of the cone?", "options": ["A) 6 cm", "B) 8 cm", "C) 10 cm", "D) 12 cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A solid metallic right circular cylinder possessing a base radius of 8 cm and a height of 2 cm is melted down and completely recast into a solid right circular cone having a height of 6 cm. What is the base radius of the new cone?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Volume is conserved! Volume of Cylinder = Volume of Cone => π(r_cyl)^2(h_cyl) = (1/3)π(r_cone)^2(h_cone). Substitute values: π × 8^2 × 2 = (1/3) × π × (r_cone)^2 × 6. Cancel π from both sides: 64 × 2 = (r_cone)^2 × 2 => 128 = 2 × (r_cone)^2 => (r_cone)^2 = 64. Thus, the radius of the cone is 8 cm.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-vol-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Volume of Largest Cone in a Cube] Find the volume of the largest right circular cone that can be cut out of a cube of edge 14 cm.", "options": ["A) 718.66 cubic cm", "B) 1078.0 cubic cm", "C) 1232.0 cubic cm", "D) 2156.0 cubic cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the exact mathematical volume of the largest possible solid right circular cone that can be perfectly carved out of a solid wooden cube whose edges measure exactly 14 cm? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "To be the largest possible cone, its circular base must perfectly touch the midpoints of the bottom face of the cube, making its diameter equal to the cube's edge (Diameter = 14 cm => Radius r = 7 cm). The height of the cone must extend fully to the top face, so h = 14 cm. Volume of Cone = (1/3)πr^2h = (1/3) × (22/7) × (7^2) × 14 = (1/3) × 22 × 7 × 14 = (1/3) × 2156 = 718.66 cubic cm.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-vol-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Increase in Volume of Cylinder] If the radius of a cylinder is doubled and its height is halved, find the ratio of the new volume to the old volume.", "options": ["A) 1 : 1", "B) 2 : 1", "C) 4 : 1", "D) 1 : 2"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the base radius of a solid cylinder is mathematically doubled and its vertical height is simultaneously halved, what is the exact ratio of its newly formed volume to its original volume?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Original Volume V1 = πr^2h. New parameters: R = 2r, H = h/2. New Volume V2 = π(2r)^2(h/2) = π(4r^2)(h/2) = 2πr^2h. Ratio = V2 / V1 = (2πr^2h) / (πr^2h) = 2 : 1.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-vol-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Surface Area of Hemisphere] Find the Total Surface Area of a solid hemisphere of radius 21 cm.", "options": ["A) 2,772 sq cm", "B) 3,124 sq cm", "C) 4,158 sq cm", "D) 5,544 sq cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the exact Total Surface Area (TSA) of a perfectly solid hemisphere possessing an exact radius of 21 cm? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "For a SOLID hemisphere, Total Surface Area = Curved Surface Area + Area of flat circular base = 2πr^2 + πr^2 = 3πr^2. TSA = 3 × (22/7) × (21)^2 = 3 × (22/7) × 441 = 3 × 22 × 63 = 66 × 63 = 4,158 sq cm.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-vol-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Number of Lead Shots from a Cube] How many lead shots, each 3 mm in diameter, can be made from a cuboid of dimensions 9cm × 11cm × 12cm?", "options": ["A) 84,000", "B) 88,000", "C) 90,000", "D) 92,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly how many identical spherical lead shots, each possessing an outer diameter of exactly 3 mm, can be completely cast by melting down a solid cuboid measuring exactly 9 cm by 11 cm by 12 cm? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Convert all dimensions to cm! Diameter of shot = 3 mm = 0.3 cm => Radius r = 0.15 cm. 1) Volume of cuboid = 9 × 11 × 12 = 1188 cubic cm. 2) Volume of one spherical shot = (4/3)πr^3 = (4/3) × (22/7) × (0.15)^3 = (88/21) × 0.003375 = 0.0141428 cubic cm. 3) Number of shots = Total Volume / Volume of 1 shot = 1188 / [(88/21) × (3.375/1000)] = (1188 × 21 × 1000) / (88 × 3.375) = 24948000 / 297 = 84,000 lead shots.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-vol-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Hollowing out a Cylinder] A hollow cylindrical tube 20 cm long is made of iron and its external and internal diameters are 8 cm and 6 cm. Find volume of iron used.", "options": ["A) 330 cubic cm", "B) 440 cubic cm", "C) 550 cubic cm", "D) 660 cubic cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A hollow cylindrical tube strictly 20 cm long is cast out of iron. Its external outer diameter is exactly 8 cm, and its internal empty diameter is exactly 6 cm. What is the exact total mathematical volume of iron used in fabricating the tube? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Outer radius R = 8/2 = 4 cm. Inner radius r = 6/2 = 3 cm. The volume of material = Volume of outer cylinder - Volume of inner empty cylinder = πR^2h - πr^2h = πh(R^2 - r^2). Volume = (22/7) × 20 × (4^2 - 3^2) = (22/7) × 20 × (16 - 9) = (22/7) × 20 × 7 = 22 × 20 = 440 cubic cm.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-vol-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Percentage Error in Cylinder Volume] If the radius of a cylinder decreases by 10% and height increases by 20%, what is the change in volume?", "options": ["A) 2.8% decrease", "B) 2.8% increase", "C) 1.2% decrease", "D) 1.2% increase"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the base radius of a solid cylinder is decreased by exactly 10%, while its vertical height is simultaneously increased by exactly 20%, what is the exact net percentage change in its overall volume?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Volume V = πr^2h. V ∝ r^2 × h. Let initial V = 100 × 100 × 100 (conceptually). New radius = 90% of r (multiplier 0.9). New height = 120% of h (multiplier 1.2). New Volume factor = (0.9)^2 × 1.2 = 0.81 × 1.2 = 0.972. Thus, the new volume is exactly 97.2% of the original volume. Change = 100 - 97.2 = 2.8% decrease.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-vol-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Ratio of Surface Areas of Spheres] If the ratio of volumes of two spheres is 8:27, find the ratio of their surface areas.", "options": ["A) 2 : 3", "B) 4 : 9", "C) 8 : 27", "D) 16 : 81"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The mathematical volumes of two distinct perfect solid spheres are strictly in the ratio 8 : 27. What is the exact ratio of their respective total surface areas?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Volume ratio = (r1)^3 / (r2)^3 = 8 / 27. Taking the cube root of both sides gives the ratio of their radii: r1 / r2 = 2 / 3. Surface Area ratio = (r1)^2 / (r2)^2 = (2)^2 / (3)^2 = 4 / 9. Ratio is 4 : 9.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-vol-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Earth Dug out of a Well] A well of diameter 3m is dug 14m deep. The earth taken out is spread evenly around it to form an embankment 4m wide. Find the height of the embankment.", "options": ["A) 1.125 meters", "B) 1.250 meters", "C) 1.500 meters", "D) 2.250 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A cylindrical well with an inner diameter of exactly 3m is dug 14m deep into the ground. All the earth (soil) taken out is spread flawlessly and evenly around it to form an elevated circular embankment strictly 4m wide. What is the exact height of this new embankment?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Volume of earth dug out = Volume of well = πr^2h = π × (1.5)^2 × 14 = π × 2.25 × 14 = 31.5π cubic meters. 2) The embankment forms a hollow cylinder completely around the well! Inner radius = 1.5m. Outer radius = 1.5m + 4m = 5.5m. 3) Area of the embankment base = π(R^2 - r^2) = π(5.5^2 - 1.5^2) = π(30.25 - 2.25) = 28π sq meters. 4) Volume of embankment = Base Area × Height => 31.5π = 28π × H => H = 31.5 / 28 = 9/8 = 1.125 meters.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-vol-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Surface Area of a Cuboid Painted] Dimensions of a room are 10m × 8m × 5m. Find the cost of painting its 4 walls and the ceiling at ₹10 per sq m.", "options": ["A) ₹1,800", "B) ₹2,100", "C) ₹2,600", "D) ₹3,400"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The interior dimensions of a room are exactly 10m long, 8m broad, and 5m high. What is the exact total monetary cost of painting its 4 walls and the ceiling (ignoring the floor) at the rate of ₹10 per square meter?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Area of 4 walls = Lateral Surface Area = 2(L + B) × H = 2(10 + 8) × 5 = 2(18) × 5 = 180 sq m. 2) Area of ceiling = L × B = 10 × 8 = 80 sq m. 3) Total area to paint = 180 + 80 = 260 sq m. 4) Total cost = 260 sq m × ₹10 = ₹2,600.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-vol-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Frustum Volume Application] A drinking glass is in the shape of a frustum of a cone of height 14 cm. Diameters of its two circular ends are 4 cm and 2 cm. Find capacity.", "options": ["A) 88 cubic cm", "B) 102.66 cubic cm", "C) 112 cubic cm", "D) 154 cubic cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A solid drinking glass is specifically shaped as a frustum of a right circular cone having a vertical height of exactly 14 cm. If the diameters of its two circular flat ends are 4 cm and 2 cm, what is the total fluid capacity (volume) of the glass? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Formula for Volume of a Frustum of a cone = (1/3)πh(R^2 + r^2 + Rr). Here, Top Radius R = 4/2 = 2 cm, Bottom Radius r = 2/2 = 1 cm, Height h = 14 cm. Volume = (1/3) × (22/7) × 14 × (2^2 + 1^2 + 2×1) = (1/3) × 44 × (4 + 1 + 2) = (1/3) × 44 × 7 = 308 / 3 = 102.66 cubic cm.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-vol-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Cuboid Diagonal vs Edges Sum] Sum of length, breadth, and depth of a cuboid is 19 cm, and its diagonal is 5√5 cm. Surface area?", "options": ["A) 125 sq cm", "B) 176 sq cm", "C) 236 sq cm", "D) 361 sq cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The mathematical sum of the length, breadth, and depth of a solid cuboid is exactly 19 cm, and the length of its longest internal diagonal is exactly 5√5 cm. What is the total outer surface area of the cuboid?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) We are given L + B + H = 19. 2) Diagonal = √(L^2 + B^2 + H^2) = 5√5 => L^2 + B^2 + H^2 = 125. 3) Using algebraic identity: (L + B + H)^2 = L^2 + B^2 + H^2 + 2(LB + BH + HL). Notice that 2(LB + BH + HL) IS the Total Surface Area (TSA)! 4) 19^2 = 125 + TSA => 361 = 125 + TSA => TSA = 361 - 125 = 236 sq cm.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-vol-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Weight of an Iron Pipe] An iron pipe is 21 cm long and its exterior diameter is 8 cm. If the thickness of the pipe is 1 cm and iron weighs 8 g/cm^3, find weight of the pipe.", "options": ["A) 2.456 kg", "B) 3.696 kg", "C) 4.224 kg", "D) 5.120 kg"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A straight iron pipe is exactly 21 cm long, and its exterior diameter is exactly 8 cm. If the structural thickness of the iron pipe is strictly 1 cm and the physical density of iron is exactly 8 grams per cubic centimeter, what is the exact weight of the pipe? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Outer radius R = 8/2 = 4 cm. Since thickness = 1 cm, Inner radius r = 4 - 1 = 3 cm. 2) Volume of iron = πh(R^2 - r^2) = (22/7) × 21 × (4^2 - 3^2) = 66 × (16 - 9) = 66 × 7 = 462 cubic cm. 3) Total weight = Volume × Density = 462 × 8 g = 3696 grams. Convert to kg = 3.696 kg.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-vol-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Water Conserved After Submersion] A rectangular tank is 15m × 11m and contains water to a depth of 5m. 200 solid spherical blocks of radius 21 cm are dropped. Rise in water level?", "options": ["A) 4.12 cm", "B) 4.60 cm", "C) 4.704 cm", "D) 5.25 cm"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A rectangular tank base measures exactly 15m by 11m, and it presently contains water to a depth of 5m. Exactly 200 solid spherical steel blocks, each having a radius of exactly 21 cm, are completely dropped and submerged into the tank. What is the exact mathematical rise in the water level of the tank? (Take π = 22/7)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) The rise in water volume equals the total volume of all submerged blocks! 2) Volume of ONE sphere = (4/3)πr^3 = (4/3) × (22/7) × (0.21 m)^3 = (88/21) × 0.009261 = 0.038808 cubic meters. 3) Volume of 200 spheres = 200 × 0.038808 = 7.7616 cubic meters. 4) The accumulated volume causes the water to rise across the entire tank base. Area of tank base = 15 × 11 = 165 sq m. 5) Rise in level = Total Submerged Volume / Base Area = 7.7616 / 165 = 0.04704 meters = 4.704 cm.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22141	Lecture 1: Introduction to Node.js — Client vs. Server Architecture, V8 Engine, libuv & Why Node.js Excels at Scalable Backends	node-lecture-1-introduction-to-nodejs-architecture-v8-libuv	Understand what Node.js is, how Client-Side and Server-Side responsibilities differ, and how V8 and libuv combine to power non-blocking, event-driven backend architectures.	2026-10-06 21:56:18.722653+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\n**Node.js** is neither a programming language nor a framework—it is an open-source, cross-platform **JavaScript Runtime Environment** created by Ryan Dahl in 2009 that executes JavaScript code outside of a web browser using Chrome's **V8 Engine** and the **`libuv`** C library.\n\n### 💡 Client-Side (Browser) vs. Server-Side (Node.js) Responsibilities\n* **Client-Side (Browser JS / React):** Runs on the end user's device. Responsible for rendering UI, handling DOM events, animations, and sending HTTP requests. Has **window** and **document** objects, but cannot directly access server databases or the OS file system for security reasons.\n* **Server-Side (Node.js):** Runs on cloud servers. Responsible for business logic, database queries (SQL/NoSQL), authentication/authorization, file system operations, and serving REST APIs or dynamic HTML. Has **global** and **process** objects, and no DOM (`window` or `document` do not exist).\n\n### 💡 Internal Architecture of Node.js (V8 + `libuv`)\n* **Google V8 Engine (C++):** Parses and compiles JavaScript code directly into fast native machine code using Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation.\n* **`libuv` (C Library):** Gives Node.js access to the operating system's **asynchronous, non-blocking I/O** (file system, network sockets, DNS, child processes), manages the **Event Loop**, and maintains a background **Worker Thread Pool** for heavy I/O tasks.\n* **Why Node.js Excels at I/O-Heavy Workloads:** Traditional multi-threaded servers (like Apache/Java Tomcat) spawn a dedicated OS thread per request (consuming ~1–2 MB RAM per thread and blocking on DB waits). Node.js uses a **single main event-loop thread** that delegates I/O waits to `libuv`, allowing a single lightweight server to handle tens of thousands of concurrent connections!\n\n### 🛠️ Browser Global vs. Node.js Runtime Environment\n```javascript\n// In Node.js, DOM objects (window, document) do NOT exist:\n// console.log(window); // ReferenceError: window is not defined\n\n// Instead, Node.js provides server-level globals:\nconsole.log("Node.js Version:", process.version);\nconsole.log("Operating System Platform:", process.platform);\nconsole.log("Current Working Directory:", process.cwd());\n```	[{"url": "https://nodejs.org/en/learn/getting-started/introduction-to-nodejs", "link": "https://nodejs.org/en/learn/getting-started/introduction-to-nodejs", "title": "Node.js Official Docs — Introduction to Node.js"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AZzV3wZCvI4", "title": "Lecture 1: Introduction to NodeJS | NodeJS Complete Course in Hindi", "duration": "46m", "creator_name": "Complete Coding by Prashant Sir", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir"}]	[{"id": "node-lec-1-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Is Node.js a programming language or a framework? How does it work internally?", "title": "Q1: Is Node.js a programming language or a framework? How does it work internally?", "answer": "Node.js is neither a language nor a framework—it is a cross-platform JavaScript runtime environment built on Chrome's V8 JavaScript engine and the libuv C++ library. V8 compiles and executes JavaScript code into native machine code, while libuv provides the Event Loop, Worker Thread Pool, and non-blocking OS-level I/O bindings (file system, TCP/UDP sockets, DNS).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Node.js is neither a language nor a framework—it is a cross-platform JavaScript runtime environment built on Chrome's V8 JavaScript engine and the libuv C++ library. V8 compiles and executes JavaScript code into native machine code, while libuv provides the Event Loop, Worker Thread Pool, and non-blocking OS-level I/O bindings (file system, TCP/UDP sockets, DNS)."}, {"id": "node-lec-1-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: How can a single-threaded Node.js server handle 10,000+ concurrent requests without blocking, compared to traditional thread-per-request servers?", "title": "Q2: How can a single-threaded Node.js server handle 10,000+ concurrent requests without blocking, compared to traditional thread-per-request servers?", "answer": "In traditional thread-per-request servers, each incoming request occupies a separate OS thread that sits idle and blocks RAM while waiting for database queries or disk I/O. Node.js uses an Event-Driven, Non-Blocking I/O model: the single main thread receives a request, immediately hands off any slow I/O operation (DB query, file read, network call) to the OS kernel or libuv thread pool, and immediately moves on to serve the next user's request. When the I/O finishes, a callback is queued in the Event Loop.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "In traditional thread-per-request servers, each incoming request occupies a separate OS thread that sits idle and blocks RAM while waiting for database queries or disk I/O. Node.js uses an Event-Driven, Non-Blocking I/O model: the single main thread receives a request, immediately hands off any slow I/O operation (DB query, file read, network call) to the OS kernel or libuv thread pool, and immediately moves on to serve the next user's request. When the I/O finishes, a callback is queued in the Event Loop."}, {"id": "node-lec-1-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: For what types of applications is Node.js ideal, and when should you avoid running heavy tasks directly on the Node.js main thread?", "title": "Q3: For what types of applications is Node.js ideal, and when should you avoid running heavy tasks directly on the Node.js main thread?", "answer": "Node.js is ideal for I/O-bound, data-intensive, real-time applications such as REST/GraphQL APIs, chat/WebSocket servers, streaming platforms, and microservices. You should avoid running heavy CPU-bound synchronous tasks (such as video encoding, image compression, matrix multiplication, or synchronous cryptography) directly on the main thread because a CPU-heavy task blocks the single Event Loop thread and freezes responses for all connected users (unless offloaded to Worker Threads or a separate microservice).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Node.js is ideal for I/O-bound, data-intensive, real-time applications such as REST/GraphQL APIs, chat/WebSocket servers, streaming platforms, and microservices. You should avoid running heavy CPU-bound synchronous tasks (such as video encoding, image compression, matrix multiplication, or synchronous cryptography) directly on the main thread because a CPU-heavy task blocks the single Event Loop thread and freezes responses for all connected users (unless offloaded to Worker Threads or a separate microservice)."}, {"id": "node-lec-1-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What are the key differences between JavaScript running in a Browser vs. JavaScript running in Node.js?", "title": "Q4: What are the key differences between JavaScript running in a Browser vs. JavaScript running in Node.js?", "answer": "1) Global Object & APIs: Browsers provide window, document (DOM), localStorage, and Web APIs; Node.js provides global, process, Buffer, and OS modules like fs, path, http, and crypto. 2) Environment Control: In a browser, you cannot control which browser version the user has installed; in Node.js, you control the exact runtime version on your server. 3) Sandbox vs. OS Access: Browser JS is sandboxed from the user's hard drive; Node.js has full access to the server's file system and network ports.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) Global Object & APIs: Browsers provide window, document (DOM), localStorage, and Web APIs; Node.js provides global, process, Buffer, and OS modules like fs, path, http, and crypto. 2) Environment Control: In a browser, you cannot control which browser version the user has installed; in Node.js, you control the exact runtime version on your server. 3) Sandbox vs. OS Access: Browser JS is sandboxed from the user's hard drive; Node.js has full access to the server's file system and network ports."}, {"id": "node-lec-1-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What role does libuv play in Node.js if the V8 engine already executes JavaScript?", "title": "Q5: What role does libuv play in Node.js if the V8 engine already executes JavaScript?", "answer": "The V8 engine only knows how to parse ECMAScript, manage the Call Stack and Memory Heap, and run garbage collection—it has no concept of files, network sockets, or OS timers. libuv is a multi-platform C library that implements the Node.js Event Loop, asynchronous TCP/UDP sockets, DNS resolution, file system operations, and a C thread pool (defaulting to 4 threads) to make cross-platform non-blocking I/O possible.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "The V8 engine only knows how to parse ECMAScript, manage the Call Stack and Memory Heap, and run garbage collection—it has no concept of files, network sockets, or OS timers. libuv is a multi-platform C library that implements the Node.js Event Loop, asynchronous TCP/UDP sockets, DNS resolution, file system operations, and a C thread pool (defaulting to 4 threads) to make cross-platform non-blocking I/O possible."}, {"id": "node-lec-1-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why should critical validation and business logic always be enforced on the Backend (Node.js) even if Frontend validation exists?", "title": "Q6: Why should critical validation and business logic always be enforced on the Backend (Node.js) even if Frontend validation exists?", "answer": "Frontend code runs entirely on the client's machine and can be inspected, modified, or bypassed using browser DevTools, Postman, or cURL. Therefore, client-side validation is only for user experience (UX), while server-side validation, authentication, and pricing/business rules in Node.js are mandatory for actual security and data integrity.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Frontend code runs entirely on the client's machine and can be inspected, modified, or bypassed using browser DevTools, Postman, or cURL. Therefore, client-side validation is only for user experience (UX), while server-side validation, authentication, and pricing/business rules in Node.js are mandatory for actual security and data integrity."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\n**Node.js** is neither a programming language nor a framework—it is an open-source, cross-platform **JavaScript Runtime Environment** created by Ryan Dahl in 2009 that executes JavaScript code outside of a web browser using Chrome's **V8 Engine** and the **`libuv`** C library.\n\n### 💡 Client-Side (Browser) vs. Server-Side (Node.js) Responsibilities\n* **Client-Side (Browser JS / React):** Runs on the end user's device. Responsible for rendering UI, handling DOM events, animations, and sending HTTP requests. Has **window** and **document** objects, but cannot directly access server databases or the OS file system for security reasons.\n* **Server-Side (Node.js):** Runs on cloud servers. Responsible for business logic, database queries (SQL/NoSQL), authentication/authorization, file system operations, and serving REST APIs or dynamic HTML. Has **global** and **process** objects, and no DOM (`window` or `document` do not exist).\n\n### 💡 Internal Architecture of Node.js (V8 + `libuv`)\n* **Google V8 Engine (C++):** Parses and compiles JavaScript code directly into fast native machine code using Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation.\n* **`libuv` (C Library):** Gives Node.js access to the operating system's **asynchronous, non-blocking I/O** (file system, network sockets, DNS, child processes), manages the **Event Loop**, and maintains a background **Worker Thread Pool** for heavy I/O tasks.\n* **Why Node.js Excels at I/O-Heavy Workloads:** Traditional multi-threaded servers (like Apache/Java Tomcat) spawn a dedicated OS thread per request (consuming ~1–2 MB RAM per thread and blocking on DB waits). Node.js uses a **single main event-loop thread** that delegates I/O waits to `libuv`, allowing a single lightweight server to handle tens of thousands of concurrent connections!\n\n### 🛠️ Browser Global vs. Node.js Runtime Environment\n```javascript\n// In Node.js, DOM objects (window, document) do NOT exist:\n// console.log(window); // ReferenceError: window is not defined\n\n// Instead, Node.js provides server-level globals:\nconsole.log("Node.js Version:", process.version);\nconsole.log("Operating System Platform:", process.platform);\nconsole.log("Current Working Directory:", process.cwd());\n```	Complete Coding by Prashant Sir	https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir	24	Lecture 1: Introduction to Node.js — Client vs. Server Architecture, V8 Engine, libuv & Why Node.js Excels at Scalable Backends	Understand what Node.js is, how Client-Side and Server-Side responsibilities differ, and how V8 and libuv combine to power non-blocking, event-driven backend architectures.	20	Easy	\N	1	[{"id": "node-lec-1-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Is Node.js a programming language or a framework? How does it work internally?", "title": "Q1: Is Node.js a programming language or a framework? How does it work internally?", "answer": "Node.js is neither a language nor a framework—it is a cross-platform JavaScript runtime environment built on Chrome's V8 JavaScript engine and the libuv C++ library. V8 compiles and executes JavaScript code into native machine code, while libuv provides the Event Loop, Worker Thread Pool, and non-blocking OS-level I/O bindings (file system, TCP/UDP sockets, DNS).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Node.js is neither a language nor a framework—it is a cross-platform JavaScript runtime environment built on Chrome's V8 JavaScript engine and the libuv C++ library. V8 compiles and executes JavaScript code into native machine code, while libuv provides the Event Loop, Worker Thread Pool, and non-blocking OS-level I/O bindings (file system, TCP/UDP sockets, DNS)."}, {"id": "node-lec-1-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: How can a single-threaded Node.js server handle 10,000+ concurrent requests without blocking, compared to traditional thread-per-request servers?", "title": "Q2: How can a single-threaded Node.js server handle 10,000+ concurrent requests without blocking, compared to traditional thread-per-request servers?", "answer": "In traditional thread-per-request servers, each incoming request occupies a separate OS thread that sits idle and blocks RAM while waiting for database queries or disk I/O. Node.js uses an Event-Driven, Non-Blocking I/O model: the single main thread receives a request, immediately hands off any slow I/O operation (DB query, file read, network call) to the OS kernel or libuv thread pool, and immediately moves on to serve the next user's request. When the I/O finishes, a callback is queued in the Event Loop.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "In traditional thread-per-request servers, each incoming request occupies a separate OS thread that sits idle and blocks RAM while waiting for database queries or disk I/O. Node.js uses an Event-Driven, Non-Blocking I/O model: the single main thread receives a request, immediately hands off any slow I/O operation (DB query, file read, network call) to the OS kernel or libuv thread pool, and immediately moves on to serve the next user's request. When the I/O finishes, a callback is queued in the Event Loop."}, {"id": "node-lec-1-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: For what types of applications is Node.js ideal, and when should you avoid running heavy tasks directly on the Node.js main thread?", "title": "Q3: For what types of applications is Node.js ideal, and when should you avoid running heavy tasks directly on the Node.js main thread?", "answer": "Node.js is ideal for I/O-bound, data-intensive, real-time applications such as REST/GraphQL APIs, chat/WebSocket servers, streaming platforms, and microservices. You should avoid running heavy CPU-bound synchronous tasks (such as video encoding, image compression, matrix multiplication, or synchronous cryptography) directly on the main thread because a CPU-heavy task blocks the single Event Loop thread and freezes responses for all connected users (unless offloaded to Worker Threads or a separate microservice).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Node.js is ideal for I/O-bound, data-intensive, real-time applications such as REST/GraphQL APIs, chat/WebSocket servers, streaming platforms, and microservices. You should avoid running heavy CPU-bound synchronous tasks (such as video encoding, image compression, matrix multiplication, or synchronous cryptography) directly on the main thread because a CPU-heavy task blocks the single Event Loop thread and freezes responses for all connected users (unless offloaded to Worker Threads or a separate microservice)."}, {"id": "node-lec-1-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What are the key differences between JavaScript running in a Browser vs. JavaScript running in Node.js?", "title": "Q4: What are the key differences between JavaScript running in a Browser vs. JavaScript running in Node.js?", "answer": "1) Global Object & APIs: Browsers provide window, document (DOM), localStorage, and Web APIs; Node.js provides global, process, Buffer, and OS modules like fs, path, http, and crypto. 2) Environment Control: In a browser, you cannot control which browser version the user has installed; in Node.js, you control the exact runtime version on your server. 3) Sandbox vs. OS Access: Browser JS is sandboxed from the user's hard drive; Node.js has full access to the server's file system and network ports.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) Global Object & APIs: Browsers provide window, document (DOM), localStorage, and Web APIs; Node.js provides global, process, Buffer, and OS modules like fs, path, http, and crypto. 2) Environment Control: In a browser, you cannot control which browser version the user has installed; in Node.js, you control the exact runtime version on your server. 3) Sandbox vs. OS Access: Browser JS is sandboxed from the user's hard drive; Node.js has full access to the server's file system and network ports."}, {"id": "node-lec-1-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What role does libuv play in Node.js if the V8 engine already executes JavaScript?", "title": "Q5: What role does libuv play in Node.js if the V8 engine already executes JavaScript?", "answer": "The V8 engine only knows how to parse ECMAScript, manage the Call Stack and Memory Heap, and run garbage collection—it has no concept of files, network sockets, or OS timers. libuv is a multi-platform C library that implements the Node.js Event Loop, asynchronous TCP/UDP sockets, DNS resolution, file system operations, and a C thread pool (defaulting to 4 threads) to make cross-platform non-blocking I/O possible.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "The V8 engine only knows how to parse ECMAScript, manage the Call Stack and Memory Heap, and run garbage collection—it has no concept of files, network sockets, or OS timers. libuv is a multi-platform C library that implements the Node.js Event Loop, asynchronous TCP/UDP sockets, DNS resolution, file system operations, and a C thread pool (defaulting to 4 threads) to make cross-platform non-blocking I/O possible."}, {"id": "node-lec-1-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why should critical validation and business logic always be enforced on the Backend (Node.js) even if Frontend validation exists?", "title": "Q6: Why should critical validation and business logic always be enforced on the Backend (Node.js) even if Frontend validation exists?", "answer": "Frontend code runs entirely on the client's machine and can be inspected, modified, or bypassed using browser DevTools, Postman, or cURL. Therefore, client-side validation is only for user experience (UX), while server-side validation, authentication, and pricing/business rules in Node.js are mandatory for actual security and data integrity.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "1-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Frontend code runs entirely on the client's machine and can be inspected, modified, or bypassed using browser DevTools, Postman, or cURL. Therefore, client-side validation is only for user experience (UX), while server-side validation, authentication, and pricing/business rules in Node.js are mandatory for actual security and data integrity."}]	https://nodejs.org/en/learn/getting-started/introduction-to-nodejs	\N	\N	\N
22142	Lecture 2: Installing Node.js & Environment Setup — IDE Configuration, Running Scripts, Node REPL & First fs Script	node-lecture-2-installing-nodejs-ide-setup-repl-first-script	Set up Node.js LTS across Windows, macOS, and Linux, configure VS Code for backend development, execute JavaScript files from the terminal, and use the interactive Node REPL.	2026-10-06 21:56:18.722653+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nBefore building backend servers, a developer needs a properly configured **Node.js LTS (Long-Term Support)** runtime, an **IDE (Integrated Development Environment)** like VS Code, and familiarity with executing scripts via the CLI and testing expressions in the **Node REPL**.\n\n### 💡 Node.js Installation & Version Verification\n* **LTS vs. Current Version:** Always prefer the **LTS (Long-Term Support)** version for production stability and enterprise library compatibility.\n* **Verifying CLI Installation:** Installing Node.js automatically installs both the **node** runtime binary and **npm** (Node Package Manager):\n  * `node -v` (or `node --version`) — prints installed Node.js version.\n  * `npm -v` — prints installed NPM version.\n\n### 💡 Executing Node.js Code: Script Mode vs. Interactive REPL\n1. **Script Mode (`node <filename.js>`):** Executes an entire JavaScript file from top to bottom (e.g., `node first.js`).\n2. **Node REPL (Read-Eval-Print Loop):** Typing `node` in the terminal with no filename launches an interactive shell (just like the browser DevTools Console):\n   * **Read:** Reads the JavaScript expression typed by the user.\n   * **Eval:** Evaluates/executes the expression in V8.\n   * **Print:** Prints the returned result to the terminal.\n   * **Loop:** Loops back to wait for the next command (use `.exit` or `Ctrl + C` twice to quit).\n\n### 🛠️ Writing & Running Your First Node.js File System Script (`first.js`)\n```javascript\n// File: first.js\n// Run in terminal: node first.js\nconst fs = require("fs");\n\nconsole.log("Completing Coding is the best!");\n\n// Asynchronously write data to output.txt using the built-in 'fs' core module\nfs.writeFile("output.txt", "Writing File using Node.js fs module", (err) => {\n  if (err) {\n    console.log("Error occurred:", err);\n  } else {\n    console.log("File written successfully!");\n  }\n});\n```	[{"url": "https://nodejs.org/api/repl.html", "link": "https://nodejs.org/api/repl.html", "title": "Node.js Official Docs — REPL (Read-Eval-Print Loop)"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sqTH6_xjhBY", "title": "Lecture 2: Installing NodeJS | NodeJS Complete Course in Hindi", "duration": "47m", "creator_name": "Complete Coding by Prashant Sir", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir"}]	[{"id": "node-lec-2-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What is the Node.js REPL, what do the 4 letters stand for, and when do developers use it?", "title": "Q1: What is the Node.js REPL, what do the 4 letters stand for, and when do developers use it?", "answer": "REPL stands for Read-Eval-Print Loop. Launching node in the terminal without arguments opens an interactive environment that: 1) Reads JavaScript input, 2) Evaluates the code in V8, 3) Prints the result, and 4) Loops back for the next input. Developers use it for quick prototyping, testing regular expressions, inspecting built-in modules, and debugging small snippets without creating a file.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "REPL stands for Read-Eval-Print Loop. Launching node in the terminal without arguments opens an interactive environment that: 1) Reads JavaScript input, 2) Evaluates the code in V8, 3) Prints the result, and 4) Loops back for the next input. Developers use it for quick prototyping, testing regular expressions, inspecting built-in modules, and debugging small snippets without creating a file."}, {"id": "node-lec-2-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: What is the difference between Node.js LTS (Even-numbered) and Current (Odd-numbered) releases?", "title": "Q2: What is the difference between Node.js LTS (Even-numbered) and Current (Odd-numbered) releases?", "answer": "Even-numbered major releases (like Node 20, 22) enter Active LTS (Long-Term Support) and receive 30 months of critical bug fixes, security patches, and stability guarantees—making them the mandatory choice for production servers. Odd-numbered releases (like Node 21, 23) are short-lived experimental feature releases supported for only ~6 months.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Even-numbered major releases (like Node 20, 22) enter Active LTS (Long-Term Support) and receive 30 months of critical bug fixes, security patches, and stability guarantees—making them the mandatory choice for production servers. Odd-numbered releases (like Node 21, 23) are short-lived experimental feature releases supported for only ~6 months."}, {"id": "node-lec-2-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: In the Node REPL, what does the special underscore (_) variable hold?", "title": "Q3: In the Node REPL, what does the special underscore (_) variable hold?", "answer": "In the Node.js REPL, the underscore (_) variable automatically stores the evaluated result of the most recently executed expression (for example, if you type 10 + 20 and press Enter, typing _ * 2 on the next line evaluates to 60).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In the Node.js REPL, the underscore (_) variable automatically stores the evaluated result of the most recently executed expression (for example, if you type 10 + 20 and press Enter, typing _ * 2 on the next line evaluates to 60)."}, {"id": "node-lec-2-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why does fs.writeFile(\\"output.txt\\", data, callback) take a callback function as its 3rd argument?", "title": "Q4: Why does fs.writeFile(\\"output.txt\\", data, callback) take a callback function as its 3rd argument?", "answer": "Because fs.writeFile is an asynchronous, non-blocking I/O method! Instead of freezing the Node.js main thread while the hard drive writes the file, Node hands the disk write task to libuv and continues executing the file. Once the OS finishes writing (or encounters a permission/disk error), libuv invokes the error-first callback (err) => { ... }.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Because fs.writeFile is an asynchronous, non-blocking I/O method! Instead of freezing the Node.js main thread while the hard drive writes the file, Node hands the disk write task to libuv and continues executing the file. Once the OS finishes writing (or encounters a permission/disk error), libuv invokes the error-first callback (err) => { ... }."}, {"id": "node-lec-2-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: How do you access command-line arguments passed when running a Node script (e.g., node app.js port=4000)?", "title": "Q5: How do you access command-line arguments passed when running a Node script (e.g., node app.js port=4000)?", "answer": "Using the built-in process.argv array! process.argv[0] is the path to the node executable, process.argv[1] is the path to the executed script file (app.js), and process.argv.slice(2) contains all additional user-supplied command-line arguments.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Using the built-in process.argv array! process.argv[0] is the path to the node executable, process.argv[1] is the path to the executed script file (app.js), and process.argv.slice(2) contains all additional user-supplied command-line arguments."}, {"id": "node-lec-2-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: What is an IDE (Integrated Development Environment), and how does it differ from a plain text editor?", "title": "Q6: What is an IDE (Integrated Development Environment), and how does it differ from a plain text editor?", "answer": "A plain text editor (like Notepad) only edits raw text characters. An IDE (or extensible code editor like VS Code) consolidates the code editor, syntax highlighter, IntelliSense/autocompletion, integrated terminal, Git version control client, and step-through debugger into a single unified workspace.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "A plain text editor (like Notepad) only edits raw text characters. An IDE (or extensible code editor like VS Code) consolidates the code editor, syntax highlighter, IntelliSense/autocompletion, integrated terminal, Git version control client, and step-through debugger into a single unified workspace."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nBefore building backend servers, a developer needs a properly configured **Node.js LTS (Long-Term Support)** runtime, an **IDE (Integrated Development Environment)** like VS Code, and familiarity with executing scripts via the CLI and testing expressions in the **Node REPL**.\n\n### 💡 Node.js Installation & Version Verification\n* **LTS vs. Current Version:** Always prefer the **LTS (Long-Term Support)** version for production stability and enterprise library compatibility.\n* **Verifying CLI Installation:** Installing Node.js automatically installs both the **node** runtime binary and **npm** (Node Package Manager):\n  * `node -v` (or `node --version`) — prints installed Node.js version.\n  * `npm -v` — prints installed NPM version.\n\n### 💡 Executing Node.js Code: Script Mode vs. Interactive REPL\n1. **Script Mode (`node <filename.js>`):** Executes an entire JavaScript file from top to bottom (e.g., `node first.js`).\n2. **Node REPL (Read-Eval-Print Loop):** Typing `node` in the terminal with no filename launches an interactive shell (just like the browser DevTools Console):\n   * **Read:** Reads the JavaScript expression typed by the user.\n   * **Eval:** Evaluates/executes the expression in V8.\n   * **Print:** Prints the returned result to the terminal.\n   * **Loop:** Loops back to wait for the next command (use `.exit` or `Ctrl + C` twice to quit).\n\n### 🛠️ Writing & Running Your First Node.js File System Script (`first.js`)\n```javascript\n// File: first.js\n// Run in terminal: node first.js\nconst fs = require("fs");\n\nconsole.log("Completing Coding is the best!");\n\n// Asynchronously write data to output.txt using the built-in 'fs' core module\nfs.writeFile("output.txt", "Writing File using Node.js fs module", (err) => {\n  if (err) {\n    console.log("Error occurred:", err);\n  } else {\n    console.log("File written successfully!");\n  }\n});\n```	Complete Coding by Prashant Sir	https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir	24	Lecture 2: Installing Node.js & Environment Setup — IDE Configuration, Running Scripts, Node REPL & First fs Script	Set up Node.js LTS across Windows, macOS, and Linux, configure VS Code for backend development, execute JavaScript files from the terminal, and use the interactive Node REPL.	20	Easy	\N	2	[{"id": "node-lec-2-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What is the Node.js REPL, what do the 4 letters stand for, and when do developers use it?", "title": "Q1: What is the Node.js REPL, what do the 4 letters stand for, and when do developers use it?", "answer": "REPL stands for Read-Eval-Print Loop. Launching node in the terminal without arguments opens an interactive environment that: 1) Reads JavaScript input, 2) Evaluates the code in V8, 3) Prints the result, and 4) Loops back for the next input. Developers use it for quick prototyping, testing regular expressions, inspecting built-in modules, and debugging small snippets without creating a file.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "REPL stands for Read-Eval-Print Loop. Launching node in the terminal without arguments opens an interactive environment that: 1) Reads JavaScript input, 2) Evaluates the code in V8, 3) Prints the result, and 4) Loops back for the next input. Developers use it for quick prototyping, testing regular expressions, inspecting built-in modules, and debugging small snippets without creating a file."}, {"id": "node-lec-2-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: What is the difference between Node.js LTS (Even-numbered) and Current (Odd-numbered) releases?", "title": "Q2: What is the difference between Node.js LTS (Even-numbered) and Current (Odd-numbered) releases?", "answer": "Even-numbered major releases (like Node 20, 22) enter Active LTS (Long-Term Support) and receive 30 months of critical bug fixes, security patches, and stability guarantees—making them the mandatory choice for production servers. Odd-numbered releases (like Node 21, 23) are short-lived experimental feature releases supported for only ~6 months.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Even-numbered major releases (like Node 20, 22) enter Active LTS (Long-Term Support) and receive 30 months of critical bug fixes, security patches, and stability guarantees—making them the mandatory choice for production servers. Odd-numbered releases (like Node 21, 23) are short-lived experimental feature releases supported for only ~6 months."}, {"id": "node-lec-2-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: In the Node REPL, what does the special underscore (_) variable hold?", "title": "Q3: In the Node REPL, what does the special underscore (_) variable hold?", "answer": "In the Node.js REPL, the underscore (_) variable automatically stores the evaluated result of the most recently executed expression (for example, if you type 10 + 20 and press Enter, typing _ * 2 on the next line evaluates to 60).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In the Node.js REPL, the underscore (_) variable automatically stores the evaluated result of the most recently executed expression (for example, if you type 10 + 20 and press Enter, typing _ * 2 on the next line evaluates to 60)."}, {"id": "node-lec-2-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why does fs.writeFile(\\"output.txt\\", data, callback) take a callback function as its 3rd argument?", "title": "Q4: Why does fs.writeFile(\\"output.txt\\", data, callback) take a callback function as its 3rd argument?", "answer": "Because fs.writeFile is an asynchronous, non-blocking I/O method! Instead of freezing the Node.js main thread while the hard drive writes the file, Node hands the disk write task to libuv and continues executing the file. Once the OS finishes writing (or encounters a permission/disk error), libuv invokes the error-first callback (err) => { ... }.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Because fs.writeFile is an asynchronous, non-blocking I/O method! Instead of freezing the Node.js main thread while the hard drive writes the file, Node hands the disk write task to libuv and continues executing the file. Once the OS finishes writing (or encounters a permission/disk error), libuv invokes the error-first callback (err) => { ... }."}, {"id": "node-lec-2-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: How do you access command-line arguments passed when running a Node script (e.g., node app.js port=4000)?", "title": "Q5: How do you access command-line arguments passed when running a Node script (e.g., node app.js port=4000)?", "answer": "Using the built-in process.argv array! process.argv[0] is the path to the node executable, process.argv[1] is the path to the executed script file (app.js), and process.argv.slice(2) contains all additional user-supplied command-line arguments.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Using the built-in process.argv array! process.argv[0] is the path to the node executable, process.argv[1] is the path to the executed script file (app.js), and process.argv.slice(2) contains all additional user-supplied command-line arguments."}, {"id": "node-lec-2-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: What is an IDE (Integrated Development Environment), and how does it differ from a plain text editor?", "title": "Q6: What is an IDE (Integrated Development Environment), and how does it differ from a plain text editor?", "answer": "A plain text editor (like Notepad) only edits raw text characters. An IDE (or extensible code editor like VS Code) consolidates the code editor, syntax highlighter, IntelliSense/autocompletion, integrated terminal, Git version control client, and step-through debugger into a single unified workspace.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "2-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "A plain text editor (like Notepad) only edits raw text characters. An IDE (or extensible code editor like VS Code) consolidates the code editor, syntax highlighter, IntelliSense/autocompletion, integrated terminal, Git version control client, and step-through debugger into a single unified workspace."}]	https://nodejs.org/api/repl.html	\N	\N	\N
22107	Lecture 22: Multi-Faceted Aggregations & Missing Data Imputation — $facet & $fill	mongo-lecture-22-aggregation-facet-and-fill	Execute multiple independent aggregation sub-pipelines in a single database query using $facet, and impute missing or null field values using $fill (constant value, locf, and linear interpolation).	2026-10-06 19:28:34.895846+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nDashboard APIs often need multiple analytics metrics from the same collection at once (e.g., top-selling products, total company revenue, and regional order counts). Instead of sending 3 separate database queries, **`$facet`** executes multiple independent sub-pipelines within a single `aggregate()` call. Additionally, **`$fill`** populates missing or `null` fields dynamically using constant values, prior records (`locf`), or linear interpolation (`linear`).\n\n### 💡 Multi-Pipeline Analytics with `$facet`\n* Syntax: `{ $facet: { <outputField1>: [ stage1, stage2 , <outputField2>: [ stage1, ...  } }`.\n* Each key inside `$facet` runs its own independent array of pipeline stages on the same input documents and outputs its results as a dedicated array in a single combined response document.\n\n### 💡 Imputing Missing / Null Values with `$fill`\n* **Constant Value Fill:** `output: { per: { value: 0 } }` replaces any missing or `null` `per` field with `0`. (Note: specify either `value` OR `method`, never both together).\n* **Last Observation Carried Forward (`method: "locf"`):** `output: { per: { method: "locf" } }` fills a missing value with the value from the immediately preceding document.\n* **Linear Interpolation (`method: "linear"`):** Calculates the midpoint between the preceding document's value and the following document's value (e.g., between `83` and `77` -> fills `80`). **Requires `sortBy`** inside `$fill`.\n* **`partitionBy` (Optional):** Partitions documents into independent groups (e.g., `partitionBy: { class: "$class" }`) before applying `locf` or `linear` filling.\n\n### 🛠️ Practical $facet & $fill Examples\n```javascript\n// 1. $facet: Compute Top 3 Products, Total Revenue, and Sales by Region in 1 query\ndb.sales.aggregate([\n  {\n    $facet: {\n      topProducts: [\n        {\n          $group: {\n            _id: "$product",\n            totalSales: { $sum: { $multiply: ["$price", "$quantity" } }\n          }\n        },\n        { $sort: { totalSales: -1 } },\n        { $limit: 3 }\n      ,\n      totalRevenue: [\n        {\n          $group: {\n            _id: null,\n            totalRevenue: { $sum: { $multiply: ["$price", "$quantity" } }\n          }\n        }\n      ,\n      salesByRegion: [\n        {\n          $group: {\n            _id: "$region",\n            count: { $sum: 1 }\n          }\n        },\n        { $sort: { count: -1 } }\n      \n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 2. $fill with a constant default value (0)\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $fill: {\n      output: {\n        per: { value: 0 }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n\n// 3. $fill with linear interpolation partitioned by class and sorted by _id\ndb.students.aggregate([\n  {\n    $fill: {\n      partitionBy: { class: "$class" },\n      sortBy: { _id: 1 },\n      output: {\n        per: { method: "linear" }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n)\n```	[{"url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/facet/", "link": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/facet/", "title": "MongoDB Official Manual — Lecture 22: Multi-Faceted Aggregations & Missing Data Imputation"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O3YTVz98XMw", "title": "MongoDB $facet & $fill Operator Tutorial in Hindi / Urdu", "duration": "31m", "creator_name": "Yahoo Baba", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@YahooBaba"}]	[{"id": "mongo-22-prob-1", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37648", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37648", "name": "Problem #109: Find reviews with rating 1", "title": "Problem #109: Find reviews with rating 1", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "109", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return reviews where **rating** is 1.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37648"}, {"id": "mongo-22-prob-2", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37642", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37642", "name": "Problem #111: Find discontinued inventory", "title": "Problem #111: Find discontinued inventory", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "111", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return inventory where **discontinued** is true.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37642"}, {"id": "mongo-22-prob-3", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3763f", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3763f", "name": "Problem #112: Find non discontinued inventory", "title": "Problem #112: Find non discontinued inventory", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "112", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return inventory where **discontinued** is false.", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db3763f"}, {"id": "mongo-22-prob-4", "url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37636", "link": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37636", "name": "Problem #115: Find inventory by warehouse C", "title": "Problem #115: Find inventory by warehouse C", "platform": "MongoPractice", "problemId": "115", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return inventory where **warehouse** is \\"C\\".", "external_url": "https://mongopractice.online/mongodb-practice-questions/69aab9bf7a3fe74e0db37636"}, {"id": "mongo-22-prob-5