22143	Lecture 3: First Node.js Server — How DNS & Web Protocols (HTTP/HTTPS/TCP) Work, Core Modules, require() & http.createServer()	node-lecture-3-first-node-server-dns-http-protocols-core-modules	Trace how DNS resolution (Root, TLD, Authoritative servers) and TCP/HTTP/HTTPS protocols route web traffic, import built-in Core Modules via require(), and launch your first Node.js HTTP server.	2026-10-06 21:56:18.722653+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nBefore writing a backend server, you must understand how a user typing `https://example.com` in a browser actually reaches your Node.js process running on a remote machine—and how Node's built-in **`http` Core Module** listens for those incoming connections.\n\n### 💡 How DNS (Domain Name System) & the Web Work\n1. **DNS Resolution (The Internet's Phonebook):** Computers communicate via numeric **IP Addresses** (e.g., `87.52.0.13`), not human-readable domain names. When a browser requests `example.com`, the **DNS Resolver** queries a 3-tier hierarchy:\n   * **Root DNS Server:** Directs the query to the appropriate Top-Level Domain server based on the extension (`.com`, `.in`, `.org`, `.ai`).\n   * **TLD (Top-Level Domain) Server:** Manages domains for that specific extension (e.g., Verisign for `.com`) and points to the domain's Authoritative Name Server.\n   * **Authoritative DNS Server:** Returns the exact **IP Address** registered for `example.com`.\n2. **TCP Handshake & HTTP/HTTPS Protocols:**\n   * **TCP (Transmission Control Protocol):** Establishes a reliable, ordered connection (3-way handshake) between the client IP and server IP and re-transmits any lost packets.\n   * **HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol):** Defines the request-response message format over TCP (transmits data in plain text; default port `80`).\n   * **HTTPS (HTTP Secure):** Encrypts HTTP payloads using **SSL/TLS** so attackers on the network cannot intercept passwords or tokens (default port `443`).\n\n### 💡 Node.js Core Modules & The `require()` Keyword\n* **Core Modules:** Built directly into the Node.js binary (`fs`, `http`, `https`, `path`, `os`, `crypto`, `url`). They require **zero `npm install`** and are high-performance.\n* **How `require()` Works:**\n  * Imports core modules (`require('http')`), third-party `node_modules` packages (`require('express')`), or local files (`require('./myModule')` — `.js` extension is optional).\n  * **Module Caching:** Modules are **cached after the first `require()` call**, so requiring the same module in 10 files does not reload or re-execute it 10 times!\n\n### 🛠️ Creating & Listening on Your First Node.js HTTP Server\n```javascript\n// File: app.js\nconst http = require("http");\n\n// Create an HTTP server with a request listener callback (req, res)\nconst server = http.createServer((req, res) => {\n  console.log("Incoming Request Received:", req.url, req.method, req.headers);\n});\n\nconst PORT = 3001;\n\n// Bind server to PORT so the Event Loop continuously listens for client requests\nserver.listen(PORT, () => {\n  console.log(`Server running on address http://localhost:${PORT}`);\n});\n```	[{"url": "https://nodejs.org/api/http.html", "link": "https://nodejs.org/api/http.html", "title": "Node.js Official Docs — HTTP Core Module & http.createServer()"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nNihy9kZmIU", "title": "Lecture 3: First Node Server | NodeJS Complete Course in Hindi", "duration": "39m", "creator_name": "Complete Coding by Prashant Sir", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir"}]	[{"id": "node-lec-3-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Walk through the complete lifecycle of what happens when a user types a URL (like https://google.com) into a browser and presses Enter.", "title": "Q1: Walk through the complete lifecycle of what happens when a user types a URL (like https://google.com) into a browser and presses Enter.", "answer": "1) DNS Resolution: The browser checks its cache, then asks the DNS Resolver to resolve the domain name through the Root DNS Server -> TLD (.com) DNS Server -> Authoritative DNS Server to obtain the server's IP address. 2) TCP & TLS Handshake: The browser establishes a TCP 3-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) with the server IP on port 443 (and completes the TLS encryption handshake for HTTPS). 3) HTTP Request: The browser sends an HTTP GET request packet containing headers, path, and cookies. 4) Server Processing & HTTP Response: The Node.js server processes the request and sends back an HTTP status code, headers, and HTML/JSON payload, which the browser renders.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "1) DNS Resolution: The browser checks its cache, then asks the DNS Resolver to resolve the domain name through the Root DNS Server -> TLD (.com) DNS Server -> Authoritative DNS Server to obtain the server's IP address. 2) TCP & TLS Handshake: The browser establishes a TCP 3-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) with the server IP on port 443 (and completes the TLS encryption handshake for HTTPS). 3) HTTP Request: The browser sends an HTTP GET request packet containing headers, path, and cookies. 4) Server Processing & HTTP Response: The Node.js server processes the request and sends back an HTTP status code, headers, and HTML/JSON payload, which the browser renders."}, {"id": "node-lec-3-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: How does Node.js resolve a module path when you call require(\\"X\\"), and what is Module Caching?", "title": "Q2: How does Node.js resolve a module path when you call require(\\"X\\"), and what is Module Caching?", "answer": "When require(\\"X\\") is called: 1) If X does not start with \\"./\\" or \\"/\\", Node first checks if X is a built-in Core Module (like \\"http\\" or \\"fs\\"). 2) If not a core module, Node searches the local node_modules folder, walking up parent directories if needed. 3) If X starts with \\"./\\", \\"../\\", or \\"/\\", Node resolves it as a local file or directory path (automatically appending .js, .json, or /index.js). Once loaded the first time, Node caches the exported object in require.cache—subsequent require(\\"X\\") calls return the cached instance immediately in O(1) time.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "When require(\\"X\\") is called: 1) If X does not start with \\"./\\" or \\"/\\", Node first checks if X is a built-in Core Module (like \\"http\\" or \\"fs\\"). 2) If not a core module, Node searches the local node_modules folder, walking up parent directories if needed. 3) If X starts with \\"./\\", \\"../\\", or \\"/\\", Node resolves it as a local file or directory path (automatically appending .js, .json, or /index.js). Once loaded the first time, Node caches the exported object in require.cache—subsequent require(\\"X\\") calls return the cached instance immediately in O(1) time."}, {"id": "node-lec-3-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: Why did running node app.js exit immediately when we only called http.createServer(), and what does server.listen(PORT) actually do?", "title": "Q3: Why did running node app.js exit immediately when we only called http.createServer(), and what does server.listen(PORT) actually do?", "answer": "http.createServer(callback) only instantiates a Server object in memory—it does not bind to a network socket, so Node sees no active handles in the Event Loop and exits immediately. Calling server.listen(PORT) binds the process to a specific TCP port on the OS network interface and registers an active listener handle in the Event Loop so the server stays alive indefinitely waiting for incoming requests.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "http.createServer(callback) only instantiates a Server object in memory—it does not bind to a network socket, so Node sees no active handles in the Event Loop and exits immediately. Calling server.listen(PORT) binds the process to a specific TCP port on the OS network interface and registers an active listener handle in the Event Loop so the server stays alive indefinitely waiting for incoming requests."}, {"id": "node-lec-3-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What is a Port Number, what does the error Error: listen EADDRINUSE: address already in use :::3000 mean, and how do you fix it?", "title": "Q4: What is a Port Number, what does the error Error: listen EADDRINUSE: address already in use :::3000 mean, and how do you fix it?", "answer": "While an IP address identifies a specific machine on the network (like a building address), a Port Number (0–65535) identifies the specific application/process door on that machine (e.g., 80 for HTTP, 443 for HTTPS, 3000 for dev server). EADDRINUSE means another process (such as a previous Node server instance you forgot to stop) is already bound to port 3000. Fix it by stopping the existing terminal process with Ctrl + C (or killing the PID) or binding to another port like 3001.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "While an IP address identifies a specific machine on the network (like a building address), a Port Number (0–65535) identifies the specific application/process door on that machine (e.g., 80 for HTTP, 443 for HTTPS, 3000 for dev server). EADDRINUSE means another process (such as a previous Node server instance you forgot to stop) is already bound to port 3000. Fix it by stopping the existing terminal process with Ctrl + C (or killing the PID) or binding to another port like 3001."}, {"id": "node-lec-3-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is the difference between HTTP, HTTPS, and TCP?", "title": "Q5: What is the difference between HTTP, HTTPS, and TCP?", "answer": "TCP is a Transport Layer (Layer 4) protocol responsible for establishing a connection, splitting data into packets, guaranteeing packet delivery, and reassembling them in exact order. HTTP is an Application Layer (Layer 7) protocol built on top of TCP that defines the structure of web requests and responses (headers, methods, status codes) in plain text. HTTPS is HTTP layered over TLS/SSL encryption so the data payload cannot be eavesdropped or tampered with in transit.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "TCP is a Transport Layer (Layer 4) protocol responsible for establishing a connection, splitting data into packets, guaranteeing packet delivery, and reassembling them in exact order. HTTP is an Application Layer (Layer 7) protocol built on top of TCP that defines the structure of web requests and responses (headers, methods, status codes) in plain text. HTTPS is HTTP layered over TLS/SSL encryption so the data payload cannot be eavesdropped or tampered with in transit."}, {"id": "node-lec-3-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: What is 127.0.0.1 (localhost), and does a request to localhost go out to the internet or DNS server?", "title": "Q6: What is 127.0.0.1 (localhost), and does a request to localhost go out to the internet or DNS server?", "answer": "127.0.0.1 (mapped to the hostname localhost in the OS hosts file) is the standard IPv4 Loopback Address. Requests sent to localhost:3000 bypass external DNS servers and physical network cards entirely, looping back directly inside the local operating system kernel to reach your local Node.js server.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "127.0.0.1 (mapped to the hostname localhost in the OS hosts file) is the standard IPv4 Loopback Address. Requests sent to localhost:3000 bypass external DNS servers and physical network cards entirely, looping back directly inside the local operating system kernel to reach your local Node.js server."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nBefore writing a backend server, you must understand how a user typing `https://example.com` in a browser actually reaches your Node.js process running on a remote machine—and how Node's built-in **`http` Core Module** listens for those incoming connections.\n\n### 💡 How DNS (Domain Name System) & the Web Work\n1. **DNS Resolution (The Internet's Phonebook):** Computers communicate via numeric **IP Addresses** (e.g., `87.52.0.13`), not human-readable domain names. When a browser requests `example.com`, the **DNS Resolver** queries a 3-tier hierarchy:\n   * **Root DNS Server:** Directs the query to the appropriate Top-Level Domain server based on the extension (`.com`, `.in`, `.org`, `.ai`).\n   * **TLD (Top-Level Domain) Server:** Manages domains for that specific extension (e.g., Verisign for `.com`) and points to the domain's Authoritative Name Server.\n   * **Authoritative DNS Server:** Returns the exact **IP Address** registered for `example.com`.\n2. **TCP Handshake & HTTP/HTTPS Protocols:**\n   * **TCP (Transmission Control Protocol):** Establishes a reliable, ordered connection (3-way handshake) between the client IP and server IP and re-transmits any lost packets.\n   * **HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol):** Defines the request-response message format over TCP (transmits data in plain text; default port `80`).\n   * **HTTPS (HTTP Secure):** Encrypts HTTP payloads using **SSL/TLS** so attackers on the network cannot intercept passwords or tokens (default port `443`).\n\n### 💡 Node.js Core Modules & The `require()` Keyword\n* **Core Modules:** Built directly into the Node.js binary (`fs`, `http`, `https`, `path`, `os`, `crypto`, `url`). They require **zero `npm install`** and are high-performance.\n* **How `require()` Works:**\n  * Imports core modules (`require('http')`), third-party `node_modules` packages (`require('express')`), or local files (`require('./myModule')` — `.js` extension is optional).\n  * **Module Caching:** Modules are **cached after the first `require()` call**, so requiring the same module in 10 files does not reload or re-execute it 10 times!\n\n### 🛠️ Creating & Listening on Your First Node.js HTTP Server\n```javascript\n// File: app.js\nconst http = require("http");\n\n// Create an HTTP server with a request listener callback (req, res)\nconst server = http.createServer((req, res) => {\n  console.log("Incoming Request Received:", req.url, req.method, req.headers);\n});\n\nconst PORT = 3001;\n\n// Bind server to PORT so the Event Loop continuously listens for client requests\nserver.listen(PORT, () => {\n  console.log(`Server running on address http://localhost:${PORT}`);\n});\n```	Complete Coding by Prashant Sir	https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir	24	Lecture 3: First Node.js Server — How DNS & Web Protocols (HTTP/HTTPS/TCP) Work, Core Modules, require() & http.createServer()	Trace how DNS resolution (Root, TLD, Authoritative servers) and TCP/HTTP/HTTPS protocols route web traffic, import built-in Core Modules via require(), and launch your first Node.js HTTP server.	20	Easy	\N	3	[{"id": "node-lec-3-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Walk through the complete lifecycle of what happens when a user types a URL (like https://google.com) into a browser and presses Enter.", "title": "Q1: Walk through the complete lifecycle of what happens when a user types a URL (like https://google.com) into a browser and presses Enter.", "answer": "1) DNS Resolution: The browser checks its cache, then asks the DNS Resolver to resolve the domain name through the Root DNS Server -> TLD (.com) DNS Server -> Authoritative DNS Server to obtain the server's IP address. 2) TCP & TLS Handshake: The browser establishes a TCP 3-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) with the server IP on port 443 (and completes the TLS encryption handshake for HTTPS). 3) HTTP Request: The browser sends an HTTP GET request packet containing headers, path, and cookies. 4) Server Processing & HTTP Response: The Node.js server processes the request and sends back an HTTP status code, headers, and HTML/JSON payload, which the browser renders.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "1) DNS Resolution: The browser checks its cache, then asks the DNS Resolver to resolve the domain name through the Root DNS Server -> TLD (.com) DNS Server -> Authoritative DNS Server to obtain the server's IP address. 2) TCP & TLS Handshake: The browser establishes a TCP 3-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) with the server IP on port 443 (and completes the TLS encryption handshake for HTTPS). 3) HTTP Request: The browser sends an HTTP GET request packet containing headers, path, and cookies. 4) Server Processing & HTTP Response: The Node.js server processes the request and sends back an HTTP status code, headers, and HTML/JSON payload, which the browser renders."}, {"id": "node-lec-3-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: How does Node.js resolve a module path when you call require(\\"X\\"), and what is Module Caching?", "title": "Q2: How does Node.js resolve a module path when you call require(\\"X\\"), and what is Module Caching?", "answer": "When require(\\"X\\") is called: 1) If X does not start with \\"./\\" or \\"/\\", Node first checks if X is a built-in Core Module (like \\"http\\" or \\"fs\\"). 2) If not a core module, Node searches the local node_modules folder, walking up parent directories if needed. 3) If X starts with \\"./\\", \\"../\\", or \\"/\\", Node resolves it as a local file or directory path (automatically appending .js, .json, or /index.js). Once loaded the first time, Node caches the exported object in require.cache—subsequent require(\\"X\\") calls return the cached instance immediately in O(1) time.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "When require(\\"X\\") is called: 1) If X does not start with \\"./\\" or \\"/\\", Node first checks if X is a built-in Core Module (like \\"http\\" or \\"fs\\"). 2) If not a core module, Node searches the local node_modules folder, walking up parent directories if needed. 3) If X starts with \\"./\\", \\"../\\", or \\"/\\", Node resolves it as a local file or directory path (automatically appending .js, .json, or /index.js). Once loaded the first time, Node caches the exported object in require.cache—subsequent require(\\"X\\") calls return the cached instance immediately in O(1) time."}, {"id": "node-lec-3-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: Why did running node app.js exit immediately when we only called http.createServer(), and what does server.listen(PORT) actually do?", "title": "Q3: Why did running node app.js exit immediately when we only called http.createServer(), and what does server.listen(PORT) actually do?", "answer": "http.createServer(callback) only instantiates a Server object in memory—it does not bind to a network socket, so Node sees no active handles in the Event Loop and exits immediately. Calling server.listen(PORT) binds the process to a specific TCP port on the OS network interface and registers an active listener handle in the Event Loop so the server stays alive indefinitely waiting for incoming requests.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "http.createServer(callback) only instantiates a Server object in memory—it does not bind to a network socket, so Node sees no active handles in the Event Loop and exits immediately. Calling server.listen(PORT) binds the process to a specific TCP port on the OS network interface and registers an active listener handle in the Event Loop so the server stays alive indefinitely waiting for incoming requests."}, {"id": "node-lec-3-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What is a Port Number, what does the error Error: listen EADDRINUSE: address already in use :::3000 mean, and how do you fix it?", "title": "Q4: What is a Port Number, what does the error Error: listen EADDRINUSE: address already in use :::3000 mean, and how do you fix it?", "answer": "While an IP address identifies a specific machine on the network (like a building address), a Port Number (0–65535) identifies the specific application/process door on that machine (e.g., 80 for HTTP, 443 for HTTPS, 3000 for dev server). EADDRINUSE means another process (such as a previous Node server instance you forgot to stop) is already bound to port 3000. Fix it by stopping the existing terminal process with Ctrl + C (or killing the PID) or binding to another port like 3001.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "While an IP address identifies a specific machine on the network (like a building address), a Port Number (0–65535) identifies the specific application/process door on that machine (e.g., 80 for HTTP, 443 for HTTPS, 3000 for dev server). EADDRINUSE means another process (such as a previous Node server instance you forgot to stop) is already bound to port 3000. Fix it by stopping the existing terminal process with Ctrl + C (or killing the PID) or binding to another port like 3001."}, {"id": "node-lec-3-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is the difference between HTTP, HTTPS, and TCP?", "title": "Q5: What is the difference between HTTP, HTTPS, and TCP?", "answer": "TCP is a Transport Layer (Layer 4) protocol responsible for establishing a connection, splitting data into packets, guaranteeing packet delivery, and reassembling them in exact order. HTTP is an Application Layer (Layer 7) protocol built on top of TCP that defines the structure of web requests and responses (headers, methods, status codes) in plain text. HTTPS is HTTP layered over TLS/SSL encryption so the data payload cannot be eavesdropped or tampered with in transit.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "TCP is a Transport Layer (Layer 4) protocol responsible for establishing a connection, splitting data into packets, guaranteeing packet delivery, and reassembling them in exact order. HTTP is an Application Layer (Layer 7) protocol built on top of TCP that defines the structure of web requests and responses (headers, methods, status codes) in plain text. HTTPS is HTTP layered over TLS/SSL encryption so the data payload cannot be eavesdropped or tampered with in transit."}, {"id": "node-lec-3-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: What is 127.0.0.1 (localhost), and does a request to localhost go out to the internet or DNS server?", "title": "Q6: What is 127.0.0.1 (localhost), and does a request to localhost go out to the internet or DNS server?", "answer": "127.0.0.1 (mapped to the hostname localhost in the OS hosts file) is the standard IPv4 Loopback Address. Requests sent to localhost:3000 bypass external DNS servers and physical network cards entirely, looping back directly inside the local operating system kernel to reach your local Node.js server.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "3-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "127.0.0.1 (mapped to the hostname localhost in the OS hosts file) is the standard IPv4 Loopback Address. Requests sent to localhost:3000 bypass external DNS servers and physical network cards entirely, looping back directly inside the local operating system kernel to reach your local Node.js server."}]	https://nodejs.org/api/http.html	\N	\N	\N
22144	Lecture 4: Request & Response Lifecycle — Inspecting req (url, method, headers), Sending HTML via res, URL Routing & 302 Redirects	node-lecture-4-request-response-routing-headers-redirects	Inspect incoming HTTP requests (req.url, req.method, req.headers), construct HTML responses using res.setHeader, res.write, and res.end, implement multi-page routing, and trigger 302 redirects.	2026-10-06 21:56:18.722653+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nEvery time a client hits a Node.js server, the `http.createServer((req, res) => { ... })` callback receives two critical objects:\n1. **`req` (`http.IncomingMessage`):** Contains everything sent by the client.\n2. **`res` (`http.ServerResponse`):** Used by the server to configure headers, status codes, and send data back to the client.\n\n### 💡 Inspecting the Request Object (`req`) & Exiting the Event Loop\n* **`req.url`:** The path requested by the client after the domain/port (e.g., `"/"`, `"/products"`, `"/submit-details"`).\n* **`req.method`:** The HTTP verb used (`"GET"` when navigating to a URL, `"POST"` when submitting a form).\n* **`req.headers`:** Key-value metadata about the client (`host`, `user-agent`, `accept-language`, `cookie`).\n* **`process.exit()`:** Immediately terminates the Node.js Event Loop and shuts down the server process (rarely used in live servers, which are meant to run 24/7).\n\n### 💡 Sending Responses (`res`), Routing & `302` Redirects\n* **`res.setHeader("Content-Type", "text/html")`:** Informs the browser that the response payload is HTML.\n* **`res.write("...")` & `res.end()`:** `res.write()` streams chunks of response data to the client, and **`res.end()`** signals that the response is complete.\n* **Crucial Rule:** Calling `res.write()` or `res.setHeader()` **after** `res.end()` has already been executed throws a fatal runtime error (`ERR_HTTP_HEADERS_SENT` / `write after end`)! Always `return res.end();` inside conditional route blocks so execution stops immediately.\n* **Redirecting the Client (`302 Found`):** To redirect a user after a `POST` form submission back to `"/"`, set **`res.statusCode = 302`** and **`res.setHeader("Location", "/")`** before calling **`res.end()`**.\n\n### 🛠️ Multi-Route Node.js Server with Form Input & 302 Redirect\n```javascript\nconst http = require("http");\nconst fs = require("fs");\n\nconst server = http.createServer((req, res) => {\n  if (req.url === "/") {\n    res.setHeader("Content-Type", "text/html");\n    res.write("<html><head><title>Complete Coding</title></head><body>");\n    res.write("<h1>Enter Your Details:</h1>");\n    res.write('<form action="/submit-details" method="POST">');\n    res.write('<input type="text" name="username" placeholder="Enter your name"><br>');\n    res.write('<label for="male">Male</label><input type="radio" id="male" name="gender" value="male">');\n    res.write('<label for="female">Female</label><input type="radio" id="female" name="gender" value="female"><br>');\n    res.write('<input type="submit" value="Submit">');\n    res.write("</form></body></html>");\n    return res.end(); // Mandatory 'return' so code below does not execute!\n  } else if (req.url.toLowerCase() === "/submit-details" && req.method === "POST") {\n    fs.writeFileSync("user.txt", "Prashant Jain");\n    res.statusCode = 302;               // 302 = Temporary Redirect\n    res.setHeader("Location", "/");     // Redirect browser back to Home '/'\n    return res.end();\n  }\n\n  // Fallback response for any other route\n  res.setHeader("Content-Type", "text/html");\n  res.write("<html><body><h1>Like / Share / Subscribe</h1></body></html>");\n  res.end();\n});\n\nserver.listen(3001);\n```	[{"url": "https://nodejs.org/en/learn/modules/anatomy-of-an-http-transaction", "link": "https://nodejs.org/en/learn/modules/anatomy-of-an-http-transaction", "title": "Node.js Official Docs — Anatomy of an HTTP Transaction"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9HYAaXwS7I4", "title": "Lecture 4: Request & Response | NodeJS Complete Course in Hindi", "duration": "63m", "creator_name": "Complete Coding by Prashant Sir", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir"}]	[{"id": "node-lec-4-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Why do we get the error \\"Cannot set headers after they are sent to the client\\" (ERR_HTTP_HEADERS_SENT) in Node.js, and how do we prevent it?", "title": "Q1: Why do we get the error \\"Cannot set headers after they are sent to the client\\" (ERR_HTTP_HEADERS_SENT) in Node.js, and how do we prevent it?", "answer": "Once res.end() (or res.send() in Express) is called, the HTTP headers and response body are flushed over the TCP socket and the response is closed. However, calling res.end() does NOT automatically exit the JavaScript function! If an if (req.url === \\"/\\") block calls res.end() without a return statement (or without an else block), execution continues to the lines below and tries to call res.setHeader() or res.write() a second time on the same closed response. Always write return res.end(); to exit the callback immediately.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Once res.end() (or res.send() in Express) is called, the HTTP headers and response body are flushed over the TCP socket and the response is closed. However, calling res.end() does NOT automatically exit the JavaScript function! If an if (req.url === \\"/\\") block calls res.end() without a return statement (or without an else block), execution continues to the lines below and tries to call res.setHeader() or res.write() a second time on the same closed response. Always write return res.end(); to exit the callback immediately."}, {"id": "node-lec-4-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: How do you perform an HTTP Redirect in a raw Node.js http server, and what is the difference between Status Code 301 and 302?", "title": "Q2: How do you perform an HTTP Redirect in a raw Node.js http server, and what is the difference between Status Code 301 and 302?", "answer": "To redirect in raw Node.js, set res.statusCode = 302, set the target URL via res.setHeader(\\"Location\\", \\"/new-path\\"), and call res.end(). Status 302 (Found / Temporary Redirect) tells the browser that the redirect is temporary (used after POST form submissions in the Post/Redirect/Get pattern). Status 301 (Moved Permanently) tells browsers and search engines to cache the redirect permanently and update bookmarks/SEO links.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "To redirect in raw Node.js, set res.statusCode = 302, set the target URL via res.setHeader(\\"Location\\", \\"/new-path\\"), and call res.end(). Status 302 (Found / Temporary Redirect) tells the browser that the redirect is temporary (used after POST form submissions in the Post/Redirect/Get pattern). Status 301 (Moved Permanently) tells browsers and search engines to cache the redirect permanently and update bookmarks/SEO links."}, {"id": "node-lec-4-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is the difference between GET and POST requests when submitting an HTML <form>?", "title": "Q3: What is the difference between GET and POST requests when submitting an HTML <form>?", "answer": "1) GET appends form fields directly into the URL query string (?username=rohit&gender=male), making them visible in browser history/bookmarks and limited by URL length; GET should only be used for idempotent data retrieval/search. 2) POST sends form fields inside the HTTP Request Body stream (not in the URL), supports large payloads and file uploads, and is used when creating or mutating state on the server.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) GET appends form fields directly into the URL query string (?username=rohit&gender=male), making them visible in browser history/bookmarks and limited by URL length; GET should only be used for idempotent data retrieval/search. 2) POST sends form fields inside the HTTP Request Body stream (not in the URL), supports large payloads and file uploads, and is used when creating or mutating state on the server."}, {"id": "node-lec-4-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why does a browser send TWO requests to your Node.js server when you open http://localhost:3001/ in a tab?", "title": "Q4: Why does a browser send TWO requests to your Node.js server when you open http://localhost:3001/ in a tab?", "answer": "The first request is GET / for the webpage HTML itself. Immediately after, the browser automatically sends a second background request GET /favicon.ico to fetch the small icon displayed on the browser tab!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "The first request is GET / for the webpage HTML itself. Immediately after, the browser automatically sends a second background request GET /favicon.ico to fetch the small icon displayed on the browser tab!"}, {"id": "node-lec-4-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is the difference between res.write() and res.end()?", "title": "Q5: What is the difference between res.write() and res.end()?", "answer": "res.write(chunk) sends a chunk of the response body to the client stream and can be called multiple times to stream HTML or data progressively. res.end([data]) MUST be called exactly once on every response: it optionally sends a final chunk of data and signals to the client that the response transmission is complete.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "res.write(chunk) sends a chunk of the response body to the client stream and can be called multiple times to stream HTML or data progressively. res.end([data]) MUST be called exactly once on every response: it optionally sends a final chunk of data and signals to the client that the response transmission is complete."}, {"i