ody stream (not in the URL), supports large payloads and file uploads, and is used when creating or mutating state on the server.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) GET appends form fields directly into the URL query string (?username=rohit&gender=male), making them visible in browser history/bookmarks and limited by URL length; GET should only be used for idempotent data retrieval/search. 2) POST sends form fields inside the HTTP Request Body stream (not in the URL), supports large payloads and file uploads, and is used when creating or mutating state on the server."}, {"id": "node-lec-4-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why does a browser send TWO requests to your Node.js server when you open http://localhost:3001/ in a tab?", "title": "Q4: Why does a browser send TWO requests to your Node.js server when you open http://localhost:3001/ in a tab?", "answer": "The first request is GET / for the webpage HTML itself. Immediately after, the browser automatically sends a second background request GET /favicon.ico to fetch the small icon displayed on the browser tab!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "4-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "The first request is GET / for the webpage HTML itself. Immediately after, the browser automatically sends a second background request GET /favicon.ico to fetch the small icon displayed on the browser tab!"}, {"id": "node-lec-4-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is the difference between res.write() and res.end()?", "title": "Q5: What is the difference between res.write() and res.end()?", "answer": "res.write(chunk) sends a chunk of the response body to the client stream and can be called multiple times to stream HTML or data progressively. res.end([data]) MUST be called exactly once on every response: it optionally sends a final chunk of data and signals to the client that the response transmission is complete.", "plat