761	Capacity-Bounded Partition Search: Least Capacity to Ship Packages Within D Days	dsa-capacity-to-ship-packages-within-d-days	Find the minimum conveyor ship weight capacity to ship all packages within D days in O(N * log(sum - max)) time using greedy day simulation.	2026-10-02 23:53:37.1184+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Capacity-Bounded Partition Search: Least Capacity to Ship Packages Within D Days\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MG-Ac4TAvTY", "title": "Lecture 59: Capacity to Ship Packages within D Days", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/split-array-largest-sum/", "title": "Split Array Largest Sum", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Apply the exact [maxElement, sumOfArray] binary search pattern to minimize the largest subarray sum."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-limit-of-balls-in-a-bag/", "title": "Minimum Limit of Balls in a Bag", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use binary search on the penalty answer space to minimize the maximum bag size."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/the-painters-partition-problem1535/1", "title": "The Painter's Partition Problem-II", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Minimize the maximum time to paint all boards using binary search on capacity."}]	Module 13: Binary Search on Answer Space & Monotonic Predicate Functions	### 📌 Topic Overview\nA conveyor belt has packages with weights `weights[i]` that must be shipped **in order** within `D` days. Find the **least weight capacity** of the ship so that all packages can be shipped within `D` days.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Defining the Search Range (`[max(weights), sum(weights)]`):**\n  - **Minimum Possible Capacity (`low = max(weights)`):** The ship's capacity cannot be less than the heaviest single package, or that package could never be loaded onto the ship!\n  - **Maximum Needed Capacity (`high = sum(weights)`):** If the ship's capacity equals the sum of all weights, every package can be shipped in just `1` day.\n- **Simulating Days Required for Capacity `cap` (`findDays(weights, cap)`):**\n  - Start on `days = 1` with current `load = 0`.\n  - For each package `weights[i]`:\n    - If `load + weights[i] > cap`: It doesn't fit today—move to the next day (`days++`) and start the new day's load with `load = weights[i]`.\n    - Otherwise: Load it onto today's ship (`load += weights[i]`).\n- **Binary Search (O(N * log2(sum - max + 1)) Time, O(1) Space):**\n  - If `findDays(weights, mid) <= D`: Capacity `mid` works! Search left (`high = mid - 1`) for a smaller capacity.\n  - Otherwise: Capacity `mid` is too small (takes too many days), so search right (`low = mid + 1`).\n\n### 💻 Capacity to Ship Packages Blueprint\n```cpp\nint findDays(vector<int> &weights, int cap) {\n    int days = 1, load = 0;\n    for (int w : weights) {\n        if (load + w > cap) {\n            days += 1;\n            load = w; // Load current package on the next day\n        } else {\n            load += w;\n        }\n    }\n    return days;\n}\n\nint leastWeightCapacity(vector<int> &weights, int d) {\n    int low = *max_element(weights.begin(), weights.end());\n    int high = accumulate(weights.begin(), weights.end(), 0);\n\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        int numberOfDays = findDays(weights, mid);\n        if (numberOfDays <= d) {\n            high = mid - 1; // Possible; try smaller capacity\n        } else {\n            low = mid + 1;  // Too many days; increase capacity\n        }\n    }\n    return low;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
760	Ceiling-Sum Monotonicity: Finding the Smallest Divisor Given a Threshold	dsa-smallest-divisor-given-threshold	Find the smallest positive divisor such that the sum of ceiling divisions is <= threshold in O(N * log(max)) time using answer space search.	2026-10-02 23:53:37.114543+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Ceiling-Sum Monotonicity: Finding the Smallest Divisor Given a Threshold\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UvBKTVaG6U8", "title": "Lecture 58: Find the Smallest Divisor Given a Threshold", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-candies-allocated-to-k-children/", "title": "Maximum Candies Allocated to K Children", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Binary search for the largest divisor pile size such that at least k sub-piles can be formed."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sum-of-mutated-array-closest-to-target/", "title": "Sum of Mutated Array Closest to Target", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use binary search on the answer space to find the cap value that brings the array sum closest to target."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/smallest-divisor/1", "title": "Smallest Divisor", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the smallest positive integer divisor such that the sum of ceiling divisions is <= K."}]	Module 13: Binary Search on Answer Space & Monotonic Predicate Functions	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array of integers `nums` and an integer `threshold`, find the **smallest positive integer divisor `d`** such that if you divide every element in the array by `d` (rounding up via `ceil`) and sum the results, the total sum is `<= threshold`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Defining the Search Space (`[1, max(nums)]`):**\n  - The smallest positive divisor is `1`.\n  - Dividing by `max(nums)` (or anything larger) makes every element's ceiling division equal to `1`, producing the minimum possible sum of `N` (the array's size).\n  - *Impossible Edge Case:* If `nums.size() > threshold`, even dividing by `max(nums)` gives a sum of `N > threshold`, so return `-1`!\n- **Monotonicity of Divisor `d`:**\n  - Smaller divisor `d` → Larger division sum (might exceed `threshold`).\n  - Larger divisor `d` → Smaller division sum (fits under `threshold`).\n- **Binary Search (O(N * log2(maxElement)) Time, O(1) Space):**\n  - At `mid`, compute `sumByD(nums, mid)`.\n  - If `sum <= threshold`: `mid` is valid! Since we want the **smallest** divisor, search left (`high = mid - 1`).\n  - Otherwise (`sum > threshold`): `mid` is too small (sum is too big), so search right (`low = mid + 1`) to use a larger divisor.\n\n### 💻 Smallest Divisor Blueprint\n```cpp\nint sumByD(vector<int> &arr, int div) {\n    int sum = 0;\n    for (int x : arr) {\n        sum += ceil((double)x / (double)div);\n    }\n    return sum;\n}\n\nint smallestDivisor(vector<int> &arr, int threshold) {\n    if (arr.size() > threshold) return -1;\n    int low = 1, high = *max_element(arr.begin(), arr.end());\n\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (sumByD(arr, mid) <= threshold) {\n            high = mid - 1; // Valid divisor; look for a smaller one on the left\n        } else {\n            low = mid + 1;  // Sum exceeded threshold; need a larger divisor\n        }\n    }\n    return low;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22146	Lecture 6: Event Loop & Asynchronous Code — Event-Driven Programming, V8 vs. libuv Thread Pool, Event Loop Phases & writeFileSync vs. writeFile	node-lecture-6-event-loop-phases-libuv-worker-threads-async-code	Master the 6 phases of the Node.js Event Loop (Timers, Pending Callbacks, Poll, Check, Close), process.nextTick() priority, libuv Worker Threads, and why fs.writeFileSync must be replaced with async fs.writeFile.	2026-10-06 21:56:18.722653+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThis lecture dives deep into **Event-Driven Programming** in Node.js: how the single-threaded **Event Loop** coordinates with **`libuv`'s Worker Thread Pool**, the exact phase order of the Node.js Event Loop, and why blocking synchronous methods like `fs.writeFileSync()` are dangerous in web servers.\n\n### 💡 The 6 Phases of the Node.js Event Loop & Microtask Priority\nUnlike the simplified browser Event Loop diagram, the **Node.js `libuv` Event Loop** cycles through distinct phases in order:\n1. **Timers Phase:** Executes callbacks scheduled by **`setTimeout()`** and **`setInterval()`** whose threshold time has expired.\n2. **Pending Callbacks (I/O Callbacks) Phase:** Executes I/O callbacks deferred to the next loop iteration (such as certain TCP socket errors).\n3. **Idle, Prepare Phase:** Used internally by Node/`libuv`.\n4. **Poll Phase:** Retrieves new I/O events and executes I/O-related callbacks (such as **`fs.readFile` / `fs.writeFile`**, incoming HTTP/network data).\n5. **Check Phase:** Executes callbacks scheduled specifically via **`setImmediate()`**.\n6. **Close Callbacks Phase:** Executes close event callbacks (e.g., `socket.on("close", ...)`).\n* **Microtask Priority Between Phases:** Before moving from one phase to the next, Node drains the **`process.nextTick()` queue** (highest priority!) and the **Promise Microtask queue** (`Promise.resolve().then()`).\n\n### 💡 Blocking (`fs.writeFileSync`) vs. Non-Blocking (`fs.writeFile`)\n* **`fs.writeFileSync("user.txt", data)` (Blocking — BAD in Request Handlers):** Forces the single main Event Loop thread to wait for the OS disk write to finish before doing anything else—freezing the server for all other users!\n* **`fs.writeFile("user.txt", data, err => { ... })` (Non-Blocking — GOOD):** Offloads the file write to a **`libuv` Worker Thread** and only sends the **`302` redirect inside the callback** once the file write completes.\n\n### 🛠️ Proper Asynchronous File Write + Redirect Inside `req.on("end")`\n```javascript\n// Correct Non-Blocking Pattern: Send 302 Redirect ONLY inside fs.writeFile callback!\nreq.on("end", () => {\n  const fullBody = Buffer.concat(body).toString();\n  const params = new URLSearchParams(fullBody);\n  const bodyObject = Object.fromEntries(params);\n\n  // Offloads disk write to libuv Worker Thread Pool without blocking Event Loop:\n  fs.writeFile("user.txt", JSON.stringify(bodyObject), (err) => {\n    console.log("Data written successfully!");\n    res.statusCode = 302;\n    res.setHeader("Location", "/");\n    return res.end();\n  });\n});\n```	[{"url": "https://nodejs.org/en/learn/asynchronous-work/event-loop-timers-and-nexttick", "link": "https://nodejs.org/en/learn/asynchronous-work/event-loop-timers-and-nexttick", "title": "Node.js Official Docs — The Node.js Event Loop, Timers, and process.nextTick()"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pk9J0SvNXUs", "title": "Lecture 6: Event Loop and Async Code | NodeJS Complete Course in Hindi", "duration": "50m", "creator_name": "Complete Coding by Prashant Sir", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir"}]	[{"id": "node-lec-6-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Why was it a bug in Lecture 5 to place res.statusCode = 302 and res.end() OUTSIDE the req.on(\\"end\\") and fs.writeFile() callbacks?", "title": "Q1: Why was it a bug in Lecture 5 to place res.statusCode = 302 and res.end() OUTSIDE the req.on(\\"end\\") and fs.writeFile() callbacks?", "answer": "Because req.on(\\"end\\") and fs.writeFile() are asynchronous! If res.end() is placed outside req.on(\\"end\\"), the server immediately sends the 302 redirect response back to the client BEFORE the request chunks have even finished arriving or been written to disk! Furthermore, if downstream code depends on the newly written file (or database record) on the redirected page, the redirected GET / request will arrive before fs.writeFile has finished writing. Placing res.end() inside the fs.writeFile callback guarantees the response is sent only after the write succeeds.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Because req.on(\\"end\\") and fs.writeFile() are asynchronous! If res.end() is placed outside req.on(\\"end\\"), the server immediately sends the 302 redirect response back to the client BEFORE the request chunks have even finished arriving or been written to disk! Furthermore, if downstream code depends on the newly written file (or database record) on the redirected page, the redirected GET / request will arrive before fs.writeFile has finished writing. Placing res.end() inside the fs.writeFile callback guarantees the response is sent only after the write succeeds."}, {"id": "node-lec-6-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Explain the difference between fs.writeFileSync() and fs.writeFile(). When (if ever) is it acceptable to use Sync methods in Node.js?", "title": "Q2: Explain the difference between fs.writeFileSync() and fs.writeFile(). When (if ever) is it acceptable to use Sync methods in Node.js?", "answer": "fs.writeFileSync() blocks the single main Event Loop thread until the disk operation finishes, preventing the server from accepting or processing any other user requests during that time. fs.writeFile() is non-blocking: it delegates the disk write to libuv's worker thread pool and invokes a callback when done. Sync methods (readFileSync) are ONLY acceptable once at initial server startup (e.g., loading config/SSL certificates before server.listen()), NEVER inside HTTP request handlers!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "fs.writeFileSync() blocks the single main Event Loop thread until the disk operation finishes, preventing the server from accepting or processing any other user requests during that time. fs.writeFile() is non-blocking: it delegates the disk write to libuv's worker thread pool and invokes a callback when done. Sync methods (readFileSync) are ONLY acceptable once at initial server startup (e.g., loading config/SSL certificates before server.listen()), NEVER inside HTTP request handlers!"}, {"id": "node-lec-6-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is the difference between process.nextTick(), Promise.resolve().then(), setTimeout(fn, 0), and setImmediate()?", "title": "Q3: What is the difference between process.nextTick(), Promise.resolve().then(), setTimeout(fn, 0), and setImmediate()?", "answer": "1) process.nextTick(fn) has the highest priority of all asynchronous callbacks in Node.js—it runs immediately after the current synchronous operation completes, even before Promises and before the Event Loop continues! 2) Promise.resolve().then(fn) runs in the Microtask queue right after process.nextTick. 3) setTimeout(fn, 0) runs in the Timers phase (Phase 1) of the Event Loop. 4) setImmediate(fn) runs in the Check phase (Phase 5) immediately after the Poll I/O phase.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q3", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "1) process.nextTick(fn) has the highest priority of all asynchronous callbacks in Node.js—it runs immediately after the current synchronous operation completes, even before Promises and before the Event Loop continues! 2) Promise.resolve().then(fn) runs in the Microtask queue right after process.nextTick. 3) setTimeout(fn, 0) runs in the Timers phase (Phase 1) of the Event Loop. 4) setImmediate(fn) runs in the Check phase (Phase 5) immediately after the Poll I/O phase."}, {"id": "node-lec-6-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Inside an I/O callback (like fs.readFile), which executes first: setTimeout(fn, 0) or setImmediate(fn)?", "title": "Q4: Inside an I/O callback (like fs.readFile), which executes first: setTimeout(fn, 0) or setImmediate(fn)?", "answer": "Inside an fs.readFile callback, setImmediate(fn) is 100% guaranteed to execute BEFORE setTimeout(fn, 0)! Why? Because fs.readFile callbacks execute during the Poll Phase (Phase 4) of the Event Loop. The very next phase after Poll is the Check Phase (Phase 5), which runs setImmediate() callbacks—whereas setTimeout() must wait for the loop to wrap all the way back around to the Timers Phase (Phase 1).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q4", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Inside an fs.readFile callback, setImmediate(fn) is 100% guaranteed to execute BEFORE setTimeout(fn, 0)! Why? Because fs.readFile callbacks execute during the Poll Phase (Phase 4) of the Event Loop. The very next phase after Poll is the Check Phase (Phase 5), which runs setImmediate() callbacks—whereas setTimeout() must wait for the loop to wrap all the way back around to the Timers Phase (Phase 1)."}, {"id": "node-lec-6-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: How many worker threads does libuv's Thread Pool have by default, what tasks use it, and can it be increased?", "title": "Q5: How many worker threads does libuv's Thread Pool have by default, what tasks use it, and can it be increased?", "answer": "By default, libuv's Thread Pool has 4 worker threads. It is used for operations that do not have native non-blocking OS kernel APIs across all platforms: specifically File System (fs.*) operations, DNS lookups (dns.lookup), crypto (crypto.pbkdf2, randomBytes), and zlib compression. You can increase the pool size (up to 1024) by setting the environment variable process.env.UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE = 8.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "By default, libuv's Thread Pool has 4 worker threads. It is used for operations that do not have native non-blocking OS kernel APIs across all platforms: specifically File System (fs.*) operations, DNS lookups (dns.lookup), crypto (crypto.pbkdf2, randomBytes), and zlib compression. You can increase the pool size (up to 1024) by setting the environment variable process.env.UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE = 8."}, {"id": "node-lec-6-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Trace the exact console output order of: console.log(\\"1\\"); setTimeout(() => console.log(\\"2\\"), 0); setImmediate(() => console.log(\\"3\\")); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\\"4\\")); process.nextTick(() => console.log(\\"5\\")); console.log(\\"6\\");", "title": "Q6: Trace the exact console output order of: console.log(\\"1\\"); setTimeout(() => console.log(\\"2\\"), 0); setImmediate(() => console.log(\\"3\\")); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\\"4\\")); process.nextTick(() => console.log(\\"5\\")); console.log(\\"6\\");", "answer": "First, synchronous code runs on the Call Stack: \\"1\\", then \\"6\\". Next, Node drains process.nextTick: \\"5\\". Next, Node drains Promise microtasks: \\"4\\". Finally, the Event Loop enters Timers and Check phases: \\"2\\" and \\"3\\" (in top-level main module, \\"2\\" usually runs before \\"3\\"). Output: 1 -> 6 -> 5 -> 4 -> 2 -> 3.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q6", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "First, synchronous code runs on the Call Stack: \\"1\\", then \\"6\\". Next, Node drains process.nextTick: \\"5\\". Next, Node drains Promise microtasks: \\"4\\". Finally, the Event Loop enters Timers and Check phases: \\"2\\" and \\"3\\" (in top-level main module, \\"2\\" usually runs before \\"3\\"). Output: 1 -> 6 -> 5 -> 4 -> 2 -> 3."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThis lecture dives deep into **Event-Driven Programming** in Node.js: how the single-threaded **Event Loop** coordinates with **`libuv`'s Worker Thread Pool**, the exact phase order of the Node.js Event Loop, and why blocking synchronous methods like `fs.writeFileSync()` are dangerous in web servers.\n\n### 💡 The 6 Phases of the Node.js Event Loop & Microtask Priority\nUnlike the simplified browser Event Loop diagram, the **Node.js `libuv` Event Loop** cycles through distinct phases in order:\n1. **Timers Phase:** Executes callbacks scheduled by **`setTimeout()`** and **`setInterval()`** whose threshold time has expired.\n2. **Pending Callbacks (I/O Callbacks) Phase:** Executes I/O callbacks deferred to the next loop iteration (such as certain TCP socket errors).\n3. **Idle, Prepare Phase:** Used internally by Node/`libuv`.\n4. **Poll Phase:** Retrieves new I/O events and executes I/O-related callbacks (such as **`fs.readFile` / `fs.writeFile`**, incoming HTTP/network data).\n5. **Check Phase:** Executes callbacks scheduled specifically via **`setImmediate()`**.\n6. **Close Callbacks Phase:** Executes close event callbacks (e.g., `socket.on("close", ...)`).\n* **Microtask Priority Between Phases:** Before moving from one phase to the next, Node drains the **`process.nextTick()` queue** (highest priority!) and the **Promise Microtask queue** (`Promise.resolve().then()`).\n\n### 💡 Blocking (`fs.writeFileSync`) vs. Non-Blocking (`fs.writeFile`)\n* **`fs.writeFileSync("user.txt", data)` (Blocking — BAD in Request Handlers):** Forces the single main Event Loop thread to wait for the OS disk write to finish before doing anything else—freezing the server for all other users!\n* **`fs.writeFile("user.txt", data, err => { ... })` (Non-Blocking — GOOD):** Offloads the file write to a **`libuv` Worker Thread** and only sends the **`302` redirect inside the callback** once the file write completes.\n\n### 🛠️ Proper Asynchronous File Write + Redirect Inside `req.on("end")`\n```javascript\n// Correct Non-Blocking Pattern: Send 302 Redirect ONLY inside fs.writeFile callback!\nreq.on("end", () => {\n  const fullBody = Buffer.concat(body).toString();\n  const params = new URLSearchParams(fullBody);\n  const bodyObject = Object.fromEntries(params);\n\n  // Offloads disk write to libuv Worker Thread Pool without blocking Event Loop:\n  fs.writeFile("user.txt", JSON.stringify(bodyObject), (err) => {\n    console.log("Data written successfully!");\n    res.statusCode = 302;\n    res.setHeader("Location", "/");\n    return res.end();\n  });\n});\n```	Complete Coding by Prashant Sir	https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir	24	Lecture 6: Event Loop & Asynchronous Code — Event-Driven Programming, V8 vs. libuv Thread Pool, Event Loop Phases & writeFileSync vs. writeFile	Master the 6 phases of the Node.js Event Loop (Timers, Pending Callbacks, Poll, Check, Close), process.nextTick() priority, libuv Worker Threads, and why fs.writeFileSync must be replaced with async fs.writeFile.	20	Easy	\N	6	[{"id": "node-lec-6-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Why was it a bug in Lecture 5 to place res.statusCode = 302 and res.end() OUTSIDE the req.on(\\"end\\") and fs.writeFile() callbacks?", "title": "Q1: Why was it a bug in Lecture 5 to place res.statusCode = 302 and res.end() OUTSIDE the req.on(\\"end\\") and fs.writeFile() callbacks?", "answer": "Because req.on(\\"end\\") and fs.writeFile() are asynchronous! If res.end() is placed outside req.on(\\"end\\"), the server immediately sends the 302 redirect response back to the client BEFORE the request chunks have even finished arriving or been written to disk! Furthermore, if downstream code depends on the newly written file (or database record) on the redirected page, the redirected GET / request will arrive before fs.writeFile has finished writing. Placing res.end() inside the fs.writeFile callback guarantees the response is sent only after the write succeeds.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Because req.on(\\"end\\") and fs.writeFile() are asynchronous! If res.end() is placed outside req.on(\\"end\\"), the server immediately sends the 302 redirect response back to the client BEFORE the request chunks have even finished arriving or been written to disk! Furthermore, if downstream code depends on the newly written file (or database record) on the redirected page, the redirected GET / request will arrive before fs.writeFile has finished writing. Placing res.end() inside the fs.writeFile callback guarantees the response is sent only after the write succeeds."}, {"id": "node-lec-6-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Explain the difference between fs.writeFileSync() and fs.writeFile(). When (if ever) is it acceptable to use Sync methods in Node.js?", "title": "Q2: Explain the difference between fs.writeFileSync() and fs.writeFile(). When (if ever) is it acceptable to use Sync methods in Node.js?", "answer": "fs.writeFileSync() blocks the single main Event Loop thread until the disk operation finishes, preventing the server from accepting or processing any other user requests during that time. fs.writeFile() is non-blocking: it delegates the disk write to libuv's worker thread pool and invokes a callback when done. Sync methods (readFileSync) are ONLY acceptable once at initial server startup (e.g., loading config/SSL certificates before server.listen()), NEVER inside HTTP request handlers!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "fs.writeFileSync() blocks the single main Event Loop thread until the disk operation finishes, preventing the server from accepting or processing any other user requests during that time. fs.writeFile() is non-blocking: it delegates the disk write to libuv's worker thread pool and invokes a callback when done. Sync methods (readFileSync) are ONLY acceptable once at initial server startup (e.g., loading config/SSL certificates before server.listen()), NEVER inside HTTP request handlers!"}, {"id": "node-lec-6-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is the difference between process.nextTick(), Promise.resolve().then(), setTimeout(fn, 0), and setImmediate()?", "title": "Q3: What is the difference between process.nextTick(), Promise.resolve().then(), setTimeout(fn, 0), and setImmediate()?", "answer": "1) process.nextTick(fn) has the highest priority of all asynchronous callbacks in Node.js—it runs immediately after the current synchronous operation completes, even before Promises and before the Event Loop continues! 2) Promise.resolve().then(fn) runs in the Microtask queue right after process.nextTick. 3) setTimeout(fn, 0) runs in the Timers phase (Phase 1) of the Event Loop. 4) setImmediate(fn) runs in the Check phase (Phase 5) immediately after the Poll I/O phase.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q3", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "1) process.nextTick(fn) has the highest priority of all asynchronous callbacks in Node.js—it runs immediately after the current synchronous operation completes, even before Promises and before the Event Loop continues! 2) Promise.resolve().then(fn) runs in the Microtask queue right after process.nextTick. 3) setTimeout(fn, 0) runs in the Timers phase (Phase 1) of the Event Loop. 4) setImmediate(fn) runs in the Check phase (Phase 5) immediately after the Poll I/O phase."}, {"id": "node-lec-6-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Inside an I/O callback (like fs.readFile), which executes first: setTimeout(fn, 0) or setImmediate(fn)?", "title": "Q4: Inside an I/O callback (like fs.readFile), which executes first: setTimeout(fn, 0) or setImmediate(fn)?", "answer": "Inside an fs.readFile callback, setImmediate(fn) is 100% guaranteed to execute BEFORE setTimeout(fn, 0)! Why? Because fs.readFile callbacks execute during the Poll Phase (Phase 4) of the Event Loop. The very next phase after Poll is the Check Phase (Phase 5), which runs setImmediate() callbacks—whereas setTimeout() must wait for the loop to wrap all the way back around to the Timers Phase (Phase 1).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q4", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Inside an fs.readFile callback, setImmediate(fn) is 100% guaranteed to execute BEFORE setTimeout(fn, 0)! Why? Because fs.readFile callbacks execute during the Poll Phase (Phase 4) of the Event Loop. The very next phase after Poll is the Check Phase (Phase 5), which runs setImmediate() callbacks—whereas setTimeout() must wait for the loop to wrap all the way back around to the Timers Phase (Phase 1)."}, {"id": "node-lec-6-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: How many worker threads does libuv's Thread Pool have by default, what tasks use it, and can it be increased?", "title": "Q5: How many worker threads does libuv's Thread Pool have by default, what tasks use it, and can it be increased?", "answer": "By default, libuv's Thread Pool has 4 worker threads. It is used for operations that do not have native non-blocking OS kernel APIs across all platforms: specifically File System (fs.*) operations, DNS lookups (dns.lookup), crypto (crypto.pbkdf2, randomBytes), and zlib compression. You can increase the pool size (up to 1024) by setting the environment variable process.env.UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE = 8.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "By default, libuv's Thread Pool has 4 worker threads. It is used for operations that do not have native non-blocking OS kernel APIs across all platforms: specifically File System (fs.*) operations, DNS lookups (dns.lookup), crypto (crypto.pbkdf2, randomBytes), and zlib compression. You can increase the pool size (up to 1024) by setting the environment variable process.env.UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE = 8."}, {"id": "node-lec-6-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Trace the exact console output order of: console.log(\\"1\\"); setTimeout(() => console.log(\\"2\\"), 0); setImmediate(() => console.log(\\"3\\")); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\\"4\\")); process.nextTick(() => console.log(\\"5\\")); console.log(\\"6\\");", "title": "Q6: Trace the exact console output order of: console.log(\\"1\\"); setTimeout(() => console.log(\\"2\\"), 0); setImmediate(() => console.log(\\"3\\")); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\\"4\\")); process.nextTick(() => console.log(\\"5\\")); console.log(\\"6\\");", "answer": "First, synchronous code runs on the Call Stack: \\"1\\", then \\"6\\". Next, Node drains process.nextTick: \\"5\\". Next, Node drains Promise microtasks: \\"4\\". Finally, the Event Loop enters Timers and Check phases: \\"2\\" and \\"3\\" (in top-level main module, \\"2\\" usually runs before \\"3\\"). Output: 1 -> 6 -> 5 -> 4 -> 2 -> 3.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "6-q6", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "First, synchronous code runs on the Call Stack: \\"1\\", then \\"6\\". Next, Node drains process.nextTick: \\"5\\". Next, Node drains Promise microtasks: \\"4\\". Finally, the Event Loop enters Timers and Check phases: \\"2\\" and \\"3\\" (in top-level main module, \\"2\\" usually runs before \\"3\\"). Output: 1 -> 6 -> 5 -> 4 -> 2 -> 3."}]	https://nodejs.org/en/learn/asynchronous-work/event-loop-timers-and-nexttick	\N	\N	\N
10021	Tortoise & Hare Algorithm: Find the Middle Element of a Linked List	dsa-find-middle-element-of-linked-list-tortoise-hare	Find the middle node of a linked list in a single pass using slow (+1) and fast (+2) pointers with safe termination conditions.	2026-10-03 15:04:07.054065+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Tortoise & Hare Algorithm: Find the Middle Element of a Linked List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7LjQ57RqgEc", "title": "Lecture 249: Find Middle Element of Linked List | Tortoise and Hare", "duration": "15m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/middle-of-the-linked-list/", "title": "Middle of the Linked List (LC 876)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Given the head of a singly linked list, return the middle node of the linked list (or the second middle node if there are two)."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-the-middle-node-of-a-linked-list/", "title": "Delete the Middle Node of a Linked List (LC 2095)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use Tortoise and Hare pointers to delete the middle node of a linked list in one pass."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/finding-middle-element-in-a-linked-list/1", "title": "Middle of a Linked List", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the data of the middle element of a singly linked list."}]	Module 37: Linked List Medium Problems: Dummy Nodes, In-Place Re-Linking & Tortoise-Hare Pointers	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the `head` of a singly linked list of length `N`, return the **middle node** (if `N` is even, so there are two middle nodes `m1` and `m2`, return the **second middle node `m2`** at 1-based position `(N / 2) + 1`).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Optimal)\n- **1. Brute Force Two-Pass (`O(N + N/2)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Pass 1: Count length `N`. Pass 2: Walk `(N / 2)` steps from `head` to reach node `(N / 2) + 1`.\n- **2. Optimal Tortoise & Hare Algorithm (`O(N / 2)` Single Pass, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Initialize `slow = head` (Tortoise, moves `1` step) and `fast = head` (Hare, moves `2` steps).\n  - Because `fast` moves at **twice the speed (`2x`)** of `slow` (`x`), when `fast` covers the full distance `D`, `slow` has covered **exactly `D / 2`**!\n  - **Stopping Condition (`while (fast != nullptr && fast->next != nullptr)`):**\n    - For **Odd length `N`**, `fast` stops on the **last node** (`fast->next == nullptr`).\n    - For **Even length `N`**, `fast` jumps past the tail and stops at **`nullptr`** (`fast == nullptr`), placing `slow` on the **second middle node `m2`**!\n\n### 💻 Find Middle of Linked List (Tortoise & Hare) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nNode* middleNode(Node* head) {\n    Node* slow = head;\n    Node* fast = head;\n\n    while (fast != nullptr && fast->next != nullptr) {\n        slow = slow->next;       // Move 1 step\n        fast = fast->next->next; // Move 2 steps\n    }\n    return slow;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Writing `while (fast->next != nullptr && fast != nullptr)` in the Wrong Order:** Always check **`fast != nullptr` FIRST** before dereferencing `fast->next != nullptr`! On an even-length list, `fast` becomes `nullptr`, so checking `fast->next` first causes a segmentation fault!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22185	Problems on Trains	aptitude-quant-problems-on-trains	Master relative speed concepts, crossing stationary vs. moving objects, platform length math, and same vs. opposite direction train overtakes.	2026-10-07 11:35:53.411147+05:30	### 📌 1. Fundamentals of Train Problems\n* **Distance is Additive:** Whenever a train crosses an object with length (like a platform, bridge, or another train), the total distance traveled is the **Sum of their lengths**.\n* **Zero Length Objects:** For a stationary or moving man, pole, or car (unless specified), the length of the object is taken as **0**. Total distance = Length of the train.\n* **Unit Conversions:** Always ensure units are consistent.\n  * `km/hr` to `m/s` $\\rightarrow$ Multiply by **`5/18`**\n  * `m/s` to `km/hr` $\\rightarrow$ Multiply by **`18/5`**\n\n### 💡 2. Relative Speed Concepts\n* **Opposite Directions (Attract):** Speeds are added $\\rightarrow$ **`Relative Speed = S1 + S2`**\n* **Same Direction (Overtake):** Speeds are subtracted $\\rightarrow$ **`Relative Speed = S1 - S2`**\n* Time taken for two trains of lengths `L1` and `L2` to cross each other:\n  * Opposite Directions: **`T = (L1 + L2) / (S1 + S2)`**\n  * Same Direction: **`T = (L1 + L2) / (S1 - S2)`**\n\n### 💡 3. High-Yield Shortcuts\n* If two trains start at the same time from points A and B towards each other, and after crossing they take `T1` and `T2` hours to reach B and A respectively, the ratio of their speeds is:\n  **`S1 / S2 = √(T2 / T1)`**\n* When a train passes two objects of different lengths `L1` and `L2` in times `T1` and `T2`, equate the speed:\n  **`(Length of Train + L1) / T1 = (Length of Train + L2) / T2`**	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=78b4Jn4rw44", "type": "youtube", "title": "Problems on Trains - Shortcuts & Tricks for Placement Tests, Job Interviews & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-trains-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Train Length via Platform Crossing] A train passes a stationary pole in 8 seconds and a 200 m long bridge in 28 seconds. What is the length of the train?", "options": ["A) 60 meters", "B) 80 meters", "C) 100 meters", "D) 120 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A train moving at a constant speed passes a stationary telegraph pole in exactly 8 seconds, and it completely crosses a 200-meter long bridge in 28 seconds. What is the exact length of the train?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the length of the train be L. Speed of train = L/8. When crossing the bridge, Distance = L + 200, and Speed = (L + 200)/28. Equating speeds: L/8 = (L + 200)/28 => 28L = 8L + 1600 => 20L = 1600 => L = 80 meters.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-trains-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Crossing Trains Opposite Direction] Two trains 126 m and 119 m long are moving towards each other at 12 m/s and 23 m/s. How much time will they take to cross each other?", "options": ["A) 5 seconds", "B) 7 seconds", "C) 9 seconds", "D) 11 seconds"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains, 126 meters and 119 meters long respectively, are moving towards each other on parallel tracks at speeds of 12 m/s and 23 m/s. How much time will they take to completely cross each other?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total distance to be covered = Sum of lengths = 126 + 119 = 245 m. Since they are moving in opposite directions, relative speed = 12 + 23 = 35 m/s. Time taken = Distance / Relative Speed = 245 / 35 = 7 seconds.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-trains-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Finding Train Length from Two Moving Men] A train overtakes two boys walking in the same direction at 8 km/hr and 16 km/hr in 36 and 40 seconds respectively. Find the length of the train.", "options": ["A) 400 meters", "B) 600 meters", "C) 800 meters", "D) 1,000 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A train running at a uniform speed overtakes two boys walking in the same direction along the railway track. The first boy is walking at 8 km/hr and the second at 16 km/hr. The train passes them completely in 36 seconds and 40 seconds respectively. What is the length of the train?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let train speed be S km/hr. Relative speeds are (S - 8) and (S - 16). Distance (Length of train) is constant. So, (S - 8) × (36/3600) = (S - 16) × (40/3600). Simplify: 36(S - 8) = 40(S - 16) => 36S - 288 = 40S - 640 => 4S = 352 => S = 88 km/hr. Length of train = Relative Speed × Time = (88 - 8) × (5/18) × 36 = 80 × 10 = 800 meters.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-trains-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Equal Length Trains Crossing] Two trains P and Q of equal length cross a stationary pole in 5s and 6s. How long will they take to cross each other moving in the same direction?", "options": ["A) 30 seconds", "B) 45 seconds", "C) 60 seconds", "D) 90 seconds"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains P and Q of identical lengths cross a stationary pole in 5 seconds and 6 seconds respectively. If they are moving in the same direction on parallel tracks, how much time will the faster train take to completely overtake the slower train?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the length of each train be L. Speed of P = L/5, Speed of Q = L/6. Relative speed in the same direction = L/5 - L/6 = L/30. Total distance to overtake = L + L = 2L. Time taken = Total Distance / Relative Speed = 2L / (L/30) = 60 seconds.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-trains-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Difference in Distances Met] Two trains start from P and Q towards each other at 120 km/hr and 100 km/hr. When they meet, one train covered 40 km more. Distance between P and Q?", "options": ["A) 400 km", "B) 440 km", "C) 480 km", "D) 520 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "From stations P and Q, two trains start simultaneously moving towards each other at speeds of 120 km/hr and 100 km/hr respectively. When they meet, it is found that one train has covered exactly 40 km more than the other train. What is the total distance between P and Q?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Difference in speed = 120 - 100 = 20 km/hr. Since they start simultaneously, their travel time until meeting is the same. The extra 40 km was covered due to the 20 km/hr speed difference. Time taken = 40 km / 20 km/hr = 2 hours. Total distance = Sum of speeds × Time = (120 + 100) × 2 = 220 × 2 = 440 km.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-trains-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Trains Starting at Different Times] Two trains A and B leave Kolkata for Sikkim at 8:00 PM and 8:30 PM at 90 km/hr and 120 km/hr. At what distance from Kolkata will they meet?", "options": ["A) 150 km", "B) 160 km", "C) 180 km", "D) 210 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains A and B leave Kolkata for Sikkim at 8:00 PM and 8:30 PM respectively. They run at speeds of 90 km/hr and 120 km/hr. At what exact distance from Kolkata will the two trains meet?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Train A travels for 30 minutes (0.5 hr) before B starts, covering 90 × 0.5 = 45 km. Relative speed of B to catch A = 120 - 90 = 30 km/hr. Time taken for B to catch A = 45 / 30 = 1.5 hours. Distance from Kolkata = Speed of B × Time of B = 120 × 1.5 = 180 km.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-trains-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Stoppage Minutes per Hour] With stoppages, a train travels at 36 km/hr, and without stoppages at 40 km/hr. For how many minutes does it stop per hour?", "options": ["A) 5 minutes", "B) 6 minutes", "C) 8 minutes", "D) 10 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "With stoppages, the average speed of a train is 36 km/hr. Without stoppages, its average speed is 40 km/hr. For exactly how many minutes does the train stop per hour?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Difference in distance per hour = 40 - 36 = 4 km. This 4 km is lost due to stopping. At the ideal speed of 40 km/hr, the time taken to cover 4 km is (4 / 40) hours = 1/10 hours. Convert to minutes: (1/10) × 60 = 6 minutes. The train stops for 6 minutes per hour.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-trains-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Train Passing Two Bridges] A train passes two bridges of length 800m and 400m in 100 seconds and 60 seconds. Length of the train?", "options": ["A) 150 meters", "B) 200 meters", "C) 250 meters", "D) 300 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A train running at a uniform speed passes two bridges of lengths 800 m and 400 m in exactly 100 seconds and 60 seconds respectively. What is the length of the train?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let train length be L. Speeds are equal: (L + 800) / 100 = (L + 400) / 60. Simplify denominator by dividing by 20: (L + 800) / 5 = (L + 400) / 3. Cross multiply: 3L + 2400 = 5L + 2000 => 2L = 400 => L = 200 meters.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-trains-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Speed of Train Crossing Running Man] A train 125m long passes a man running at 5 km/hr in the same direction in 10 seconds. What is the speed of the train?", "options": ["A) 45 km/hr", "B) 50 km/hr", "C) 54 km/hr", "D) 60 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A train 125 meters long completely passes a man running at 5 km/hr in the same direction in exactly 10 seconds. What is the speed of the train in km/hr?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Relative speed = Distance / Time = 125 m / 10 s = 12.5 m/s. Convert relative speed to km/hr: 12.5 × (18/5) = 45 km/hr. Since the man is running in the same direction, Relative Speed = Speed of Train - Speed of Man => 45 = S - 5 => S = 50 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-trains-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Multiple Crossings and Platform Length] A train at 48 km/hr crosses an opposite train (half its length) at 42 km/hr in 12s. It passes a platform in 45s. Find platform length.", "options": ["A) 300 meters", "B) 400 meters", "C) 500 meters", "D) 600 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A train traveling at 48 km/hr crosses another train, having half its length and traveling in the opposite direction at 42 km/hr, in exactly 12 seconds. The first train also completely passes a railway platform in 45 seconds. What is the length of the platform?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Relative speed = 48 + 42 = 90 km/hr = 90 × 5/18 = 25 m/s. 2) Distance covered = Length of Train 1 (L) + Length of Train 2 (L/2) = 1.5L. So, 1.5L = 25 × 12 = 300 m => L = 200 m. 3) Speed of first train = 48 km/hr = 48 × 5/18 = 40/3 m/s. 4) Distance covered passing platform in 45s = (40/3) × 45 = 600 m. Platform Length = Total Distance - Train Length = 600 - 200 = 400 meters.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-trains-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Catching Up with a Jogger] A jogger is 240m ahead of a 120m long train running at 45 km/hr. Jogger speed is 9 km/hr. Time to cross jogger?", "options": ["A) 24 seconds", "B) 30 seconds", "C) 36 seconds", "D) 40 seconds"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A jogger is running at 9 km/hr alongside a railway track. He is currently 240 meters ahead of the engine of a 120-meter long train running in the same direction at 45 km/hr. In how many seconds will the train completely pass the jogger?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Relative speed = 45 - 9 = 36 km/hr = 36 × 5/18 = 10 m/s. Total distance the train must cover relative to the jogger = initial gap (240 m) + train length (120 m) = 360 m. Time = Distance / Relative Speed = 360 / 10 = 36 seconds.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-trains-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Meeting Time with Different Starts] Stations A and B are 110 km apart. Train 1 starts from A at 7 AM at 20 km/hr. Train 2 from B at 8 AM at 25 km/hr. Meeting time?", "options": ["A) 9:30 AM", "B) 10:00 AM", "C) 10:30 AM", "D) 11:00 AM"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two stations A and B are 110 km apart on a straight line. One train starts from A at 7:00 AM and travels towards B at 20 km/hr. Another train starts from B at 8:00 AM and travels towards A at a speed of 25 km/hr. At what exact time will they meet?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "By 8:00 AM, the first train has traveled 1 hour × 20 km/hr = 20 km. Remaining distance between them = 110 - 20 = 90 km. At 8:00 AM, both trains are moving towards each other. Relative speed = 20 + 25 = 45 km/hr. Time to cover 90 km = 90 / 45 = 2 hours. Meeting time = 8:00 AM + 2 hours = 10:00 AM.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-trains-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Crossing Poles and Each Other] Two trains of equal lengths running in opposite directions pass a pole in 18s and 12s. Time to cross each other?", "options": ["A) 14.4 seconds", "B) 15.0 seconds", "C) 15.5 seconds", "D) 16.2 seconds"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains of perfectly equal lengths are running on parallel tracks in opposite directions. They completely pass a stationary pole in 18 seconds and 12 seconds respectively. In how many seconds will they cross each other completely?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let the length of each train be L. Speed of Train 1 = L/18. Speed of Train 2 = L/12. Since they move in opposite directions, Relative Speed = L/18 + L/12 = (2L + 3L)/36 = 5L/36. Total distance to cross each other = L + L = 2L. Time taken = 2L / (5L/36) = 72 / 5 = 14.4 seconds.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-trains-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Train Speed from Two Opposite Men] A train overtakes two persons walking in opposite direction at 5 m/s and 10 m/s in 6s and 5s. Speed of train?", "options": ["A) 15 m/s", "B) 18 m/s", "C) 20 m/s", "D) 25 m/s"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A train completely passes two persons walking in the opposite direction to the train at speeds of 5 m/s and 10 m/s in exactly 6 seconds and 5 seconds respectively. What is the speed of the train in m/s?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let train speed be S and train length be L. Since they walk in opposite direction, relative speeds are S+5 and S+10. L = (S + 5) × 6, and L = (S + 10) × 5. Equating lengths: 6S + 30 = 5S + 50 => S = 20 m/s.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-trains-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Cross-Timing Square Root Shortcut] Two trains cross each other. They take 4h and 9h to reach destination. Ratio of speeds?", "options": ["A) 2 : 3", "B) 3 : 2", "C) 4 : 9", "D) 9 : 4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains start simultaneously from stations A and B towards each other. After crossing each other on the way, they take exactly 4 hours and 9 hours to reach stations B and A respectively. What is the ratio of their speeds?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "If two bodies start simultaneously from opposite ends, cross, and then take times T1 and T2 to finish their journeys, the ratio of their speeds is S1/S2 = √(T2 / T1). So, S1/S2 = √(9 / 4) = 3 / 2. Ratio is 3 : 2.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-trains-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Intersection Time from Departure and Arrival] Train X from A 4 PM, to B 5 PM. Train Y from B 4 PM, to A 5:30 PM. Time they cross?", "options": ["A) 4:30 PM", "B) 4:36 PM", "C) 4:40 PM", "D) 4:45 PM"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Train X starts from station A at 4:00 PM and reaches station B at 5:00 PM. Train Y starts from station B at 4:00 PM and reaches station A at 5:30 PM. At what exact time will the two trains cross each other?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Time taken by X = 1 hour. Time taken by Y = 1.5 hours (3/2 hours). Let distance AB = 3 km. Speed X = 3 / 1 = 3 km/hr. Speed Y = 3 / 1.5 = 2 km/hr. Relative speed = 3 + 2 = 5 km/hr. Time to meet = Distance / Relative Speed = 3 / 5 hours = (3/5) × 60 = 36 minutes. They meet at 4:00 PM + 36 mins = 4:36 PM.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-trains-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Passenger and Goods Train Ratio] Goods train and passenger train run same direction. Passenger overtakes completely in 60s. Passenger in train sees crossing goods in 40s. Ratio of lengths?", "options": ["A) 1 : 2", "B) 2 : 1", "C) 2 : 3", "D) 3 : 2"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A goods train and a passenger train run on parallel tracks in the same direction. The driver of the goods train observes that the passenger train coming from behind overtakes his train completely in 60 seconds. A passenger inside the passenger train notices that he crosses the goods train completely in 40 seconds. What is the ratio of the length of the goods train to that of the passenger train?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let lengths be L_p and L_g. Let relative speed be V. 1) The passenger train completely overtaking the goods train means total distance covered relative to each other is (L_p + L_g). So, L_p + L_g = 60 × V. 2) A passenger sitting inside the passenger train acts as a single point moving at V relative to the goods train. The distance to cross the goods train is just L_g. So, L_g = 40 × V. 3) Substituting L_g: L_p + 40V = 60V => L_p = 20V. 4) Ratio of Goods to Passenger lengths = L_g : L_p = 40V : 20V = 2 : 1.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-trains-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Finding Speed using Multiple Landmarks] A train passes a telegraph post in 8s and a 264m bridge in 20s. Speed of the train in km/hr?", "options": ["A) 72.0 km/hr", "B) 75.5 km/hr", "C) 79.2 km/hr", "D) 82.5 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A train moving at a constant speed completely passes a telegraph post in exactly 8 seconds, and a 264-meter long bridge in 20 seconds. What is the speed of the train in km/hr?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let train length be L. Speed S = L/8. For the bridge, S = (L + 264)/20. Equating: L/8 = (L + 264)/20 => 20L = 8L + 2112 => 12L = 2112 => L = 176 m. Speed S = 176 / 8 = 22 m/s. Convert to km/hr: 22 × (18/5) = 396 / 5 = 79.2 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-trains-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Time to Cross Opposite Trains] Two trains 140m and 160m long run at 60 km/hr and 40 km/hr in opposite directions. Time to cross?", "options": ["A) 9.6 seconds", "B) 10.8 seconds", "C) 11.2 seconds", "D) 12.0 seconds"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains, 140 meters and 160 meters long, run at speeds of 60 km/hr and 40 km/hr respectively in opposite directions on parallel tracks. In how many seconds will they completely cross each other?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total distance = 140 + 160 = 300 meters. Relative speed = 60 + 40 = 100 km/hr. Convert to m/s: 100 × 5/18 = 500/18 = 250/9 m/s. Time = Distance / Speed = 300 / (250/9) = 2700 / 250 = 10.8 seconds.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-trains-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Passing Moving Person in Train] Two trains travel same direction at 50 km/hr and 30 km/hr. Faster completely passes a man in slower in 18s. Length of faster train?", "options": ["A) 80 meters", "B) 100 meters", "C) 120 meters", "D) 150 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains are traveling in the exact same direction at speeds of 50 km/hr and 30 km/hr respectively. The faster train completely passes a man sitting in the slower train in exactly 18 seconds. What is the length of the faster train?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The man sitting in the slower train behaves as a point object moving at 30 km/hr. The relative speed of the faster train to the man is 50 - 30 = 20 km/hr. Convert to m/s: 20 × (5/18) = 100/18 = 50/9 m/s. The distance covered to pass the man is exactly the length of the faster train. Length = Speed × Time = (50/9) × 18 = 100 meters.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. Fundamentals of Train Problems\n* **Distance is Additive:** Whenever a train crosses an object with length (like a platform, bridge, or another train), the total distance traveled is the **Sum of their lengths**.\n* **Zero Length Objects:** For a stationary or moving man, pole, or car (unless specified), the length of the object is taken as **0**. Total distance = Length of the train.\n* **Unit Conversions:** Always ensure units are consistent.\n  * `km/hr` to `m/s` $\\rightarrow$ Multiply by **`5/18`**\n  * `m/s` to `km/hr` $\\rightarrow$ Multiply by **`18/5`**\n\n### 💡 2. Relative Speed Concepts\n* **Opposite Directions (Attract):** Speeds are added $\\rightarrow$ **`Relative Speed = S1 + S2`**\n* **Same Direction (Overtake):** Speeds are subtracted $\\rightarrow$ **`Relative Speed = S1 - S2`**\n* Time taken for two trains of lengths `L1` and `L2` to cross each other:\n  * Opposite Directions: **`T = (L1 + L2) / (S1 + S2)`**\n  * Same Direction: **`T = (L1 + L2) / (S1 - S2)`**\n\n### 💡 3. High-Yield Shortcuts\n* If two trains start at the same time from points A and B towards each other, and after crossing they take `T1` and `T2` hours to reach B and A respectively, the ratio of their speeds is:\n  **`S1 / S2 = √(T2 / T1)`**\n* When a train passes two objects of different lengths `L1` and `L2` in times `T1` and `T2`, equate the speed:\n  **`(Length of Train + L1) / T1 = (Length of Train + L2) / T2`**	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Problems on Trains	Master relative speed concepts, crossing stationary vs. moving objects, platform length math, and same vs. opposite direction train overtakes.	44	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	19	[{"id": "apt-trains-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Train Length via Platform Crossing] A train passes a stationary pole in 8 seconds and a 200 m long bridge in 28 seconds. What is the length of the train?", "options": ["A) 60 meters", "B) 80 meters", "C) 100 meters", "D) 120 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A train moving at a constant speed passes a stationary telegraph pole in exactly 8 seconds, and it completely crosses a 200-meter long bridge in 28 seconds. What is the exact length of the train?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the length of the train be L. Speed of train = L/8. When crossing the bridge, Distance = L + 200, and Speed = (L + 200)/28. Equating speeds: L/8 = (L + 200)/28 => 28L = 8L + 1600 => 20L = 1600 => L = 80 meters.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-trains-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Crossing Trains Opposite Direction] Two trains 126 m and 119 m long are moving towards each other at 12 m/s and 23 m/s. How much time will they take to cross each other?", "options": ["A) 5 seconds", "B) 7 seconds", "C) 9 seconds", "D) 11 seconds"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains, 126 meters and 119 meters long respectively, are moving towards each other on parallel tracks at speeds of 12 m/s and 23 m/s. How much time will they take to completely cross each other?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total distance to be covered = Sum of lengths = 126 + 119 = 245 m. Since they are moving in opposite directions, relative speed = 12 + 23 = 35 m/s. Time taken = Distance / Relative Speed = 245 / 35 = 7 seconds.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-trains-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Finding Train Length from Two Moving Men] A train overtakes two boys walking in the same direction at 8 km/hr and 16 km/hr in 36 and 40 seconds respectively. Find the length of the train.", "options": ["A) 400 meters", "B) 600 meters", "C) 800 meters", "D) 1,000 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A train running at a uniform speed overtakes two boys walking in the same direction along the railway track. The first boy is walking at 8 km/hr and the second at 16 km/hr. The train passes them completely in 36 seconds and 40 seconds respectively. What is the length of the train?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let train speed be S km/hr. Relative speeds are (S - 8) and (S - 16). Distance (Length of train) is constant. So, (S - 8) × (36/3600) = (S - 16) × (40/3600). Simplify: 36(S - 8) = 40(S - 16) => 36S - 288 = 40S - 640 => 4S = 352 => S = 88 km/hr. Length of train = Relative Speed × Time = (88 - 8) × (5/18) × 36 = 80 × 10 = 800 meters.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-trains-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Equal Length Trains Crossing] Two trains P and Q of equal length cross a stationary pole in 5s and 6s. How long will they take to cross each other moving in the same direction?", "options": ["A) 30 seconds", "B) 45 seconds", "C) 60 seconds", "D) 90 seconds"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains P and Q of identical lengths cross a stationary pole in 5 seconds and 6 seconds respectively. If they are moving in the same direction on parallel tracks, how much time will the faster train take to completely overtake the slower train?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the length of each train be L. Speed of P = L/5, Speed of Q = L/6. Relative speed in the same direction = L/5 - L/6 = L/30. Total distance to overtake = L + L = 2L. Time taken = Total Distance / Relative Speed = 2L / (L/30) = 60 seconds.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-trains-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Difference in Distances Met] Two trains start from P and Q towards each other at 120 km/hr and 100 km/hr. When they meet, one train covered 40 km more. Distance between P and Q?", "options": ["A) 400 km", "B) 440 km", "C) 480 km", "D) 520 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "From stations P and Q, two trains start simultaneously moving towards each other at speeds of 120 km/hr and 100 km/hr respectively. When they meet, it is found that one train has covered exactly 40 km more than the other train. What is the total distance between P and Q?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Difference in speed = 120 - 100 = 20 km/hr. Since they start simultaneously, their travel time until meeting is the same. The extra 40 km was covered due to the 20 km/hr speed difference. Time taken = 40 km / 20 km/hr = 2 hours. Total distance = Sum of speeds × Time = (120 + 100) × 2 = 220 × 2 = 440 km.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-trains-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Trains Starting at Different Times] Two trains A and B leave Kolkata for Sikkim at 8:00 PM and 8:30 PM at 90 km/hr and 120 km/hr. At what distance from Kolkata will they meet?", "options": ["A) 150 km", "B) 160 km", "C) 180 km", "D) 210 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains A and B leave Kolkata for Sikkim at 8:00 PM and 8:30 PM respectively. They run at speeds of 90 km/hr and 120 km/hr. At what exact distance from Kolkata will the two trains meet?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Train A travels for 30 minutes (0.5 hr) before B starts, covering 90 × 0.5 = 45 km. Relative speed of B to catch A = 120 - 90 = 30 km/hr. Time taken for B to catch A = 45 / 30 = 1.5 hours. Distance from Kolkata = Speed of B × Time of B = 120 × 1.5 = 180 km.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-trains-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Stoppage Minutes per Hour] With stoppages, a train travels at 36 km/hr, and without stoppages at 40 km/hr. For how many minutes does it stop per hour?", "options": ["A) 5 minutes", "B) 6 minutes", "C) 8 minutes", "D) 10 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "With stoppages, the average speed of a train is 36 km/hr. Without stoppages, its average speed is 40 km/hr. For exactly how many minutes does the train stop per hour?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Difference in distance per hour = 40 - 36 = 4 km. This 4 km is lost due to stopping. At the ideal speed of 40 km/hr, the time taken to cover 4 km is (4 / 40) hours = 1/10 hours. Convert to minutes: (1/10) × 60 = 6 minutes. The train stops for 6 minutes per hour.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-trains-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Train Passing Two Bridges] A train passes two bridges of length 800m and 400m in 100 seconds and 60 seconds. Length of the train?", "options": ["A) 150 meters", "B) 200 meters", "C) 250 meters", "D) 300 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A train running at a uniform speed passes two bridges of lengths 800 m and 400 m in exactly 100 seconds and 60 seconds respectively. What is the length of the train?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let train length be L. Speeds are equal: (L + 800) / 100 = (L + 400) / 60. Simplify denominator by dividing by 20: (L + 800) / 5 = (L + 400) / 3. Cross multiply: 3L + 2400 = 5L + 2000 => 2L = 400 => L = 200 meters.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-trains-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Speed of Train Crossing Running Man] A train 125m long passes a man running at 5 km/hr in the same direction in 10 seconds. What is the speed of the train?", "options": ["A) 45 km/hr", "B) 50 km/hr", "C) 54 km/hr", "D) 60 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A train 125 meters long completely passes a man running at 5 km/hr in the same direction in exactly 10 seconds. What is the speed of the train in km/hr?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Relative speed = Distance / Time = 125 m / 10 s = 12.5 m/s. Convert relative speed to km/hr: 12.5 × (18/5) = 45 km/hr. Since the man is running in the same direction, Relative Speed = Speed of Train - Speed of Man => 45 = S - 5 => S = 50 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-trains-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Multiple Crossings and Platform Length] A train at 48 km/hr crosses an opposite train (half its length) at 42 km/hr in 12s. It passes a platform in 45s. Find platform length.", "options": ["A) 300 meters", "B) 400 meters", "C) 500 meters", "D) 600 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A train traveling at 48 km/hr crosses another train, having half its length and traveling in the opposite direction at 42 km/hr, in exactly 12 seconds. The first train also completely passes a railway platform in 45 seconds. What is the length of the platform?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Relative speed = 48 + 42 = 90 km/hr = 90 × 5/18 = 25 m/s. 2) Distance covered = Length of Train 1 (L) + Length of Train 2 (L/2) = 1.5L. So, 1.5L = 25 × 12 = 300 m => L = 200 m. 3) Speed of first train = 48 km/hr = 48 × 5/18 = 40/3 m/s. 4) Distance covered passing platform in 45s = (40/3) × 45 = 600 m. Platform Length = Total Distance - Train Length = 600 - 200 = 400 meters.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-trains-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Catching Up with a Jogger] A jogger is 240m ahead of a 120m long train running at 45 km/hr. Jogger speed is 9 km/hr. Time to cross jogger?", "options": ["A) 24 seconds", "B) 30 seconds", "C) 36 seconds", "D) 40 seconds"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A jogger is running at 9 km/hr alongside a railway track. He is currently 240 meters ahead of the engine of a 120-meter long train running in the same direction at 45 km/hr. In how many seconds will the train completely pass the jogger?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Relative speed = 45 - 9 = 36 km/hr = 36 × 5/18 = 10 m/s. Total distance the train must cover relative to the jogger = initial gap (240 m) + train length (120 m) = 360 m. Time = Distance / Relative Speed = 360 / 10 = 36 seconds.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-trains-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Meeting Time with Different Starts] Stations A and B are 110 km apart. Train 1 starts from A at 7 AM at 20 km/hr. Train 2 from B at 8 AM at 25 km/hr. Meeting time?", "options": ["A) 9:30 AM", "B) 10:00 AM", "C) 10:30 AM", "D) 11:00 AM"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two stations A and B are 110 km apart on a straight line. One train starts from A at 7:00 AM and travels towards B at 20 km/hr. Another train starts from B at 8:00 AM and travels towards A at a speed of 25 km/hr. At what exact time will they meet?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "By 8:00 AM, the first train has traveled 1 hour × 20 km/hr = 20 km. Remaining distance between them = 110 - 20 = 90 km. At 8:00 AM, both trains are moving towards each other. Relative speed = 20 + 25 = 45 km/hr. Time to cover 90 km = 90 / 45 = 2 hours. Meeting time = 8:00 AM + 2 hours = 10:00 AM.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-trains-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Crossing Poles and Each Other] Two trains of equal lengths running in opposite directions pass a pole in 18s and 12s. Time to cross each other?", "options": ["A) 14.4 seconds", "B) 15.0 seconds", "C) 15.5 seconds", "D) 16.2 seconds"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains of perfectly equal lengths are running on parallel tracks in opposite directions. They completely pass a stationary pole in 18 seconds and 12 seconds respectively. In how many seconds will they cross each other completely?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let the length of each train be L. Speed of Train 1 = L/18. Speed of Train 2 = L/12. Since they move in opposite directions, Relative Speed = L/18 + L/12 = (2L + 3L)/36 = 5L/36. Total distance to cross each other = L + L = 2L. Time taken = 2L / (5L/36) = 72 / 5 = 14.4 seconds.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-trains-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Train Speed from Two Opposite Men] A train overtakes two persons walking in opposite direction at 5 m/s and 10 m/s in 6s and 5s. Speed of train?", "options": ["A) 15 m/s", "B) 18 m/s", "C) 20 m/s", "D) 25 m/s"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A train completely passes two persons walking in the opposite direction to the train at speeds of 5 m/s and 10 m/s in exactly 6 seconds and 5 seconds respectively. What is the speed of the train in m/s?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let train speed be S and train length be L. Since they walk in opposite direction, relative speeds are S+5 and S+10. L = (S + 5) × 6, and L = (S + 10) × 5. Equating lengths: 6S + 30 = 5S + 50 => S = 20 m/s.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-trains-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Cross-Timing Square Root Shortcut] Two trains cross each other. They take 4h and 9h to reach destination. Ratio of speeds?", "options": ["A) 2 : 3", "B) 3 : 2", "C) 4 : 9", "D) 9 : 4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains start simultaneously from stations A and B towards each other. After crossing each other on the way, they take exactly 4 hours and 9 hours to reach stations B and A respectively. What is the ratio of their speeds?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "If two bodies start simultaneously from opposite ends, cross, and then take times T1 and T2 to finish their journeys, the ratio of their speeds is S1/S2 = √(T2 / T1). So, S1/S2 = √(9 / 4) = 3 / 2. Ratio is 3 : 2.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-trains-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Intersection Time from Departure and Arrival] Train X from A 4 PM, to B 5 PM. Train Y from B 4 PM, to A 5:30 PM. Time they cross?", "options": ["A) 4:30 PM", "B) 4:36 PM", "C) 4:40 PM", "D) 4:45 PM"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Train X starts from station A at 4:00 PM and reaches station B at 5:00 PM. Train Y starts from station B at 4:00 PM and reaches station A at 5:30 PM. At what exact time will the two trains cross each other?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Time taken by X = 1 hour. Time taken by Y = 1.5 hours (3/2 hours). Let distance AB = 3 km. Speed X = 3 / 1 = 3 km/hr. Speed Y = 3 / 1.5 = 2 km/hr. Relative speed = 3 + 2 = 5 km/hr. Time to meet = Distance / Relative Speed = 3 / 5 hours = (3/5) × 60 = 36 minutes. They meet at 4:00 PM + 36 mins = 4:36 PM.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-trains-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Passenger and Goods Train Ratio] Goods train and passenger train run same direction. Passenger overtakes completely in 60s. Passenger in train sees crossing goods in 40s. Ratio of lengths?", "options": ["A) 1 : 2", "B) 2 : 1", "C) 2 : 3", "D) 3 : 2"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A goods train and a passenger train run on parallel tracks in the same direction. The driver of the goods train observes that the passenger train coming from behind overtakes his train completely in 60 seconds. A passenger inside the passenger train notices that he crosses the goods train completely in 40 seconds. What is the ratio of the length of the goods train to that of the passenger train?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let lengths be L_p and L_g. Let relative speed be V. 1) The passenger train completely overtaking the goods train means total distance covered relative to each other is (L_p + L_g). So, L_p + L_g = 60 × V. 2) A passenger sitting inside the passenger train acts as a single point moving at V relative to the goods train. The distance to cross the goods train is just L_g. So, L_g = 40 × V. 3) Substituting L_g: L_p + 40V = 60V => L_p = 20V. 4) Ratio of Goods to Passenger lengths = L_g : L_p = 40V : 20V = 2 : 1.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-trains-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Finding Speed using Multiple Landmarks] A train passes a telegraph post in 8s and a 264m bridge in 20s. Speed of the train in km/hr?", "options": ["A) 72.0 km/hr", "B) 75.5 km/hr", "C) 79.2 km/hr", "D) 82.5 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A train moving at a constant speed completely passes a telegraph post in exactly 8 seconds, and a 264-meter long bridge in 20 seconds. What is the speed of the train in km/hr?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let train length be L. Speed S = L/8. For the bridge, S = (L + 264)/20. Equating: L/8 = (L + 264)/20 => 20L = 8L + 2112 => 12L = 2112 => L = 176 m. Speed S = 176 / 8 = 22 m/s. Convert to km/hr: 22 × (18/5) = 396 / 5 = 79.2 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-trains-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Time to Cross Opposite Trains] Two trains 140m and 160m long run at 60 km/hr and 40 km/hr in opposite directions. Time to cross?", "options": ["A) 9.6 seconds", "B) 10.8 seconds", "C) 11.2 seconds", "D) 12.0 seconds"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains, 140 meters and 160 meters long, run at speeds of 60 km/hr and 40 km/hr respectively in opposite directions on parallel tracks. In how many seconds will they completely cross each other?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total distance = 140 + 160 = 300 meters. Relative speed = 60 + 40 = 100 km/hr. Convert to m/s: 100 × 5/18 = 500/18 = 250/9 m/s. Time = Distance / Speed = 300 / (250/9) = 2700 / 250 = 10.8 seconds.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-trains-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Passing Moving Person in Train] Two trains travel same direction at 50 km/hr and 30 km/hr. Faster completely passes a man in slower in 18s. Length of faster train?", "options": ["A) 80 meters", "B) 100 meters", "C) 120 meters", "D) 150 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two trains are traveling in the exact same direction at speeds of 50 km/hr and 30 km/hr respectively. The faster train completely passes a man sitting in the slower train in exactly 18 seconds. What is the length of the faster train?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The man sitting in the slower train behaves as a point object moving at 30 km/hr. The relative speed of the faster train to the man is 50 - 30 = 20 km/hr. Convert to m/s: 20 × (5/18) = 100/18 = 50/9 m/s. The distance covered to pass the man is exactly the length of the faster train. Length = Speed × Time = (50/9) × 18 = 100 meters.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22131	Lecture 13: DOM Architecture & Selectors — How HTML Becomes a Tree of JS Objects, querySelector & HTMLCollection vs. NodeList	js-lecture-13-dom-architecture-selectors-htmlcollection-vs-nodelist	Understand how the browser parses raw HTML text into the Document Object Model (DOM) tree, select elements with getElementById and querySelectorAll, and contrast live HTMLCollections with static NodeLists.	2026-10-06 21:36:53.504856+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nJavaScript cannot directly manipulate raw text inside an `.html` file. When a browser loads a webpage, its rendering engine parses the HTML document and converts every tag, attribute, and text node into a hierarchical tree of **JavaScript Objects** in memory called the **DOM (Document Object Model)**, exposed under `window.document`.\n\n### 💡 Selecting DOM Elements\n* **Single Element Selectors:**\n  * **`document.getElementById("first")`:** Fastest lookup by unique `id` attribute.\n  * **`document.querySelector(".card > h2")`:** Returns the **first** element matching any valid CSS selector (`#id`, `.class`, `tag`, attribute selectors).\n* **Multiple Element Selectors:**\n  * **`document.getElementsByClassName("item")` / `getElementsByTagName("li")`:** Returns a **Live `HTMLCollection`**.\n  * **`document.querySelectorAll(".item")`:** Returns a **Static `NodeList`**.\n\n### 💡 `HTMLCollection` vs. `NodeList` & Converting to Arrays\n* **Live `HTMLCollection`:** Automatically updates itself if matching elements are later added to or removed from the DOM. Does **not** have `.forEach()` or `.map()`.\n* **Static `NodeList` (from `querySelectorAll`):** A snapshot at the time of query. Supports `.forEach()`, but still lacks `.map()`, `.filter()`, or `.reduce()`.\n* **Converting to a Real Array:** Use **`Array.from(collection)`** or the spread operator **`[...collection`** to unlock all `Array.prototype` methods.\n\n### 💡 `textContent` vs. `innerText` vs. `innerHTML`\n* **`el.textContent`:** Returns or sets all raw text inside the element, **including hidden text** (e.g., `display: none`), without parsing HTML tags. Fast and safe.\n* **`el.innerText`:** Layout-aware—returns only **visibly rendered text** (ignores `display: none` elements and triggers a CSS reflow calculation).\n* **`el.innerHTML`:** Parses and renders raw HTML markup strings inside the element.\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: DOM Selection, Traversal & Array Conversion\n```javascript\n// 1. Selecting & Styling an Element (camelCase CSS properties)\nconst heading = document.getElementById("first");\nheading.textContent = "Hello Coder Army!";\nheading.style.backgroundColor = "orange";\nheading.style.color = "brown";\n\n// 2. Converting NodeList / HTMLCollection into a true JS Array\nconst items = document.querySelectorAll(".course-item");\nconst itemTexts = Array.from(items).map(el => el.textContent.trim());\n\n// 3. DOM Tree Traversal (Element-only properties ignore whitespace text nodes)\nconst list = document.querySelector("#course-list");\nconsole.log(list.parentElement);          // Parent element\nconsole.log(list.children);               // HTMLCollection of child element nodes\nconsole.log(list.firstElementChild);      // First child element\nconsole.log(list.lastElementChild);       // Last child element\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Document_Object_Model/Introduction", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Document_Object_Model/Introduction", "title": "MDN Web Docs — Introduction to the DOM (Document Object Model)"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wLp99HGG8GQ", "title": "JavaScript DOM Manipulation | The Document Object Model (DOM) Explained | Javascript Full Course #13", "duration": "52m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-13-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What is the difference between an HTMLCollection and a NodeList in the DOM?", "title": "Q1: What is the difference between an HTMLCollection and a NodeList in the DOM?", "answer": "1) Live vs. Static: getElementsByClassName() and getElementsByTagName() return a LIVE HTMLCollection—if a new matching element is appended to the DOM later, the collection length updates automatically. querySelectorAll() returns a STATIC NodeList snapshot that does not change if the DOM mutates later. 2) Node Types: HTMLCollection contains only Element nodes, whereas a NodeList can contain any node type (Element nodes, Text nodes, Comment nodes). 3) Iteration: NodeList has a built-in .forEach() method; HTMLCollection does not (both can be converted to real arrays via Array.from()).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "1) Live vs. Static: getElementsByClassName() and getElementsByTagName() return a LIVE HTMLCollection—if a new matching element is appended to the DOM later, the collection length updates automatically. querySelectorAll() returns a STATIC NodeList snapshot that does not change if the DOM mutates later. 2) Node Types: HTMLCollection contains only Element nodes, whereas a NodeList can contain any node type (Element nodes, Text nodes, Comment nodes). 3) Iteration: NodeList has a built-in .forEach() method; HTMLCollection does not (both can be converted to real arrays via Array.from())."}, {"id": "js-lec-13-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: What is the exact difference between innerHTML, innerText, and textContent?", "title": "Q2: What is the exact difference between innerHTML, innerText, and textContent?", "answer": "1) innerHTML parses string content as HTML tags (e.g., \\"<b>Hi</b>\\" creates a bold element), which is slower and vulnerable to XSS if used with untrusted user input. 2) textContent reads or writes raw text literally (treating \\"<b>Hi</b>\\" as plain characters) and includes text inside hidden (<span style=\\"display:none\\">) elements without triggering a layout reflow. 3) innerText is CSS/layout-aware: it respects styling, ignores hidden elements, and triggers a browser reflow calculation.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) innerHTML parses string content as HTML tags (e.g., \\"<b>Hi</b>\\" creates a bold element), which is slower and vulnerable to XSS if used with untrusted user input. 2) textContent reads or writes raw text literally (treating \\"<b>Hi</b>\\" as plain characters) and includes text inside hidden (<span style=\\"display:none\\">) elements without triggering a layout reflow. 3) innerText is CSS/layout-aware: it respects styling, ignores hidden elements, and triggers a browser reflow calculation."}, {"id": "js-lec-13-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: Why is childNodes different from children when traversing the DOM tree?", "title": "Q3: Why is childNodes different from children when traversing the DOM tree?", "answer": "el.childNodes returns a NodeList of ALL child nodes, which includes invisible whitespace/newline Text nodes (#text) and HTML comments (<!-- -->) between tags! el.children returns an HTMLCollection containing strictly HTML Element nodes, which is almost always what developers want when traversing UI elements.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "el.childNodes returns a NodeList of ALL child nodes, which includes invisible whitespace/newline Text nodes (#text) and HTML comments (<!-- -->) between tags! el.children returns an HTMLCollection containing strictly HTML Element nodes, which is almost always what developers want when traversing UI elements."}, {"id": "js-lec-13-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why can't you call .map() or .filter() directly on the result of document.querySelectorAll(), and how do you fix it?", "title": "Q4: Why can't you call .map() or .filter() directly on the result of document.querySelectorAll(), and how do you fix it?", "answer": "document.querySelectorAll() returns a NodeList, not an Array. Its prototype is NodeList.prototype (which only implements forEach, entries, keys, values), not Array.prototype. To use .map() or .filter(), convert it into a real Array using Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(\\"...\\")) or [...document.querySelectorAll(\\"...\\")].", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "document.querySelectorAll() returns a NodeList, not an Array. Its prototype is NodeList.prototype (which only implements forEach, entries, keys, values), not Array.prototype. To use .map() or .filter(), convert it into a real Array using Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(\\"...\\")) or [...document.querySelectorAll(\\"...\\")]."}, {"id": "js-lec-13-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is the difference between the window object and the document object in a browser?", "title": "Q5: What is the difference between the window object and the document object in a browser?", "answer": "window is the top-level global object representing the browser tab/window itself (providing BOM APIs like localStorage, location, history, setTimeout, fetch, and innerWidth). document (window.document) is a property of window that specifically represents the parsed HTML/DOM tree loaded inside that tab.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "window is the top-level global object representing the browser tab/window itself (providing BOM APIs like localStorage, location, history, setTimeout, fetch, and innerWidth). document (window.document) is a property of window that specifically represents the parsed HTML/DOM tree loaded inside that tab."}, {"id": "js-lec-13-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why are CSS properties written in camelCase in JavaScript (e.g., el.style.backgroundColor) instead of kebab-case (background-color)?", "title": "Q6: Why are CSS properties written in camelCase in JavaScript (e.g., el.style.backgroundColor) instead of kebab-case (background-color)?", "answer": "In JavaScript syntax, a hyphen (-) is parsed as the subtraction operator! Writing el.style.background-color would attempt to subtract the variable color from el.style.background. Therefore, the CSSOM maps hyphenated CSS property names to camelCase identifiers (backgroundColor, fontSize, zIndex)—or you can use bracket notation el.style[\\"background-color\\"].", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In JavaScript syntax, a hyphen (-) is parsed as the subtraction operator! Writing el.style.background-color would attempt to subtract the variable color from el.style.background. Therefore, the CSSOM maps hyphenated CSS property names to camelCase identifiers (backgroundColor, fontSize, zIndex)—or you can use bracket notation el.style[\\"background-color\\"]."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nJavaScript cannot directly manipulate raw text inside an `.html` file. When a browser loads a webpage, its rendering engine parses the HTML document and converts every tag, attribute, and text node into a hierarchical tree of **JavaScript Objects** in memory called the **DOM (Document Object Model)**, exposed under `window.document`.\n\n### 💡 Selecting DOM Elements\n* **Single Element Selectors:**\n  * **`document.getElementById("first")`:** Fastest lookup by unique `id` attribute.\n  * **`document.querySelector(".card > h2")`:** Returns the **first** element matching any valid CSS selector (`#id`, `.class`, `tag`, attribute selectors).\n* **Multiple Element Selectors:**\n  * **`document.getElementsByClassName("item")` / `getElementsByTagName("li")`:** Returns a **Live `HTMLCollection`**.\n  * **`document.querySelectorAll(".item")`:** Returns a **Static `NodeList`**.\n\n### 💡 `HTMLCollection` vs. `NodeList` & Converting to Arrays\n* **Live `HTMLCollection`:** Automatically updates itself if matching elements are later added to or removed from the DOM. Does **not** have `.forEach()` or `.map()`.\n* **Static `NodeList` (from `querySelectorAll`):** A snapshot at the time of query. Supports `.forEach()`, but still lacks `.map()`, `.filter()`, or `.reduce()`.\n* **Converting to a Real Array:** Use **`Array.from(collection)`** or the spread operator **`[...collection`** to unlock all `Array.prototype` methods.\n\n### 💡 `textContent` vs. `innerText` vs. `innerHTML`\n* **`el.textContent`:** Returns or sets all raw text inside the element, **including hidden text** (e.g., `display: none`), without parsing HTML tags. Fast and safe.\n* **`el.innerText`:** Layout-aware—returns only **visibly rendered text** (ignores `display: none` elements and triggers a CSS reflow calculation).\n* **`el.innerHTML`:** Parses and renders raw HTML markup strings inside the element.\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: DOM Selection, Traversal & Array Conversion\n```javascript\n// 1. Selecting & Styling an Element (camelCase CSS properties)\nconst heading = document.getElementById("first");\nheading.textContent = "Hello Coder Army!";\nheading.style.backgroundColor = "orange";\nheading.style.color = "brown";\n\n// 2. Converting NodeList / HTMLCollection into a true JS Array\nconst items = document.querySelectorAll(".course-item");\nconst itemTexts = Array.from(items).map(el => el.textContent.trim());\n\n// 3. DOM Tree Traversal (Element-only properties ignore whitespace text nodes)\nconst list = document.querySelector("#course-list");\nconsole.log(list.parentElement);          // Parent element\nconsole.log(list.children);               // HTMLCollection of child element nodes\nconsole.log(list.firstElementChild);      // First child element\nconsole.log(list.lastElementChild);       // Last child element\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 13: DOM Architecture & Selectors — How HTML Becomes a Tree of JS Objects, querySelector & HTMLCollection vs. NodeList	Understand how the browser parses raw HTML text into the Docume