s-lec-13-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why can't you call .map() or .filter() directly on the result of document.querySelectorAll(), and how do you fix it?", "title": "Q4: Why can't you call .map() or .filter() directly on the result of document.querySelectorAll(), and how do you fix it?", "answer": "document.querySelectorAll() returns a NodeList, not an Array. Its prototype is NodeList.prototype (which only implements forEach, entries, keys, values), not Array.prototype. To use .map() or .filter(), convert it into a real Array using Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(\\"...\\")) or [...document.querySelectorAll(\\"...\\")].", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "document.querySelectorAll() returns a NodeList, not an Array. Its prototype is NodeList.prototype (which only implements forEach, entries, keys, values), not Array.prototype. To use .map() or .filter(), convert it into a real Array using Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(\\"...\\")) or [...document.querySelectorAll(\\"...\\")]."}, {"id": "js-lec-13-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is the difference between the window object and the document object in a browser?", "title": "Q5: What is the difference between the window object and the document object in a browser?", "answer": "window is the top-level global object representing the browser tab/window itself (providing BOM APIs like localStorage, location, history, setTimeout, fetch, and innerWidth). document (window.document) is a property of window that specifically represents the parsed HTML/DOM tree loaded inside that tab.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "window is the top-level global object representing the browser tab/window itself (providing BOM APIs like localStorage, location, history, setTimeout, fetch, and innerWidth). document (window.document) is a property of window that specifically represents the parsed HTML/DOM tree loaded inside that tab."}, {"id": "js-lec-13-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why are CSS properties written in camelCase in JavaScript (e.g., el.style.backgroundColor) instead of kebab-case (background-color)?", "title": "Q6: Why are CSS properties written in camelCase in JavaScript (e.g., el.style.backgroundColor) instead of kebab-case (background-color)?", "answer": "In JavaScript syntax, a hyphen (-) is parsed as the subtraction operator! Writing el.style.background-color would attempt to subtract the variable color from el.style.background. Therefore, the CSSOM maps hyphenated CSS property names to camelCase identifiers (backgroundColor, fontSize, zIndex)—or you can use bracket notation el.style[\\"background-color\\"].", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "13-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In JavaScript syntax, a hyphen (-) is parsed as the subtraction operator! Writing el.style.background-color would attempt to subtract the variable color from el.style.background. Therefore, the CSSOM maps hyphenated CSS property names to camelCase identifiers (backgroundColor, fontSize, zIndex)—or you can use bracket notation el.style[\\"background-color\\"]."}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Document_Object_Model/Introduction	\N	\N	\N
22145	Lecture 5: Parsing Requests — Streams, Chunks, Buffers (req.on("data"), req.on("end"), Buffer.concat), URLSearchParams & CommonJS Modules	node-lecture-5-parsing-requests-streams-chunks-buffers-commonjs-modules	Process incoming POST payloads using Node.js Streams, Chunks, and Buffers (req.on("data"), req.on("end"), Buffer.concat), decode form data with URLSearchParams, and modularize code using module.exports.	2026-10-06 21:56:18.722653+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhen a client sends a `POST` request (whether a small login form or a 6 GB video upload), the payload does **not** arrive at the server in one giant piece. Instead, data travels across the network as a continuous **Stream** of small binary packets called **Chunks**.\n\n### 💡 Streams, Chunks & Buffers in Node.js\n* **Stream:** Continuous flow of data over time from source (Client socket) to destination (Server). Allows Node.js to start processing data immediately without waiting for the entire file to finish uploading, keeping RAM usage minimal.\n* **Chunk:** Each individual fragment of binary data emitted by a stream as it arrives.\n* **Buffer:** A temporary fixed-size allocation of raw binary memory (outside the V8 Heap) that collects and holds binary **Chunks** until they are ready to be decoded into a UTF-8 string via **`Buffer.concat(body).toString()`**.\n\n### 💡 Step-by-Step Pipeline for Parsing a `POST` Request Body\n1. **Listen for `"data"` Events (`req.on("data", chunk => ...)`):** Fires every time a new binary chunk arrives. Push each `chunk` into an array `const body = [`.\n2. **Listen for the `"end"` Event (`req.on("end", () => ...)`):** Fires once all chunks have arrived.\n3. **Concatenate & Decode (`Buffer.concat(body).toString()`):** Combines all binary chunks and decodes them into a URL-encoded string like `"username=Prashant+Jain&gender=male"`.\n4. **Parse Key-Value Pairs (`URLSearchParams` & `Object.fromEntries`):**\n   * Pass the string into `const params = new URLSearchParams(fullBody)`.\n   * Convert it into a clean JavaScript object: `const bodyObject = Object.fromEntries(params)`.\n\n### 💡 Splitting Code into Custom Modules (`module.exports`)\nTo keep `app.js` clean, move the request handler into `user.js` and export it using **`module.exports = userRequestHandler`** (or export multiple items as an object `module.exports = { handler: ..., extra: ... }`).\n\n### 🛠️ Complete Stream Buffering & `URLSearchParams` Parsing Example\n```javascript\n// File: user.js\nconst fs = require("fs");\n\nconst userRequestHandler = (req, res) => {\n  if (req.url === "/submit-details" && req.method === "POST") {\n    const body = [;\n\n    // 1. Collect each incoming binary Buffer chunk from the Readable Stream\n    req.on("data", (chunk) => {\n      console.log("Received Chunk:", chunk);\n      body.push(chunk);\n    });\n\n    // 2. When stream ends, concatenate chunks, decode to string, and parse key-values\n    req.on("end", () => {\n      const fullBody = Buffer.concat(body).toString();\n      console.log("Raw Decoded Body:", fullBody); // e.g. "username=Prashant+Jain&gender=male"\n\n      const params = new URLSearchParams(fullBody);\n      const bodyObject = Object.fromEntries(params);\n      console.log("Parsed Object:", bodyObject); // { username: "Prashant Jain", gender: "male" }\n\n      fs.writeFileSync("user.txt", JSON.stringify(bodyObject));\n    });\n\n    res.statusCode = 302;\n    res.setHeader("Location", "/");\n    return res.end();\n  }\n};\n\nmodule.exports = userRequestHandler;\n```	[{"url": "https://nodejs.org/api/buffer.html", "link": "https://nodejs.org/api/buffer.html", "title": "Node.js Official Docs — Buffer & Stream APIs"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u2CKVFcvaxA", "title": "Lecture 5: Parsing Request | NodeJS Complete Course in Hindi", "duration": "77m", "creator_name": "Complete Coding by Prashant Sir", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir"}]	[{"id": "node-lec-5-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What are Streams, Chunks, and Buffers in Node.js, and why does Node.js receive POST request bodies as Streams instead of a single pre-loaded object?", "title": "Q1: What are Streams, Chunks, and Buffers in Node.js, and why does Node.js receive POST request bodies as Streams instead of a single pre-loaded object?", "answer": "A Stream is an abstract interface for working with continuous flowing data over time. A Chunk is a small fragment of data emitted by a stream, and a Buffer is a raw binary memory allocation used to temporarily hold chunks before processing. Node.js receives HTTP bodies as Streams because a client might upload a 2 GB file—if Node waited to load the entire 2 GB payload into RAM before invoking your handler, just a few concurrent uploads would crash the server with an Out-Of-Memory error!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "A Stream is an abstract interface for working with continuous flowing data over time. A Chunk is a small fragment of data emitted by a stream, and a Buffer is a raw binary memory allocation used to temporarily hold chunks before processing. Node.js receives HTTP bodies as Streams because a client might upload a 2 GB file—if Node waited to load the entire 2 GB payload into RAM before invoking your handler, just a few concurrent uploads would crash the server with an Out-Of-Memory error!"}, {"id": "node-lec-5-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why can't you access the parsed POST body synchronously right after calling req.on(\\"data\\", ...) and req.on(\\"end\\", ...)?", "title": "Q2: Why can't you access the parsed POST body synchronously right after calling req.on(\\"data\\", ...) and req.on(\\"end\\", ...)?", "answer": "req.on(\\"data\\", cb) and req.on(\\"end\\", cb) only register asynchronous event listeners—they do NOT pause execution! The chunks arrive over the network asynchronously in the background. Therefore, the complete body is ONLY available inside the req.on(\\"end\\", () => { ... }) callback after all chunks have been received.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "req.on(\\"data\\", cb) and req.on(\\"end\\", cb) only register asynchronous event listeners—they do NOT pause execution! The chunks arrive over the network asynchronously in the background. Therefore, the complete body is ONLY available inside the req.on(\\"end\\", () => { ... }) callback after all chunks have been received."}, {"id": "node-lec-5-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: How does Buffer.concat(body).toString() work, and how does URLSearchParams convert \\"username=Prashant+Jain&gender=male\\" into a JS object?", "title": "Q3: How does Buffer.concat(body).toString() work, and how does URLSearchParams convert \\"username=Prashant+Jain&gender=male\\" into a JS object?", "answer": "Each chunk pushed into the body array during \\"data\\" events is a binary Buffer instance. Buffer.concat(body) merges all those Buffer instances into a single contiguous Buffer, and .toString() decodes the UTF-8 bytes into a URL-encoded string (\\"username=Prashant+Jain&gender=male\\"). Passing that string to new URLSearchParams(fullBody) parses the key=value pairs and decodes \\"+\\" / \\"%20\\" into spaces, and Object.fromEntries(params) converts those [key, value] entries into a plain object { username: \\"Prashant Jain\\", gender: \\"male\\" }.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Each chunk pushed into the body array during \\"data\\" events is a binary Buffer instance. Buffer.concat(body) merges all those Buffer instances into a single contiguous Buffer, and .toString() decodes the UTF-8 bytes into a URL-encoded string (\\"username=Prashant+Jain&gender=male\\"). Passing that string to new URLSearchParams(fullBody) parses the key=value pairs and decodes \\"+\\" / \\"%20\\" into spaces, and Object.fromEntries(params) converts those [key, value] entries into a plain object { username: \\"Prashant Jain\\", gender: \\"male\\" }."}, {"id": "node-lec-5-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What are the 4 types of Streams in Node.js?", "title": "Q4: What are the 4 types of Streams in Node.js?", "answer": "1) Readable Streams: Streams from which data can be read (e.g., http.IncomingMessage req, fs.createReadStream). 2) Writable Streams: Streams to which data can be written (e.g., http.ServerResponse res, fs.createWriteStream). 3) Duplex Streams: Streams that are both Readable and Writable simultaneously (e.g., TCP net.Socket). 4) Transform Streams: Duplex streams that modify or transform data as it passes through (e.g., zlib.createGzip compression).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "1) Readable Streams: Streams from which data can be read (e.g., http.IncomingMessage req, fs.createReadStream). 2) Writable Streams: Streams to which data can be written (e.g., http.ServerResponse res, fs.createWriteStream). 3) Duplex Streams: Streams that are both Readable and Writable simultaneously (e.g., TCP net.Socket). 4) Transform Streams: Duplex streams that modify or transform data as it passes through (e.g., zlib.createGzip compression)."}, {"id": "node-lec-5-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is the difference between module.exports and exports in Node.js CommonJS modules?", "title": "Q5: What is the difference between module.exports and exports in Node.js CommonJS modules?", "answer": "In every CommonJS file, module.exports is the actual object returned by require(). The variable exports is simply a shorthand reference pointer initialized to point to module.exports (let exports = module.exports). If you attach properties via exports.handler = fn, it works because it mutates the shared object. However, if you reassign exports = fn directly, you break the reference to module.exports, and require() will return an empty object {}! Therefore, always use module.exports = ... when exporting a single function or object.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q5", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "In every CommonJS file, module.exports is the actual object returned by require(). The variable exports is simply a shorthand reference pointer initialized to point to module.exports (let exports = module.exports). If you attach properties via exports.handler = fn, it works because it mutates the shared object. However, if you reassign exports = fn directly, you break the reference to module.exports, and require() will return an empty object {}! Therefore, always use module.exports = ... when exporting a single function or object."}, {"id": "node-lec-5-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Where are Node.js Buffer instances allocated in memory?", "title": "Q6: Where are Node.js Buffer instances allocated in memory?", "answer": "Buffer instances represent fixed-length sequences of raw binary bytes and are allocated in C++ memory outside the V8 JavaScript Heap (though backed by ArrayBuffer references), allowing Node.js to manipulate binary network packets and file streams directly without V8 string encoding overhead.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q6", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Buffer instances represent fixed-length sequences of raw binary bytes and are allocated in C++ memory outside the V8 JavaScript Heap (though backed by ArrayBuffer references), allowing Node.js to manipulate binary network packets and file streams directly without V8 string encoding overhead."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhen a client sends a `POST` request (whether a small login form or a 6 GB video upload), the payload does **not** arrive at the server in one giant piece. Instead, data travels across the network as a continuous **Stream** of small binary packets called **Chunks**.\n\n### 💡 Streams, Chunks & Buffers in Node.js\n* **Stream:** Continuous flow of data over time from source (Client socket) to destination (Server). Allows Node.js to start processing data immediately without waiting for the entire file to finish uploading, keeping RAM usage minimal.\n* **Chunk:** Each individual fragment of binary data emitted by a stream as it arrives.\n* **Buffer:** A temporary fixed-size allocation of raw binary memory (outside the V8 Heap) that collects and holds binary **Chunks** until they are ready to be decoded into a UTF-8 string via **`Buffer.concat(body).toString()`**.\n\n### 💡 Step-by-Step Pipeline for Parsing a `POST` Request Body\n1. **Listen for `"data"` Events (`req.on("data", chunk => ...)`):** Fires every time a new binary chunk arrives. Push each `chunk` into an array `const body = [`.\n2. **Listen for the `"end"` Event (`req.on("end", () => ...)`):** Fires once all chunks have arrived.\n3. **Concatenate & Decode (`Buffer.concat(body).toString()`):** Combines all binary chunks and decodes them into a URL-encoded string like `"username=Prashant+Jain&gender=male"`.\n4. **Parse Key-Value Pairs (`URLSearchParams` & `Object.fromEntries`):**\n   * Pass the string into `const params = new URLSearchParams(fullBody)`.\n   * Convert it into a clean JavaScript object: `const bodyObject = Object.fromEntries(params)`.\n\n### 💡 Splitting Code into Custom Modules (`module.exports`)\nTo keep `app.js` clean, move the request handler into `user.js` and export it using **`module.exports = userRequestHandler`** (or export multiple items as an object `module.exports = { handler: ..., extra: ... }`).\n\n### 🛠️ Complete Stream Buffering & `URLSearchParams` Parsing Example\n```javascript\n// File: user.js\nconst fs = require("fs");\n\nconst userRequestHandler = (req, res) => {\n  if (req.url === "/submit-details" && req.method === "POST") {\n    const body = [;\n\n    // 1. Collect each incoming binary Buffer chunk from the Readable Stream\n    req.on("data", (chunk) => {\n      console.log("Received Chunk:", chunk);\n      body.push(chunk);\n    });\n\n    // 2. When stream ends, concatenate chunks, decode to string, and parse key-values\n    req.on("end", () => {\n      const fullBody = Buffer.concat(body).toString();\n      console.log("Raw Decoded Body:", fullBody); // e.g. "username=Prashant+Jain&gender=male"\n\n      const params = new URLSearchParams(fullBody);\n      const bodyObject = Object.fromEntries(params);\n      console.log("Parsed Object:", bodyObject); // { username: "Prashant Jain", gender: "male" }\n\n      fs.writeFileSync("user.txt", JSON.stringify(bodyObject));\n    });\n\n    res.statusCode = 302;\n    res.setHeader("Location", "/");\n    return res.end();\n  }\n};\n\nmodule.exports = userRequestHandler;\n```	Complete Coding by Prashant Sir	https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir	24	Lecture 5: Parsing Requests — Streams, Chunks, Buffers (req.on("data"), req.on("end"), Buffer.concat), URLSearchParams & CommonJS Modules	Process incoming POST payloads using Node.js Streams, Chunks, and Buffers (req.on("data"), req.on("end"), Buffer.concat), decode form data with URLSearchParams, and modularize code using module.exports.	20	Easy	\N	5	[{"id": "node-lec-5-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What are Streams, Chunks, and Buffers in Node.js, and why does Node.js receive POST request bodies as Streams instead of a single pre-loaded object?", "title": "Q1: What are Streams, Chunks, and Buffers in Node.js, and why does Node.js receive POST request bodies as Streams instead of a single pre-loaded object?", "answer": "A Stream is an abstract interface for working with continuous flowing data over time. A Chunk is a small fragment of data emitted by a stream, and a Buffer is a raw binary memory allocation used to temporarily hold chunks before processing. Node.js receives HTTP bodies as Streams because a client might upload a 2 GB file—if Node waited to load the entire 2 GB payload into RAM before invoking your handler, ju