ow do you read a Node.js Error Stack Trace to quickly find the bug in your own code?", "answer": "The first line of a stack trace shows the Error Type and message (e.g., TypeError: num is not a function). Below that is the Call Stack trace starting from the deepest point where the error occurred. Ignore lines pointing to internal node:... modules (like node:internal/modules/cjs/loader) and look for the topmost line containing your own project file path and line:column number (e.g., at runtime (/workspace/runtime.js:4:3)).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "8-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "The first line of a stack trace shows the Error Type and message (e.g., TypeError: num is not a function). Below that is the Call Stack trace starting from the deepest point where the error occurred. Ignore lines pointing to internal node:... modules (like node:internal/modules/cjs/loader) and look for the topmost line containing your own project file path and line:column number (e.g., at runtime (/workspace/runtime.js:4:3))."}, {"id": "node-lec-8-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: What can you do with the Debug Console and Variables panel while paused at a Breakpoint in VS Code?", "title": "Q6: What can you do with the Debug Console and Variables panel while paused at a Breakpoint in VS Code?", "answer": "While paused at a breakpoint, the Variables panel lets you inspect all Local, Closure, and Global variables in scope and even modify their values in memory on the fly to test different branches. The Debug Console acts as a live REPL scoped to the paused line, allowing you to evaluate arbitrary JS expressions against the live variables.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "8-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "While paused at a breakpoint, the Variables panel lets you inspect all Local, Closure, and Global variables in scope and even modify their values in memory on the fly to test different branches. The Debug Console acts as a live REPL scoped to the paused line, allowing you to evaluate arbitrary JS expressions against the live variables."}]	https://nodejs.org/en/learn/getting-started/debugging	\N	\N	\N
3023	Ordered Neighbor Search: Inorder Successor and Predecessor in a BST	dsa-inorder-successor-and-predecessor-in-bst	Find the inorder successor (smallest node strictly greater) and predecessor (largest node strictly smaller) in a BST in O(H) time without storing full traversals.	2026-10-03 01:35:42.025792+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Ordered Neighbor Search: Inorder Successor and Predecessor in a BST\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SXKAD2svfmI", "title": "Lecture 121: Inorder Successor and Predecessor in BST", "duration": "22m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/inorder-successor-in-bst/", "title": "Inorder Successor in BST (LC 285)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the in-order successor of a given node in a Binary Search Tree in O(H) time."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/inorder-successor-in-bst-ii/", "title": "Inorder Successor in BST II (LC 510)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the in-order successor of a BST node when given parent pointers instead of the root."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/predecessor-and-successor/1", "title": "Predecessor and Successor", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find both the inorder predecessor and successor of a given key in a BST."}]	Module 20: Advanced Binary Search Tree Operations & Validation	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a BST and a target node `p`, the **Inorder Successor** is the node with the smallest key **strictly greater than `p->val`**, and the **Inorder Predecessor** is the node with the largest key **strictly smaller than `p->val`**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Better → Optimal)\n- **1. Brute Force (`O(N)` Time, `O(N)` Space):** Store the full inorder traversal in an array and binary search for the element right after `p->val`.\n- **2. Better Approach (`O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Space):** Perform an inorder traversal (e.g., Morris traversal) and return the first node encountered with `val > p->val`.\n- **3. Optimal BST Branch Elimination (`O(H)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - **For Inorder Successor:**\n    - Initialize `successor = nullptr` and start at `curr = root`.\n    - If `p->val >= curr->val`: `curr` and its entire left subtree are `<= p->val`, so they cannot be the successor. Move right: `curr = curr->right`.\n    - If `p->val < curr->val`: `curr` is strictly greater than `p`, so it is a potential successor! Record `successor = curr`, and move left (`curr = curr->left`) to look for an even smaller valid value.\n  - **For Inorder Predecessor:**\n    - Mirror the logic: when `curr->val < p->val`, record `predecessor = curr` and move right (`curr = curr->right`); otherwise move left (`curr = curr->left`).\n\n### 💻 Inorder Successor & Predecessor Blueprint\n```cpp\nTreeNode* inorderSuccessor(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* p) {\n    TreeNode* successor = nullptr;\n    while (root != nullptr) {\n        if (p->val >= root->val) {\n            root = root->right;\n        } else {\n            successor = root;\n            root = root->left;\n        }\n    }\n    return successor;\n}\n\nTreeNode* inorderPredecessor(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* p) {\n    TreeNode* predecessor = nullptr;\n    while (root != nullptr) {\n        if (p->val <= root->val) {\n            root = root->left;\n        } else {\n            predecessor = root;\n            root = root->right;\n        }\n    }\n    return predecessor;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting Equality in `p->val >= root->val`:** When `root->val == p->val`, the node itself cannot be its own successor—you must move right (`root = root->right`) to find a strictly larger value!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
10013	Dummy Node & Carry Propagation: Add Two Numbers in a Linked List	dsa-add-two-numbers-in-linked-list-dummy-node	Sum two numbers represented by linked lists in reverse digit order using a dummy node and carry propagation in a single pass.	2026-10-03 15:04:07.021461+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Dummy Node & Carry Propagation: Add Two Numbers in a Linked List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XmRrGzR6udg", "title": "Lecture 241: Add 2 Numbers in Linked List", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/add-two-numbers/", "title": "Add Two Numbers (LC 2)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Add two non-negative numbers whose digits are stored in reverse order in two linked lists and return the sum as a linked list."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/add-two-numbers-ii/", "title": "Add Two Numbers II (LC 445)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Add two numbers represented by linked lists when the most significant digit comes first."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/add-two-numbers-represented-by-linked-lists/1", "title": "Add two numbers represented by linked lists", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Sum two numbers represented by linked lists and return the head of the sum list."}]	Module 37: Linked List Medium Problems: Dummy Nodes, In-Place Re-Linking & Tortoise-Hare Pointers	### 📌 Topic Overview\nYou are given two non-empty linked lists `num1` and `num2` representing two non-negative integers where the digits are stored in **reverse order** (the head node holds the units digit `10^0`, the second node holds `10^1`, etc.). Add the two numbers and return the sum as a linked list in the same reversed digit order.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why the Dummy Node Pattern Simplifies List Construction:**\n  - Whenever a problem asks you to **build and return a brand-new linked list**, allocate a **`Node* dummyNode = new Node(-1);`** and set `Node* curr = dummyNode;`.\n  - Without a dummy node, you have to write awkward `if (head == nullptr)` checks on the first iteration. With `dummyNode`, you simply attach `curr->next = newNode; curr = curr->next;` on every step and return **`dummyNode->next`** at the end!\n- **Digit-by-Digit Addition With `carry` (`O(max(N1, N2))` Time, `O(max(N1, N2))` Space):**\n  - Initialize `carry = 0`, `t1 = num1`, `t2 = num2`.\n  - Loop `while (t1 != nullptr || t2 != nullptr)`:\n    - `sum = carry`\n    - If `t1` exists: `sum += t1->data; t1 = t1->next;`\n    - If `t2` exists: `sum += t2->data; t2 = t2->next;`\n    - Create `new Node(sum % 10)`, attach to `curr->next`, advance `curr`, and update **`carry = sum / 10`**.\n  - **Crucial Final Check:** After the loop finishes, if **`carry > 0`**, attach one final node **`curr->next = new Node(carry)`**!\n\n### 💻 Add Two Numbers in Linked List Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nNode* addTwoNumbers(Node* num1, Node* num2) {\n    Node* dummyHead = new Node(-1);\n    Node* curr = dummyHead;\n    Node* t1 = num1;\n    Node* t2 = num2;\n    int carry = 0;\n\n    while (t1 != nullptr || t2 != nullptr) {\n        int sum = carry;\n        if (t1 != nullptr) {\n            sum += t1->data;\n            t1 = t1->next;\n        }\n        if (t2 != nullptr) {\n            sum += t2->data;\n            t2 = t2->next;\n        }\n\n        Node* newNode = new Node(sum % 10);\n        carry = sum / 10;\n        curr->next = newNode;\n        curr = curr->next;\n    }\n\n    // Don't forget leftover carry after the most significant digits!\n    if (carry > 0) {\n        curr->next = new Node(carry);\n    }\n\n    return dummyHead->next;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting Leftover `carry` After the Loop (e.g., `99 + 1 = 100`):** When both `t1` and `t2` become `nullptr`, `carry` can still be `1`! Always check `if (carry > 0) curr->next = new Node(carry);` after the `while` loop.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
1958	Index-Window Boundary Splitting: Construct Binary Tree from Preorder and Inorder Traversal	dsa-construct-binary-tree-from-preorder-and-inorder	Construct a unique binary tree from preorder and inorder arrays in O(N) time using hash-mapped index windows.	2026-10-03 01:18:07.170339+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Index-Window Boundary Splitting: Construct Binary Tree from Preorder and Inorder Traversal\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aZNaLrVebKQ", "title": "Lecture 106: Construct A Binary Tree from Inorder and Preorder Traversal", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/construct-binary-tree-from-preorder-and-inorder-traversal/", "title": "Construct Binary Tree from Preorder and Inorder Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Build and return the binary tree from preorder and inorder arrays."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/construct-binary-tree-from-preorder-and-postorder-traversal/", "title": "Construct Binary Tree from Preorder and Postorder Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Reconstruct a tree using preorder and postorder with full tree assumptions."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/construct-tree-1/1", "title": "Construct Tree from Inorder & Preorder", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Build a tree from inorder and preorder traversal arrays."}]	Module 18: Binary Trees: Node Distances, Tree Modifications & Reconstruction	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven two integer arrays `preorder` and `inorder` with unique values, reconstruct and return the unique binary tree in `O(N)` time.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Precomputing Inorder Indices in a Hash Map (`O(N)` Time):**\n  - Store each value's index in `inMap[inorder[i]] = i` for `O(1)` root lookups.\n- **The Index-Window Recurrence:**\n  - In `preorder`, the root node is always `preorder[preStart]`.\n  - Look up root in `inMap`: `inRoot = inMap[root->val]`.\n  - Count left subtree size: **`numsLeft = inRoot - inStart`**.\n  - **Left Subtree Windows:**\n    - Preorder: `[preStart + 1 ... preStart + numsLeft]`\n    - Inorder: `[inStart ... inRoot - 1]`\n  - **Right Subtree Windows:**\n    - Preorder: `[preStart + numsLeft + 1 ... preEnd]`\n    - Inorder: `[inRoot + 1 ... inEnd]`\n\n### 💻 Construct from Preorder and Inorder Blueprint\n```cpp\nTreeNode* build(vector<int> &preorder, int preStart, int preEnd,\n                vector<int> &inorder, int inStart, int inEnd,\n                unordered_map<int, int> &inMap) {\n    if (preStart > preEnd || inStart > inEnd) return nullptr;\n\n    TreeNode* root = new TreeNode(preorder[preStart]);\n    int inRoot = inMap[root->val];\n    int numsLeft = inRoot - inStart;\n\n    root->left = build(preorder, preStart + 1, preStart + numsLeft,\n                       inorder, inStart, inRoot - 1, inMap);\n    root->right = build(preorder, preStart + numsLeft + 1, preEnd,\n                        inorder, inRoot + 1, inEnd, inMap);\n    return root;\n}\n\nTreeNode* buildTree(vector<int> &preorder, vector<int> &inorder) {\n    unordered_map<int, int> inMap;\n    for (int i = 0; i < inorder.size(); i++) inMap[inorder[i]] = i;\n    return build(preorder, 0, preorder.size() - 1,\n                 inorder, 0, inorder.size() - 1, inMap);\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
5812	Blocked-State Pruning in Grid DP: Unique Paths II (Maze With Obstacles)	dsa-unique-paths-ii-grid-with-obstacles	Count unique grid paths in the presence of obstacles by pruning blocked cells to zero and applying 1D space optimization.	2026-10-03 11:17:17.116122+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Blocked-State Pruning in Grid DP: Unique Paths II (Maze With Obstacles)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TmhpgXScLyY", "title": "Lecture 190: Unique Paths II (Maze With Obstacles)", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/unique-paths-ii/", "title": "Unique Paths II (LC 63)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the number of unique paths from top-left to bottom-right in an m x n grid containing obstacles."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/grid-path-2/1", "title": "Grid Path 2", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count unique paths in a grid with obstacles modulo 10^9 + 7."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/unique-paths-iii/", "title": "Unique Paths III (LC 980)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Explore grid paths with obstacles when every non-obstacle square must be visited exactly once."}]	Module 29: 2D Dynamic Programming on Multi-Choice States & Grids	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an `M x N` `obstacleGrid` where `0` is an open space and `1` (or `-1`) is a blocked obstacle, count the number of unique paths from `(0, 0)` to `(M - 1, N - 1)` moving only Right or Down.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The 1-Line Obstacle Base Case Addition:**\n  - This problem is 99% identical to **Grid Unique Paths I**, with **one extra base case at the very top**:\n    - If `(i >= 0 && j >= 0 && obstacleGrid[i][j] == 1)` → **Return `0`** (`cur[j] = 0`)!\n  - Why check the obstacle condition **before** `if (i == 0 && j == 0) return 1;`?\n    - Because if the starting cell `(0, 0)` itself has an obstacle (`obstacleGrid[0][0] == 1`), there are **`0` ways** to start! Checking the obstacle condition first handles a blocked `(0, 0)` automatically.\n- **Complexity:** `O(M * N)` Time and `O(N)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Unique Paths II (With Obstacles, Space Optimized) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint uniquePathsWithObstacles(vector<vector<int>> &obstacleGrid) {\n    int m = obstacleGrid.size(), n = obstacleGrid[0].size();\n    vector<int> prev(n, 0);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {\n        vector<int> cur(n, 0);\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n            // 1. Check for obstacle FIRST (even at (0, 0))!\n            if (obstacleGrid[i][j] == 1) {\n                cur[j] = 0;\n            } else if (i == 0 && j == 0) {\n                cur[j] = 1;\n            } else {\n                int up = (i > 0) ? prev[j] : 0;\n                int left = (j > 0) ? cur[j - 1] : 0;\n                cur[j] = up + left;\n            }\n        }\n        prev = cur;\n    }\n    return prev[n - 1];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Checking `i == 0 && j == 0` Before Checking `obstacleGrid[i][j] == 1`:** If `obstacleGrid[0][0] == 1` (the starting cell is blocked), checking `i == 0 && j == 0` first falsely sets `cur[0] = 1` instead of `0`! Always check for an obstacle first.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
4216	Implicit State-Space BFS: Word Ladder I (Shortest Transformation Sequence)	dsa-word-ladder-i-shortest-paths	Find shortest transformation sequence length from startWord to targetWord using level-order BFS and set pruning.	2026-10-03 11:01:59.910929+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Implicit State-Space BFS: Word Ladder I (Shortest Transformation Sequence)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tRPda0rcf8E", "title": "Lecture 154: Word Ladder - I | Shortest Paths", "duration": "22m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/word-ladder/", "title": "Word Ladder (LC 127)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Return the number of words in the shortest transformation sequence from beginWord to endWord."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-genetic-mutation/", "title": "Minimum Genetic Mutation (LC 433)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the minimum number of single-character gene mutations needed to reach endGene using BFS."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/open-the-lock/", "title": "Open the Lock (LC 752)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Model 4-wheel lock combinations as an implicit unit-weight graph and find the shortest path via BFS."}]	Module 24: Shortest Path Algorithms in Graphs: DAG Relaxation, BFS & Dijkstra's Algorithm	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a `startWord`, a `targetWord`, and a dictionary `wordList`, find the **length of the shortest transformation sequence** from `startWord` to `targetWord` such that:\n1. Only **one letter** can be changed at a time.\n2. Every transformed intermediate word must exist in `wordList`.\nReturn `0` if no such sequence exists.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Modeling Word Transitions as an Implicit Unit-Weight Graph:**\n  - Treat each word as a node. An edge of weight `1` connects two words if they differ by exactly 1 character.\n  - Because we want the **shortest** sequence in an unweighted graph, we use **Level-Order BFS**!\n- **Fast Neighbor Discovery via `unordered_set` (`26 * wordLength` Check):**\n  - Insert all words from `wordList` into an `unordered_set<string> st` for `O(1)` lookup and deletion.\n  - Push `{startWord, 1}` into `queue<pair<string, int>> q` and erase `startWord` from `st` (erasing acts as marking visited!).\n  - When popping `{word, steps}`:\n    - For each character index `i` from `0` to `word.size() - 1`, try replacing `word[i]` with `'a'` through `'z'`.\n    - If the mutated `word` exists in `st`, erase it from `st` and push `{word, steps + 1}` into `q`.\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * wordLength * 26)` Time and `O(N * wordLength)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Word Ladder I Blueprint\n```cpp\nint wordLadderLength(string startWord, string targetWord, vector<string> &wordList) {\n    unordered_set<string> st(wordList.begin(), wordList.end());\n    if (st.find(targetWord) == st.end()) return 0;\n\n    queue<pair<string, int>> q;\n    q.push({startWord, 1});\n    st.erase(startWord); // Mark startWord as visited\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        auto [word, steps] = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n\n        if (word == targetWord) return steps;\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < word.size(); i++) {\n            char originalChar = word[i];\n            for (char ch = 'a'; ch <= 'z'; ch++) {\n                word[i] = ch;\n                if (st.find(word) != st.end()) {\n                    st.erase(word); // Erase immediately to prevent duplicate queue pushes\n                    q.push({word, steps + 1});\n                }\n            }\n            word[i] = originalChar; // Restore character before moving to next index\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting to Restore `word[i] = originalChar`:** After testing `'a'` through `'z'` at index `i`, you must restore `word[i]` back to `originalChar` before moving to index `i + 1`!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22134	Lecture 16: Building 10 Interactive DOM & State Projects — Quotes, Timers, Calculators, To-Do CRUD & Dynamic Quiz Engine	js-lecture-16-10-dom-projects-forms-timers-todo-crud-quiz-engine	Synthesize DOM manipulation, Event Delegation, FormData extraction, setInterval/setTimeout timers, and dynamic state rendering across 10 real-world interactive frontend applications.	2026-10-06 21:36:53.504856+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThis hands-on engineering session combines **DOM Selection**, **Event Listeners**, **Form Handling (`FormData`)**, **Timers (`setInterval` / `setTimeout`)**, and **Data Structures** to build 10 complete interactive mini-applications from scratch.\n\n### 💡 Core Architectural Patterns Across the 10 Projects\n1. **Random Quote Generator & Color Switcher:** Uses bounded random index generation (`Math.floor(Math.random() * quotes.length)`) and **Event Delegation** on a parent button container.\n2. **Love Calculator & Income Tax Calculator:** Uses `form.addEventListener("submit", (e) => { e.preventDefault(); ... })` to prevent page reloads, extracts numeric inputs with `Number(input.value)`, and applies multi-slab tax bracket logic.\n3. **Digital Clock & Olympic Countdown Timer:** Uses **`setInterval(callback, 1000)`** with `new Date().toLocaleTimeString()` and epoch millisecond math (`targetMs - Date.now()`) to update live UI counters every second.\n4. **Clicker Particle Animation:** Reads click coordinates via **`event.clientX`** and **`event.clientY`**, dynamically creates a positioned particle `<div>`, and cleans it up from the DOM after animation using `setTimeout(() => el.remove(), 5000)` to prevent memory leaks.\n5. **To-Do List CRUD App:** Dynamically creates task rows with Delete and Complete actions.\n6. **Dynamic Quiz Engine with `FormData`:** Randomly samples K questions out of a 100-question pool without duplicates, renders radio inputs dynamically, and grades all answers in one shot using **`new FormData(quizForm)`**.\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: `FormData` Quiz Grading & Particle Cleanup\n```javascript\n// 1. Grading a Multi-Question Quiz Form Cleanly Using the FormData API\nconst quizForm = document.getElementById("quiz-form");\nconst answerKey = { q1: "B", q2: "A", q3: "D", q4: "C", q5: "A" };\n\nquizForm.addEventListener("submit", (e) => {\n  e.preventDefault(); // Prevent browser form reload!\n  const formData = new FormData(quizForm);\n  let score = 0;\n\n  for (let [questionName, userAnswer of formData.entries()) {\n    if (answerKey[questionName === userAnswer) {\n      score++;\n    }\n  }\n  document.getElementById("result").textContent = `Score: ${score} / 5`;\n});\n\n// 2. Sampling N Unique Random Questions from a Question Bank\nfunction pickRandomQuestions(pool, count) {\n  const copy = [...pool;\n  const selected = [;\n  for (let i = 0; i < count && copy.length > 0; i++) {\n    const randIdx = Math.floor(Math.random() * copy.length);\n    selected.push(copy[randIdx);\n    // Swap-and-pop in O(1) to avoid duplicate selection:\n    copy[randIdx = copy[copy.length - 1;\n    copy.pop();\n  }\n  return selected;\n}\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/FormData", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/FormData", "title": "MDN Web Docs — FormData API Reference"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dQDteO0RXrc", "title": "10 JavaScript Projects for Beginners | Learn JS by Building Real Apps | Javascript Full Course #16", "duration": "113m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-16-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Why must you call event.preventDefault() inside a <form> submit event listener in Single-Page Applications?", "title": "Q1: Why must you call event.preventDefault() inside a <form> submit event listener in Single-Page Applications?", "answer": "By default, submitting an HTML <form> causes the browser to serialize the inputs and perform a full-page HTTP navigation/reload, which wipes out all in-memory JavaScript state! Calling event.preventDefault() suppresses the native page reload so JavaScript can validate inputs, compute results, or send an asynchronous fetch() request.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "By default, submitting an HTML <form> causes the browser to serialize the inputs and perform a full-page HTTP navigation/reload, which wipes out all in-memory JavaScript state! Calling event.preventDefault() suppresses the native page reload so JavaScript can validate inputs, compute results, or send an asynchronous fetch() request."}, {"id": "js-lec-16-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: How does the FormData API simplify reading forms with 20+ inputs compared to selecting every input with getElementById()?", "title": "Q2: How does the FormData API simplify reading forms with 20+ inputs compared to selecting every input with getElementById()?", "answer": "Instead of writing 20 separate document.getElementById() queries, passing the <form> element into new FormData(formElement) automatically collects the name and current value of every enabled input, select, textarea, and checked radio/checkbox inside the form. You can then iterate over formData.entries() or convert it into a plain object in one line via Object.fromEntries(new FormData(formElement)).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Instead of writing 20 separate document.getElementById() queries, passing the <form> element into new FormData(formElement) automatically collects the name and current value of every enabled input, select, textarea, and checked radio/checkbox inside the form. You can then iterate over formData.entries() or convert it into a plain object in one line via Object.fromEntries(new FormData(formElement))."}, {"id": "js-lec-16-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: How do you randomly select 10 unique questions from an array of 100 questions in O(K) time without picking duplicates?", "title": "Q3: How do you randomly select 10 unique questions from an array of 100 questions in O(K) time without picking duplicates?", "answer": "Using a partial Fisher-Yates shuffle (or Swap-and-Pop): pick a random index randIdx between 0 and currentLength - 1, push arr[randIdx] to your result, overwrite arr[randIdx] with the last element arr[currentLength - 1], and decrement currentLength (or pop()). This guarantees zero duplicates in O(K) time without O(N) array splicing.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Using a partial Fisher-Yates shuffle (or Swap-and-Pop): pick a random index randIdx between 0 and currentLength - 1, push arr[randIdx] to your result, overwrite arr[randIdx] with the last element arr[currentLength - 1], and decrement currentLength (or pop()). This guarantees zero duplicates in O(K) time without O(N) array splicing."}, {"id": "js-lec-16-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: In the Clicker Particle project, why is it critical to call circleElement.remove() inside setTimeout after the CSS animation ends?", "title": "Q4: In the Clicker Particle project, why is it critical to call circleElement.remove() inside setTimeout after the CSS animation ends?", "answer": "Even if a CSS keyframe animation fades an element's opacity to 0, the DOM node itself remains mounted in the document tree consuming RAM and layout calculation overhead. If a user clicks 1,000 times without removing finished nodes, 1,000 invisible <div> elements accumulate in the DOM (a classic DOM memory leak). Removing the node via setTimeout(() => el.remove(), duration) frees the memory.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Even if a CSS keyframe animation fades an element's opacity to 0, the DOM node itself remains mounted in the document tree consuming RAM and layout calculation overhead. If a user clicks 1,000 times without removing finished nodes, 1,000 invisible <div> elements accumulate in the DOM (a classic DOM memory leak). Removing the node via setTimeout(() => el.remove(), duration) frees the memory."}, {"id": "js-lec-16-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Why should a Countdown Timer recalculate targetTime - Date.now() on every tick instead of simply decrementing secondsLeft-- inside setInterval(fn, 1000)?", "title": "Q5: Why should a Countdown Timer recalculate targetTime - Date.now() on every tick instead of simply decrementing secondsLeft-- inside setInterval(fn, 1000)?", "answer": "setInterval(fn, 1000) does NOT guarantee exact 1000ms execution—if the main thread is busy or the browser tab is backgrounded/throttled, ticks can be delayed to 1050ms or paused entirely! Decrementing secondsLeft-- causes cumulative timer drift. Recomputing targetTimestamp - Date.now() on each tick compares against the real system clock so the timer stays 100% accurate even if the tab was minimized.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "setInterval(fn, 1000) does NOT guarantee exact 1000ms execution—if the main thread is busy or the browser tab is backgrounded/throttled, ticks can be delayed to 1050ms or paused entirely! Decrementing secondsLeft-- causes cumulative timer drift. Recomputing targetTimestamp - Date.now() on each tick compares against the real system clock so the timer stays 100% accurate even if the tab was minimized."}, {"id": "js-lec-16-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why does reading inputElement.value always return a String even when the HTML input has type=\\"number\\"?", "title": "Q6: Why does reading inputElement.value always return a String even when the HTML input has type=\\"number\\"?", "answer": "In the DOM specification, HTMLInputElement.value is always typed as a DOMString. If a user types 50 into <input type=\\"number\\">, input.value is \\"50\\", so input1.value + input2.value performs string concatenation (\\"50\\" + \\"20\\" = \\"5020\\"). Always wrap numeric inputs with Number(input.value) or input.valueAsNumber.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In the DOM specification, HTMLInputElement.value is always typed as a DOMString. If a user types 50 into <input type=\\"number\\">, input.value is \\"50\\", so input1.value + input2.value performs string concatenation (\\"50\\" + \\"20\\" = \\"5020\\"). Always wrap numeric inputs with Number(input.value) or input.valueAsNumber."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThis hands-on engineering session combines **DOM Selection**, **Event Listeners**, **Form Handling (`FormData`)**, **Timers (`setInterval` / `setTimeout`)**, and **Data Structures** to build 10 complete interactive mini-applications from scratch.\n\n### 💡 Core Architectural Patterns Across the 10 Projects\n1. **Random Quote Generator & Color Switcher:** Uses bounded random index generation (`Math.floor(Math.random() * quotes.length)`) and **Event Delegation** on a parent button container.\n2. **Love Calculator & Income Tax Calculator:** Uses `form.addEventListener("submit", (e) => { e.preventDefault(); ... })` to prevent page reloads, extracts numeric inputs with `Number(input.value)`, and applies multi-slab tax bracket logic.\n3. **Digital Clock & Olympic Countdown Timer:** Uses **`setInterval(callback, 1000)`** with `new Date().toLocaleTimeString()` and epoch millisecond math (`targetMs - Date.now()`) to update live UI counters every second.\n4. **Clicker Particle Animation:** Reads click coordinates via **`event.clientX`** and **`event.clientY`**, dynamically creates a positioned particle `<div>`, and cleans it up from the DOM after animation using `setTimeout(() => el.remove(), 5000)` to prevent memory leaks.\n5. **To-Do List CRUD App:** Dynamically creates task rows with Delete and Complete actions.\n6. **Dynamic Quiz Engine with `FormData`:** Randomly samples K questions out of a 100-question pool without duplicates, renders radio inputs dynamically, and grades all answers in one shot using **`new FormData(quizForm)`**.\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: `FormData` Quiz Grading & Particle Cleanup\n```javascript\n// 1. Grading a Multi-Question Quiz Form Cleanly Using the FormData API\nconst quizForm = document.getElementById("quiz-form");\nconst answerKey = { q1: "B", q2: "A", q3: "D", q4: "C", q5: "A" };\n\nquizForm.addEventListener("submit", (e) => {\n  e.preventDefault(); // Prevent browser form reload!\n  const formData = new FormData(quizForm);\n  let score = 0;\n\n  for (let [questionName, userAnswer of formData.entries()) {\n    if (answerKey[questionName === userAnswer) {\n      score++;\n    }\n  }\n  document.getElementById("result").textContent = `Score: ${score} / 5`;\n});\n\n// 2. Sampling N Unique Random Questions from a Question Bank\nfunction pickRandomQuestions(pool, count) {\n  const copy = [...pool;\n  const selected = [;\n  for (let i = 0; i < count && copy.length > 0; i++) {\n    const randIdx = Math.floor(Math.random() * copy.length);\n    selected.push(copy[randIdx);\n    // Swap-and-pop in O(1) to avoid duplicate selection:\n    copy[randIdx = copy[copy.length - 1;\n    copy.pop();\n  }\n  return selected;\n}\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 16: Building 10 Interactive DOM & State Projects — Quotes, Timers, Calculators, To-Do CRUD & Dynamic Quiz Engine	Synthesize DOM manipulation, Event Delegation, FormData extraction, setInterval/setTimeout timers, and dynamic state rendering across 10 real-world interactive frontend applications.	20	Easy	\N	16	[{"id": "js-lec-16-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Why must you call event.preventDefault() inside a <form> submit event listener in Single-Page Applications?", "title": "Q1: Why must you call event.preventDefault() inside a <form> submit event listener in Single-Page Applications?", "answer": "By default, submitting an HTML <form> causes the browser to serialize the inputs and perform a full-page HTTP navigation/reload, which wipes out all in-memory JavaScript state! Calling event.preventDefault() suppresses the native page reload so JavaScript can validate inputs, compute results, or send an asynchronous fetch() request.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "By default, submitting an HTML <form> causes the browser to serialize the inputs and perform a full-page HTTP navigation/reload, which wipes out all in-memory JavaScript state! Calling event.preventDefault() suppresses the native page reload so JavaScript can validate inputs, compute results, or send an asynchronous fetch() request."}, {"id": "js-lec-16-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: How does the FormData API simplify reading forms with 20+ inputs compared to selecting every input with getElementById()?", "title": "Q2: How does the FormData API simplify reading forms with 20+ inputs compared to selecting every input with getElementById()?", "answer": "Instead of writing 20 separate document.getElementById() queries, passing the <form> element into new FormData(formElement) automatically collects the name and current value of every enabled input, select, textarea, and checked radio/checkbox inside the form. You can then iterate over formData.entries() or convert it into a plain object in one line via Object.fromEntries(new FormData(formElement)).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Instead of writing 20 separate document.getElementById() queries, passing the <form> element into new FormData(formElement) automatically collects the name and current value of every enabled input, select, textarea, and checked radio/checkbox inside the form. You can then iterate over formData.entries() or convert it into a plain object in one line via Object.fromEntries(new FormData(formElement))."}, {"id": "js-lec-16-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: How do you randomly select 10 unique questions from an array of 100 questions in O(K) time without picking duplicates?", "title": "Q3: How do you randomly select 10 unique questions from an array of 100 questions in O(K) time without picking duplicates?", "answer": "Using a partial Fisher-Yates shuffle (or Swap-and-Pop): pick a random index randIdx between 0 and currentLength - 1, push arr[randIdx] to your result, overwrite arr[randIdx] with the last element arr[currentLength - 1], and decrement currentLength (or pop()). This guarantees zero duplicates in O(K) time without O(N) array splicing.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Using a partial Fisher-Yates shuffle (or Swap-and-Pop): pick a random index randIdx between 0 and currentLength - 1, push arr[randIdx] to your result, overwrite arr[randIdx] with the last element arr[currentLength - 1], and decrement currentLength (or pop()). This guarantees zero duplicates in O(K) time without O(N) array splicing."}, {"id": "js-lec-16-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: In the Clicker Particle project, why is it critical to call circleElement.remove() inside setTimeout after the CSS animation ends?", "title": "Q4: In the Clicker Particle project, why is it critical to call circleElement.remove() inside setTimeout after the CSS animation ends?", "answer": "Even if a CSS keyframe animation fades an element's opacity to 0, the DOM node itself remains mounted in the document tree consuming RAM and layout calculation overhead. If a user clicks 1,000 times without removing finished nodes, 1,000 invisible <div> elements accumulate in the DOM (a classic DOM memory leak). Removing the node via setTimeout(() => el.remove(), duration) frees the memory.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Even if a CSS keyframe animation fades an element's opacity to 0, the DOM node itself remains mounted in the document tree consuming RAM and layout calculation overhead. If a user clicks 1,000 times without removing finished nodes, 1,000 invisible <div> elements accumulate in the DOM (a classic DOM memory leak). Removing the node via setTimeout(() => el.remove(), duration) frees the memory."}, {"id": "js-lec-16-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Why should a Countdown Timer recalculate targetTime - Date.now() on every tick instead of simply decrementing secondsLeft-- inside setInterval(fn, 1000)?", "title": "Q5: Why should a Countdown Timer recalculate targetTime - Date.now() on every tick instead of simply decrementing secondsLeft-- inside setInterval(fn, 1000)?", "answer": "setInterval(fn, 1000) does NOT guarantee exact 1000ms execution—if the main thread is busy or the browser tab is backgrounded/throttled, ticks can be delayed to 1050ms or paused entirely! Decrementing secondsLeft-- causes cumulative timer drift. Recomputing targetTimestamp - Date.now() on each tick compares against the real system clock so the timer stays 100% accurate even if the tab was minimized.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "setInterval(fn, 1000) does NOT guarantee exact 1000ms execution—if the main thread is busy or the browser tab is backgrounded/throttled, ticks can be delayed to 1050ms or paused entirely! Decrementing secondsLeft-- causes cumulative timer drift. Recomputing targetTimestamp - Date.now() on each tick compares against the real system clock so the timer stays 100% accurate even if the tab was minimized."}, {"id": "js-lec-16-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why does reading inputElement.value always return a String even when the HTML input has type=\\"number\\"?", "title": "Q6: Why does reading inputElement.value always return a String even when the HTML input has type=\\"number\\"?", "answer": "In the DOM specification, HTMLInputElement.value is always typed as a DOMString. If a user types 50 into <input type=\\"number\\">, input.value is \\"50\\", so input1.value + input2.value performs string concatenation (\\"50\\" + \\"20\\" = \\"5020\\"). Always wrap numeric inputs with Number(input.value) or input.valueAsNumber.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "16-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In the DOM specification, HTMLInputElement.value is always typed as a DOMString. If a user types 50 into <input type=\\"number\\">, input.value is \\"50\\", so input1.value + input2.value performs string concatenation (\\"50\\" + \\"20\\" = \\"5020\\"). Always wrap numeric inputs with Number(input.value) or input.valueAsNumber."}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/FormData	\N	\N	\N
22149	Lecture 9: Introduction to Express.js — Why Express?, Middleware Pipeline (app.use, req, res, next), Execution Order, res.send() & Route Matching	node-lecture-9-introduction-to-expressjs-middleware-routing-res-send	Transition from verbose raw Node.js http code to Express.js, master the Middleware pipeline (app.use, req, res, next), understand path matching differences between app.use() and app.get()/app.post(), and send responses with res.send().	2026-10-06 21:56:18.722653+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWriting a large production web application using only Node's raw `http.createServer` quickly becomes painful: you have to manually concatenate stream chunks, set headers, and write giant `if/else` chains for `req.url` and `req.method`. **Express.js** is a minimal, fast, unopinionated web framework built on top of Node.js that organizes request processing into a clean **Middleware Pipeline** and **Routing table**.\n\n### 💡 What Is Middleware in Express.js? (`(req, res, next) => { ... }`)\n* Instead of one monolithic request handler function, Express processes an incoming request through a **chain of Middleware functions** executed in the exact top-to-bottom order they are defined in code.\n* Every middleware function receives 3 arguments: **`(req, res, next)`**:\n  * It can inspect or modify `req` and `res` (e.g., logging, authentication, parsing `req.body`).\n  * It must either **end the request-response cycle** (via **`res.send()`**) OR call **`next()`** to pass control to the next middleware in the chain!\n  * **Warning:** If a middleware neither calls `next()` nor sends a response, the client browser hangs forever! And once `res.send()` is called, you must **not** call `next()` if downstream middleware also tries to call `res.send()`.\n\n### 💡 Crucial Distinction: `app.use(path, ...)` vs. `app.get(path, ...)` / `app.post(path, ...)`\n* **`app.use("/", callback)` (Prefix & All-Method Matching):**\n  * Matches **ALL HTTP methods** (`GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`).\n  * Matches **prefix paths**: `app.use("/", ...)` matches `"/"`, `"/contact-us"`, `"/products/123"`—every single URL starts with `"/"`!\n* **`app.get("/", callback)` & `app.post("/contact-us", callback)` (Exact Path & Exact Method Matching):**\n  * Matches **only the specified HTTP verb** (`GET` or `POST`) AND matches the **exact URL path** (`app.get("/", ...)` only matches `"/"`, NOT `"/contact-us"`).\n* **How `res.send()` Simplifies Responses:** Unlike `res.write()` + `res.end()`, **`res.send("<h1>Hello</h1>")`** automatically sets the `Content-Type` header (`text/html` for strings, `application/json` for objects/arrays), sets `Content-Length`, and ends the response in a single call!\n\n### 🛠️ Express.js Server, Logging Middleware & Route Handling\n```javascript\nconst express = require("express");\nconst app = express();\n\n// 1. Global Logging Middleware (calls next() so request continues downstream)\napp.use((req, res, next) => {\n  console.log("1st Dummy Middleware:", req.url, req.method);\n  next();\n});\n\n// 2. Exact GET Route for Home Page '/'\napp.get("/", (req, res, next) => {\n  res.send("<h1>Welcome to Complete Coding</h1>");\n});\n\n// 3. GET & POST Routes for '/contact-us'\napp.get("/contact-us", (req, res, next) => {\n  res.send(`\n    <h1>Please give your details here</h1>\n    <form action="/contact-us" method="POST">\n      <input type="text" name="name" placeholder="Enter your name" />\n      <input type="email" name="email" placeholder="Enter your email" />\n      <input type="submit" />\n    </form>\n  `);\n});\n\napp.post("/contact-us", (req, res, next) => {\n  res.send("<h1>We will contact you shortly!</h1>");\n});\n\n// app.listen() internally calls http.createServer(app) and binds to port 3002\napp.listen(3002, () => {\n  console.log("Server running on address http://localhost:3002");\n});\n```	[{"url": "https://expressjs.com/en/guide/using-middleware.html", "link": "https://expressjs.com/en/guide/using-middleware.html", "title": "Express.js Official Docs — Using Middleware"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=J-QgmSzyA_A", "title": "Lecture 9: Introduction to express.js | NodeJS Complete Course in Hindi", "duration": "59m", "creator_name": "Complete Coding by Prashant Sir", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir"}]	[{"id": "node-lec-9-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What is Middleware in Express.js, what are its 3 parameters, and what happens if you forget to call next() or res.send()?", "title": "Q1: What is Middleware in Express.js, what are its 3 parameters, and what happens if you forget to call next() or res.send()?", "answer": "Middleware in Express.js is a function that has access to the request object (req), the response object (res), and the next middleware function (next) in the application's request-response pipeline. Middleware can execute any code, mutate req/res (like attaching req.body or req.user), end the request-response cycle (via res.send()), or pass control to the next middleware via next(). If a middleware neither ends the response nor calls next(), the request hangs indefinitely until the client browser times out.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "9-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Middleware in Express.js is a function that has access to the request object (req), the response object (res), and the next middleware function (next) in the application's request-response pipeline. Middleware can execute any code, mutate req/res (like attaching req.body or req.user), end the request-response cycle (via res.send()), or pass control to the next middleware via next(). If a middleware neither ends the response nor calls next(), the request hangs indefinitely until the client browser times out."}, {"id": "node-lec-9-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why does placing app.use(\\"/\\", (req, res) => res.send(\\"Home\\")) at the top of your file block \\"/contact-us\\" from ever being reached, whereas app.get(\\"/\\", ...) does not?", "title": "Q2: Why does placing app.use(\\"/\\", (req, res) => res.send(\\"Home\\")) at the top of your file block \\"/contact-us\\" from ever being reached, whereas app.get(\\"/\\", ...) does not?", "answer": "Because app.use(path, fn) performs PREFIX matching across ALL HTTP methods! Since every path (including \\"/contact-us\\" and \\"/users\\") starts with the prefix \\"/\\", app.use(\\"/\\", ...) intercepts every incoming request and sends the \\"Home\\" response immediately without calling next(). In contrast, app.get(\\"/\\", fn) performs EXACT path matching (and only for GET requests), so GET \\"/contact-us\\" skips past app.get(\\"/\\", ...) and reaches app.get(\\"/contact-us\\", ...).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "9-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Because app.use(path, fn) performs PREFIX matching across ALL HTTP methods! Since every path (including \\"/contact-us\\" and \\"/users\\") starts with the prefix \\"/\\", app.use(\\"/\\", ...) intercepts every incoming request and sends the \\"Home\\" response immediately without calling next(). In contrast, app.get(\\"/\\", fn) performs EXACT path matching (and only for GET requests), so GET \\"/contact-us\\" skips past app.get(\\"/\\", ...) and reaches app.get(\\"/contact-us\\", ...)."}, {"id": "node-lec-9-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What advantages does Express's res.send() provide over raw Node.js res.setHeader(), res.write(), and res.end()?", "title": "Q3: What advantages does Express's res.send() provide over raw Node.js res.setHeader(), res.write(), and res.end()?", "answer": "res.send(body) inspects the data type of body and automatically sets the correct Content-Type header (text/html for strings, application/json for objects/arrays, application/octet-stream for Buffers), automatically calculates and sets the Content-Length header, serializes objects to JSON if needed, writes the payload, and closes the response stream—all in a single method call.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "9-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "res.send(body) inspects the data type of body and automatically sets the correct Content-Type header (text/html for strings, application/json for objects/arrays, application/octet-stream for Buffers), automatically calculates and sets the Content-Length header, serializes objects to JSON if needed, writes the payload, and closes the response stream—all in a single method call."}, {"id": "node-lec-9-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why is the top-to-bottom order of middleware and route declarations critical in Express.js?", "title": "Q4: Why is the top-to-bottom order of middleware and route declarations critical in Express.js?", "answer": "Express evaluates middleware and routes strictly in the sequential order they are registered in your code. When a request arrives, Express runs each matching middleware from top to bottom until one sends a response and stops calling next(). Therefore, logging and body-parsing middleware must be placed at the top before route handlers, and 404 catch-all middleware must be placed at the very bottom.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "9-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Express evaluates middleware and routes strictly in the sequential order they are registered in your code. When a request arrives, Express runs each matching middleware from top to bottom until one sends a response and stops calling next(). Therefore, logging and body-parsing middleware must be placed at the top before route handlers, and 404 catch-all middleware must be placed at the very bottom."}, {"id": "node-lec-9-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: How does app.listen(PORT) work under the hood in Express? Why don't we need to require(\\"http\\") anymore?", "title": "Q5: How does app.listen(PORT) work under the hood in Express? Why don't we need to require(\\"http\\") anymore?", "answer": "In Express, the app object returned by express() is actually a valid Node.js (req, res) request handler function! When you call app.listen(PORT, callback), Express internally imports Node's core http module and executes return http.createServer(this).listen(...).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "9-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "In Express, the app object returned by express() is actually a valid Node.js (req, res) request handler function! When you call app.listen(PORT, callback), Express internally imports Node's core http module and executes return http.createServer(this).listen(...)."}, {"id": "node-lec-9-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Can you call res.send() twice inside the same route handler or across two middlewares chained with next()?", "title": "Q6: Can you call res.send() twice inside the same route handler or across two middlewares chained with next()?", "answer": "No! Because res.send() flushes the HTTP headers and closes the response stream (internally calling res.end()), calling res.send() a second time on the same request throws Error [ERR_HTTP_HEADERS_SENT]: Cannot set headers after they are sent to the client.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "9-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "No! Because res.send() flushes the HTTP headers and closes the response stream (internally calling res.end()), calling res.send() a second time on the same request throws Error [ERR_HTTP_HEADERS_SENT]: Cannot set headers after they are sent to the client."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWriting a large production web application using only Node's raw `http.createServer` quickly becomes painful: you have to manually concatenate stream chunks, set headers, and write giant `if/else` chains for `req.url` and `req.method`. **Express.js** is a minimal, fast, unopinionated web framework built on top of Node.js that organizes request processing into a clean **Middleware Pipeline** and **Routing table**.\n\n### 💡 What Is Middleware in Express.js? (`(req, res, next) => { ... }`)\n* Instead of one monolithic request handler function, Express processes an incoming request through a **chain of Middleware functions** executed in the exact top-to-bottom order they are defined in code.\n* Every middleware function receives 3 arguments: **`(req, res, next)`**:\n  * It can inspect or modify `req` and `res` (e.g., logging, authentication, parsing `req.body`).\n  * It must either **end the request-response cycle** (via **`res.send()`**) OR call **`next()`** to pass control to the next middleware in the chain!\n  * **Warning:** If a middleware neither calls `next()` nor sends a response, the client browser hangs forever! And once `res.send()` is called, you must **not** call `next()` if downstream middleware also tries to call `res.send()`.\n\n### 💡 Crucial Distinction: `app.use(path, ...)` vs. `app.get(path, ...)` / `app.post(path, ...)`\n* **`app.use("/", callback)` (Prefix & All-Method Matching):**\n  * Matches **ALL HTTP methods** (`GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`).\n  * Matches **prefix paths**: `app.use("/", ...)` matches `"/"`, `"/contact-us"`, `"/products/123"`—every single URL starts with `"/"`!\n* **`app.get("/", callback)` & `app.post("/contact-us", callback)` (Exact Path & Exact Method Matching):**\n  * Matches **only the specified HTTP verb** (`GET` or `POST`) AND matches the **exact URL path** (`app.get("/", ...)` only matches `"/"`, NOT `"/contact-us"`).\n* **How `res.send()` Simplifies Responses:** Unlike `res.write()` + `res.end()`, **`res.send("<h1>Hello</h1>")`** automatically sets the `Content-Type` header (`text/html` for strings, `application/json` for objects/arrays), sets `Content-Length`, and ends the response in a single call!\n\n### 🛠️ Express.js Server, Logging Middleware & Route Handling\n```javascript\nconst express = require("express");\nconst app = express();\n\n// 1. Global Logging Middleware (calls next() so request continues downstream)\napp.use((req, res, next) => {\n  console.log("1st Dummy Middleware:", req.url, req.method);\n  next();\n});\n\n// 2. Exact GET Route for Home Page '/'\napp.get("/", (req, res, next) => {\n  res.send("<h1>Welcome to Complete Coding</h1>");\n});\n\n// 3. GET & POST Routes for '/contact-us'\napp.get("/contact-us", (req, res, next) => {\n  res.send(`\n    <h1>Please give your details here</h1>\n    <form action="/contact-us" method="POST">\n      <input type="text" name="name" placeholder="Enter your name" />\n      <input type="email" name="email" placeholder="Enter your email" />\n      <input type="submit" />\n    </form>\n  `);\n});\n\napp.post("/contact-us", (req, res, next) => {\n  res.send("<h1>We will contact you shortly!</h1>");\n});\n\n// app.listen() internally calls http.createServer(app) and binds to port 3002\napp.listen(3002, () => {\n  console.log("Server running on address http://localhost:3002");\n});\n```	Complete Coding by Prashant Sir	https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir	24	Lecture 9: Introduction to Express.js — Why Express?, Middleware Pipeline (app.use, req, res, next), Execution Order, res.send() & Route Matching	Transition from verbose raw Node.js http code to Express.js, master the Middleware pipeline (app.use, req, res, next), understand path matching differences between app.use() and app.get()/app.post(), and send responses with res.send().	20	Easy	\N	9	[{"id": "node-lec-9-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What is Middleware in Express.js, what are its 3 parameters, and what happens if you forget to call next() or res.send()?", "title": "Q1: What is Middleware in Express.js, what are its 3 parameters, and what happens if you forget to call next() or res.send()?", "answer": "Middleware in Express.js is a function that has access to the request object (req), the response object (res), and the next middleware function (next) in the application's request-response pipeline. Middleware can execute any code, mutate req/res (like attaching req.body or req.user), end the request-response cycle (via res.send()), or pass control to the next middleware via next(). If a middleware neither ends the response nor calls next(), the request hangs indefinitely until the client browser times out.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "9-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Middleware in Express.js is a function that has access to the request object (req), the response object (res), and the next middleware function (next) in the application's request-response pipeline. Middleware can execute any code, mutate req/res (like attaching req.body or req.user), end the request-response cycle (via res.send()), or pass control to the next middleware via next(). If a middleware neither ends the response nor calls next(), the request hangs indefinitely until the client browser times out."}, {"id": "node-lec-9-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why does placing app.use(\\"/\\", (req, res) => res.send(\\"Home\\")) at the top of your file block \\"/contact-us\\" from ever being reached, whereas app.get(\\"/\\", ...) does not?", "title": "Q2: Why does placing app.use(\\"/\\", (req, res) => res.send(\\"Home\\")) at the top of your file block \\"/contact-us\\" from ever being reached, whereas app.get(\\"/\\", ...) does not?", "answer": "Because app.use(path, fn) performs PREFIX matching across ALL HTTP methods! Since every path (including \\"/contact-us\\" and \\"/users\\") starts with the prefix \\"/\\", app.use(\\"/\\", ...) intercepts every incoming request and sends the \\"Home\\" response immediately without calling next(). In contrast, app.get(\\"/\\", fn) performs EXACT path matching (and only for GET requests), so GET \\"/contact-us\\" skips past app.get(\\"/\\", ...) and reaches app.get(\\"/contact-us\\", ...).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "9-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Because app.use(path, fn) performs PREFIX matching across ALL HTTP methods! Since every path (including \\"/contact-us\\" and \\"/users\\") starts with the prefix \\"/\\", app.use(\\"/\\", ...) intercepts every incoming request and sends the \\"Home\\" response immediately without calling next(). In contrast, app.get(\\"/\\", fn) performs EXACT path matching (and only for GET requests), so GET \\"/contact-us\\" skips past app.get(\\"/\\", ...) and reaches app.get(\\"/contact-us\\", ...)."}, {"id": "node-lec-9-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What advantages does Express's res.send() provide over raw Node.js res.setHeader(), res.write(), and res.end()?", "title": "Q3: What advantages does Express's res.send() provide over raw Node.js res.setHeader(), res.write(), and res.end()?", "answer": "res.send(body) inspects the data type of body and automatically sets the correct Content-Type header (text/html for strings, application/json for objects/arrays, application/octet-stream for Buffers), automatically calculates and sets the Content-Length header, serializes objects to JSON if needed, writes the payload, and closes the response stream—all in a single method call.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "9-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "res.send(body) inspects the data type of body and automatically sets the correct Content-Type header (text/html for strings, application/json for objects/arrays, application/octet-stream for Buffers), automatically calculates and sets the Content-Length header, serializes objects to JSON if needed, writes the payload, and closes the response stream—all in a single method call."}, {"id": "node-lec-9-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why is the top-to-bottom order of middleware and route declarations critical in Express.js?", "title": "Q4: Why is the top-to-bottom order of middleware and route declarations critical in Express.js?", "answer": "Express evaluates middleware and routes strictly in the sequential order they are registered in your code. When a request arrives, Express runs each matching middleware from top to bottom until one sends a response and stops calling next(). Therefore, logging and body-parsing middleware must be placed at the top before route handlers, and 404 catch-all middleware must be placed at the very bottom.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "9-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Express evaluates middleware and routes strictly in the sequential order they are registered in your code. When a request arrives, Express runs each matching middleware from top to bottom until one sends a response and stops calling next(). Therefore, logging and body-parsing middleware must be placed at the top before route handlers, and 404 catch-all middleware must be placed at the very bottom."}, {"id": "node-lec-9-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: How does app.listen(PORT) work under the hood in Express? Why don't we need to require(\\"http\\") anymore?", "title": "Q5: How does app.listen(PORT) work under the hood in Express? Why don't we need to require(\\"http\\") anymore?", "answer": "In Express, the app object returned by express() is actually a valid Node.js (req, res) request handler function! When you call app.listen(PORT, callback), Express internally imports Node's core http module and executes return http.createServer(this).listen(...).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "9-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "In Express, the app object returned by express() is actually a valid Node.js (req, res) request handler function! When you call app.listen(PORT, callback), Express internally imports Node's core http module and executes return http.createServer(this).listen(...)."}, {"id": "node-lec-9-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Can you call res.send() twice inside the same route handler or across two middlewares chained with next()?", "title": "Q6: Can you call res.send() twice inside the same route handler or across two middlewares chained with next()?", "answer": "No! Because res.send() flushes the HTTP headers and closes the response stream (internally calling res.end()), calling res.send() a second time on the same request throws Error [ERR_HTTP_HEADERS_SENT]: Cannot set headers after they are sent to the client.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "9-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "No! Because res.send() flushes the HTTP headers and closes the response stream (internally calling res.end()), calling res.send() a second time on the same request throws Error [ERR_HTTP_HEADERS_SENT]: Cannot set headers after they are sent to the client."}]	https://expressjs.com/en/guide/using-middleware.html	\N	\N	\N
22186	Problems on Numbers	aptitude-quant-problems-on-numbers	Solve equations involving digit reversals (two/three-digit numbers), consecutive integer math, reciprocal sums, and heads-and-legs puzzles.	2026-10-07 11:35:53.411147+05:30	### 📌 1. Place Value Representation of Digits\n* **Two-Digit Numbers:** Always represent a two-digit number as **`10x + y`** (where `x` is the tens digit and `y` is the units digit).\n  * Number obtained by **reversing** the digits: **`10y + x`**.\n  * Difference between original and reversed: `(10x + y) - (10y + x) = 9(x - y)`. Always a multiple of **9**.\n  * Sum of original and reversed: `(10x + y) + (10y + x) = 11(x + y)`. Always a multiple of **11**.\n* **Three-Digit Numbers:** Represent as **`100h + 10t + u`**.\n\n### 💡 2. Common Number Problem Archetypes\n* **Sum of Reciprocals:** If the sum of two numbers is `S` and their product is `P`, the sum of their reciprocals `1/x + 1/y = (x+y)/(xy) = S / P`.\n* **Heads and Legs Problems (e.g., Hens and Pigs):**\n  * Let `x` be 2-legged animals and `y` be 4-legged animals.\n  * `x + y = Total Heads`\n  * `2x + 4y = Total Legs`\n* **Consecutive Integers:**\n  * Consecutive integers: `x, x+1, x+2...`\n  * Consecutive even/odd integers: `x, x+2, x+4...`\n  * Average of an odd number of consecutive integers is always the **exact middle integer**.\n* **Mistaken Operations:** If a student was supposed to multiply by `X` but divided by `X` (which is multiplying by `1/X`), the error equation is `X*m - m/X = Difference`.	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vsBpWgNYjtQ", "type": "youtube", "title": "Problems on Numbers - Shortcuts & Tricks for Placement Tests, Job Interviews & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-num-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Sum of Reciprocals] The sum of two numbers is 36 and their product is 248. What will be the sum of their reciprocals?", "options": ["A) 7 / 62", "B) 9 / 62", "C) 11 / 62", "D) 13 / 62"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The sum of two positive numbers is exactly 36 and their product is exactly 248. What is the exact sum of their reciprocals?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the numbers be a and b. We want 1/a + 1/b. Finding a common denominator yields (a + b) / (ab). We are given a + b = 36 and ab = 248. Thus, the sum of reciprocals is 36 / 248. Dividing numerator and denominator by 4 gives 9 / 62.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-num-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Heads and Legs] In a poultry farm having hens and pigs, Rohan can see 84 heads and 282 legs. How many hens are there?", "options": ["A) 25", "B) 27", "C) 30", "D) 57"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a poultry farm consisting exclusively of hens and pigs, Rohan counts a total of 84 heads and 282 legs. How many hens are there in the farm?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let H = number of hens, P = number of pigs. Heads equation: H + P = 84. Legs equation: 2H + 4P = 282 (since hens have 2 legs and pigs have 4). Divide legs equation by 2: H + 2P = 141. Subtract heads equation from this: (H + 2P) - (H + P) = 141 - 84 => P = 57. Thus, there are 57 pigs. Hens H = 84 - 57 = 27 hens.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-num-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Consecutive Numbers Sum] The sum of five consecutive numbers is 335. If we add the largest and smallest numbers, what will we get?", "options": ["A) 132", "B) 134", "C) 136", "D) 138"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The sum of five consecutive integers is exactly 335. What is the sum of the largest and the smallest integers in this sequence?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "The average of an odd number of consecutive integers is always the middle term! Middle term = 335 / 5 = 67. The sequence is 65, 66, 67, 68, 69. Largest + Smallest = 65 + 69 = 134. (Shortcut: Sum of extremes = 2 × Middle term = 2 × 67 = 134).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-num-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Mistaken Operation Equation] Instead of multiplying m by 3/4, Raj divided by 3/4. Difference is 14. What is m?", "options": ["A) 18", "B) 21", "C) 24", "D) 28"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Raj was supposed to multiply a number m by 3/4. Instead, he mistakenly divided m by 3/4. If this error led to a difference of 14 between his incorrect answer and the correct answer, what is the value of m?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Incorrect result = m ÷ (3/4) = 4m/3. Correct result = 3m/4. Difference = 4m/3 - 3m/4 = (16m - 9m) / 12 = 7m / 12. We are given 7m / 12 = 14 => 7m = 168 => m = 24.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-num-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Three-Digit Number Position Shift] The digit in the units place is 2x 100s, 10s place is 3x 100s. Exchanging unit and tens makes difference 27. 75% of original?", "options": ["A) 279", "B) 285", "C) 297", "D) 315"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A 3-digit number has its units digit exactly 2 times the hundreds digit, and its tens digit exactly 3 times the hundreds digit. If the units and tens digits are exchanged, the absolute difference between the original number and the new number is 27. What is exactly 75% of the original number?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let the hundreds digit be h. Tens digit t = 3h. Units digit u = 2h. Original number A = 100h + 10t + u. New number B (exchanging tens and units) = 100h + 10u + t. Difference = |A - B| = |9t - 9u| = 27 => |t - u| = 3. Substitute t = 3h and u = 2h: |3h - 2h| = 3 => h = 3. Therefore, h = 3, t = 9, u = 6. Original number = 396. Finally, 75% of 396 = (3/4) × 396 = 3 × 99 = 297.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-num-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Percentages Cascading into Family Count] 100% cascade: 2% have 5, 8% have 0. Rest: 18% have 4, 27% have 1. Rest: 297 have 2 or 3. Total families?", "options": ["A) 500", "B) 600", "C) 750", "D) 800"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a neighborhood, 2% of families have 5 children and 8% have no children. Among the REST of the families, 18% have 4 children and 27% have 1 child. If exactly 297 families have either 2 or 3 children, what is the total number of families in the neighborhood?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let Total Families = 100x. 10% have 0 or 5 children (2% + 8%). The REST = 90x. Among this rest (90x), 18% + 27% = 45% have 1 or 4 children. The remaining 55% of the rest must have 2 or 3 children! Number of families with 2 or 3 children = 55% of 90x = 0.55 × 90x = 49.5x. We are given 49.5x = 297 => x = 297 / 49.5 = 6. Total families = 100x = 600.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-num-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Fraction of Tank Capacity] 3/4 part of a tank is full of water. When 30 liters is taken out, it becomes empty. Capacity of tank?", "options": ["A) 36 liters", "B) 40 liters", "C) 45 liters", "D) 50 liters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly 3/4 part of a tank is currently full of water. When exactly 30 liters of water is taken out, the tank becomes completely empty. What is the total capacity of the tank?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "If removing 30 liters makes the tank entirely empty, the current volume of water must be exactly 30 liters. We are told the tank is 3/4 full. So, (3/4) × Capacity = 30 => Capacity = 30 × 4 / 3 = 40 liters.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-num-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Quadratic Generation from Group Contribution] Each member contributes twice member count. Gov contributes same as member count. Diff scenario, total 210 less. Members?", "options": ["A) 12", "B) 14", "C) 15", "D) 18"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A group wants to renovate. Each member contributes an amount equal to twice the total number of members. The government contributes an amount equal to the number of members. Had each member contributed an amount equal to the number of members, and the government contributed twice the number of members, the total collected would be exactly ₹210 less. How many members are in the group?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let number of members = m. Scenario 1 total = m(2m) + m = 2m^2 + m. Scenario 2 total = m(m) + 2m = m^2 + 2m. Given: Scenario 1 - Scenario 2 = 210. (2m^2 + m) - (m^2 + 2m) = 210 => m^2 - m - 210 = 0. Factoring: (m - 15)(m + 14) = 0. Since m must be positive, m = 15.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-num-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Two Digit Number Reversal Difference] Two digit number A formed by reversing digits of B is 45 less than A. Sum of digits of B is 9. Find A.", "options": ["A) 63", "B) 72", "C) 81", "D) 54"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A two-digit number A is formed by reversing the digits of another two-digit number B. If B is exactly 45 less than A, and the sum of the digits of B is 9, what is the number A?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let B = 10t + u. Then A = 10u + t. We are given A - B = 45 => (10u + t) - (10t + u) = 45 => 9u - 9t = 45 => u - t = 5. We also know the sum of the digits t + u = 9. Adding both equations gives 2u = 14 => u = 7. Thus t = 2. So B = 27 and A = 72.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-num-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Distributing Items into Fixed Groups] 280 oranges divided among 50 boys and girls. Boy gets 5, girl gets 7. Number of girls?", "options": ["A) 15", "B) 20", "C) 25", "D) 35"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly 280 oranges are completely divided among a group of 50 boys and girls. If each boy receives exactly 5 oranges and each girl receives exactly 7 oranges, how many girls are there in the group?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Boys = B and Girls = G. B + G = 50. Oranges equation: 5B + 7G = 280. Substitute B = 50 - G: 5(50 - G) + 7G = 280 => 250 - 5G + 7G = 280 => 2G = 30 => G = 15. There are 15 girls (and 35 boys).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-num-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Sum of Squares of Consecutive Evens] The sum of the squares of two consecutive even integers is 340. Find the integers.", "options": ["A) 10 and 12", "B) 12 and 14", "C) 14 and 16", "D) 16 and 18"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The sum of the squares of two consecutive positive even integers is exactly 340. What are the two integers?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the integers be x and x+2. x^2 + (x+2)^2 = 340 => 2x^2 + 4x + 4 = 340 => 2x^2 + 4x - 336 = 0 => x^2 + 2x - 168 = 0. Factoring gives (x+14)(x-12) = 0. Since they are positive, x = 12. The integers are 12 and 14. (Check: 144 + 196 = 340).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-num-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Difference of Digits from Number Reversal] Difference between a two digit number and the number obtained by interchanging the digits is 63. What is the difference between the digits?", "options": ["A) 6", "B) 7", "C) 8", "D) 9"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The absolute difference between a two-digit number and the number obtained by interchanging its digits is exactly 63. What is the absolute difference between the two digits of the original number?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Let the number be 10x + y. The reversed number is 10y + x. Difference = |(10x + y) - (10y + x)| = |9x - 9y| = 9|x - y|. We are given 9|x - y| = 63 => |x - y| = 63 / 9 = 7.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-num-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Sum of Consecutive Multiples] Sum of three consecutive multiples of 3 is 72. Largest number?", "options": ["A) 24", "B) 27", "C) 30", "D) 33"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The sum of three consecutive multiples of 3 is exactly 72. What is the value of the largest number among them?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the three consecutive multiples of 3 be (x - 3), x, and (x + 3). Sum = 3x = 72 => x = 24. The numbers are 21, 24, and 27. The largest number is 27.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-num-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Fractional Algebraic Equation] A number whose fifth part increased by 4 is equal to its fourth part diminished by 10. Number?", "options": ["A) 240", "B) 260", "C) 280", "D) 300"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Find the number whose exactly one-fifth part increased by 4 is mathematically equal to its one-fourth part diminished by 10.", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the number be x. x/5 + 4 = x/4 - 10. Rearrange to group x: 14 = x/4 - x/5. Find common denominator: 14 = (5x - 4x) / 20 => 14 = x / 20 => x = 14 × 20 = 280.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-num-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Basic Word Equation] If a number is multiplied by 22 and the same number is added to it, we get half of 46. Number?", "options": ["A) 1", "B) 2", "C) 3", "D) 4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a certain number is multiplied by 22 and the exact same number is added to the result, we get exactly half of 46. What is the number?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Let the number be x. Multiply by 22: 22x. Add the same number: 22x + x = 23x. We are given this equals half of 46, which is 23. Thus, 23x = 23 => x = 1.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-num-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Percentage vs Fraction of a Number] Difference between 4/5 of a number and 45% of the number is 56. Number?", "options": ["A) 120", "B) 140", "C) 160", "D) 180"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The absolute difference between exactly 4/5 of a number and 45% of the same number is 56. What is the number?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Convert 4/5 into percentage: 4/5 = 80%. The equation is 80% of x - 45% of x = 56 => 35% of x = 56 => 0.35x = 56 => x = 56 / 0.35 = 5600 / 35 = 160.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-num-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Sum and Difference of Digits] Sum of digits of a two-digit number is 15. Difference between the digits is 3. What is the product of the digits?", "options": ["A) 54", "B) 56", "C) 63", "D) 72"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The sum of the two digits of a two-digit number is exactly 15. The absolute difference between the two digits is exactly 3. What is the product of the two digits?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Let the digits be x and y. x + y = 15, and |x - y| = 3. Adding gives 2x = 18 => x = 9. Subtracting gives 2y = 12 => y = 6. The product of the digits is 9 × 6 = 54.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-num-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Multiplying Error] A boy was asked to multiply a number by 25. He multiplied by 52 and got 324 more than correct answer. Number?", "options": ["A) 12", "B) 14", "C) 15", "D) 18"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A boy was instructed to multiply a certain number by 25. Due to carelessness, he multiplied it by 52 instead and obtained an answer that was exactly 324 more than the correct answer. What was the number?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the number be x. Incorrect answer = 52x. Correct answer = 25x. The difference is 52x - 25x = 27x. We are given 27x = 324 => x = 324 / 27 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-num-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Sum of Squares from Ratio] Three numbers are in ratio 3:4:5. Sum of their squares is 1250. Sum of the numbers?", "options": ["A) 40", "B) 50", "C) 60", "D) 80"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Three positive numbers are strictly in the ratio 3 : 4 : 5. If the sum of their squares is exactly 1,250, what is the exact sum of the three numbers?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let numbers be 3k, 4k, 5k. Sum of squares = (3k)^2 + (4k)^2 + (5k)^2 = 9k^2 + 16k^2 + 25k^2 = 50k^2. We know 50k^2 = 1250 => k^2 = 25 => k = 5. Sum of numbers = 3k + 4k + 5k = 12k = 12 × 5 = 60.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-num-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Fraction Value Shift] If 1 is added to numerator and denominator, fraction becomes 1/2. If 1 is subtracted, fraction becomes 1/3. Fraction?", "options": ["A) 2/5", "B) 3/7", "C) 4/9", "D) 5/11"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If exactly 1 is added to both the numerator and the denominator of a fraction, its value becomes 1/2. If exactly 1 is subtracted from both the numerator and denominator of the original fraction, its value becomes 1/3. What is the original fraction?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let fraction be x/y. 1) (x+1)/(y+1) = 1/2 => 2x + 2 = y + 1 => 2x - y = -1. 2) (x-1)/(y-1) = 1/3 => 3x - 3 = y - 1 => 3x - y = 2. Subtracting the first equation from the second: (3x - y) - (2x - y) = 2 - (-1) => x = 3. Substituting into first: 2(3) - y = -1 => 6 - y = -1 => y = 7. Fraction is 3/7.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. Place Value Representation of Digits\n* **Two-Digit Numbers:** Always represent a two-digit number as **`10x + y`** (where `x` is the tens digit and `y` is the units digit).\n  * Number obtained by **reversing** the digits: **`10y + x`**.\n  * Difference between original and reversed: `(10x + y) - (10y + x) = 9(x - y)`. Always a multiple of **9**.\n  * Sum of original and reversed: `(10x + y) + (10y + x) = 11(x + y)`. Always a multiple of **11**.\n* **Three-Digit Numbers:** Represent as **`100h + 10t + u`**.\n\n### 💡 2. Common Number Problem Archetypes\n* **Sum of Reciprocals:** If the sum of two numbers is `S` and their product is `P`, the sum of their reciprocals `1/x + 1/y = (x+y)/(xy) = S / P`.\n* **Heads and Legs Problems (e.g., Hens and Pigs):**\n  * Let `x` be 2-legged animals and `y` be 4-legged animals.\n  * `x + y = Total Heads`\n  * `2x + 4y = Total Legs`\n* **Consecutive Integers:**\n  * Consecutive integers: `x, x+1, x+2...`\n  * Consecutive even/odd integers: `x, x+2, x+4...`\n  * Average of an odd number of consecutive integers is always the **exact middle integer**.\n* **Mistaken Operations:** If a student was supposed to multiply by `X` but divided by `X` (which is multiplying by `1/X`), the error equation is `X*m - m/X = Difference`.	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Problems on Numbers	Solve equations involving digit reversals (two/three-digit numbers), consecutive integer math, reciprocal sums, and heads-and-legs puzzles.	32	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	20	[{"id": "apt-num-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Sum of Reciprocals] The sum of two numbers is 36 and their product is 248. What will be the sum of their reciprocals?", "options": ["A) 7 / 62", "B) 9 / 62", "C) 11 / 62", "D) 13 / 62"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The sum of two positive numbers is exactly 36 and their product is exactly 248. What is the exact sum of their reciprocals?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the numbers be a and b. We want 1/a + 1/b. Finding a common denominator yields (a + b) / (ab). We are given a + b = 36 and ab = 248. Thus, the sum of reciprocals is 36 / 248. Dividing numerator and denominator by 4 gives 9 / 62.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-num-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Heads and Legs] In a poultry farm having hens and pigs, Rohan can see 84 heads and 282 legs. How many hens are there?", "options": ["A) 25", "B) 27", "C) 30", "D) 57"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a poultry farm consisting exclusively of hens and pigs, Rohan counts a total of 84 heads and 282 legs. How many hens are there in the farm?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let H = number of hens, P = number of pigs. Heads equation: H + P = 84. Legs equation: 2H + 4P = 282 (since hens have 2 legs and pigs have 4). Divide legs equation by 2: H + 2P = 141. Subtract heads equation from this: (H + 2P) - (H + P) = 141 - 84 => P = 57. Thus, there are 57 pigs. Hens H = 84 - 57 = 27 hens.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-num-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Consecutive Numbers Sum] The sum of five consecutive numbers is 335. If we add the largest and smallest numbers, what will we get?", "options": ["A) 132", "B) 134", "C) 136", "D) 138"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The sum of five consecutive integers is exactly 335. What is the sum of the largest and the smallest integers in this sequence?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "The average of an odd number of consecutive integers is always the middle term! Middle term = 335 / 5 = 67. The sequence is 65, 66, 67, 68, 69. Largest + Smallest = 65 + 69 = 134. (Shortcut: Sum of extremes = 2 × Middle term = 2 × 67 = 134).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-num-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Mistaken Operation Equation] Instead of multiplying m by 3/4, Raj divided by 3/4. Difference is 14. What is m?", "options": ["A) 18", "B) 21", "C) 24", "D) 28"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Raj was supposed to multiply a number m by 3/4. Instead, he mistakenly divided m by 3/4. If this error led to a difference of 14 between his incorrect answer and the correct answer, what is the value of m?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Incorrect result = m ÷ (3/4) = 4m/3. Correct result = 3m/4. Difference = 4m/3 - 3m/4 = (16m - 9m) / 12 = 7m / 12. We are given 7m / 12 = 14 => 7m = 168 => m = 24.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-num-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Three-Digit Number Position Shift] The digit in the units place is 2x 100s, 10s place is 3x 100s. Exchanging unit and tens makes difference 27. 75% of original?", "options": ["A) 279", "B) 285", "C) 297", "D) 315"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A 3-digit number has its units digit exactly 2 times the hundreds digit, and its tens digit exactly 3 times the hundreds digit. If the units and tens digits are exchanged, the absolute difference between the original number and the new number is 27. What is exactly 75% of the original number?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let the hundreds digit be h. Tens digit t = 3h. Units digit u = 2h. Original number A = 100h + 10t + u. New number B (exchanging tens and units) = 100h + 10u + t. Difference = |A - B| = |9t - 9u| = 27 => |t - u| = 3. Substitute t = 3h and u = 2h: |3h - 2h| = 3 => h = 3. Therefore, h = 3, t = 9, u = 6. Original number = 396. Finally, 75% of 396 = (3/4) × 396 = 3 × 99 = 297.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-num-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Percentages Cascading into Family Count] 100% cascade: 2% have 5, 8% have 0. Rest: 18% have 4, 27% have 1. Rest: 297 have 2 or 3. Total families?", "options": ["A) 500", "B) 600", "C) 750", "D) 800"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a neighborhood, 2% of families have 5 children and 8% have no children. Among the REST of the families, 18% have 4 children and 27% have 1 child. If exactly 297 families have either 2 or 3 children, what is the total number of families in the neighborhood?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let Total Families = 100x. 10% have 0 or 5 children (2% + 8%). The REST = 90x. Among this rest (90x), 18% + 27% = 45% have 1 or 4 children. The remaining 55% of the rest must have 2 or 3 children! Number of families with 2 or 3 children = 55% of 90x = 0.55 × 90x = 49.5x. We are given 49.5x = 297 => x = 297 / 49.5 = 6. Total families = 100x = 600.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-num-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Fraction of Tank Capacity] 3/4 part of a tank is full of water. When 30 liters is taken out, it becomes empty. Capacity of tank?", "options": ["A) 36 liters", "B) 40 liters", "C) 45 liters", "D) 50 liters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly 3/4 part of a tank is currently full of water. When exactly 30 liters of water is taken out, the tank becomes completely empty. What is the total capacity of the tank?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "If removing 30 liters makes the tank entirely empty, the current volume of water must be exactly 30 liters. We are told the tank is 3/4 full. So, (3/4) × Capacity = 30 => Capacity = 30 × 4 / 3 = 40 liters.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-num-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Quadratic Generation from Group Contribution] Each member contributes twice member count. Gov contributes same as member count. Diff scenario, total 210 less. Members?", "options": ["A) 12", "B) 14", "C) 15", "D) 18"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A group wants to renovate. Each member contributes an amount equal to twice the total number of members. The government contributes an amount equal to the number of members. Had each member contributed an amount equal to the number of members, and the government contributed twice the number of members, the total collected would be exactly ₹210 less. How many members are in the group?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let number of members = m. Scenario 1 total = m(2m) + m = 2m^2 + m. Scenario 2 total = m(m) + 2m = m^2 + 2m. Given: Scenario 1 - Scenario 2 = 210. (2m^2 + m) - (m^2 + 2m) = 210 => m^2 - m - 210 = 0. Factoring: (m - 15)(m + 14) = 0. Since m must be positive, m = 15.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-num-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Two Digit Number Reversal Difference] Two digit number A formed by reversing digits of B is 45 less than A. Sum of digits of B is 9. Find A.", "options": ["A) 63", "B) 72", "C) 81", "D) 54"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A two-digit number A is formed by reversing the digits of another two-digit number B. If B is exactly 45 less than A, and the sum of the digits of B is 9, what is the number A?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let B = 10t + u. Then A = 10u + t. We are given A - B = 45 => (10u + t) - (10t + u) = 45 => 9u - 9t = 45 => u - t = 5. We also know the sum of the digits t + u = 9. Adding both equations gives 2u = 14 => u = 7. Thus t = 2. So B = 27 and A = 72.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-num-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Distributing Items into Fixed Groups] 280 oranges divided among 50 boys and girls. Boy gets 5, girl gets 7. Number of girls?", "options": ["A) 15", "B) 20", "C) 25", "D) 35"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly 280 oranges are completely divided among a group of 50 boys and girls. If each boy receives exactly 5 oranges and each girl receives exactly 7 oranges, how many girls are there in the group?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Boys = B and Girls = G. B + G = 50. Oranges equation: 5B + 7G = 280. Substitute B = 50 - G: 5(50 - G) + 7G = 280 => 250 - 5G + 7G = 280 => 2G = 30 => G = 15. There are 15 girls (and 35 boys).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-num-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Sum of Squares of Consecutive Evens] The sum of the squares of two consecutive even integers is 340. Find the integers.", "options": ["A) 10 and 12", "B) 12 and 14", "C) 14 and 16", "D) 16 and 18"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The sum of the squares of two consecutive positive even integers is exactly 340. What are the two integers?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the integers be x and x+2. x^2 + (x+2)^2 = 340 => 2x^2 + 4x + 4 = 340 => 2x^2 + 4x - 336 = 0 => x^2 + 2x - 168 = 0. Factoring gives (x+14)(x-12) = 0. Since they are positive, x = 12. The integers are 12 and 14. (Check: 144 + 196 = 340).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-num-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Difference of Digits from Number Reversal] Difference between a two digit number and the number obtained by interchanging the digits is 63. What is the difference between the digits?", "options": ["A) 6", "B) 7", "C) 8", "D) 9"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The absolute difference between a two-digit number and the number obtained by interchanging its digits is exactly 63. What is the absolute difference between the two digits of the original number?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Let the number be 10x + y. The reversed number is 10y + x. Difference = |(10x + y) - (10y + x)| = |9x - 9y| = 9|x - y|. We are given 9|x - y| = 63 => |x - y| = 63 / 9 = 7.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-num-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Sum of Consecutive Multiples] Sum of three consecutive multiples of 3 is 72. Largest number?", "options": ["A) 24", "B) 27", "C) 30", "D) 33"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The sum of three consecutive multiples of 3 is exactly 72. What is the value of the largest number among them?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the three consecutive multiples of 3 be (x - 3), x, and (x + 3). Sum = 3x = 72 => x = 24. The numbers are 21, 24, and 27. The largest number is 27.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-num-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Fractional Algebraic Equation] A number whose fifth part increased by 4 is equal to its fourth part diminished by 10. Number?", "options": ["A) 240", "B) 260", "C) 280", "D) 300"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Find the number whose exactly one-fifth part increased by 4 is mathematically equal to its one-fourth part diminished by 10.", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the number be x. x/5 + 4 = x/4 - 10. Rearrange to group x: 14 = x/4 - x/5. Find common denominator: 14 = (5x - 4x) / 20 => 14 = x / 20 => x = 14 × 20 = 280.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-num-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Basic Word Equation] If a number is multiplied by 22 and the same number is added to it, we get half of 46. Number?", "options": ["A) 1", "B) 2", "C) 3", "D) 4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a certain number is multiplied by 22 and the exact same number is added to the result, we get exactly half of 46. What is the number?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Let the number be x. Multiply by 22: 22x. Add the same number: 22x + x = 23x. We are given this equals half of 46, which is 23. Thus, 23x = 23 => x = 1.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-num-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Percentage vs Fraction of a Number] Difference between 4/5 of a number and 45% of the number is 56. Number?", "options": ["A) 120", "B) 140", "C) 160", "D) 180"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The absolute difference between exactly 4/5 of a number and 45% of the same number is 56. What is the number?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Convert 4/5 into percentage: 4/5 = 80%. The equation is 80% of x - 45% of x = 56 => 35% of x = 56 => 0.35x = 56 => x = 56 / 0.35 = 5600 / 35 = 160.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-num-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Sum and Difference of Digits] Sum of digits of a two-digit number is 15. Difference between the digits is 3. What is the product of the digits?", "options": ["A) 54", "B) 56", "C) 63", "D) 72"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The sum of the two digits of a two-digit number is exactly 15. The absolute difference between the two digits is exactly 3. What is the product of the two digits?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Let the digits be x and y. x + y = 15, and |x - y| = 3. Adding gives 2x = 18 => x = 9. Subtracting gives 2y = 12 => y = 6. The product of the digits is 9 × 6 = 54.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-num-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Multiplying Error] A boy was asked to multiply a number by 25. He multiplied by 52 and got 324 more than correct answer. Number?", "options": ["A) 12", "B) 14", "C) 15", "D) 18"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A boy was instructed to multiply a certain number by 25. Due to carelessness, he multiplied it by 52 instead and obtained an answer that was exactly 324 more than the correct answer. What was the number?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the number be x. Incorrect answer = 52x. Correct answer = 25x. The difference is 52x - 25x = 27x. We are given 27x = 324 => x = 324 / 27 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-num-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Sum of Squares from Ratio] Three numbers are in ratio 3:4:5. Sum of their squares is 1250. Sum of the numbers?", "options": ["A) 40", "B) 50", "C) 60", "D) 80"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Three positive numbers are strictly in the ratio 3 : 4 : 5. If the sum of their squares is exactly 1,250, what is the exact sum of the three numbers?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let numbers be 3k, 4k, 5k. Sum of squares = (3k)^2 + (4k)^2 + (5k)^2 = 9k^2 + 16k^2 + 25k^2 = 50k^2. We know 50k^2 = 1250 => k^2 = 25 => k = 5. Sum of numbers = 3k + 4k + 5k = 12k = 12 × 5 = 60.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-num-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Fraction Value Shift] If 1 is added to numerator and denominator, fraction becomes 1/2. If 1 is subtracted, fraction becomes 1/3. Fraction?", "options": ["A) 2/5", "B) 3/7", "C) 4/9", "D) 5/11"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If exactly 1 is added to both the numerator and the denominator of a fraction, its value becomes 1/2. If exactly 1 is subtracted from both the numerator and denominator of the original fraction, its value becomes 1/3. What is the original fraction?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let fraction be x/y. 1) (x+1)/(y+1) = 1/2 => 2x + 2 = y + 1 => 2x - y = -1. 2) (x-1)/(y-1) = 1/3 => 3x - 3 = y - 1 => 3x - y = 2. Subtracting the first equation from the second: (3x - y) - (2x - y) = 2 - (-1) => x = 3. Substituting into first: 2(3) - y = -1 => 6 - y = -1 => y = 7. Fraction is 3/7.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22133	Lecture 15: DOM Events In-Depth — addEventListener, Event Object, Event Bubbling vs. Capturing & Event Delegation	js-lecture-15-dom-events-addeventlistener-bubbling-capturing-delegation	Handle user interactions with addEventListener(), inspect the Event object, master the 3 phases of Event Propagation (Capturing, Target, Bubbling), and optimize memory with Event Delegation.	2026-10-06 21:36:53.504856+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nBrowser **Events** (`click`, `dblclick`, `input`, `keydown`, `submit`) occur continuously whenever a user interacts with a webpage. **Event Listeners** allow JavaScript to subscribe to specific events and execute callback logic in response.\n\n### 💡 3 Ways to Attach Event Handlers (And Why `addEventListener` Wins)\n1. **Inline HTML (`<h1 onclick="handleClick()">` — Avoid):** Mixes HTML markup with JS logic and requires global functions.\n2. **DOM Property (`btn.onclick = fn` — Avoid):** Can only hold **one** function at a time! Assigning a second `btn.onclick = fn2` overwrites and destroys the first listener.\n3. **`el.addEventListener(eventType, callback, useCapture)` (Best Practice):**\n   * Allows attaching **multiple independent listeners** to the same element and event.\n   * Supports removing listeners via `el.removeEventListener(eventType, namedCallback)` (requires passing the exact same function reference).\n   * Controls propagation phase via the 3rd boolean parameter (`false` = Bubbling by default, `true` = Capturing).\n\n### 💡 Event Propagation: Capturing (Trickling) vs. Bubbling\nBecause a child element `<button>` physically sits inside its `<div class="parent">`, which sits inside `<body>` and `window`, clicking the child also clicks the parent! The W3C DOM Event model processes every event in **3 phases**:\n1. **Capturing Phase (Trickling Down: `window -> document -> body -> grandparent -> parent`):** Travels from root down to the target. Triggered only when the 3rd argument of `addEventListener` is **true**.\n2. **Target Phase:** Reaches the actual clicked element (`event.target`).\n3. **Bubbling Phase (Bubbling Up: `child -> parent -> grandparent -> body -> window`):** Travels from the target back up to the root. **Default behavior (false)**.\n\n### 💡 Event Delegation (High-Performance Pattern)\n* Instead of attaching **100 separate `addEventListener` callbacks** to 100 child buttons (which wastes memory and fails for dynamically added children), attach **1 single listener to the parent container**!\n* Because click events bubble up from the clicked child to the parent, the parent listener inspects **`event.target`** to identify which specific child was clicked.\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Event Bubbling, Capturing & Event Delegation\n```javascript\n// 1. Event Bubbling (false - default) vs. Event Capturing (true)\ngrandParent.addEventListener("click", () => console.log("GrandParent"), false);\nparent.addEventListener("click", () => console.log("Parent"), true); // Capturing!\nchild.addEventListener("click", (e) => {\n  console.log("Child clicked!");\n  // e.stopPropagation(); // Uncomment to stop event from bubbling up further\n}, false);\n// Clicking Child logs: "Parent" (captured on way down) -> "Child clicked!" -> "GrandParent" (bubbled up)\n\n// 2. Event Delegation: 1 Listener on Parent handles all Child color buttons!\nconst container = document.getElementById("color-palette");\ncontainer.addEventListener("click", (event) => {\n  // event.target = exact child button clicked; event.currentTarget = container\n  if (event.target.tagName === "BUTTON") {\n    document.body.style.backgroundColor = event.target.id;\n  }\n});\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/EventTarget/addEventListener", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/EventTarget/addEventListener", "title": "MDN Web Docs — EventTarget.addEventListener() & Event Propagation"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CGjHpnm8TeE", "title": "Events in Javascript | Even and Event handler in Javascript | Javascript Full Course #15", "duration": "65m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-15-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Explain the 3 phases of DOM Event Propagation: Event Capturing vs. Target vs. Event Bubbling.", "title": "Q1: Explain the 3 phases of DOM Event Propagation: Event Capturing vs. Target vs. Event Bubbling.", "answer": "When an event occurs on a nested DOM element: 1) Capturing Phase (Trickling): The event travels from the top-level window/document down through ancestor elements to the target element (listeners registered with addEventListener(type, fn, true) fire here). 2) Target Phase: The event reaches the actual element that triggered it (event.target). 3) Bubbling Phase: The event bubbles back up from the target element through its parent, grandparent, body, and document to window (default listeners with useCapture = false fire here).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "When an event occurs on a nested DOM element: 1) Capturing Phase (Trickling): The event travels from the top-level window/document down through ancestor elements to the target element (listeners registered with addEventListener(type, fn, true) fire here). 2) Target Phase: The event reaches the actual element that triggered it (event.target). 3) Bubbling Phase: The event bubbles back up from the target element through its parent, grandparent, body, and document to window (default listeners with useCapture = false fire here)."}, {"id": "js-lec-15-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: What is Event Delegation in JavaScript, and what are its two biggest advantages?", "title": "Q2: What is Event Delegation in JavaScript, and what are its two biggest advantages?", "answer": "Event Delegation is the pattern of attaching a single event listener to a common parent container rather than attaching individual listeners to dozens or hundreds of child elements. Leveraging Event Bubbling, the parent listener checks event.target (or event.target.closest(selector)) to handle actions for the clicked child. Advantages: 1) Massive memory & performance savings (1 listener function in RAM instead of 1,000). 2) Dynamic elements added to the parent later automatically work without needing to attach new listeners!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Event Delegation is the pattern of attaching a single event listener to a common parent container rather than attaching individual listeners to dozens or hundreds of child elements. Leveraging Event Bubbling, the parent listener checks event.target (or event.target.closest(selector)) to handle actions for the clicked child. Advantages: 1) Massive memory & performance savings (1 listener function in RAM instead of 1,000). 2) Dynamic elements added to the parent later automatically work without needing to attach new listeners!"}, {"id": "js-lec-15-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is the difference between event.target and event.currentTarget?", "title": "Q3: What is the difference between event.target and event.currentTarget?", "answer": "event.target refers to the exact deepest DOM element that originally triggered the event (e.g., the specific <button> the user clicked). event.currentTarget refers to the element to which the currently executing addEventListener handler is attached (e.g., the parent <div> in Event Delegation).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "event.target refers to the exact deepest DOM element that originally triggered the event (e.g., the specific <button> the user clicked). event.currentTarget refers to the element to which the currently executing addEventListener handler is attached (e.g., the parent <div> in Event Delegation)."}, {"id": "js-lec-15-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What is the difference between event.stopPropagation() and event.preventDefault()?", "title": "Q4: What is the difference between event.stopPropagation() and event.preventDefault()?", "answer": "event.stopPropagation() stops the event from travelling further up (bubbling) or down (capturing) the DOM tree to other ancestor/child listeners, but does NOT cancel the browser's default action. event.preventDefault() cancels the browser's built-in default behavior for that element (such as preventing a <form> submit from reloading the page or preventing an <a href> link from navigating), without stopping propagation.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "event.stopPropagation() stops the event from travelling further up (bubbling) or down (capturing) the DOM tree to other ancestor/child listeners, but does NOT cancel the browser's default action. event.preventDefault() cancels the browser's built-in default behavior for that element (such as preventing a <form> submit from reloading the page or preventing an <a href> link from navigating), without stopping propagation."}, {"id": "js-lec-15-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Why does el.removeEventListener(\\"click\\", () => console.log(\\"hi\\")) fail to remove a listener added with el.addEventListener(\\"click\\", () => console.log(\\"hi\\"))?", "title": "Q5: Why does el.removeEventListener(\\"click\\", () => console.log(\\"hi\\")) fail to remove a listener added with el.addEventListener(\\"click\\", () => console.log(\\"hi\\"))?", "answer": "Because two inline anonymous arrow functions () => console.log(\\"hi\\") create two completely separate function objects in the Heap with different memory addresses! For removeEventListener to work, you must store the handler in a named variable (const handleClick = () => ...) and pass that exact same function reference to both addEventListener and removeEventListener.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Because two inline anonymous arrow functions () => console.log(\\"hi\\") create two completely separate function objects in the Heap with different memory addresses! For removeEventListener to work, you must store the handler in a named variable (const handleClick = () => ...) and pass that exact same function reference to both addEventListener and removeEventListener."}, {"id": "js-lec-15-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: What is the difference between event.stopPropagation() and event.stopImmediatePropagation()?", "title": "Q6: What is the difference between event.stopPropagation() and event.stopImmediatePropagation()?", "answer": "If an element has three separate click listeners attached to it, calling event.stopPropagation() inside the first listener stops the event from bubbling up to parent elements, but the 2nd and 3rd click listeners on that SAME element will still execute! Calling event.stopImmediatePropagation() stops both parent bubbling AND prevents any remaining sibling listeners on that same element from running.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q6", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "If an element has three separate click listeners attached to it, calling event.stopPropagation() inside the first listener stops the event from bubbling up to parent elements, but the 2nd and 3rd click listeners on that SAME element will still execute! Calling event.stopImmediatePropagation() stops both parent bubbling AND prevents any remaining sibling listeners on that same element from running."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nBrowser **Events** (`click`, `dblclick`, `input`, `keydown`, `submit`) occur continuously whenever a user interacts with a webpage. **Event Listeners** allow JavaScript to subscribe to specific events and execute callback logic in response.\n\n### 💡 3 Ways to Attach Event Handlers (And Why `addEventListener` Wins)\n1. **Inline HTML (`<h1 on