rs with useCapture = false fire here).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "When an event occurs on a nested DOM element: 1) Capturing Phase (Trickling): The event travels from the top-level window/document down through ancestor elements to the target element (listeners registered with addEventListener(type, fn, true) fire here). 2) Target Phase: The event reaches the actual element that triggered it (event.target). 3) Bubbling Phase: The event bubbles back up from the target element through its parent, grandparent, body, and document to window (default listeners with useCapture = false fire here)."}, {"id": "js-lec-15-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: What is Event Delegation in JavaScript, and what are its two biggest advantages?", "title": "Q2: What is Event Delegation in JavaScript, and what are its two biggest advantages?", "answer": "Event Delegation is the pattern of attaching a single event listener to a common parent container rather than attaching individual listeners to dozens or hundreds of child elements. Leveraging Event Bubbling, the parent listener checks event.target (or event.target.closest(selector)) to handle actions for the clicked child. Advantages: 1) Massive memory & performance savings (1 listener function in RAM instead of 1,000). 2) Dynamic elements added to the parent later automatically work without needing to attach new listeners!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Event Delegation is the pattern of attaching a single event listener to a common parent container rather than attaching individual listeners to dozens or hundreds of child elements. Leveraging Event Bubbling, the parent listener checks event.target (or event.target.closest(selector)) to handle actions for the clicked child. Advantages: 1) Massive memory & performance savings (1 listener function in RAM instead of 1,000). 2) Dynamic elements added to the parent later automatically work without needing to attach new listeners!"}, {"id": "js-lec-15-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is the difference between event.target and event.currentTarget?", "title": "Q3: What is the difference between event.target and event.currentTarget?", "answer": "event.target refers to the exact deepest DOM element that originally triggered the event (e.g., the specific <button> the user clicked). event.currentTarget refers to the element to which the currently executing addEventListener handler is attached (e.g., the parent <div> in Event Delegation).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "event.target refers to the exact deepest DOM element that originally triggered the event (e.g., the specific <button> the user clicked). event.currentTarget refers to the element to which the currently executing addEventListener handler is attached (e.g., the parent <div> in Event Delegation)."}, {"id": "js-lec-15-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What is the difference between event.stopPropagation() and event.preventDefault()?", "title": "Q4: What is the difference between event.stopPropagation() and event.preventDefault()?", "answer": "event.stopPropagation() stops the event from travelling further up (bubbling) or down (capturing) the DOM tree to other ancestor/child listeners, but does NOT cancel the browser's default action. event.preventDefault() cancels the browser's built-in default behavior for that element (such as preventing a <form> submit from reloading the page or preventing an <a href> link from navigating), without stopping propagation.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "event.stopPropagation() stops the event from travelling further up (bubbling) or down (capturing) the DOM tree to other ancestor/child listeners, but does NOT cancel the browser's default action. event.preventDefault() cancels the browser's built-in default behavior for that element (such as preventing a <form> submit from reloading the page or preventing an <a href> link from navigating), without stopping propagation."}, {"id": "js-lec-15-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Why does el.removeEventListener(\\"click\\", () => console.log(\\"hi\\")) fail to remove a listener added with el.addEventListener(\\"click\\", () => console.log(\\"hi\\"))?", "title": "Q5: Why does el.removeEventListener(\\"click\\", () => console.log(\\"hi\\")) fail to remove a listener added with el.addEventListener(\\"click\\", () => console.log(\\"hi\\"))?", "answer": "Because two inline anonymous arrow functions () => console.log(\\"hi\\") create two completely separate function objects in the Heap with different memory addresses! For removeEventListener to work, you must store the handler in a named variable (const handleClick = () => ...) and pass that exact same function reference to both addEventListener and removeEventListener.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "15-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Because two inline anonymous arrow functions () => console.log(\\"hi\\") create two completely separate function objects in the Heap with different memory addresses! For removeEventListener to work, you must store the handler in a named variable (const handleClick = () => ...) and pass that exact same function reference to both addEventListener and removeEventListener."}, {"id": "js-lec-15-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: What is the difference between event.stopPropagation() and event.stopImmediatePropagation()?", "title": "Q6: What is the difference between event.stopPropagation() and event.stopImmediatePropagation()?", "answer": "If an element has three separate click listeners attached to it, calling event.stopPropagation() inside the first listener stops the event from bubbling up to parent elements, but the 2nd and 3rd click listen