CDN downloads a massive JavaScript runtime and generates styles on the client's browser at runtime. The Tailwind CLI scans your ./views/*.html files at build time and outputs ONLY the exact CSS utility classes you actually used into public/output.css (often under 10 KB). 2) Performance & Caching: A static output.css file is cached by the browser and renders immediately without waiting for client-side JavaScript execution."}, {"id": "node-lec-11-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why did Tailwind CSS fail to generate utility classes in public/output.css when the content array in tailwind.config.js had the wrong path glob?", "title": "Q4: Why did Tailwind CSS fail to generate utility classes in public/output.css when the content array in tailwind.config.js had the wrong path glob?", "answer": "Tailwind's JIT (Just-In-Time) compiler only generates CSS rules for utility classes it finds inside the files matched by the content array in tailwind.config.js. If content is empty or points to the wrong folder, Tailwind assumes zero utility classes are used and outputs a base reset file without your utility classes (like text-red-700).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "11-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Tailwind's JIT (Just-In-Time) compiler only generates CSS rules for utility classes it finds inside the files matched by the content array in tailwind.config.js. If content is empty or points to the wrong folder, Tailwind assumes zero utility classes are used and outputs a base reset file without your utility classes (like text-red-700)."}, {"id": "node-lec-11-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What does \\"Mobile-First Responsive Design\\" mean in Tailwind CSS?", "title": "Q5: What does \\"Mobile-First Responsive Design\\" mean in Tailwind CSS?", "answer": "In Tailwind CSS, unprefixed utility classes (like text-sm or flex-col) apply to all screen sizes starting from mobile screens upward. Breakpoint prefixes like md:flex-row or lg:text-4xl apply at that breakpoint and above (min-width media queries), encouraging developers to design for mobile screens first and progressively enhance layouts for tablets and desktops.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "11-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In Tailwind CSS, unprefixed utility classes (like text-sm or flex-col) apply to all screen sizes starting from mobile screens upward. Breakpoint prefixes like md:flex-row or lg:text-4xl apply at that breakpoint and above (min-width media queries), encouraging developers to design for mobile screens first and progressively enhance layouts for tablets and desktops."}, {"id": "node-lec-11-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Can you register multiple static directories or mount express.static() under a virtual URL prefix?", "title": "Q6: Can you register multiple static directories or mount express.static() under a virtual URL prefix?", "answer": "Yes! You can call app.use(express.static(...)) multiple times for different directories (Express searches them in registration order), or mount a virtual path prefix via app.use(\\"/static\\", express.static(path.join(rootDir, \\"public\\"))), which serves files only when the URL starts with \\"/static/output.css\\".", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "11-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Yes! You can call app.use(express.static(...)) multiple times for different directories (Express searches them in registration order), or mount a virtual path prefix via app.use(\\"/static\\", express.static(path.join(rootDir, \\"public\\"))), which serves files only when the URL starts with \\"/static/output.css\\"."}]	https://expressjs.com/en/starter/static-files.html	\N	\N	\N
22152	Lecture 12: Dynamic Server-Side UI with EJS — Template Engines (app.set("view engine", "ejs")), res.render(), EJS Tags & Reusable Partials	node-lecture-12-dynamic-ui-using-ejs-templating-engine-partials	Render dynamic, personalized server-side HTML using EJS (Embedded JavaScript Templates), pass runtime data into res.render(), use EJS tags (<%= %>, <% %>, <%- %>), and eliminate duplicate markup with EJS Partials.	2026-10-06 21:56:18.722653+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nStatic `.html` files served via `res.sendFile()` show the exact same hardcoded content to every user. Real-world applications (like Airbnb home listings, stock tickers, or user dashboards) require **Dynamic UI** where HTML is generated at runtime based on database records or user state. **EJS (Embedded JavaScript)** is a template engine that lets you embed JavaScript variables, loops, and conditionals directly inside HTML templates on the server!\n\n### 💡 Configuring EJS in Express (`app.set` & `res.render`)\n1. **Install EJS:** `npm install ejs` (Express loads it automatically—no `require('ejs')` needed).\n2. **Configure Express View Engine:**\n   * **`app.set("view engine", "ejs");`** — tells Express to use EJS to compile templates.\n   * **`app.set("views", "views");`** — tells Express which folder holds `.ejs` files (`views/home.ejs`).\n3. **Render a Template with Dynamic Data:**\n   * Replace `res.sendFile(...)` with **`res.render("home", { registeredHomes, pageTitle: "airbnb Home" })`**.\n   * Express compiles `views/home.ejs`, injects the passed data object into the template, and sends pure HTML to the browser!\n\n### 💡 Essential EJS Tags & Reusable Partials (`include`)\n* **`<%= variable %>` (Escaped Output Tag):** Evaluates the JS expression and outputs the value into HTML safely (escapes `<` and `>` to prevent XSS). Use for user data like `<%= home.houseName %>` or `<%= pageTitle %>`.\n* **`<% code %>` (Control-Flow Scriptlet Tag):** Executes JavaScript logic (`forEach` loops, `if/else` statements) with **no direct HTML output**:\n  `<% registeredHomes.forEach(home => { %> ... <% }) %>`\n* **`<%- include('partials/nav') %>` (Unescaped / Partial Include Tag):** Injects raw HTML from reusable **Partials** (`views/partials/head.ejs`, `views/partials/nav.ejs`) so common headers and navigation bars are defined in a single file instead of duplicated across 20 pages!\n* **Remember Tailwind Config:** Update `tailwind.config.js` to `content: ["./views/**/*.{html,ejs}"` so Tailwind scans `.ejs` templates and nested `partials/` folders!\n\n### 🛠️ Configuring EJS, Rendering Data & Using Partials\n```javascript\n// 1. In app.js: Configure EJS as the Express View Engine\napp.set("view engine", "ejs");\napp.set("views", "views");\n\n// 2. In routes/userRouter.js: Pass dynamic array & pageTitle to res.render()\nuserRouter.get("/", (req, res, next) => {\n  res.render("home", {\n    registeredHomes: registeredHomes,\n    pageTitle: "airbnb Home",\n    currentPage: "Home"\n  });\n});\n```\n\n```html\n<!-- 3. In views/home.ejs: Reusable Partials + Dynamic EJS Loop -->\n<%- include('partials/head') %>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <%- include('partials/nav') %>\n    <main class="container mx-auto mt-8">\n      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-red-500">Here are our registered homes:</h2>\n      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2">\n        <% registeredHomes.forEach(home => { %>\n          <li class="p-4 bg-white shadow rounded-lg">\n            <span class="font-semibold"><%= home.houseName %></span> — ₹<%= home.price %> / night\n          </li>\n        <% }) %>\n      </ul>\n    </main>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```	[{"url": "https://expressjs.com/en/guide/using-template-engines.html", "link": "https://expressjs.com/en/guide/using-template-engines.html", "title": "Express.js Official Docs — Using Template Engines with Express"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WaObzvMEgd4", "title": "Lecture 12: Dynamic UI using EJS | NodeJS Complete Course in Hindi", "duration": "66m", "creator_name": "Complete Coding by Prashant Sir", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir"}]	[{"id": "node-lec-12-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What is the difference between <%= value %>, <%- html %>, and <% code %> in EJS?", "title": "Q1: What is the difference between <%= value %>, <%- html %>, and <% code %> in EJS?", "answer": "1) <%= value %> evaluates the JavaScript expression and outputs the result as HTML-escaped text (converting <, >, & into safe HTML entities to prevent XSS attacks)—used for displaying dynamic variables like <%= home.houseName %>. 2) <%- html %> outputs the unescaped raw HTML string—used when including partials via <%- include(\\"partials/nav\\") %>. 3) <% code %> is a scriptlet tag that executes control-flow JavaScript (such as if/else conditions or registeredHomes.forEach() loops) without outputting anything to the HTML.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) <%= value %> evaluates the JavaScript expression and outputs the result as HTML-escaped text (converting <, >, & into safe HTML entities to prevent XSS attacks)—used for displaying dynamic variables like <%= home.houseName %>. 2) <%- html %> outputs the unescaped raw HTML string—used when including partials via <%- include(\\"partials/nav\\") %>. 3) <% code %> is a scriptlet tag that executes control-flow JavaScript (such as if/else conditions or registeredHomes.forEach() loops) without outputting anything to the HTML."}, {"id": "node-lec-12-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: What is the difference between res.sendFile() and res.render() in Express?", "title": "Q2: What is the difference between res.sendFile() and res.render() in Express?", "answer": "res.sendFile() streams a static file (like home.html) directly from disk to the client as-is without any variable substitution. res.render(viewName, localsObj) invokes the configured template engine (like EJS), compiles the views/<viewName>.ejs template by injecting the dynamic data from localsObj, and sends the resulting generated HTML string to the browser.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "res.sendFile() streams a static file (like home.html) directly from disk to the client as-is without any variable substitution. res.render(viewName, localsObj) invokes the configured template engine (like EJS), compiles the views/<viewName>.ejs template by injecting the dynamic data from localsObj, and sends the resulting generated HTML string to the browser."}, {"id": "node-lec-12-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What are EJS Partials, and how do they enforce the DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle?", "title": "Q3: What are EJS Partials, and how do they enforce the DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle?", "answer": "Partials (stored in a folder like views/partials/head.ejs and views/partials/nav.ejs) are reusable template fragments containing shared UI components like the HTML <head>, navigation bar, or footer. By including them across all pages via <%- include(\\"partials/nav\\") %>, any change to the navbar only needs to be made in one file instead of editing 20 separate page templates.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Partials (stored in a folder like views/partials/head.ejs and views/partials/nav.ejs) are reusable template fragments containing shared UI components like the HTML <head>, navigation bar, or footer. By including them across all pages via <%- include(\\"partials/nav\\") %>, any change to the navbar only needs to be made in one file instead of editing 20 separate page templates."}, {"id": "node-lec-12-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Does the client's web browser ever see EJS tags like <%= home.houseName %> when viewing page source?", "title": "Q4: Does the client's web browser ever see EJS tags like <%= home.houseName %> when viewing page source?", "answer": "No! EJS is a Server-Side Rendering (SSR) template engine. All <% %> and <%= %> tags are executed and stripped on the Node.js server during res.render(), which sends 100% standard, plain HTML to the client's browser.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "No! EJS is a Server-Side Rendering (SSR) template engine. All <% %> and <%= %> tags are executed and stripped on the Node.js server during res.render(), which sends 100% standard, plain HTML to the client's browser."}, {"id": "node-lec-12-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: When storing submitted homes in an in-memory array (const registeredHomes = []) inside hostRouter.js, why do all users see the same list and why does it reset when the server restarts?", "title": "Q5: When storing submitted homes in an in-memory array (const registeredHomes = []) inside hostRouter.js, why do all users see the same list and why does it reset when the server restarts?", "answer": "Because module-scoped variables in a Node.js server live in the server's RAM and are shared across the entire Node process (and cached across require() calls). Every user hitting the server reads from that same in-memory array, and whenever nodemon or the server restarts, RAM is cleared—which is why real production apps persist state in a database like MongoDB or PostgreSQL.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Because module-scoped variables in a Node.js server live in the server's RAM and are shared across the entire Node process (and cached across require() calls). Every user hitting the server reads from that same in-memory array, and whenever nodemon or the server restarts, RAM is cleared—which is why real production apps persist state in a database like MongoDB or PostgreSQL."}, {"id": "node-lec-12-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why did we need to update tailwind.config.js from \\"./views/*.html\\" to \\"./views/**/*.{html,ejs}\\" after migrating to EJS and Partials?", "title": "Q6: Why did we need to update tailwind.config.js from \\"./views/*.html\\" to \\"./views/**/*.{html,ejs}\\" after migrating to EJS and Partials?", "answer": "Two reasons: 1) Our view files now have the .ejs extension instead of .html. 2) We created a nested subfolder views/partials/ for head.ejs and nav.ejs. The recursive glob **/*.{html,ejs} ensures Tailwind scans both top-level views/ and all nested subdirectories for Tailwind utility classes.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Two reasons: 1) Our view files now have the .ejs extension instead of .html. 2) We created a nested subfolder views/partials/ for head.ejs and nav.ejs. The recursive glob **/*.{html,ejs} ensures Tailwind scans both top-level views/ and all nested subdirectories for Tailwind utility classes."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nStatic `.html` files served via `res.sendFile()` show the exact same hardcoded content to every user. Real-world applications (like Airbnb home listings, stock tickers, or user dashboards) require **Dynamic UI** where HTML is generated at runtime based on database records or user state. **EJS (Embedded JavaScript)** is a template engine that lets you embed JavaScript variables, loops, and conditionals directly inside HTML templates on the server!\n\n### 💡 Configuring EJS in Express (`app.set` & `res.render`)\n1. **Install EJS:** `npm install ejs` (Express loads it automatically—no `require('ejs')` needed).\n2. **Configure Express View Engine:**\n   * **`app.set("view engine", "ejs");`** — tells Express to use EJS to compile templates.\n   * **`app.set("views", "views");`** — tells Express which folder holds `.ejs` files (`views/home.ejs`).\n3. **Render a Template with Dynamic Data:**\n   * Replace `res.sendFile(...)` with **`res.render("home", { registeredHomes, pageTitle: "airbnb Home" })`**.\n   * Express compiles `views/home.ejs`, injects the passed data object into the template, and sends pure HTML to the browser!\n\n### 💡 Essential EJS Tags & Reusable Partials (`include`)\n* **`<%= variable %>` (Escaped Output Tag):** Evaluates the JS expression and outputs the value into HTML safely (escapes `<` and `>` to prevent XSS). Use for user data like `<%= home.houseName %>` or `<%= pageTitle %>`.\n* **`<% code %>` (Control-Flow Scriptlet Tag):** Executes JavaScript logic (`forEach` loops, `if/else` statements) with **no direct HTML output**:\n  `<% registeredHomes.forEach(home => { %> ... <% }) %>`\n* **`<%- include('partials/nav') %>` (Unescaped / Partial Include Tag):** Injects raw HTML from reusable **Partials** (`views/partials/head.ejs`, `views/partials/nav.ejs`) so common headers and navigation bars are defined in a single file instead of duplicated across 20 pages!\n* **Remember Tailwind Config:** Update `tailwind.config.js` to `content: ["./views/**/*.{html,ejs}"` so Tailwind scans `.ejs` templates and nested `partials/` folders!\n\n### 🛠️ Configuring EJS, Rendering Data & Using Partials\n```javascript\n// 1. In app.js: Configure EJS as the Express View Engine\napp.set("view engine", "ejs");\napp.set("views", "views");\n\n// 2. In routes/userRouter.js: Pass dynamic array & pageTitle to res.render()\nuserRouter.get("/", (req, res, next) => {\n  res.render("home", {\n    registeredHomes: registeredHomes,\n    pageTitle: "airbnb Home",\n    currentPage: "Home"\n  });\n});\n```\n\n```html\n<!-- 3. In views/home.ejs: Reusable Partials + Dynamic EJS Loop -->\n<%- include('partials/head') %>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <%- include('partials/nav') %>\n    <main class="container mx-auto mt-8">\n      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-red-500">Here are our registered homes:</h2>\n      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2">\n        <% registeredHomes.forEach(home => { %>\n          <li class="p-4 bg-white shadow rounded-lg">\n            <span class="font-semibold"><%= home.houseName %></span> — ₹<%= home.price %> / night\n          </li>\n        <% }) %>\n      </ul>\n    </main>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```	Complete Coding by Prashant Sir	https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir	24	Lecture 12: Dynamic Server-Side UI with EJS — Template Engines (app.set("view engine", "ejs")), res.render(), EJS Tags & Reusable Partials	Render dynamic, personalized server-side HTML using EJS (Embedded JavaScript Templates), pass runtime data into res.render(), use EJS tags (<%= %>, <% %>, <%- %>), and eliminate duplicate markup with EJS Partials.	20	Easy	\N	12	[{"id": "node-lec-12-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What is the difference between <%= value %>, <%- html %>, and <% code %> in EJS?", "title": "Q1: What is the difference between <%= value %>, <%- html %>, and <% code %> in EJS?", "answer": "1) <%= value %> evaluates the JavaScript expression and outputs the result as HTML-escaped text (converting <, >, & into safe HTML entities to prevent XSS attacks)—used for displaying dynamic variables like <%= home.houseName %>. 2) <%- html %> outputs the unescaped raw HTML string—used when including partials via <%- include(\\"partials/nav\\") %>. 3) <% code %> is a scriptlet tag that executes control-flow JavaScript (such as if/else conditions or registeredHomes.forEach() loops) without outputting anything to the HTML.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "12-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) <%= value %> evaluates the JavaScript expression and outputs the result as HTML-escaped text (converting <, >, & into safe HTML entities to prevent XSS attacks)—used for displaying dynamic variables like <%= home.houseName %>. 2) <%- html %> outputs the unescaped raw HTML string—used when including partials via <%- include(\\"partials/nav\\") %>. 3) <% code %> is a scriptlet tag that executes control-flow JavaScript (such as if/else conditions or registeredHomes.forEach() loops) without outputting anything 