30	OOP Design Principles & SOLID Concepts in Java	oop-solid-java	Encapsulation, Polymorphism, Abstraction, and the 5 SOLID engineering principles.	2026-09-27 22:28:22.385996+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: OOP Design Principles & SOLID Concepts in Java\nPart of the **Core Java & Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=OOP%20Design%20Principles%20%26%20SOLID%20Concepts%20in%20Java%20tutorial", "title": "OOP Design Principles & SOLID Concepts in Java Full Guide", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"type": "assignment", "title": "Build & Test a Working Implementation of OOP Design Principles & SOLID Concepts in Java", "checklist": ["Implement core logic with validation and boundary checks", "Write tests verifying expected output and edge cases"], "description": "Implement a production-grade module demonstrating the core principles of OOP Design Principles & SOLID Concepts in Java.", "starterCodeUrl": ""}]	\N	\N	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	15	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
7669	Bounded-Capacity 3D Stock DP: Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock III (At Most 2 Transactions)	dsa-best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-iii-at-most-2-transactions	Bound stock trading to at most 2 transactions by adding a capacity counter cap that decrements upon selling, solvable in O(1) space.	2026-10-03 11:39:06.546528+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Bounded-Capacity 3D Stock DP: Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock III (At Most 2 Transactions)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-uQGzhYj8BQ", "title": "Lecture 217: Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock III (DP 37)", "duration": "24m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-iii/", "title": "Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock III (LC 123)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find the maximum profit you can achieve when you may complete at most two transactions."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-iv/", "title": "Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock IV (LC 188)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Generalize the 3D (ind, buy, cap) state from cap = 2 to cap = k transactions."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/buy-and-sell-a-share-at-most-twice/1", "title": "Buy and Sell a Share at most twice", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Calculate the maximum profit from at most 2 stock buy-sell transactions."}]	Module 32: Dynamic Programming on Stocks: State Machines, Transaction Caps, Cooldowns & Fees	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array `prices` of length `N`, find the maximum profit you can achieve if you are allowed to complete **at most `2` transactions** (a transaction is a complete `Buy + Sell` pair, and you cannot hold multiple shares simultaneously).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Adding the 3rd State Parameter `cap` (`(ind, buy, cap)`):**\n  - Extend Stock II's `(ind, buy)` state with **`cap`** (`2, 1, 0`), representing how many transactions we have left!\n  - **When does `cap` decrement (`cap - 1`)?** A transaction is only completed when we **SELL** a stock!\n    - When **Buying (`buy == 1`)**: `cap` stays **`cap`** (`-prices[ind] + f(ind + 1, 0, cap)`).\n    - When **Selling (`buy == 0`)**: `cap` becomes **`cap - 1`** (`+prices[ind] + f(ind + 1, 1, cap - 1)`)!\n  - **Base Cases:** If `ind == N` OR **`cap == 0`**, return `0`.\n- **Alternative `N x 4` Transaction-Number Method (`tranNo = 0, 1, 2, 3`):**\n  - Instead of `(buy, cap)`, track a single integer `tranNo` from `0` to `3` (`0: Buy1, 1: Sell1, 2: Buy2, 3: Sell2`). Even `tranNo` (`tranNo % 2 == 0`) means **Buy**, odd `tranNo` means **Sell**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * 2 * 3)` Time and **`O(2 * 3) = O(1)` Auxiliary Space** (using `after[2][3]` and `cur[2][3]`).\n\n### 💻 Buy and Sell Stock III (Constant O(1) Space DP) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint maxProfitStockIII(vector<int> &prices) {\n    int n = prices.size();\n    // 2 x 3 constant-size tables: buy in {0, 1}, cap in {0, 1, 2}\n    vector<vector<int>> after(2, vector<int>(3, 0));\n    vector<vector<int>> cur(2, vector<int>(3, 0));\n\n    for (int ind = n - 1; ind >= 0; ind--) {\n        for (int buy = 0; buy <= 1; buy++) {\n            // cap == 0 is always 0 (base case), so loop cap from 1 to 2\n            for (int cap = 1; cap <= 2; cap++) {\n                if (buy == 1) {\n                    cur[buy][cap] = max(-prices[ind] + after[0][cap],\n                                         0 + after[1][cap]);\n                } else {\n                    // Selling completes 1 transaction -> cap - 1!\n                    cur[buy][cap] = max(prices[ind] + after[1][cap - 1],\n                                        0 + after[0][cap]);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        after = cur;\n    }\n    return after[1][2]; // Start at day 0 with buy = 1 and cap = 2\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Decrementing `cap` on Both Buy and Sell:** A single transaction consists of **both** a buy and a sell. If you decrement `cap` on buy *and* on sell, `cap = 2` will only allow 1 full transaction! Decrement `cap - 1` **only when selling**.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22182	Pipes and Cisterns	aptitude-quant-pipes-and-cisterns	Master the "1-Hour Work" inversion trick, multi-tap scheduling, leak/emptying deductions, and complex timeline shifts when pipes are closed prematurely.	2026-10-07 11:28:34.878899+05:30	### 📌 1. The Core Inversion Rule\n* **Filling/Emptying Rate:** If a pipe can completely fill (or empty) a tank in **`N` hours**, the amount of tank it fills (or empties) in **1 hour** is exactly **`1/N`**.\n* Conversely, if the total combined 1-hour work of all open pipes is **`1/T`**, the entire tank will be full (or empty) in exactly **`T` hours**.\n\n### 💡 2. Filling vs. Emptying (The Sign Convention)\n* **Inlet Pipes (Filling):** Add their 1-hour work (**`+ 1/x`**).\n* **Outlet Pipes / Leaks (Emptying):** Subtract their 1-hour work (**`- 1/y`**).\n* **Net 1-Hour Work:** If an inlet fills in `X` hours and a leak empties in `Y` hours, their combined 1-hour work is **`(1/X) - (1/Y)`**.\n  * If the result is positive, the tank is slowly filling.\n  * If the result is negative, the tank is slowly emptying.\n\n### 💡 3. High-Yield Placement Shortcuts\n* **Pipes Closed Mid-Way (Line Diagram Method):**\n  * If Pipe A and B run together for `t` minutes, and then B is closed, calculate the fractional work done in the first `t` minutes: `t × (1/A + 1/B)`.\n  * Subtract this from `1` to find the remaining work, which is done solely by A.\n* **Alternating Pipes:**\n  * If Pipe A runs in the 1st hour and Pipe B runs in the 2nd hour, treat a **2-hour block** as a single cycle. Calculate the work done in 1 cycle, find the maximum integer number of cycles that fit below `1` (total work), and then step hour-by-hour for the remainder.	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mBtBD1N7ywQ", "type": "youtube", "title": "Pipes and Cisterns - Shortcuts & Tricks for 2026 Placement Tests, Job Interviews & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-pipe-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Three Pipes Combined Rate] Pipes M and N fill a tank in 22.5 min and 15 min respectively. If both are opened, after how much time should N be closed so the tank is full in 18 minutes?", "options": ["A) 3 minutes", "B) 4 minutes", "C) 5 minutes", "D) 6 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two pipes M and N can fill a tank in 22.5 minutes and 15 minutes respectively. If both pipes are opened simultaneously, exactly after how many minutes should pipe N be closed so that the tank is completely filled in exactly 18 minutes from the start?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Pipe M's 1-min work = 1 / 22.5 = 2/45. Pipe N's 1-min work = 1/15. Pipe M is kept open for the entire 18 minutes. Work done by M in 18 minutes = 18 × (2/45) = 36/45 = 4/5 of the tank. The remaining work = 1 - 4/5 = 1/5 of the tank. This 1/5 was filled exclusively by pipe N before it was closed. Time N was open = (Work / Rate) = (1/5) / (1/15) = 15/5 = 3 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Leak Extension Time] A tap fills a tub in 24 hours. Due to a leak, it fills in 36 hours. If the tub is full, how long will the leak take to empty it?", "options": ["A) 48 hours", "B) 60 hours", "C) 72 hours", "D) 96 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A tap can normally fill a tub in 24 hours. Due to a leak at the bottom, the tap now takes 36 hours to fill the tub. If the tub is completely full and the tap is closed, how much time will the leak alone take to empty the tub?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1-hour work of Tap = 1/24. 1-hour net work (Tap + Leak) = 1/36. Therefore, (1-hour work of Tap) - (1-hour work of Leak) = 1/36 => 1/24 - 1/L = 1/36 => 1/L = 1/24 - 1/36 = (3 - 2)/72 = 1/72. So the leak empties 1/72 of the tub in 1 hour. It will take 72 hours to empty the full tub.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Accidental Third Pipe Opened] P and Q fill in 15h and 10h. R empties in 30h. All 3 opened. After 2h, R is closed. How much EXTRA time is needed?", "options": ["A) 12 minutes", "B) 18 minutes", "C) 24 minutes", "D) 30 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipes P and Q can fill a tank in 15 and 10 hours respectively. Pipe R can empty the full tank in 30 hours. Ram unknowingly opened all three pipes. After 2 hours, Shyam realized the mistake and closed pipe R. Due to this mistake, how much MORE time in total does it take to fill the tank compared to the normal time?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Normal Time (P and Q only): 1-hr work = 1/15 + 1/10 = 5/30 = 1/6. Normal time = 6 hours. 2) Abnormal Time: For 2 hours, P, Q, and R are open. 1-hr work = 1/15 + 1/10 - 1/30 = 4/30 = 2/15. In 2 hours, they fill 2 × (2/15) = 4/15. 3) Remaining work = 1 - 4/15 = 11/15. 4) Now R is closed, so P and Q fill the remaining 11/15 at their normal rate of 1/6 per hour. Time for remainder = (11/15) / (1/6) = 66 / 15 = 4.4 hours. 5) Total abnormal time = 2 + 4.4 = 6.4 hours. 6) Extra time = 6.4 - 6.0 = 0.4 hours = 0.4 × 60 = 24 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Efficiency and Time Difference] Pipe A fills 5 times faster than B and takes 32 mins less. Find time if both opened together.", "options": ["A) 5 1/3 minutes", "B) 6 2/3 minutes", "C) 8 minutes", "D) 10 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipe A can fill a tank 5 times faster than Pipe B and therefore takes 32 minutes less than Pipe B to fill the tank when acting alone. If both pipes are opened together, in exactly how many minutes will the tank be full?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Since A is 5 times faster, B takes 5 times as long. Let Time_A = T, then Time_B = 5T. Given Time_B - Time_A = 32 => 5T - T = 32 => 4T = 32 => T = 8 mins. So A takes 8 mins, B takes 40 mins. 1-min work together = 1/8 + 1/40 = 6/40 = 3/20. Total time = 20/3 = 6 2/3 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [System of Unknown Rates] System filled by P and Q in 6h. P, Q, and R fill in 5h. P fills twice as fast as Q. Find time for Q and R together.", "options": ["A) 10 1/4 hours", "B) 11 1/4 hours", "C) 12 1/2 hours", "D) 13 1/3 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A cistern can be filled in 6 hours by taps P and Q working together. If tap R also joins them, the cistern is filled in 5 hours. If tap P can fill the cistern at exactly twice the rate of tap Q, in what exact time will taps Q and R working together fill the cistern?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) 1/P + 1/Q = 1/6. 2) 1/P + 1/Q + 1/R = 1/5 => 1/6 + 1/R = 1/5 => 1/R = 1/5 - 1/6 = 1/30. 3) P is twice as fast as Q => 1/P = 2/Q. Substitute into (1): 2/Q + 1/Q = 1/6 => 3/Q = 1/6 => 1/Q = 1/18. 4) We need the time for Q and R together: 1/Q + 1/R = 1/18 + 1/30. LCM of 18 and 30 is 90. 5/90 + 3/90 = 8/90 = 4/45. Total time = 45 / 4 = 11.25 hours = 11 1/4 hours.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Capacity of System from Flow Rate] A and B fill in 2.4 hours. B alone takes 4 hours. If A flows at 100 L/hr, what is the capacity?", "options": ["A) 480 Liters", "B) 500 Liters", "C) 600 Liters", "D) 720 Liters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A cistern is filled by pipe A and pipe B together in 2.4 hours. Pipe B alone can fill the cistern in exactly 4 hours. If pipe A alone pumps water at the constant rate of 100 liters per hour, what is the total capacity of the cistern?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1-hr combined work = 1/2.4 = 10/24 = 5/12. B's 1-hr work = 1/4. A's 1-hr work = 5/12 - 1/4 = 5/12 - 3/12 = 2/12 = 1/6. Thus, pipe A alone takes 6 hours to fill the tank. Since A fills 100 liters in 1 hour, in 6 hours it fills 600 liters. Total capacity = 600 Liters.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Alternating Pipes Exact Minute] Pipe A fills in 20 min, B empties in 30 min. They open alternately for 1 min each. When will it be full?", "options": ["A) 115 minutes", "B) 116 minutes", "C) 120 minutes", "D) 121 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipe A can fill a tank in 20 minutes, while Pipe B can empty the full tank in 30 minutes. Starting with Pipe A, they are opened alternately for exactly 1 minute each. In exactly how many minutes will the tank be full for the first time?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "A's 1-min work = +1/20 = +3/60. B's 1-min work = -1/30 = -2/60. In 1 cycle of 2 minutes, net work = 3/60 - 2/60 = 1/60. Caution: Do NOT just multiply by 60! The tank is considered full the moment it reaches the top (during A's turn). We must reserve the last cycle for A. A fills 3/60 in its last minute. So we cycle until the work done is 1 - 3/60 = 57/60. Since 1 cycle (2 mins) fills 1/60, it takes 57 cycles = 114 minutes to fill 57/60. In the 115th minute, A opens and fills the remaining 3/60 perfectly. Total time = 114 + 1 = 115 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Three Pipes with Flow Rates] Pipes A, B, C fill a tank. A and B fill in 20 and 30 hours. C empties 25 L/min. If all open, it takes 24 hours to fill. Find capacity.", "options": ["A) 18,000 Liters", "B) 24,000 Liters", "C) 36,000 Liters", "D) 45,000 Liters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipes A and B can fill a tank in 20 hours and 30 hours respectively. Pipe C is an emptying pipe that drains water at a constant rate of 25 liters per minute. If all three pipes are opened together, the tank is completely filled in 24 hours. What is the total capacity of the tank?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1-hr work of (A + B + C) = 1/24. 1/20 + 1/30 + 1/C = 1/24. LCM of 20, 30 is 60: 3/60 + 2/60 = 5/60 = 1/12. So 1/12 + 1/C = 1/24 => 1/C = 1/24 - 1/12 = -1/24. Thus, pipe C alone can empty the tank in exactly 24 hours. C's drain rate = 25 L/min = 25 × 60 = 1,500 L/hr. Total capacity = 1,500 L/hr × 24 hours = 36,000 Liters.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Two Filling, One Emptying Cycle] Pipes A, B fill in 10 and 15 mins. C empties in 12 mins. A, B, C opened alternately for 1 min each. Find time.", "options": ["A) 30 minutes", "B) 32 minutes", "C) 35 minutes", "D) 40 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipes A and B can fill a tank in 10 and 15 minutes respectively. Pipe C can empty the full tank in 12 minutes. The three pipes are opened alternately for exactly 1 minute each, starting with A, then B, then C. In how many exact minutes will the tank be full for the first time?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Rates per minute: A = +6/60, B = +4/60, C = -5/60. In 1 cycle of 3 mins, net work = 6/60 + 4/60 - 5/60 = 5/60. Target before final positive fill = 1 - 10/60 = 50/60. 10 cycles (30 mins) fills exactly 10 × (5/60) = 50/60 of the tank. In the 31st minute, pipe A opens and adds 6/60, bringing total to 56/60. In the 32nd minute, pipe B opens and adds 4/60, bringing total to 60/60 (completely full!). The tank is full in exactly 32 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Multiple Leaks] A cistern has 3 leaks. The first two alone empty in 12 and 15 mins. The third alone in 20 mins. If all open, how long to empty?", "options": ["A) 4 minutes", "B) 5 minutes", "C) 6 minutes", "D) 8 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A cistern is completely full of water. It has three leaks at its base. The first leak alone can empty the cistern in 12 minutes, the second alone in 15 minutes, and the third alone in 20 minutes. If all three leaks start simultaneously, in how many minutes will the cistern be completely emptied?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Combined emptying rate = 1/12 + 1/15 + 1/20. LCM of 12, 15, 20 is 60. Rate = 5/60 + 4/60 + 3/60 = 12/60 = 1/5. Total time = 5 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Pipe Re-opened] A and B fill in 20 and 30 mins. Both are opened. B is closed after some time. Tank fills in 18 mins. When was B closed?", "options": ["A) 3 minutes", "B) 6 minutes", "C) 8 minutes", "D) 9 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipe A can fill a tank in 20 minutes and Pipe B can fill it in 30 minutes. Both pipes are opened together. Pipe B is closed after some time, and the entire tank is subsequently filled in exactly 18 minutes from the start. After how many minutes was Pipe B closed?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Pipe A operates for the entire 18 minutes. Work done by A = 18 × (1/20) = 18/20 = 9/10. Remaining work = 1 - 9/10 = 1/10. This 1/10 was filled exclusively by B before it was closed. Time B was open = (1/10) / (1/30) = 30 / 10 = 3 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Cistern Filling with Delayed Starts] Tap P fills in 15h, Q in 20h. P opened at 8 AM, Q at 10 AM. When will it be full?", "options": ["A) 4:00 PM", "B) 4:34 PM", "C) 5:25 PM", "D) 6:15 PM"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Tap P can fill a tank in 15 hours and Tap Q can fill it in 20 hours. Tap P is opened at 8:00 AM and Tap Q is opened at 10:00 AM. At what exact time will the tank be completely full?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "From 8 AM to 10 AM (2 hours), only P is open. Work done by P = 2 × (1/15) = 2/15. Remaining work = 13/15. From 10 AM, both are open. Combined rate = 1/15 + 1/20 = 4/60 + 3/60 = 7/60. Time taken for remaining work = (13/15) / (7/60) = 13/15 × 60/7 = 13 × 4 / 7 = 52/7 hours = 7 hours and 3/7 hours. 3/7 hours ≈ 25.7 minutes. Time = 10:00 AM + 7 hours 25.7 mins ≈ 5:25 PM.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Middle Leak with Proportional Drainage] A leak at the BOTTOM empties in 12h. A second leak at exactly HALF the height empties the top half in 10h. If tank is full, how long to empty?", "options": ["A) 9 hours 45 mins", "B) 10 hours 20 mins", "C) 8 hours 40 mins", "D) 9 hours 30 mins"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A tank is full of water. A leak exactly at the bottom can empty the whole tank in 12 hours. A second leak exactly at half the height of the tank can empty the top half of the tank in 10 hours. If both leaks are active, how long will it take to completely empty the full tank?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Bottom leak (L1) empties the whole tank at rate 1/12 per hour. 2) Middle leak (L2) empties the TOP HALF (Volume = 1/2) in 10 hours, so its rate for the whole tank is (1/2)/10 = 1/20 per hour. 3) Top half emptying: Both L1 and L2 are active. Combined rate = 1/12 + 1/20 = 5/60 + 3/60 = 8/60 = 2/15. Volume to empty = 1/2. Time for top half = (1/2) / (2/15) = 15/4 = 3.75 hours. 4) Bottom half emptying: Only L1 is active (water level is below L2). Volume to empty = 1/2. Rate = 1/12. Time = (1/2) / (1/12) = 6 hours. 5) Total time = 3.75 + 6 = 9.75 hours = 9 hours 45 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Fractions of Tank Filled] Pipe A fills 1/3 of tank in 5 min. Pipe B fills 1/4 in 5 min. If both open, time to fill 7/16 of tank?", "options": ["A) 3 1/2 minutes", "B) 3 3/4 minutes", "C) 4 1/8 minutes", "D) 4 3/4 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipe A can fill 1/3 of a tank in exactly 5 minutes, while Pipe B can fill 1/4 of the same tank in exactly 5 minutes. If both pipes are opened together, how many minutes will they take to fill exactly 7/16 of the tank?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "A's time for whole tank = 5 / (1/3) = 15 mins (rate = 1/15). B's time for whole tank = 5 / (1/4) = 20 mins (rate = 1/20). Combined rate = 1/15 + 1/20 = 4/60 + 3/60 = 7/60 per minute. Time to fill 7/16 of tank = Work / Rate = (7/16) / (7/60) = (7/16) × (60/7) = 60/16 = 15/4 = 3 3/4 minutes (3.75 minutes).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Efficiency Given as Percentage] A tap fills 20% more efficiently than a second tap. Together they fill in 30 mins. Find time for second tap alone.", "options": ["A) 54 minutes", "B) 60 minutes", "C) 66 minutes", "D) 72 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Tap A is 20% more efficient than Tap B in filling a tank. If both taps are opened together, they can fill the tank completely in exactly 30 minutes. How many minutes would Tap B take to fill the tank if opened alone?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Efficiency of A = 1.2 × Efficiency of B. So Ratio of Rates (A : B) = 1.2 : 1 = 6 : 5. Let B's rate be 5k and A's rate be 6k. Combined rate = 11k. We know Combined Rate = 1 / 30. So 11k = 1/30 => k = 1/330. Rate of B = 5k = 5 / 330 = 1 / 66. Thus, Tap B alone takes 66 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Invert Trick with Three Pipes] Pipes A and B fill in 10 and 12 mins. C empties. All 3 take 15 mins to fill. Find C's emptying time.", "options": ["A) 8 4/7 minutes", "B) 10 1/2 minutes", "C) 11 2/3 minutes", "D) 12 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipes A and B can fill a tank in 10 minutes and 12 minutes respectively. Pipe C is an emptying pipe. When all three pipes are opened together, the tank is completely filled in 15 minutes. In how many exact minutes can Pipe C alone empty the completely full tank?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Combined rate 1/A + 1/B - 1/C = 1/15. We have 1/10 + 1/12 - 1/C = 1/15. LCM of 10, 12 is 60: 6/60 + 5/60 - 1/C = 1/15 => 11/60 - 1/C = 4/60 => 1/C = 11/60 - 4/60 = 7/60. Thus, C takes 60/7 minutes = 8 4/7 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Pipe Radii Proportional to Area] Two pipes have diameters 1 cm and 2 cm. Flow is proportional to cross-section area. If smaller pipe fills in 40 mins, find time for both.", "options": ["A) 8 minutes", "B) 10 minutes", "C) 12 minutes", "D) 13.33 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two pipes have internal diameters of 1 cm and 2 cm respectively. The volume of water flowing through them per minute is strictly proportional to the cross-sectional area of the pipes. If the smaller pipe alone can fill a tank in 40 minutes, in how many minutes will both pipes working together fill the tank?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Flow rate ∝ Area ∝ (Diameter)^2. Ratio of Diameters = 1 : 2. Ratio of Areas = 1^2 : 2^2 = 1 : 4. So the larger pipe is 4 times as efficient as the smaller pipe! Rate of smaller pipe = 1/40. Rate of larger pipe = 4 × (1/40) = 4/40. Combined rate = 1/40 + 4/40 = 5/40 = 1/8. Both together fill the tank in exactly 8 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Proportion of Volume Left] A tank is 3/4 full. Pipe A fills in 12 min, Pipe B empties in 8 min. Both opened. When will it be empty?", "options": ["A) 16 minutes", "B) 18 minutes", "C) 20 minutes", "D) 24 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A tank is currently exactly 3/4 full of water. Pipe A can fill the whole tank in 12 minutes, and Pipe B can empty the whole tank in 8 minutes. If both pipes are opened simultaneously, in how many minutes will the tank become completely empty?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Combined rate = +1/12 (filling) - 1/8 (emptying) = 2/24 - 3/24 = -1/24. The negative sign means the tank is emptying at a net rate of 1/24 of the tank per minute. The amount of water currently in the tank is 3/4. Time to empty = Volume / Rate = (3/4) / (1/24) = 3/4 × 24 = 18 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [System of 3 Equations] Pipes A, B fill in 20 min. B, C fill in 30 min. C, A fill in 40 min. Find time for A alone.", "options": ["A) 40 minutes", "B) 48 minutes", "C) 52 minutes", "D) 60 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipes A and B can fill a cistern in 20 minutes. Pipes B and C can fill it in 30 minutes. Pipes C and A can fill it in 40 minutes. How many minutes will Pipe A alone take to fill the entire cistern?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1/A + 1/B = 1/20; 1/B + 1/C = 1/30; 1/C + 1/A = 1/40. Add all three: 2(1/A + 1/B + 1/C) = 1/20 + 1/30 + 1/40. LCM of 20, 30, 40 is 120. Sum = 6/120 + 4/120 + 3/120 = 13/120. So 1/A + 1/B + 1/C = 13/240. We need A, so subtract (1/B + 1/C): 1/A = 13/240 - 1/30 = 13/240 - 8/240 = 5/240 = 1/48. Thus A takes exactly 48 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Equal Time Filling and Emptying Phase] A fills in 10h, B empties in 15h. A is opened. B is opened after 4h. Find total time.", "options": ["A) 16 hours", "B) 18 hours", "C) 20 hours", "D) 22 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipe A can fill a tank in 10 hours, and Pipe B can empty the full tank in 15 hours. Pipe A is opened initially. Exactly 4 hours later, Pipe B is also opened. What is the total time required from the start to completely fill the tank?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "For the first 4 hours, only A is open. Work done by A = 4 × (1/10) = 4/10 = 2/5. Remaining work = 1 - 2/5 = 3/5. From 4 hours onward, both A and B are open. Combined rate = 1/10 - 1/15 = 3/30 - 2/30 = 1/30. Time to fill the remaining 3/5 at rate 1/30 = (3/5) / (1/30) = (3/5) × 30 = 18 hours. Total time from the start = 4 (initial) + 18 (combined) = 22 hours.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. The Core Inversion Rule\n* **Filling/Emptying Rate:** If a pipe can completely fill (or empty) a tank in **`N` hours**, the amount of tank it fills (or empties) in **1 hour** is exactly **`1/N`**.\n* Conversely, if the total combined 1-hour work of all open pipes is **`1/T`**, the entire tank will be full (or empty) in exactly **`T` hours**.\n\n### 💡 2. Filling vs. Emptying (The Sign Convention)\n* **Inlet Pipes (Filling):** Add their 1-hour work (**`+ 1/x`**).\n* **Outlet Pipes / Leaks (Emptying):** Subtract their 1-hour work (**`- 1/y`**).\n* **Net 1-Hour Work:** If an inlet fills in `X` hours and a leak empties in `Y` hours, their combined 1-hour work is **`(1/X) - (1/Y)`**.\n  * If the result is positive, the tank is slowly filling.\n  * If the result is negative, the tank is slowly emptying.\n\n### 💡 3. High-Yield Placement Shortcuts\n* **Pipes Closed Mid-Way (Line Diagram Method):**\n  * If Pipe A and B run together for `t` minutes, and then B is closed, calculate the fractional work done in the first `t` minutes: `t × (1/A + 1/B)`.\n  * Subtract this from `1` to find the remaining work, which is done solely by A.\n* **Alternating Pipes:**\n  * If Pipe A runs in the 1st hour and Pipe B runs in the 2nd hour, treat a **2-hour block** as a single cycle. Calculate the work done in 1 cycle, find the maximum integer number of cycles that fit below `1` (total work), and then step hour-by-hour for the remainder.	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Pipes and Cisterns	Master the "1-Hour Work" inversion trick, multi-tap scheduling, leak/emptying deductions, and complex timeline shifts when pipes are closed prematurely.	47	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	16	[{"id": "apt-pipe-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Three Pipes Combined Rate] Pipes M and N fill a tank in 22.5 min and 15 min respectively. If both are opened, after how much time should N be closed so the tank is full in 18 minutes?", "options": ["A) 3 minutes", "B) 4 minutes", "C) 5 minutes", "D) 6 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two pipes M and N can fill a tank in 22.5 minutes and 15 minutes respectively. If both pipes are opened simultaneously, exactly after how many minutes should pipe N be closed so that the tank is completely filled in exactly 18 minutes from the start?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Pipe M's 1-min work = 1 / 22.5 = 2/45. Pipe N's 1-min work = 1/15. Pipe M is kept open for the entire 18 minutes. Work done by M in 18 minutes = 18 × (2/45) = 36/45 = 4/5 of the tank. The remaining work = 1 - 4/5 = 1/5 of the tank. This 1/5 was filled exclusively by pipe N before it was closed. Time N was open = (Work / Rate) = (1/5) / (1/15) = 15/5 = 3 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Leak Extension Time] A tap fills a tub in 24 hours. Due to a leak, it fills in 36 hours. If the tub is full, how long will the leak take to empty it?", "options": ["A) 48 hours", "B) 60 hours", "C) 72 hours", "D) 96 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A tap can normally fill a tub in 24 hours. Due to a leak at the bottom, the tap now takes 36 hours to fill the tub. If the tub is completely full and the tap is closed, how much time will the leak alone take to empty the tub?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1-hour work of Tap = 1/24. 1-hour net work (Tap + Leak) = 1/36. Therefore, (1-hour work of Tap) - (1-hour work of Leak) = 1/36 => 1/24 - 1/L = 1/36 => 1/L = 1/24 - 1/36 = (3 - 2)/72 = 1/72. So the leak empties 1/72 of the tub in 1 hour. It will take 72 hours to empty the full tub.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Accidental Third Pipe Opened] P and Q fill in 15h and 10h. R empties in 30h. All 3 opened. After 2h, R is closed. How much EXTRA time is needed?", "options": ["A) 12 minutes", "B) 18 minutes", "C) 24 minutes", "D) 30 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipes P and Q can fill a tank in 15 and 10 hours respectively. Pipe R can empty the full tank in 30 hours. Ram unknowingly opened all three pipes. After 2 hours, Shyam realized the mistake and closed pipe R. Due to this mistake, how much MORE time in total does it take to fill the tank compared to the normal time?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Normal Time (P and Q only): 1-hr work = 1/15 + 1/10 = 5/30 = 1/6. Normal time = 6 hours. 2) Abnormal Time: For 2 hours, P, Q, and R are open. 1-hr work = 1/15 + 1/10 - 1/30 = 4/30 = 2/15. In 2 hours, they fill 2 × (2/15) = 4/15. 3) Remaining work = 1 - 4/15 = 11/15. 4) Now R is closed, so P and Q fill the remaining 11/15 at their normal rate of 1/6 per hour. Time for remainder = (11/15) / (1/6) = 66 / 15 = 4.4 hours. 5) Total abnormal time = 2 + 4.4 = 6.4 hours. 6) Extra time = 6.4 - 6.0 = 0.4 hours = 0.4 × 60 = 24 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Efficiency and Time Difference] Pipe A fills 5 times faster than B and takes 32 mins less. Find time if both opened together.", "options": ["A) 5 1/3 minutes", "B) 6 2/3 minutes", "C) 8 minutes", "D) 10 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipe A can fill a tank 5 times faster than Pipe B and therefore takes 32 minutes less than Pipe B to fill the tank when acting alone. If both pipes are opened together, in exactly how many minutes will the tank be full?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Since A is 5 times faster, B takes 5 times as long. Let Time_A = T, then Time_B = 5T. Given Time_B - Time_A = 32 => 5T - T = 32 => 4T = 32 => T = 8 mins. So A takes 8 mins, B takes 40 mins. 1-min work together = 1/8 + 1/40 = 6/40 = 3/20. Total time = 20/3 = 6 2/3 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [System of Unknown Rates] System filled by P and Q in 6h. P, Q, and R fill in 5h. P fills twice as fast as Q. Find time for Q and R together.", "options": ["A) 10 1/4 hours", "B) 11 1/4 hours", "C) 12 1/2 hours", "D) 13 1/3 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A cistern can be filled in 6 hours by taps P and Q working together. If tap R also joins them, the cistern is filled in 5 hours. If tap P can fill the cistern at exactly twice the rate of tap Q, in what exact time will taps Q and R working together fill the cistern?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) 1/P + 1/Q = 1/6. 2) 1/P + 1/Q + 1/R = 1/5 => 1/6 + 1/R = 1/5 => 1/R = 1/5 - 1/6 = 1/30. 3) P is twice as fast as Q => 1/P = 2/Q. Substitute into (1): 2/Q + 1/Q = 1/6 => 3/Q = 1/6 => 1/Q = 1/18. 4) We need the time for Q and R together: 1/Q + 1/R = 1/18 + 1/30. LCM of 18 and 30 is 90. 5/90 + 3/90 = 8/90 = 4/45. Total time = 45 / 4 = 11.25 hours = 11 1/4 hours.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Capacity of System from Flow Rate] A and B fill in 2.4 hours. B alone takes 4 hours. If A flows at 100 L/hr, what is the capacity?", "options": ["A) 480 Liters", "B) 500 Liters", "C) 600 Liters", "D) 720 Liters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A cistern is filled by pipe A and pipe B together in 2.4 hours. Pipe B alone can fill the cistern in exactly 4 hours. If pipe A alone pumps water at the constant rate of 100 liters per hour, what is the total capacity of the cistern?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1-hr combined work = 1/2.4 = 10/24 = 5/12. B's 1-hr work = 1/4. A's 1-hr work = 5/12 - 1/4 = 5/12 - 3/12 = 2/12 = 1/6. Thus, pipe A alone takes 6 hours to fill the tank. Since A fills 100 liters in 1 hour, in 6 hours it fills 600 liters. Total capacity = 600 Liters.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Alternating Pipes Exact Minute] Pipe A fills in 20 min, B empties in 30 min. They open alternately for 1 min each. When will it be full?", "options": ["A) 115 minutes", "B) 116 minutes", "C) 120 minutes", "D) 121 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipe A can fill a tank in 20 minutes, while Pipe B can empty the full tank in 30 minutes. Starting with Pipe A, they are opened alternately for exactly 1 minute each. In exactly how many minutes will the tank be full for the first time?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "A's 1-min work = +1/20 = +3/60. B's 1-min work = -1/30 = -2/60. In 1 cycle of 2 minutes, net work = 3/60 - 2/60 = 1/60. Caution: Do NOT just multiply by 60! The tank is considered full the moment it reaches the top (during A's turn). We must reserve the last cycle for A. A fills 3/60 in its last minute. So we cycle until the work done is 1 - 3/60 = 57/60. Since 1 cycle (2 mins) fills 1/60, it takes 57 cycles = 114 minutes to fill 57/60. In the 115th minute, A opens and fills the remaining 3/60 perfectly. Total time = 114 + 1 = 115 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Three Pipes with Flow Rates] Pipes A, B, C fill a tank. A and B fill in 20 and 30 hours. C empties 25 L/min. If all open, it takes 24 hours to fill. Find capacity.", "options": ["A) 18,000 Liters", "B) 24,000 Liters", "C) 36,000 Liters", "D) 45,000 Liters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipes A and B can fill a tank in 20 hours and 30 hours respectively. Pipe C is an emptying pipe that drains water at a constant rate of 25 liters per minute. If all three pipes are opened together, the tank is completely filled in 24 hours. What is the total capacity of the tank?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1-hr work of (A + B + C) = 1/24. 1/20 + 1/30 + 1/C = 1/24. LCM of 20, 30 is 60: 3/60 + 2/60 = 5/60 = 1/12. So 1/12 + 1/C = 1/24 => 1/C = 1/24 - 1/12 = -1/24. Thus, pipe C alone can empty the tank in exactly 24 hours. C's drain rate = 25 L/min = 25 × 60 = 1,500 L/hr. Total capacity = 1,500 L/hr × 24 hours = 36,000 Liters.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Two Filling, One Emptying Cycle] Pipes A, B fill in 10 and 15 mins. C empties in 12 mins. A, B, C opened alternately for 1 min each. Find time.", "options": ["A) 30 minutes", "B) 32 minutes", "C) 35 minutes", "D) 40 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipes A and B can fill a tank in 10 and 15 minutes respectively. Pipe C can empty the full tank in 12 minutes. The three pipes are opened alternately for exactly 1 minute each, starting with A, then B, then C. In how many exact minutes will the tank be full for the first time?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Rates per minute: A = +6/60, B = +4/60, C = -5/60. In 1 cycle of 3 mins, net work = 6/60 + 4/60 - 5/60 = 5/60. Target before final positive fill = 1 - 10/60 = 50/60. 10 cycles (30 mins) fills exactly 10 × (5/60) = 50/60 of the tank. In the 31st minute, pipe A opens and adds 6/60, bringing total to 56/60. In the 32nd minute, pipe B opens and adds 4/60, bringing total to 60/60 (completely full!). The tank is full in exactly 32 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Multiple Leaks] A cistern has 3 leaks. The first two alone empty in 12 and 15 mins. The third alone in 20 mins. If all open, how long to empty?", "options": ["A) 4 minutes", "B) 5 minutes", "C) 6 minutes", "D) 8 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A cistern is completely full of water. It has three leaks at its base. The first leak alone can empty the cistern in 12 minutes, the second alone in 15 minutes, and the third alone in 20 minutes. If all three leaks start simultaneously, in how many minutes will the cistern be completely emptied?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Combined emptying rate = 1/12 + 1/15 + 1/20. LCM of 12, 15, 20 is 60. Rate = 5/60 + 4/60 + 3/60 = 12/60 = 1/5. Total time = 5 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Pipe Re-opened] A and B fill in 20 and 30 mins. Both are opened. B is closed after some time. Tank fills in 18 mins. When was B closed?", "options": ["A) 3 minutes", "B) 6 minutes", "C) 8 minutes", "D) 9 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipe A can fill a tank in 20 minutes and Pipe B can fill it in 30 minutes. Both pipes are opened together. Pipe B is closed after some time, and the entire tank is subsequently filled in exactly 18 minutes from the start. After how many minutes was Pipe B closed?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Pipe A operates for the entire 18 minutes. Work done by A = 18 × (1/20) = 18/20 = 9/10. Remaining work = 1 - 9/10 = 1/10. This 1/10 was filled exclusively by B before it was closed. Time B was open = (1/10) / (1/30) = 30 / 10 = 3 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Cistern Filling with Delayed Starts] Tap P fills in 15h, Q in 20h. P opened at 8 AM, Q at 10 AM. When will it be full?", "options": ["A) 4:00 PM", "B) 4:34 PM", "C) 5:25 PM", "D) 6:15 PM"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Tap P can fill a tank in 15 hours and Tap Q can fill it in 20 hours. Tap P is opened at 8:00 AM and Tap Q is opened at 10:00 AM. At what exact time will the tank be completely full?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "From 8 AM to 10 AM (2 hours), only P is open. Work done by P = 2 × (1/15) = 2/15. Remaining work = 13/15. From 10 AM, both are open. Combined rate = 1/15 + 1/20 = 4/60 + 3/60 = 7/60. Time taken for remaining work = (13/15) / (7/60) = 13/15 × 60/7 = 13 × 4 / 7 = 52/7 hours = 7 hours and 3/7 hours. 3/7 hours ≈ 25.7 minutes. Time = 10:00 AM + 7 hours 25.7 mins ≈ 5:25 PM.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Middle Leak with Proportional Drainage] A leak at the BOTTOM empties in 12h. A second leak at exactly HALF the height empties the top half in 10h. If tank is full, how long to empty?", "options": ["A) 9 hours 45 mins", "B) 10 hours 20 mins", "C) 8 hours 40 mins", "D) 9 hours 30 mins"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A tank is full of water. A leak exactly at the bottom can empty the whole tank in 12 hours. A second leak exactly at half the height of the tank can empty the top half of the tank in 10 hours. If both leaks are active, how long will it take to completely empty the full tank?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Bottom leak (L1) empties the whole tank at rate 1/12 per hour. 2) Middle leak (L2) empties the TOP HALF (Volume = 1/2) in 10 hours, so its rate for the whole tank is (1/2)/10 = 1/20 per hour. 3) Top half emptying: Both L1 and L2 are active. Combined rate = 1/12 + 1/20 = 5/60 + 3/60 = 8/60 = 2/15. Volume to empty = 1/2. Time for top half = (1/2) / (2/15) = 15/4 = 3.75 hours. 4) Bottom half emptying: Only L1 is active (water level is below L2). Volume to empty = 1/2. Rate = 1/12. Time = (1/2) / (1/12) = 6 hours. 5) Total time = 3.75 + 6 = 9.75 hours = 9 hours 45 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Fractions of Tank Filled] Pipe A fills 1/3 of tank in 5 min. Pipe B fills 1/4 in 5 min. If both open, time to fill 7/16 of tank?", "options": ["A) 3 1/2 minutes", "B) 3 3/4 minutes", "C) 4 1/8 minutes", "D) 4 3/4 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipe A can fill 1/3 of a tank in exactly 5 minutes, while Pipe B can fill 1/4 of the same tank in exactly 5 minutes. If both pipes are opened together, how many minutes will they take to fill exactly 7/16 of the tank?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "A's time for whole tank = 5 / (1/3) = 15 mins (rate = 1/15). B's time for whole tank = 5 / (1/4) = 20 mins (rate = 1/20). Combined rate = 1/15 + 1/20 = 4/60 + 3/60 = 7/60 per minute. Time to fill 7/16 of tank = Work / Rate = (7/16) / (7/60) = (7/16) × (60/7) = 60/16 = 15/4 = 3 3/4 minutes (3.75 minutes).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Efficiency Given as Percentage] A tap fills 20% more efficiently than a second tap. Together they fill in 30 mins. Find time for second tap alone.", "options": ["A) 54 minutes", "B) 60 minutes", "C) 66 minutes", "D) 72 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Tap A is 20% more efficient than Tap B in filling a tank. If both taps are opened together, they can fill the tank completely in exactly 30 minutes. How many minutes would Tap B take to fill the tank if opened alone?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Efficiency of A = 1.2 × Efficiency of B. So Ratio of Rates (A : B) = 1.2 : 1 = 6 : 5. Let B's rate be 5k and A's rate be 6k. Combined rate = 11k. We know Combined Rate = 1 / 30. So 11k = 1/30 => k = 1/330. Rate of B = 5k = 5 / 330 = 1 / 66. Thus, Tap B alone takes 66 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Invert Trick with Three Pipes] Pipes A and B fill in 10 and 12 mins. C empties. All 3 take 15 mins to fill. Find C's emptying time.", "options": ["A) 8 4/7 minutes", "B) 10 1/2 minutes", "C) 11 2/3 minutes", "D) 12 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipes A and B can fill a tank in 10 minutes and 12 minutes respectively. Pipe C is an emptying pipe. When all three pipes are opened together, the tank is completely filled in 15 minutes. In how many exact minutes can Pipe C alone empty the completely full tank?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Combined rate 1/A + 1/B - 1/C = 1/15. We have 1/10 + 1/12 - 1/C = 1/15. LCM of 10, 12 is 60: 6/60 + 5/60 - 1/C = 1/15 => 11/60 - 1/C = 4/60 => 1/C = 11/60 - 4/60 = 7/60. Thus, C takes 60/7 minutes = 8 4/7 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Pipe Radii Proportional to Area] Two pipes have diameters 1 cm and 2 cm. Flow is proportional to cross-section area. If smaller pipe fills in 40 mins, find time for both.", "options": ["A) 8 minutes", "B) 10 minutes", "C) 12 minutes", "D) 13.33 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two pipes have internal diameters of 1 cm and 2 cm respectively. The volume of water flowing through them per minute is strictly proportional to the cross-sectional area of the pipes. If the smaller pipe alone can fill a tank in 40 minutes, in how many minutes will both pipes working together fill the tank?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Flow rate ∝ Area ∝ (Diameter)^2. Ratio of Diameters = 1 : 2. Ratio of Areas = 1^2 : 2^2 = 1 : 4. So the larger pipe is 4 times as efficient as the smaller pipe! Rate of smaller pipe = 1/40. Rate of larger pipe = 4 × (1/40) = 4/40. Combined rate = 1/40 + 4/40 = 5/40 = 1/8. Both together fill the tank in exactly 8 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Proportion of Volume Left] A tank is 3/4 full. Pipe A fills in 12 min, Pipe B empties in 8 min. Both opened. When will it be empty?", "options": ["A) 16 minutes", "B) 18 minutes", "C) 20 minutes", "D) 24 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A tank is currently exactly 3/4 full of water. Pipe A can fill the whole tank in 12 minutes, and Pipe B can empty the whole tank in 8 minutes. If both pipes are opened simultaneously, in how many minutes will the tank become completely empty?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Combined rate = +1/12 (filling) - 1/8 (emptying) = 2/24 - 3/24 = -1/24. The negative sign means the tank is emptying at a net rate of 1/24 of the tank per minute. The amount of water currently in the tank is 3/4. Time to empty = Volume / Rate = (3/4) / (1/24) = 3/4 × 24 = 18 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [System of 3 Equations] Pipes A, B fill in 20 min. B, C fill in 30 min. C, A fill in 40 min. Find time for A alone.", "options": ["A) 40 minutes", "B) 48 minutes", "C) 52 minutes", "D) 60 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipes A and B can fill a cistern in 20 minutes. Pipes B and C can fill it in 30 minutes. Pipes C and A can fill it in 40 minutes. How many minutes will Pipe A alone take to fill the entire cistern?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1/A + 1/B = 1/20; 1/B + 1/C = 1/30; 1/C + 1/A = 1/40. Add all three: 2(1/A + 1/B + 1/C) = 1/20 + 1/30 + 1/40. LCM of 20, 30, 40 is 120. Sum = 6/120 + 4/120 + 3/120 = 13/120. So 1/A + 1/B + 1/C = 13/240. We need A, so subtract (1/B + 1/C): 1/A = 13/240 - 1/30 = 13/240 - 8/240 = 5/240 = 1/48. Thus A takes exactly 48 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-pipe-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Equal Time Filling and Emptying Phase] A fills in 10h, B empties in 15h. A is opened. B is opened after 4h. Find total time.", "options": ["A) 16 hours", "B) 18 hours", "C) 20 hours", "D) 22 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Pipe A can fill a tank in 10 hours, and Pipe B can empty the full tank in 15 hours. Pipe A is opened initially. Exactly 4 hours later, Pipe B is also opened. What is the total time required from the start to completely fill the tank?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "For the first 4 hours, only A is open. Work done by A = 4 × (1/10) = 4/10 = 2/5. Remaining work = 1 - 2/5 = 3/5. From 4 hours onward, both A and B are open. Combined rate = 1/10 - 1/15 = 3/30 - 2/30 = 1/30. Time to fill the remaining 3/5 at rate 1/30 = (3/5) / (1/30) = (3/5) × 30 = 18 hours. Total time from the start = 4 (initial) + 18 (combined) = 22 hours.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22188	Partnership	aptitude-quant-partnership	Master the fundamental partnership equation mapping investment and time to profit ratios, active vs sleeping partners, and proportional business share distributions.	2026-10-07 11:40:48.866111+05:30	### 📌 1. The Core Partnership Equation\n* **Investment × Time = Profit:**\n  The fundamental rule of partnership states that the ratio of profits obtained by the partners is directly proportional to the product of their invested capital and the time period for which it was invested.\n* **`Ratio of Profit of A : Profit of B = (Investment of A × Time of A) : (Investment of B × Time of B)`**\n  * If the time periods are identical, the profit ratio equals the investment ratio.\n  * If the investments are identical, the profit ratio equals the time ratio.\n\n### 💡 2. Finding Unknowns\n* **Finding Investment Ratio from Time and Profit:**\n  * `Investment Ratio = Profit Ratio / Time Ratio`\n  * If Profit ratio is `P_a : P_b` and Time ratio is `T_a : T_b`, then Investment ratio is `(P_a / T_a) : (P_b / T_b)`. Multiply by the LCM of denominators to get whole numbers.\n* **Finding Time Ratio from Investment and Profit:**\n  * `Time Ratio = Profit Ratio / Investment Ratio`\n\n### 💡 3. Active vs. Sleeping Partners & Donations\n* **Active Partner Salary:** If a partner is an "active" or "working" partner, they usually receive a fixed salary or a fixed percentage of the total profit for managing the business.\n  * *Crucial Step:* ALWAYS deduct this salary (or charity donation) from the **Total Profit** first.\n  * The **Remaining Profit** is then distributed among all partners (including the active one) strictly according to their `(Investment × Time)` ratio.\n  * The Active Partner's Total Earnings = Salary + Profit Share.	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hn9TKnr8L_8", "type": "youtube", "title": "Partnership - Shortcuts & Tricks for Placement Tests, Job Interviews & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-part-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Direct Profit Distribution] P and Q buy a hotel. P invests 6 times the investment of Q. On receiving a total profit of ₹84,000, what will be P's exact share?", "options": ["A) ₹12,000", "B) ₹60,000", "C) ₹70,000", "D) ₹72,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "P and Q jointly buy a hotel. P invests exactly 6 times the amount invested by Q. On receiving a total business profit of ₹84,000 at the end of the year, what will be P's exact share?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Since the time period is identical for both, Profit Ratio = Investment Ratio. Let Q's investment be 1 unit, so P's investment is 6 units. Profit ratio P : Q = 6 : 1. P's share = [6 / (6 + 1)] × 84000 = (6/7) × 84000 = 6 × 12000 = ₹72,000.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-part-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Different Time Periods] Rohit starts an agency investing ₹40,000. After 4 months, Raj joins with ₹50,000. Find Raj's share in a total profit of ₹1,87,000 at year end.", "options": ["A) ₹80,000", "B) ₹85,000", "C) ₹90,000", "D) ₹1,02,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Rohit starts a travel agency by investing ₹40,000. Exactly 4 months later, Raj joins the agency by investing ₹50,000. If they earn a total profit of ₹1,87,000 at the end of the year, what is the profit share of Raj?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Rohit's investment time = 12 months. Raj's investment time = 12 - 4 = 8 months. Profit ratio (Rohit : Raj) = (40000 × 12) : (50000 × 8) = 480000 : 400000 = 48 : 40 = 6 : 5. Raj's share = [5 / (6 + 5)] × 187000 = (5/11) × 187000 = 5 × 17000 = ₹85,000.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-part-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Finding Unknown Time Parameter] M invests ₹4,000. After 3 months N joins with ₹8,000. P joins later with ₹20,000. If profit ratio is 6:9:5, for how many months was P involved?", "options": ["A) 2 months", "B) 3 months", "C) 4 months", "D) 5 months"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "M invests ₹4,000 in a business. After 3 months, N joins with ₹8,000. P joins later with an investment of ₹20,000. At the end of the year, the profits of M, N, and P are in the ratio 6 : 9 : 5. For exactly how many months was P involved in the business?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "M's time = 12 months. N's time = 9 months. P's time = T months. The profit ratio is (4000×12) : (8000×9) : (20000×T) = 48000 : 72000 : 20000T. We are given the actual profit ratio is 6 : 9 : 5. Let the common multiplier be k, so 6k = 48000 => k = 8000. Therefore, P's share = 5k = 40000. Equating P's ratio value: 20000T = 40000 => T = 2 months.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-part-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Finding Investment Ratio from Time and Profit] Profit ratio of P, Q, R is 5:8:9. Time period ratio is 2:5:8. Find the exact investment ratio.", "options": ["A) 100 : 64 : 45", "B) 100 : 45 : 64", "C) 45 : 64 : 100", "D) 64 : 100 : 45"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The ratio of profits of partners P, Q, and R in a business is 5 : 8 : 9 respectively. If the ratio of their respective investment time periods is 2 : 5 : 8, what is the exact ratio of their invested capitals?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Investment Ratio = Profit Ratio / Time Ratio. I_P : I_Q : I_R = 5/2 : 8/5 : 9/8. To clear denominators, find the LCM of 2, 5, 8, which is 40. Multiply all terms by 40: (5/2)×40 : (8/5)×40 : (9/8)×40 = 100 : 64 : 45.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-part-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Donation Deduction Before Profit Split] Ajay and Vijay invest in ratio 5:2. After donating 10% of total profit, Ajay gets ₹3,420. Find Vijay's share.", "options": ["A) ₹1,248", "B) ₹1,368", "C) ₹1,480", "D) ₹1,710"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Ajay and Vijay's investments are in the ratio 5 : 2. Exactly 10% of the total business profit is donated to an orphanage, and the remaining profit is distributed between them. If Ajay's final received profit is ₹3,420, what is Vijay's exact share?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Since the remaining profit is strictly distributed in the investment ratio (5:2), we do not need to calculate the original total profit before donation! We know Ajay's share corresponds to 5 parts of the distributed profit, and Vijay's corresponds to 2 parts. Ajay's share = 5 parts = 3420 => 1 part = 3420 / 5 = 684. Vijay's share = 2 parts = 2 × 684 = ₹1,368.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-part-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Fractional Investment Logic] P, Q, R start a hotel. Q and R invest equally, but P invests 1/5 of the total. Total profit is ₹2,25,000. Difference between P and R's profit?", "options": ["A) ₹25,000", "B) ₹35,000", "C) ₹45,000", "D) ₹55,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "P, Q, and R start a hotel. Q and R invest exactly equal amounts, but P invests exactly 1/5 of the total investment amount. On receiving a total profit of ₹2,25,000, what is the exact difference between the profit shares of P and R?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Since P invests 1/5 of the total, P's profit share = (1/5) × 225000 = ₹45,000. The remaining profit = 225000 - 45000 = ₹1,80,000. Since Q and R invested equally, they share this remaining profit equally: R's profit = Q's profit = 180000 / 2 = ₹90,000. The difference between R's profit and P's profit = 90000 - 45000 = ₹45,000.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-part-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Active Partner Salary Calculation] P and Q invest ₹20k and ₹40k. Total profit is ₹15k. P gets his profit share PLUS a salary. If P receives ₹7,000 total, what is his salary?", "options": ["A) ₹1,500", "B) ₹2,000", "C) ₹2,500", "D) ₹3,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "P and Q start a cafe with capitals of ₹20,000 and ₹40,000 respectively. After a year, out of a total profit of ₹15,000, P (as an active partner) gets his profit share PLUS some money as his salary. If P receives a total of ₹7,000, what is the exact amount of his salary?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Ratio of investments P : Q = 20,000 : 40,000 = 1 : 2. P's proportional profit share from business = (1 / 3) × 15,000 = ₹5,000. P receives a total of ₹7,000. The salary amount received by P = Total received - Profit share = 7,000 - 5,000 = ₹2,000.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-part-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Unknown Investment Amount] Ram and Shyam jointly invest ₹15,000. Total profit is ₹2,000. If Shyam's profit share is ₹600, how much did Ram invest?", "options": ["A) ₹8,500", "B) ₹9,000", "C) ₹10,500", "D) ₹11,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A total of ₹15,000 was invested by Ram and Shyam together to start a business. They earned a total profit of ₹2,000 at the end of the year. If Shyam took his profit share of exactly ₹600, how much money did Ram invest originally?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total profit = 2000. Shyam's profit = 600. Therefore, Ram's profit = 2000 - 600 = 1400. Profit ratio (Ram : Shyam) = 1400 : 600 = 14 : 6 = 7 : 3. Since investment ratio equals profit ratio, Ram's investment share is 7 parts out of 10. Ram's investment = (7 / 10) × 15000 = 7 × 1500 = ₹10,500.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-part-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Unequal Addition and Withdrawals] A starts with ₹50,000. After 6 months, he adds ₹10,000 and B joins with ₹40,000. Find B's share in a ₹45,000 profit after 1 year.", "options": ["A) ₹12,000", "B) ₹15,000", "C) ₹16,000", "D) ₹18,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A starts a business with ₹50,000. After 6 months, A adds an additional ₹10,000 to his capital, and B joins the business with a capital of ₹40,000. What is B's share in a total profit of ₹45,000 at the end of the year?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Calculate \\"investment-months\\" for A: (50000 × 6) + (60000 × 6) = 300,000 + 360,000 = 660,000. Calculate for B: (40000 × 6) = 240,000. Profit ratio A : B = 660,000 : 240,000 = 66 : 24 = 11 : 4. B's share = [4 / (11 + 4)] × 45,000 = (4 / 15) × 45,000 = 4 × 3,000 = ₹12,000.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-part-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Multiple Exits and Additions] A, B, C start with ₹120k, ₹135k, ₹150k. After 4 months, C leaves. After 8 months, B leaves. Total profit ₹65,000. Find A's share.", "options": ["A) ₹24,000", "B) ₹27,000", "C) ₹28,800", "D) ₹30,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A, B, and C start a business with capitals of ₹1,20,000, ₹1,35,000, and ₹1,50,000 respectively. After exactly 4 months, C leaves the business. After exactly 8 months from the start, B leaves the business. If they earn a total profit of ₹65,000 at the end of the year, what is A's exact share?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Time periods: A = 12 months, B = 8 months, C = 4 months. Investment ratio A:B:C = 120 : 135 : 150 = 8 : 9 : 10. Product (Ratio × Time) -> A = 8 × 12 = 96. B = 9 × 8 = 72. C = 10 × 4 = 40. Profit ratio = 96 : 72 : 40. Divide by 8 -> 12 : 9 : 5. Total parts = 12 + 9 + 5 = 26. A's share = (12 / 26) × 65,000 = (6 / 13) × 65,000 = 6 × 5,000 = ₹30,000.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-part-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Finding Profit Ratio with Complex Fractions] A invests 1/6 capital for 1/6 time, B invests 1/3 capital for 1/3 time, C invests rest of capital for whole time. Find C's share in ₹4,600 profit.", "options": ["A) ₹2,400", "B) ₹3,000", "C) ₹3,300", "D) ₹3,600"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A invests 1/6 of the total capital for 1/6 of the total time. B invests 1/3 of the total capital for 1/3 of the total time. C invests the rest of the capital for the entire whole time. Out of a total profit of ₹4,600, what is C's exact share?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) C's capital = 1 - (1/6 + 1/3) = 1 - 1/2 = 1/2 of the total capital. 2) Profit ratios = (A_inv × A_time) : (B_inv × B_time) : (C_inv × C_time) = (1/6 × 1/6) : (1/3 × 1/3) : (1/2 × 1) = 1/36 : 1/9 : 1/2. 3) Multiply by 36 to get whole numbers: 1 : 4 : 18. Total parts = 1 + 4 + 18 = 23. C's share = (18 / 23) × 4600 = 18 × 200 = ₹3,600.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-part-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Working Partner Percentage Commission] A and B invest ₹30k and ₹40k. A receives 10% of profit for managing. Rest is divided in investment ratio. If A receives ₹3,400 total, find total profit.", "options": ["A) ₹7,000", "B) ₹8,000", "C) ₹9,000", "D) ₹10,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A and B invest ₹30,000 and ₹40,000 respectively in a business. A receives 10% of the total profit for managing the business, and the remaining profit is divided according to their investments. If A receives a total of ₹3,400, what is the total profit of the business?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Total Profit = P. A's managing fee = 0.10P. Remaining profit to divide = 0.90P. Investment ratio A : B = 30 : 40 = 3 : 4. A's share of remaining profit = (3/7) × 0.90P. Total received by A = 0.10P + (2.70P / 7) = (0.70P + 2.70P) / 7 = 3.40P / 7. Given: 3.40P / 7 = 3400 => 3.40P = 23800 => P = ₹7,000.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-part-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Finding Profit Ratio with Unknown Withdrawals] X and Y start with ₹16k and ₹12k. After 3 months X withdraws ₹5k and Y adds ₹5k. Z joins with ₹21k. Profit ratio at end of year?", "options": ["A) 143 : 189 : 189", "B) 147 : 189 : 189", "C) 147 : 185 : 189", "D) 145 : 189 : 185"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "X and Y start a business with ₹16,000 and ₹12,000 respectively. After exactly 3 months, X withdraws ₹5,000 and Y adds ₹5,000 to his investment. At the exact same time, Z joins the business with ₹21,000. What will be the ratio of their profits at the end of the year?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "X's investment = (16000 × 3) + (11000 × 9) = 48000 + 99000 = 147,000. Y's investment = (12000 × 3) + (17000 × 9) = 36000 + 153000 = 189,000. Z's investment = 21000 × 9 = 189,000. Ratio = 147 : 189 : 189. (Divide by 21 to get 7 : 9 : 9).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-part-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Time Parameter Equivalent Investments] A, B, C rent a pasture. A puts 10 oxen for 7 months, B puts 12 oxen for 5 months, C puts 15 oxen for 3 months. If rent is ₹175, how much must C pay?", "options": ["A) ₹45", "B) ₹50", "C) ₹55", "D) ₹60"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A, B, and C rent a pasture. A puts 10 oxen for 7 months, B puts 12 oxen for 5 months, and C puts 15 oxen for 3 months for grazing. If the total rent of the pasture is ₹175, how much must C pay as his exact share of the rent?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Rent is shared in the ratio of (Number of animals × Time). A : B : C = (10 × 7) : (12 × 5) : (15 × 3) = 70 : 60 : 45. Divide by 5: 14 : 12 : 9. Total parts = 14 + 12 + 9 = 35. C's share = (9 / 35) × 175. Since 175 / 35 = 5, C's share = 9 × 5 = ₹45.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-part-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Finding Total Capital from Profit Percentage] A, B, C share profits. A gets 3/10, B gets 1/3, C gets the rest. If C's profit is ₹1,100, what is the total profit?", "options": ["A) ₹2,000", "B) ₹2,400", "C) ₹3,000", "D) ₹3,300"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A, B, and C are partners sharing business profits. A gets exactly 3/10 of the profit, B gets exactly 1/3 of the profit, and C gets the remaining profit. If C's actual profit share is ₹1,100, what is the total profit of the business?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "C's fractional share = 1 - (3/10 + 1/3) = 1 - (9/30 + 10/30) = 1 - 19/30 = 11/30. Let total profit be P. (11/30) × P = 1100 => P = (1100 × 30) / 11 = 100 × 30 = ₹3,000.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-part-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Compound Investment Return] A invests ₹X at start. B invests ₹Y after 6 months. If they share profit equally at the end of the year, what is the relation between X and Y?", "options": ["A) X = 2Y", "B) 2X = Y", "C) 3X = 2Y", "D) X = Y"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A invests ₹X at the start of the year in a business. B invests ₹Y exactly 6 months later. If they share the profit equally at the end of the year, what is the exact mathematical relation between X and Y?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "A's investment × Time = X × 12. B's investment × Time = Y × 6. Since profits are equal, X × 12 = Y × 6 => 12X = 6Y => 2X = Y.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-part-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Variable Addition and Constant End] A and B start with ₹50k and ₹20k. A withdraws ₹10k every month, B adds ₹10k every month. When will their total investments be equal?", "options": ["A) 3 months", "B) 4 months", "C) 5 months", "D) 6 months"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A and B start a business with ₹50,000 and ₹20,000 respectively. Every subsequent month, A withdraws ₹10,000 while B adds ₹10,000. After how many months will their total cumulative investments become exactly equal?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Cumulative investment for A after n months is an arithmetic progression: 50 + 40 + 30 + ... (n terms). Cumulative investment for B: 20 + 30 + 40 + ... (n terms). Sum A = (n/2)[2(50) + (n-1)(-10)]. Sum B = (n/2)[2(20) + (n-1)(10)]. Equating the bracketed terms: 100 - 10n + 10 = 40 + 10n - 10 => 110 - 10n = 30 + 10n => 80 = 20n => n = 4. They will be equal after 4 months.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-part-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Capital Multipliers] In a partnership, A invests 1/2 of the capital for 1/3 of the time. B invests 1/4 of the capital for 1/4 of the time. C invests the rest for the whole time. Find A's share out of ₹4,600.", "options": ["A) ₹1,600", "B) ₹1,800", "C) ₹2,000", "D) ₹2,400"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a partnership, A invests exactly 1/2 of the capital for 1/3 of the time. B invests exactly 1/4 of the capital for 1/4 of the time. C invests the rest of the capital for the whole time. Out of a total business profit of ₹4,600, what is A's share?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) C's capital = 1 - (1/2 + 1/4) = 1 - 3/4 = 1/4. 2) Ratios = (1/2 × 1/3) : (1/4 × 1/4) : (1/4 × 1) = 1/6 : 1/16 : 1/4. 3) LCM of 6, 16, 4 is 48. Multiply by 48: 8 : 3 : 12. Total parts = 8 + 3 + 12 = 23. A's share = (8 / 23) × 4600 = 8 × 200 = ₹1,600.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-part-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Reinvesting Profit Percentages] A and B start with ₹30k and ₹40k. They agree to reinvest 40% of the profit back into the business and distribute the rest. If A gets ₹1,800, find total profit.", "options": ["A) ₹5,000", "B) ₹6,000", "C) ₹7,000", "D) ₹8,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A and B start a business with ₹30,000 and ₹40,000 respectively. They agree to reinvest exactly 40% of the total profit back into the business and distribute the remaining profit in their investment ratio. If A's received distributed share is ₹1,800, what is the total overall profit of the business?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Investment ratio A : B = 3 : 4. Total distributed profit = 7 parts. A gets 3 parts. 3 parts = 1800 => 1 part = 600. Total distributed profit = 7 × 600 = ₹4,200. This 4200 represents the 60% (100% - 40%) of the total profit that was not reinvested. 60% of Total = 4200 => (60/100) × Total = 4200 => Total = (4200 × 100) / 60 = 70 × 100 = ₹7,000.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-part-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Equal Profit Shares Reverse Logic] A, B, C invest capitals in ratio 2:3:4. If they receive equal profits at the end, what is the ratio of their time periods?", "options": ["A) 2 : 3 : 4", "B) 4 : 3 : 2", "C) 6 : 4 : 3", "D) 12 : 8 : 6"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Three partners A, B, and C invest their capitals in a business in the ratio 2 : 3 : 4. If they receive exactly equal profits at the end of the year, what is the ratio of their invested time periods?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Profit = Investment × Time. Since Profits are equal, let Profit = 1 for all. Time = Profit / Investment. T_A : T_B : T_C = 1/2 : 1/3 : 1/4. Multiply by LCM 12: 6 : 4 : 3.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. The Core Partnership Equation\n* **Investment × Time = Profit:**\n  The fundamental rule of partnership states that the ratio of profits obtained by the partners is directly proportional to the product of their invested capital and the time period for which it was invested.\n* **`Ratio of Profit of A : Profit of B = (Investment of A × Time of A) : (Investment of B × Time of B)`**\n  * If the time periods are identical, the profit ratio equals the investment ratio.\n  * If the investments are identical, the profit ratio equals the time ratio.\n\n### 💡 2. Finding Unknowns\n* **Finding Investment Ratio from Time and Profit:**\n  * `Investment Ratio = Profit Ratio / Time Ratio`\n  * If Profit ratio is `P_a : P_b` and Time ratio is `T_a : T_b`, then Investment ratio is `(P_a / T_a) : (P_b / T_b)`. Multiply by the LCM of denominators to get whole numbers.\n* **Finding Time Ratio from Investment and Profit:**\n  * `Time Ratio = Profit Ratio / Investment Ratio`\n\n### 💡 3. Active vs. Sleeping Partners & Donations\n* **Active Partner Salary:** If a partner is an "active" or "working" partner, they usually receive a fixed salary or a fixed percentage of the total profit for managing the business.\n  * *Crucial Step:* ALWAYS deduct this salary (or charity donation) from the **Total Profit** first.\n  * The **Remaining Profit** is then distributed among all partners (including the active one) strictly according to their `(Investment × Time)` ratio.\n  * The Active Partner's Total Earnings = Salary + Profit Share.	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Partnership	Master the fundamental partnership equation mapping investment and time to profit ratios, active vs sleeping partners, and proportional business share distributions.	31	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	22	[{"id": "apt-part-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Direct Profit Distribution] P and Q buy a hotel. P invests 6 times the investment of Q. On receiving a total profit of ₹84,000, what will be P's exact share?", "options": ["A) ₹12,000", "B) ₹60,000", "C) ₹70,000", "D) ₹72,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "P and Q jointly buy a hotel. P invests exactly 6 times the amount invested by Q. On receiving a total business profit of ₹84,000 at the end of the year, what will be P's exact share?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Since the time period is identical for both, Profit Ratio = Investment Ratio. Let Q's investment be 1 unit, so P's investment is 6 units. Profit ratio P : Q = 6 : 1. P's share = [6 / (6 + 1)] × 84000 = (6/7) × 84000 = 6 × 12000 = ₹72,000.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-part-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Different Time Periods] Rohit starts an agency investing ₹40,000. After 4 months, Raj joins with ₹50,000. Find Raj's share in a total profit of ₹1,87,000 at year end.", "options": ["A) ₹80,000", "B) ₹85,000", "C) ₹90,000", "D) ₹1,02,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Rohit starts a travel agency by investing ₹40,000. Exactly 4 months later, Raj joins the agency by investing ₹50,000. If they earn a total profit of ₹1,87,000 at the end of the year, what is the profit share of Raj?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Rohit's investment time = 12 months. Raj's investment time = 12 - 4 = 8 months. Profit ratio (Rohit : Raj) = (40000 × 12) : (50000 × 8) = 480000 : 400000 = 48 : 40 = 6 : 5. Raj's share = [5 / (6 + 5)] × 187000 = (5/11) × 187000 = 5 × 17000 = ₹85,000.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-part-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Finding Unknown Time Parameter] M invests ₹4,000. After 3 months N joins with ₹8,000. P joins later with ₹20,000. If profit ratio is 6:9:5, for how many months was P involved?", "options": ["A) 2 months", "B) 3 months", "C) 4 months", "D) 5 months"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "M invests ₹4,000 in a business. After 3 months, N joins with ₹8,000. P joins later with an investment of ₹20,000. At the end of the year, the profits of M, N, and P are in the ratio 6 : 9 : 5. For exactly how many months was P involved in the business?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "M's time = 12 months. N's time = 9 months. P's time = T months. The profit ratio is (4000×12) : (8000×9) : (20000×T) = 48000 : 72000 : 20000T. We are given the actual profit ratio is 6 : 9 : 5. Let the common multiplier be k, so 6k = 48000 => k = 8000. Therefore, P's share = 5k = 40000. Equating P's ratio value: 20000T = 40000 => T = 2 months.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-part-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Finding Investment Ratio from Time and Profit] Profit ratio of P, Q, R is 5:8:9. Time period ratio is 2:5:8. Find the exact investment ratio.", "options": ["A) 100 : 64 : 45", "B) 100 : 45 : 64", "C) 45 : 64 : 100", "D) 64 : 100 : 45"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The ratio of profits of partners P, Q, and R in a business is 5 : 8 : 9 respectively. If the ratio of their respective investment time periods is 2 : 5 : 8, what is the exact ratio of their invested capitals?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Investment Ratio = Profit Ratio / Time Ratio. I_P : I_Q : I_R = 5/2 : 8/5 : 9/8. To clear denominators, find the LCM of 2, 5, 8, which is 40. Multiply all terms by 40: (5/2)×40 : (8/5)×40 : (9/8)×40 = 100 : 64 : 45.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-part-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Donation Deduction Before Profit Split] Ajay and Vijay invest in ratio 5:2. After donating 10% of total profit, Ajay gets ₹3,420. Find Vijay's share.", "options": ["A) ₹1,248", "B) ₹1,368", "C) ₹1,480", "D) ₹1,710"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Ajay and Vijay's investments are in the ratio 5 : 2. Exactly 10% of the total business profit is donated to an orphanage, and the remaining profit is distributed between them. If Ajay's final received profit is ₹3,420, what is Vijay's exact share?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Since the remaining profit is strictly distributed in the investment ratio (5:2), we do not need to calculate the original total profit before donation! We know Ajay's share corresponds to 5 parts of the distributed profit, and Vijay's corresponds to 2 parts. Ajay's share = 5 parts = 3420 => 1 part = 3420 / 5 = 684. Vijay's share = 2 parts = 2 × 684 = ₹1,368.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-part-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Fractional Investment Logic] P, Q, R start a hotel. Q and R invest equally, but P invests 1/5 of the total. Total profit is ₹2,25,000. Difference between P and R's profit?", "options": ["A) ₹25,000", "B) ₹35,000", "C) ₹45,000", "D) ₹55,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "P, Q, and R start a hotel. Q and R invest exactly equal amounts, but P invests exactly 1/5 of the total investment amount. On receiving a total profit of ₹2,25,000, what is the exact difference between the profit shares of P and R?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Since P invests 1/5 of the total, P's profit share = (1/5) × 225000 = ₹45,000. The remaining profit = 225000 - 45000 = ₹1,80,000. Since Q and R invested equally, they share this remaining profit equally: R's profit = Q's profit = 180000 / 2 = ₹90,000. The difference between R's profit and P's profit = 90000 - 45000 = ₹45,000.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-part-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Active Partner Salary Calculation] P and Q invest ₹20k and ₹40k. Total profit is ₹15k. P gets his profit share PLUS a salary. If P receives ₹7,000 total, what is his salary?", "options": ["A) ₹1,500", "B) ₹2,000", "C) ₹2,500", "D) ₹3,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "P and Q start a cafe with capitals of ₹20,000 and ₹40,000 respectively. After a year, out of a total profit of ₹15,000, P (as an active partner) gets his profit share PLUS some money as his salary. If P receives a total of ₹7,000, what is the exact amount of his salary?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Ratio of investments P : Q = 20,000 : 40,000 = 1 : 2. P's proportional profit share from business = (1 / 3) × 15,000 = ₹5,000. P receives a total of ₹7,000. The salary amount received by P = Total received - Profit share = 7,000 - 5,000 = ₹2,000.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-part-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Unknown Investment Amount] Ram and Shyam jointly invest ₹15,000. Total profit is ₹2,000. If Shyam's profit share is ₹600, how much did Ram invest?", "options": ["A) ₹8,500", "B) ₹9,000", "C) ₹10,500", "D) ₹11,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A total of ₹15,000 was invested by Ram and Shyam together to start a business. They earned a total profit of ₹2,000 at the end of the year. If Shyam took his profit share of exactly ₹600, how much money did Ram invest originally?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total profit = 2000. Shyam's profit = 600. Therefore, Ram's profit = 2000 - 600 = 1400. Profit ratio (Ram : Shyam) = 1400 : 600 = 14 : 6 = 7 : 3. Since investment ratio equals profit ratio, Ram's investment share is 7 parts out of 10. Ram's investment = (7 / 10) × 15000 = 7 × 1500 = ₹10,500.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-part-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Unequal Addition and Withdrawals] A starts with ₹50,000. After 6 months, he adds ₹10,000 and B joins with ₹40,000. Find B's share in a ₹45,000 profit after 1 year.", "options": ["A) ₹12,000", "B) ₹15,000", "C) ₹16,000", "D) ₹18,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A starts a business with ₹50,000. After 6 months, A adds an additional ₹10,000 to his capital, and B joins the business with a capital of ₹40,000. What is B's share in a total profit of ₹45,000 at the end of the year?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Calculate \\"investment-months\\" for A: (50000 × 6) + (60000 × 6) = 300,000 + 360,000 = 660,000. Calculate for B: (40000 × 6) = 240,000. Profit ratio A : B = 660,000 : 240,000 = 66 : 24 = 11 : 4. B's share = [4 / (11 + 4)] × 45,000 = (4 / 15) × 45,000 = 4 × 3,000 = ₹12,000.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-part-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Multiple Exits and Additions] A, B, C start with ₹120k, ₹135k, ₹150k. After 4 months, C leaves. After 8 months, B leaves. Total profit ₹65,000. Find A's share.", "options": ["A) ₹24,000", "B) ₹27,000", "C) ₹28,800", "D) ₹30,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A, B, and C start a business with capitals of ₹1,20,000, ₹1,35,000, and ₹1,50,000 respectively. After exactly 4 months, C leaves the business. After exactly 8 months from the start, B leaves the business. If they earn a total profit of ₹65,000 at the end of the year, what is A's exact share?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Time periods: A = 12 months, B = 8 months, C = 4 months. Investment ratio A:B:C = 120 : 135 : 150 = 8 : 9 : 10. Product (Ratio × Time) -> A = 8 × 12 = 96. B = 9 × 8 = 72. C = 10 × 4 = 40. Profit ratio = 96 : 72 : 40. Divide by 8 -> 12 : 9 : 5. Total parts = 12 + 9 + 5 = 26. A's share = (12 / 26) × 65,000 = (6 / 13) × 65,000 = 6 × 5,000 = ₹30,000.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-part-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Finding Profit Ratio with Complex Fractions] A invests 1/6 capital for 1/6 time, B invests 1/3 capital for 1/3 time, C invests rest of capital for whole time. Find C's share in ₹4,600 profit.", "options": ["A) ₹2,400", "B) ₹3,000", "C) ₹3,300", "D) ₹3,600"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A invests 1/6 of the total capital for 1/6 of the total time. B invests 1/3 of the total capital for 1/3 of the total time. C invests the rest of the capital for the entire whole time. Out of a total profit of ₹4,600, what is C's exact share?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) C's capital = 1 - (1/6 + 1/3) = 1 - 1/2 = 1/2 of the total capital. 2) Profit ratios = (A_inv × A_time) : (B_inv × B_time) : (C_inv × C_time) = (1/6 × 1/6) : (1/3 × 1/3) : (1/2 × 1) = 1/36 : 1/9 : 1/2. 3) Multiply by 36 to get whole numbers: 1 : 4 : 18. Total parts = 1 + 4 + 18 = 23. C's share = (18 / 23) × 4600 = 18 × 200 = ₹3,600.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-part-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Working Partner Percentage Commission] A and B invest ₹30k and ₹40k. A receives 10% of profit for managing. Rest is divided in investment ratio. If A receives ₹3,400 total, find total profit.", "options": ["A) ₹7,000", "B) ₹8,000", "C) ₹9,000", "D) ₹10,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A and B invest ₹30,000 and ₹40,000 respectively in a business. A receives 10% of the total profit for managing the business, and the remaining profit is divided according to their investments. If A receives a total of ₹3,400, what is the total profit of the business?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Total Profit = P. A's managing fee = 0.10P. Remaining profit to divide = 0.90P. Investment ratio A : B = 30 : 40 = 3 : 4. A's share of remaining profit = (3/7) × 0.90P. Total received by A = 0.10P + (2.70P / 7) = (0.70P + 2.70P) / 7 = 3.40P / 7. Given: 3.40P / 7 = 3400 => 3.40P = 23800 => P = ₹7,000.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-part-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Finding Profit Ratio with Unknown Withdrawals] X and Y start with ₹16k and ₹12k. After 3 months X withdraws ₹5k and Y adds ₹5k. Z joins with ₹21k. Profit ratio at end of year?", "options": ["A) 143 : 189 : 189", "B) 147 : 189 : 189", "C) 147 : 185 : 189", "D) 145 : 189 : 185"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "X and Y start a business with ₹16,000 and ₹12,000 respectively. After exactly 3 months, X withdraws ₹5,000 and Y adds ₹5,000 to his investment. At the exact same time, Z joins the business with ₹21,000. What will be the ratio of their profits at the end of the year?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "X's investment = (16000 × 3) + (11000 × 9) = 48000 + 99000 = 147,000. Y's investment = (12000 × 3) + (17000 × 9) = 36000 + 153000 = 189,000. Z's investment = 21000 × 9 = 189,000. Ratio = 147 : 189 : 189. (Divide by 21 to get 7 : 9 : 9).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-part-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Time Parameter Equivalent Investments] A, B, C rent a pasture. A puts 10 oxen for 7 months, B puts 12 oxen for 5 months, C puts 15 oxen for 3 months. If rent is ₹175, how much must C pay?", "options": ["A) ₹45", "B) ₹50", "C) ₹55", "D) ₹60"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A, B, and C rent a pasture. A puts 10 oxen for 7 months, B puts 12 oxen for 5 months, and C puts 15 oxen for 3 months for grazing. If the total rent of the pasture is ₹175, how much must C pay as his exact share of the rent?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Rent is shared in the ratio of (Number of animals × Time). A : B : C = (10 × 7) : (12 × 5) : (15 × 3) = 70 : 60 : 45. Divide by 5: 14 : 12 : 9. Total parts = 14 + 12 + 9 = 35. C's share = (9 / 35) × 175. Since 175 / 35 = 5, C's share = 9 × 5 = ₹45.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-part-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Finding Total Capital from Profit Percentage] A, B, C share profits. A gets 3/10, B gets 1/3, C gets the rest. If C's profit is ₹1,100, what is the total profit?", "options": ["A) ₹2,000", "B) ₹2,400", "C) ₹3,000", "D) ₹3,300"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A, B, and C are partners sharing business profits. A gets exactly 3/10 of the profit, B gets exactly 1/3 of the profit, and C gets the remaining profit. If C's actual profit share is ₹1,100, what is the total profit of the business?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "C's fractional share = 1 - (3/10 + 1/3) = 1 - (9/30 + 10/30) = 1 - 19/30 = 11/30. Let total profit be P. (11/30) × P = 1100 => P = (1100 × 30) / 11 = 100 × 30 = ₹3,000.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-part-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Compound Investment Return] A invests ₹X at start. B invests ₹Y after 6 months. If they share profit equally at the end of the year, what is the relation between X and Y?", "options": ["A) X = 2Y", "B) 2X = Y", "C) 3X = 2Y", "D) X = Y"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A invests ₹X at the start of the year in a business. B invests ₹Y exactly 6 months later. If they share the profit equally at the end of the year, what is the exact mathematical relation between X and Y?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "A's investment × Time = X × 12. B's investment × Time = Y × 6. Since profits are equal, X × 12 = Y × 6 => 12X = 6Y => 2X = Y.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-part-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Variable Addition and Constant End] A and B start with ₹50k and ₹20k. A withdraws ₹10k every month, B adds ₹10k every month. When will their total investments be equal?", "options": ["A) 3 months", "B) 4 months", "C) 5 months", "D) 6 months"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A and B start a business with ₹50,000 and ₹20,000 respectively. Every subsequent month, A withdraws ₹10,000 while B adds ₹10,000. After how many months will their total cumulative investments become exactly equal?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Cumulative investment for A after n months is an arithmetic progression: 50 + 40 + 30 + ... (n terms). Cumulative investment for B: 20 + 30 + 40 + ... (n terms). Sum A = (n/2)[2(50) + (n-1)(-10)]. Sum B = (n/2)[2(20) + (n-1)(10)]. Equating the bracketed terms: 100 - 10n + 10 = 40 + 10n - 10 => 110 - 10n = 30 + 10n => 80 = 20n => n = 4. They will be equal after 4 months.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-part-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Capital Multipliers] In a partnership, A invests 1/2 of the capital for 1/3 of the time. B invests 1/4 of the capital for 1/4 of the time. C invests the rest for the whole time. Find A's share out of ₹4,600.", "options": ["A) ₹1,600", "B) ₹1,800", "C) ₹2,000", "D) ₹2,400"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a partnership, A invests exactly 1/2 of the capital for 1/3 of the time. B invests exactly 1/4 of the capital for 1/4 of the time. C invests the rest of the capital for the whole time. Out of a total business profit of ₹4,600, what is A's share?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) C's capital = 1 - (1/2 + 1/4) = 1 - 3/4 = 1/4. 2) Ratios = (1/2 × 1/3) : (1/4 × 1/4) : (1/4 × 1) = 1/6 : 1/16 : 1/4. 3) LCM of 6, 16, 4 is 48. Multiply by 48: 8 : 3 : 12. Total parts = 8 + 3 + 12 = 23. A's share = (8 / 23) × 4600 = 8 × 200 = ₹1,600.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-part-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Reinvesting Profit Percentages] A and B start with ₹30k and ₹40k. They agree to reinvest 40% of the profit back into the business and distribute the rest. If A gets ₹1,800, find total profit.", "options": ["A) ₹5,000", "B) ₹6,000", "C) ₹7,000", "D) ₹8,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A and B start a business with ₹30,000 and ₹40,000 respectively. They agree to reinvest exactly 40% of the total profit back into the business and distribute the remaining profit in their investment ratio. If A's received distributed share is ₹1,800, what is the total overall profit of the business?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Investment ratio A : B = 3 : 4. Total distributed profit = 7 parts. A gets 3 parts. 3 parts = 1800 => 1 part = 600. Total distributed profit = 7 × 600 = ₹4,200. This 4200 represents the 60% (100% - 40%) of the total profit that was not reinvested. 60% of Total = 4200 => (60/100) × Total = 4200 => Total = (4200 × 100) / 60 = 70 × 100 = ₹7,000.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-part-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Equal Profit Shares Reverse Logic] A, B, C invest capitals in ratio 2:3:4. If they receive equal profits at the end, what is the ratio of their time periods?", "options": ["A) 2 : 3 : 4", "B) 4 : 3 : 2", "C) 6 : 4 : 3", "D) 12 : 8 : 6"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Three partners A, B, and C invest their capitals in a business in the ratio 2 : 3 : 4. If they receive exactly equal profits at the end of the year, what is the ratio of their invested time periods?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Profit = Investment × Time. Since Profits are equal, let Profit = 1 for all. Time = Profit / Investment. T_A : T_B : T_C = 1/2 : 1/3 : 1/4. Multiply by LCM 12: 6 : 4 : 3.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22135	Lecture 17: Event Loop Architecture — Why JS Is Single-Threaded Synchronous, Web APIs, Callback Queue & Microtask Queue	js-lecture-17-event-loop-single-threaded-web-apis-microtask-macrotask-queues	Understand how single-threaded synchronous JavaScript achieves non-blocking concurrency using Browser Web APIs, the Macrotask (Callback) Queue, the higher-priority Microtask Queue, and the Event Loop.	2026-10-06 21:36:53.504856+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nCore JavaScript is a **Single-Threaded, Synchronous** language: it has only **one Call Stack** and **one Main Thread**, meaning it can execute only one instruction at a time from top to bottom. Yet JavaScript handles network requests (`fetch`), timers (`setTimeout`), and click listeners without freezing the UI—thanks to the runtime's **Web APIs**, **Task Queues**, and the **Event Loop**.\n\n### 💡 The 5 Components of the JavaScript Runtime Architecture\n1. **Call Stack (Inside JS Engine):** Executes synchronous JS code line-by-line (LIFO). If a heavy synchronous `for` loop runs 3 billion iterations on the Call Stack, the entire browser UI freezes because the single thread is blocked!\n2. **Web APIs (Browser C++ Threads) / C++ APIs (Node.js libuv):** Features like `setTimeout`, `fetch`, DOM events, and `localStorage` are **not** part of JS—they belong to the host environment. When JS calls `setTimeout(cb, 2000)`, the Call Stack hands `cb` to the Web API timer and **immediately moves to the next line**.\n3. **Callback Queue / Macrotask Queue (Task Queue):** Once a Web API timer finishes or a DOM click fires, its callback is pushed into the **Callback Queue**—it is **never** pushed directly onto the Call Stack (to prevent race conditions mid-execution).\n4. **Microtask Queue (High Priority Queue):** Holds callbacks from **Promises (`.then`, `.catch`, `.finally`, `await`)** and `MutationObserver`. **The Microtask Queue has strictly higher priority than the Callback (Macrotask) Queue!**\n5. **The Event Loop:** A continuous coordinator loop with one job:\n   * Wait until the **Call Stack is completely empty** (all synchronous code has finished).\n   * Drain **ALL** tasks in the **Microtask Queue** first until it is empty.\n   * Pick the next task from the **Callback (Macrotask) Queue** and push it onto the Call Stack.\n\n### 🛠️ Tracing Execution Order: Synchronous vs. Microtask vs. Macrotask\n```javascript\nconsole.log("1. Start of Synchronous Script");\n\n// Handed to Web API -> Queued in Macrotask (Callback) Queue\nsetTimeout(() => {\n  console.log("4. setTimeout Callback (Macrotask Queue - Lower Priority)");\n}, 0);\n\n// Handed to Web API / Promise -> Queued in Microtask Queue\nPromise.resolve().then(() => {\n  console.log("3. Promise.then Callback (Microtask Queue - Higher Priority)");\n});\n\nconsole.log("2. End of Synchronous Script");\n\n/* Exact Output Order:\n   1. Start of Synchronous Script\n   2. End of Synchronous Script\n   3. Promise.then Callback (Microtask Queue - Higher Priority)\n   4. setTimeout Callback (Macrotask Queue - Lower Priority)\n*/\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Event_loop", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Event_loop", "title": "MDN Web Docs — The JavaScript Event Loop & Concurrency Model"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rsepF1ugeC8", "title": "Event Loop in Javascript | Why JS is Single threaded and synchronous| Javascript Full Course #17", "duration": "49m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-17-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: If JavaScript is single-threaded, how can it run a 5-second timer or a fetch() request while still executing the next lines of code?", "title": "Q1: If JavaScript is single-threaded, how can it run a 5-second timer or a fetch() request while still executing the next lines of code?", "answer": "The JavaScript engine (V8) itself is single-threaded, meaning it has only one Call Stack. However, the surrounding Host Runtime (the Browser or Node.js) is written in multi-threaded C++! When JS calls setTimeout() or fetch(), V8 delegates the timer or network socket operation to the Browser's C++ Web APIs (or Node's libuv thread pool/OS kernel) and immediately pops the call off the Call Stack to continue running synchronous JS.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "The JavaScript engine (V8) itself is single-threaded, meaning it has only one Call Stack. However, the surrounding Host Runtime (the Browser or Node.js) is written in multi-threaded C++! When JS calls setTimeout() or fetch(), V8 delegates the timer or network socket operation to the Browser's C++ Web APIs (or Node's libuv thread pool/OS kernel) and immediately pops the call off the Call Stack to continue running synchronous JS."}, {"id": "js-lec-17-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why don't Web APIs push completed callbacks directly onto the Call Stack as soon as the timer expires?", "title": "Q2: Why don't Web APIs push completed callbacks directly onto the Call Stack as soon as the timer expires?", "answer": "Because JavaScript is single-threaded! If a Web API forcibly injected a callback into the middle of the Call Stack while another function was half-executed, it would interrupt synchronous execution and cause unpredictable race conditions and corrupted state. Instead, Web APIs place completed callbacks into the Queue, and the Event Loop waits until the Call Stack is 100% empty before pushing the next callback.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Because JavaScript is single-threaded! If a Web API forcibly injected a callback into the middle of the Call Stack while another function was half-executed, it would interrupt synchronous execution and cause unpredictable race conditions and corrupted state. Instead, Web APIs place completed callbacks into the Queue, and the Event Loop waits until the Call Stack is 100% empty before pushing the next callback."}, {"id": "js-lec-17-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is the difference between the Microtask Queue and the Callback (Macrotask) Queue? Which one executes first?", "title": "Q3: What is the difference between the Microtask Queue and the Callback (Macrotask) Queue? Which one executes first?", "answer": "The Microtask Queue holds Promise callbacks (.then, .catch, .finally, code after await) and queueMicrotask(). The Macrotask (Callback) Queue holds setTimeout, setInterval, setImmediate, and DOM event callbacks. Whenever the Call Stack becomes empty, the Event Loop ALWAYS drains the entire Microtask Queue completely before executing even a single task from the Macrotask Queue!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "The Microtask Queue holds Promise callbacks (.then, .catch, .finally, code after await) and queueMicrotask(). The Macrotask (Callback) Queue holds setTimeout, setInterval, setImmediate, and DOM event callbacks. Whenever the Call Stack becomes empty, the Event Loop ALWAYS drains the entire Microtask Queue completely before executing even a single task from the Macrotask Queue!"}, {"id": "js-lec-17-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: If you call setTimeout(fn, 0) followed by a synchronous loop that takes 5 seconds, when does fn actually execute?", "title": "Q4: If you call setTimeout(fn, 0) followed by a synchronous loop that takes 5 seconds, when does fn actually execute?", "answer": "fn will execute AFTER at least 5 seconds! The 0ms parameter in setTimeout(fn, 0) is the minimum delay before the Web API places fn into the Callback Queue—NOT a guaranteed execution time. Because the Event Loop cannot move fn from the Callback Queue to the Call Stack until the synchronous 5-second loop finishes and the Global Execution Context pops off the Call Stack, fn waits the entire 5 seconds.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "fn will execute AFTER at least 5 seconds! The 0ms parameter in setTimeout(fn, 0) is the minimum delay before the Web API places fn into the Callback Queue—NOT a guaranteed execution time. Because the Event Loop cannot move fn from the Callback Queue to the Call Stack until the synchronous 5-second loop finishes and the Global Execution Context pops off the Call Stack, fn waits the entire 5 seconds."}, {"id": "js-lec-17-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is \\"Microtask Starvation\\" in the Event Loop?", "title": "Q5: What is \\"Microtask Starvation\\" in the Event Loop?", "answer": "Because the Event Loop continues draining the Microtask Queue until it is completely empty before moving to the Macrotask Queue or browser UI rendering, if a microtask recursively schedules another microtask (e.g., a Promise .then() that endlessly calls queueMicrotask() or Promise.resolve().then()), the Microtask Queue never empties—starving setTimeout callbacks and freezing the browser UI!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q5", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Because the Event Loop continues draining the Microtask Queue until it is completely empty before moving to the Macrotask Queue or browser UI rendering, if a microtask recursively schedules another microtask (e.g., a Promise .then() that endlessly calls queueMicrotask() or Promise.resolve().then()), the Microtask Queue never empties—starving setTimeout callbacks and freezing the browser UI!"}, {"id": "js-lec-17-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why do all buttons and hover effects on a webpage freeze when a heavy synchronous while/for loop is running in JavaScript?", "title": "Q6: Why do all buttons and hover effects on a webpage freeze when a heavy synchronous while/for loop is running in JavaScript?", "answer": "In the browser, the main thread handles both JavaScript Call Stack execution and UI rendering/repainting. While a heavy synchronous loop is occupying the Call Stack, the Event Loop is blocked from picking up click events from the Callback Queue and the browser cannot repaint the screen.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In the browser, the main thread handles both JavaScript Call Stack execution and UI rendering/repainting. While a heavy synchronous loop is occupying the Call Stack, the Event Loop is blocked from picking up click events from the Callback Queue and the browser cannot repaint the screen."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nCore JavaScript is a **Single-Threaded, Synchronous** language: it has only **one Call Stack** and **one Main Thread**, meaning it can execute only one instruction at a time from top to bottom. Yet JavaScript handles network requests (`fetch`), timers (`setTimeout`), and click listeners without freezing the UI—thanks to the runtime's **Web APIs**, **Task Queues**, and the **Event Loop**.\n\n### 💡 The 5 Components of the JavaScript Runtime Architecture\n1. **Call Stack (Inside JS Engine):** Executes synchronous JS code line-by-line (LIFO). If a heavy synchronous `for` loop runs 3 billion iterations on the Call Stack, the entire browser UI freezes because the single thread is blocked!\n2. **Web APIs (Browser C++ Threads) / C++ APIs (Node.js libuv):** Features like `setTimeout`, `fetch`, DOM events, and `localStorage` are **not** part of JS—they belong to the host environment. When JS calls `setTimeout(cb, 2000)`, the Call Stack hands `cb` to the Web API timer and **immediately moves to the next line**.\n3. **Callback Queue / Macrotask Queue (Task Queue):** Once a Web API timer finishes or a DOM click fires, its callback is pushed into the **Callback Queue**—it is **never** pushed directly onto the Call Stack (to prevent race conditions mid-execution).\n4. **Microtask Queue (High Priority Queue):** Holds callbacks from **Promises (`.then`, `.catch`, `.finally`, `await`)** and `MutationObserver`. **The Microtask Queue has strictly higher priority than the Callback (Macrotask) Queue!**\n5. **The Event Loop:** A continuous coordinator loop with one job:\n   * Wait until the **Call Stack is completely empty** (all synchronous code has finished).\n   * Drain **ALL** tasks in the **Microtask Queue** first until it is empty.\n   * Pick the next task from the **Callback (Macrotask) Queue** and push it onto the Call Stack.\n\n### 🛠️ Tracing Execution Order: Synchronous vs. Microtask vs. Macrotask\n```javascript\nconsole.log("1. Start of Synchronous Script");\n\n// Handed to Web API -> Queued in Macrotask (Callback) Queue\nsetTimeout(() => {\n  console.log("4. setTimeout Callback (Macrotask Queue - Lower Priority)");\n}, 0);\n\n// Handed to Web API / Promise -> Queued in Microtask Queue\nPromise.resolve().then(() => {\n  console.log("3. Promise.then Callback (Microtask Queue - Higher Priority)");\n});\n\nconsole.log("2. End of Synchronous Script");\n\n/* Exact Output Order:\n   1. Start of Synchronous Script\n   2. End of Synchronous Script\n   3. Promise.then Callback (Microtask Queue - Higher Priority)\n   4. setTimeout Callback (Macrotask Queue - Lower Priority)\n*/\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 17: Event Loop Architecture — Why JS Is Single-Threaded Synchronous, Web APIs, Callback Queue & Microtask Queue	Understand how single-threaded synchronous JavaScript achieves non-blocking concurrency using Browser Web APIs, the Macrotask (Callback) Queue, the higher-priority Microtask Queue, and the Event Loop.	20	Easy	\N	17	[{"id": "js-lec-17-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: If JavaScript is single-threaded, how can it run a 5-second timer or a fetch() request while still executing the next lines of code?", "title": "Q1: If JavaScript is single-threaded, how can it run a 5-second timer or a fetch() request while still executing the next lines of code?", "answer": "The JavaScript engine (V8) itself is single-threaded, meaning it has only one Call Stack. However, the surrounding Host Runtime (the Browser or Node.js) is written in multi-threaded C++! When JS calls setTimeout() or fetch(), V8 delegates the timer or network socket operation to the Browser's C++ Web APIs (or Node's libuv thread pool/OS kernel) and immediately pops the call off the Call Stack to continue running synchronous JS.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "The JavaScript engine (V8) itself is single-threaded, meaning it has only one Call Stack. However, the surrounding Host Runtime (the Browser or Node.js) is written in multi-threaded C++! When JS calls setTimeout() or fetch(), V8 delegates the timer or network socket operation to the Browser's C++ Web APIs (or Node's libuv thread pool/OS kernel) and immediately pops the call off the Call Stack to continue running synchronous JS."}, {"id": "js-lec-17-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why don't Web APIs push completed callbacks directly onto the Call Stack as soon as the timer expires?", "title": "Q2: Why don't Web APIs push completed callbacks directly onto the Call Stack as soon as the timer expires?", "answer": "Because JavaScript is single-threaded! If a Web API forcibly injected a callback into the middle of the Call Stack while another function was half-executed, it would interrupt synchronous execution and cause unpredictable race conditions and corrupted state. Instead, Web APIs place completed callbacks into the Queue, and the Event Loop waits until the Call Stack is 100% empty before pushing the next callback.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Because JavaScript is single-threaded! If a Web API forcibly injected a callback into the middle of the Call Stack while another function was half-executed, it would interrupt synchronous execution and cause unpredictable race conditions and corrupted state. Instead, Web APIs place completed callbacks into the Queue, and the Event Loop waits until the Call Stack is 100% empty before pushing the next callback."}, {"id": "js-lec-17-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is the difference between the Microtask Queue and the Callback (Macrotask) Queue? Which one executes first?", "title": "Q3: What is the difference between the Microtask Queue and the Callback (Macrotask) Queue? Which one executes first?", "answer": "The Microtask Queue holds Promise callbacks (.then, .catch, .finally, code after await) and queueMicrotask(). The Macrotask (Callback) Queue holds setTimeout, setInterval, setImmediate, and DOM event callbacks. Whenever the Call Stack becomes empty, the Event Loop ALWAYS drains the entire Microtask Queue completely before executing even a single task from the Macrotask Queue!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "The Microtask Queue holds Promise callbacks (.then, .catch, .finally, code after await) and queueMicrotask(). The Macrotask (Callback) Queue holds setTimeout, setInterval, setImmediate, and DOM event callbacks. Whenever the Call Stack becomes empty, the Event Loop ALWAYS drains the entire Microtask Queue completely before executing even a single task from the Macrotask Queue!"}, {"id": "js-lec-17-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: If you call setTimeout(fn, 0) followed by a synchronous loop that takes 5 seconds, when does fn actually execute?", "title": "Q4: If you call setTimeout(fn, 0) followed by a synchronous loop that takes 5 seconds, when does fn actually execute?", "answer": "fn will execute AFTER at least 5 seconds! The 0ms parameter in setTimeout(fn, 0) is the minimum delay before the Web API places fn into the Callback Queue—NOT a guaranteed execution time. Because the Event Loop cannot move fn from the Callback Queue to the Call Stack until the synchronous 5-second loop finishes and the Global Execution Context pops off the Call Stack, fn waits the entire 5 seconds.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "fn will execute AFTER at least 5 seconds! The 0ms parameter in setTimeout(fn, 0) is the minimum delay before the Web API places fn into the Callback Queue—NOT a guaranteed execution time. Because the Event Loop cannot move fn from the Callback Queue to the Call Stack until the synchronous 5-second loop finishes and the Global Execution Context pops off the Call Stack, fn waits the entire 5 seconds."}, {"id": "js-lec-17-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is \\"Microtask Starvation\\" in the Event Loop?", "title": "Q5: What is \\"Microtask Starvation\\" in the Event Loop?", "answer": "Because the Event Loop continues draining the Microtask Queue until it is completely empty before moving to the Macrotask Queue or browser UI rendering, if a microtask recursively schedules another microtask (e.g., a Promise .then() that endlessly calls queueMicrotask() or Promise.resolve().then()), the Microtask Queue never empties—starving setTimeout callbacks and freezing the browser UI!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q5", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Because the Event Loop continues draining the Microtask Queue until it is completely empty before moving to the Macrotask Queue or browser UI rendering, if a microtask recursively schedules another microtask (e.g., a Promise .then() that endlessly calls queueMicrotask() or Promise.resolve().then()), the Microtask Queue never empties—starving setTimeout callbacks and freezing the browser UI!"}, {"id": "js-lec-17-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why do all buttons and hover effects on a webpage freeze when a heavy synchronous while/for loop is running in JavaScript?", "title": "Q6: Why do all buttons and hover effects on a webpage freeze when a heavy synchronous while/for loop is running in JavaScript?", "answer": "In the browser, the main thread handles both JavaScript Call Stack execution and UI rendering/repainting. While a heavy synchronous loop is occupying the Call Stack, the Event Loop is blocked from picking up click events from the Callback Queue and the browser cannot repaint the screen.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "17-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In the browser, the main thread handles both JavaScript Call Stack execution and UI rendering/repainting. While a heavy synchronous loop is occupying the Call Stack, the Event Loop is blocked from picking up click events from the Callback Queue and the browser cannot repaint the screen."}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Event_loop	\N	\N	\N
22136	Lecture 18: Asynchronous Workflows & Callback Hell — Pyramid of Doom, Error Propagation & Inversion of Control	js-lecture-18-callback-hell-pyramid-of-doom-inversion-of-control	Model multi-step dependent asynchronous workflows (Order -> Prepare -> Pickup -> Deliver), understand why nested callbacks create Callback Hell, and analyze Inversion of Control.	2026-10-06 21:36:53.504856+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn real-world applications (like a Zomato order pipeline), asynchronous tasks often depend sequentially on the completion of the previous step:\n1. **`placeOrder(orderDetail)`** (Process payment first)\n2. **`preparingOrder(orderDetail)`** (Notify restaurant only *after* payment succeeds)\n3. **`pickupOrder(orderDetail)`** (Assign delivery partner only *after* food is prepared)\n4. **`deliverOrder(orderDetail)`** (Deliver to customer only *after* order is picked up)\n\n### 💡 Why Calling Async Functions Line-by-Line Fails\n* If you call `placeOrder(); preparingOrder();` on consecutive lines, JavaScript does not wait for the 3-second payment timer inside `placeOrder()`—it immediately starts `preparingOrder()` before payment is even received!\n* Before ES6 Promises existed, the only way to enforce sequential order was to pass the next step as a **Callback Function** into the previous step.\n\n### 💡 What Is Callback Hell (The Pyramid of Doom)?\nWhen 4 or more dependent asynchronous operations are nested inside each other using anonymous wrapper callbacks to pass data (`orderDetail`) forward, the code indents horizontally into a deeply nested triangle known as **Callback Hell**:\n\n```javascript\n// Classic Callback Hell (Pyramid of Doom)\nplacedOrder(orderDetail, (orderDetail1) => {\n  preparingOrder(orderDetail1, (orderDetail2) => {\n    pickupOrder(orderDetail2, (orderDetail3) => {\n      deliverOrder(orderDetail3);\n    });\n  });\n});\n```\n\n### 💡 The 3 Fatal Flaws of Callback Hell\n1. **Unreadable & Unmaintainable Code (Pyramid of Doom):** Deeply nested anonymous callbacks passing arguments (`orderDetail`) down multiple levels are extremely hard for engineers to read or modify.\n2. **Brittle Error Handling & Debugging:** If an error occurs at Step 2 (e.g., restaurant out of stock) or Step 3, there is no unified `try...catch` block—every single level must manually check and propagate errors.\n3. **Inversion of Control (IoC — The Biggest Architectural Danger):** When you pass `preparingOrder` as a callback into `placedOrder` (which might be maintained by a different team or third-party payment SDK), you surrender control of your program's execution! If a bug in `placedOrder` forgets to call `callback()`, calls `callback()` **twice** (charging or preparing twice!), or passes corrupted arguments, your entire downstream pipeline breaks silently.\n\n### 🛠️ Full Simulated Zomato Order Pipeline Using Callbacks\n```javascript\nconst orderDetail = {\n  orderId: 123123,\n  food: ["Pizza", "Biryani", "Coke",\n  cost: 620,\n  customer_name: "Rohit",\n  customer_location: "Dwarka",\n  restaurant_location: "Delhi"\n};\n\nfunction placedOrder(orderDetail, callback) {\n  console.log(`₹${orderDetail.cost} Payment is in progress...`);\n  setTimeout(() => {\n    console.log("Payment received and order got placed!");\n    orderDetail.status = true;\n    callback(orderDetail);\n  }, 3000);\n}\n\nfunction preparingOrder(orderDetail, callback) {\n  console.log(`Food preparation started for ${orderDetail.food}`);\n  setTimeout(() => {\n    console.log("Your order is now prepared!");\n    orderDetail.token = 123;\n    callback(orderDetail);\n  }, 3000);\n}\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Extensions/Async_JS/Introducing", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Extensions/Async_JS/Introducing", "title": "MDN Web Docs — Introducing Asynchronous JavaScript & Callbacks"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CTJLBCYod9E", "title": "Callback Hell in JavaScript | Async Programming Tutorial | Javascript Full Course #18", "duration": "38m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-18-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What are the two main problems with Callback-based asynchronous programming (Callback Hell and Inversion of Control)?", "title": "Q1: What are the two main problems with Callback-based asynchronous programming (Callback Hell and Inversion of Control)?", "answer": "1) Callback Hell (Pyramid of Doom): Nesting multiple dependent asynchronous callbacks inside one another creates deeply indented, horizontally sprawling code that is difficult to read, maintain, and debug. 2) Inversion of Control (IoC): By passing your continuation callback into an external or third-party function, you hand over control of when and how your code runs. You have to trust that the external function will call your callback exactly once, not zero times, not multiple times, and not synchronously or with wrong arguments.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "18-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "1) Callback Hell (Pyramid of Doom): Nesting multiple dependent asynchronous callbacks inside one another creates deeply indented, horizontally sprawling code that is difficult to read, maintain, and debug. 2) Inversion of Control (IoC): By passing your continuation callback into an external or third-party function, you hand over control of when and how your code runs. You have to trust that the external function will call your callback exactly once, not zero times, not multiple times, and not synchronously or with wrong arguments."}, {"id": "js-lec-18-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why did placedOrder(preparingOrder(pickupOrder)) crash and execute immediately out of order when passed without an arrow function wrapper?", "title": "Q2: Why did placedOrder(preparingOrder(pickupOrder)) crash and execute immediately out of order when passed without an arrow function wrapper?", "answer": "Writing preparingOrder(pickupOrder) with parentheses invokes preparingOrder immediately at the moment arguments are evaluated—before placedOrder even starts running! And since preparingOrder returns undefined, placedOrder receives undefined instead of a function reference. Wrapping it in an arrow function () => preparingOrder(pickupOrder) delays execution until placedOrder explicitly invokes callback().", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "18-q2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Writing preparingOrder(pickupOrder) with parentheses invokes preparingOrder immediately at the moment arguments are evaluated—before placedOrder even starts running! And since preparingOrder returns undefined, placedOrder receives undefined instead of a function reference. Wrapping it in an arrow function () => preparingOrder(pickupOrder) delays execution until placedOrder explicitly invokes callback()."}, {"id": "js-lec-18-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: Why shouldn't placedOrder() simply hardcode a direct call to preparingOrder() at the end of its setTimeout block?", "title": "Q3: Why shouldn't placedOrder() simply hardcode a direct call to preparingOrder() at the end of its setTimeout block?", "answer": "Hardcoding preparingOrder() inside placedOrder() tightly couples the two functions and destroys reusability. For example, if a user buys a digital gift card or chooses self-pickup where delivery/preparation steps differ, placedOrder() could not be reused. Passing the next action dynamically (or returning a Promise) keeps each step modular and decoupled.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "18-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Hardcoding preparingOrder() inside placedOrder() tightly couples the two functions and destroys reusability. For example, if a user buys a digital gift card or chooses self-pickup where delivery/preparation steps differ, placedOrder() could not be reused. Passing the next action dynamically (or returning a Promise) keeps each step modular and decoupled."}, {"id": "js-lec-18-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why does standard synchronous try { ... } catch (e) { ... } fail to catch errors thrown inside a setTimeout callback?", "title": "Q4: Why does standard synchronous try { ... } catch (e) { ... } fail to catch errors thrown inside a setTimeout callback?", "answer": "Because try...catch only catches errors that occur while that synchronous execution frame is still on the Call Stack! When setTimeout(cb, 3000) is called, the synchronous try...catch block finishes and pops off the Call Stack immediately. When cb finally runs 3 seconds later via the Event Loop, it executes in a brand-new Call Stack frame outside the original try...catch.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "18-q4", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Because try...catch only catches errors that occur while that synchronous execution frame is still on the Call Stack! When setTimeout(cb, 3000) is called, the synchronous try...catch block finishes and pops off the Call Stack immediately. When cb finally runs 3 seconds later via the Event Loop, it executes in a brand-new Call Stack frame outside the original try...catch."}, {"id": "js-lec-18-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is the Node.js \\"Error-First Callback\\" convention that was used before Promises?", "title": "Q5: What is the Node.js \\"Error-First Callback\\" convention that was used before Promises?", "answer": "In classic Node.js APIs (like fs.readFile), callbacks followed the signature (err, data) => { ... }, where the first parameter is reserved for an Error object (null if successful) and the second parameter holds the result data. Every nested level had to manually check if (err) return handleError(err); before proceeding.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "18-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "In classic Node.js APIs (like fs.readFile), callbacks followed the signature (err, data) => { ... }, where the first parameter is reserved for an Error object (null if successful) and the second parameter holds the result data. Every nested level had to manually check if (err) return handleError(err); before proceeding."}, {"id": "js-lec-18-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: How do Promises solve the Inversion of Control problem of callbacks?", "title": "Q6: How do Promises solve the Inversion of Control problem of callbacks?", "answer": "With Promises, instead of passing your callback into a third-party function and hoping it calls you properly, the third-party function immediately returns a Promise object to you. JavaScript's native Promise specification guarantees that a Promise can only settle once (either fulfilled or rejected, never both and never twice) and that your attached .then() callback will be invoked predictably via the Microtask Queue.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "18-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "With Promises, instead of passing your callback into a third-party function and hoping it calls you properly, the third-party function immediately returns a Promise object to you. JavaScript's native Promise specification guarantees that a Promise can only settle once (either fulfilled or rejected, never both and never twice) and that your attached .then() callback will be invoked predictably via the Microtask Queue."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn real-world applications (like a Zomato order pipeline), asynchronous tasks often depend sequentially on the completion of the previous step:\n1. **`placeOrder(orderDetail)`** (Process payment first)\n2. **`preparingOrder(orderDetail)`** (Notify restaurant only *after* payment succeeds)\n3. **`pickupOrder(orderDetail)`** (Assign delivery partner only *after* food is prepared)\n4. **`deliverOrder(orderDetail)`** (Deliver to customer only *after* order is picked up)\n\n### 💡 Why Calling Async Functions Line-by-Line Fails\n* If you call `placeOrder(); preparingOrder();` on consecutive lines, JavaScript does not wait for the 3-second payment timer inside `placeOrder()`—it immediately starts `preparingOrder()` before payment is even received!\n* Before ES6 Promises existed, the only way to enforce sequential order was to pass the next step as a **Callback Function** into the previous step.\n\n### 💡 What Is Callback Hell (The Pyramid of Doom)?\nWhen 4 or more dependent asynchronous operations are nested inside each other using anonymous wrapper callbacks to pass data (`orderDetail`) forward, the code indents horizontally into a deeply nested triangle known as **Callback Hell**:\n\n```javascript\n// Classic Callback Hell (Pyramid of Doom)\nplacedOrder(orderDetail, (orderDetail1) => {\n  preparingOrder(orderDetail1, (orderDetail2) => {\n    pickupOrder(orderDetail2, (orderDetail3) => {\n      deliverOrder(orderDetail3);\n    });\n  });\n});\n```\n\n### 💡 The 3 Fatal Flaws of Callback Hell\n1. **Unreadable & Unmaintainable Code (Pyramid of Doom):** Deeply nested anonymous callbacks passing arguments (`orderDetail`) down multiple levels are extremely hard for engineers to read or modify.\n2. **Brittle Error Handling & Debugging:** If an error occurs at Step 2 (e.g., restaurant out of stock) or Step 3, there is no unified `try...catch` block—every single level must manually check and propagate errors.\n3. **Inversion of Control (IoC — The Biggest Architectural Danger):** When you pass `preparingOrder` as a callback into `placedOrder` (which might be maintained by a different team or third-party payment SDK), you surrender control of your program's execution! If a bug in `placedOrder` forgets to call `callback()`, calls `callback()` **twice** (charging or preparing twice!), or passes corrupted arguments, your entire downstream pipeline breaks silently.\n\n### 🛠️ Full Simulated Zomato Order Pipeline Using Callbacks\n```javascript\nconst orderDetail = {\n  orderId: 123123,\n  food: ["Pizza", "Biryani", "Coke",\n  cost: 620,\n  customer_name: "Rohit",\n  customer_location: "Dwarka",\n  restaurant_location: "Delhi"\n};\n\nfunction placedOrder(orderDetail, callback) {\n  console.log(`₹${orderDetail.cost} Payment is in progress...`);\n  setTimeout(() => {\n    console.log("Payment received and order got placed!");\n    orderDetail.status = true;\n    callback(orderDetail);\n  }, 3000);\n}\n\nfunction preparingOrder(orderDetail, callback) {\n  console.log(`Food preparation started for ${orderDetail.food}`);\n  setTimeout(() => {\n    console.log("Your order is now prepared!");\n    orderDetail.token = 123;\n    callback(orderDetail);\n  }, 3000);\n}\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 18: Asynchronous Workflows & Callback Hell — Pyramid of Doom, Error Propagation & Inversion of Control	Model multi-step dependent asynchronous workflows (Order -> Prepare -> Pickup -> Deliver), understand why nested callbacks create Callback Hell, and analyze Inversion of Control.	20	Easy	\N	18	[{"id": "js-lec-18-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What are the two main problems with Callback-based asynchronous programming (Callback Hell and Inversion of Control)?", "title": "Q1: What are the two main problems with Callback-based asynchronous programming (Callback Hell and Inversion of Control)?", "answer": "1) Callback Hell (Pyramid of Doom): Nesting multiple dependent asynchronous callbacks inside one another creates deeply indented, horizontally sprawling code that is difficult to read, maintain, and debug. 2) Inversion of Control (IoC): By passing your continuation callback into an external or third-party function, you hand over control of when and how your code runs. You have to trust that the external function will call your callback exactly once, not zero times, not multiple times, and not synchronously or with wrong arguments.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "18-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "1) Callback Hell (Pyramid of Doom): Nesting multiple dependent asynchronous callbacks inside one another creates deeply indented, horizontally sprawling code that is difficult to read, maintain, and debug. 2) Inversion of Control (IoC): By passing your continuation callback into an external or third-party function, you hand over control of when and how your code runs. You have to trust that the external function will call your callback exactly once, not zero times, not multiple times, and not synchronously or with wrong arguments."}, {"id": "js-lec-18-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why did placedOrder(preparingOrder(pickupOrder)) crash and execute immediately out of order when passed without an arrow function wrapper?", "title": "Q2: Why did placedOrder(preparingOrder(pickupOrder)) crash and execute immediately out of order when passed without an arrow function wrapper?", "answer": "Writing preparingOrder(pickupOrder) with parentheses invokes preparingOrder immediately at the moment arguments are evaluated—before placedOrder even starts running! And since preparingOrder returns undefined, placedOrder receives undefined instead of a function reference. Wrapping it in an arrow function () => preparingOrder(pickupOrder) delays execution until placedOrder explicitly invokes callback().", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "18-q2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Writing preparingOrder(pickupOrder) with parentheses invokes preparingOrder immediately at the moment arguments are evaluated—before placedOrder even starts running! And since preparingOrder returns undefined, placedOrder receives undefined instead of a function reference. Wrapping it in an arrow function () => preparingOrder(pickupOrder) delays execution until placedOrder explicitly invokes callback()."}, {"id": "js-lec-18-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: Why shouldn't placedOrder() simply hardcode a direct call to preparingOrder() at the end of its setTimeout block?", "title": "Q3: Why shouldn't placedOrder() simply hardcode a direct call to preparingOrder() at the end of its setTimeout block?", "answer": "Hardcoding preparingOrder() inside placedOrder() tightly couples the two functions and destroys reusability. For example, if a user buys a digital gift card or chooses self-pickup where delivery/preparation steps differ, placedOrder() could not be reused. Passing the next action dynamically (or returning a Promise) keeps each step modular and decoupled.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "18-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Hardcoding preparingOrder() inside placedOrder() tightly couples the two functions and destroys reusability. For example, if a user buys a digital gift card or chooses self-pickup where delivery/preparation steps differ, placedOrder() could not be reused. Passing the next action dynamically (or returning a Promise) keeps each step modular and decoupled."}, {"id": "js-lec-18-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why does standard synchronous try { ... } catch (e) { ... } fail to catch errors thrown inside a setTimeout callback?", "title": "Q4: Why does standard synchronous try { ... } catch (e) { ... } fail to catch errors thrown inside a setTimeout callback?", "answer": "Because try...catch only catches errors that occur while that synchronous execution frame is still on the Call Stack! When setTimeout(cb, 3000) is called, the synchronous try...catch block finishes and pops off the Call Stack immediately. When cb finally runs 3 seconds later via the Event Loop, it executes in a brand-new Call Stack frame outside the original try...catch.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "18-q4", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Because try...catch only catches errors that occur while that synchronous execution frame is still on the Call Stack! When setTimeout(cb, 3000) is called, the synchronous try...catch block finishes and pops off the Call Stack immediately. When cb finally runs 3 seconds later via the Event Loop, it executes in a brand-new Call Stack frame outside the original try...catch."}, {"id": "js-lec-18-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What is the Node.js \\"Error-First Callback\\" convention that was used before Promises?", "title": "Q5: What is the Node.js \\"Error-First Callback\\" convention that was used before Promises?", "answer": "In classic Node.js APIs (like fs.readFile), callbacks followed the signature (err, data) => { ... }, where the first parameter is reserved for an Error object (null if successful) and the second parameter holds the result data. Every nested level had to manually check if (err) return handleError(err); before proceeding.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "18-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "In classic Node.js APIs (like fs.readFile), callbacks followed the signature (err, data) => { ... }, where the first parameter is reserved for an Error object (null if successful) and the second parameter holds the result data. Every nested level had to manually check if (err) return handleError(err); before proceeding."}, {"id": "js-lec-18-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: How do Promises solve the Inversion of Control problem of callbacks?", "title": "Q6: How do Promises solve the Inversion of Control problem of callbacks?", "answer": "With Promises, instead of passing your callback into a third-party function and hoping it calls you properly, the third-party function immediately returns a Promise object to you. JavaScript's native Promise specification guarantees that a Promise can only settle once (either fulfilled or rejected, never both and never twice) and that your attached .then() callback will be invoked predictably via the Microtask Queue.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "18-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "With Promises, instead of passing your callback into a third-party function and hoping it calls you properly, the third-party function immediately returns a Promise object to you. JavaScript's native Promise specification guarantees that a Promise can only settle once (either fulfilled or rejected, never both and never twice) and that your attached .then() callback will be invoked predictably via the Microtask Queue."}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Extensions/Async_JS/Introducing	\N	\N	\N
22137	Lecture 19: JavaScript Promises & JSON vs. JS Objects — Promise States, .then/.catch/.finally, fetch & Custom Promises	js-lecture-19-promises-in-depth-states-chaining-fetch-json-vs-object	Master Promise lifecycle states (pending, fulfilled, rejected), consume APIs with fetch() and .then/.catch/.finally, contrast JSON with JS Objects, and build custom Promises to eliminate Callback Hell.	2026-10-06 21:36:53.504856+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nA **Promise** is a JavaScript object representing the eventual completion (or failure) of an asynchronous operation and its resulting value. Promises replace nested Callback Hell with flat, composable **Promise Chaining** and unified error handling.\n\n### 💡 The 3 States of a Promise & Consuming Promises\n1. **`pending`:** Initial state when the asynchronous operation is still in flight.\n2. **`fulfilled` (Resolved):** The operation completed successfully and produced a value (handled by **`.then(data => ...)`**).\n3. **`rejected`:** The operation failed and produced an error reason (handled by **`.catch(err => ...)`**).\n* **`.finally(() => ...)`:** Runs unconditionally once the Promise settles (whether fulfilled or rejected)—ideal for hiding loading spinners or closing database connections.\n\n### 💡 Crucial Interview Fact: When Does `fetch()` Reject vs. Resolve?\n* A `fetch()` Promise **ONLY enters the `rejected` state** if the request could not reach the server at all (e.g., user internet disconnected, DNS lookup failure, or CORS block).\n* If the server responds with **HTTP 404 (Not Found)** or **HTTP 500 (Internal Server Error)**, `fetch()` still considers the network round-trip **`fulfilled`**! You must manually inspect **`if (!response.ok) throw new Error(...)`** inside `.then()` to route HTTP errors into `.catch()`.\n\n### 💡 JSON vs. JavaScript Object\n* **JSON (JavaScript Object Notation):** A language-independent **text string format** for transmitting data between servers (C++, Java, Python, Go, Node.js) and clients.\n* **Strict JSON Rules:** All keys **must be wrapped in double quotes** (`"name": "Rohit"`), strings must use double quotes, **no trailing commas** are allowed, and **functions, `undefined`, and `Symbol`s are forbidden**.\n* **Conversion:** `response.json()` (async Promise) or `JSON.parse(jsonString)` converts JSON text into a JS Object; `JSON.stringify(jsObj)` serializes a JS Object into a JSON string.\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Custom Promises, Flat Chaining & `fetch` Error Handling\n```javascript\n// 1. Creating a Custom Promise with new Promise((resolve, reject) => ...)\nfunction placedOrder(orderDetail) {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    console.log(`₹${orderDetail.cost} Payment is in progress...`);\n    setTimeout(() => {\n      if (Math.random() > 0.1) {\n        orderDetail.status = true;\n        resolve(orderDetail); // Transitions Promise to 'fulfilled'\n      } else {\n        reject("Payment failed at bank gateway!"); // Transitions to 'rejected'\n      }\n    }, 2000);\n  });\n}\n\n// 2. Flat Promise Chaining (Replaces Callback Hell!) + Unified .catch() & .finally()\nplacedOrder({ orderId: 101, cost: 620, food: ["Pizza" })\n  .then((orderDetail) => preparingOrder(orderDetail))\n  .then((orderDetail) => pickupOrder(orderDetail))\n  .then((orderDetail) => deliverOrder(orderDetail))\n  .catch((error) => console.log("Order Pipeline Error:", error))\n  .finally(() => console.log("Hide loading spinner / cleanup complete"));\n\n// 3. Proper fetch() Consumption Checking response.ok\nfetch("https://api.github.com/users")\n  .then((response) => {\n    if (!response.ok) {\n      throw new Error("HTTP Error Status: " + response.status);\n    }\n    return response.json(); // Returns a Promise!\n  })\n  .then((users) => console.log("Fetched users count:", users.length))\n  .catch((err) => console.log("Network or HTTP Error:", err.message));\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise", "title": "MDN Web Docs — Promise Built-In Object Reference"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LYe1my0KkPo", "title": "Promises in Javascript | JSON vs JS Object| Javascript Full Course #19", "duration": "72m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-19-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: If an API endpoint returns an HTTP 404 Not Found or 500 Internal Server Error, does fetch() trigger the .catch() block automatically?", "title": "Q1: If an API endpoint returns an HTTP 404 Not Found or 500 Internal Server Error, does fetch() trigger the .catch() block automatically?", "answer": "No! The fetch() Promise only rejects (triggering .catch()) on network-level failures where the browser could not communicate with the server at all (such as DNS failure, no internet connection, or CORS refusal). If the server responds with an HTTP 404 or 500 status code, the server DID respond, so fetch() resolves as fulfilled! To handle 404/500 in .catch(), you must check if (!response.ok) throw new Error(response.statusText) inside the first .then() block.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "19-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "No! The fetch() Promise only rejects (triggering .catch()) on network-level failures where the browser could not communicate with the server at all (such as DNS failure, no internet connection, or CORS refusal). If the server responds with an HTTP 404 or 500 status code, the server DID respond, so fetch() resolves as fulfilled! To handle 404/500 in .catch(), you must check if (!response.ok) throw new Error(response.statusText) inside the first .then() block."}, {"id": "js-lec-19-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: What are the exact differences between JSON and a JavaScript Object?", "title": "Q2: What are the exact differences between JSON and a JavaScript Object?", "answer": "1) Nature: A JavaScript Object is an in-memory runtime data structure tied to the JS engine; JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a language-agnostic plain-text string format understood by Python, Java, Go, C++, and JS. 2) Syntax Rules: In JSON, all property keys MUST be enclosed in double quotes (\\"key\\"), strings must use double quotes (no single quotes or backticks), and trailing commas are syntax errors. 3) Allowed Values: JSON cannot store functions, undefined, Symbols, or BigInts.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "19-q2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "1) Nature: A JavaScript Object is an in-memory runtime data structure tied to the JS engine; JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a language-agnostic plain-text string format understood by Python, Java, Go, C++, and JS. 2) Syntax Rules: In JSON, all property keys MUST be enclosed in double quotes (\\"key\\"), strings must use double quotes (no single quotes or backticks), and trailing commas are syntax errors. 3) Allowed Values: JSON cannot store functions, undefined, Symbols, or BigInts."}, {"id": "js-lec-19-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: Why do we need two .then() calls when using fetch() (one for fetch() and a second for response.json())?", "title": "Q3: Why do we need two .then() calls when using fetch() (one for fetch() and a second for response.json())?", "answer": "The first Promise returned by fetch(url) resolves as soon as the browser receives the initial HTTP response headers from the server—before the full response body stream has finished downloading! Calling response.json() reads the entire body stream asynchronously and parses the JSON text into a JS object, returning a second Promise that resolves with the parsed data.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "19-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "The first Promise returned by fetch(url) resolves as soon as the browser receives the initial HTTP response headers from the server—before the full response body stream has finished downloading! Calling response.json() reads the entire body stream asynchronously and parses the JSON text into a JS object, returning a second Promise that resolves with the parsed data."}, {"id": "js-lec-19-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What is the purpose of Promise.prototype.finally(), and how does it differ from .then() and .catch()?", "title": "Q4: What is the purpose of Promise.prototype.finally(), and how does it differ from .then() and .catch()?", "answer": ".finally(callback) executes whenever the Promise settles, regardless of whether it was fulfilled or rejected. Unlike .then() and .catch(), the .finally() callback receives no arguments (because it doesn't care whether the promise succeeded or failed) and automatically passes through the original resolved value or rejection error. It is used to avoid duplicating cleanup code—such as hiding a loading spinner or closing a DB connection.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "19-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": ".finally(callback) executes whenever the Promise settles, regardless of whether it was fulfilled or rejected. Unlike .then() and .catch(), the .finally() callback receives no arguments (because it doesn't care whether the promise succeeded or failed) and automatically passes through the original resolved value or rejection error. It is used to avoid duplicating cleanup code—such as hiding a loading spinner or closing a DB connection."}, {"id": "js-lec-19-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: What happens if you forget the return keyword inside a .then() callback during Promise Chaining?", "title": "Q5: What happens if you forget the return keyword inside a .then() callback during Promise Chaining?", "answer": "In a Promise chain, each .then() passes its return value to the next .then() in the chain. If you forget to write return preparingOrder(orderDetail) inside a multi-line .then({ ... }) block, that function implicitly returns undefined, causing the next .then() in the chain to fire immediately with undefined instead of waiting for preparingOrder's Promise to resolve!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "19-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "In a Promise chain, each .then() passes its return value to the next .then() in the chain. If you forget to write return preparingOrder(orderDetail) inside a multi-line .then({ ... }) block, that function implicitly returns undefined, causing the next .then() in the chain to fire immediately with undefined instead of waiting for preparingOrder's Promise to resolve!"}, {"id": "js-lec-19-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Can a Promise change its state from fulfilled to rejected (or vice versa) if resolve() and reject() are both called inside new Promise()?", "title": "Q6: Can a Promise change its state from fulfilled to rejected (or vice versa) if resolve() and reject() are both called inside new Promise()?", "answer": "No. Once a Promise transitions from pending to either fulfilled (via resolve) or rejected (via reject), it is permanently \\"settled\\" and immutable. Any subsequent calls to resolve() or reject() inside the executor function are silently ignored.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "19-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "No. Once a Promise transitions from pending to either fulfilled (via resolve) or rejected (via reject), it is permanently \\"settled\\" and immutable. Any subsequent calls to resolve() or reject() inside the executor function are silently ignored."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nA **Promise** is a JavaScript object representing the eventual completion (or failure) of an asynchronous operation and its resulting value. Promises replace nested Callback Hell with flat, composable **Promise Chaining** and unified error handling.\n\n### 💡 The 3 States of a Promise & Consuming Promises\n1. **`pending`:** Initial state when the asynchronous operation is still in flight.\n2. **`fulfilled` (Resolved):** The operation completed successfully and produced a value (handled by **`.then(data => ...)`**).\n3. **`rejected`:** The operation failed and produced an error reason (handled by **`.catch(err => ...)`**).\n* **`.finally(() => ...)`:** Runs unconditionally once the Promise settles (whether fulfilled or rejected)—ideal for hiding loading spinners or closing database connections.\n\n### 💡 Crucial Interview Fact: When Does `fetch()` Reject vs. Resolve?\n* A `fetch()` Promise **ONLY enters the `rejected` state** if the request could not reach the server at all (e.g., user internet disconnected, DNS lookup failure, or CORS block).\n* If the server responds with **HTTP 404 (Not Found)** or **HTTP 500 (Internal Server Error)**, `fetch()` still considers the network round-trip **`fulfilled`**! You must manually inspect **`if (!response.ok) throw new Error(...)`** inside `.then()` to route HTTP errors into `.catch()`.\n\n### 💡 JSON vs. JavaScript Object\n* **JSON (JavaScript Object Notation):** A language-independent **text string format** for transmitting data between servers (C++, Java, Python, Go, Node.js) and clients.\n* **Strict JSON Rules:** All keys **must be wrapped in double quotes** (`"name": "Rohit"`), strings must use double quotes, **no trailing commas** are allowed, and **functions, `undefined`, and `Symbol`s are forbidden**.\n* **Conversion:** `response.json()` (async Promise) or `JSON.parse(jsonString)` converts JSON text into a JS Object; `JSON.stringify(jsObj)` serializes a JS Object into a JSON string.\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Custom Promises, Flat Chaining & `fetch` Error Handling\n```javascript\n// 1. Creating a Custom Promise with new Promise((resolve, reject) => ...)\nfunction placedOrder(orderDetail) {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    console.log(`₹${orderDetail.cost} Payment is in progress...`);\n    setTimeout(() => {\n      if (Math.random() > 0.1) {\n        orderDetail.status = true;\n        resolve(orderDetail); // Transitions Promise to 'fulfilled'\n      } else {\n        reject("Payment failed at bank gateway!"); // Transitions to 'rejected'\n      }\n    }, 2000);\n  });\n}\n\n// 2. Flat Promise Chaining (Replaces Callback Hell!) + Unified .catch() & .finally()\nplacedOrder({ orderId: 101, cost: 620, food: ["Pizza" })\n  .then((orderDetail) => preparingOrder(orderDetail))\n  .then((orderDetail) => pickupOrder(orderDetail))\n  .then((orderDetail) => deliverOrder(orderDetail))\n  .catch((error) => console.log("Order Pipeline Error:", error))\n  .finally(() => console.log("Hide loading spinner / cleanup complete"));\n\n// 3. Proper fetch() Consumption Checking response.ok\nfetch("https://api.github.com/users")\n  .then((response) => {\n    if (!response.ok) {\n      throw new Error("HTTP Error Status: " + response.status);\n    }\n    return response.json(); // Returns a Promise!\n  })\n  .then((users) => console.log("Fetched users count:", users.length))\n  .catch((err) => console.log("Network or HTTP Error:", err.message));\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 19: JavaScript Promises & JSON vs. JS Objects — Promise States, .then/.catch/.finally, fetch & Custom Promises	Master Promise lifecycle states (pending, fulfilled, rejected), consume APIs with fetch() and .then/.catch/.finally, contrast JSON with JS Objects, and build custom Promises to eliminate Callback Hell.	20	Easy	\N	19	[{"id": "js-lec-19-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: If an API endpoint returns an HTTP 404 Not Found or 500 Internal Server Error, does fetch() trigger the .catch() block automatically?", "title": "Q1: If an API endpoint returns an HTTP 404 Not Found or 500 Internal Server Error, does fetch() trigger the .catch() block automatically?", "answer": "No! The fetch() Promise only rejects (triggering .catch()) on network-level failures where the browser could not communicate with the server at