from fulfilled to rejected (or vice versa) if resolve() and reject() are both called inside new Promise()?", "answer": "No. Once a Promise transitions from pending to either fulfilled (via resolve) or rejected (via reject), it is permanently \\"settled\\" and immutable. Any subsequent calls to resolve() or reject() inside the executor function are silently ignored.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "19-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "No. Once a Promise transitions from pending to either fulfilled (via resolve) or rejected (via reject), it is permanently \\"settled\\" and immutable. Any subsequent calls to resolve() or reject() inside the executor function are silently ignored."}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise	\N	\N	\N
22363	System Design Concepts: Computer Architecture	computer-architecture-basics	Comprehensive overview of System Design Concepts covering low-level transport mechanisms, architectural scaling, and system persistence.	2026-10-12 02:59:57.98668+05:30	### 🎯 Computer Science Fundamentals: System Design Concepts: Computer Architecture\nCore systems concepts frequently tested in technical screening rounds and engineering interviews.\n\n- **System Architecture**: How modern operating systems, networks, and processors coordinate resources.\n- **Key Concepts to Master**: Concurrency primitives, resource isolation, latency vs throughput tradeoffs, and fault tolerance.\n- **Interview Key Takeaways**:\n  - Be prepared to draw diagrams explaining data flow and component lifecycles.\n  - Explain practical real-world applications (e.g. why databases use WAL, why HTTP/2 uses multiplexing).	[{"url": "https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/", "title": "OS Three Easy Pieces Guide"}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/operating-systems/", "title": "GeeksforGeeks CS Fundamentals"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/um06azpAmSk", "title": "System Design Concepts: Computer Architecture", "creator_name": "Hayk Simonyan", "duration_minutes": 4, "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan"}]	[{"id": 1, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["Hexadecimal code", "Binary digits (bits) consisting of 0s and 1s", "Assembly Language", "UTF-8 strings"], "question": "At the absolute lowest architectural level, what data format do computers exclusively understand and process?", "explanation": "Every piece of software, image, and network packet is fundamentally boiled down to electronic states (on/off, represented as 1s and 0s) that the CPU processes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "Binary digits (bits) consisting of 0s and 1s"}, {"id": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["It permanently archives data even without power.", "It is much slower than a hard drive.", "It acts as a lightning-fast, highly volatile temporary storage for active data and running applications, requiring continuous power to retain its contents.", "It executes the machine code logic."], "question": "What is the defining characteristic of Random Access Memory (RAM)?", "explanation": "RAM holds the active working set of a computer. It is vastly faster than disk storage but loses all its data the moment the computer is restarted or loses power.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "It acts as a lightning-fast, highly volatile temporary storage for active data and running applications, requiring continuous power to retain its contents."}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["To permanently store the operating system.", "To process graphical output for monitors.", "Because accessing RAM is relatively slow for a modern CPU; cache memory operates in nanoseconds, drastically speeding up access to the most frequently used instructions.", "To act as a backup hard drive."], "question": "Why do modern CPUs contain L1, L2, and L3 Cache memory directly on the processor chip?", "explanation": "The CPU is so incredibly fast that waiting for data to travel from the RAM module causes bottlenecks. The CPU stores upcoming micro-instructions in its ultra-fast L1 cache to prevent stalling.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "Because accessing RAM is relatively slow for a modern CPU; cache memory operates in nanoseconds, drastically speeding up access to the most frequently used instructions."}, {"id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "A", "options": ["An HDD uses physical spinning magnetic platters and mechanical read heads; an SSD uses flash memory chips with zero moving parts, yielding drastically faster read/write speeds.", "An HDD is volatile; an SSD is non-volatile.", "An HDD stores data in the cloud; an SSD stores data locally.", "An HDD is used for RAM; an SSD is used for CPU caching."], "question": "What is the core functional difference between an HDD (Hard Disk Drive) and an SSD (Solid State Drive)?", "explanation": "SSDs eliminate the mechanical latency of waiting for a disk to physically spin to the correct sector, making database queries and file access astronomically faster.", "correctAnswer": "A", "correct_index": 0, "correct_answer": "An HDD uses physical spinning magnetic platters and mechanical read heads; an SSD uses flash memory chips with zero moving parts, yielding drastically faster read/write speeds."}, {"id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["It generates physical CPU hardware.", "It connects the RAM to the Motherboard.", "It translates human-readable, high-level programming languages (like C++ or Java) entirely into raw binary machine code that the CPU can natively execute.", "It compresses files to save disk space."], "question": "What role does a 'Compiler' play in computer architecture?", "explanation": "The CPU cannot understand `if/else` statements. A compiler acts as a translator, parsing the complex logic down into the specific instruction set architecture (ISA) binary understood by that specific processor.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "It translates human-readable, high-level programming languages (like C++ or Java) entirely into raw binary machine code that the CPU can natively execute."}]	\N	> **Architecture Overview:** Essential technical concepts and structural principles vital for robust, scalable web architectures. Focus deeply on the mechanical trade-offs discussed in this module.	Hayk Simonyan	https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan	\N	System Design Concepts: Computer Architecture	Comprehensive overview of System Design Concepts covering low-level transport mechanisms, architectural scaling, and system persistence.	4	Easy	Advanced System Concepts	28	[]	\N	4	https://youtu.be/um06azpAmSk	System Design Concepts: Com