"Inside a `useEffect` block", "Inside the global scope", "Only at the top level of a Custom Hook"], "question": "Where is the ONLY valid place to call `useDebugValue`?", "explanation": "Calling useDebugValue inside a standard functional component will do nothing or cause linting errors. It is exclusively designed to label Custom Hooks.", "correctAnswer": "D", "correct_index": 3, "correct_answer": "Only at the top level of a Custom Hook"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["Wrap the hook in `useMemo`", "Pass a formatting callback function as the second argument to `useDebugValue`", "Use `setTimeout`", "You cannot optimize it"], "question": "How can you optimize `useDebugValue` if formatting the debug string requires a heavy computation?", "explanation": "useDebugValue accepts an optional formatting function as a second argument. This function is only executed when the React DevTools panel is actually open and inspecting the component.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "Pass a formatting callback function as the second argument to `useDebugValue`"}]	\N	> **Architectural Deep Dive:** This lecture covers the strict technical nuances, reconciliation behavior, and performance implications of using useDebugValue in production environments. Pay special attention to concurrent rendering behaviors in React 18+.	Web Dev Simplified	https://www.youtube.com/@WebDevSimplified	\N	The Perfect Hook For Debugging	Deep dive architectural lecture focusing on the mechanics, use cases, and anti-patterns of the useDebugValue hook.	15	Easy	Core Hooks & API	13	[]	\N	15	https://youtu.be/pTF86K8JZBQ	The Perfect Hook For Debugging
1370	Longest Inter-Node Path: Diameter of a Binary Tree in O(N) Time	dsa-diameter-of-binary-tree	Compute the longest path between any two nodes in a tree in a single O(N) postorder pass by tracking left plus right subtree heights.	2026-10-03 00:50:37.361007+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Longest Inter-Node Path: Diameter of a Binary Tree in O(N) Time\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rezetez59Nk", "title": "Lecture 88: Diameter of Binary Tree", "duration": "22m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/diameter-of-binary-tree/", "title": "Diameter of Binary Tree", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Compute the length of the longest path between any two nodes in a tree."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/tree-diameter/", "title": "Tree Diameter", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Calculate diameter on an undirected tree using two-pass BFS/DFS."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/diameter-of-binary-tree/1", "title": "Diameter of a Binary Tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the longest path between any two end nodes of a binary tree."}]	Module 16: Binary Trees: Structural Properties & Path Sums	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThe **Diameter** of a binary tree is the length of the longest path between any two nodes. This path **does not need to pass through the root**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Longest Path Through a Node:**\n  - The longest path turning at any node `curr` is: **`lh + rh`** (left subtree height + right subtree height).\n- **Optimal Single Pass (`O(N)` Time, `O(H)` Space):**\n  - Pass a reference `diameter = 0` into the `maxDepth` function.\n  - At every node:\n    1. Compute `lh = height(curr->left)` and `rh = height(curr->right)`.\n    2. Update global diameter: `diameter = max(diameter, lh + rh)`.\n    3. Return node height to parent: `1 + max(lh, rh)`.\n\n### 💻 Diameter of Binary Tree Blueprint\n```cpp\nint calculateHeight(TreeNode* node, int &diameter) {\n    if (node == nullptr) return 0;\n    int lh = calculateHeight(node->left, diameter);\n    int rh = calculateHeight(node->right, diameter);\n\n    diameter = max(diameter, lh + rh); // Update longest path seen so far\n    return 1 + max(lh, rh);            // Return height to parent\n}\n\nint diameterOfBinaryTree(TreeNode* root) {\n    int diameter = 0;\n    calculateHeight(root, diameter);\n    return diameter;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22183	Time and Work	aptitude-quant-time-and-work	Master 1-day work inversion, efficiency ratios, alternate day scheduling, partial work deductions, and the MDH chain rule for equivalent worker groups.	2026-10-07 11:28:34.878899+05:30	### 📌 1. The Core "Invert" Principle\n* **Total Days to 1-Day Work:** If a person completes the entire work in **`N` days**, their 1-day work is exactly **`1/N`**.\n* **Combined Work:** If A does `1/X` work in a day and B does `1/Y` work in a day, working together they do **`1/X + 1/Y`** work in a day. Just sum the fractions, and INVERT the final sum to get the total days needed!\n\n### 💡 2. Efficiency and Time\n* **Inverse Proportion:** Efficiency (speed of doing work) is strictly **inversely proportional** to the time taken.\n  * If A is **`k` times faster** than B, then Time taken by A = `(1/k) × Time taken by B`.\n  * If A is **`25% more efficient`** than B, A's efficiency is 125% of B's. Ratio of Efficiencies `A : B = 5 : 4`. Ratio of Times `A : B = 4 : 5`.\n\n### 💡 3. Advanced Placement Shortcuts\n* **The MDH Rule (Chain Rule):** For two groups doing comparable work:\n  **`(M1 × D1 × H1) / W1 = (M2 × D2 × H2) / W2`**\n  *(M = Men/Workers, D = Days, H = Hours/Day, W = Amount of Work/Wages)*\n* **Men/Women/Boys Equivalence:**\n  * If `x` Men OR `y` Women can complete a task in `D` days, equate their 1-day work to find the ratio `1 Man = (y/x) Women`. Convert all workers in the target question to a single unit (e.g., all Women) before solving.\n* **Alternate Day Work:**\n  * Calculate the work done in **1 full cycle** (e.g., A on Day 1 + B on Day 2 = 1 Cycle of 2 Days). Multiply the cycle until it gets as close to `1` (100% work) as possible without exceeding it, then manually add the remaining fraction for the final days.	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KE7tQf9spPg", "type": "youtube", "title": "Time and Work - Shortcuts & Tricks for Placement Tests, Job Interviews & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-work-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Efficiency and Time Difference] A is 5 times faster than B and takes 60 days less to finish. Find A's individual time.", "options": ["A) 10 days", "B) 12 days", "C) 15 days", "D) 20 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A can work 5 times faster than B and therefore takes 60 days less than B to complete a specific job. In exactly how many days can A individually complete the entire work?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let time taken by A be T. Since A is 5 times faster, B is 5 times slower and takes 5T days. The difference is 5T - T = 4T. We are given 4T = 60 => T = 15 days. (B takes 75 days. Difference = 60).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-work-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [MDH Rule with Inverse Proportion] 24 men finish a work in 10 days. Find days for 30 men to complete the same work.", "options": ["A) 8 days", "B) 12.5 days", "C) 14 days", "D) 15 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 24 men can completely finish a work in exactly 10 days, how many days are required to complete the exact same work if 30 men are employed?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Use MDH formula: M1 × D1 = M2 × D2. 24 × 10 = 30 × D2 => 240 = 30 × D2 => D2 = 240 / 30 = 8 days. (More men = Less days, inversely proportional, do NOT cross multiply directly!).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-work-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Combined Work of 3 People] A does work in 3 days, B in 6 days, C in 7 days. Find time taken together.", "options": ["A) 1 5/9 days", "B) 1 4/9 days", "C) 2 1/7 days", "D) 2 1/3 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A can do a piece of work in 3 days, B can do the same work in 6 days, and C can do it in 7 days. If they all work together, in exactly how many days will they complete the work?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Combined 1-day work = 1/3 + 1/6 + 1/7. LCM of 3, 6, 7 is 42. (14/42) + (7/42) + (6/42) = 27/42. Simplify 27/42 by dividing by 3 -> 9/14. Total time is the inverse = 14/9 days = 1 5/9 days.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-work-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Overlapping Pairs] P & Q in 12 days, Q & R in 16 days, R & P in 24 days. Find time for P, Q, R together.", "options": ["A) 8 1/3 days", "B) 9 3/5 days", "C) 10 2/3 days", "D) 11 1/5 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "P and Q can complete a work together in 12 days. Q and R can complete the same work in 16 days. R and P can complete it in 24 days. If P, Q, and R all work together, in how many days will they finish the work?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) 1/P + 1/Q = 1/12. 2) 1/Q + 1/R = 1/16. 3) 1/R + 1/P = 1/24. Add all three: 2(1/P + 1/Q + 1/R) = 1/12 + 1/16 + 1/24. LCM of 12, 16, 24 is 48. Sum = 4/48 + 3/48 + 2/48 = 9/48 = 3/16. So, 1/P + 1/Q + 1/R = 3/32. The time taken together is 32/3 = 10 2/3 days.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-work-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Efficiency Given as Percentage] P does work in 30 days. Q is 25% more efficient than P. Find time for Q alone.", "options": ["A) 20 days", "B) 24 days", "C) 25 days", "D) 37.5 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "P can complete a specific work in exactly 30 days. Q is 25% more efficient than P in completing the exact same work. In how many days will Q completely finish the work if acting alone?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Efficiency of Q = 125% of Efficiency of P = 1.25 × (1/30) = (5/4) × (1/30) = 5/120 = 1/24. Thus, Q's 1-day work is 1/24. Q will finish the work in exactly 24 days.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-work-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Men and Boys Equivalency] 3 men in 2 days. 4 boys in 6 days. Find time for 8 men and 8 boys together.", "options": ["A) 0.6 days", "B) 0.8 days", "C) 1.2 days", "D) 1.5 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "3 men can complete a piece of work in exactly 2 days, and 4 boys can complete the exact same work in 6 days. In how many days will the same work be completed by a team of 8 men and 8 boys working together?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Total Men-days = 3 × 2 = 6 Men-days to finish 1 work. So 1 Man's 1-day work = 1/6. 2) Total Boy-days = 4 × 6 = 24 Boy-days to finish 1 work. So 1 Boy's 1-day work = 1/24. 3) Team = 8 Men + 8 Boys. Their combined 1-day work = 8(1/6) + 8(1/24) = 8/6 + 8/24 = 32/24 + 8/24 = 40/24 = 5/3. 4) Total time = Inverse of 5/3 = 3/5 days = 0.6 days.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-work-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Person Leaves Mid-Way] Sita in 20 days, Gita in 25 days. Both begin, Sita leaves after some days. Gita finishes rest in 10 days. Find when Sita left.", "options": ["A) 5 2/3 days", "B) 6 2/3 days", "C) 8 1/3 days", "D) 10 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Sita and Gita can do a work in 20 days and 25 days respectively. Both begin the work together, but after a few days, Sita leaves. Gita then finishes the remaining work strictly alone in exactly 10 days. After exactly how many days did Sita leave?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Gita works alone for the last 10 days. Gita's 10-day work = 10 × (1/25) = 10/25 = 2/5 of the total work. 2) The remaining work (1 - 2/5 = 3/5) was done by Sita and Gita TOGETHER before Sita left. 3) Combined 1-day work of Sita and Gita = 1/20 + 1/25 = 5/100 + 4/100 = 9/100. 4) Time they worked together = (Work done together) / (Combined 1-day work) = (3/5) / (9/100) = (3/5) × (100/9) = 300 / 45 = 60 / 9 = 20 / 3 = 6 2/3 days.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-work-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Formula for Unknown Team Efficiencies] P alone takes 25 days more than P+Q. Q alone takes 9 days more than P+Q. Find time for P+Q.", "options": ["A) 12 days", "B) 15 days", "C) 18 days", "D) 20 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "When P alone does a work, he takes exactly 25 days MORE than the time taken by P and Q working together. When Q alone does the same work, he takes exactly 9 days MORE than the time taken by P and Q working together. In how many days can P and Q together finish this work?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let time taken by P+Q together be N. Then P takes N + 25 days, and Q takes N + 9 days. The standard shortcut formula for this exact scenario is N = √(Extra days of P × Extra days of Q). Therefore, N = √(25 × 9) = √225 = 15 days.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-work-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Hours and Days Mixed Work] A in 12 days (8h/day). B in 8 days (10h/day). Find days for A and B together (8h/day).", "options": ["A) 5 5/11 days", "B) 6 2/11 days", "C) 7 1/11 days", "D) 8 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A can complete a work in 12 days working 8 hours every day. B can complete the same work in 8 days working 10 hours every day. If A and B start working together for exactly 8 hours per day, in how many days will they complete the work?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Convert everything to hours first! A's total hours = 12 × 8 = 96 hrs. B's total hours = 8 × 10 = 80 hrs. 1) Combined 1-hour work = 1/96 + 1/80. LCM is 480. (5/480) + (6/480) = 11/480. 2) Total hours needed together = 480 / 11 hours. 3) They work 8 hours per day. Total days = (480 / 11) / 8 = 60 / 11 = 5 5/11 days.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-work-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Alternate Day Scheduling Exact Cycle] Raj in 16 days, Suraj in 12 days. They work alternate days starting with Raj. Find total days.", "options": ["A) 13 1/2 days", "B) 13 3/4 days", "C) 14 days", "D) 14 1/4 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Raj can build a house alone in 16 days, and Suraj alone can build it in 12 days. They work on alternate days. If Raj works on the very first day, in exactly how many days will the house be completely built?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Raj's 1-day work = 1/16. Suraj's 1-day work = 1/12. 2) 1 Cycle = 2 days (Raj then Suraj). Work in 1 Cycle = 1/16 + 1/12 = 3/48 + 4/48 = 7/48. 3) Find max cycles without exceeding 1: 7/48 × 6 cycles = 42/48 = 7/8. So 6 cycles = 12 days completed. Remaining work = 1 - 42/48 = 6/48 = 1/8. 4) Day 13: It's Raj's turn. He does 1/16. Remaining work = 1/8 - 1/16 = 1/16. 5) Day 14: Suraj's turn. He can do 1/12 in a full day. He needs to do 1/16. Time taken = (1/16) / (1/12) = 12/16 = 3/4 of a day. Total time = 12 (cycles) + 1 (Raj) + 3/4 (Suraj) = 13 3/4 days.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-work-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [MDH Rule with Work Amount] 15 men take 20 days to build 10 km of road. How many days for 20 men to build 15 km of road?", "options": ["A) 18 days", "B) 20.5 days", "C) 22.5 days", "D) 25 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 15 men take exactly 20 days to build a 10 km long stretch of road, how many days will it take for 20 men to build a 15 km long stretch of the identical road?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Using the MDH formula: (M1 × D1) / W1 = (M2 × D2) / W2. Here W is the distance of road built. (15 × 20) / 10 = (20 × D2) / 15 => 300 / 10 = 20 × D2 / 15 => 30 = 4 × D2 / 3 => 90 = 4 × D2 => D2 = 90 / 4 = 22.5 days.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-work-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Third Person Destructive Work] A in 10 days, B in 15 days. C destroys the work in 20 days. If all work together, find time.", "options": ["A) 8 4/7 days", "B) 10 2/3 days", "C) 12 days", "D) 15 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A can build a wall in 10 days, and B can build it in 15 days. However, C is a demolisher and can completely destroy the built wall in exactly 20 days. If A, B, and C all start working simultaneously on an empty site, in how many days will the wall be fully built?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Net 1-day work = 1/A + 1/B - 1/C = 1/10 + 1/15 - 1/20. LCM of 10, 15, 20 is 60. Sum = 6/60 + 4/60 - 3/60 = 7/60. The total time taken to build the wall is the inverse: 60/7 days = 8 4/7 days.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-work-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Food Provision Exhaustion] A garrison of 500 men had provisions for 27 days. After 3 days, a reinforcement of 300 men arrived. For how many more days will the remaining food last?", "options": ["A) 12 days", "B) 15 days", "C) 18 days", "D) 20 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A military garrison of 500 men had enough food provisions to last for 27 days. Exactly 3 days later, a reinforcement of 300 men arrived at the garrison. For how many MORE days will the remaining food provisions last the new total population?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) After 3 days, the 500 men have enough food left for (27 - 3) = 24 days. Total \\"man-days\\" of food remaining = 500 × 24 = 12,000 man-days. 2) The new population is 500 + 300 = 800 men. 3) The number of days the 12,000 man-days of food will last for 800 men is 12000 / 800 = 15 days.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-work-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Work Remaining Fraction] A can do a work in 20 days. He works for 5 days and leaves. What fraction of work is left for B?", "options": ["A) 1/4", "B) 2/5", "C) 1/2", "D) 3/4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A can completely finish a job in exactly 20 days. He starts the job and works alone for exactly 5 days before leaving. What exact fraction of the original total work is left unfinished?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "A's 1-day work = 1/20. Work done by A in 5 days = 5 × (1/20) = 5/20 = 1/4 of the total work. The fraction of work left unfinished is 1 - 1/4 = 3/4.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-work-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Efficiency Ratio Given in Fraction] A is 30% more efficient than B. How much time will they take working together to complete a job B does in 23 days?", "options": ["A) 10 days", "B) 11 days", "C) 13 days", "D) 15 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Worker A is exactly 30% more efficient than Worker B. If Worker B alone takes exactly 23 days to complete a specific job, how many days will it take for A and B to complete the exact same job working together?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Efficiency A : Efficiency B = 130 : 100 = 13 : 10. 2) This means if B completes 10 units of work per day, A completes 13 units. 3) Total work = B's daily work × B's days = 10 units/day × 23 days = 230 units. 4) When working together, their combined daily work = 13 + 10 = 23 units/day. 5) Total time taken together = Total Work / Combined Rate = 230 / 23 = 10 days.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-work-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Two Variables MDH Equality] If 4 men and 6 women can do a work in 8 days, while 3 men and 7 women can do it in 10 days, find time for 10 women.", "options": ["A) 32 days", "B) 36 days", "C) 40 days", "D) 45 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 4 men and 6 women can complete a work in 8 days, while 3 men and 7 women can complete the exact same work in 10 days, how many days will exactly 10 women working alone take to complete the work?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Equate total work: 8(4M + 6W) = 10(3M + 7W) => 32M + 48W = 30M + 70W => 2M = 22W => 1M = 11W. Substitute 1M = 11W into the first group: (4(11W) + 6W) × 8 days = (44W + 6W) × 8 = 50W × 8 = 400 Woman-days to complete the work. Total time for 10 women = 400 / 10 = 40 days.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-work-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Alternative Joiners in Time and Work] A in 20, B in 30, C in 60. A works continuously. B and C help him every 3rd day. Find total days.", "options": ["A) 12 days", "B) 15 days", "C) 16 days", "D) 18 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A can do a work in 20 days, B in 30 days, and C in 60 days. A works continuously every day. B and C help him together every third day. In how many days will the entire work be finished?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Total work = LCM(20, 30, 60) = 60 units. A's rate = 3 units/day. B's rate = 2 units/day. C's rate = 1 unit/day. Day 1: A works alone = 3 units. Day 2: A works alone = 3 units. Day 3: A, B, and C work together = 3 + 2 + 1 = 6 units. 1 Cycle of 3 days completes 3 + 3 + 6 = 12 units. Number of cycles required = 60 / 12 = 5 full cycles. Total days = 5 × 3 = 15 days.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-work-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Fractional Rate Comparison] A does half as much work as B in three-fourth of the time. If together they take 18 days, find B's time.", "options": ["A) 30 days", "B) 36 days", "C) 40 days", "D) 45 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A does half as much work as B in three-fourths of the time taken by B. If together they complete the work in exactly 18 days, how many days shall B take to complete the work alone?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let B's 1-day work be 1 unit (so B takes W days). In (3/4) of B's time, A does 1/2 of B's work. Thus, A's rate = (1/2 Work) / (3/4 Time) = 2/3 of B's rate! So Efficiency A : B = 2 : 3. Combined rate = 5 units/day. Total work = 18 days × 5 units/day = 90 units. B alone (3 units/day) takes 90 / 3 = 30 days.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-work-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Deduction from Total Wage] If 6 men and 8 boys can do a piece of work in 10 days while 26 men and 48 boys can do the same in 2 days, what is the ratio of their efficiencies?", "options": ["A) 1 : 2", "B) 2 : 1", "C) 3 : 2", "D) 4 : 3"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 6 men and 8 boys can complete a piece of work in 10 days, while 26 men and 48 boys can complete the exact same work in 2 days, what is the ratio of the daily work capacity of a man to that of a boy?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total work = 10(6M + 8B) = 2(26M + 48B). 60M + 80B = 52M + 96B => 8M = 16B => 1M = 2B. The ratio of the daily work capacity of a man to a boy is 2 : 1.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-work-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Equalizing Work with Multiple Variables] A takes twice as much time as B and C together, and B takes thrice as much time as A and C together. All 3 in 24 days. Find A's time.", "options": ["A) 56 days", "B) 64 days", "C) 72 days", "D) 96 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "To complete a piece of work, A takes twice as much time as B and C working together. B takes thrice as much time as A and C working together. If all three working together can complete the work in exactly 24 days, how many days will A alone take to complete it?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Time taken is inversely proportional to Efficiency. 1) Time A = 2 × Time (B+C) => Efficiency A = 1/2 × Efficiency (B+C). So A does 1/3 of the total combined work! 2) Time B = 3 × Time (A+C) => Efficiency B = 1/3 × Efficiency (A+C). So B does 1/4 of the total combined work! 3) All three take 24 days. Since A does 1/3 of the combined work, A's rate is (1/3) of (1/24) = 1/72 per day. Thus, A alone takes 72 days.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. The Core "Invert" Principle\n* **Total Days to 1-Day Work:** If a person completes the entire work in **`N` days**, their 1-day work is exactly **`1/N`**.\n* **Combined Work:** If A does `1/X` work in a day and B does `1/Y` work in a day, working together they do **`1/X + 1/Y`** work in a day. Just sum the fractions, and INVERT the final sum to get the total days needed!\n\n### 💡 2. Efficiency and Time\n* **Inverse Proportion:** Efficiency (speed of doing work) is strictly **inversely proportional** to the time taken.\n  * If A is **`k` times faster** than B, then Time taken by A = `(1/k) × Time taken by B`.\n  * If A is **`25% more efficient`** than B, A's efficiency is 125% of B's. Ratio of Efficiencies `A : B = 5 : 4`. Ratio of Times `A : B = 4 : 5`.\n\n### 💡 3. Advanced Placement Shortcuts\n* **The MDH Rule (Chain Rule):** For two groups doing comparable work:\n  **`(M1 × D1 × H1) / W1 = (M2 × D2 × H2) / W2`**\n  *(M = Men/Workers, D = Days, H = Hours/Day, W = Amount of Work/Wages)*\n* **Men/Women/Boys Equivalence:**\n  * If `x` Men OR `y` Women can complete a task in `D` days, equate their 1-day work to find the ratio `1 Man = (y/x) Women`. Convert all workers in the target question to a single unit (e.g., all Women) before solving.\n* **Alternate Day Work:**\n  * Calculate the work done in **1 full cycle** (e.g., A on Day 1 + B on Day 2 = 1 Cycle of 2 Days). Multiply the cycle until it gets as close to `1` (100% work) as possible without exceeding it, then manually add the remaining fraction for the final days.	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Time and Work	Master 1-day work inversion, efficiency ratios, alternate day scheduling, partial work deductions, and the MDH chain rule for equivalent worker groups.	43	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	17	[{"id": "apt-work-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Efficiency and Time Difference] A is 5 times faster than B and takes 60 days less to finish. Find A's individual time.", "options": ["A) 10 days", "B) 12 days", "C) 15 days", "D) 20 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A can work 5 times faster than B and therefore takes 60 days less than B to complete a specific job. In exactly how many days can A individually complete the entire work?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let time taken by A be T. Since A is 5 times faster, B is 5 times slower and takes 5T days. The difference is 5T - T = 4T. We are given 4T = 60 => T = 15 days. (B takes 75 days. Difference = 60).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-work-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [MDH Rule with Inverse Proportion] 24 men finish a work in 10 days. Find days for 30 men to complete the same work.", "options": ["A) 8 days", "B) 12.5 days", "C) 14 days", "D) 15 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 24 men can completely finish a work in exactly 10 days, how many days are required to complete the exact same work if 30 men are employed?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Use MDH formula: M1 × D1 = M2 × D2. 24 × 10 = 30 × D2 => 240 = 30 × D2 => D2 = 240 / 30 = 8 days. (More men = Less days, inversely proportional, do NOT cross multiply directly!).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-work-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Combined Work of 3 People] A does work in 3 days, B in 6 days, C in 7 days. Find time taken together.", "options": ["A) 1 5/9 days", "B) 1 4/9 days", "C) 2 1/7 days", "D) 2 1/3 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A can do a piece of work in 3 days, B can do the same work in 6 days, and C can do it in 7 days. If they all work together, in exactly how many days will they complete the work?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Combined 1-day work = 1/3 + 1/6 + 1/7. LCM of 3, 6, 7 is 42. (14/42) + (7/42) + (6/42) = 27/42. Simplify 27/42 by dividing by 3 -> 9/14. Total time is the inverse = 14/9 days = 1 5/9 days.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-work-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Overlapping Pairs] P & Q in 12 days, Q & R in 16 days, R & P in 24 days. Find time for P, Q, R together.", "options": ["A) 8 1/3 days", "B) 9 3/5 days", "C) 10 2/3 days", "D) 11 1/5 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "P and Q can complete a work together in 12 days. Q and R can complete the same work in 16 days. R and P can complete it in 24 days. If P, Q, and R all work together, in how many days will they finish the work?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) 1/P + 1/Q = 1/12. 2) 1/Q + 1/R = 1/16. 3) 1/R + 1/P = 1/24. Add all three: 2(1/P + 1/Q + 1/R) = 1/12 + 1/16 + 1/24. LCM of 12, 16, 24 is 48. Sum = 4/48 + 3/48 + 2/48 = 9/48 = 3/16. So, 1/P + 1/Q + 1/R = 3/32. The time taken together is 32/3 = 10 2/3 days.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-work-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Efficiency Given as Percentage] P does work in 30 days. Q is 25% more efficient than P. Find time for Q alone.", "options": ["A) 20 days", "B) 24 days", "C) 25 days", "D) 37.5 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "P can complete a specific work in exactly 30 days. Q is 25% more efficient than P in completing the exact same work. In how many days will Q completely finish the work if acting alone?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Efficiency of Q = 125% of Efficiency of P = 1.25 × (1/30) = (5/4) × (1/30) = 5/120 = 1/24. Thus, Q's 1-day work is 1/24. Q will finish the work in exactly 24 days.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-work-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Men and Boys Equivalency] 3 men in 2 days. 4 boys in 6 days. Find time for 8 men and 8 boys together.", "options": ["A) 0.6 days", "B) 0.8 days", "C) 1.2 days", "D) 1.5 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "3 men can complete a piece of work in exactly 2 days, and 4 boys can complete the exact same work in 6 days. In how many days will the same work be completed by a team of 8 men and 8 boys working together?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Total Men-days = 3 × 2 = 6 Men-days to finish 1 work. So 1 Man's 1-day work = 1/6. 2) Total Boy-days = 4 × 6 = 24 Boy-days to finish 1 work. So 1 Boy's 1-day work = 1/24. 3) Team = 8 Men + 8 Boys. Their combined 1-day work = 8(1/6) + 8(1/24) = 8/6 + 8/24 = 32/24 + 8/24 = 40/24 = 5/3. 4) Total time = Inverse of 5/3 = 3/5 days = 0.6 days.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-work-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Person Leaves Mid-Way] Sita in 20 days, Gita in 25 days. Both begin, Sita leaves after some days. Gita finishes rest in 10 days. Find when Sita left.", "options": ["A) 5 2/3 days", "B) 6 2/3 days", "C) 8 1/3 days", "D) 10 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Sita and Gita can do a work in 20 days and 25 days respectively. Both begin the work together, but after a few days, Sita leaves. Gita then finishes the remaining work strictly alone in exactly 10 days. After exactly how many days did Sita leave?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Gita works alone for the last 10 days. Gita's 10-day work = 10 × (1/25) = 10/25 = 2/5 of the total work. 2) The remaining work (1 - 2/5 = 3/5) was done by Sita and Gita TOGETHER before Sita left. 3) Combined 1-day work of Sita and Gita = 1/20 + 1/25 = 5/100 + 4/100 = 9/100. 4) Time they worked together = (Work done together) / (Combined 1-day work) = (3/5) / (9/100) = (3/5) × (100/9) = 300 / 45 = 60 / 9 = 20 / 3 = 6 2/3 days.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-work-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Formula for Unknown Team Efficiencies] P alone takes 25 days more than P+Q. Q alone takes 9 days more than P+Q. Find time for P+Q.", "options": ["A) 12 days", "B) 15 days", "C) 18 days", "D) 20 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "When P alone does a work, he takes exactly 25 days MORE than the time taken by P and Q working together. When Q alone does the same work, he takes exactly 9 days MORE than the time taken by P and Q working together. In how many days can P and Q together finish this work?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let time taken by P+Q together be N. Then P takes N + 25 days, and Q takes N + 9 days. The standard shortcut formula for this exact scenario is N = √(Extra days of P × Extra days of Q). Therefore, N = √(25 × 9) = √225 = 15 days.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-work-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Hours and Days Mixed Work] A in 12 days (8h/day). B in 8 days (10h/day). Find days for A and B together (8h/day).", "options": ["A) 5 5/11 days", "B) 6 2/11 days", "C) 7 1/11 days", "D) 8 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A can complete a work in 12 days working 8 hours every day. B can complete the same work in 8 days working 10 hours every day. If A and B start working together for exactly 8 hours per day, in how many days will they complete the work?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Convert everything to hours first! A's total hours = 12 × 8 = 96 hrs. B's total hours = 8 × 10 = 80 hrs. 1) Combined 1-hour work = 1/96 + 1/80. LCM is 480. (5/480) + (6/480) = 11/480. 2) Total hours needed together = 480 / 11 hours. 3) They work 8 hours per day. Total days = (480 / 11) / 8 = 60 / 11 = 5 5/11 days.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-work-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Alternate Day Scheduling Exact Cycle] Raj in 16 days, Suraj in 12 days. They work alternate days starting with Raj. Find total days.", "options": ["A) 13 1/2 days", "B) 13 3/4 days", "C) 14 days", "D) 14 1/4 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Raj can build a house alone in 16 days, and Suraj alone can build it in 12 days. They work on alternate days. If Raj works on the very first day, in exactly how many days will the house be completely built?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Raj's 1-day work = 1/16. Suraj's 1-day work = 1/12. 2) 1 Cycle = 2 days (Raj then Suraj). Work in 1 Cycle = 1/16 + 1/12 = 3/48 + 4/48 = 7/48. 3) Find max cycles without exceeding 1: 7/48 × 6 cycles = 42/48 = 7/8. So 6 cycles = 12 days completed. Remaining work = 1 - 42/48 = 6/48 = 1/8. 4) Day 13: It's Raj's turn. He does 1/16. Remaining work = 1/8 - 1/16 = 1/16. 5) Day 14: Suraj's turn. He can do 1/12 in a full day. He needs to do 1/16. Time taken = (1/16) / (1/12) = 12/16 = 3/4 of a day. Total time = 12 (cycles) + 1 (Raj) + 3/4 (Suraj) = 13 3/4 days.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-work-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [MDH Rule with Work Amount] 15 men take 20 days to build 10 km of road. How many days for 20 men to build 15 km of road?", "options": ["A) 18 days", "B) 20.5 days", "C) 22.5 days", "D) 25 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 15 men take exactly 20 days to build a 10 km long stretch of road, how many days will it take for 20 men to build a 15 km long stretch of the identical road?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Using the MDH formula: (M1 × D1) / W1 = (M2 × D2) / W2. Here W is the distance of road built. (15 × 20) / 10 = (20 × D2) / 15 => 300 / 10 = 20 × D2 / 15 => 30 = 4 × D2 / 3 => 90 = 4 × D2 => D2 = 90 / 4 = 22.5 days.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-work-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Third Person Destructive Work] A in 10 days, B in 15 days. C destroys the work in 20 days. If all work together, find time.", "options": ["A) 8 4/7 days", "B) 10 2/3 days", "C) 12 days", "D) 15 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A can build a wall in 10 days, and B can build it in 15 days. However, C is a demolisher and can completely destroy the built wall in exactly 20 days. If A, B, and C all start working simultaneously on an empty site, in how many days will the wall be fully built?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Net 1-day work = 1/A + 1/B - 1/C = 1/10 + 1/15 - 1/20. LCM of 10, 15, 20 is 60. Sum = 6/60 + 4/60 - 3/60 = 7/60. The total time taken to build the wall is the inverse: 60/7 days = 8 4/7 days.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-work-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Food Provision Exhaustion] A garrison of 500 men had provisions for 27 days. After 3 days, a reinforcement of 300 men arrived. For how many more days will the remaining food last?", "options": ["A) 12 days", "B) 15 days", "C) 18 days", "D) 20 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A military garrison of 500 men had enough food provisions to last for 27 days. Exactly 3 days later, a reinforcement of 300 men arrived at the garrison. For how many MORE days will the remaining food provisions last the new total population?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) After 3 days, the 500 men have enough food left for (27 - 3) = 24 days. Total \\"man-days\\" of food remaining = 500 × 24 = 12,000 man-days. 2) The new population is 500 + 300 = 800 men. 3) The number of days the 12,000 man-days of food will last for 800 men is 12000 / 800 = 15 days.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-work-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Work Remaining Fraction] A can do a work in 20 days. He works for 5 days and leaves. What fraction of work is left for B?", "options": ["A) 1/4", "B) 2/5", "C) 1/2", "D) 3/4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A can completely finish a job in exactly 20 days. He starts the job and works alone for exactly 5 days before leaving. What exact fraction of the original total work is left unfinished?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "A's 1-day work = 1/20. Work done by A in 5 days = 5 × (1/20) = 5/20 = 1/4 of the total work. The fraction of work left unfinished is 1 - 1/4 = 3/4.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-work-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Efficiency Ratio Given in Fraction] A is 30% more efficient than B. How much time will they take working together to complete a job B does in 23 days?", "options": ["A) 10 days", "B) 11 days", "C) 13 days", "D) 15 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Worker A is exactly 30% more efficient than Worker B. If Worker B alone takes exactly 23 days to complete a specific job, how many days will it take for A and B to complete the exact same job working together?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Efficiency A : Efficiency B = 130 : 100 = 13 : 10. 2) This means if B completes 10 units of work per day, A completes 13 units. 3) Total work = B's daily work × B's days = 10 units/day × 23 days = 230 units. 4) When working together, their combined daily work = 13 + 10 = 23 units/day. 5) Total time taken together = Total Work / Combined Rate = 230 / 23 = 10 days.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-work-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Two Variables MDH Equality] If 4 men and 6 women can do a work in 8 days, while 3 men and 7 women can do it in 10 days, find time for 10 women.", "options": ["A) 32 days", "B) 36 days", "C) 40 days", "D) 45 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 4 men and 6 women can complete a work in 8 days, while 3 men and 7 women can complete the exact same work in 10 days, how many days will exactly 10 women working alone take to complete the work?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Equate total work: 8(4M + 6W) = 10(3M + 7W) => 32M + 48W = 30M + 70W => 2M = 22W => 1M = 11W. Substitute 1M = 11W into the first group: (4(11W) + 6W) × 8 days = (44W + 6W) × 8 = 50W × 8 = 400 Woman-days to complete the work. Total time for 10 women = 400 / 10 = 40 days.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-work-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Alternative Joiners in Time and Work] A in 20, B in 30, C in 60. A works continuously. B and C help him every 3rd day. Find total days.", "options": ["A) 12 days", "B) 15 days", "C) 16 days", "D) 18 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A can do a work in 20 days, B in 30 days, and C in 60 days. A works continuously every day. B and C help him together every third day. In how many days will the entire work be finished?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Total work = LCM(20, 30, 60) = 60 units. A's rate = 3 units/day. B's rate = 2 units/day. C's rate = 1 unit/day. Day 1: A works alone = 3 units. Day 2: A works alone = 3 units. Day 3: A, B, and C work together = 3 + 2 + 1 = 6 units. 1 Cycle of 3 days completes 3 + 3 + 6 = 12 units. Number of cycles required = 60 / 12 = 5 full cycles. Total days = 5 × 3 = 15 days.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-work-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Fractional Rate Comparison] A does half as much work as B in three-fourth of the time. If together they take 18 days, find B's time.", "options": ["A) 30 days", "B) 36 days", "C) 40 days", "D) 45 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A does half as much work as B in three-fourths of the time taken by B. If together they complete the work in exactly 18 days, how many days shall B take to complete the work alone?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let B's 1-day work be 1 unit (so B takes W days). In (3/4) of B's time, A does 1/2 of B's work. Thus, A's rate = (1/2 Work) / (3/4 Time) = 2/3 of B's rate! So Efficiency A : B = 2 : 3. Combined rate = 5 units/day. Total work = 18 days × 5 units/day = 90 units. B alone (3 units/day) takes 90 / 3 = 30 days.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-work-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Deduction from Total Wage] If 6 men and 8 boys can do a piece of work in 10 days while 26 men and 48 boys can do the same in 2 days, what is the ratio of their efficiencies?", "options": ["A) 1 : 2", "B) 2 : 1", "C) 3 : 2", "D) 4 : 3"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 6 men and 8 boys can complete a piece of work in 10 days, while 26 men and 48 boys can complete the exact same work in 2 days, what is the ratio of the daily work capacity of a man to that of a boy?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total work = 10(6M + 8B) = 2(26M + 48B). 60M + 80B = 52M + 96B => 8M = 16B => 1M = 2B. The ratio of the daily work capacity of a man to a boy is 2 : 1.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-work-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Equalizing Work with Multiple Variables] A takes twice as much time as B and C together, and B takes thrice as much time as A and C together. All 3 in 24 days. Find A's time.", "options": ["A) 56 days", "B) 64 days", "C) 72 days", "D) 96 days"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "To complete a piece of work, A takes twice as much time as B and C working together. B takes thrice as much time as A and C working together. If all three working together can complete the work in exactly 24 days, how many days will A alone take to complete it?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Time taken is inversely proportional to Efficiency. 1) Time A = 2 × Time (B+C) => Efficiency A = 1/2 × Efficiency (B+C). So A does 1/3 of the total combined work! 2) Time B = 3 × Time (A+C) => Efficiency B = 1/3 × Efficiency (A+C). So B does 1/4 of the total combined work! 3) All three take 24 days. Since A does 1/3 of the combined work, A's rate is (1/3) of (1/24) = 1/72 per day. Thus, A alone takes 72 days.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22184	Boats and Streams	aptitude-quant-boats-and-streams	Master upstream/downstream relative speed models, simultaneous equation time-distance systems, and the still-water velocity deduction shortcut.	2026-10-07 11:28:34.878899+05:30	### 📌 1. Core Speed Relationships\n* Let **`x`** be the speed of the Boat (or Man) in **Still Water**.\n* Let **`y`** be the speed of the River Current (Stream).\n* **Downstream Speed (DS):** Traveling with the current $\\rightarrow$ **`x + y`**.\n* **Upstream Speed (US):** Traveling against the current $\\rightarrow$ **`x - y`**.\n\n### 💡 2. Deducing Unknowns from Upstream & Downstream\n* If Downstream Speed (`DS`) and Upstream Speed (`US`) are known, you can instantly find `x` and `y` by adding/subtracting the two equations:\n  * **Speed in Still Water (`x`):** **`(DS + US) / 2`**\n  * **Speed of Stream (`y`):** **`(DS - US) / 2`**\n\n### 💡 3. Advanced Placement Shortcuts\n* **Constant Distance Round-Trip (Same Time Ratio):**\n  * If a boat takes time `T_u` to go Upstream and `T_d` to go Downstream over the same distance, the ratio of the Boat's speed to the Stream's speed is:\n  * **`x / y = (T_u + T_d) / (T_u - T_d)`**\n* **System of Equations (Simultaneous Distance Trips):**\n  * Often given two trips: `(D1/US) + (D2/DS) = Time1` and `(D3/US) + (D4/DS) = Time2`.\n  * Do NOT expand `x - y` and `x + y`! Instead, substitute `U = 1/US` and `D = 1/DS` to solve linear equations, then invert back to find `x` and `y`.	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-EdJ4kAW-j4", "type": "youtube", "title": "Boats and Streams - Shortcuts & Tricks for 2026 Placement Tests, Job Interviews & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-boat-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Extracting Speeds from Times] A boat takes 12 hours to row 48 km upstream and 8 hours to row the same distance downstream. Find the speed of the river.", "options": ["A) 1 km/hr", "B) 2 km/hr", "C) 4 km/hr", "D) 5 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the speed of the river current if a boat takes exactly 12 hours to row 48 km upstream, and exactly 8 hours to row the same 48 km distance downstream?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Upstream speed (x - y) = 48 / 12 = 4 km/hr. 2) Downstream speed (x + y) = 48 / 8 = 6 km/hr. 3) Speed of stream (y) = (Downstream - Upstream) / 2 = (6 - 4) / 2 = 2 / 2 = 1 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-boat-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Ratio of Speeds from Time Ratio] Simran takes twice as long to swim up as to swim down. If her speed in still water is 12 km/hr, find stream speed.", "options": ["A) 2 km/hr", "B) 3 km/hr", "C) 4 km/hr", "D) 6 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Simran takes exactly twice as long to swim upstream as she does to swim the identical distance downstream. If her swimming speed in completely still water is 12 km/hr, what is the speed of the river stream?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Time Upstream = 2 × Time Downstream. Since Time is inversely proportional to Speed (for the same distance): Downstream Speed = 2 × Upstream Speed => (x + y) = 2(x - y) => x + y = 2x - 2y => x = 3y. We know x = 12 km/hr, so 12 = 3y => y = 4 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-boat-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Total Round Trip Time Formula] P takes 1 hour to row to a place and back. If stream is 2.4 km/hr and still water speed is 12 km/hr, find distance.", "options": ["A) 5.24 km", "B) 5.76 km", "C) 6.00 km", "D) 6.25 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "It takes a rower exactly 1 hour in total to row to a place and come back. If the river current is flowing at 2.4 km/hr and the rower's speed in still water is 12 km/hr, what is the one-way distance to the place?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Downstream speed (x + y) = 12 + 2.4 = 14.4 km/hr. 2) Upstream speed (x - y) = 12 - 2.4 = 9.6 km/hr. 3) Total Time = D / 14.4 + D / 9.6 = 1. Multiply by 28.8 (LCM of 14.4 and 9.6): 2D + 3D = 28.8 => 5D = 28.8 => D = 28.8 / 5 = 5.76 km.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-boat-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [System of Two Distance/Time Equations] Raj swims 6.5h for 24km DS and 36km US. Also 6h for 36km DS and 24km US. Find river speed.", "options": ["A) 2 km/hr", "B) 3 km/hr", "C) 4 km/hr", "D) 5 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Raj swims for 6.5 hours while going 24 km downstream and 36 km upstream. On another day, he takes exactly 6 hours to swim 36 km downstream and 24 km upstream. At what exact rate is the river flowing?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let D = Downstream Speed and U = Upstream Speed. 1) 24/D + 36/U = 6.5. 2) 36/D + 24/U = 6. Multiply (2) by 1.5: 54/D + 36/U = 9. Subtract (1) from this: 30/D = 2.5 => D = 12 km/hr. Substitute D = 12 into (2): 36/12 + 24/U = 6 => 3 + 24/U = 6 => 24/U = 3 => U = 8 km/hr. Speed of river (y) = (D - U) / 2 = (12 - 8) / 2 = 2 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-boat-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Ratio of Speeds from Unspecified Distance] Time ratio for 10km UP to 10km DOWN is 11:5. Find ratio of still water to river speed.", "options": ["A) 5 : 3", "B) 8 : 3", "C) 11 : 5", "D) 16 : 6"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Find the exact ratio of the swimming speed of Raj in still water to the speed of the river, given that the ratio of the time taken to go 10 km upstream to the time taken to go 10 km downstream is 11 : 5.", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Time Up / Time Down = 11 / 5. Since Distance is identical (10 km), Speed is inversely proportional to Time. Thus, Downstream Speed / Upstream Speed = 11 / 5 => (x + y) / (x - y) = 11 / 5. By Componendo-Dividendo: x / y = (11 + 5) / (11 - 5) = 16 / 6 = 8 / 3. The ratio is 8 : 3.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-boat-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Proportional Time with Variable Distance] Ratio of still water speed to stream is 7:1. Swims 4.2km UP in 14 mins. Find time for 18.4km DOWN.", "options": ["A) 40 minutes", "B) 46 minutes", "C) 52 minutes", "D) 56 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The ratio of Guddi's swimming speed in still water to the speed of the river is 7 : 1. She swims 4.2 km UP the river in exactly 14 minutes. How much time will Guddi take to swim 18.4 km DOWN the river?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) x/y = 7/1 => x = 7y. Upstream speed (x - y) = 6y. Downstream speed (x + y) = 8y. 2) Upstream speed = Distance / Time = 4.2 km / 14 mins = 0.3 km/min. So 6y = 0.3 => y = 0.05 km/min. 3) Downstream speed = 8y = 8 × 0.05 = 0.4 km/min. 4) Time for 18.4 km downstream = Distance / Speed = 18.4 / 0.4 = 184 / 4 = 46 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-boat-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Deducing Total Distance from Fraction] A boat travels 15 km upstream and 22 km downstream in 5 hours. In 13/2 hours it travels 20 km upstream and 27.5 km downstream. Find speed of stream.", "options": ["A) 2 km/hr", "B) 3 km/hr", "C) 4 km/hr", "D) 5 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A boat travels 15 km upstream and 22 km downstream in exactly 5 hours. On another occasion, it travels 20 km upstream and 27.5 km downstream in 6.5 hours (13/2 hours). What is the exact speed of the stream?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let D = Downstream Speed, U = Upstream Speed. 1) 15/U + 22/D = 5. 2) 20/U + 27.5/D = 6.5. Multiply (1) by 4: 60/U + 88/D = 20. Multiply (2) by 3: 60/U + 82.5/D = 19.5. Subtract: 5.5/D = 0.5 => D = 11 km/hr. Substitute D=11 into (1): 15/U + 22/11 = 5 => 15/U + 2 = 5 => 15/U = 3 => U = 5 km/hr. Stream speed y = (D - U) / 2 = (11 - 5) / 2 = 3 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-boat-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Equal Distance Stream and Boat Ratio] A man can row 30 km downstream and return in 8 hours. If the speed of the boat in still water is four times the speed of the current, find speed of current.", "options": ["A) 2 km/hr", "B) 3 km/hr", "C) 4 km/hr", "D) 5 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man can row 30 km downstream and return 30 km upstream to the starting point in exactly 8 hours total. If his speed in still water is exactly 4 times the speed of the current, what is the speed of the current?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Given x = 4y. Downstream speed = x + y = 5y. Upstream speed = x - y = 3y. Total Time = 30/(5y) + 30/(3y) = 8 => 6/y + 10/y = 8 => 16/y = 8 => y = 16 / 8 = 2 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-boat-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Difference in Time for Equal Distance] The speed of a boat in still water is 15 km/hr. It goes 30 km upstream and returns downstream to the original point in 4 hrs 30 mins. Find speed of stream.", "options": ["A) 3 km/hr", "B) 4 km/hr", "C) 5 km/hr", "D) 6 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The speed of a motorboat in still water is exactly 15 km/hr. It travels 30 km upstream and returns back 30 km downstream to the original starting point in 4 hours and 30 minutes. What is the speed of the stream?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "x = 15. Total time = 4.5 hours = 9/2 hours. 30/(15 - y) + 30/(15 + y) = 9/2. Factor out 30: 30 * [ (15+y + 15-y) / (225 - y^2) ] = 9/2 => 30 × 30 / (225 - y^2) = 9/2 => 900 / (225 - y^2) = 9/2 => 100 / (225 - y^2) = 1/2 => 225 - y^2 = 200 => y^2 = 25 => y = 5 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-boat-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Fraction of Distance Over Time] A man rows to a place 48 km distant and back in 14 hours. He finds that he can row 4 km with the stream in the same time as 3 km against the stream. Find speed of stream.", "options": ["A) 0.5 km/hr", "B) 1.0 km/hr", "C) 1.5 km/hr", "D) 2.0 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man rows to a place 48 km away and comes back, taking a total of 14 hours. He notes that he can row 4 km with the stream (downstream) in the exact same time it takes him to row 3 km against the stream (upstream). What is the speed of the stream?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Downstream Speed / Upstream Speed = Distance Down / Distance Up (for same time) = 4 / 3. Let D = 4k and U = 3k. 2) Total Time = 48/D + 48/U = 14 => 48/(4k) + 48/(3k) = 14 => 12/k + 16/k = 14 => 28/k = 14 => k = 2. 3) Thus, D = 8 km/hr and U = 6 km/hr. 4) Speed of stream = (D - U) / 2 = (8 - 6) / 2 = 1 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-boat-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Finding Original Speed After Time Delay] A man can row 6 km/hr in still water. When river is running at 1.2 km/hr, it takes him 1 hour to row to a place and back. How far is the place?", "options": ["A) 2.40 km", "B) 2.88 km", "C) 3.12 km", "D) 3.60 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man can row at a speed of 6 km/hr in still water. If the river is flowing at 1.2 km/hr, it takes him exactly 1 hour to row to a certain location and return to the starting point. How far is the location?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "x = 6, y = 1.2. Downstream D = 7.2. Upstream U = 4.8. Total time = D_dist / 7.2 + D_dist / 4.8 = 1. LCM of 7.2 and 4.8 is 14.4. So (2D_dist + 3D_dist) / 14.4 = 1 => 5D_dist = 14.4 => D_dist = 14.4 / 5 = 2.88 km.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-boat-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Two Boats Meeting Mid-Stream] Boats A and B start towards each other from two places 108 km apart. A goes downstream, B upstream. If their still water speeds are 12 and 15 km/hr, when will they meet?", "options": ["A) 3.5 hours", "B) 4.0 hours", "C) 4.5 hours", "D) 5.0 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Boats A and B start simultaneously towards each other from two places 108 km apart. Boat A travels downstream while Boat B travels upstream. If their respective speeds in still water are 12 km/hr and 15 km/hr, after how many hours will they meet?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "When two boats travel towards each other on a river (one downstream, one upstream), their relative speed is (x_A + y) + (x_B - y) = x_A + x_B! The river speed (y) completely cancels out! Relative Speed = 12 + 15 = 27 km/hr. Time = Distance / Relative Speed = 108 / 27 = 4 hours.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-boat-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Escaping a Chasing Boat] Boat A chases Boat B, both traveling downstream. A is 10 km behind B. If A's still water speed is 20 and B's is 15, when will A catch B?", "options": ["A) 1.5 hours", "B) 2.0 hours", "C) 2.5 hours", "D) 3.0 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Boat A is chasing Boat B, and both are traveling downstream on a river flowing at 3 km/hr. Boat A is currently 10 km behind Boat B. If A's speed in still water is 20 km/hr and B's speed in still water is 15 km/hr, in exactly how many hours will Boat A catch up to Boat B?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Since both are traveling downstream, their absolute speeds over ground are (20 + 3) = 23 km/hr and (15 + 3) = 18 km/hr. Their Relative Speed = 23 - 18 = 5 km/hr. (Notice the river speed cancels out again: (20+y) - (15+y) = 5!). Time to close 10 km gap = Distance / Relative Speed = 10 / 5 = 2 hours.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-boat-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Proportional Time with Variable Speed] A boat covers a round trip. If its still water speed doubles, the round trip takes 40% of original time. Find ratio of original boat speed to stream speed.", "options": ["A) 2 : 1", "B) 3 : 2", "C) 5 : 3", "D) 4 : 1"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A boat covers a certain round trip (downstream and then back upstream). If the boat's speed in still water is exactly doubled (while the stream speed remains constant), the total time taken for the identical round trip becomes exactly 40% of the original time. What is the ratio of the boat's original speed in still water to the speed of the stream?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let original time = T1 = D/(x+y) + D/(x-y) = 2Dx / (x^2 - y^2). Let doubled speed time = T2 = D/(2x+y) + D/(2x-y) = 4Dx / (4x^2 - y^2). Given T2 = 40% of T1 = (2/5)T1 => 4Dx / (4x^2 - y^2) = (2/5) × [2Dx / (x^2 - y^2)]. Cancel 4Dx from both sides: 1 / (4x^2 - y^2) = 1 / [5(x^2 - y^2)]. Cross-multiply: 5(x^2 - y^2) = 4x^2 - y^2 => 5x^2 - 5y^2 = 4x^2 - y^2 => x^2 = 4y^2 => x / y = 2 / 1 = 2 : 1.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-boat-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Boat Average Speed Over Equal Distance] A boat goes downstream at 20 km/hr and upstream at 10 km/hr. Find average speed for the round trip.", "options": ["A) 13.33 km/hr", "B) 14.50 km/hr", "C) 15.00 km/hr", "D) 16.66 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A motorboat travels a certain distance downstream at a constant speed of 20 km/hr, and returns upstream along the identical route at a constant speed of 10 km/hr. What is the average speed of the boat for the entire round trip?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For equal distances, the average speed is NEVER the simple average (15). Instead, use the harmonic mean formula: Average Speed = (2 × DS × US) / (DS + US) = (2 × 20 × 10) / (20 + 10) = 400 / 30 = 40 / 3 = 13.33 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-boat-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Distance using Variable Flow] A man rows downstream 15 km/hr and upstream 9 km/hr. A piece of wood is dropped into the river. How far will it float in 4 hours?", "options": ["A) 8 km", "B) 12 km", "C) 16 km", "D) 24 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man can row downstream at an effective speed of 15 km/hr and upstream at an effective speed of 9 km/hr. If a piece of solid wood is dropped into the same river, how far will it float away in exactly 4 hours?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "A piece of floating wood has no engine, so its speed is exactly the speed of the river current (y). River speed y = (DS - US) / 2 = (15 - 9) / 2 = 6 / 2 = 3 km/hr. Distance covered in 4 hours = Speed × Time = 3 × 4 = 12 km.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-boat-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Loss of Motor Downstream] A motorboat goes downstream. Engine fails midway. Time taken is doubled. Find ratio of boat speed to river speed.", "options": ["A) 2 : 1", "B) 3 : 1", "C) 4 : 1", "D) 5 : 1"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A motorboat starts traveling downstream between two ports. Exactly halfway through the journey, its engine fails completely, and it merely floats the rest of the way with the river current. If the total time taken for the journey is exactly twice the time it would have taken if the engine had not failed, what is the ratio of the boat's still water speed to the river's speed?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let D be the half-distance (so total distance is 2D). Normal time = 2D / (x + y). Time taken with failure = D / (x + y) + D / y (floating speed is y). We are given: D/(x+y) + D/y = 2 × [2D / (x+y)]. Divide by D: 1/(x+y) + 1/y = 4/(x+y). Subtract 1/(x+y) from both sides: 1/y = 3/(x+y). Cross-multiply: x + y = 3y => x = 2y. Therefore, x / y = 2 / 1 = 2 : 1.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-boat-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [River with Moving Walkway Analogy] An escalator moves down at 2 steps/sec. A person walks down it at 3 steps/sec, reaching bottom in 20 sec. How long to walk UP the down-escalator at same speed?", "options": ["A) 40 seconds", "B) 60 seconds", "C) 80 seconds", "D) 100 seconds"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An escalator moves downwards at a constant rate of 2 steps/sec. A person walks down the escalator at a rate of 3 steps/sec, reaching the bottom in exactly 20 seconds. If the person decides to walk UP the descending escalator at the same walking effort (3 steps/sec), how many seconds will it take to reach the top?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Escalator problems perfectly mirror Boats and Streams! Speed of stream (escalator) = 2 steps/sec. Speed of person in still air (walking speed) = 3 steps/sec. Downstream speed = 3 + 2 = 5 steps/sec. Distance (total steps of escalator) = DS × Time = 5 × 20 = 100 steps. Upstream speed (walking up the down-escalator) = 3 - 2 = 1 step/sec. Time to reach top = Distance / US = 100 / 1 = 100 seconds.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-boat-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Deducing Velocity without Total Distance] A boat goes 2 km against the stream in 1 hour and 1 km with the stream in 10 minutes. How long will it take to go 5 km in stationary water?", "options": ["A) 1 hour 15 mins", "B) 1 hour 30 mins", "C) 1 hour 45 mins", "D) 2 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A boat goes exactly 2 km against the stream (upstream) in 1 hour, and exactly 1 km with the stream (downstream) in 10 minutes. How long will it take the boat to travel exactly 5 km in stationary (still) water?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Upstream speed = 2 km / 1 hr = 2 km/hr. 2) Downstream speed = 1 km / 10 mins = 1 km / (1/6 hr) = 6 km/hr. 3) Speed of boat in still water (x) = (DS + US) / 2 = (6 + 2) / 2 = 4 km/hr. 4) Time to go 5 km in still water = Distance / Speed = 5 / 4 = 1.25 hours = 1 hour 15 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-boat-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Equal Time Fractional Stream Check] A man rows downstream 60 km and upstream 20 km, taking 4 hours for EACH journey. What is the velocity of the stream?", "options": ["A) 5 km/hr", "B) 6.5 km/hr", "C) 8 km/hr", "D) 10 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man rows a boat downstream for 60 km and upstream for 20 km, taking exactly 4 hours for EACH individual journey. What is the velocity of the river stream?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Downstream speed (x + y) = 60 km / 4 hr = 15 km/hr. 2) Upstream speed (x - y) = 20 km / 4 hr = 5 km/hr. 3) Speed of the stream (y) = (DS - US) / 2 = (15 - 5) / 2 = 10 / 2 = 5 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. Core Speed Relationships\n* Let **`x`** be the speed of the Boat (or Man) in **Still Water**.\n* Let **`y`** be the speed of the River Current (Stream).\n* **Downstream Speed (DS):** Traveling with the current $\\rightarrow$ **`x + y`**.\n* **Upstream Speed (US):** Traveling against the current $\\rightarrow$ **`x - y`**.\n\n### 💡 2. Deducing Unknowns from Upstream & Downstream\n* If Downstream Speed (`DS`) and Upstream Speed (`US`) are known, you can instantly find `x` and `y` by adding/subtracting the two equations:\n  * **Speed in Still Water (`x`):** **`(DS + US) / 2`**\n  * **Speed of Stream (`y`):** **`(DS - US) / 2`**\n\n### 💡 3. Advanced Placement Shortcuts\n* **Constant Distance Round-Trip (Same Time Ratio):**\n  * If a boat takes time `T_u` to go Upstream and `T_d` to go Downstream over the same distance, the ratio of the Boat's speed to the Stream's speed is:\n  * **`x / y = (T_u + T_d) / (T_u - T_d)`**\n* **System of Equations (Simultaneous Distance Trips):**\n  * Often given two trips: `(D1/US) + (D2/DS) = Time1` and `(D3/US) + (D4/DS) = Time2`.\n  * Do NOT expand `x - y` and `x + y`! Instead, substitute `U = 1/US` and `D = 1/DS` to solve linear equations, then invert back to find `x` and `y`.	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Boats and Streams	Master upstream/downstream relative speed models, simultaneous equation time-distance systems, and the still-water velocity deduction shortcut.	34	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	18	[{"id": "apt-boat-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Extracting Speeds from Times] A boat takes 12 hours to row 48 km upstream and 8 hours to row the same distance downstream. Find the speed of the river.", "options": ["A) 1 km/hr", "B) 2 km/hr", "C) 4 km/hr", "D) 5 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the speed of the river current if a boat takes exactly 12 hours to row 48 km upstream, and exactly 8 hours to row the same 48 km distance downstream?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Upstream speed (x - y) = 48 / 12 = 4 km/hr. 2) Downstream speed (x + y) = 48 / 8 = 6 km/hr. 3) Speed of stream (y) = (Downstream - Upstream) / 2 = (6 - 4) / 2 = 2 / 2 = 1 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-boat-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Ratio of Speeds from Time Ratio] Simran takes twice as long to swim up as to swim down. If her speed in still water is 12 km/hr, find stream speed.", "options": ["A) 2 km/hr", "B) 3 km/hr", "C) 4 km/hr", "D) 6 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Simran takes exactly twice as long to swim upstream as she does to swim the identical distance downstream. If her swimming speed in completely still water is 12 km/hr, what is the speed of the river stream?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Time Upstream = 2 × Time Downstream. Since Time is inversely proportional to Speed (for the same distance): Downstream Speed = 2 × Upstream Speed => (x + y) = 2(x - y) => x + y = 2x - 2y => x = 3y. We know x = 12 km/hr, so 12 = 3y => y = 4 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-boat-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Total Round Trip Time Formula] P takes 1 hour to row to a place and back. If stream is 2.4 km/hr and still water speed is 12 km/hr, find distance.", "options": ["A) 5.24 km", "B) 5.76 km", "C) 6.00 km", "D) 6.25 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "It takes a rower exactly 1 hour in total to row to a place and come back. If the river current is flowing at 2.4 km/hr and the rower's speed in still water is 12 km/hr, what is the one-way distance to the place?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Downstream speed (x + y) = 12 + 2.4 = 14.4 km/hr. 2) Upstream speed (x - y) = 12 - 2.4 = 9.6 km/hr. 3) Total Time = D / 14.4 + D / 9.6 = 1. Multiply by 28.8 (LCM of 14.4 and 9.6): 2D + 3D = 28.8 => 5D = 28.8 => D = 28.8 / 5 = 5.76 km.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-boat-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [System of Two Distance/Time Equations] Raj swims 6.5h for 24km DS and 36km US. Also 6h for 36km DS and 24km US. Find river speed.", "options": ["A) 2 km/hr", "B) 3 km/hr", "C) 4 km/hr", "D) 5 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Raj swims for 6.5 hours while going 24 km downstream and 36 km upstream. On another day, he takes exactly 6 hours to swim 36 km downstream and 24 km upstream. At what exact rate is the river flowing?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let D = Downstream Speed and U = Upstream Speed. 1) 24/D + 36/U = 6.5. 2) 36/D + 24/U = 6. Multiply (2) by 1.5: 54/D + 36/U = 9. Subtract (1) from this: 30/D = 2.5 => D = 12 km/hr. Substitute D = 12 into (2): 36/12 + 24/U = 6 => 3 + 24/U = 6 => 24/U = 3 => U = 8 km/hr. Speed of river (y) = (D - U) / 2 = (12 - 8) / 2 = 2 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-boat-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Ratio of Speeds from Unspecified Distance] Time ratio for 10km UP to 10km DOWN is 11:5. Find ratio of still water to river speed.", "options": ["A) 5 : 3", "B) 8 : 3", "C) 11 : 5", "D) 16 : 6"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Find the exact ratio of the swimming speed of Raj in still water to the speed of the river, given that the ratio of the time taken to go 10 km upstream to the time taken to go 10 km downstream is 11 : 5.", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Time Up / Time Down = 11 / 5. Since Distance is identical (10 km), Speed is inversely proportional to Time. Thus, Downstream Speed / Upstream Speed = 11 / 5 => (x + y) / (x - y) = 11 / 5. By Componendo-Dividendo: x / y = (11 + 5) / (11 - 5) = 16 / 6 = 8 / 3. The ratio is 8 : 3.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-boat-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Proportional Time with Variable Distance] Ratio of still water speed to stream is 7:1. Swims 4.2km UP in 14 mins. Find time for 18.4km DOWN.", "options": ["A) 40 minutes", "B) 46 minutes", "C) 52 minutes", "D) 56 minutes"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The ratio of Guddi's swimming speed in still water to the speed of the river is 7 : 1. She swims 4.2 km UP the river in exactly 14 minutes. How much time will Guddi take to swim 18.4 km DOWN the river?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) x/y = 7/1 => x = 7y. Upstream speed (x - y) = 6y. Downstream speed (x + y) = 8y. 2) Upstream speed = Distance / Time = 4.2 km / 14 mins = 0.3 km/min. So 6y = 0.3 => y = 0.05 km/min. 3) Downstream speed = 8y = 8 × 0.05 = 0.4 km/min. 4) Time for 18.4 km downstream = Distance / Speed = 18.4 / 0.4 = 184 / 4 = 46 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-boat-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Deducing Total Distance from Fraction] A boat travels 15 km upstream and 22 km downstream in 5 hours. In 13/2 hours it travels 20 km upstream and 27.5 km downstream. Find speed of stream.", "options": ["A) 2 km/hr", "B) 3 km/hr", "C) 4 km/hr", "D) 5 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A boat travels 15 km upstream and 22 km downstream in exactly 5 hours. On another occasion, it travels 20 km upstream and 27.5 km downstream in 6.5 hours (13/2 hours). What is the exact speed of the stream?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let D = Downstream Speed, U = Upstream Speed. 1) 15/U + 22/D = 5. 2) 20/U + 27.5/D = 6.5. Multiply (1) by 4: 60/U + 88/D = 20. Multiply (2) by 3: 60/U + 82.5/D = 19.5. Subtract: 5.5/D = 0.5 => D = 11 km/hr. Substitute D=11 into (1): 15/U + 22/11 = 5 => 15/U + 2 = 5 => 15/U = 3 => U = 5 km/hr. Stream speed y = (D - U) / 2 = (11 - 5) / 2 = 3 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-boat-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Equal Distance Stream and Boat Ratio] A man can row 30 km downstream and return in 8 hours. If the speed of the boat in still water is four times the speed of the current, find speed of current.", "options": ["A) 2 km/hr", "B) 3 km/hr", "C) 4 km/hr", "D) 5 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man can row 30 km downstream and return 30 km upstream to the starting point in exactly 8 hours total. If his speed in still water is exactly 4 times the speed of the current, what is the speed of the current?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Given x = 4y. Downstream speed = x + y = 5y. Upstream speed = x - y = 3y. Total Time = 30/(5y) + 30/(3y) = 8 => 6/y + 10/y = 8 => 16/y = 8 => y = 16 / 8 = 2 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-boat-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Difference in Time for Equal Distance] The speed of a boat in still water is 15 km/hr. It goes 30 km upstream and returns downstream to the original point in 4 hrs 30 mins. Find speed of stream.", "options": ["A) 3 km/hr", "B) 4 km/hr", "C) 5 km/hr", "D) 6 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The speed of a motorboat in still water is exactly 15 km/hr. It travels 30 km upstream and returns back 30 km downstream to the original starting point in 4 hours and 30 minutes. What is the speed of the stream?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "x = 15. Total time = 4.5 hours = 9/2 hours. 30/(15 - y) + 30/(15 + y) = 9/2. Factor out 30: 30 * [ (15+y + 15-y) / (225 - y^2) ] = 9/2 => 30 × 30 / (225 - y^2) = 9/2 => 900 / (225 - y^2) = 9/2 => 100 / (225 - y^2) = 1/2 => 225 - y^2 = 200 => y^2 = 25 => y = 5 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-boat-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Fraction of Distance Over Time] A man rows to a place 48 km distant and back in 14 hours. He finds that he can row 4 km with the stream in the same time as 3 km against the stream. Find speed of stream.", "options": ["A) 0.5 km/hr", "B) 1.0 km/hr", "C) 1.5 km/hr", "D) 2.0 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man rows to a place 48 km away and comes back, taking a total of 14 hours. He notes that he can row 4 km with the stream (downstream) in the exact same time it takes him to row 3 km against the stream (upstream). What is the speed of the stream?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Downstream Speed / Upstream Speed = Distance Down / Distance Up (for same time) = 4 / 3. Let D = 4k and U = 3k. 2) Total Time = 48/D + 48/U = 14 => 48/(4k) + 48/(3k) = 14 => 12/k + 16/k = 14 => 28/k = 14 => k = 2. 3) Thus, D = 8 km/hr and U = 6 km/hr. 4) Speed of stream = (D - U) / 2 = (8 - 6) / 2 = 1 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-boat-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Finding Original Speed After Time Delay] A man can row 6 km/hr in still water. When river is running at 1.2 km/hr, it takes him 1 hour to row to a place and back. How far is the place?", "options": ["A) 2.40 km", "B) 2.88 km", "C) 3.12 km", "D) 3.60 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man can row at a speed of 6 km/hr in still water. If the river is flowing at 1.2 km/hr, it takes him exactly 1 hour to row to a certain location and return to the starting point. How far is the location?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "x = 6, y = 1.2. Downstream D = 7.2. Upstream U = 4.8. Total time = D_dist / 7.2 + D_dist / 4.8 = 1. LCM of 7.2 and 4.8 is 14.4. So (2D_dist + 3D_dist) / 14.4 = 1 => 5D_dist = 14.4 => D_dist = 14.4 / 5 = 2.88 km.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-boat-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Two Boats Meeting Mid-Stream] Boats A and B start towards each other from two places 108 km apart. A goes downstream, B upstream. If their still water speeds are 12 and 15 km/hr, when will they meet?", "options": ["A) 3.5 hours", "B) 4.0 hours", "C) 4.5 hours", "D) 5.0 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Boats A and B start simultaneously towards each other from two places 108 km apart. Boat A travels downstream while Boat B travels upstream. If their respective speeds in still water are 12 km/hr and 15 km/hr, after how many hours will they meet?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "When two boats travel towards each other on a river (one downstream, one upstream), their relative speed is (x_A + y) + (x_B - y) = x_A + x_B! The river speed (y) completely cancels out! Relative Speed = 12 + 15 = 27 km/hr. Time = Distance / Relative Speed = 108 / 27 = 4 hours.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-boat-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Escaping a Chasing Boat] Boat A chases Boat B, both traveling downstream. A is 10 km behind B. If A's still water speed is 20 and B's is 15, when will A catch B?", "options": ["A) 1.5 hours", "B) 2.0 hours", "C) 2.5 hours", "D) 3.0 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Boat A is chasing Boat B, and both are traveling downstream on a river flowing at 3 km/hr. Boat A is currently 10 km behind Boat B. If A's speed in still water is 20 km/hr and B's speed in still water is 15 km/hr, in exactly how many hours will Boat A catch up to Boat B?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Since both are traveling downstream, their absolute speeds over ground are (20 + 3) = 23 km/hr and (15 + 3) = 18 km/hr. Their Relative Speed = 23 - 18 = 5 km/hr. (Notice the river speed cancels out again: (20+y) - (15+y) = 5!). Time to close 10 km gap = Distance / Relative Speed = 10 / 5 = 2 hours.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-boat-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Proportional Time with Variable Speed] A boat covers a round trip. If its still water speed doubles, the round trip takes 40% of original time. Find ratio of original boat speed to stream speed.", "options": ["A) 2 : 1", "B) 3 : 2", "C) 5 : 3", "D) 4 : 1"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A boat covers a certain round trip (downstream and then back upstream). If the boat's speed in still water is exactly doubled (while the stream speed remains constant), the total time taken for the identical round trip becomes exactly 40% of the original time. What is the ratio of the boat's original speed in still water to the speed of the stream?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let original time = T1 = D/(x+y) + D/(x-y) = 2Dx / (x^2 - y^2). Let doubled speed time = T2 = D/(2x+y) + D/(2x-y) = 4Dx / (4x^2 - y^2). Given T2 = 40% of T1 = (2/5)T1 => 4Dx / (4x^2 - y^2) = (2/5) × [2Dx / (x^2 - y^2)]. Cancel 4Dx from both sides: 1 / (4x^2 - y^2) = 1 / [5(x^2 - y^2)]. Cross-multiply: 5(x^2 - y^2) = 4x^2 - y^2 => 5x^2 - 5y^2 = 4x^2 - y^2 => x^2 = 4y^2 => x / y = 2 / 1 = 2 : 1.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-boat-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Boat Average Speed Over Equal Distance] A boat goes downstream at 20 km/hr and upstream at 10 km/hr. Find average speed for the round trip.", "options": ["A) 13.33 km/hr", "B) 14.50 km/hr", "C) 15.00 km/hr", "D) 16.66 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A motorboat travels a certain distance downstream at a constant speed of 20 km/hr, and returns upstream along the identical route at a constant speed of 10 km/hr. What is the average speed of the boat for the entire round trip?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For equal distances, the average speed is NEVER the simple average (15). Instead, use the harmonic mean formula: Average Speed = (2 × DS × US) / (DS + US) = (2 × 20 × 10) / (20 + 10) = 400 / 30 = 40 / 3 = 13.33 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-boat-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Distance using Variable Flow] A man rows downstream 15 km/hr and upstream 9 km/hr. A piece of wood is dropped into the river. How far will it float in 4 hours?", "options": ["A) 8 km", "B) 12 km", "C) 16 km", "D) 24 km"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man can row downstream at an effective speed of 15 km/hr and upstream at an effective speed of 9 km/hr. If a piece of solid wood is dropped into the same river, how far will it float away in exactly 4 hours?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "A piece of floating wood has no engine, so its speed is exactly the speed of the river current (y). River speed y = (DS - US) / 2 = (15 - 9) / 2 = 6 / 2 = 3 km/hr. Distance covered in 4 hours = Speed × Time = 3 × 4 = 12 km.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-boat-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Loss of Motor Downstream] A motorboat goes downstream. Engine fails midway. Time taken is doubled. Find ratio of boat speed to river speed.", "options": ["A) 2 : 1", "B) 3 : 1", "C) 4 : 1", "D) 5 : 1"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A motorboat starts traveling downstream between two ports. Exactly halfway through the journey, its engine fails completely, and it merely floats the rest of the way with the river current. If the total time taken for the journey is exactly twice the time it would have taken if the engine had not failed, what is the ratio of the boat's still water speed to the river's speed?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let D be the half-distance (so total distance is 2D). Normal time = 2D / (x + y). Time taken with failure = D / (x + y) + D / y (floating speed is y). We are given: D/(x+y) + D/y = 2 × [2D / (x+y)]. Divide by D: 1/(x+y) + 1/y = 4/(x+y). Subtract 1/(x+y) from both sides: 1/y = 3/(x+y). Cross-multiply: x + y = 3y => x = 2y. Therefore, x / y = 2 / 1 = 2 : 1.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-boat-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [River with Moving Walkway Analogy] An escalator moves down at 2 steps/sec. A person walks down it at 3 steps/sec, reaching bottom in 20 sec. How long to walk UP the down-escalator at same speed?", "options": ["A) 40 seconds", "B) 60 seconds", "C) 80 seconds", "D) 100 seconds"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An escalator moves downwards at a constant rate of 2 steps/sec. A person walks down the escalator at a rate of 3 steps/sec, reaching the bottom in exactly 20 seconds. If the person decides to walk UP the descending escalator at the same walking effort (3 steps/sec), how many seconds will it take to reach the top?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Escalator problems perfectly mirror Boats and Streams! Speed of stream (escalator) = 2 steps/sec. Speed of person in still air (walking speed) = 3 steps/sec. Downstream speed = 3 + 2 = 5 steps/sec. Distance (total steps of escalator) = DS × Time = 5 × 20 = 100 steps. Upstream speed (walking up the down-escalator) = 3 - 2 = 1 step/sec. Time to reach top = Distance / US = 100 / 1 = 100 seconds.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-boat-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Deducing Velocity without Total Distance] A boat goes 2 km against the stream in 1 hour and 1 km with the stream in 10 minutes. How long will it take to go 5 km in stationary water?", "options": ["A) 1 hour 15 mins", "B) 1 hour 30 mins", "C) 1 hour 45 mins", "D) 2 hours"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A boat goes exactly 2 km against the stream (upstream) in 1 hour, and exactly 1 km with the stream (downstream) in 10 minutes. How long will it take the boat to travel exactly 5 km in stationary (still) water?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Upstream speed = 2 km / 1 hr = 2 km/hr. 2) Downstream speed = 1 km / 10 mins = 1 km / (1/6 hr) = 6 km/hr. 3) Speed of boat in still water (x) = (DS + US) / 2 = (6 + 2) / 2 = 4 km/hr. 4) Time to go 5 km in still water = Distance / Speed = 5 / 4 = 1.25 hours = 1 hour 15 minutes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-boat-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Equal Time Fractional Stream Check] A man rows downstream 60 km and upstream 20 km, taking 4 hours for EACH journey. What is the velocity of the stream?", "options": ["A) 5 km/hr", "B) 6.5 km/hr", "C) 8 km/hr", "D) 10 km/hr"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man rows a boat downstream for 60 km and upstream for 20 km, taking exactly 4 hours for EACH individual journey. What is the velocity of the river stream?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Downstream speed (x + y) = 60 km / 4 hr = 15 km/hr. 2) Upstream speed (x - y) = 20 km / 4 hr = 5 km/hr. 3) Speed of the stream (y) = (DS - US) / 2 = (15 - 5) / 2 = 10 / 2 = 5 km/hr.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22139	Lecture 21: Prototypes, Prototype Chain (__proto__) & ES6 Classes — Object.create, extends, super() & Method Sharing	js-lecture-21-prototypes-prototype-chain-es6-classes-inheritance	Uncover how Prototypal Inheritance powers every Object, Array, and Function in JavaScript, trace the Prototype Chain up to null, and master ES6 Classes, extends, and super().	2026-10-06 21:36:53.504856+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhen you create an array `const arr = [10, 20` or an object `const obj = { name: "Rohit" }`, how can you call `arr.push()`, `arr.toString()`, or `obj.hasOwnProperty("name")` when you never defined those methods on `arr` or `obj`? The answer is **Prototypal Inheritance**—the core mechanism behind all objects and classes in JavaScript.\n\n### 💡 The Prototype Chain (`[[Prototype` / `__proto__`)\n* Every JavaScript object has a hidden internal link (`[[Prototype`, accessible via `Object.getPrototypeOf(obj)` or `__proto__`) pointing to another object called its **Prototype**.\n* When you access `obj.prop`, the engine first searches `obj` itself. If not found, it walks up the **Prototype Chain** one level at a time until it either finds the property or reaches **`null`** at the top of the chain:\n  * **Object Chain:** `obj -> Object.prototype -> null`\n  * **Array Chain:** `arr -> Array.prototype -> Object.prototype -> null`\n  * **Function Chain:** `fn -> Function.prototype -> Object.prototype -> null`\n* Because both `Array.prototype` and `Function.prototype` inherit from **`Object.prototype`**, Arrays and Functions are ultimately **Objects** in JavaScript!\n* **Custom Prototype Linking:** Use **`const obj2 = Object.create(obj1)`** to create a new object whose prototype points directly to `obj1`.\n\n### 💡 ES6 `class`, `constructor`, `extends` & `super()` (DRY Principle)\n* If you create 100 user object literals manually, defining a `sayHi()` method inside each object duplicates that function 100 times in memory!\n* **How ES6 `class` Works Under the Hood:**\n  * Instance data properties (`this.name`, `this.age`) set inside `constructor(name, age)` are placed on each individual instance object.\n  * Class methods (`sayHi()`) are automatically placed **once** on **`Person.prototype`** so all 100 instances share a single method in memory (following the **DRY — Don't Repeat Yourself** principle)!\n* **Class Inheritance (`extends` & `super`):** `class Customer extends Person` links `Customer.prototype` to `Person.prototype`, and calling **`super(name, age)`** inside the child `constructor` invokes the parent `Person` constructor to initialize inherited properties.\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Prototype Chain, `Object.create` & ES6 Class Inheritance\n```javascript\n// 1. Verifying the Prototype Chain of an Array up to null\nconst arr = [10, 20, 30;\nconsole.log(arr.__proto__ === Array.prototype);                  // true\nconsole.log(arr.__proto__.__proto__ === Object.prototype);       // true\nconsole.log(arr.__proto__.__proto__.__proto__ === null);         // true\n\n// 2. Linking Prototypes Cleanly with Object.create()\nconst baseUser = { greet() { return `Hello ${this.name}`; } };\nconst admin = Object.create(baseUser);\nadmin.name = "Rohit";\nconsole.log(admin.greet()); // "Hello Rohit" (inherited via prototype chain)\n\n// 3. ES6 Classes, Prototype Method Sharing & Inheritance (extends + super)\nclass Person {\n  constructor(name, age) {\n    this.name = name;\n    this.age = age;\n  }\n  sayHi() {\n    return `Hi ${this.name}`;\n  }\n}\n\nclass Customer extends Person {\n  constructor(name, age, account, balance) {\n    super(name, age); // Calls Person's constructor first!\n    this.account = account;\n    this.balance = balance;\n  }\n  checkBalance() {\n    return this.balance;\n  }\n}\n\nconst c1 = new Customer("Mohan", 20, 1002, 5400);\nconsole.log(c1.sayHi(), c1.checkBalance()); // "Hi Mohan", 5400\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Inheritance_and_the_prototype_chain", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Inheritance_and_the_prototype_chain", "title": "MDN Web Docs — Inheritance and the Prototype Chain"}, {"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Classes", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Classes", "title": "MDN Web Docs — ES6 Classes Reference"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9KwCG9aVwvQ", "title": "Prototypes and class in Javascript| Javascript Full Course #21", "duration": "48m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-21-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Trace the complete Prototype Chain of a JavaScript Array const arr = [1, 2, 3]. Why does arr.toString() work, and where does the chain end?", "title": "Q1: Trace the complete Prototype Chain of a JavaScript Array const arr = [1, 2, 3]. Why does arr.toString() work, and where does the chain end?", "answer": "The prototype chain of an array is: arr -> Array.prototype -> Object.prototype -> null. When you call arr.toString() or arr.hasOwnProperty(), the engine first looks on the arr instance itself. Not finding it there, it looks on arr.__proto__ (Array.prototype, which has sort, push, map, and an overridden toString). If you call arr.hasOwnProperty(), it is not on Array.prototype either, so the engine walks one level higher to Array.prototype.__proto__ (Object.prototype), where hasOwnProperty lives. Object.prototype.__proto__ is null, which marks the termination point of the search.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "The prototype chain of an array is: arr -> Array.prototype -> Object.prototype -> null. When you call arr.toString() or arr.hasOwnProperty(), the engine first looks on the arr instance itself. Not finding it there, it looks on arr.__proto__ (Array.prototype, which has sort, push, map, and an overridden toString). If you call arr.hasOwnProperty(), it is not on Array.prototype either, so the engine walks one level higher to Array.prototype.__proto__ (Object.prototype), where hasOwnProperty lives. Object.prototype.__proto__ is null, which marks the termination point of the search."}, {"id": "js-lec-21-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: What is the difference between .prototype and .__proto__ (or Object.getPrototypeOf())?", "title": "Q2: What is the difference between .prototype and .__proto__ (or Object.getPrototypeOf())?", "answer": "1) .prototype is a property that exists ONLY on Constructor Functions and ES6 Classes (e.g., Person.prototype or Array.prototype). It is the blueprint object that will be assigned as the prototype of any future instances created with new Person(). 2) .__proto__ (standardized as Object.getPrototypeOf(instance)) is the actual internal link on every object instance that points to its constructor's .prototype object (so p1.__proto__ === Person.prototype is true).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q2", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "1) .prototype is a property that exists ONLY on Constructor Functions and ES6 Classes (e.g., Person.prototype or Array.prototype). It is the blueprint object that will be assigned as the prototype of any future instances created with new Person(). 2) .__proto__ (standardized as Object.getPrototypeOf(instance)) is the actual internal link on every object instance that points to its constructor's .prototype object (so p1.__proto__ === Person.prototype is true)."}, {"id": "js-lec-21-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: When you define a method sayHi() inside an ES6 class Person, why doesn't sayHi appear when you console.log(person1)? Why wasn't it placed directly on the instance?", "title": "Q3: When you define a method sayHi() inside an ES6 class Person, why doesn't sayHi appear when you console.log(person1)? Why wasn't it placed directly on the instance?", "answer": "To follow the DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle and save memory! While instance variables (this.name, this.age) differ per person and are created on each instance inside constructor(), the sayHi() method logic is identical for every Person. Therefore, ES6 classes attach sayHi() once onto Person.prototype (non-enumerable), so 10,000 Person instances share a single function in memory instead of allocating 10,000 duplicate function copies.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "To follow the DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle and save memory! While instance variables (this.name, this.age) differ per person and are created on each instance inside constructor(), the sayHi() method logic is identical for every Person. Therefore, ES6 classes attach sayHi() once onto Person.prototype (non-enumerable), so 10,000 Person instances share a single function in memory instead of allocating 10,000 duplicate function copies."}, {"id": "js-lec-21-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What are the 4 exact steps that happen under the hood when you call new Person(\\"Rohit\\", 20)?", "title": "Q4: What are the 4 exact steps that happen under the hood when you call new Person(\\"Rohit\\", 20)?", "answer": "When a constructor or class is invoked with the new keyword: 1) A brand-new empty object {} is allocated in memory. 2) The new object's internal [[Prototype]] (__proto__) is linked to Person.prototype. 3) The Person constructor function executes with this bound to that new empty object (populating this.name and this.age). 4) The constructor automatically returns that newly created object (unless the constructor explicitly returns a different non-primitive object).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "When a constructor or class is invoked with the new keyword: 1) A brand-new empty object {} is allocated in memory. 2) The new object's internal [[Prototype]] (__proto__) is linked to Person.prototype. 3) The Person constructor function executes with this bound to that new empty object (populating this.name and this.age). 4) The constructor automatically returns that newly created object (unless the constructor explicitly returns a different non-primitive object)."}, {"id": "js-lec-21-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: In a child class that uses extends, why does accessing this before calling super() inside the constructor throw a ReferenceError?", "title": "Q5: In a child class that uses extends, why does accessing this before calling super() inside the constructor throw a ReferenceError?", "answer": "In ES6 class inheritance, the parent class constructor (invoked via super()) is responsible for allocating and initializing the base instance object assigned to this. Until super() has been called inside the derived class constructor, this is uninitialized, so touching this.account = 12 before super() throws a ReferenceError: Must call super constructor in derived class before accessing 'this'.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "In ES6 class inheritance, the parent class constructor (invoked via super()) is responsible for allocating and initializing the base instance object assigned to this. Until super() has been called inside the derived class constructor, this is uninitialized, so touching this.account = 12 before super() throws a ReferenceError: Must call super constructor in derived class before accessing 'this'."}, {"id": "js-lec-21-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why is Object.create(protoObj) preferred over mutating obj.__proto__ = protoObj at runtime?", "title": "Q6: Why is Object.create(protoObj) preferred over mutating obj.__proto__ = protoObj at runtime?", "answer": "__proto__ is a legacy getter/setter kept mainly for backward compatibility, and mutating an existing object's [[Prototype]] after creation (obj.__proto__ = ... or Object.setPrototypeOf()) deoptimizes V8's Hidden Classes and Inline Caches, severely hurting performance. Object.create(protoObj) sets the prototype link cleanly at the moment the new object is created.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "__proto__ is a legacy getter/setter kept mainly for backward compatibility, and mutating an existing object's [[Prototype]] after creation (obj.__proto__ = ... or Object.setPrototypeOf()) deoptimizes V8's Hidden Classes and Inline Caches, severely hurting performance. Object.create(protoObj) sets the prototype link cleanly at the moment the new object is created."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhen you create an array `const arr = [10, 20` or an object `const obj = { name: "Rohit" }`, how can you call `arr.push()`, `arr.toString()`, or `obj.hasOwnProperty("name")` when you never defined those methods on `arr` or `obj`? The answer is **Prototypal Inheritance**—the core mechanism behind all objects and classes in JavaScript.\n\n### 💡 The Prototype Chain (`[[Prototype` / `__proto__`)\n* Every JavaScript object has a hidden internal link (`[[Prototype`, accessible via `Object.getPrototypeOf(obj)` or `__proto__`) pointing to another object called its **Prototype**.\n* When you access `obj.prop`, the engine first searches `obj` itself. If not found, it walks up the **Prototype Chain** one level at a time until it either finds the property or reaches **`null`** at the top of the chain:\n  * **Object Chain:** `obj -> Object.prototype -> null`\n  * **Array Chain:** `arr -> Array.prototype -> Object.prototype -> null`\n  * **Function Chain:** `fn -> Function.prototype -> Object.prototype -> null`\n* Because both `Array.prototype` and `Function.prototype` inherit from **`Object.prototype`**, Arrays and Functions are ultimately **Objects** in JavaScript!\n* **Custom Prototype Linking:** Use **`const obj2 = Object.create(obj1)`** to create a new object whose prototype points directly to `obj1`.\n\n### 💡 ES6 `class`, `constructor`, `extends` & `super()` (DRY Principle)\n* If you create 100 user object literals manually, defining a `sayHi()` method inside each object duplicates that function 100 times in memory!\n* **How ES6 `class` Works Under the Hood:**\n  * Instance data properties (`this.name`, `this.age`) set inside `constructor(name, age)` are placed on each individual instance object.\n  * Class methods (`sayHi()`) are automatically placed **once** on **`Person.prototype`** so all 100 instances share a single method in memory (following the **DRY — Don't Repeat Yourself** principle)!\n* **Class Inheritance (`extends` & `super`):** `class Customer extends Person` links `Customer.prototype` to `Person.prototype`, and calling **`super(name, age)`** inside the child `constructor` invokes the parent `Person` constructor to initialize inherited properties.\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: Prototype Chain, `Object.create` & ES6 Class Inheritance\n```javascript\n// 1. Verifying the Prototype Chain of an Array up to null\nconst arr = [10, 20, 30;\nconsole.log(arr.__proto__ === Array.prototype);                  // true\nconsole.log(arr.__proto__.__proto__ === Object.prototype);       // true\nconsole.log(arr.__proto__.__proto__.__proto__ === null);         // true\n\n// 2. Linking Prototypes Cleanly with Object.create()\nconst baseUser = { greet() { return `Hello ${this.name}`; } };\nconst admin = Object.create(baseUser);\nadmin.name = "Rohit";\nconsole.log(admin.greet()); // "Hello Rohit" (inherited via prototype chain)\n\n// 3. ES6 Classes, Prototype Method Sharing & Inheritance (extends + super)\nclass Person {\n  constructor(name, age) {\n    this.name = name;\n    this.age = age;\n  }\n  sayHi() {\n    return `Hi ${this.name}`;\n  }\n}\n\nclass Customer extends Person {\n  constructor(name, age, account, balance) {\n    super(name, age); // Calls Person's constructor first!\n    this.account = account;\n    this.balance = balance;\n  }\n  checkBalance() {\n    return this.balance;\n  }\n}\n\nconst c1 = new Customer("Mohan", 20, 1002, 5400);\nconsole.log(c1.sayHi(), c1.checkBalance()); // "Hi Mohan", 5400\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 21: Prototypes, Prototype Chain (__proto__) & ES6 Classes — Object.create, extends, super() & Method Sharing	Uncover how Prototypal Inheritance powers every Object, Array, and Function in JavaScript, trace the Prototype Chain up to null, and master ES6 Classes, extends, and super().	20	Easy	\N	21	[{"id": "js-lec-21-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Trace the complete Prototype Chain of a JavaScript Array const arr = [1, 2, 3]. Why does arr.toString() work, and where does the chain end?", "title": "Q1: Trace the complete Prototype Chain of a JavaScript Array const arr = [1, 2, 3]. Why does arr.toString() work, and where does the chain end?", "answer": "The prototype chain of an array is: arr -> Array.prototype -> Object.prototype -> null. When you call arr.toString() or arr.hasOwnProperty(), the engine first looks on the arr instance itself. Not finding it there, it looks on arr.__proto__ (Array.prototype, which has sort, push, map, and an overridden toString). If you call arr.hasOwnProperty(), it is not on Array.prototype either, so the engine walks one level higher to Array.prototype.__proto__ (Object.prototype), where hasOwnProperty lives. Object.prototype.__proto__ is null, which marks the termination point of the search.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q1", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "The prototype chain of an array is: arr -> Array.prototype -> Object.prototype -> null. When you call arr.toString() or arr.hasOwnProperty(), the engine first looks on the arr instance itself. Not finding it there, it looks on arr.__proto__ (Array.prototype, which has sort, push, map, and an overridden toString). If you call arr.hasOwnProperty(), it is not on Array.prototype either, so the engine walks one level higher to Array.prototype.__proto__ (Object.prototype), where hasOwnProperty lives. Object.prototype.__proto__ is null, which marks the termination point of the search."}, {"id": "js-lec-21-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: What is the difference between .prototype and .__proto__ (or Object.getPrototypeOf())?", "title": "Q2: What is the difference between .prototype and .__proto__ (or Object.getPrototypeOf())?", "answer": "1) .prototype is a property that exists ONLY on Constructor Functions and ES6 Classes (e.g., Person.prototype or Array.prototype). It is the blueprint object that will be assigned as the prototype of any future instances created with new Person(). 2) .__proto__ (standardized as Object.getPrototypeOf(instance)) is the actual internal link on every object instance that points to its constructor's .prototype object (so p1.__proto__ === Person.prototype is true).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q2", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "1) .prototype is a property that exists ONLY on Constructor Functions and ES6 Classes (e.g., Person.prototype or Array.prototype). It is the blueprint object that will be assigned as the prototype of any future instances created with new Person(). 2) .__proto__ (standardized as Object.getPrototypeOf(instance)) is the actual internal link on every object instance that points to its constructor's .prototype object (so p1.__proto__ === Person.prototype is true)."}, {"id": "js-lec-21-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: When you define a method sayHi() inside an ES6 class Person, why doesn't sayHi appear when you console.log(person1)? Why wasn't it placed directly on the instance?", "title": "Q3: When you define a method sayHi() inside an ES6 class Person, why doesn't sayHi appear when you console.log(person1)? Why wasn't it placed directly on the instance?", "answer": "To follow the DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle and save memory! While instance variables (this.name, this.age) differ per person and are created on each instance inside constructor(), the sayHi() method logic is identical for every Person. Therefore, ES6 classes attach sayHi() once onto Person.prototype (non-enumerable), so 10,000 Person instances share a single function in memory instead of allocating 10,000 duplicate function copies.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "To follow the DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle and save memory! While instance variables (this.name, this.age) differ per person and are created on each instance inside constructor(), the sayHi() method logic is identical for every Person. Therefore, ES6 classes attach sayHi() once onto Person.prototype (non-enumerable), so 10,000 Person instances share a single function in memory instead of allocating 10,000 duplicate function copies."}, {"id": "js-lec-21-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What are the 4 exact steps that happen under the hood when you call new Person(\\"Rohit\\", 20)?", "title": "Q4: What are the 4 exact steps that happen under the hood when you call new Person(\\"Rohit\\", 20)?", "answer": "When a constructor or class is invoked with the new keyword: 1) A brand-new empty object {} is allocated in memory. 2) The new object's internal [[Prototype]] (__proto__) is linked to Person.prototype. 3) The Person constructor function executes with this bound to that new empty object (populating this.name and this.age). 4) The constructor automatically returns that newly created object (unless the constructor explicitly returns a different non-primitive object).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "When a constructor or class is invoked with the new keyword: 1) A brand-new empty object {} is allocated in memory. 2) The new object's internal [[Prototype]] (__proto__) is linked to Person.prototype. 3) The Person constructor function executes with this bound to that new empty object (populating this.name and this.age). 4) The constructor automatically returns that newly created object (unless the constructor explicitly returns a different non-primitive object)."}, {"id": "js-lec-21-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: In a child class that uses extends, why does accessing this before calling super() inside the constructor throw a ReferenceError?", "title": "Q5: In a child class that uses extends, why does accessing this before calling super() inside the constructor throw a ReferenceError?", "answer": "In ES6 class inheritance, the parent class constructor (invoked via super()) is responsible for allocating and initializing the base instance object assigned to this. Until super() has been called inside the derived class constructor, this is uninitialized, so touching this.account = 12 before super() throws a ReferenceError: Must call super constructor in derived class before accessing 'this'.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "In ES6 class inheritance, the parent class constructor (invoked via super()) is responsible for allocating and initializing the base instance object assigned to this. Until super() has been called inside the derived class constructor, this is uninitialized, so touching this.account = 12 before super() throws a ReferenceError: Must call super constructor in derived class before accessing 'this'."}, {"id": "js-lec-21-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why is Object.create(protoObj) preferred over mutating obj.__proto__ = protoObj at runtime?", "title": "Q6: Why is Object.create(protoObj) preferred over mutating obj.__proto__ = protoObj at runtime?", "answer": "__proto__ is a legacy getter/setter kept mainly for backward compatibility, and mutating an existing object's [[Prototype]] after creation (obj.__proto__ = ... or Object.setPrototypeOf()) deoptimizes V8's Hidden Classes and Inline Caches, severely hurting performance. Object.create(protoObj) sets the prototype link cleanly at the moment the new object is created.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "__proto__ is a legacy getter/setter kept mainly for backward compatibility, and mutating an existing object's [[Prototype]] after creation (obj.__proto__ = ... or Object.setPrototypeOf()) deoptimizes V8's Hidden Classes and Inline Caches, severely hurting performance. Object.create(protoObj) sets the prototype link cleanly at the moment the new object is created."}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Inheritance_and_the_prototype_chain	\N	\N	\N
22138	Lecture 20: async / await & Concurrent Promises — try...catch Error Handling, Top-Level await & Promise.all()	js-lecture-20-async-await-try-catch-promise-all-concurrency	Write clean asynchronous code with async/await and try...catch, understand how async functions yield control without freezing the main thread, and eliminate sequential waterfalls using Promise.all().	2026-10-06 21:36:53.504856+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIntroduced in ES2017 (ES8), **`async`** and **`await`** allow you to write asynchronous Promise-based code that reads like clean, top-to-bottom synchronous code—eliminating both Callback Hell and verbose `.then()` chains.\n\n### 💡 Core Rules of `async` and `await`\n* **Rule 1 — An `async` Function ALWAYS Returns a Promise:**\n  * If you write `async function greet() { return "Rohit"; }`, JavaScript automatically wraps `"Rohit"` inside `Promise.resolve("Rohit")`.\n* **Rule 2 — How `await` Works Without Freezing the Main Thread:**\n  * Always place `await` **inside** an `async` function (avoid bare top-level `await` in UI scripts because top-level `await` pauses the entire module!).\n  * When JavaScript hits `const response = await fetch(url)` inside an `async` function, it **suspends only that `async` function**, pops it off the Call Stack, and immediately returns to execute synchronous code outside the function (like rendering UI or logging `"Hello Ji"`). Once the Promise resolves, the rest of the `async` function resumes via the Microtask Queue!\n* **Rule 3 — Error Handling with `try...catch`:**\n  * Wrap `await` calls in a standard `try { ... } catch (error) { ... }` block, and check `if (!response.ok) throw new Error(...)` for HTTP errors.\n\n### 💡 Sequential Waterfall vs. Parallel Execution with `Promise.all()`\n* **The Sequential Waterfall Trap:** If a dashboard needs 3 **independent** API calls (`comments`, `photos`, `chat`), writing three separate `await` lines one after another forces them to run sequentially (2s + 2s + 2s = 6s).\n* **The `Promise.all()` Solution:** Fire all independent Promises in parallel and await them together in O(max(t1, t2, t3)) = 2s:\n  `const [comments, photos, chat = await Promise.all([fetch(u1), fetch(u2), fetch(u3));`\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: `async/await` Pipeline & `Promise.all()` Concurrency\n```javascript\n// 1. Clean Zomato Order Pipeline Using async/await + try...catch\nasync function processOrder(orderDetail) {\n  try {\n    const res1 = await placedOrder(orderDetail);\n    const res2 = await preparingOrder(res1);\n    const res3 = await pickupOrder(res2);\n    const res4 = await deliverOrder(res3);\n    console.log("Final Order Status:", res4);\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.log("Order failed:", error);\n  }\n}\n\n// 2. Fetching GitHub Users with response.ok Check\nasync function loadGitHubUsers() {\n  try {\n    const response = await fetch("https://api.github.com/users");\n    if (!response.ok) {\n      throw new Error("Data is not present (HTTP " + response.status + ")");\n    }\n    const users = await response.json();\n    console.log("Loaded", users.length, "GitHub profiles");\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.log("Error:", error.message);\n  }\n}\n\n// 3. Running 3 Independent Requests in Parallel with Promise.all()\nasync function fetchUserDashboard() {\n  const [comments, photos, chat = await Promise.all([\n    fetch("/api/user/comments"),\n    fetch("/api/user/photos"),\n    fetch("/api/user/chat")\n  );\n}\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/async_function", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/async_function", "title": "MDN Web Docs — async function & await Operator"}, {"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/all", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/all", "title": "MDN Web Docs — Promise.all() Concurrency Reference"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AyBpssNYB6g", "title": "Async Await in Javascript | Javascript Full Course #20", "duration": "49m", "creator_name": "Coder Army", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9"}]	[{"id": "js-lec-20-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What does an async function return if you return a plain string like return \\"Rohit\\" inside it?", "title": "Q1: What does an async function return if you return a plain string like return \\"Rohit\\" inside it?", "answer": "An async function ALWAYS returns a Promise! If you return a non-Promise value like \\"Rohit\\", JavaScript automatically wraps it in a fulfilled Promise (equivalent to return Promise.resolve(\\"Rohit\\")). If you throw an error inside an async function, it returns a rejected Promise.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "20-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "An async function ALWAYS returns a Promise! If you return a non-Promise value like \\"Rohit\\", JavaScript automatically wraps it in a fulfilled Promise (equivalent to return Promise.resolve(\\"Rohit\\")). If you throw an error inside an async function, it returns a rejected Promise."}, {"id": "js-lec-20-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Does the await keyword block the JavaScript main thread / Call Stack? Why should await be used inside an async function rather than at the top level?", "title": "Q2: Does the await keyword block the JavaScript main thread / Call Stack? Why should await be used inside an async function rather than at the top level?", "answer": "When used inside an async function, await does NOT block the main thread! As soon as await encounters a pending Promise, the engine suspends that specific async function's execution context, pops it off the Call Stack, and continues executing synchronous code outside the async function. However, using bare top-level await outside an async function blocks the execution of the rest of that module until the Promise settles, which can freeze downstream initialization.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "20-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "When used inside an async function, await does NOT block the main thread! As soon as await encounters a pending Promise, the engine suspends that specific async function's execution context, pops it off the Call Stack, and continues executing synchronous code outside the async function. However, using bare top-level await outside an async function blocks the execution of the rest of that module until the Promise settles, which can freeze downstream initialization."}, {"id": "js-lec-20-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: When should you use sequential await vs. await Promise.all([...])?", "title": "Q3: When should you use sequential await vs. await Promise.all([...])?", "answer": "Use sequential await ONLY when the second asynchronous operation depends on the output of the first (for example, const response = await fetch(url); const data = await response.json(); or passing an orderId from step 1 into step 2). When multiple asynchronous tasks are independent of each other (e.g., fetching user comments, user photos, and chat history, or connecting to 3 databases), always use await Promise.all([p1, p2, p3]) so all requests run in parallel instead of waiting in a slow sequential waterfall.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "20-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use sequential await ONLY when the second asynchronous operation depends on the output of the first (for example, const response = await fetch(url); const data = await response.json(); or passing an orderId from step 1 into step 2). When multiple asynchronous tasks are independent of each other (e.g., fetching user comments, user photos, and chat history, or connecting to 3 databases), always use await Promise.all([p1, p2, p3]) so all requests run in parallel instead of waiting in a slow sequential waterfall."}, {"id": "js-lec-20-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What happens in Promise.all([p1, p2, p3]) if p2 rejects, and how does Promise.allSettled() differ?", "title": "Q4: What happens in Promise.all([p1, p2, p3]) if p2 rejects, and how does Promise.allSettled() differ?", "answer": "Promise.all() is \\"all-or-nothing\\" (fail-fast): if even one Promise (p2) rejects, Promise.all() immediately rejects with p2's error, discarding the results of p1 and p3. If you want all Promises to finish regardless of individual failures (e.g., showing partial dashboard widgets even if one widget API fails), use Promise.allSettled([p1, p2, p3]), which waits for all of them and returns an array of { status: \\"fulfilled\\", value } or { status: \\"rejected\\", reason } objects.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "20-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Promise.all() is \\"all-or-nothing\\" (fail-fast): if even one Promise (p2) rejects, Promise.all() immediately rejects with p2's error, discarding the results of p1 and p3. If you want all Promises to finish regardless of individual failures (e.g., showing partial dashboard widgets even if one widget API fails), use Promise.allSettled([p1, p2, p3]), which waits for all of them and returns an array of { status: \\"fulfilled\\", value } or { status: \\"rejected\\", reason } objects."}, {"id": "js-lec-20-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Trace the exact console output order: async function fn() { console.log(\\"A\\"); await Promise.resolve(); console.log(\\"B\\"); } console.log(\\"C\\"); fn(); console.log(\\"D\\");", "title": "Q5: Trace the exact console output order: async function fn() { console.log(\\"A\\"); await Promise.resolve(); console.log(\\"B\\"); } console.log(\\"C\\"); fn(); console.log(\\"D\\");", "answer": "The exact output is: C -> A -> D -> B. First, synchronous \\"C\\" logs. Next, fn() is called and its synchronous body up to the first await runs immediately, logging \\"A\\". When await Promise.resolve() is reached, fn() is suspended and the rest of fn() (logging \\"B\\") is scheduled into the Microtask Queue. Synchronous execution continues in the global scope, logging \\"D\\". Finally, once the Call Stack is empty, the Microtask Queue runs and logs \\"B\\".", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "20-q5", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "The exact output is: C -> A -> D -> B. First, synchronous \\"C\\" logs. Next, fn() is called and its synchronous body up to the first await runs immediately, logging \\"A\\". When await Promise.resolve() is reached, fn() is suspended and the rest of fn() (logging \\"B\\") is scheduled into the Microtask Queue. Synchronous execution continues in the global scope, logging \\"D\\". Finally, once the Call Stack is empty, the Microtask Queue runs and logs \\"B\\"."}, {"id": "js-lec-20-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why can async/await use a standard synchronous try...catch block to catch asynchronous Promise rejections, whereas setTimeout callbacks could not?", "title": "Q6: Why can async/await use a standard synchronous try...catch block to catch asynchronous Promise rejections, whereas setTimeout callbacks could not?", "answer": "Because the JavaScript compiler transforms async/await under the hood into a state machine (similar to generators and Promises). When an awaited Promise rejects, the engine resumes the suspended async function execution context back on the Call Stack and throws the rejection reason right at the await point—inside the active try...catch block!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "20-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Because the JavaScript compiler transforms async/await under the hood into a state machine (similar to generators and Promises). When an awaited Promise rejects, the engine resumes the suspended async function execution context back on the Call Stack and throws the rejection reason right at the await point—inside the active try...catch block!"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIntroduced in ES2017 (ES8), **`async`** and **`await`** allow you to write asynchronous Promise-based code that reads like clean, top-to-bottom synchronous code—eliminating both Callback Hell and verbose `.then()` chains.\n\n### 💡 Core Rules of `async` and `await`\n* **Rule 1 — An `async` Function ALWAYS Returns a Promise:**\n  * If you write `async function greet() { return "Rohit"; }`, JavaScript automatically wraps `"Rohit"` inside `Promise.resolve("Rohit")`.\n* **Rule 2 — How `await` Works Without Freezing the Main Thread:**\n  * Always place `await` **inside** an `async` function (avoid bare top-level `await` in UI scripts because top-level `await` pauses the entire module!).\n  * When JavaScript hits `const response = await fetch(url)` inside an `async` function, it **suspends only that `async` function**, pops it off the Call Stack, and immediately returns to execute synchronous code outside the function (like rendering UI or logging `"Hello Ji"`). Once the Promise resolves, the rest of the `async` function resumes via the Microtask Queue!\n* **Rule 3 — Error Handling with `try...catch`:**\n  * Wrap `await` calls in a standard `try { ... } catch (error) { ... }` block, and check `if (!response.ok) throw new Error(...)` for HTTP errors.\n\n### 💡 Sequential Waterfall vs. Parallel Execution with `Promise.all()`\n* **The Sequential Waterfall Trap:** If a dashboard needs 3 **independent** API calls (`comments`, `photos`, `chat`), writing three separate `await` lines one after another forces them to run sequentially (2s + 2s + 2s = 6s).\n* **The `Promise.all()` Solution:** Fire all independent Promises in parallel and await them together in O(max(t1, t2, t3)) = 2s:\n  `const [comments, photos, chat = await Promise.all([fetch(u1), fetch(u2), fetch(u3));`\n\n### 🛠️ Code Examples: `async/await` Pipeline & `Promise.all()` Concurrency\n```javascript\n// 1. Clean Zomato Order Pipeline Using async/await + try...catch\nasync function processOrder(orderDetail) {\n  try {\n    const res1 = await placedOrder(orderDetail);\n    const res2 = await preparingOrder(res1);\n    const res3 = await pickupOrder(res2);\n    const res4 = await deliverOrder(res3);\n    console.log("Final Order Status:", res4);\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.log("Order failed:", error);\n  }\n}\n\n// 2. Fetching GitHub Users with response.ok Check\nasync function loadGitHubUsers() {\n  try {\n    const response = await fetch("https://api.github.com/users");\n    if (!response.ok) {\n      throw new Error("Data is not present (HTTP " + response.status + ")");\n    }\n    const users = await response.json();\n    console.log("Loaded", users.length, "GitHub profiles");\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.log("Error:", error.message);\n  }\n}\n\n// 3. Running 3 Independent Requests in Parallel with Promise.all()\nasync function fetchUserDashboard() {\n  const [comments, photos, chat = await Promise.all([\n    fetch("/api/user/comments"),\n    fetch("/api/user/photos"),\n    fetch("/api/user/chat")\n  );\n}\n```	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy9	708	Lecture 20: async / await & Concurrent Promises — try...catch Error Handling, Top-Level await & Promise.all()	Write clean asynchronous code with async/await and try...catch, understand how async functions yield control without freezing the main thread, and eliminate sequential waterfalls using Promise.all().	20	Easy	\N	20	[{"id": "js-lec-20-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: What does an async function return if you return a plain string like return \\"Rohit\\" inside it?", "title": "Q1: What does an async function return if you return a plain string like return \\"Rohit\\" inside it?", "answer": "An async function ALWAYS returns a Promise! If you return a non-Promise value like \\"Rohit\\", JavaScript automatically wraps it in a fulfilled Promise (equivalent to return Promise.resolve(\\"Rohit\\")). If you throw an error inside an async function, it returns a rejected Promise.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "20-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "An async function ALWAYS returns a Promise! If you return a non-Promise value like \\"Rohit\\", JavaScript automatically wraps it in a fulfilled Promise (equivalent to return Promise.resolve(\\"Rohit\\")). If you throw an error inside an async function, it returns a rejected Promise."}, {"id": "js-lec-20-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Does the await keyword block the JavaScript main thread / Call Stack? Why should await be used inside an async function rather than at the top level?", "title": "Q2: Does the await keyword block the JavaScript main thread / Call Stack? Why should await be used inside an async function rather than at the top level?", "answer": "When used inside an async function, await does NOT block the main thread! As soon as await encounters a pending Promise, the engine suspends that specific async function's execution context, pops it off the Call Stack, and continues executing synchronous code outside the async function. However, using bare top-level await outside an async function blocks the execution of the rest of that module until the Promise settles, which can freeze downstream initialization.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "20-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "When used inside an async function, await does NOT block the main thread! As soon as await encounters a pending Promise, the engine suspends that specific async function's execution context, pops it off the Call Stack, and continues executing synchronous code outside the async function. However, using bare top-level await outside an async function blocks the execution of the rest of that module until the Promise settles, which can freeze downstream initialization."}, {"id": "js-lec-20-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: When should you use sequential await vs. await Promise.all([...])?", "title": "Q3: When should you use sequential await vs. await Promise.all([...])?", "answer": "Use sequential await ONLY when the second asynchronous operation depends on the output of the first (for example, const response = await fetch(url); const data = await response.json(); or passing an orderId from step 1 into step 2). When multiple asynchronous tasks are independent of each other (e.g., fetching user comments, user photos, and chat history, or connecting to 3 databases), always use await Promise.all([p1, p2, p3]) so all requests run in parallel instead of waiting in a slow sequential waterfall.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "20-q3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use sequential await ONLY when the second asynchronous operation depends on the output of the first (for example, const response = await fetch(url); const data = await response.json(); or passing an orderId from step 1 into step 2). When multiple asynchronous tasks are independent of each other (e.g., fetching user comments, user photos, and chat history, or connecting to 3 databases), always use await Promise.all([p1, p2, p3]) so all requests run in parallel instead of waiting in a slow sequential waterfall."}, {"id": "js-lec-20-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What happens in Promise.all([p1, p2, p3]) if p2 rejects, and how does Promise.allSettled() differ?", "title": "Q4: What happens in Promise.all([p1, p2, p3]) if p2 rejects, and how does Promise.allSettled() differ?", "answer": "Promise.all() is \\"all-or-nothing\\" (fail-fast): if even one Promise (p2) rejects, Promise.all() immediately rejects with p2's error, discarding the results of p1 and p3. If you want all Promises to finish regardless of individual failures (e.g., showing partial dashboard widgets even if one widget API fails), use Promise.allSettled([p1, p2, p3]), which waits for all of them and returns an array of { status: \\"fulfilled\\", value } or { status: \\"rejected\\", reason } objects.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "20-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Promise.all() is \\"all-or-nothing\\" (fail-fast): if even one Promise (p2) rejects, Promise.all() immediately rejects with p2's error, discarding the results of p1 and p3. If you want all Promises to finish regardless of individual failures (e.g., showing partial dashboard widgets even if one widget API fails), use Promise.allSettled([p1, p2, p3]), which waits for all of them and returns an array of { status: \\"fulfilled\\", value } or { status: \\"rejected\\", reason } objects."}, {"id": "js-lec-20-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Trace the exact console output order: async function fn() { console.log(\\"A\\"); await Promise.resolve(); console.log(\\"B\\"); } console.log(\\"C\\"); fn(); console.log(\\"D\\");", "title": "Q5: Trace the exact console output order: async function fn() { console.log(\\"A\\"); await Promise.resolve(); console.log(\\"B\\"); } console.log(\\"C\\"); fn(); console.log(\\"D\\");", "answer": "The exact output is: C -> A -> D -> B. First, synchronous \\"C\\" logs. Next, fn() is called and its synchronous body up to the first await runs immediately, logging \\"A\\". When await Promise.resolve() is reached, fn() is suspended and the rest of fn() (logging \\"B\\") is scheduled into the Microtask Queue. Synchronous execution continues in the global scope, logging \\"D\\". Finally, once the Call Stack is empty, the Microtask Queue runs and logs \\"B\\".", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "20-q5", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "The exact output is: C -> A -> D -> B. First, synchronous \\"C\\" logs. Next, fn() is called and its synchronous body up to the first await runs immediately, logging \\"A\\". When await Promise.resolve() is reached, fn() is suspended and the rest of fn() (logging \\"B\\") is scheduled into the Microtask Queue. Synchronous execution continues in the global scope, logging \\"D\\". Finally, once the Call Stack is empty, the Microtask Queue runs and logs \\"B\\"."}, {"id": "js-lec-20-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why can async/await use a standard synchronous try...catch block to catch asynchronous Promise rejections, whereas setTimeout callbacks could not?", "title": "Q6: Why can async/await use a standard synchronous try...catch block to catch asynchronous Promise rejections, whereas setTimeout callbacks could not?", "answer": "Because the JavaScript compiler transforms async/await under the hood into a state machine (similar to generators and Promises). When an awaited Promise rejects, the engine resumes the suspended async function execution context back on the Call Stack and throws the rejection reason right at the await point—inside the active try...catch block!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "20-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Because the JavaScript compiler transforms async/await under the hood into a state machine (similar to generators and Promises). When an awaited Promise rejects, the engine resumes the suspended async function execution context back on the Call Stack and throws the rejection reason right at the await point—inside the active try...catch block!"}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/async_function	\N	\N	\N
