) { this.count++; }, 1000) inside an object method fail to increment the object's count, and how do Arrow Functions fix it?", "title": "Q4: Why does a regular function inside setInterval(function() { this.count++; }, 1000) inside an object method fail to increment the object's count, and how do Arrow Functions fix it?", "answer": "When setInterval invokes a regular callback function(), it invokes it as a standalone function (not as obj.callback()), so this inside the callback points to window (or undefined in strict mode) rather than your object! Replacing the callback with an Arrow Function setInterval(() => { this.count++; }, 1000) fixes the bug because the arrow function has no this of its own and lexically inherits this from the enclosing start() method (where this points to your object).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "22-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "When setInterval invokes a regular callback function(), it invokes it as a standalone function (not as obj.callback()), so this inside the callback points to window (or undefined in strict mode) rather than your object! Replacing the callback with an Arrow Function setInterval(() => { this.count++; }, 1000) fixes the bug because the arrow function has no this of its own and lexically inherits this from the enclosing start() method (where this points to your object)."}, {"id": "js-lec-22-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Can you change the this of an Arrow Function using .call(), .apply(), or .bind()?", "title": "Q5: Can you change the this of an Arrow Function using .call(), .apply(), or .bind()?", "answer": "No. Because an Arrow Function has no internal [[ThisMode]] slot of its own and resolves this lexically from its defining scope, passing a thisArg to arrowFn.call(obj), .apply(obj), or .bind(obj) is completely ignored (only the regular arguments are passed through).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "22-q5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "No. Because an Arrow Function has no internal [[ThisMode]] slot of its own and resolves this lexically from its defining scope, passing a thisArg to arrowFn.call(obj), .apply(obj), or .bind(obj) is completely ignored (only the regular arguments are passed through)."}, {"id": "js-lec-22-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why does console.log(this) at the top level of a file print window in the browser, but print {} (an empty object) in Node.js?", "title": "Q6: Why does console.log(this) at the top level of a file print window in the browser, but print {} (an empty object) in Node.js?", "answer": "In a browser script, top-level code executes directly in the global window scope. In Node.js (CommonJS), every .js file is wrapped inside a module wrapper function (function(exports, require, module, __filename, __dirname) { ... }) and invoked with this bound to module.exports, which starts as an empty object {}! To access the true global object universally across both browsers and Node.js, use globalThis.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "22-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "In a browser script, top-level code executes directly in the global window scope. In Node.js (CommonJS), every .js file is wrapped inside a module wrapper function (function(exports, require, module, __filename, __dirname) { ... }) and invoked with this bound to module.exports, which starts as an empty object {}! To access the true global object universally across both browsers and Node.js, use globalThis."}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/this	\N	\N	\N
7665	Subsequence Occurrence Counting & 1D Array Optimization: Distinct Subsequences	dsa-distinct-subsequences-1d-array-optimization	Count distinct occurrences of pattern string t as a subsequence in string s using right-to-left 1D single-array space optimization.	2026-10-03 11:39:06.529993+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Subsequence Occurrence Counting & 1D Array Optimization: Distinct Subsequences\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nVG7eTiD2bY", "title": "Lecture 213: Distinct Subsequences (1D Array Optimization - DP 32)", "duration": "24m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/distinct-subsequences/", "title": "Distinct Subsequences (LC 115)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Given two strings s and t, return the number of distinct subsequences of s which equals t."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/distinct-occurrences/1", "title": "Distinct occurrences", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count the number of distinct occurrences of string T as a subsequence in string S."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/distinct-subsequences-ii/", "title": "Distinct Subsequences II (LC 940)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Count the number of distinct non-empty subsequences of a single string s modulo 10^9 + 7."}]	Module 31: Dynamic Programming on Strings: LCS, Substrings & Palindromic Subsequences	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven two strings `s` (length `N`) and `t` (length `M`), count how many **distinct subsequences of `s`** equal the target string `t`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Top-Down Recurrence (`f(i, j)` = ways to match `t[0..j]` inside `s[0..i]`):**\n  - **Base Case 1 (`j < 0` or `j == 0` in 1-based indexing):** All characters of `t` have been matched! Return **`1`**.\n  - **Base Case 2 (`i < 0` or `i == 0` while `j > 0`):** String `s` is exhausted, but `t` still has unmatched characters! Return **`0`**.\n  - **Transitions:**\n    1. **If `s[i - 1] == t[j - 1]` (Characters Match):** We can *either* match `s[i - 1]` with `t[j - 1]` (`f(i - 1, j - 1)`) *or* skip `s[i - 1]` and look for an earlier occurrence (`f(i - 1, j)`):\n       **`dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + dp[i - 1][j]`**.\n    2. **Else (`s[i - 1] != t[j - 1]`):** We must skip `s[i - 1]`:\n       **`dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j]`**.\n- **The 1D Single-Array Right-to-Left Optimization (`O(M)` Space):**\n  - Notice that row `i` only depends on `prev[j]` and `prev[j - 1]` (exactly like 0/1 Knapsack!).\n  - By iterating `j` **from Right to Left (`M` down to `1`)** on a single 1D array `dp(M + 1, 0)` with `dp[0] = 1`, when `s[i - 1] == t[j - 1]` we simply do **`dp[j] = dp[j] + dp[j - 1]`**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * M)` Time and `O(M)` Single-Array Space.\n\n### 💻 Distinct Subsequences (1-Array Space Optimized) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint numDistinct(string s, string t) {\n    int n = s.size(), m = t.size();\n    // Use double or unsigned long long to prevent intermediate overflow on LeetCode 115\n    vector<double> dp(m + 1, 0);\n\n    // Base case: empty target t (j == 0) is matched in 1 way\n    dp[0] = 1;\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        // Iterate j from RIGHT TO LEFT (m down to 1) for single-array optimization!\n        for (int j = m; j >= 1; j--) {\n            if (s[i - 1] == t[j - 1]) {\n                dp[j] = dp[j - 1] + dp[j];\n            }\n            // If s[i - 1] != t[j - 1], dp[j] stays dp[j] (no update needed!)\n        }\n    }\n    return (int)dp[m];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Swapping the Order of Base Cases (`if (i == 0)` before `if (j == 0)`):** When both `i == 0` and `j == 0`, both strings are empty, which is a **valid match (`1`)**! Always set `dp[0] = 1` (or check `if (j < 0) return 1;` **before** `if (i < 0) return 0;`)!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
