if the resulting hash string matches storedHash."}, {"id": "node-lec-20-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What is the difference between Input Validation and Input Sanitization in express-validator?", "title": "Q4: What is the difference between Input Validation and Input Sanitization in express-validator?", "answer": "Validation checks whether the input meets required rules (e.g., .isEmail(), .isLength({ min: 8 }), .isIn([\\"guest\\", \\"host\\"])) and records an error if it fails. Sanitization modifies and cleans the input before processing (e.g., .trim() removes leading/trailing spaces, .normalizeEmail() lowercases and canonicalizes email addresses, .escape() escapes HTML entities).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "20-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Validation checks whether the input meets required rules (e.g., .isEmail(), .isLength({ min: 8 }), .isIn([\\"guest\\", \\"host\\"])) and records an error if it fails. Sanitization modifies and cleans the input before processing (e.g., .trim() removes leading/trailing spaces, .normalizeEmail() lowercases and canonicalizes email addresses, .escape() escapes HTML entities)."}, {"id": "node-lec-20-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: When a signup form fails validation (HTTP 422), why do we pass oldInput back into res.render(\\"auth/signup\\", { errors, oldInput })?", "title": "Q5: When a signup form fails validation (HTTP 422), why do we pass oldInput back into res.render(\\"auth/signup\\", { errors, oldInput })?", "answer": "For good user experience (UX)! Without oldInput, if a user fills out 6 fields on a signup form and makes a small typo in confirmPassword, re-rendering the page would wipe out all their typed inputs and force them to retype everything from scratch. Pre-filling value=\\"<%= oldInput.firstName %>\\" preserves their valid inputs while displaying the specific validation error messages.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "20-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "For good user experience (UX)! Without oldInput, if a user fills out 6 fields on a signup form and makes a small typo in confirmPassword, re-rendering the page would wipe out all their typed inputs and force them to retype everything from scratch. Pre-filling value=\\"<%= oldInput.firstName %>\\" preserves their valid inputs while displaying the specific validation error messages."}, {"id": "node-lec-20-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: Why did we move the favourites array inside the User Mongoose Schema instead of keeping a separate global Favourite collection?", "title": "Q6: Why did we move the favourites array inside the User Mongoose Schema instead of keeping a separate global Favourite collection?", "answer": "In a multi-user application, each logged-in user must have their own personal list of favourited homes! Storing favourites: [{ type: mongoose.Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: \\"Home\\" }] as an array on the User document scopes favourites per user and allows fetching a user's favourite homes in one query via User.findById(req.session.user._id).populate(\\"favourites\\").", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "20-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In a multi-user application, each logged-in user must have their own personal list of favourited homes! Storing favourites: [{ type: mongoose.Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: \\"Home\\" }] as an array on the User document scopes favourites per user and allows fetching a user's favourite homes in one query via User.findById(req.session.user._id).populate(\\"favourites\\")."}]	https://express-validator.github.io/docs/	\N	\N	\N
22161	Lecture 21: File Uploads & Downloads — multipart/form-data, multer Storage & MIME Filters, Static Serving & fs.unlink() Cleanup	node-lecture-21-file-upload-and-download-multer-multipart-form-data-fs-unlink	Configure HTML file pickers with enctype="multipart/form-data", process binary image uploads in Express using multer (diskStorage and fileFilter), store file paths in MongoDB, and clean up replaced files with fs.unlink().	2026-10-06 23:02:53.291009+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nStandard HTML forms use `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, which can only transmit text strings—if you put `<input type="file" name="photo">` inside a default form, only the filename string is sent, **not the actual binary file**! Uploading images or PDFs requires setting **`enctype="multipart/form-data"`** on the HTML `<form>` and using the **`multer`** middleware on the Express server.\n\n### 💡 Configuring `multer` (`diskStorage` & `fileFilter`)\nBecause `express.urlencoded()` cannot parse `multipart/form-data` binary streams, install and configure **`multer`**:\n1. **`multer.diskStorage({ destination, filename })`:**\n   * **`destination: (req, file, cb) => cb(null, "uploads/")`** — specifies the folder where uploaded files are saved on disk.\n   * **`filename: (req, file, cb) => cb(null, randomString(10) + "-" + file.originalname)`** — prefixes a random string so two users uploading `"house.jpg"` never overwrite each other's files!\n2. **`fileFilter: (req, file, cb) => ...`:**\n   * Inspects **`file.mimetype`** on the server (`["image/png", "image/jpg", "image/jpeg".includes(file.mimetype)`) because client-side `accept="image/*"` on `<input type="file">` can be easily bypassed by users!\n3. **Accessing Uploaded File vs. Text Fields:**\n   * `app.use(multer({ storage, fileFilter }).single("photo"))` populates **`req.file`** (with `req.file.path`, `filename`, `mimetype`) AND populates **`req.body`** with the remaining text fields!\n\n### 💡 Serving Uploaded Images & Deleting Old Files (`fs.unlink`)\n* **Never store binary images directly inside MongoDB documents** (BSON documents have a 16 MB limit and large blobs slow down queries). Store the file on disk (or S3/Cloudinary) and save **only the relative path (`req.file.path`)** in MongoDB!\n* **Expose `/uploads` Statically:** Mount `app.use("/uploads", express.static(path.join(rootDir, "uploads")))` so `<img src="/<%= home.photo %>">` loads the image.\n* **Clean Up Orphaned Files on Edit (`fs.unlink`):** When a host uploads a new photo while editing a home, delete the old photo from disk using **`fs.unlink(oldFilePath, err => ...)`** so old unused images don't fill up the server hard drive!\n\n### 🛠️ Complete `multer` Configuration & `fs.unlink` File Cleanup\n```javascript\nconst multer = require("multer");\nconst fs = require("fs");\n\n// 1. Configure Custom Disk Storage (Destination + Collision-Safe Filename)\nconst storage = multer.diskStorage({\n  destination: (req, file, cb) => {\n    cb(null, "uploads/");\n  },\n  filename: (req, file, cb) => {\n    const uniquePrefix = Math.random().toString(36).substring(2, 12);\n    cb(null, uniquePrefix + "-" + file.originalname);\n  }\n});\n\n// 2. Server-Side MIME Type Filter (Allow only PNG/JPG/JPEG)\nconst fileFilter = (req, file, cb) => {\n  if (["image/png", "image/jpg", "image/jpeg".includes(file.mimetype)) {\n    cb(null, true);  // Accept file\n  } else {\n    cb(null, false); // Reject file\n  }\n};\n\napp.use(multer({ storage, fileFilter }).single("photo"));\n\n// 3. In postEditHome Controller: Delete old image file when a new photo is uploaded\nif (req.file) {\n  fs.unlink(home.photo, (err) => {\n    if (err) console.log("Error deleting old image:", err);\n  });\n  home.photo = req.file.path;\n}\n```	[{"url": "https://github.com/expressjs/multer#readme", "link": "https://github.com/expressjs/multer#readme", "title": "Multer Official Documentation — Handling multipart/form-data in Express"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=837d-bTG6Xs", "title": "Lecture 20: File Upload & Download | NodeJS Complete Course in Hindi", "duration": "65m", "creator_name": "Complete Coding by Prashant Sir", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir"}]	[{"id": "node-lec-21-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Why must an HTML <form> include enctype=\\"multipart/form-data\\" when uploading files, and why can't express.urlencoded() parse it?", "title": "Q1: Why must an HTML <form> include enctype=\\"multipart/form-data\\" when uploading files, and why can't express.urlencoded() parse it?", "answer": "By default, HTML forms use enctype=\\"application/x-www-form-urlencoded\\", which URL-encodes text keys and values into a single string (key1=val1&key2=val2) and cannot efficiently encode raw binary file streams (it only sends the filename string). Setting enctype=\\"multipart/form-data\\" splits the HTTP request body into multiple MIME parts separated by a boundary string—allowing raw binary file bytes and text fields to be streamed together. Because express.urlencoded() only understands URL-encoded text, a multipart parser like multer is required.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "By default, HTML forms use enctype=\\"application/x-www-form-urlencoded\\", which URL-encodes text keys and values into a single string (key1=val1&key2=val2) and cannot efficiently encode raw binary file streams (it only sends the filename string). Setting enctype=\\"multipart/form-data\\" splits the HTTP request body into multiple MIME parts separated by a boundary string—allowing raw binary file bytes and text fields to be streamed together. Because express.urlencoded() only understands URL-encoded text, a multipart parser like multer is required."}, {"id": "node-lec-21-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: Why do we store only the file path/URL (req.file.path) in MongoDB instead of storing the raw binary image file directly inside the MongoDB document?", "title": "Q2: Why do we store only the file path/URL (req.file.path) in MongoDB instead of storing the raw binary image file directly inside the MongoDB document?", "answer": "1) Document Size Limit: MongoDB enforces a strict 16 MB maximum size per BSON document. 2) RAM & Query Performance: Databases cache working sets of documents in RAM for fast indexing and querying. Stuffing multi-megabyte binary images inside documents bloats RAM, evicts indexes from cache, and makes simple queries slow and expensive. Storing files on disk or object storage (AWS S3 / Cloudinary) and saving only the lightweight URL string in MongoDB is the universal industry standard.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "1) Document Size Limit: MongoDB enforces a strict 16 MB maximum size per BSON document. 2) RAM & Query Performance: Databases cache working sets of documents in RAM for fast indexing and querying. Stuffing multi-megabyte binary images inside documents bloats RAM, evicts indexes from cache, and makes simple queries slow and expensive. Storing files on disk or object storage (AWS S3 / Cloudinary) and saving only the lightweight URL string in MongoDB is the universal industry standard."}, {"id": "node-lec-21-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: Why is server-side MIME type validation in multer's fileFilter mandatory even if you already added accept=\\"image/jpeg, image/png\\" to the HTML <input type=\\"file\\">?", "title": "Q3: Why is server-side MIME type validation in multer's fileFilter mandatory even if you already added accept=\\"image/jpeg, image/png\\" to the HTML <input type=\\"file\\">?", "answer": "The HTML accept attribute is merely a UI hint for the user's OS file picker dialog—a user can easily select \\"All Files (*.*)\\" in the dialog or send a malicious .exe / .sh / .html file directly via Postman or cURL! multer's server-side fileFilter inspects file.mimetype on the server and rejects unauthorized file types before they are written to disk.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "The HTML accept attribute is merely a UI hint for the user's OS file picker dialog—a user can easily select \\"All Files (*.*)\\" in the dialog or send a malicious .exe / .sh / .html file directly via Postman or cURL! multer's server-side fileFilter inspects file.mimetype on the server and rejects unauthorized file types before they are written to disk."}, {"id": "node-lec-21-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why did we prefix a random string (or timestamp/UUID) to file.originalname inside multer.diskStorage's filename callback?", "title": "Q4: Why did we prefix a random string (or timestamp/UUID) to file.originalname inside multer.diskStorage's filename callback?", "answer": "If User A uploads a photo named \\"image.jpg\\" and User B later uploads a different photo also named \\"image.jpg\\", saving both under file.originalname in the same uploads/ directory would cause User B's file to overwrite User A's photo! Prefixing a unique random string or timestamp guarantees unique filenames on disk.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "If User A uploads a photo named \\"image.jpg\\" and User B later uploads a different photo also named \\"image.jpg\\", saving both under file.originalname in the same uploads/ directory would cause User B's file to overwrite User A's photo! Prefixing a unique random string or timestamp guarantees unique filenames on disk."}, {"id": "node-lec-21-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: When a host edits a home without selecting a new file in the file picker, what is the value of req.file, and how should the controller handle it?", "title": "Q5: When a host edits a home without selecting a new file in the file picker, what is the value of req.file, and how should the controller handle it?", "answer": "If the user leaves the file input empty during an Edit submission (or uploads an invalid file type rejected by fileFilter), req.file is undefined. In postEditHome, you must check if (req.file) before updating home.photo—if req.file is undefined, keep the existing home.photo path intact; if req.file exists, delete the old file via fs.unlink(home.photo) and update home.photo = req.file.path.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "If the user leaves the file input empty during an Edit submission (or uploads an invalid file type rejected by fileFilter), req.file is undefined. In postEditHome, you must check if (req.file) before updating home.photo—if req.file is undefined, keep the existing home.photo path intact; if req.file exists, delete the old file via fs.unlink(home.photo) and update home.photo = req.file.path."}, {"id": "node-lec-21-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: How do you restrict a downloadable file (like house rules PDFs) so only authenticated users can download it instead of exposing it publicly via express.static()?", "title": "Q6: How do you restrict a downloadable file (like house rules PDFs) so only authenticated users can download it instead of exposing it publicly via express.static()?", "answer": "Store the private files in a non-public folder (e.g., rules/ outside of public/), create an authenticated GET route GET /rules/:homeId protected by your auth middleware (if (!req.session.isLoggedIn) return res.redirect(\\"/login\\")), and use res.download(filePath, customFileName) to stream the file only to authorized users.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Store the private files in a non-public folder (e.g., rules/ outside of public/), create an authenticated GET route GET /rules/:homeId protected by your auth middleware (if (!req.session.isLoggedIn) return res.redirect(\\"/login\\")), and use res.download(filePath, customFileName) to stream the file only to authorized users."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nStandard HTML forms use `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, which can only transmit text strings—if you put `<input type="file" name="photo">` inside a default form, only the filename string is sent, **not the actual binary file**! Uploading images or PDFs requires setting **`enctype="multipart/form-data"`** on the HTML `<form>` and using the **`multer`** middleware on the Express server.\n\n### 💡 Configuring `multer` (`diskStorage` & `fileFilter`)\nBecause `express.urlencoded()` cannot parse `multipart/form-data` binary streams, install and configure **`multer`**:\n1. **`multer.diskStorage({ destination, filename })`:**\n   * **`destination: (req, file, cb) => cb(null, "uploads/")`** — specifies the folder where uploaded files are saved on disk.\n   * **`filename: (req, file, cb) => cb(null, randomString(10) + "-" + file.originalname)`** — prefixes a random string so two users uploading `"house.jpg"` never overwrite each other's files!\n2. **`fileFilter: (req, file, cb) => ...`:**\n   * Inspects **`file.mimetype`** on the server (`["image/png", "image/jpg", "image/jpeg".includes(file.mimetype)`) because client-side `accept="image/*"` on `<input type="file">` can be easily bypassed by users!\n3. **Accessing Uploaded File vs. Text Fields:**\n   * `app.use(multer({ storage, fileFilter }).single("photo"))` populates **`req.file`** (with `req.file.path`, `filename`, `mimetype`) AND populates **`req.body`** with the remaining text fields!\n\n### 💡 Serving Uploaded Images & Deleting Old Files (`fs.unlink`)\n* **Never store binary images directly inside MongoDB documents** (BSON documents have a 16 MB limit and large blobs slow down queries). Store the file on disk (or S3/Cloudinary) and save **only the relative path (`req.file.path`)** in MongoDB!\n* **Expose `/uploads` Statically:** Mount `app.use("/uploads", express.static(path.join(rootDir, "uploads")))` so `<img src="/<%= home.photo %>">` loads the image.\n* **Clean Up Orphaned Files on Edit (`fs.unlink`):** When a host uploads a new photo while editing a home, delete the old photo from disk using **`fs.unlink(oldFilePath, err => ...)`** so old unused images don't fill up the server hard drive!\n\n### 🛠️ Complete `multer` Configuration & `fs.unlink` File Cleanup\n```javascript\nconst multer = require("multer");\nconst fs = require("fs");\n\n// 1. Configure Custom Disk Storage (Destination + Collision-Safe Filename)\nconst storage = multer.diskStorage({\n  destination: (req, file, cb) => {\n    cb(null, "uploads/");\n  },\n  filename: (req, file, cb) => {\n    const uniquePrefix = Math.random().toString(36).substring(2, 12);\n    cb(null, uniquePrefix + "-" + file.originalname);\n  }\n});\n\n// 2. Server-Side MIME Type Filter (Allow only PNG/JPG/JPEG)\nconst fileFilter = (req, file, cb) => {\n  if (["image/png", "image/jpg", "image/jpeg".includes(file.mimetype)) {\n    cb(null, true);  // Accept file\n  } else {\n    cb(null, false); // Reject file\n  }\n};\n\napp.use(multer({ storage, fileFilter }).single("photo"));\n\n// 3. In postEditHome Controller: Delete old image file when a new photo is uploaded\nif (req.file) {\n  fs.unlink(home.photo, (err) => {\n    if (err) console.log("Error deleting old image:", err);\n  });\n  home.photo = req.file.path;\n}\n```	Complete Coding by Prashant Sir	https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir	24	Lecture 21: File Uploads & Downloads — multipart/form-data, multer Storage & MIME Filters, Static Serving & fs.unlink() Cleanup	Configure HTML file pickers with enctype="multipart/form-data", process binary image uploads in Express using multer (diskStorage and fileFilter), store file paths in MongoDB, and clean up replaced files with fs.unlink().	20	Easy	\N	21	[{"id": "node-lec-21-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Why must an HTML <form> include enctype=\\"multipart/form-data\\" when uploading files, and why can't express.u