de fileFilter inspects file.mimetype on the server and rejects unauthorized file types before they are written to disk."}, {"id": "node-lec-21-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: Why did we prefix a random string (or timestamp/UUID) to file.originalname inside multer.diskStorage's filename callback?", "title": "Q4: Why did we prefix a random string (or timestamp/UUID) to file.originalname inside multer.diskStorage's filename callback?", "answer": "If User A uploads a photo named \\"image.jpg\\" and User B later uploads a different photo also named \\"image.jpg\\", saving both under file.originalname in the same uploads/ directory would cause User B's file to overwrite User A's photo! Prefixing a unique random string or timestamp guarantees unique filenames on disk.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "If User A uploads a photo named \\"image.jpg\\" and User B later uploads a different photo also named \\"image.jpg\\", saving both under file.originalname in the same uploads/ directory would cause User B's file to overwrite User A's photo! Prefixing a unique random string or timestamp guarantees unique filenames on disk."}, {"id": "node-lec-21-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: When a host edits a home without selecting a new file in the file picker, what is the value of req.file, and how should the controller handle it?", "title": "Q5: When a host edits a home without selecting a new file in the file picker, what is the value of req.file, and how should the controller handle it?", "answer": "If the user leaves the file input empty during an Edit submission (or uploads an invalid file type rejected by fileFilter), req.file is undefined. In postEditHome, you must check if (req.file) before updating home.photo—if req.file is undefined, keep the existing home.photo path intact; if req.file exists, delete the old file via fs.unlink(home.photo) and update home.photo = req.file.path.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "If the user leaves the file input empty during an Edit submission (or uploads an invalid file type rejected by fileFilter), req.file is undefined. In postEditHome, you must check if (req.file) before updating home.photo—if req.file is undefined, keep the existing home.photo path intact; if req.file exists, delete the old file via fs.unlink(home.photo) and update home.photo = req.file.path."}, {"id": "node-lec-21-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: How do you restrict a downloadable file (like house rules PDFs) so only authenticated users can download it instead of exposing it publicly via express.static()?", "title": "Q6: How do you restrict a downloadable file (like house rules PDFs) so only authenticated users can download it instead of exposing it publicly via express.static()?", "answer": "Store the private files in a non-public folder (e.g., rules/ outside of public/), create an authenticated GET route GET /rules/:homeId protected by your auth middleware (if (!req.session.isLoggedIn) return res.redirect(\\"/login\\")), and use res.download(filePath, customFileName) to stream the file only to authorized users.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "21-q6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Store the private files in a non-public folder (e.g., rules/ outside of public/), create an authenticated GET route GET /rules/:homeId protected by your auth middleware (if (!req.session.isLoggedIn) return res.redirect(\\"/login\\")), and use res.download(filePath, customFileName) to stream the file only to authorized users."}]	https://github.com/expressjs/multer#readme	\N	\N	\N
1643	Alternating Direction BFS: Zig-Zag / Spiral Level Order Traversal	dsa-zigzag-spiral-level-order-traversal	Traverse a binary tree level by level alternating direction between left-to-right and right-to-left using index mapping.	2026-10-03 01:02:00.041403+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Alternating Direction BFS: Zig-Zag / Spiral Level Order Traversal\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3OXWEdlIGl4", "title": "Lecture 91: Zig-Zag or Spiral Traversal in Binary Tree", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-level-order-traversal-ii/", "title": "Binary Tree Level Order Traversal II", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return bottom-up level order traversal alternating from the leaf levels up to root."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/average-of-levels-in-binary-tree/", "title": "Average of Levels in Binary Tree", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Capture level snapshots during BFS to compute averages per level."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/zigzag-tree-traversal/1", "title": "ZigZag Tree Traversal", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Traverse a tree in alternating left-to-right and right-to-left order."}]	Module 16: Binary Trees: Structural Properties & Path Sums	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn a **Zig-Zag (Spiral) Traversal**, we visit level 1 from left to right, level 2 from right to left, level 3 from left to right, and continue alternating direction at every subsequent level.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Flag Index-Mapping Trick (`leftToRight` boolean):**\n  - We do not need to reverse the queue or push children in reverse order!\n  - Run standard BFS with a `queue<TreeNode*> q`.\n  - Maintain a boolean `leftToRight = true`.\n  - At each level with `size` nodes, create a fixed-size row vector: `vector<int> row(size)`.\n  - For each node at loop index `i` (from `0` to `size - 1`):\n    - If `leftToRight == true`, place the node value at `row[i]`.\n    - If `leftToRight == false`, place the node value in reverse at `row[size - 1 - i]`.\n  - Toggle `leftToRight = !leftToRight` after completing each level.\n- **Complexity:** `O(N)` Time and `O(N)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Zig-Zag Traversal Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<vector<int>> zigzagLevelOrder(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<vector<int>> result;\n    if (root == nullptr) return result;\n\n    queue<TreeNode*> q;\n    q.push(root);\n    bool leftToRight = true;\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int size = q.size();\n        vector<int> row(size);\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n            TreeNode* node = q.front();\n            q.pop();\n\n            // Direct index placement based on flag\n            int index = leftToRight ? i : (size - 1 - i);\n            row[index] = node->val;\n\n            if (node->left != nullptr) q.push(node->left);\n            if (node->right != nullptr) q.push(node->right);\n        }\n        leftToRight = !leftToRight; // Switch direction for next level\n        result.push_back(row);\n    }\n    return result;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22296	2D & 3D Spatial Transforms	css-2d-3d-transforms	Geometric element manipulations: translate, rotate, scale, skew, and transform origins.	2026-10-12 00:22:55.469715+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: 2D & 3D Spatial Transforms\nPart of the **CSS3** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/1Yfn7lH_8uA", "title": "2D & 3D Spatial Transforms", "creator_name": "CodeHelp - by Babbar", "duration_minutes": 35, "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CodeHelp"}]	[{"type": "assignment", "title": "Build & Test a Working Implementation of 2D & 3D Spatial Transforms", "checklist": ["Implement core logic with validation and boundary checks", "Write tests verifying expected output and edge cases"], "description": "Implement a production-grade module demonstrating the core principles of 2D & 3D Spatial Transforms.", "starterCodeUrl": ""}]	\N	> **Notice:** Practical evaluation questions are not required for this design-focused track. Take visual notes and replicate the UI components in your local environment.	CodeHelp - by Babbar	https://www.youtube.com/@CodeHelp	\N	2D & 3D Spatial Transforms	Geometric element manipulations: translate, rotate, scale, skew, and transform origins.	35	Easy	CSS3 & UI Layouts	12	[]	\N	35	https://youtu.be/1Yfn7lH_8uA	2D & 3D Spatial Transforms
759	Adjacency-Constrained Answer Search: Minimum Days to Make M Bouquets	dsa-minimum-days-to-make-m-bouquets	Determine the minimum bloom days required to make m bouquets of k adjacent flowers in O(N * log(max - min)) time using streak feasibility counting.	2026-10-02 23:53:37.107083+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Adjacency-Constrained Answer Search: Minimum Days to Make M Bouquets\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TXAuxeYBTdg", "title": "Lecture 57: Minimum days to make M bouquets", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/magnetic-force-between-two-balls/", "title": "Magnetic Force Between Two Balls", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Binary search on the answer space using a greedy placement feasibility check."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimized-maximum-of-products-distributed-to-any-store/", "title": "Minimized Maximum of Products Distributed to Any Store", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the minimum maximum quantity distributed per store via binary search."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/allocate-minimum-number-of-pages0937/1", "title": "Allocate Minimum Pages", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use binary search on the answer range to minimize the maximum pages allocated."}]	Module 13: Binary Search on Answer Space & Monotonic Predicate Functions	### 📌 Topic Overview\nYou are given an array `bloomDay` where flower `i` blooms on day `bloomDay[i]`. You need to make `m` bouquets, and each bouquet requires **`k` adjacent bloomed flowers**. Find the **minimum number of days** you must wait to make `m` bouquets, or return `-1` if it is impossible.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **1. The Impossible Case Check (`m * k > n`):**\n  - Making `m` bouquets of `k` flowers each requires `m * k` total flowers.\n  - If `(long long)m * k > arr.size()`, even waiting forever won't give enough flowers—immediately return `-1`!\n- **2. Defining the Tightest Search Range (`[min(bloomDay), max(bloomDay)]`):**\n  - No flower blooms before `min(bloomDay)`, and *every* flower is bloomed by `max(bloomDay)`.\n  - So set `low = *min_element(...)` and `high = *max_element(...)`.\n- **3. Counting Bouquets on Day `mid` (`possible(arr, mid, m, k)`):**\n  - Scan the array while maintaining a streak counter `cnt = 0` of consecutive bloomed flowers (`arr[i] <= day`).\n  - Whenever you hit an unbloomed flower (`arr[i] > day`), add **`noOfB += (cnt / k)`** to your bouquet total and reset `cnt = 0`.\n  - After the loop ends, remember to add the final streak: **`noOfB += (cnt / k)`**.\n  - Return `noOfB >= m`.\n\n### 💻 Minimum Days to Make M Bouquets Blueprint\n```cpp\nbool possible(vector<int> &arr, int day, int m, int k) {\n    int cnt = 0, noOfB = 0;\n    for (int bloom : arr) {\n        if (bloom <= day) {\n            cnt++;\n        } else {\n            noOfB += (cnt / k);\n            cnt = 0;\n        }\n    }\n    noOfB += (cnt / k); // Don't forget the last streak at the end of the array!\n    return noOfB >= m;\n}\n\nint minDays(vector<int> &bloomDay, int m, int k) {\n    long long val = 1LL * m * k;\n    if (val > bloomDay.size()) return -1;\n\n    int low = *min_element(bloomDay.begin(), bloomDay.end());\n    int high = *max_element(bloomDay.begin(), bloomDay.end());\n\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (possible(bloomDay, mid, m, k)) {\n            high = mid - 1; // Possible; try earlier days\n        } else {\n            low = mid + 1;  // Not enough bouquets; wait more days\n        }\n    }\n    return low;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22187	Problems on Ages	aptitude-quant-problems-on-ages	Master chronological age shifts, ratio-based age equations, and calculating average family ages during births and member replacements.	2026-10-07 11:35:53.411147+05:30	### 📌 1. Translation to Algebraic Equations\n* Let present ages be denoted by variables `x`, `y`, `z`.\n* Age `N` years **ago** $\\rightarrow$ `x - N`\n* Age `N` years **from now (hence)** $\\rightarrow$ `x + N`\n* Always construct equations strictly matching the timeline (e.g., if ratio 5 years ago was 2:3, the equation is `(x-5)/(y-5) = 2/3`).\n\n### 💡 2. High-Yield Ratios and Shortcuts\n* **Constant Age Difference:** The absolute difference in age between two people ALWAYS remains the same regardless of past or future! If A is 5 years older than B today, A will be 5 years older than B in 100 years.\n* **The Average Age Trick:**\n  * If the average age of `N` people today is `A`, their total age is `N × A`.\n  * After `k` years, the new total age will be `N × A + N × k` (because EVERY person grows by `k` years!). The new average simply becomes `A + k`.\n  * `k` years ago, the average was `A - k`.\n* **Middle Person Reference:** If A is as much younger than B as he is older than C, then A sits exactly in the middle of their ages. Thus, `A's age = (B's age + C's age) / 2`.\n\n### 💡 3. Family Birth and Replacement Scenarios\n* **Average at Birth:** If the average age of a family of 4 is 36 today, and the youngest child is 6, the sum of their ages *at the time of birth* (6 years ago) requires subtracting `6 × 4 = 24` from the current sum. However, the number of family members *at the time of birth* is considered **3** (since the child is 0). The new average is `(Sum - 24) / 3`.	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tJHl73PBnwY", "type": "youtube", "title": "Problems on Ages - Shortcuts & Tricks for Placement Tests, Job Interviews & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-age-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Past and Present Ratios] Ram is 3 times as old as Sam. 2 years ago, he was 5 times as old as Sam. Present age of Ram?", "options": ["A) 12 years", "B) 15 years", "C) 18 years", "D) 24 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Ram is currently 3 times as old as Sam. Exactly 2 years ago, Ram was 5 times as old as Sam. What is the present age of Ram?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Sam's present age = S. Ram's present age R = 3S. Two years ago, R - 2 = 5(S - 2). Substitute R = 3S: 3S - 2 = 5S - 10 => 2S = 8 => S = 4. Ram's present age = 3 × 4 = 12 years.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-age-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Past and Future Ratios Linked] 12 years ago, P was 3 times Q. After 12 years, ratio of Q to P will be 2:3. Present age of P?", "options": ["A) 36 years", "B) 48 years", "C) 54 years", "D) 60 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly 12 years ago, P was 3 times as old as Q. Exactly 12 years from now, the ratio of Q's age to P's age will be 2 : 3. What is the present age of P?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let present ages be P and Q. 12 years ago: (P - 12) = 3(Q - 12) => P = 3Q - 36 + 12 => P = 3Q - 24. 12 years from now: (Q + 12) / (P + 12) = 2 / 3. Cross multiply: 3Q + 36 = 2P + 24. Substitute P: 3Q + 36 = 2(3Q - 24) + 24 => 3Q + 36 = 6Q - 48 + 24 => 3Q + 36 = 6Q - 24 => 3Q = 60 => Q = 20. Present age of P = 3(20) - 24 = 36.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-age-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Average Middle Age Reference] Rohan is as much younger than Ajay as he is older than Meena. Sum of ages of Ajay and Meena is 108. Rohan's age?", "options": ["A) 50 years", "B) 54 years", "C) 58 years", "D) Cannot be determined"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Rohan is as much younger than Ajay as he is older than Meena. If the sum of the ages of Ajay and Meena is exactly 108 years, how old is Rohan?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Since Rohan is perfectly in the middle, the age difference is the same: Ajay - Rohan = Rohan - Meena. Rearranging gives: 2 × Rohan = Ajay + Meena. We are given Ajay + Meena = 108. So, 2 × Rohan = 108 => Rohan = 54 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-age-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Three Variable Ratios] Rohan's age is 5 times Ajay's and 7/18 of Meena's. Sum of all three is 132. How much younger is Ajay than Meena?", "options": ["A) 75 years", "B) 80 years", "C) 83 years", "D) 85 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Rohan's age is exactly 5 times Ajay's age, and simultaneously 7/18 of Meena's age. The sum of the ages of all three of them is 132 years. How much younger is Ajay than Meena?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let Ajay's age = A. Rohan's age R = 5A. We know R = (7/18) × Meena's age (M) => M = (18/7) × R = (18/7) × 5A = 90A/7. Sum: A + 5A + 90A/7 = 132 => 6A + 90A/7 = 132. Multiply by 7: 42A + 90A = 924 => 132A = 924 => A = 7. Thus, Ajay = 7, Rohan = 35, Meena = (90×7)/7 = 90. Difference between Meena and Ajay = 90 - 7 = 83 years.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-age-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Overlapping Average Age Deductions] Average age of Ram and Shyam is 65. Average age of Ram, Shyam, John is 53. John's age?", "options": ["A) 27 years", "B) 29 years", "C) 31 years", "D) 33 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The average age of Ram and Shyam is 65 years. The average age of Ram, Shyam, and John together is 53 years. What is the exact age of John?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Sum of Ram and Shyam = 2 × 65 = 130. Sum of Ram, Shyam, and John = 3 × 53 = 159. John's age is the difference in the sums = 159 - 130 = 29 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-age-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Group Averages Subtracting Sets] Average age of 10 students and teacher is 15. First 7 students is 15. Last 3 is 11. Teacher's age?", "options": ["A) 25 years", "B) 27 years", "C) 30 years", "D) 32 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The average age of 10 students and their teacher is exactly 15 years. The average age of the first 7 students is 15 years, and that of the remaining 3 students is exactly 11 years. What is the teacher's age?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total age of 11 people (10 students + 1 teacher) = 11 × 15 = 165. Total age of first 7 students = 7 × 15 = 105. Total age of remaining 3 students = 3 × 11 = 33. Total age of 10 students = 105 + 33 = 138. Teacher's age = 165 - 138 = 27 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-age-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Average Age at Time of Birth] Average age of 4 friends is 36. Youngest is 6. Average age at the time of birth of youngest?", "options": ["A) 30 years", "B) 35 years", "C) 40 years", "D) 45 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The present average age of a group of 4 friends is 36 years. The youngest friend among them is exactly 6 years old. What was the average age of the group exactly at the time of the birth of the youngest friend?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Total present age of 4 friends = 4 × 36 = 144 years. Exactly 6 years ago, each of the 4 friends was 6 years younger, so the total age decreases by 4 × 6 = 24 years. Total age 6 years ago = 144 - 24 = 120 years. At the exact time of birth, the youngest friend was 0 years old, meaning only the remaining 3 friends contribute to the meaningful average. The average of the remaining 3 friends = 120 / 3 = 40 years.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-age-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Age Constant Average with New Birth] Average age of family of 4 was 19 years 4 years back. Birth of child kept average same today. Child's age?", "options": ["A) 2 years", "B) 3 years", "C) 4 years", "D) 5 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The average age of a family of 4 members was 19 years exactly 4 years back. A new child was born in the meantime, which magically kept the average age of the family exactly the same today. How old is the child today?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Total age of the 4 members 4 years ago = 4 × 19 = 76. Today, those 4 members have all aged 4 years, adding 4 × 4 = 16 years. Sum of the 4 original members today = 76 + 16 = 92. Today, the family has 5 members and their average is still 19! Total age today = 5 × 19 = 95. Therefore, the child's age is 95 - 92 = 3 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-age-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Future Ratio Shift] Ratio of ages of A and B is 3:5. 10 years hence, ratio will be 5:7. Present age of B?", "options": ["A) 20 years", "B) 25 years", "C) 30 years", "D) 35 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The present ages of A and B are in the ratio 3 : 5. Exactly 10 years from now, the ratio of their ages will become 5 : 7. What is the present age of B?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let present ages be 3x and 5x. After 10 years, ages are (3x + 10) and (5x + 10). Ratio is (3x + 10) / (5x + 10) = 5 / 7. Cross multiply: 7(3x + 10) = 5(5x + 10) => 21x + 70 = 25x + 50 => 4x = 20 => x = 5. Present age of B = 5x = 5(5) = 25 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-age-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Three Variable Algebraic Age Relation] A is 2 years older than B who is twice as old as C. If total of ages of A, B and C is 27, how old is B?", "options": ["A) 8 years", "B) 10 years", "C) 12 years", "D) 14 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A is exactly 2 years older than B, and B is exactly twice as old as C. If the total sum of the present ages of A, B, and C is 27 years, how old is B?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let C's age be x. Then B's age is 2x. A's age is 2x + 2. Summing them up: (2x + 2) + 2x + x = 27 => 5x + 2 = 27 => 5x = 25 => x = 5. Thus, B's age = 2x = 10 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-age-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Past Relationship to Present Ratio] Ten years ago, A was half of B in age. If ratio of their present ages is 3:4, total of present ages?", "options": ["A) 35 years", "B) 40 years", "C) 45 years", "D) 50 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Ten years ago, A was exactly half of B in age. If the ratio of their present ages is exactly 3 : 4, what is the total sum of their present ages?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let present ages be 3x and 4x. Ten years ago, their ages were (3x - 10) and (4x - 10). We are given (3x - 10) = (1/2)(4x - 10) => 6x - 20 = 4x - 10 => 2x = 10 => x = 5. Present ages are 15 and 20. Total = 15 + 20 = 35 years.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-age-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Quadratic Generation from Product] Sum of ages of a father and his son is 45 years. 5 years ago, product of their ages was 34. Father's age?", "options": ["A) 37 years", "B) 39 years", "C) 41 years", "D) 43 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The sum of the present ages of a father and his son is exactly 45 years. Exactly 5 years ago, the product of their ages was exactly 34. What is the present age of the father?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let father = F and son = S. F + S = 45. Five years ago: (F - 5)(S - 5) = 34. Substitute S = 45 - F into the product: (F - 5)(45 - F - 5) = 34 => (F - 5)(40 - F) = 34 => 40F - F^2 - 200 + 5F = 34 => F^2 - 45F + 234 = 0. Factoring: (F - 39)(F - 6) = 0. Since the father must be older, F = 39 (and S = 6).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-age-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Past Ratio to Future Ratio] 6 years ago, ratio of ages of Kunal and Sagar was 6:5. 4 years hence, ratio will be 11:10. Sagar's present age?", "options": ["A) 16 years", "B) 18 years", "C) 20 years", "D) 22 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly 6 years ago, the ratio of the ages of Kunal and Sagar was 6 : 5. Exactly 4 years from now, the ratio of their ages will be 11 : 10. What is Sagar's present age?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let ages 6 years ago be 6x and 5x. Present ages are (6x + 6) and (5x + 6). 4 years from now, ages will be (6x + 10) and (5x + 10). Ratio is (6x + 10) / (5x + 10) = 11 / 10. Cross multiply: 60x + 100 = 55x + 110 => 5x = 10 => x = 2. Sagar's present age = 5x + 6 = 10 + 6 = 16 years.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-age-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Ratio and Product Combination] Ratio of father to son is 7:3. Product of their ages is 756. Ratio after 6 years?", "options": ["A) 5 : 2", "B) 2 : 1", "C) 3 : 1", "D) 5 : 3"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The present ratio of a father's age to his son's age is 7 : 3. The product of their present ages is exactly 756. What will be the ratio of their ages exactly 6 years from now?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let ages be 7x and 3x. Product = (7x)(3x) = 21x^2 = 756 => x^2 = 36 => x = 6. Present ages: Father = 42, Son = 18. After 6 years, Father = 48, Son = 24. Ratio = 48 : 24 = 2 : 1.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-age-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Three Person Chain Referencing] Sneha's age is 1/6th of her father's age. Father's age will be twice Vimal's age after 10 years. If Vimal's 8th birthday was 2 years ago, Sneha's age?", "options": ["A) 4 years", "B) 5 years", "C) 6 years", "D) 8 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Sneha's age is exactly 1/6th of her father's age. Her father's age will be exactly twice Vimal's age after 10 years. If Vimal celebrated his 8th birthday exactly 2 years ago, what is Sneha's present age?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Vimal was 8 years old 2 years ago, so Vimal's present age is 10. 2) After 10 years, Vimal will be 20. 3) Father's age after 10 years will be twice Vimal's = 2 × 20 = 40. 4) Therefore, Father's present age is 40 - 10 = 30. 5) Sneha's present age is 1/6 of her father's = 30 / 6 = 5 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-age-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Percentage Based Timelines] A man's age is 125% of what it was 10 years ago, but 83 1/3% of what it will be after 10 years. His present age?", "options": ["A) 40 years", "B) 45 years", "C) 50 years", "D) 60 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man's present age is exactly 125% of what it was 10 years ago, and exactly 83 1/3% of what it will be 10 years from now. What is his present age?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let present age be M. Equation 1: M = 1.25(M - 10) => M = 1.25M - 12.5 => 0.25M = 12.5 => M = 50. Let's verify Equation 2: 83 1/3% = 5/6. M = (5/6)(M + 10) => 6M = 5M + 50 => M = 50. Both equations perfectly yield 50 years.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-age-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Class Average Replacements] Average age of a class of 30 students is 15 years. A student aged 20 leaves, and two new students with age difference 5 join. Average age remains 15. Age of younger new student?", "options": ["A) 12 years", "B) 15 years", "C) 17 years", "D) 20 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The average age of a class of 30 students is 15 years. A 20-year-old student leaves the class, and two new students join, whose absolute age difference is 5 years. If the average age of the class remains 15 years even after this swap, what is the age of the younger new student?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Original total age = 30 × 15 = 450. When 1 student leaves and 2 join, the new class size is 31. The new average is 15, so new total age = 31 × 15 = 465. Let the ages of the two new students be x and x+5. 450 - 20 (leaving student) + x + x + 5 = 465 => 435 + 2x = 465 => 2x = 30 => x = 15. The younger student is 15 years old.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-age-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Sum Constraint on Ratios] The present ages of three persons are in proportions 4:7:9. Eight years ago, sum of their ages was 56. Present ages?", "options": ["A) 12, 21, 27", "B) 16, 28, 36", "C) 20, 35, 45", "D) 24, 42, 54"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The present ages of three persons are strictly in the proportion 4 : 7 : 9. Exactly eight years ago, the total sum of their ages was exactly 56. What are their present ages?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let present ages be 4x, 7x, and 9x. Eight years ago, their ages were (4x-8), (7x-8), and (9x-8). Sum: 4x-8 + 7x-8 + 9x-8 = 56 => 20x - 24 = 56 => 20x = 80 => x = 4. Present ages are 4(4)=16, 7(4)=28, and 9(4)=36.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-age-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Father's Classic Riddle] A father says to his son, \\"I was as old as you are at present at the time of your birth.\\" If father's age is 38 now, son's age 5 years back?", "options": ["A) 11 years", "B) 14 years", "C) 17 years", "D) 19 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A father tells his son, \\"I was as old as you are at present exactly at the time of your birth.\\" If the father's present age is exactly 38 years, what was the son's age exactly 5 years ago?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Son's present age be S. At the time of the son's birth, the father's age was (Father's present age - S). The father states this equaled the son's present age S. So, F - S = S => F = 2S. Given F = 38, S = 19. The son's age 5 years ago was 19 - 5 = 14 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-age-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Family of Four Complex Relationships] Father is 3 times daughter, mother is twice the son. Wife is 9 years younger to husband. Son is 7 years older to daughter. Age of mother?", "options": ["A) 45 years", "B) 50 years", "C) 55 years", "D) 60 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a family, a couple has a son and a daughter. The age of the father is 3 times that of his daughter, and the age of the mother is twice that of her son. The wife is exactly 9 years younger than her husband. The son is exactly 7 years older than his sister (the daughter). What is the exact age of the mother?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let Daughter's age = D. Then Son's age = D + 7. Father's age = 3D. Mother's age = 2(Son) = 2(D + 7) = 2D + 14. We are also given Father - Mother = 9. So, 3D - (2D + 14) = 9 => D - 14 = 9 => D = 23. Thus, Daughter = 23, Son = 30. Father = 3(23) = 69. Mother = 2(30) = 60 years. (Check: 69 - 60 = 9). The mother is 60.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. Translation to Algebraic Equations\n* Let present ages be denoted by variables `x`, `y`, `z`.\n* Age `N` years **ago** $\\rightarrow$ `x - N`\n* Age `N` years **from now (hence)** $\\rightarrow$ `x + N`\n* Always construct equations strictly matching the timeline (e.g., if ratio 5 years ago was 2:3, the equation is `(x-5)/(y-5) = 2/3`).\n\n### 💡 2. High-Yield Ratios and Shortcuts\n* **Constant Age Difference:** The absolute difference in age between two people ALWAYS remains the same regardless of past or future! If A is 5 years older than B today, A will be 5 years older than B in 100 years.\n* **The Average Age Trick:**\n  * If the average age of `N` people today is `A`, their total age is `N × A`.\n  * After `k` years, the new total age will be `N × A + N × k` (because EVERY person grows by `k` years!). The new average simply becomes `A + k`.\n  * `k` years ago, the average was `A - k`.\n* **Middle Person Reference:** If A is as much younger than B as he is older than C, then A sits exactly in the middle of their ages. Thus, `A's age = (B's age + C's age) / 2`.\n\n### 💡 3. Family Birth and Replacement Scenarios\n* **Average at Birth:** If the average age of a family of 4 is 36 today, and the youngest child is 6, the sum of their ages *at the time of birth* (6 years ago) requires subtracting `6 × 4 = 24` from the current sum. However, the number of family members *at the time of birth* is considered **3** (since the child is 0). The new average is `(Sum - 24) / 3`.	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Problems on Ages	Master chronological age shifts, ratio-based age equations, and calculating average family ages during births and member replacements.	27	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	21	[{"id": "apt-age-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Past and Present Ratios] Ram is 3 times as old as Sam. 2 years ago, he was 5 times as old as Sam. Present age of Ram?", "options": ["A) 12 years", "B) 15 years", "C) 18 years", "D) 24 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Ram is currently 3 times as old as Sam. Exactly 2 years ago, Ram was 5 times as old as Sam. What is the present age of Ram?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Sam's present age = S. Ram's present age R = 3S. Two years ago, R - 2 = 5(S - 2). Substitute R = 3S: 3S - 2 = 5S - 10 => 2S = 8 => S = 4. Ram's present age = 3 × 4 = 12 years.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-age-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Past and Future Ratios Linked] 12 years ago, P was 3 times Q. After 12 years, ratio of Q to P will be 2:3. Present age of P?", "options": ["A) 36 years", "B) 48 years", "C) 54 years", "D) 60 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly 12 years ago, P was 3 times as old as Q. Exactly 12 years from now, the ratio of Q's age to P's age will be 2 : 3. What is the present age of P?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let present ages be P and Q. 12 years ago: (P - 12) = 3(Q - 12) => P = 3Q - 36 + 12 => P = 3Q - 24. 12 years from now: (Q + 12) / (P + 12) = 2 / 3. Cross multiply: 3Q + 36 = 2P + 24. Substitute P: 3Q + 36 = 2(3Q - 24) + 24 => 3Q + 36 = 6Q - 48 + 24 => 3Q + 36 = 6Q - 24 => 3Q = 60 => Q = 20. Present age of P = 3(20) - 24 = 36.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-age-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Average Middle Age Reference] Rohan is as much younger than Ajay as he is older than Meena. Sum of ages of Ajay and Meena is 108. Rohan's age?", "options": ["A) 50 years", "B) 54 years", "C) 58 years", "D) Cannot be determined"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Rohan is as much younger than Ajay as he is older than Meena. If the sum of the ages of Ajay and Meena is exactly 108 years, how old is Rohan?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Since Rohan is perfectly in the middle, the age difference is the same: Ajay - Rohan = Rohan - Meena. Rearranging gives: 2 × Rohan = Ajay + Meena. We are given Ajay + Meena = 108. So, 2 × Rohan = 108 => Rohan = 54 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-age-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Three Variable Ratios] Rohan's age is 5 times Ajay's and 7/18 of Meena's. Sum of all three is 132. How much younger is Ajay than Meena?", "options": ["A) 75 years", "B) 80 years", "C) 83 years", "D) 85 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Rohan's age is exactly 5 times Ajay's age, and simultaneously 7/18 of Meena's age. The sum of the ages of all three of them is 132 years. How much younger is Ajay than Meena?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let Ajay's age = A. Rohan's age R = 5A. We know R = (7/18) × Meena's age (M) => M = (18/7) × R = (18/7) × 5A = 90A/7. Sum: A + 5A + 90A/7 = 132 => 6A + 90A/7 = 132. Multiply by 7: 42A + 90A = 924 => 132A = 924 => A = 7. Thus, Ajay = 7, Rohan = 35, Meena = (90×7)/7 = 90. Difference between Meena and Ajay = 90 - 7 = 83 years.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-age-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Overlapping Average Age Deductions] Average age of Ram and Shyam is 65. Average age of Ram, Shyam, John is 53. John's age?", "options": ["A) 27 years", "B) 29 years", "C) 31 years", "D) 33 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The average age of Ram and Shyam is 65 years. The average age of Ram, Shyam, and John together is 53 years. What is the exact age of John?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Sum of Ram and Shyam = 2 × 65 = 130. Sum of Ram, Shyam, and John = 3 × 53 = 159. John's age is the difference in the sums = 159 - 130 = 29 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-age-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Group Averages Subtracting Sets] Average age of 10 students and teacher is 15. First 7 students is 15. Last 3 is 11. Teacher's age?", "options": ["A) 25 years", "B) 27 years", "C) 30 years", "D) 32 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The average age of 10 students and their teacher is exactly 15 years. The average age of the first 7 students is 15 years, and that of the remaining 3 students is exactly 11 years. What is the teacher's age?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total age of 11 people (10 students + 1 teacher) = 11 × 15 = 165. Total age of first 7 students = 7 × 15 = 105. Total age of remaining 3 students = 3 × 11 = 33. Total age of 10 students = 105 + 33 = 138. Teacher's age = 165 - 138 = 27 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-age-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Average Age at Time of Birth] Average age of 4 friends is 36. Youngest is 6. Average age at the time of birth of youngest?", "options": ["A) 30 years", "B) 35 years", "C) 40 years", "D) 45 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The present average age of a group of 4 friends is 36 years. The youngest friend among them is exactly 6 years old. What was the average age of the group exactly at the time of the birth of the youngest friend?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Total present age of 4 friends = 4 × 36 = 144 years. Exactly 6 years ago, each of the 4 friends was 6 years younger, so the total age decreases by 4 × 6 = 24 years. Total age 6 years ago = 144 - 24 = 120 years. At the exact time of birth, the youngest friend was 0 years old, meaning only the remaining 3 friends contribute to the meaningful average. The average of the remaining 3 friends = 120 / 3 = 40 years.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-age-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Age Constant Average with New Birth] Average age of family of 4 was 19 years 4 years back. Birth of child kept average same today. Child's age?", "options": ["A) 2 years", "B) 3 years", "C) 4 years", "D) 5 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The average age of a family of 4 members was 19 years exactly 4 years back. A new child was born in the meantime, which magically kept the average age of the family exactly the same today. How old is the child today?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Total age of the 4 members 4 years ago = 4 × 19 = 76. Today, those 4 members have all aged 4 years, adding 4 × 4 = 16 years. Sum of the 4 original members today = 76 + 16 = 92. Today, the family has 5 members and their average is still 19! Total age today = 5 × 19 = 95. Therefore, the child's age is 95 - 92 = 3 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-age-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Future Ratio Shift] Ratio of ages of A and B is 3:5. 10 years hence, ratio will be 5:7. Present age of B?", "options": ["A) 20 years", "B) 25 years", "C) 30 years", "D) 35 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The present ages of A and B are in the ratio 3 : 5. Exactly 10 years from now, the ratio of their ages will become 5 : 7. What is the present age of B?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let present ages be 3x and 5x. After 10 years, ages are (3x + 10) and (5x + 10). Ratio is (3x + 10) / (5x + 10) = 5 / 7. Cross multiply: 7(3x + 10) = 5(5x + 10) => 21x + 70 = 25x + 50 => 4x = 20 => x = 5. Present age of B = 5x = 5(5) = 25 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-age-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Three Variable Algebraic Age Relation] A is 2 years older than B who is twice as old as C. If total of ages of A, B and C is 27, how old is B?", "options": ["A) 8 years", "B) 10 years", "C) 12 years", "D) 14 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A is exactly 2 years older than B, and B is exactly twice as old as C. If the total sum of the present ages of A, B, and C is 27 years, how old is B?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let C's age be x. Then B's age is 2x. A's age is 2x + 2. Summing them up: (2x + 2) + 2x + x = 27 => 5x + 2 = 27 => 5x = 25 => x = 5. Thus, B's age = 2x = 10 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-age-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Past Relationship to Present Ratio] Ten years ago, A was half of B in age. If ratio of their present ages is 3:4, total of present ages?", "options": ["A) 35 years", "B) 40 years", "C) 45 years", "D) 50 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Ten years ago, A was exactly half of B in age. If the ratio of their present ages is exactly 3 : 4, what is the total sum of their present ages?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let present ages be 3x and 4x. Ten years ago, their ages were (3x - 10) and (4x - 10). We are given (3x - 10) = (1/2)(4x - 10) => 6x - 20 = 4x - 10 => 2x = 10 => x = 5. Present ages are 15 and 20. Total = 15 + 20 = 35 years.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-age-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Quadratic Generation from Product] Sum of ages of a father and his son is 45 years. 5 years ago, product of their ages was 34. Father's age?", "options": ["A) 37 years", "B) 39 years", "C) 41 years", "D) 43 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The sum of the present ages of a father and his son is exactly 45 years. Exactly 5 years ago, the product of their ages was exactly 34. What is the present age of the father?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let father = F and son = S. F + S = 45. Five years ago: (F - 5)(S - 5) = 34. Substitute S = 45 - F into the product: (F - 5)(45 - F - 5) = 34 => (F - 5)(40 - F) = 34 => 40F - F^2 - 200 + 5F = 34 => F^2 - 45F + 234 = 0. Factoring: (F - 39)(F - 6) = 0. Since the father must be older, F = 39 (and S = 6).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-age-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Past Ratio to Future Ratio] 6 years ago, ratio of ages of Kunal and Sagar was 6:5. 4 years hence, ratio will be 11:10. Sagar's present age?", "options": ["A) 16 years", "B) 18 years", "C) 20 years", "D) 22 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly 6 years ago, the ratio of the ages of Kunal and Sagar was 6 : 5. Exactly 4 years from now, the ratio of their ages will be 11 : 10. What is Sagar's present age?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let ages 6 years ago be 6x and 5x. Present ages are (6x + 6) and (5x + 6). 4 years from now, ages will be (6x + 10) and (5x + 10). Ratio is (6x + 10) / (5x + 10) = 11 / 10. Cross multiply: 60x + 100 = 55x + 110 => 5x = 10 => x = 2. Sagar's present age = 5x + 6 = 10 + 6 = 16 years.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-age-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Ratio and Product Combination] Ratio of father to son is 7:3. Product of their ages is 756. Ratio after 6 years?", "options": ["A) 5 : 2", "B) 2 : 1", "C) 3 : 1", "D) 5 : 3"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The present ratio of a father's age to his son's age is 7 : 3. The product of their present ages is exactly 756. What will be the ratio of their ages exactly 6 years from now?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let ages be 7x and 3x. Product = (7x)(3x) = 21x^2 = 756 => x^2 = 36 => x = 6. Present ages: Father = 42, Son = 18. After 6 years, Father = 48, Son = 24. Ratio = 48 : 24 = 2 : 1.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-age-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Three Person Chain Referencing] Sneha's age is 1/6th of her father's age. Father's age will be twice Vimal's age after 10 years. If Vimal's 8th birthday was 2 years ago, Sneha's age?", "options": ["A) 4 years", "B) 5 years", "C) 6 years", "D) 8 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Sneha's age is exactly 1/6th of her father's age. Her father's age will be exactly twice Vimal's age after 10 years. If Vimal celebrated his 8th birthday exactly 2 years ago, what is Sneha's present age?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Vimal was 8 years old 2 years ago, so Vimal's present age is 10. 2) After 10 years, Vimal will be 20. 3) Father's age after 10 years will be twice Vimal's = 2 × 20 = 40. 4) Therefore, Father's present age is 40 - 10 = 30. 5) Sneha's present age is 1/6 of her father's = 30 / 6 = 5 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-age-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Percentage Based Timelines] A man's age is 125% of what it was 10 years ago, but 83 1/3% of what it will be after 10 years. His present age?", "options": ["A) 40 years", "B) 45 years", "C) 50 years", "D) 60 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man's present age is exactly 125% of what it was 10 years ago, and exactly 83 1/3% of what it will be 10 years from now. What is his present age?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let present age be M. Equation 1: M = 1.25(M - 10) => M = 1.25M - 12.5 => 0.25M = 12.5 => M = 50. Let's verify Equation 2: 83 1/3% = 5/6. M = (5/6)(M + 10) => 6M = 5M + 50 => M = 50. Both equations perfectly yield 50 years.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-age-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Class Average Replacements] Average age of a class of 30 students is 15 years. A student aged 20 leaves, and two new students with age difference 5 join. Average age remains 15. Age of younger new student?", "options": ["A) 12 years", "B) 15 years", "C) 17 years", "D) 20 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The average age of a class of 30 students is 15 years. A 20-year-old student leaves the class, and two new students join, whose absolute age difference is 5 years. If the average age of the class remains 15 years even after this swap, what is the age of the younger new student?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Original total age = 30 × 15 = 450. When 1 student leaves and 2 join, the new class size is 31. The new average is 15, so new total age = 31 × 15 = 465. Let the ages of the two new students be x and x+5. 450 - 20 (leaving student) + x + x + 5 = 465 => 435 + 2x = 465 => 2x = 30 => x = 15. The younger student is 15 years old.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-age-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Sum Constraint on Ratios] The present ages of three persons are in proportions 4:7:9. Eight years ago, sum of their ages was 56. Present ages?", "options": ["A) 12, 21, 27", "B) 16, 28, 36", "C) 20, 35, 45", "D) 24, 42, 54"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The present ages of three persons are strictly in the proportion 4 : 7 : 9. Exactly eight years ago, the total sum of their ages was exactly 56. What are their present ages?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let present ages be 4x, 7x, and 9x. Eight years ago, their ages were (4x-8), (7x-8), and (9x-8). Sum: 4x-8 + 7x-8 + 9x-8 = 56 => 20x - 24 = 56 => 20x = 80 => x = 4. Present ages are 4(4)=16, 7(4)=28, and 9(4)=36.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-age-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Father's Classic Riddle] A father says to his son, \\"I was as old as you are at present at the time of your birth.\\" If father's age is 38 now, son's age 5 years back?", "options": ["A) 11 years", "B) 14 years", "C) 17 years", "D) 19 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A father tells his son, \\"I was as old as you are at present exactly at the time of your birth.\\" If the father's present age is exactly 38 years, what was the son's age exactly 5 years ago?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Son's present age be S. At the time of the son's birth, the father's age was (Father's present age - S). The father states this equaled the son's present age S. So, F - S = S => F = 2S. Given F = 38, S = 19. The son's age 5 years ago was 19 - 5 = 14 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-age-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Family of Four Complex Relationships] Father is 3 times daughter, mother is twice the son. Wife is 9 years younger to husband. Son is 7 years older to daughter. Age of mother?", "options": ["A) 45 years", "B) 50 years", "C) 55 years", "D) 60 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a family, a couple has a son and a daughter. The age of the father is 3 times that of his daughter, and the age of the mother is twice that of her son. The wife is exactly 9 years younger than her husband. The son is exactly 7 years older than his sister (the daughter). What is the exact age of the mother?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let Daughter's age = D. Then Son's age = D + 7. Father's age = 3D. Mother's age = 2(Son) = 2(D + 7) = 2D + 14. We are also given Father - Mother = 9. So, 3D - (2D + 14) = 9 => D - 14 = 9 => D = 23. Thus, Daughter = 23, Son = 30. Father = 3(23) = 69. Mother = 2(30) = 60 years. (Check: 69 - 60 = 9). The mother is 60.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
