ogether (e.g., `<label htmlFor={id}>` and `<input id={id}>`) for accessibility", "Creating user session IDs", "Setting dynamic CSS class names"], "question": "What is the primary, recommended use case for `useId`?", "explanation": "It is specifically designed for generating unique accessible IDs for ARIA attributes and form labels, ensuring no ID collisions occur if a component is rendered multiple times.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "Linking HTML form elements together (e.g., `<label htmlFor={id}>` and `<input id={id}>`) for accessibility"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["Because it requires asynchronous execution", "Because the ID string is too long", "Because keys must map to your data's unique identity, while `useId` generates a component tree structural ID", "Because it is incompatible with arrays"], "question": "Why should `useId` NEVER be used to generate keys for a mapped list?", "explanation": "Using useId for map keys is an anti-pattern. Keys should come from the data (like `item.database_id`) so React can accurately track item reordering or deletions.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "Because keys must map to your data's unique identity, while `useId` generates a component tree structural ID"}]	\N	> **Architectural Deep Dive:** This lecture covers the strict technical nuances, reconciliation behavior, and performance implications of using useId in production environments. Pay special attention to concurrent rendering behaviors in React 18+.	Web Dev Simplified	https://www.youtube.com/@WebDevSimplified	\N	The Easiest React Hook (useId)	Deep dive architectural lecture focusing on the mechanics, use cases, and anti-patterns of the useId hook.	15	Easy	Core Hooks & API	14	[]	\N	15	https://youtu.be/_vwCKV7f_eA	The Easiest React Hook (useId)
2301	Branch Split Point: Lowest Common Ancestor (LCA) in a Binary Search Tree	dsa-lowest-common-ancestor-in-bst	Find the lowest common ancestor of two nodes in a BST in O(H) time and O(1) space by tracking the branch split point.	2026-10-03 01:26:48.311347+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Branch Split Point: Lowest Common Ancestor (LCA) in a Binary Search Tree\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cX_kPV_foZc", "title": "Lecture 119: LCA in Binary Search Tree", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-search-tree/", "title": "Lowest Common Ancestor of a Binary Search Tree", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the LCA of two nodes in a BST in O(H) time without searching both subtrees."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-tree/", "title": "Lowest Common Ancestor of a Binary Tree", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Contrast BST branch splitting with general binary tree divergence checks."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/lowest-common-ancestor-in-a-bst/1", "title": "Lowest Common Ancestor in a BST", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Find the lowest common ancestor node in a BST."}]	Module 19: Binary Search Trees: Concepts, Validations & Structural Mutations	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a BST and two nodes `p` and `q`, find their **Lowest Common Ancestor (LCA)**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Split Point Invariant:**\n  - In a general binary tree, finding LCA requires searching both subtrees (`O(N)`).\n  - In a BST, keys guide the search:\n    1. If both `p->val < curr->val` and `q->val < curr->val`: Both targets lie in the left subtree—move `curr = curr->left`.\n    2. If both `p->val > curr->val` and `q->val > curr->val`: Both targets lie in the right subtree—move `curr = curr->right`.\n    3. **The Split Point:** If one target is on the left and the other is on the right, or if `curr == p` or `curr == q`, the paths to `p` and `q` diverge here! **`curr` is the LCA**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(H)` Time and `O(1)` Space.\n\n### 💻 LCA in BST Blueprint\n```cpp\nTreeNode* lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* p, TreeNode* q) {\n    TreeNode* curr = root;\n    while (curr != nullptr) {\n        if (p->val < curr->val && q->val < curr->val) {\n            curr = curr->left;\n        } else if (p->val > curr->val && q->val > curr->val) {\n            curr = curr->right;\n        } else {\n            return curr; // First point of divergence is the LCA!\n        }\n    }\n    return nullptr;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Using General Tree LCA (O(N)):** General tree LCA searches both left and right branches blindly. In a BST interview, you are expected to follow the split point in `O(H)` time and `O(1)` space.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
2295	Lower-Bound Invariant Search: Ceil in a Binary Search Tree	dsa-ceil-in-a-binary-search-tree	Find the smallest node value greater than or equal to a given key in a BST in O(H) time and O(1) space.	2026-10-03 01:26:48.292306+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Lower-Bound Invariant Search: Ceil in a Binary Search Tree\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KSsk8AhdOZA", "title": "Lecture 113: Ceil in a Binary Search Tree", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/implementing-ceil-in-bst/1", "title": "Ceil in BST", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the ceil of a given key X in a binary search tree."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/closest-binary-search-tree-value/", "title": "Closest Binary Search Tree Value", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Compare ceil and floor values to find the closest element to target."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/floor-in-bst/1", "title": "Floor in BST", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Contrast ceil branch decisions with floor branch decisions."}]	Module 19: Binary Search Trees: Concepts, Validations & Structural Mutations	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThe **Ceil** of a key `X` in a BST is the **smallest node value strictly greater than or equal to `X`** (`val >= X`). If no such element exists, return `-1`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Branch Elimination Strategy:**\n  - Maintain `ceilVal = -1`. Start at `curr = root`.\n  - While `curr != nullptr`:\n    1. If `curr->val == key`: Exact match found! Return `curr->val`.\n    2. If `curr->val > key`: `curr->val` is a valid candidate for Ceil! Record `ceilVal = curr->val`, and move **left** (`curr = curr->left`) to search for an even smaller candidate that is still `>= key`.\n    3. If `curr->val < key`: `curr->val` is too small to be a Ceil—move **right** (`curr = curr->right`) to find larger values.\n- **Complexity:** `O(H)` Time and `O(1)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Ceil in BST Blueprint\n```cpp\nint findCeil(TreeNode* root, int key) {\n    int ceilVal = -1;\n    TreeNode* curr = root;\n\n    while (curr != nullptr) {\n        if (curr->val == key) {\n            return curr->val;\n        }\n        if (curr->val > key) {\n            ceilVal = curr->val; // Potential ceil candidate\n            curr = curr->left;   // Search for smaller valid candidate\n        } else {\n            curr = curr->right;  // Key is larger; search right\n        }\n    }\n    return ceilVal;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Moving Right When curr->val > key:** When `curr->val > key`, going right will only make the value larger! Move left to minimize the candidate.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
