7021	Reverse-String LCS Reduction: Longest Palindromic Subsequence (LPS)	dsa-longest-palindromic-subsequence	Find the longest palindromic subsequence by reducing LPS(s) directly to LCS(s, reverse(s)) in O(N^2) time and O(N) space.	2026-10-03 11:31:13.756605+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Reverse-String LCS Reduction: Longest Palindromic Subsequence (LPS)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6i_T5kkfv4A", "title": "Lecture 209: Longest Palindromic Subsequence (DP 28)", "duration": "15m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-palindromic-subsequence/", "title": "Longest Palindromic Subsequence (LC 516)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given a string s, find the longest palindromic subsequence's length in s."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/longest-palindromic-subsequence-1612327878/1", "title": "Longest Palindromic Subsequence", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the length of the longest palindromic subsequence of a given string."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-insertion-steps-to-make-a-string-palindrome/", "title": "Minimum Insertion Steps to Make a String Palindrome (LC 1312)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Use the Longest Palindromic Subsequence length to compute minimum palindrome insertions."}]	Module 31: Dynamic Programming on Strings: LCS, Substrings & Palindromic Subsequences	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a string `s`, find the length of its **Longest Palindromic Subsequence (LPS)**—the longest subsequence of `s` that reads the exact same forward and backward.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Reverse-String Insight (`LPS(s) == LCS(s, reverse(s))`):**\n  - What is a palindrome? A sequence that reads the same from left-to-right (in `s`) as it does from right-to-left (which is left-to-right in **`t = reverse(s)`**!).\n  - Therefore, any subsequence that is common to both `s` and `reverse(s)` is a palindromic subsequence, and the **Longest Palindromic Subsequence of `s` is simply the Longest Common Subsequence of `s` and `reverse(s)`**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N²)` Time and `O(N)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Longest Palindromic Subsequence Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint longestCommonSubsequence(string &s1, string &s2) {\n    int n = s1.size(), m = s2.size();\n    vector<int> prev(m + 1, 0), cur(m + 1, 0);\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {\n            if (s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]) {\n                cur[j] = 1 + prev[j - 1];\n            } else {\n                cur[j] = max(prev[j], cur[j - 1]);\n            }\n        }\n        prev = cur;\n    }\n    return prev[m];\n}\n\nint longestPalindromeSubseq(string s) {\n    string t = s;\n    reverse(t.begin(), t.end());\n    return longestCommonSubsequence(s, t);\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Confusing Longest Palindromic Subsequence With Longest Palindromic Substring:** `LCS(s, reverse(s))` works 100% for **Subsequence**, but does *not* work unconditionally for *Substring* (because a non-palindromic substring can appear reversed elsewhere in `s`). For Palindromic **Subsequence**, `LCS(s, reverse(s))` is always exact!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
5803	Cut-Vertex Discovery: Articulation Points in a Graph Using Tarjan's DFS Timestamps	dsa-articulation-point-in-graph-tarjans-algorithm	Detect articulation points whose removal disconnects the graph using DFS discovery times, non-root condition low[it] >= tin[node], and root child counting.	2026-10-03 11:17:17.077942+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Cut-Vertex Discovery: Articulation Points in a Graph Using Tarjan's DFS Timestamps\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=j1QDfU21iZk", "title": "Lecture 181: Articulation Point in Graph (Tarjan's Algorithm)", "duration": "24m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/articulation-point-1/1", "title": "Articulation Point - I", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find all articulation points (cut vertices) in an undirected connected graph in sorted order."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-number-of-days-to-disconnect-island/", "title": "Minimum Number of Days to Disconnect Island (LC 1568)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Check if an island grid contains an articulation point to disconnect it in 1 day instead of 2."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/critical-connections-in-a-network/", "title": "Critical Connections in a Network (LC 1192)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Contrast Tarjan's strict inequality for bridges (low[it] > tin[node]) with articulation points (low[it] >= tin[node])."}]	Module 27: Strongly Connected Components (Kosaraju's) & Tarjan's Bridge Detection	### 📌 Topic Overview\nAn **Articulation Point (Cut Vertex)** in an undirected graph is any vertex whose removal (along with all its incident edges) **increases the number of connected components** in the graph.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Crucial Differences From Bridges (`low[it] >= tin[node]`):**\n  1. **Condition Change (`>=` instead of `>`):**\n     - In Bridges, if a child `it` could reach `node` itself (`low[it] == tin[node]`), removing edge `(node, it)` didn't disconnect `it`.\n     - For **Articulation Points**, we are removing `node` itself! So if child `it` can only reach as high as `node` (`low[it] == tin[node]`) and **cannot** reach an ancestor strictly before `node` (`< tin[node]`), removing `node` still disconnects `it`!\n     - Thus, for any non-root vertex (`parent != -1`), `node` is an articulation point if **`low[it] >= tin[node]`**!\n  2. **Visited Back-Edge Update (`tin[it]` instead of `low[it]`):**\n     - When `it` is already visited (`vis[it] == 1`), update **`low[node] = min(low[node], tin[it])`**. Why `tin[it]` instead of `low[it]`? Because if `it` is an articulation point itself, we cannot pass through `it` to reach even higher ancestors if `it` were removed!\n  3. **Special Root Rule (`parent == -1 && child > 1`):**\n     - The starting root node of a DFS tree has no ancestors above it, so `low[it] >= tin[root]` is always true. Instead, the root is an articulation point **if and only if it has more than 1 independent DFS child (`child > 1`)**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(V + 2E)` Time and `O(3V)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Articulation Point Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nclass Solution {\n    int timer = 1;\n\n    void dfsArticulation(int node, int parent, vector<int> &vis, int tin[], int low[],\n                         vector<int> &mark, vector<int> adj[]) {\n        vis[node] = 1;\n        tin[node] = low[node] = timer++;\n        int child = 0;\n\n        for (int it : adj[node]) {\n            if (it == parent) continue;\n            if (!vis[it]) {\n                dfsArticulation(it, node, vis, tin, low, mark, adj);\n                low[node] = min(low[node], low[it]);\n\n                // Non-root articulation point check\n                if (low[it] >= tin[node] && parent != -1) {\n                    mark[node] = 1;\n                }\n                child++;\n            } else {\n                // Back-edge to an already-visited ancestor: compare with tin[it]!\n                low[node] = min(low[node], tin[it]);\n            }\n        }\n        // Special check for the DFS root node\n        if (child > 1 && parent == -1) {\n            mark[node] = 1;\n        }\n    }\n\npublic:\n    vector<int> articulationPoints(int n, vector<int> adj[]) {\n        vector<int> vis(n, 0), mark(n, 0);\n        int tin[n], low[n];\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            if (!vis[i]) {\n                dfsArticulation(i, -1, vis, tin, low, mark, adj);\n            }\n        }\n        vector<int> ans;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            if (mark[i] == 1) ans.push_back(i);\n        }\n        if (ans.empty()) return {-1};\n        return ans;\n    }\n};\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Pushing `node` Directly Into `ans` Inside the DFS Loop:** A single vertex `node` can satisfy `low[it] >= tin[node]` for multiple child branches `it`. Always mark `mark[node] = 1` in a hash/boolean array first to avoid duplicate entries in your output!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
959	Target-Rank Partitioning: K-th Element of Two Sorted Arrays	dsa-kth-element-of-two-sorted-arrays	Find the K-th smallest element of two sorted arrays in O(log(min(N1, N2))) time using bounded partition binary search.	2026-10-03 00:19:12.454933+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Target-Rank Partitioning: K-th Element of Two Sorted Arrays\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D1oDwWCq50g", "title": "Lecture 67: K-th Element of Two Sorted Arrays", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/kth-smallest-element-in-a-sorted-matrix/", "title": "Kth Smallest Element in a Sorted Matrix", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the kth smallest element in an n x n matrix where each row and column is sorted."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-k-th-smallest-pair-distance/", "title": "Find K-th Smallest Pair Distance", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Combine two-pointer counting with binary search on difference values."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/k-th-element-of-two-sorted-arrays1317/1", "title": "K-th element of two Arrays", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the element that would be at the kth position in the final sorted array."}]	Module 14: Multi-Array Partitioning & 2D Matrix Binary Search	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven two sorted arrays `arr1` and `arr2` and an integer `k`, find the element that would appear at index `k - 1` (1-based position `k`) in the merged sorted array in `O(log(min(N1, N2)))` time.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Direct Extension of Median Partitioning:**\n  - In Median, the left partition size was fixed at `(n1 + n2 + 1) / 2`.\n  - In K-th Element, the left partition size is fixed at exactly **`k`**!\n- **Search Space Bounds for `mid1`:**\n  - `low = max(0, k - n2)`: If `k > n2`, even taking all elements of `arr2` is not enough, so we must take at least `k - n2` elements from `arr1`.\n  - `high = min(k, n1)`: We cannot take more elements from `arr1` than its total size `n1`, nor more than `k`.\n- **Result:**\n  - Once `l1 <= r2 && l2 <= r1`, the k-th element is simply **`max(l1, l2)`**!\n\n### 💻 K-th Element Blueprint\n```cpp\nint kthElement(vector<int> &a, vector<int> &b, int n1, int n2, int k) {\n    if (n1 > n2) return kthElement(b, a, n2, n1, k);\n\n    int low = max(0, k - n2), high = min(k, n1);\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid1 = (low + high) >> 1;\n        int mid2 = k - mid1;\n        int l1 = (mid1 > 0) ? a[mid1 - 1] : INT_MIN;\n        int l2 = (mid2 > 0) ? b[mid2 - 1] : INT_MIN;\n        int r1 = (mid1 < n1) ? a[mid1] : INT_MAX;\n        int r2 = (mid2 < n2) ? b[mid2] : INT_MAX;\n\n        if (l1 <= r2 && l2 <= r1) {\n            return max(l1, l2);\n        } else if (l1 > r2) {\n            high = mid1 - 1;\n        } else {\n            low = mid1 + 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
8569	Dual Length & Frequency DP Arrays: Number of Longest Increasing Subsequences	dsa-number-of-longest-increasing-subsequences	Track both maximum LIS lengths and occurrence frequencies using dual 1D arrays with reset and accumulate transition rules.	2026-10-03 11:44:14.785635+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Dual Length & Frequency DP Arrays: Number of Longest Increasing Subsequences\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cKVl1TFdNXg", "title": "Lecture 227: Number of Longest Increasing Subsequences (DP 47)", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-longest-increasing-subsequences/", "title": "Number of Longest Increasing Subsequences (LC 673)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given an integer array nums, return the number of longest increasing subsequences."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/number-of-longest-increasing-subsequence/1", "title": "Number of Longest Increasing Subsequence", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count the total number of Longest Increasing Subsequences in a given array."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-ways-to-arrive-at-destination/", "title": "Number of Ways to Arrive at Destination (LC 1976)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Compare the dual-array (reset vs. accumulate) pattern in LIS counting with shortest-path counting."}]	Module 33: Dynamic Programming on Longest Increasing Subsequences (LIS) & Chain Variants	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an integer array `arr` of length `N`, find the **total number of Longest Increasing Subsequences** (if the maximum LIS length is `L`, count how many distinct index subsequences of length `L` exist).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Maintaining Two 1D Arrays (`dp[i]` and `cnt[i]`):**\n  - `dp[i]` = length of the LIS ending at index `i` (initialized to `1`).\n  - `cnt[i]` = number of such LIS of length `dp[i]` ending at index `i` (initialized to `1`).\n- **The Reset vs. Accumulate Rule When `arr[prev] < arr[i]`:**\n  1. **Strictly Longer LIS Found (`dp[prev] + 1 > dp[i]`):**\n     - Update length: **`dp[i] = dp[prev] + 1`**.\n     - **Inherit count:** `cnt[i] = cnt[prev]` (all longest subsequences ending at `prev` now extend to `i`!).\n  2. **Another Equal-Length LIS Found (`dp[prev] + 1 == dp[i]`):**\n     - Length stays `dp[i]`.\n     - **Accumulate count:** **`cnt[i] += cnt[prev]`** (add the new ways from `prev`!).\n- **Final Aggregation:**\n  - Find the global maximum length `maxi`. Sum `cnt[i]` across **all indices `i` where `dp[i] == maxi`**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N²)` Time and `O(N)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Number of Longest Increasing Subsequences Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint findNumberOfLIS(vector<int> &arr) {\n    int n = arr.size();\n    vector<int> dp(n, 1), cnt(n, 1);\n    int maxi = 1;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        for (int prev = 0; prev < i; prev++) {\n            if (arr[prev] < arr[i] && 1 + dp[prev] > dp[i]) {\n                dp[i] = 1 + dp[prev];\n                cnt[i] = cnt[prev]; // Inherit count for the new longer length\n            } else if (arr[prev] < arr[i] && 1 + dp[prev] == dp[i]) {\n                cnt[i] += cnt[prev]; // Add ways of achieving the same max length\n            }\n        }\n        maxi = max(maxi, dp[i]);\n    }\n\n    int totalWays = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        if (dp[i] == maxi) {\n            totalWays += cnt[i];\n        }\n    }\n    return totalWays;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Writing `cnt[i]++` Instead of `cnt[i] += cnt[prev]`:** If there were `3` ways to form an LIS of length `4` ending at `prev`, extending `prev` to `i` creates **`3` new ways** of length `5`, not just `1`! Always add `cnt[prev]`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
10019	Recursive Backtracking Carry Propagation: Add 1 to a Number Represented by a Linked List	dsa-add-1-to-number-represented-by-linked-list	Add 1 to a most-significant-digit-first linked list using single-pass recursive backtracking without reversing the list.	2026-10-03 15:04:07.044515+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Recursive Backtracking Carry Propagation: Add 1 to a Number Represented by a Linked List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aXQWhbvT3w0", "title": "Lecture 247: Add 1 to a Number Represented as Linked List", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/add-1-to-a-number-represented-as-linked-list/1", "title": "Add 1 to a Linked List Number", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "A number is represented in a linked list such that each digit corresponds to a node. Add 1 to it."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/plus-one-linked-list/", "title": "Plus One Linked List (LC 369)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given a non-negative integer represented as a linked list of digits (most significant digit first), plus one to the integer."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/double-a-number-represented-as-a-linked-list/", "title": "Double a Number Represented as a Linked List (LC 2816)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use recursive backtracking carry propagation to double a linked-list number in a single pass."}]	Module 37: Linked List Medium Problems: Dummy Nodes, In-Place Re-Linking & Tortoise-Hare Pointers	### 📌 Topic Overview\nA number is represented by a singly linked list where the **head is the most significant digit** and the **tail is the units digit** (e.g., `1 -> 5 -> 9` represents `159`, and `9 -> 9 -> 9` represents `999`). Add `1` to the number and return the updated head.\n\n### 💡 Comparing the Two Approaches\n- **1. Iterative Approach via 2 Reversals (`O(3N)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  1. Reverse the list (`O(N)`) so the units digit is at the head.\n  2. Traverse with `carry = 1` adding to each node (`O(N)`), breaking early as soon as `carry == 0`.\n  3. Reverse the list back (`O(N)`), and if `carry == 1` still remains, prepend `new Node(1, head)`.\n- **2. Optimal Single-Pass Recursive Backtracking (`O(N)` Time, `O(N)` Stack Space — Zero List Reversals!):**\n  - Because singly linked lists cannot move backward iteratively, **recursion gives us natural backward traversal during backtracking**!\n  - Write `int addHelper(Node* temp)`:\n    - **Base Case (`temp == nullptr`):** We just stepped past the units digit! Return **`1`** (the `+1` we want to add!).\n    - **Recursive Call:** `int carry = addHelper(temp->next);`\n    - **Backtracking Step:** Add `temp->data += carry;`:\n      - If **`temp->data < 10`**: No further carry! Return **`0`**.\n      - Else (`temp->data == 10`): Set **`temp->data = 0;`** and return **`1`**!\n  - In `addOne(head)`: if `addHelper(head) == 1`, prepend `new Node(1)` in front of `head`!\n\n### 💻 Add 1 to Linked List (Recursive Backtracking) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint addHelper(Node* temp) {\n    // Base case: past the tail node, return initial carry of 1\n    if (temp == nullptr) return 1;\n\n    int carry = addHelper(temp->next);\n    temp->data = temp->data + carry;\n\n    if (temp->data < 10) {\n        return 0; // Carry absorbed; no further carry needed\n    }\n    temp->data = 0;\n    return 1; // Propagate carry of 1 to the previous digit\n}\n\nNode* addOne(Node* head) {\n    int carry = addHelper(head);\n    if (carry == 1) {\n        Node* newNode = new Node(1);\n        newNode->next = head;\n        return newNode;\n    }\n    return head;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Comparing `temp->data` Values Instead of Returning `carry`:** In `9 -> 9 -> 9`, every node becomes `0` and a brand-new head node `1` must be allocated (`1 -> 0 -> 0 -> 0`). Always check `if (carry == 1)` at the root call!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
