": "What HTTP header dictates to the browser exactly how long specific content should remain cached?", "explanation": "The Cache-Control header (e.g., max-age=7200) sets the expiration time for cached assets, dictating when the browser must fetch a fresh copy.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "Cache-Control"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["Data is written directly to permanent storage, bypassing the cache entirely.", "Data is written to the cache first, and later asynchronously synced to the database.", "Data is simultaneously written to both the temporary cache and the permanent database storage.", "Data is deleted from the cache upon every write."], "question": "In server-side caching, what characterizes a 'Write-Through' cache?", "explanation": "Write-through caching ensures perfect data consistency between the cache and the database, though it introduces slightly higher latency during write operations.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "Data is simultaneously written to both the temporary cache and the permanent database storage."}, {"id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["A security protocol banning malicious IP addresses.", "A set of rules (like Least Recently Used - LRU) that determines which items are removed from memory when the cache becomes 100% full.", "A method for encrypting cached objects.", "A strategy for scaling CDN edge nodes."], "question": "What is an 'Eviction Policy' in the context of memory caches like Redis?", "explanation": "Because RAM is limited, caches must drop old data to make room for new data. LRU drops the data that hasn't been requested in the longest amount of time.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "A set of rules (like Least Recently Used - LRU) that determines which items are removed from memory when the cache becomes 100% full."}, {"id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "A", "options": ["Pull CDNs automatically fetch missing content from the origin server when a user first requests it; Push CDNs require manual uploads ahead of time.", "Pull CDNs pull data from databases; Push CDNs push data to local storage.", "Pull CDNs are used for backend microservices; Push CDNs are used for frontend assets.", "There is no functional difference."], "question": "How does a 'Pull-based' CDN behave differently from a 'Push-based' CDN?", "explanation": "Pull CDNs are great for dynamic web apps, acting as a smart proxy that caches resources automatically upon the very first 'Cache Miss'.", "correctAnswer": "A", "correct_index": 0, "correct_answer": "Pull CDNs automatically fetch missing content from the origin server when a user first requests it; Push CDNs require manual uploads ahead of time."}]	\N	> **Architecture Overview:** Essential technical concepts and structural principles vital for robust, scalable web architectures. Focus deeply on the mechanical trade-offs discussed in this module.	Hayk Simonyan	https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan	\N	The Ultimate Guide to Caching and CDNs	Comprehensive overview of The Ultimate Guide to Caching and CDNs covering low-level transport mechanisms, architectural scaling, and system persistence.	7	Easy	Advanced System Concepts	21	[]	\N	7	https://youtu.be/RCdAGvtTYI0	The Ultimate Guide to Caching and CDNs
22162	Lecture 22: REST APIs, JSON Requests & CORS — Decoupled MERN Architecture, express.json(), HTTP Status Codes & Full-Stack Integration	node-lecture-22-rest-apis-json-requests-cors-decoupled-mern-architecture	Architect decoupled RESTful APIs for React and mobile clients, parse JSON payloads with express.json(), configure CORS middleware, use semantic HTTP verbs and status codes, and connect a React frontend to an Express/MongoDB backend.	2026-10-06 23:02:53.291009+05:30	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn Lectures 1–21, our Express server rendered full HTML pages on the server using **EJS (Server-Side Rendering)**. However, modern **MERN Stack** and multi-platform architectures decouple the **Frontend** (a standalone React SPA on port `5173`, plus iOS and Android apps) from the **Backend** (an Express **REST API** on port `3000`). Instead of returning HTML views via `res.render()`, a REST API exchanges lightweight **JSON data** via **`res.json()`**!\n\n### 💡 Core Principles of REST (Representational State Transfer) APIs\n1. **Client-Server Separation:** The React frontend handles UI state and routing; the Express backend handles data persistence and business rules. Because the backend speaks pure JSON, the **exact same REST API** serves Web (React), iOS, and Android apps simultaneously!\n2. **Statelessness:** Every API request from the client contains all the information needed to process it (e.g., JWT token in `Authorization` header, JSON payload in `req.body`) without relying on server-rendered view state.\n3. **Resource-Based Nouns & Semantic HTTP Verbs:**\n   * **`GET /api/todo`:** Fetch all todo items (`200 OK`).\n   * **`POST /api/todo`:** Create a new todo item (`201 Created`).\n   * **`PUT /api/todo/:id` or `PATCH /api/todo/:id/completed`:** Update an existing item (`200 OK`).\n   * **`DELETE /api/todo/:id`:** Delete an item (`200 OK` or `204 No Content`).\n\n### 💡 Essential Express Middleware for REST APIs (`express.json()` & `cors`)\n* **`app.use(express.json())`:** Parses incoming `Content-Type: application/json` payloads sent by `fetch()` or Axios into **`req.body`**.\n* **CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing — `app.use(cors())`):**\n  * When your React frontend (`http://localhost:5173`) calls your Express backend (`http://localhost:3000`), the browser's **Same-Origin Policy** blocks the request because the ports (origins) differ!\n  * Installing and registering **`const cors = require("cors"); app.use(cors());`** adds the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` headers so the browser allows your React app to communicate with your Express API.\n\n### 🛠️ Complete Express REST API Server + Controller (`todoItemsController.js`)\n```javascript\n// File: app.js (Pure REST API Backend)\nconst express = require("express");\nconst cors = require("cors");\nconst mongoose = require("mongoose");\nconst todoItemsRouter = require("./routes/todoItemsRouter");\n\nconst app = express();\n\n// 1. Parse JSON request bodies & enable Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS)\napp.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\napp.use(express.json());\napp.use(cors());\n\n// 2. Mount REST API routes\napp.use("/api/todo", todoItemsRouter);\n\n// File: controllers/todoItemsController.js\nconst TodoItem = require("../models/TodoItem");\n\nexports.createTodoItem = async (req, res, next) => {\n  const { task, date } = req.body;\n  const todoItem = new TodoItem({ task, date });\n  await todoItem.save();\n  res.status(201).json(todoItem); // 201 Created + JSON response\n};\n\nexports.getTodoItems = async (req, res, next) => {\n  const todoItems = await TodoItem.find();\n  res.json(todoItems);\n};\n\nexports.markCompleted = async (req, res, next) => {\n  const { id } = req.params;\n  const todoItem = await TodoItem.findById(id);\n  todoItem.completed = true;\n  await todoItem.save();\n  res.json(todoItem);\n};\n\nexports.deleteTodoItem = async (req, res, next) => {\n  const { id } = req.params;\n  await TodoItem.findByIdAndDelete(id);\n  res.status(204).json({ _id: id });\n};\n```	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/CORS", "link": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/CORS", "title": "MDN Web Docs — Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) Guide"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n-7KgmUFvuI", "title": "Lecture 21: REST API and JSON Requests | NodeJS Complete Course in Hindi", "duration": "97m", "creator_name": "Complete Coding by Prashant Sir", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir"}]	[{"id": "node-lec-22-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Compare Server-Side Rendering (EJS) with a Decoupled REST API Architecture (React + Express). Why do modern companies prefer REST APIs?", "title": "Q1: Compare Server-Side Rendering (EJS) with a Decoupled REST API Architecture (React + Express). Why do modern companies prefer REST APIs?", "answer": "In Server-Side Rendering (EJS), the backend fetches data, compiles HTML templates on the server, and sends full HTML pages on every navigation—tightly coupling the backend to web HTML. In a REST API architecture, the Express backend only sends and receives raw JSON data (res.json()), while a decoupled frontend (React) renders the UI on the client. Companies prefer REST APIs because: 1) Multi-Platform Reuse: The exact same Express REST API can simultaneously power a React web app, an iOS app, and an Android app. 2) Faster UX: Only tiny JSON payloads travel over the network instead of full-page reloads. 3) Independent Scaling: Frontend and backend teams can deploy and scale independently.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "22-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In Server-Side Rendering (EJS), the backend fetches data, compiles HTML templates on the server, and sends full HTML pages on every navigation—tightly coupling the backend to web HTML. In a REST API architecture, the Express backend only sends and receives raw JSON data (res.json()), while a decoupled frontend (React) renders the UI on the client. Companies prefer REST APIs because: 1) Multi-Platform Reuse: The exact same Express REST API can simultaneously power a React web app, an iOS app, and an Android app. 2) Faster UX: Only tiny JSON payloads travel over the network instead of full-page reloads. 3) Independent Scaling: Frontend and backend teams can deploy and scale independently."}, {"id": "node-lec-22-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: What is CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing), why does a browser block React (localhost:5173) from calling Express (localhost:3000) by default, and how does the cors middleware fix it?", "title": "Q2: What is CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing), why does a browser block React (localhost:5173) from calling Express (localhost:3000) by default, and how does the cors middleware fix it?", "answer": "For security, browsers enforce the Same-Origin Policy, which restricts scripts loaded from one Origin (protocol + domain + port, e.g., http://localhost:5173) from reading responses from a different Origin (http://localhost:3000)—preventing malicious websites from making unauthorized requests to APIs in your browser. The cors() middleware in Express attaches HTTP response headers like Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *, Access-Control-Allow-Methods, and Access-Control-Allow-Headers, explicitly telling the browser that the frontend origin is permitted to access the API.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "22-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "For security, browsers enforce the Same-Origin Policy, which restricts scripts loaded from one Origin (protocol + domain + port, e.g., http://localhost:5173) from reading responses from a different Origin (http://localhost:3000)—preventing malicious websites from making unauthorized requests to APIs in your browser. The cors() middleware in Express attaches HTTP response headers like Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *, Access-Control-Allow-Methods, and Access-Control-Allow-Headers, explicitly telling the browser that the frontend origin is permitted to access the API."}, {"id": "node-lec-22-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is a CORS Preflight Request (HTTP OPTIONS method), and when does the browser send it?", "title": "Q3: What is a CORS Preflight Request (HTTP OPTIONS method), and when does the browser send it?", "answer": "Before sending a \\"non-simple\\" cross-origin request—such as a DELETE, PUT, or PATCH request, or a POST request with Content-Type: application/json or custom Authorization headers—the browser automatically sends a lightweight HTTP OPTIONS preflight request to the server first. The server responds with Access-Control-Allow-Methods and Access-Control-Allow-Headers confirming whether those methods/headers are allowed; only after the preflight succeeds does the browser send the actual request!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "22-q3", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Before sending a \\"non-simple\\" cross-origin request—such as a DELETE, PUT, or PATCH request, or a POST request with Content-Type: application/json or custom Authorization headers—the browser automatically sends a lightweight HTTP OPTIONS preflight request to the server first. The server responds with Access-Control-Allow-Methods and Access-Control-Allow-Headers confirming whether those methods/headers are allowed; only after the preflight succeeds does the browser send the actual request!"}, {"id": "node-lec-22-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What is the difference between HTTP PUT and HTTP PATCH in a REST API, and which HTTP methods are Idempotent?", "title": "Q4: What is the difference between HTTP PUT and HTTP PATCH in a REST API, and which HTTP methods are Idempotent?", "answer": "PUT is used to completely replace an entire resource representation at a given URI (sending all fields), whereas PATCH is used to apply a partial update to specific fields of a resource (such as toggling { completed: true } on a Todo item). An HTTP method is \\"Idempotent\\" if making the exact same request 1 time or 10 times produces the exact same server state: GET, PUT, and DELETE are idempotent, whereas POST is NOT idempotent (calling POST /api/todo 10 times creates 10 separate todo items!).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "22-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "PUT is used to completely replace an entire resource representation at a given URI (sending all fields), whereas PATCH is used to apply a partial update to specific fields of a resource (such as toggling { completed: true } on a Todo item). An HTTP method is \\"Idempotent\\" if making the exact same request 1 time or 10 times produces the exact same server state: GET, PUT, and DELETE are idempotent, whereas POST is NOT idempotent (calling POST /api/todo 10 times creates 10 separate todo items!)."}, {"id": "node-lec-22-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Why did we need app.use(express.json()) in Lecture 22 when we already had app.use(express.urlencoded()) in earlier lectures?", "title": "Q5: Why did we need app.use(express.json()) in Lecture 22 when we already had app.use(express.urlencoded()) in earlier lectures?", "answer": "In earlier lectures, data was submitted via standard HTML <form> tags, which encode payloads as application/x-www-form-urlencoded (parsed by express.urlencoded()). In a React REST client, fetch() sends payloads via JSON.stringify(data) with header Content-Type: application/json—which requires app.use(express.json()) to parse the JSON stream into req.body.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "22-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In earlier lectures, data was submitted via standard HTML <form> tags, which encode payloads as application/x-www-form-urlencoded (parsed by express.urlencoded()). In a React REST client, fetch() sends payloads via JSON.stringify(data) with header Content-Type: application/json—which requires app.use(express.json()) to parse the JSON stream into req.body."}, {"id": "node-lec-22-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: What are the standard HTTP Status Codes used across REST API responses (200, 201, 204, 400, 401, 403, 404, 422, 500)?", "title": "Q6: What are the standard HTTP Status Codes used across REST API responses (200, 201, 204, 400, 401, 403, 404, 422, 500)?", "answer": "2xx Success: 200 OK (standard GET/PUT/PATCH success), 201 Created (resource created via POST), 204 No Content (successful DELETE with empty body). 4xx Client Errors: 400 Bad Request (malformed syntax), 401 Unauthorized (not logged in / invalid token), 403 Forbidden (logged in, but insufficient role permissions), 404 Not Found (resource ID does not exist), 422 Unprocessable Entity (validation failed). 5xx Server Errors: 500 Internal Server Error (unhandled backend crash/exception).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "22-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "2xx Success: 200 OK (standard GET/PUT/PATCH success), 201 Created (resource created via POST), 204 No Content (successful DELETE with empty body). 4xx Client Errors: 400 Bad Request (malformed syntax), 401 Unauthorized (not logged in / invalid token), 403 Forbidden (logged in, but insufficient role permissions), 404 Not Found (resource ID does not exist), 422 Unprocessable Entity (validation failed). 5xx Server Errors: 500 Internal Server Error (unhandled backend crash/exception)."}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn Lectures 1–21, our Express server rendered full HTML pages on the server using **EJS (Server-Side Rendering)**. However, modern **MERN Stack** and multi-platform architectures decouple the **Frontend** (a standalone React SPA on port `5173`, plus iOS and Android apps) from the **Backend** (an Express **REST API** on port `3000`). Instead of returning HTML views via `res.render()`, a REST API exchanges lightweight **JSON data** via **`res.json()`**!\n\n### 💡 Core Principles of REST (Representational State Transfer) APIs\n1. **Client-Server Separation:** The React frontend handles UI state and routing; the Express backend handles data persistence and business rules. Because the backend speaks pure JSON, the **exact same REST API** serves Web (React), iOS, and Android apps simultaneously!\n2. **Statelessness:** Every API request from the client contains all the information needed to process it (e.g., JWT token in `Authorization` header, JSON payload in `req.body`) without relying on server-rendered view state.\n3. **Resource-Based Nouns & Semantic HTTP Verbs:**\n   * **`GET /api/todo`:** Fetch all todo items (`200 OK`).\n   * **`POST /api/todo`:** Create a new todo item (`201 Created`).\n   * **`PUT /api/todo/:id` or `PATCH /api/todo/:id/completed`:** Update an existing item (`200 OK`).\n   * **`DELETE /api/todo/:id`:** Delete an item (`200 OK` or `204 No Content`).\n\n### 💡 Essential Express Middleware for REST APIs (`express.json()` & `cors`)\n* **`app.use(express.json())`:** Parses incoming `Content-Type: application/json` payloads sent by `fetch()` or Axios into **`req.body`**.\n* **CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing — `app.use(cors())`):**\n  * When your React frontend (`http://localhost:5173`) calls your Express backend (`http://localhost:3000`), the browser's **Same-Origin Policy** blocks the request because the ports (origins) differ!\n  * Installing and registering **`const cors = require("cors"); app.use(cors());`** adds the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` headers so the browser allows your React app to communicate with your Express API.\n\n### 🛠️ Complete Express REST API Server + Controller (`todoItemsController.js`)\n```javascript\n// File: app.js (Pure REST API Backend)\nconst express = require("express");\nconst cors = require("cors");\nconst mongoose = require("mongoose");\nconst todoItemsRouter = require("./routes/todoItemsRouter");\n\nconst app = express();\n\n// 1. Parse JSON request bodies & enable Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS)\napp.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\napp.use(express.json());\napp.use(cors());\n\n// 2. Mount REST API routes\napp.use("/api/todo", todoItemsRouter);\n\n// File: controllers/todoItemsController.js\nconst TodoItem = require("../models/TodoItem");\n\nexports.createTodoItem = async (req, res, next) => {\n  const { task, date } = req.body;\n  const todoItem = new TodoItem({ task, date });\n  await todoItem.save();\n  res.status(201).json(todoItem); // 201 Created + JSON response\n};\n\nexports.getTodoItems = async (req, res, next) => {\n  const todoItems = await TodoItem.find();\n  res.json(todoItems);\n};\n\nexports.markCompleted = async (req, res, next) => {\n  const { id } = req.params;\n  const todoItem = await TodoItem.findById(id);\n  todoItem.completed = true;\n  await todoItem.save();\n  res.json(todoItem);\n};\n\nexports.deleteTodoItem = async (req, res, next) => {\n  const { id } = req.params;\n  await TodoItem.findByIdAndDelete(id);\n  res.status(204).json({ _id: id });\n};\n```	Complete Coding by Prashant Sir	https://www.youtube.com/@CompleteCodingByPrashantSir	24	Lecture 22: REST APIs, JSON Requests & CORS — Decoupled MERN Architecture, express.json(), HTTP Status Codes & Full-Stack Integration	Architect decoupled RESTful APIs for React and mobile clients, parse JSON payloads with express.json(), configure CORS middleware, use semantic HTTP verbs and status codes, and connect a React frontend to an Express/MongoDB backend.	20	Easy	\N	22	[{"id": "node-lec-22-q1", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q1: Compare Server-Side Rendering (EJS) with a Decoupled REST API Architecture (React + Express). Why do modern companies prefer REST APIs?", "title": "Q1: Compare Server-Side Rendering (EJS) with a Decoupled REST API Architecture (React + Express). Why do modern companies prefer REST APIs?", "answer": "In Server-Side Rendering (EJS), the backend fetches data, compiles HTML templates on the server, and sends full HTML pages on every navigation—tightly coupling the backend to web HTML. In a REST API architecture, the Express backend only sends and receives raw JSON data (res.json()), while a decoupled frontend (React) renders the UI on the client. Companies prefer REST APIs because: 1) Multi-Platform Reuse: The exact same Express REST API can simultaneously power a React web app, an iOS app, and an Android app. 2) Faster UX: Only tiny JSON payloads travel over the network instead of full-page reloads. 3) Independent Scaling: Frontend and backend teams can deploy and scale independently.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "22-q1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In Server-Side Rendering (EJS), the backend fetches data, compiles HTML templates on the server, and sends full HTML pages on every navigation—tightly coupling the backend to web HTML. In a REST API architecture, the Express backend only sends and receives raw JSON data (res.json()), while a decoupled frontend (React) renders the UI on the client. Companies prefer REST APIs because: 1) Multi-Platform Reuse: The exact same Express REST API can simultaneously power a React web app, an iOS app, and an Android app. 2) Faster UX: Only tiny JSON payloads travel over the network instead of full-page reloads. 3) Independent Scaling: Frontend and backend teams can deploy and scale independently."}, {"id": "node-lec-22-q2", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q2: What is CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing), why does a browser block React (localhost:5173) from calling Express (localhost:3000) by default, and how does the cors middleware fix it?", "title": "Q2: What is CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing), why does a browser block React (localhost:5173) from calling Express (localhost:3000) by default, and how does the cors middleware fix it?", "answer": "For security, browsers enforce the Same-Origin Policy, which restricts scripts loaded from one Origin (protocol + domain + port, e.g., http://localhost:5173) from reading responses from a different Origin (http://localhost:3000)—preventing malicious websites from making unauthorized requests to APIs in your browser. The cors() middleware in Express attaches HTTP response headers like Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *, Access-Control-Allow-Methods, and Access-Control-Allow-Headers, explicitly telling the browser that the frontend origin is permitted to access the API.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "22-q2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "For security, browsers enforce the Same-Origin Policy, which restricts scripts loaded from one Origin (protocol + domain + port, e.g., http://localhost:5173) from reading responses from a different Origin (http://localhost:3000)—preventing malicious websites from making unauthorized requests to APIs in your browser. The cors() middleware in Express attaches HTTP response headers like Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *, Access-Control-Allow-Methods, and Access-Control-Allow-Headers, explicitly telling the browser that the frontend origin is permitted to access the API."}, {"id": "node-lec-22-q3", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q3: What is a CORS Preflight Request (HTTP OPTIONS method), and when does the browser send it?", "title": "Q3: What is a CORS Preflight Request (HTTP OPTIONS method), and when does the browser send it?", "answer": "Before sending a \\"non-simple\\" cross-origin request—such as a DELETE, PUT, or PATCH request, or a POST request with Content-Type: application/json or custom Authorization headers—the browser automatically sends a lightweight HTTP OPTIONS preflight request to the server first. The server responds with Access-Control-Allow-Methods and Access-Control-Allow-Headers confirming whether those methods/headers are allowed; only after the preflight succeeds does the browser send the actual request!", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "22-q3", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Before sending a \\"non-simple\\" cross-origin request—such as a DELETE, PUT, or PATCH request, or a POST request with Content-Type: application/json or custom Authorization headers—the browser automatically sends a lightweight HTTP OPTIONS preflight request to the server first. The server responds with Access-Control-Allow-Methods and Access-Control-Allow-Headers confirming whether those methods/headers are allowed; only after the preflight succeeds does the browser send the actual request!"}, {"id": "node-lec-22-q4", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q4: What is the difference between HTTP PUT and HTTP PATCH in a REST API, and which HTTP methods are Idempotent?", "title": "Q4: What is the difference between HTTP PUT and HTTP PATCH in a REST API, and which HTTP methods are Idempotent?", "answer": "PUT is used to completely replace an entire resource representation at a given URI (sending all fields), whereas PATCH is used to apply a partial update to specific fields of a resource (such as toggling { completed: true } on a Todo item). An HTTP method is \\"Idempotent\\" if making the exact same request 1 time or 10 times produces the exact same server state: GET, PUT, and DELETE are idempotent, whereas POST is NOT idempotent (calling POST /api/todo 10 times creates 10 separate todo items!).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "22-q4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "PUT is used to completely replace an entire resource representation at a given URI (sending all fields), whereas PATCH is used to apply a partial update to specific fields of a resource (such as toggling { completed: true } on a Todo item). An HTTP method is \\"Idempotent\\" if making the exact same request 1 time or 10 times produces the exact same server state: GET, PUT, and DELETE are idempotent, whereas POST is NOT idempotent (calling POST /api/todo 10 times creates 10 separate todo items!)."}, {"id": "node-lec-22-q5", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q5: Why did we need app.use(express.json()) in Lecture 22 when we already had app.use(express.urlencoded()) in earlier lectures?", "title": "Q5: Why did we need app.use(express.json()) in Lecture 22 when we already had app.use(express.urlencoded()) in earlier lectures?", "answer": "In earlier lectures, data was submitted via standard HTML <form> tags, which encode payloads as application/x-www-form-urlencoded (parsed by express.urlencoded()). In a React REST client, fetch() sends payloads via JSON.stringify(data) with header Content-Type: application/json—which requires app.use(express.json()) to parse the JSON stream into req.body.", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "22-q5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "In earlier lectures, data was submitted via standard HTML <form> tags, which encode payloads as application/x-www-form-urlencoded (parsed by express.urlencoded()). In a React REST client, fetch() sends payloads via JSON.stringify(data) with header Content-Type: application/json—which requires app.use(express.json()) to parse the JSON stream into req.body."}, {"id": "node-lec-22-q6", "url": null, "link": null, "name": "Q6: What are the standard HTTP Status Codes used across REST API responses (200, 201, 204, 400, 401, 403, 404, 422, 500)?", "title": "Q6: What are the standard HTTP Status Codes used across REST API responses (200, 201, 204, 400, 401, 403, 404, 422, 500)?", "answer": "2xx Success: 200 OK (standard GET/PUT/PATCH success), 201 Created (resource created via POST), 204 No Content (successful DELETE with empty body). 4xx Client Errors: 400 Bad Request (malformed syntax), 401 Unauthorized (not logged in / invalid token), 403 Forbidden (logged in, but insufficient role permissions), 404 Not Found (resource ID does not exist), 422 Unprocessable Entity (validation failed). 5xx Server Errors: 500 Internal Server Error (unhandled backend crash/exception).", "platform": "Technical Interview QA", "problemId": "22-q6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "2xx Success: 200 OK (standard GET/PUT/PATCH success), 201 Created (resource created via POST), 204 No Content (successful DELETE with empty body). 4xx Client Errors: 400 Bad Request (malformed syntax), 401 Unauthorized (not logged in / invalid token), 403 Forbidden (logged in, but insufficient role permissions), 404 Not Found (resource ID does not exist), 422 Unprocessable Entity (validation failed). 5xx Server Errors: 500 Internal Server Error (unhandled backend crash/exception)."}]	https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/CORS	\N	\N	\N
22196	Arithmetic Progression	aptitude-quant-arithmetic-progression	Master the common difference shortcuts, Nth term derivations, Sum of N terms, symmetric triplet selection (a-d, a, a+d), and recursive sequences.	2026-10-07 11:51:32.526467+05:30	### 📌 1. Fundamentals of Arithmetic Progression (AP)\n* An Arithmetic Progression (AP) is a series where the difference between any two consecutive terms is constant, known as the **Common Difference (`d`)**.\n  * `d = a_2 - a_1 = a_3 - a_2`\n* **N-th Term Formula:** **`a_n = a + (n - 1)d`**\n* **Finding Total Number of Terms (`n`):**\n  * **`n = [ (Last Term - First Term) / d ] + 1`**\n\n### 💡 2. Sum of N Terms & Arithmetic Mean\n* **Sum Formula 1 (when `d` is known):**\n  **`S_n = (n / 2) × [2a + (n - 1)d]`**\n* **Sum Formula 2 (when Last Term `L` is known):**\n  **`S_n = (n / 2) × (First Term + Last Term)`**\n* **Arithmetic Mean (AM):**\n  * The AM of `a` and `b` is `(a + b) / 2`.\n  * For an AP with an ODD number of terms, the exact middle term IS the average of the series!\n\n### 💡 3. High-Yield Placement Shortcuts\n* **Symmetric Term Assumption (CRITICAL!):**\n  If a problem gives the *sum* of 3 terms in an AP, NEVER assume them as `a`, `a+d`, `a+2d`.\n  Always assume them as: **`(a - d), a, (a + d)`**. Adding them instantly cancels out `d`, giving `3a = Sum`.\n  * For 4 terms: **`(a - 3d), (a - d), (a + d), (a + 3d)`** (Note: common difference here is `2d`).\n* **Properties of AP Transformations:**\n  * If a constant is added/subtracted to every term, the resulting series remains in AP with the *same* common difference `d`.\n  * If every term is multiplied/divided by a constant `K`, the series remains in AP, and the new common difference becomes `K × d`.\n  * The term-wise addition of two separate APs forms a new AP.	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G-gqX4Oo9PA", "type": "youtube", "title": "Arithmetic Progression - Shortcuts & Tricks for 2026 Placement Tests, Job Interviews & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-ap-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [First Term from Subtracted Equations] If the 3rd and 7th terms of an AP are 19 and 29 respectively, find its first term.", "options": ["A) 11", "B) 12", "C) 14", "D) 15"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In an Arithmetic Progression (AP), the exactly 3rd term is 19 and the exactly 7th term is 29. What is the exact mathematical value of the very first term in this sequence?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the first term be a and the common difference be d. 3rd term = a + 2d = 19. 7th term = a + 6d = 29. Subtract the first equation from the second: (a + 6d) - (a + 2d) = 29 - 19 => 4d = 10 => d = 2.5. Substitute d into the first equation: a + 2(2.5) = 19 => a + 5 = 19 => a = 14.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-ap-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Nth Term Deduction by Options] 5th term of an AP is 7, 7th term is 17. Which of the following formulas correctly represents its nth term?", "options": ["A) 8n - 30", "B) 1/2 (10n - 36)", "C) 1/2 (10n - 18)", "D) 5n - 18"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The 5th term of an Arithmetic Progression is exactly 7, while its 7th term is exactly 17. Which of the following algebraic options perfectly represents the general n-th term of this AP?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Test Option B: 1/2(10n - 36). For n=5: 1/2(50 - 36) = 1/2(14) = 7. For n=7: 1/2(70 - 36) = 1/2(34) = 17. Option B perfectly satisfies both conditions. (Algebraic derivation: a + 4d = 7, a + 6d = 17 => 2d = 10 => d = 5. a = 7 - 20 = -13. Nth term = -13 + (n-1)5 = 5n - 18. Both B and D are equivalent; B represents the exact options layout).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-ap-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Ratio of Terms Yielding Arithmetic Mean] Ratio of 2nd to 7th term of an AP is 1:3. 4th term is 9. Find the Arithmetic Mean of this AP with 15 terms.", "options": ["A) 15", "B) 17", "C) 19", "D) 21"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The ratio of the 2nd term to the 7th term of a 15-term Arithmetic Progression is exactly 1 : 3. If the 4th term is exactly 9, what is the exact Arithmetic Mean (average) of all 15 terms in this AP?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Ratio: (a+d) / (a+6d) = 1/3 => 3a + 3d = a + 6d => 2a = 3d => a = 1.5d. 2) 4th term: a + 3d = 9 => 1.5d + 3d = 9 => 4.5d = 9 => d = 2. So a = 3. 3) For an AP with 15 terms (odd count), the Arithmetic Mean is exactly the middle term! The middle term is the 8th term. 8th term = a + 7d = 3 + 7(2) = 3 + 14 = 17. The Arithmetic Mean is exactly 17.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-ap-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Equalizing Multiples of Terms] Find the value of 16th term of an AP if 6 times its 6th term is equal to 10 times its 10th term.", "options": ["A) -1", "B) 0", "C) 1", "D) Cannot be determined"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In an Arithmetic Progression, if exactly 6 times its 6th term is mathematically equal to exactly 10 times its 10th term, what is the exact value of its 16th term?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Equation: 6(a + 5d) = 10(a + 9d). Expanding: 6a + 30d = 10a + 90d. Rearranging: -4a = 60d => a = -15d. The 16th term is a + 15d. Substituting a: (-15d) + 15d = 0. The 16th term is 0. (Shortcut: If p × T_p = q × T_q, then the (p+q)-th term T_(p+q) is ALWAYS 0!).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-ap-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Cyclic Term Property] If p-th term is a, q-th term is b, r-th term is c. Evaluate a(q-r) + b(r-p) + c(p-q).", "options": ["A) a + b + c", "B) p + q + r", "C) 0", "D) 1"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In an Arithmetic Progression, the p-th term is a, the q-th term is b, and the r-th term is c. What is the exact mathematical value of the cyclic expression:\\na(q - r) + b(r - p) + c(p - q)?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let the first term be X and common difference be D. a = X + (p-1)D, b = X + (q-1)D, c = X + (r-1)D. Substitute these into a(q-r) + b(r-p) + c(p-q). Since (q-r) + (r-p) + (p-q) = 0, the X terms completely cancel out to 0. Similarly, the D terms expand cyclically and all cross-cancel precisely to 0. The result is always 0.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-ap-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Sum of First N Terms Given 12th Term] 12th term of an AP is -10 and sum of first 4 terms is 36. Find the first term.", "options": ["A) 12", "B) 14", "C) 16", "D) 18"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The 12th term of an Arithmetic Progression is exactly -10, and the sum of its first 4 terms is exactly 36. What is the exact value of the very first term?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) a + 11d = -10. 2) Sum of first 4 terms = (4/2)[2a + 3d] = 2(2a + 3d) = 4a + 6d. Given 4a + 6d = 36 => 2a + 3d = 18. We have system: a + 11d = -10 (multiply by 2 -> 2a + 22d = -20). Subtract this from 2a + 3d = 18: (2a + 3d) - (2a + 22d) = 18 - (-20) => -19d = 38 => d = -2. Substitute d into first equation: a + 11(-2) = -10 => a - 22 = -10 => a = 12.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-ap-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Greatest Possible Sum of Decreasing AP] What is the greatest possible sum of the AP: 23, 21, 19, ...?", "options": ["A) 132", "B) 140", "C) 144", "D) 150"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the mathematically greatest possible sum of the strictly decreasing Arithmetic Progression sequence:\\n23, 21, 19, ... ?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The sum is maximized right before the terms become negative! Since the series decreases by 2, it hits 1, then -1. We must sum all terms from 23 down to the last positive term, which is 1. Number of terms n = [(Last - First) / d] + 1 = [(1 - 23) / -2] + 1 = [-22 / -2] + 1 = 11 + 1 = 12 terms. Sum = (n/2)(First + Last) = (12/2)(23 + 1) = 6 × 24 = 144.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-ap-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Sum of Intertwined Series] Find sum to 60 terms: 1 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 7 + 7 + 10 + 9 + ...", "options": ["A) 2,145", "B) 2,295", "C) 2,345", "D) 2,495"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Find the exact total sum up to exactly 60 terms of the following intertwined sequence:\\n1 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 7 + 7 + 10 + 9 + ...", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "This is not an AP! But by grouping pairs of terms, we can form an AP. Pair sums: (1+3)=4, (4+5)=9, (7+7)=14, (10+9)=19... The new paired series is 4, 9, 14, 19... which is a perfect AP with a = 4, d = 5! Since we grouped pairs, 60 original terms become 30 paired terms. Find S_30 for this AP: S_30 = (30/2)[2(4) + (29)5] = 15[8 + 145] = 15[153] = 2,295.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-ap-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Sum Subsets Deriving Nth Term] Sum of first 5 terms is 1/4 of sum of next 5 terms. First term is 3. Find 25th term.", "options": ["A) -210", "B) -213", "C) -216", "D) -220"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In an AP, the first term is exactly 3. The sum of its first 5 terms is exactly 1/4 of the sum of the NEXT 5 terms. What is the exact value of the 25th term of this AP?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let S_n be the sum of first n terms. Given: S_5 = (1/4)(S_10 - S_5) => 4 S_5 = S_10 - S_5 => 5 S_5 = S_10. Use Sum formula with a = 3: 5 × (5/2)[2(3) + 4d] = (10/2)[2(3) + 9d] => 25/2 [6 + 4d] = 5 [6 + 9d]. Divide both sides by 5: 5/2 [6 + 4d] = [6 + 9d] => 5[6 + 4d] = 2[6 + 9d] => 30 + 20d = 12 + 18d => 2d = -18 => d = -9. The 25th term is a + 24d = 3 + 24(-9) = 3 - 216 = -213.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-ap-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Triplet Assumption for Squares] Sum of three numbers in AP is 18 and sum of their squares is 158. Smallest number?", "options": ["A) 1", "B) 2", "C) 3", "D) 4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The sum of three numbers in an Arithmetic Progression is exactly 18, and the sum of their squares is exactly 158. What is the exact value of the strictly smallest number among the three?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let the terms be (a - d), a, and (a + d). Their sum is 3a = 18 => a = 6. Sum of squares: (6 - d)^2 + 6^2 + (6 + d)^2 = 158. Expanding: (36 - 12d + d^2) + 36 + (36 + 12d + d^2) = 158 => 108 + 2d^2 = 158 => 2d^2 = 50 => d^2 = 25 => d = 5 (or -5). The three numbers are 6-5, 6, 6+5, which are 1, 6, 11. The smallest number is 1.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-ap-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Pattern Recognition in Rows] Find the sum of numbers in the nth row of the triangular pattern: R1=1, R2=2,3,4, R3=5,6,7,8,9.", "options": ["A) n^2 + 1", "B) 2n^2 - 1", "C) n^3 + (n - 1)^3", "D) n^3 + 1"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A triangular series is built such that Row 1 has {1}, Row 2 has {2, 3, 4}, Row 3 has {5, 6, 7, 8, 9}, etc. Which of the following formulas correctly represents the exact sum of the numbers located precisely in the n-th row?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Test the options using actual row sums! Sum of R1 = 1. Sum of R2 = 2+3+4 = 9. Sum of R3 = 5+6+7+8+9 = 35. Test Option C for n=2: 2^3 + 1^3 = 8 + 1 = 9 (Matches R2). Test Option C for n=3: 3^3 + 2^3 = 27 + 8 = 35 (Matches R3). Formula perfectly satisfies all rows.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-ap-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Sum of Odd Numbers Below N] Find the sum of all odd numbers up to 100.", "options": ["A) 2400", "B) 2500", "C) 2600", "D) 2700"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the exact mathematical sum of all positive odd integers that are strictly less than 100?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The series is 1, 3, 5, ..., 99. First term a = 1, Last term L = 99. Common difference d = 2. Number of terms n = [(99 - 1) / 2] + 1 = [98 / 2] + 1 = 49 + 1 = 50 terms. Sum = (n/2)(First + Last) = (50/2)(1 + 99) = 25 × 100 = 2,500. (Shortcut: Sum of first N odd numbers is always exactly N^2. Here N=50, so 50^2 = 2500).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-ap-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Multiples Range Sum] Find the sum of all multiples of 7 lying between 100 and 300.", "options": ["A) 5,586", "B) 5,684", "C) 5,880", "D) 6,104"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the exact mathematical sum of all integer multiples of exactly 7 that lie strictly between 100 and 300?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "First multiple of 7 > 100 is 105 (since 105/7 = 15). Last multiple of 7 < 300 is 294 (since 294/7 = 42). This is an AP with a = 105, L = 294, d = 7. Number of terms n = [(294 - 105) / 7] + 1 = [189 / 7] + 1 = 27 + 1 = 28 terms. Sum = (n/2)(a + L) = (28/2)(105 + 294) = 14 × 399. 14 × 400 = 5600, minus 14 = 5,586.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-ap-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Ratio of Sums] The sum of n terms of two APs are in the ratio (3n + 8) : (7n + 15). Find the ratio of their 12th terms.", "options": ["A) 7 : 16", "B) 8 : 17", "C) 9 : 19", "D) 10 : 21"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The exact sums of the first n terms of two distinct Arithmetic Progressions are in the ratio (3n + 8) : (7n + 15). What is the exact mathematical ratio of their respective 12th terms?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "To find the ratio of the k-th terms from the ratio of their n-term sums, substitute n = 2k - 1. Here, k = 12, so substitute n = 2(12) - 1 = 23 into the sum ratio formula! Ratio = [3(23) + 8] / [7(23) + 15] = [69 + 8] / [161 + 15] = 77 / 176. Divide numerator and denominator by 11: 7 / 16. The ratio is 7 : 16.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-ap-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Constant Addition Property] If a, b, c are in AP, then (a-b), (b-c), (c-a) will form:", "options": ["A) An Arithmetic Progression", "B) A Geometric Progression", "C) A Harmonic Progression", "D) No mathematical progression"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If positive integers a, b, and c are strictly in an Arithmetic Progression, then the three transformed values (a - c), (b - c), and (c - c) will form:", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "The property of an AP states that if a constant is subtracted from every term of an AP, the resulting series remains perfectly in AP. Here, the constant 'c' is systematically subtracted from a, b, and c. Therefore, (a - c), (b - c), and 0 strictly form an Arithmetic Progression.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-ap-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Inserting Arithmetic Means] Insert 3 arithmetic means between 3 and 19. The 2nd mean is:", "options": ["A) 7", "B) 9", "C) 11", "D) 13"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly 3 arithmetic means are perfectly inserted between the numbers 3 and 19 to form a valid contiguous 5-term Arithmetic Progression. What is the exact value of the 2nd arithmetic mean (the middle term)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The sequence has 5 terms: 3, m1, m2, m3, 19. Common difference d = (Last - First) / (Number of means + 1) = (19 - 3) / (3 + 1) = 16 / 4 = 4. The series is 3, 7, 11, 15, 19. The 2nd arithmetic mean (which is the 3rd term of the entire series) is 11. (Shortcut: For an odd number of elements, the exact center term is the simple average of the extremes: (3 + 19)/2 = 11).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-ap-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Solving for Common Difference via S_n] If the sum of n terms of an AP is 3n^2 + 5n, find the common difference.", "options": ["A) 4", "B) 5", "C) 6", "D) 8"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the total sum of the first n terms of an Arithmetic Progression is governed exactly by the formula S_n = 3n^2 + 5n, what is the exact common difference of this AP?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "S_1 (Sum of 1 term = 1st term) = 3(1)^2 + 5(1) = 8. S_2 (Sum of 1st and 2nd terms) = 3(2)^2 + 5(2) = 12 + 10 = 22. The 2nd term = S_2 - S_1 = 22 - 8 = 14. The common difference d = 2nd term - 1st term = 14 - 8 = 6. (Shortcut: In a quadratic sum formula An^2 + Bn, the common difference d is ALWAYS exactly 2A. Here, A = 3, so d = 6).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-ap-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Zero Sum of Continuous AP Terms] In an AP, sum of first p terms equals sum of first q terms. Find sum of first (p+q) terms.", "options": ["A) 0", "B) 1", "C) p + q", "D) Cannot be determined"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a certain Arithmetic Progression, the exact mathematical sum of its first p terms is strictly equal to the exact sum of its first q terms (where p ≠ q). What is the exact sum of its first (p + q) terms?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "This is a standard algebraic property of Arithmetic Progressions. If S_p = S_q (and p ≠ q), it means the series crosses from positive into negative (or vice versa), and the terms from p to q precisely cancel out the earlier accumulated sum when expanding the boundary to p+q. Mathematically, S_(p+q) is ALWAYS exactly 0.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-ap-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Number of Common Terms] Find the number of common terms between the two APs: 3, 7, 11, ... to 40 terms, and 2, 7, 12, ... to 50 terms.", "options": ["A) 8", "B) 9", "C) 10", "D) 11"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Consider two distinct Arithmetic Progressions:\\nAP1: 3, 7, 11, ... up to 40 terms.\\nAP2: 2, 7, 12, ... up to 50 terms.\\nExactly how many common terms do these two sequences share?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) AP1: d1 = 4. Last term = 3 + 39(4) = 3 + 156 = 159. 2) AP2: d2 = 5. Last term = 2 + 49(5) = 2 + 245 = 247. 3) The common terms will form a new AP. The first common term is 7. The common difference of this new AP is LCM(d1, d2) = LCM(4, 5) = 20. 4) The new AP is 7, 27, 47, ... up to a maximum limit. The limit is the smaller of the two last terms, which is 159. 5) 7 + (n-1)20 ≤ 159 => (n-1)20 ≤ 152 => n-1 ≤ 7.6 => n ≤ 8.6. The max integer n is 8. There are exactly 8 common terms.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-ap-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Equalizing Consecutive Segment Sums] If the sum of 3 consecutive terms of an AP is 24, and their product is 440, find the terms.", "options": ["A) 4, 8, 12", "B) 5, 8, 11", "C) 6, 8, 10", "D) 2, 8, 14"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The exact sum of 3 strictly consecutive terms of an Arithmetic Progression is exactly 24, and their exact mathematical product is exactly 440. Which of the following correctly represents the three terms in ascending order?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the terms be (a - d), a, (a + d). Sum = 3a = 24 => a = 8. Product = (8 - d)(8)(8 + d) = 440 => 8(64 - d^2) = 440 => 64 - d^2 = 55 => d^2 = 9 => d = 3. The terms are 8-3, 8, 8+3, which are exactly 5, 8, and 11.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. Fundamentals of Arithmetic Progression (AP)\n* An Arithmetic Progression (AP) is a series where the difference between any two consecutive terms is constant, known as the **Common Difference (`d`)**.\n  * `d = a_2 - a_1 = a_3 - a_2`\n* **N-th Term Formula:** **`a_n = a + (n - 1)d`**\n* **Finding Total Number of Terms (`n`):**\n  * **`n = [ (Last Term - First Term) / d ] + 1`**\n\n### 💡 2. Sum of N Terms & Arithmetic Mean\n* **Sum Formula 1 (when `d` is known):**\n  **`S_n = (n / 2) × [2a + (n - 1)d]`**\n* **Sum Formula 2 (when Last Term `L` is known):**\n  **`S_n = (n / 2) × (First Term + Last Term)`**\n* **Arithmetic Mean (AM):**\n  * The AM of `a` and `b` is `(a + b) / 2`.\n  * For an AP with an ODD number of terms, the exact middle term IS the average of the series!\n\n### 💡 3. High-Yield Placement Shortcuts\n* **Symmetric Term Assumption (CRITICAL!):**\n  If a problem gives the *sum* of 3 terms in an AP, NEVER assume them as `a`, `a+d`, `a+2d`.\n  Always assume them as: **`(a - d), a, (a + d)`**. Adding them instantly cancels out `d`, giving `3a = Sum`.\n  * For 4 terms: **`(a - 3d), (a - d), (a + d), (a + 3d)`** (Note: common difference here is `2d`).\n* **Properties of AP Transformations:**\n  * If a constant is added/subtracted to every term, the resulting series remains in AP with the *same* common difference `d`.\n  * If every term is multiplied/divided by a constant `K`, the series remains in AP, and the new common difference becomes `K × d`.\n  * The term-wise addition of two separate APs forms a new AP.	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Arithmetic Progression	Master the common difference shortcuts, Nth term derivations, Sum of N terms, symmetric triplet selection (a-d, a, a+d), and recursive sequences.	58	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	30	[{"id": "apt-ap-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [First Term from Subtracted Equations] If the 3rd and 7th terms of an AP are 19 and 29 respectively, find its first term.", "options": ["A) 11", "B) 12", "C) 14", "D) 15"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In an Arithmetic Progression (AP), the exactly 3rd term is 19 and the exactly 7th term is 29. What is the exact mathematical value of the very first term in this sequence?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the first term be a and the common difference be d. 3rd term = a + 2d = 19. 7th term = a + 6d = 29. Subtract the first equation from the second: (a + 6d) - (a + 2d) = 29 - 19 => 4d = 10 => d = 2.5. Substitute d into the first equation: a + 2(2.5) = 19 => a + 5 = 19 => a = 14.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-ap-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Nth Term Deduction by Options] 5th term of an AP is 7, 7th term is 17. Which of the following formulas correctly represents its nth term?", "options": ["A) 8n - 30", "B) 1/2 (10n - 36)", "C) 1/2 (10n - 18)", "D) 5n - 18"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The 5th term of an Arithmetic Progression is exactly 7, while its 7th term is exactly 17. Which of the following algebraic options perfectly represents the general n-th term of this AP?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Test Option B: 1/2(10n - 36). For n=5: 1/2(50 - 36) = 1/2(14) = 7. For n=7: 1/2(70 - 36) = 1/2(34) = 17. Option B perfectly satisfies both conditions. (Algebraic derivation: a + 4d = 7, a + 6d = 17 => 2d = 10 => d = 5. a = 7 - 20 = -13. Nth term = -13 + (n-1)5 = 5n - 18. Both B and D are equivalent; B represents the exact options layout).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-ap-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Ratio of Terms Yielding Arithmetic Mean] Ratio of 2nd to 7th term of an AP is 1:3. 4th term is 9. Find the Arithmetic Mean of this AP with 15 terms.", "options": ["A) 15", "B) 17", "C) 19", "D) 21"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The ratio of the 2nd term to the 7th term of a 15-term Arithmetic Progression is exactly 1 : 3. If the 4th term is exactly 9, what is the exact Arithmetic Mean (average) of all 15 terms in this AP?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Ratio: (a+d) / (a+6d) = 1/3 => 3a + 3d = a + 6d => 2a = 3d => a = 1.5d. 2) 4th term: a + 3d = 9 => 1.5d + 3d = 9 => 4.5d = 9 => d = 2. So a = 3. 3) For an AP with 15 terms (odd count), the Arithmetic Mean is exactly the middle term! The middle term is the 8th term. 8th term = a + 7d = 3 + 7(2) = 3 + 14 = 17. The Arithmetic Mean is exactly 17.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-ap-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Equalizing Multiples of Terms] Find the value of 16th term of an AP if 6 times its 6th term is equal to 10 times its 10th term.", "options": ["A) -1", "B) 0", "C) 1", "D) Cannot be determined"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In an Arithmetic Progression, if exactly 6 times its 6th term is mathematically equal to exactly 10 times its 10th term, what is the exact value of its 16th term?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Equation: 6(a + 5d) = 10(a + 9d). Expanding: 6a + 30d = 10a + 90d. Rearranging: -4a = 60d => a = -15d. The 16th term is a + 15d. Substituting a: (-15d) + 15d = 0. The 16th term is 0. (Shortcut: If p × T_p = q × T_q, then the (p+q)-th term T_(p+q) is ALWAYS 0!).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-ap-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Cyclic Term Property] If p-th term is a, q-th term is b, r-th term is c. Evaluate a(q-r) + b(r-p) + c(p-q).", "options": ["A) a + b + c", "B) p + q + r", "C) 0", "D) 1"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In an Arithmetic Progression, the p-th term is a, the q-th term is b, and the r-th term is c. What is the exact mathematical value of the cyclic expression:\\na(q - r) + b(r - p) + c(p - q)?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let the first term be X and common difference be D. a = X + (p-1)D, b = X + (q-1)D, c = X + (r-1)D. Substitute these into a(q-r) + b(r-p) + c(p-q). Since (q-r) + (r-p) + (p-q) = 0, the X terms completely cancel out to 0. Similarly, the D terms expand cyclically and all cross-cancel precisely to 0. The result is always 0.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-ap-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Sum of First N Terms Given 12th Term] 12th term of an AP is -10 and sum of first 4 terms is 36. Find the first term.", "options": ["A) 12", "B) 14", "C) 16", "D) 18"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The 12th term of an Arithmetic Progression is exactly -10, and the sum of its first 4 terms is exactly 36. What is the exact value of the very first term?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) a + 11d = -10. 2) Sum of first 4 terms = (4/2)[2a + 3d] = 2(2a + 3d) = 4a + 6d. Given 4a + 6d = 36 => 2a + 3d = 18. We have system: a + 11d = -10 (multiply by 2 -> 2a + 22d = -20). Subtract this from 2a + 3d = 18: (2a + 3d) - (2a + 22d) = 18 - (-20) => -19d = 38 => d = -2. Substitute d into first equation: a + 11(-2) = -10 => a - 22 = -10 => a = 12.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-ap-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Greatest Possible Sum of Decreasing AP] What is the greatest possible sum of the AP: 23, 21, 19, ...?", "options": ["A) 132", "B) 140", "C) 144", "D) 150"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the mathematically greatest possible sum of the strictly decreasing Arithmetic Progression sequence:\\n23, 21, 19, ... ?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The sum is maximized right before the terms become negative! Since the series decreases by 2, it hits 1, then -1. We must sum all terms from 23 down to the last positive term, which is 1. Number of terms n = [(Last - First) / d] + 1 = [(1 - 23) / -2] + 1 = [-22 / -2] + 1 = 11 + 1 = 12 terms. Sum = (n/2)(First + Last) = (12/2)(23 + 1) = 6 × 24 = 144.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-ap-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Sum of Intertwined Series] Find sum to 60 terms: 1 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 7 + 7 + 10 + 9 + ...", "options": ["A) 2,145", "B) 2,295", "C) 2,345", "D) 2,495"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Find the exact total sum up to exactly 60 terms of the following intertwined sequence:\\n1 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 7 + 7 + 10 + 9 + ...", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "This is not an AP! But by grouping pairs of terms, we can form an AP. Pair sums: (1+3)=4, (4+5)=9, (7+7)=14, (10+9)=19... The new paired series is 4, 9, 14, 19... which is a perfect AP with a = 4, d = 5! Since we grouped pairs, 60 original terms become 30 paired terms. Find S_30 for this AP: S_30 = (30/2)[2(4) + (29)5] = 15[8 + 145] = 15[153] = 2,295.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-ap-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Sum Subsets Deriving Nth Term] Sum of first 5 terms is 1/4 of sum of next 5 terms. First term is 3. Find 25th term.", "options": ["A) -210", "B) -213", "C) -216", "D) -220"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In an AP, the first term is exactly 3. The sum of its first 5 terms is exactly 1/4 of the sum of the NEXT 5 terms. What is the exact value of the 25th term of this AP?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let S_n be the sum of first n terms. Given: S_5 = (1/4)(S_10 - S_5) => 4 S_5 = S_10 - S_5 => 5 S_5 = S_10. Use Sum formula with a = 3: 5 × (5/2)[2(3) + 4d] = (10/2)[2(3) + 9d] => 25/2 [6 + 4d] = 5 [6 + 9d]. Divide both sides by 5: 5/2 [6 + 4d] = [6 + 9d] => 5[6 + 4d] = 2[6 + 9d] => 30 + 20d = 12 + 18d => 2d = -18 => d = -9. The 25th term is a + 24d = 3 + 24(-9) = 3 - 216 = -213.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-ap-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Triplet Assumption for Squares] Sum of three numbers in AP is 18 and sum of their squares is 158. Smallest number?", "options": ["A) 1", "B) 2", "C) 3", "D) 4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The sum of three numbers in an Arithmetic Progression is exactly 18, and the sum of their squares is exactly 158. What is the exact value of the strictly smallest number among the three?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let the terms be (a - d), a, and (a + d). Their sum is 3a = 18 => a = 6. Sum of squares: (6 - d)^2 + 6^2 + (6 + d)^2 = 158. Expanding: (36 - 12d + d^2) + 36 + (36 + 12d + d^2) = 158 => 108 + 2d^2 = 158 => 2d^2 = 50 => d^2 = 25 => d = 5 (or -5). The three numbers are 6-5, 6, 6+5, which are 1, 6, 11. The smallest number is 1.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-ap-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Pattern Recognition in Rows] Find the sum of numbers in the nth row of the triangular pattern: R1=1, R2=2,3,4, R3=5,6,7,8,9.", "options": ["A) n^2 + 1", "B) 2n^2 - 1", "C) n^3 + (n - 1)^3", "D) n^3 + 1"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A triangular series is built such that Row 1 has {1}, Row 2 has {2, 3, 4}, Row 3 has {5, 6, 7, 8, 9}, etc. Which of the following formulas correctly represents the exact sum of the numbers located precisely in the n-th row?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Test the options using actual row sums! Sum of R1 = 1. Sum of R2 = 2+3+4 = 9. Sum of R3 = 5+6+7+8+9 = 35. Test Option C for n=2: 2^3 + 1^3 = 8 + 1 = 9 (Matches R2). Test Option C for n=3: 3^3 + 2^3 = 27 + 8 = 35 (Matches R3). Formula perfectly satisfies all rows.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-ap-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Sum of Odd Numbers Below N] Find the sum of all odd numbers up to 100.", "options": ["A) 2400", "B) 2500", "C) 2600", "D) 2700"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the exact mathematical sum of all positive odd integers that are strictly less than 100?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The series is 1, 3, 5, ..., 99. First term a = 1, Last term L = 99. Common difference d = 2. Number of terms n = [(99 - 1) / 2] + 1 = [98 / 2] + 1 = 49 + 1 = 50 terms. Sum = (n/2)(First + Last) = (50/2)(1 + 99) = 25 × 100 = 2,500. (Shortcut: Sum of first N odd numbers is always exactly N^2. Here N=50, so 50^2 = 2500).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-ap-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Multiples Range Sum] Find the sum of all multiples of 7 lying between 100 and 300.", "options": ["A) 5,586", "B) 5,684", "C) 5,880", "D) 6,104"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the exact mathematical sum of all integer multiples of exactly 7 that lie strictly between 100 and 300?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "First multiple of 7 > 100 is 105 (since 105/7 = 15). Last multiple of 7 < 300 is 294 (since 294/7 = 42). This is an AP with a = 105, L = 294, d = 7. Number of terms n = [(294 - 105) / 7] + 1 = [189 / 7] + 1 = 27 + 1 = 28 terms. Sum = (n/2)(a + L) = (28/2)(105 + 294) = 14 × 399. 14 × 400 = 5600, minus 14 = 5,586.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-ap-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Ratio of Sums] The sum of n terms of two APs are in the ratio (3n + 8) : (7n + 15). Find the ratio of their 12th terms.", "options": ["A) 7 : 16", "B) 8 : 17", "C) 9 : 19", "D) 10 : 21"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The exact sums of the first n terms of two distinct Arithmetic Progressions are in the ratio (3n + 8) : (7n + 15). What is the exact mathematical ratio of their respective 12th terms?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "To find the ratio of the k-th terms from the ratio of their n-term sums, substitute n = 2k - 1. Here, k = 12, so substitute n = 2(12) - 1 = 23 into the sum ratio formula! Ratio = [3(23) + 8] / [7(23) + 15] = [69 + 8] / [161 + 15] = 77 / 176. Divide numerator and denominator by 11: 7 / 16. The ratio is 7 : 16.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-ap-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Constant Addition Property] If a, b, c are in AP, then (a-b), (b-c), (c-a) will form:", "options": ["A) An Arithmetic Progression", "B) A Geometric Progression", "C) A Harmonic Progression", "D) No mathematical progression"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If positive integers a, b, and c are strictly in an Arithmetic Progression, then the three transformed values (a - c), (b - c), and (c - c) will form:", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "The property of an AP states that if a constant is subtracted from every term of an AP, the resulting series remains perfectly in AP. Here, the constant 'c' is systematically subtracted from a, b, and c. Therefore, (a - c), (b - c), and 0 strictly form an Arithmetic Progression.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-ap-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Inserting Arithmetic Means] Insert 3 arithmetic means between 3 and 19. The 2nd mean is:", "options": ["A) 7", "B) 9", "C) 11", "D) 13"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly 3 arithmetic means are perfectly inserted between the numbers 3 and 19 to form a valid contiguous 5-term Arithmetic Progression. What is the exact value of the 2nd arithmetic mean (the middle term)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The sequence has 5 terms: 3, m1, m2, m3, 19. Common difference d = (Last - First) / (Number of means + 1) = (19 - 3) / (3 + 1) = 16 / 4 = 4. The series is 3, 7, 11, 15, 19. The 2nd arithmetic mean (which is the 3rd term of the entire series) is 11. (Shortcut: For an odd number of elements, the exact center term is the simple average of the extremes: (3 + 19)/2 = 11).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-ap-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Solving for Common Difference via S_n] If the sum of n terms of an AP is 3n^2 + 5n, find the common difference.", "options": ["A) 4", "B) 5", "C) 6", "D) 8"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the total sum of the first n terms of an Arithmetic Progression is governed exactly by the formula S_n = 3n^2 + 5n, what is the exact common difference of this AP?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "S_1 (Sum of 1 term = 1st term) = 3(1)^2 + 5(1) = 8. S_2 (Sum of 1st and 2nd terms) = 3(2)^2 + 5(2) = 12 + 10 = 22. The 2nd term = S_2 - S_1 = 22 - 8 = 14. The common difference d = 2nd term - 1st term = 14 - 8 = 6. (Shortcut: In a quadratic sum formula An^2 + Bn, the common difference d is ALWAYS exactly 2A. Here, A = 3, so d = 6).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-ap-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Zero Sum of Continuous AP Terms] In an AP, sum of first p terms equals sum of first q terms. Find sum of first (p+q) terms.", "options": ["A) 0", "B) 1", "C) p + q", "D) Cannot be determined"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a certain Arithmetic Progression, the exact mathematical sum of its first p terms is strictly equal to the exact sum of its first q terms (where p ≠ q). What is the exact sum of its first (p + q) terms?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "This is a standard algebraic property of Arithmetic Progressions. If S_p = S_q (and p ≠ q), it means the series crosses from positive into negative (or vice versa), and the terms from p to q precisely cancel out the earlier accumulated sum when expanding the boundary to p+q. Mathematically, S_(p+q) is ALWAYS exactly 0.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-ap-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Number of Common Terms] Find the number of common terms between the two APs: 3, 7, 11, ... to 40 terms, and 2, 7, 12, ... to 50 terms.", "options": ["A) 8", "B) 9", "C) 10", "D) 11"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Consider two distinct Arithmetic Progressions:\\nAP1: 3, 7, 11, ... up to 40 terms.\\nAP2: 2, 7, 12, ... up to 50 terms.\\nExactly how many common terms do these two sequences share?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) AP1: d1 = 4. Last term = 3 + 39(4) = 3 + 156 = 159. 2) AP2: d2 = 5. Last term = 2 + 49(5) = 2 + 245 = 247. 3) The common terms will form a new AP. The first common term is 7. The common difference of this new AP is LCM(d1, d2) = LCM(4, 5) = 20. 4) The new AP is 7, 27, 47, ... up to a maximum limit. The limit is the smaller of the two last terms, which is 159. 5) 7 + (n-1)20 ≤ 159 => (n-1)20 ≤ 152 => n-1 ≤ 7.6 => n ≤ 8.6. The max integer n is 8. There are exactly 8 common terms.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-ap-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Equalizing Consecutive Segment Sums] If the sum of 3 consecutive terms of an AP is 24, and their product is 440, find the terms.", "options": ["A) 4, 8, 12", "B) 5, 8, 11", "C) 6, 8, 10", "D) 2, 8, 14"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The exact sum of 3 strictly consecutive terms of an Arithmetic Progression is exactly 24, and their exact mathematical product is exactly 440. Which of the following correctly represents the three terms in ascending order?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let the terms be (a - d), a, (a + d). Sum = 3a = 24 => a = 8. Product = (8 - d)(8)(8 + d) = 440 => 8(64 - d^2) = 440 => 64 - d^2 = 55 => d^2 = 9 => d = 3. The terms are 8-3, 8, 8+3, which are exactly 5, 8, and 11.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
21114	Lecture 1: Relational Database Foundations — Database, DBMS, RDBMS & SQL Architecture	pg-lecture-1-rdbms-and-sql-foundations	Understand the core differences between raw data, Databases, DBMS, RDBMS (PostgreSQL/MySQL), NoSQL (MongoDB), and Structured Query Language (SQL).	2026-10-05 17:59:36.035454+05:30	### 🎯 Computer Science Fundamentals: Lecture 1: Relational Database Foundations — Database, DBMS, RDBMS & SQL Architecture\nCore systems concepts frequently tested in technical screening rounds and engineering interviews.\n\n- **System Architecture**: How modern operating systems, networks, and processors coordinate resources.\n- **Key Concepts to Master**: Concurrency primitives, resource isolation, latency vs throughput tradeoffs, and fault tolerance.\n- **Interview Key Takeaways**:\n  - Be prepared to draw diagrams explaining data flow and component lifecycles.\n  - Explain practical real-world applications (e.g. why databases use WAL, why HTTP/2 uses multiplexing).	[{"url": "https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/", "title": "OS Three Easy Pieces Guide"}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/operating-systems/", "title": "GeeksforGeeks CS Fundamentals"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WtA1pfOz5uU", "title": "PostgreSQL Tutorial for Beginners 2026 — Day 1 | Database, DBMS, RDBMS & SQL Explained", "duration": "19m", "creator_name": "Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical"}]	[{"id": "pg-1-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/", "name": "Recyclable and Low Fat Products", "title": "Recyclable and Low Fat Products", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Recyclable and Low Fat Products\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/"}, {"id": "pg-1-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "name": "Find Customer Referee", "title": "Find Customer Referee", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1_2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Find Customer Referee\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/"}, {"id": "pg-1-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "name": "Big Countries", "title": "Big Countries", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1_3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Big Countries\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/"}, {"id": "pg-1-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/", "name": "Article Views I", "title": "Article Views I", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1_4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Article Views I\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/article-views-i/"}, {"id": "pg-1-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/", "name": "Invalid Tweets", "title": "Invalid Tweets", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1_5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Invalid Tweets\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/"}, {"id": "pg-1-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/", "name": "Combine Two Tables", "title": "Combine Two Tables", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1_6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Combine Two Tables\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combine-two-tables/"}, {"id": "pg-1-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "name": "Customers Who Never Order", "title": "Customers Who Never Order", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1_7", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Customers Who Never Order\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/"}, {"id": "pg-1-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "name": "Duplicate Emails", "title": "Duplicate Emails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1_8", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Duplicate Emails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/"}, {"id": "pg-1-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "name": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "title": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1_9", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Employees Earning More Than Their Managers\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/"}, {"id": "pg-1-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "name": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "title": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1_10", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Delete Duplicate Emails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nBefore writing complex queries or designing backend APIs, every engineer must understand the architectural hierarchy of data storage: **Data -> Database -> DBMS -> RDBMS -> PostgreSQL**, and where **SQL** fits as the communication protocol.\n\n### 💡 Core Architectural Concepts\n* **Data:** Raw, isolated facts, numbers, or strings — `"Vinod"`, `23`, `"vinod@college.com"`\n* **Database (DB):** An organized collection of actual stored data grouped logically — Student records of a university, E-commerce orders.\n* **DBMS (Database Management System):** Software system used to create, manage, query, and secure databases — PostgreSQL, MySQL, MongoDB, SQLite.\n* **RDBMS (Relational DBMS):** Relational DBMS that stores data strictly in **tables** (rows & columns) linked via keys — PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle, SQL Server.\n* **NoSQL (Non-RDBMS):** Stores data as flexible JSON/BSON documents, key-value pairs, or graphs — MongoDB, Redis, Cassandra.\n* **SQL (Structured Query Language):** The standard language used to talk to an RDBMS (NOT a database software itself) — `SELECT * FROM users;`\n\n### 🏗️ Anatomy of a Relational Table (Excel vs. PostgreSQL)\nWhile both Microsoft Excel and PostgreSQL organize data into rows and columns, Excel breaks down at millions of rows and lacks relational integrity constraints.\n\n* **Table (Relation):** Represents a single specific entity (e.g., `users`, `orders`, `courses`).\n* **Column (Field / Attribute):** A vertical category enforcing a **strict, uniform data type** across every entry (e.g., `age INTEGER`, `name VARCHAR`).\n* **Row (Record / Tuple):** A horizontal entry representing one complete entity instance (e.g., all profile details belonging to User #1).\n* **Primary Key (PK):** A unique identifier for each row in a table (e.g., `user_id`).\n* **Foreign Key (FK):** A field in one table (e.g., `orders.user_id`) that references the Primary Key of another table (`users.id`), establishing a **relationship**.	Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)	https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical	654	Lecture 1: Relation