21121	Lecture 8: Precision Filtering with WHERE — Comparison, Logical Operators & The Parentheses Trap	pg-lecture-8-where-clause-logical-operators-parentheses-trap	Filter rows using comparison and logical operators (AND, OR, NOT), understand operator precedence, avoid the Parentheses Trap, and handle NULL values properly.	2026-10-05 17:59:36.035454+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Lecture 8: Precision Filtering with WHERE — Comparison, Logical Operators & The Parentheses Trap\nPart of the **PostgreSQL & Relational SQL Mastery** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r1MNnkDwlik", "title": "PostgreSQL WHERE Clause Full Tutorial | Comparison, Logical Operators & Common Mistakes", "duration": "33m", "creator_name": "Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical"}]	[{"id": "pg-8-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "name": "Big Countries (OR Filtering)", "title": "Big Countries (OR Filtering)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Big Countries (OR Filtering)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/", "name": "Recyclable and Low Fat Products (AND Filtering)", "title": "Recyclable and Low Fat Products (AND Filtering)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Recyclable and Low Fat Products (AND Filtering)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "name": "Find Customer Referee (Not Equal & IS NULL)", "title": "Find Customer Referee (Not Equal & IS NULL)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Find Customer Referee (Not Equal & IS NULL)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/not-boring-movies/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/not-boring-movies/", "name": "Not Boring Movies (Logical AND & Inequality)", "title": "Not Boring Movies (Logical AND & Inequality)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Not Boring Movies (Logical AND & Inequality)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/not-boring-movies/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/triangle-judgement/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/triangle-judgement/", "name": "Triangle Judgement (Multi-Condition Inequalities)", "title": "Triangle Judgement (Multi-Condition Inequalities)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Triangle Judgement (Multi-Condition Inequalities)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/triangle-judgement/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "name": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "title": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Employees Earning More Than Their Managers\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/rising-temperature/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/rising-temperature/", "name": "Rising Temperature", "title": "Rising Temperature", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_7", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Rising Temperature\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/rising-temperature/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/classes-more-than-5-students/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/classes-more-than-5-students/", "name": "Classes More Than 5 Students", "title": "Classes More Than 5 Students", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_8", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Classes More Than 5 Students\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/classes-more-than-5-students/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customer-placing-the-largest-number-of-orders/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customer-placing-the-largest-number-of-orders/", "name": "Customer Placing the Largest Number of Orders", "title": "Customer Placing the Largest Number of Orders", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_9", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Customer Placing the Largest Number of Orders\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customer-placing-the-largest-number-of-orders/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/consecutive-numbers/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/consecutive-numbers/", "name": "Consecutive Numbers", "title": "Consecutive Numbers", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_10", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Consecutive Numbers\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/consecutive-numbers/"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThe `WHERE` clause acts as the gatekeeper of your query: PostgreSQL evaluates the boolean expression for every row and only includes rows that evaluate to `TRUE`.\n\n### ⚖️ Comparison & Logical Operators\n\n* **Comparison:** `=` — Exactly equals — `role = 'professor'`\n* **Comparison:** `!=` or `<>` — Does not equal — `department_id != 1`\n* **Comparison:** `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` — Less / Greater than (or equal) — `fee >= 4000`\n* **Logical:** `AND` — Both conditions must be TRUE — `is_active = true AND role = 'professor'`\n* **Logical:** `OR` — At least one condition must be TRUE — `role = 'admin' OR role = 'professor'`\n* **Logical:** `NOT` — Inverts boolean condition — `WHERE NOT status = 'inactive'`\n\n### 🚨 The Parentheses Trap (`AND` vs. `OR` Precedence)\nIn SQL, **`AND` has higher operator precedence than `OR`** (just like multiplication binds tighter than addition in math).\n\n**Scenario:** Fetch all **inactive** users (`is_active = false`) who belong to **either Department 1 OR Department 2**.\n\n```sql\n-- ❌ BROKEN QUERY (The Parentheses Trap):\n-- PostgreSQL evaluates (department_id = 2 AND is_active = false) first,\n-- returning ALL users from Department 1 even if they are active!\nSELECT name, department_id, is_active\nFROM users\nWHERE department_id = 1 OR department_id = 2 AND is_active = false;\n\n-- ✅ CORRECT QUERY:\n-- Explicit parentheses force OR evaluation first, then filter by is_active = false\nSELECT name, department_id, is_active\nFROM users\nWHERE (department_id = 1 OR department_id = 2)\n  AND is_active = false;\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Two Classic Interview Traps\n1. **String Literals Are Case-Sensitive:** Inside single quotes, `WHERE role = 'Admin'` will return 0 rows if the database stores `'admin'`.\n2. **NULLs Are Ghosts (`IS NULL` vs `= NULL`):** You can never use `= NULL` or `!= NULL` because `NULL` represents an unknown value. Always use:\n   ```sql\n   SELECT * FROM users WHERE department_id IS NULL;\n   SELECT * FROM users WHERE department_id IS NOT NULL;\n   ```	Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)	https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical	654	Lecture 8: Precision Filtering with WHERE — Comparison, Logical Operators & The Parentheses Trap	Filter rows using comparison and logical operators (AND, OR, NOT), understand operator precedence, avoid the Parentheses Trap, and handle NULL values properly.	33	Easy	Module 3: Filtering, Pattern Matching & Pagination	8	[{"id": "pg-8-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/", "name": "Big Countries (OR Filtering)", "title": "Big Countries (OR Filtering)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Big Countries (OR Filtering)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/big-countries/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/", "name": "Recyclable and Low Fat Products (AND Filtering)", "title": "Recyclable and Low Fat Products (AND Filtering)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Recyclable and Low Fat Products (AND Filtering)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "name": "Find Customer Referee (Not Equal & IS NULL)", "title": "Find Customer Referee (Not Equal & IS NULL)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Find Customer Referee (Not Equal & IS NULL)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/not-boring-movies/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/not-boring-movies/", "name": "Not Boring Movies (Logical AND & Inequality)", "title": "Not Boring Movies (Logical AND & Inequality)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Not Boring Movies (Logical AND & Inequality)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/not-boring-movies/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/triangle-judgement/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/triangle-judgement/", "name": "Triangle Judgement (Multi-Condition Inequalities)", "title": "Triangle Judgement (Multi-Condition Inequalities)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Triangle Judgement (Multi-Condition Inequalities)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/triangle-judgement/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "name": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "title": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Employees Earning More Than Their Managers\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/rising-temperature/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/rising-temperature/", "name": "Rising Temperature", "title": "Rising Temperature", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_7", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Rising Temperature\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/rising-temperature/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/classes-more-than-5-students/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/classes-more-than-5-students/", "name": "Classes More Than 5 Students", "title": "Classes More Than 5 Students", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_8", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Classes More Than 5 Students\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/classes-more-than-5-students/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customer-placing-the-largest-number-of-orders/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customer-placing-the-largest-number-of-orders/", "name": "Customer Placing the Largest Number of Orders", "title": "Customer Placing the Largest Number of Orders", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_9", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Customer Placing the Largest Number of Orders\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customer-placing-the-largest-number-of-orders/"}, {"id": "pg-8-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/consecutive-numbers/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/consecutive-numbers/", "name": "Consecutive Numbers", "title": "Consecutive Numbers", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "8_10", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Consecutive Numbers\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/consecutive-numbers/"}]	https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/queries-table-expressions.html	\N	\N	\N
22195	Logarithms	aptitude-quant-logarithms	Master exponential "Just Swap" conversion, power jumping rules, base changing identities, nested logarithmic cancellations, and computing digits using Mantissa/Characteristics.	2026-10-07 11:51:32.526467+05:30	### 📌 1. Fundamental Definition & "Just Swap" Method\n* **Exponent to Log Conversion:**\n  If **`a^m = x`**, then **`log_a(x) = m`**.\n  *The base `a` ALWAYS stays the base. The power `m` and outcome `x` simply swap sides across the equals sign!*\n* **Implicit Base:** If no base is written (e.g., `log(100)`), it is universally assumed to be **base 10**.\n\n### 💡 2. High-Yield Logarithmic Properties\n* **Identity Rules:**\n  * `log_a(1) = 0` (for any valid base `a`)\n  * `log_a(a) = 1`\n  * `log_a(0)` is **Undefined**.\n* **Product & Quotient Rules:**\n  * `log_a(x × y) = log_a(x) + log_a(y)`\n  * `log_a(x / y) = log_a(x) - log_a(y)`\n* **The Power Jumping Rules (CRITICAL!):**\n  * Power on the **Number**: `log_a(x^y) = y × log_a(x)` *(Power jumps out directly)*\n  * Power on the **Base**: `log_(a^b)(x) = (1/b) × log_a(x)` *(Power jumps out as an inverse/fraction!)*\n\n### 💡 3. Base Change and Characteristics\n* **Base Switch / Inverse Rule:** `log_a(x) = 1 / log_x(a)`\n* **Change of Base Rule:** `log_a(x) = log_c(x) / log_c(a)`\n* **Finding Number of Digits (Using Characteristics):**\n  * If `log(N) = C.M` (where `C` is the Characteristic integer and `M` is the Mantissa fraction).\n  * **Number of digits in `N` = `Characteristic + 1`**.\n  * Example: Number of digits in `4^10 × 6^10` → Compute `log(4^10 × 6^10)` → extract integer `C`, add 1.	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=w-7mbazOx6o", "type": "youtube", "title": "Logarithms - Shortcuts & Tricks for 2026 Placement Tests, Job Interviews & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-log-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Base Power Inversion] Find the exact value of log(3) to the base 81.", "options": ["A) 1/4", "B) 1/3", "C) 3", "D) 4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of:\\nlog(3) to the base 81", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "We need to evaluate log_81(3). Write the base 81 as a power of 3: 81 = 3^4. So, log_(3^4)(3). By the base power rule, the power 4 on the base comes out as an inverse (1/4). Thus, (1/4) × log_3(3) = (1/4) × 1 = 1/4.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-log-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Double Negative Power Evaluation] Find the exact value of log(1/343) to the base √7.", "options": ["A) -3", "B) -6", "C) -8", "D) -9"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of:\\nlog(1/343) to the base √7", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1/343 = 7^(-3). Base = 7^(1/2). Value = (-3) / (1/2) = -6. Option B = -6 is mathematically correct.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-log-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Negative Base Power Expansion] Find the value of log(0.0001) to the base 1000.", "options": ["A) -3/4", "B) -4/3", "C) 3/4", "D) 4/3"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of:\\nlog(0.0001) to the base 1000", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Number = 0.0001 = 10^(-4). Base = 1000 = 10^3. Expression = log_(10^3)(10^(-4)). The power -4 on the number comes out directly. The power 3 on the base comes out as an inverse (1/3). Result = (-4) × (1/3) × log_10(10) = -4/3.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-log-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Combining Log Products] Evaluate log(512 × 256 / 32) to the base 2.", "options": ["A) 12", "B) 14", "C) 15", "D) 16"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of the expression:\\nlog_2 [ (512 × 256) / 32 ]", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Convert all numbers to powers of 2. 512 = 2^9, 256 = 2^8, 32 = 2^5. Inside the log: (2^9 × 2^8) / 2^5 = 2^(9 + 8 - 5) = 2^12. The expression is log_2(2^12). The 12 jumps out: 12 × log_2(2) = 12 × 1 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-log-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Fractional Decimal Logarithm] Evaluate log(0.0625) to the base 2.", "options": ["A) -2", "B) -3", "C) -4", "D) -5"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of:\\nlog(0.0625) to the base 2", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Convert the decimal to a fraction: 0.0625 = 625 / 10000 = 1 / 16. Rewrite 1/16 as a power of 2: 1/16 = 1/(2^4) = 2^(-4). The expression is log_2(2^(-4)). The -4 comes out: -4 × log_2(2) = -4 × 1 = -4.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-log-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Equation Conversion \\"Just Swap\\"] If log(25/9) to the base y is equal to -2, find y.", "options": ["A) 3/5", "B) 5/3", "C) 9/25", "D) 25/9"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If log_y (25/9) = -2, what is the exact value of y?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Using the \\"Just Swap\\" conversion rule: y^(-2) = 25 / 9. This means 1 / y^2 = 25 / 9 => y^2 = 9 / 25. Taking the positive square root gives y = 3 / 5.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-log-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Simultaneous Exponential Substitution] If log_9(u) = 36 and log_3(v) = 6, find relation between u and v.", "options": ["A) u = v^6", "B) u = v^12", "C) u = v^18", "D) u = v^36"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If log_9(u) = 36 and log_3(v) = 6, what is the exact mathematical relation between u and v?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) From log_9(u) = 36, we swap to exponents: 9^36 = u. Since 9 = 3^2, u = (3^2)^36 = 3^72. 2) From log_3(v) = 6, we swap to exponents: 3^6 = v. 3) We want u in terms of v. u = 3^72 = (3^6)^12. Since 3^6 = v, u = v^12.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-log-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Implicit Base 10 Evaluation] Evaluate [10 log(10000)] / [5 log(100)].", "options": ["A) 2", "B) 4", "C) 8", "D) 10"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of the expression:\\n[ 10 × log(10000) ] / [ 5 × log(100) ]", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "When no base is specified, base 10 is implied. Numerator: 10 × log_10(10^4) = 10 × 4 × log_10(10) = 40 × 1 = 40. Denominator: 5 × log_10(10^2) = 5 × 2 × log_10(10) = 10 × 1 = 10. Result = 40 / 10 = 4.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-log-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Sum of First N Numbers in Power] Evaluate log_2 (2^1 × 2^2 × 2^3 × ... × 2^9).", "options": ["A) 36", "B) 45", "C) 55", "D) 90"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of the expression:\\nlog_2 (2^1 × 2^2 × 2^3 × ... × 2^9)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "The argument inside the log is 2^(1 + 2 + 3 + ... + 9). The sum of the first N natural numbers is N(N + 1)/2. For N=9, Sum = 9 × 10 / 2 = 45. The expression is log_2(2^45) = 45 × log_2(2) = 45.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-log-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Nested Logarithmic Undefined Trap] Evaluate log_2 (log_2 (log_3 3)).", "options": ["A) -1", "B) 0", "C) 1", "D) Undefined"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of the completely nested expression:\\nlog_2 [ log_2 ( log_3 (3) ) ]", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Solve from the inside out. 1) Innermost: log_3(3) = 1. 2) Next level: log_2(1). We know log_a(1) = 0 for any base. So log_2(1) = 0. 3) Outermost level: log_2(0). The logarithm of 0 is mathematically Undefined. Thus, the whole expression is Undefined.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-log-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Logarithmic Base Change Expansion] If log 2 = p and log 7 = q, find log_5 (28).", "options": ["A) (2p + q) / (1 - p)", "B) (p + 2q) / (1 - p)", "C) (2p + q) / (p - 1)", "D) (p + q) / (1 + p)"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given that log_10(2) = p and log_10(7) = q, what is the exact algebraic expression for log_5(28)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Change the base of log_5(28) to base 10 using the formula: log_10(28) / log_10(5). 2) Expand the numerator: log(28) = log(4 × 7) = log(2^2 × 7) = log(2^2) + log(7) = 2log(2) + log(7) = 2p + q. 3) Expand the denominator: log(5) = log(10 / 2) = log(10) - log(2) = 1 - p. 4) The final expression is (2p + q) / (1 - p).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-log-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Quadratic in Log Argument] If log(x^2 - 6x + 10) = 0, find x.", "options": ["A) 2", "B) 3", "C) 4", "D) 5"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If log(x^2 - 6x + 10) = 0, what is the exact numerical value of x?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "If log(expression) = 0, then the expression must equal 1 (since log(1) = 0 for any valid base). Thus, x^2 - 6x + 10 = 1 => x^2 - 6x + 9 = 0. This is a perfect square: (x - 3)^2 = 0. Therefore, x = 3.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-log-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Telescoping Logarithmic Product] Evaluate log_100(101) × log_101(102) × ... × log_104(105).", "options": ["A) P / 2", "B) 2P", "C) P^2", "D) P / 4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If log_10(105) = P, evaluate the exact value of the telescoping logarithmic product:\\nlog_100(101) × log_101(102) × log_102(103) × log_103(104) × log_104(105)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Using the change of base rule: log_a(b) = log(b)/log(a). The product becomes: [log(101)/log(100)] × [log(102)/log(101)] × ... × [log(105)/log(104)]. Everything telescopes (cancels out cross-wise) except log(105) in the numerator and log(100) in the denominator. Result = log(105) / log(100). We are given log(105) = P. And log(100) = log(10^2) = 2. So the result is exactly P / 2.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-log-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Logarithm of Inverse Identity] If log_10(11) = a, find log_10(1/110).", "options": ["A) - (a + 1)", "B) a - 1", "C) - (a - 1)", "D) 1 - a"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given that log_10(11) = a, what is the exact algebraic expression for log_10(1/110)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) log(1/110) = log(110^(-1)) = -1 × log(110). 2) log(110) = log(11 × 10) = log(11) + log(10). 3) We know log(11) = a and log(10) = 1. So log(110) = a + 1. 4) Thus, the value is - (a + 1).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-log-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Finding Value with Given Log Decimal] If log(2) = 0.3010, find log(1024).", "options": ["A) 2.4080", "B) 3.0100", "C) 3.3110", "D) 6.0200"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given that log_10(2) ≈ 0.3010, what is the exact numerical value of log_10(1024)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Express 1024 as a power of 2: 1024 = 2^10. Therefore, log(1024) = log(2^10) = 10 × log(2). Given log(2) = 0.3010, the value is 10 × 0.3010 = 3.0100.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-log-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Number of Digits from Characteristics] Find the number of digits in 4^10 × 6^10, given log(2)=0.3010 and log(3)=0.4771.", "options": ["A) 13 digits", "B) 14 digits", "C) 15 digits", "D) 16 digits"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given that log(2) = 0.3010 and log(3) = 0.4771, calculate the exact number of digits in the expanded integer value of (4^10 × 6^10).", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "To find the number of digits of N, find log(N) and add 1 to its characteristic. Let N = 4^10 × 6^10. log(N) = log((2^2)^10 × (2×3)^10) = log(2^20 × 2^10 × 3^10) = log(2^30 × 3^10) = 30log(2) + 10log(3). Substitute values: 30(0.3010) + 10(0.4771) = 9.030 + 4.771 = 13.801. The characteristic (integer part) is 13. Number of digits = Characteristic + 1 = 13 + 1 = 14 digits.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-log-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Solving Exponential Equations with Logs] If 2^(x+1) = 3^(x-1), express x in terms of logs.", "options": ["A) log(6) / log(1.5)", "B) log(5) / log(2)", "C) log(6) / log(2.5)", "D) log(1.5) / log(6)"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 2^(x+1) = 3^(x-1), which of the following is the correct exact expression for x in terms of logarithms?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Take log on both sides: (x + 1)log(2) = (x - 1)log(3) => x log(2) + log(2) = x log(3) - log(3). Rearrange to group x: log(2) + log(3) = x log(3) - x log(2) => log(2×3) = x(log(3) - log(2)) => log(6) = x log(3/2) => log(6) = x log(1.5). Thus, x = log(6) / log(1.5).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-log-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Sum of Logs of G.P.] Evaluate log(2) + log(4) + log(8) + ... to 10 terms.", "options": ["A) 45", "B) 50", "C) 55", "D) 100"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of the logarithmic series sum:\\nlog_2(2) + log_2(4) + log_2(8) + log_2(16) + ... up to exactly 10 terms.", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Write each argument as a power of 2: log_2(2^1) + log_2(2^2) + log_2(2^3) + ... + log_2(2^10). By power rule, this simplifies to 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + 10. The sum of the first 10 natural numbers is N(N+1)/2 = 10(11)/2 = 55.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-log-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [System of Log Equations] If log_x(y) = 2 and log_y(z) = 3, find log_z(x).", "options": ["A) 1/5", "B) 1/6", "C) 5", "D) 6"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If log_x(y) = 2 and log_y(z) = 3, what is the exact algebraic value of log_z(x)?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "By the \\"Just Swap\\" rule: 1) x^2 = y. 2) y^3 = z. Substitute y from (1) into (2): (x^2)^3 = z => x^6 = z. Convert this back to a log: log_x(z) = 6. By the base change inverse rule: log_z(x) = 1 / log_x(z) = 1 / 6.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-log-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Simplifying Complex Log Fractions] Evaluate [log(125) - log(25)] / log(5).", "options": ["A) 1", "B) 2", "C) 3", "D) 5"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of the expression:\\n[ log_10(125) - log_10(25) ] / log_10(5)", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Using the quotient rule on the numerator: log(125) - log(25) = log(125 / 25) = log(5). The expression simplifies to log(5) / log(5) = 1. (Alternatively, log(5^3) - log(5^2) = 3log(5) - 2log(5) = 1log(5). log(5)/log(5) = 1).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. Fundamental Definition & "Just Swap" Method\n* **Exponent to Log Conversion:**\n  If **`a^m = x`**, then **`log_a(x) = m`**.\n  *The base `a` ALWAYS stays the base. The power `m` and outcome `x` simply swap sides across the equals sign!*\n* **Implicit Base:** If no base is written (e.g., `log(100)`), it is universally assumed to be **base 10**.\n\n### 💡 2. High-Yield Logarithmic Properties\n* **Identity Rules:**\n  * `log_a(1) = 0` (for any valid base `a`)\n  * `log_a(a) = 1`\n  * `log_a(0)` is **Undefined**.\n* **Product & Quotient Rules:**\n  * `log_a(x × y) = log_a(x) + log_a(y)`\n  * `log_a(x / y) = log_a(x) - log_a(y)`\n* **The Power Jumping Rules (CRITICAL!):**\n  * Power on the **Number**: `log_a(x^y) = y × log_a(x)` *(Power jumps out directly)*\n  * Power on the **Base**: `log_(a^b)(x) = (1/b) × log_a(x)` *(Power jumps out as an inverse/fraction!)*\n\n### 💡 3. Base Change and Characteristics\n* **Base Switch / Inverse Rule:** `log_a(x) = 1 / log_x(a)`\n* **Change of Base Rule:** `log_a(x) = log_c(x) / log_c(a)`\n* **Finding Number of Digits (Using Characteristics):**\n  * If `log(N) = C.M` (where `C` is the Characteristic integer and `M` is the Mantissa fraction).\n  * **Number of digits in `N` = `Characteristic + 1`**.\n  * Example: Number of digits in `4^10 × 6^10` → Compute `log(4^10 × 6^10)` → extract integer `C`, add 1.	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Logarithms	Master exponential "Just Swap" conversion, power jumping rules, base changing identities, nested logarithmic cancellations, and computing digits using Mantissa/Characteristics.	63	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	29	[{"id": "apt-log-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Base Power Inversion] Find the exact value of log(3) to the base 81.", "options": ["A) 1/4", "B) 1/3", "C) 3", "D) 4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of:\\nlog(3) to the base 81", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "We need to evaluate log_81(3). Write the base 81 as a power of 3: 81 = 3^4. So, log_(3^4)(3). By the base power rule, the power 4 on the base comes out as an inverse (1/4). Thus, (1/4) × log_3(3) = (1/4) × 1 = 1/4.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-log-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Double Negative Power Evaluation] Find the exact value of log(1/343) to the base √7.", "options": ["A) -3", "B) -6", "C) -8", "D) -9"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of:\\nlog(1/343) to the base √7", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1/343 = 7^(-3). Base = 7^(1/2). Value = (-3) / (1/2) = -6. Option B = -6 is mathematically correct.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-log-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Negative Base Power Expansion] Find the value of log(0.0001) to the base 1000.", "options": ["A) -3/4", "B) -4/3", "C) 3/4", "D) 4/3"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of:\\nlog(0.0001) to the base 1000", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Number = 0.0001 = 10^(-4). Base = 1000 = 10^3. Expression = log_(10^3)(10^(-4)). The power -4 on the number comes out directly. The power 3 on the base comes out as an inverse (1/3). Result = (-4) × (1/3) × log_10(10) = -4/3.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-log-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Combining Log Products] Evaluate log(512 × 256 / 32) to the base 2.", "options": ["A) 12", "B) 14", "C) 15", "D) 16"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of the expression:\\nlog_2 [ (512 × 256) / 32 ]", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Convert all numbers to powers of 2. 512 = 2^9, 256 = 2^8, 32 = 2^5. Inside the log: (2^9 × 2^8) / 2^5 = 2^(9 + 8 - 5) = 2^12. The expression is log_2(2^12). The 12 jumps out: 12 × log_2(2) = 12 × 1 = 12.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-log-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Fractional Decimal Logarithm] Evaluate log(0.0625) to the base 2.", "options": ["A) -2", "B) -3", "C) -4", "D) -5"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of:\\nlog(0.0625) to the base 2", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Convert the decimal to a fraction: 0.0625 = 625 / 10000 = 1 / 16. Rewrite 1/16 as a power of 2: 1/16 = 1/(2^4) = 2^(-4). The expression is log_2(2^(-4)). The -4 comes out: -4 × log_2(2) = -4 × 1 = -4.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-log-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Equation Conversion \\"Just Swap\\"] If log(25/9) to the base y is equal to -2, find y.", "options": ["A) 3/5", "B) 5/3", "C) 9/25", "D) 25/9"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If log_y (25/9) = -2, what is the exact value of y?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Using the \\"Just Swap\\" conversion rule: y^(-2) = 25 / 9. This means 1 / y^2 = 25 / 9 => y^2 = 9 / 25. Taking the positive square root gives y = 3 / 5.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-log-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Simultaneous Exponential Substitution] If log_9(u) = 36 and log_3(v) = 6, find relation between u and v.", "options": ["A) u = v^6", "B) u = v^12", "C) u = v^18", "D) u = v^36"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If log_9(u) = 36 and log_3(v) = 6, what is the exact mathematical relation between u and v?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) From log_9(u) = 36, we swap to exponents: 9^36 = u. Since 9 = 3^2, u = (3^2)^36 = 3^72. 2) From log_3(v) = 6, we swap to exponents: 3^6 = v. 3) We want u in terms of v. u = 3^72 = (3^6)^12. Since 3^6 = v, u = v^12.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-log-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Implicit Base 10 Evaluation] Evaluate [10 log(10000)] / [5 log(100)].", "options": ["A) 2", "B) 4", "C) 8", "D) 10"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of the expression:\\n[ 10 × log(10000) ] / [ 5 × log(100) ]", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "When no base is specified, base 10 is implied. Numerator: 10 × log_10(10^4) = 10 × 4 × log_10(10) = 40 × 1 = 40. Denominator: 5 × log_10(10^2) = 5 × 2 × log_10(10) = 10 × 1 = 10. Result = 40 / 10 = 4.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-log-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Sum of First N Numbers in Power] Evaluate log_2 (2^1 × 2^2 × 2^3 × ... × 2^9).", "options": ["A) 36", "B) 45", "C) 55", "D) 90"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of the expression:\\nlog_2 (2^1 × 2^2 × 2^3 × ... × 2^9)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "The argument inside the log is 2^(1 + 2 + 3 + ... + 9). The sum of the first N natural numbers is N(N + 1)/2. For N=9, Sum = 9 × 10 / 2 = 45. The expression is log_2(2^45) = 45 × log_2(2) = 45.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-log-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Nested Logarithmic Undefined Trap] Evaluate log_2 (log_2 (log_3 3)).", "options": ["A) -1", "B) 0", "C) 1", "D) Undefined"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of the completely nested expression:\\nlog_2 [ log_2 ( log_3 (3) ) ]", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Solve from the inside out. 1) Innermost: log_3(3) = 1. 2) Next level: log_2(1). We know log_a(1) = 0 for any base. So log_2(1) = 0. 3) Outermost level: log_2(0). The logarithm of 0 is mathematically Undefined. Thus, the whole expression is Undefined.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-log-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Logarithmic Base Change Expansion] If log 2 = p and log 7 = q, find log_5 (28).", "options": ["A) (2p + q) / (1 - p)", "B) (p + 2q) / (1 - p)", "C) (2p + q) / (p - 1)", "D) (p + q) / (1 + p)"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given that log_10(2) = p and log_10(7) = q, what is the exact algebraic expression for log_5(28)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Change the base of log_5(28) to base 10 using the formula: log_10(28) / log_10(5). 2) Expand the numerator: log(28) = log(4 × 7) = log(2^2 × 7) = log(2^2) + log(7) = 2log(2) + log(7) = 2p + q. 3) Expand the denominator: log(5) = log(10 / 2) = log(10) - log(2) = 1 - p. 4) The final expression is (2p + q) / (1 - p).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-log-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Quadratic in Log Argument] If log(x^2 - 6x + 10) = 0, find x.", "options": ["A) 2", "B) 3", "C) 4", "D) 5"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If log(x^2 - 6x + 10) = 0, what is the exact numerical value of x?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "If log(expression) = 0, then the expression must equal 1 (since log(1) = 0 for any valid base). Thus, x^2 - 6x + 10 = 1 => x^2 - 6x + 9 = 0. This is a perfect square: (x - 3)^2 = 0. Therefore, x = 3.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-log-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Telescoping Logarithmic Product] Evaluate log_100(101) × log_101(102) × ... × log_104(105).", "options": ["A) P / 2", "B) 2P", "C) P^2", "D) P / 4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If log_10(105) = P, evaluate the exact value of the telescoping logarithmic product:\\nlog_100(101) × log_101(102) × log_102(103) × log_103(104) × log_104(105)", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Using the change of base rule: log_a(b) = log(b)/log(a). The product becomes: [log(101)/log(100)] × [log(102)/log(101)] × ... × [log(105)/log(104)]. Everything telescopes (cancels out cross-wise) except log(105) in the numerator and log(100) in the denominator. Result = log(105) / log(100). We are given log(105) = P. And log(100) = log(10^2) = 2. So the result is exactly P / 2.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-log-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Logarithm of Inverse Identity] If log_10(11) = a, find log_10(1/110).", "options": ["A) - (a + 1)", "B) a - 1", "C) - (a - 1)", "D) 1 - a"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given that log_10(11) = a, what is the exact algebraic expression for log_10(1/110)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) log(1/110) = log(110^(-1)) = -1 × log(110). 2) log(110) = log(11 × 10) = log(11) + log(10). 3) We know log(11) = a and log(10) = 1. So log(110) = a + 1. 4) Thus, the value is - (a + 1).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-log-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Finding Value with Given Log Decimal] If log(2) = 0.3010, find log(1024).", "options": ["A) 2.4080", "B) 3.0100", "C) 3.3110", "D) 6.0200"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given that log_10(2) ≈ 0.3010, what is the exact numerical value of log_10(1024)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Express 1024 as a power of 2: 1024 = 2^10. Therefore, log(1024) = log(2^10) = 10 × log(2). Given log(2) = 0.3010, the value is 10 × 0.3010 = 3.0100.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-log-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Number of Digits from Characteristics] Find the number of digits in 4^10 × 6^10, given log(2)=0.3010 and log(3)=0.4771.", "options": ["A) 13 digits", "B) 14 digits", "C) 15 digits", "D) 16 digits"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given that log(2) = 0.3010 and log(3) = 0.4771, calculate the exact number of digits in the expanded integer value of (4^10 × 6^10).", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "To find the number of digits of N, find log(N) and add 1 to its characteristic. Let N = 4^10 × 6^10. log(N) = log((2^2)^10 × (2×3)^10) = log(2^20 × 2^10 × 3^10) = log(2^30 × 3^10) = 30log(2) + 10log(3). Substitute values: 30(0.3010) + 10(0.4771) = 9.030 + 4.771 = 13.801. The characteristic (integer part) is 13. Number of digits = Characteristic + 1 = 13 + 1 = 14 digits.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-log-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Solving Exponential Equations with Logs] If 2^(x+1) = 3^(x-1), express x in terms of logs.", "options": ["A) log(6) / log(1.5)", "B) log(5) / log(2)", "C) log(6) / log(2.5)", "D) log(1.5) / log(6)"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If 2^(x+1) = 3^(x-1), which of the following is the correct exact expression for x in terms of logarithms?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Take log on both sides: (x + 1)log(2) = (x - 1)log(3) => x log(2) + log(2) = x log(3) - log(3). Rearrange to group x: log(2) + log(3) = x log(3) - x log(2) => log(2×3) = x(log(3) - log(2)) => log(6) = x log(3/2) => log(6) = x log(1.5). Thus, x = log(6) / log(1.5).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-log-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Sum of Logs of G.P.] Evaluate log(2) + log(4) + log(8) + ... to 10 terms.", "options": ["A) 45", "B) 50", "C) 55", "D) 100"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of the logarithmic series sum:\\nlog_2(2) + log_2(4) + log_2(8) + log_2(16) + ... up to exactly 10 terms.", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Write each argument as a power of 2: log_2(2^1) + log_2(2^2) + log_2(2^3) + ... + log_2(2^10). By power rule, this simplifies to 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + 10. The sum of the first 10 natural numbers is N(N+1)/2 = 10(11)/2 = 55.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-log-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [System of Log Equations] If log_x(y) = 2 and log_y(z) = 3, find log_z(x).", "options": ["A) 1/5", "B) 1/6", "C) 5", "D) 6"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If log_x(y) = 2 and log_y(z) = 3, what is the exact algebraic value of log_z(x)?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "By the \\"Just Swap\\" rule: 1) x^2 = y. 2) y^3 = z. Substitute y from (1) into (2): (x^2)^3 = z => x^6 = z. Convert this back to a log: log_x(z) = 6. By the base change inverse rule: log_z(x) = 1 / log_x(z) = 1 / 6.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-log-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Simplifying Complex Log Fractions] Evaluate [log(125) - log(25)] / log(5).", "options": ["A) 1", "B) 2", "C) 3", "D) 5"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Evaluate the exact numerical value of the expression:\\n[ log_10(125) - log_10(25) ] / log_10(5)", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Using the quotient rule on the numerator: log(125) - log(25) = log(125 / 25) = log(5). The expression simplifies to log(5) / log(5) = 1. (Alternatively, log(5^3) - log(5^2) = 3log(5) - 2log(5) = 1log(5). log(5)/log(5) = 1).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
21124	Lecture 11: Data Integrity & Constraints — NOT NULL, UNIQUE, CHECK & ALTER TABLE	pg-lecture-11-constraints-not-null-unique-check-alter-table	Enforce strict schema rules on existing tables using ALTER TABLE, handle the Existing Data Trap when adding NOT NULL, and create named UNIQUE and CHECK constraints.	2026-10-05 17:59:36.035454+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Lecture 11: Data Integrity & Constraints — NOT NULL, UNIQUE, CHECK & ALTER TABLE\nPart of the **PostgreSQL & Relational SQL Mastery** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SKhrfndLAP0", "title": "PostgreSQL Constraints Explained | NOT NULL, UNIQUE, CHECK", "duration": "25m", "creator_name": "Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical"}]	[{"id": "pg-11-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "name": "Delete Duplicate Emails (Uniqueness Integrity)", "title": "Delete Duplicate Emails (Uniqueness Integrity)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "11_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Delete Duplicate Emails (Uniqueness Integrity)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/"}, {"id": "pg-11-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/primary-department-for-each-employee/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/primary-department-for-each-employee/", "name": "Primary Department for Each Employee", "title": "Primary Department for Each Employee", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "11_2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Primary Department for Each Employee\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/primary-department-for-each-employee/"}, {"id": "pg-11-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/triangle-judgement/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/triangle-judgement/", "name": "Triangle Judgement (Domain Check Logic)", "title": "Triangle Judgement (Domain Check Logic)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "11_3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Triangle Judgement (Domain Check Logic)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/triangle-judgement/"}, {"id": "pg-11-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-with-missing-information/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-with-missing-information/", "name": "Employees With Missing Information (NULL Integrity Check)", "title": "Employees With Missing Information (NULL Integrity Check)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "11_4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Employees With Missing Information (NULL Integrity Check)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-with-missing-information/"}, {"id": "pg-11-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-users-with-valid-e-mails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-users-with-valid-e-mails/", "name": "Find Users With Valid E-Mails", "title": "Find Users With Valid E-Mails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "11_5", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Find Users With Valid E-Mails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-users-with-valid-e-mails/"}, {"id": "pg-11-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/", "name": "Invalid Tweets", "title": "Invalid Tweets", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "11_6", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Invalid Tweets\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invalid-tweets/"}, {"id": "pg-11-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/product-sales-analysis-iii/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/product-sales-analysis-iii/", "name": "Product Sales Analysis III", "title": "Product Sales Analysis III", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "11_7", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Product Sales Analysis III\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/product-sales-analysis-iii/"}, {"id": "pg-11-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/confirmation-rate/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/confirmation-rate/", "name": "Confirmation Rate", "title": "Confirmation Rate", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "11_8", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Confirmation Rate\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/confirmation-rate/"}, {"id": "pg-11-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/", "name": "Recyclable and Low Fat Products", "title": "Recyclable and Low Fat Products", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "11_9", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Recyclable and Low Fat Products\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recyclable-and-low-fat-products/"}, {"id": "pg-11-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/", "name": "Find Customer Referee", "title": "Find Customer Referee", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "11_10", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Find Customer Referee\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-customer-referee/"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nApplication-level validation (like Zod or Express middleware) can be bypassed if another script or service writes directly to the database. **PostgreSQL Constraints** enforce ironclad data rules at the database storage engine level.\n\n### 🛡️ 1. Adding `NOT NULL` & "The Existing Data Trap"\nIf a table already contains rows where a column is `NULL`, running `SET NOT NULL` immediately fails because existing data violates the new rule. Always backfill `NULL` rows first:\n\n```sql\n-- Step 1: Backfill any existing NULL values first\nUPDATE users\nSET is_active = true\nWHERE is_active IS NULL;\n\n-- Step 2: Alter the column to enforce NOT NULL\nALTER TABLE users\nALTER COLUMN is_active SET NOT NULL;\n```\n\n### 🔑 2. Adding a Column & Enforcing a Named `UNIQUE` Constraint\nAlways give your constraints explicit, readable names so error logs immediately tell your backend which rule failed:\n\n```sql\n-- Add a new column to an existing table\nALTER TABLE users\nADD COLUMN phone_number VARCHAR(20);\n\n-- Add a named UNIQUE constraint\nALTER TABLE users\nADD CONSTRAINT unique_phone_rule UNIQUE (phone_number);\n```\n\n### ✅ 3. Enforcing Business Logic with `CHECK` Constraints\nUse `CHECK` to validate boolean expressions (e.g., guaranteeing payment amounts are strictly positive):\n\n```sql\nALTER TABLE payments\nADD CONSTRAINT positive_payment_check CHECK (amount > 0);\n```\n\n### 🔍 4. Inspecting & Dropping Constraints\n```sql\n-- Inspect all active constraints on the 'users' table\nSELECT constraint_name, column_name\nFROM information_schema.constraint_column_usage\nWHERE table_name = 'users';\n\n-- Drop a constraint by name\nALTER TABLE users\nDROP CONSTRAINT unique_phone_rule;\n```	Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)	https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical	654	Lecture 11: Data Integrity & Constraints — NOT NULL, UNIQUE, CHECK & ALTER TABLE	Enforce strict schema rules on existing tables using ALTER TABLE, handle the Existing Data Trap when adding NOT NULL, and create named UNIQUE and CHECK constraints.	25	Easy	Module 4: Schema Integrity, Keys & Relationships	11	[{"id": "pg-11-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "name": "Delete Duplicate Emails (Uniqueness Integrity)", "title": "Delete Duplicate Emails (Uniqueness Integrity)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "11_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Delete Duplicate Emails (Uniqueness Integrity)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/"}, {"id": "pg-11-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/primary-department-for-each-employee/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/primary-department-for-each-employee/", "name": "Primary Department for Each Employee", "title": "Primary Department for Each Employee", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "11_2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Primary Department for Each Employee\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/primary-department-for-each-employee/"}, {"id": "pg-11-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/triangle-judgement/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/triangle-judgement/", "name": "Triangle Judgement (Domain Check Logic)", "title": "Triangle Judgement (Domain Check Logic)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "11_3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Triangle Judgement (Domain Check Logic)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/triangle-judgement/"}, {"id": "pg-11-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-with-missing-information