10790	Circular Tail-to-Head Ring Splicing: Rotate a Linked List by K Places	dsa-rotate-a-linked-list	Rotate a linked list by k positions by connecting tail to head into a ring, reducing k = k % len, and breaking the link at the (len - k)-th node in O(N) time.	2026-10-03 15:07:44.964082+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Circular Tail-to-Head Ring Splicing: Rotate a Linked List by K Places\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uT7YI7XbTY8", "title": "Lecture 258: Rotate a Linked List", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/rotate-list/", "title": "Rotate List (LC 61)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given the head of a linked list, rotate the list to the right by k places."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/rotate-a-linked-list/1", "title": "Rotate a Linked List", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Rotate a singly linked list by k nodes in O(N) time and O(1) space."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/split-linked-list-in-parts/", "title": "Split Linked List in Parts (LC 725)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Practice length-based index arithmetic and tail severing in singly linked lists."}]	Module 38: Doubly Linked List Medium Problems & Hard Linked List Re-Wiring (K-Group Reversal, Rotation & Multi-Level Flattening)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the `head` of a singly linked list of length `len` and a non-negative integer `k`, **rotate the list to the right by `k` places** (moving the last `k % len` nodes to the front) in **`O(N)` time and `O(1)` space**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **1. Modulo Reduction for Large `k` (`k = k % len`):**\n  - Rotating a list of length `5` by `5`, `10`, or `15` times brings it right back to its original configuration!\n  - Therefore:\n    - If **`k % len == 0`**, no rotation is needed → **return `head` immediately**!\n    - Otherwise, reduce **`k = k % len`**.\n- **2. The Circular Ring Cut Technique (`len - k`-th Node Becomes the New Tail):**\n  1. Walk `tail` from `head` to the last node (`while (tail->next != nullptr)`), counting `len` (starting at `len = 1`).\n  2. Check `if (k % len == 0) return head;` and set `k = k % len;`.\n  3. **Connect the Tail to the Head:** Set **`tail->next = head;`** (forming a temporary circular ring!).\n  4. **Find the New Last Node (`(len - k)`-th node from `head`):** Walk to the `(len - k)`-th node `newLastNode`.\n  5. **Break the Ring:** Set **`head = newLastNode->next;`** and **`newLastNode->next = nullptr;`**!\n\n### 💻 Rotate a Linked List Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nNode* findNthNode(Node* temp, int k) {\n    int cnt = 1;\n    while (temp != nullptr) {\n        if (cnt == k) return temp;\n        cnt++;\n        temp = temp->next;\n    }\n    return temp;\n}\n\nNode* rotateRight(Node* head, int k) {\n    if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr || k == 0) return head;\n\n    // 1. Find length and locate the current tail node\n    Node* tail = head;\n    int len = 1;\n    while (tail->next != nullptr) {\n        tail = tail->next;\n        len++;\n    }\n\n    // 2. Handle multiples of len\n    if (k % len == 0) return head;\n    k = k % len;\n\n    // 3. Attach tail to head, find the (len - k)-th node, and break the ring!\n    tail->next = head;\n    Node* newLastNode = findNthNode(head, len - k);\n\n    head = newLastNode->next;\n    newLastNode->next = nullptr;\n\n    return head;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Simulating `k` Rotations One by One:** When `k = 2 * 10^9` (as on LeetCode 61), rotating 1 step at a time causes Time Limit Exceeded! Reducing `k = k % len` upfront guarantees at most `2` linear passes regardless of how huge `k` is.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
