utube.com/@takeUforward	1	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
11586	Bidirectional Navigation State Machine: Design a Browser History Using a Doubly Linked List	dsa-design-browser-history-doubly-linked-list	Master Bidirectional Navigation State Machine: Design a Browser History Using a Doubly Linked List with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:12:24.300227+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Bidirectional Navigation State Machine: Design a Browser History Using a Doubly Linked List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mG3KLugbOdc", "title": "Bidirectional Navigation State Machine: Design a Browser History Using a Doubly Linked List - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/design-browser-history/", "title": "LeetCode 1472: Design Browser History", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Implement BrowserHistory(homepage), visit(url), back(steps), and forward(steps)."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/design-linked-list/", "title": "LeetCode 707: Design Linked List", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Design and implement a custom Doubly Linked List class from scratch."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/design-a-text-editor/", "title": "LeetCode 2296: Design a Text Editor", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Use bidirectional pointer movement to simulate cursor left/right movement and text insertion/deletion."}]	Module 38: Doubly Linked List Medium Problems & Hard Linked List Re-Wiring (K-Group Reversal, Rotation & Multi-Level Flattening)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nDesign a **Browser History** system for a single tab that starts on a `homepage` and supports:\n1. **`visit(string url)`**: Visits `url` from the current page and **clears all forward history**.\n2. **`back(int steps)`**: Moves `steps` back in history (or as far back as the `homepage` if `steps` exceeds available history) and returns the current URL.\n3. **`forward(int steps)`**: Moves `steps` forward in history (or as far forward as possible) and returns the current URL.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why a Doubly Linked List (`Node` with `string data`, `Node* next`, `Node* back`) Is Ideal:**\n  - Maintain a single pointer **`Node* currentPage;`**.\n  - **Constructor `Browser(homepage)` (`O(1)`):** Set `currentPage = new Node(homepage);`.\n  - **`visit(url)` (`O(1)`):**\n    - Allocate `Node* newNode = new Node(url);`.\n    - Link **`newNode->back = currentPage;`** and **`currentPage->next = newNode;`** (overwriting `currentPage->next` automatically detaches all old forward history!).\n    - Advance **`currentPage = newNode;`**.\n  - **`back(steps)` (`O(steps)`):**\n    - Loop `while (steps > 0 && currentPage->back != nullptr)`: move `currentPage = currentPage->back;` and `steps--;`. Return `currentPage->data`.\n  - **`forward(steps)` (`O(steps)`):**\n    - Loop `while (steps > 0 && currentPage->next != nullptr)`: move `currentPage = currentPage->next;` and `steps--;`. Return `currentPage->data`.\n\n### 💻 Design Browser History (Doubly Linked List) Blueprint\n```cpp\nclass BrowserNode {\npublic:\n    string data;\n    BrowserNode* next;\n    BrowserNode* back;\n\n    BrowserNode(string x) : data(x), next(nullptr), back(nullptr) {}\n};\n\nclass BrowserHistory {\n    BrowserNode* currentPage;\n\npublic:\n    BrowserHistory(string homepage) {\n        currentPage = new BrowserNode(homepage);\n    }\n\n    void visit(string url) {\n        BrowserNode* newNode = new BrowserNode(url);\n        currentPage->next = newNode; // Detaches any old forward history\n        newNode->back = currentPage;\n        currentPage = newNode;\n    }\n\n    string back(int steps) {\n        while (steps > 0) {\n            if (currentPage->back != nullptr) {\n                currentPage = currentPage->back;\n            } else {\n                break; // Reached homepage; cannot go back further\n            }\n            steps--;\n        }\n        return currentPage->data;\n    }\n\n    string forward(int steps) {\n        while (steps > 0) {\n            if (currentPage->next != nullptr) {\n                currentPage = currentPage->next;\n            } else {\n                break; // Reached latest page; cannot go forward further\n            }\n            steps--;\n        }\n        return currentPage->data;\n    }\n};\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting `newNode->back = currentPage;` in `visit(url)`:** If you only set `currentPage->next = newNode` without setting `newNode->back = currentPage`, subsequent `back()` calls will hit `nullptr` immediately!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
7019	2D DP Table Backtracking: Print Longest Common Subsequence	dsa-print-longest-common-subsequence	Backtrack through the completed 2D LCS dynamic programming table to reconstruct and output the actual longest common subsequence string.	2026-10-03 11:31:13.747352+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: 2D DP Table Backtracking: Print Longest Common Subsequence\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-zI4mrF2Pb4", "title": "Lecture 207: Print Longest Common Subsequence (DP 26)", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/print-all-lcs-sequences3413/1", "title": "Print all LCS sequences", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Reconstruct the Longest Common Subsequence string(s) from the 2D LCS tabulation table."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/shortest-common-supersequence/", "title": "Shortest Common Supersequence (LC 1092)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Use the exact same 2D LCS table backtracking from (n, m) to (0, 0) to build the Shortest Common Supersequence."}, {"url": "https://atcoder.jp/contests/dp/tasks/dp_f", "title": "LCS (DP Contest F)", "platform": "AtCoder", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find and print any one Longest Common Subsequence of strings s and t."}]	Module 31: Dynamic Programming on Strings: LCS, Substrings & Palindromic Subsequences	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven two strings `s1` and `s2`, reconstruct and **return the actual Longest Common Subsequence string** (not just its length!).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **How to Backtrack the 2D `dp[n + 1][m + 1]` Table:**\n  1. First, build the full 2D LCS table `dp[n + 1][m + 1]`. The length of the LCS is `len = dp[n][m]`.\n  2. Pre-allocate a result string `ans` of size `len` filled with dummy characters (`''`), and set `index = len - 1` (filling from back to front in `O(1)` without string reversal!).\n  3. Start at the bottom-right cell **`i = n, j = m`**, and loop **`while (i > 0 && j > 0)`**:\n     - **If `s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]`:** Where did `dp[i][j]` come from? It came diagonally from `(i - 1, j - 1)`! Place `ans[index--] = s1[i - 1]`, and move diagonally: **`i--, j--`**.\n     - **Else if `dp[i - 1][j] > dp[i][j - 1]`:** Move Up: **`i--`**.\n     - **Else:** Move Left: **`j--`**.\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * M)` Time (to build table + `O(N + M)` to backtrack) and `O(N * M)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Print Longest Common Subsequence Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nstring findLCSString(int n, int m, string &s1, string &s2) {\n    vector<vector<int>> dp(n + 1, vector<int>(m + 1, 0));\n\n    // 1. Build the 2D LCS DP Table\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {\n            if (s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]) {\n                dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i - 1][j - 1];\n            } else {\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // 2. Backtrack from (n, m) to reconstruct the LCS string\n    int len = dp[n][m];\n    string ans(len, '');\n    int index = len - 1;\n    int i = n, j = m;\n\n    while (i > 0 && j > 0) {\n        if (s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]) {\n            ans[index] = s1[i - 1];\n            index--;\n            i--;\n            j--;\n        } else if (dp[i - 1][j] > dp[i][j - 1]) {\n            i--;\n        } else {\n            j--;\n        }\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Trying to Reconstruct the LCS String Using Only 1D Space-Optimized Arrays:** To trace the path from `(n, m)` back to `(0, 0)`, you need the full 2D `dp[n + 1][m + 1]` table in memory!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22242	LLD Project: Payment Gateway	lld-project-payment-gateway	Architect a robust Payment Gateway bridging multiple vendor APIs (Razorpay, Stripe) using Proxy, Template Method, and Factory patterns with native retry mechanisms.	2026-10-07 17:16:35.515424+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: LLD Project: Payment Gateway\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/36FDqIRBGRg", "title": "Build Payment Gateway System | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v23-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Retry Logic via Proxy vs Client Orchestration", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf the `CheckoutController` implements a `for` loop to retry failures, it becomes tightly coupled to the volatility of the external network. \\nFurthermore, different gateways require different retry policies: Paytm might permit 3 rapid retries, while Razorpay mandates exponential backoff.\\n\\nBy placing the retry logic inside a `PaymentGatewayProxy`, the client simply calls `gateway->processPayment()`. The Proxy traps failures and retries internally according to vendor-specific logic. The client code remains pristine, dealing only with the ultimate boolean success/failure of the transaction.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is it an architectural anti-pattern to place payment network retry logic (e.g., 3 retries on timeout) inside the client Application Controller instead of a Proxy?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v23-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Standardizing Flow with Template Method", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nFinancial operations cannot be executed out of order (e.g., you cannot confirm a payment before validating the account balance).\\n\\nThe base `PaymentGateway` class establishes a `final` Template Method:\\n```cpp\\nbool processPayment(Request req) {\\n    if (!validate(req)) return false;\\n    if (!initiate(req)) return false;\\n    return confirm(req);\\n}\\n```\\nWhile the `PaytmGateway` subclass implements `validate()` using Paytm's specific token SDK and `RazorpayGateway` uses Stripe's Secret Key SDK, neither subclass can override the core `processPayment` routing, guaranteeing structural security.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Payment Gateway LLD utilize the Template Method pattern to enforce financial security flows across disparate third-party vendors?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v23-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Dynamic Gateway Instantiation via Factory", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n```cpp\\nclass GatewayFactory {\\npublic:\\n    static IPaymentGateway* getGateway(GatewayType type) {\\n        IPaymentGateway* realGateway = nullptr;\\n        int retries = 0;\\n\\n        if (type == PAYTM) {\\n            realGateway = new PaytmGateway(new PaytmBankingSystem());\\n            retries = 3;\\n        } else if (type == RAZORPAY) {\\n            realGateway = new RazorpayGateway(new RazorpayBankingSystem());\\n            retries = 1;\\n        }\\n\\n        // Wrap in Proxy before returning to client\\n        return new PaymentGatewayProxy(realGateway, retries);\\n    }\\n};\\n```\\nThe Client receives what it believes is a standard `IPaymentGateway`, oblivious that it is actually a proxy shield.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Design the GatewayFactory logic. How does the factory seamlessly wrap the concrete gateway inside the Retry Proxy before returning it to the client?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v23-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Gateway vs Adapter Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe architectural intent of a \\"Gateway\\" is identical to an Adapter: it translates an internal domain model into an external, incompatible vendor format.\\n\\nThe core application generates an internal `PaymentRequest` object (amount, internal user ID). The `RazorpayGateway` (acting as the Adapter) intercepts this internal model, transforms it into the specific JSON/XML payload required by Razorpay's REST API, and translates the webhook response back into a standard internal boolean.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In this context, the PaymentGateway acts as an external boundary to the application. Structurally, how does this mirror the Adapter Pattern?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v23-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Handling In-Flight Reversals (2-Phase Commit context)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThis is a classic distributed transaction failure. The Gateway cannot simply return `false`, as the user's bank account has already been debited.\\n\\nArchitecturally, the Gateway must implement a **Compensating Transaction** (Saga Pattern paradigm):\\n1. The `PaymentGateway` must possess a `rollback()` or `refund()` method.\\n2. Inside the template method, if `initiate()` succeeds but `confirm()` throws a timeout exception, a `catch` block intercepts it.\\n3. The catch block actively fires the `rollback()` API to the vendor, instructing them to reverse the capture.\\n4. Only if the rollback acknowledges successfully does the system return `false` to the client, guaranteeing ledger consistency.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "If initiate() succeeds but confirm() fails due to a network partition, how should the Gateway architecturally handle the money that was already deducted?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Gateway Abstraction\n* A Payment Gateway must be a "Plug & Play" module. The core application (Checkout Service) should not know whether it is interacting with Paytm, Razorpay, or Stripe.\n* The Gateway handles the external HTTP network boundary, shielding the internal app from third-party vendor SDKs.\n\n### 💡 2. Enforcing Flow via Template Method\n* Payment transactions must strictly follow a secure chronological lifecycle: **Validate $\\rightarrow$ Initiate $\\rightarrow$ Confirm**.\n* An abstract `PaymentGateway` class defines the `processPayment()` Template Method holding this locked execution sequence.\n* Concrete subclasses (`PaytmGateway`, `RazorpayGateway`) override *how* validation and initiation occur against their specific bank endpoints, but cannot alter the master sequence.\n\n### 💡 3. Resiliency via Proxy Pattern\n* Network calls fail. If the `processPayment()` call fails, forcing the core application's `CheckoutService` to implement `for`-loop retry logic creates massive tight coupling.\n* **The Protection/Retry Proxy:** The Factory returns a `PaymentGatewayProxy` object wrapping the real Gateway.\n* The Proxy intercepts the `processPayment()` call, executes a localized `for(int attempt=0; attempt < maxRetries; attempt++)` loop, and calls the Real Gateway. The client remains completely agnostic to the retry logic.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	LLD Project: Payment Gateway	Architect a robust Payment Gateway bridging multiple vendor APIs (Razorpay, Stripe) using Proxy, Template Method, and Factory patterns with native retry mechanisms.	61	Hard	Low Level Design	37	[{"id": "lld-v23-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Retry Logic via Proxy vs Client Orchestration", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf the `CheckoutController` implements a `for` loop to retry failures, it becomes tightly coupled to the volatility of the external network. \\nFurthermore, different gateways require different retry policies: Paytm might permit 3 rapid retries, while Razorpay mandates exponential backoff.\\n\\nBy placing the retry logic inside a `PaymentGatewayProxy`, the client simply calls `gateway->processPayment()`. The Proxy traps failures and retries internally according to vendor-specific logic. The client code remains pristine, dealing only with the ultimate boolean success/failure of the transaction.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is it an architectural anti-pattern to place payment network retry logic (e.g., 3 retries on timeout) inside the client Application Controller instead of a Proxy?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v23-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Standardizing Flow with Template Method", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nFinancial operations cannot be executed out of order (e.g., you cannot confirm a payment before validating the account balance).\\n\\nThe base `PaymentGateway` class establishes a `final` Template Method:\\n```cpp\\nbool processPayment(Request req) {\\n    if (!validate(req)) return false;\\n    if (!initiate(req)) return false;\\n    return confirm(req);\\n}\\n```\\nWhile the `PaytmGateway` subclass implements `validate()` using Paytm's specific token SDK and `RazorpayGateway` uses Stripe's Secret Key SDK, neither subclass can override the core `processPayment` routing, guaranteeing structural security.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Payment Gateway LLD utilize the Template Method pattern to enforce financial security flows across disparate third-party vendors?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v23-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Dynamic Gateway Instantiation via Factory", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n```cpp\\nclass GatewayFactory {\\npublic:\\n    static IPaymentGateway* getGateway(GatewayType type) {\\n        IPaymentGateway* realGateway = nullptr;\\n        int retries = 0;\\n\\n        if (type == PAYTM) {\\n            realGateway = new PaytmGateway(new PaytmBankingSystem());\\n            retries = 3;\\n        } else if (type == RAZORPAY) {\\n            realGateway = new RazorpayGateway(new RazorpayBankingSystem());\\n            retries = 1;\\n        }\\n\\n        // Wrap in Proxy before returning to client\\n        return new PaymentGatewayProxy(realGateway, retries);\\n    }\\n};\\n```\\nThe Client receives what it believes is a standard `IPaymentGateway`, oblivious that it is actually a proxy shield.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Design the GatewayFactory logic. How does the factory seamlessly wrap the concrete gateway inside the Retry Proxy before returning it to the client?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v23-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Gateway vs Adapter Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe architectural intent of a \\"Gateway\\" is identical to an Adapter: it translates an internal domain model into an external, incompatible vendor format.\\n\\nThe core application generates an internal `PaymentRequest` object (amount, internal user ID). The `RazorpayGateway` (acting as the Adapter) intercepts this internal model, transforms it into the specific JSON/XML payload required by Razorpay's REST API, and translates the webhook response back into a standard internal boolean.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In this context, the PaymentGateway acts as an external boundary to the application. Structurally, how does this mirror the Adapter Pattern?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v23-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Handling In-Flight Reversals (2-Phase Commit context)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThis is a classic distributed transaction failure. The Gateway cannot simply return `false`, as the user's bank account has already been debited.\\n\\nArchitecturally, the Gateway must implement a **Compensating Transaction** (Saga Pattern paradigm):\\n1. The `PaymentGateway` must possess a `rollback()` or `refund()` method.\\n2. Inside the template method, if `initiate()` succeeds but `confirm()` throws a timeout exception, a `catch` block intercepts it.\\n3. The catch block actively fires the `rollback()` API to the vendor, instructing them to reverse the capture.\\n4. Only if the rollback acknowledges successfully does the system return `false` to the client, guaranteeing ledger consistency.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "If initiate() succeeds but confirm() fails due to a network partition, how should the Gateway architecturally handle the money that was already deducted?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
452	In-Place Sorted Array Merging: Two-Pointer Swap & Shell-Sort Gap Method	dsa-merge-two-sorted-arrays-without-extra-space	Merge two sorted arrays in-place without extra space using the cross-array two-pointer swap method or the Shell-sort gap reduction algorithm.	2026-10-02 23:12:19.384175+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: In-Place Sorted Array Merging: Two-Pointer Swap & Shell-Sort Gap Method\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n7uwj04E0I4", "title": "Lecture 40: Merge two Sorted Arrays Without Extra Space", "duration": "36m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-sorted-array/", "title": "Merge Sorted Array (In-Place from Back)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Merge two sorted arrays using a backward three-pointer approach into nums1."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/squares-of-a-sorted-array/", "title": "Squares of a Sorted Array", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Use two pointers converging from both ends to build a sorted squared array."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/merge-two-sorted-arrays-1587115620/1", "title": "Merge Without Extra Space", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Merge two sorted arrays in-place without using any auxiliary data structure."}]	Module 11: Advanced Array Patterns & Hard Interview Problems	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven two sorted arrays `arr1` (size `N`) and `arr2` (size `M`), merge them in-place so that `arr1` contains the first `N` smallest elements in sorted order and `arr2` contains the remaining `M` elements in sorted order, **without using any extra space**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (2 Optimal Solutions)\n- **Optimal Approach 1: Two-Pointer Swap + Sort (O(min(N, M) + N log N + M log M) Time, O(1) Space):**\n  - Place `left = n - 1` at the end of `arr1` (largest element) and `right = 0` at the start of `arr2` (smallest element).\n  - If `arr1[left] > arr2[right]`, swap them and move `left--`, `right++`.\n  - The moment `arr1[left] <= arr2[right]`, stop.\n  - Sort both `arr1` and `arr2` individually to restore sorted order within each array.\n- **Optimal Approach 2: Shell Sort "Gap Method" (O((N + M) * log(N + M)) Time, O(1) Space):**\n  - Treat both arrays as one virtual array of length `len = N + M`.\n  - Initialize `gap = ceil(len / 2.0)`.\n  - Compare and swap elements at distance `gap` (`left` and `right = left + gap`).\n  - After each pass, reduce `gap = ceil(gap / 2.0)` until `gap == 0`.\n\n### 💻 Two-Pointer Swap Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid merge(vector<int> &arr1, vector<int> &arr2, int n, int m) {\n    int left = n - 1, right = 0;\n    while (left >= 0 && right < m) {\n        if (arr1[left] > arr2[right]) {\n            swap(arr1[left], arr2[right]);\n            left--;\n            right++;\n        } else {\n            break; // Elements are partitioned correctly\n        }\n    }\n    sort(arr1.begin(), arr1.end());\n    sort(arr2.begin(), arr2.end());\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22200	Data Interpretation - Line Graph	aptitude-quant-data-interpretation-line-graph	Analyze multi-series line graphs, deduce underlying totals from ratio metrics (Numerator/Denominator limits), and perform multi-step table computations.	2026-10-07 12:03:13.413481+05:30	### 📌 1. Line Graph Data Decoding\n* **Ratio Line Graphs:** When a line graph plots a ratio (e.g., `Girls / Boys`), the value directly indicates which quantity is larger.\n  * If `Ratio > 1`, the Numerator is strictly greater than the Denominator.\n  * If `Ratio < 1`, the Denominator is strictly greater than the Numerator.\n* **Proportion Extrema:** \n  * "Minimum Proportionate" implies the lowest point on the ratio graph.\n  * "Maximum Proportionate" implies the highest point on the ratio graph.\n* **Data Insufficiency Trap:** You cannot determine the absolute numerical increase/decrease (e.g., actual number of girls added) between two years simply by looking at the change in their ratios, unless the absolute base value for both years is provided.\n\n### 💡 2. Complex Tabular Data\n* **Formula Mapping:** Tabular DI often includes a note defining a formula (e.g., `Net Salary = Gross + HA + Special - Tax`). Always establish this algebraic relationship before filling out missing blanks.\n* **Percentages of Changing Bases:** If "Special Allowance" is `80%` of "Gross Salary", ensure you define Gross Salary as an unknown variable `x` and construct an algebraic equation to solve for `x` rather than guessing numbers.\n\n### 💡 3. Profit Margin Extrapolations\n* **Profit Percentage:** In financial graphs, `Profit % = (Profit / Investment) × 100`.\n* **Total Income:** `Income = Investment + Profit`.\n* **Calculating Unknowns:** If Absolute Profit and Profit % are given, use `Investment = (Absolute Profit / Profit %) × 100` to find the investment, then add them to find the Total Income.	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wLTYn8MVIrA", "type": "youtube", "title": "Line Graph & Table Data - Shortcuts & Tricks for Placement Tests, Jobs & Exams | Data Interpretation"}]	[{"id": "apt-line-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Ratio Graph Deduction] A line graph shows the ratio of Girls to Boys appearing for an exam. The values are: 2011=0.45, 2012=0.85, 2013=1.05, 2014=0.95, 2015=1.25. In how many years did girls outnumber boys?", "options": ["A) 1 year", "B) 2 years", "C) 3 years", "D) 4 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A line graph displays the mathematical ratio of Girls to Boys (Girls / Boys) appearing for a board exam over 5 years. The ratio values are recorded as: 2011 = 0.45, 2012 = 0.85, 2013 = 1.05, 2014 = 0.95, and 2015 = 1.25. In exactly how many of these years did the absolute number of girls outnumber the absolute number of boys?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Girls outnumber boys when the numerator is strictly greater than the denominator. This happens exclusively when the ratio (Girls / Boys) is strictly greater than 1. Looking at the data, the ratio is > 1 in exactly two years: 2013 (1.05) and 2015 (1.25).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-line-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Minimum Proportionate Boys] Using the same ratio graph (Girls/Boys): 2011=0.45, 2012=0.85, 2013=1.05, 2014=0.95, 2015=1.25. In which year were the BOYS minimum proportionate?", "options": ["A) 2011", "B) 2013", "C) 2014", "D) 2015"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Using the exact same line graph data showing the ratio of Girls to Boys (G/B): 2011 = 0.45, 2012 = 0.85, 2013 = 1.05, 2014 = 0.95, and 2015 = 1.25. In which specific year were the BOYS considered \\"minimum proportionate\\" relative to the girls?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The boys are \\"minimum proportionate\\" when the ratio of Boys to Girls (B/G) is at its absolute minimum. This strictly occurs when the reciprocal ratio (G/B) is at its absolute MAXIMUM. The maximum G/B ratio on the graph is 1.25 in the year 2015.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-line-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Absolute Calculation from Ratio Line] If the Girls/Boys ratio in 2014 was 0.95, and the absolute number of boys appearing was 500, what was the exact number of girls appearing?", "options": ["A) 450", "B) 475", "C) 525", "D) 550"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Based on the line graph, the exact ratio of Girls to Boys (G/B) in the year 2014 was recorded as exactly 0.95. If the absolute numerical count of boys appearing for the exam in 2014 was exactly 500, what was the exact number of girls appearing for the exam that year?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Ratio G/B = 0.95. We are given B = 500. Therefore, G / 500 = 0.95 => G = 0.95 × 500 = (95 / 100) × 500 = 95 × 5 = 475 girls.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-line-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Data Insufficiency in Ratio Changes] The G/B ratio was 0.85 in 2012 and 1.05 in 2013. What is the percentage increase in the absolute number of girls from 2012 to 2013?", "options": ["A) 20.00%", "B) 23.52%", "C) 25.00%", "D) Cannot be determined"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A line graph shows the G/B ratio was exactly 0.85 in 2012 and exactly 1.05 in 2013. Based strictly on this information, what is the exact percentage increase in the absolute number of girls appearing in the exam from 2012 to 2013?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "We are only given the ratios G1/B1 = 0.85 and G2/B2 = 1.05. Without knowing the absolute base values of B1 and B2 (the number of boys in 2012 and 2013), it is mathematically impossible to calculate the absolute values of G1 and G2. Therefore, the percentage increase cannot be determined.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-line-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Income Deduction from Profit %] Profit % of Company A in India is 17.5%. Absolute profit is 1,75,000 USD. Find Total Income. (Note: Profit % = Profit / Investment × 100; Income = Investment + Profit)", "options": ["A) 10,00,000 USD", "B) 11,75,000 USD", "C) 12,50,000 USD", "D) 13,25,000 USD"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A line graph plots Profit Percentages. In India, Company A records a Profit Percentage of exactly 17.5%. If Company A's absolute monetary profit generated in India is exactly 1,75,000 USD, what is the exact Total Income of Company A in India? \\n(Note: Profit % = [Profit / Total Investment] × 100, and Total Income = Total Investment + Profit).", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Profit % = 17.5%. Absolute Profit = 1,75,000. 2) Investment = (Profit / Profit %) × 100 = (1,75,000 / 17.5) × 100 = 10,000 × 100 = 10,00,000 USD. 3) Total Income = Investment + Profit = 10,00,000 + 1,75,000 = 11,75,000 USD.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-line-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Ratio of Incomes given Equal Investment] Company A Profit % in Dubai is 18%. Company B Profit % in Dubai is 14%. If their absolute investments in Dubai are exactly identical, what is the ratio of their Total Incomes?", "options": ["A) 9 : 7", "B) 54 : 57", "C) 59 : 57", "D) 61 : 58"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A line graph shows Company A has an 18% profit margin in Dubai, while Company B has a 14% profit margin in Dubai. If Company A and Company B both invested the exact same absolute monetary amount in Dubai, what is the exact mathematical ratio of the Total Income of Company A to the Total Income of Company B?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let identical investment = x. Income = Investment + Profit. Income of A = x + 18% of x = 118% of x = 1.18x. Income of B = x + 14% of x = 114% of x = 1.14x. Ratio Income A : Income B = 1.18x / 1.14x = 118 / 114. Divide numerator and denominator by 2: 59 : 57.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-line-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Tabular Algebraic Substitution] Net Salary = Gross + HA + Special - Tax. Level 2: HA=8000, Tax=200, Net=25800. Special = 80% of Gross. Find Gross Salary.", "options": ["A) ₹8,000", "B) ₹10,000", "C) ₹12,000", "D) ₹14,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A data table operates on the formula: Net Salary = Gross Salary + HA + Special Allowance - Professional Tax. For a Level 2 employee, the table shows: HA = ₹8,000, Professional Tax = ₹200, and Net Salary = ₹25,800. The notes state that Special Allowance is exactly 80% of the Gross Salary. What is the exact Gross Salary?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Gross Salary = x. Special Allowance = 0.8x. Formula: 25800 = x + 8000 + 0.8x - 200 => 25800 = 1.8x + 7800 => 1.8x = 25800 - 7800 = 18000 => 1.8x = 18000 => x = 18000 / 1.8 = ₹10,000.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-line-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Reverse Tabular Calculation] Level 1: Gross=9000, HA=5000, Tax=200. Net is double the Gross. Find Special Allowance.", "options": ["A) ₹3,800", "B) ₹4,000", "C) ₹4,200", "D) ₹4,600"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Using the formula: Net Salary = Gross Salary + HA + Special Allowance - Professional Tax. For a Level 1 employee, Gross Salary = ₹9,000, HA = ₹5,000, and Professional Tax = ₹200. If the Net Salary is exactly double the Gross Salary, what is the exact absolute value of the Special Allowance?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Gross = 9000. Net Salary is double the Gross = 2 × 9000 = 18,000. Equation: 18000 = 9000 + 5000 + Special - 200 => 18000 = 14000 + Special - 200 => 18000 = 13800 + Special => Special = 18000 - 13800 = ₹4,200.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-line-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Percentage Comparison between Tabular Rows] Special Allowance Level 2 = 8000. Special Allowance Level 1 = 4200. Special Level 2 is what percent of Special Level 1?", "options": ["A) 175.5%", "B) 180.2%", "C) 190.4%", "D) 195.8%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a completed data table, the Special Allowance for a Level 2 employee is strictly ₹8,000, and the Special Allowance for a Level 1 employee is strictly ₹4,200. The Special Allowance paid to the Level 2 employee is exactly what percentage of the Special Allowance paid to the Level 1 employee?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "We need to find (Special L2 / Special L1) × 100. Percentage = (8000 / 4200) × 100 = (80 / 42) × 100 = (40 / 21) × 100 = 4000 / 21. 4000 / 21 ≈ 190.47%. Option C (190.4%) matches closest.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-line-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Irrelevant Multiplier Trap] Level 4: HA=8000, Special=10000. If 3 employees work at level 4, ratio of total HA to total Special?", "options": ["A) 12 : 15", "B) 4 : 5", "C) 8 : 15", "D) 24 : 10"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "For a Level 4 employee, the base HA is strictly ₹8,000 and the base Special Allowance is strictly ₹10,000. If exactly 3 distinct employees are concurrently working at Level 4, what is the exact mathematical ratio of the total aggregate amount spent by the company on HA to the total aggregate amount spent on Special Allowance for these three employees?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total HA for 3 employees = 3 × 8000. Total Special for 3 employees = 3 × 10000. Ratio = (3 × 8000) / (3 × 10000). The multiplier '3' strictly cancels out! Ratio = 8000 / 10000 = 8 / 10 = 4 : 5. The number of employees is irrelevant decoy data.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-line-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Line Graph Intersect Deduction] Line A (Revenue) and Line B (Cost) intersect at Year 3 with value $50,000. What is the profit in Year 3?", "options": ["A) -$50,000", "B) $0", "C) $50,000", "D) $100,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A multiple-series line graph plots Total Revenue (Line A) and Total Cost (Line B) over 5 years. In Year 3, Line A and Line B perfectly intersect each other at the exact value of $50,000 on the y-axis. What is the exact absolute monetary profit generated in Year 3? (Note: Profit = Revenue - Cost)", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "An intersection point means the two values are mathematically identical. Revenue = $50,000 and Cost = $50,000. Profit = Revenue - Cost = 50000 - 50000 = $0 (Break-even point).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-line-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Dual Axis Line Graph] Axis 1 (Production in thousands): 10, 15, 20. Axis 2 (Defect Rate %): 5%, 4%, 2%. What is the exact total absolute number of non-defective units produced over all 3 years combined?", "options": ["A) 41,500", "B) 42,600", "C) 43,500", "D) 45,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A dual-axis line graph tracks a factory over 3 years. The left axis tracks Total Production (in thousands of units): Year 1 = 10, Year 2 = 15, Year 3 = 20. The right axis tracks the Defect Rate Percentage: Year 1 = 5%, Year 2 = 4%, Year 3 = 2%. What is the exact absolute total number of NON-defective units produced over all 3 years combined?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Year 1: Total = 10,000. Non-defective = 95% of 10,000 = 9,500. 2) Year 2: Total = 15,000. Non-defective = 96% of 15,000 = 14,400. 3) Year 3: Total = 20,000. Non-defective = 98% of 20,000 = 19,600. Total non-defective = 9500 + 14400 + 19600 = 43,500 units.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-line-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Slope as Growth Rate] A line graph of revenue is a perfectly straight line passing through (Year 1, $20k) and (Year 4, $80k). What is the projected revenue in Year 6?", "options": ["A) $100,000", "B) $110,000", "C) $120,000", "D) $140,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A line graph tracking a company's annual revenue forms a perfectly straight diagonal line. The data point for Year 1 is exactly $20,000 and the data point for Year 4 is exactly $80,000. Assuming the strictly linear trend continues, what will be the exact projected revenue in Year 6?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Calculate the constant linear slope (growth per year): Slope = (Change in Revenue) / (Change in Years) = (80000 - 20000) / (4 - 1) = 60000 / 3 = $20,000 per year. Revenue in Year 6 = Revenue in Year 4 + (2 years × $20,000) = 80000 + 40000 = $120,000.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-line-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Averaging Ratios vs Ratio of Averages] Is the average of the yearly G/B ratios equal to the ratio of total Girls to total Boys over the period?", "options": ["A) Yes, because averages distribute linearly over fractions", "B) Yes, if the absolute number of girls is constant each year", "C) No, it requires the absolute number of total students for each year to compute weighted components", "D) No, it requires strictly the absolute number of girls for each year"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A line graph provides the specific mathematical ratio of Girls to Boys (G/B) for 5 consecutive years. An analyst wants to find the true overall ratio of the total number of girls across all 5 years combined to the total number of boys across all 5 years combined. Can this be mathematically computed by simply taking the simple arithmetic average of the 5 yearly G/B ratios?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "A simple average of ratios is mathematically meaningless when trying to find an aggregate ratio, because the denominators (the absolute base numbers of boys each year) are not uniformly weighted. To find the true aggregate ratio, the absolute base values (or total student populations) for EACH specific year must be known to compute the weighted totals. (Option C captures the fundamental necessity of base totals).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-line-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Index Graph Base Year Logic] Base Year 2010 Index = 100. Year 2015 Index = 150. Year 2020 Index = 225. Percentage growth from 2015 to 2020?", "options": ["A) 50%", "B) 75%", "C) 125%", "D) 150%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An economic index line graph is normalized such that the Base Year 2010 is set to an Index of exactly 100. The Index reaches exactly 150 in 2015, and reaches exactly 225 in 2020. What is the exact percentage growth of the economy strictly from 2015 to 2020?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The growth is calculated between the specific data points of 2015 (Index 150) and 2020 (Index 225). Absolute index growth = 225 - 150 = 75. The \\"base\\" for this specific period is 150. Percentage growth = (75 / 150) × 100 = 1/2 × 100 = 50%.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-line-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Extrapolating Total from Table Formula] Gross=20k. HA=2k. Special=x. Tax=500. If Special is 25% of Net, find Net.", "options": ["A) ₹26,500", "B) ₹27,200", "C) ₹28,666.67", "D) ₹30,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A customized tabular formula dictates: Net Salary = Gross Salary + HA + Special Allowance - Tax. An employee's record shows: Gross Salary = ₹20,000, HA = ₹2,000, and Tax = ₹500. If the exact value of the Special Allowance is dynamically defined as exactly 25% of the FINAL Net Salary, what is the exact mathematical value of the Net Salary?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Net Salary = N. Special Allowance = 0.25N. Formula: N = 20000 + 2000 + 0.25N - 500 => N = 21500 + 0.25N => 0.75N = 21500 => (3/4)N = 21500 => N = 21500 × 4 / 3 = 86000 / 3 = ₹28,666.67.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-line-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Line Graph Area Under Curve] A line graph plots Speed (m/s) on y-axis vs Time (s) on x-axis. Data points: (0,0), (10,20), (30,20). Total distance?", "options": ["A) 300 meters", "B) 400 meters", "C) 500 meters", "D) 600 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A line graph plots a vehicle's Speed in m/s on the y-axis against Time in seconds on the x-axis. The vehicle starts at point (0, 0), uniformly accelerates to point (10, 20), and then maintains that constant speed until point (30, 20). What is the exact total absolute distance covered by the vehicle in these 30 seconds?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The total distance is the area under the Speed-Time line graph. 1) Triangle area from t=0 to t=10: 1/2 × Base × Height = 1/2 × 10 × 20 = 100 meters. 2) Rectangle area from t=10 to t=30: Base × Height = (30 - 10) × 20 = 20 × 20 = 400 meters. Total distance = 100 + 400 = 500 meters.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-line-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Cumulative Line Graph Extraction] Cumulative sales graph: Jan=10, Feb=25, Mar=45, Apr=50. Sales in March alone?", "options": ["A) 15", "B) 20", "C) 25", "D) 45"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A specific line graph plots the CUMULATIVE year-to-date sales (in thousands) of a product. The plotted data points are: January = 10, February = 25, March = 45, and April = 50. What were the exact absolute sales achieved strictly in the month of March alone?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Cumulative sales for March means the total sum of sales in Jan, Feb, and March is 45. Cumulative sales for February means the total sum of sales in Jan and Feb is 25. The sales exclusively in March is the difference between March's cumulative total and February's cumulative total. Sales in March = 45 - 25 = 20 (thousand).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-line-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Profit vs Revenue Line Divergence] Line A (Revenue) grows 10% per year. Line B (Cost) grows 5% per year. Base Year Rev=$100, Cost=$80. Profit in Year 2?", "options": ["A) $28.00", "B) $30.50", "C) $32.80", "D) $35.00"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A line graph tracks Revenue and Cost. In the Base Year (Year 0), absolute Revenue is exactly $100 and absolute Cost is exactly $80. If Revenue strictly grows by a constant 10% each subsequent year, and Cost strictly grows by a constant 5% each subsequent year, what is the exact absolute mathematical Profit in Year 2? (Note: Profit = Revenue - Cost)", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Revenue in Year 2 = 100 × (1.10)^2 = 100 × 1.21 = $121.00. 2) Cost in Year 2 = 80 × (1.05)^2 = 80 × 1.1025 = $88.20. 3) Profit in Year 2 = Revenue - Cost = 121.00 - 88.20 = $32.80.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-line-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Interpreting Market Share vs Market Size] Line Graph 1: Company X Market Share = 20%, 30%, 40%. Line Graph 2: Total Market Size = $10M, $8M, $5M. Does Company X's absolute revenue increase or decrease over time?", "options": ["A) Strictly increasing ($2M, $2.4M, $3M)", "B) Strictly decreasing ($2M, $1.6M, $1.2M)", "C) Increases then decreases ($2M, $2.4M, $2M)", "D) Decreases then increases ($2M, $1.8M, $2M)"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two synchronized line graphs are presented. Graph 1 shows Company X's Market Share Percentage growing consistently: Year 1 = 20%, Year 2 = 30%, Year 3 = 40%. Graph 2 shows the Total Market Size in absolute Dollars shrinking consistently: Year 1 = $10M, Year 2 = $8M, Year 3 = $5M. What is the exact sequential trend of Company X's absolute monetary revenue over these 3 years?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Calculate absolute revenue for each year (Market Share % × Total Market Size). Year 1: 20% of 10M = $2.0M. Year 2: 30% of 8M = $2.4M. Year 3: 40% of 5M = $2.0M. The trend goes $2.0M -> $2.4M -> $2.0M. Therefore, the absolute revenue increases from Year 1 to Year 2, and then strictly decreases from Year 2 to Year 3.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. Line Graph Data Decoding\n* **Ratio Line Graphs:** When a line graph plots a ratio (e.g., `Girls / Boys`), the value directly indicates which quantity is larger.\n  * If `Ratio > 1`, the Numerator is strictly greater than the Denominator.\n  * If `Ratio < 1`, the Denominator is strictly greater than the Numerator.\n* **Proportion Extrema:** \n  * "Minimum Proportionate" implies the lowest point on the ratio graph.\n  * "Maximum Proportionate" implies the highest point on the ratio graph.\n* **Data Insufficiency Trap:** You cannot determine the absolute numerical increase/decrease (e.g., actual number of girls added) between two years simply by looking at the change in their ratios, unless the absolute base value for both years is provided.\n\n### 💡 2. Complex Tabular Data\n* **Formula Mapping:** Tabular DI often includes a note defining a formula (e.g., `Net Salary = Gross + HA + Special - Tax`). Always establish this algebraic relationship before filling out missing blanks.\n* **Percentages of Changing Bases:** If "Special Allowance" is `80%` of "Gross Salary", ensure you define Gross Salary as an unknown variable `x` and construct an algebraic equation to solve for `x` rather than guessing numbers.\n\n### 💡 3. Profit Margin Extrapolations\n* **Profit Percentage:** In financial graphs, `Profit % = (Profit / Investment) × 100`.\n* **Total Income:** `Income = Investment + Profit`.\n* **Calculating Unknowns:** If Absolute Profit and Profit % are given, use `Investment = (Absolute Profit / Profit %) × 100` to find the investment, then add them to find the Total Income.	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Data Interpretation - Line Graph	Analyze multi-series line graphs, deduce underlying totals from ratio metrics (Numerator/Denominator limits), and perform multi-step table computations.	31	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	34	[{"id": "apt-line-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Ratio Graph Deduction] A line graph shows the ratio of Girls to Boys appearing for an exam. The values are: 2011=0.45, 2012=0.85, 2013=1.05, 2014=0.95, 2015=1.25. In how many years did girls outnumber boys?", "options": ["A) 1 year", "B) 2 years", "C) 3 years", "D) 4 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A line graph displays the mathematical ratio of Girls to Boys (Girls / Boys) appearing for a board exam over 5 years. The ratio values are recorded as: 2011 = 0.45, 2012 = 0.85, 2013 = 1.05, 2014 = 0.95, and 2015 = 1.25. In exactly how many of these years did the absolute number of girls outnumber the absolute number of boys?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Girls outnumber boys when the numerator is strictly greater than the denominator. This happens exclusively when the ratio (Girls / Boys) is strictly greater than 1. Looking at the data, the ratio is > 1 in exactly two years: 2013 (1.05) and 2015 (1.25).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-line-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Minimum Proportionate Boys] Using the same ratio graph (Girls/Boys): 2011=0.45, 2012=0.85, 2013=1.05, 2014=0.95, 2015=1.25. In which year were the BOYS minimum proportionate?", "options": ["A) 2011", "B) 2013", "C) 2014", "D) 2015"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Using the exact same line graph data showing the ratio of Girls to Boys (G/B): 2011 = 0.45, 2012 = 0.85, 2013 = 1.05, 2014 = 0.95, and 2015 = 1.25. In which specific year were the BOYS considered \\"minimum proportionate\\" relative to the girls?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The boys are \\"minimum proportionate\\" when the ratio of Boys to Girls (B/G) is at its absolute minimum. This strictly occurs when the reciprocal ratio (G/B) is at its absolute MAXIMUM. The maximum G/B ratio on the graph is 1.25 in the year 2015.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-line-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Absolute Calculation from Ratio Line] If the Girls/Boys ratio in 2014 was 0.95, and the absolute number of boys appearing was 500, what was the exact number of girls appearing?", "options": ["A) 450", "B) 475", "C) 525", "D) 550"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Based on the line graph, the exact ratio of Girls to Boys (G/B) in the year 2014 was recorded as exactly 0.95. If the absolute numerical count of boys appearing for the exam in 2014 was exactly 500, what was the exact number of girls appearing for the exam that year?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Ratio G/B = 0.95. We are given B = 500. Therefore, G / 500 = 0.95 => G = 0.95 × 500 = (95 / 100) × 500 = 95 × 5 = 475 girls.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-line-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Data Insufficiency in Ratio Changes] The G/B ratio was 0.85 in 2012 and 1.05 in 2013. What is the percentage increase in the absolute number of girls from 2012 to 2013?", "options": ["A) 20.00%", "B) 23.52%", "C) 25.00%", "D) Cannot be determined"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A line graph shows the G/B ratio was exactly 0.85 in 2012 and exactly 1.05 in 2013. Based strictly on this information, what is the exact percentage increase in the absolute number of girls appearing in the exam from 2012 to 2013?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "We are only given the ratios G1/B1 = 0.85 and G2/B2 = 1.05. Without knowing the absolute base values of B1 and B2 (the number of boys in 2012 and 2013), it is mathematically impossible to calculate the absolute values of G1 and G2. Therefore, the percentage increase cannot be determined.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-line-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Income Deduction from Profit %] Profit % of Company A in India is 17.5%. Absolute profit is 1,75,000 USD. Find Total Income. (Note: Profit % = Profit / Investment × 100; Income = Investment + Profit)", "options": ["A) 10,00,000 USD", "B) 11,75,000 USD", "C) 12,50,000 USD", "D) 13,25,000 USD"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A line graph plots Profit Percentages. In India, Company A records a Profit Percentage of exactly 17.5%. If Company A's absolute monetary profit generated in India is exactly 1,75,000 USD, what is the exact Total Income of Company A in India? \\n(Note: Profit % = [Profit / Total Investment] × 100, and Total Income = Total Investment + Profit).", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Profit % = 17.5%. Absolute Profit = 1,75,000. 2) Investment = (Profit / Profit %) × 100 = (1,75,000 / 17.5) × 100 = 10,000 × 100 = 10,00,000 USD. 3) Total Income = Investment + Profit = 10,00,000 + 1,75,000 = 11,75,000 USD.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-line-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Ratio of Incomes given Equal Investment] Company A Profit % in Dubai is 18%. Company B Profit % in Dubai is 14%. If their absolute investments in Dubai are exactly identical, what is the ratio of their Total Incomes?", "options": ["A) 9 : 7", "B) 54 : 57", "C) 59 : 57", "D) 61 : 58"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A line graph shows Company A has an 18% profit margin in Dubai, while Company B has a 14% profit margin in Dubai. If Company A and Company B both invested the exact same absolute monetary amount in Dubai, what is the exact mathematical ratio of the Total Income of Company A to the Total Income of Company B?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let identical investment = x. Income = Investment + Profit. Income of A = x + 18% of x = 118% of x = 1.18x. Income of B = x + 14% of x = 114% of x = 1.14x. Ratio Income A : Income B = 1.18x / 1.14x = 118 / 114. Divide numerator and denominator by 2: 59 : 57.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-line-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Tabular Algebraic Substitution] Net Salary = Gross + HA + Special - Tax. Level 2: HA=8000, Tax=200, Net=25800. Special = 80% of Gross. Find Gross Salary.", "options": ["A) ₹8,000", "B) ₹10,000", "C) ₹12,000", "D) ₹14,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A data table operates on the formula: Net Salary = Gross Salary + HA + Special Allowance - Professional Tax. For a Level 2 employee, the table shows: HA = ₹8,000, Professional Tax = ₹200, and Net Salary = ₹25,800. The notes state that Special Allowance is exactly 80% of the Gross Salary. What is the exact Gross Salary?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Gross Salary = x. Special Allowance = 0.8x. Formula: 25800 = x + 8000 + 0.8x - 200 => 25800 = 1.8x + 7800 => 1.8x = 25800 - 7800 = 18000 => 1.8x = 18000 => x = 18000 / 1.8 = ₹10,000.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-line-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Reverse Tabular Calculation] Level 1: Gross=9000, HA=5000, Tax=200. Net is double the Gross. Find Special Allowance.", "options": ["A) ₹3,800", "B) ₹4,000", "C) ₹4,200", "D) ₹4,600"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Using the formula: Net Salary = Gross Salary + HA + Special Allowance - Professional Tax. For a Level 1 employee, Gross Salary = ₹9,000, HA = ₹5,000, and Professional Tax = ₹200. If the Net Salary is exactly double the Gross Salary, what is the exact absolute value of the Special Allowance?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Gross = 9000. Net Salary is double the Gross = 2 × 9000 = 18,000. Equation: 18000 = 9000 + 5000 + Special - 200 => 18000 = 14000 + Special - 200 => 18000 = 13800 + Special => Special = 18000 - 13800 = ₹4,200.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-line-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Percentage Comparison between Tabular Rows] Special Allowance Level 2 = 8000. Special Allowance Level 1 = 4200. Special Level 2 is what percent of Special Level 1?", "options": ["A) 175.5%", "B) 180.2%", "C) 190.4%", "D) 195.8%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a completed data table, the Special Allowance for a Level 2 employee is strictly ₹8,000, and the Special Allowance for a Level 1 employee is strictly ₹4,200. The Special Allowance paid to the Level 2 employee is exactly what percentage of the Special Allowance paid to the Level 1 employee?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "We need to find (Special L2 / Special L1) × 100. Percentage = (8000 / 4200) × 100 = (80 / 42) × 100 = (40 / 21) × 100 = 4000 / 21. 4000 / 21 ≈ 190.47%. Option C (190.4%) matches closest.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-line-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Irrelevant Multiplier Trap] Level 4: HA=8000, Special=10000. If 3 employees work at level 4, ratio of total HA to total Special?", "options": ["A) 12 : 15", "B) 4 : 5", "C) 8 : 15", "D) 24 : 10"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "For a Level 4 employee, the base HA is strictly ₹8,000 and the base Special Allowance is strictly ₹10,000. If exactly 3 distinct employees are concurrently working at Level 4, what is the exact mathematical ratio of the total aggregate amount spent by the company on HA to the total aggregate amount spent on Special Allowance for these three employees?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Total HA for 3 employees = 3 × 8000. Total Special for 3 employees = 3 × 10000. Ratio = (3 × 8000) / (3 × 10000). The multiplier '3' strictly cancels out! Ratio = 8000 / 10000 = 8 / 10 = 4 : 5. The number of employees is irrelevant decoy data.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-line-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Line Graph Intersect Deduction] Line A (Revenue) and Line B (Cost) intersect at Year 3 with value $50,000. What is the profit in Year 3?", "options": ["A) -$50,000", "B) $0", "C) $50,000", "D) $100,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A multiple-series line graph plots Total Revenue (Line A) and Total Cost (Line B) over 5 years. In Year 3, Line A and Line B perfectly intersect each other at the exact value of $50,000 on the y-axis. What is the exact absolute monetary profit generated in Year 3? (Note: Profit = Revenue - Cost)", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "An intersection point means the two values are mathematically identical. Revenue = $50,000 and Cost = $50,000. Profit = Revenue - Cost = 50000 - 50000 = $0 (Break-even point).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-line-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Dual Axis Line Graph] Axis 1 (Production in thousands): 10, 15, 20. Axis 2 (Defect Rate %): 5%, 4%, 2%. What is the exact total absolute number of non-defective units produced over all 3 years combined?", "options": ["A) 41,500", "B) 42,600", "C) 43,500", "D) 45,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A dual-axis line graph tracks a factory over 3 years. The left axis tracks Total Production (in thousands of units): Year 1 = 10, Year 2 = 15, Year 3 = 20. The right axis tracks the Defect Rate Percentage: Year 1 = 5%, Year 2 = 4%, Year 3 = 2%. What is the exact absolute total number of NON-defective units produced over all 3 years combined?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Year 1: Total = 10,000. Non-defective = 95% of 10,000 = 9,500. 2) Year 2: Total = 15,000. Non-defective = 96% of 15,000 = 14,400. 3) Year 3: Total = 20,000. Non-defective = 98% of 20,000 = 19,600. Total non-defective = 9500 + 14400 + 19600 = 43,500 units.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-line-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Slope as Growth Rate] A line graph of revenue is a perfectly straight line passing through (Year 1, $20k) and (Year 4, $80k). What is the projected revenue in Year 6?", "options": ["A) $100,000", "B) $110,000", "C) $120,000", "D) $140,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A line graph tracking a company's annual revenue forms a perfectly straight diagonal line. The data point for Year 1 is exactly $20,000 and the data point for Year 4 is exactly $80,000. Assuming the strictly linear trend continues, what will be the exact projected revenue in Year 6?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Calculate the constant linear slope (growth per year): Slope = (Change in Revenue) / (Change in Years) = (80000 - 20000) / (4 - 1) = 60000 / 3 = $20,000 per year. Revenue in Year 6 = Revenue in Year 4 + (2 years × $20,000) = 80000 + 40000 = $120,000.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-line-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Averaging Ratios vs Ratio of Averages] Is the average of the yearly G/B ratios equal to the ratio of total Girls to total Boys over the period?", "options": ["A) Yes, because averages distribute linearly over fractions", "B) Yes, if the absolute number of girls is constant each year", "C) No, it requires the absolute number of total students for each year to compute weighted components", "D) No, it requires strictly the absolute number of girls for each year"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A line graph provides the specific mathematical ratio of Girls to Boys (G/B) for 5 consecutive years. An analyst wants to find the true overall ratio of the total number of girls across all 5 years combined to the total number of boys across all 5 years combined. Can this be mathematically computed by simply taking the simple arithmetic average of the 5 yearly G/B ratios?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "A simple average of ratios is mathematically meaningless when trying to find an aggregate ratio, because the denominators (the absolute base numbers of boys each year) are not uniformly weighted. To find the true aggregate ratio, the absolute base values (or total student populations) for EACH specific year must be known to compute the weighted totals. (Option C captures the fundamental necessity of base totals).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-line-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Index Graph Base Year Logic] Base Year 2010 Index = 100. Year 2015 Index = 150. Year 2020 Index = 225. Percentage growth from 2015 to 2020?", "options": ["A) 50%", "B) 75%", "C) 125%", "D) 150%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An economic index line graph is normalized such that the Base Year 2010 is set to an Index of exactly 100. The Index reaches exactly 150 in 2015, and reaches exactly 225 in 2020. What is the exact percentage growth of the economy strictly from 2015 to 2020?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The growth is calculated between the specific data points of 2015 (Index 150) and 2020 (Index 225). Absolute index growth = 225 - 150 = 75. The \\"base\\" for this specific period is 150. Percentage growth = (75 / 150) × 100 = 1/2 × 100 = 50%.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-line-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Extrapolating Total from Table Formula] Gross=20k. HA=2k. Special=x. Tax=500. If Special is 25% of Net, find Net.", "options": ["A) ₹26,500", "B) ₹27,200", "C) ₹28,666.67", "D) ₹30,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A customized tabular formula dictates: Net Salary = Gross Salary + HA + Special Allowance - Tax. An employee's record shows: Gross Salary = ₹20,000, HA = ₹2,000, and Tax = ₹500. If the exact value of the Special Allowance is dynamically defined as exactly 25% of the FINAL Net Salary, what is the exact mathematical value of the Net Salary?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Net Salary = N. Special Allowance = 0.25N. Formula: N = 20000 + 2000 + 0.25N - 500 => N = 21500 + 0.25N => 0.75N = 21500 => (3/4)N = 21500 => N = 21500 × 4 / 3 = 86000 / 3 = ₹28,666.67.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-line-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Line Graph Area Under Curve] A line graph plots Speed (m/s) on y-axis vs Time (s) on x-axis. Data points: (0,0), (10,20), (30,20). Total distance?", "options": ["A) 300 meters", "B) 400 meters", "C) 500 meters", "D) 600 meters"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A line graph plots a vehicle's Speed in m/s on the y-axis against Time in seconds on the x-axis. The vehicle starts at point (0, 0), uniformly accelerates to point (10, 20), and then maintains that constant speed until point (30, 20). What is the exact total absolute distance covered by the vehicle in these 30 seconds?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The total distance is the area under the Speed-Time line graph. 1) Triangle area from t=0 to t=10: 1/2 × Base × Height = 1/2 × 10 × 20 = 100 meters. 2) Rectangle area from t=10 to t=30: Base × Height = (30 - 10) × 20 = 20 × 20 = 400 meters. Total distance = 100 + 400 = 500 meters.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-line-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Cumulative Line Graph Extraction] Cumulative sales graph: Jan=10, Feb=25, Mar=45, Apr=50. Sales in March alone?", "options": ["A) 15", "B) 20", "C) 25", "D) 45"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A specific line graph plots the CUMULATIVE year-to-date sales (in thousands) of a product. The plotted data points are: January = 10, February = 25, March = 45, and April = 50. What were the exact absolute sales achieved strictly in the month of March alone?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Cumulative sales for March means the total sum of sales in Jan, Feb, and March is 45. Cumulative sales for February means the total sum of sales in Jan and Feb is 25. The sales exclusively in March is the difference between March's cumulative total and February's cumulative total. Sales in March = 45 - 25 = 20 (thousand).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-line-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Profit vs Revenue Line Divergence] Line A (Revenue) grows 10% per year. Line B (Cost) grows 5% per year. Base Year Rev=$100, Cost=$80. Profit in Year 2?", "options": ["A) $28.00", "B) $30.50", "C) $32.80", "D) $35.00"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A line graph tracks Revenue and Cost. In the Base Year (Year 0), absolute Revenue is exactly $100 and absolute Cost is exactly $80. If Revenue strictly grows by a constant 10% each subsequent year, and Cost strictly grows by a constant 5% each subsequent year, what is the exact absolute mathematical Profit in Year 2? (Note: Profit = Revenue - Cost)", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Revenue in Year 2 = 100 × (1.10)^2 = 100 × 1.21 = $121.00. 2) Cost in Year 2 = 80 × (1.05)^2 = 80 × 1.1025 = $88.20. 3) Profit in Year 2 = Revenue - Cost = 121.00 - 88.20 = $32.80.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-line-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Interpreting Market Share vs Market Size] Line Graph 1: Company X Market Share = 20%, 30%, 40%. Line Graph 2: Total Market Size = $10M, $8M, $5M. Does Company X's absolute revenue increase or decrease over time?", "options": ["A) Strictly increasing ($2M, $2.4M, $3M)", "B) Strictly decreasing ($2M, $1.6M, $1.2M)", "C) Increases then decreases ($2M, $2.4M, $2M)", "D) Decreases then increases ($2M, $1.8M, $2M)"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Two synchronized line graphs are presented. Graph 1 shows Company X's Market Share Percentage growing consistently: Year 1 = 20%, Year 2 = 30%, Year 3 = 40%. Graph 2 shows the Total Market Size in absolute Dollars shrinking consistently: Year 1 = $10M, Year 2 = $8M, Year 3 = $5M. What is the exact sequential trend of Company X's absolute monetary revenue over these 3 years?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Calculate absolute revenue for each year (Market Share % × Total Market Size). Year 1: 20% of 10M = $2.0M. Year 2: 30% of 8M = $2.4M. Year 3: 40% of 5M = $2.0M. The trend goes $2.0M -> $2.4M -> $2.0M. Therefore, the absolute revenue increases from Year 1 to Year 2, and then strictly decreases from Year 2 to Year 3.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
2298	Subtree Re-Wiring & Predecessor Splicing: Delete a Node in a Binary Search Tree	dsa-delete-a-node-in-binary-search-tree	Delete nodes from a BST across 0-child, 1-child, and 2-child cases in O(H) time using inorder predecessor splicing.	2026-10-03 01:26:48.302802+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Subtree Re-Wiring & Predecessor Splicing: Delete a Node in a Binary Search Tree\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kouxiP_H5WE", "title": "Lecture 116: Delete a Node in Binary Search Tree", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-node-in-a-bst/", "title": "Delete Node in a BST", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Delete a node with a given key in a BST and restore valid BST properties."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/trim-a-binary-search-tree/", "title": "Trim a Binary Search Tree", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Trim nodes whose values fall outside [low, high] by splicing child subtrees."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/delete-a-node-from-bst/1", "title": "Delete a node from BST", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Delete a specified key from a binary search tree."}]	Module 19: Binary Search Trees: Concepts, Validations & Structural Mutations	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the root of a BST and a `key`, delete the node with `key` and return the new root while preserving all BST invariants.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The 3 Deletion Cases for Target Node `target`:**\n  1. **Leaf Node (0 children):** Simply remove the node and return `nullptr`.\n  2. **1 Child:** Bypass the target by connecting target's parent directly to target's existing child (`target->left` or `target->right`).\n  3. **2 Children (Splicing Subtrees):**\n     - Keep `target->left` as the new replacement subtree root.\n     - Where does `target->right` go?\n     - Find the **inorder predecessor** (the rightmost node of `target->left`).\n     - Attach `target->right` to the right of this predecessor (`findLastRight(target->left)->right = target->right`)!\n- **Complexity:** `O(H)` Time and `O(1)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Delete Node in BST Blueprint\n```cpp\nTreeNode* findLastRight(TreeNode* root) {\n    if (root->right == nullptr) return root;\n    return findLastRight(root->right);\n}\n\nTreeNode* helper(TreeNode* root) {\n    if (root->left == nullptr) return root->right;\n    if (root->right == nullptr) return root->left;\n\n    TreeNode* rightChild = root->right;\n    TreeNode* lastRight = findLastRight(root->left);\n    lastRight->right = rightChild; // Splice right subtree to predecessor\n    return root->left;\n}\n\nTreeNode* deleteNode(TreeNode* root, int key) {\n    if (root == nullptr) return nullptr;\n    if (root->val == key) return helper(root);\n\n    TreeNode* curr = root;\n    while (curr != nullptr) {\n        if (curr->val > key) {\n            if (curr->left != nullptr && curr->left->val == key) {\n                curr->left = helper(curr->left);\n                break;\n            } else {\n                curr = curr->left;\n            }\n        } else {\n            if (curr->right != nullptr && curr->right->val == key) {\n                curr->right = helper(curr->right);\n                break;\n            } else {\n                curr = curr->right;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return root;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Deleting the Root Node:** If `root->val == key`, handle it upfront via `if (root->val == key) return helper(root);`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22163	OOP: Complete Object-Oriented Programming in 1 Shot (C++ / Placement & GATE Edition)	cs-oop-complete-oneshot-mastery	Master Object-Oriented Programming from first principles—memory alignment and padding, constructors/destructors, deep vs. shallow copy, the 4 Pillars (Encapsulation, Abstraction, Inheritance, Polymorphism), VTable/VPTR internals, Virtual Inheritance, and SOLID design principles—followed by a 50-question timed evaluation test.	2026-10-06 23:38:10.717796+05:30	### 📌 1. Why Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) Over Procedural Programming?\nIn procedural programming, data (global or loose local variables) and functions operate separately. Modeling 1,000 students requires maintaining disconnected arrays (`id[]`, `age[]`, `name[]`) with zero access protection—any function can accidentally corrupt global state. **Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)** models software around **Objects** that bundle **State (Data Members / Attributes)** and **Behavior (Member Functions / Methods)** into a single self-contained unit defined by a **Class** blueprint.\n\n### 💡 2. Classes, Objects & Memory Layout Internals (Padding & Greedy Alignment)\n* **Class vs. Object:** A **Class** is a user-defined blueprint that consumes **0 bytes of RAM** at compile time. An **Object** is a runtime instance of a class allocated on the **Stack** (`Student s1;`) or the **Heap** (`Student* s2 = new Student();`).\n* **Why an Empty Class Has Size `1 Byte` (`sizeof(Empty) == 1`):** The C++ standard mandates that every distinct object instance must have a **unique memory address**. If an empty class had size `0`, two consecutive objects `Empty a, b;` on the stack would share the exact same address (`&a == &b`). Therefore, the compiler inserts 1 silent byte. (Exception: when an empty class is used as a base class, compilers apply **Empty Base Optimization — EBO**, reducing its base subobject contribution to `0` bytes!).\n* **Struct/Class Padding & Greedy Alignment:** CPUs read memory in word-sized chunks (4 or 8 bytes) at aligned address multiples. A member of size K bytes must be placed at a memory offset divisible by K, and the total `sizeof(Class)` must be a multiple of the **largest member's alignment requirement**.\n* **What Contributes to `sizeof(Object)`?**\n  * Non-static data members + alignment padding bytes.\n  * **`vptr` (Virtual Pointer):** `8 bytes` on 64-bit systems (`4 bytes` on 32-bit) added automatically if the class has one or more `virtual` functions.\n  * **Excluded from `sizeof(Object)`:** Member functions (stored in the Code/Text segment) and `static` data members (stored in the Data/BSS segment) contribute **0 bytes** to individual objects.\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n\n// Bad ordering: char(1) + pad(7) + double(8) + int(4) + char(1) + pad(3) = 24 bytes!\nclass Unoptimized {\n  char a;\n  double b;\n  int c;\n  char d;\n};\n\n// Optimal ordering (largest to smallest): double(8) + int(4) + char(1) + char(1) + pad(2) = 16 bytes!\nclass Optimized {\n  double b;\n  int c;\n  char a;\n  char d;\n};\n```\n\n### 💡 3. Access Modifiers, Getters/Setters & The `this` Pointer\n* **`private` (Default in C++ `class`):** Accessible **only** inside the class's own member functions and `friend` functions/classes.\n* **`protected`:** Accessible inside the class itself and inside **derived (child) classes**, but hidden from external code.\n* **`public` (Default in C++ `struct`):** Accessible from anywhere.\n* **The `this` Pointer:** A hidden constant pointer (`ClassName* const this`) automatically passed as an implicit first argument to every **non-static** member function, holding the memory address of the current object that invoked the method.\n\n### 💡 4. Constructors, Destructors, Shallow vs. Deep Copy & Rule of Three/Five\n* **Default & Parameterized Constructors:** Invoked automatically when an object is instantiated to initialize data members.\n* **Member Initializer List (`Student(int r, int &ref) : rollNo(r), refVar(ref) {}`):** Executes **before** the constructor body. **Mandatory** for initializing:\n  1. `const` non-static data members.\n  2. Reference data members (`int&`).\n  3. Member objects of another class that lack a default constructor.\n  4. Base class constructors that require arguments.\n* **Copy Constructor (`Student(const Student &other)`):**\n  * **Why `const Student &other` Must Be Passed by Reference:** If passed by value (`Student(Student other)`), copying `other` into the parameter would itself call the Copy Constructor, causing **infinite recursion** (caught as a compile-time error)!\n* **Shallow Copy vs. Deep Copy:**\n  * The compiler-generated default Copy Constructor and Copy Assignment Operator (`operator=`) perform a **bitwise Shallow Copy**. If a class holds a dynamically allocated heap pointer (`int* data = new int[100]`), a shallow copy copies only the pointer address—so both objects point to the **same heap block**! When both objects go out of scope, their destructors call `delete[] data` twice on the same address, crashing with a **Double-Free (`SIGABRT`)** error!\n  * **Deep Copy (Rule of Three):** Any class managing raw heap memory must explicitly define a custom **Destructor**, **Deep Copy Constructor** (allocating a separate `new` memory block and copying values), and **Copy Assignment Operator** (with a self-assignment guard `if (this == &other) return *this;`).\n\n```cpp\nclass Buffer {\n  int* data;\n  int size;\npublic:\n  Buffer(int s) : size(s), data(new int[s]) {}\n\n  // Deep Copy Constructor: Allocates independent heap memory\n  Buffer(const Buffer& other) : size(other.size), data(new int[other.size]) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) data[i] = other.data[i];\n  }\n\n  // Safe Copy Assignment Operator with Self-Assignment Guard\n  Buffer& operator=(const Buffer& other) {\n    if (this == &other) return *this; // Prevent self-deletion on b1 = b1!\n    delete[] data;\n    size = other.size;\n    data = new int[size];\n    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) data[i] = other.data[i];\n    return *this;\n  }\n\n  ~Buffer() { delete[] data; }\n};\n```\n\n### 💡 5. Pillar 1 & 2: Encapsulation & Abstraction\n* **Encapsulation (Data Hiding + Bundling):** Wrapping data members and member functions together inside a class while restricting direct external access to internal state (declaring all data members `private` and exposing validated `public` getters/setters).\n* **Abstraction (Implementation Hiding):** Exposing only essential high-level interfaces to the user while hiding complex underlying implementation details. Achieved via **Abstract Classes** (classes containing at least one **Pure Virtual Function** `virtual void pay(double amount) = 0;`) and header/implementation separation.\n* **Rules of Abstract Classes:**\n  * You **cannot instantiate** an object of an Abstract Class (`Shape s;` is a compile error), though you **can** declare pointers and references to it (`Shape* ptr = new Circle();`).\n  * If a derived class fails to override all pure virtual functions of its base class, the derived class **also becomes abstract**.\n\n### 💡 6. Pillar 3: Inheritance, Constructor Order & The Diamond Problem\n* **Modes of Inheritance:**\n  * **`public` inheritance:** `public` stays `public`; `protected` stays `protected`. (Models an **IS-A** relationship).\n  * **`protected` inheritance:** Both `public` and `protected` base members become `protected` in the derived class.\n  * **`private` inheritance:** Both `public` and `protected` base members become `private` in the derived class.\n  * Base `private` members are **never directly accessible** in the derived class regardless of inheritance mode.\n* **Constructor vs. Destructor Order in Inheritance:**\n  * **Constructors** run from **Top to Bottom** (**Base -> Member Objects (in declaration order) -> Derived**).\n  * **Destructors** run in exact reverse order from **Bottom to Top** (**Derived -> Member Objects -> Base**).\n* **The Diamond Problem & Virtual Inheritance:**\n  * When `B` and `C` both inherit from `A`, and `D` inherits from both `B` and `C`, `D` receives **two separate copies** of `A`'s data members, causing compile-time ambiguity (`d.x` is ambiguous).\n  * **Solution (`class B : virtual public A` and `class C : virtual public A`):** Adds a virtual base pointer (**`vbptr`**) so `D` contains only **one shared subobject of `A`**, and the **most-derived class (`D`)** becomes directly responsible for invoking `A`'s constructor!\n\n### 💡 7. Pillar 4: Polymorphism, VTable / VPTR Internals & Virtual Destructors\n* **Compile-Time (Static) Polymorphism (Early Binding):**\n  1. **Function Overloading:** Same function name with different parameter types, count, or order. (Cannot overload by return type alone!).\n  2. **Operator Overloading:** Customizing operators (`+`, `==`, `<<`, `[]`). Operators that **cannot** be overloaded: `::`, `.`, `.*`, `?:`, `sizeof`, `typeid`.\n* **Run-Time (Dynamic) Polymorphism (Late Binding via `virtual` Functions):**\n  * Achieved via **Method Overriding** (`virtual` in Base + `override` in Derived) accessed through a **Base class pointer or reference** (`Base* ptr = new Derived(); ptr->speak();`).\n* **How `vtable` and `vptr` Work Under the Hood:**\n  * **1. `vtable` (Virtual Method Table):** A static per-class lookup array of function pointers created at **compile time** in read-only memory for every polymorphic class.\n  * **2. `vptr` (Virtual Pointer):** A hidden pointer (`8 bytes` on 64-bit) added inside **every object instance** of a polymorphic class. When an object's constructor runs, it sets `this->vptr` to point to that class's `vtable`.\n  * **3. Dynamic Dispatch:** Calling `ptr->speak()` executes `(*(ptr->vptr[index]))(ptr)` at runtime in O(1) time, invoking `Derived::speak()`.\n* **Why Base Destructors Must Always Be `virtual`:**\n  * If you execute `Base* ptr = new Derived(); delete ptr;` and `~Base()` is **not** declared `virtual`, the compiler uses static binding and calls **only `~Base()`**, skipping `~Derived()` completely and leaking all heap resources allocated by `Derived`! Declaring `virtual ~Base()` routes `delete ptr` through the `vtable` so `~Derived()` runs first, followed by `~Base()`.\n\n### 💡 8. `static`, `const`, `friend`, `inline` & SOLID Design Principles\n* **`static` Members:** Shared across all instances of a class (`static int count;`). Initialized outside the class definition. `static` member functions have **no `this` pointer**, can only access `static` members, and cannot be `virtual` or `const`.\n* **`friend` Functions & Classes:** Can access `private` and `protected` members of a class. Friendship is **not mutual**, **not inherited** by child classes, and **not transitive**.\n* **SOLID Principles for Production Object-Oriented Design:**\n  * **S — Single Responsibility Principle (SRP):** A class should have only one reason to change.\n  * **O — Open/Closed Principle (OCP):** Software entities should be open for extension (via polymorphic interfaces) but closed for modification.\n  * **L — Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP):** Subtypes must be substitutable for their base types without breaking program invariants.\n  * **I — Interface Segregation Principle (ISP):** Clients should not be forced to implement fat interfaces they do not use.\n  * **D — Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP):** High-level modules should depend on abstractions (interfaces), not concrete low-level implementations.	[{"url": "https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/classes", "link": "https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/classes", "title": "CPPReference — C++ Classes, Inheritance & Virtual Functions Specification"}]	[{"url": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JeYx8vJB75Q", "title": "Complete C++ OOPS in 1 Shot", "duration": "497m", "creator_name": "CodeHelp - by Babbar", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@CodeHelp"}]	[{"id": "oop-test-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: Why is sizeof(EmptyClass) equal to 1 byte in C++, whereas an empty base class subobject inside a derived class often adds 0 bytes?", "options": ["A) C++ requires every standalone object in memory to have a unique address, while compilers apply Empty Base Optimization (EBO) when an empty class is inherited.", "B) The 1 byte stores a hidden virtual pointer (vptr) for Object.prototype.", "C) The 1 byte stores the null terminator of the class name for RTTI.", "D) Standalone empty classes are allocated on the Heap, whereas derived classes are allocated on the Stack."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Why is sizeof(EmptyClass) equal to 1 byte in C++, whereas an empty base class subobject inside a derived class with an int member often adds 0 bytes (sizeof(Derived) == 4)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "The C++ standard mandates that two distinct standalone objects (e.g., Empty e1, e2; or Empty arr[2];) must have distinct memory addresses (&e1 != &e2). If sizeof(Empty) were 0, pointer arithmetic and address uniqueness would break. However, when Empty is inherited by a Derived class that already has data members (like int x), the compiler applies Empty Base Optimization (EBO), collapsing the base subobject overhead to 0 bytes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "oop-test-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: Class B inherits from Class A using protected inheritance (class B : protected A), and Class C inherits from Class B using public inheritance (class C : public B). What is the accessibility of a public member x of Class A inside Class C and inside main()?", "options": ["A) Accessible inside Class C (as protected), but inaccessible inside main().", "B) Inaccessible in both Class C and main().", "C) Accessible in both Class C and main().", "D) Accessible inside Class C (as private), and inaccessible in any class derived from C."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Class B inherits from Class A using protected inheritance (class B : protected A), and Class C inherits from Class B using public inheritance (class C : public B). What is the accessibility of a public member x of Class A inside Class C and inside main()?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "When B inherits from A via protected inheritance, A's public member x becomes protected inside B. When C inherits publicly from B, B's protected member x remains protected inside C—so C's member functions can access x directly, but external code in main() cannot access x on an instance of B or C.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "oop-test-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: Why must the parameter of a C++ Copy Constructor be passed by reference (e.g., Sample(const Sample &obj)) rather than by value (Sample(Sample obj))?", "options": ["A) Passing by value would require copying the argument into the parameter, which itself invokes the Copy Constructor, leading to infinite recursion (compile-time error).", "B) Passing by value only copies public members and ignores private members.", "C) Passing by value allocates the object on the Heap instead of the Stack.", "D) Passing by value prevents the destructor from being called when the function exits."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Why must the parameter of a C++ Copy Constructor be passed by reference (e.g., Sample(const Sample &obj)) rather than by value (Sample(Sample obj))?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In C++, passing an object by value to any function initializes the formal parameter by calling the class's Copy Constructor. If the Copy Constructor itself took its argument by value (Sample(Sample obj)), calling it would require calling Sample(Sample obj) to copy the argument, creating an infinite chain of copy constructor calls. Therefore, the C++ compiler rejects pass-by-value copy constructors as a compile error.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "oop-test-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: Which statement accurately describes when and where the Virtual Method Table (vtable) and Virtual Pointer (vptr) are created and initialized in C++?", "options": ["A) Both vtable and vptr are created at runtime on the Heap when new is called.", "B) One vtable per polymorphic class is generated at compile time in read-only memory, while each object instance contains its own vptr initialized at runtime inside the constructor.", "C) One vptr per class is stored as a static pointer, while each object stores its own private copy of the vtable.", "D) Both vtable and vptr exist only at compile time and are stripped out before runtime."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which statement accurately describes when and where the Virtual Method Table (vtable) and Virtual Pointer (vptr) are created and initialized in C++?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The compiler creates a single static vtable (an array of function pointers) per polymorphic class at compile time (stored in read-only data/rodata). Every object of that class contains a hidden vptr member variable (8 bytes on 64-bit). At runtime, when an object's constructor executes, compiler-injected prologue code sets the object's vptr to point to its class's vtable.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "oop-test-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: If a Base class constructor calls a virtual function display() that is overridden in the Derived class, which version of display() is executed when instantiating a Derived object?", "options": ["A) Derived::display(), because dynamic dispatch always uses the most-derived type.", "B) Base::display(), because while the Base constructor is running, the Derived subobject has not been constructed yet and vptr points to Base's vtable.", "C) It results in a compile-time error because virtual functions cannot be called inside constructors.", "D) It throws a runtime segmentation fault."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "If a Base class constructor calls a virtual function display() that is overridden in the Derived class, which version of display() is executed when instantiating a Derived object?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "During the construction of a Derived object, the Base constructor runs first before any Derived data members are initialized. While inside the Base constructor, the object's dynamic type is treated as Base (its vptr points to Base's vtable) so that uninitialized Derived members are never accessed. Once the Base constructor finishes and execution enters the Derived constructor, vptr is updated to point to Derived's vtable. The same rule applies in reverse inside ~Base().", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "oop-test-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: For which of the following class members is a Constructor Member Initializer List strictly mandatory (cannot be assigned inside the constructor body)?", "options": ["A) 1 and 2 only", "B) 1, 2, and 3 only", "C) 1, 2, 3, and 4", "D) 2 and 4 only"], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "For which of the following class members is a Constructor Member Initializer List strictly mandatory (cannot be assigned inside the constructor body)?\\n1. Non-static const data members (const int id;)\\n2. Reference data members (int &ref;)\\n3. Member objects of a class that has no default constructor\\n4. Static data members (static int count;)", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Non-static const members, reference members (T&), and member subobjects lacking a default constructor must be initialized before the constructor body begins—which is only possible via the Member Initializer List. Static data members (4) do not belong to individual instances and must be defined outside the class (or inline static), never in a constructor initializer list.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "oop-test-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: Which set of C++ operators CANNOT be overloaded under any circumstances, and which set can ONLY be overloaded as non-static member functions (never as friend/global functions)?", "options": ["A) Cannot overload: ::, ., .*, ?:, sizeof | Must be member functions: =, (), [], ->", "B) Cannot overload: =, (), [], -> | Must be member functions: <<, >>, +, -", "C) Cannot overload: new, delete, ->*, , | Must be member functions: ==, !=, <, >", "D) Cannot overload: ::, ?, [], -> | Must be member functions: +, -, *, /"], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which set of C++ operators CANNOT be overloaded under any circumstances, and which set can ONLY be overloaded as non-static member functions (never as friend/global functions)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "The scope resolution (::), member access (.), pointer-to-member (.*), ternary conditional (?:), and sizeof/typeid operators cannot be overloaded in C++. Furthermore, the assignment (=), function call (()), subscript ([]), and class member access (->) operators must be overloaded as non-static member functions to guarantee that the left-hand operand is always a class instance (*this).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "oop-test-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: Can a Pure Virtual Destructor (virtual ~Base() = 0;) exist in C++, and what special rule applies to it?", "options": ["A) No, destructors can be virtual, but they can never be declared pure virtual (= 0).", "B) Yes, it makes the class abstract, and you MUST still provide a function body definition for ~Base() outside the class, otherwise the linker fails when a Derived object is destroyed.", "C) Yes, and providing a body for it is a syntax error because pure virtual functions cannot have bodies.", "D) Yes, and derived classes are prevented from defining their own destructors."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Can a Pure Virtual Destructor (virtual ~Base() = 0;) exist in C++, and what special rule applies to it?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Declaring virtual ~Base() = 0; is a valid way to make a class abstract when no other method needs to be pure virtual. However, unlike normal pure virtual functions, whenever a Derived object is destroyed, its destructor automatically chains a call to Base::~Base() at the end! If Base::~Base() has no function body definition, the linker throws an \\"undefined reference to Base::~Base()\\" error.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "oop-test-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: Why does C++ forbid declaring a static member function as virtual or const (e.g., virtual static void foo() or static void bar() const)?", "options": ["A) Because static member functions are stored on the Stack instead of the Code segment.", "B) Because static member functions belong to the class rather than any object instance and therefore do not have a \\"this\\" pointer (which is required both to access vptr for virtual dispatch and to qualify \\"*this\\" as const).", "C) Because static functions can only return integer types.", "D) Because static functions are automatically inlined at compile time."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Why does C++ forbid declaring a static member function as virtual or const (e.g., virtual static void foo() or static void bar() const)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "A const member function qualifies the implicit this pointer as const ClassName* const this, and a virtual function relies on dereferencing this->vptr to look up the vtable at runtime. Because a static member function is invoked without an object instance and has no this pointer at all, neither virtual nor const has any meaning on a static function.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "oop-test-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: Which of the following statements about the friend keyword in C++ is TRUE?", "options": ["A) If Class A is a friend of Class B, and Class B is a friend of Class C, then Class A automatically becomes a friend of Class C.", "B) If Class Base declares Function F as a friend, Function F automatically has access to private members of classes derived from Base.", "C) Friendship is neither mutual, nor transitive, nor inherited, and a friend declaration behaves identically whether placed in the private, protected, or public section of a class.", "D) A friend function receives an implicit this pointer just like a regular member function."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which of the following statements about the friend keyword in C++ is TRUE?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In C++, friendship is explicitly granted, one-way (not symmetric/mutual), not transitive (friend of a friend is not a friend), and not inherited by derived classes. Because a friend function is not a member of the class, access specifiers (private/protected/public) do not affect where the friend declaration is placed inside the class body.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "oop-test-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: What is \\"Object Slicing\\" in C++, and when does it occur?", "options": ["A) When a Derived object is assigned or passed by VALUE to a Base object variable (Base b = d;), causing the Derived-specific data members to be sliced off and b's vptr to point to Base's vtable.", "B) When a Derived object is deleted through a Base pointer.", "C) When a dynamic_cast fails and returns nullptr.", "D) When an array of objects is split across two memory pages."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "What is \\"Object Slicing\\" in C++, and when does it occur?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "When a Derived instance is copied by value into a standalone Base variable (Base b = derivedObj; or void fn(Base b)), Base's copy constructor runs. Because b only has enough memory allocated for sizeof(Base), all additional Derived member variables are permanently \\"sliced off,\\" and b's vptr is set to Base's vtable—destroying polymorphic behavior. Passing by pointer (Base*) or reference (Base&) avoids object slicing.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "oop-test-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: Class Base defines void fun() and void fun(int x). Class Derived (inheriting publicly from Base) defines void fun(double y). What happens if you call d.fun() (with no arguments) on a Derived object d?", "options": ["A) It calls Base::fun() normally via inheritance.", "B) It calls Derived::fun(0.0) via implicit conversion.", "C) Compile-time error due to Name Hiding: declaring any function named fun in Derived hides all overloaded versions of fun in Base (unless \\"using Base::fun;\\" is added in Derived).", "D) Runtime exception due to ambiguous overload resolution."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Class Base defines void fun() and void fun(int x). Class Derived (inheriting publicly from Base) defines void fun(double y). What happens if you call d.fun() (with no arguments) on a Derived object d?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "In C++, overload resolution does NOT cross scope boundaries by default! As soon as the compiler finds the name \\"fun\\" inside Derived's scope, it stops searching Base's scope—hiding Base::fun() and Base::fun(int). Thus d.fun() fails to compile because Derived::fun(double) requires 1 argument. Bringing Base's overloads into Derived's scope via using Base::fun; fixes it.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "oop-test-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: Which keyword allows a specific non-static data member of a class to be modified even inside a const member function (e.g., for caching or mutex locking)?", "options": ["A) volatile", "B) mutable", "C) auto", "D) explicit"], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which keyword allows a specific non-static data member of a class to be modified even inside a const member function (e.g., for caching or mutex locking)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Inside a const member function (int getValue() const), the this pointer has type const ClassName* const, preventing modification of any normal member variable. Declaring a member variable as mutable (e.g., mutable int accessCount; or mutable std::mutex mtx;) allows logical constness by permitting that specific member to be modified inside const methods.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "oop-test-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: In Virtual Inheritance (where B and C virtually inherit from A, and D inherits from B and C), which class is responsible for calling the constructor of virtual base class A when an object of D is instantiated?", "options": ["A) Class B, because it is listed first in D's inheritance list.", "B) Both B and C call A's constructor, and the second call overwrites the first.", "C) The most-derived class being instantiated (Class D) invokes A's constructor directly before B and C run, and the calls to A's constructor in B and C's initializer lists are ignored.", "D) Class A's constructor is not called in virtual inheritance."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "In Virtual Inheritance (where B and C virtually inherit from A, and D inherits from B and C), which class is responsible for calling the constructor of virtual base class A when an object of D is instantiated?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "In virtual inheritance, there is only one shared A subobject inside D. If both B and C initialized A, A would be initialized twice with conflicting arguments! Therefore, the C++ standard specifies that the most-derived class (D) is solely responsible for initializing the virtual base class A before any non-virtual base constructors (B and C) run.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "oop-test-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: What is the prerequisite for using dynamic_cast<Derived*>(basePtr) for safe downcasting, and what happens if the cast fails at runtime for a pointer vs. a reference?", "options": ["A) Base must be polymorphic (have at least one virtual function); on failure, pointer cast returns nullptr while reference cast throws std::bad_cast.", "B) Base must have a default constructor; on failure, both pointer and reference casts return nullptr.", "C) Base must inherit from std::object; on failure, both throw std::runtime_error.", "D) No prerequisite is needed; dynamic_cast is resolved entirely at compile time."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "What is the prerequisite for using dynamic_cast<Derived*>(basePtr) for safe downcasting, and what happens if the cast fails at runtime for a pointer vs. a reference?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "dynamic_cast relies on Run-Time Type Information (RTTI), which is stored in the class's vtable. Therefore, the Base class must be polymorphic (contain at least one virtual function). If basePtr actually points to a different derived type, dynamic_cast<Derived*>(basePtr) safely returns nullptr. Since a C++ reference cannot be null, dynamic_cast<Derived&>(baseRef) throws a std::bad_cast exception on failure.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "oop-test-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: How does C++ bind default arguments on virtual functions when called through a Base pointer pointing to a Derived object?", "options": ["A) Both the function body and the default argument value are dynamically bound at runtime from the Derived class.", "B) Both the function body and the default argument value are statically bound at compile time from the Base class.", "C) The function body is dynamically bound at runtime from the Derived class, but the default argument value is statically bound at compile time from the Base pointer type!", "D) C++ prohibits default arguments on virtual functions."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "How does C++ bind default arguments on virtual functions when called through a Base pointer pointing to a Derived object?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For runtime efficiency, C++ compilers determine default parameter values statically at compile time based on the static type of the pointer/reference (Base*), whereas the virtual function call itself is resolved dynamically at runtime via the vtable (Derived::fn). Hence, calling basePtr->fn() executes Derived's function body using Base's default parameter value!", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "oop-test-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: If a class declares its Destructor as private, what is the consequence for creating objects of that class?", "options": ["A) No objects of the class can ever be created on either the Stack or the Heap.", "B) Objects cannot be created as local Stack variables (compile error), and can only be allocated dynamically on the Heap via new and destroyed via a public member/friend function.", "C) Objects can only be created on the Stack, and Heap allocation via new fails.", "D) The compiler ignores the private specifier on destructors."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "If a class declares its Destructor as private, what is the consequence for creating objects of that class?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For any local Stack object (or global/static object), the compiler automatically inserts an implicit destructor call when the variable goes out of scope—which fails to compile if ~MyClass() is private! However, Heap allocation (MyClass* p = new MyClass();) only calls the constructor, so it succeeds! To free the object, the class provides a public method like void destroy() { delete this; }.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "oop-test-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: Why should single-argument constructors in C++ (such as Array(int size)) usually be marked with the explicit keyword?", "options": ["A) To allocate the object in CPU registers.", "B) To prevent the compiler from using that constructor for unintended implicit type conversions (such as Array arr = 10; or passing an int to a function expecting an Array).", "C) To make the constructor virtual.", "D) To prevent the class from being inherited."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Why should single-argument constructors in C++ (such as Array(int size)) usually be marked with the explicit keyword?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In C++, any constructor that can be called with a single argument acts as an implicit conversion operator from the parameter type to the class type (allowing bizarre code like Array a = 50; to silently construct a 50-element Array). Marking the constructor explicit forces developers to write explicit construction syntax Array a(50);.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "oop-test-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: How does a C++11 Move Constructor (Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept) optimize performance compared to a Deep Copy Constructor?", "options": ["A) It compresses the heap array using zlib.", "B) When initialized from a temporary R-value object, it \\"steals\\" the internal heap pointer in O(1) time and sets other.data = nullptr instead of allocating new memory and copying elements in O(N) time.", "C) It skips calling the destructor of the moved-from object.", "D) It moves the object from the Heap to the Stack."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "How does a C++11 Move Constructor (Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept) optimize performance compared to a Deep Copy Constructor?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "When an object is initialized from a temporary R-value (which is about to be destroyed anyway), allocating a brand-new heap buffer and deep-copying N elements is wasteful. The Move Constructor copies the raw pointer address (this->data = other.data) in O(1) time and nulls out the source pointer (other.data = nullptr) so the temporary's destructor safely does nothing (delete[] nullptr is a safe no-op).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "oop-test-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: Normally, an overriding virtual function in a Derived class must have the exact same return type as the Base class function. What is the one exception allowed by C++ (\\"Covariant Return Types\\")?", "options": ["A) Returning a double instead of a float.", "B) Returning a Derived* (or Derived&) when the Base virtual function returns a Base* (or Base&), such as in a virtual clone() method.", "C) Returning void when the Base function returns int.", "D) Returning a std::shared_ptr<Derived> when Base returns std::shared_ptr<Base>."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Normally, an overriding virtual function in a Derived class must have the exact same return type as the Base class function. What is the one exception allowed by C++ (\\"Covariant Return Types\\")?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "C++ supports Covariant Return Types for raw pointers and references: if Base::clone() returns Base*, then Derived::clone() override is permitted to return Derived* because every Derived* is-a Base*. (Note: this built-in language rule applies only to raw pointers/references, not smart pointer templates).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 20}, {"id": "oop-test-q21", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q21: How does the C++ compiler distinguish between overloaded Pre-increment (++obj) and Post-increment (obj++) operators, and which one is more memory-efficient?", "options": ["A) Post-increment takes a dummy int parameter (operator++(int)) and returns the old state by value (creating a temporary copy), making Pre-increment (operator++(), returning *this by reference) more efficient.", "B) Pre-increment takes a dummy int parameter and is slower than Post-increment.", "C) Post-increment must be a friend function while Pre-increment is a member function.", "D) Both have identical signatures and return types."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "How does the C++ compiler distinguish between overloaded Pre-increment (++obj) and Post-increment (obj++) operators, and which one is more memory-efficient?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Pre-increment has signature Counter& operator++()—it increments the object in place and returns *this by reference with zero temporary copies. Post-increment has signature Counter operator++(int) (with a dummy unused int parameter)—it must save a copy of the original state, increment *this, and return the saved copy by value.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 21}, {"id": "oop-test-q22", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q22: Why are C++ inline functions safer than C preprocessor macros (#define SQUARE(x) x*x), and when might a compiler ignore the inline keyword?", "options": ["A) Macros perform blind text substitution without type checking or operator precedence safety (e.g., SQUARE(3+1) expands to 3+1*3+1 = 7), whereas inline functions enforce type safety and evaluate arguments once; compilers ignore inline for recursive or virtual calls.", "B) Macros are evaluated at runtime whereas inline functions run in the preprocessor.", "C) Compilers are strictly required by the C++ standard to inline every function marked inline.", "D) Inline functions cannot access private class members."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Why are C++ inline functions safer than C preprocessor macros (#define SQUARE(x) x*x), and when might a compiler ignore the inline keyword?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Preprocessor macros perform blind text replacement before compilation—causing precedence bugs (3 + 1 * 3 + 1 = 7 instead of 16) and double-evaluation bugs (SQUARE(++a) increments a twice!). Inline functions are real functions with type checking and scope rules. Note that inline is only a hint/request to the compiler (and provides linkage rules for headers); compilers will not inline recursive functions or dynamically dispatched virtual calls.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 22}, {"id": "oop-test-q23", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q23: What happens to destructors if an exception is thrown halfway through executing a class's constructor body?", "options": ["A) The destructor of the partially constructed class itself is called immediately.", "B) The destructor of the partially constructed class itself is NOT called, but the destructors of all already-constructed base classes and member subobjects ARE automatically called during stack unwinding.", "C) No destructors are called at all, and the program terminates immediately.", "D) Exceptions cannot be thrown inside constructors in C++."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "What happens to destructors if an exception is thrown halfway through executing a class's constructor body?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "In C++, an object's destructor is only invoked if its constructor completed execution! If a constructor throws an exception midway, ~MyClass() is NOT called. However, C++ guarantees that any base class subobjects and member objects (like std::string or std::unique_ptr) that finished constructing before the throw WILL have their destructors called automatically during stack unwinding.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 23}, {"id": "oop-test-q24", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q24: When you overload operator new and operator delete inside a class, why are they implicitly static member functions even if you omit the static keyword?", "options": ["A) Because operator new is called BEFORE the object is constructed (to allocate raw memory for it), and operator delete is called AFTER the destructor has already destroyed the object—so no valid \\"this\\" object exists inside either function!", "B) Because they are inherited from Object.prototype.", "C) Because they can only allocate static variables.", "D) They are not static; they require a valid this pointer."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "When you overload operator new and operator delete inside a class, why are they implicitly static member functions even if you omit the static keyword?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "When new MyClass() runs, Step 1 calls operator new(sizeof(MyClass)) to allocate raw bytes BEFORE the constructor runs, and Step 2 invokes the constructor on that raw memory. When delete p runs, Step 1 invokes ~MyClass() to destroy the object, and Step 2 calls operator delete(void*) to free the raw bytes. Because no constructed object exists when operator new or operator delete runs, both are implicitly static!", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 24}, {"id": "oop-test-q25", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q25: What is the key architectural difference between Aggregation and Composition in Object-Oriented Design?", "options": ["A) Aggregation is an \\"IS-A\\" relationship, while Composition is a \\"HAS-A\\" relationship.", "B) Both model \\"HAS-A\\" relationships, but in Composition (strong ownership, e.g., House and Room) the part's lifecycle is strictly bound to the whole, whereas in Aggregation (weak ownership, e.g., Department and Professor) the part can exist independently if the container is destroyed.", "C) Composition requires multiple inheritance, while Aggregation uses friend classes.", "D) Aggregation only works with primitive types."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "What is the key architectural difference between Aggregation and Composition in Object-Oriented Design?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Both Aggregation and Composition represent \\"HAS-A\\" associations. In Composition (\\"part-of\\", e.g., a Car owning an Engine member object by value/unique_ptr), destroying the Car automatically destroys the Engine. In Aggregation (e.g., a Department holding pointers/references to Professor objects), destroying the Department does not destroy the Professors.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 25}, {"id": "oop-test-q26", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q26: [Memory Alignment Math] On a standard 64-bit system (char = 1B, int = 4B, double = 8B), what is sizeof(SensorPacket) for the class below?", "options": ["A) 14 bytes", "B) 16 bytes", "C) 24 bytes", "D) 32 bytes"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "On a standard 64-bit system (char = 1B, int = 4B, double = 8B), what is sizeof(SensorPacket) for the class below?\\n\\nclass SensorPacket {\\n  char status;      // 1 byte\\n  double reading;   // 8 bytes\\n  int sensorId;     // 4 bytes\\n  char checksum;    // 1 byte\\n};", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Step-by-step offset calculation: 1) status (char) at offset 0 (1 byte). 2) reading (double, 8B) requires an offset divisible by 8 -> 7 padding bytes added at offsets 1..7, reading placed at offsets 8..15 (total = 16B). 3) sensorId (int, 4B) requires offset divisible by 4 -> placed at offsets 16..19 (total = 20B). 4) checksum (char, 1B) placed at offset 20 (total = 21B). 5) Total struct size must be a multiple of the largest alignment (8B) -> rounded up from 21 to 24 bytes (3 trailing padding bytes).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 26}, {"id": "oop-test-q27", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q27: [Memory Optimization Math] If you reorder the members of SensorPacket from Q26 in descending order of size (double, int, char, char), how many Megabytes (MB) of RAM are saved when allocating an array of 1,000,000 (10^6) SensorPacket objects?", "options": ["A) New size = 16 bytes; RAM saved = 8,000,000 bytes (~8 MB)", "B) New size = 14 bytes; RAM saved = 10,000,000 bytes (~10 MB)", "C) New size = 20 bytes; RAM saved = 4,000,000 bytes (~4 MB)", "D) New size = 24 bytes; RAM saved = 0 bytes"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "If you reorder the members of SensorPacket from Q26 in descending order of size (double reading; int sensorId; char status; char checksum;), what is the new sizeof(SensorPacket), and how much RAM is saved across an array of 1,000,000 (10^6) objects?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "With members ordered as double(8B, offsets 0..7) + int(4B, offsets 8..11) + char(1B, offset 12) + char(1B, offset 13) + 2 bytes tail padding (to reach a multiple of 8), the new sizeof(SensorPacket) is 16 bytes instead of 24 bytes! Saving 8 bytes per object across 1,000,000 objects saves exactly 8,000,000 bytes (~8 MB) of RAM.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 27}, {"id": "oop-test-q28", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q28: [Polymorphic sizeof Math] On a 64-bit architecture (pointers = 8B, int = 4B, char = 1B), what is sizeof(Widget) for the class below?", "options": ["A) 8 bytes", "B) 16 bytes", "C) 24 bytes", "D) 32 bytes"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "On a 64-bit architecture (pointers = 8B, int = 4B, char = 1B), what is sizeof(Widget) for the class below?\\n\\nclass Widget {\\n  int id;\\n  char tag;\\n  static double sharedMetric;\\npublic:\\n  virtual void render();\\n  virtual void update();\\n};", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "1) Even though there are 2 virtual functions, a class has only ONE vptr (8 bytes on a 64-bit system, placed at offsets 0..7). 2) static double sharedMetric is stored in the static data segment and adds 0 bytes to the object. 3) int id (4B) is placed at offsets 8..11, and char tag (1B) at offset 12 (total = 13 bytes). 4) Rounding 13 up to the nearest multiple of the largest alignment (8 bytes for vptr) adds 3 padding bytes = 16 bytes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 28}, {"id": "oop-test-q29", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q29: [Multiple Inheritance vptr Math] On a 64-bit system (pointers = 8B, int = 4B), what is sizeof(Derived) for the multiple inheritance hierarchy below?", "options": ["A) 20 bytes", "B) 24 bytes", "C) 32 bytes", "D) 40 bytes"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "On a 64-bit system (pointers = 8B, int = 4B), what is sizeof(Derived) for the multiple inheritance hierarchy below?\\n\\nclass Base1 {\\n  int a;\\n  virtual void f1() {}\\n};\\nclass Base2 {\\n  int b;\\n  virtual void f2() {}\\n};\\nclass Derived : public Base1, public Base2 {\\n  int c;\\n};", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "In standard 64-bit ABI (Itanium C++ ABI / GCC / Clang) or MSVC: Base1 has vptr1 (8B) + int a (4B) = 12B (padded to 16B standalone). Base2 has vptr2 (8B) + int b (4B) = 12B (padded to 16B). When Derived inherits from both Base1 and Base2, it contains Base1's vptr1 (8B) + a (4B) + pad(4B) = 16B, followed by Base2's vptr2 (8B) + b (4B), and int c (4B) packs into Base2's tail padding (or brings total to 32 bytes) -> 32 bytes total!", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 29}, {"id": "oop-test-q30", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q30: [Pointer Offset Upcasting Math] Suppose an object d of class Derived (from Q29, where sizeof(Base1) == 16) is allocated at memory address 0x1000. If we execute Base1* p1 = &d; and Base2* p2 = &d;, what addresses do p1 and p2 hold?", "options": ["A) p1 = 0x1000, p2 = 0x1000", "B) p1 = 0x1000, p2 = 0x1010 (offset by +16 bytes so p2 points directly to the Base2 subobject inside d)", "C) p1 = 0x1010, p2 = 0x1000", "D) Upcasting to the second base class is a compile-time error."], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "Suppose an object d of class Derived (inheriting from Base1 of size 16 bytes, followed by Base2 of size 16 bytes) is allocated at memory address 0x1000. If we execute Base1* p1 = &d; and Base2* p2 = &d;, what numeric addresses do p1 and p2 hold?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Inside a Derived object starting at 0x1000, the Base1 subobject occupies bytes 0x1000..0x100F (16 bytes), and the Base2 subobject starts immediately after at 0x1010 (0x1000 + 16 = 0x1010 in hex). Therefore, when upcasting Base2* p2 = &d;, the compiler automatically adjusts the pointer address by +16 bytes (0x1010) so p2 points directly to the start of the Base2 subobject!", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 30}, {"id": "oop-test-q31", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q31: [Constructor/Destructor Call Count] How many times are Base's constructor and Derived's constructor called in the code snippet below?", "options": ["A) Base constructor: 4 times | Derived constructor: 2 times", "B) Base constructor: 7 times | Derived constructor: 2 times", "C) Base constructor: 2 times | Derived constructor: 2 times", "D) Base constructor: 5 times | Derived constructor: 5 times"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "How many times are Base's constructor and Derived's constructor called in total during the execution of main()?\\n\\nclass Base { public: Base() {} virtual ~Base() {} };\\nclass Derived : public Base { public: Derived() {} ~Derived() {} };\\n\\nint main() {\\n  Base bArr[2];\\n  Base* ptrArr[3];\\n  Derived* dPtr = new Derived[2];\\n  delete[] dPtr;\\n  return 0;\\n}", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Let's count carefully: 1) Base bArr[2]; constructs 2 Base objects -> Base ctor called 2 times. 2) Base* ptrArr[3]; declares an array of 3 uninitialized pointers (NOT objects!) -> 0 constructors called! 3) new Derived[2] constructs 2 Derived objects; each Derived object first calls Base's constructor (+2) and then Derived's constructor (+2). Total: Base constructor = 2 + 2 = 4 times; Derived constructor = 2 times.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 31}, {"id": "oop-test-q32", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q32: [Copy Constructor Count Math] Assuming compiler copy elision (RVO) is disabled (-fno-elide-constructors) and no move constructor is defined, how many times is the Copy Constructor of Widget invoked in the code below?", "options": ["A) 2 times", "B) 3 times", "C) 4 times", "D) 5 times"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "Assuming compiler copy elision is disabled (-fno-elide-constructors) and no move constructor is defined, how many times is the Copy Constructor of Widget invoked when main() runs?\\n\\nWidget process(Widget u) {\\n  Widget v(u);\\n  Widget w = v;\\n  return w;\\n}\\nint main() {\\n  Widget x;\\n  Widget y = process(x);\\n  return 0;\\n}", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Without copy elision: 1) Passing x by value into parameter u calls Copy Ctor (#1). 2) Widget v(u); calls Copy Ctor (#2). 3) Widget w = v; is declaration-initialization (NOT assignment!), which calls Copy Ctor (#3). 4) return w; copies w into the temporary return slot (#4). 5) Initializing Widget y from the returned temporary calls Copy Ctor (#5). Total = 5 Copy Constructor invocations!", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 32}, {"id": "oop-test-q33", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q33: [Static Counter Trace] What is the exact numerical output printed by the program below?", "options": ["A) 3 1", "B) 3 2", "C) 2 1", "D) 3 3"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "What is the exact numerical output printed by the program below?\\n\\nclass Tracker {\\npublic:\\n  static int live;\\n  Tracker() { live++; }\\n  Tracker(const Tracker&) { live++; }\\n  ~Tracker() { live--; }\\n};\\nint Tracker::live = 0;\\n\\nint main() {\\n  Tracker t1;\\n  {\\n    Tracker t2 = t1;\\n    Tracker* t3 = new Tracker();\\n    cout << Tracker::live << \\" \\";\\n  }\\n  cout << Tracker::live;\\n  return 0;\\n}", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "1) Tracker t1; increments live to 1. 2) Inside the inner block, Tracker t2 = t1; calls the Copy Constructor, incrementing live to 2. 3) Tracker* t3 = new Tracker(); allocates an object on the Heap, incrementing live to 3. The first cout prints 3. 4) When the inner block exits, Stack object t2 is destroyed (~Tracker() decrements live to 2), but Heap object *t3 was never deleted (`delete t3` was not called), so *t3 remains alive on the Heap! The second cout prints 2. Output: \\"3 2\\".", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 33}, {"id": "oop-test-q34", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q34: [Virtual Dispatch + Default Parameter Math] What is the exact numerical output of the C++ program below?", "options": ["A) 40 40", "B) 10 40", "C) 20 40", "D) 20 20"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "What is the exact numerical output of the C++ program below?\\n\\nclass Base {\\npublic:\\n  virtual int compute(int factor = 5) {\\n    return factor * 2;\\n  }\\n};\\nclass Derived : public Base {\\npublic:\\n  int compute(int factor = 10) override {\\n    return factor * 4;\\n  }\\n};\\nint main() {\\n  Derived d;\\n  Base* ptr = &d;\\n  cout << ptr->compute() << \\" \\" << d.compute();\\n  return 0;\\n}", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) For ptr->compute(): Since ptr has static type Base*, the compiler binds the default argument from Base (factor = 5) at compile time, but dispatches the virtual call at runtime to Derived::compute(5), returning 5 * 4 = 20! 2) For d.compute(): Since d has static type Derived, it uses Derived's default argument (factor = 10) and calls Derived::compute(10), returning 10 * 4 = 40. Output: \\"20 40\\".", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 34}, {"id": "oop-test-q35", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q35: [Virtual Diamond Inheritance Value Trace] What is the exact integer printed by the program below?", "options": ["A) 18", "B) 28", "C) 38", "D) 58"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "What is the exact integer printed by the program below?\\n\\nclass A {\\npublic:\\n  int val;\\n  A(int v = 10) : val(v) {}\\n};\\nclass B : virtual public A {\\npublic:\\n  B() : A(20) { val += 2; }\\n};\\nclass C : virtual public A {\\npublic:\\n  C() : A(30) { val += 5; }\\n};\\nclass D : public B, public C {\\npublic:\\n  D() { val += 1; }\\n};\\nint main() {\\n  D obj;\\n  cout << obj.val;\\n  return 0;\\n}", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "In virtual inheritance, the most-derived class (D) is responsible for calling the virtual base A's constructor, and the calls A(20) in B and A(30) in C are ignored! Since D() does not explicitly specify an A constructor in its initializer list, A's default constructor A(10) runs first, setting val = 10. Next, B's constructor body runs: val += 2 (12). Next, C's constructor body runs: val += 5 (17). Finally, D's constructor body runs: val += 1 (18). Output: 18.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 35}, {"id": "oop-test-q36", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q36: [Member Initializer Order Trap] What is the exact output of the C++ code below?", "options": ["A) 10 20", "B) 20 10", "C) b is 10, but a is initialized with a garbage/indeterminate value because a is declared before b in the class body and therefore initialized BEFORE b!", "D) Compile-time error: initializer list order must match declaration order."], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "What is the exact output of the C++ code below?\\n\\nclass Test {\\n  int a;\\n  int b;\\npublic:\\n  Test(int val) : b(val), a(b * 2) {}\\n  void print() { cout << b << \\" \\" << a; }\\n};\\nint main() {\\n  Test t(10);\\n  t.print();\\n}", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In C++, class data members are ALWAYS initialized in the exact order they are declared inside the class definition (int a; then int b;), NOT the order written in the constructor's Member Initializer List! Therefore, a(b * 2) executes FIRST while b still holds uninitialized garbage memory, and b(10) executes second.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 36}, {"id": "oop-test-q37", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q37: [#pragma pack(1) vs. Natural Alignment Math] On a 64-bit system, what is the byte difference between the naturally aligned size and the #pragma pack(1) size of class Header { char v; int len; short flags; double ts; }?", "options": ["A) Natural = 24 bytes, Packed = 15 bytes -> Difference = 9 bytes", "B) Natural = 16 bytes, Packed = 15 bytes -> Difference = 1 byte", "C) Natural = 32 bytes, Packed = 16 bytes -> Difference = 16 bytes", "D) Natural = 20 bytes, Packed = 15 bytes -> Difference = 5 bytes"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "On a 64-bit system (char = 1B, short = 2B, int = 4B, double = 8B), what is the byte difference between the naturally aligned sizeof(Header) and the #pragma pack(1) tightly packed sizeof(Header) for: class Header { char v; int len; short flags; double ts; };?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "1) With #pragma pack(1), zero padding is inserted: 1 (char) + 4 (int) + 2 (short) + 8 (double) = 15 bytes. 2) With natural alignment: v(1B at 0) + pad(3B) + len(4B at 4..7) + flags(2B at 8..9) + pad(6B at 10..15 so double aligns on 16) + ts(8B at 16..23) = 24 bytes. Difference = 24 - 15 = 9 bytes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 37}, {"id": "oop-test-q38", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q38: [Construction & Destruction Sequence Trace] What exact string of digits is printed when main() executes and exits?", "options": ["A) 315624", "B) 135642", "C) 351624", "D) 531426"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "What exact string of digits is printed when main() executes and exits?\\n\\nstruct M { M() { cout << \\"1\\"; } ~M() { cout << \\"2\\"; } };\\nstruct Base { Base() { cout << \\"3\\"; } ~Base() { cout << \\"4\\"; } };\\nstruct Derived : public Base {\\n  M member;\\n  Derived() { cout << \\"5\\"; }\\n  ~Derived() { cout << \\"6\\"; }\\n};\\nint main() { Derived d; return 0; }", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "When Derived d is constructed: 1) Base class constructor runs first -> prints \\"3\\". 2) Member objects (M member) are constructed next -> prints \\"1\\". 3) Derived constructor body runs last -> prints \\"5\\" (so far: \\"315\\"). When d goes out of scope, destructors run in exact reverse order: 1) ~Derived() body runs first -> prints \\"6\\". 2) Member destructor ~M() runs next -> prints \\"2\\". 3) ~Base() runs last -> prints \\"4\\". Total output: \\"315624\\".", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 38}, {"id": "oop-test-q39", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q39: [Bit-Fields & Zero-Width Alignment Math] Assuming unsigned int is 4 bytes (32 bits), what is sizeof(RegisterFlags) for the struct below?", "options": ["A) 2 bytes", "B) 4 bytes", "C) 8 bytes", "D) 12 bytes"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "Assuming unsigned int is 4 bytes (32 bits), what is sizeof(RegisterFlags) for the struct below?\\n\\nstruct RegisterFlags {\\n  unsigned int mode : 4;\\n  unsigned int enable : 1;\\n  unsigned int : 0; // Zero-width unnamed bit-field\\n  unsigned int status : 6;\\n};", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Normally, mode (4 bits), enable (1 bit), and status (6 bits) = 11 bits would easily fit inside a single 4-byte unsigned int allocation unit. However, an unnamed zero-width bit-field (unsigned int : 0;) instructs the compiler to force padding up to the next storage unit boundary! Therefore, mode and enable occupy the first 4-byte unsigned int, and status is forced into a second 4-byte unsigned int: 4 + 4 = 8 bytes.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 39}, {"id": "oop-test-q40", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q40: [Object Slicing vs. Reference Dispatch Math] What is the numerical output of the program below?", "options": ["A) 8", "B) 11", "C) 14", "D) Compile-time error"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "What is the numerical output of the program below?\\n\\nclass Account {\\npublic:\\n  virtual int getInterest() { return 4; }\\n};\\nclass SavingsAccount : public Account {\\npublic:\\n  int getInterest() override { return 7; }\\n};\\nint byVal(Account a) { return a.getInterest(); }\\nint byRef(Account& a) { return a.getInterest(); }\\n\\nint main() {\\n  SavingsAccount sa;\\n  cout << byVal(sa) + byRef(sa);\\n  return 0;\\n}", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "1) In byVal(sa), sa is passed by VALUE into Account a, causing Object Slicing! Inside byVal, a is a pure Account instance whose vptr points to Account's vtable, so a.getInterest() returns 4. 2) In byRef(sa), sa is passed by reference (Account& a), preserving polymorphic dynamic dispatch via sa's vptr, so a.getInterest() calls SavingsAccount::getInterest() and returns 7. Sum = 4 + 7 = 11.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 40}, {"id": "oop-test-q41", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q41: [Production Memory Leak Diagnosis] In a backend service, ImageProcessor* proc = new JpegProcessor(); processes a 50 MB image buffer allocated in JpegProcessor's constructor and then executes delete proc;. Over 1,000 requests, server RAM usage balloons by 50 GB and crashes. What is the root cause and fix?", "options": ["A) delete cannot free objects larger than 1 MB; free() must be used instead.", "B) The base class destructor ~ImageProcessor() was not declared virtual, causing delete proc; to statically invoke only ~ImageProcessor() and skip ~JpegProcessor() entirely!", "C) JpegProcessor inherited privately instead of publicly.", "D) The constructor of ImageProcessor was not declared virtual."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "In a backend service, ImageProcessor* proc = new JpegProcessor(); allocates a 50 MB heap buffer inside JpegProcessor() and frees it inside ~JpegProcessor(). After each request, delete proc; is called, yet server RAM leaks 50 MB per request. What is the root cause and fix?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Deleting a derived object through a base class pointer (delete proc;) when the base class has a non-virtual destructor causes undefined behavior where only ~ImageProcessor() runs and ~JpegProcessor() is completely skipped—leaking the 50 MB buffer on every request! Adding virtual ~ImageProcessor() {} to the base class fixes the leak immediately.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 41}, {"id": "oop-test-q42", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q42: [Double-Free Crash Diagnosis] A developer creates a StringWrapper class with a char* buf member allocated via new char[len] and freed in ~StringWrapper(). Passing a StringWrapper object by value to a logging function causes the program to crash with \\"free(): double free detected in tcache\\" when main() exits. Why?", "options": ["A) Because the developer did not define a custom Deep Copy Constructor, so passing s by value used the compiler's default Shallow Copy Constructor—causing both s and the original object to point to the same char* address and call delete[] on it twice!", "B) Because char* arrays must be freed with delete instead of delete[].", "C) Because destructors cannot be called inside main().", "D) Because logging functions must be declared friend."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "A developer creates a StringWrapper class with a char* buf member allocated via new char[len] and freed in ~StringWrapper(). Passing a StringWrapper object by value to a logging function void log(StringWrapper s) causes the program to crash with \\"double free detected\\" when main() exits. Why?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "When StringWrapper s is passed by value to log(StringWrapper s), the compiler-synthesized Shallow Copy Constructor copies the pointer value s.buf = orig.buf. When log() finishes, ~StringWrapper() runs on s and frees buf! Later, when main() exits, ~StringWrapper() runs on orig and calls delete[] on the already-freed address. Implementing a Deep Copy Constructor (or passing const StringWrapper& by reference) prevents this crash.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 42}, {"id": "oop-test-q43", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q43: [Self-Assignment Bug in operator=] In an overloaded copy assignment operator (operator=), a developer writes: delete[] this->data; this->data = new int[rhs.size]; memcpy(this->data, rhs.data, ...); without checking if (this == &rhs). What happens if client code executes arr[i] = arr[j] when i == j?", "options": ["A) It works normally with a minor performance penalty.", "B) Since this and &rhs refer to the exact same object, delete[] this->data frees rhs.data BEFORE memcpy copies from it—resulting in use-after-free memory corruption!", "C) The compiler automatically skips operator= when both operands are the same object.", "D) It throws a compile-time error."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "In an overloaded copy assignment operator, a developer writes: delete[] this->data; this->data = new int[rhs.size]; memcpy(this->data, rhs.data, ...); without checking if (this == &rhs). What happens if client code executes arr[i] = arr[j] when i == j?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "When a = a (or arr[i] = arr[j] where i == j) occurs, this and &rhs point to the exact same object in memory. Executing delete[] this->data immediately destroys rhs.data as well! Subsequently trying to read rhs.data copies freed garbage memory (use-after-free). Always guard with if (this == &rhs) return *this; or use the Copy-and-Swap idiom.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 43}, {"id": "oop-test-q44", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q44: [SOLID — Open/Closed Principle & Strategy Pattern] A CheckoutService class has a 200-line if/else-if chain checking if (type == \\"UPI\\") ... else if (type == \\"CreditCard\\") ... else if (type == \\"Crypto\\"). Every time a new payment method is added, CheckoutService must be modified and retested. Which SOLID principle is violated and how do you refactor it?", "options": ["A) Violates Liskov Substitution Principle; fix using Singleton Pattern.", "B) Violates Open/Closed Principle (OCP); fix using the Strategy Pattern by defining an abstract PaymentMethod interface with a pure virtual pay() method and passing concrete implementations (UpiPayment, CardPayment) polymorphically.", "C) Violates Interface Segregation Principle; fix using Friend functions.", "D) Violates Encapsulation; fix by making all variables global."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "A CheckoutService class has a giant if/else-if chain checking if (type == \\"UPI\\") ... else if (type == \\"Card\\") .... Every time a new payment method is added, CheckoutService must be modified. Which SOLID principle is violated and which OOP pattern fixes it?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "The Open/Closed Principle states that a class should be open for extension but closed for modification. By introducing an abstract PaymentStrategy interface (virtual bool pay(double amt) = 0;), adding a new payment method (e.g., ApplePayStrategy : public PaymentStrategy) requires zero modifications to CheckoutService!", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 44}, {"id": "oop-test-q45", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q45: [SOLID — Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)] A developer models class Square : public Rectangle where setWidth(w) in Square overrides Rectangle::setWidth(w) to set BOTH width and height to w. A test function void verifyArea(Rectangle& r) { r.setWidth(5); r.setHeight(4); assert(r.area() == 20); } fails when passed a Square(16 != 20). Which principle is violated?", "options": ["A) Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)", "B) Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) — a derived class (Square) broke the behavioral postcondition/invariant expected of its base class (Rectangle) that width and height can be modified independently.", "C) Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)", "D) Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)"], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "A developer models class Square : public Rectangle where Square::setWidth(w) sets both width and height to w. A function void test(Rectangle& r) { r.setWidth(5); r.setHeight(4); assert(r.area() == 20); } fails when passed a Square (area is 16). Which SOLID principle does this violate?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Even though mathematically a square \\"is a\\" rectangle, in mutable OOP behavior a Square does NOT satisfy the behavioral contract of a mutable Rectangle (where mutating width leaves height unchanged). When substituting a Square& for a Rectangle& breaks client assertions, it is the textbook violation of the Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 45}, {"id": "oop-test-q46", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q46: [Design Patterns — Thread-Safe Singleton] You are designing a Logger Singleton class in C++11+. What is the complete set of OOP access controls and mechanisms required to guarantee that only ONE instance can ever exist and that initialization is thread-safe?", "options": ["A) Make the constructor public, and use a global variable.", "B) Make the default constructor private, delete the Copy Constructor and Copy Assignment Operator (= delete), and expose a static Logger& getInstance() method containing a local static Logger instance;", "C) Declare the class abstract with a pure virtual constructor.", "D) Use friend classes to instantiate Logger in every file."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "You are designing a Logger Singleton class in C++11+. What is the complete set of OOP controls required to guarantee that only ONE instance can ever exist and that lazy initialization is thread-safe?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "To enforce a strict Singleton: 1) Make Logger() private so external code cannot instantiate it. 2) Delete Logger(const Logger&) = delete; and operator=(const Logger&) = delete; so the singleton instance cannot be copied! 3) Inside static Logger& getInstance(), declare static Logger instance; return instance; (Meyer's Singleton)—since C++11, the standard guarantees that block-scope static variable initialization is 100% thread-safe!", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 46}, {"id": "oop-test-q47", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q47: [RAII & Exception Safety] In a multi-threaded banking transfer function, mtx.lock() is called at the start and mtx.unlock() at the end. However, if transfer() throws an InsufficientFundsException midway, mtx.unlock() is skipped and all other threads deadlock permanently! How does OOP's RAII idiom solve this?", "options": ["A) By wrapping the mutex inside a local stack object (like std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);) whose constructor acquires the lock and whose destructor automatically releases the lock during stack unwinding even if an exception is thrown.", "B) By allocating the mutex on the Heap with new.", "C) By declaring the mutex mutable.", "D) By disabling exceptions across the project."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "In a multi-threaded banking function, mtx.lock() is called at the start and mtx.unlock() at the end. If an exception is thrown midway, mtx.unlock() is skipped and all threads deadlock! How does C++'s RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) idiom solve this?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) binds the lifecycle of a resource (mutex lock, file handle, socket, heap memory) to the lifetime of a stack-allocated object. Because C++ guarantees that destructors of all local stack objects are invoked during stack unwinding when an exception is thrown or a function returns early, ~lock_guard() is 100% guaranteed to unlock the mutex.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 47}, {"id": "oop-test-q48", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q48: [Safeguarding Virtual Overrides in Large Teams] In a large codebase, a Base class method virtual void onPacketReceived(int id) const is refactored to take (long id). A developer's Derived class still has void onPacketReceived(int id) const, which silently stops overriding the base method and introduces a production bug. Which C++11 keyword prevents this at compile time?", "options": ["A) virtual", "B) override", "C) final", "D) explicit"], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "In a large codebase, Base::onPacketReceived(int id) const is updated to take (long id). A Derived class still has void onPacketReceived(int id) const, which silently stops overriding the Base method. Which C++11 keyword catches this mismatch immediately at compile time?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Appending the override specifier to a derived method declaration (void onPacketReceived(int id) const override;) instructs the compiler to verify that a virtual function with the EXACT same signature, const-qualification, and parameter types exists in the Base class. If the Base signature changes, compilation fails immediately instead of silently hiding/overloading the function.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 48}, {"id": "oop-test-q49", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q49: [Factory Design Pattern] An application needs to instantiate different document parsers (PdfParser, DocxParser, CsvParser) based on a file extension string at runtime without scattering new PdfParser() calls across 50 business-logic files. Which OOP creational design pattern solves this?", "options": ["A) Observer Pattern", "B) Factory Method / Simple Factory Pattern (returning a polymorphic std::unique_ptr<IDocumentParser> base pointer from a centralized ParserFactory::createParser(ext) method).", "C) Decorator Pattern", "D) Adapter Pattern"], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "An application needs to instantiate different document parsers (PdfParser, DocxParser, CsvParser) based on a file extension at runtime without scattering concrete new calls across 50 files. Which OOP design pattern solves this?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "The Factory Pattern centralizes object creation logic behind a factory method that returns an abstract interface pointer (IDocumentParser*). Client code depends only on the IDocumentParser abstraction and never couples directly to concrete PdfParser or CsvParser classes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 49}, {"id": "oop-test-q50", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q50: [SOLID — Dependency Inversion Principle & Unit Testing] OrderService directly instantiates SmtpEmailClient inside its constructor (this->mailer = new SmtpEmailClient();). When running automated unit tests, every test sends real emails to customers! How do you refactor OrderService using the Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)?", "options": ["A) Add a boolean flag isTesting inside SmtpEmailClient.", "B) Define an abstract IEmailClient interface (with pure virtual sendEmail() = 0), make OrderService accept an IEmailClient* (or reference) via Constructor Dependency Injection, and pass SmtpEmailClient in production and MockEmailClient in unit tests.", "C) Make SmtpEmailClient a friend of OrderService.", "D) Use private inheritance between OrderService and SmtpEmailClient."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "OrderService directly instantiates SmtpEmailClient inside its constructor. When running unit tests, every test sends real emails! How do you refactor OrderService using the Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) states that high-level modules (OrderService) should not depend on concrete low-level modules (SmtpEmailClient); both should depend on abstractions (IEmailClient). Injecting IEmailClient& via the constructor allows seamless swapping between SmtpEmailClient in production and an in-memory MockEmailClient during unit testing.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 50}]	\N	### 📌 1. Why Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) Over Procedural Programming?\nIn procedural programming, data (global or loose local variables) and functions operate separately. Modeling 1,000 students requires maintaining disconnected arrays (`id[]`, `age[]`, `name[]`) with zero access protection—any function can accidentally corrupt global state. **Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)** models software around **Objects** that bundle **State (Data Members / Attributes)** and **Behavior (Member Functions / Methods)** into a single self-contained unit defined by a **Class** blueprint.\n\n### 💡 2. Classes, Objects & Memory Layout Internals (Padding & Greedy Alignment)\n* **Class vs. Object:** A **Class** is a user-defined blueprint that consumes **0 bytes of RAM** at compile time. An **Object** is a runtime instance of a class allocated on the **Stack** (`Student s1;`) or the **Heap** (`Student* s2 = new Student();`).\n* **Why an Empty Class Has Size `1 Byte` (`sizeof(Empty) == 1`):** The C++ standard mandates that every distinct object instance must have a **unique memory address**. If an empty class had size `0`, two consecutive objects `Empty a, b;` on the stack would share the exact same address (`&a == &b`). Therefore, the compiler inserts 1 silent byte. (Exception: when an empty class is used as a base class, compilers apply **Empty Base Optimization — EBO**, reducing its base subobject contribution to `0` bytes!).\n* **Struct/Class Padding & Greedy Alignment:** CPUs read memory in word-sized chunks (4 or 8 bytes) at aligned address multiples. A member of size K bytes must be placed at a memory offset divisible by K, and the total `sizeof(Class)` must be a multiple of the **largest member's alignment requirement**.\n* **What Contributes to `sizeof(Object)`?**\n  * Non-static data members + alignment padding bytes.\n  * **`vptr` (Virtual Pointer):** `8 bytes` on 64-bit systems (`4 bytes` on 32-bit) added automatically if the class has one or more `virtual` functions.\n  * **Excluded from `sizeof(Object)`:** Member functions (stored in the Code/Text segment) and `static` data members (stored in the Data/BSS segment) contribute **0 bytes** to individual objects.\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n\n// Bad ordering: char(1) + pad(7) + double(8) + int(4) + char(1) + pad(3) = 24 bytes!\nclass Unoptimized {\n  char a;\n  double b;\n  int c;\n  char d;\n};\n\n// Optimal ordering (largest to smallest): double(8) + int(4) + char(1) + char(1) + pad(2) = 16 bytes!\nclass Optimized {\n  double b;\n  int c;\n  char a;\n  char d;\n};\n```\n\n### 💡 3. Access Modifiers, Getters/Setters & The `this` Pointer\n* **`private` (Default in C++ `class`):** Accessible **only** inside the class's own member functions and `friend` functions/classes.\n* **`protected`:** Accessible inside the class itself and inside **derived (child) classes**, but hidden from external code.\n* **`public` (Default in C++ `struct`):** Accessible from anywhere.\n* **The `this` Pointer:** A hidden constant pointer (`ClassName* const this`) automatically passed as an implicit first argument to every **non-static** member function, holding the memory address of the current object that invoked the method.\n\n### 💡 4. Constructors, Destructors, Shallow vs. Deep Copy & Rule of Three/Five\n* **Default & Parameterized Constructors:** Invoked automatically when an object is instantiated to initialize data members.\n* **Member Initializer List (`Student(int r, int &ref) : rollNo(r), refVar(ref) {}`):** Executes **before** the constructor body. **Mandatory** for initializing:\n  1. `const` non-static data members.\n  2. Reference data members (`int&`).\n  3. Member objects of another class that lack a default constructor.\n  4. Base class constructors that require arguments.\n* **Copy Constructor (`Student(const Student &other)`):**\n  * **Why `const Student &other` Must Be Passed by Reference:** If passed by value (`Student(Student other)`), copying `other` into the parameter would itself call the Copy Constructor, causing **infinite recursion** (caught as a compile-time error)!\n* **Shallow Copy vs. Deep Copy:**\n  * The compiler-generated default Copy Constructor and Copy Assignment Operator (`operator=`) perform a **bitwise Shallow Copy**. If a class holds a dynamically allocated heap pointer (`int* data = new int[100]`), a shallow copy copies only the pointer address—so both objects point to the **same heap block**! When both objects go out of scope, their destructors call `delete[] data` twice on the same address, crashing with a **Double-Free (`SIGABRT`)** error!\n  * **Deep Copy (Rule of Three):** Any class managing raw heap memory must explicitly define a custom **Destructor**, **Deep Copy Constructor** (allocating a separate `new` memory block and copying values), and **Copy Assignment Operator** (with a self-assignment guard `if (this == &other) return *this;`).\n\n```cpp\nclass Buffer {\n  int* data;\n  int size;\npublic:\n  Buffer(int s) : size(s), data(new int[s]) {}\n\n  // Deep Copy Constructor: Allocates independent heap memory\n  Buffer(const Buffer& other) : size(other.size), data(new int[other.size]) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) data[i] = other.data[i];\n  }\n\n  // Safe Copy Assignment Operator with Self-Assignment Guard\n  Buffer& operator=(const Buffer& other) {\n    if (this == &other) return *this; // Prevent self-deletion on b1 = b1!\n    delete[] data;\n    size = other.size;\n    data = new int[size];\n    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) data[i] = other.data[i];\n    return *this;\n  }\n\n  ~Buffer() { delete[] data; }\n};\n```\n\n### 💡 5. Pillar 1 & 2: Encapsulation & Abstraction\n* **Encapsulation (Data Hiding + Bundling):** Wrapping data members and member functions together inside a class while restricting direct external access to internal state (declaring all data members `private` and exposing validated `public` getters/setters).\n* **Abstraction (Implementation Hiding):** Exposing only essential high-level interfaces to the user while hiding complex underlying implementation details. Achieved via **Abstract Classes** (classes containing at least one **Pure Virtual Function** `virtual void pay(double amount) = 0;`) and header/implementation separation.\n* **Rules of Abstract Classes:**\n  * You **cannot instantiate** an object of an Abstract Class (`Shape s;` is a compile error), though you **can** declare pointers and references to it (`Shape* ptr = new Circle();`).\n  * If a derived class fails to override all pure virtual functions of its base class, the derived class **also becomes abstract**.\n\n### 💡 6. Pillar 3: Inheritance, Constructor Order & The Diamond Problem\n* **Modes of Inheritance:**\n  * **`public` inheritance:** `public` stays `public`; `protected` stays `protected`. (Models an **IS-A** relationship).\n  * **`protected` inheritance:** Both `public` and `protected` base members become `protected` in the derived class.\n  * **`private` inheritance:** Both `public` and `protected` base members become `private` in the derived class.\n  * Base `private` members are **never directly accessible** in the derived class regardless of inheritance mode.\n* **Constructor vs. Destructor Order in Inheritance:**\n  * **Constructors** run from **Top to Bottom** (**Base -> Member Objects (in declaration order) -> Derived**).\n  * **Destructors** run in exact reverse order from **Bottom to Top** (**Derived -> Member Objects -> Base**).\n* **The Diamond Problem & Virtual Inheritance:**\n  * When `B` and `C` both inherit from `A`, and `D` inherits from both `B` and `C`, `D` receives **two separate copies** of `A`'s data members, causing compile-time ambiguity (`d.x` is ambiguous).\n  * **Solution (`class B : virtual public A` and `class C : virtual public A`):** Adds a virtual base pointer (**`vbptr`**) so `D` contains only **one shared subobject of `A`**, and the **most-derived class (`D`)** becomes directly responsible for invoking `A`'s constructor!\n\n### 💡 7. Pillar 4: Polymorphism, VTable / VPTR Internals & Virtual Destructors\n* **Compile-Time (Static) Polymorphism (Early Binding):**\n  1. **Function Overloading:** Same function name with different parameter types, count, or order. (Cannot overload by return type alone!).\n  2. **Operator Overloading:** Customizing operators (`+`, `==`, `<<`, `[]`). Operators that **cannot** be overloaded: `::`, `.`, `.*`, `?:`, `sizeof`, `typeid`.\n* **Run-Time (Dynamic) Polymorphism (Late Binding via `virtual` Functions):**\n  * Achieved via **Method Overriding** (`virtual` in Base + `override` in Derived) accessed through a **Base class pointer or reference** (`Base* ptr = new Derived(); ptr->speak();`).\n* **How `vtable` and `vptr` Work Under the Hood:**\n  * **1. `vtable` (Virtual Method Table):** A static per-class lookup array of function pointers created at **compile time** in read-only memory for every polymorphic class.\n  * **2. `vptr` (Virtual Pointer):** A hidden pointer (`8 bytes` on 64-bit) added inside **every object instance** of a polymorphic class. When an object's constructor runs, it sets `this->vptr` to point to that class's `vtable`.\n  * **3. Dynamic Dispatch:** Calling `ptr->speak()` executes `(*(ptr->vptr[index]))(ptr)` at runtime in O(1) time, invoking `Derived::speak()`.\n* **Why Base Destructors Must Always Be `virtual`:**\n  * If you execute `Base* ptr = new Derived(); delete ptr;` and `~Base()` is **not** declared `virtual`, the compiler uses static binding and calls **only `~Base()`**, skipping `~Derived()` completely and leaking all heap resources allocated by `Derived`! Declaring `virtual ~Base()` routes `delete ptr` through the `vtable` so `~Derived()` runs first, followed by `~Base()`.\n\n### 💡 8. `static`, `const`, `friend`, `inline` & SOLID Design Principles\n* **`static` Members:** Shared across all instances of a class (`static int count;`). Initialized outside the class definition. `static` member functions have **no `this` pointer**, can only access `static` members, and cannot be `virtual` or `const`.\n* **`friend` Functions & Classes:** Can access `private` and `protected` members of a class. Friendship is **not mutual**, **not inherited** by child classes, and **not transitive**.\n* **SOLID Principles for Production Object-Oriented Design:**\n  * **S — Single Responsibility Principle (SRP):** A class should have only one reason to change.\n  * **O — Open/Closed Principle (OCP):** Software entities should be open for extension (via polymorphic interfaces) but closed for modification.\n  * **L — Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP):** Subtypes must be substitutable for their base types without breaking program invariants.\n  * **I — Interface Segregation Principle (ISP):** Clients should not be forced to implement fat interfaces they do not use.\n  * **D — Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP):** High-level modules should depend on abstractions (interfaces), not concrete low-level implementations.	CodeHelp - by Babbar	https://www.youtube.com/@CodeHelp	22	OOP: Complete Object-Oriented Programming in 1 Shot (C++ / Placement & GATE Edition)	Master Object-Oriented Programming from first principles—memory alignment and padding, constructors/destructors, deep vs. shallow copy, the 4 Pillars (Encapsulation, Abstraction, Inheritance, Polymorphism), VTable/VPTR internals, Virtual Inheritance, and SOLID design principles—followed by a 50-question timed evaluation test.	20	Easy	\N	1	[{"id": "oop-test-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: Why is sizeof(EmptyClass) equal to 1 byte in C++, whereas an empty base class subobject inside a derived class often adds 0 bytes?", "options": ["A) C++ requires every standalone object in memory to have a unique address, while compilers apply Empty Base Optimization (EBO) when an empty class is inherited.", "B) The 1 byte stores a hidden virtual pointer (vptr) for Object.prototype.", "C) The 1 byte stores the null terminator of the class name for RTTI.", "D) Standalone empty classes are allocated on the Heap, whereas derived classes are allocated on the Stack."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Why is sizeof(EmptyClass) equal to 1 byte in C++, whereas an empty base class subobject inside a derived class with an int member often adds 0 bytes (sizeof(Derived) == 4)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "The C++ standard mandates that two distinct standalone objects (e.g., Empty e1, e2; or Empty arr[2];) must have distinct memory addresses (&e1 != &e2). If sizeof(Empty) were 0, pointer arithmetic and address uniqueness would break. However, when Empty is inherited by a Derived class that already has data members (like int x), the compiler applies Empty Base Optimization (EBO), collapsing the base subobject overhead to 0 bytes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "oop-test-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: Class B inherits from Class A using protected inheritance (class B : protected A), and Class C inherits from Class B using public inheritance (class C : public B). What is the accessibility of a public member x of Class A inside Class C and inside main()?", "options": ["A) Accessible inside Class C (as protected), but inaccessible inside main().", "B) Inaccessible in both Class C and main().", "C) Accessible in both Class C and main().", "D) Accessible inside Class C (as private), and inaccessible in any class derived from C."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Class B inherits from Class A using protected inheritance (class B : protected A), and Class C inherits from Class B using public inheritance (class C : public B). What is the accessibility of a public member x of Class A inside Class C and inside main()?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "When B inherits from A via protected inheritance, A's public member x becomes protected inside B. When C inherits publicly from B, B's protected member x remains protected inside C—so C's member functions can access x directly, but external code in main() cannot access x on an instance of B or C.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "oop-test-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: Why must the parameter of a C++ Copy Constructor be passed by reference (e.g., Sample(const Sample &obj)) rather than by value (Sample(Sample obj))?", "options": ["A) Passing by value would require copying the argument into the parameter, which itself invokes the Copy Constructor, leading to infinite recursion (compile-time error).", "B) Passing by value only copies public members and ignores private members.", "C) Passing by value allocates the object on the Heap instead of the Stack.", "D) Passing by value prevents the destructor from being called when the function exits."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Why must the parameter of a C++ Copy Constructor be passed by reference (e.g., Sample(const Sample &obj)) rather than by value (Sample(Sample obj))?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In C++, passing an object by value to any function initializes the formal parameter by calling the class's Copy Constructor. If the Copy Constructor itself took its argument by value (Sample(Sample obj)), calling it would require calling Sample(Sample obj) to copy the argument, creating an infinite chain of copy constructor calls. Therefore, the C++ compiler rejects pass-by-value copy constructors as a compile error.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "oop-test-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: Which statement accurately describes when and where the Virtual Method Table (vtable) and Virtual Pointer (vptr) are created and initialized in C++?", "options": ["A) Both vtable and vptr are created at runtime on the Heap when new is called.", "B) One vtable per polymorphic class is generated at compile time in read-only memory, while each object instance contains its own vptr initialized at runtime inside the constructor.", "C) One vptr per class is stored as a static pointer, while each object stores its own private copy of the vtable.", "D) Both vtable and vptr exist only at compile time and are stripped out before runtime."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which statement accurately describes when and where the Virtual Method Table (vtable) and Virtual Pointer (vptr) are created and initialized in C++?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The compiler creates a single static vtable (an array of function pointers) per polymorphic class at compile time (stored in read-only data/rodata). Every object of that class contains a hidden vptr member variable (8 bytes on 64-bit). At runtime, when an object's constructor executes, compiler-injected prologue code sets the object's vptr to point to its class's vtable.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "oop-test-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: If a Base class constructor calls a virtual function display() that is overridden in the Derived class, which version of display() is executed when instantiating a Derived object?", "options": ["A) Derived::display(), because dynamic dispatch always uses the most-derived type.", "B) Base::display(), because while the Base constructor is running, the Derived subobject has not been constructed yet and vptr points to Base's vtable.", "C) It results in a compile-time error because virtual functions cannot be called inside constructors.", "D) It throws a runtime segmentation fault."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "If a Base class constructor calls a virtual function display() that is overridden in the Derived class, which version of display() is executed when instantiating a Derived object?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "During the construction of a Derived object, the Base constructor runs first before any Derived data members are initialized. While inside the Base constructor, the object's dynamic type is treated as Base (its vptr points to Base's vtable) so that uninitialized Derived members are never accessed. Once the Base constructor finishes and execution enters the Derived constructor, vptr is updated to point to Derived's vtable. The same rule applies in reverse inside ~Base().", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "oop-test-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: For which of the following class members is a Constructor Member Initializer List strictly mandatory (cannot be assigned inside the constructor body)?", "options": ["A) 1 and 2 only", "B) 1, 2, and 3 only", "C) 1, 2, 3, and 4", "D) 2 and 4 only"], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "For which of the following class members is a Constructor Member Initializer List strictly mandatory (cannot be assigned inside the constructor body)?\\n1. Non-static const data members (const int id;)\\n2. Reference data members (int &ref;)\\n3. Member objects of a class that has no default constructor\\n4. Static data members (static int count;)", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Non-static const members, reference members (T&), and member subobjects lacking a default constructor must be initialized before the constructor body begins—which is only possible via the Member Initializer List. Static data members (4) do not belong to individual instances and must be defined outside the class (or inline static), never in a constructor initializer list.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "oop-test-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: Which set of C++ operators CANNOT be overloaded under any circumstances, and which set can ONLY be overloaded as non-static member functions (never as friend/global functions)?", "options": ["A) Cannot overload: ::, ., .*, ?:, sizeof | Must be member functions: =, (), [], ->", "B) Cannot overload: =, (), [], -> | Must be member functions: <<, >>, +, -", "C) Cannot overload: new, delete, ->*, , | Must be member functions: ==, !=, <, >", "D) Cannot overload: ::, ?, [], -> | Must be member functions: +, -, *, /"], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which set of C++ operators CANNOT be overloaded under any circumstances, and which set can ONLY be overloaded as non-static member functions (never as friend/global functions)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "The scope resolution (::), member access (.), pointer-to-member (.*), ternary conditional (?:), and sizeof/typeid operators cannot be overloaded in C++. Furthermore, the assignment (=), function call (()), subscript ([]), and class member access (->) operators must be overloaded as non-static member functions to guarantee that the left-hand operand is always a class instance (*this).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "oop-test-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: Can a Pure Virtual Destructor (virtual ~Base() = 0;) exist in C++, and what special rule applies to it?", "options": ["A) No, destructors can be virtual, but they can never be declared pure virtual (= 0).", "B) Yes, it makes the class abstract, and you MUST still provide a function body definition for ~Base() outside the class, otherwise the linker fails when a Derived object is destroyed.", "C) Yes, and providing a body for it is a syntax error because pure virtual functions cannot have bodies.", "D) Yes, and derived classes are prevented from defining their own destructors."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Can a Pure Virtual Destructor (virtual ~Base() = 0;) exist in C++, and what special rule applies to it?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Declaring virtual ~Base() = 0; is a valid way to make a class abstract when no other method needs to be pure virtual. However, unlike normal pure virtual functions, whenever a Derived object is destroyed, its destructor automatically chains a call to Base::~Base() at the end! If Base::~Base() has no function body definition, the linker throws an \\"undefined reference to Base::~Base()\\" error.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "oop-test-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: Why does C++ forbid declaring a static member function as virtual or const (e.g., virtual static void foo() or static void bar() const)?", "options": ["A) Because static member functions are stored on the Stack instead of the Code segment.", "B) Because static member functions belong to the class rather than any object instance and therefore do not have a \\"this\\" pointer (which is required both to access vptr for virtual dispatch and to qualify \\"*this\\" as const).", "C) Because static functions can only return integer types.", "D) Because static functions are automatically inlined at compile time."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Why does C++ forbid declaring a static member function as virtual or const (e.g., virtual static void foo() or static void bar() const)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "A const member function qualifies the implicit this pointer as const ClassName* const this, and a virtual function relies on dereferencing this->vptr to look up the vtable at runtime. Because a static member function is invoked without an object instance and has no this pointer at all, neither virtual nor const has any meaning on a static function.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "oop-test-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: Which of the following statements about the friend keyword in C++ is TRUE?", "options": ["A) If Class A is a friend of Class B, and Class B is a friend of Class C, then Class A automatically becomes a friend of Class C.", "B) If Class Base declares Function F as a friend, Function F automatically has access to private members of classes derived from Base.", "C) Friendship is neither mutual, nor transitive, nor inherited, and a friend declaration behaves identically whether placed in the private, protected, or public section of a class.", "D) A friend function receives an implicit this pointer just like a regular member function."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which of the following statements about the friend keyword in C++ is TRUE?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In C++, friendship is explicitly granted, one-way (not symmetric/mutual), not transitive (friend of a friend is not a friend), and not inherited by derived classes. Because a friend function is not a member of the class, access specifiers (private/protected/public) do not affect where the friend declaration is placed inside the class body.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "oop-test-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: What is \\"Object Slicing\\" in C++, and when does it occur?", "options": ["A) When a Derived object is assigned or passed by VALUE to a Base object variable (Base b = d;), causing the Derived-specific data members to be sliced off and b's vptr to point to Base's vtable.", "B) When a Derived object is deleted through a Base pointer.", "C) When a dynamic_cast fails and returns nullptr.", "D) When an array of objects is split across two memory pages."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "What is \\"Object Slicing\\" in C++, and when does it occur?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "When a Derived instance is copied by value into a standalone Base variable (Base b = derivedObj; or void fn(Base b)), Base's copy constructor runs. Because b only has enough memory allocated for sizeof(Base), all additional Derived member variables are permanently \\"sliced off,\\" and b's vptr is set to Base's vtable—destroying polymorphic behavior. Passing by pointer (Base*) or reference (Base&) avoids object slicing.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "oop-test-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: Class Base defines void fun() and void fun(int x). Class Derived (inheriting publicly from Base) defines void fun(double y). What happens if you call d.fun() (with no arguments) on a Derived object d?", "options": ["A) It calls Base::fun() normally via inheritance.", "B) It calls Derived::fun(0.0) via implicit conversion.", "C) Compile-time error due to Name Hiding: declaring any function named fun in Derived hides all overloaded versions of fun in Base (unless \\"using Base::fun;\\" is added in Derived).", "D) Runtime exception due to ambiguous overload resolution."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Class Base defines void fun() and void fun(int x). Class Derived (inheriting publicly from Base) defines void fun(double y). What happens if you call d.fun() (with no arguments) on a Derived object d?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "In C++, overload resolution does NOT cross scope boundaries by default! As soon as the compiler finds the name \\"fun\\" inside Derived's scope, it stops searching Base's scope—hiding Base::fun() and Base::fun(int). Thus d.fun() fails to compile because Derived::fun(double) requires 1 argument. Bringing Base's overloads into Derived's scope via using Base::fun; fixes it.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "oop-test-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: Which keyword allows a specific non-static data member of a class to be modified even inside a const member function (e.g., for caching or mutex locking)?", "options": ["A) volatile", "B) mutable", "C) auto", "D) explicit"], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Which keyword allows a specific non-static data member of a class to be modified even inside a const member function (e.g., for caching or mutex locking)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Inside a const member function (int getValue() const), the this pointer has type const ClassName* const, preventing modification of any normal member variable. Declaring a member variable as mutable (e.g., mutable int accessCount; or mutable std::mutex mtx;) allows logical constness by permitting that specific member to be modified inside const methods.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "oop-test-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: In Virtual Inheritance (where B and C virtually inherit from A, and D inherits from B and C), which class is responsible for calling the constructor of virtual base class A when an object of D is instantiated?", "options": ["A) Class B, because it is listed first in D's inheritance list.", "B) Both B and C call A's constructor, and the second call overwrites the first.", "C) The most-derived class being instantiated (Class D) invokes A's constructor directly before B and C run, and the calls to A's constructor in B and C's initializer lists are ignored.", "D) Class A's constructor is not called in virtual inheritance."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "In Virtual Inheritance (where B and C virtually inherit from A, and D inherits from B and C), which class is responsible for calling the constructor of virtual base class A when an object of D is instantiated?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "In virtual inheritance, there is only one shared A subobject inside D. If both B and C initialized A, A would be initialized twice with conflicting arguments! Therefore, the C++ standard specifies that the most-derived class (D) is solely responsible for initializing the virtual base class A before any non-virtual base constructors (B and C) run.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "oop-test-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: What is the prerequisite for using dynamic_cast<Derived*>(basePtr) for safe downcasting, and what happens if the cast fails at runtime for a pointer vs. a reference?", "options": ["A) Base must be polymorphic (have at least one virtual function); on failure, pointer cast returns nullptr while reference cast throws std::bad_cast.", "B) Base must have a default constructor; on failure, both pointer and reference casts return nullptr.", "C) Base must inherit from std::object; on failure, both throw std::runtime_error.", "D) No prerequisite is needed; dynamic_cast is resolved entirely at compile time."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "What is the prerequisite for using dynamic_cast<Derived*>(basePtr) for safe downcasting, and what happens if the cast fails at runtime for a pointer vs. a reference?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "dynamic_cast relies on Run-Time Type Information (RTTI), which is stored in the class's vtable. Therefore, the Base class must be polymorphic (contain at least one virtual function). If basePtr actually points to a different derived type, dynamic_cast<Derived*>(basePtr) safely returns nullptr. Since a C++ reference cannot be null, dynamic_cast<Derived&>(baseRef) throws a std::bad_cast exception on failure.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "oop-test-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: How does C++ bind default arguments on virtual functions when called through a Base pointer pointing to a Derived object?", "options": ["A) Both the function body and the default argument value are dynamically bound at runtime from the Derived class.", "B) Both the function body and the default argument value are statically bound at compile time from the Base class.", "C) The function body is dynamically bound at runtime from the Derived class, but the default argument value is statically bound at compile time from the Base pointer type!", "D) C++ prohibits default arguments on virtual functions."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "How does C++ bind default arguments on virtual functions when called through a Base pointer pointing to a Derived object?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For runtime efficiency, C++ compilers determine default parameter values statically at compile time based on the static type of the pointer/reference (Base*), whereas the virtual function call itself is resolved dynamically at runtime via the vtable (Derived::fn). Hence, calling basePtr->fn() executes Derived's function body using Base's default parameter value!", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "oop-test-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: If a class declares its Destructor as private, what is the consequence for creating objects of that class?", "options": ["A) No objects of the class can ever be created on either the Stack or the Heap.", "B) Objects cannot be created as local Stack variables (compile error), and can only be allocated dynamically on the Heap via new and destroyed via a public member/friend function.", "C) Objects can only be created on the Stack, and Heap allocation via new fails.", "D) The compiler ignores the private specifier on destructors."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "If a class declares its Destructor as private, what is the consequence for creating objects of that class?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For any local Stack object (or global/static object), the compiler automatically inserts an implicit destructor call when the variable goes out of scope—which fails to compile if ~MyClass() is private! However, Heap allocation (MyClass* p = new MyClass();) only calls the constructor, so it succeeds! To free the object, the class provides a public method like void destroy() { delete this; }.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "oop-test-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: Why should single-argument constructors in C++ (such as Array(int size)) usually be marked with the explicit keyword?", "options": ["A) To allocate the object in CPU registers.", "B) To prevent the compiler from using that constructor for unintended implicit type conversions (such as Array arr = 10; or passing an int to a function expecting an Array).", "C) To make the constructor virtual.", "D) To prevent the class from being inherited."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Why should single-argument constructors in C++ (such as Array(int size)) usually be marked with the explicit keyword?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In C++, any constructor that can be called with a single argument acts as an implicit conversion operator from the parameter type to the class type (allowing bizarre code like Array a = 50; to silently construct a 50-element Array). Marking the constructor explicit forces developers to write explicit construction syntax Array a(50);.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "oop-test-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: How does a C++11 Move Constructor (Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept) optimize performance compared to a Deep Copy Constructor?", "options": ["A) It compresses the heap array using zlib.", "B) When initialized from a temporary R-value object, it \\"steals\\" the internal heap pointer in O(1) time and sets other.data = nullptr instead of allocating new memory and copying elements in O(N) time.", "C) It skips calling the destructor of the moved-from object.", "D) It moves the object from the Heap to the Stack."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "How does a C++11 Move Constructor (Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept) optimize performance compared to a Deep Copy Constructor?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "When an object is initialized from a temporary R-value (which is about to be destroyed anyway), allocating a brand-new heap buffer and deep-copying N elements is wasteful. The Move Constructor copies the raw pointer address (this->data = other.data) in O(1) time and nulls out the source pointer (other.data = nullptr) so the temporary's destructor safely does nothing (delete[] nullptr is a safe no-op).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "oop-test-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: Normally, an overriding virtual function in a Derived class must have the exact same return type as the Base class function. What is the one exception allowed by C++ (\\"Covariant Return Types\\")?", "options": ["A) Returning a double instead of a float.", "B) Returning a Derived* (or Derived&) when the Base virtual function returns a Base* (or Base&), such as in a virtual clone() method.", "C) Returning void when the Base function returns int.", "D) Returning a std::shared_ptr<Derived> when Base returns std::shared_ptr<Base>."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Normally, an overriding virtual function in a Derived class must have the exact same return type as the Base class function. What is the one exception allowed by C++ (\\"Covariant Return Types\\")?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "C++ supports Covariant Return Types for raw pointers and references: if Base::clone() returns Base*, then Derived::clone() override is permitted to return Derived* because every Derived* is-a Base*. (Note: this built-in language rule applies only to raw pointers/references, not smart pointer templates).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 20}, {"id": "oop-test-q21", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q21: How does the C++ compiler distinguish between overloaded Pre-increment (++obj) and Post-increment (obj++) operators, and which one is more memory-efficient?", "options": ["A) Post-increment takes a dummy int parameter (operator++(int)) and returns the old state by value (creating a temporary copy), making Pre-increment (operator++(), returning *this by reference) more efficient.", "B) Pre-increment takes a dummy int parameter and is slower than Post-increment.", "C) Post-increment must be a friend function while Pre-increment is a member function.", "D) Both have identical signatures and return types."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "How does the C++ compiler distinguish between overloaded Pre-increment (++obj) and Post-increment (obj++) operators, and which one is more memory-efficient?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Pre-increment has signature Counter& operator++()—it increments the object in place and returns *this by reference with zero temporary copies. Post-increment has signature Counter operator++(int) (with a dummy unused int parameter)—it must save a copy of the original state, increment *this, and return the saved copy by value.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 21}, {"id": "oop-test-q22", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q22: Why are C++ inline functions safer than C preprocessor macros (#define SQUARE(x) x*x), and when might a compiler ignore the inline keyword?", "options": ["A) Macros perform blind text substitution without type checking or operator precedence safety (e.g., SQUARE(3+1) expands to 3+1*3+1 = 7), whereas inline functions enforce type safety and evaluate arguments once; compilers ignore inline for recursive or virtual calls.", "B) Macros are evaluated at runtime whereas inline functions run in the preprocessor.", "C) Compilers are strictly required by the C++ standard to inline every function marked inline.", "D) Inline functions cannot access private class members."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "Why are C++ inline functions safer than C preprocessor macros (#define SQUARE(x) x*x), and when might a compiler ignore the inline keyword?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Preprocessor macros perform blind text replacement before compilation—causing precedence bugs (3 + 1 * 3 + 1 = 7 instead of 16) and double-evaluation bugs (SQUARE(++a) increments a twice!). Inline functions are real functions with type checking and scope rules. Note that inline is only a hint/request to the compiler (and provides linkage rules for headers); compilers will not inline recursive functions or dynamically dispatched virtual calls.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 22}, {"id": "oop-test-q23", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q23: What happens to destructors if an exception is thrown halfway through executing a class's constructor body?", "options": ["A) The destructor of the partially constructed class itself is called immediately.", "B) The destructor of the partially constructed class itself is NOT called, but the destructors of all already-constructed base classes and member subobjects ARE automatically called during stack unwinding.", "C) No destructors are called at all, and the program terminates immediately.", "D) Exceptions cannot be thrown inside constructors in C++."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "What happens to destructors if an exception is thrown halfway through executing a class's constructor body?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "In C++, an object's destructor is only invoked if its constructor completed execution! If a constructor throws an exception midway, ~MyClass() is NOT called. However, C++ guarantees that any base class subobjects and member objects (like std::string or std::unique_ptr) that finished constructing before the throw WILL have their destructors called automatically during stack unwinding.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 23}, {"id": "oop-test-q24", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q24: When you overload operator new and operator delete inside a class, why are they implicitly static member functions even if you omit the static keyword?", "options": ["A) Because operator new is called BEFORE the object is constructed (to allocate raw memory for it), and operator delete is called AFTER the destructor has already destroyed the object—so no valid \\"this\\" object exists inside either function!", "B) Because they are inherited from Object.prototype.", "C) Because they can only allocate static variables.", "D) They are not static; they require a valid this pointer."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "When you overload operator new and operator delete inside a class, why are they implicitly static member functions even if you omit the static keyword?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "When new MyClass() runs, Step 1 calls operator new(sizeof(MyClass)) to allocate raw bytes BEFORE the constructor runs, and Step 2 invokes the constructor on that raw memory. When delete p runs, Step 1 invokes ~MyClass() to destroy the object, and Step 2 calls operator delete(void*) to free the raw bytes. Because no constructed object exists when operator new or operator delete runs, both are implicitly static!", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 24}, {"id": "oop-test-q25", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q25: What is the key architectural difference between Aggregation and Composition in Object-Oriented Design?", "options": ["A) Aggregation is an \\"IS-A\\" relationship, while Composition is a \\"HAS-A\\" relationship.", "B) Both model \\"HAS-A\\" relationships, but in Composition (strong ownership, e.g., House and Room) the part's lifecycle is strictly bound to the whole, whereas in Aggregation (weak ownership, e.g., Department and Professor) the part can exist independently if the container is destroyed.", "C) Composition requires multiple inheritance, while Aggregation uses friend classes.", "D) Aggregation only works with primitive types."], "category": "Theory & Concept", "question": "What is the key architectural difference between Aggregation and Composition in Object-Oriented Design?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Both Aggregation and Composition represent \\"HAS-A\\" associations. In Composition (\\"part-of\\", e.g., a Car owning an Engine member object by value/unique_ptr), destroying the Car automatically destroys the Engine. In Aggregation (e.g., a Department holding pointers/references to Professor objects), destroying the Department does not destroy the Professors.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 25}, {"id": "oop-test-q26", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q26: [Memory Alignment Math] On a standard 64-bit system (char = 1B, int = 4B, double = 8B), what is sizeof(SensorPacket) for the class below?", "options": ["A) 14 bytes", "B) 16 bytes", "C) 24 bytes", "D) 32 bytes"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "On a standard 64-bit system (char = 1B, int = 4B, double = 8B), what is sizeof(SensorPacket) for the class below?\\n\\nclass SensorPacket {\\n  char status;      // 1 byte\\n  double reading;   // 8 bytes\\n  int sensorId;     // 4 bytes\\n  char checksum;    // 1 byte\\n};", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Step-by-step offset calculation: 1) status (char) at offset 0 (1 byte). 2) reading (double, 8B) requires an offset divisible by 8 -> 7 padding bytes added at offsets 1..7, reading placed at offsets 8..15 (total = 16B). 3) sensorId (int, 4B) requires offset divisible by 4 -> placed at offsets 16..19 (total = 20B). 4) checksum (char, 1B) placed at offset 20 (total = 21B). 5) Total struct size must be a multiple of the largest alignment (8B) -> rounded up from 21 to 24 bytes (3 trailing padding bytes).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 26}, {"id": "oop-test-q27", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q27: [Memory Optimization Math] If you reorder the members of SensorPacket from Q26 in descending order of size (double, int, char, char), how many Megabytes (MB) of RAM are saved when allocating an array of 1,000,000 (10^6) SensorPacket objects?", "options": ["A) New size = 16 bytes; RAM saved = 8,000,000 bytes (~8 MB)", "B) New size = 14 bytes; RAM saved = 10,000,000 bytes (~10 MB)", "C) New size = 20 bytes; RAM saved = 4,000,000 bytes (~4 MB)", "D) New size = 24 bytes; RAM saved = 0 bytes"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "If you reorder the members of SensorPacket from Q26 in descending order of size (double reading; int sensorId; char status; char checksum;), what is the new sizeof(SensorPacket), and how much RAM is saved across an array of 1,000,000 (10^6) objects?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "With members ordered as double(8B, offsets 0..7) + int(4B, offsets 8..11) + char(1B, offset 12) + char(1B, offset 13) + 2 bytes tail padding (to reach a multiple of 8), the new sizeof(SensorPacket) is 16 bytes instead of 24 bytes! Saving 8 bytes per object across 1,000,000 objects saves exactly 8,000,000 bytes (~8 MB) of RAM.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 27}, {"id": "oop-test-q28", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q28: [Polymorphic sizeof Math] On a 64-bit architecture (pointers = 8B, int = 4B, char = 1B), what is sizeof(Widget) for the class below?", "options": ["A) 8 bytes", "B) 16 bytes", "C) 24 bytes", "D) 32 bytes"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "On a 64-bit architecture (pointers = 8B, int = 4B, char = 1B), what is sizeof(Widget) for the class below?\\n\\nclass Widget {\\n  int id;\\n  char tag;\\n  static double sharedMetric;\\npublic:\\n  virtual void render();\\n  virtual void update();\\n};", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "1) Even though there are 2 virtual functions, a class has only ONE vptr (8 bytes on a 64-bit system, placed at offsets 0..7). 2) static double sharedMetric is stored in the static data segment and adds 0 bytes to the object. 3) int id (4B) is placed at offsets 8..11, and char tag (1B) at offset 12 (total = 13 bytes). 4) Rounding 13 up to the nearest multiple of the largest alignment (8 bytes for vptr) adds 3 padding bytes = 16 bytes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 28}, {"id": "oop-test-q29", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q29: [Multiple Inheritance vptr Math] On a 64-bit system (pointers = 8B, int = 4B), what is sizeof(Derived) for the multiple inheritance hierarchy below?", "options": ["A) 20 bytes", "B) 24 bytes", "C) 32 bytes", "D) 40 bytes"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "On a 64-bit system (pointers = 8B, int = 4B), what is sizeof(Derived) for the multiple inheritance hierarchy below?\\n\\nclass Base1 {\\n  int a;\\n  virtual void f1() {}\\n};\\nclass Base2 {\\n  int b;\\n  virtual void f2() {}\\n};\\nclass Derived : public Base1, public Base2 {\\n  int c;\\n};", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "In standard 64-bit ABI (Itanium C++ ABI / GCC / Clang) or MSVC: Base1 has vptr1 (8B) + int a (4B) = 12B (padded to 16B standalone). Base2 has vptr2 (8B) + int b (4B) = 12B (padded to 16B). When Derived inherits from both Base1 and Base2, it contains Base1's vptr1 (8B) + a (4B) + pad(4B) = 16B, followed by Base2's vptr2 (8B) + b (4B), and int c (4B) packs into Base2's tail padding (or brings total to 32 bytes) -> 32 bytes total!", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 29}, {"id": "oop-test-q30", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q30: [Pointer Offset Upcasting Math] Suppose an object d of class Derived (from Q29, where sizeof(Base1) == 16) is allocated at memory address 0x1000. If we execute Base1* p1 = &d; and Base2* p2 = &d;, what addresses do p1 and p2 hold?", "options": ["A) p1 = 0x1000, p2 = 0x1000", "B) p1 = 0x1000, p2 = 0x1010 (offset by +16 bytes so p2 points directly to the Base2 subobject inside d)", "C) p1 = 0x1010, p2 = 0x1000", "D) Upcasting to the second base class is a compile-time error."], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "Suppose an object d of class Derived (inheriting from Base1 of size 16 bytes, followed by Base2 of size 16 bytes) is allocated at memory address 0x1000. If we execute Base1* p1 = &d; and Base2* p2 = &d;, what numeric addresses do p1 and p2 hold?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Inside a Derived object starting at 0x1000, the Base1 subobject occupies bytes 0x1000..0x100F (16 bytes), and the Base2 subobject starts immediately after at 0x1010 (0x1000 + 16 = 0x1010 in hex). Therefore, when upcasting Base2* p2 = &d;, the compiler automatically adjusts the pointer address by +16 bytes (0x1010) so p2 points directly to the start of the Base2 subobject!", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 30}, {"id": "oop-test-q31", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q31: [Constructor/Destructor Call Count] How many times are Base's constructor and Derived's constructor called in the code snippet below?", "options": ["A) Base constructor: 4 times | Derived constructor: 2 times", "B) Base constructor: 7 times | Derived constructor: 2 times", "C) Base constructor: 2 times | Derived constructor: 2 times", "D) Base constructor: 5 times | Derived constructor: 5 times"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "How many times are Base's constructor and Derived's constructor called in total during the execution of main()?\\n\\nclass Base { public: Base() {} virtual ~Base() {} };\\nclass Derived : public Base { public: Derived() {} ~Derived() {} };\\n\\nint main() {\\n  Base bArr[2];\\n  Base* ptrArr[3];\\n  Derived* dPtr = new Derived[2];\\n  delete[] dPtr;\\n  return 0;\\n}", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Let's count carefully: 1) Base bArr[2]; constructs 2 Base objects -> Base ctor called 2 times. 2) Base* ptrArr[3]; declares an array of 3 uninitialized pointers (NOT objects!) -> 0 constructors called! 3) new Derived[2] constructs 2 Derived objects; each Derived object first calls Base's constructor (+2) and then Derived's constructor (+2). Total: Base constructor = 2 + 2 = 4 times; Derived constructor = 2 times.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 31}, {"id": "oop-test-q32", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q32: [Copy Constructor Count Math] Assuming compiler copy elision (RVO) is disabled (-fno-elide-constructors) and no move constructor is defined, how many times is the Copy Constructor of Widget invoked in the code below?", "options": ["A) 2 times", "B) 3 times", "C) 4 times", "D) 5 times"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "Assuming compiler copy elision is disabled (-fno-elide-constructors) and no move constructor is defined, how many times is the Copy Constructor of Widget invoked when main() runs?\\n\\nWidget process(Widget u) {\\n  Widget v(u);\\n  Widget w = v;\\n  return w;\\n}\\nint main() {\\n  Widget x;\\n  Widget y = process(x);\\n  return 0;\\n}", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Without copy elision: 1) Passing x by value into parameter u calls Copy Ctor (#1). 2) Widget v(u); calls Copy Ctor (#2). 3) Widget w = v; is declaration-initialization (NOT assignment!), which calls Copy Ctor (#3). 4) return w; copies w into the temporary return slot (#4). 5) Initializing Widget y from the returned temporary calls Copy Ctor (#5). Total = 5 Copy Constructor invocations!", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 32}, {"id": "oop-test-q33", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q33: [Static Counter Trace] What is the exact numerical output printed by the program below?", "options": ["A) 3 1", "B) 3 2", "C) 2 1", "D) 3 3"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "What is the exact numerical output printed by the program below?\\n\\nclass Tracker {\\npublic:\\n  static int live;\\n  Tracker() { live++; }\\n  Tracker(const Tracker&) { live++; }\\n  ~Tracker() { live--; }\\n};\\nint Tracker::live = 0;\\n\\nint main() {\\n  Tracker t1;\\n  {\\n    Tracker t2 = t1;\\n    Tracker* t3 = new Tracker();\\n    cout << Tracker::live << \\" \\";\\n  }\\n  cout << Tracker::live;\\n  return 0;\\n}", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "1) Tracker t1; increments live to 1. 2) Inside the inner block, Tracker t2 = t1; calls the Copy Constructor, incrementing live to 2. 3) Tracker* t3 = new Tracker(); allocates an object on the Heap, incrementing live to 3. The first cout prints 3. 4) When the inner block exits, Stack object t2 is destroyed (~Tracker() decrements live to 2), but Heap object *t3 was never deleted (`delete t3` was not called), so *t3 remains alive on the Heap! The second cout prints 2. Output: \\"3 2\\".", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 33}, {"id": "oop-test-q34", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q34: [Virtual Dispatch + Default Parameter Math] What is the exact numerical output of the C++ program below?", "options": ["A) 40 40", "B) 10 40", "C) 20 40", "D) 20 20"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "What is the exact numerical output of the C++ program below?\\n\\nclass Base {\\npublic:\\n  virtual int compute(int factor = 5) {\\n    return factor * 2;\\n  }\\n};\\nclass Derived : public Base {\\npublic:\\n  int compute(int factor = 10) override {\\n    return factor * 4;\\n  }\\n};\\nint main() {\\n  Derived d;\\n  Base* ptr = &d;\\n  cout << ptr->compute() << \\" \\" << d.compute();\\n  return 0;\\n}", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) For ptr->compute(): Since ptr has static type Base*, the compiler binds the default argument from Base (factor = 5) at compile time, but dispatches the virtual call at runtime to Derived::compute(5), returning 5 * 4 = 20! 2) For d.compute(): Since d has static type Derived, it uses Derived's default argument (factor = 10) and calls Derived::compute(10), returning 10 * 4 = 40. Output: \\"20 40\\".", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 34}, {"id": "oop-test-q35", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q35: [Virtual Diamond Inheritance Value Trace] What is the exact integer printed by the program below?", "options": ["A) 18", "B) 28", "C) 38", "D) 58"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "What is the exact integer printed by the program below?\\n\\nclass A {\\npublic:\\n  int val;\\n  A(int v = 10) : val(v) {}\\n};\\nclass B : virtual public A {\\npublic:\\n  B() : A(20) { val += 2; }\\n};\\nclass C : virtual public A {\\npublic:\\n  C() : A(30) { val += 5; }\\n};\\nclass D : public B, public C {\\npublic:\\n  D() { val += 1; }\\n};\\nint main() {\\n  D obj;\\n  cout << obj.val;\\n  return 0;\\n}", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "In virtual inheritance, the most-derived class (D) is responsible for calling the virtual base A's constructor, and the calls A(20) in B and A(30) in C are ignored! Since D() does not explicitly specify an A constructor in its initializer list, A's default constructor A(10) runs first, setting val = 10. Next, B's constructor body runs: val += 2 (12). Next, C's constructor body runs: val += 5 (17). Finally, D's constructor body runs: val += 1 (18). Output: 18.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 35}, {"id": "oop-test-q36", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q36: [Member Initializer Order Trap] What is the exact output of the C++ code below?", "options": ["A) 10 20", "B) 20 10", "C) b is 10, but a is initialized with a garbage/indeterminate value because a is declared before b in the class body and therefore initialized BEFORE b!", "D) Compile-time error: initializer list order must match declaration order."], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "What is the exact output of the C++ code below?\\n\\nclass Test {\\n  int a;\\n  int b;\\npublic:\\n  Test(int val) : b(val), a(b * 2) {}\\n  void print() { cout << b << \\" \\" << a; }\\n};\\nint main() {\\n  Test t(10);\\n  t.print();\\n}", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "In C++, class data members are ALWAYS initialized in the exact order they are declared inside the class definition (int a; then int b;), NOT the order written in the constructor's Member Initializer List! Therefore, a(b * 2) executes FIRST while b still holds uninitialized garbage memory, and b(10) executes second.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 36}, {"id": "oop-test-q37", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q37: [#pragma pack(1) vs. Natural Alignment Math] On a 64-bit system, what is the byte difference between the naturally aligned size and the #pragma pack(1) size of class Header { char v; int len; short flags; double ts; }?", "options": ["A) Natural = 24 bytes, Packed = 15 bytes -> Difference = 9 bytes", "B) Natural = 16 bytes, Packed = 15 bytes -> Difference = 1 byte", "C) Natural = 32 bytes, Packed = 16 bytes -> Difference = 16 bytes", "D) Natural = 20 bytes, Packed = 15 bytes -> Difference = 5 bytes"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "On a 64-bit system (char = 1B, short = 2B, int = 4B, double = 8B), what is the byte difference between the naturally aligned sizeof(Header) and the #pragma pack(1) tightly packed sizeof(Header) for: class Header { char v; int len; short flags; double ts; };?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "1) With #pragma pack(1), zero padding is inserted: 1 (char) + 4 (int) + 2 (short) + 8 (double) = 15 bytes. 2) With natural alignment: v(1B at 0) + pad(3B) + len(4B at 4..7) + flags(2B at 8..9) + pad(6B at 10..15 so double aligns on 16) + ts(8B at 16..23) = 24 bytes. Difference = 24 - 15 = 9 bytes.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 37}, {"id": "oop-test-q38", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q38: [Construction & Destruction Sequence Trace] What exact string of digits is printed when main() executes and exits?", "options": ["A) 315624", "B) 135642", "C) 351624", "D) 531426"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "What exact string of digits is printed when main() executes and exits?\\n\\nstruct M { M() { cout << \\"1\\"; } ~M() { cout << \\"2\\"; } };\\nstruct Base { Base() { cout << \\"3\\"; } ~Base() { cout << \\"4\\"; } };\\nstruct Derived : public Base {\\n  M member;\\n  Derived() { cout << \\"5\\"; }\\n  ~Derived() { cout << \\"6\\"; }\\n};\\nint main() { Derived d; return 0; }", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "When Derived d is constructed: 1) Base class constructor runs first -> prints \\"3\\". 2) Member objects (M member) are constructed next -> prints \\"1\\". 3) Derived constructor body runs last -> prints \\"5\\" (so far: \\"315\\"). When d goes out of scope, destructors run in exact reverse order: 1) ~Derived() body runs first -> prints \\"6\\". 2) Member destructor ~M() runs next -> prints \\"2\\". 3) ~Base() runs last -> prints \\"4\\". Total output: \\"315624\\".", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 38}, {"id": "oop-test-q39", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q39: [Bit-Fields & Zero-Width Alignment Math] Assuming unsigned int is 4 bytes (32 bits), what is sizeof(RegisterFlags) for the struct below?", "options": ["A) 2 bytes", "B) 4 bytes", "C) 8 bytes", "D) 12 bytes"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "Assuming unsigned int is 4 bytes (32 bits), what is sizeof(RegisterFlags) for the struct below?\\n\\nstruct RegisterFlags {\\n  unsigned int mode : 4;\\n  unsigned int enable : 1;\\n  unsigned int : 0; // Zero-width unnamed bit-field\\n  unsigned int status : 6;\\n};", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Normally, mode (4 bits), enable (1 bit), and status (6 bits) = 11 bits would easily fit inside a single 4-byte unsigned int allocation unit. However, an unnamed zero-width bit-field (unsigned int : 0;) instructs the compiler to force padding up to the next storage unit boundary! Therefore, mode and enable occupy the first 4-byte unsigned int, and status is forced into a second 4-byte unsigned int: 4 + 4 = 8 bytes.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 39}, {"id": "oop-test-q40", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q40: [Object Slicing vs. Reference Dispatch Math] What is the numerical output of the program below?", "options": ["A) 8", "B) 11", "C) 14", "D) Compile-time error"], "category": "Numerical & Memory Calculation", "question": "What is the numerical output of the program below?\\n\\nclass Account {\\npublic:\\n  virtual int getInterest() { return 4; }\\n};\\nclass SavingsAccount : public Account {\\npublic:\\n  int getInterest() override { return 7; }\\n};\\nint byVal(Account a) { return a.getInterest(); }\\nint byRef(Account& a) { return a.getInterest(); }\\n\\nint main() {\\n  SavingsAccount sa;\\n  cout << byVal(sa) + byRef(sa);\\n  return 0;\\n}", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "1) In byVal(sa), sa is passed by VALUE into Account a, causing Object Slicing! Inside byVal, a is a pure Account instance whose vptr points to Account's vtable, so a.getInterest() returns 4. 2) In byRef(sa), sa is passed by reference (Account& a), preserving polymorphic dynamic dispatch via sa's vptr, so a.getInterest() calls SavingsAccount::getInterest() and returns 7. Sum = 4 + 7 = 11.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 40}, {"id": "oop-test-q41", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q41: [Production Memory Leak Diagnosis] In a backend service, ImageProcessor* proc = new JpegProcessor(); processes a 50 MB image buffer allocated in JpegProcessor's constructor and then executes delete proc;. Over 1,000 requests, server RAM usage balloons by 50 GB and crashes. What is the root cause and fix?", "options": ["A) delete cannot free objects larger than 1 MB; free() must be used instead.", "B) The base class destructor ~ImageProcessor() was not declared virtual, causing delete proc; to statically invoke only ~ImageProcessor() and skip ~JpegProcessor() entirely!", "C) JpegProcessor inherited privately instead of publicly.", "D) The constructor of ImageProcessor was not declared virtual."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "In a backend service, ImageProcessor* proc = new JpegProcessor(); allocates a 50 MB heap buffer inside JpegProcessor() and frees it inside ~JpegProcessor(). After each request, delete proc; is called, yet server RAM leaks 50 MB per request. What is the root cause and fix?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Deleting a derived object through a base class pointer (delete proc;) when the base class has a non-virtual destructor causes undefined behavior where only ~ImageProcessor() runs and ~JpegProcessor() is completely skipped—leaking the 50 MB buffer on every request! Adding virtual ~ImageProcessor() {} to the base class fixes the leak immediately.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 41}, {"id": "oop-test-q42", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q42: [Double-Free Crash Diagnosis] A developer creates a StringWrapper class with a char* buf member allocated via new char[len] and freed in ~StringWrapper(). Passing a StringWrapper object by value to a logging function causes the program to crash with \\"free(): double free detected in tcache\\" when main() exits. Why?", "options": ["A) Because the developer did not define a custom Deep Copy Constructor, so passing s by value used the compiler's default Shallow Copy Constructor—causing both s and the original object to point to the same char* address and call delete[] on it twice!", "B) Because char* arrays must be freed with delete instead of delete[].", "C) Because destructors cannot be called inside main().", "D) Because logging functions must be declared friend."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "A developer creates a StringWrapper class with a char* buf member allocated via new char[len] and freed in ~StringWrapper(). Passing a StringWrapper object by value to a logging function void log(StringWrapper s) causes the program to crash with \\"double free detected\\" when main() exits. Why?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "When StringWrapper s is passed by value to log(StringWrapper s), the compiler-synthesized Shallow Copy Constructor copies the pointer value s.buf = orig.buf. When log() finishes, ~StringWrapper() runs on s and frees buf! Later, when main() exits, ~StringWrapper() runs on orig and calls delete[] on the already-freed address. Implementing a Deep Copy Constructor (or passing const StringWrapper& by reference) prevents this crash.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 42}, {"id": "oop-test-q43", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q43: [Self-Assignment Bug in operator=] In an overloaded copy assignment operator (operator=), a developer writes: delete[] this->data; this->data = new int[rhs.size]; memcpy(this->data, rhs.data, ...); without checking if (this == &rhs). What happens if client code executes arr[i] = arr[j] when i == j?", "options": ["A) It works normally with a minor performance penalty.", "B) Since this and &rhs refer to the exact same object, delete[] this->data frees rhs.data BEFORE memcpy copies from it—resulting in use-after-free memory corruption!", "C) The compiler automatically skips operator= when both operands are the same object.", "D) It throws a compile-time error."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "In an overloaded copy assignment operator, a developer writes: delete[] this->data; this->data = new int[rhs.size]; memcpy(this->data, rhs.data, ...); without checking if (this == &rhs). What happens if client code executes arr[i] = arr[j] when i == j?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "When a = a (or arr[i] = arr[j] where i == j) occurs, this and &rhs point to the exact same object in memory. Executing delete[] this->data immediately destroys rhs.data as well! Subsequently trying to read rhs.data copies freed garbage memory (use-after-free). Always guard with if (this == &rhs) return *this; or use the Copy-and-Swap idiom.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 43}, {"id": "oop-test-q44", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q44: [SOLID — Open/Closed Principle & Strategy Pattern] A CheckoutService class has a 200-line if/else-if chain checking if (type == \\"UPI\\") ... else if (type == \\"CreditCard\\") ... else if (type == \\"Crypto\\"). Every time a new payment method is added, CheckoutService must be modified and retested. Which SOLID principle is violated and how do you refactor it?", "options": ["A) Violates Liskov Substitution Principle; fix using Singleton Pattern.", "B) Violates Open/Closed Principle (OCP); fix using the Strategy Pattern by defining an abstract PaymentMethod interface with a pure virtual pay() method and passing concrete implementations (UpiPayment, CardPayment) polymorphically.", "C) Violates Interface Segregation Principle; fix using Friend functions.", "D) Violates Encapsulation; fix by making all variables global."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "A CheckoutService class has a giant if/else-if chain checking if (type == \\"UPI\\") ... else if (type == \\"Card\\") .... Every time a new payment method is added, CheckoutService must be modified. Which SOLID principle is violated and which OOP pattern fixes it?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "The Open/Closed Principle states that a class should be open for extension but closed for modification. By introducing an abstract PaymentStrategy interface (virtual bool pay(double amt) = 0;), adding a new payment method (e.g., ApplePayStrategy : public PaymentStrategy) requires zero modifications to CheckoutService!", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 44}, {"id": "oop-test-q45", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q45: [SOLID — Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)] A developer models class Square : public Rectangle where setWidth(w) in Square overrides Rectangle::setWidth(w) to set BOTH width and height to w. A test function void verifyArea(Rectangle& r) { r.setWidth(5); r.setHeight(4); assert(r.area() == 20); } fails when passed a Square(16 != 20). Which principle is violated?", "options": ["A) Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)", "B) Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) — a derived class (Square) broke the behavioral postcondition/invariant expected of its base class (Rectangle) that width and height can be modified independently.", "C) Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)", "D) Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)"], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "A developer models class Square : public Rectangle where Square::setWidth(w) sets both width and height to w. A function void test(Rectangle& r) { r.setWidth(5); r.setHeight(4); assert(r.area() == 20); } fails when passed a Square (area is 16). Which SOLID principle does this violate?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Even though mathematically a square \\"is a\\" rectangle, in mutable OOP behavior a Square does NOT satisfy the behavioral contract of a mutable Rectangle (where mutating width leaves height unchanged). When substituting a Square& for a Rectangle& breaks client assertions, it is the textbook violation of the Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 45}, {"id": "oop-test-q46", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q46: [Design Patterns — Thread-Safe Singleton] You are designing a Logger Singleton class in C++11+. What is the complete set of OOP access controls and mechanisms required to guarantee that only ONE instance can ever exist and that initialization is thread-safe?", "options": ["A) Make the constructor public, and use a global variable.", "B) Make the default constructor private, delete the Copy Constructor and Copy Assignment Operator (= delete), and expose a static Logger& getInstance() method containing a local static Logger instance;", "C) Declare the class abstract with a pure virtual constructor.", "D) Use friend classes to instantiate Logger in every file."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "You are designing a Logger Singleton class in C++11+. What is the complete set of OOP controls required to guarantee that only ONE instance can ever exist and that lazy initialization is thread-safe?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "To enforce a strict Singleton: 1) Make Logger() private so external code cannot instantiate it. 2) Delete Logger(const Logger&) = delete; and operator=(const Logger&) = delete; so the singleton instance cannot be copied! 3) Inside static Logger& getInstance(), declare static Logger instance; return instance; (Meyer's Singleton)—since C++11, the standard guarantees that block-scope static variable initialization is 100% thread-safe!", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 46}, {"id": "oop-test-q47", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q47: [RAII & Exception Safety] In a multi-threaded banking transfer function, mtx.lock() is called at the start and mtx.unlock() at the end. However, if transfer() throws an InsufficientFundsException midway, mtx.unlock() is skipped and all other threads deadlock permanently! How does OOP's RAII idiom solve this?", "options": ["A) By wrapping the mutex inside a local stack object (like std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);) whose constructor acquires the lock and whose destructor automatically releases the lock during stack unwinding even if an exception is thrown.", "B) By allocating the mutex on the Heap with new.", "C) By declaring the mutex mutable.", "D) By disabling exceptions across the project."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "In a multi-threaded banking function, mtx.lock() is called at the start and mtx.unlock() at the end. If an exception is thrown midway, mtx.unlock() is skipped and all threads deadlock! How does C++'s RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) idiom solve this?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) binds the lifecycle of a resource (mutex lock, file handle, socket, heap memory) to the lifetime of a stack-allocated object. Because C++ guarantees that destructors of all local stack objects are invoked during stack unwinding when an exception is thrown or a function returns early, ~lock_guard() is 100% guaranteed to unlock the mutex.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 47}, {"id": "oop-test-q48", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q48: [Safeguarding Virtual Overrides in Large Teams] In a large codebase, a Base class method virtual void onPacketReceived(int id) const is refactored to take (long id). A developer's Derived class still has void onPacketReceived(int id) const, which silently stops overriding the base method and introduces a production bug. Which C++11 keyword prevents this at compile time?", "options": ["A) virtual", "B) override", "C) final", "D) explicit"], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "In a large codebase, Base::onPacketReceived(int id) const is updated to take (long id). A Derived class still has void onPacketReceived(int id) const, which silently stops overriding the Base method. Which C++11 keyword catches this mismatch immediately at compile time?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Appending the override specifier to a derived method declaration (void onPacketReceived(int id) const override;) instructs the compiler to verify that a virtual function with the EXACT same signature, const-qualification, and parameter types exists in the Base class. If the Base signature changes, compilation fails immediately instead of silently hiding/overloading the function.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 48}, {"id": "oop-test-q49", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q49: [Factory Design Pattern] An application needs to instantiate different document parsers (PdfParser, DocxParser, CsvParser) based on a file extension string at runtime without scattering new PdfParser() calls across 50 business-logic files. Which OOP creational design pattern solves this?", "options": ["A) Observer Pattern", "B) Factory Method / Simple Factory Pattern (returning a polymorphic std::unique_ptr<IDocumentParser> base pointer from a centralized ParserFactory::createParser(ext) method).", "C) Decorator Pattern", "D) Adapter Pattern"], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "An application needs to instantiate different document parsers (PdfParser, DocxParser, CsvParser) based on a file extension at runtime without scattering concrete new calls across 50 files. Which OOP design pattern solves this?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "The Factory Pattern centralizes object creation logic behind a factory method that returns an abstract interface pointer (IDocumentParser*). Client code depends only on the IDocumentParser abstraction and never couples directly to concrete PdfParser or CsvParser classes.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 49}, {"id": "oop-test-q50", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q50: [SOLID — Dependency Inversion Principle & Unit Testing] OrderService directly instantiates SmtpEmailClient inside its constructor (this->mailer = new SmtpEmailClient();). When running automated unit tests, every test sends real emails to customers! How do you refactor OrderService using the Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)?", "options": ["A) Add a boolean flag isTesting inside SmtpEmailClient.", "B) Define an abstract IEmailClient interface (with pure virtual sendEmail() = 0), make OrderService accept an IEmailClient* (or reference) via Constructor Dependency Injection, and pass SmtpEmailClient in production and MockEmailClient in unit tests.", "C) Make SmtpEmailClient a friend of OrderService.", "D) Use private inheritance between OrderService and SmtpEmailClient."], "category": "Real-World Engineering Scenario", "question": "OrderService directly instantiates SmtpEmailClient inside its constructor. When running unit tests, every test sends real emails! How do you refactor OrderService using the Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) states that high-level modules (OrderService) should not depend on concrete low-level modules (SmtpEmailClient); both should depend on abstractions (IEmailClient). Injecting IEmailClient& via the constructor allows seamless swapping between SmtpEmailClient in production and an in-memory MockEmailClient during unit testing.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 50}]	https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/classes	\N	\N	\N
22252	LLD Project: Tic Tac Toe Game	lld-project-tic-tac-toe	Architect a scalable N x N board game. Use a Deque for turn orchestration, evaluate complex winning conditions via Strategy, and decouple the UI from core game state.	2026-10-07 22:38:19.438928+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: LLD Project: Tic Tac Toe Game\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/BGFzYjGtRP4", "title": "Build Tic Tac Toe Game | UML + Code | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v33-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Single Responsibility of the Board", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nPlacing algorithmic evaluation inside the `Board` class violates the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP).\\n\\nThe `Board` is a state container. Its sole responsibility is to manage spatial memory: verifying coordinates are within bounds, checking if a cell is empty, and placing a symbol at `(X, Y)`.\\n`checkWin()` is business logic (Game Rules). If the developers decide to invent a new rule (e.g., \\"Connect 4\\" logic on a 5x5 board), the `Board` should not have to be modified. By moving `checkWin()` to an independent `GameRules` Strategy class, the `Board` remains a reusable, dumb data structure.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is it an architectural flaw to place the checkWin() diagonal-scanning algorithm directly inside the Board class?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v33-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Designing N-Player Turn Orchestration", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWhile a boolean toggle (`isPlayerOne = !isPlayerOne`) works for exactly 2 players, it fundamentally breaks if the game scales to 3 or 4 players on a larger board.\\n\\nAn integer tracker (`index = (index + 1) % numPlayers`) works, but fails if game mechanics change (e.g., a player plays a \\"Skip Turn\\" or \\"Reverse Order\\" card).\\n\\nA Deque is the ultimate scalable solution:\\n1. Extract the current player: `player = deque.pop_front()`.\\n2. Execute move.\\n3. Requeue the player: `deque.push_back(player)`.\\nIf a player is eliminated, they are simply not pushed back. If turn order reverses, the queue logic is simply reversed. It natively supports $N$ players and complex game state manipulation.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain why using a Double-Ended Queue (Deque) for player turn orchestration is vastly superior to a boolean toggle or integer index tracker.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v33-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: $O(1)$ Optimization for Winning Conditions", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nInstead of full-board scans, we track running totals for each player based on their moves.\\nFor an NxN board, we maintain arrays inside the `GameRules` tracker for each player:\\n* `vector<int> rows[N]`\\n* `vector<int> cols[N]`\\n* `int diag1`, `int diag2`\\n\\nWhen Player X places a symbol at `(row, col)`:\\n1. Increment `rows[row]++` and `cols[col]++`.\\n2. If `row == col`, increment `diag1++`.\\n3. If `row + col == N - 1`, increment `diag2++`.\\n4. Check Win condition: If `rows[row] == N` or `cols[col] == N` or `diag1 == N` or `diag2 == N`, the player has won.\\nThis resolves win checking instantly in $O(1)$ time per move.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Standard Tic Tac Toe evaluates a win by scanning the entire NxN board in $O(N^2)$ time. How can you optimize the architecture to evaluate winning conditions in $O(1)$ constant time?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v33-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Orchestration via the Game Loop", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe central loop orchestrates the following exact sequence:\\n1. Pop the `current_player` from the Deque.\\n2. Prompt/await the player's move input `(X, Y)`.\\n3. Invoke `gameRules.isValidMove(board, X, Y)`. If false, prompt again.\\n4. Invoke `board.placeSymbol(X, Y, current_player.symbol)`.\\n5. Invoke `gameRules.checkWin(board, current_player.symbol)`. If true, set `isGameOver = true`, broadcast victory, and break loop.\\n6. Invoke `gameRules.checkDraw(board)`. If true, set `isGameOver = true`, broadcast draw, and break loop.\\n7. Push `current_player` to the back of the Deque.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Describe the sequence of actions occurring inside the core Game Orchestrator's while(!isGameOver) loop.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v33-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Real-time In-App Notifications (Observer Pattern)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe core `Game` orchestrator implements the Observable (Subject) interface.\\nThe remote clients (or WebSocket handler services) register themselves as Observers: `game.addObserver(clientA); game.addObserver(clientB);`\\n\\nInside the game loop, immediately after `board.placeSymbol()` successfully executes, the Game calls `notifyObservers(UpdatePayload)`. The payload contains the new board state or the delta move. \\nThe Observers receive the `update()` trigger, serialize the payload, and push it over the WebSocket to the frontend UI, ensuring decoupling between the game engine and the network communication layer.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "If two users are playing across the network, how is the Observer pattern utilized to update User B's screen immediately when User A makes a move?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Scalable Board Entity\n* Standard Tic Tac Toe is 3x3, but the LLD must seamlessly support NxN grids.\n* The `Board` is a model class containing an `N x N` 2D array of `Symbol`s. \n* The Board is a "Dumb Object"—it validates if a cell is out of bounds or already occupied, but it possesses absolutely zero knowledge of the game's rules or winning mechanics.\n\n### 💡 2. Turn Management via Deque\n* Game loops require alternating player turns. Using boolean flags (`isPlayerOneTurn`) breaks if the game is scaled to 3+ players.\n* **The Solution:** A Double-Ended Queue (`std::deque<Player>`).\n* The `Game` loop pops the `current_player` from the front of the deque. After the player successfully executes a valid move, they are pushed to the back of the deque. The next player naturally shifts to the front.\n\n### 💡 3. Rule Evaluation via Strategy\n* Winning algorithms ($O(N)$ scans of rows, columns, and diagonals) are extracted into an `IGameRules` strategy interface.\n* The `Game` orchestrator applies the player's move to the Board, then invokes `rules->checkWin(board, symbol)`. This separates structural data management from algorithmic evaluation.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	LLD Project: Tic Tac Toe Game	Architect a scalable N x N board game. Use a Deque for turn orchestration, evaluate complex winning conditions via Strategy, and decouple the UI from core game state.	52	Hard	Low Level Design	47	[{"id": "lld-v33-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Single Responsibility of the Board", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nPlacing algorithmic evaluation inside the `Board` class violates the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP).\\n\\nThe `Board` is a state container. Its sole responsibility is to manage spatial memory: verifying coordinates are within bounds, checking if a cell is empty, and placing a symbol at `(X, Y)`.\\n`checkWin()` is business logic (Game Rules). If the developers decide to invent a new rule (e.g., \\"Connect 4\\" logic on a 5x5 board), the `Board` should not have to be modified. By moving `checkWin()` to an independent `GameRules` Strategy class, the `Board` remains a reusable, dumb data structure.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is it an architectural flaw to place the checkWin() diagonal-scanning algorithm directly inside the Board class?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v33-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Designing N-Player Turn Orchestration", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWhile a boolean toggle (`isPlayerOne = !isPlayerOne`) works for exactly 2 players, it fundamentally breaks if the game scales to 3 or 4 players on a larger board.\\n\\nAn integer tracker (`index = (index + 1) % numPlayers`) works, but fails if game mechanics change (e.g., a player plays a \\"Skip Turn\\" or \\"Reverse Order\\" card).\\n\\nA Deque is the ultimate scalable solution:\\n1. Extract the current player: `player = deque.pop_front()`.\\n2. Execute move.\\n3. Requeue the player: `deque.push_back(player)`.\\nIf a player is eliminated, they are simply not pushed back. If turn order reverses, the queue logic is simply reversed. It natively supports $N$ players and complex game state manipulation.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain why using a Double-Ended Queue (Deque) for player turn orchestration is vastly superior to a boolean toggle or integer index tracker.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v33-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: $O(1)$ Optimization for Winning Conditions", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nInstead of full-board scans, we track running totals for each player based on their moves.\\nFor an NxN board, we maintain arrays inside the `GameRules` tracker for each player:\\n* `vector<int> rows[N]`\\n* `vector<int> cols[N]`\\n* `int diag1`, `int diag2`\\n\\nWhen Player X places a symbol at `(row, col)`:\\n1. Increment `rows[row]++` and `cols[col]++`.\\n2. If `row == col`, increment `diag1++`.\\n3. If `row + col == N - 1`, increment `diag2++`.\\n4. Check Win condition: If `rows[row] == N` or `cols[col] == N` or `diag1 == N` or `diag2 == N`, the player has won.\\nThis resolves win checking instantly in $O(1)$ time per move.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Standard Tic Tac Toe evaluates a win by scanning the entire NxN board in $O(N^2)$ time. How can you optimize the architecture to evaluate winning conditions in $O(1)$ constant time?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v33-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Orchestration via the Game Loop", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe central loop orchestrates the following exact sequence:\\n1. Pop the `current_player` from the Deque.\\n2. Prompt/await the player's move input `(X, Y)`.\\n3. Invoke `gameRules.isValidMove(board, X, Y)`. If false, prompt again.\\n4. Invoke `board.placeSymbol(X, Y, current_player.symbol)`.\\n5. Invoke `gameRules.checkWin(board, current_player.symbol)`. If true, set `isGameOver = true`, broadcast victory, and break loop.\\n6. Invoke `gameRules.checkDraw(board)`. If true, set `isGameOver = true`, broadcast draw, and break loop.\\n7. Push `current_player` to the back of the Deque.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Describe the sequence of actions occurring inside the core Game Orchestrator's while(!isGameOver) loop.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v33-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Real-time In-App Notifications (Observer Pattern)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe core `Game` orchestrator implements the Observable (Subject) interface.\\nThe remote clients (or WebSocket handler services) register themselves as Observers: `game.addObserver(clientA); game.addObserver(clientB);`\\n\\nInside the game loop, immediately after `board.placeSymbol()` successfully executes, the Game calls `notifyObservers(UpdatePayload)`. The payload contains the new board state or the delta move. \\nThe Observers receive the `update()` trigger, serialize the payload, and push it over the WebSocket to the frontend UI, ensuring decoupling between the game engine and the network communication layer.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "If two users are playing across the network, how is the Observer pattern utilized to update User B's screen immediately when User A makes a move?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
5243	Dynamic Graph Connectivity: Disjoint Set (Union by Rank, Union by Size & Path Compression)	dsa-disjoint-set-union-by-rank-size-path-compression	Perform near-constant time component lookups and dynamic component unions using path compression and union by rank/size.	2026-10-03 11:10:35.764919+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Dynamic Graph Connectivity: Disjoint Set (Union by Rank, Union by Size & Path Compression)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aBxjDBC4M1U", "title": "Lecture 171: Disjoint Set (Union by Rank & Size, Path Compression)", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/disjoint-set-union-find/1", "title": "Disjoint set (Union-Find)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Implement find and unionSet operations for a Disjoint Set data structure."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/redundant-connection/", "title": "Redundant Connection (LC 684)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use Disjoint Set Union to identify the first edge that connects two vertices already in the same component."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/satisfiability-of-equality-equations/", "title": "Satisfiability of Equality Equations (LC 990)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Union all '==' variable pairs first, then verify that no '!=' pair shares the same ultimate parent."}]	Module 26: Disjoint Set Union (DSU), Kruskal's MST & Dynamic Connectivity	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a graph whose edges are added dynamically, checking whether two nodes `u` and `v` belong to the same connected component using BFS/DFS takes `O(V + E)` time per query. The **Disjoint Set Union (DSU)** data structure answers connectivity queries and merges components in **nearly constant `O(4 * alpha) ≈ O(1)` time**!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Two Core Operations of DSU:**\n  1. **`findUPar(node)` (Find Ultimate Parent with Path Compression):**\n     - Recursively climbs the `parent[]` tree until `node == parent[node]`, and rewires every intermediate node along the path to point **directly** to the ultimate root (`return parent[node] = findUPar(parent[node])`).\n  2. **`unionByRank(u, v)` / `unionBySize(u, v)`:**\n     - Find ultimate parents `ulp_u = findUPar(u)` and `ulp_v = findUPar(v)`. If `ulp_u == ulp_v`, they are already in the same set (`return`).\n     - **Why attach the smaller tree under the larger tree?** Attaching the smaller tree under the larger one keeps the overall tree depth minimal, ensuring shorter paths for future lookups!\n     - In **Union by Size**, maintain `size[i] = 1` initially; when attaching `ulp_u` under `ulp_v`, update `parent[ulp_u] = ulp_v` and `size[ulp_v] += size[ulp_u]`.\n- **Complexity:** `O(4 * alpha) ≈ O(1)` amortized Time per operation (where `alpha` is the Inverse Ackermann function) and `O(N)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Disjoint Set Class Blueprint (Union by Rank & Size)\n~~~cpp\nclass DisjointSet {\n    vector<int> rank, parent, size;\npublic:\n    DisjointSet(int n) {\n        rank.resize(n + 1, 0);\n        parent.resize(n + 1);\n        size.resize(n + 1, 1);\n        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) parent[i] = i;\n    }\n\n    int findUPar(int node) {\n        if (node == parent[node]) return node;\n        return parent[node] = findUPar(parent[node]); // Path compression\n    }\n\n    void unionByRank(int u, int v) {\n        int ulp_u = findUPar(u);\n        int ulp_v = findUPar(v);\n        if (ulp_u == ulp_v) return;\n        if (rank[ulp_u] < rank[ulp_v]) {\n            parent[ulp_u] = ulp_v;\n        } else if (rank[ulp_v] < rank[ulp_u]) {\n            parent[ulp_v] = ulp_u;\n        } else {\n            parent[ulp_v] = ulp_u;\n            rank[ulp_u]++;\n        }\n    }\n\n    void unionBySize(int u, int v) {\n        int ulp_u = findUPar(u);\n        int ulp_v = findUPar(v);\n        if (ulp_u == ulp_v) return;\n        if (size[ulp_u] < size[ulp_v]) {\n            parent[ulp_u] = ulp_v;\n            size[ulp_v] += size[ulp_u];\n        } else {\n            parent[ulp_v] = ulp_u;\n            size[ulp_u] += size[ulp_v];\n        }\n    }\n\n    int getSize(int node) {\n        return size[findUPar(node)];\n    }\n};\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Comparing `parent[u] == parent[v]` Instead of `findUPar(u) == findUPar(v)`:** Because path compression happens lazily during `findUPar`, `parent[u]` might not be up to date yet—always call `findUPar(u) == findUPar(v)` to check if two nodes share a component!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
