583	Algebraic Sum-of-Squares & XOR Bit-Bucketing: Find the Repeating and Missing Number	dsa-find-missing-and-repeating-number	Find the repeating and missing elements in an array of size N from 1 to N in O(N) time and O(1) space using algebraic difference of sums or bitwise XOR bucketing.	2026-10-02 23:24:14.95614+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Algebraic Sum-of-Squares & XOR Bit-Bucketing: Find the Repeating and Missing Number\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2D0D8HE6uak", "title": "Lecture 41: Find the repeating and missing number", "duration": "32m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-the-duplicate-number/", "title": "Find the Duplicate Number", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the single repeated integer in an array of N + 1 integers in the range [1, N] using O(1) extra space."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-all-numbers-disappeared-in-an-array/", "title": "Find All Numbers Disappeared in an Array", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Identify all missing numbers in the range [1, N] in O(N) time and O(1) auxiliary space."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/two-repeated-elements-1587115621/1", "title": "Two Repeated Elements", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the two repeating numbers in an array of N + 2 elements ranging from 1 to N."}]	Module 11: Advanced Array Patterns & Hard Interview Problems	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array of `N` integers where values are supposed to be from `1` to `N`, one number `X` appears **twice** (repeating) and one number `Y` is **missing**. Your goal is to find `{X, Y}` in `O(N)` time and `O(1)` extra space.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Better → 2 Optimal Solutions)\n- **1. Brute Force (O(N²) Time, O(1) Space):** For each number from `1` to `N`, scan the array to count its occurrences (`count == 2` is repeating, `count == 0` is missing).\n- **2. Better Approach via Hashing (O(2N) Time, O(N) Space):** Use a frequency array `hash[N + 1] = {0}` to record counts in one pass, then scan `1` to `N` to find which number has count `2` and which has count `0`.\n- **3. Optimal Approach 1 — Basic Algebra (O(N) Time, O(1) Space):**\n  - Let `S` be the sum of array elements and `SN = N * (N + 1) / 2` be the sum of `1` to `N`.\n  - Subtracting gives Equation 1: **`X - Y = S - SN`** (`val1`).\n  - Let `S2` be the sum of squares of array elements and `S2N = N * (N + 1) * (2N + 1) / 6`.\n  - Subtracting gives: `X² - Y² = (X - Y)(X + Y) = S2 - S2N` (`val2`).\n  - Divide `val2 / val1` to get Equation 2: **`X + Y = val2 / val1`**.\n  - Add both equations to solve for `X = (val1 + val2) / 2` and `Y = X - val1`!\n- **4. Optimal Approach 2 — Bitwise XOR Bucketing (O(N) Time, O(1) Space):**\n  - XOR all array elements with `1..N` to get `xr = X ^ Y`.\n  - Find the rightmost set bit where `X` and `Y` differ (`number = xr & ~(xr - 1)`), split all array elements and `1..N` into two XOR buckets (bit set vs. bit unset) to isolate `X` and `Y`, and verify which one appears twice in the array.\n\n### 💻 Optimal Algebraic Solution Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> findMissingRepeatingNumbers(vector<int> a) {\n    long long n = a.size();\n    long long SN = (n * (n + 1)) / 2;\n    long long S2N = (n * (n + 1) * (2 * n + 1)) / 6;\n    long long S = 0, S2 = 0;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        S += a[i];\n        S2 += (long long)a[i] * (long long)a[i];\n    }\n    long long val1 = S - SN;         // X - Y\n    long long val2 = S2 - S2N;       // X^2 - Y^2\n    val2 = val2 / val1;              // X + Y\n    long long x = (val1 + val2) / 2; // Repeating number\n    long long y = x - val1;          // Missing number\n    return {(int)x, (int)y};\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
8564	1D Tabulation & Backtrack Hash Array: Printing the Longest Increasing Subsequence	dsa-printing-longest-increasing-subsequence-tabulation	Tabulate LIS in a single 1D array where dp[i] represents the LIS ending at index i, and reconstruct the full sequence using a parent hash array.	2026-10-03 11:44:14.759984+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: 1D Tabulation & Backtrack Hash Array: Printing the Longest Increasing Subsequence\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IFfYfonAFGc", "title": "Lecture 222: Printing Longest Increasing Subsequence | Tabulation | Algorithm (DP 42)", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/printing-longest-increasing-subsequence/1", "title": "Print Longest Increasing Subsequence", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find and return the actual Longest Increasing Subsequence array using 1D DP and a parent hash array."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-increasing-subsequence/", "title": "Longest Increasing Subsequence (LC 300)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Implement the 1D O(N^2) DP array where dp[i] stores the LIS ending at index i."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/best-team-with-no-conflicts/", "title": "Best Team With No Conflicts (LC 1626)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Sort players by age and apply the 1D LIS tabulation loop to maximize team score."}]	Module 33: Dynamic Programming on Longest Increasing Subsequences (LIS) & Chain Variants	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn addition to converting the 2D LIS state into a **2-Row Space-Optimized `O(N)` table**, there is a much more powerful **Single-Array `1D` Tabulation (`dp[N]`)** that is required whenever you need to **trace back and print the actual LIS** (and serves as the foundation for DP 44, 45, 46, and 47!).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The 1D `dp[i]` Definition (`LIS Ending at Index i`):**\n  - Define `dp[i]` = length of the Longest Increasing Subsequence that **ends at index `i`**.\n  - Initially, every element forms a valid LIS of length `1` by itself: **`vector<int> dp(n, 1);`**.\n  - For each index `i` from `0` to `n - 1`, scan all previous indices `prev` from `0` to `i - 1`:\n    - If **`arr[prev] < arr[i]`** and **`1 + dp[prev] > dp[i]`**:\n      - Update **`dp[i] = 1 + dp[prev]`**!\n- **How to Print the LIS Using a `hash[]` (Parent) Array:**\n  - Maintain `hash[i] = i` initially (each element is its own start).\n  - Whenever `1 + dp[prev] > dp[i]`, also record **`hash[i] = prev`** (remembering that `i` extended the LIS from `prev`!).\n  - Track `lastIndex` where the global maximum `maxi = max(maxi, dp[i])` occurred.\n  - Backtrack via `while (hash[lastIndex] != lastIndex)` pushing `arr[lastIndex]`, then push the final starting element and **reverse the result**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N²)` Time and `O(N)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Print Longest Increasing Subsequence Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nvector<int> printingLongestIncreasingSubsequence(vector<int> &arr, int n) {\n    vector<int> dp(n, 1), hash(n);\n    int maxi = 1;\n    int lastIndex = 0;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        hash[i] = i;\n        for (int prev = 0; prev < i; prev++) {\n            if (arr[prev] < arr[i] && 1 + dp[prev] > dp[i]) {\n                dp[i] = 1 + dp[prev];\n                hash[i] = prev; // Record predecessor index for backtracking!\n            }\n        }\n        if (dp[i] > maxi) {\n            maxi = dp[i];\n            lastIndex = i;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Backtrack from lastIndex using hash[] to reconstruct the LIS\n    vector<int> temp;\n    temp.push_back(arr[lastIndex]);\n    while (hash[lastIndex] != lastIndex) {\n        lastIndex = hash[lastIndex];\n        temp.push_back(arr[lastIndex]);\n    }\n    reverse(temp.begin(), temp.end());\n    return temp;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Initializing `dp(n, 0)` Instead of `dp(n, 1)`:** Every single element `arr[i]` is already an increasing subsequence of length `1` on its own! Always initialize `dp` with **`1`**, not `0`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
9278	Bottom-Right Corner Square Expansion: Count Square Submatrices With All Ones	dsa-count-square-submatrices-with-all-ones-dp-on-rectangles	Count all-1 square submatrices by defining dp[i][j] = 1 + min({up, left, diag}) as the side length of the largest square with bottom-right corner at (i, j).	2026-10-03 14:56:36.910791+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Bottom-Right Corner Square Expansion: Count Square Submatrices With All Ones\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=auS1fynpnjo", "title": "Lecture 236: Count Square Submatrices with All Ones | DP on Rectangles (DP 56)", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/count-square-submatrices-with-all-ones/", "title": "Count Square Submatrices with All Ones (LC 1277)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given an m x n matrix of ones and zeros, return how many square submatrices have all ones."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximal-square/", "title": "Maximal Square (LC 221)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use the exact same dp[i][j] = 1 + min({up, left, diag}) recurrence to find the largest square's side length."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/count-square-submatrices-with-all-ones/1", "title": "Count Square Submatrices with all Ones", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Calculate the total number of square submatrices composed entirely of 1s."}]	Module 35: Dynamic Programming on Rectangles & Submatrices	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an `N x M` binary matrix of `0`s and `1`s, count the **total number of square submatrices** (of size `1x1`, `2x2`, `3x3`, etc.) that contain **all `1`s**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Direct Bottom-Up Tabulation Is More Intuitive Than Recursion For Squares:**\n  - Define **`dp[i][j]`** = the number of all-`1` square submatrices whose **bottom-right corner is at `(i, j)`** (which is also equal to the side length of the largest all-`1` square ending at `(i, j)`!).\n  - **1. First Row & First Column (`i == 0` or `j == 0`):**\n    - A cell on row `0` or column `0` can only form a `1x1` square if it is `1`: **`dp[i][j] = arr[i][j]`**.\n  - **2. Interior Cells (`i > 0 && j > 0`):**\n    - If `arr[i][j] == 0`, then `0` squares can end at `(i, j)` → **`dp[i][j] = 0`**.\n    - If `arr[i][j] == 1`, how big of a square can end at `(i, j)`? It is constrained by the **minimum** of its three neighbors (Up, Left, and Up-Left Diagonal), plus `1` for the `1x1` square itself:\n      **`dp[i][j] = 1 + min({dp[i - 1][j], dp[i - 1][j - 1], dp[i][j - 1]})`**!\n  - **3. Final Answer:** Sum `dp[i][j]` across all cells `(i, j)` in the table!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * M)` Time and `O(N * M)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Count Square Submatrices With All Ones Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint countSquares(int n, int m, vector<vector<int>> &arr) {\n    vector<vector<int>> dp(n, vector<int>(m, 0));\n\n    // 1. Copy first row and first column directly\n    for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) dp[0][j] = arr[0][j];\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][0] = arr[i][0];\n\n    // 2. Fill interior cells using the 3-neighbor minimum rule\n    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j < m; j++) {\n            if (arr[i][j] == 0) {\n                dp[i][j] = 0;\n            } else {\n                dp[i][j] = 1 + min({dp[i - 1][j], dp[i - 1][j - 1], dp[i][j - 1]});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // 3. Sum all dp[i][j] values\n    int sum = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {\n            sum += dp[i][j];\n        }\n    }\n    return sum;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Taking `max` Instead of `min` of the 3 Neighbors:** If Up is `2`, Diagonal is `2`, and Left is `0`, you **cannot** form a `2x2` square because the left cell is `0`! A square requires **all three** neighboring directions to support it, which is why we take **`min`**.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
3876	Active Call-Stack Path Tracking: Detect a Cycle in a Directed Graph Using DFS	dsa-detect-cycle-in-directed-graph-dfs	Detect directed cycles by distinguishing visited cross-edges from active recursion stack back-edges.	2026-10-03 02:09:36.72605+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Active Call-Stack Path Tracking: Detect a Cycle in a Directed Graph Using DFS\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9twcmtQj4DU", "title": "Lecture 144: Detect Cycle in a Directed Graph using DFS", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/detect-cycle-in-a-directed-graph/1", "title": "Directed Graph Cycle", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given a Directed Graph with V vertices and E edges, check whether it contains any cycle."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/course-schedule/", "title": "Course Schedule (LC 207)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Detect if a circular prerequisite dependency exists in a directed course graph."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-cycle-in-a-graph/", "title": "Longest Cycle in a Graph (LC 2360)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Track active path timestamps during directed DFS to measure the length of the longest cycle."}]	Module 23: Graph Coloring, Directed Cycle Detection & Topological Sort (DAGs)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhy does standard undirected cycle detection fail on a **Directed Graph**? Because in a directed graph, two different paths `A → B → C` and `A → D → C` can converge at `C` without forming a cycle! A cycle in a directed graph exists **if and only if** we reach a node that is currently active **on the exact same ongoing DFS path**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Dual State Tracking (`seen[]` and `inCurrentPath[]`):**\n  - `seen[u] = 1`: Node `u` has been visited at some point (prevents re-exploring finished branches).\n  - `inCurrentPath[u] = 1`: Node `u` is currently on the active recursion stack!\n- **Backtracking Reset Rule:**\n  - When entering `u`: mark both `seen[u] = 1` and `inCurrentPath[u] = 1`.\n  - For each `neighbor` of `u`:\n    - If `!seen[neighbor]`: recurse. If it returns `true`, return `true`.\n    - Else if `inCurrentPath[neighbor] == 1`: we looped back onto our own active path—**Cycle detected (`return true`)**!\n  - When leaving `u` (backtracking): reset **`inCurrentPath[u] = 0`**, while keeping `seen[u] = 1`!\n- **Complexity:** `O(V + E)` Time and `O(2V)` Space (can also be combined into a single array using states `0` = unvisited, `1` = visited, `2` = path-visited).\n\n### 💻 Directed Graph Cycle Detection (DFS) Blueprint\n```cpp\nbool checkCycleDFS(int curr, const vector<int> adjList[], vector<int> &seen, vector<int> &inCurrentPath) {\n    seen[curr] = 1;\n    inCurrentPath[curr] = 1;\n\n    for (int neighbor : adjList[curr]) {\n        if (!seen[neighbor]) {\n            if (checkCycleDFS(neighbor, adjList, seen, inCurrentPath)) {\n                return true;\n            }\n        } else if (inCurrentPath[neighbor] == 1) {\n            return true; // Node is already active on the current recursion path!\n        }\n    }\n\n    inCurrentPath[curr] = 0; // Unmark from current path upon backtracking\n    return false;\n}\n\nbool isCyclic(int V, vector<int> adjList[]) {\n    vector<int> seen(V, 0), inCurrentPath(V, 0);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {\n        if (!seen[i]) {\n            if (checkCycleDFS(i, adjList, seen, inCurrentPath)) return true;\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting `inCurrentPath[curr] = 0` When Backtracking:** If you forget to unmark `inCurrentPath[curr]` before returning `false`, cross-edges from later branches will falsely trigger cycle detection!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22201	Algebra	aptitude-quant-algebra	Master the Remainder Theorem, value substitution for complex rational expressions, inverse reciprocal equations (x + 1/x), and high-yield algebraic identities.	2026-10-07 12:03:13.413481+05:30	### 📌 1. The Remainder & Factor Theorems\n* **Remainder Theorem:** If a polynomial `f(x)` is divided by `(x - a)`, the remainder is exactly equal to **`f(a)`**.\n  * Shortcut: Set the divisor to 0 (`x - a = 0` → `x = a`). Substitute `x = a` into the polynomial.\n  * If divided by `(ax + b)`, substitute **`x = -b/a`**.\n* **Factor Theorem:** If the remainder is exactly 0, then `(x - a)` is a **perfect factor** of the polynomial. This means `f(a) = 0`.\n\n### 💡 2. The Power of Substitution\n* When asked to evaluate or simplify highly complex algebraic or rational expressions with variables (e.g., `x, y, a`), **assume simple numerical values** for the variables (like 0, 1, or 2).\n* **Golden Rule of Substitution:** NEVER choose a value that forces any denominator to become **0** (which makes the expression undefined).\n* Substitute the same assumed values into the multiple-choice options. The correct option will yield the exact same numerical result as the question's expression.\n\n### 💡 3. Inverse Reciprocal Shortcuts `(x + 1/x)`\n* If **`x + 1/x = a`**:\n  * **`x^2 + 1/x^2 = a^2 - 2`**\n  * **`x^3 + 1/x^3 = a^3 - 3a`**\n* If **`x - 1/x = a`**:\n  * **`x^2 + 1/x^2 = a^2 + 2`**\n* **Reverse Finding:** If given `x^2 + 1/x^2 = A`, to find `x + 1/x`, add 2 and take the square root: **`√(A + 2)`**. To find `x - 1/x`, subtract 2 and take the square root: **`√(A - 2)`**.\n\n### 💡 4. Rationalizing Surds\n* If `x = A + B√C` and the difference of squares `A^2 - (B√C)^2 = 1`, then **`1/x = A - B√C`**.\n* Adding them yields `x + 1/x = 2A` (exactly twice the larger term). Subtracting them yields `x - 1/x = 2B√C` (exactly twice the smaller term).	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TV9rQm15sWo", "type": "youtube", "title": "Algebra - Shortcuts & Tricks for 2026 Placement Tests, Jobs & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-alg-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Applying the Remainder Theorem] Find the remainder when the polynomial 2x^3 + 13x^2 - 5x + 1 is divided by x + 4.", "options": ["A) 101", "B) 105", "C) 121", "D) 229"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the exact mathematical remainder when the algebraic polynomial (2x^3 + 13x^2 - 5x + 1) is completely divided by the linear binomial (x + 4)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Set divisor to 0: x + 4 = 0 => x = -4. Substitute x = -4 into the polynomial: 2(-4)^3 + 13(-4)^2 - 5(-4) + 1 = 2(-64) + 13(16) + 20 + 1 = -128 + 208 + 20 + 1 = 101. The remainder is 101.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-alg-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Factor Theorem Unknown Parameter] 5x^3 - 3x^2 + cx + 2 divided by x + 3 leaves remainder 5. Find c.", "options": ["A) -50", "B) -55", "C) -60", "D) -65"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the polynomial (5x^3 - 3x^2 + cx + 2) is divided by the binomial (x + 3), it leaves a remainder of exactly 5. What is the exact numerical value of the constant c?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Set x + 3 = 0 => x = -3. Substitute x = -3 and set the result equal to the remainder (5): 5(-3)^3 - 3(-3)^2 + c(-3) + 2 = 5 => 5(-27) - 3(9) - 3c + 2 = 5 => -135 - 27 - 3c + 2 = 5 => -160 - 3c = 5 => -3c = 165 => c = -165 / 3 = -55.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-alg-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [High Power Polynomial Remainder] Find the remainder when x^529 - 1 is divided by x + 1.", "options": ["A) -2", "B) -1", "C) 0", "D) 1"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the exact mathematical remainder when the extremely high-degree polynomial (x^529 - 1) is completely divided by the linear binomial (x + 1)?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Set x + 1 = 0 => x = -1. Substitute x = -1 into the polynomial: (-1)^529 - 1. Since 529 is an ODD power, (-1)^529 = -1. The expression evaluates to -1 - 1 = -2. The remainder is exactly -2.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-alg-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Polynomial Divisor x^2 - a^2] 3x^3 + 6x^2 + 4a divided by x^2 - 4 leaves remainder 12x. Find a.", "options": ["A) -4", "B) -6", "C) -8", "D) -12"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "When the algebraic expression (3x^3 + 6x^2 + 4a) is divided by the quadratic binomial (x^2 - 4), it leaves a remainder of exactly 12x. What is the exact mathematical value of the constant a?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Set the divisor x^2 - 4 = 0 => x^2 = 4. Substitute x^2 = 4 directly into the expression. Rewrite the expression to isolate x^2 terms: 3x(x^2) + 6(x^2) + 4a. Substitute x^2 = 4: 3x(4) + 6(4) + 4a = 12x + 24 + 4a. This evaluated expression is exactly equal to the given remainder 12x. So, 12x + 24 + 4a = 12x. Cancel 12x from both sides: 24 + 4a = 0 => 4a = -24 => a = -6.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-alg-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Completely Divisible Constraint] x^1001 + 5x^999 + a is completely divisible by x + 1. Find a.", "options": ["A) 2", "B) 4", "C) 6", "D) 8"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The massive polynomial expression (x^1001 + 5x^999 + a) is known to be completely and exactly divisible by the linear binomial (x + 1). What is the exact numerical value of the constant a?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "\\"Completely divisible\\" means the remainder is exactly 0. Set x + 1 = 0 => x = -1. Substitute x = -1 into the polynomial and set equal to 0: (-1)^1001 + 5(-1)^999 + a = 0. Since 1001 and 999 are both ODD powers, (-1)^odd = -1. So, -1 + 5(-1) + a = 0 => -1 - 5 + a = 0 => -6 + a = 0 => a = 6.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-alg-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Exponential Equation Simplification] If 5^x - 5^(x-1) = 500, find the value of x^x.", "options": ["A) 64", "B) 125", "C) 256", "D) 3,125"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a positive real number x satisfies the exponential equation 5^x - 5^(x-1) = 500, what is the exact numerical value of x^x?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Factor out the smaller power 5^(x-1) from the left side: 5^(x-1) × (5^1 - 1) = 500 => 5^(x-1) × (4) = 500 => 5^(x-1) = 500 / 4 = 125. Since 125 is 5^3, we equate the exponents: x - 1 = 3 => x = 4. We are asked for x^x, which is 4^4 = 256.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-alg-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Evaluating Complex Rational Expressions with Substitution] Evaluate the expression [2(x+3)]/[3(x-2)] - [2(x+1)]/[3(x-3)] using substitution.", "options": ["A) 7 / 9", "B) -7 / 9", "C) 14 / 18", "D) -5 / 9"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Use the strategic substitution method to find which numerical fraction correctly simplifies the following complex algebraic expression when x = 0:\\n[ 2(x+3) ] / [ 3(x-2) ]  -  [ 2(x+1) ] / [ 3(x-3) ]", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Substitute x = 0 into the expression. Term 1: [2(0+3)] / [3(0-2)] = 6 / -6 = -1. Term 2: [2(0+1)] / [3(0-3)] = 2 / -9 = -2/9. Expression = -1 - (-2/9) = -1 + 2/9 = (-9 + 2) / 9 = -7/9.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-alg-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Multi-Variable Zero Denominator Trap] Find value of (a+1)/(a-1) + (a-1)/(a+1). What value of a should be assumed for fast calculation?", "options": ["A) a = 1", "B) a = -1", "C) a = 0", "D) Both A and B"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A student wants to use the variable substitution method to quickly evaluate the expression:\\n[ (a+1) / (a-1) ] + [ (a-1) / (a+1) ]\\nWhich of the following values of \\"a\\" is the mathematically SAFEST and FASTEST to substitute without creating undefined fractions?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "To use the substitution method, we must NEVER choose a value that makes any denominator 0. If a = 1, the first fraction denominator (a-1) becomes 0. If a = -1, the second fraction denominator (a+1) becomes 0. Therefore, a = 0 is the safest and fastest choice. (If a=0, Expression = 1/-1 + (-1)/1 = -1 - 1 = -2).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-alg-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Zero-ing Out Unnecessary Variables] If x+y=1, find sum of [3x^2+4y]/[x-4] and its reciprocal. Use substitution y=0.", "options": ["A) -2", "B) 0", "C) 2", "D) 4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given the constraint equation x + y = 1. Using the advanced algebraic substitution method by assuming y = 0, what is the exact mathematical value of the sum of the expression [ (3x^2 + 4y) / (x - 4) ] and its direct reciprocal?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Assume y = 0. Then x + 0 = 1 => x = 1. Substitute x=1 and y=0 into the expression: [3(1)^2 + 4(0)] / [1 - 4] = 3 / -3 = -1. The expression evaluates to -1. Its reciprocal is 1 / -1 = -1. The sum of the expression and its reciprocal is -1 + (-1) = -2.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-alg-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Eliminating Target Expression Terms] If x + 5y + 3 = 0, find value of x^3 + 5y^3 + 12xy + 27.", "options": ["A) -27", "B) 0", "C) 27", "D) 54"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given the linear constraint equation x + 5y + 3 = 0. By utilizing the zero-substitution trick for the variable y, evaluate the exact numerical value of the large polynomial:\\nx^3 + 5y^3 + 12xy + 27", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Assume y = 0. The constraint becomes x + 0 + 3 = 0 => x = -3. Substitute x = -3 and y = 0 into the target polynomial. Since y=0, all terms containing y vanish (5y^3 and 12xy become 0). The polynomial collapses entirely to just x^3 + 27. Substitute x = -3: (-3)^3 + 27 = -27 + 27 = 0.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-alg-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Simultaneous Three-Variable Algebraic Reduction] If a+b+c = 25 and a^2+b^2+c^2 = 225, find 4a^2b^2 + c(a-b).", "options": ["A) 16,000", "B) 62,500", "C) 160,000", "D) 400,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a + b + c = 25 and simultaneously a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = 225. By assuming the variable c = 0 to rapidly simplify the system, what is the exact numerical value of the expression [ 4a^2b^2 + c(a - b) ]?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Assume c = 0. The constraints become: a + b = 25 and a^2 + b^2 = 225. The target expression collapses to just 4a^2b^2 (since c(a-b) = 0). We know (a+b)^2 = a^2 + b^2 + 2ab. Substitute known values: 25^2 = 225 + 2ab => 625 = 225 + 2ab => 2ab = 400. We need 4a^2b^2, which is exactly (2ab)^2. So (400)^2 = 160,000.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-alg-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Two Equations, Three Variables] x = a + 2/a, y = 2a - 2/a. Find value of (x^2 - y^2)(y^2 + 3) / (x^2 - 6).", "options": ["A) 3", "B) 6", "C) 9", "D) 12"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given the parametric equations x = a + 2/a and y = 2a - 2/a. By using a safe numerical substitution for the parameter \\"a\\" (such as a = 1), evaluate the exact numerical value of the fraction:\\n[ (x^2 - y^2)(y^2 + 3) ] / [ x^2 - 6 ]", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let a = 1. Then x = 1 + 2/1 = 3. And y = 2(1) - 2/1 = 0. Substitute x=3 and y=0 into the target fraction: [ (3^2 - 0^2)(0^2 + 3) ] / [ 3^2 - 6 ] = [ (9)(3) ] / [ 9 - 6 ] = 27 / 3 = 9. (The mathematical value of this algebraic expression evaluates identically to a constant 9 for any valid non-zero choice of a).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-alg-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Inverse Reciprocal Square Trick] If x + 1/x = √47, find x^2 + 1/x^2.", "options": ["A) 45", "B) 47", "C) 49", "D) 2207"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a positive real number x satisfies the equation x + 1/x = √47, what is the exact numerical value of the expression x^2 + 1/x^2?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Use the shortcut identity: x^2 + 1/x^2 = (x + 1/x)^2 - 2. Given x + 1/x = √47. Squaring it gives (√47)^2 = 47. Subtract 2: 47 - 2 = 45.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-alg-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Multi-Stage Reciprocal Squaring] If x + 1/x = √6, find x^8 + 1/x^8.", "options": ["A) 194", "B) 196", "C) 198", "D) 254"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given the algebraic relation x + 1/x = √6. By sequentially applying the square expansion shortcut multiple times, what is the exact numerical value of x^8 + 1/x^8?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) x^2 + 1/x^2 = (√6)^2 - 2 = 6 - 2 = 4. 2) x^4 + 1/x^4 = (x^2 + 1/x^2)^2 - 2 = 4^2 - 2 = 16 - 2 = 14. 3) x^8 + 1/x^8 = (x^4 + 1/x^4)^2 - 2 = 14^2 - 2 = 196 - 2 = 194.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-alg-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Descending Power Reverse Trick] If x^4 + 1/x^4 = 23, find the positive value of x + 1/x.", "options": ["A) √5", "B) √7", "C) 5", "D) 7"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a positive real number x satisfies the high-order equation x^4 + 1/x^4 = 23. By stepping downwards using the reverse square trick, what is the exact positive numerical value of x + 1/x?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) We know (x^2 + 1/x^2)^2 = x^4 + 1/x^4 + 2. So, (x^2 + 1/x^2)^2 = 23 + 2 = 25. Taking the positive square root gives x^2 + 1/x^2 = 5. 2) We know (x + 1/x)^2 = x^2 + 1/x^2 + 2. So, (x + 1/x)^2 = 5 + 2 = 7. Taking the positive square root gives x + 1/x = √7.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-alg-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Alternating Sign Shortcut] If x - 1/x = 3, find x^4 + 1/x^4.", "options": ["A) 119", "B) 121", "C) 123", "D) 125"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given the inverse subtraction relation x - 1/x = 3. What is the exact mathematical value of the expression x^4 + 1/x^4?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) For subtraction: x^2 + 1/x^2 = (x - 1/x)^2 + 2 = 3^2 + 2 = 9 + 2 = 11. 2) Once we have x^2 + 1/x^2 = 11, the next step uses the standard addition rule! x^4 + 1/x^4 = (x^2 + 1/x^2)^2 - 2 = 11^2 - 2 = 121 - 2 = 119. (Note: Many mistakenly ADD 2 in the second step, but the sign rule flips back to subtraction once the base term is positive!).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-alg-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Inverse Reciprocal Cubing Trick] If x + 1/x = 3√2, find x^3 + 1/x^3.", "options": ["A) 45√2", "B) 54√2", "C) 45", "D) 54"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given the algebraic relation x + 1/x = 3√2. Using the direct cubing expansion shortcut, what is the exact mathematical value of x^3 + 1/x^3?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Use the shortcut identity: x^3 + 1/x^3 = a^3 - 3a. Here a = 3√2. 1) a^3 = (3√2)^3 = 27 × 2√2 = 54√2. 2) 3a = 3 × 3√2 = 9√2. 3) Subtract: 54√2 - 9√2 = 45√2.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-alg-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Rationalizing a Surd Inverse] If x = 5 + 2√6, find the value of x - 1/x.", "options": ["A) 10", "B) 10 - 4√6", "C) 4√6", "D) 5√6"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If an irrational parameter is defined as x = 5 + 2√6, what is the exact simplified mathematical value of the expression (x - 1/x)?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) First, rationalize 1/x. 1/x = 1 / (5 + 2√6). Multiply top and bottom by conjugate (5 - 2√6). Denominator becomes 5^2 - (2√6)^2 = 25 - 24 = 1. So 1/x = 5 - 2√6. 2) Now compute x - 1/x = (5 + 2√6) - (5 - 2√6) = 5 + 2√6 - 5 + 2√6. The 5s cancel out, leaving exactly 4√6. (Shortcut: x - 1/x is always exactly twice the smaller radical term when difference of squares is 1).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-alg-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [High Power Conjugate Cancellation] If x^45 = 12 + 3√7, find 1 / x^45.", "options": ["A) (12 - 3√7) / 81", "B) (12 + 3√7) / 81", "C) (12 - 3√7) / 10", "D) (12 - 3√7) / 12"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a massive exponential term evaluates to x^45 = 12 + 3√7. Without attempting to find the value of x itself, what is the exact rationalized value of its reciprocal, 1 / x^45?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let y = x^45. So y = 12 + 3√7. We need 1/y = 1 / (12 + 3√7). Rationalize by multiplying top and bottom by (12 - 3√7). Numerator = 12 - 3√7. Denominator = 12^2 - (3√7)^2 = 144 - (9 × 7) = 144 - 63 = 81. Thus, 1/y = (12 - 3√7) / 81.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-alg-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Double Base Rationalization Shortcut] If x = 13 + 3√2, find the denominator of 1/x after rationalization.", "options": ["A) 100", "B) 151", "C) 169", "D) 187"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If an irrational value is given as x = 13 + 3√2, what will be the exact mathematical denominator of the fractional expression 1/x after it has been completely rationalized?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1/x = 1 / (13 + 3√2). To rationalize, multiply top and bottom by conjugate (13 - 3√2). The denominator becomes the difference of squares: 13^2 - (3√2)^2 = 169 - (9 × 2) = 169 - 18 = 151.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. The Remainder & Factor Theorems\n* **Remainder Theorem:** If a polynomial `f(x)` is divided by `(x - a)`, the remainder is exactly equal to **`f(a)`**.\n  * Shortcut: Set the divisor to 0 (`x - a = 0` → `x = a`). Substitute `x = a` into the polynomial.\n  * If divided by `(ax + b)`, substitute **`x = -b/a`**.\n* **Factor Theorem:** If the remainder is exactly 0, then `(x - a)` is a **perfect factor** of the polynomial. This means `f(a) = 0`.\n\n### 💡 2. The Power of Substitution\n* When asked to evaluate or simplify highly complex algebraic or rational expressions with variables (e.g., `x, y, a`), **assume simple numerical values** for the variables (like 0, 1, or 2).\n* **Golden Rule of Substitution:** NEVER choose a value that forces any denominator to become **0** (which makes the expression undefined).\n* Substitute the same assumed values into the multiple-choice options. The correct option will yield the exact same numerical result as the question's expression.\n\n### 💡 3. Inverse Reciprocal Shortcuts `(x + 1/x)`\n* If **`x + 1/x = a`**:\n  * **`x^2 + 1/x^2 = a^2 - 2`**\n  * **`x^3 + 1/x^3 = a^3 - 3a`**\n* If **`x - 1/x = a`**:\n  * **`x^2 + 1/x^2 = a^2 + 2`**\n* **Reverse Finding:** If given `x^2 + 1/x^2 = A`, to find `x + 1/x`, add 2 and take the square root: **`√(A + 2)`**. To find `x - 1/x`, subtract 2 and take the square root: **`√(A - 2)`**.\n\n### 💡 4. Rationalizing Surds\n* If `x = A + B√C` and the difference of squares `A^2 - (B√C)^2 = 1`, then **`1/x = A - B√C`**.\n* Adding them yields `x + 1/x = 2A` (exactly twice the larger term). Subtracting them yields `x - 1/x = 2B√C` (exactly twice the smaller term).	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Algebra	Master the Remainder Theorem, value substitution for complex rational expressions, inverse reciprocal equations (x + 1/x), and high-yield algebraic identities.	78	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	35	[{"id": "apt-alg-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Applying the Remainder Theorem] Find the remainder when the polynomial 2x^3 + 13x^2 - 5x + 1 is divided by x + 4.", "options": ["A) 101", "B) 105", "C) 121", "D) 229"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the exact mathematical remainder when the algebraic polynomial (2x^3 + 13x^2 - 5x + 1) is completely divided by the linear binomial (x + 4)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Set divisor to 0: x + 4 = 0 => x = -4. Substitute x = -4 into the polynomial: 2(-4)^3 + 13(-4)^2 - 5(-4) + 1 = 2(-64) + 13(16) + 20 + 1 = -128 + 208 + 20 + 1 = 101. The remainder is 101.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-alg-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Factor Theorem Unknown Parameter] 5x^3 - 3x^2 + cx + 2 divided by x + 3 leaves remainder 5. Find c.", "options": ["A) -50", "B) -55", "C) -60", "D) -65"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the polynomial (5x^3 - 3x^2 + cx + 2) is divided by the binomial (x + 3), it leaves a remainder of exactly 5. What is the exact numerical value of the constant c?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Set x + 3 = 0 => x = -3. Substitute x = -3 and set the result equal to the remainder (5): 5(-3)^3 - 3(-3)^2 + c(-3) + 2 = 5 => 5(-27) - 3(9) - 3c + 2 = 5 => -135 - 27 - 3c + 2 = 5 => -160 - 3c = 5 => -3c = 165 => c = -165 / 3 = -55.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-alg-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [High Power Polynomial Remainder] Find the remainder when x^529 - 1 is divided by x + 1.", "options": ["A) -2", "B) -1", "C) 0", "D) 1"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the exact mathematical remainder when the extremely high-degree polynomial (x^529 - 1) is completely divided by the linear binomial (x + 1)?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Set x + 1 = 0 => x = -1. Substitute x = -1 into the polynomial: (-1)^529 - 1. Since 529 is an ODD power, (-1)^529 = -1. The expression evaluates to -1 - 1 = -2. The remainder is exactly -2.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-alg-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Polynomial Divisor x^2 - a^2] 3x^3 + 6x^2 + 4a divided by x^2 - 4 leaves remainder 12x. Find a.", "options": ["A) -4", "B) -6", "C) -8", "D) -12"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "When the algebraic expression (3x^3 + 6x^2 + 4a) is divided by the quadratic binomial (x^2 - 4), it leaves a remainder of exactly 12x. What is the exact mathematical value of the constant a?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Set the divisor x^2 - 4 = 0 => x^2 = 4. Substitute x^2 = 4 directly into the expression. Rewrite the expression to isolate x^2 terms: 3x(x^2) + 6(x^2) + 4a. Substitute x^2 = 4: 3x(4) + 6(4) + 4a = 12x + 24 + 4a. This evaluated expression is exactly equal to the given remainder 12x. So, 12x + 24 + 4a = 12x. Cancel 12x from both sides: 24 + 4a = 0 => 4a = -24 => a = -6.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-alg-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Completely Divisible Constraint] x^1001 + 5x^999 + a is completely divisible by x + 1. Find a.", "options": ["A) 2", "B) 4", "C) 6", "D) 8"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The massive polynomial expression (x^1001 + 5x^999 + a) is known to be completely and exactly divisible by the linear binomial (x + 1). What is the exact numerical value of the constant a?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "\\"Completely divisible\\" means the remainder is exactly 0. Set x + 1 = 0 => x = -1. Substitute x = -1 into the polynomial and set equal to 0: (-1)^1001 + 5(-1)^999 + a = 0. Since 1001 and 999 are both ODD powers, (-1)^odd = -1. So, -1 + 5(-1) + a = 0 => -1 - 5 + a = 0 => -6 + a = 0 => a = 6.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-alg-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Exponential Equation Simplification] If 5^x - 5^(x-1) = 500, find the value of x^x.", "options": ["A) 64", "B) 125", "C) 256", "D) 3,125"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a positive real number x satisfies the exponential equation 5^x - 5^(x-1) = 500, what is the exact numerical value of x^x?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Factor out the smaller power 5^(x-1) from the left side: 5^(x-1) × (5^1 - 1) = 500 => 5^(x-1) × (4) = 500 => 5^(x-1) = 500 / 4 = 125. Since 125 is 5^3, we equate the exponents: x - 1 = 3 => x = 4. We are asked for x^x, which is 4^4 = 256.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-alg-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Evaluating Complex Rational Expressions with Substitution] Evaluate the expression [2(x+3)]/[3(x-2)] - [2(x+1)]/[3(x-3)] using substitution.", "options": ["A) 7 / 9", "B) -7 / 9", "C) 14 / 18", "D) -5 / 9"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Use the strategic substitution method to find which numerical fraction correctly simplifies the following complex algebraic expression when x = 0:\\n[ 2(x+3) ] / [ 3(x-2) ]  -  [ 2(x+1) ] / [ 3(x-3) ]", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Substitute x = 0 into the expression. Term 1: [2(0+3)] / [3(0-2)] = 6 / -6 = -1. Term 2: [2(0+1)] / [3(0-3)] = 2 / -9 = -2/9. Expression = -1 - (-2/9) = -1 + 2/9 = (-9 + 2) / 9 = -7/9.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-alg-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Multi-Variable Zero Denominator Trap] Find value of (a+1)/(a-1) + (a-1)/(a+1). What value of a should be assumed for fast calculation?", "options": ["A) a = 1", "B) a = -1", "C) a = 0", "D) Both A and B"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A student wants to use the variable substitution method to quickly evaluate the expression:\\n[ (a+1) / (a-1) ] + [ (a-1) / (a+1) ]\\nWhich of the following values of \\"a\\" is the mathematically SAFEST and FASTEST to substitute without creating undefined fractions?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "To use the substitution method, we must NEVER choose a value that makes any denominator 0. If a = 1, the first fraction denominator (a-1) becomes 0. If a = -1, the second fraction denominator (a+1) becomes 0. Therefore, a = 0 is the safest and fastest choice. (If a=0, Expression = 1/-1 + (-1)/1 = -1 - 1 = -2).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-alg-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Zero-ing Out Unnecessary Variables] If x+y=1, find sum of [3x^2+4y]/[x-4] and its reciprocal. Use substitution y=0.", "options": ["A) -2", "B) 0", "C) 2", "D) 4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given the constraint equation x + y = 1. Using the advanced algebraic substitution method by assuming y = 0, what is the exact mathematical value of the sum of the expression [ (3x^2 + 4y) / (x - 4) ] and its direct reciprocal?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Assume y = 0. Then x + 0 = 1 => x = 1. Substitute x=1 and y=0 into the expression: [3(1)^2 + 4(0)] / [1 - 4] = 3 / -3 = -1. The expression evaluates to -1. Its reciprocal is 1 / -1 = -1. The sum of the expression and its reciprocal is -1 + (-1) = -2.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-alg-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Eliminating Target Expression Terms] If x + 5y + 3 = 0, find value of x^3 + 5y^3 + 12xy + 27.", "options": ["A) -27", "B) 0", "C) 27", "D) 54"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given the linear constraint equation x + 5y + 3 = 0. By utilizing the zero-substitution trick for the variable y, evaluate the exact numerical value of the large polynomial:\\nx^3 + 5y^3 + 12xy + 27", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Assume y = 0. The constraint becomes x + 0 + 3 = 0 => x = -3. Substitute x = -3 and y = 0 into the target polynomial. Since y=0, all terms containing y vanish (5y^3 and 12xy become 0). The polynomial collapses entirely to just x^3 + 27. Substitute x = -3: (-3)^3 + 27 = -27 + 27 = 0.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-alg-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Simultaneous Three-Variable Algebraic Reduction] If a+b+c = 25 and a^2+b^2+c^2 = 225, find 4a^2b^2 + c(a-b).", "options": ["A) 16,000", "B) 62,500", "C) 160,000", "D) 400,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a + b + c = 25 and simultaneously a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = 225. By assuming the variable c = 0 to rapidly simplify the system, what is the exact numerical value of the expression [ 4a^2b^2 + c(a - b) ]?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Assume c = 0. The constraints become: a + b = 25 and a^2 + b^2 = 225. The target expression collapses to just 4a^2b^2 (since c(a-b) = 0). We know (a+b)^2 = a^2 + b^2 + 2ab. Substitute known values: 25^2 = 225 + 2ab => 625 = 225 + 2ab => 2ab = 400. We need 4a^2b^2, which is exactly (2ab)^2. So (400)^2 = 160,000.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-alg-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Two Equations, Three Variables] x = a + 2/a, y = 2a - 2/a. Find value of (x^2 - y^2)(y^2 + 3) / (x^2 - 6).", "options": ["A) 3", "B) 6", "C) 9", "D) 12"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given the parametric equations x = a + 2/a and y = 2a - 2/a. By using a safe numerical substitution for the parameter \\"a\\" (such as a = 1), evaluate the exact numerical value of the fraction:\\n[ (x^2 - y^2)(y^2 + 3) ] / [ x^2 - 6 ]", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let a = 1. Then x = 1 + 2/1 = 3. And y = 2(1) - 2/1 = 0. Substitute x=3 and y=0 into the target fraction: [ (3^2 - 0^2)(0^2 + 3) ] / [ 3^2 - 6 ] = [ (9)(3) ] / [ 9 - 6 ] = 27 / 3 = 9. (The mathematical value of this algebraic expression evaluates identically to a constant 9 for any valid non-zero choice of a).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-alg-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Inverse Reciprocal Square Trick] If x + 1/x = √47, find x^2 + 1/x^2.", "options": ["A) 45", "B) 47", "C) 49", "D) 2207"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a positive real number x satisfies the equation x + 1/x = √47, what is the exact numerical value of the expression x^2 + 1/x^2?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Use the shortcut identity: x^2 + 1/x^2 = (x + 1/x)^2 - 2. Given x + 1/x = √47. Squaring it gives (√47)^2 = 47. Subtract 2: 47 - 2 = 45.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-alg-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Multi-Stage Reciprocal Squaring] If x + 1/x = √6, find x^8 + 1/x^8.", "options": ["A) 194", "B) 196", "C) 198", "D) 254"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given the algebraic relation x + 1/x = √6. By sequentially applying the square expansion shortcut multiple times, what is the exact numerical value of x^8 + 1/x^8?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) x^2 + 1/x^2 = (√6)^2 - 2 = 6 - 2 = 4. 2) x^4 + 1/x^4 = (x^2 + 1/x^2)^2 - 2 = 4^2 - 2 = 16 - 2 = 14. 3) x^8 + 1/x^8 = (x^4 + 1/x^4)^2 - 2 = 14^2 - 2 = 196 - 2 = 194.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-alg-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Descending Power Reverse Trick] If x^4 + 1/x^4 = 23, find the positive value of x + 1/x.", "options": ["A) √5", "B) √7", "C) 5", "D) 7"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a positive real number x satisfies the high-order equation x^4 + 1/x^4 = 23. By stepping downwards using the reverse square trick, what is the exact positive numerical value of x + 1/x?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) We know (x^2 + 1/x^2)^2 = x^4 + 1/x^4 + 2. So, (x^2 + 1/x^2)^2 = 23 + 2 = 25. Taking the positive square root gives x^2 + 1/x^2 = 5. 2) We know (x + 1/x)^2 = x^2 + 1/x^2 + 2. So, (x + 1/x)^2 = 5 + 2 = 7. Taking the positive square root gives x + 1/x = √7.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-alg-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Alternating Sign Shortcut] If x - 1/x = 3, find x^4 + 1/x^4.", "options": ["A) 119", "B) 121", "C) 123", "D) 125"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given the inverse subtraction relation x - 1/x = 3. What is the exact mathematical value of the expression x^4 + 1/x^4?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) For subtraction: x^2 + 1/x^2 = (x - 1/x)^2 + 2 = 3^2 + 2 = 9 + 2 = 11. 2) Once we have x^2 + 1/x^2 = 11, the next step uses the standard addition rule! x^4 + 1/x^4 = (x^2 + 1/x^2)^2 - 2 = 11^2 - 2 = 121 - 2 = 119. (Note: Many mistakenly ADD 2 in the second step, but the sign rule flips back to subtraction once the base term is positive!).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-alg-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Inverse Reciprocal Cubing Trick] If x + 1/x = 3√2, find x^3 + 1/x^3.", "options": ["A) 45√2", "B) 54√2", "C) 45", "D) 54"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Given the algebraic relation x + 1/x = 3√2. Using the direct cubing expansion shortcut, what is the exact mathematical value of x^3 + 1/x^3?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Use the shortcut identity: x^3 + 1/x^3 = a^3 - 3a. Here a = 3√2. 1) a^3 = (3√2)^3 = 27 × 2√2 = 54√2. 2) 3a = 3 × 3√2 = 9√2. 3) Subtract: 54√2 - 9√2 = 45√2.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-alg-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Rationalizing a Surd Inverse] If x = 5 + 2√6, find the value of x - 1/x.", "options": ["A) 10", "B) 10 - 4√6", "C) 4√6", "D) 5√6"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If an irrational parameter is defined as x = 5 + 2√6, what is the exact simplified mathematical value of the expression (x - 1/x)?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) First, rationalize 1/x. 1/x = 1 / (5 + 2√6). Multiply top and bottom by conjugate (5 - 2√6). Denominator becomes 5^2 - (2√6)^2 = 25 - 24 = 1. So 1/x = 5 - 2√6. 2) Now compute x - 1/x = (5 + 2√6) - (5 - 2√6) = 5 + 2√6 - 5 + 2√6. The 5s cancel out, leaving exactly 4√6. (Shortcut: x - 1/x is always exactly twice the smaller radical term when difference of squares is 1).", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-alg-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [High Power Conjugate Cancellation] If x^45 = 12 + 3√7, find 1 / x^45.", "options": ["A) (12 - 3√7) / 81", "B) (12 + 3√7) / 81", "C) (12 - 3√7) / 10", "D) (12 - 3√7) / 12"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a massive exponential term evaluates to x^45 = 12 + 3√7. Without attempting to find the value of x itself, what is the exact rationalized value of its reciprocal, 1 / x^45?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let y = x^45. So y = 12 + 3√7. We need 1/y = 1 / (12 + 3√7). Rationalize by multiplying top and bottom by (12 - 3√7). Numerator = 12 - 3√7. Denominator = 12^2 - (3√7)^2 = 144 - (9 × 7) = 144 - 63 = 81. Thus, 1/y = (12 - 3√7) / 81.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-alg-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Double Base Rationalization Shortcut] If x = 13 + 3√2, find the denominator of 1/x after rationalization.", "options": ["A) 100", "B) 151", "C) 169", "D) 187"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If an irrational value is given as x = 13 + 3√2, what will be the exact mathematical denominator of the fractional expression 1/x after it has been completely rationalized?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1/x = 1 / (13 + 3√2). To rationalize, multiply top and bottom by conjugate (13 - 3√2). The denominator becomes the difference of squares: 13^2 - (3√2)^2 = 169 - (9 × 2) = 169 - 18 = 151.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22202	Statistics	aptitude-quant-statistics	Calculate mean, median, mode, variance, standard deviation, and coefficient of variance. Master the Assumed Mean Method and cumulative frequency median formulas.	2026-10-07 12:03:13.413481+05:30	### 📌 1. Measures of Central Tendency\n* **Mean (Average):** `x̄ = (Σx) / n`\n  * For grouped frequency data: `x̄ = (Σfx) / Σf`\n  * **Assumed Mean Shortcut:** `x̄ = A + (Σfd / Σf)`, where `A` is the assumed mean and `d = x - A`.\n* **Mode:** The value that appears most frequently.\n  * For grouped data: **`Mode = L + [ (f1 - f0) / (2f1 - f0 - f2) ] × h`**\n    (where `L` = lower limit of modal class, `f1` = frequency of modal class, `f0` = frequency of previous class, `f2` = frequency of next class, `h` = class range).\n* **Median:** The exact middle value when sorted.\n  * For odd `N`: `((N+1)/2)`-th term.\n  * For even `N`: Average of `(N/2)`-th and `(N/2 + 1)`-th terms.\n  * For grouped data: **`Median = L + [ (N/2 - CF) / f ] × h`**\n    (where `CF` = cumulative frequency of preceding class).\n\n### 💡 2. The Golden Empirical Relationship\n* **`Mode = 3(Median) - 2(Mean)`**\n  *(Memorize this! Countless objective questions ask for one value given the other two).*\n\n### 💡 3. Measures of Dispersion & Transformation Properties\n* **Variance (`σ^2`):** `σ^2 = Σ(x - x̄)^2 / n`\n  (Measures how spread out the data points are from the mean. ALWAYS positive).\n* **Standard Deviation (`σ`):** `σ = √(Variance)`\n* **Coefficient of Variance (CV):** `CV = (σ / x̄) × 100 %`\n* **Transformation Rules for Mean:**\n  * If a constant `K` is added/subtracted to every observation, the new Mean is exactly **`Old Mean ± K`**.\n  * If every observation is multiplied/divided by `K`, the new Mean is exactly **`Old Mean ×/÷ K`**.	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Jrb4wEloCQ", "type": "youtube", "title": "Statistics - Shortcuts & Tricks for Placement Tests, Jobs & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-stat-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Grouped Data Class Mark Calculation] Find the class mark for the interval 40 to 60.", "options": ["A) 20", "B) 40", "C) 50", "D) 100"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a grouped frequency distribution table, what is the exact mathematical \\"class mark\\" (mid-point) for the specific class interval defined as 40 to 60?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "The class mark is the midpoint of the class interval. Formula: Class Mark = (Lower Limit + Upper Limit) / 2 = (40 + 60) / 2 = 100 / 2 = 50.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-stat-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Mean of Simple Frequency Distribution] Scores: 25(f=10), 30(f=8), 32(f=10), 35(f=4). Total f=50, Total fx=1755. Find mean.", "options": ["A) 34.1", "B) 35.1", "C) 36.5", "D) 37.0"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a discrete frequency distribution table tracking student marks, the sum of all frequencies (Σf) is exactly 50, and the sum of the products of marks and their respective frequencies (Σfx) is exactly 1755. What is the exact mean score of the class?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The formula for the mean of a frequency distribution is x̄ = (Σfx) / Σf. Substitute the given values: x̄ = 1755 / 50 = 351 / 10 = 35.1.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-stat-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Applying Assumed Mean Method] If assumed mean A=50, total frequency Σf=400, and Σfd = -900, find the true mean.", "options": ["A) 47.75", "B) 48.25", "C) 52.25", "D) 54.50"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A statistician uses the Assumed Mean Method to calculate the average of a massive dataset. She selects an assumed mean (A) of exactly 50. Her calculations show that the sum of all frequencies (Σf) is 400, and the sum of the frequency-deviation products (Σfd) is exactly -900. What is the true mathematical mean of the dataset?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Formula for True Mean: x̄ = A + (Σfd / Σf). Substitute values: x̄ = 50 + (-900 / 400) = 50 - 9/4 = 50 - 2.25 = 47.75.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-stat-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Property of Mean - Transformation] The mean of 17 terms is 25. What is the new mean if 5 is subtracted from each observation?", "options": ["A) 20", "B) 25", "C) 30", "D) 125"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The mathematically calculated mean of a dataset comprising 17 terms is exactly 25. If a constant value of 5 is completely subtracted from every single observation in the dataset, what will be the exact value of the new mean?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Property of Mean: If a constant K is subtracted from every single observation in a dataset, the overall mean strictly decreases by that exact same constant K. New Mean = Old Mean - 5 = 25 - 5 = 20.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-stat-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Property of Mean - Multiplication] The mean of 10 terms is 15. What is the new mean if each observation is multiplied by 2?", "options": ["A) 15", "B) 17", "C) 30", "D) 150"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The calculated mean of a dataset of 10 numerical observations is exactly 15. If every single observation in the dataset is strictly multiplied by a constant factor of 2, what will be the exact value of the new mean?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Property of Mean: If every observation in a dataset is mathematically multiplied by a constant factor K, the overall mean is strictly multiplied by that exact same factor K. New Mean = Old Mean × 2 = 15 × 2 = 30.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-stat-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Calculating Variance from Scratch] Find the variance of the dataset: 10, 9, 12, 10, 9, 4. (Mean = 9)", "options": ["A) 5", "B) 6", "C) 7.5", "D) 36"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Calculate the exact mathematical variance (σ^2) of the following small dataset of 6 numbers: 10, 9, 12, 10, 9, 4. (Hint: The mean of this dataset is exactly 9).", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Mean x̄ = 9. 2) Find deviations (x - x̄): (10-9)=1, (9-9)=0, (12-9)=3, (10-9)=1, (9-9)=0, (4-9)=-5. 3) Square the deviations: 1, 0, 9, 1, 0, 25. 4) Sum of squared deviations = 1 + 0 + 9 + 1 + 0 + 25 = 36. 5) Variance = Sum / N = 36 / 6 = 6.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-stat-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Standard Deviation from Variance] If the calculated variance of a dataset is 4, what is its standard deviation?", "options": ["A) -2", "B) 2", "C) 8", "D) 16"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the mathematically calculated variance (σ^2) of an extensive statistical dataset is exactly 4, what is its exact Standard Deviation (σ)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Standard Deviation (σ) is defined strictly as the POSITIVE square root of the variance. σ = √4 = +2. (A standard deviation cannot be a negative value).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-stat-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Coefficient of Variance Calculation] Mean = 16, Variance = 4. Find Coefficient of Variance.", "options": ["A) 12.5%", "B) 15.0%", "C) 25.0%", "D) 50.0%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The calculated mean of a given dataset is exactly 16, and its variance is exactly 4. What is the exact Coefficient of Variance (CV) expressed as a percentage?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Standard Deviation (σ) = √Variance = √4 = 2. 2) Coefficient of Variance (CV) = (σ / Mean) × 100 %. 3) CV = (2 / 16) × 100 = (1 / 8) × 100 = 100 / 8 = 12.5%.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-stat-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Identifying Mode vs Modal Frequency] Data: value 2 occurs 3 times, value 3 occurs 2 times, value 4 occurs 2 times. Find mode.", "options": ["A) 2", "B) 3", "C) 4", "D) Cannot be determined"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a statistical observation sheet, the numeric value \\"2\\" occurs 3 times, the value \\"3\\" occurs 2 times, and the value \\"4\\" occurs 2 times. Which of the following is the true mathematical Mode of this dataset?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Mode is defined strictly as the specific data value that appears the absolute maximum number of times in the dataset (i.e., has the highest frequency). The highest frequency is 3, which corresponds strictly to the data value \\"2\\". The Mode is 2.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-stat-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Grouped Data Mode Calculation] Modal class limits L=4000, f1=18, f0=4, f2=11, range h=1000. Calculate the exact Mode.", "options": ["A) 4,333.33", "B) 4,666.66", "C) 4,800.00", "D) 5,100.00"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "For a grouped frequency distribution, the identified modal class has a lower limit L = 4000 and a class width h = 1000. The frequencies are: modal class f1 = 18, preceding class f0 = 4, and succeeding class f2 = 11. What is the exact mathematical Mode of this dataset?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Use the Mode formula: Mode = L + [ (f1 - f0) / (2f1 - f0 - f2) ] × h. 1) Numerator: f1 - f0 = 18 - 4 = 14. 2) Denominator: 2(18) - 4 - 11 = 36 - 15 = 21. 3) Fraction: 14 / 21 = 2 / 3. 4) Multiply by h: (2/3) × 1000 = 2000 / 3 ≈ 666.66. 5) Add L: 4000 + 666.66 = 4666.66.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-stat-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Finding Median Position for Even N] A sorted dataset has exactly 6 elements. Which positions determine the median?", "options": ["A) 2nd and 3rd terms", "B) 3rd and 4th terms", "C) 4th and 5th terms", "D) Just the 3rd term"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An unorganized dataset containing exactly 6 numerical elements is completely sorted into strict ascending order. Which specific positional terms must be averaged to determine the exact median of this dataset?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For a dataset with an EVEN number of total terms (N = 6), the median cannot be a single central term. It must be computed as the exact arithmetic average of the (N/2)-th term and the (N/2 + 1)-th term. Here, 6/2 = 3rd term, and 3+1 = 4th term. Thus, average the 3rd and 4th terms.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-stat-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Median Calculation for Odd N Dataset] Sorted data: 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 9, 11. Find the median.", "options": ["A) 4", "B) 5", "C) 6", "D) 8"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Consider the following statistical dataset which has already been sorted into strict ascending order: 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 9, 11. What is the exact mathematical median of this dataset?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The dataset has exactly an ODD number of total elements (N = 7). The median is precisely located at the ((N + 1) / 2)-th position. (7 + 1) / 2 = 8 / 2 = 4th position. Counting from the left, the 4th term is exactly 5.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-stat-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Finding the Median Class via CF] Cumulative frequency row: 5, 9, 16, 22, 30. Total N=30. N/2 = 15. Which CF range contains the median?", "options": ["A) 9", "B) 16", "C) 22", "D) 30"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A statistician constructs a cumulative frequency (CF) table for grouped data. The consecutive CF values are: 5, 9, 16, 22, 30. The total number of observations N is 30, making N/2 = 15. The median mathematically resides in the class interval corresponding to which specific CF value?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "To locate the median class, we find the cumulative frequency that is STRICTLY GREATER THAN (or equal to) N/2. Since N/2 = 15, we look sequentially through the CF row: 5 (too small), 9 (too small), 16 (first value ≥ 15!). Therefore, the median class is definitively the one associated with the CF of 16.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-stat-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Grouped Data Median Formula] Median class limits: L=50, N/2=15, CF(preceding)=9, f=7, h=5. Calculate the exact Median.", "options": ["A) 53.14", "B) 54.28", "C) 55.00", "D) 56.42"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "For a grouped frequency distribution, the identified median class has a lower limit L = 50 and a class width h = 5. The total observations yield N/2 = 15. The cumulative frequency of the preceding class (CF) is 9, and the frequency of the median class itself (f) is 7. What is the exact mathematical Median of this dataset?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Use the Median formula: Median = L + [ (N/2 - CF) / f ] × h. 1) Numerator: N/2 - CF = 15 - 9 = 6. 2) Fraction: 6 / 7. 3) Multiply by h: (6/7) × 5 = 30 / 7 ≈ 4.285. 4) Add L: 50 + 4.285 = 54.285... Rounding to two decimal places gives 54.28.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-stat-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Golden Empirical Rule: Finding Mode] If the ratio of Mean to Median is 3:7, find the ratio of Mean to Mode.", "options": ["A) 1 : 4", "B) 1 : 5", "C) 3 : 15", "D) Both B and C"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a moderately skewed statistical distribution, the ratio of the Mean to the Median is precisely 3 : 7. Using the standard empirical relationship between the measures of central tendency, what is the exact mathematical ratio of the Mean to the Mode?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Mean = 3x and Median = 7x. Empirical Formula: Mode = 3(Median) - 2(Mean). Substitute values: Mode = 3(7x) - 2(3x) = 21x - 6x = 15x. The question asks for the ratio of Mean to Mode: 3x / 15x = 3 / 15. Dividing by 3 simplifies this to 1 / 5. Thus, both 3:15 and 1:5 are mathematically correct.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-stat-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Golden Empirical Rule: Finding Mean] If Mode is 24 and Median is 20, what is the Mean?", "options": ["A) 16", "B) 18", "C) 22", "D) 26"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a moderately skewed continuous frequency distribution, the calculated Mode is exactly 24 and the calculated Median is exactly 20. Utilizing the fundamental empirical relationship, what must be the exact value of the Mean?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Empirical Formula: Mode = 3(Median) - 2(Mean). Substitute the known values: 24 = 3(20) - 2(Mean) => 24 = 60 - 2(Mean). Rearrange to isolate the Mean: 2(Mean) = 60 - 24 = 36. Divide by 2: Mean = 36 / 2 = 18.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-stat-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Identifying Median from a Frequency Table without CF] Given frequencies: 2, 4, 6, 8. What is the position of the median?", "options": ["A) 10th position", "B) Average of 10th and 11th positions", "C) 11th position", "D) Average of 11th and 12th positions"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A discrete frequency table lists four consecutive increasing data values with frequencies of exactly 2, 4, 6, and 8 respectively. At which specific position(s) does the exact mathematical median of this entire dataset reside?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "First, find the total number of observations N by summing the frequencies: N = 2 + 4 + 6 + 8 = 20. Since N is an EVEN number (20), the median is not a single term. It must be computed as the exact arithmetic average of the (N/2)-th term and the (N/2 + 1)-th term. 20/2 = 10. Thus, average the 10th and 11th positional terms.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-stat-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Finding the Range of Grouped Data] If the lowest class interval is 0-10 and highest is 40-50, what is the maximum possible range of the data?", "options": ["A) 30", "B) 40", "C) 50", "D) 60"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a grouped frequency distribution table, the absolutely lowest class interval is defined as 0-10, and the absolutely highest populated class interval is defined as 40-50. What is the absolute maximum possible mathematical range of the underlying raw dataset?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The Range of any dataset is defined strictly as the absolute difference between the Maximum possible value and the Minimum possible value. The maximum possible theoretical value in the highest class (40-50) is 50. The minimum possible theoretical value in the lowest class (0-10) is 0. Maximum theoretical Range = 50 - 0 = 50.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-stat-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Impact of Outliers on Central Tendency] Which measure of central tendency is the most stable and robust when a massive outlier is suddenly introduced into the dataset?", "options": ["A) Mean", "B) Median", "C) Variance", "D) Standard Deviation"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A statistician has perfectly calculated the Mean, Median, and Mode of a standard dataset. Suddenly, a massive, extremely large outlier value is mistakenly appended to the dataset. Which primary measure of central tendency will mathematically be the LEAST affected (most robust) by this single extreme outlier?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "The Mean is highly sensitive to outliers because it mathematically sums all values (a massive value pulls the average up significantly). Variance and Standard Deviation are measures of dispersion that square the massive outlier's deviation, making them highly unstable. The Median only relies strictly on positional order; adding one extreme value merely shifts the central position by one half-step, making it highly robust and stable against extreme outliers.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-stat-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Mean of Combined Datasets] Dataset A has 5 items with mean 10. Dataset B has 15 items with mean 20. Find combined mean.", "options": ["A) 15.0", "B) 16.5", "C) 17.5", "D) 18.0"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Dataset A contains exactly 5 numerical items and possesses a calculated mean of exactly 10. Dataset B contains exactly 15 numerical items and possesses a calculated mean of exactly 20. If both datasets are completely merged into a single large dataset, what will be the exact overall combined mean?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Use the \\"Total Sum\\" rule! Total sum of Dataset A = 5 × 10 = 50. Total sum of Dataset B = 15 × 20 = 300. Overall combined sum = 50 + 300 = 350. Overall total number of items = 5 + 15 = 20. Combined Mean = Combined Sum / Total Items = 350 / 20 = 17.5.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. Measures of Central Tendency\n* **Mean (Average):** `x̄ = (Σx) / n`\n  * For grouped frequency data: `x̄ = (Σfx) / Σf`\n  * **Assumed Mean Shortcut:** `x̄ = A + (Σfd / Σf)`, where `A` is the assumed mean and `d = x - A`.\n* **Mode:** The value that appears most frequently.\n  * For grouped data: **`Mode = L + [ (f1 - f0) / (2f1 - f0 - f2) ] × h`**\n    (where `L` = lower limit of modal class, `f1` = frequency of modal class, `f0` = frequency of previous class, `f2` = frequency of next class, `h` = class range).\n* **Median:** The exact middle value when sorted.\n  * For odd `N`: `((N+1)/2)`-th term.\n  * For even `N`: Average of `(N/2)`-th and `(N/2 + 1)`-th terms.\n  * For grouped data: **`Median = L + [ (N/2 - CF) / f ] × h`**\n    (where `CF` = cumulative frequency of preceding class).\n\n### 💡 2. The Golden Empirical Relationship\n* **`Mode = 3(Median) - 2(Mean)`**\n  *(Memorize this! Countless objective questions ask for one value given the other two).*\n\n### 💡 3. Measures of Dispersion & Transformation Properties\n* **Variance (`σ^2`):** `σ^2 = Σ(x - x̄)^2 / n`\n  (Measures how spread out the data points are from the mean. ALWAYS positive).\n* **Standard Deviation (`σ`):** `σ = √(Variance)`\n* **Coefficient of Variance (CV):** `CV = (σ / x̄) × 100 %`\n* **Transformation Rules for Mean:**\n  * If a constant `K` is added/subtracted to every observation, the new Mean is exactly **`Old Mean ± K`**.\n  * If every observation is multiplied/divided by `K`, the new Mean is exactly **`Old Mean ×/÷ K`**.	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Statistics	Calculate mean, median, mode, variance, standard deviation, and coefficient of variance. Master the Assumed Mean Method and cumulative frequency median formulas.	79	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	36	[{"id": "apt-stat-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Grouped Data Class Mark Calculation] Find the class mark for the interval 40 to 60.", "options": ["A) 20", "B) 40", "C) 50", "D) 100"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a grouped frequency distribution table, what is the exact mathematical \\"class mark\\" (mid-point) for the specific class interval defined as 40 to 60?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "The class mark is the midpoint of the class interval. Formula: Class Mark = (Lower Limit + Upper Limit) / 2 = (40 + 60) / 2 = 100 / 2 = 50.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-stat-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Mean of Simple Frequency Distribution] Scores: 25(f=10), 30(f=8), 32(f=10), 35(f=4). Total f=50, Total fx=1755. Find mean.", "options": ["A) 34.1", "B) 35.1", "C) 36.5", "D) 37.0"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a discrete frequency distribution table tracking student marks, the sum of all frequencies (Σf) is exactly 50, and the sum of the products of marks and their respective frequencies (Σfx) is exactly 1755. What is the exact mean score of the class?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The formula for the mean of a frequency distribution is x̄ = (Σfx) / Σf. Substitute the given values: x̄ = 1755 / 50 = 351 / 10 = 35.1.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-stat-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Applying Assumed Mean Method] If assumed mean A=50, total frequency Σf=400, and Σfd = -900, find the true mean.", "options": ["A) 47.75", "B) 48.25", "C) 52.25", "D) 54.50"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A statistician uses the Assumed Mean Method to calculate the average of a massive dataset. She selects an assumed mean (A) of exactly 50. Her calculations show that the sum of all frequencies (Σf) is 400, and the sum of the frequency-deviation products (Σfd) is exactly -900. What is the true mathematical mean of the dataset?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Formula for True Mean: x̄ = A + (Σfd / Σf). Substitute values: x̄ = 50 + (-900 / 400) = 50 - 9/4 = 50 - 2.25 = 47.75.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-stat-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Property of Mean - Transformation] The mean of 17 terms is 25. What is the new mean if 5 is subtracted from each observation?", "options": ["A) 20", "B) 25", "C) 30", "D) 125"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The mathematically calculated mean of a dataset comprising 17 terms is exactly 25. If a constant value of 5 is completely subtracted from every single observation in the dataset, what will be the exact value of the new mean?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Property of Mean: If a constant K is subtracted from every single observation in a dataset, the overall mean strictly decreases by that exact same constant K. New Mean = Old Mean - 5 = 25 - 5 = 20.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-stat-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Property of Mean - Multiplication] The mean of 10 terms is 15. What is the new mean if each observation is multiplied by 2?", "options": ["A) 15", "B) 17", "C) 30", "D) 150"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The calculated mean of a dataset of 10 numerical observations is exactly 15. If every single observation in the dataset is strictly multiplied by a constant factor of 2, what will be the exact value of the new mean?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Property of Mean: If every observation in a dataset is mathematically multiplied by a constant factor K, the overall mean is strictly multiplied by that exact same factor K. New Mean = Old Mean × 2 = 15 × 2 = 30.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-stat-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Calculating Variance from Scratch] Find the variance of the dataset: 10, 9, 12, 10, 9, 4. (Mean = 9)", "options": ["A) 5", "B) 6", "C) 7.5", "D) 36"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Calculate the exact mathematical variance (σ^2) of the following small dataset of 6 numbers: 10, 9, 12, 10, 9, 4. (Hint: The mean of this dataset is exactly 9).", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Mean x̄ = 9. 2) Find deviations (x - x̄): (10-9)=1, (9-9)=0, (12-9)=3, (10-9)=1, (9-9)=0, (4-9)=-5. 3) Square the deviations: 1, 0, 9, 1, 0, 25. 4) Sum of squared deviations = 1 + 0 + 9 + 1 + 0 + 25 = 36. 5) Variance = Sum / N = 36 / 6 = 6.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-stat-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Standard Deviation from Variance] If the calculated variance of a dataset is 4, what is its standard deviation?", "options": ["A) -2", "B) 2", "C) 8", "D) 16"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the mathematically calculated variance (σ^2) of an extensive statistical dataset is exactly 4, what is its exact Standard Deviation (σ)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Standard Deviation (σ) is defined strictly as the POSITIVE square root of the variance. σ = √4 = +2. (A standard deviation cannot be a negative value).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-stat-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Coefficient of Variance Calculation] Mean = 16, Variance = 4. Find Coefficient of Variance.", "options": ["A) 12.5%", "B) 15.0%", "C) 25.0%", "D) 50.0%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The calculated mean of a given dataset is exactly 16, and its variance is exactly 4. What is the exact Coefficient of Variance (CV) expressed as a percentage?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Standard Deviation (σ) = √Variance = √4 = 2. 2) Coefficient of Variance (CV) = (σ / Mean) × 100 %. 3) CV = (2 / 16) × 100 = (1 / 8) × 100 = 100 / 8 = 12.5%.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-stat-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Identifying Mode vs Modal Frequency] Data: value 2 occurs 3 times, value 3 occurs 2 times, value 4 occurs 2 times. Find mode.", "options": ["A) 2", "B) 3", "C) 4", "D) Cannot be determined"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a statistical observation sheet, the numeric value \\"2\\" occurs 3 times, the value \\"3\\" occurs 2 times, and the value \\"4\\" occurs 2 times. Which of the following is the true mathematical Mode of this dataset?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "Mode is defined strictly as the specific data value that appears the absolute maximum number of times in the dataset (i.e., has the highest frequency). The highest frequency is 3, which corresponds strictly to the data value \\"2\\". The Mode is 2.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-stat-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Grouped Data Mode Calculation] Modal class limits L=4000, f1=18, f0=4, f2=11, range h=1000. Calculate the exact Mode.", "options": ["A) 4,333.33", "B) 4,666.66", "C) 4,800.00", "D) 5,100.00"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "For a grouped frequency distribution, the identified modal class has a lower limit L = 4000 and a class width h = 1000. The frequencies are: modal class f1 = 18, preceding class f0 = 4, and succeeding class f2 = 11. What is the exact mathematical Mode of this dataset?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Use the Mode formula: Mode = L + [ (f1 - f0) / (2f1 - f0 - f2) ] × h. 1) Numerator: f1 - f0 = 18 - 4 = 14. 2) Denominator: 2(18) - 4 - 11 = 36 - 15 = 21. 3) Fraction: 14 / 21 = 2 / 3. 4) Multiply by h: (2/3) × 1000 = 2000 / 3 ≈ 666.66. 5) Add L: 4000 + 666.66 = 4666.66.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-stat-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Finding Median Position for Even N] A sorted dataset has exactly 6 elements. Which positions determine the median?", "options": ["A) 2nd and 3rd terms", "B) 3rd and 4th terms", "C) 4th and 5th terms", "D) Just the 3rd term"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An unorganized dataset containing exactly 6 numerical elements is completely sorted into strict ascending order. Which specific positional terms must be averaged to determine the exact median of this dataset?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For a dataset with an EVEN number of total terms (N = 6), the median cannot be a single central term. It must be computed as the exact arithmetic average of the (N/2)-th term and the (N/2 + 1)-th term. Here, 6/2 = 3rd term, and 3+1 = 4th term. Thus, average the 3rd and 4th terms.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-stat-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Median Calculation for Odd N Dataset] Sorted data: 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 9, 11. Find the median.", "options": ["A) 4", "B) 5", "C) 6", "D) 8"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Consider the following statistical dataset which has already been sorted into strict ascending order: 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 9, 11. What is the exact mathematical median of this dataset?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The dataset has exactly an ODD number of total elements (N = 7). The median is precisely located at the ((N + 1) / 2)-th position. (7 + 1) / 2 = 8 / 2 = 4th position. Counting from the left, the 4th term is exactly 5.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-stat-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Finding the Median Class via CF] Cumulative frequency row: 5, 9, 16, 22, 30. Total N=30. N/2 = 15. Which CF range contains the median?", "options": ["A) 9", "B) 16", "C) 22", "D) 30"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A statistician constructs a cumulative frequency (CF) table for grouped data. The consecutive CF values are: 5, 9, 16, 22, 30. The total number of observations N is 30, making N/2 = 15. The median mathematically resides in the class interval corresponding to which specific CF value?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "To locate the median class, we find the cumulative frequency that is STRICTLY GREATER THAN (or equal to) N/2. Since N/2 = 15, we look sequentially through the CF row: 5 (too small), 9 (too small), 16 (first value ≥ 15!). Therefore, the median class is definitively the one associated with the CF of 16.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-stat-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Grouped Data Median Formula] Median class limits: L=50, N/2=15, CF(preceding)=9, f=7, h=5. Calculate the exact Median.", "options": ["A) 53.14", "B) 54.28", "C) 55.00", "D) 56.42"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "For a grouped frequency distribution, the identified median class has a lower limit L = 50 and a class width h = 5. The total observations yield N/2 = 15. The cumulative frequency of the preceding class (CF) is 9, and the frequency of the median class itself (f) is 7. What is the exact mathematical Median of this dataset?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Use the Median formula: Median = L + [ (N/2 - CF) / f ] × h. 1) Numerator: N/2 - CF = 15 - 9 = 6. 2) Fraction: 6 / 7. 3) Multiply by h: (6/7) × 5 = 30 / 7 ≈ 4.285. 4) Add L: 50 + 4.285 = 54.285... Rounding to two decimal places gives 54.28.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-stat-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Golden Empirical Rule: Finding Mode] If the ratio of Mean to Median is 3:7, find the ratio of Mean to Mode.", "options": ["A) 1 : 4", "B) 1 : 5", "C) 3 : 15", "D) Both B and C"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a moderately skewed statistical distribution, the ratio of the Mean to the Median is precisely 3 : 7. Using the standard empirical relationship between the measures of central tendency, what is the exact mathematical ratio of the Mean to the Mode?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let Mean = 3x and Median = 7x. Empirical Formula: Mode = 3(Median) - 2(Mean). Substitute values: Mode = 3(7x) - 2(3x) = 21x - 6x = 15x. The question asks for the ratio of Mean to Mode: 3x / 15x = 3 / 15. Dividing by 3 simplifies this to 1 / 5. Thus, both 3:15 and 1:5 are mathematically correct.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-stat-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Golden Empirical Rule: Finding Mean] If Mode is 24 and Median is 20, what is the Mean?", "options": ["A) 16", "B) 18", "C) 22", "D) 26"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a moderately skewed continuous frequency distribution, the calculated Mode is exactly 24 and the calculated Median is exactly 20. Utilizing the fundamental empirical relationship, what must be the exact value of the Mean?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Empirical Formula: Mode = 3(Median) - 2(Mean). Substitute the known values: 24 = 3(20) - 2(Mean) => 24 = 60 - 2(Mean). Rearrange to isolate the Mean: 2(Mean) = 60 - 24 = 36. Divide by 2: Mean = 36 / 2 = 18.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-stat-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Identifying Median from a Frequency Table without CF] Given frequencies: 2, 4, 6, 8. What is the position of the median?", "options": ["A) 10th position", "B) Average of 10th and 11th positions", "C) 11th position", "D) Average of 11th and 12th positions"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A discrete frequency table lists four consecutive increasing data values with frequencies of exactly 2, 4, 6, and 8 respectively. At which specific position(s) does the exact mathematical median of this entire dataset reside?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "First, find the total number of observations N by summing the frequencies: N = 2 + 4 + 6 + 8 = 20. Since N is an EVEN number (20), the median is not a single term. It must be computed as the exact arithmetic average of the (N/2)-th term and the (N/2 + 1)-th term. 20/2 = 10. Thus, average the 10th and 11th positional terms.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-stat-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Finding the Range of Grouped Data] If the lowest class interval is 0-10 and highest is 40-50, what is the maximum possible range of the data?", "options": ["A) 30", "B) 40", "C) 50", "D) 60"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "In a grouped frequency distribution table, the absolutely lowest class interval is defined as 0-10, and the absolutely highest populated class interval is defined as 40-50. What is the absolute maximum possible mathematical range of the underlying raw dataset?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "The Range of any dataset is defined strictly as the absolute difference between the Maximum possible value and the Minimum possible value. The maximum possible theoretical value in the highest class (40-50) is 50. The minimum possible theoretical value in the lowest class (0-10) is 0. Maximum theoretical Range = 50 - 0 = 50.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-stat-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Impact of Outliers on Central Tendency] Which measure of central tendency is the most stable and robust when a massive outlier is suddenly introduced into the dataset?", "options": ["A) Mean", "B) Median", "C) Variance", "D) Standard Deviation"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A statistician has perfectly calculated the Mean, Median, and Mode of a standard dataset. Suddenly, a massive, extremely large outlier value is mistakenly appended to the dataset. Which primary measure of central tendency will mathematically be the LEAST affected (most robust) by this single extreme outlier?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "The Mean is highly sensitive to outliers because it mathematically sums all values (a massive value pulls the average up significantly). Variance and Standard Deviation are measures of dispersion that square the massive outlier's deviation, making them highly unstable. The Median only relies strictly on positional order; adding one extreme value merely shifts the central position by one half-step, making it highly robust and stable against extreme outliers.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-stat-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Mean of Combined Datasets] Dataset A has 5 items with mean 10. Dataset B has 15 items with mean 20. Find combined mean.", "options": ["A) 15.0", "B) 16.5", "C) 17.5", "D) 18.0"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Dataset A contains exactly 5 numerical items and possesses a calculated mean of exactly 10. Dataset B contains exactly 15 numerical items and possesses a calculated mean of exactly 20. If both datasets are completely merged into a single large dataset, what will be the exact overall combined mean?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Use the \\"Total Sum\\" rule! Total sum of Dataset A = 5 × 10 = 50. Total sum of Dataset B = 15 × 20 = 300. Overall combined sum = 50 + 300 = 350. Overall total number of items = 5 + 15 = 20. Combined Mean = Combined Sum / Total Items = 350 / 20 = 17.5.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
13281	Descending-Profit Latest-Slot Allocation: Job Sequencing Problem	dsa-job-sequencing-problem-greedy-algorithm	Master Descending-Profit Latest-Slot Allocation: Job Sequencing Problem with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:19:12.172975+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Descending-Profit Latest-Slot Allocation: Job Sequencing Problem\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QbwltemZbRg", "title": "Descending-Profit Latest-Slot Allocation: Job Sequencing Problem - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/job-sequencing-problem-1587115620/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Job Sequencing Problem", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given a set of N jobs where each job has a deadline and profit associated with it, find the number of jobs done and the maximum profit."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/course-schedule-iii/", "title": "LeetCode 630: Course Schedule III", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Schedule courses with durations and deadlines to maximize the number of completed courses."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-profit-in-job-scheduling/", "title": "LeetCode 1235: Maximum Profit in Job Scheduling", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Contrast unit-duration deadline scheduling (Greedy) with variable-duration interval profit scheduling (DP + Binary Search)."}]	Module 41: Greedy Algorithms: Sorting Heuristics, Reachability Horizons & Interval Scheduling	### 📌 Topic Overview\nYou are given `N` jobs where each job has an `id`, a `deadline` (1-based day index), and a `profit`. Every job takes **1 single day** to complete, and only 1 job can be scheduled per day. Maximize the **total profit** and return `{countJobs, totalProfit}`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Two-Part Greedy Strategy:**\n  1. **Sort Jobs by `profit` in Descending Order:** Always consider the highest-paying jobs first!\n  2. **Schedule Each Job on the LATEST Possible Free Day (`<= deadline`):**\n     - Why schedule a job with `deadline = 4` on **Day `4`** instead of Day `1`?\n     - Because performing it on Day `4` leaves Days `1, 2, 3` **open for other jobs that might have tighter deadlines** (`deadline = 1, 2, 3`)!\n- **Slot Array Implementation (`O(N log N + N * maxDeadline)` Time):**\n  - Find `maxDeadline` across all jobs and create `vector<int> slot(maxDeadline + 1, -1)`.\n  - For each job `i` in sorted profit order:\n    - Scan **`j` backwards from `arr[i].dead` down to `1`**:\n      - If **`slot[j] == -1`** (Day `j` is free!):\n        - Assign `slot[j] = arr[i].id`, increment `countJobs++`, add `totalProfit += arr[i].profit`, and **`break`**!\n- *(Interview Bonus Note: In competitive programming, the inner backward loop `j` can be optimized from `O(maxDeadline)` to `O(1)` amortized using Disjoint Set Union (DSU)!)*\n\n### 💻 Job Sequencing Problem Blueprint\n```cpp\nstruct Job {\n    int id;\n    int dead;\n    int profit;\n};\n\nstatic bool comparison(Job a, Job b) {\n    return (a.profit > b.profit); // Sort by profit descending!\n}\n\npair<int, int> JobScheduling(Job arr[], int n) {\n    // 1. Sort all jobs in descending order of profit\n    sort(arr, arr + n, comparison);\n\n    // 2. Find the maximum deadline to size our timeline slot array\n    int maxi = arr[0].dead;\n    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n        maxi = max(maxi, arr[i].dead);\n    }\n\n    // 1-based day slots (days 1 .. maxi); -1 means the day is free\n    vector<int> slot(maxi + 1, -1);\n\n    int countJobs = 0, jobProfit = 0;\n\n    // 3. Greedily assign each job to the LATEST available day <= its deadline\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        for (int j = arr[i].dead; j > 0; j--) {\n            if (slot[j] == -1) {\n                slot[j] = arr[i].id;\n                countJobs++;\n                jobProfit += arr[i].profit;\n                break; // Job scheduled; move to next job!\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    return {countJobs, jobProfit};\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Looping `j` Down to `0` (`j >= 0`) on 1-Based Deadlines:** On GeeksforGeeks and standard problem statements, a deadline of `1` means the job can only be performed on **Day `1`** (not Day `0`!). Always loop `for (int j = arr[i].dead; j > 0; j--)` so each job gets at most `dead` slots (`1 .. dead`).	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22254	Mediator Design Pattern	lld-mediator-design-pattern	Prevent spaghetti communication networks. Force interacting objects (Colleagues) to communicate exclusively through a central hub (Mediator), reducing O(N^2) couplings to O(N).	2026-10-07 22:38:19.438928+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Mediator Design Pattern\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/3lGIICzgyQQ", "title": "Mediator Design Pattern | Build Chat Room | UML + Code | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v35-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Resolving the O(N^2) Coupling Problem", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn a tightly coupled peer-to-peer system, every object must hold a direct reference to every other object to communicate. For $N$ objects, there are $N(N-1)$ connections, approaching $O(N^2)$. Adding a new node requires updating $N$ existing nodes.\\n\\nWith a Mediator, every object only requires a single connection: a pointer to the central Mediator. The Mediator maintains $N$ connections to the objects. \\nTotal connections = $N$ (from nodes to mediator) + $N$ (from mediator to nodes) = $2N$, which simplifies to $O(N)$ linear complexity. Adding a new node only requires notifying the central Mediator.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain mathematically how the Mediator Pattern reduces the architectural coupling matrix of a distributed system from O(N^2) connections to O(N).", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v35-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Mediator vs Observer Pattern Differences", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Observer Pattern (Push Notifications):** Used for **One-to-Many** unidirectional communication. The Observable Subject is the definitive source of truth. When its state changes, it blindly pushes that state outward to passive Observers. Observers do not talk back to the Subject to route messages to other Observers.\\n* **Mediator Pattern (Routing Hub):** Used for **Many-to-Many** bidirectional communication. The Mediator itself contains no domain state; it is purely a traffic router. Active Colleague objects intentionally ask the Mediator to route complex messages and commands to specific other Colleague objects. It replaces a spaghetti web of interactions with a star-topology hub.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Structurally, the Mediator and Observer patterns both use centralized lists to notify objects. Architecturally, what is the profound difference in their design intent?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v35-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Implementing Private Messaging in a Chat Room", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nUser A invokes a method on the Mediator: `mediator->sendPrivateMessage(this, \\"UserB_ID\\", \\"Hello B\\");`\\n\\nThe Mediator takes over the routing responsibility:\\n1. It searches its internal `std::unordered_map<std::string, IUser*>` for the key `\\"UserB_ID\\"`.\\n2. If found, the Mediator executes `targetUser->receiveMessage(senderId, \\"Hello B\\")`.\\n3. If not found, the Mediator returns an error to User A.\\nUser A never communicates with User B directly, relying entirely on the Mediator to map IDs to physical object pointers.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In a Mediator-based Chat Room, how is a private message routed from User A to User B without User A holding a reference to User B?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v35-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Single Responsibility Principle Trade-off", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe fundamental risk of the Mediator pattern is that ALL inter-object communication logic is centralized into one class.\\n\\nOver time, a `ChatMediator` might accumulate routing logic, mute/block logic, profanity filtering, connection throttling, and data logging. It rapidly transforms into an unmaintainable \\"God Object\\" controlling the entire application.\\nThis violates SRP, as the Mediator now has a dozen reasons to change. \\n*Mitigation:* The Mediator should strictly act as a dumb router. Complex business logic (like profanity filtering) should be extracted into separate Decorators or Strategy classes that the Mediator delegates to, keeping the core Mediator lean.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is the Mediator pattern often criticized for creating \\"God Objects\\", and how does this conflict with the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v35-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Real-World Use Cases for Mediator", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Complex GUI Dialog Windows:**\\nConsider an Airplane Booking UI containing a DatePicker, a \\"Return Flight\\" Checkbox, and a \\"Submit\\" Button.\\n* If the User unchecks \\"Return Flight\\", the DatePicker for the return date must be disabled.\\n* If the DatePicker is empty, the \\"Submit\\" Button must be disabled.\\n\\nIf the Checkbox object has hardcoded pointers to the DatePicker and Button objects to update their states, the UI components become hopelessly entangled $O(N^2)$.\\nInstead, the `DialogWindow` acts as the Mediator. When the Checkbox is toggled, it simply tells the Mediator `mediator->notify(this, \\"TOGGLED\\")`. The Mediator contains the localized logic to disable the DatePicker and Button, keeping the raw UI components entirely decoupled and reusable.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Beyond Chat Rooms, identify and explain a common real-world UI scenario where the Mediator pattern is essential for managing complexity.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The $O(N^2)$ Spaghetti Network\n* If 5 Users in a Chat Room need to broadcast messages to each other, a peer-to-peer approach requires every User to store an array of pointers to every other User.\n* This results in $N(N-1)$ hardcoded couplings. If a User is added, disconnected, or implements a "Mute User" feature, every single peer object must update its internal registry, causing an architectural nightmare.\n\n### 💡 2. The Mediator Hub\n* **Definition:** Define an object that encapsulates how a set of objects interact. Mediator promotes loose coupling by keeping objects from referring to each other explicitly.\n* **The Structure:**\n  * **Mediator (ChatRoom):** The central hub. It alone maintains the `std::vector<IUser*>` of active participants.\n  * **Colleagues (Users):** Users hold exactly one pointer: a reference to the `Mediator`. They have absolutely zero knowledge of the other users.\n\n### 💡 3. Decentralized Execution\n* When User A wants to broadcast "Hello":\n  1. User A executes `mediator->broadcast(this, "Hello")`.\n  2. The `Mediator` iterates through its internal list of Users.\n  3. The `Mediator` executes `user->receive("Hello")` on everyone except User A.\n* If a "Mute" feature is added, the logic is safely encapsulated entirely inside the `Mediator` routing logic, leaving the `User` class completely untouched.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	Mediator Design Pattern	Prevent spaghetti communication networks. Force interacting objects (Colleagues) to communicate exclusively through a central hub (Mediator), reducing O(N^2) couplings to O(N).	48	Hard	Low Level Design	49	[{"id": "lld-v35-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Resolving the O(N^2) Coupling Problem", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn a tightly coupled peer-to-peer system, every object must hold a direct reference to every other object to communicate. For $N$ objects, there are $N(N-1)$ connections, approaching $O(N^2)$. Adding a new node requires updating $N$ existing nodes.\\n\\nWith a Mediator, every object only requires a single connection: a pointer to the central Mediator. The Mediator maintains $N$ connections to the objects. \\nTotal connections = $N$ (from nodes to mediator) + $N$ (from mediator to nodes) = $2N$, which simplifies to $O(N)$ linear complexity. Adding a new node only requires notifying the central Mediator.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain mathematically how the Mediator Pattern reduces the architectural coupling matrix of a distributed system from O(N^2) connections to O(N).", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v35-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Mediator vs Observer Pattern Differences", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Observer Pattern (Push Notifications):** Used for **One-to-Many** unidirectional communication. The Observable Subject is the definitive source of truth. When its state changes, it blindly pushes that state outward to passive Observers. Observers do not talk back to the Subject to route messages to other Observers.\\n* **Mediator Pattern (Routing Hub):** Used for **Many-to-Many** bidirectional communication. The Mediator itself contains no domain state; it is purely a traffic router. Active Colleague objects intentionally ask the Mediator to route complex messages and commands to specific other Colleague objects. It replaces a spaghetti web of interactions with a star-topology hub.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Structurally, the Mediator and Observer patterns both use centralized lists to notify objects. Architecturally, what 