esponsibility Principle (SRP)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v35-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Real-World Use Cases for Mediator", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Complex GUI Dialog Windows:**\\nConsider an Airplane Booking UI containing a DatePicker, a \\"Return Flight\\" Checkbox, and a \\"Submit\\" Button.\\n* If the User unchecks \\"Return Flight\\", the DatePicker for the return date must be disabled.\\n* If the DatePicker is empty, the \\"Submit\\" Button must be disabled.\\n\\nIf the Checkbox object has hardcoded pointers to the DatePicker and Button objects to update their states, the UI components become hopelessly entangled $O(N^2)$.\\nInstead, the `DialogWindow` acts as the Mediator. When the Checkbox is toggled, it simply tells the Mediator `mediator->notify(this, \\"TOGGLED\\")`. The Mediator contains the localized logic to disable the DatePicker and Button, keeping the raw UI components entirely decoupled and reusable.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Beyond Chat Rooms, identify and explain a common real-world UI scenario where the Mediator pattern is essential for managing complexity.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
4220	Ordered Set Pruning: Dijkstra's Shortest Path Algorithm Using std::set (Part 2)	dsa-dijkstras-algorithm-using-set	Implement Dijkstra's shortest path algorithm using std::set with active stale-entry deletion.	2026-10-03 11:01:59.931902+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Ordered Set Pruning: Dijkstra's Shortest Path Algorithm Using std::set (Part 2)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PATgNiuTP20", "title": "Lecture 158: Dijkstra's Algorithm - Using Set - Part 2", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/implementing-dijkstra-set-1-adjacency-matrix/1", "title": "Implementing Dijkstra Algorithm (Set)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Implement Dijkstra's algorithm using std::set to erase stale distance entries upon relaxation."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/design-graph-with-shortest-path-calculator/", "title": "Design Graph With Shortest Path Calculator (LC 2642)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Support dynamic edge additions and shortest-path queries using Dijkstra's algorithm."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/path-with-minimum-effort/", "title": "Path With Minimum Effort (LC 1631)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use Dijkstra's relaxation on a 2D grid to minimize the maximum absolute height difference."}]	Module 24: Shortest Path Algorithms in Graphs: DAG Relaxation, BFS & Dijkstra's Algorithm	### 📌 Topic Overview\nHow does implementing **Dijkstra's Algorithm using `std::set<pair<int, int>>`** differ from using a `priority_queue`? Because `std::set` keeps elements in sorted ascending order **and** supports `st.erase()`, we can actively delete outdated `{oldDist, v}` pairs whenever we find a shorter path to `v`!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Use a `set`?**\n  - In a `priority_queue`, if `dist[v]` improves from `10` to `8`, `{10, v}` stays in the heap as a stale entry alongside `{8, v}`.\n  - In a `set`, before updating `dist[v] = 8`, we check if `dist[v] != 1e9`: if so, we call **`st.erase({dist[v], v})`** to remove the outdated pair `{10, v}`, avoiding redundant future iterations!\n- **Does `set` Beat `priority_queue` Asymptotically?**\n  - No! `st.erase()` itself takes `O(log V)` time, and `std::set` has higher memory/pointer overhead than a contiguous vector-backed `priority_queue`. Both run in **`O(E log V)`** time.\n\n### 💻 Dijkstra's Algorithm (Using Set) Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> dijkstraUsingSet(int V, vector<vector<int>> adj[], int S) {\n    set<pair<int, int>> st; // Stores {distance, node} in ascending order\n    vector<int> dist(V, 1e9);\n\n    st.insert({0, S});\n    dist[S] = 0;\n\n    while (!st.empty()) {\n        auto it = *(st.begin());\n        int d = it.first;\n        int u = it.second;\n        st.erase(it);\n\n        for (const auto &edge : adj[u]) {\n            int v = edge[0];\n            int wt = edge[1];\n\n            if (d + wt < dist[v]) {\n                // If v was already visited with a larger distance, erase the stale entry\n                if (dist[v] != 1e9) {\n                    st.erase({dist[v], v});\n                }\n                dist[v] = d + wt;\n                st.insert({dist[v], v});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return dist;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Erasing After Updating `dist[v]`:** You must call `st.erase({dist[v], v})` **before** overwriting `dist[v] = d + wt`, because the set currently holds the *old* distance `dist[v]`!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
4222	Parent-Pointer Backtracking: Print Shortest Path in a Weighted Graph Using Dijkstra's Algorithm	dsa-print-shortest-path-dijkstras-algorithm	Reconstruct and return the exact vertex sequence of the shortest path between two nodes using parent tracking.	2026-10-03 11:01:59.944304+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Parent-Pointer Backtracking: Print Shortest Path in a Weighted Graph Using Dijkstra's Algorithm\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rp1SMw7HSO8", "title": "Lecture 160: Print Shortest Path - Dijkstra's Algorithm", "duration": "22m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/shortest-path-in-weighted-undirected-graph/1", "title": "Shortest Path in Weighted undirected graph", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Reconstruct and return the actual shortest path from vertex 1 to vertex N in a weighted undirected graph."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-ways-to-arrive-at-destination/", "title": "Number of Ways to Arrive at Destination (LC 1976)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Combine Dijkstra's relaxation with dynamic programming path counting."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-weighted-subgraph-with-the-required-paths/", "title": "Minimum Weighted Subgraph With the Required Paths (LC 2203)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Run Dijkstra from multiple sources to reconstruct optimal meeting paths."}]	Module 24: Shortest Path Algorithms in Graphs: DAG Relaxation, BFS & Dijkstra's Algorithm	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a weighted undirected graph with `N` vertices (`1` to `N`) and `M` edges, find and **reconstruct the actual sequence of vertices** along the shortest path from vertex `1` to vertex `N` (or return `{-1}` if vertex `N` is unreachable).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The `parent[]` Memoization Array:**\n  - How do we remember *where* the shortest path to each node came from?\n  - Create a `parent[N + 1]` array where initially every node is its own parent: **`parent[i] = i`**.\n  - During Dijkstra's relaxation, whenever **`d + wt < dist[v]`**:\n    1. Update `dist[v] = d + wt`.\n    2. Record **`parent[v] = u`** (remembering that the best path to `v` came directly from `u`)!\n- **Backtracking the Path From Destination `N` to Source `1`:**\n  - If `dist[N] == 1e9`, return `{-1}`.\n  - Start at `node = N`. While `parent[node] != node`:\n    - Push `node` into `path` and step backward: `node = parent[node]`.\n  - Push `1` into `path`, **reverse `path`**, and return it!\n- **Complexity:** `O(E log V + V)` Time and `O(V + E)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Print Shortest Path (Dijkstra + Parent Array) Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> shortestPath(int n, int m, vector<vector<int>> &edges) {\n    vector<pair<int, int>> adj[n + 1];\n    for (const auto &e : edges) {\n        adj[e[0]].push_back({e[1], e[2]});\n        adj[e[1]].push_back({e[0], e[2]});\n    }\n\n    priority_queue<pair<int, int>, vector<pair<int, int>>, greater<pair<int, int>>> pq;\n    vector<int> dist(n + 1, 1e9), parent(n + 1);\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) parent[i] = i;\n\n    dist[1] = 0;\n    pq.push({0, 1});\n\n    while (!pq.empty()) {\n        auto [d, u] = pq.top();\n        pq.pop();\n\n        for (auto [v, wt] : adj[u]) {\n            if (d + wt < dist[v]) {\n                dist[v] = d + wt;\n                pq.push({dist[v], v});\n                parent[v] = u; // Record where v came from along its shortest path\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (dist[n] == 1e9) return {-1};\n\n    // Reconstruct path backwards from n to 1\n    vector<int> path;\n    int node = n;\n    while (parent[node] != node) {\n        path.push_back(node);\n        node = parent[node];\n    }\n    path.push_back(1);\n    reverse(path.begin(), path.end());\n    return path;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting to Push the Source Vertex `1` After the `while (parent[node] != node)` Loop:** Because `parent[1] == 1`, the `while` loop terminates as soon as `node` reaches `1`. Always call `path.push_back(1)` after the loop before reversing!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22203	Installments - Compound Interest	aptitude-quant-installments-compound-interest	Master EMI calculations under Compound Interest using the proportional growth factor method, avoiding complex formula memorization.	2026-10-07 12:07:56.264899+05:30	### 📌 1. The Core EMI Concept (Compound Interest)\n* When repaying a loan in equal installments, each payment effectively repays a portion of the principal along with the accumulated interest on that specific portion.\n* Instead of memorizing the complex standard formula `P = x/(1+R/100) + x/(1+R/100)^2 + ...`, use the **Growth Factor Ratio Method**.\n\n### 💡 2. The Growth Factor Ratio Method (No-Formula Shortcut)\n* **Step 1:** Find the Growth Factor. For a 10% rate, ₹10 grows to ₹11. Growth Factor = `11/10`.\n* **Step 2:** Write out the powers for the number of years. For 2 years, write `10 → 11` and `100 → 121`.\n* **Step 3:** Make the final values (installments) equal. Multiply the first equation by 11: `110 → 121` and `100 → 121`.\n* **Step 4:** Sum the left side to find the "Reference Loan". `110 + 100 = 210`. \n  * *Meaning:* A loan of **₹210** requires two equal installments of **₹121** each.\n* **Step 5:** Scale up! If your actual loan is ₹420 (which is 2 × 210), your actual installment is simply `121 × 2 = ₹242`.\n\n### 💡 3. Important Concepts\n* **Down Payment:** Always subtract the down payment from the total cash price *before* applying installment logic. The remaining amount is your True Principal (Loan).\n* **Semi-Annual Compounding:** If installments are paid half-yearly, halve the rate (`R/2`) and double the time periods (`2N`) before calculating the growth factor.\n* **Total Interest Paid:** `Total Interest = (Number of Installments × Value of 1 Installment) - Loan Amount`.	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xxWwphuwOBs", "type": "youtube", "title": "Installments - Shortcuts & Tricks for 2026 - 2027 Placement Tests, Job Interviews & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-ci-inst-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Standard 2-Year Installment] Rahul borrows ₹58,800 to be paid back in two equal annual installments at 10% p.a. compound interest. Value of each installment?", "options": ["A) ₹30,000", "B) ₹32,480", "C) ₹33,880", "D) ₹35,280"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Rahul borrows a sum of ₹58,800 from a bank. This is to be completely paid back in exactly two equal annual installments. If the rate of interest is 10% per annum compounded annually, what is the exact value of each installment?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "At 10% CI, the growth factor is 11/10. Year 1: 10 → 11. Year 2: 100 → 121. To equalize installments to 121, multiply Year 1 by 11: 110 → 121. Total reference loan = 110 + 100 = 210. Reference installment = 121. Actual loan is 58,800. Multiplier = 58800 / 210 = 280. Actual installment = 121 × 280 = ₹33,880.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [3-Year Installment with 20% Rate] A sum of ₹68,250 is borrowed at 20% p.a. CI and paid back in 3 equal annual installments. Amount of each installment?", "options": ["A) ₹30,000", "B) ₹32,400", "C) ₹34,560", "D) ₹36,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A sum of ₹68,250 is borrowed at 20% per annum compound interest and paid back in exactly three equal annual installments. What is the exact mathematical amount of each installment?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "At 20% CI, the growth factor is 6/5. Years 1, 2, 3: 5→6, 25→36, 125→216. Equalize to 216: multiply Y1 by 36 (180→216), Y2 by 6 (150→216), Y3 is 125→216. Reference loan = 180 + 150 + 125 = 455. Reference installment = 216. Actual loan = 68250. Multiplier = 68250 / 455 = 150. Actual installment = 216 × 150 = ₹32,400.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Finding Principal from Installment] A loan has to be returned in two equal annual installments of ₹44,100 each. If rate is 5% p.a. CI, find total interest paid.", "options": ["A) ₹4,200", "B) ₹5,000", "C) ₹6,200", "D) ₹8,200"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A loan has to be returned in two equal annual installments, each of exactly ₹44,100. If the rate of interest is 5% per annum compounded annually, what is the exact total interest paid over the entire transaction?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "At 5% CI, growth factor is 21/20. Y1: 20→21. Y2: 400→441. Equalize to 441: Y1 becomes 420→441. Reference loan = 420 + 400 = 820. Reference installment = 441. Actual installment = 44,100. Multiplier = 44100 / 441 = 100. Actual loan = 820 × 100 = 82,000. Total amount paid = 2 × 44,100 = 88,200. Total interest paid = 88,200 - 82,000 = ₹6,200.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Down Payment Deduction] A bike costs ₹79,860 cash. It can be bought with ₹60,000 down payment and 3 equal annual installments at 10% CI. Value of installment?", "options": ["A) ₹6,655", "B) ₹7,260", "C) ₹7,986", "D) ₹8,450"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A bike can be bought for exactly ₹79,860 in cash, or by paying ₹60,000 as an immediate down payment and the remaining amount in exactly 3 equal annual installments at an interest rate of 10% per annum compounded annually. What will be the exact value of each installment?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Loan amount = Cash Price - Down Payment = 79860 - 60000 = 19,860. At 10% CI for 3 years, reference loan is 3310 for an installment of 1331. Actual loan is 19860. Multiplier = 19860 / 3310 = 6. Actual installment = 1331 × 6 = ₹7,986.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Semi-Annual Installments] Bike cash price is ₹73,490. Down payment is ₹23,840. The rest is paid in 3 equal HALF-YEARLY installments at 20% p.a. compounded semi-annually. Installment value?", "options": ["A) ₹18,500", "B) ₹19,965", "C) ₹21,200", "D) ₹23,840"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Mohan can buy a bike for ₹73,490 in cash, or by paying ₹23,840 as down payment and the rest in exactly three equal half-yearly installments. If the interest rate charged is 20% per annum compounded semi-annually, what is the exact value of each half-yearly installment?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Loan = 73490 - 23840 = 49,650. Rate is 20% p.a. = 10% per half-year. Number of periods = 3 half-years. At 10% for 3 periods, reference loan is 3310 for an installment of 1331. Actual loan is 49650. Multiplier = 49650 / 3310 = 15. Actual installment = 1331 × 15 = ₹19,965.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Finding Principal from Total Interest Paid] A sum is paid back in two equal installments at 12.5% p.a. CI. If total interest paid is ₹7,800, find the principal.", "options": ["A) ₹36,400", "B) ₹38,600", "C) ₹40,800", "D) ₹44,800"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Rea borrows a certain sum of money from the bank and promises to pay it back in exactly two equal annual installments at a rate of 12.5% per annum compounded annually. If she pays a total absolute interest of exactly ₹7,800, what was the original principal amount borrowed?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Rate = 12.5% = 1/8. Growth factor = 9/8. Y1: 8→9. Y2: 64→81. Equalize to 81: Y1 becomes 72→81. Reference loan = 72 + 64 = 136. Reference installment = 81. Total paid = 2 × 81 = 162. Reference interest = 162 - 136 = 26. Actual interest is 7800. Multiplier = 7800 / 26 = 300. Actual Principal = 136 × 300 = ₹40,800.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Quarterly Installments] A loan is paid in 2 equal quarterly installments at 40% p.a. compounded quarterly. If each installment is ₹14,520, find the principal.", "options": ["A) ₹25,200", "B) ₹26,000", "C) ₹26,400", "D) ₹27,500"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A loan is paid back in exactly 2 equal quarterly installments at a nominal interest rate of 40% per annum compounded quarterly. If the exact mathematical value of each quarterly installment is ₹14,520, what was the original loan amount?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Nominal rate = 40% p.a. -> Quarterly rate = 10%. Growth factor = 11/10. Y1: 10→11. Y2: 100→121. Equalize: 110→121. Reference loan = 210. Reference installment = 121. Actual installment = 14520. Multiplier = 14520 / 121 = 120. Actual loan = 210 × 120 = ₹25,200.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Three Installments with 5% Rate] A man repays a loan of ₹25,220 in 3 equal annual installments at 5% p.a. compound interest. Find the value of each installment.", "options": ["A) ₹8,820", "B) ₹9,261", "C) ₹9,600", "D) ₹10,500"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man strictly borrows a loan of ₹25,220 and agrees to repay it in exactly 3 equal annual installments at 5% per annum compound interest. What is the exact mathematical value of each installment?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Rate = 5% = 1/20. Growth factor = 21/20. Powers: 20→21, 400→441, 8000→9261. Equalize to 9261: Y1 is 20×441=8820. Y2 is 400×21=8400. Y3 is 8000. Reference loan = 8820 + 8400 + 8000 = 25,220. Reference installment = 9261. Since the actual loan perfectly matches the reference loan (25220), the actual installment is exactly ₹9,261.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Difference in Total Payment Cash vs Installment] A TV costs ₹21,000 cash. If paid in 2 equal annual installments at 10% p.a. CI, how much extra is paid?", "options": ["A) ₹2,100", "B) ₹2,520", "C) ₹3,200", "D) ₹4,200"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A smart TV is available for ₹21,000 in direct cash. A customer opts to buy it entirely on credit, paying it off in exactly 2 equal annual installments at 10% per annum compound interest. Exactly how much extra money (total interest) does the customer end up paying compared to the cash price?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For 10% CI over 2 years, reference loan is 210 for an installment of 121. Actual loan is 21000. Multiplier = 21000 / 210 = 100. Actual installment = 121 × 100 = 12,100. Total amount paid = 2 × 12100 = 24,200. Extra money paid = 24200 - 21000 = ₹3,200.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Non-Equal Installments] A loan of ₹8,000 is to be repaid in two annual installments. The second installment is twice the first. Rate is 20% p.a. CI. Find the first installment.", "options": ["A) ₹3,600", "B) ₹4,000", "C) ₹4,500", "D) ₹5,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A loan of ₹8,000 is to be completely repaid in two annual installments at 20% per annum compound interest. The agreement dictates that the numerical value of the second installment must be exactly twice the value of the first installment. What is the exact value of the first installment?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let first installment be x, second be 2x. Present value equation: 8000 = x / 1.2 + 2x / 1.44 = 5x/6 + 50x/36 = 80x/36 = 20x/9. 8000 = 20x / 9 => 20x = 72000 => x = ₹3,600.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Finding Original Debt from Paid Installments] A man paid 3 equal annual installments of ₹10,648 each at 10% p.a. CI to clear his debt. Find the principal.", "options": ["A) ₹26,480", "B) ₹27,000", "C) ₹28,500", "D) ₹30,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "To completely clear a debt, a man pays exactly 3 equal annual installments of exactly ₹10,648 each. If the rate of interest charged was 10% per annum compounded annually, what was the exact original principal borrowed?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "At 10% CI for 3 years, reference installment is 1331 for a loan of 3310. The actual installment is 10648. Multiplier = 10648 / 1331 = 8. Actual loan = 3310 × 8 = ₹26,480.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Two Installments with 15% Rate] A sum of ₹25,800 is borrowed at 15% p.a. CI. Paid back in 2 equal annual installments. Find installment.", "options": ["A) ₹15,870", "B) ₹16,000", "C) ₹17,192", "D) ₹18,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A sum of exactly ₹25,800 is safely borrowed at 15% per annum compound interest. It is completely paid back in exactly 2 equal annual installments. What is the mathematical amount of each installment?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Rate = 15% = 3/20. Growth factor = 23/20. Y1: 20→23. Y2: 400→529. Equalize: Y1 becomes 460→529. Reference loan = 460 + 400 = 860. Reference installment = 529. Actual loan = 25800. Multiplier = 25800 / 860 = 30. Actual installment = 529 × 30 = ₹15,870.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Percentage of Loan as Installment] At 25% p.a. CI for 2 years, what approximate percentage of the principal is each installment?", "options": ["A) 55.5%", "B) 60.9%", "C) 62.5%", "D) 69.4%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a loan is completely paid back in exactly 2 equal annual installments at an interest rate of 25% per annum compounded annually, each exact installment represents approximately what percentage of the original loan amount?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Rate = 25% = 1/4. Growth factor = 5/4. Y1: 4→5. Y2: 16→25. Equalize: Y1 becomes 20→25. Reference loan = 20 + 16 = 36. Reference installment = 25. Percentage = (Installment / Loan) × 100 = (25 / 36) × 100 = 2500 / 36 = 625 / 9 ≈ 69.44%.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Down Payment and Missing Principal] A phone is bought for ₹4,000 cash down payment and 2 equal annual installments of ₹5,760 each at 20% CI. Cash price?", "options": ["A) ₹12,000", "B) ₹12,800", "C) ₹13,000", "D) ₹14,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A premium mobile phone is bought by paying exactly ₹4,000 as an immediate cash down payment, followed by exactly 2 equal annual installments of ₹5,760 each. If the rate of interest charged on the remaining balance is 20% per annum compounded annually, what is the true total cash price of the phone?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Rate = 20% = 1/5. Growth factor = 6/5. Reference loan for 2 years = (5/6) + (25/36) = 30/36 + 25/36 = 55/36 of installment. Here, installment is 5760. Loan = 5760 × (55/36). 5760 / 36 = 160. Loan = 160 × 55 = 8800. Cash Price = Down Payment + Loan = 4000 + 8800 = ₹12,800.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Finding Rate given Loan and Installments] A loan of ₹6,800 is discharged in two equal annual installments of ₹4,050 each. Find the rate of interest compounded annually.", "options": ["A) 10.00%", "B) 12.50%", "C) 15.00%", "D) 20.00%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A loan of exactly ₹6,800 is completely discharged in exactly two equal annual installments of ₹4,050 each. What is the exact mathematical rate of interest per annum compounded annually?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let R% be the rate and r = 1 + R/100. P = x/r + x/r^2 => 6800 = 4050/r + 4050/r^2. Divide by 50: 136 = 81/r + 81/r^2. Multiply by r^2: 136r^2 - 81r - 81 = 0. Solving this quadratic for positive r gives r = 9/8. Since r = 1 + R/100 = 9/8, R/100 = 1/8. Therefore, R = 12.5%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Two Equal Installments Difference in Principal] ₹25,500 is borrowed at 4% CI to be repaid in two annual installments. Value of installment?", "options": ["A) ₹13,520", "B) ₹13,750", "C) ₹14,000", "D) ₹14,280"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly ₹25,500 is borrowed at 4% per annum compound interest and is to be entirely repaid in exactly two equal annual installments. What is the exact value of each installment?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Rate = 4% = 1/25. Growth factor = 26/25. Y1: 25→26. Y2: 625→676. Equalize: 650→676. Reference loan = 650 + 625 = 1275. Reference installment = 676. Actual loan = 25500. Multiplier = 25500 / 1275 = 20. Actual installment = 676 × 20 = ₹13,520.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Four Installments Calculation] A man borrows ₹16,400 at 5% CI p.a. to be paid in 4 equal annual installments. The closest installment value?", "options": ["A) ₹4,200", "B) ₹4,625", "C) ₹4,800", "D) ₹5,125"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man borrows exactly ₹16,400 at 5% per annum compound interest, completely payable in exactly 4 equal annual installments. What is the closest integer value of each installment?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Rate = 5% = 1/20. Growth factor = 21/20 = 1.05. Loan = x / 1.05 + x / 1.05^2 + x / 1.05^3 + x / 1.05^4 = x (0.95238 + 0.90703 + 0.86384 + 0.82270) = x (3.54595). 16400 = 3.54595x => x = 16400 / 3.54595 ≈ ₹4,625.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Interest Portion of First Installment] A loan is repaid in 2 equal installments of ₹1,452 each at 10% p.a. CI. How much of the FIRST installment is purely principal?", "options": ["A) ₹1,200", "B) ₹1,320", "C) ₹1,350", "D) ₹1,452"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A loan is mathematically repaid in exactly 2 equal annual installments of exactly ₹1,452 each at 10% per annum compound interest. In the very first installment paid at the end of Year 1, exactly what absolute monetary amount is strictly applied towards reducing the Principal?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Total loan = 1452/1.1 + 1452/1.21 = 1320 + 1200 = 2520. At the end of Year 1, the total interest accrued on the entire 2520 loan is 10% of 2520 = ₹252. The first installment is ₹1,452. The amount applied to principal = Installment - Interest = 1452 - 252 = ₹1,200.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Difference Between Total Payment and Loan] Loan = ₹7,620. 3 equal installments at 16 2/3% p.a. CI. Extra amount paid?", "options": ["A) ₹2,320", "B) ₹2,450", "C) ₹2,670", "D) ₹3,200"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A person borrows exactly ₹7,620 and completely repays it in exactly 3 equal annual installments at exactly 16 2/3 % per annum compound interest. Exactly how much total aggregate interest is paid over the life of the loan?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Rate = 16 2/3% = 1/6. Growth factor = 7/6. Y1: 6→7, Y2: 36→49, Y3: 216→343. Equalize to 343: Y1 is 294, Y2 is 252, Y3 is 216. Reference loan = 294 + 252 + 216 = 762. Actual loan = 7620. Multiplier = 10. Actual installment = 3430. Total paid = 3 × 3430 = 10,290. Total interest paid = 10,290 - 7,620 = ₹2,670.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Equalizing Different Rates per Year] A loan of ₹3,000 is to be paid in 2 equal installments. Rate for 1st year is 10%, 2nd year is 20%. First installment value?", "options": ["A) ₹1,650", "B) ₹1,716", "C) ₹1,800", "D) ₹1,920"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A mathematical loan of exactly ₹3,000 is to be entirely repaid in exactly 2 equal annual installments. The interest rate charged is 10% strictly for the first year, and 20% strictly for the second year. What must be the exact value of each installment?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let installment be x. Present value of first installment (discounted by 10%) = x / 1.1. Present value of second installment (discounted by 10% then 20%) = x / (1.1 × 1.2) = x / 1.32. Total Loan = x/1.1 + x/1.32 = 3000. Find common denominator 1.32: (1.2x + x) / 1.32 = 3000 => 2.2x = 3000 × 1.32 = 3960 => x = 3960 / 2.2 = ₹1,800.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. The Core EMI Concept (Compound Interest)\n* When repaying a loan in equal installments, each payment effectively repays a portion of the principal along with the accumulated interest on that specific portion.\n* Instead of memorizing the complex standard formula `P = x/(1+R/100) + x/(1+R/100)^2 + ...`, use the **Growth Factor Ratio Method**.\n\n### 💡 2. The Growth Factor Ratio Method (No-Formula Shortcut)\n* **Step 1:** Find the Growth Factor. For a 10% rate, ₹10 grows to ₹11. Growth Factor = `11/10`.\n* **Step 2:** Write out the powers for the number of years. For 2 years, write `10 → 11` and `100 → 121`.\n* **Step 3:** Make the final values (installments) equal. Multiply the first equation by 11: `110 → 121` and `100 → 121`.\n* **Step 4:** Sum the left side to find the "Reference Loan". `110 + 100 = 210`. \n  * *Meaning:* A loan of **₹210** requires two equal installments of **₹121** each.\n* **Step 5:** Scale up! If your actual loan is ₹420 (which is 2 × 210), your actual installment is simply `121 × 2 = ₹242`.\n\n### 💡 3. Important Concepts\n* **Down Payment:** Always subtract the down payment from the total cash price *before* applying installment logic. The remaining amount is your True Principal (Loan).\n* **Semi-Annual Compounding:** If installments are paid half-yearly, halve the rate (`R/2`) and double the time periods (`2N`) before calculating the growth factor.\n* **Total Interest Paid:** `Total Interest = (Number of Installments × Value of 1 Installment) - Loan Amount`.	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Installments - Compound Interest	Master EMI calculations under Compound Interest using the proportional growth factor method, avoiding complex formula memorization.	56	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	37	[{"id": "apt-ci-inst-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Standard 2-Year Installment] Rahul borrows ₹58,800 to be paid back in two equal annual installments at 10% p.a. compound interest. Value of each installment?", "options": ["A) ₹30,000", "B) ₹32,480", "C) ₹33,880", "D) ₹35,280"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Rahul borrows a sum of ₹58,800 from a bank. This is to be completely paid back in exactly two equal annual installments. If the rate of interest is 10% per annum compounded annually, what is the exact value of each installment?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "At 10% CI, the growth factor is 11/10. Year 1: 10 → 11. Year 2: 100 → 121. To equalize installments to 121, multiply Year 1 by 11: 110 → 121. Total reference loan = 110 + 100 = 210. Reference installment = 121. Actual loan is 58,800. Multiplier = 58800 / 210 = 280. Actual installment = 121 × 280 = ₹33,880.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [3-Year Installment with 20% Rate] A sum of ₹68,250 is borrowed at 20% p.a. CI and paid back in 3 equal annual installments. Amount of each installment?", "options": ["A) ₹30,000", "B) ₹32,400", "C) ₹34,560", "D) ₹36,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A sum of ₹68,250 is borrowed at 20% per annum compound interest and paid back in exactly three equal annual installments. What is the exact mathematical amount of each installment?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "At 20% CI, the growth factor is 6/5. Years 1, 2, 3: 5→6, 25→36, 125→216. Equalize to 216: multiply Y1 by 36 (180→216), Y2 by 6 (150→216), Y3 is 125→216. Reference loan = 180 + 150 + 125 = 455. Reference installment = 216. Actual loan = 68250. Multiplier = 68250 / 455 = 150. Actual installment = 216 × 150 = ₹32,400.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Finding Principal from Installment] A loan has to be returned in two equal annual installments of ₹44,100 each. If rate is 5% p.a. CI, find total interest paid.", "options": ["A) ₹4,200", "B) ₹5,000", "C) ₹6,200", "D) ₹8,200"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A loan has to be returned in two equal annual installments, each of exactly ₹44,100. If the rate of interest is 5% per annum compounded annually, what is the exact total interest paid over the entire transaction?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "At 5% CI, growth factor is 21/20. Y1: 20→21. Y2: 400→441. Equalize to 441: Y1 becomes 420→441. Reference loan = 420 + 400 = 820. Reference installment = 441. Actual installment = 44,100. Multiplier = 44100 / 441 = 100. Actual loan = 820 × 100 = 82,000. Total amount paid = 2 × 44,100 = 88,200. Total interest paid = 88,200 - 82,000 = ₹6,200.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Down Payment Deduction] A bike costs ₹79,860 cash. It can be bought with ₹60,000 down payment and 3 equal annual installments at 10% CI. Value of installment?", "options": ["A) ₹6,655", "B) ₹7,260", "C) ₹7,986", "D) ₹8,450"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A bike can be bought for exactly ₹79,860 in cash, or by paying ₹60,000 as an immediate down payment and the remaining amount in exactly 3 equal annual installments at an interest rate of 10% per annum compounded annually. What will be the exact value of each installment?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Loan amount = Cash Price - Down Payment = 79860 - 60000 = 19,860. At 10% CI for 3 years, reference loan is 3310 for an installment of 1331. Actual loan is 19860. Multiplier = 19860 / 3310 = 6. Actual installment = 1331 × 6 = ₹7,986.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Semi-Annual Installments] Bike cash price is ₹73,490. Down payment is ₹23,840. The rest is paid in 3 equal HALF-YEARLY installments at 20% p.a. compounded semi-annually. Installment value?", "options": ["A) ₹18,500", "B) ₹19,965", "C) ₹21,200", "D) ₹23,840"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Mohan can buy a bike for ₹73,490 in cash, or by paying ₹23,840 as down payment and the rest in exactly three equal half-yearly installments. If the interest rate charged is 20% per annum compounded semi-annually, what is the exact value of each half-yearly installment?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Loan = 73490 - 23840 = 49,650. Rate is 20% p.a. = 10% per half-year. Number of periods = 3 half-years. At 10% for 3 periods, reference loan is 3310 for an installment of 1331. Actual loan is 49650. Multiplier = 49650 / 3310 = 15. Actual installment = 1331 × 15 = ₹19,965.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Finding Principal from Total Interest Paid] A sum is paid back in two equal installments at 12.5% p.a. CI. If total interest paid is ₹7,800, find the principal.", "options": ["A) ₹36,400", "B) ₹38,600", "C) ₹40,800", "D) ₹44,800"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Rea borrows a certain sum of money from the bank and promises to pay it back in exactly two equal annual installments at a rate of 12.5% per annum compounded annually. If she pays a total absolute interest of exactly ₹7,800, what was the original principal amount borrowed?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Rate = 12.5% = 1/8. Growth factor = 9/8. Y1: 8→9. Y2: 64→81. Equalize to 81: Y1 becomes 72→81. Reference loan = 72 + 64 = 136. Reference installment = 81. Total paid = 2 × 81 = 162. Reference interest = 162 - 136 = 26. Actual interest is 7800. Multiplier = 7800 / 26 = 300. Actual Principal = 136 × 300 = ₹40,800.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Quarterly Installments] A loan is paid in 2 equal quarterly installments at 40% p.a. compounded quarterly. If each installment is ₹14,520, find the principal.", "options": ["A) ₹25,200", "B) ₹26,000", "C) ₹26,400", "D) ₹27,500"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A loan is paid back in exactly 2 equal quarterly installments at a nominal interest rate of 40% per annum compounded quarterly. If the exact mathematical value of each quarterly installment is ₹14,520, what was the original loan amount?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Nominal rate = 40% p.a. -> Quarterly rate = 10%. Growth factor = 11/10. Y1: 10→11. Y2: 100→121. Equalize: 110→121. Reference loan = 210. Reference installment = 121. Actual installment = 14520. Multiplier = 14520 / 121 = 120. Actual loan = 210 × 120 = ₹25,200.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Three Installments with 5% Rate] A man repays a loan of ₹25,220 in 3 equal annual installments at 5% p.a. compound interest. Find the value of each installment.", "options": ["A) ₹8,820", "B) ₹9,261", "C) ₹9,600", "D) ₹10,500"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man strictly borrows a loan of ₹25,220 and agrees to repay it in exactly 3 equal annual installments at 5% per annum compound interest. What is the exact mathematical value of each installment?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Rate = 5% = 1/20. Growth factor = 21/20. Powers: 20→21, 400→441, 8000→9261. Equalize to 9261: Y1 is 20×441=8820. Y2 is 400×21=8400. Y3 is 8000. Reference loan = 8820 + 8400 + 8000 = 25,220. Reference installment = 9261. Since the actual loan perfectly matches the reference loan (25220), the actual installment is exactly ₹9,261.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Difference in Total Payment Cash vs Installment] A TV costs ₹21,000 cash. If paid in 2 equal annual installments at 10% p.a. CI, how much extra is paid?", "options": ["A) ₹2,100", "B) ₹2,520", "C) ₹3,200", "D) ₹4,200"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A smart TV is available for ₹21,000 in direct cash. A customer opts to buy it entirely on credit, paying it off in exactly 2 equal annual installments at 10% per annum compound interest. Exactly how much extra money (total interest) does the customer end up paying compared to the cash price?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For 10% CI over 2 years, reference loan is 210 for an installment of 121. Actual loan is 21000. Multiplier = 21000 / 210 = 100. Actual installment = 121 × 100 = 12,100. Total amount paid = 2 × 12100 = 24,200. Extra money paid = 24200 - 21000 = ₹3,200.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Non-Equal Installments] A loan of ₹8,000 is to be repaid in two annual installments. The second installment is twice the first. Rate is 20% p.a. CI. Find the first installment.", "options": ["A) ₹3,600", "B) ₹4,000", "C) ₹4,500", "D) ₹5,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A loan of ₹8,000 is to be completely repaid in two annual installments at 20% per annum compound interest. The agreement dictates that the numerical value of the second installment must be exactly twice the value of the first installment. What is the exact value of the first installment?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let first installment be x, second be 2x. Present value equation: 8000 = x / 1.2 + 2x / 1.44 = 5x/6 + 50x/36 = 80x/36 = 20x/9. 8000 = 20x / 9 => 20x = 72000 => x = ₹3,600.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Finding Original Debt from Paid Installments] A man paid 3 equal annual installments of ₹10,648 each at 10% p.a. CI to clear his debt. Find the principal.", "options": ["A) ₹26,480", "B) ₹27,000", "C) ₹28,500", "D) ₹30,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "To completely clear a debt, a man pays exactly 3 equal annual installments of exactly ₹10,648 each. If the rate of interest charged was 10% per annum compounded annually, what was the exact original principal borrowed?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "At 10% CI for 3 years, reference installment is 1331 for a loan of 3310. The actual installment is 10648. Multiplier = 10648 / 1331 = 8. Actual loan = 3310 × 8 = ₹26,480.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Two Installments with 15% Rate] A sum of ₹25,800 is borrowed at 15% p.a. CI. Paid back in 2 equal annual installments. Find installment.", "options": ["A) ₹15,870", "B) ₹16,000", "C) ₹17,192", "D) ₹18,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A sum of exactly ₹25,800 is safely borrowed at 15% per annum compound interest. It is completely paid back in exactly 2 equal annual installments. What is the mathematical amount of each installment?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Rate = 15% = 3/20. Growth factor = 23/20. Y1: 20→23. Y2: 400→529. Equalize: Y1 becomes 460→529. Reference loan = 460 + 400 = 860. Reference installment = 529. Actual loan = 25800. Multiplier = 25800 / 860 = 30. Actual installment = 529 × 30 = ₹15,870.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Percentage of Loan as Installment] At 25% p.a. CI for 2 years, what approximate percentage of the principal is each installment?", "options": ["A) 55.5%", "B) 60.9%", "C) 62.5%", "D) 69.4%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a loan is completely paid back in exactly 2 equal annual installments at an interest rate of 25% per annum compounded annually, each exact installment represents approximately what percentage of the original loan amount?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Rate = 25% = 1/4. Growth factor = 5/4. Y1: 4→5. Y2: 16→25. Equalize: Y1 becomes 20→25. Reference loan = 20 + 16 = 36. Reference installment = 25. Percentage = (Installment / Loan) × 100 = (25 / 36) × 100 = 2500 / 36 = 625 / 9 ≈ 69.44%.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Down Payment and Missing Principal] A phone is bought for ₹4,000 cash down payment and 2 equal annual installments of ₹5,760 each at 20% CI. Cash price?", "options": ["A) ₹12,000", "B) ₹12,800", "C) ₹13,000", "D) ₹14,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A premium mobile phone is bought by paying exactly ₹4,000 as an immediate cash down payment, followed by exactly 2 equal annual installments of ₹5,760 each. If the rate of interest charged on the remaining balance is 20% per annum compounded annually, what is the true total cash price of the phone?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Rate = 20% = 1/5. Growth factor = 6/5. Reference loan for 2 years = (5/6) + (25/36) = 30/36 + 25/36 = 55/36 of installment. Here, installment is 5760. Loan = 5760 × (55/36). 5760 / 36 = 160. Loan = 160 × 55 = 8800. Cash Price = Down Payment + Loan = 4000 + 8800 = ₹12,800.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Finding Rate given Loan and Installments] A loan of ₹6,800 is discharged in two equal annual installments of ₹4,050 each. Find the rate of interest compounded annually.", "options": ["A) 10.00%", "B) 12.50%", "C) 15.00%", "D) 20.00%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A loan of exactly ₹6,800 is completely discharged in exactly two equal annual installments of ₹4,050 each. What is the exact mathematical rate of interest per annum compounded annually?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let R% be the rate and r = 1 + R/100. P = x/r + x/r^2 => 6800 = 4050/r + 4050/r^2. Divide by 50: 136 = 81/r + 81/r^2. Multiply by r^2: 136r^2 - 81r - 81 = 0. Solving this quadratic for positive r gives r = 9/8. Since r = 1 + R/100 = 9/8, R/100 = 1/8. Therefore, R = 12.5%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Two Equal Installments Difference in Principal] ₹25,500 is borrowed at 4% CI to be repaid in two annual installments. Value of installment?", "options": ["A) ₹13,520", "B) ₹13,750", "C) ₹14,000", "D) ₹14,280"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Exactly ₹25,500 is borrowed at 4% per annum compound interest and is to be entirely repaid in exactly two equal annual installments. What is the exact value of each installment?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Rate = 4% = 1/25. Growth factor = 26/25. Y1: 25→26. Y2: 625→676. Equalize: 650→676. Reference loan = 650 + 625 = 1275. Reference installment = 676. Actual loan = 25500. Multiplier = 25500 / 1275 = 20. Actual installment = 676 × 20 = ₹13,520.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Four Installments Calculation] A man borrows ₹16,400 at 5% CI p.a. to be paid in 4 equal annual installments. The closest installment value?", "options": ["A) ₹4,200", "B) ₹4,625", "C) ₹4,800", "D) ₹5,125"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man borrows exactly ₹16,400 at 5% per annum compound interest, completely payable in exactly 4 equal annual installments. What is the closest integer value of each installment?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Rate = 5% = 1/20. Growth factor = 21/20 = 1.05. Loan = x / 1.05 + x / 1.05^2 + x / 1.05^3 + x / 1.05^4 = x (0.95238 + 0.90703 + 0.86384 + 0.82270) = x (3.54595). 16400 = 3.54595x => x = 16400 / 3.54595 ≈ ₹4,625.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Interest Portion of First Installment] A loan is repaid in 2 equal installments of ₹1,452 each at 10% p.a. CI. How much of the FIRST installment is purely principal?", "options": ["A) ₹1,200", "B) ₹1,320", "C) ₹1,350", "D) ₹1,452"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A loan is mathematically repaid in exactly 2 equal annual installments of exactly ₹1,452 each at 10% per annum compound interest. In the very first installment paid at the end of Year 1, exactly what absolute monetary amount is strictly applied towards reducing the Principal?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Total loan = 1452/1.1 + 1452/1.21 = 1320 + 1200 = 2520. At the end of Year 1, the total interest accrued on the entire 2520 loan is 10% of 2520 = ₹252. The first installment is ₹1,452. The amount applied to principal = Installment - Interest = 1452 - 252 = ₹1,200.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Difference Between Total Payment and Loan] Loan = ₹7,620. 3 equal installments at 16 2/3% p.a. CI. Extra amount paid?", "options": ["A) ₹2,320", "B) ₹2,450", "C) ₹2,670", "D) ₹3,200"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A person borrows exactly ₹7,620 and completely repays it in exactly 3 equal annual installments at exactly 16 2/3 % per annum compound interest. Exactly how much total aggregate interest is paid over the life of the loan?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Rate = 16 2/3% = 1/6. Growth factor = 7/6. Y1: 6→7, Y2: 36→49, Y3: 216→343. Equalize to 343: Y1 is 294, Y2 is 252, Y3 is 216. Reference loan = 294 + 252 + 216 = 762. Actual loan = 7620. Multiplier = 10. Actual installment = 3430. Total paid = 3 × 3430 = 10,290. Total interest paid = 10,290 - 7,620 = ₹2,670.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-ci-inst-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Equalizing Different Rates per Year] A loan of ₹3,000 is to be paid in 2 equal installments. Rate for 1st year is 10%, 2nd year is 20%. First installment value?", "options": ["A) ₹1,650", "B) ₹1,716", "C) ₹1,800", "D) ₹1,920"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A mathematical loan of exactly ₹3,000 is to be entirely repaid in exactly 2 equal annual installments. The interest rate charged is 10% strictly for the first year, and 20% strictly for the second year. What must be the exact value of each installment?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Let installment be x. Present value of first installment (discounted by 10%) = x / 1.1. Present value of second installment (discounted by 10% then 20%) = x / (1.1 × 1.2) = x / 1.32. Total Loan = x/1.1 + x/1.32 = 3000. Find common denominator 1.32: (1.2x + x) / 1.32 = 3000 => 2.2x = 3000 × 1.32 = 3960 => x = 3960 / 2.2 = ₹1,800.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
7671	Post-Sale State Skipping (ind + 2): Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock With Cooldown	dsa-best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-with-cooldown	Implement a mandatory 1-day cooldown period after every stock sale by jumping to day ind + 2 upon selling in O(N) time and O(1) space.	2026-10-03 11:39:06.552935+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Post-Sale State Skipping (ind + 2): Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock With Cooldown\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IGIe46xw3YY", "title": "Lecture 219: Buy and Sell Stock With Cooldown (DP 39)", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-with-cooldown/", "title": "Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock with Cooldown (LC 309)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the maximum profit with unlimited transactions where after you sell your stock, you cannot buy stock on the next day (1-day cooldown)."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/buy-stock-with-cooldown/1", "title": "Buy Stock with Cooldown", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Maximize stock profit under a mandatory 1-day cooldown period after each sale."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/domino-and-tromino-tiling/", "title": "Domino and Tromino Tiling (LC 790)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Practice multi-step state machine DP transitions."}]	Module 32: Dynamic Programming on Stocks: State Machines, Transaction Caps, Cooldowns & Fees	### 📌 Topic Overview\nYou can complete unlimited stock transactions, **with one restriction**: after you **sell** your stock on day `ind`, you **cannot buy stock on the next day (`ind + 1`)** (a mandatory 1-day cooldown!). Find the maximum profit.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The 2-Character Modification From Stock II (`ind + 2` on Sell!):**\n  - Everything is identical to **Buy and Sell Stock II (DP 36)**, except when we **sell** on day `ind`:\n  - Because we cannot buy on `ind + 1`, after selling on day `ind` we simply jump directly to **`ind + 2`** with `buy = 1`:\n    **`dp[ind][0] = max(prices[ind] + dp[ind + 2][1], 0 + dp[ind + 1][0])`**!\n- **Base Case Adjustment (`ind >= N`):**\n  - Because `ind + 2` can jump from `N - 1` to `N + 1`, our base case is `if (ind >= N) return 0;`, and in tabulation we allocate **`dp(N + 2, vector<int>(2, 0))`**!\n- **3-Row Space Optimization (`O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Maintain `front1(2, 0)` (for `ind + 1`), `front2(2, 0)` (for `ind + 2`), and `cur(2, 0)`.\n\n### 💻 Buy and Sell Stock With Cooldown (O(1) Space) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint maxProfitWithCooldown(vector<int> &prices) {\n    int n = prices.size();\n    vector<int> front2(2, 0); // Represents dp[ind + 2]\n    vector<int> front1(2, 0); // Represents dp[ind + 1]\n    vector<int> cur(2, 0);    // Represents dp[ind]\n\n    for (int ind = n - 1; ind >= 0; ind--) {\n        // buy == 1: allowed to buy\n        cur[1] = max(-prices[ind] + front1[0], 0 + front1[1]);\n\n        // buy == 0: selling triggers 1-day cooldown -> jump to front2[1] (ind + 2)!\n        cur[0] = max(prices[ind] + front2[1], 0 + front1[0]);\n\n        front2 = front1;\n        front1 = cur;\n    }\n    return cur[1];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Allocating `dp(n + 1)` Instead of `dp(n + 2)` in Tabulation:** On day `ind = n - 1`, `dp[ind + 2][1]` accesses index **`n + 1`**! If you use a 2D DP table, always size it **`n + 2`**!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
8563	Coordinate-Shifted 2D State Recurrence: Longest Increasing Subsequence (Recursion & Memoization)	dsa-longest-increasing-subsequence-memoization	Find the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence using the 2D (ind, prev_ind) state and +1 coordinate shift memoization.	2026-10-03 11:44:14.756361+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Coordinate-Shifted 2D State Recurrence: Longest Increasing Subsequence (Recursion & Memoization)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ekcwMsSIzVc", "title": "Lecture 221: Longest Increasing Subsequence | Recursion to Tabulation (DP 41)", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-increasing-subsequence/", "title": "Longest Increasing Subsequence (LC 300)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given an integer array nums, return the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/longest-increasing-subsequence-1587115620/1", "title": "Longest Increasing Subsequence", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the length of the longest subsequence of a given array such that all elements of the subsequence are sorted in strictly increasing order."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-length-of-pair-chain/", "title": "Maximum Length of Pair Chain (LC 646)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Extend the LIS pick/not-pick recurrence to chains of intervals."}]	Module 33: Dynamic Programming on Longest Increasing Subsequences (LIS) & Chain Variants	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an integer array `arr` of length `N`, find the length of the **Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS)**—the longest subsequence in which every element is **strictly greater** than the previous element picked.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The 2D State `(ind, prev_ind)`:**\n  - To decide whether we can pick `arr[ind]`, we must know the index of the **previous element we picked (`prev_ind`)**, starting at `prev_ind = -1` (nothing picked yet).\n  - **Base Case (`ind == N`):** No elements left → `return 0`.\n  - **Transitions:**\n    1. **Not Take:** Skip `arr[ind]` and keep `prev_ind` unchanged:\n       `len = 0 + f(ind + 1, prev_ind)`\n    2. **Take:** Allowed **if and only if `prev_ind == -1 || arr[ind] > arr[prev_ind]`**:\n       `len = max(len, 1 + f(ind + 1, ind))` (now `ind` becomes the new `prev_ind`!).\n- **The `+1` Coordinate Shift for `prev_ind` in Memoization:**\n  - Because `prev_ind` starts at `-1` (`-1` to `N - 1`), we cannot index `dp[ind][-1]` directly.\n  - **The Fix:** Store `prev_ind` at column **`prev_ind + 1`** inside an `N x (N + 1)` DP table `dp[N][N + 1]`!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N²)` Time and `O(N²)` Space (reduced to `O(N)` in DP 42!).\n\n### 💻 Longest Increasing Subsequence (Coordinate-Shifted Memoization) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint solveLIS(int ind, int prev_ind, int n, const vector<int> &arr, vector<vector<int>> &dp) {\n    if (ind == n) return 0;\n\n    // Coordinate shift: prev_ind (-1 .. n-1) is stored at (prev_ind + 1) (0 .. n)\n    if (dp[ind][prev_ind + 1] != -1) return dp[ind][prev_ind + 1];\n\n    // Choice 1: Do not pick arr[ind]\n    int len = 0 + solveLIS(ind + 1, prev_ind, n, arr, dp);\n\n    // Choice 2: Pick arr[ind] if it is strictly greater than arr[prev_ind]\n    if (prev_ind == -1 || arr[ind] > arr[prev_ind]) {\n        len = max(len, 1 + solveLIS(ind + 1, ind, n, arr, dp));\n    }\n\n    return dp[ind][prev_ind + 1] = len;\n}\n\nint lengthOfLIS(vector<int> &nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    vector<vector<int>> dp(n, vector<int>(n + 1, -1));\n    return solveLIS(0, -1, n, nums, dp);\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Indexing `arr[prev_ind + 1]` Instead of `arr[prev_ind]`:** The `+1` shift is **only** for the `dp` table column (`dp[ind][prev_ind + 1]`)! When comparing values in the original array `arr`, always compare `arr[ind] > arr[prev_ind]`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
