8571	Bottom-Up Interval Tabulation: Matrix Chain Multiplication (Tabulation)	dsa-matrix-chain-multiplication-bottom-up-tabulation	Convert top-down MCM recursion into bottom-up iterative tabulation by reversing loop bounds: i from N-1 down to 1, and j from i+1 up to N-1.	2026-10-03 11:44:14.793213+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Bottom-Up Interval Tabulation: Matrix Chain Multiplication (Tabulation)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pDCXsbAw5Cg", "title": "Lecture 229: Matrix Chain Multiplication | Tabulation (DP 49)", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/matrix-chain-multiplication0303/1", "title": "Matrix Chain Multiplication (Tabulation)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Convert recursive Matrix Chain Multiplication into bottom-up tabulation without recursion stack overhead."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-score-triangulation-of-polygon/", "title": "Minimum Score Triangulation of Polygon (LC 1039)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use the exact bottom-up loop order (i from N - 1 down to 1, j from i + 1 to N - 1) to triangulate a polygon."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/brackets-in-matrix-chain-multiplication1024/1", "title": "Brackets in Matrix Chain Multiplication", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Record the optimal split index k in a 2D table to reconstruct the optimal parenthesization string."}]	Module 34: Partition Dynamic Programming (MCM Pattern & Interval Splitting)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nHow do we convert top-down Partition DP (`f(1, N - 1)`) into **Bottom-Up Tabulation** without memorizing awkward diagonal length loops? By reversing the direction of the top-down parameters `i` and `j`!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Deriving the Exact Loop Directions for Partition DP Tabulation:**\n  1. **Base Case:** For all `i` from `1` to `N - 1`, `dp[i][i] = 0`.\n  2. **Direction of `i`:** In top-down recursion, `i` started at `1` and moved toward `N - 1` (`f(k + 1, j)`). Therefore, in bottom-up tabulation, **`i` goes from `N - 1` down to `1`**!\n  3. **Direction of `j`:** In a valid subproblem `(i, j)`, `j` is always to the right of `i` (`j >= i`). Since `j == i` is already `0`, **`j` goes from `i + 1` up to `N - 1`**!\n  4. **Inner Split Loop `k`:** Copy the exact inner loop `for (int k = i; k < j; k++)` and replace recursive calls with `dp[i][k] + dp[k + 1][j]`.\n  5. Return **`dp[1][N - 1]`**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N³)` Time and `O(N²)` Space (with zero recursion stack space).\n\n### 💻 Matrix Chain Multiplication (Bottom-Up Tabulation) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint matrixMultiplicationTabulation(vector<int> &arr, int N) {\n    vector<vector<int>> dp(N, vector<int>(N, 0));\n\n    // Base case: dp[i][i] = 0 is already initialized\n\n    // 1. i moves backwards from N - 1 down to 1\n    for (int i = N - 1; i >= 1; i--) {\n        // 2. j is always strictly to the right of i: i + 1 to N - 1\n        for (int j = i + 1; j < N; j++) {\n            int mini = 1e9;\n            // 3. Try all split points k between i and j - 1\n            for (int k = i; k < j; k++) {\n                int steps = arr[i - 1] * arr[k] * arr[j]\n                            + dp[i][k]\n                            + dp[k + 1][j];\n                mini = min(mini, steps);\n            }\n            dp[i][j] = mini;\n        }\n    }\n    return dp[1][N - 1];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Looping `j` From `1` to `N - 1`:** Whenever `j < i`, the interval `[i ... j]` is invalid (empty), and when `j == i`, it is the base case `0`. Starting `j` at **`i + 1`** skips all invalid and base states automatically!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22260	Complete Git and GitHub Tutorial for Beginners	git-github-one-shot	A comprehensive crash course covering Version Control Systems, Git CLI, staging, committing, branching, remote repositories (GitHub), and collaboration workflows.	2026-10-07 22:58:12.794227+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Complete Git and GitHub Tutorial for Beginners\nPart of the **Version Control Systems** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/RDxQEzXN8AU", "title": "Complete Git and GitHub Tutorial for Beginners", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"type": "assignment", "title": "Build & Test a Working Implementation of Complete Git and GitHub Tutorial for Beginners", "checklist": ["Implement core logic with validation and boundary checks", "Write tests verifying expected output and edge cases"], "description": "Implement a production-grade module demonstrating the core principles of Complete Git and GitHub Tutorial for Beginners.", "starterCodeUrl": ""}]	Version Control	### 📌 1. Version Control & Git Fundamentals\n* **Version Control System (VCS):** A system that records changes to a file or set of files over time so that you can recall specific versions later. It prevents code loss and tracks exactly who modified what and when.\n* **Git vs. GitHub:** \n  * **Git** is the local, open-source VCS software running on your machine.\n  * **GitHub** is a remote hosting service (a remote server) that provides a UI and collaboration tools built on top of Git.\n* **Initialization:** Running `git init` inside a folder creates a hidden `.git` directory, transforming the folder into a tracked repository.\n\n### 💡 2. The Git Workflow (Local to Remote)\n* **Untracked to Staging:** Newly created files are untracked. Running `git add .` moves modified/new files into the **Staging Area** (a holding zone for the next commit).\n* **Staging to Commit:** Running `git commit -m "Message"` packages the staged files into a permanent snapshot (a **Commit**) with a unique SHA-1 ID, forming a timeline of changes.\n* **Local to Remote:** To collaborate, you link your local Git to a remote GitHub repository using `git remote add origin <URL>`. You then upload your commits using `git push`, and retrieve others' commits using `git pull`.\n\n### 💡 3. Branching, Merging, and Advanced Operations\n* **Branching:** Allows you to isolate experimental work or new features without breaking the `main` codebase (`git checkout -b feature-branch`). \n* **Merging vs. Rebasing:** \n  * **Merge:** Combines two branches, creating a new "merge commit" that ties the histories together.\n  * **Rebase:** Rewrites the commit history by placing your feature commits linearly on top of the latest main commits, resulting in a cleaner, linear history but rewriting the SHA IDs.\n* **Reverting vs. Resetting:** `git revert` creates a *new* commit that undoes a previous change, keeping history intact. `git reset --hard` moves the `HEAD` pointer backward, physically deleting subsequent commits from history.\n* **Stashing:** `git stash` temporarily shelves uncommitted changes so you can switch branches or pull remote changes without losing your local work.	Piyush Garg	https://www.youtube.com/@piyushgargdev	709	Complete Git and GitHub Tutorial for Beginners	A comprehensive crash course covering Version Control Systems, Git CLI, staging, committing, branching, remote repositories (GitHub), and collaboration workflows.	111	Beginner	Version Control	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
