d": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["`fetch` and `catch`", "`subscribe` and `getSnapshot`", "`init` and `update`", "`mount` and `unmount`"], "question": "What are the two mandatory arguments for `useSyncExternalStore`?", "explanation": "It requires a `subscribe` function to listen to store changes, and a `getSnapshot` function that returns the current immutable value of the store.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "`subscribe` and `getSnapshot`"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["Handling input field changes", "Subscribing to browser APIs like `window.innerWidth` or `navigator.onLine` without useEffect bugs", "Fetching data from a REST API", "Creating CSS transitions"], "question": "Which of the following is a common real-world use case for `useSyncExternalStore`?", "explanation": "Because the browser APIs exist outside of React's state tree, useSyncExternalStore provides a perfectly synchronous, tear-free way to subscribe to events like network status or window resizing.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "Subscribing to browser APIs like `window.innerWidth` or `navigator.onLine` without useEffect bugs"}]	\N	> **Architectural Deep Dive:** This lecture covers the strict technical nuances, reconciliation behavior, and performance implications of using useSyncExternalStore in production environments. Pay special attention to concurrent rendering behaviors in React 18+.	Web Dev Simplified	https://www.youtube.com/@WebDevSimplified	\N	I Cannot Believe React Made A Hook For This (useSyncExternalStore)	Deep dive architectural lecture focusing on the mechanics, use cases, and anti-patterns of the useSyncExternalStore hook.	15	Easy	Core Hooks & API	20	[]	\N	15	https://youtu.be/NBjycPpPHQQ	I Cannot Believe React Made A Hook For This (useSyncExternalStore)
180	Array Rotations & Two-Pointer Partitioning: Reversal Algorithm, Move Zeroes & Sorted Set Operations	dsa-array-rotations-move-zeroes-union-intersection	Master left and right array rotation by D places using 3-step reversals in O(1) space, moving zeroes to the end, and two-pointer union/intersection.	2026-10-02 22:22:31.479021+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Array Rotations & Two-Pointer Partitioning: Reversal Algorithm, Move Zeroes & Sorted Set Operations\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wvcQg43_V8U", "title": "Lecture 18: Left Rotate Array by D Places, Move Zeroes to End, Union & Intersection", "duration": "45m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sort-array-by-parity/", "title": "Sort Array By Parity", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "905", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Apply the Move Zeroes two-pointer swap pattern to move all even integers to the front."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/intersection-of-two-arrays/", "title": "Intersection of Two Arrays", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "349", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Compute the unique intersection of two integer arrays."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/segregate-0s-and-1s5106/1", "title": "Segregate 0s and 1s", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "problemId": "gfg-segregate-01", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Partition 0s and 1s in-place in a single O(N) pass."}]	Module 8: Array Manipulation, Two-Pointer Patterns & Prefix Sums	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThis lecture covers four essential in-place and two-pointer array techniques: **Rotating an Array by `D` Places**, **Moving All Zeros to the End**, **Linear Search**, and finding the **Union and Intersection of Two Sorted Arrays**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **1. Rotating an Array by `D` Places:**\n  - Always normalize `d = d % n` first, because rotating an array of size `n` by `n` places brings it right back to the original order.\n  - **Optimal 3-Step Reversal Trick (`O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Extra Space):** To left-rotate by `d` places without a temp array:\n    1. Reverse the first `d` elements: `reverse(0, d - 1)`\n    2. Reverse the remaining `n - d` elements: `reverse(d, n - 1)`\n    3. Reverse the entire array: `reverse(0, n - 1)`\n- **2. Moving All Zeros to the End (`O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Find the index `j` of the very first `0` in the array.\n  - Loop `i` from `j + 1` to `n - 1`. Whenever `arr[i] != 0`, swap `arr[i]` with `arr[j]` and advance `j++`.\n- **3. Union & Intersection of Two Sorted Arrays (`O(N1 + N2)` Time):**\n  - Use two pointers `i` (on array `A`) and `j` (on array `B`).\n  - For **Union**, always pick the smaller element (`A[i]` or `B[j]`), append it if it isn't a duplicate of `unionArr.back()`, and advance the pointer.\n  - For **Intersection**, advance whichever pointer has the smaller value; when `A[i] == B[j]`, add the element to the answer and advance **both** `i++` and `j++`.\n\n### 💻 3-Step Array Rotation & Move Zeroes\n```cpp\n// Optimal Left Rotate by D places in O(1) extra space\nvoid leftRotate(vector<int> &arr, int d) {\n    int n = arr.size();\n    d = d % n;\n    reverse(arr.begin(), arr.begin() + d);\n    reverse(arr.begin() + d, arr.end());\n    reverse(arr.begin(), arr.end());\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
9276	Bounded-Window Front Partitioning: Partition Array for Maximum Sum	dsa-partition-array-for-maximum-sum-front-partition-dp	Partition an array into subarrays of length at most k to maximize total element sum using 1D front partition DP in O(N*k) time.	2026-10-03 14:56:36.89765+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Bounded-Window Front Partitioning: Partition Array for Maximum Sum\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PhWWJmaKfMc", "title": "Lecture 234: Partition Array for Maximum Sum | Front Partition (DP 54)", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/partition-array-for-maximum-sum/", "title": "Partition Array for Maximum Sum (LC 1043)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Partition the array into contiguous subarrays of length at most k. After partitioning, each subarray has its values changed to become the maximum value of that subarray. Return the largest sum."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/filling-bookcase-shelves/", "title": "Filling Bookcase Shelves (LC 1105)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use the exact same bounded Front Partition DP to group consecutive books onto shelves."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/partition-array-for-maximum-sum/1", "title": "Partition Array for Maximum Sum", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Maximize the array sum by partitioning into contiguous subarrays of size at most K."}]	Module 34: Partition Dynamic Programming (MCM Pattern & Interval Splitting)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an integer array `num` of size `N` and an integer `k`, partition the array into contiguous subarrays of length **at most `k`**. After partitioning, every element inside a subarray is replaced by the **maximum value in that subarray**. Find the **largest possible total sum** after partitioning.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Applying Front Partition DP (`f(ind)`):**\n  - Starting at index `ind`, how far can the current subarray `[ind ... j]` extend?\n  - At most `k` elements (`ind` up to `ind + k - 1`), provided `j` does not exceed `N - 1`!\n  - Therefore, loop **`j` from `ind` to `min(n, ind + k) - 1`**:\n    - Increment `len++`.\n    - Update the running maximum in `[ind ... j]`: **`maxi = max(maxi, num[j])`**.\n    - Since all `len` elements in `[ind ... j]` become `maxi`, the sum of this subarray is **`len * maxi`**!\n    - Total sum for this partition choice: **`sum = (len * maxi) + f(j + 1)`**.\n    - Track `maxAns = max(maxAns, sum)`.\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * k)` Time and `O(N)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Partition Array for Maximum Sum (Tabulation) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint maxSumAfterPartitioning(vector<int> &num, int k) {\n    int n = num.size();\n    vector<int> dp(n + 1, 0);\n\n    // Move ind backwards from n - 1 down to 0\n    for (int ind = n - 1; ind >= 0; ind--) {\n        int len = 0;\n        int maxi = INT_MIN;\n        int maxAns = INT_MIN;\n\n        // Subarray [ind ... j] can have length at most k (and cannot exceed n - 1)\n        for (int j = ind; j < min(n, ind + k); j++) {\n            len++;\n            maxi = max(maxi, num[j]);\n            int sum = (len * maxi) + dp[j + 1];\n            maxAns = max(maxAns, sum);\n        }\n        dp[ind] = maxAns;\n    }\n    return dp[0];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Looping `j < ind + k` Without `min(n, ind + k)`:** When `ind` is near the end of the array (e.g., `ind = n - 1` and `k = 3`), `ind + k` exceeds `n`, causing an out-of-bounds read on `num[j]`! Always bound the loop with **`min(n, ind + k)`**.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22204	Installments - Simple Interest	aptitude-quant-installments-simple-interest	Calculate simple interest EMI payments by analyzing future value accumulations of each installment avoiding complex formulas.	2026-10-07 12:07:56.264899+05:30	### 📌 1. The Core EMI Concept (Simple Interest)\n* **Future Value Accumulation:** In Simple Interest, you must look at what debt the installments are *clearing* at the END of the loan period.\n* **The Formula-Free Approach:**\n  Assume the installment is **₹100**.\n  * The last installment (paid at the very end) earns **0 years** of interest → Value = 100.\n  * The second-to-last installment earns **1 year** of interest → Value = 100 + (100 × R × 1)/100 = 100 + R.\n  * The third-to-last earns **2 years** of interest → Value = 100 + 2R.\n  * Sum these accumulated values to find the "Reference Debt".\n\n### 💡 2. The Relationship Between Reference Debt and Actual Debt\n* **Due Debt vs Loan Amount:** \n  The sum of the accumulated ₹100 installments equals the **Due Debt** (Total Amount to be paid = Principal + Total Simple Interest on the Principal for the entire period), NOT the original Principal!\n* **Steps to Solve:**\n  1. Calculate the actual Due Debt: `Debt = Principal + (Principal × Rate × Time) / 100`.\n  2. Calculate the Reference Debt for a ₹100 installment (e.g., for 3 years at 10%, it is 120 + 110 + 100 = 330).\n  3. Find the Multiplier: `Multiplier = Actual Debt / Reference Debt`.\n  4. Actual Installment = `₹100 × Multiplier`.\n\n### 💡 3. High-Yield Placement Shortcuts\n* **Direct Calculation of Debt Discharged:** If the installment `x` is given, simply calculate the reference debt for 100, and scale it up by `(x/100)`.\n* **Monthly Installments:** Convert the annual rate to a monthly rate (`R/12`). Ensure time periods are counted in months. Calculate the Due Debt using the monthly rate and total months.	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GDA1VYZNQ6c", "type": "youtube", "title": "Installments (Simple Interest) - Shortcuts & Tricks for 2026 - 2027 Placements"}]	[{"id": "apt-si-inst-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Direct Due Debt Installment] What annual installment will discharge a debt of ₹1,308 due in 4 years at 6% p.a. Simple Interest?", "options": ["A) ₹280", "B) ₹300", "C) ₹320", "D) ₹350"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What exact annual mathematical installment will completely discharge a total accumulated debt of exactly ₹1,308 due in 4 years at a 6% per annum Simple Interest rate?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Assume an installment of ₹100. Y1 payment grows for 3 years: 100 + (6×3) = 118. Y2 payment grows for 2 years: 100 + (6×2) = 112. Y3 payment grows for 1 year: 100 + (6×1) = 106. Y4 payment grows for 0 years: 100. Reference Debt = 118 + 112 + 106 + 100 = 436. Actual Debt = 1308. Multiplier = 1308 / 436 = 3. Actual installment = 100 × 3 = ₹300.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Finding Installment from Loan Amount] A loan of ₹15,000 at 10% p.a. SI for 3 years is to be paid in 3 equal annual installments. Value of installment?", "options": ["A) ₹5,000", "B) ₹5,500", "C) ₹5,909.09", "D) ₹6,500"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man strictly borrows a principal loan of exactly ₹15,000 at a 10% per annum Simple Interest rate for exactly 3 years. He wishes to completely repay the entire amount in 3 equal annual installments. What will be the exact mathematical value of each installment?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) First calculate the Due Debt! Debt = Principal + Total SI = 15000 + (15000 × 10 × 3)/100 = 15000 + 4500 = 19,500. 2) Reference Debt for ₹100 installment at 10% for 3 years: 120 + 110 + 100 = 330. 3) Multiplier = 19500 / 330 = 1950 / 33 = 650 / 11 = 59.0909... 4) Actual Installment = 100 × (650 / 11) ≈ 5909.09.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Finding Debt Discharged by Installment] An annual payment of ₹750 in 6 years at 6% p.a. SI will discharge a debt of what amount?", "options": ["A) ₹4,500", "B) ₹5,050", "C) ₹5,175", "D) ₹5,400"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An exact annual installment payment of exactly ₹750 strictly paid over exactly 6 years at a 6% per annum Simple Interest rate will completely discharge a total accumulated debt of exactly what amount?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Assume ₹100 installment. Reference Debt = 100×6 + (6+12+18+24+30) = 600 + 90 = 690. Since the actual installment is 750, the multiplier is 750 / 100 = 7.5. Actual Debt discharged = 690 × 7.5 = ₹5,175.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Monthly Installments SI] A computer costs ₹45,000 cash or ₹14,000 down payment plus 5 equal monthly installments at 20% p.a. SI. Find monthly installment.", "options": ["A) ₹6,000", "B) ₹6,250", "C) ₹6,500", "D) ₹6,800"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A computer can be bought for exactly ₹45,000 in cash, or by making an immediate down payment of ₹14,000 followed by exactly 5 equal MONTHLY installments. If the nominal rate of interest charged is 20% per annum Simple Interest, what will be the exact mathematical value of each monthly installment?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Loan Amount = 45000 - 14000 = 31,000. 2) Monthly rate = 20% / 12 = 1/60 (as a fraction) or 5/3 % per month. 3) Total Due Debt for 5 months = 31000 + (31000 × 20/100 × 5/12) = 31000 + 31000(1/12) = 31000(13/12) = 33,583.33. 4) Reference Debt for ₹60 installment (to clear fraction): Y1 earns for 4 months: 60 + 4(60 × 1/60) = 64. Reference debt = 64 + 63 + 62 + 61 + 60 = 310. 5) Multiplier = Actual Debt / Reference Debt = 31000(13/12) / 310 = 100 × 13 / 12 = 1300 / 12. 6) Actual Installment = Reference Inst × Multiplier = 60 × (1300 / 12) = 5 × 1300 = ₹6,500.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Finding Rate Given Debt and Installment] An annual installment of ₹6,000 clears a debt of ₹36,000 due in 5 years at R% SI. Find R.", "options": ["A) 8%", "B) 10%", "C) 12%", "D) 15%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An exact annual installment of exactly ₹6,000 completely clears a total accumulated debt of exactly ₹36,000 due in exactly 5 years at R% per annum Simple Interest. What is the exact mathematical value of R?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Multiplier = Actual Installment / 100 = 6000 / 100 = 60. Therefore, the Reference Debt (for a 100 installment) = Actual Debt / 60 = 36000 / 60 = 600. Using the formula for Reference Debt for 5 years: 100×5 + R(1+2+3+4) = 500 + 10R. So, 500 + 10R = 600 => 10R = 100 => R = 10%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Iterative Balance Method] A cycle costs ₹10,000. Paid via ₹4,000 down payment and 5 equal monthly installments of ₹1,300. Annual SI rate?", "options": ["A) 24.5%", "B) 30.0%", "C) 35.3%", "D) 40.0%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The cash price of a cycle is ₹10,000. It is purchased via an immediate cash down payment of ₹4,000 and the remaining balance is paid in exactly 5 equal monthly installments of ₹1,300 each. What is the exact annual rate of simple interest charged?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Initial Loan = 10000 - 4000 = 6000. Total payment made via installments = 5 × 1300 = 6500. Total Interest Paid = 6500 - 6000 = 500. In iterative method, interest is calculated on outstanding balances each month: M1: 6000, M2: 6000-1300=4700, M3: 3400, M4: 2100, M5: 800. Sum of balances = 6000 + 4700 + 3400 + 2100 + 800 = 17,000. Total Interest = (Sum of Balances × Rate_per_month) / 100. 500 = (17000 × R_m) / 100 => 500 = 170 R_m => R_m = 50/17 % per month. Annual Rate = (50/17) × 12 = 600 / 17 ≈ 35.29%. Option C matches 35.3%.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [3-Year Debt Clearance] What annual installment will discharge a debt of ₹8,480 due in 4 years at 4% p.a. SI?", "options": ["A) ₹2,000", "B) ₹2,100", "C) ₹2,200", "D) ₹2,400"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What exact annual installment will completely discharge a total accumulated debt of exactly ₹8,480 due in exactly 4 years at 4% per annum Simple Interest?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Assume ₹100 installment. Reference Debt = 100×4 + 4(3+2+1+0) = 400 + 4(6) = 424. Actual Debt = 8480. Multiplier = 8480 / 424 = 20. Actual Installment = 100 × 20 = ₹2,000.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Total Payment Over 5 Years] A man repays a loan with 5 annual installments of ₹1,200. Rate is 5% p.a. SI. Total debt discharged?", "options": ["A) ₹6,000", "B) ₹6,300", "C) ₹6,600", "D) ₹7,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man repays a loan strictly using 5 equal annual installments of exactly ₹1,200 each. If the rate is 5% per annum Simple Interest, what is the exact mathematical total debt that was discharged at the end of the 5th year?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Reference Debt for 100 installment = 100×5 + 5(4+3+2+1+0) = 500 + 5(10) = 550. Since actual installment is 1200, the multiplier is 1200 / 100 = 12. Actual Debt discharged = 550 × 12 = ₹6,600.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Unequal Installments in SI] A debt is paid in 3 years. The 2nd installment is twice the 1st, the 3rd is thrice the 1st. Rate is 10%. If total debt cleared is ₹6,400, find the 1st installment.", "options": ["A) ₹800", "B) ₹1,000", "C) ₹1,200", "D) ₹1,500"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A debt is paid entirely in 3 annual installments. The second installment is exactly twice the first, and the third installment is exactly thrice the first. The rate is 10% p.a. Simple Interest. If the total accumulated debt cleared at the end is exactly ₹6,400, what is the value of the 1st installment?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let 1st installment = x. It grows for 2 years: x + x(10)(2)/100 = 1.2x. Let 2nd installment = 2x. It grows for 1 year: 2x + 2x(10)(1)/100 = 2.2x. Let 3rd installment = 3x. It grows for 0 years: 3x. Total Debt Cleared = 1.2x + 2.2x + 3x = 6.4x. Given 6.4x = 6400 => x = ₹1,000.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Comparing Cash Price to Installment Price in SI] A TV is ₹10k cash or 2 annual installments of ₹5.5k. Find SI rate.", "options": ["A) 5.50%", "B) 6.89%", "C) 10.00%", "D) 12.50%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A smart TV can be purchased for exactly ₹10,000 in cash, or by completely paying exactly 2 equal annual installments of ₹5,500 each (the first installment being paid after 1 year). What is the exact annual Simple Interest rate being charged?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Using the accumulated Debt method: Due Debt = 10000 + 10000(R)(2)/100 = 10000 + 200R. Value of installments = 5500(1 + R/100) + 5500 = 11000 + 55R. Equating: 10000 + 200R = 11000 + 55R => 145R = 1000 => R = 1000 / 145 = 200 / 29 ≈ 6.89%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Finding Time from Debt and Installment] An annual installment of ₹800 clears a debt of ₹3,440 at 5% p.a. SI. Find the number of years.", "options": ["A) 3 years", "B) 4 years", "C) 5 years", "D) 6 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An exact annual mathematical installment of exactly ₹800 completely clears an accumulated debt of exactly ₹3,440 at exactly 5% per annum Simple Interest. How many years is the tenure of this loan?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Reference installment = 100. Actual = 800. Multiplier = 8. So Reference Debt = 3440 / 8 = 430. We test years n. For n=4: Ref Debt = 100(4) + 5(3+2+1+0) = 400 + 30 = 430. Perfect match. Tenure is 4 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Quarterly SI Installments] A loan of ₹10,000 is taken at 12% p.a. SI. Repaid in 4 equal quarterly installments. Value of installment?", "options": ["A) ₹2,600.00", "B) ₹2,679.42", "C) ₹2,705.50", "D) ₹2,750.00"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A loan of exactly ₹10,000 is taken at exactly 12% per annum Simple Interest. It is completely repaid in exactly 4 equal QUARTERLY installments. What is the exact mathematical value of each installment?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Rate per quarter = 12% / 4 = 3%. Total Due Debt after 4 quarters (1 year) = 10000 + 10000(12/100)(1) = 11,200. Reference Debt for 100 installment for 4 quarters at 3%: 100(4) + 3(3+2+1+0) = 400 + 18 = 418. Multiplier = 11200 / 418 ≈ 26.794. Actual Installment = 100 × 26.794 = ₹2,679.42.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Partial Year Installment] What is the due debt if an installment of ₹500 is paid half-yearly for 1.5 years at 10% p.a. SI?", "options": ["A) ₹1,550", "B) ₹1,575", "C) ₹1,600", "D) ₹1,650"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a mathematical installment of exactly ₹500 is paid HALF-YEARLY for a total period of exactly 1.5 years at exactly 10% per annum Simple Interest, what is the total accumulated debt that is being completely discharged?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1.5 years = 3 half-years. Rate per half-year = 5%. Assume 100 installment. Ref Debt = 100(3) + 5(2+1+0) = 315. Actual Installment is 500, so Multiplier = 5. Actual Debt = 315 × 5 = ₹1,575.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Error in Calculation Concept] A person miscalculates EMI by summing Principal and Interest and simply dividing by N. What is the effect?", "options": ["A) It is perfectly equal to the true installment", "B) It is strictly LESS than the true installment", "C) It is strictly GREATER than the true installment", "D) Depends entirely on the interest rate"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A person calculates their Simple Interest EMI by simply taking the Total Due Debt (Principal + Total Simple Interest on the Principal) and strictly dividing it by the number of years (N). Mathematically, how does this mistakenly calculated installment compare to the true required mathematical Simple Interest installment?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "True installment mathematically accounts for the fact that the installments paid EARLY in the loan period earn interest for the bank, subsidizing the total payment. If you simply divide the Due Debt by N, you ignore this subsidized interest, resulting in an artificially inflated, strictly GREATER installment value.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Finding Total Amount Paid] An installment of ₹840 clears a debt in 5 years at 10% p.a. SI. What is the total cash out of pocket?", "options": ["A) ₹4,200", "B) ₹4,600", "C) ₹5,040", "D) ₹5,400"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An exact annual installment of exactly ₹840 completely clears a specific mathematical debt in exactly 5 years at a 10% per annum Simple Interest rate. What is the exact total absolute cash out-of-pocket paid by the borrower over these 5 years?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "The question strictly asks for the total out-of-pocket cash paid. Regardless of the debt discharged or the interest rate, the borrower pays 5 installments of ₹840 each. Total cash paid = 5 × 840 = ₹4,200.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Difference between CI and SI Installment] Debt of ₹1,210 due in 2 years at 10%. Difference between SI installment and CI installment?", "options": ["A) ₹0.00", "B) ₹18.25", "C) ₹26.19", "D) ₹30.00"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An accumulated debt of exactly ₹1,210 is completely due in exactly 2 years. The nominal interest rate is exactly 10% per annum. What is the exact mathematical difference between the required annual installment if calculated using Compound Interest versus Simple Interest on the same due debt?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For 2 years at 10%: The first installment earns 1 year of simple interest, which is identical to compound interest for 1 year. The second installment earns 0 interest in both cases. Therefore, the required annual installment to clear the exact same due debt over 2 years is mathematically identical in both methods, making the difference exactly ₹0.00.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Solving for Loan with Mixed Interest Options] A loan is repaid in 4 annual installments of ₹1000 at 5% SI. Find Principal.", "options": ["A) ₹3,583.33", "B) ₹3,600.00", "C) ₹3,820.00", "D) ₹4,000.00"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A loan is mathematically completely repaid using exactly 4 equal annual installments of exactly ₹1,000 each. The rate is exactly 5% per annum Simple Interest. What was the exact original Principal (Loan Amount) borrowed on Day 1?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Actual Installment = 1000. 2) Ref Debt for 100 inst at 5% for 4 yrs = 100(4) + 5(3+2+1+0) = 400 + 30 = 430. 3) Multiplier = 10. Actual Debt Discharged = 430 × 10 = 4300. 4) Debt = Principal + SI on Principal for 4 yrs = P + P(5×4)/100 = 1.2P. 5) 1.2P = 4300 => P = 4300 / 1.2 ≈ ₹3,583.33.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Proportional Scaling] Installment I clears Debt D in 3 years at 10%. If Debt is doubled, what happens to Installment?", "options": ["A) It increases by 50%", "B) It exactly doubles (2I)", "C) It more than doubles", "D) It less than doubles"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An exact annual mathematical installment I completely clears an accumulated Due Debt D in exactly 3 years at 10% per annum Simple Interest. If the Due Debt is mathematically exactly doubled (2D), what happens to the exact required annual installment, assuming all other parameters remain unchanged?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "In Simple Interest installment calculations, the Reference Debt is a constant scalar based entirely on Rate and Time. Since Multiplier = Actual Debt / Reference Debt, if the Actual Debt doubles, the multiplier exactly doubles, meaning the Actual Installment exactly doubles.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Installment with Changing Rates] A 3-year loan. Installment is ₹1000. Interest is 5% in Y1, 10% in Y2, 15% in Y3. Due debt?", "options": ["A) ₹3,150", "B) ₹3,400", "C) ₹3,450", "D) ₹3,600"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An individual pays exactly 3 equal annual installments of exactly ₹1,000 each to clear a debt. However, the Simple Interest rate is variable: 5% in Year 1, 10% in Year 2, and 15% in Year 3. What is the total accumulated Due Debt that is mathematically discharged by these payments?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Installment 1 (paid end of Y1) earns 10% in Y2 and 15% in Y3. Value = 1000 + 1000(10+15)/100 = 1250. Installment 2 (paid end of Y2) earns 15% in Y3. Value = 1000 + 1000(15)/100 = 1150. Installment 3 (paid end of Y3) earns 0%. Value = 1000. Total Debt Discharged = 1250 + 1150 + 1000 = ₹3,400.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Equalizing Total Paid vs Debt Discharged] A man pays 4 installments of ₹1000 at 5% SI. He effectively saves money compared to paying the full debt at the end. How much did he save?", "options": ["A) ₹150", "B) ₹200", "C) ₹300", "D) ₹400"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man clears a loan by strictly paying 4 equal annual installments of exactly ₹1,000 each. The rate is exactly 5% per annum Simple Interest. Exactly how much mathematical interest did he \\"save\\" by paying in installments rather than allowing the debt to accumulate fully to the end of the 4 years?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Total out-of-pocket cash paid via installments = 4 × 1000 = 4,000. 2) The actual Due Debt discharged by this payment structure is: Ref Debt = 100(4) + 5(6) = 430. Multiplier = 10. Actual Debt = 430 × 10 = 4,300. 3) If he had paid at the end, he would have had to pay the full Due Debt of ₹4,300. By paying early installments, he only paid ₹4,000. He \\"saved\\" exactly 4300 - 4000 = ₹300.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. The Core EMI Concept (Simple Interest)\n* **Future Value Accumulation:** In Simple Interest, you must look at what debt the installments are *clearing* at the END of the loan period.\n* **The Formula-Free Approach:**\n  Assume the installment is **₹100**.\n  * The last installment (paid at the very end) earns **0 years** of interest → Value = 100.\n  * The second-to-last installment earns **1 year** of interest → Value = 100 + (100 × R × 1)/100 = 100 + R.\n  * The third-to-last earns **2 years** of interest → Value = 100 + 2R.\n  * Sum these accumulated values to find the "Reference Debt".\n\n### 💡 2. The Relationship Between Reference Debt and Actual Debt\n* **Due Debt vs Loan Amount:** \n  The sum of the accumulated ₹100 installments equals the **Due Debt** (Total Amount to be paid = Principal + Total Simple Interest on the Principal for the entire period), NOT the original Principal!\n* **Steps to Solve:**\n  1. Calculate the actual Due Debt: `Debt = Principal + (Principal × Rate × Time) / 100`.\n  2. Calculate the Reference Debt for a ₹100 installment (e.g., for 3 years at 10%, it is 120 + 110 + 100 = 330).\n  3. Find the Multiplier: `Multiplier = Actual Debt / Reference Debt`.\n  4. Actual Installment = `₹100 × Multiplier`.\n\n### 💡 3. High-Yield Placement Shortcuts\n* **Direct Calculation of Debt Discharged:** If the installment `x` is given, simply calculate the reference debt for 100, and scale it up by `(x/100)`.\n* **Monthly Installments:** Convert the annual rate to a monthly rate (`R/12`). Ensure time periods are counted in months. Calculate the Due Debt using the monthly rate and total months.	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Installments - Simple Interest	Calculate simple interest EMI payments by analyzing future value accumulations of each installment avoiding complex formulas.	41	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	38	[{"id": "apt-si-inst-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Direct Due Debt Installment] What annual installment will discharge a debt of ₹1,308 due in 4 years at 6% p.a. Simple Interest?", "options": ["A) ₹280", "B) ₹300", "C) ₹320", "D) ₹350"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What exact annual mathematical installment will completely discharge a total accumulated debt of exactly ₹1,308 due in 4 years at a 6% per annum Simple Interest rate?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Assume an installment of ₹100. Y1 payment grows for 3 years: 100 + (6×3) = 118. Y2 payment grows for 2 years: 100 + (6×2) = 112. Y3 payment grows for 1 year: 100 + (6×1) = 106. Y4 payment grows for 0 years: 100. Reference Debt = 118 + 112 + 106 + 100 = 436. Actual Debt = 1308. Multiplier = 1308 / 436 = 3. Actual installment = 100 × 3 = ₹300.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Finding Installment from Loan Amount] A loan of ₹15,000 at 10% p.a. SI for 3 years is to be paid in 3 equal annual installments. Value of installment?", "options": ["A) ₹5,000", "B) ₹5,500", "C) ₹5,909.09", "D) ₹6,500"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man strictly borrows a principal loan of exactly ₹15,000 at a 10% per annum Simple Interest rate for exactly 3 years. He wishes to completely repay the entire amount in 3 equal annual installments. What will be the exact mathematical value of each installment?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) First calculate the Due Debt! Debt = Principal + Total SI = 15000 + (15000 × 10 × 3)/100 = 15000 + 4500 = 19,500. 2) Reference Debt for ₹100 installment at 10% for 3 years: 120 + 110 + 100 = 330. 3) Multiplier = 19500 / 330 = 1950 / 33 = 650 / 11 = 59.0909... 4) Actual Installment = 100 × (650 / 11) ≈ 5909.09.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Finding Debt Discharged by Installment] An annual payment of ₹750 in 6 years at 6% p.a. SI will discharge a debt of what amount?", "options": ["A) ₹4,500", "B) ₹5,050", "C) ₹5,175", "D) ₹5,400"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An exact annual installment payment of exactly ₹750 strictly paid over exactly 6 years at a 6% per annum Simple Interest rate will completely discharge a total accumulated debt of exactly what amount?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Assume ₹100 installment. Reference Debt = 100×6 + (6+12+18+24+30) = 600 + 90 = 690. Since the actual installment is 750, the multiplier is 750 / 100 = 7.5. Actual Debt discharged = 690 × 7.5 = ₹5,175.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Monthly Installments SI] A computer costs ₹45,000 cash or ₹14,000 down payment plus 5 equal monthly installments at 20% p.a. SI. Find monthly installment.", "options": ["A) ₹6,000", "B) ₹6,250", "C) ₹6,500", "D) ₹6,800"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A computer can be bought for exactly ₹45,000 in cash, or by making an immediate down payment of ₹14,000 followed by exactly 5 equal MONTHLY installments. If the nominal rate of interest charged is 20% per annum Simple Interest, what will be the exact mathematical value of each monthly installment?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Loan Amount = 45000 - 14000 = 31,000. 2) Monthly rate = 20% / 12 = 1/60 (as a fraction) or 5/3 % per month. 3) Total Due Debt for 5 months = 31000 + (31000 × 20/100 × 5/12) = 31000 + 31000(1/12) = 31000(13/12) = 33,583.33. 4) Reference Debt for ₹60 installment (to clear fraction): Y1 earns for 4 months: 60 + 4(60 × 1/60) = 64. Reference debt = 64 + 63 + 62 + 61 + 60 = 310. 5) Multiplier = Actual Debt / Reference Debt = 31000(13/12) / 310 = 100 × 13 / 12 = 1300 / 12. 6) Actual Installment = Reference Inst × Multiplier = 60 × (1300 / 12) = 5 × 1300 = ₹6,500.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Finding Rate Given Debt and Installment] An annual installment of ₹6,000 clears a debt of ₹36,000 due in 5 years at R% SI. Find R.", "options": ["A) 8%", "B) 10%", "C) 12%", "D) 15%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An exact annual installment of exactly ₹6,000 completely clears a total accumulated debt of exactly ₹36,000 due in exactly 5 years at R% per annum Simple Interest. What is the exact mathematical value of R?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Multiplier = Actual Installment / 100 = 6000 / 100 = 60. Therefore, the Reference Debt (for a 100 installment) = Actual Debt / 60 = 36000 / 60 = 600. Using the formula for Reference Debt for 5 years: 100×5 + R(1+2+3+4) = 500 + 10R. So, 500 + 10R = 600 => 10R = 100 => R = 10%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Iterative Balance Method] A cycle costs ₹10,000. Paid via ₹4,000 down payment and 5 equal monthly installments of ₹1,300. Annual SI rate?", "options": ["A) 24.5%", "B) 30.0%", "C) 35.3%", "D) 40.0%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The cash price of a cycle is ₹10,000. It is purchased via an immediate cash down payment of ₹4,000 and the remaining balance is paid in exactly 5 equal monthly installments of ₹1,300 each. What is the exact annual rate of simple interest charged?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Initial Loan = 10000 - 4000 = 6000. Total payment made via installments = 5 × 1300 = 6500. Total Interest Paid = 6500 - 6000 = 500. In iterative method, interest is calculated on outstanding balances each month: M1: 6000, M2: 6000-1300=4700, M3: 3400, M4: 2100, M5: 800. Sum of balances = 6000 + 4700 + 3400 + 2100 + 800 = 17,000. Total Interest = (Sum of Balances × Rate_per_month) / 100. 500 = (17000 × R_m) / 100 => 500 = 170 R_m => R_m = 50/17 % per month. Annual Rate = (50/17) × 12 = 600 / 17 ≈ 35.29%. Option C matches 35.3%.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [3-Year Debt Clearance] What annual installment will discharge a debt of ₹8,480 due in 4 years at 4% p.a. SI?", "options": ["A) ₹2,000", "B) ₹2,100", "C) ₹2,200", "D) ₹2,400"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What exact annual installment will completely discharge a total accumulated debt of exactly ₹8,480 due in exactly 4 years at 4% per annum Simple Interest?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Assume ₹100 installment. Reference Debt = 100×4 + 4(3+2+1+0) = 400 + 4(6) = 424. Actual Debt = 8480. Multiplier = 8480 / 424 = 20. Actual Installment = 100 × 20 = ₹2,000.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Total Payment Over 5 Years] A man repays a loan with 5 annual installments of ₹1,200. Rate is 5% p.a. SI. Total debt discharged?", "options": ["A) ₹6,000", "B) ₹6,300", "C) ₹6,600", "D) ₹7,000"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man repays a loan strictly using 5 equal annual installments of exactly ₹1,200 each. If the rate is 5% per annum Simple Interest, what is the exact mathematical total debt that was discharged at the end of the 5th year?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Reference Debt for 100 installment = 100×5 + 5(4+3+2+1+0) = 500 + 5(10) = 550. Since actual installment is 1200, the multiplier is 1200 / 100 = 12. Actual Debt discharged = 550 × 12 = ₹6,600.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Unequal Installments in SI] A debt is paid in 3 years. The 2nd installment is twice the 1st, the 3rd is thrice the 1st. Rate is 10%. If total debt cleared is ₹6,400, find the 1st installment.", "options": ["A) ₹800", "B) ₹1,000", "C) ₹1,200", "D) ₹1,500"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A debt is paid entirely in 3 annual installments. The second installment is exactly twice the first, and the third installment is exactly thrice the first. The rate is 10% p.a. Simple Interest. If the total accumulated debt cleared at the end is exactly ₹6,400, what is the value of the 1st installment?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Let 1st installment = x. It grows for 2 years: x + x(10)(2)/100 = 1.2x. Let 2nd installment = 2x. It grows for 1 year: 2x + 2x(10)(1)/100 = 2.2x. Let 3rd installment = 3x. It grows for 0 years: 3x. Total Debt Cleared = 1.2x + 2.2x + 3x = 6.4x. Given 6.4x = 6400 => x = ₹1,000.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Comparing Cash Price to Installment Price in SI] A TV is ₹10k cash or 2 annual installments of ₹5.5k. Find SI rate.", "options": ["A) 5.50%", "B) 6.89%", "C) 10.00%", "D) 12.50%"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A smart TV can be purchased for exactly ₹10,000 in cash, or by completely paying exactly 2 equal annual installments of ₹5,500 each (the first installment being paid after 1 year). What is the exact annual Simple Interest rate being charged?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Using the accumulated Debt method: Due Debt = 10000 + 10000(R)(2)/100 = 10000 + 200R. Value of installments = 5500(1 + R/100) + 5500 = 11000 + 55R. Equating: 10000 + 200R = 11000 + 55R => 145R = 1000 => R = 1000 / 145 = 200 / 29 ≈ 6.89%.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Finding Time from Debt and Installment] An annual installment of ₹800 clears a debt of ₹3,440 at 5% p.a. SI. Find the number of years.", "options": ["A) 3 years", "B) 4 years", "C) 5 years", "D) 6 years"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An exact annual mathematical installment of exactly ₹800 completely clears an accumulated debt of exactly ₹3,440 at exactly 5% per annum Simple Interest. How many years is the tenure of this loan?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Reference installment = 100. Actual = 800. Multiplier = 8. So Reference Debt = 3440 / 8 = 430. We test years n. For n=4: Ref Debt = 100(4) + 5(3+2+1+0) = 400 + 30 = 430. Perfect match. Tenure is 4 years.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Quarterly SI Installments] A loan of ₹10,000 is taken at 12% p.a. SI. Repaid in 4 equal quarterly installments. Value of installment?", "options": ["A) ₹2,600.00", "B) ₹2,679.42", "C) ₹2,705.50", "D) ₹2,750.00"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A loan of exactly ₹10,000 is taken at exactly 12% per annum Simple Interest. It is completely repaid in exactly 4 equal QUARTERLY installments. What is the exact mathematical value of each installment?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Rate per quarter = 12% / 4 = 3%. Total Due Debt after 4 quarters (1 year) = 10000 + 10000(12/100)(1) = 11,200. Reference Debt for 100 installment for 4 quarters at 3%: 100(4) + 3(3+2+1+0) = 400 + 18 = 418. Multiplier = 11200 / 418 ≈ 26.794. Actual Installment = 100 × 26.794 = ₹2,679.42.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Partial Year Installment] What is the due debt if an installment of ₹500 is paid half-yearly for 1.5 years at 10% p.a. SI?", "options": ["A) ₹1,550", "B) ₹1,575", "C) ₹1,600", "D) ₹1,650"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If a mathematical installment of exactly ₹500 is paid HALF-YEARLY for a total period of exactly 1.5 years at exactly 10% per annum Simple Interest, what is the total accumulated debt that is being completely discharged?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1.5 years = 3 half-years. Rate per half-year = 5%. Assume 100 installment. Ref Debt = 100(3) + 5(2+1+0) = 315. Actual Installment is 500, so Multiplier = 5. Actual Debt = 315 × 5 = ₹1,575.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Error in Calculation Concept] A person miscalculates EMI by summing Principal and Interest and simply dividing by N. What is the effect?", "options": ["A) It is perfectly equal to the true installment", "B) It is strictly LESS than the true installment", "C) It is strictly GREATER than the true installment", "D) Depends entirely on the interest rate"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A person calculates their Simple Interest EMI by simply taking the Total Due Debt (Principal + Total Simple Interest on the Principal) and strictly dividing it by the number of years (N). Mathematically, how does this mistakenly calculated installment compare to the true required mathematical Simple Interest installment?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "True installment mathematically accounts for the fact that the installments paid EARLY in the loan period earn interest for the bank, subsidizing the total payment. If you simply divide the Due Debt by N, you ignore this subsidized interest, resulting in an artificially inflated, strictly GREATER installment value.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Finding Total Amount Paid] An installment of ₹840 clears a debt in 5 years at 10% p.a. SI. What is the total cash out of pocket?", "options": ["A) ₹4,200", "B) ₹4,600", "C) ₹5,040", "D) ₹5,400"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An exact annual installment of exactly ₹840 completely clears a specific mathematical debt in exactly 5 years at a 10% per annum Simple Interest rate. What is the exact total absolute cash out-of-pocket paid by the borrower over these 5 years?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "The question strictly asks for the total out-of-pocket cash paid. Regardless of the debt discharged or the interest rate, the borrower pays 5 installments of ₹840 each. Total cash paid = 5 × 840 = ₹4,200.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Difference between CI and SI Installment] Debt of ₹1,210 due in 2 years at 10%. Difference between SI installment and CI installment?", "options": ["A) ₹0.00", "B) ₹18.25", "C) ₹26.19", "D) ₹30.00"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An accumulated debt of exactly ₹1,210 is completely due in exactly 2 years. The nominal interest rate is exactly 10% per annum. What is the exact mathematical difference between the required annual installment if calculated using Compound Interest versus Simple Interest on the same due debt?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For 2 years at 10%: The first installment earns 1 year of simple interest, which is identical to compound interest for 1 year. The second installment earns 0 interest in both cases. Therefore, the required annual installment to clear the exact same due debt over 2 years is mathematically identical in both methods, making the difference exactly ₹0.00.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Solving for Loan with Mixed Interest Options] A loan is repaid in 4 annual installments of ₹1000 at 5% SI. Find Principal.", "options": ["A) ₹3,583.33", "B) ₹3,600.00", "C) ₹3,820.00", "D) ₹4,000.00"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A loan is mathematically completely repaid using exactly 4 equal annual installments of exactly ₹1,000 each. The rate is exactly 5% per annum Simple Interest. What was the exact original Principal (Loan Amount) borrowed on Day 1?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "1) Actual Installment = 1000. 2) Ref Debt for 100 inst at 5% for 4 yrs = 100(4) + 5(3+2+1+0) = 400 + 30 = 430. 3) Multiplier = 10. Actual Debt Discharged = 430 × 10 = 4300. 4) Debt = Principal + SI on Principal for 4 yrs = P + P(5×4)/100 = 1.2P. 5) 1.2P = 4300 => P = 4300 / 1.2 ≈ ₹3,583.33.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Proportional Scaling] Installment I clears Debt D in 3 years at 10%. If Debt is doubled, what happens to Installment?", "options": ["A) It increases by 50%", "B) It exactly doubles (2I)", "C) It more than doubles", "D) It less than doubles"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An exact annual mathematical installment I completely clears an accumulated Due Debt D in exactly 3 years at 10% per annum Simple Interest. If the Due Debt is mathematically exactly doubled (2D), what happens to the exact required annual installment, assuming all other parameters remain unchanged?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "In Simple Interest installment calculations, the Reference Debt is a constant scalar based entirely on Rate and Time. Since Multiplier = Actual Debt / Reference Debt, if the Actual Debt doubles, the multiplier exactly doubles, meaning the Actual Installment exactly doubles.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Installment with Changing Rates] A 3-year loan. Installment is ₹1000. Interest is 5% in Y1, 10% in Y2, 15% in Y3. Due debt?", "options": ["A) ₹3,150", "B) ₹3,400", "C) ₹3,450", "D) ₹3,600"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "An individual pays exactly 3 equal annual installments of exactly ₹1,000 each to clear a debt. However, the Simple Interest rate is variable: 5% in Year 1, 10% in Year 2, and 15% in Year 3. What is the total accumulated Due Debt that is mathematically discharged by these payments?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Installment 1 (paid end of Y1) earns 10% in Y2 and 15% in Y3. Value = 1000 + 1000(10+15)/100 = 1250. Installment 2 (paid end of Y2) earns 15% in Y3. Value = 1000 + 1000(15)/100 = 1150. Installment 3 (paid end of Y3) earns 0%. Value = 1000. Total Debt Discharged = 1250 + 1150 + 1000 = ₹3,400.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-si-inst-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Equalizing Total Paid vs Debt Discharged] A man pays 4 installments of ₹1000 at 5% SI. He effectively saves money compared to paying the full debt at the end. How much did he save?", "options": ["A) ₹150", "B) ₹200", "C) ₹300", "D) ₹400"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A man clears a loan by strictly paying 4 equal annual installments of exactly ₹1,000 each. The rate is exactly 5% per annum Simple Interest. Exactly how much mathematical interest did he \\"save\\" by paying in installments rather than allowing the debt to accumulate fully to the end of the 4 years?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Total out-of-pocket cash paid via installments = 4 × 1000 = 4,000. 2) The actual Due Debt discharged by this payment structure is: Ref Debt = 100(4) + 5(6) = 430. Multiplier = 10. Actual Debt = 430 × 10 = 4,300. 3) If he had paid at the end, he would have had to pay the full Due Debt of ₹4,300. By paying early installments, he only paid ₹4,000. He \\"saved\\" exactly 4300 - 4000 = ₹300.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22205	Quadratic Equations	aptitude-quant-quadratic-equations	Master factorization shortcuts, Discriminant properties (D), Shridharacharya Formula, and advanced conjugate root relationships.	2026-10-07 12:07:56.264899+05:30	### 📌 1. Fundamentals of Quadratic Equations\n* A quadratic equation is a polynomial of degree 2: **`ax^2 + bx + c = 0`**.\n* The coefficient `a` **cannot** be equal to 0 (otherwise it becomes a linear equation).\n* It always has exactly **two roots** (let's call them `α` and `β`).\n* **Root Relationships:**\n  * **Sum of roots (`α + β`) = `-b / a`**\n  * **Product of roots (`αβ`) = `c / a`**\n* **Forming an equation from roots:** `x^2 - (Sum)x + (Product) = 0`\n\n### 💡 2. The Discriminant and Nature of Roots\n* The **Discriminant (`D`)** = **`b^2 - 4ac`**\n* **Nature of Roots:**\n  * `D > 0`: Two distinct real roots. (If D is a perfect square, roots are rational).\n  * `D = 0`: Two identical real roots (the parabola just touches the x-axis).\n  * `D < 0`: No real roots (roots are complex conjugate pairs).\n* **Conjugate Root Pairs:** If one root of a quadratic with rational coefficients is an irrational surd `m + √n`, the other root MUST strictly be its conjugate **`m - √n`**. Same applies to complex roots (`a + ib` and `a - ib`).\n\n### 💡 3. High-Yield Placement Shortcuts\n* **Bi-Quadratic Substitution:** If the equation has a degree of 4 (e.g., `ax^4 + bx^2 + c = 0`), substitute **`y = x^2`** to reduce it to a standard quadratic equation. Find `y`, then take the square root to find all 4 values of `x`.\n* **Zero Constant:** If one root is 0, the product of roots is 0, which means **`c = 0`**.\n* **Opposite Sign Roots:** If the two roots are equal in magnitude but opposite in sign (`α` and `-α`), their sum is 0, which means **`b = 0`**.\n* **Reciprocal Roots:** If one root is the exact reciprocal of the other (`α` and `1/α`), their product is 1, which means **`c = a`**.	[]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zlhOmaIxD6s", "type": "youtube", "title": "Quadratic Equations - Shortcuts & Tricks for 2026 - 2027 Placement Tests, Job Interviews & Exams"}]	[{"id": "apt-quad-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Forming Quadratic from Sum and Product] The sum of roots is -7 and their product is 21. Find the quadratic equation.", "options": ["A) x^2 - 7x + 21 = 0", "B) x^2 + 7x + 21 = 0", "C) x^2 - 7x - 21 = 0", "D) x^2 + 7x - 21 = 0"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The exact mathematical sum of the roots of a quadratic equation is -7, and their exact product is 21. Which of the following is the correct quadratic equation?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "The standard form using sum and product is: x^2 - (Sum)x + (Product) = 0. Substituting the given values: x^2 - (-7)x + 21 = 0 => x^2 + 7x + 21 = 0.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-quad-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Equation with Fractional Roots] If the roots are -7/2 and -5/7, find the quadratic equation with integer coefficients.", "options": ["A) 14x^2 + 59x + 35 = 0", "B) 14x^2 - 59x + 35 = 0", "C) 14x^2 + 59x - 35 = 0", "D) 28x^2 + 118x + 70 = 0"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the two exact roots of a quadratic equation are exactly -7/2 and -5/7, which of the following represents the correct quadratic equation stripped of all fractional coefficients?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Sum = (-7/2) + (-5/7) = (-49 - 10) / 14 = -59/14. Product = (-7/2) × (-5/7) = 35/14 = 5/2. Equation: x^2 - (-59/14)x + (35/14) = 0 => x^2 + (59/14)x + 35/14 = 0. Multiply entirely by 14: 14x^2 + 59x + 35 = 0.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-quad-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Finding Roots Using the Shridharacharya Formula] Find the roots of 2x^2 + 3x - 7 = 0.", "options": ["A) (-3 ± √55) / 4", "B) (-3 ± √65) / 4", "C) (3 ± √65) / 4", "D) (-3 ± √65) / 2"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Using the Shridharacharya Formula (Quadratic Formula), what are the exact mathematically precise roots of the equation 2x^2 + 3x - 7 = 0?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Here a=2, b=3, c=-7. Discriminant D = b^2 - 4ac = 3^2 - 4(2)(-7) = 9 - (-56) = 9 + 56 = 65. Formula: x = (-b ± √D) / 2a = (-3 ± √65) / (2 × 2) = (-3 ± √65) / 4.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-quad-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Condition for Equal Magnitude Opposite Sign Roots] If roots are equal in magnitude but opposite in sign, which is true?", "options": ["A) a = 0", "B) b = 0", "C) c = 0", "D) a = c"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the two mathematical roots of a standard quadratic equation (ax^2 + bx + c = 0) are strictly equal in absolute magnitude but opposite in sign, which of the following algebraic conditions must definitely be true?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "If the roots are α and -α, their sum is α + (-α) = 0. The sum of the roots is given by -b / a. Therefore, -b / a = 0, which strictly implies that the coefficient b must be 0.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-quad-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Condition for Reciprocal Roots] If one root is the reciprocal of the other, which is true?", "options": ["A) a = 0", "B) b = 0", "C) c = 0", "D) a = c"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If one root of a standard quadratic equation (ax^2 + bx + c = 0) is the exact mathematical reciprocal of the other root, which of the following algebraic conditions must definitely be true?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "If the roots are α and 1/α, their product is α × (1/α) = 1. The product of the roots is given by c / a. Therefore, c / a = 1, which strictly implies that a = c.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-quad-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Sum of Squares of Roots] If α, β are roots of 4x^2 - 6x + 4 = 0, find α^2 + β^2.", "options": ["A) 1/4", "B) 1/2", "C) 3/4", "D) 9/4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If α and β are the exact mathematical roots of the quadratic equation 4x^2 - 6x + 4 = 0, what is the exact algebraic value of the expression (α^2 + β^2)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Sum of roots (α + β) = -b/a = -(-6)/4 = 6/4 = 3/2. 2) Product of roots (αβ) = c/a = 4/4 = 1. 3) Expression: α^2 + β^2 = (α + β)^2 - 2αβ = (3/2)^2 - 2(1) = 9/4 - 2 = 9/4 - 8/4 = 1/4.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-quad-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Finding Constant k for Equal Roots] If the roots of kx^2 - 8x + 8 = 0 are equal, find k.", "options": ["A) 1", "B) 2", "C) 4", "D) 8"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the quadratic equation kx^2 - 8x + 8 = 0 mathematically possesses two perfectly equal real roots, what is the exact numerical value of the constant k?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "For roots to be equal, the discriminant D must be exactly 0. D = b^2 - 4ac = (-8)^2 - 4(k)(8) = 64 - 32k. Setting D = 0 gives 64 - 32k = 0 => 32k = 64 => k = 2.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-quad-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Conjugate Root Generation] If one root of a quadratic equation with rational coefficients is 3 + 2√5, find the other root.", "options": ["A) -3 - 2√5", "B) -3 + 2√5", "C) 3 - 2√5", "D) Cannot be determined"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If one exact mathematical root of a quadratic equation possessing purely rational coefficients is strictly (3 + 2√5), what must fundamentally be the exact value of the other root?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "By the Conjugate Root Theorem, if a quadratic equation with rational coefficients has an irrational root of the form m + √n, its other root MUST strictly be its conjugate pair: m - √n. Thus, the other root is exactly 3 - 2√5.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-quad-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Difference Between Roots] Find the difference between the roots of x^2 + 6x - 91 = 0.", "options": ["A) 13", "B) 14", "C) 20", "D) 26"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the absolute mathematical difference between the two exact roots of the quadratic equation x^2 + 6x - 91 = 0?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "We need two numbers that multiply to -91 and sum to +6. The numbers are +13 and -7 (since 13 × -7 = -91 and 13 - 7 = 6). The factored equation is (x + 13)(x - 7) = 0. The roots are x = -13 and x = 7. The absolute difference between them is |7 - (-13)| = 7 + 13 = 20.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-quad-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Bi-Quadratic Equation Solving] Solve for p: 7p^4 - 21p^2 + 14 = 0.", "options": ["A) ±1, ±2", "B) ±1, ±√2", "C) ±√2, ±√3", "D) ±2, ±4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Solve the bi-quadratic equation for the variable p: 7p^4 - 21p^2 + 14 = 0. What are all the exact possible mathematical values of p?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Divide the entire equation by 7: p^4 - 3p^2 + 2 = 0. Substitute x = p^2 to get a standard quadratic: x^2 - 3x + 2 = 0. Factor it: (x - 1)(x - 2) = 0. So x = 1 or x = 2. Since x = p^2, we have p^2 = 1 (giving p = ±1) and p^2 = 2 (giving p = ±√2).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-quad-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Identity Condition Constants] For what value of P is (P-1)x^2 + (P^2-1)x + (P^2-3P+2) = 0 an identity?", "options": ["A) -1", "B) 1", "C) 2", "D) 3"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "For what exact numerical value of the constant P does the quadratic equation (P-1)x^2 + (P^2-1)x + (P^2-3P+2) = 0 become a mathematical identity (meaning it is absolutely true for ALL possible values of x)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For a quadratic equation to become a universal identity (having more than two roots), ALL its coefficients must exactly equal 0. 1) P - 1 = 0 => P = 1. 2) P^2 - 1 = 0 => P = ±1. 3) P^2 - 3P + 2 = 0 => (P-1)(P-2) = 0 => P = 1 or 2. The only common value satisfying all three conditions simultaneously is P = 1.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-quad-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Forming Equation with Squared Roots] If α, β are roots of x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0, form an equation whose roots are α^2 and β^2.", "options": ["A) x^2 - 13x + 36 = 0", "B) x^2 - 25x + 36 = 0", "C) x^2 - 13x + 12 = 0", "D) x^2 - 25x + 12 = 0"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If α and β are the exact roots of the quadratic equation x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0, which of the following is the correct quadratic equation whose roots are exactly α^2 and β^2?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Sum α + β = 5. Product αβ = 6. 2) New sum = α^2 + β^2 = (α + β)^2 - 2αβ = 5^2 - 2(6) = 25 - 12 = 13. 3) New product = (αβ)^2 = 6^2 = 36. 4) New Equation: x^2 - (New Sum)x + (New Product) = 0 => x^2 - 13x + 36 = 0.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-quad-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Transforming Complex Bi-Quadratic into Quadratic] Solve for y: y^4 - 25 = 0.", "options": ["A) ±5, ±5i", "B) ±√5, ±i√5", "C) ±5, ±√5i", "D) ±25, ±i√25"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Solve the pure bi-quadratic equation y^4 - 25 = 0. What are all four exact possible mathematical roots for the variable y?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Substitute x = y^2. The equation becomes x^2 - 25 = 0 => x^2 = 25 => x = ±5. This yields two branches: 1) y^2 = 5 => y = ±√5. 2) y^2 = -5 => y = ±√(-5) = ±i√5. All four roots are ±√5, ±i√5.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-quad-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Equation whose Roots are Multiplied by Constant] The roots of 14x^2 + 59x + 35 = 0 are multiplied by 2. Find new equation.", "options": ["A) 14x^2 + 118x + 140 = 0", "B) 7x^2 + 59x + 70 = 0", "C) 28x^2 + 118x + 70 = 0", "D) 14x^2 + 59x + 140 = 0"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A quadratic equation is defined as 14x^2 + 59x + 35 = 0. If both of its roots are mathematically multiplied by exactly 2, which of the following represents the new resulting quadratic equation?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "If roots of ax^2 + bx + c = 0 are multiplied by k, the new equation is a(x/k)^2 + b(x/k) + c = 0. Here k = 2. So, 14(x/2)^2 + 59(x/2) + 35 = 0 => 14(x^2 / 4) + 59x / 2 + 35 = 0. Multiply entirely by 4 to clear denominators: 14x^2 + 118x + 140 = 0. Divide by 2: 7x^2 + 59x + 70 = 0.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-quad-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Finding Roots with High Constant] Find the roots of x^2 + 19x + 84 = 0.", "options": ["A) -12, -7", "B) 12, 7", "C) -14, -5", "D) 14, 5"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What are the two exact mathematical roots of the quadratic equation x^2 + 19x + 84 = 0?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "We need two numbers that multiply to exactly +84 and sum to exactly +19. Testing factors of 84: 12 × 7 = 84, and 12 + 7 = 19. The factored equation is (x + 12)(x + 7) = 0. Therefore, the exact roots (by reversing the signs) are x = -12 and x = -7.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-quad-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Complex Reduction Substitution] Solve for x: x^(2/3) - 3x^(1/3) - 18 = 0.", "options": ["A) -27, 216", "B) 27, -216", "C) -9, 36", "D) 9, -36"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Solve the following non-standard fractional-power equation for the real variable x:\\nx^(2/3) - 3x^(1/3) - 18 = 0", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Substitute y = x^(1/3). The equation becomes y^2 - 3y - 18 = 0. Factoring: we need numbers that multiply to -18 and sum to -3. These are -6 and +3. So (y - 6)(y + 3) = 0. This gives y = 6 and y = -3. Since y = x^(1/3), we cube both sides to find x. For y = 6: x = 6^3 = 216. For y = -3: x = (-3)^3 = -27. The roots are -27 and 216.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-quad-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Using Value Substitution to Evaluate Root Form] What form do the roots of x^2 - 4ax + b + 1 = 0 take if D < 0?", "options": ["A) Real, distinct, and rational", "B) Real, distinct, and irrational", "C) Real and exactly equal", "D) Non-real, complex conjugate pairs"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Consider a quadratic equation where the discriminant D is strictly less than zero (D < 0). Mathematically, what is the absolute definite nature of the two roots generated by this equation?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "When the Discriminant D = b^2 - 4ac is strictly less than 0, the term under the square root in the Shridharacharya Formula becomes negative. Since the square root of a negative number yields an imaginary unit (i), the roots strictly become non-real complex numbers. Furthermore, they invariably occur as conjugate pairs (m + in and m - in).", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-quad-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Algebraic Condition for Non-Real Roots] For x^2 - 4ax + b + 1 = 0 to have non-real roots, what condition holds?", "options": ["A) 4a^2 - b - 1 < 0", "B) 16a^2 - 4b - 4 > 0", "C) b > 4a^2 - 1", "D) b < 4a^2 + 1"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "For the specific quadratic equation x^2 - 4ax + b + 1 = 0 to possess strictly non-real (complex) roots, which of the following exact algebraic inequality conditions must definitely hold true?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "For non-real roots, the Discriminant D < 0. Here a_coeff = 1, b_coeff = -4a, c_coeff = b + 1. D = (-4a)^2 - 4(1)(b + 1) < 0 => 16a^2 - 4(b + 1) < 0 => 16a^2 - 4b - 4 < 0. Divide the entire inequality by 4: 4a^2 - b - 1 < 0. Rearrange to isolate b: 4a^2 - 1 < b, which is mathematically identical to b > 4a^2 - 1.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-quad-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Inverse Root Generation] If roots of an equation are -7/2 and -5/7, what is the equation?", "options": ["A) 14x^2 + 59x + 35 = 0", "B) 14x^2 - 59x + 35 = 0", "C) 14x^2 + 59x - 35 = 0", "D) 14x^2 - 59x - 35 = 0"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the two exact mathematical roots of a quadratic equation are strictly -7/2 and -5/7, what is the correct representation of the equation?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "We form the equation from the roots. Sum = -7/2 - 5/7 = (-49 - 10)/14 = -59/14. Product = (-7/2) × (-5/7) = 35/14. Equation is x^2 - (Sum)x + Product = 0 => x^2 - (-59/14)x + 35/14 = 0 => x^2 + (59/14)x + 35/14 = 0. Multiply by 14 to clear fractions: 14x^2 + 59x + 35 = 0.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-quad-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Evaluating High-Power Expression of Conjugate Roots] If α, β are roots of ax^2 + bx + c = 0, find α^4 - β^4.", "options": ["A) (α+β)(α-β)(α^2+β^2)", "B) (α+β)^2 - 2αβ", "C) (α^2+β^2)^2 - 2α^2β^2", "D) (α-β)(α^2+β^2)^2"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If α and β are the exact mathematical roots of the quadratic equation ax^2 + bx + c = 0, which of the following algebraic expressions correctly represents the exact value of (α^4 - β^4)?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Using standard algebraic factorization: α^4 - β^4 represents a difference of squares. 1) (α^2)^2 - (β^2)^2 = (α^2 - β^2)(α^2 + β^2). 2) Further factor the first bracket (difference of squares again): (α^2 - β^2) = (α + β)(α - β). 3) Substituting this back in gives the full expansion: (α + β)(α - β)(α^2 + β^2).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 20}]	Quantitative Aptitude	### 📌 1. Fundamentals of Quadratic Equations\n* A quadratic equation is a polynomial of degree 2: **`ax^2 + bx + c = 0`**.\n* The coefficient `a` **cannot** be equal to 0 (otherwise it becomes a linear equation).\n* It always has exactly **two roots** (let's call them `α` and `β`).\n* **Root Relationships:**\n  * **Sum of roots (`α + β`) = `-b / a`**\n  * **Product of roots (`αβ`) = `c / a`**\n* **Forming an equation from roots:** `x^2 - (Sum)x + (Product) = 0`\n\n### 💡 2. The Discriminant and Nature of Roots\n* The **Discriminant (`D`)** = **`b^2 - 4ac`**\n* **Nature of Roots:**\n  * `D > 0`: Two distinct real roots. (If D is a perfect square, roots are rational).\n  * `D = 0`: Two identical real roots (the parabola just touches the x-axis).\n  * `D < 0`: No real roots (roots are complex conjugate pairs).\n* **Conjugate Root Pairs:** If one root of a quadratic with rational coefficients is an irrational surd `m + √n`, the other root MUST strictly be its conjugate **`m - √n`**. Same applies to complex roots (`a + ib` and `a - ib`).\n\n### 💡 3. High-Yield Placement Shortcuts\n* **Bi-Quadratic Substitution:** If the equation has a degree of 4 (e.g., `ax^4 + bx^2 + c = 0`), substitute **`y = x^2`** to reduce it to a standard quadratic equation. Find `y`, then take the square root to find all 4 values of `x`.\n* **Zero Constant:** If one root is 0, the product of roots is 0, which means **`c = 0`**.\n* **Opposite Sign Roots:** If the two roots are equal in magnitude but opposite in sign (`α` and `-α`), their sum is 0, which means **`b = 0`**.\n* **Reciprocal Roots:** If one root is the exact reciprocal of the other (`α` and `1/α`), their product is 1, which means **`c = a`**.	CareerRide	https://www.youtube.com/@CareerRide	27	Quadratic Equations	Master factorization shortcuts, Discriminant properties (D), Shridharacharya Formula, and advanced conjugate root relationships.	109	Hard	Quantitative Aptitude	39	[{"id": "apt-quad-q1", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q1: [Forming Quadratic from Sum and Product] The sum of roots is -7 and their product is 21. Find the quadratic equation.", "options": ["A) x^2 - 7x + 21 = 0", "B) x^2 + 7x + 21 = 0", "C) x^2 - 7x - 21 = 0", "D) x^2 + 7x - 21 = 0"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "The exact mathematical sum of the roots of a quadratic equation is -7, and their exact product is 21. Which of the following is the correct quadratic equation?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "explanation": "The standard form using sum and product is: x^2 - (Sum)x + (Product) = 0. Substituting the given values: x^2 - (-7)x + 21 = 0 => x^2 + 7x + 21 = 0.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "apt-quad-q2", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q2: [Equation with Fractional Roots] If the roots are -7/2 and -5/7, find the quadratic equation with integer coefficients.", "options": ["A) 14x^2 + 59x + 35 = 0", "B) 14x^2 - 59x + 35 = 0", "C) 14x^2 + 59x - 35 = 0", "D) 28x^2 + 118x + 70 = 0"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the two exact roots of a quadratic equation are exactly -7/2 and -5/7, which of the following represents the correct quadratic equation stripped of all fractional coefficients?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "Sum = (-7/2) + (-5/7) = (-49 - 10) / 14 = -59/14. Product = (-7/2) × (-5/7) = 35/14 = 5/2. Equation: x^2 - (-59/14)x + (35/14) = 0 => x^2 + (59/14)x + 35/14 = 0. Multiply entirely by 14: 14x^2 + 59x + 35 = 0.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "apt-quad-q3", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q3: [Finding Roots Using the Shridharacharya Formula] Find the roots of 2x^2 + 3x - 7 = 0.", "options": ["A) (-3 ± √55) / 4", "B) (-3 ± √65) / 4", "C) (3 ± √65) / 4", "D) (-3 ± √65) / 2"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Using the Shridharacharya Formula (Quadratic Formula), what are the exact mathematically precise roots of the equation 2x^2 + 3x - 7 = 0?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Here a=2, b=3, c=-7. Discriminant D = b^2 - 4ac = 3^2 - 4(2)(-7) = 9 - (-56) = 9 + 56 = 65. Formula: x = (-b ± √D) / 2a = (-3 ± √65) / (2 × 2) = (-3 ± √65) / 4.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "apt-quad-q4", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q4: [Condition for Equal Magnitude Opposite Sign Roots] If roots are equal in magnitude but opposite in sign, which is true?", "options": ["A) a = 0", "B) b = 0", "C) c = 0", "D) a = c"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the two mathematical roots of a standard quadratic equation (ax^2 + bx + c = 0) are strictly equal in absolute magnitude but opposite in sign, which of the following algebraic conditions must definitely be true?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "If the roots are α and -α, their sum is α + (-α) = 0. The sum of the roots is given by -b / a. Therefore, -b / a = 0, which strictly implies that the coefficient b must be 0.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "apt-quad-q5", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q5: [Condition for Reciprocal Roots] If one root is the reciprocal of the other, which is true?", "options": ["A) a = 0", "B) b = 0", "C) c = 0", "D) a = c"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If one root of a standard quadratic equation (ax^2 + bx + c = 0) is the exact mathematical reciprocal of the other root, which of the following algebraic conditions must definitely be true?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "If the roots are α and 1/α, their product is α × (1/α) = 1. The product of the roots is given by c / a. Therefore, c / a = 1, which strictly implies that a = c.", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 5}, {"id": "apt-quad-q6", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q6: [Sum of Squares of Roots] If α, β are roots of 4x^2 - 6x + 4 = 0, find α^2 + β^2.", "options": ["A) 1/4", "B) 1/2", "C) 3/4", "D) 9/4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If α and β are the exact mathematical roots of the quadratic equation 4x^2 - 6x + 4 = 0, what is the exact algebraic value of the expression (α^2 + β^2)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Sum of roots (α + β) = -b/a = -(-6)/4 = 6/4 = 3/2. 2) Product of roots (αβ) = c/a = 4/4 = 1. 3) Expression: α^2 + β^2 = (α + β)^2 - 2αβ = (3/2)^2 - 2(1) = 9/4 - 2 = 9/4 - 8/4 = 1/4.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 6}, {"id": "apt-quad-q7", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q7: [Finding Constant k for Equal Roots] If the roots of kx^2 - 8x + 8 = 0 are equal, find k.", "options": ["A) 1", "B) 2", "C) 4", "D) 8"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the quadratic equation kx^2 - 8x + 8 = 0 mathematically possesses two perfectly equal real roots, what is the exact numerical value of the constant k?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "For roots to be equal, the discriminant D must be exactly 0. D = b^2 - 4ac = (-8)^2 - 4(k)(8) = 64 - 32k. Setting D = 0 gives 64 - 32k = 0 => 32k = 64 => k = 2.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 7}, {"id": "apt-quad-q8", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q8: [Conjugate Root Generation] If one root of a quadratic equation with rational coefficients is 3 + 2√5, find the other root.", "options": ["A) -3 - 2√5", "B) -3 + 2√5", "C) 3 - 2√5", "D) Cannot be determined"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If one exact mathematical root of a quadratic equation possessing purely rational coefficients is strictly (3 + 2√5), what must fundamentally be the exact value of the other root?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "By the Conjugate Root Theorem, if a quadratic equation with rational coefficients has an irrational root of the form m + √n, its other root MUST strictly be its conjugate pair: m - √n. Thus, the other root is exactly 3 - 2√5.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 8}, {"id": "apt-quad-q9", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q9: [Difference Between Roots] Find the difference between the roots of x^2 + 6x - 91 = 0.", "options": ["A) 13", "B) 14", "C) 20", "D) 26"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What is the absolute mathematical difference between the two exact roots of the quadratic equation x^2 + 6x - 91 = 0?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "We need two numbers that multiply to -91 and sum to +6. The numbers are +13 and -7 (since 13 × -7 = -91 and 13 - 7 = 6). The factored equation is (x + 13)(x - 7) = 0. The roots are x = -13 and x = 7. The absolute difference between them is |7 - (-13)| = 7 + 13 = 20.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 9}, {"id": "apt-quad-q10", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q10: [Bi-Quadratic Equation Solving] Solve for p: 7p^4 - 21p^2 + 14 = 0.", "options": ["A) ±1, ±2", "B) ±1, ±√2", "C) ±√2, ±√3", "D) ±2, ±4"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Solve the bi-quadratic equation for the variable p: 7p^4 - 21p^2 + 14 = 0. What are all the exact possible mathematical values of p?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Divide the entire equation by 7: p^4 - 3p^2 + 2 = 0. Substitute x = p^2 to get a standard quadratic: x^2 - 3x + 2 = 0. Factor it: (x - 1)(x - 2) = 0. So x = 1 or x = 2. Since x = p^2, we have p^2 = 1 (giving p = ±1) and p^2 = 2 (giving p = ±√2).", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 10}, {"id": "apt-quad-q11", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q11: [Identity Condition Constants] For what value of P is (P-1)x^2 + (P^2-1)x + (P^2-3P+2) = 0 an identity?", "options": ["A) -1", "B) 1", "C) 2", "D) 3"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "For what exact numerical value of the constant P does the quadratic equation (P-1)x^2 + (P^2-1)x + (P^2-3P+2) = 0 become a mathematical identity (meaning it is absolutely true for ALL possible values of x)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "For a quadratic equation to become a universal identity (having more than two roots), ALL its coefficients must exactly equal 0. 1) P - 1 = 0 => P = 1. 2) P^2 - 1 = 0 => P = ±1. 3) P^2 - 3P + 2 = 0 => (P-1)(P-2) = 0 => P = 1 or 2. The only common value satisfying all three conditions simultaneously is P = 1.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 11}, {"id": "apt-quad-q12", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q12: [Forming Equation with Squared Roots] If α, β are roots of x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0, form an equation whose roots are α^2 and β^2.", "options": ["A) x^2 - 13x + 36 = 0", "B) x^2 - 25x + 36 = 0", "C) x^2 - 13x + 12 = 0", "D) x^2 - 25x + 12 = 0"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If α and β are the exact roots of the quadratic equation x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0, which of the following is the correct quadratic equation whose roots are exactly α^2 and β^2?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "1) Sum α + β = 5. Product αβ = 6. 2) New sum = α^2 + β^2 = (α + β)^2 - 2αβ = 5^2 - 2(6) = 25 - 12 = 13. 3) New product = (αβ)^2 = 6^2 = 36. 4) New Equation: x^2 - (New Sum)x + (New Product) = 0 => x^2 - 13x + 36 = 0.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 12}, {"id": "apt-quad-q13", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q13: [Transforming Complex Bi-Quadratic into Quadratic] Solve for y: y^4 - 25 = 0.", "options": ["A) ±5, ±5i", "B) ±√5, ±i√5", "C) ±5, ±√5i", "D) ±25, ±i√25"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Solve the pure bi-quadratic equation y^4 - 25 = 0. What are all four exact possible mathematical roots for the variable y?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Substitute x = y^2. The equation becomes x^2 - 25 = 0 => x^2 = 25 => x = ±5. This yields two branches: 1) y^2 = 5 => y = ±√5. 2) y^2 = -5 => y = ±√(-5) = ±i√5. All four roots are ±√5, ±i√5.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 13}, {"id": "apt-quad-q14", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q14: [Equation whose Roots are Multiplied by Constant] The roots of 14x^2 + 59x + 35 = 0 are multiplied by 2. Find new equation.", "options": ["A) 14x^2 + 118x + 140 = 0", "B) 7x^2 + 59x + 70 = 0", "C) 28x^2 + 118x + 70 = 0", "D) 14x^2 + 59x + 140 = 0"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "A quadratic equation is defined as 14x^2 + 59x + 35 = 0. If both of its roots are mathematically multiplied by exactly 2, which of the following represents the new resulting quadratic equation?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "If roots of ax^2 + bx + c = 0 are multiplied by k, the new equation is a(x/k)^2 + b(x/k) + c = 0. Here k = 2. So, 14(x/2)^2 + 59(x/2) + 35 = 0 => 14(x^2 / 4) + 59x / 2 + 35 = 0. Multiply entirely by 4 to clear denominators: 14x^2 + 118x + 140 = 0. Divide by 2: 7x^2 + 59x + 70 = 0.", "correctAnswer": "B", "questionNumber": 14}, {"id": "apt-quad-q15", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q15: [Finding Roots with High Constant] Find the roots of x^2 + 19x + 84 = 0.", "options": ["A) -12, -7", "B) 12, 7", "C) -14, -5", "D) 14, 5"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "What are the two exact mathematical roots of the quadratic equation x^2 + 19x + 84 = 0?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "We need two numbers that multiply to exactly +84 and sum to exactly +19. Testing factors of 84: 12 × 7 = 84, and 12 + 7 = 19. The factored equation is (x + 12)(x + 7) = 0. Therefore, the exact roots (by reversing the signs) are x = -12 and x = -7.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 15}, {"id": "apt-quad-q16", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q16: [Complex Reduction Substitution] Solve for x: x^(2/3) - 3x^(1/3) - 18 = 0.", "options": ["A) -27, 216", "B) 27, -216", "C) -9, 36", "D) 9, -36"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Solve the following non-standard fractional-power equation for the real variable x:\\nx^(2/3) - 3x^(1/3) - 18 = 0", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Substitute y = x^(1/3). The equation becomes y^2 - 3y - 18 = 0. Factoring: we need numbers that multiply to -18 and sum to -3. These are -6 and +3. So (y - 6)(y + 3) = 0. This gives y = 6 and y = -3. Since y = x^(1/3), we cube both sides to find x. For y = 6: x = 6^3 = 216. For y = -3: x = (-3)^3 = -27. The roots are -27 and 216.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 16}, {"id": "apt-quad-q17", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q17: [Using Value Substitution to Evaluate Root Form] What form do the roots of x^2 - 4ax + b + 1 = 0 take if D < 0?", "options": ["A) Real, distinct, and rational", "B) Real, distinct, and irrational", "C) Real and exactly equal", "D) Non-real, complex conjugate pairs"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "Consider a quadratic equation where the discriminant D is strictly less than zero (D < 0). Mathematically, what is the absolute definite nature of the two roots generated by this equation?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "When the Discriminant D = b^2 - 4ac is strictly less than 0, the term under the square root in the Shridharacharya Formula becomes negative. Since the square root of a negative number yields an imaginary unit (i), the roots strictly become non-real complex numbers. Furthermore, they invariably occur as conjugate pairs (m + in and m - in).", "correctAnswer": "D", "questionNumber": 17}, {"id": "apt-quad-q18", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q18: [Algebraic Condition for Non-Real Roots] For x^2 - 4ax + b + 1 = 0 to have non-real roots, what condition holds?", "options": ["A) 4a^2 - b - 1 < 0", "B) 16a^2 - 4b - 4 > 0", "C) b > 4a^2 - 1", "D) b < 4a^2 + 1"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "For the specific quadratic equation x^2 - 4ax + b + 1 = 0 to possess strictly non-real (complex) roots, which of the following exact algebraic inequality conditions must definitely hold true?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "For non-real roots, the Discriminant D < 0. Here a_coeff = 1, b_coeff = -4a, c_coeff = b + 1. D = (-4a)^2 - 4(1)(b + 1) < 0 => 16a^2 - 4(b + 1) < 0 => 16a^2 - 4b - 4 < 0. Divide the entire inequality by 4: 4a^2 - b - 1 < 0. Rearrange to isolate b: 4a^2 - 1 < b, which is mathematically identical to b > 4a^2 - 1.", "correctAnswer": "C", "questionNumber": 18}, {"id": "apt-quad-q19", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q19: [Inverse Root Generation] If roots of an equation are -7/2 and -5/7, what is the equation?", "options": ["A) 14x^2 + 59x + 35 = 0", "B) 14x^2 - 59x + 35 = 0", "C) 14x^2 + 59x - 35 = 0", "D) 14x^2 - 59x - 35 = 0"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If the two exact mathematical roots of a quadratic equation are strictly -7/2 and -5/7, what is the correct representation of the equation?", "difficulty": "Hard", "explanation": "We form the equation from the roots. Sum = -7/2 - 5/7 = (-49 - 10)/14 = -59/14. Product = (-7/2) × (-5/7) = 35/14. Equation is x^2 - (Sum)x + Product = 0 => x^2 - (-59/14)x + 35/14 = 0 => x^2 + (59/14)x + 35/14 = 0. Multiply by 14 to clear fractions: 14x^2 + 59x + 35 = 0.", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 19}, {"id": "apt-quad-q20", "type": "mcq", "title": "Q20: [Evaluating High-Power Expression of Conjugate Roots] If α, β are roots of ax^2 + bx + c = 0, find α^4 - β^4.", "options": ["A) (α+β)(α-β)(α^2+β^2)", "B) (α+β)^2 - 2αβ", "C) (α^2+β^2)^2 - 2α^2β^2", "D) (α-β)(α^2+β^2)^2"], "category": "Quantitative Aptitude", "question": "If α and β are the exact mathematical roots of the quadratic equation ax^2 + bx + c = 0, which of the following algebraic expressions correctly represents the exact value of (α^4 - β^4)?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "explanation": "Using standard algebraic factorization: α^4 - β^4 represents a difference of squares. 1) (α^2)^2 - (β^2)^2 = (α^2 - β^2)(α^2 + β^2). 2) Further factor the first bracket (difference of squares again): (α^2 - β^2) = (α + β)(α - β). 3) Substituting this back in gives the full expansion: (α + β)(α - β)(α^2 + β^2).", "correctAnswer": "A", "questionNumber": 20}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
7664	LCS Table Merge Reconstruction: Shortest Common Supersequence (SCS)	dsa-shortest-common-supersequence-dp-on-strings	Reconstruct the shortest string containing both s1 and s2 as subsequences by backtracking the 2D LCS table and appending leftover prefixes.	2026-10-03 11:39:06.526794+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: LCS Table Merge Reconstruction: Shortest Common Supersequence (SCS)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xElxAuBcvsU", "title": "Lecture 212: Shortest Common Supersequence (DP 31)", "duration": "22m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/shortest-common-supersequence/", "title": "Shortest Common Supersequence (LC 1092)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Given two strings str1 and str2, return the shortest string that has both str1 and str2 as subsequences."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/shortest-common-supersequence0322/1", "title": "Shortest Common Supersequence", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the length of the smallest string which has both X and Y as subsequences."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-common-subsequence/", "title": "Longest Common Subsequence (LC 1143)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Review the 2D LCS DP table used to reconstruct the Shortest Common Supersequence."}]	Module 31: Dynamic Programming on Strings: LCS, Substrings & Palindromic Subsequences	### 📌 Topic Overview\nA **Supersequence** of two strings `s1` (length `N`) and `s2` (length `M`) is a string that contains **both `s1` and `s2` as subsequences**. Find the **Shortest Common Supersequence (SCS)**—both its length and the actual string itself!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **1. Length of the Shortest Common Supersequence:**\n  - Concatenating `s1 + s2` gives a trivial supersequence of length `N + M`, but it includes their common characters twice!\n  - To make the supersequence as short as possible, include the **Longest Common Subsequence (`LCS`) only once**:\n    **`SCS Length = N + M - LCS(s1, s2)`**!\n- **2. Reconstructing the SCS String From the 2D LCS Table:**\n  - Build the standard 2D LCS table `dp[N + 1][M + 1]` and start backtracking at `i = N, j = M`:\n    - **If `s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]` (LCS Match):** Include the shared character **once** (`ans += s1[i - 1]`), and move diagonally: **`i--, j--`**.\n    - **Else if `dp[i - 1][j] > dp[i][j - 1]` (Move Up):** Before leaving row `i`, we **must include `s1[i - 1]`** (`ans += s1[i - 1]`), then **`i--`**.\n    - **Else (Move Left):** Before leaving col `j`, we **must include `s2[j - 1]`** (`ans += s2[j - 1]`), then **`j--`**.\n  - **Crucial Leftover Loops:** After `while (i > 0 && j > 0)` ends, append any remaining prefix characters via `while (i > 0) { ans += s1[i - 1]; i--; }` and `while (j > 0) { ans += s2[j - 1]; j--; }`, then **reverse `ans`**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * M)` Time and `O(N * M)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Shortest Common Supersequence Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nstring shortestCommonSupersequence(string s1, string s2) {\n    int n = s1.size(), m = s2.size();\n    vector<vector<int>> dp(n + 1, vector<int>(m + 1, 0));\n\n    // 1. Build 2D LCS DP table\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {\n            if (s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]) dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i - 1][j - 1];\n            else dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // 2. Backtrack from (n, m) collecting both common and non-common characters\n    string ans = "";\n    int i = n, j = m;\n    while (i > 0 && j > 0) {\n        if (s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]) {\n            ans += s1[i - 1];\n            i--;\n            j--;\n        } else if (dp[i - 1][j] > dp[i][j - 1]) {\n            ans += s1[i - 1];\n            i--;\n        } else {\n            ans += s2[j - 1];\n            j--;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // 3. Append any remaining characters when one string finishes before the other!\n    while (i > 0) { ans += s1[i - 1]; i--; }\n    while (j > 0) { ans += s2[j - 1]; j--; }\n\n    reverse(ans.begin(), ans.end());\n    return ans;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting the Two Leftover `while (i > 0)` and `while (j > 0)` Loops:** In Print LCS (DP 26), we stopped as soon as `i == 0 || j == 0`. In **SCS**, if `j` hits `0` while `i > 0`, the remaining `i` characters of `s1` are still needed to form a valid supersequence!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
181	Bitwise XOR & Mathematical Array Tricks: Missing Number, Consecutive Streaks & Single Number	dsa-arrays-xor-missing-number-consecutive-ones	Solve Missing Number, Maximum Consecutive Ones, and Single Number in O(N) time and O(1) space leveraging XOR self-cancellation properties.	2026-10-02 22:22:31.481926+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Bitwise XOR & Mathematical Array Tricks: Missing Number, Consecutive Streaks & Single Number\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bYWLJb3vCWY", "title": "Lecture 19: Find Missing Number, Maximum Consecutive Ones & Find Single Number in Array", "duration": "35m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/set-mismatch/", "title": "Set Mismatch", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "645", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Find both the duplicated number and the missing number in a 1-to-N array."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/consecutive-characters/", "title": "Consecutive Characters", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1446", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Apply the consecutive streak counter pattern to find the longest repeating character substring."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-the-difference/", "title": "Find the Difference", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "389", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Use bitwise XOR cancellation to identify the single added character between two strings."}]	Module 8: Array Manipulation, Two-Pointer Patterns & Prefix Sums	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMany array problems that seem to require hash maps (`O(N)` extra space) can actually be solved in `O(1)` space using simple math or the **Bitwise XOR (`^`)** operator. This lecture covers **Missing Number**, **Maximum Consecutive Ones**, and **Single Number**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The 2 Golden Properties of XOR (`^`):**\n  1. **Same numbers cancel out to zero:** `a ^ a = 0` (e.g., `5 ^ 5 = 0`).\n  2. **Zero XOR any number is the number itself:** `0 ^ a = a` (e.g., `0 ^ 5 = 5`).\n- **1. Finding the Missing Number (`1` to `N`):**\n  - *Sum Formula (`O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Space):* Subtract the sum of the array from `N * (N + 1) / 2`. (Note: requires `long long` when `N = 10^5` so `N * (N + 1)` doesn't overflow).\n  - *XOR Approach (`O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Space, Zero Overflow):* XOR all numbers from `1` to `N` (`xor1`) and XOR all elements in the array (`xor2`). Every present number cancels out, leaving `xor1 ^ xor2` as the missing number!\n- **2. Maximum Consecutive Ones (`O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Keep a running `cnt` and `maxi`. Increment `cnt++` when you see `1` (and update `maxi = max(maxi, cnt)`), and reset `cnt = 0` whenever you see `0`.\n- **3. Finding the Number That Appears Once (When Others Appear Twice):**\n  - Simply XOR all elements in the array together! Every pair cancels to `0`, leaving only the unique single number.\n\n### 💻 Bitwise XOR Single Number Blueprint\n```cpp\nint singleNumber(vector<int> &nums) {\n    int xorr = 0;\n    for (int num : nums) {\n        xorr = xorr ^ num; // Duplicate pairs cancel out to 0\n    }\n    return xorr;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
451	Interval Sorting & Boundary Expansion: Merge Overlapping Sub-Intervals	dsa-merge-overlapping-intervals	Sort intervals by start time and dynamically expand right boundaries in a single O(N log N) pass with O(1) extra space.	2026-10-02 23:12:19.380284+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Interval Sorting & Boundary Expansion: Merge Overlapping Sub-Intervals\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IexN60k62jo", "title": "Lecture 39: Merge Overlapping Sub-intervals", "duration": "32m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/insert-interval/", "title": "Insert Interval", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Insert a new interval into a sorted list of non-overlapping intervals and merge if necessary."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/non-overlapping-intervals/", "title": "Non-overlapping Intervals", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the minimum number of intervals to remove to make the rest non-overlapping."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/overlapping-intervals--170633/1", "title": "Overlapping Intervals", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Merge all overlapping intervals in-place and return the sorted result."}]	Module 11: Advanced Array Patterns & Hard Interview Problems	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array of intervals where `intervals[i] = [start, end]`, merge all overlapping intervals and return an array of non-overlapping intervals that cover all the original intervals.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Sort by Start Time:** Sorting intervals by their start time (`intervals.begin(), intervals.end()`) ensures that any intervals that could potentially overlap are placed next to each other.\n- **Optimal Single-Pass Merge (O(N log N) Time, O(1) Extra Space):**\n  - Iterate through the sorted intervals:\n    1. If `ans` is empty or the current interval's `start` is strictly greater than the last merged interval's `end` (`intervals[i][0] > ans.back()[1]`), it cannot overlap—push `intervals[i]` as a new interval into `ans`.\n    2. Otherwise, it overlaps with the last interval in `ans`! Expand the end boundary:\n       `ans.back()[1] = max(ans.back()[1], intervals[i][1])`.\n\n### 💻 Merge Overlapping Intervals Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<vector<int>> mergeIntervals(vector<vector<int>> &intervals) {\n    if (intervals.empty()) return {};\n    sort(intervals.begin(), intervals.end());\n    vector<vector<int>> ans;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < intervals.size(); i++) {\n        // If current interval does not overlap with last merged interval\n        if (ans.empty() || intervals[i][0] > ans.back()[1]) {\n            ans.push_back(intervals[i]);\n        } else {\n            // Overlaps: expand current boundary\n            ans.back()[1] = max(ans.back()[1], intervals[i][1]);\n        }\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
