ltiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["Yes, strict mode requires them", "No, the entire point of `useEffectEvent` is to abstract those dependencies so they don't trigger re-runs", "Only if they are primitive types", "Yes, otherwise memory leaks occur"], "question": "Are variables inside a `useEffectEvent` callback required in the `useEffect` dependency array?", "explanation": "React guarantees the function returned by useEffectEvent always sees the latest state/props without needing to be included in any dependency array.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "No, the entire point of `useEffectEvent` is to abstract those dependencies so they don't trigger re-runs"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["Yes, it behaves exactly like `useCallback`", "No, it is strictly intended to be called ONLY from within an effect in the same component", "Yes, but only in Server Components", "Yes, if wrapped in React.memo"], "question": "Can a function created with `useEffectEvent` be passed down as a prop to child components?", "explanation": "useEffectEvent functions are completely isolated to effect execution logic and will throw errors if used as event handlers or passed down as props.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "No, it is strictly intended to be called ONLY from within an effect in the same component"}]	\N	> **Architectural Deep Dive:** This lecture covers the strict technical nuances, reconciliation behavior, and performance implications of using useEffectEvent in production environments. Pay special attention to concurrent rendering behaviors in React 18+.	Web Dev Simplified	https://www.youtube.com/@WebDevSimplified	\N	New React useEffectEvent Hook	Deep dive architectural lecture focusing on the mechanics, use cases, and anti-patterns of the useEffectEvent hook.	15	Easy	Core Hooks & API	15	[]	\N	15	https://youtu.be/NZJUEzn10FI	New React useEffectEvent Hook
1648	Level-Depth Projections: Right Side View & Left Side View of a Binary Tree	dsa-right-and-left-view-of-binary-tree	Capture rightmost and leftmost nodes at each depth level in O(N) time and O(H) auxiliary space using reverse preorder DFS.	2026-10-03 01:02:00.06721+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Level-Depth Projections: Right Side View & Left Side View of a Binary Tree\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KV4mRzTjlAk", "title": "Lecture 96: Right and Left View of Binary Tree", "duration": "24m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-right-side-view/", "title": "Binary Tree Right Side View", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the values of nodes visible from the right side."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/left-view-of-binary-tree/1", "title": "Left View of Binary Tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Print the nodes visible when viewed from the left side."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-depth-of-binary-tree/", "title": "Maximum Depth of Binary Tree", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Leverage depth tracking to record the first node discovered at each level."}]	Module 17: Binary Trees: Views, Projections & Ancestry Analysis	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThe **Right View** of a binary tree contains the rightmost node at each depth level. The **Left View** contains the leftmost node at each depth level.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Reverse Preorder Trick (`Root → Right → Left` for Right View):**\n  - By visiting `Right` before `Left`, the first node we encounter at any level `level` is guaranteed to be the rightmost node on that level!\n  - Condition: If `level == ans.size()`, this is our first time reaching this depth—append `node->val` to `ans`.\n- **For Left View (`Root → Left → Right`):**\n  - Simply reverse the recursion order to visit `Left` before `Right`!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N)` Time and `O(H)` Space (auxiliary stack), which is strictly superior to queue-based `O(N)` space.\n\n### 💻 Right View (Reverse Preorder) Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid recursionRight(TreeNode* root, int level, vector<int> &res) {\n    if (root == nullptr) return;\n\n    // First time arriving at this level\n    if (res.size() == level) res.push_back(root->val);\n\n    recursionRight(root->right, level + 1, res); // Explore right first\n    recursionRight(root->left, level + 1, res);  // Explore left second\n}\n\nvector<int> rightSideView(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<int> res;\n    recursionRight(root, 0, res);\n    return res;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22214	System Design of UPI Payments	hld-upi-payments-system-design	Architectural breakdown of India's Unified Payments Interface: VPA abstractions, NPCI central switch, TPAP apps, and multi-bank 2PC transaction settlements.	2026-10-07 16:41:22.814808+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: System Design of UPI Payments\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/fqySz1Me2pI", "title": "System Design of UPI Payments", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "hld-v9-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: The VPA Abstraction and Security", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nTraditional bank transfers require the payee to disclose their exact bank account number, account holder name, and IFSC branch code. Exposing this information introduces risks of social engineering, identity theft, and fraudulent mandates.\\n\\n**The VPA Abstraction:**\\nA Virtual Payment Address (VPA, e.g., `user@okicici`) decouples identity from physical bank storage:\\n1. **Zero Exposure:** The payer and merchant only ever see the string alias; the underlying 16-digit account number and branch routing code remain hidden inside NPCI's encrypted registry.\\n2. **Portability:** A user can switch their underlying savings account from HDFC to SBI without changing their public VPA identifier.\\n3. **Interoperability:** Any TPAP app can transfer funds to any other TPAP app because the VPA resolves globally through the central NPCI directory.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "How does the Virtual Payment Address (VPA) in UPI eliminate the systemic security vulnerabilities of traditional bank transfers (NEFT/IMPS)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v9-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Roles of TPAP vs PSP Bank vs NPCI", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **TPAP (Third-Party Application Provider - e.g., PhonePe, GPay):**\\n   * Consumer-facing UI layer.\\n   * Manages user login, QR code scanning, transaction history, and initiates the payment intent.\\n   * TPAPs are NOT licensed banks and have ZERO direct access to the NPCI core financial switch.\\n\\n2. **PSP Bank (Payment Service Provider - e.g., Axis Bank, ICICI Bank):**\\n   * Licensed banking institution acting as the sponsor/gateway for the TPAP.\\n   * Validates client digital signatures and securely connects to NPCI's closed financial network on behalf of the TPAP.\\n\\n3. **NPCI Central Switch (National Payments Corporation of India):**\\n   * The central clearinghouse and routing engine.\\n   * Maintains the global lookup table mapping VPAs to Core Banking System (CBS) account numbers.\\n   * Orchestrates the inter-bank debit/credit instructions and guarantees multilateral netting and final settlement between banks.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Differentiate the architectural responsibilities between a TPAP (like Google Pay or PhonePe), a PSP Bank (like Yes Bank or ICICI), and the NPCI Central Switch in a UPI transaction.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v9-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: End-to-End Push Transaction Lifecycle", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Intent Creation:** User A enters user B's VPA and amount on PhonePe. PhonePe sends this intent to its sponsor PSP Bank (e.g., Yes Bank).\\n2. **NPCI Address Resolution:** PSP Bank routes the request to NPCI. NPCI resolves user B's VPA to their underlying bank (e.g., HDFC Bank, Account XYZ).\\n3. **PIN Entry via Common Library:** PhonePe renders NPCI's encrypted Common Library (CL) MPIN screen. The PIN is encrypted on the device hardware using the remitter bank's public key and sent securely via NPCI to the Remitter Bank.\\n4. **Debit Phase:** Remitter Bank verifies the MPIN and balance. It debits user A's account by 1,000 INR and sends a signed `Debit Success` acknowledgment to NPCI.\\n5. **Credit Phase:** NPCI sends a credit instruction to Beneficiary Bank (HDFC). HDFC credits user B's account by 1,000 INR and responds with `Credit Success`.\\n6. **Dual Confirmation:** NPCI marks the transaction `COMPLETED`, records the inter-bank settlement obligation, and broadcasts success notifications down to both Remitter and Beneficiary PSPs, which notify the mobile apps via WebSockets/push.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Trace the end-to-end lifecycle of a successful UPI Push transaction from the moment user A enters their UPI PIN to when user B receives funds.", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v9-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Handling Beneficiary Bank Downtime & Reversals", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThis represents a distributed two-phase transaction partition:\\n1. **Timeout Detection:** When NPCI sends the credit instruction to the Beneficiary Bank, it starts a strict timeout timer (e.g., 5–10 seconds). If the Beneficiary Bank times out or throws a 5xx error, the credit state is marked `UNKNOWN` or `FAILED`.\\n2. **Automatic Reversal Instruction:** NPCI refuses to mark the transaction successful. Because user A was debited, NPCI emits a compensating reversal command to the Remitter Bank: *ReverseDebit(Amount)*.\\n3. **De-escalation to Auto-Reconciliation:** If immediate reversal fails due to network partitions, the transaction enters an asynchronous clearing queue. At end-of-day reconciliation (or during automated batch settlement rounds), NPCI reconciles bank statement files. If the Beneficiary Bank confirms no credit took place, NPCI forces an automated refund (T+1 days mandate) back to User A's account, ensuring absolute mathematical balance across ledgers.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "During a UPI transaction, user A's account is debited, but user B's bank crashes before acknowledging the credit. How does the distributed transaction resolve without money vanishing?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v9-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Push vs Pull Transactions in UPI", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Push Transaction (Payer Initiated):** The payer initiates the transfer (e.g., sending money to a friend or scanning a merchant QR). The payer proactively pushes funds to the receiver.\\n\\n2. **Pull Transaction (Payee Initiated / Collect Request):** The payee initiates a payment demand (e.g., a merchant or food delivery app requests 500 INR from `user@upi`).\\n\\n**Security Controls on Pull Requests:**\\n* In a Pull transaction, the request arrives on the payer's app as a \\"Collect Request Notification\\".\\n* Money CANNOT be automatically deducted. The payer must explicitly inspect the requester's verified merchant name, approve the request, and physically enter their UPI PIN on the secure NPCI library screen.\\n* Spam Filters: NPCI and TPAPs enforce daily limits and spam heuristics to auto-block unknown VPAs from flooding users with fraudulent collect requests.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Differentiate between a Push transaction and a Pull transaction (Collect Request) in UPI architecture. What additional security controls govern Pull requests?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	High Level Design	### 📌 1. Legacy Digital Payments vs. UPI\n* **Pre-UPI Banking:** IMPS, NEFT, RTGS required sharing sensitive 16-digit account numbers, IFSC codes, and branch identifiers.\n* **UPI (Unified Payments Interface):** Built by NPCI (National Payments Corporation of India), introduces the **Virtual Payment Address (VPA)** abstraction (e.g., `username@bankhandle`) eliminating raw bank detail exposure.\n\n### 💡 2. Architectural Participants in UPI\n* **TPAP (Third-Party Application Provider):** Consumer-facing frontends (Google Pay, PhonePe, Paytm).\n* **Payer / Payee PSP (Payment Service Provider Banks):** TPAPs partner with authorized banking members (e.g., Axis, ICICI, Yes Bank) who possess direct gateway clearance into NPCI.\n* **NPCI Central Switch:** The central, secure national clearinghouse and message routing backbone that manages settlement and inter-bank handshakes.\n* **Remitter (Payer) Bank & Beneficiary (Payee) Bank:** Core Banking Systems (CBS) holding actual user checking accounts.\n\n### 💡 3. Transaction Flow & 2-Phase Commit\n* **Push Transaction (Pay):** Payer authorizes PIN on trusted NPCI library $\\rightarrow$ Remitter Bank debits funds $\\rightarrow$ NPCI instructs Beneficiary Bank to credit funds $\\rightarrow$ Dual acknowledgment triggers success.\n* **Rollback on Timeout:** If Beneficiary bank fails to acknowledge credit within timeout thresholds, NPCI triggers automatic reversal credit back to the remitter to preserve ledger consistency.	Piyush Garg	https://www.youtube.com/@piyushgarg195	28	System Design of UPI Payments	Architectural breakdown of India's Unified Payments Interface: VPA abstractions, NPCI central switch, TPAP apps, and multi-bank 2PC transaction settlements.	25	Hard	High Level Design	9	[{"id": "hld-v9-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: The VPA Abstraction and Security", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nTraditional bank transfers require the payee to disclose their exact bank account number, account holder name, and IFSC branch code. Exposing this information introduces risks of social engineering, identity theft, and fraudulent mandates.\\n\\n**The VPA Abstraction:**\\nA Virtual Payment Address (VPA, e.g., `user@okicici`) decouples identity from physical bank storage:\\n1. **Zero Exposure:** The payer and merchant only ever see the string alias; the underlying 16-digit account number and branch routing code remain hidden inside NPCI's encrypted registry.\\n2. **Portability:** A user can switch their underlying savings account from HDFC to SBI without changing their public VPA identifier.\\n3. **Interoperability:** Any TPAP app can transfer funds to any other TPAP app because the VPA resolves globally through the central NPCI directory.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "How does the Virtual Payment Address (VPA) in UPI eliminate the systemic security vulnerabilities of traditional bank transfers (NEFT/IMPS)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v9-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Roles of TPAP vs PSP Bank vs NPCI", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **TPAP (Third-Party Application Provider - e.g., PhonePe, GPay):**\\n   * Consumer-facing UI layer.\\n   * Manages user login, QR code scanning, transaction history, and initiates the payment intent.\\n   * TPAPs are NOT licensed banks and have ZERO direct access to the NPCI core financial switch.\\n\\n2. **PSP Bank (Payment Service Provider - e.g., Axis Bank, ICICI Bank):**\\n   * Licensed banking institution acting as the sponsor/gateway for the TPAP.\\n   * Validates client digital signatures and securely connects to NPCI's closed financial network on behalf of the TPAP.\\n\\n3. **NPCI Central Switch (National Payments Corporation of India):**\\n   * The central clearinghouse and routing engine.\\n   * Maintains the global lookup table mapping VPAs to Core Banking System (CBS) account numbers.\\n   * Orchestrates the inter-bank debit/credit instructions and guarantees multilateral netting and final settlement between banks.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Differentiate the architectural responsibilities between a TPAP (like Google Pay or PhonePe), a PSP Bank (like Yes Bank or ICICI), and the NPCI Central Switch in a UPI transaction.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v9-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: End-to-End Push Transaction Lifecycle", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Intent Creation:** User A enters user B's VPA and amount on PhonePe. PhonePe sends this intent to its sponsor PSP Bank (e.g., Yes Bank).\\n2. **NPCI Address Resolution:** PSP Bank routes the request to NPCI. NPCI resolves user B's VPA to their underlying bank (e.g., HDFC Bank, Account XYZ).\\n3. **PIN Entry via Common Library:** PhonePe renders NPCI's encrypted Common Library (CL) MPIN screen. The PIN is encrypted on the device hardware using the remitter bank's public key and sent securely via NPCI to the Remitter Bank.\\n4. **Debit Phase:** Remitter Bank verifies the MPIN and balance. It debits user A's account by 1,000 INR and sends a signed `Debit Success` acknowledgment to NPCI.\\n5. **Credit Phase:** NPCI sends a credit instruction to Beneficiary Bank (HDFC). HDFC credits user B's account by 1,000 INR and responds with `Credit Success`.\\n6. **Dual Confirmation:** NPCI marks the transaction `COMPLETED`, records the inter-bank settlement obligation, and broadcasts success notifications down to both Remitter and Beneficiary PSPs, which notify the mobile apps via WebSockets/push.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Trace the end-to-end lifecycle of a successful UPI Push transaction from the moment user A enters their UPI PIN to when user B receives funds.", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v9-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Handling Beneficiary Bank Downtime & Reversals", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThis represents a distributed two-phase transaction partition:\\n1. **Timeout Detection:** When NPCI sends the credit instruction to the Beneficiary Bank, it starts a strict timeout timer (e.g., 5–10 seconds). If the Beneficiary Bank times out or throws a 5xx error, the credit state is marked `UNKNOWN` or `FAILED`.\\n2. **Automatic Reversal Instruction:** NPCI refuses to mark the transaction successful. Because user A was debited, NPCI emits a compensating reversal command to the Remitter Bank: *ReverseDebit(Amount)*.\\n3. **De-escalation to Auto-Reconciliation:** If immediate reversal fails due to network partitions, the transaction enters an asynchronous clearing queue. At end-of-day reconciliation (or during automated batch settlement rounds), NPCI reconciles bank statement files. If the Beneficiary Bank confirms no credit took place, NPCI forces an automated refund (T+1 days mandate) back to User A's account, ensuring absolute mathematical balance across ledgers.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "During a UPI transaction, user A's account is debited, but user B's bank crashes before acknowledging the credit. How does the distributed transaction resolve without money vanishing?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v9-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Push vs Pull Transactions in UPI", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Push Transaction (Payer Initiated):** The payer initiates the transfer (e.g., sending money to a friend or scanning a merchant QR). The payer proactively pushes funds to the receiver.\\n\\n2. **Pull Transaction (Payee Initiated / Collect Request):** The payee initiates a payment demand (e.g., a merchant or food delivery app requests 500 INR from `user@upi`).\\n\\n**Security Controls on Pull Requests:**\\n* In a Pull transaction, the request arrives on the payer's app as a \\"Collect Request Notification\\".\\n* Money CANNOT be automatically deducted. The payer must explicitly inspect the requester's verified merchant name, approve the request, and physically enter their UPI PIN on the secure NPCI library screen.\\n* Spam Filters: NPCI and TPAPs enforce daily limits and spam heuristics to auto-block unknown VPAs from flooding