11587	Square-Root Factor Pairing: Print All Divisors of a Number	dsa-print-all-divisors-of-a-number	Master Square-Root Factor Pairing: Print All Divisors of a Number with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:12:24.302327+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Square-Root Factor Pairing: Print All Divisors of a Number\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ae_Ag_saG9s", "title": "Square-Root Factor Pairing: Print All Divisors of a Number - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/all-divisors-of-a-number/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: All divisors of a Number", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Given an integer N, print all the divisors of N in ascending order in O(sqrt(N)) time."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/the-kth-factor-of-n/", "title": "LeetCode 1492: The kth Factor of n", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find all factors of n using the sqrt(n) pairing property and return the k-th smallest factor."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/perfect-number/", "title": "LeetCode 507: Perfect Number", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Sum all positive divisors of num (excluding num itself) in O(sqrt(num)) time to check if num is a perfect number."}]	Module 39: Mathematical & Number Theory Algorithms: Divisors, Primes, Binary Exponentiation & Sieve Precomputation	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a positive integer `N`, find and return **all divisors of `N`**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute O(N) → Optimal O(sqrt(N)))\n- **The Complementary Divisor Pair Observation (`i` and `N / i`):**\n  - Divisors always come in pairs that multiply to `N`! For `N = 36`:\n    - `1 * 36 = 36`\n    - `2 * 18 = 36`\n    - `3 * 12 = 36`\n    - `4 * 9  = 36`\n    - `6 * 6  = 36` (the turning point at `sqrt(36) = 6`!).\n  - Notice that in every pair `(i, N / i)`, the smaller divisor `i` is **at most `sqrt(N)`**!\n  - Therefore, we only need to loop `i` from `1` up to **`i * i <= N`**! Whenever `N % i == 0`, we immediately get **two divisors at once**: **`i` and `N / i`**!\n- **The Perfect-Square Duplicate Guard (`if ((N / i) != i)`):**\n  - When `N = 36` and `i = 6`, both `i` and `N / i` equal `6`. Always check **`if ((N / i) != i)`** before pushing `N / i` so `6` is not added twice!\n- **Why Write `i * i <= N` Instead of `i <= sqrt(N)`?**\n  - `sqrt(N)` calls an STL floating-point library function on every iteration, whereas `i * i <= N` uses fast integer multiplication!\n- **Complexity:** `O(sqrt(N))` Time (plus `O(D log D)` if sorting the `D` divisors) and `O(D)` Space for the output list.\n\n### 💻 Print All Divisors in O(sqrt(N)) Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> printDivisors(int n) {\n    vector<int> divisors;\n\n    // Loop up to sqrt(n) using i * i <= n\n    for (int i = 1; i * i <= n; i++) {\n        if (n % i == 0) {\n            divisors.push_back(i);\n\n            // Add the complementary divisor (n / i) if it is distinct from i\n            if ((n / i) != i) {\n                divisors.push_back(n / i);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Optional: sort if ascending order is required\n    sort(divisors.begin(), divisors.end());\n    return divisors;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting `if ((n / i) != i)`:** On every perfect square (`1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36...`), omitting this check pushes the square root twice into your divisor list!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
591	Rotated Array Binary Search II: Handling Duplicate Boundary Ambiguity (low == mid == high)	dsa-search-in-rotated-sorted-array-duplicates	Search in a rotated sorted array containing duplicates by shrinking ambiguous low and high boundaries when arr[low] == arr[mid] == arr[high].	2026-10-02 23:24:14.982492+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Rotated Array Binary Search II: Handling Duplicate Boundary Ambiguity (low == mid == high)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=w2G2W8l__pc", "title": "Lecture 49: Search in Rotated Sorted Array II with Duplicates", "duration": "28m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-minimum-in-rotated-sorted-array-ii/", "title": "Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array II", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Handle the low == mid == high duplicate edge case while finding the minimum element."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-in-mountain-array/", "title": "Find in Mountain Array", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Combine peak finding with binary search on ascending and descending halves."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/search-in-rotated-array-2/1", "title": "Search in Rotated Array 2", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Check whether a target key exists in a rotated sorted array containing duplicate values."}]	Module 12: 1D Binary Search, Bounds & Rotated Array Invariants	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhat happens to "Search in a Rotated Sorted Array" when the array contains **duplicate elements** (e.g., `[3, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 3]`)?\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Where Part I's Logic Breaks Down:**\n  - In Part I, we compared `arr[low] <= arr[mid]` to tell which half was sorted.\n  - When duplicates exist, we can hit the edge case: **`arr[low] == arr[mid] && arr[mid] == arr[high]`** (for example, all three are `3`).\n  - When `low`, `mid`, and `high` all have the exact same value, it is impossible to tell whether the left half or the right half is sorted!\n- **The 3-Line Shrink Fix:**\n  - Since we already checked `if (arr[mid] == target)` at the top of the loop, if `arr[low] == arr[mid] && arr[mid] == arr[high]`, then `arr[low]` and `arr[high]` are also **not** the target!\n  - Simply shrink both ends inward: **`low++`, `high--`, and `continue`** to the next iteration until the tie is broken!\n- **Time Complexity:**\n  - **Average Case:** `O(log2 N)`.\n  - **Worst Case:** `O(N / 2) → O(N)` when almost all elements are identical (e.g., `[3, 3, 1, 3, 3, 3, 3]`) and we have to shrink `low++` and `high--` step by step.\n\n### 💻 Search in Rotated Sorted Array II Blueprint\n```cpp\nbool searchRotatedWithDuplicates(vector<int> &arr, int target) {\n    int low = 0, high = (int)arr.size() - 1;\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (arr[mid] == target) return true;\n\n        // Edge case caused by duplicates: shrink both boundaries\n        if (arr[low] == arr[mid] && arr[mid] == arr[high]) {\n            low++;\n            high--;\n            continue;\n        }\n        if (arr[low] <= arr[mid]) {\n            if (arr[low] <= target && target <= arr[mid]) high = mid - 1;\n            else low = mid + 1;\n        } else {\n            if (arr[mid] <= target && target <= arr[high]) low = mid + 1;\n            else high = mid - 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
592	Pivot Minimum Extraction: Finding the Minimum Element in a Rotated Sorted Array	dsa-minimum-in-rotated-sorted-array	Find the minimum element in a rotated sorted array in O(log N) time by repeatedly recording the lowest value of the sorted half and eliminating it.	2026-10-02 23:24:14.987504+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Pivot Minimum Extraction: Finding the Minimum Element in a Rotated Sorted Array\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nhEMDKMB44g", "title": "Lecture 50: Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-minimum-in-rotated-sorted-array-ii/", "title": "Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array II", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find the minimum element in a rotated sorted array that may contain duplicates."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-peak-element/", "title": "Find Peak Element", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use binary search to find a local maximum in O(log N) time."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/rotation4723/1", "title": "Find Kth Rotation", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Find how many times a sorted array has been rotated by locating the index of its minimum element."}]	Module 12: 1D Binary Search, Bounds & Rotated Array Invariants	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a rotated sorted array of unique elements (e.g., `[4, 5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 2]`), find the **minimum element** in **O(log2 N) time**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Core Idea ("Pick the Minimum from the Sorted Half & Eliminate It"):**\n  - Maintain `ans = INT_MAX`.\n  - At every step `mid`, identify which half is sorted:\n    1. **If the Left Half `[low ... mid]` is sorted (`arr[low] <= arr[mid]`):**\n       - Because `[low ... mid]` is sorted, its smallest element is guaranteed to be `arr[low]`!\n       - Update `ans = min(ans, arr[low])`, then **eliminate the entire left half** (`low = mid + 1`) and search the right half.\n    2. **Otherwise, the Right Half `[mid ... high]` is sorted:**\n       - Because `[mid ... high]` is sorted, its smallest element is guaranteed to be `arr[mid]`!\n       - Update `ans = min(ans, arr[mid])`, then **eliminate the entire right half** (`high = mid - 1`) and search the left half.\n- **Early Exit Optimization:**\n  - If at any point `arr[low] <= arr[high]`, the entire remaining search space `[low ... high]` is already sorted—simply take `ans = min(ans, arr[low])` and `break` immediately!\n\n### 💻 Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array Blueprint\n```cpp\nint findMin(vector<int> &arr) {\n    int low = 0, high = (int)arr.size() - 1, ans = INT_MAX;\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n\n        // Optimization: entire current search space [low...high] is already sorted\n        if (arr[low] <= arr[high]) {\n            ans = min(ans, arr[low]);\n            break;\n        }\n        // Left half is sorted: take arr[low] and eliminate left half\n        if (arr[low] <= arr[mid]) {\n            ans = min(ans, arr[low]);\n            low = mid + 1;\n        }\n        // Right half is sorted: take arr[mid] and eliminate right half\n        else {\n            ans = min(ans, arr[mid]);\n            high = mid - 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
