>`, `<head>`, and `<body>`.	CodeHelp - by Babbar	https://www.youtube.com/@CodeHelp	\N	HTML5 Document Structure & Development Setup	Modern development environment setup with VS Code and Live Server, document declarations (<!DOCTYPE html>), and core page architecture.	33	Easy	HTML Basics	1	[]	\N	33	https://youtu.be/0gU-qrq3gjU	HTML5 Document Structure & Development Setup
9273	Reverse-Elimination Partition DP: Burst Balloons	dsa-burst-balloons-partition-dp	Solve the Burst Balloons problem by considering which balloon bursts last in an interval [i..j], keeping subproblems independent.	2026-10-03 14:56:36.879529+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Reverse-Elimination Partition DP: Burst Balloons\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Yz4LlDSlkns", "title": "Lecture 231: Burst Balloons | Partition DP (DP 51)", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/burst-balloons/", "title": "Burst Balloons (LC 312)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Return the maximum coins you can collect by bursting all balloons wisely."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/burst-balloons/1", "title": "Burst Balloons", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find the maximum coins obtainable by bursting N balloons in an optimal sequence."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-cost-to-merge-stones/", "title": "Minimum Cost to Merge Stones (LC 1000)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Practice bottom-up interval partition DP where subproblems depend on surviving outer boundaries."}]	Module 34: Partition Dynamic Programming (MCM Pattern & Interval Splitting)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nYou are given `n` balloons indexed `0` to `n - 1`, each painted with a number `a[i]`. If you burst balloon `i`, you earn **`a[i - 1] * a[i] * a[i + 1]`** coins (where out-of-bounds neighbors at `-1` and `n` have a virtual value of `1`), and afterward balloons `i - 1` and `i + 1` become adjacent! Find the **maximum coins** you can collect by bursting all balloons.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Choosing Which Balloon to Burst FIRST Fails:**\n  - Suppose you burst balloon `ind` **first** in `[b1, b2, b3, b4, b5]`. Once `b3` disappears, `b2` (in the left subarray) and `b4` (in the right subarray) become adjacent!\n  - Because bursting a balloon in the left subarray now depends on which balloons survive in the right subarray, the left and right subproblems `[i .. ind - 1]` and `[ind + 1 .. j]` are **NOT independent**!\n- **The Genius Fix — Choose Which Balloon in `[i .. j]` Is Burst LAST!**\n  - Think in reverse: in the range `[i .. j]`, suppose `ind` is the **very last balloon burst** inside `[i .. j]`.\n  - When `ind` is burst last in `[i .. j]`, every other balloon in `[i .. j]` has already been burst, while the boundary balloons **`i - 1` and `j + 1` just outside the range `[i .. j]` are still alive**!\n  - Therefore:\n    1. Coins earned when `ind` bursts last in `[i .. j]`: **`a[i - 1] * a[ind] * a[j + 1]`**!\n    2. While `ind` is still alive separating `[i .. ind - 1]` and `[ind + 1 .. j]`, the left and right subproblems are **100% independent** (`f(i, ind - 1) + f(ind + 1, j)`)!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N³)` Time and `O(N²)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Burst Balloons (Bottom-Up Tabulation) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint maxCoins(vector<int> &a) {\n    int n = a.size();\n    // 1. Pad array with 1 at the front (index 0) and 1 at the back (index n + 1)\n    a.push_back(1);\n    a.insert(a.begin(), 1);\n\n    vector<vector<int>> dp(n + 2, vector<int>(n + 2, 0));\n\n    // 2. Bottom-up tabulation: i from n down to 1, j from 1 to n\n    for (int i = n; i >= 1; i--) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {\n            if (i > j) continue; // Base case: empty range yields 0 coins\n\n            int maxi = INT_MIN;\n            // Try every balloon 'ind' as the LAST balloon burst in [i .. j]\n            for (int ind = i; ind <= j; ind++) {\n                int cost = a[i - 1] * a[ind] * a[j + 1]\n                           + dp[i][ind - 1]\n                           + dp[ind + 1][j];\n                maxi = max(maxi, cost);\n            }\n            dp[i][j] = maxi;\n        }\n    }\n    return dp[1][n];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Multiplying `a[ind - 1] * a[ind] * a[ind + 1]` Instead of `a[i - 1] * a[ind] * a[j + 1]`:** Because `ind` is the **last** balloon burst in the interval `[i .. j]`, all balloons between `i .. ind - 1` and `ind + 1 .. j` are already gone! Its immediate surviving neighbors at the moment it bursts are **`i - 1` and `j + 1`**.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
