l scaling adds more power (RAM/CPU) to a single machine; horizontal scaling adds more independent server instances to a pool."}, {"id": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["It minifies JavaScript and CSS.", "It acts as a reverse proxy, distributing incoming client traffic efficiently across multiple backend servers.", "It compresses database tables.", "It acts as a primary DNS resolver."], "question": "What architectural role does a Load Balancer play?", "explanation": "A load balancer stands in front of a cluster of servers, preventing any single server from becoming a bottleneck by routing requests using algorithms like Round-Robin.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "It acts as a reverse proxy, distributing incoming client traffic efficiently across multiple backend servers."}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["To execute backend logic near the user.", "To store encrypted passwords safely.", "To cache and deliver static assets (images, videos, JS) from edge servers physically closer to the end-user.", "To resolve IP addresses."], "question": "What is the primary function of a Content Delivery Network (CDN)?", "explanation": "CDNs replicate static content across global Points of Presence (PoPs). A user in Japan will download an image from a Tokyo CDN node instead of waiting for a server in New York.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "To cache and deliver static assets (images, videos, JS) from edge servers physically closer to the end-user."}, {"id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "A", "options": ["Pull CDNs automatically fetch missing content from the origin server on demand; Push CDNs require developers to actively upload assets to the CDN ahead of time.", "Pull CDNs are used for desktops; Push CDNs are for mobile devices.", "Pull CDNs fetch data from databases; Push CDNs push data to users.", "There is no practical difference."], "question": "What distinguishes a 'Pull CDN' from a 'Push CDN'?", "explanation": "In a Pull CDN, the edge server automatically proxies the request to the origin if it lacks the file, caches it, and serves it. In a Push CDN, you manage the storage actively.", "correctAnswer": "A", "correct_index": 0, "correct_answer": "Pull CDNs automatically fetch missing content from the origin server on demand; Push CDNs require developers to actively upload assets to the CDN ahead of time."}, {"id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["By permanently archiving old database tables to tape drives.", "By temporarily storing frequently queried results in exceptionally fast, in-memory datastores like Redis or Memcached.", "By forcing the client's browser to store the entire SQL database.", "By bypassing the load balancer."], "question": "How does database caching reduce backend latency?", "explanation": "Fetching data from a disk-based SQL database is computationally heavy. Caching stores the pre-computed query result in RAM, drastically reducing retrieval time to milliseconds.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "By temporarily storing frequently queried results in exceptionally fast, in-memory datastores like Redis or Memcached."}]	\N	> **Architecture Overview:** Essential technical concepts and network principles vital for robust, scalable web architectures. Focus on the core mechanics and trade-offs of the system discussed.	Hayk Simonyan	https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan	\N	6 System Design Interview Concepts	Deep dive into 6 System Design Interview Concepts covering architectural implementations, networking, and system resilience.	10	Easy	Advanced System Concepts	12	[]	\N	10	https://youtu.be/HpE0TAuBv3c	6 System Design Interview Concepts
