all N days."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/daily-temperatures/", "title": "LeetCode 739: Daily Temperatures", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Compare looking for the Next Greater Element (Daily Temperatures) with the Previous Greater Element (Stock Span)."}]	Module 44: Advanced Stack, Monotonic Deque & Cache Design: Stock Span, Sliding Window Max, Celebrity & LRU/LFU Caches	### 📌 Topic Overview\nDesign a `StockSpanner` class where each call to `next(int price)` returns the **span** of the stock's price today—defined as the **maximum number of consecutive days** (starting from today and going backward) for which the stock price was **less than or equal to (`<=`)** today's price.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute O(N²) → Optimal Monotonic PGE Stack O(2N) Total)\n- **Reframing Stock Span as Previous Greater Element (PGE):**\n  - What stops the consecutive backward streak of days with price `<= today's price`?\n  - The **first day in the past whose price was STRICTLY GREATER than today's price (PGE)**!\n  - Therefore, if today is at 0-based index `ind` and the **Previous Greater Element** sits at index `pgeIndex` (or `-1` if no previous day was strictly greater), then the number of consecutive valid days is simply:\n    **`span = ind - pgeIndex`**!\n- **Maintaining a Monotonic Decreasing Stack of `{price, index}`:**\n  - Maintain `stack<pair<int, int>> st` and `ind = -1`.\n  - On each `next(val)` call:\n    1. Increment **`ind++`**.\n    2. Pop all previous days with price **`<= val`**:\n       `while (!st.empty() && st.top().first <= val) st.pop();`\n    3. Compute `ans = ind - (st.empty() ? -1 : st.top().second);`.\n    4. Push `st.push({val, ind});` and return `ans`.\n- **Complexity:** **`O(2N)`** overall time across `N` calls to `next()` (**`O(1)` amortized per call**) and `O(N)` space.\n\n### 💻 Online Stock Span Blueprint\n```cpp\nclass StockSpanner {\n    stack<pair<int, int>> st; // Stores {price, dayIndex}\n    int ind;\n\npublic:\n    StockSpanner() {\n        ind = -1;\n    }\n\n    int next(int val) {\n        ind = ind + 1;\n\n        // Pop all previous days whose price is <= today's price (find Previous Greater Element)\n        while (!st.empty() && st.top().first <= val) {\n            st.pop();\n        }\n\n        int pgeIndex = st.empty() ? -1 : st.top().second;\n        int ans = ind - pgeIndex;\n\n        st.push({val, ind});\n        return ans;\n    }\n};\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Pushing `{val, ind}` Onto the Stack BEFORE Computing `ans`:** If you push `{val, ind}` before reading `st.top().second`, `st.top()` will be `{val, ind}` itself instead of the Previous Greater Element! Always compute `ans` **before** `st.push({val, ind})`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
10786	Bidirectional Target Deletion: Delete All Occurrences of a Key in a Doubly Linked List	dsa-delete-all-occurrences-of-key-in-doubly-linked-list	Delete all occurrences of a target key in a doubly linked list in a single pass with head shifting, pointer re-wiring, and null-boundary guards.	2026-10-03 15:07:44.952849+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Bidirectional Target Deletion: Delete All Occurrences of a Key in a Doubly Linked List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Mh0NH_SD92k", "title": "Lecture 254: Delete All Occurrences of a Key in Doubly Linked List", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/delete-all-occurrences-of-a-given-key-in-a-doubly-linked-list/1", "title": "Delete all occurrences of a given key in a doubly linked list", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Delete all occurrences of a given key x in a doubly linked list and return the updated head."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/remove-linked-list-elements/", "title": "Remove Linked List Elements (LC 203)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Remove all nodes of a singly linked list that have Node.val == val and return the new head."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-nodes-from-linked-list-present-in-array/", "title": "Delete Nodes From Linked List Present in Array (LC 3217)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Delete all occurrences of a set of target keys from a linked list in a single pass."}]	Module 38: Doubly Linked List Medium Problems & Hard Linked List Re-Wiring (K-Group Reversal, Rotation & Multi-Level Flattening)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the `head` of a **Doubly Linked List (DLL)** and an integer `key`, delete **every node** whose value equals `key`, re-wire the remaining `next` and `prev` pointers, and return the updated `head`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Handling Head Updates & Null Boundary Guards (`O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Traverse `temp` starting from `head`.\n  - Whenever **`temp->data == key`**:\n    1. **Head Check (`if (temp == head)`):** If `temp` is currently the `head` of the DLL, advance **`head = head->next;`** so `head` never points to a deleted node!\n    2. Grab neighbors: `Node* nextNode = temp->next;` and `Node* prevNode = temp->prev;`.\n    3. **Link `nextNode` back to `prevNode` (if `nextNode` exists):**\n       `if (nextNode != nullptr) nextNode->prev = prevNode;`\n    4. **Link `prevNode` forward to `nextNode` (if `prevNode` exists):**\n       `if (prevNode != nullptr) prevNode->next = nextNode;`\n    5. Free memory (`delete temp;` or `free(temp);`) and advance **`temp = nextNode;`**.\n  - Else (`temp->data != key`): simply advance `temp = temp->next;`.\n\n### 💻 Delete All Occurrences of a Key in DLL Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nNode* deleteAllOccurrences(Node* head, int key) {\n    Node* temp = head;\n\n    while (temp != nullptr) {\n        if (temp->data == key) {\n            // 1. If deleting the current head, shift head forward first!\n            if (temp == head) {\n                head = head->next;\n            }\n\n            Node* nextNode = temp->next;\n            Node* prevNode = temp->prev;\n\n            // 2. Re-wire neighbors with null-pointer guards\n            if (nextNode != nullptr) nextNode->prev = prevNode;\n            if (prevNode != nullptr) prevNode->next = nextNode;\n\n            delete temp;\n            temp = nextNode;\n        } else {\n            temp = temp->next;\n        }\n    }\n    return head;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Only Checking `if (temp == head)` Once Before the Loop:** If the DLL starts with **multiple consecutive `key` nodes** (e.g., `10 <-> 10 <-> 4` with `key = 10`), the second `10` becomes the *new* `head` after the first `10` is deleted! Checking `if (temp == head)` **inside** the loop handles any number of leading `key` nodes automatically.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
