: "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["Rendering CSS animations", "Executing heavy machine learning models", "Authentication, Rate Limiting, and SSL Termination", "Storing raw video files"], "question": "Which of the following is a common cross-cutting concern typically handled by an API Gateway?", "explanation": "The API Gateway centralizes tasks that every microservice needs. Instead of building rate limiting and JWT validation into every single microservice, the Gateway handles it once at the front door.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "Authentication, Rate Limiting, and SSL Termination"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["It limits the size of JSON payloads", "It restricts the number of API requests a client can make within a specific time window, protecting backend services from DDoS attacks and abuse", "It slows down the database queries to save CPU", "It limits the number of routes a developer can create"], "question": "What does 'Rate Limiting' at the API Gateway level achieve?", "explanation": "Rate limiting (e.g., 100 requests per minute) ensures fair usage and prevents a single malicious or malfunctioning client from crashing the backend infrastructure.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "It restricts the number of API requests a client can make within a specific time window, protecting backend services from DDoS attacks and abuse"}, {"id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["Combining multiple CSS files into one", "Receiving a single client request, calling multiple microservices simultaneously, combining their responses into one JSON object, and sending it back to the client", "Caching all API responses in local storage", "Automatically retrying failed requests"], "question": "What is 'Response Aggregation' (or Data Fetching) in an API Gateway?", "explanation": "Response aggregation significantly reduces network latency for mobile devices by allowing the client to make just one request, while the gateway handles the complex, multi-service fetching over the fast internal network.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "Receiving a single client request, calling multiple microservices simultaneously, combining their responses into one JSON object, and sending it back to the client"}, {"id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["Because it forces the use of HTTP/1.1", "Because it is a Single Point of Failure; if the gateway goes down, all frontend clients are completely cut off from all backend services", "Because it requires manual routing tables written in binary", "Because it limits the backend to a single programming language"], "question": "Why might an API Gateway become a bottleneck?", "explanation": "Because all traffic flows through the API Gateway, it must be highly available and clustered. If it crashes, the entire application appears offline to users, even if the backend microservices are healthy.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "Because it is a Single Point of Failure; if the gateway goes down, all frontend clients are completely cut off from all backend services"}]	\N	> **Architecture Overview:** Essential systems architecture principles governing how modern web applications communicate, route data, balance loads, and scale effectively.	Hayk Simonyan	https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan	\N	API Gateway Explained	Core system design concepts focusing on api gateway and distributed network architecture.	10	Easy	Web Architecture	9	[]	\N	10	https://youtu.be/20tpk8A_xa0	API Gateway Explained
3027	Postorder Subtree Envelope Aggregation: Largest BST in a Binary Tree	dsa-largest-bst-in-binary-tree	Find the size of the largest subtree that is a valid BST in an arbitrary binary tree using bottom-up postorder property aggregation in O(N) time.	2026-10-03 01:35:42.034781+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Postorder Subtree Envelope Aggregation: Largest BST in a Binary Tree\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X0oXMdtUDwo", "title": "Lecture 125: Largest BST in Binary Tree", "duration": "26m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/largest-bst/1", "title": "Largest BST", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the size (number of nodes) of the largest subtree that is a valid BST."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-sum-bst-in-binary-tree/", "title": "Maximum Sum BST in Binary Tree (LC 1373)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Apply the exact postorder {minNode, maxNode, maxSum} tuple technique to find the maximum sum BST."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/largest-bst-subtree/", "title": "Largest BST Subtree (LC 333)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the number of nodes in the largest subtree that is a Binary Search Tree."}]	Module 20: Advanced Binary Search Tree Operations & Validation	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an arbitrary Binary Tree, find the **size (number of nodes) of the largest subtree that is also a valid Binary Search Tree (BST)**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Optimal)\n- **1. Brute Force (`O(N²)` Time):** At every node, call `isValidBST(node)` (`O(N)`) and if valid, count its nodes.\n- **2. Optimal Postorder Bottom-Up Aggregation (`O(N)` Time, `O(H)` Space):**\n  - For a subtree rooted at `root` to be a valid BST, we need info from both its `left` and `right` subtrees *first*—so we use **Postorder Traversal (`Left → Right → Root`)**!\n  - Every recursive call returns a `NodeValue(minNode, maxNode, maxSize)` object:\n    - **Base Case (`root == nullptr`):** Return `NodeValue(INT_MAX, INT_MIN, 0)` so an empty child always satisfies `left.maxNode < root->val < right.minNode`.\n    - **Valid BST Condition:** If **`left.maxNode < root->val && root->val < right.minNode`**, then `root` forms a valid BST! Return:\n      - `minNode = min(root->val, left.minNode)`\n      - `maxNode = max(root->val, right.maxNode)`\n      - `maxSize = left.maxSize + right.maxSize + 1`\n    - **Invalid BST Condition:** Otherwise, `root` is NOT a BST. Poison the bounds by returning `NodeValue(INT_MIN, INT_MAX, max(left.maxSize, right.maxSize))` so no ancestor can ever include `root` in a valid BST, while preserving the largest BST size found so far!\n\n### 💻 Largest BST Subtree Blueprint\n```cpp\nclass NodeValue {\npublic:\n    int minNode, maxNode, maxSize;\n    NodeValue(int minNode, int maxNode, int maxSize)\n        : minNode(minNode), maxNode(maxNode), maxSize(maxSize) {}\n};\n\nclass Solution {\n    NodeValue helper(TreeNode* root) {\n        if (!root) return NodeValue(INT_MAX, INT_MIN, 0);\n\n        auto left = helper(root->left);\n        auto right = helper(root->right);\n\n        // Current subtree is a valid BST\n        if (left.maxNode < root->val && root->val < right.minNode) {\n            return NodeValue(\n                min(root->val, left.minNode),\n                max(root->val, right.maxNode),\n                left.maxSize + right.maxSize + 1\n            );\n        }\n        // Not a BST: poison bounds [INT_MIN, INT_MAX] so ancestors fail check\n        return NodeValue(INT_MIN, INT_MAX, max(left.maxSize, right.maxSize));\n    }\n\npublic:\n    int largestBst(TreeNode* root) {\n        return helper(root).maxSize;\n    }\n};\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Swapping Base Case Bounds (`INT_MAX` vs. `INT_MIN`):** For a `nullptr` node, `minNode` must be `INT_MAX` and `maxNode` must be `INT_MIN` so that leaf checks (`INT_MIN < leaf->val < INT_MAX`) succeed. Conversely, when poisoning an invalid subtree, return `minNode = INT_MIN` and `maxNode = INT_MAX` so parent checks always fail!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
