iculty": "Medium", "description": "Group swappable character indices using DSU, sort characters within each component, and reconstruct the string."}]	Module 26: Disjoint Set Union (DSU), Kruskal's MST & Dynamic Connectivity	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a list of `N` accounts where `accounts[i][0]` is a person's name and the remaining strings `accounts[i][1...]` are their emails, merge any two accounts that share **at least one common email** and return each merged account with its emails sorted alphabetically.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Treat Account Indices `0 .. N - 1` as DSU Nodes:**\n  - Maintain a hash map `unordered_map<string, int> mapMailNode` that maps each email string to the account index `i` where it was first seen.\n- **3-Step DSU Pipeline:**\n  1. **Map & Union:** For each account `i` and each of its emails `mail` (starting at index `j = 1`):\n     - If `mail` is not in `mapMailNode`, set `mapMailNode[mail] = i`.\n     - If `mail` **already exists** in `mapMailNode`, union the current account `i` with the previous owner: **`ds.unionBySize(i, mapMailNode[mail])`**!\n  2. **Group Emails by Ultimate Parent:** Iterate through `{mail, node}` in `mapMailNode` and append `mail` into `mergedMail[ds.findUPar(node)]`.\n  3. **Sort & Format Output:** For each account index `i` from `0` to `N - 1`, if `mergedMail[i]` is non-empty, sort `mergedMail[i]`, prepend the owner's name `accounts[i][0]`, and push into `ans`.\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * K * log(N * K))` Time (where `K` is average emails per account) and `O(N * K)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Accounts Merge (DSU) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nvector<vector<string>> accountsMerge(vector<vector<string>> &details) {\n    int n = details.size();\n    DisjointSet ds(n);\n    unordered_map<string, int> mapMailNode;\n\n    // 1. Map emails to account index and union accounts sharing an email\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j < details[i].size(); j++) {\n            string mail = details[i][j];\n            if (mapMailNode.find(mail) == mapMailNode.end()) {\n                mapMailNode[mail] = i;\n            } else {\n                ds.unionBySize(i, mapMailNode[mail]);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // 2. Group unique emails under their ultimate parent account index\n    vector<string> mergedMail[n];\n    for (const auto &it : mapMailNode) {\n        string mail = it.first;\n        int uNode = ds.findUPar(it.second);\n        mergedMail[uNode].push_back(mail);\n    }\n\n    // 3. Sort each merged account's emails and attach the account holder's name\n    vector<vector<string>> ans;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        if (mergedMail[i].empty()) continue;\n        sort(mergedMail[i].begin(), mergedMail[i].end());\n        vector<string> temp;\n        temp.push_back(details[i][0]);\n        for (const string &mail : mergedMail[i]) {\n            temp.push_back(mail);\n        }\n        ans.push_back(temp);\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Starting the Email Loop at `j = 0` Instead of `j = 1`:** Index `0` (`details[i][0]`) is the **person's name**, not an email! Different people can have the exact same name (e.g., `"John"`), so never union accounts by name—always start the email loop at **`j = 1`**!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
3433	Recursive Depth-First Exploration: Depth-First Search (DFS) in Graphs	dsa-depth-first-search-dfs-in-graphs	Explore graphs deeply using recursive backtracking with a visited array in O(V + 2E) time.	2026-10-03 01:51:47.340466+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Recursive Depth-First Exploration: Depth-First Search (DFS) in Graphs\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Qzf1a--rhp8", "title": "Lecture 131: Depth First Search (DFS) in Graph", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/depth-first-traversal-for-a-graph/1", "title": "DFS of Graph", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return a list containing the DFS traversal of a connected undirected graph starting from vertex 0."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/keys-and-rooms/", "title": "Keys and Rooms (LC 841)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use recursive DFS to check if all rooms in a directed graph can be visited starting from room 0."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-if-path-exists-in-graph/", "title": "Find if Path Exists in Graph (LC 1971)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Apply recursive DFS with a visited array to verify reachability between two vertices."}]	Module 21: Graph Theory Foundations, Representations & Traversals	### 📌 Topic Overview\n**Depth-First Search (DFS)** explores a graph by diving as deep as possible along each branch before backtracking. Starting at a source vertex, it recursively visits an unvisited neighbor, explores that neighbor's entire connected path to the bottom, and then backtracks to explore remaining neighbors.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Core Recursive Mechanism:**\n  1. Maintain a `seen` (visited) array initialized to `0` so vertices are never processed twice.\n  2. Upon entering `explore(u)`, mark `seen[u] = 1` and append `u` to the result list.\n  3. Loop through each neighbor `v` in `adjList[u]`: if `!seen[v]`, recursively call `explore(v)`.\n- **Time & Space Complexity:**\n  - **Time Complexity:** `O(V + 2E)` for an undirected graph (each vertex is visited once, and all incident edges summing to total degree `2E` are checked) or `O(V + E)` for a directed graph.\n  - **Space Complexity:** `O(V)` for the `seen` array, output list, and worst-case recursion call stack depth on a skewed graph.\n\n### 💻 Graph DFS Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid exploreDFS(int curr, const vector<int> adjList[], vector<int> &seen, vector<int> &order) {\n    seen[curr] = 1;\n    order.push_back(curr);\n\n    for (int nextNode : adjList[curr]) {\n        if (!seen[nextNode]) {\n            exploreDFS(nextNode, adjList, seen, order);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nvector<int> dfsTraversal(int numVertices, vector<int> adjList[]) {\n    vector<int> seen(numVertices, 0);\n    vector<int> order;\n    exploreDFS(0, adjList, seen, order);\n    return order;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting to Pass Vectors by Reference:** Always pass seen and order by reference (&) into the recursive helper; passing by value copies the arrays on every call and breaks visited tracking!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
