1644	Perimeter Decomposition: Anti-Clockwise Boundary Traversal of a Binary Tree	dsa-boundary-traversal-binary-tree	Collect the outermost perimeter nodes of a binary tree in anti-clockwise order by decomposing into left boundary, leaf nodes, and reverse right boundary.	2026-10-03 01:02:00.051723+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Perimeter Decomposition: Anti-Clockwise Boundary Traversal of a Binary Tree\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0ca1nvR0be4", "title": "Lecture 92: Boundary Traversal of a Binary Tree", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/boundary-traversal-of-binary-tree/1", "title": "Boundary Traversal of binary tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Print the boundary nodes of a binary tree in anti-clockwise direction starting from root."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/boundary-of-binary-tree/", "title": "Boundary of Binary Tree", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Deconstruct anti-clockwise perimeter traversal into left boundary, leaves, and reverse right boundary."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-right-side-view/", "title": "Binary Tree Right Side View", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Compare right boundary traversal with level-order right-side projection."}]	Module 17: Binary Trees: Views, Projections & Ancestry Analysis	### 📌 Topic Overview\nAn **Anti-Clockwise Boundary Traversal** collects the outermost perimeter nodes of a binary tree: starting at the root, moving down the left boundary, across all leaf nodes from left to right, and back up the right boundary in reverse.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The 3-Part Decomposition:**\n  1. **Left Boundary (Excluding Leaves):** Start at `root->left`. Traverse downward taking `curr = curr->left` whenever possible; if `left` is null, take `curr = curr->right`. Stop when reaching a leaf node.\n  2. **Leaf Nodes (Inorder / DFS):** Perform standard DFS preorder or inorder and append any node where `node->left == nullptr && node->right == nullptr`.\n  3. **Right Boundary in Reverse (Excluding Leaves):** Start at `root->right`. Traverse downward taking `curr = curr->right` (or `curr->left` if right is null). Store nodes in an auxiliary stack/vector and push them into the result in reverse order!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N)` Time and `O(H)` Space (where `H` is tree height).\n\n### 💻 Boundary Traversal Blueprint\n```cpp\nbool isLeaf(TreeNode* node) {\n    return (node->left == nullptr && node->right == nullptr);\n}\n\nvoid addLeftBoundary(TreeNode* root, vector<int> &res) {\n    TreeNode* curr = root->left;\n    while (curr) {\n        if (!isLeaf(curr)) res.push_back(curr->val);\n        if (curr->left) curr = curr->left;\n        else curr = curr->right;\n    }\n}\n\nvoid addLeaves(TreeNode* root, vector<int> &res) {\n    if (isLeaf(root)) {\n        res.push_back(root->val);\n        return;\n    }\n    if (root->left) addLeaves(root->left, res);\n    if (root->right) addLeaves(root->right, res);\n}\n\nvoid addRightBoundary(TreeNode* root, vector<int> &res) {\n    TreeNode* curr = root->right;\n    vector<int> temp;\n    while (curr) {\n        if (!isLeaf(curr)) temp.push_back(curr->val);\n        if (curr->right) curr = curr->right;\n        else curr = curr->left;\n    }\n    for (int i = temp.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) res.push_back(temp[i]);\n}\n\nvector<int> boundaryTraversal(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<int> res;\n    if (root == nullptr) return res;\n    if (!isLeaf(root)) res.push_back(root->val);\n    addLeftBoundary(root, res);\n    addLeaves(root, res);\n    addRightBoundary(root, res);\n    return res;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
3440	Manhattan Distance Wavefront: Distance of Nearest Cell Having 1 (0/1 Matrix)	dsa-distance-of-nearest-cell-having-1-01-matrix	Compute shortest distances to nearest target cells across a 2D matrix using reverse multi-source BFS.	2026-10-03 01:51:47.35995+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Manhattan Distance Wavefront: Distance of Nearest Cell Having 1 (0/1 Matrix)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=edXdVwkYHF8", "title": "Lecture 138: Distance of nearest cell having 1 | 0/1 Matrix", "duration": "22m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/01-matrix/", "title": "01 Matrix (LC 542)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the distance of the nearest 0 for each cell in a binary matrix using multi-source BFS."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/as-far-from-land-as-possible/", "title": "As Far from Land as Possible (LC 1162)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Push all land cells into a BFS queue to find the water cell with maximum distance to the nearest land."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/map-of-highest-peak/", "title": "Map of Highest Peak (LC 1765)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Assign heights starting from all water cells at height 0 using multi-source BFS."}]	Module 22: Graph Cycle Detection, Multi-Source BFS & Boundary Flood Algorithms	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an `N x M` binary grid, find the Manhattan distance (`|r1 - r2| + |c1 - c2|`) to the **nearest `1`** for every cell in the grid. (Note: LeetCode 542 "01 Matrix" asks the exact mirror question: distance to the nearest `0`).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Reverse Multi-Source BFS Intuition:**\n  - Instead of running a separate BFS from every `0` to find its nearest `1` (which takes `O((N * M)²)` time), **start from all `1`s simultaneously** at distance `0`!\n  - Push every cell `(r, c)` that contains a `1` into the queue as `{{r, c}, 0}` and mark it visited.\n  - Expand outward 1 step at a time in 4 directions: the first time the wavefront reaches any `0` cell at step `d`, `d` is guaranteed to be its shortest distance to the nearest `1`!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * M)` Time and `O(N * M)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Nearest Cell Distance (Multi-Source BFS) Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<vector<int>> nearestOneDistance(vector<vector<int>> &grid) {\n    int rows = grid.size(), cols = grid[0].size();\n    vector<vector<int>> seen(rows, vector<int>(cols, 0));\n    vector<vector<int>> dist(rows, vector<int>(cols, 0));\n    queue<pair<pair<int, int>, int>> q;\n\n    // 1. Push all target cells (1s) as starting sources with distance 0\n    for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {\n            if (grid[r][c] == 1) {\n                q.push({{r, c}, 0});\n                seen[r][c] = 1;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    int dr[] = {-1, 0, 1, 0}, dc[] = {0, 1, 0, -1};\n\n    // 2. Expand wavefront step by step\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        auto [coords, steps] = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        int r = coords.first, c = coords.second;\n        dist[r][c] = steps;\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n            int nr = r + dr[i], nc = c + dc[i];\n            if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols && !seen[nr][nc]) {\n                seen[nr][nc] = 1;\n                q.push({{nr, nc}, steps + 1});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return dist;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Running BFS From Each 0 Individually:** Starting a fresh BFS from every 0 cell causes Time Limit Exceeded (O((N*M)²)). Always push all sources into a single queue upfront so each cell is visited only once!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
757	Overflow-Safe Power Evaluation: Finding the Nth Root of an Integer via Binary Search	dsa-nth-root-of-an-integer-binary-search	Find the exact integer Nth root of M in O(N * log M) time using binary search with an overflow-safe early-exit multiplication helper.	2026-10-02 23:53:37.098537+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Overflow-Safe Power Evaluation: Finding the Nth Root of an Integer via Binary Search\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rjEJeYCasHs", "title": "Lecture 55: Nth Root of a Number using Binary Search", "duration": "26m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/powx-n/", "title": "Pow(x, n)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Implement fast binary exponentiation in O(log N) time."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/arranging-coins/", "title": "Arranging Coins", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Use binary search on the answer range [1, n] with overflow-safe multiplication."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/power-of-numbers-1587115620/1", "title": "Power Of Numbers", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Compute modular exponentiation for large integer powers."}]	Module 13: Binary Search on Answer Space & Monotonic Predicate Functions	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven two integers `n` and `m`, find the exact integer **`n`-th root of `m`** (the integer `x` such that `x^n == m`), or return `-1` if no exact integer root exists.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Binary Search Range (`[1, m]`):**\n  - Set `low = 1` and `high = m`.\n  - At each `mid`, we want to compare `mid^n` against `m`.\n- **Why Computing `pow(mid, n)` Directly Fails (Massive Overflow):**\n  - Suppose `n = 10` and `m = 10^9`. In the first step, `mid` is around `5 * 10^8`.\n  - Raising `5 * 10^8` to the power `10` produces a number around `10^87`, which overflows even a 64-bit `long long`!\n- **The Early-Exit Multiplication Helper (`func(mid, n, m)`):**\n  - We don't actually care about the exact value of `mid^n` once it exceeds `m`!\n  - Multiply `ans = ans * mid` in a loop `n` times, and **the moment `ans > m`, immediately stop and return `2`** (meaning too large)!\n  - If the loop finishes and `ans == m`, return `1` (exact match found!).\n  - Otherwise (`ans < m`), return `0` (too small).\n\n### 💻 Overflow-Safe Nth Root Blueprint\n```cpp\n// Returns 1 if mid^n == m, 0 if mid^n < m, 2 if mid^n > m\nint checkPower(int mid, int n, int m) {\n    long long ans = 1;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        ans = ans * mid;\n        if (ans > m) return 2; // Early exit prevents overflow!\n    }\n    if (ans == m) return 1;\n    return 0;\n}\n\nint NthRoot(int n, int m) {\n    int low = 1, high = m;\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        int midN = checkPower(mid, n, m);\n        if (midN == 1) return mid;\n        else if (midN == 0) low = mid + 1;\n        else high = mid - 1;\n    }\n    return -1;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
4213	Lexicographical Dependency Graph: Alien Dictionary via Topological Sort	dsa-alien-dictionary-topological-sort	Derive alien alphabet character order from adjacent lexicographical word comparisons using Kahn's topological sort.	2026-10-03 11:01:59.89556+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Lexicographical Dependency Graph: Alien Dictionary via Topological Sort\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3N_je7tWAs", "title": "Lecture 151: Alien Dictionary - Topological Sort", "duration": "24m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/alien-dictionary/1", "title": "Alien Dictionary", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Given a sorted dictionary of an alien language having N words and K starting alphabets, find the order of characters."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/alien-dictionary/", "title": "Alien Dictionary (LC 269)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Derive the lexicographical order of letters in an alien language from a sorted list of words."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/verifying-an-alien-dictionary/", "title": "Verifying an Alien Dictionary (LC 953)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Verify whether a given sequence of words is sorted lexicographically according to a custom alphabet order."}]	Module 23: Graph Coloring, Directed Cycle Detection & Topological Sort (DAGs)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a sorted dictionary of `N` words from an alien language using the first `K` lowercase letters (`'a'` to `'a' + K - 1`), determine a valid **alphabet order** of characters in that alien language.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Building Directed Edges from Adjacent Words:**\n  - Compare every pair of adjacent words `s1 = dict[i]` and `s2 = dict[i + 1]`.\n  - Scan character by character up to `min(s1.size(), s2.size())` until you find the **first mismatching character** `s1[ptr] != s2[ptr]`.\n  - Because `s1` comes before `s2` in the dictionary, character `s1[ptr]` must come before `s2[ptr]` in the alien alphabet!\n  - Add a directed edge `(s1[ptr] - 'a') -> (s2[ptr] - 'a')` and **`break` immediately** (subsequent characters in `s1` and `s2` do not determine their relative order).\n- **Running Kahn's Topological Sort:**\n  - Run Kahn's BFS topological sort over the `K` character nodes (`0` to `K - 1`) and convert each popped integer `u` back to `char(u + 'a')`.\n- **Two Invalid Dictionary Edge Cases (When Order Is Impossible):**\n  1. **Prefix Violation:** A longer word appears *before* its own exact prefix (e.g., `"abcd"` appears before `"abc"`).\n  2. **Cyclic Dependency:** `"abc"`, `"bat"`, `"ade"` implies `a < b < a`, which forms a cycle (`topoOrder.size() < K`).\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * len + K + E)` Time and `O(K + E)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Alien Dictionary Blueprint\n```cpp\nstring findOrder(string dict[], int N, int K) {\n    vector<vector<int>> adjList(K);\n    vector<int> indegree(K, 0);\n\n    // 1. Compare adjacent words to extract directed character dependencies\n    for (int i = 0; i < N - 1; i++) {\n        string s1 = dict[i], s2 = dict[i + 1];\n        int len = min(s1.size(), s2.size());\n        for (int ptr = 0; ptr < len; ptr++) {\n            if (s1[ptr] != s2[ptr]) {\n                int u = s1[ptr] - 'a';\n                int v = s2[ptr] - 'a';\n                adjList[u].push_back(v);\n                indegree[v]++;\n                break; // Only the FIRST mismatching character defines the edge!\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // 2. Kahn's BFS Topological Sort over K characters\n    queue<int> q;\n    for (int i = 0; i < K; i++) {\n        if (indegree[i] == 0) q.push(i);\n    }\n\n    string order = "";\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int u = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        order += char(u + 'a');\n\n        for (int v : adjList[u]) {\n            indegree[v]--;\n            if (indegree[v] == 0) q.push(v);\n        }\n    }\n    return order;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Continuing Comparison Past the First Mismatch:** Once you find the first index `ptr` where `s1[ptr] != s2[ptr]`, you **must `break`**. Characters after `ptr` carry zero information about the relative ordering of `s1` and `s2`!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
4217	Level-Delayed Set Erasure: Word Ladder II (All Shortest Transformation Sequences)	dsa-word-ladder-ii-shortest-paths-bfs	Enumerate all shortest transformation sequences by delaying word erasure until each BFS level completes.	2026-10-03 11:01:59.916422+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Level-Delayed Set Erasure: Word Ladder II (All Shortest Transformation Sequences)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DREutrv2XD0", "title": "Lecture 155: Word Ladder - II | Shortest Paths", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/word-ladder-ii/1", "title": "Word Ladder II", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find all possible shortest transformation sequences from startWord to targetWord."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/word-ladder-ii/", "title": "Word Ladder II (LC 126)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Return all shortest transformation sequences from beginWord to endWord."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/word-ladder/", "title": "Word Ladder (LC 127)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Contrast finding the shortest sequence length with enumerating all shortest sequences."}]	Module 24: Shortest Path Algorithms in Graphs: DAG Relaxation, BFS & Dijkstra's Algorithm	### 📌 Topic Overview\nUnlike Word Ladder I (which only asks for the shortest length), **Word Ladder II** asks you to return **all possible shortest transformation sequences** from `startWord` to `targetWord`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Storing Full Sequences in the Queue (`queue<vector<string>>`):**\n  - Instead of storing a single word in the queue, store the **entire path vector** `vector<string>` taken so far (e.g., `{"der", "dfr", "dfs"}`).\n  - To expand a path, look at its last word `word = vec.back()` and try all `'a'..'z'` single-character replacements.\n- **Why We Must Delay Erasing Words Until the Level Finishes:**\n  - Suppose two different paths at Level 2 (`{"der", "dfr"}` and `{"der", "dkr"}`) both transition into `"dfs"` at Level 3.\n  - If `"dfr"` immediately erases `"dfs"` from the dictionary set `st`, then `"dkr"` will not be able to use `"dfs"`, causing us to miss a valid shortest path!\n  - **The Fix:** Track `usedOnLevel` words during the current BFS level, and only erase them from `st` **when advancing to the next level** (`if (vec.size() > level)`).\n\n### 💻 Word Ladder II (Path-Queue BFS) Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<vector<string>> findSequences(string beginWord, string endWord, vector<string> &wordList) {\n    unordered_set<string> st(wordList.begin(), wordList.end());\n    queue<vector<string>> q;\n    q.push({beginWord});\n\n    vector<string> usedOnLevel;\n    usedOnLevel.push_back(beginWord);\n    int level = 0;\n    vector<vector<string>> ans;\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        vector<string> vec = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n\n        // Erase all words used in the previous level once we advance to a new level\n        if (vec.size() > level) {\n            level++;\n            for (const string &used : usedOnLevel) {\n                st.erase(used);\n            }\n            usedOnLevel.clear();\n        }\n\n        string word = vec.back();\n        if (word == endWord) {\n            if (ans.empty() || ans[0].size() == vec.size()) {\n                ans.push_back(vec);\n            }\n        }\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < word.size(); i++) {\n            char original = word[i];\n            for (char c = 'a'; c <= 'z'; c++) {\n                word[i] = c;\n                if (st.count(word) > 0) {\n                    vec.push_back(word);\n                    q.push(vec);\n                    usedOnLevel.push_back(word);\n                    vec.pop_back(); // Backtrack path vector for next neighbor\n                }\n            }\n            word[i] = original;\n        }\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Erasing Words Immediately Upon Push:** Immediate erasure works for Word Ladder I, but in Word Ladder II it prunes away alternative shortest paths that converge on the same intermediate word at the same level!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
