9282	In-Place Bidirectional Pointer Swap: Reverse a Doubly Linked List	dsa-reverse-a-doubly-linked-list	Reverse a doubly linked list in a single pass in O(N) time and O(1) space by swapping next and back pointers at every node.	2026-10-03 14:56:36.929343+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: In-Place Bidirectional Pointer Swap: Reverse a Doubly Linked List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u3WUW2qe6ww", "title": "Lecture 240: Reverse a Doubly Linked List | In-Place Swap", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/reverse-a-doubly-linked-list/1", "title": "Reverse a Doubly Linked List", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Given a doubly linked list, reverse it in-place in a single traversal and return the new head."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/reverse-linked-list/", "title": "Reverse Linked List (LC 206)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Compare reversing a Doubly Linked List with reversing a Singly Linked List."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/reverse-linked-list-ii/", "title": "Reverse Linked List II (LC 92)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Reverse a sublist of a linked list from position left to right in one pass."}]	Module 36: Singly & Doubly Linked Lists: Memory Architecture, Pointer Mutations & Reversal	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the `head` of a **Doubly Linked List**, reverse the list in-place and return the new head.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Optimal)\n- **1. Brute Force Using a Stack (`O(2N)` Time, `O(N)` Space):**\n  - Pass 1: Traverse the DLL and push all `temp->data` values onto a `stack<int> st`.\n  - Pass 2: Traverse the DLL again and overwrite `temp->data = st.top(); st.pop();`.\n  - **Why Interviewers Reject This:** It takes two passes, uses `O(N)` extra memory, and only swaps data instead of reversing the actual node links!\n- **2. Optimal In-Place Single-Pass Link Swap (`O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Just like swapping two integers `a` and `b` using a temporary variable (`temp = a; a = b; b = temp;`), at every node `current` we simply **swap its `back` and `next` pointers**!\n  - Let `prev = nullptr` and `current = head`.\n  - While `current != nullptr`:\n    1. Save **`prev = current->back;`**\n    2. Redirect **`current->back = current->next;`**\n    3. Redirect **`current->next = prev;`**\n    4. **How do we move `current` forward to the next node in the original list?** Since we just swapped `back` and `next`, the original `next` node is now stored in **`current->back`**! So advance via **`current = current->back;`**!\n  - When `current` becomes `nullptr`, `prev` holds the `back` pointer of the new head (which is the second node in the reversed list), so the **new head is `prev->back`**!\n\n### 💻 Reverse a Doubly Linked List (Single-Pass O(1) Space) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nNode* reverseDLL(Node* head) {\n    // Edge case: empty list or single-node list\n    if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr) {\n        return head;\n    }\n\n    Node* prev = nullptr;\n    Node* current = head;\n\n    while (current != nullptr) {\n        // Swap current->back and current->next\n        prev = current->back;\n        current->back = current->next;\n        current->next = prev;\n\n        // Because back and next were swapped, move to the next original node via current->back!\n        current = current->back;\n    }\n\n    // 'prev' sits at the old 2nd-to-last node, so prev->back is the old tail (new head)!\n    return prev->back;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Advancing `current = current->next` After Swapping Pointers:** Once you swap `current->back` and `current->next`, `current->next` points *backward*! To move forward to the next original node, you **must** write **`current = current->back;`**.\n- **Forgetting the Single-Node Edge Case (`head->next == nullptr`):** If the list has only `1` node, `prev` remains `nullptr` after the loop, so `prev->back` would crash! Always check `if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr) return head;` at the top.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22221	OOPs Foundations: Abstraction & Encapsulation	lld-oops-abstraction-encapsulation	Deep dive into object-oriented foundations: procedural bottlenecks, real-world modeling, Data Hiding (Abstraction) vs Data Security (Encapsulation).	2026-10-07 16:56:30.769208+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: OOPs Foundations: Abstraction & Encapsulation\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/QbGoqAgP_zg", "title": "OOPs Real-World Examples | OOPs Pillars | Abstraction | Encapsulation", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v2-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Abstraction vs Encapsulation Core Distinction", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Difference:**\\n* **Abstraction (Complexity Hiding):** Hides the internal implementation details and exposes only high-level behavior. \\n  * *Vehicle Example:* The steering wheel, brake pedal, and ignition button. You use them to drive without knowing the underlying mechanics of power steering pumps, hydraulic calipers, or spark plug timing.\\n* **Encapsulation (Data Protection / Security):** Bundles state variables and methods into a protective capsule and restricts unauthorized direct manipulation.\\n  * *Vehicle Example:* The Odometer and Speedometer. You cannot manually set `car.currentSpeed = 500` or rewrite the odometer reading; your speed only changes as a controlled side-effect of calling `car.accelerate()`.\\n\\n**Why They Are Confused:** Both involve hiding something. However, Abstraction hides *implementation complexity* (what the system does vs how), while Encapsulation hides and protects *internal state data* from invalid external mutation.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the fundamental difference between Abstraction and Encapsulation using a real-world vehicle example. Why do developers frequently confuse the two?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v2-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: The Problem with Public Class Variables", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Invariant Violations:**\\n1. **Invalid State Mutations:** A client or third-party module can execute `myCar.currentSpeed = -50;` or `myCar.currentSpeed = 99999;`, destroying physical domain rules (negative speed or exceeding terminal velocity).\\n2. **Unsynchronized Side Effects:** In an actual vehicle, changing speed impacts fuel consumption, gear ratios, and traction control. Direct variable mutation bypasses these crucial domain validations.\\n\\n**The Encapsulated Solution:**\\nMake `currentSpeed` private. Provide a getter `int getCurrentSpeed() const;` and mutate speed strictly through behavioral methods (`accelerate()`, `brake()`):\\n```cpp\\nvoid SportsCar::accelerate() {\\n    if (!isEngineOn) throw std::runtime_error(\\"Engine must be running\\");\\n    currentSpeed = std::min(currentSpeed + 20, maxSpeed);\\n}\\n```\\nThis guarantees internal state invariants are always maintained.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Suppose a SportsCar class declares \\"public: int currentSpeed;\\". What catastrophic invariant violations occur, and how does encapsulation resolve them?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v2-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Abstract Classes as Architectural Contracts", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nAn abstract class (e.g., `class ICar { virtual void startEngine() = 0; };`) defines an enforceable contract:\\n1. **Guaranteed Interface:** It promises the client that ANY concrete subclass (e.g., `SportsCar`, `ElectricCar`) is guaranteed to implement `startEngine()`, `shiftGear()`, and `brake()`.\\n2. **Decoupled Client Code:** The client can write business logic against the `ICar*` pointer without knowing or caring whether the concrete implementation is powered by an internal combustion engine or dual electric motors.\\n3. **Compiler Enforcement:** The compiler prevents instantiation of incomplete subclasses, ensuring that missing methods are caught at compile time rather than crashing at runtime.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is an Abstract Class with pure virtual functions considered an \\"Architectural Contract\\" between a client and a service provider?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v2-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Validated Setters vs Meaningless Getters/Setters", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Blind Setters as an Anti-Pattern:**\\nIf a class marks `int balance;` private but provides `void setBalance(int b) { balance = b; }`, it provides zero real encapsulation. Any external caller can still overwrite the balance to negative numbers or bypass audit ledgers.\\n\\n**True Encapsulation is Behavioral:**\\nClasses should represent autonomous domain entities, not dumb data containers. Instead of `setBalance(newBalance)`, the class should expose business methods with validation:\\n```cpp\\nvoid Account::deposit(double amount) {\\n    if (amount <= 0) throw std::invalid_argument(\\"Deposit must be positive\\");\\n    this->balance += amount;\\n    this->recordAuditLog(\\"DEPOSIT\\", amount);\\n}\\n```\\nSetters should only exist if external mutation is a legitimate domain requirement, and they must always validate inputs.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Many developers blindly generate getters and setters for every private variable. Why is \\"blind setter generation\\" considered an anti-pattern that violates encapsulation?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v2-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Access Modifiers Spectrum: Private, Protected, Public", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **`public`:** Accessible by any code anywhere. Exposes the outward contract.\\n* **`protected`:** Accessible within the class itself and by derived (sub)classes. Inaccessible to external callers.\\n* **`private`:** Accessible strictly within the defining class. Inaccessible even to derived child classes.\\n\\n**Why `private` should be default:**\\n1. **Least Privilege Principle:** Exposing internal variables or helper methods permanently ties your internal architecture to external callers. Once public, refactoring or changing data types breaks external consumers.\\n2. **Protection from Subclass Corruption:** `protected` variables can be mutated improperly by child classes. Defaulting to `private` forces child classes to interact via well-defined protected or public methods, preserving data integrity.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Compare the accessibility boundaries of private, protected, and public access specifiers. In low-level design, why should \\"private\\" be your default choice?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Procedural Bottleneck\n* **Procedural Code (C-style):** Code is a recipe of free-floating global functions manipulating shared raw structs/variables.\n* **The Failure:** Zero data protection, sprawling dependencies, impossible multi-instance scaling, and cognitive overload.\n\n### 💡 2. Abstraction: "Data Hiding"\n* **Definition:** Hiding internal structural complexity and exposing only the essential operational interface to the client.\n* **Real-World Analogy:** A car driver presses the accelerator pedal or shifts gears (the interface) without needing to understand fuel injection ratios or hydraulic cylinder valves.\n* **Code Mechanism:** Abstract classes with pure virtual functions (`virtual void accelerate() = 0`).\n\n### 💡 3. Encapsulation: "Data Security"\n* **Definition:** Bundling data (state variables) and the methods that operate on that data into a single unit (class), while restricting direct access using access modifiers.\n* **The Crucial Distinction:** Abstraction hides *how* something works (complexity reduction); Encapsulation protects *what* state can be mutated (data integrity).\n* **Code Mechanism:** Private member variables guarded by validated getters and setters.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	OOPs Foundations: Abstraction & Encapsulation	Deep dive into object-oriented foundations: procedural bottlenecks, real-world modeling, Data Hiding (Abstraction) vs Data Security (Encapsulation).	54	Hard	Low Level Design	16	[{"id": "lld-v2-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Abstraction vs Encapsulation Core Distinction", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Difference:**\\n* **Abstraction (Complexity Hiding):** Hides the internal implementation details and exposes only high-level behavior. \\n  * *Vehicle Example:* The steering wheel, brake pedal, and ignition button. You use them to drive without knowing the underlying mechanics of power steering pumps, hydraulic calipers, or spark plug timing.\\n* **Encapsulation (Data Protection / Security):** Bundles state variables and methods into a protective capsule and restricts unauthorized direct manipulation.\\n  * *Vehicle Example:* The Odometer and Speedometer. You cannot manually set `car.currentSpeed = 500` or rewrite the odometer reading; your speed only changes as a controlled side-effect of calling `car.accelerate()`.\\n\\n**Why They Are Confused:** Both involve hiding something. However, Abstraction hides *implementation complexity* (what the system does vs how), while Encapsulation hides and protects *internal state data* from invalid external mutation.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the fundamental difference between Abstraction and Encapsulation using a real-world vehicle example. Why do developers frequently confuse the two?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v2-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: The Problem with Public Class Variables", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Invariant Violations:**\\n1. **Invalid State Mutations:** A client or third-party module can execute `myCar.currentSpeed = -50;` or `myCar.currentSpeed = 99999;`, destroying physical domain rules (negative speed or exceeding terminal velocity).\\n2. **Unsynchronized Side Effects:** In an actual vehicle, changing speed impacts fuel consumption, gear ratios, and traction control. Direct variable mutation bypasses these crucial domain validations.\\n\\n**The Encapsulated Solution:**\\nMake `currentSpeed` private. Provide a getter `int getCurrentSpeed() const;` and mutate speed strictly through behavioral methods (`accelerate()`, `brake()`):\\n```cpp\\nvoid SportsCar::accelerate() {\\n    if (!isEngineOn) throw std::runtime_error(\\"Engine must be running\\");\\n    currentSpeed = std::min(currentSpeed + 20, maxSpeed);\\n}\\n```\\nThis guarantees internal state invariants are always maintained.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Suppose a SportsCar class declares \\"public: int currentSpeed;\\". What catastrophic invariant violations occur, and how does encapsulation resolve them?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v2-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Abstract Classes as Architectural Contracts", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nAn abstract class (e.g., `class ICar { virtual void startEngine() = 0; };`) defines an enforceable contract:\\n1. **Guaranteed Interface:** It promises the client that ANY concrete subclass (e.g., `SportsCar`, `ElectricCar`) is guaranteed to implement `startEngine()`, `shiftGear()`, and `brake()`.\\n2. **Decoupled Client Code:** The client can write business logic against the `ICar*` pointer without knowing or caring whether the concrete implementation is powered by an internal combustion engine or dual electric motors.\\n3. **Compiler Enforcement:** The compiler prevents instantiation of incomplete subclasses, ensuring that missing methods are caught at compile time rather than crashing at runtime.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is an Abstract Class with pure virtual functions considered an \\"Architectural Contract\\" between a client and a service provider?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v2-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Validated Setters vs Meaningless Getters/Setters", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Blind Setters as an Anti-Pattern:**\\nIf a class marks `int balance;` private but provides `void setBalance(int b) { balance = b; }`, it provides zero real encapsulation. Any external caller can still overwrite the balance to negative numbers or bypass audit ledgers.\\n\\n**True Encapsulation is Behavioral:**\\nClasses should represent autonomous domain entities, not dumb data containers. Instead of `setBalance(newBalance)`, the class should expose business methods with validation:\\n```cpp\\nvoid Account::deposit(double amount) {\\n    if (amount <= 0) throw std::invalid_argument(\\"Deposit must be positive\\");\\n    this->balance += amount;\\n    this->recordAuditLog(\\"DEPOSIT\\", amount);\\n}\\n```\\nSetters should only exist if external mutation is a legitimate domain requirement, and they must always validate inputs.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Many developers blindly generate getters and setters for every private variable. Why is \\"blind setter generation\\" considered an anti-pattern that violates encapsulation?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v2-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Access Modifiers Spectrum: Private, Protected, Public", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **`public`:** Accessible by any code anywhere. Exposes the outward contract.\\n* **`protected`:** Accessible within the class itself and by derived (sub)classes. Inaccessible to external callers.\\n* **`private`:** Accessible strictly within the defining class. Inaccessible even to derived child classes.\\n\\n**Why `private` should be default:**\\n1. **Least Privilege Principle:** Exposing internal variables or helper methods permanently ties your internal architecture to external callers. Once public, refactoring or changing data types breaks external consumers.\\n2. **Protection from Subclass Corruption:** `protected` variables can be mutated improperly by child classes. Defaulting to `private` forces child classes to interact via well-defined protected or public methods, preserving data integrity.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Compare the accessibility boundaries of private, protected, and public access specifiers. In low-level design, why should \\"private\\" be your default choice?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
10020	Cross-Head Pointer Synchronization: Find the Intersection Point of a Y-Shaped Linked List	dsa-find-intersection-point-of-y-linked-list	Find the merge node of two intersecting linked lists by redirecting pointers to opposite heads upon reaching null in O(N1 + N2) time and O(1) space.	2026-10-03 15:04:07.050474+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Cross-Head Pointer Synchronization: Find the Intersection Point of a Y-Shaped Linked List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0DYoPz2Tpt4", "title": "Lecture 248: Find Intersection of Two Linked Lists", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/intersection-of-two-linked-lists/", "title": "Intersection of Two Linked Lists (LC 160)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Given the heads of two singly linked-lists headA and headB, return the node at which the two lists intersect in O(N + M) time and O(1) space."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/intersection-point-in-y-shapped-linked-lists/1", "title": "Intersection Point in Y Shaped Linked Lists", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the node where two Y-shaped singly linked lists merge."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-index-sum-of-two-lists/", "title": "Minimum Index Sum of Two Lists (LC 599)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Practice finding common elements across two lists with minimal index offset."}]	Module 37: Linked List Medium Problems: Dummy Nodes, In-Place Re-Linking & Tortoise-Hare Pointers	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the heads `head1` and `head2` of two singly linked lists that merge into a shared tail (a **Y-shaped intersection**), return the **first intersecting `Node*`** (by memory address `t1 == t2`, **not** by node value!), or `nullptr` if they do not intersect.\n\n### 💡 Progression of 3 Approaches\n- **1. Brute Force Hashing (`O(N1 + N2)` Time, `O(N1)` Space):**\n  - Store all `Node*` pointers of List 1 in a hash map/set, then traverse List 2 and return the first `Node*` found in the set.\n- **2. Better Length-Difference Alignment (`O(N1 + 2*N2)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Compute lengths `N1` and `N2`, advance the longer list's pointer by `abs(N1 - N2)` steps so both pointers are equidistant from the intersection, and then step both together.\n- **3. Optimal Cross-Head Switching (`O(N1 + N2)` Time, `O(1)` Space — Clean & Concise!):**\n  - Start `t1 = head1` and `t2 = head2`.\n  - Loop **`while (t1 != t2)`**:\n    - Advance `t1 = t1->next;` and `t2 = t2->next;`.\n    - If `t1 == t2`, return `t1` (handles both intersection and the case where both hit `nullptr` simultaneously when no intersection exists!).\n    - When `t1` hits `nullptr`, redirect **`t1 = head2`**!\n    - When `t2` hits `nullptr`, redirect **`t2 = head1`**!\n  - **Why does this work?** By walking List 1 + List 2 (`N1 + N2` steps) and List 2 + List 1 (`N2 + N1` steps), both pointers automatically cancel out their length difference `|N1 - N2|` and land on the intersection node at the **exact same step**!\n\n### 💻 Intersection of Y-Shaped Linked List Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nNode* findIntersection(Node* firstHead, Node* secondHead) {\n    if (firstHead == nullptr || secondHead == nullptr) return nullptr;\n\n    Node* t1 = firstHead;\n    Node* t2 = secondHead;\n\n    while (t1 != t2) {\n        t1 = t1->next;\n        t2 = t2->next;\n\n        // Meets at the intersection node OR both reach nullptr at the exact same time!\n        if (t1 == t2) return t1;\n\n        // Switch to the opposite list's head upon reaching nullptr\n        if (t1 == nullptr) t1 = secondHead;\n        if (t2 == nullptr) t2 = firstHead;\n    }\n    return t1;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Comparing `t1->data == t2->data` Instead of `t1 == t2`:** Two distinct nodes before the merge point can easily hold the same integer value (e.g., `4`)! Intersection is defined strictly by **shared node memory address (`t1 == t2`)**.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
