in order of their appearance."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/daily-temperatures/", "title": "LeetCode 739: Daily Temperatures", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Store indices in a monotonic decreasing stack to find how many days you have to wait until a warmer temperature."}]	Module 43: Monotonic Stack & Contribution Algorithms: Next/Previous Greater/Smaller, Rainwater & Subarray Aggregations	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an integer array `arr` of size `N`, for every element `arr[i]`, find its **Next Greater Element (NGE)**—the **first element to the right of `arr[i]`** that is strictly greater than `arr[i]` (or `-1` if no such element exists).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute O(N²) → Optimal Monotonic Stack O(2N))\n- **What Is a Monotonic Stack?**\n  - A stack that maintains its elements in a **strictly specific order** (either strictly increasing or strictly decreasing from bottom to top).\n- **The Light-Pole Intuition (Why We Traverse Backwards N - 1 down to 0):**\n  - To know the first greater element to the **right** of `arr[i]`, we must already know the elements to its right—so we traverse **from right to left (`N - 1` down to `0`)**!\n  - Imagine each number is a **light pole of height `arr[i]`**. When standing at `arr[i]` looking right, any shorter or equal poles (`<= arr[i]`) immediately to the right are **blocked from view** by `arr[i]` for all future elements to the left!\n  - Therefore, while `!st.empty() && st.top() <= arr[i]`, **pop `st.top()`**!\n  - After popping all `<= arr[i]` elements:\n    - If `st.empty()`, `nge[i] = -1`; else **`nge[i] = st.top()`**.\n    - Push `arr[i]` onto `st`.\n- **Why Is Time Complexity O(2N) and Not O(N²)?**\n  - The outer `for` loop runs `N` times, and each element is pushed onto `st` once and can be popped **at most once** across the entire run (`<= N` total pops)!\n\n### 💻 Next Greater Element Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> nextGreaterElement(vector<int> &arr) {\n    int n = arr.size();\n    vector<int> nge(n, -1);\n    stack<int> st;\n\n    // Traverse backwards from right to left (n - 1 down to 0)\n    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n        // Pop all elements smaller than or equal to arr[i] (maintain decreasing order)\n        while (!st.empty() && st.top() <= arr[i]) {\n            st.pop();\n        }\n\n        if (st.empty()) {\n            nge[i] = -1;\n        } else {\n            nge[i] = st.top();\n        }\n\n        st.push(arr[i]);\n    }\n    return nge;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Using < Instead of <= in `while (!st.empty() && st.top() <= arr[i])`:** We need the next **strictly greater** element! If `st.top() == arr[i]` and you don't pop it, `nge[i]` will falsely be set to `arr[i]` itself!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
3879	Indegree Zero Peeling: Kahn's Algorithm for Topological Sort Using BFS	dsa-kahns-algorithm-topological-sort-bfs	Compute topological ordering iteratively using Kahn's algorithm by progressively removing vertices with indegree zero.	2026-10-03 02:09:36.734253+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Indegree Zero Peeling: Kahn's Algorithm for Topological Sort Using BFS\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=73sneFXuTEg", "title": "Lecture 147: Kahn's Algorithm | Topological Sort Algorithm | BFS", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/topological-sort/1", "title": "Topological sort (Kahn's BFS)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Implement Kahn's Indegree BFS algorithm to generate a valid topological ordering."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/parallel-courses/", "title": "Parallel Courses (LC 1136)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use level-by-level Kahn's BFS to find the minimum number of semesters needed to complete all courses."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-all-possible-recipes-from-given-supplies/", "title": "Find All Possible Recipes from Given Supplies (LC 2115)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Track ingredient indegrees and peel zero-indegree supplies using a BFS queue."}]	Module 23: Graph Coloring, Directed Cycle Detection & Topological Sort (DAGs)	### 📌 Topic Overview\n**Kahn's Algorithm** computes a **Topological Sort** iteratively using **BFS** and an **`indegree[]` array** (where `indegree[v]` is the number of incoming directed edges pointing into `v`).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Indegree-Zero Invariant:**\n  - Any vertex with `indegree == 0` has **no prerequisites** (no incoming edges), so it can safely be placed at the very front of our linear ordering!\n  - Every DAG is guaranteed to have at least one vertex with `indegree == 0`.\n- **Kahn's Algorithm Steps (`O(V + E)` Time, `O(V)` Space):**\n  1. Compute `indegree[v]` for all vertices by scanning `adjList`.\n  2. Push all vertices with `indegree[i] == 0` into a `queue<int> q`.\n  3. While `!q.empty()`:\n     - Pop `curr = q.front()` and append `curr` to `topoOrder`.\n     - For each `neighbor` in `adjList[curr]`:\n       - Decrement `indegree[neighbor]--` (virtually removing the edge `curr → neighbor`).\n       - If `indegree[neighbor] == 0`, all of its prerequisites are now satisfied—push `neighbor` into `q`!\n\n### 💻 Kahn's Algorithm (BFS Topological Sort) Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> kahnsTopoSort(int V, vector<int> adjList[]) {\n    vector<int> indegree(V, 0);\n    for (int u = 0; u < V; u++) {\n        for (int v : adjList[u]) {\n            indegree[v]++;\n        }\n    }\n\n    queue<int> q;\n    for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {\n        if (indegree[i] == 0) {\n            q.push(i);\n        }\n    }\n\n    vector<int> topoOrder;\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int curr = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        topoOrder.push_back(curr);\n\n        // Remove outgoing edges from curr and unlock newly independent neighbors\n        for (int neighbor : adjList[curr]) {\n            indegree[neighbor]--;\n            if (indegree[neighbor] == 0) {\n                q.push(neighbor);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return topoOrder;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Using a Visited Array Unnecessarily:** In Kahn's algorithm, a node is pushed into the queue **only at the exact moment its `indegree` drops from `1` to `0`**, so it can never be pushed twice!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
