ary-tree-in-vertical-order/1", "title": "Vertical Traversal of Binary Tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Print the vertical order traversal of nodes from leftmost to rightmost line."}]	Module 17: Binary Trees: Views, Projections & Ancestry Analysis	### 📌 Topic Overview\nAssign a 2D coordinate `(x, y)` to every node where `x` represents the vertical column (root starts at `x = 0`) and `y` represents the horizontal level (root starts at `y = 0`). Moving left is `(x - 1, y + 1)` and moving right is `(x + 1, y + 1)`. Output the nodes column by column from leftmost to rightmost.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Nested Map Data Structure:**\n  - Use `map<int, map<int, multiset<int>>> nodes`:\n    - First key `int x`: Vertical column (sorted automatically from negative to positive).\n    - Second key `int y`: Level row (sorted from top to bottom).\n    - Value `multiset<int>`: Collects duplicate/overlapping nodes at the same `(x, y)` coordinate in sorted numerical order.\n- **Queue-Based BFS Coordinates:**\n  - Push `{root, {0, 0}}` into a `queue<pair<TreeNode*, pair<int, int>>> q`.\n  - When popping `{node, {x, y}}`, insert `node->val` into `nodes[x][y]`.\n  - Push `{node->left, {x - 1, y + 1}}` and `{node->right, {x + 1, y + 1}}`.\n- **Complexity:** `O(N log N)` Time (due to sorted map and multiset insertions) and `O(N)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Vertical Order Traversal Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<vector<int>> verticalTraversal(TreeNode* root) {\n    map<int, map<int, multiset<int>>> nodes;\n    queue<pair<TreeNode*, pair<int, int>>> q;\n    q.push({root, {0, 0}});\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        auto p = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        TreeNode* node = p.first;\n        int x = p.second.first, y = p.second.second;\n\n        nodes[x][y].insert(node->val);\n        if (node->left) q.push({node->left, {x - 1, y + 1}});\n        if (node->right) q.push({node->right, {x + 1, y + 1}});\n    }\n\n    vector<vector<int>> ans;\n    for (auto p : nodes) {\n        vector<int> col;\n        for (auto q_pair : p.second) {\n            col.insert(col.end(), q_pair.second.begin(), q_pair.second.end());\n        }\n        ans.push_back(col);\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
587	Binary Search Mechanics: Search Space Halving, Iterative vs. Recursive & Overflow Prevention	dsa-binary-search-introduction-iterative-recursive	Understand binary search principles on sorted arrays, call stack mechanics in iterative vs recursive implementations, and 32-bit midpoint overflow prevention.	2026-10-02 23:24:14.969641+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Binary Search Mechanics: Search Space Halving, Iterative vs. Recursive & Overflow Prevention\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MHf6awe89xw", "title": "Lecture 45: Binary Search Explained", "duration": "28m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/guess-number-higher-or-lower/", "title": "Guess Number Higher or Lower", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Apply overflow-safe midpoint calculation to locate a secret number in [1, N]."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/first-bad-version/", "title": "First Bad Version", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Minimize API calls across a 1-to-N search space using binary search."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/who-will-win-1587115621/1", "title": "Searching an element in a sorted array", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Basic", "description": "Verify whether K is present in a sorted array in O(log N) time."}]	Module 12: 1D Binary Search, Bounds & Rotated Array Invariants	### 📌 Topic Overview\n**Binary Search** is a searching algorithm that works on any **sorted search space**. Instead of scanning elements one by one in `O(N)` time (Linear Search), Binary Search repeatedly cuts the search space in half, finding the target in **O(log2 N) time**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **How Binary Search Works:**\n  - Define the active search space between `low = 0` and `high = n - 1`.\n  - While `low <= high`, inspect the middle index `mid`:\n    1. If `arr[mid] == target`: Target found! Return `mid`.\n    2. If `target > arr[mid]`: Target can only lie on the right half—eliminate the left half by setting `low = mid + 1`.\n    3. If `target < arr[mid]`: Target can only lie on the left half—eliminate the right half by setting `high = mid - 1`.\n  - If `low` crosses `high` (`low > high`), the search space is empty and the target does not exist (return `-1`).\n- **Why Time Complexity is O(log2 N):**\n  - An array of size `32` (`2^5`) is halved `32 → 16 → 8 → 4 → 2 → 1` in just ~5 steps (`log2(32) = 5`).\n- **Preventing Integer Overflow in `mid`:**\n  - If `low` and `high` are both near `INT_MAX`, calculating `(low + high) / 2` overflows a 32-bit `int`!\n  - Always write: **`mid = low + (high - low) / 2`**.\n\n### 💻 Iterative & Recursive Binary Search Blueprint\n```cpp\nint binarySearchIterative(vector<int> &nums, int target) {\n    int low = 0, high = (int)nums.size() - 1;\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2; // Prevents (low + high) overflow\n        if (nums[mid] == target) return mid;\n        else if (target > nums[mid]) low = mid + 1;\n        else high = mid - 1;\n    }\n    return -1;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.co