3439	Back-Edge Discovery: Detect a Cycle in an Undirected Graph Using DFS	dsa-detect-cycle-in-undirected-graph-dfs	Detect cycles in undirected graphs via recursive DFS back-edge discovery to non-parent ancestors.	2026-10-03 01:51:47.357358+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Back-Edge Discovery: Detect a Cycle in an Undirected Graph Using DFS\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zQ3zgFypzX4", "title": "Lecture 137: Detect a Cycle in an Undirected Graph using DFS", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/detect-cycles-in-2d-grid/", "title": "Detect Cycles in 2D Grid (LC 1559)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use parent-tracked DFS to detect cycles of same-character cells in a 2D grid."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/detect-cycle-in-an-undirected-graph/1", "title": "Undirected Graph Cycle (DFS)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Implement recursive DFS cycle detection across all connected components."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/graph-valid-tree/", "title": "Graph Valid Tree (LC 261)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use DFS cycle detection and component counting to validate a tree."}]	Module 22: Graph Cycle Detection, Multi-Source BFS & Boundary Flood Algorithms	### 📌 Topic Overview\nTo detect a cycle in an **Undirected Graph** using **Depth-First Search (DFS)**, we pass `(currNode, parentNode)` down the recursion stack. If our DFS path encounters a neighbor that is **already visited and is not `parentNode`**, we have looped back to an ancestor in the current component—proving a cycle exists!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Recursive State `(curr, parent)`:**\n  1. Mark `seen[curr] = 1`.\n  2. For each `neighbor` in `adjList[curr]`:\n     - If `!seen[neighbor]`: recursively call `hasCycleDFS(neighbor, curr)`. If that call returns `true`, immediately propagate `true` up the call stack!\n     - Else if `neighbor != parent`: we hit a visited node that isn't our direct parent—return `true`!\n  3. If all neighbors finish without finding a cycle, return `false`.\n- **Complexity:** `O(V + 2E)` Time and `O(V)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Cycle Detection in Undirected Graph (DFS) Blueprint\n```cpp\nbool hasCycleDFS(int curr, int parent, const vector<int> adjList[], vector<int> &seen) {\n    seen[curr] = 1;\n\n    for (int neighbor : adjList[curr]) {\n        if (!seen[neighbor]) {\n            if (hasCycleDFS(neighbor, curr, adjList, seen)) {\n                return true; // Propagate cycle detection upward immediately\n            }\n        } else if (neighbor != parent) {\n            return true;     // Back-edge to an already-visited non-parent node\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n\nbool isCycle(int V, vector<int> adjList[]) {\n    vector<int> seen(V, 0);\n    for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {\n        if (!seen[i]) {\n            if (hasCycleDFS(i, -1, adjList, seen)) return true;\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Writing return hasCycleDFS(...) Unconditionally:** Never write return hasCycleDFS(neighbor, curr, ...) directly inside the for loop! If the first branch returns false, you must continue checking the remaining neighbors—only return early when hasCycleDFS(...) == true!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22256	LLD Project: Chess Game	lld-project-chess-game	Architect a multiplayer Chess engine. Model polymorphic pieces, validate complex board rules, calculate Check/Checkmate recursively, and orchestrate match-making.	2026-10-07 22:41:36.377264+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: LLD Project: Chess Game\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/eULHvaMZUks", "title": "Build Chess Game | UML + Code | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v37-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Object-Oriented Move Validation", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Piece Class (Physical Constraints):** The `Bishop` class knows how a bishop moves geometrically (diagonals). It evaluates raw board boundaries and obstacles to generate an array of mathematically legal squares. It does NOT know about game states like \\"Check\\".\\n2. **ChessRules Engine (State Constraints):** A bishop's diagonal move might be physically legal, but moving that bishop might expose the allied King to an enemy Rook. The `ChessRules` engine intercepts the physically legal moves, simulates them temporarily on the board, and runs `isCheck()`. If the move exposes the King, the Engine vetoes it.\\nThis strictly enforces the Single Responsibility Principle.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why must move validation be split between the polymorphic Piece classes (e.g., Bishop.getPossibleMoves) and the central ChessRules engine (e.g., wouldMoveCauseCheck)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v37-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Algorithmic Implementation of Checkmate", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nCheckmate is computationally intensive. The algorithm is:\\n1. Verify `isCheck(CurrentPlayerColor)` is true. If false, it's not Checkmate (it might be Stalemate).\\n2. Fetch a list of ALL pieces belonging to `CurrentPlayerColor`.\\n3. For every piece, fetch `getPossibleMoves()`.\\n4. For every possible move, execute a simulation: \\n   * Move the piece temporarily on the board.\\n   * Evaluate `isCheck(CurrentPlayerColor)` again.\\n   * Rollback the move.\\n5. If even a **single simulated move** evaluates to `isCheck() == false`, then a legal escape exists. Return false.\\n6. If the loop completes and every single possible move still results in `isCheck() == true`, return true. The player is Checkmated.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Describe the computational algorithm used inside the \\"isCheckmate()\\" method to definitively declare the game over.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v37-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: The Role of the Memento/Command Pattern in Chess", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Command Pattern:** Every executed move is wrapped in a `MoveCommand` object, storing the `from`, `to`, the piece moved, and a pointer to any `capturedPiece`. These commands are pushed onto a Stack. When a user clicks \\"Undo\\", the engine pops the command and executes its `undo()` method, which moves the piece back to `from` and revives the `capturedPiece` at `to`.\\n* **Memento Pattern:** Before every turn, the engine captures a deep serialization of the entire 8x8 Board state (the Memento) and saves it in a Caretaker stack. \\"Undo\\" simply overwrites the active board array with the previous matrix snapshot.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How could the Command Pattern or Memento Pattern be integrated into the Chess LLD to support an \\"Undo Move\\" feature?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v37-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Managing Matchmaking Concurrency", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `MatchManager` must be a Singleton because it serves as the global routing hub for the entire backend server. If multiple instances existed, User A might request a match on Instance 1, and User B on Instance 2, preventing them from ever connecting in the waiting lobby.\\n\\nThe Singleton maintains an `std::unordered_map<std::string, Match*>` (MatchID $\\\\rightarrow$ Match Object). When a WebSocket request arrives carrying a move payload `{\\"matchId\\": \\"123\\", \\"from\\": \\"e2\\", \\"to\\": \\"e4\\"}`, the Singleton performs an $O(1)$ lookup to fetch Match `123` and routes the payload directly to that specific Match's orchestration loop.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "The Chess platform supports thousands of simultaneous games. Why must the MatchManager utilize the Singleton pattern, and how does it store active matches?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v37-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Pawn Promotion and Factory Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA C++ object cannot dynamically change its own class type in memory. The `Pawn` object must be destroyed and replaced.\\n1. The `GameEngine` detects that a move placed a Pawn on rank 8 (or 1).\\n2. The engine halts the loop and prompts the Client UI for a promotion choice.\\n3. The Client returns a string/enum (e.g., \\"QUEEN\\").\\n4. The `GameEngine` passes this to the `PieceFactory::createPiece(\\"QUEEN\\", color)`.\\n5. The engine removes the Pawn from the `Board` array, calls `delete` to free the memory, and places the newly generated `Queen` object at the target coordinate.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "A Pawn reaching the final rank can promote to a Queen, Rook, Bishop, or Knight. Architecturally, how should this transformation be implemented?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Piece Polymorphism\n* A Chessboard contains 64 squares, populated by pieces.\n* `Piece` is an abstract base class with a pure virtual method `getPossibleMoves(board, currentPos)`.\n* `Knight`, `Bishop`, and `Pawn` subclasses override this method. The Knight generates an array of "L-shaped" coordinates; the Bishop iterates diagonally until hitting an obstacle.\n\n### 💡 2. The Rule Engine\n* The `Board` is a dumb 2D array. It only places and removes pieces.\n* The `ChessRules` class evaluates business logic.\n* **isCheck():** Scans the board for all opponent pieces, generates all their `PossibleMoves`, and checks if any target position equals the King's position.\n* **isCheckmate():** Evaluates if the King is in Check AND if every single `PossibleMove` of every friendly piece still results in a state of Check.\n\n### 💡 3. Game Orchestration\n* The `GameManager` acts as the ultimate Facade. It holds a Deque of Players to cycle turns.\n* On `makeMove(from, to)`, the engine validates the move, temporarily executes the move on the board, evaluates `isCheck()`, and either commits the move or rolls back the board state, returning an "Invalid Move" error to the user.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	LLD Project: Chess Game	Architect a multiplayer Chess engine. Model polymorphic pieces, validate complex board rules, calculate Check/Checkmate recursively, and orchestrate match-making.	113	Hard	Low Level Design	51	[{"id": "lld-v37-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Object-Oriented Move Validation", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Piece Class (Physical Constraints):** The `Bishop` class knows how a bishop moves geometrically (diagonals). It evaluates raw board boundaries and obstacles to generate an array of mathematically legal squares. It does NOT know about game states like \\"Check\\".\\n2. **ChessRules Engine (State Constraints):** A bishop's diagonal move might be physically legal, but moving that bishop might expose the allied King to an enemy Rook. The `ChessRules` engine intercepts the physically legal moves, simulates them temporarily on the board, and runs `isCheck()`. If the move exposes the King, the Engine vetoes it.\\nThis strictly enforces the Single Responsibility Principle.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why must move validation be split between the polymorphic Piece classes (e.g., Bishop.getPossibleMoves) and the central ChessRules engine (e.g., wouldMoveCauseCheck)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v37-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Algorithmic Implementation of Checkmate", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nCheckmate is computationally intensive. The algorithm is:\\n1. Verify `isCheck(CurrentPlayerColor)` is true. If false, it's not Checkmate (it might be Stalemate).\\n2. Fetch a list of ALL pieces belonging to `CurrentPlayerColor`.\\n3. For every piece, fetch `getPossibleMoves()`.\\n4. For every possible move, execute a simulation: \\n   * Move the piece temporarily on the board.\\n   * Evaluate `isCheck(CurrentPlayerColor)` again.\\n   * Rollback the move.\\n5. If even a **single simulated move** evaluates to `isCheck() == false`, then a legal escape exists. Return false.\\n6. If the loop completes and every single possible move still results in `isCheck() == true`, return true. The player is Checkmated.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Describe the computational algorithm used inside the \\"isCheckmate()\\" method to definitively declare the game over.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v37-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: The Role of the Memento/Command Pattern in Chess", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Command Pattern:** Every executed move is wrapped in a `MoveCommand` object, storing the `from`, `to`, the piece moved, and a pointer to any `capturedPiece`. These commands are pushed onto a Stack. When a user clicks \\"Undo\\", the engine pops the command and executes its `undo()` method, which moves the piece back to `from` and revives the `capturedPiece` at `to`.\\n* **Memento Pattern:** Before every turn, the engine captures a deep serialization of the entire 8x8 Board state (the Memento) and saves it in a Caretaker stack. \\"Undo\\" simply overwrites the active board array with the previous matrix snapshot.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How could the Command Pattern or Memento Pattern be integrated into the Chess LLD to support an \\"Undo Move\\" feature?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v37-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Managing Matchmaking Concurrency", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `MatchManager` must be a Singleton because it serves as the global routing hub for the entire backend server. If multiple instances existed, User A might request a match on Instance 1, and User B on Instance 2, preventing them from ever connecting in the waiting lobby.\\n\\nThe Singleton maintains an `std::unordered_map<std::string, Match*>` (MatchID $\\\\rightarrow$ Match Object). When a WebSocket request arrives carrying a move payload `{\\"matchId\\": \\"123\\", \\"from\\": \\"e2\\", \\"to\\": \\"e4\\"}`, the Singleton performs an $O(1)$ lookup to fetch Match `123` and routes the payload directly to that specific Match's orchestration loop.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "The Chess platform supports thousands of simultaneous games. Why must the MatchManager utilize the Singleton pattern, and how does it store active matches?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v37-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Pawn Promotion and Factory Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA C++ object cannot dynamically change its own class type in memory. The `Pawn` object must be destroyed and replaced.\\n1. The `GameEngine` detects that a move placed a Pawn on rank 8 (or 1).\\n2. The engine halts the loop and prompts the Client UI for a promotion choice.\\n3. The Client returns a string/enum (e.g., \\"QUEEN\\").\\n4. The `GameEngine` passes this to the `PieceFactory::createPiece(\\"QUEEN\\", color)`.\\n5. The engine removes the Pawn from the `Board` array, calls `delete` to free the memory, and places the newly generated `Queen` object at the target coordinate.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "A Pawn reaching the final rank can promote to a Queen, Rook, Bishop, or Knight. Architecturally, how should this transformation be implemented?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22263	Python Core Foundations & Data Architecture (Part 1)	python-foundations-masterclass-part-1	Exhaustive 10+ hour masterclass covering Python internals, memory references, garbage collection, and core data structures (Lists, Dicts, Tuples, Sets).	2026-10-11 22:39:24.729822+05:30	### 🎯 Computer Science Fundamentals: Python Core Foundations & Data Architecture (Part 1)\nCore systems concepts frequently tested in technical screening rounds and engineering interviews.\n\n- **System Architecture**: How modern operating systems, networks, and processors coordinate resources.\n- **Key Concepts to Master**: Concurrency primitives, resource isolation, latency vs throughput tradeoffs, and fault tolerance.\n- **Interview Key Takeaways**:\n  - Be prepared to draw diagrams explaining data flow and component lifecycles.\n  - Explain practical real-world applications (e.g. why databases use WAL, why HTTP/2 uses multiplexing).	[{"url": "https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/", "title": "OS Three Easy Pieces Guide"}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/operating-systems/", "title": "GeeksforGeeks CS Fundamentals"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/v9bOWjwdTlg", "title": "Complete Python for beginners in Hindi - Part 1", "creator_name": "Chai aur Code", "duration_minutes": 622, "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@chaiaurcode"}]	[{"id": "p1-1", "type": "quiz", "title": "How does Python manage memory internally?", "description": "Python uses a private heap containing all objects. Memory management is handled via Reference Counting and a cyclic Garbage Collector that clears unreferenced objects."}, {"id": "p1-2", "type": "quiz", "title": "What is the difference between `is` and `==`?", "description": "`==` checks for value equality (if data matches), while `is` checks for memory identity (if both variables point to the exact same object in CPython)."}, {"id": "p1-3", "type": "quiz", "title": "Why are Strings and Tuples considered immutable in Python?", "description": "Once created, their internal state cannot be altered. Any operation that appears to modify them actually creates an entirely new object in memory."}, {"id": "p1-4", "type": "quiz", "title": "Explain pass-by-object-reference in Python.", "description": "Variables are labels. When passing to a function, the reference is passed. If the object is mutable (List), in-place changes reflect globally; if immutable (String), reassignment creates a new local object."}, {"id": "p1-5", "type": "quiz", "title": "What happens when you slice a list (e.g., `l2 = l1[:]`)?", "description": "It creates a shallow copy. A new list object is created, but it contains references to the exact same nested objects as the original list."}, {"id": "p1-6", "type": "quiz", "title": "Can a List be used as a Dictionary key?", "description": "No. Dictionary keys must be hashable and immutable. Lists are mutable and unhashable, whereas Tuples (containing only immutable elements) can be used as keys."}, {"id": "p1-7", "type": "quiz", "title": "How does Python's dictionary maintain O(1) lookup time?", "description": "It is implemented as a Hash Map. Python hashes the key to compute an index in a sparse array. Collisions are resolved using open addressing."}, {"id": "p1-8", "type": "quiz", "title": "Explain 'Short-Circuiting' in logical operators.", "description": "In an `and` statement, if the first operand is False, Python returns False without evaluating the second. In an `or` statement, if the first is True, it returns True."}, {"id": "p1-9", "type": "quiz", "title": "What is the Time Complexity of checking `x in y` for Lists vs Sets?", "description": "For Lists, it is O(n) because it requires a linear scan. For Sets, it is O(1) average case because Sets are implemented using hash tables."}, {"id": "p1-10", "type": "quiz", "title": "What does `dict.get(key, default)` do compared to `dict[key]`?", "description": "`dict[key]` raises a `KeyError` if the key doesn't exist. `get()` safely returns `None` (or a specified default) without breaking the program execution."}, {"id": "p1-11", "type": "quiz", "title": "How does the `range()` function work in memory?", "description": "It returns an iterable range object, not a full list. It computes values dynamically on-the-fly (lazy evaluation), which saves RAM for large numbers."}, {"id": "p1-12", "type": "quiz", "title": "Explain List Comprehensions and their performance benefits.", "description": "A concise syntax to create lists. It is generally faster than a `for` loop with `.append()` because it is optimized in C underneath and avoids function call overhead."}, {"id": "p1-13", "type": "quiz", "title": "What is the difference between `append()` and `extend()`?", "description": "`append()` adds its argument as a single element to the end of a list. `extend()` iterates over its argument and adds each element sequentially to the list."}, {"id": "p1-14", "type": "quiz", "title": "How do you safely remove elements from a list while iterating over it?", "description": "Modifying a list while iterating causes index shifting bugs. You should iterate over a copy of the list (`for x in l[:]`) or use list comprehensions."}, {"id": "p1-15", "type": "quiz", "title": "What are Python's bitwise operators used for?", "description": "Operators like `&`, `|`, `<<`, `>>` manipulate integers at the binary level. Used in cryptography, flag masking, and performance-critical algorithms."}, {"id": "p1-16", "type": "quiz", "title": "How do you format strings robustly in modern Python?", "description": "Using f-strings (`f\\"Hello {name}\\"`). They are evaluated at runtime, support embedded expressions, and are faster than `.format()`."}, {"id": "p1-17", "type": "quiz", "title": "What does `//` do compared to `/`?", "description": "`/` performs true division returning a float. `//` performs floor division, returning the largest integer less than or equal to the true result."}, {"id": "p1-18", "type": "quiz", "title": "What happens when you reassign a variable inside a loop?", "description": "The label is simply moved to a new memory object. It does not overwrite the previous object in memory; the old object is garbage collected."}, {"id": "p1-19", "type": "quiz", "title": "Why should you avoid using mutable default arguments in functions?", "description": "Default arguments are evaluated only once at definition time. A mutable default (like `[]`) will be shared across all function calls."}, {"id": "p1-20", "type": "quiz", "title": "What is a tuple's primary advantage over a list?", "description": "Due to immutability, tuples have a smaller memory footprint, faster access times, and can be safely used as keys in dictionaries."}, {"id": "p1-21", "type": "quiz", "title": "Explain the `id()` function.", "description": "It returns the unique memory address (identity) of an object in CPython. Useful for debugging whether two variables point to the exact same object."}, {"id": "p1-22", "type": "quiz", "title": "How does `set.symmetric_difference()` work?", "description": "It returns a new set containing elements that are in either of the two sets, but not in both (the mathematical XOR operation)."}, {"id": "p1-23", "type": "quiz", "title": "What is tuple unpacking?", "description": "It allows assigning the elements of an iterable to multiple variables in a single line. Commonly used for swapping variables: `a, b = b, a`."}, {"id": "p1-24", "type": "quiz", "title": "How do you merge two dictionaries in Python 3.9+?", "description": "Using the union operator `|` (e.g., `d3 = d1 | d2`). Overlapping keys take the value from the rightmost dictionary."}, {"id": "p1-25", "type": "quiz", "title": "What is the `None` type?", "description": "A special singleton object used to signify the absence of a value. Functions without a return statement implicitly return `None`."}, {"id": "p1-26", "type": "quiz", "title": "Explain Python's ternary conditional operator.", "description": "A one-line shorthand for if-else: `value_if_true if condition else value_if_false`. Heavily used in clean variable assignments."}, {"id": "p1-27", "type": "quiz", "title": "What is the purpose of `enumerate()`?", "description": "It adds a counter to an iterable and returns it as an enumerate object (yielding index-value tuples), eliminating manual counter variables."}, {"id": "p1-28", "type": "quiz", "title": "How do you create a true independent copy of deeply nested lists?", "description": "Use the `copy` module's `deepcopy()` method. It recursively clones all nested objects, unlike a shallow copy which only clones the outermost container."}, {"id": "p1-29", "type": "quiz", "title": "What are 'Truthy' and 'Falsy' values?", "description": "In boolean contexts, Python evaluates empty sequences (`[]`, `\\"\\"`), numeric zeros (`0`), and `None` as False. Almost everything else evaluates to True."}, {"id": "p1-30", "type": "quiz", "title": "What is string interning?", "description": "CPython optimizes memory by implicitly caching short, identifier-like string objects. Variables assigned identical strings share the exact same memory address."}]	\N	> **Note:** This massive masterclass heavily focuses on the internal mechanics of CPython, memory handling, and data type optimizations.\n\n### 📌 Core Architecture Blueprint\n1. **Memory & References:** Variables are strictly labels pointing to objects in memory. CPython manages this via reference counting and garbage collection.\n2. **Immutability vs Mutability:** Strings and Tuples are deeply immutable. Modifying them creates new memory references. Lists and Dictionaries mutate in-place.\n3. **Data Structures:** Hash Maps (Dictionaries), Dynamic Arrays (Lists), and Uniqueness Tables (Sets).\n4. **Logic Gates:** Short-circuiting with `and`/`or` and chained comparisons.	Chai aur Code	https://www.youtube.com/@chaiaurcode	\N	Python Core Foundations & Data Architecture (Part 1)	Exhaustive 10+ hour masterclass covering Python internals, memory references, garbage collection, and core data structures (Lists, Dicts, Tuples, Sets).	622	Easy	Python Fundamentals	1	[]	\N	622	https://youtu.be/v9bOWjwdTlg	Complete Python for beginners in Hindi - Part 1
