10015	Three-Chain Dummy Partitioning: Sort a Linked List of 0's, 1's and 2's by Changing Links	dsa-sort-linked-list-of-0s-1s-and-2s	Segregate 0s, 1s, and 2s in a single pass by distributing nodes into three dummy-headed chains and splicing them with null termination.	2026-10-03 15:04:07.029205+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Three-Chain Dummy Partitioning: Sort a Linked List of 0's, 1's and 2's by Changing Links\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gRII7LhdJWc", "title": "Lecture 243: Sort a Linked List of 0's, 1's and 2's", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/given-a-linked-list-of-0s-1s-and-2s-sort-it/1", "title": "Given a linked list of 0s, 1s and 2s, sort it", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Sort a linked list of 0s, 1s, and 2s in a single pass by re-linking nodes in O(1) auxiliary space."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/partition-list/", "title": "Partition List (LC 86)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use two dummy heads to partition a linked list around a pivot value x in one pass."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sort-colors/", "title": "Sort Colors (LC 75)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Compare array-based Dutch National Flag sorting with linked-list 3-dummy-chain splicing."}]	Module 37: Linked List Medium Problems: Dummy Nodes, In-Place Re-Linking & Tortoise-Hare Pointers	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the `head` of a linked list where each node contains `0`, `1`, or `2`, sort the linked list in **a single traversal (`O(N)` time)** and **`O(1)` extra space** by **changing node links** (without overwriting `node->data`).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Optimal)\n- **1. Brute Force Data Replacement (`O(2N)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Pass 1: Count `cnt0`, `cnt1`, `cnt2`. Pass 2: Overwrite node values.\n  - Interviewers quickly follow up: *"Do it in ONE pass without modifying node data!"*\n- **2. Optimal Three-Dummy-Head Chain Splicing (`O(N)` Single Pass, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Create **3 dummy heads** and 3 moving tail pointers:\n    - `zeroHead = new Node(-1); zero = zeroHead;`\n    - `oneHead  = new Node(-1); one  = oneHead;`\n    - `twoHead  = new Node(-1); two  = twoHead;`\n  - Traverse `temp` from `head`:\n    - If `temp->data == 0`: `zero->next = temp; zero = temp;`\n    - Else if `temp->data == 1`: `one->next = temp; one = temp;`\n    - Else (`temp->data == 2`): `two->next = temp; two = temp;`\n  - **Connecting the 3 Chains Safely (Handling Empty Sub-Lists!):**\n    - **`zero->next = (oneHead->next != nullptr) ? oneHead->next : twoHead->next;`** (if there are no `1`s, `zero` connects directly to `twoHead->next`!).\n    - **`one->next = twoHead->next;`**\n    - **`two->next = nullptr;`** (critical to terminate the list and avoid a cycle!).\n  - Return `zeroHead->next` (after deleting the 3 dummy nodes).\n\n### 💻 Sort a Linked List of 0s, 1s, and 2s Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nNode* segregate012(Node* head) {\n    if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr) return head;\n\n    Node* zeroHead = new Node(-1);\n    Node* oneHead  = new Node(-1);\n    Node* twoHead  = new Node(-1);\n\n    Node* zero = zeroHead;\n    Node* one  = oneHead;\n    Node* two  = twoHead;\n    Node* temp = head;\n\n    while (temp != nullptr) {\n        if (temp->data == 0) {\n            zero->next = temp;\n            zero = temp;\n        } else if (temp->data == 1) {\n            one->next = temp;\n            one = temp;\n        } else {\n            two->next = temp;\n            two = temp;\n        }\n        temp = temp->next;\n    }\n\n    // Connect the 3 chains (handling cases where 0s, 1s, or 2s are absent!)\n    zero->next = (oneHead->next != nullptr) ? oneHead->next : twoHead->next;\n    one->next  = twoHead->next;\n    two->next  = nullptr; // Terminate the final tail!\n\n    Node* newHead = zeroHead->next;\n    delete zeroHead;\n    delete oneHead;\n    delete twoHead;\n    return newHead;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting `two->next = nullptr;`:** The last node appended to the `2`s chain might still have its old `next` pointer pointing back to a `0` or `1` node earlier in the list, creating an **infinite cycle**! Always set `two->next = nullptr;`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
