lete a resource"], "question": "What is the specific purpose of the PATCH HTTP method in REST?", "explanation": "While PUT replaces the entire resource object, PATCH is used to selectively modify only the specified fields of a resource without altering the rest.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "To partially update an existing resource"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["200 OK", "201 Created", "204 No Content", "301 Moved Permanently"], "question": "Which HTTP status code signifies that a resource was successfully created via a POST request?", "explanation": "The 201 Created status code explicitly confirms that the server successfully processed the request and a new database record/resource was instantiated as a result.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "201 Created"}, {"id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["The request cannot be cached by intermediate proxies.", "The method will automatically retry if the network fails.", "Making the exact same request multiple times will leave the server state identical to making it just once.", "The payload size is heavily compressed."], "question": "What does it mean when an HTTP method like GET is described as 'idempotent'?", "explanation": "Idempotency means repeated identical requests (like querying a product list via GET, or deleting a specific ID via DELETE) have the same ultimate effect on the backend state as a single request.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "Making the exact same request multiple times will leave the server state identical to making it just once."}, {"id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["It forces the database to keep automated backups.", "It allows developers to introduce breaking changes in a v2 without crashing external client applications still relying on v1.", "It prevents unauthorized users from accessing the API.", "It encrypts the payload for specific client versions."], "question": "Why is versioning (e.g., `/api/v1/users`) considered a critical best practice in REST API design?", "explanation": "Versioning ensures backward compatibility. You can safely release new endpoints or alter data schemas under `/v2/` while giving legacy clients time to migrate off `/v1/`.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "It allows developers to introduce breaking changes in a v2 without crashing external client applications still relying on v1."}]	\N	> **Architecture Overview:** Essential technical concepts and network principles vital for robust, scalable web architectures. Focus on the core mechanics and trade-offs of the system discussed.	Hayk Simonyan	https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan	\N	REST API Basics & Best Practices Explained	Deep dive into REST API Basics & Best Practices Explained covering architectural implementations, networking, and system resilience.	14	Easy	Advanced System Concepts	11	[]	\N	14	https://youtu.be/DkSeXHS0kAQ	REST API Basics & Best Practices Explained
