y": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Second Highest Salary (Scalar Subquery Comparison)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/second-highest-salary/"}, {"id": "pg-24-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/exchange-seats/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/exchange-seats/", "name": "Exchange Seats (Scalar Subquery COUNT)", "title": "Exchange Seats (Scalar Subquery COUNT)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "24_6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Exchange Seats (Scalar Subquery COUNT)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/exchange-seats/"}, {"id": "pg-24-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/tree-node/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/tree-node/", "name": "Tree Node (IN Subquery Classification)", "title": "Tree Node (IN Subquery Classification)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "24_7", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Tree Node (IN Subquery Classification)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/tree-node/"}, {"id": "pg-24-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/department-highest-salary/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/department-highest-salary/", "name": "Department Highest Salary (Tuple IN Subquery)", "title": "Department Highest Salary (Tuple IN Subquery)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "24_8", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Department Highest Salary (Tuple IN Subquery)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/department-highest-salary/"}, {"id": "pg-24-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/investments-in-2016/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/investments-in-2016/", "name": "Investments in 2016 (Multi-Subquery Filtering)", "title": "Investments in 2016 (Multi-Subquery Filtering)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "24_9", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Investments in 2016 (Multi-Subquery Filtering)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/investments-in-2016/"}, {"id": "pg-24-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/", "name": "Customers Who Bought All Products (Scalar Subquery in HAVING)", "title": "Customers Who Bought All Products (Scalar Subquery in HAVING)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "24_10", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Customers Who Bought All Products (Scalar Subquery in HAVING)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nSuppose you need to find all courses priced higher than the average course fee. Writing `WHERE fee > AVG(fee)` fails immediately with `ERROR: aggregate functions are not allowed in WHERE`. We solve this using a **Subquery** (a query nested inside parentheses within another query).\n\n### 🔍 How Subqueries Execute\n1. **Inner Query Runs First:** PostgreSQL executes the parenthesized inner query first.\n2. **Outer Query Consumes the Result:** The output of the inner query is substituted into the outer query's condition.\n\n### ⚖️ 1. Scalar Subquery vs. 2. List Subquery\n\n* **Scalar Subquery:** **Exactly 1 row and 1 column** (a single atomic value). — `=`, `!=`, `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=` — `WHERE fee > (SELECT AVG(fee) FROM courses)`\n* **List Subquery:** **Multiple rows** in 1 column (a list of values). — `IN`, `NOT IN`, `ANY`, `ALL` — `WHERE department_id IN (SELECT id FROM ...)`\n\n### 💻 Practical Query Blueprints\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Scalar Subquery: Find all courses priced strictly above the average fee\nSELECT title, fee\nFROM courses\nWHERE fee > (\n    SELECT AVG(fee) FROM courses\n);\n\n-- 2. List Subquery: Find all users belonging to any 'Engineering' department\n-- (Matches both 'Mechanical Engineering', 'Civil Engineering', 'Computer Science', etc.)\nSELECT name, email\nFROM users\nWHERE department_id IN (\n    SELECT id\n    FROM departments\n    WHERE name ILIKE '%Engineering%'\n);\n```\n\n### 🚨 Common Pitfall: "more than one row returned by a subquery"\n* If an inner query returns multiple rows (e.g., `SELECT id FROM departments WHERE name ILIKE '%Engineering%'` returns IDs `2` and `3`), using `=` (`WHERE department_id = (...)`) will crash!\n* Always pair multi-row subqueries with **`IN`** (or `NOT IN`).	Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)	https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical	654	Lecture 24: Subqueries in PostgreSQL — Scalar Subqueries, List (IN) Subqueries & Execution Flow	Write nested queries inside WHERE clauses, distinguish single-value Scalar Subqueries from multi-row List Subqueries, and avoid the multi-row = operator crash.	14	Medium	Module 7: Temporal Queries, Subqueries & CTEs	24	[{"id": "pg-24-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-whose-manager-left-the-company/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-whose-manager-left-the-company/", "name": "Employees Whose Manager Left the Company (IN / NOT IN Subquery)", "title": "Employees Whose Manager Left the Company (IN / NOT IN Subquery)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "24_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Employees Whose Manager Left the Company (IN / NOT IN Subquery)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-whose-manager-left-the-company/"}, {"id": "pg-24-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "name": "Customers Who Never Order (NOT IN Subquery)", "title": "Customers Who Never Order (NOT IN Subquery)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "24_2", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Customers Who Never Order (NOT IN Subquery)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/"}, {"id": "pg-24-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sales-person/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sales-person/", "name": "Sales Person (Nested IN / NOT IN Subquery)", "title": "Sales Person (Nested IN / NOT IN Subquery)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "24_3", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Sales Person (Nested IN / NOT IN Subquery)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sales-person/"}, {"id": "pg-24-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/biggest-single-number/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/biggest-single-number/", "name": "Biggest Single Number (Scalar Subquery with MAX)", "title": "Biggest Single Number (Scalar Subquery with MAX)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "24_4", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Biggest Single Number (Scalar Subquery with MAX)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/biggest-single-number/"}, {"id": "pg-24-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/second-highest-salary/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/second-highest-salary/", "name": "Second Highest Salary (Scalar Subquery Comparison)", "title": "Second Highest Salary (Scalar Subquery Comparison)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "24_5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Second Highest Salary (Scalar Subquery Comparison)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/second-highest-salary/"}, {"id": "pg-24-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/exchange-seats/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/exchange-seats/", "name": "Exchange Seats (Scalar Subquery COUNT)", "title": "Exchange Seats (Scalar Subquery COUNT)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "24_6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Exchange Seats (Scalar Subquery COUNT)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/exchange-seats/"}, {"id": "pg-24-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/tree-node/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/tree-node/", "name": "Tree Node (IN Subquery Classification)", "title": "Tree Node (IN Subquery Classification)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "24_7", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Tree Node (IN Subquery Classification)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/tree-node/"}, {"id": "pg-24-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/department-highest-salary/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/department-highest-salary/", "name": "Department Highest Salary (Tuple IN Subquery)", "title": "Department Highest Salary (Tuple IN Subquery)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "24_8", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Department Highest Salary (Tuple IN Subquery)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/department-highest-salary/"}, {"id": "pg-24-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/investments-in-2016/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/investments-in-2016/", "name": "Investments in 2016 (Multi-Subquery Filtering)", "title": "Investments in 2016 (Multi-Subquery Filtering)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "24_9", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Investments in 2016 (Multi-Subquery Filtering)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/investments-in-2016/"}, {"id": "pg-24-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/", "name": "Customers Who Bought All Products (Scalar Subquery in HAVING)", "title": "Customers Who Bought All Products (Scalar Subquery in HAVING)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "24_10", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Customers Who Bought All Products (Scalar Subquery in HAVING)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/"}]	https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/functions-subquery.html	\N	\N	\N
15091	2D Prefix-Height Histogram Reduction: Maximal Rectangle in a Binary Matrix	dsa-maximal-rectangle-in-binary-matrix-stack	Master 2D Prefix-Height Histogram Reduction: Maximal Rectangle in a Binary Matrix with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:30:52.972037+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: 2D Prefix-Height Histogram Reduction: Maximal Rectangle in a Binary Matrix\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ttVu6G7Ayik", "title": "2D Prefix-Height Histogram Reduction: Maximal Rectangle in a Binary Matrix - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximal-rectangle/", "title": "LeetCode 85: Maximal Rectangle", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Given a rows x cols binary matrix filled with 0's and 1's, find the largest rectangle containing only 1's and return its area."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/largest-rectangle-in-histogram/", "title": "LeetCode 84: Largest Rectangle in Histogram", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Use the 1-pass O(2M) histogram solver as the row-level engine for Maximal Rectangle."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/max-rectangle/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Max rectangle", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find the maximum area of a rectangle formed only of 1s in the given binary matrix."}]	Module 43: Monotonic Stack & Contribution Algorithms: Next/Previous Greater/Smaller, Rainwater & Subarray Aggregations	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an `N x M` binary matrix filled with `0`s and `1`s, find the **area of the largest rectangle containing only `1`s**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Reducing Each Row to "Largest Rectangle in Histogram" (Topic 308!):**\n  - If we treat each row `i` (`0` to `N - 1`) as the base of a histogram, the height of bar `j` on row `i` is simply the **number of consecutive `1`s stacked vertically above `(i, j)`**!\n  - **Step 1 (Column-Wise Prefix Heights `pSum[N][M]`):**\n    - For each column `j` (`0` to `M - 1`), maintain a running `sum = 0` down the rows `i` (`0` to `N - 1`):\n      - If `matrix[i][j] == 1`, `sum += 1`.\n      - If **`matrix[i][j] == 0`**, reset **`sum = 0`** (because a `0` at the base breaks the vertical bar of `1`s!).\n      - Store `pSum[i][j] = sum`.\n  - **Step 2 (Pass Each Row `pSum[i]` to `largestRectangleArea`):**\n    - Loop `i` from `0` to `N - 1`: `maxArea = max(maxArea, largestRectangleArea(pSum[i]))`.\n- **Complexity:** **`O(N * M + N * 2M) = O(N * M)`** Time and **`O(N * M)`** Space (or `O(M)` Space if updating a single 1D `heights[M]` row on the fly!).\n\n### 💻 Maximal Rectangle Blueprint\n```cpp\nint largestHist(const vector<int> &arr) {\n    int n = arr.size();\n    stack<int> st;\n    int maxArea = 0;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        while (!st.empty() && arr[st.top()] > arr[i]) {\n            int element = st.top();\n            st.pop();\n            int nse = i;\n            int pse = st.empty() ? -1 : st.top();\n            maxArea = max(maxArea, arr[element] * (nse - pse - 1));\n        }\n        st.push(i);\n    }\n    while (!st.empty()) {\n        int nse = n;\n        int element = st.top();\n        st.pop();\n        int pse = st.empty() ? -1 : st.top();\n        maxArea = max(maxArea, arr[element] * (nse - pse - 1));\n    }\n    return maxArea;\n}\n\nint maximalRectangle(vector<vector<char>> &matrix) {\n    if (matrix.empty()) return 0;\n    int n = matrix.size(), m = matrix[0].size();\n    vector<vector<int>> pSum(n, vector<int>(m, 0));\n\n    // 1. Precompute vertical consecutive 1-heights column by column\n    for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {\n        int sum = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            if (matrix[i][j] == '1') sum += 1;\n            else sum = 0; // Reset height to 0 whenever base cell is '0'!\n            pSum[i][j] = sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // 2. Run Largest Rectangle in Histogram on each row\n    int maxArea = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        maxArea = max(maxArea, largestHist(pSum[i]));\n    }\n    return maxArea;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Doing a Standard Prefix Sum Without Resetting `sum = 0` on `'0'`:** If a column has `['1', '0', '1']`, the bar height at row 2 is **`1`** (not `2`!), because the `'0'` at row 1 breaks vertical contiguity!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
14170	LIFO Last-Opened Bracket Matching: Check for Balanced Parentheses	dsa-check-for-balanced-parentheses-stack	Master LIFO Last-Opened Bracket Matching: Check for Balanced Parentheses with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:24:23.653436+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: LIFO Last-Opened Bracket Matching: Check for Balanced Parentheses\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xwjS0iZhw4I", "title": "LIFO Last-Opened Bracket Matching: Check for Balanced Parentheses - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/valid-parentheses/", "title": "LeetCode 20: Valid Parentheses", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Given a string s containing just the characters '(', ')', '{', '}', '[' and ']', determine if the input string is valid."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-valid-parentheses/", "title": "LeetCode 32: Longest Valid Parentheses", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Given a string containing just the characters '(' and ')', return the length of the longest valid (well-formed) parentheses substring."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/parenthesis-checker2744/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Parenthesis Checker", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Check if an expression string has balanced brackets (), {}, and []."}]	Module 42: Stack & Queue Foundations: Custom Data Structure Implementations, Expression Conversions & Min Stack	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a string `s` containing only three types of brackets—round `'('`, `')'`, curly `'{'`, `'}'`, and square `'['`, `']'`—determine if `s` is **balanced**:\n1. Every opening bracket must be closed by the **same type** of bracket.\n2. Open brackets must be closed in the **correct LIFO order** (the **most recently opened** bracket must be closed first).\n3. Every closing bracket must have a corresponding earlier opening bracket.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why a Stack Is Required:**\n  - Whenever we encounter a closing bracket, we must match it against the **last unmatched opening bracket** seen so far—which is the exact definition of **LIFO (Stack)**!\n- **Algorithm & The 2 Edge Cases (`O(N)` Time, `O(N)` Space):**\n  - Loop through each character `it` in `s`:\n    1. **If `it` is an Opening Bracket (`'('`, `'{'`, or `'['`):** Push `it` onto `st`.\n    2. **Else (`it` is a Closing Bracket):**\n       - **Edge Case 1 (`st.empty()`):** We found a closing bracket when the stack is empty (e.g., `"]"` or `"())"`). There is no opening bracket for it → immediately **`return false`**!\n       - Pop `char ch = st.top(); st.pop();`.\n       - Verify that `(it == ')' && ch == '(') || (it == ']' && ch == '[') || (it == '}' && ch == '{')`. If not, **`return false`**.\n  - **Edge Case 2 (Unclosed Opening Brackets at the End, e.g., `"(()"`):**\n    - After the loop finishes, return **`st.empty()`** (not `true`! If `st` still has leftover opening brackets, the string is invalid!).\n\n### 💻 Check for Balanced Parentheses Blueprint\n```cpp\nbool isValid(string s) {\n    stack<char> st;\n\n    for (char it : s) {\n        if (it == '(' || it == '{' || it == '[') {\n            st.push(it);\n        } else {\n            // Edge Case 1: Closing bracket with no matching opening bracket!\n            if (st.empty()) return false;\n\n            char ch = st.top();\n            st.pop();\n\n            if ((it == ')' && ch == '(') ||\n                (it == ']' && ch == '[') ||\n                (it == '}' && ch == '{')) {\n                continue;\n            } else {\n                return false; // Mismatched bracket types!\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Edge Case 2: Valid ONLY if all opening brackets were closed!\n    return st.empty();\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Calling `st.top()` Before Checking `if (st.empty())`:** On an input like `")"` or `"()]"`, `st` is empty when the extra closing bracket is reached—calling `st.top()` without `if (st.empty()) return false;` causes an immediate segmentation fault / runtime error!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
