le": "Customer Placing the Largest Number of Orders", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "19_9", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Customer Placing the Largest Number of Orders\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customer-placing-the-largest-number-of-orders/"}, {"id": "pg-19-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/immediate-food-delivery-ii/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/immediate-food-delivery-ii/", "name": "Immediate Food Delivery II", "title": "Immediate Food Delivery II", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "19_10", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Immediate Food Delivery II\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/immediate-food-delivery-ii/"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\n**Aggregate Functions** scan an entire set of rows and collapse them into a **single summarized value** (similar to `Array.prototype.reduce()` in JavaScript).\n\n### 📊 The 5 Core SQL Aggregate Functions\n\n* **`COUNT()`:** Counts the number of rows or non-null values. — `COUNT(*)`, `COUNT(id)`\n* **`SUM()`:** Adds up all numeric values in a column. — `SUM(amount)`\n* **`AVG()`:** Calculates the arithmetic mean of a numeric column. — `AVG(fee)`\n* **`MIN()`:** Finds the smallest value (lowest price, earliest date, A–Z first). — `MIN(fee)`\n* **`MAX()`:** Finds the largest value (highest price, latest date, Z–A last). — `MAX(fee)`\n\n### 💻 Practical Query Blueprints\n\n```sql\n-- 1. COUNT: Total active students currently enrolled in the college\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS "Total Active Students"\nFROM users\nWHERE role = 'student' AND is_active = true;\n\n-- 2. SUM: Total revenue collected from completed payments only\nSELECT SUM(amount) AS "Total Revenue Collected"\nFROM payments\nWHERE status = 'completed';\n\n-- 3. AVG + ROUND: Average course fee rounded cleanly to 2 decimal places\nSELECT ROUND(AVG(fee), 2) AS "Average Course Fee"\nFROM courses;\n\n-- 4. MIN & MAX: Cheapest and most expensive course fees\nSELECT \n    MIN(fee) AS cheapest_course,\n    MAX(fee) AS most_expensive_course\nFROM courses;\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Two High-Frequency Interview Questions\n\n1. **`COUNT(*)` vs. `COUNT(column_name)`:**\n   * `COUNT(*)` counts **every row** in the result set, including rows with `NULL` values.\n   * `COUNT(column_name)` counts **only rows where `column_name IS NOT NULL`** (silently ignoring `NULL`s).\n2. **The Non-Aggregated Column Crash:**\n   * Writing `SELECT title, MIN(fee) FROM courses;` throws `ERROR: column "courses.title" must appear in the GROUP BY clause or be used in an aggregate function`. Why? Because `MIN(fee)` collapses the table into **1 single value**, whereas `title` has 20 rows—PostgreSQL cannot squash 20 titles into 1 row without a `GROUP BY` rule.	Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)	https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical	654	Lecture 19: Aggregate Functions — COUNT, SUM, AVG, ROUND, MIN & MAX	Summarize entire columns into single metrics using COUNT(*) vs COUNT(column), SUM, AVG with ROUND(), MIN, and MAX.	19	Easy	Module 6: Aggregations, Grouping & Analytical Queries	19	[{"id": "pg-19-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-total-time-spent-by-each-employee/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-total-time-spent-by-each-employee/", "name": "Find Total Time Spent by Each Employee (SUM Aggregation)", "title": "Find Total Time Spent by Each Employee (SUM Aggregation)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "19_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Find Total Time Spent by Each Employee (SUM Aggregation)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-total-time-spent-by-each-employee/"}, {"id": "pg-19-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/average-selling-price/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/average-selling-price/", "name": "Average Selling Price (SUM & ROUND Math)", "title": "Average Selling Price (SUM & ROUND Math)", "plat