13276	Sorted Two-Pointer Greedy Matching: Assign Cookies	dsa-assign-cookies-greedy-algorithm	Master Sorted Two-Pointer Greedy Matching: Assign Cookies with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:19:12.167669+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Sorted Two-Pointer Greedy Matching: Assign Cookies\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DIX2p7vb9co", "title": "Sorted Two-Pointer Greedy Matching: Assign Cookies - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/assign-cookies/", "title": "LeetCode 455: Assign Cookies", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Maximize the number of content children by assigning at most one cookie of size s[j] >= g[i] to each child."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-matching-of-players-with-trainers/", "title": "LeetCode 2410: Maximum Matching of Players With Trainers", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Apply the exact same sorted two-pointer greedy matching to pair players with trainers."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/most-profit-assigning-work/", "title": "LeetCode 826: Most Profit Assigning Work", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Sort jobs and workers to greedily assign the most profitable feasible job to each worker."}]	Module 41: Greedy Algorithms: Sorting Heuristics, Reachability Horizons & Interval Scheduling	### 📌 Topic Overview\nYou have `N` children with greed factors `g[i]` and `M` cookies with sizes `s[j]`. Child `i` is content if assigned a cookie `j` with **`s[j] >= g[i]`**, and each child can receive at most 1 cookie. Maximize the number of content children.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Core Greedy Principle — Don't Waste Large Cookies on Small Greeds!**\n  - If a child only needs a cookie of size `1`, giving them a huge cookie of size `4` wastes the `4` cookie that could have satisfied a hungrier child!\n  - Instead, satisfy each child using the **smallest available cookie that meets their greed `s[l] >= g[r]`**!\n- **Sorting + Two Pointers (`O(N log N + M log M + M)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  1. Sort both `g` (greed) and `s` (cookie sizes) in ascending order.\n  2. Place pointer `l = 0` on cookies `s` and pointer `r = 0` on children `g`.\n  3. Loop **`while (l < m && r < n)`**:\n     - If **`s[l] >= g[r]`**: Cookie `l` satisfies child `r`! Move to the next child: **`r++`**.\n     - Regardless of whether cookie `l` was used or was too small for `g[r]` (and therefore too small for all subsequent children too!), move to the next cookie: **`l++`**.\n  4. Return **`r`** (the exact number of children satisfied!).\n\n### 💻 Assign Cookies Blueprint\n```cpp\nint findContentChildren(vector<int> &g, vector<int> &s) {\n    int n = g.size(), m = s.size();\n    sort(g.begin(), g.end());\n    sort(s.begin(), s.end());\n\n    int l = 0; // Pointer over cookie sizes 's'\n    int r = 0; // Pointer over children greed 'g'\n\n    while (l < m && r < n) {\n        if (s[l] >= g[r]) {\n            r++; // Child r is satisfied; advance to next child!\n        }\n        l++; // Move to the next cookie in all cases\n    }\n    return r;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Looping `while (l < m)` Without Checking `r < n`:** If there are more cookies than children (`m > n`) and all `n` children get satisfied early, `g[r]` will read out of bounds (`g[n]`) if you don't include **`&& r < n`** in the `while` condition!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
9280	Pointer Splicing & Memory Deallocation: 8 Deletion and Insertion Operations in a Singly Linked List	dsa-deletion-and-insertion-in-singly-linked-list	Master all 8 fundamental insertion and deletion operations in a singly linked list with proper pointer splicing and heap memory deallocation.	2026-10-03 14:56:36.919471+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Pointer Splicing & Memory Deallocation: 8 Deletion and Insertion Operations in a Singly Linked List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VaECK03Dz-g", "title": "Lecture 238: Insertion and Deletion in Singly Linked List", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/design-linked-list/", "title": "Design Linked List (LC 707)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Implement get, addAtHead, addAtTail, addAtIndex, and deleteAtIndex in a linked list."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/delete-a-node-in-single-linked-list/1", "title": "Delete a Node in Single Linked List", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Delete the x-th node (1-based index) from a singly linked list and return the updated head."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-node-in-a-linked-list/", "title": "Delete Node in a Linked List (LC 237)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Delete a node in a singly linked list when given access only to that node (not the head)."}]	Module 36: Singly & Doubly Linked Lists: Memory Architecture, Pointer Mutations & Reversal	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMaster all **8 foundational pointer mutations** on a Singly Linked List:\n- **4 Deletions:** (1) Delete Head, (2) Delete Tail, (3) Delete `K`-th Node (`1`-based), (4) Delete Node by Value `el`.\n- **4 Insertions:** (1) Insert at Head, (2) Insert at Tail, (3) Insert at `K`-th Position, (4) Insert Before Value `val`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **1. Deleting the Head (`O(1)`):**\n  - Guard `if (head == nullptr) return head;`.\n  - Save `Node* temp = head;`, advance `head = head->next;`, **`delete temp;`** (free heap memory in C++!), and return `head`.\n- **2. Deleting the Tail (`O(N)`):**\n  - Guard `if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr) return nullptr;`.\n  - Stop at the **second-to-last node** where **`temp->next->next == nullptr`**, `delete temp->next;`, and set `temp->next = nullptr;`.\n- **3. Deleting the `K`-th Node (`O(K)`):**\n  - If `k == 1`, delete head. Otherwise, track `prev = nullptr` while advancing `temp` and `cnt++`. When `cnt == k`, bypass `temp` via **`prev->next = prev->next->next;`** and `delete temp;`.\n- **4. Inserting at the `K`-th Position (`O(K)`):**\n  - If `k == 1`, return `new Node(el, head);`.\n  - Otherwise, stop at the **`(K - 1)`-th node** (`cnt == k - 1`), allocate `Node* x = new Node(el, temp->next);`, and link **`temp->next = x;`**!\n\n### 💻 Singly Linked List Deletions & Insertions Blueprint\n~~~cpp\n// 1. Delete K-th Element (1-based) in Singly Linked List\nNode* removeK(Node* head, int k) {\n    if (head == nullptr) return head;\n    if (k == 1) {\n        Node* temp = head;\n        head = head->next;\n        delete temp;\n        return head;\n    }\n    int cnt = 0;\n    Node* temp = head;\n    Node* prev = nullptr;\n    while (temp != nullptr) {\n        cnt++;\n        if (cnt == k) {\n            prev->next = prev->next->next;\n            delete temp;\n            break;\n        }\n        prev = temp;\n        temp = temp->next;\n    }\n    return head;\n}\n\n// 2. Insert at K-th Position (1 <= k <= length + 1)\nNode* insertPosition(Node* head, int el, int k) {\n    if (head == nullptr) {\n        if (k == 1) return new Node(el);\n        else return head;\n    }\n    if (k == 1) return new Node(el, head);\n\n    int cnt = 0;\n    Node* temp = head;\n    while (temp != nullptr) {\n        cnt++;\n        if (cnt == k - 1) {\n            Node* x = new Node(el, temp->next);\n            temp->next = x;\n            break;\n        }\n        temp = temp->next;\n    }\n    return head;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Overwriting `temp->next = x` Before Setting `x->next = temp->next`:** When inserting `x` after `temp`, always point `x->next = temp->next` **first** (or pass `temp->next` to the constructor `new Node(el, temp->next)`), otherwise you lose the rest of the linked list!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
