with a cycle and find the cycle entry point using Floyd's algorithm."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/find-the-first-node-of-loop-in-linked-list--170645/1", "title": "Find the first node of loop in linked list", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the first node of the loop in a linked list, or -1 if no loop exists."}]	Module 37: Linked List Medium Problems: Dummy Nodes, In-Place Re-Linking & Tortoise-Hare Pointers	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the `head` of a linked list, return the **exact starting `Node*` where the cycle begins** (or `nullptr` if there is no cycle) in **`O(N)` time and `O(1)` extra space**.\n\n### 💡 The 2-Phase Tortoise & Hare Algorithm + Mathematical Proof (`L1 == Remaining Loop Distance`)\n- **Algorithm:**\n  1. **Phase 1 (Find Collision Point):** Move `slow` by `1` step and `fast` by `2` steps from `head` until **`slow == fast`** (if `fast` hits `nullptr`, return `nullptr`).\n  2. **Phase 2 (Reset `slow` to `head` and Walk Both by 1 Step):**\n     - Place `slow = head;` while leaving `fast` at the collision point.\n     - Move **both `slow` and `fast` by `1` step at a time** (`while (slow != fast)`).\n     - The node where they collide again is the **starting point of the loop**!\n- **Mathematical Proof — Why Does Distance `head → start` Equal `collision → start`?**\n  - Let **`L1`** be the distance from `head` to the loop's starting node.\n  - Let **`d`** be the forward distance along the loop from the starting node to the collision point.\n  - When `slow` first enters the loop at the starting node (after `L1` steps), `fast` has already taken `2 * L1` steps (`L1` steps into the loop).\n  - If the total loop circumference is `C`, the remaining distance from `fast` behind `slow` is **`d = C - L1`**.\n  - Because `fast` closes the gap by `1` unit per step, they collide after `slow` walks **`d`** more steps!\n  - Since total loop length is `C = L1 + d`, and the collision point is at distance `d` from the start of the loop, the **remaining forward distance from the collision point back to the start of the loop is `C - d = (L1 + d) - d = L1`**!\n  - Because both distances equal **`L1`**, walking `slow` from `head` (`L1` steps) and `fast` from the collision point (`L1` steps) at `1` step each guarantees they meet at the **exact loop entrance**!\n\n### 💻 Find Starting Point of Loop in Linked List Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nNode* detectCycleStart(Node* head) {\n    Node* slow = head;\n    Node* fast = head;\n\n    // Phase 1: Detect cycle collision point\n    while (fast != nullptr && fast->next != nullptr) {\n        slow = slow->next;\n        fast = fast->next->next;\n\n        if (slow == fast) {\n            // Phase 2: Reset slow to head and advance both by 1 step!\n            slow = head;\n            while (slow != fast) {\n                slow = slow->next;\n                fast = fast->next;\n            }\n            return slow; // Starting node of the cycle!\n        }\n    }\n    return nullptr; // No cycle\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Storing `node->data` Instead of `Node*` in Brute Force Hashing:** Two distinct nodes can have the same integer value `15`—always hash the **pointer address `Node*`**, never `node->data`!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
754	Index Parity Elimination: Finding the Single Element in a Sorted Array	dsa-single-element-in-sorted-array	Find the single unique non-duplicate element in a sorted array in O(log N) time and O(1) space using the (even, odd) vs (odd, even) index parity invariant.	2026-10-02 23:53:37.084708+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Index Parity Elimination: Finding the Single Element in a Sorted Array\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AZOmHuHadxQ", "title": "Lecture 52: Search Single Element in a sorted array", "duration": "28m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/single-number/", "title": "Single Number", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Compare O(N) XOR cancellation on unsorted arrays against O(log N) binary search on sorted arrays."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/single-number-ii/", "title": "Single Number II", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the single element in an array where every other element appears three times."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/element-appearing-once2552/1", "title": "Element appearing once", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the element that appears once in a sorted array where all other elements appear twice."}]	Module 12: 1D Binary Search, Bounds & Rotated Array Invariants	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a **sorted** array where every element appears **twice** except for one element that appears **once** (e.g., `[1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 5, 5, 6, 6]`), find that single element in **O(log2 N) time** and **O(1) space**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The (even, odd) vs. (odd, even) Index Parity Trick:**\n  - Before the single element (`4`), every duplicate pair starts at an **even** index and ends at an **odd** index: `(even, odd)`.\n  - After the single element (`4`), the single element shifts every subsequent pair by 1, so every duplicate pair starts at an **odd** index and ends at an **even** index: `(odd, even)`!\n- **Trimming Boundary Edge Cases First:**\n  - To avoid messy out-of-bounds checks on `mid - 1` and `mid + 1`, handle `n == 1`, index `0` (`arr[0] != arr[1]`), and index `n - 1` (`arr[n - 1] != arr[n - 2]`) upfront, then run Binary Search from **`low = 1` to `high = n - 2`**!\n- **Elimination Rules at `mid`:**\n  - **Found Single Element:** If `arr[mid] != arr[mid - 1] && arr[mid] != arr[mid + 1]`, return `arr[mid]`.\n  - **Standing in the Left Half (even, odd):** If `(mid % 2 == 1 && arr[mid] == arr[mid - 1])` OR `(mid % 2 == 0 && arr[mid] == arr[mid + 1])`, the single element is to our right—eliminate the left half (`low = mid + 1`).\n  - **Standing in the Right Half (odd, even):** Otherwise, the single element is to our left—eliminate the right half (`high = mid - 1`).\n\n### 💻 Single Element in Sorted Array Blueprint\n```cpp\nint singleNonDuplicate(vector<int> &arr) {\n    int n = arr.size();\n    if (n == 1) return arr[0];\n    if (arr[0] != arr[1]) return arr[0];\n    if (arr[n - 1] != arr[n - 2]) return arr[n - 1];\n\n    int low = 1, high = n - 2;\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (arr[mid] != arr[mid + 1] && arr[mid] != arr[mid - 1]) {\n            return arr[mid];\n        }\n        // We are in the left half (even, odd) -> single element is on the right\n        if ((mid % 2 == 1 && arr[mid] == arr[mid - 1]) ||\n            (mid % 2 == 0 && arr[mid] == arr[mid + 1])) {\n            low = mid + 1;\n        } else {\n            high = mid - 1; // We are in the right half -> go left\n        }\n    }\n    return -1;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
14166	Min-Max Balance Range Tracking: Valid Parenthesis String With Wildcard ('*')	dsa-valid-parenthesis-string-greedy-range-tracking	Master Min-Max Balance Range Tracking: Valid Parenthesis String With Wildcard ('*') with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:24:23.648037+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Min-Max Balance Range Tracking: Valid Parenthesis String With Wildcard ('*')\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cHT6sG_hUZI", "title": "Min-Max Balance Range Tracking: Valid Parenthesis String With Wildcard ('*') - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/valid-parenthesis-string/", "title": "LeetCode 678: Valid Parenthesis String", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given a string s containing only three types of characters: '(', ')' and '*', return true if s is valid."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/check-if-a-parentheses-string-can-be-valid/", "title": "LeetCode 2116: Check if a Parentheses String Can Be Valid", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Determine if a parentheses string with unlocked wildcard positions can be made valid in O(N) time and O(1) space."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-remove-to-make-valid-parentheses/", "title": "LeetCode 1249: Minimum Remove to Make Valid Parentheses", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Remove the minimum number of '(' or ')' so that the resulting parentheses string is valid."}]	Module 41: Greedy Algorithms: Sorting Heuristics, Reachability Horizons & Interval Scheduling	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a string `s` containing `'('`, `')'`, and `'*'`, where `'*'` can be treated as a single `'('`, a single `')'`, or an **empty string `""`**, return `true` if `s` can be made a valid parenthesis string.\n\n### 💡 Progression (Recursion O(3^N) → DP O(N²) → Optimal Min-Max Range Greedy O(N))\n- **1. Why Standard `cnt` Becomes a Range `[minOpen, maxOpen]`:**\n  - Without `'*'`, we maintain a single integer `cnt` (`+1` for `'('`, `-1` for `')'`, invalid if `cnt < 0`, valid if `cnt == 0` at the end).\n  - With `'*'`, each `'*'` can change `cnt` by `-1`, `0`, or `+1`, creating a **contiguous range of achievable open-bracket counts `[minOpen, maxOpen]`**!\n- **2. The `O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Space Range-Tracking Rules:**\n  - Initialize **`minOpen = 0`** and **`maxOpen = 0`**.\n  - Loop through each character `s[i]`:\n    - **If `s[i] == '('`:** Both bounds increase: `minOpen++, maxOpen++`.\n    - **Else if `s[i] == ')'`:** Both bounds decrease: `minOpen--, maxOpen--`.\n    - **Else (`s[i] == '*'`):**\n      - `minOpen` treats `'*'` as `')'`: **`minOpen = minOpen - 1`**.\n      - `maxOpen` treats `'*'` as `'('`: **`maxOpen = maxOpen + 1`**.\n    - **Clamp `minOpen` at `0` (`if (minOpen < 0) minOpen = 0;`):**\n      - We never allow negative open counts in valid branches—so if `minOpen` drops to `-1`, clamp it back to `0`!\n    - **Fatal Deficit Check (`if (maxOpen < 0) return false;`):**\n      - If even our **maximum possible** open count drops below `0` (e.g., string starts with `")"`), no wildcard assignment can save it—immediately return `false`!\n  - At the end of the string, return **`minOpen == 0`** (meaning `0` lies inside `[minOpen, maxOpen]`)!\n\n### 💻 Valid Parenthesis String (O(N) Time, O(1) Space) Blueprint\n```cpp\nbool checkValidString(string s) {\n    int minOpen = 0, maxOpen = 0;\n\n    for (char c : s) {\n        if (c == '(') {\n            minOpen++;\n            maxOpen++;\n        } else if (c == ')') {\n            minOpen--;\n            maxOpen--;\n        } else { // c == '*'\n            minOpen--; // If '*' acts as ')'\n            maxOpen++; // If '*' acts as '('\n        }\n\n        // Discard invalid negative branches from the lower bound\n        if (minOpen < 0) minOpen = 0;\n\n        // If even the upper bound is negative, too many ')' exist -> impossible!\n        if (maxOpen < 0) return false;\n    }\n\n    return (minOpen == 0);\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Checking `if (minOpen < 0)` After `if (maxOpen < 0)` Without Understanding the Difference:** When `s = ")"`, both `minOpen` and `maxOpen` become `-1`. Clamping `minOpen = 0` is only valid when `maxOpen >= 0` (meaning non-negative branches still exist in the range). Checking `if (maxOpen < 0) return false;` catches the unrecoverable `")"` case immediately!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
