6397	Subsequence Target Existence: Subset Sum Equals to Target	dsa-subset-sum-equals-to-target-dp-on-subsequences	Determine whether a subset sums to target K using pick/not-pick transitions on state (ind, target) with O(K) space optimization.	2026-10-03 11:28:16.955789+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Subsequence Target Existence: Subset Sum Equals to Target\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fWX9xDmIzRI", "title": "Lecture 195: Subset Sum Equals to Target (DP 14)", "duration": "24m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/subset-sum-problem-1611555638/1", "title": "Subset Sum Problem", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given an array of non-negative integers and a value sum, check if there is a subset of the given set with sum equal to given sum."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/partition-equal-subset-sum/", "title": "Partition Equal Subset Sum (LC 416)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Reduce equal-sum array partitioning directly to Subset Sum Equals Target (target = totalSum / 2)."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimize-the-difference-between-target-and-chosen-elements/", "title": "Minimize the Difference Between Target and Chosen Elements (LC 1981)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Track reachable subset sums across rows to minimize absolute difference from target."}]	Module 30: Dynamic Programming on Subsequences, Subset Sums & 0/1 Knapsack	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array `arr` of `N` positive integers and a target integer `K`, determine whether there exists a **subset (subsequence)** of `arr` whose elements sum to **exactly `K`**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **2D State `(ind, target)`:**\n  - `f(ind, target)` answers: *"In the subarray `0 .. ind`, does there exist a subsequence that sums to `target`?"*\n  - **Base Case 1 (`target == 0`):** Return `true` (we can always form sum `0` by picking nothing!).\n  - **Base Case 2 (`ind == 0`):** Return `(arr[0] == target)` (at index `0`, we can form `target` if and only if `arr[0]` equals `target`).\n  - **Transitions:**\n    - `notTake = f(ind - 1, target)`\n    - `take = false; if (arr[ind] <= target) take = f(ind - 1, target - arr[ind]);`\n    - Return **`take || notTake`**.\n- **Tabulation Base-Case Guard (`if (arr[0] <= k) dp[0][arr[0]] = true;`):**\n  - When initializing row `0` in bottom-up tabulation, only mark `dp[0][arr[0]] = true` if **`arr[0] <= k`** (otherwise `arr[0]` goes out of column bounds!).\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * K)` Time and `O(K)` Space (using `prev` and `cur` boolean vectors of size `K + 1`).\n\n### 💻 Subset Sum Equals to Target (Space Optimized) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nbool subsetSumToK(int n, int k, vector<int> &arr) {\n    vector<bool> prev(k + 1, false), cur(k + 1, false);\n\n    // Base Case 1: target == 0 is always achievable\n    prev[0] = cur[0] = true;\n\n    // Base Case 2: index 0 can form sum arr[0] (if within target bound k)\n    if (arr[0] <= k) prev[arr[0]] = true;\n\n    for (int ind = 1; ind < n; ind++) {\n        for (int target = 1; target <= k; target++) {\n            bool notTake = prev[target];\n            bool take = false;\n            if (arr[ind] <= target) {\n                take = prev[target - arr[ind]];\n            }\n            cur[target] = take || notTake;\n        }\n        prev = cur;\n    }\n    return prev[k];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Writing `prev[arr[0]] = true;` Without Checking `if (arr[0] <= k)`:** If `arr[0] = 100` and `k = 10`, indexing `prev[100]` causes an immediate out-of-bounds memory crash!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
