10787	Converging Head-Tail Two Pointers: Find All Pairs With Given Sum in a Sorted Doubly Linked List	dsa-find-all-pairs-with-given-sum-in-sorted-dll	Find all pairs adding to target in a sorted doubly linked list using converging left and right pointers in O(N) time and O(1) space.	2026-10-03 15:07:44.954981+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Converging Head-Tail Two Pointers: Find All Pairs With Given Sum in a Sorted Doubly Linked List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YitR4dQsddE", "title": "Lecture 255: Find Pairs with Given Sum in Doubly Linked List", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/find-pairs-with-given-sum-in-doubly-linked-list/1", "title": "Find pairs with given sum in doubly linked list", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Given a sorted doubly linked list of positive distinct elements, find all pairs of nodes whose sum equals target."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/two-sum-ii-input-array-is-sorted/", "title": "Two Sum II - Input Array Is Sorted (LC 167)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Apply the exact same converging left/right two-pointer pattern on a sorted 1D array."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/two-sum-iv-input-is-a-bst/", "title": "Two Sum IV - Input is a BST (LC 653)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Extend sorted two-pointer pair finding to a Binary Search Tree."}]	Module 38: Doubly Linked List Medium Problems & Hard Linked List Re-Wiring (K-Group Reversal, Rotation & Multi-Level Flattening)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the `head` of a **sorted Doubly Linked List** of distinct positive integers and a target integer `sum`, find and return **all pairs `{a, b}`** whose values add up to `sum` in **`O(N)` time and `O(1)` auxiliary space**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Optimal)\n- **1. Brute Force Nested Traversal (`O(N²)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - For each node `temp1`, scan `temp2` from `temp1->next` onward while `temp1->data + temp2->data <= sum`.\n- **2. Optimal Converging Two Pointers (`O(2N)` Time, `O(1)` Auxiliary Space):**\n  - Because the DLL is **sorted** and has **`prev` pointers**, we can use the exact same two-pointer technique as sorted arrays!\n  - Set `left = head` and walk `right` to the **tail node** (`O(N)`).\n  - Loop **`while (left->data < right->data)`**:\n    - **If `left->data + right->data == sum`:**\n      - Add `{left->data, right->data}` to `ans`.\n      - Advance **both** inward: `left = left->next;` and `right = right->prev;`.\n    - **Else if `left->data + right->data < sum`:**\n      - We need a larger sum → move `left = left->next;`.\n    - **Else (`> sum`):**\n      - We need a smaller sum → move `right = right->prev;`.\n\n### 💻 Find All Pairs With Given Sum in Sorted DLL Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nNode* findTail(Node* head) {\n    Node* tail = head;\n    while (tail->next != nullptr) {\n        tail = tail->next;\n    }\n    return tail;\n}\n\nvector<pair<int, int>> findPairsWithGivenSum(Node* head, int k) {\n    vector<pair<int, int>> ans;\n    if (head == nullptr) return ans;\n\n    Node* left = head;\n    Node* right = findTail(head); // O(N) to locate tail\n\n    // O(N) converging two-pointer scan\n    while (left->data < right->data) {\n        int curSum = left->data + right->data;\n        if (curSum == k) {\n            ans.push_back({left->data, right->data});\n            left = left->next;\n            right = right->prev;\n        } else if (curSum < k) {\n            left = left->next;\n        } else {\n            right = right->prev;\n        }\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Using `while (left != right)` Instead of `while (left->data < right->data)`:** When `left` and `right` are adjacent and both move inward (`left = left->next; right = right->prev;`), they **cross over each other** without ever landing on the exact same node! Checking `left->data < right->data` (or `left != right && left->prev != right`) stops immediately upon crossover!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22207	System Design Crash Course - Part 2	hld-system-design-crash-course-part-2	Deep dive into traffic patterns, Serverless architecture trade-offs, containerization with Docker, and orchestration using Kubernetes.	2026-10-07 12:57:03.07802+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: System Design Crash Course - Part 2\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/YuB3OuF3MUE", "title": "System Design Crash Course - Part 2", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "hld-v2-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Traffic Patterns and Auto-Scaling Failure", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nStandard auto-scaling relies on reactive metrics: e.g., \\"If average CPU utilization exceeds 70% for 5 minutes, spin up 5 new servers.\\"\\n\\nDuring a highly anticipated live event (like a cricket finale or an unpredictable viral moment on YouTube), traffic does not grow linearly—it spikes instantly in massive vertical increments (a \\"Thundering Herd\\"). By the time the load balancer detects the 70% CPU threshold, decides to scale, provisions the new virtual machines, runs startup scripts, and registers them to the load balancer, 3 to 5 minutes have passed. By then, the initial servers have already hit 100% CPU, run out of memory, and crashed, causing a cascading failure across the system.\\n\\n**Pre-Warming (Pre-scaling):** Because live events are scheduled, engineers analyze historical data and manually spin up (pre-warm) hundreds of extra servers hours before the event begins. This guarantees the compute capacity is already active and listening when the instantaneous spike hits, ensuring zero dropped requests.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why might a standard CPU-based auto-scaling policy fail a platform like Hotstar during the live stream of a cricket match finale? How does \\"Pre-Warming\\" resolve this?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v2-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: The \\"Back Button\\" Cascade Effect", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThis is a classic architectural oversight regarding microservice blast radiuses.\\n\\nWhen scaling for a live event, companies typically dedicate massive resources to the `Live_Streaming_Service` and its edge networks. However, when the match ends or becomes boring, millions of users press the \\"Back\\" button or \\"Home\\" button on their apps almost simultaneously.\\n\\nNavigating away from the live player triggers network requests to the `Homepage_API` or `VOD_Catalog_Service` to load the list of available movies and shows. If the engineers forgot to pre-scale the database read-replicas and servers for the Homepage API, this sudden bombardment of 24 million queries will instantly overwhelm and crash the general catalog service.\\n\\n*Lesson:* In microservice architectures, an action in one isolated domain heavily impacts neighboring domains. Traffic exits from a live event must be modeled as aggressive traffic spikes on the landing pages.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "A live streaming platform successfully provisions enough servers to handle 24 million concurrent users watching a live match. However, the system crashes anyway. Architecturally, how can the users pressing the \\"Back\\" button to leave the stream cause a completely different part of the system to fail?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v2-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Serverless \\"Cold Starts\\"", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Cold Start:** In a serverless model, the cloud provider provisions a container and loads your code only when a request arrives. If the function hasn't been called recently, no active container exists. When a new request triggers the function, the provider must allocate a container, inject the runtime environment (Node, Java, Python), and execute startup scripts before running the code. This provisioning delay (often 1–3 seconds) is the \\"Cold Start\\".\\n\\n**Detrimental Scenario:** Cold starts are disastrous for unpredictable, spiky traffic with long idle periods. If a marketing site goes viral after hours of zero traffic, thousands of users will experience multi-second latency as the provider spins up thousands of fresh containers simultaneously.\\n\\n**Non-Issue Scenario:** Cold starts are negligible in environments with steady, continuous traffic. If a service receives a constant 50 requests per second, the cloud provider keeps those containers \\"warm\\" and reuses them. In this state, response times remain in the low milliseconds.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "What is a \\"Cold Start\\" in Serverless Architecture (e.g., AWS Lambda)? In what specific traffic pattern is a cold start highly detrimental, and when is it a non-issue?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v2-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Serverless Database Connection Exhaustion", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Failure:** Serverless functions are highly horizontally scalable and strictly stateless. At 10,000 requests per second, AWS will spin up 10,000 independent Lambda execution environments. Because they are stateless, *every single Lambda* will attempt to open a brand new, independent TCP connection to the PostgreSQL database.\\n\\nTraditional relational databases are not designed to handle tens of thousands of concurrent active connections. The PostgreSQL connection pool will immediately exhaust its limits, causing it to reject new connections and crash, bringing the entire system down.\\n\\n**The Fix (Database Proxy):** To fix this, you must introduce a Connection Pooling Proxy (like AWS RDS Proxy or PgBouncer) between the Lambdas and the Database.\\nThe Lambdas open connections to the Proxy. The Proxy holds a fixed, manageable pool of active connections to the Database (e.g., 200 connections) and multiplexes the thousands of Lambda requests through this small pool, protecting the database from connection exhaustion.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "You build an application entirely out of Serverless functions (AWS Lambdas) backed by a traditional relational database (PostgreSQL). When traffic spikes to 10,000 requests per second, the Lambdas scale perfectly, but the system completely goes offline. What caused the failure and how do you fix it?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v2-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Container Orchestration & Zero-Downtime Deployments", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn traditional VM environments, updating an application often required taking servers offline, upgrading the code, and rebooting, causing downtime or reduced capacity.\\n\\nKubernetes abstracts the infrastructure. You specify a desired state (e.g., \\"I want 10 replicas of Version 1 running\\"). When a new feature is merged, you update the configuration to \\"Version 2\\".\\n\\n**Rolling Update Strategy:**\\n1. Kubernetes does not kill all V1 containers at once. Instead, it spins up a new pod running **Version 2**.\\n2. Once the V2 pod passes its \\"Readiness Probe\\" (proving it is fully booted and connected to the DB), Kubernetes routes traffic to it.\\n3. Kubernetes then cleanly terminates one of the old **Version 1** pods.\\n4. It repeats this process one by one—spinning up a V2, verifying it, and killing a V1.\\n\\nThroughout the entire rollout, the system guarantees that 10 healthy pods are always actively serving user traffic, resulting in absolutely zero downtime for the end user.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Explain how Container Orchestrators like Kubernetes facilitate \\"Zero-Downtime Deployments\\" using a Rolling Update strategy.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	High Level Design	### 📌 1. Traffic Patterns & Predictive Scaling\n* **Predictable Spikes (e.g., Netflix):** Traffic spikes are linked to scheduled movie releases. Engineers can "pre-warm" (pre-scale) servers 24 hours in advance to handle the anticipated load.\n* **Unpredictable Spikes (e.g., YouTube):** Viral events happen randomly. Auto-scaling rules based on CPU/RAM thresholds often react too slowly, requiring massive baseline buffer capacities.\n* **Live Streaming (e.g., Hotstar):** Subject to the **"Thundering Herd"** problem during matches. Crucially, auxiliary services (like the "Movies/Home" API) must also be heavily scaled, because users dropping out of a live stream will instantly bombard the homepage API.\n\n### 💡 2. Serverless Architecture Trade-offs\n* **What is Serverless?** (e.g., AWS Lambda). Developers deploy code functions directly; the cloud provider automatically provisions underlying infrastructure per request.\n* **Pros:** Ultimate horizontal scaling (one container per request), zero server maintenance, and cost-efficient pay-per-invocation billing.\n* **Cons:**\n  * **Cold Starts:** If a function hasn't been invoked recently, the cloud provider takes time to spin up the container and load the runtime, causing severe latency for the first request.\n  * **Vendor Lock-in:** Code relies heavily on provider-specific ecosystems (API Gateway, CloudWatch, SQS).\n  * **State & Connection Exhaustion:** Lambdas are stateless. A massive spike invoking 10,000 Lambdas simultaneously will open 10,000 separate connections to a relational database, immediately crashing the DB.\n\n### 💡 3. Virtualization vs. Containerization\n* **Virtual Machines (VMs):** Require a hypervisor running a full Guest Operating System. They are heavy, slow to boot, and consume vast baseline RAM/CPU.\n* **Containers (Docker):** Lightweight, isolated environments that share the Host OS kernel. They package application code alongside its exact dependencies, ensuring the "It works on my machine" guarantee across all environments.\n\n### 💡 4. Container Orchestration (Kubernetes)\n* Originating from Google's internal tool "Borg," Kubernetes automates container management across a cluster of servers.\n* Provides auto-scaling, self-healing (restarting crashed containers), and handles **Zero-Downtime Deployments** (via Rolling Updates or Blue-Green deployment strategies).	Piyush Garg	https://www.youtube.com/@piyushgarg1982	28	System Design Crash Course - Part 2	Deep dive into traffic patterns, Serverless architecture trade-offs, containerization with Docker, and orchestration using Kubernetes.	50	Hard	High Level Design	2	[{"id": "hld-v2-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Traffic Patterns and Auto-Scaling Failure", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nStandard auto-scaling relies on reactive metrics: e.g., \\"If average CPU utilization exceeds 70% for 5 minutes, spin up 5 new servers.\\"\\n\\nDuring a highly anticipated live event (like a cricket finale or an unpredictable viral moment on YouTube), traffic does not grow linearly—it spikes instantly in massive vertical increments (a \\"Thundering Herd\\"). By the time the load balancer detects the 70% CPU threshold, decides to scale, provisions the new virtual machines, runs startup scripts, and registers them to the load balancer, 3 to 5 minutes have passed. By then, the initial servers have already hit 100% CPU, run out of memory, and crashed, causing a cascading failure across the system.\\n\\n**Pre-Warming (Pre-scaling):** Because live events are scheduled, engineers analyze historical data and manually spin up (pre-warm) hundreds of extra servers hours before the event begins. This guarantees the compute capacity is already active and listening when the instantaneous spike hits, ensuring zero dropped requests.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why might a standard CPU-based auto-scaling policy fail a platform like Hotstar during the live stream of a cricket match finale? How does \\"Pre-Warming\\" resolve this?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v2-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: The \\"Back Button\\" Cascade Effect", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThis is a classic architectural oversight regarding microservice blast radiuses.\\n\\nWhen scaling for a live event, companies typically dedicate massive resources to the `Live_Streaming_Service` and its edge networks. However, when the match ends or becomes boring, millions of users press the \\"Back\\" button or \\"Home\\" button on their apps almost simultaneously.\\n\\nNavigating away from the live player triggers network requests to the `Homepage_API` or `VOD_Catalog_Service` to load the list of available movies and shows. If the engineers forgot to pre-scale the database read-replicas and servers for the Homepage API, this sudden bombardment of 24 million queries will instantly overwhelm and crash the general catalog service.\\n\\n*Lesson:* In microservice architectures, an action in one isolated domain heavily impacts neighboring domains. Traffic exits from a live event must be modeled as aggressive traffic spikes on the landing pages.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "A live streaming platform successfully provisions enough servers to handle 24 million concurrent users watching a live match. However, the system crashes anyway. Architecturally, how can the users pressing the \\"Back\\" button to leave the stream cause a completely different part of the system to fail?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v2-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Serverless \\"Cold Starts\\"", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Cold Start:** In a serverless model, the cloud provider provisions a container and loads your code only when a request arrives. If the function hasn't been called recently, no active container exists. When a new request triggers the function, the provider must allocate a container, inject the runtime environment (Node, Java, Python), and execute startup scripts before running the code. This provisioning delay (often 1–3 seconds) is the \\"Cold Start\\".\\n\\n**Detrimental Scenario:** Cold starts are disastrous for unpredictable, spiky traffic with long idle periods. If a marketing site goes viral after hours of zero traffic, thousands of users will experience multi-second latency as the provider spins up thousands of fresh containers simultaneously.\\n\\n**Non-Issue Scenario:** Cold starts are negligible in environments with steady, continuous traffic. If a service receives a constant 50 requests per second, the cloud provider keeps those containers \\"warm\\" and reuses them. In this state, response times remain in the low milliseconds.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "What is a \\"Cold Start\\" in Serverless Architecture (e.g., AWS Lambda)? In what specific traffic pattern is a cold start highly detrimental, and when is it a non-issue?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v2-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Serverless Database Connection Exhaustion", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Failure:** Serverless functions are highly horizontally scalable and strictly stateless. At 10,000 requests per second, AWS will spin up 10,000 independent Lambda execution environments. Because they are stateless, *every single Lambda* will attempt to open a brand new, independent TCP connection to the PostgreSQL database.\\n\\nTraditional relational databases are not designed to handle tens of thousands of concurrent active connections. The PostgreSQL connection pool will immediately exhaust its limits, causing it to reject new connections and crash, bringing the entire system down.\\n\\n**The Fix (Database Proxy):** To fix this, you must introduce a Connection Pooling Proxy (like AWS RDS Proxy or PgBouncer) between the Lambdas and the Database.\\nThe Lambdas open connections to the Proxy. The Proxy holds a fixed, manageable pool of active connections to the Database (e.g., 200 connections) and multiplexes the thousands of Lambda requests through this small pool, protecting the database from connection exhaustion.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "You build an application entirely out of Serverless functions (AWS Lambdas) backed by a traditional relational database (PostgreSQL). When traffic spikes to 10,000 requests per second, the Lambdas scale perfectly, but the system completely goes offline. What caused the failure and how do you fix it?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v2-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Container Orchestration & Zero-Downtime Deployments", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn traditional VM environments, updating an application often required taking servers offline, upgrading the code, and rebooting, causing downtime or reduced capacity.\\n\\nKubernetes abstracts the infrastructure. You specify a desired state (e.g., \\"I want 10 replicas of Version 1 running\\"). When a new feature is merged, you update the configuration to \\"Version 2\\".\\n\\n**Rolling Update Strategy:**\\n1. Kubernetes does not kill all V1 containers at once. Instead, it spins up a new pod running **Version 2**.\\n2. Once the V2 pod passes its \\"Readiness Probe\\" (proving it is fully booted and connected to the DB), Kubernetes routes traffic to it.\\n3. Kubernetes then cleanly terminates one of the old **Version 1** pods.\\n4. It repeats this process one by one—spinning up a V2, verifying it, and killing a V1.\\n\\nThroughout the entire rollout, the system guarantees that 10 healthy pods are always actively serving user traffic, resulting in absolutely zero downtime for the end user.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Explain how Container Orchestrators like Kubernetes facilitate \\"Zero-Downtime Deployments\\" using a Rolling Update strategy.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
15095	Pairwise Two-Pointer Elimination: The Celebrity Problem	dsa-the-celebrity-problem-two-pointer-elimination	Master Pairwise Two-Pointer Elimination: The Celebrity Problem with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:30:52.975117+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Pairwise Two-Pointer Elimination: The Celebrity Problem\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cEadsbTeze4", "title": "Pairwise Two-Pointer Elimination: The Celebrity Problem - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/the-celebrity-problem/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: The Celebrity Problem", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given a square N x N matrix M representing whether person i knows person j, find the index of the celebrity (or -1 if none exists)."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-the-celebrity/", "title": "LeetCode 277: Find the Celebrity", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the celebrity among n people using the minimum number of knows(a, b) queries."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-the-town-judge/", "title": "LeetCode 997: Find the Town Judge", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Apply the indegree == N - 1 and outdegree == 0 celebrity definition to directed trust pairs."}]	Module 44: Advanced Stack, Monotonic Deque & Cache Design: Stock Span, Sliding Window Max, Celebrity & LRU/LFU Caches	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn a party of `N` people (`0` to `N - 1`), a **Celebrity** is someone who is **known by everyone else** (`N - 1` people) but **knows NO ONE** (`0` people). Given an `N x N` binary matrix `mat` where `mat[i][j] == 1` means person `i` knows person `j`, return the celebrity's index (or `-1` if no celebrity exists).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute O(N²) → Optimal Two-Pointer Elimination O(2N))\n- **Why There Can Be AT MOST 1 Celebrity:**\n  - If X is a celebrity, everyone knows X and X knows nobody—so no other person Y can also know nobody while knowing X!\n- **1. Brute Force (`knowMe[N]` and `iKnow[N]` Arrays — `O(N²)` Time, `O(2N)` Space):**\n  - Scan the entire `N x N` matrix and check for a person `i` with `knowMe[i] == N - 1 && iKnow[i] == 0`.\n- **2. Optimal Pairwise Two-Pointer Elimination (`O(2N)` Time, `O(1)` Space!):**\n  - Place `top = 0` and `down = N - 1`.\n  - Loop **`while (top < down)`**:\n    - **If `mat[top][down] == 1` (`top` knows `down`):** Since `top` knows someone, **`top` CANNOT be a celebrity** → **`top++`**!\n    - **Else if `mat[down][top] == 1` (`down` knows `top`):** Since `down` knows someone, **`down` CANNOT be a celebrity** → **`down--`**!\n    - **Else (neither knows the other):** Since a celebrity must be known by everyone, **NEITHER `top` nor `down` can be a celebrity** → **`top++, down--`**!\n  - **Final Verification Pass (`O(N)`):**\n    - If `top > down`, return `-1`.\n    - When `top == down`, verify candidate `top` by scanning row `top` and column `top` (`i` from `0` to `N - 1`, skipping diagonal `i == top`): every `mat[top][i]` must be `0` AND every `mat[i][top]` must be `1`!\n\n### 💻 The Celebrity Problem (Optimal O(2N) Time, O(1) Space) Blueprint\n```cpp\nint celebrity(vector<vector<int>> &mat) {\n    int n = mat.size();\n    int top = 0, down = n - 1;\n\n    // Step 1: Eliminate non-celebrities pairwise in O(N) time\n    while (top < down) {\n        if (mat[top][down] == 1) {\n            top++;  // 'top' knows 'down', so 'top' cannot be a celebrity\n        } else if (mat[down][top] == 1) {\n            down--; // 'down' knows 'top', so 'down' cannot be a celebrity\n        } else {\n            // Neither knows the other -> neither is known by everyone!\n            top++;\n            down--;\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (top > down) return -1;\n\n    // Step 2: Verify the single surviving candidate 'top' in O(N) time\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        if (i == top) continue; // Skip diagonal!\n        if (mat[top][i] == 0 && mat[i][top] == 1) {\n            continue;\n        } else {\n            return -1;\n        }\n    }\n    return top;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Returning `top` Without the Step 2 Verification Loop:** The `while (top < down)` loop only proves that *everyone except `top`* has been eliminated—it does not guarantee that `top` is actually a celebrity! Always verify `top`'s full row and column in `O(N)` before returning `top`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
10792	Recursive Backtracking Vertical Merge: Flattening a Linked List (Next & Child Pointers)	dsa-flattening-a-linked-list-vertical-merge	Flatten a 2D linked list with next and child pointers into a single sorted vertical chain using recursive horizontal traversal and two-pointer vertical merging.	2026-10-03 15:07:44.969029+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Recursive Backtracking Vertical Merge: Flattening a Linked List (Next & Child Pointers)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ykelywHJWLg", "title": "Lecture 260: Flattening a Linked List", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/flattening-a-linked-list/1", "title": "Flattening a Linked List", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given a linked list where every node represents a sorted vertical sub-linked-list via bottom/child pointers, flatten the list into a single sorted vertical list."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/flatten-a-multilevel-doubly-linked-list/", "title": "Flatten a Multilevel Doubly Linked List (LC 430)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Flatten a multilevel doubly linked list with next, prev, and child pointers into a single-level list."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-k-sorted-lists/", "title": "Merge k Sorted Lists (LC 23)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Compare recursive pairwise list merging with min-heap k-way merging."}]	Module 38: Doubly Linked List Medium Problems & Hard Linked List Re-Wiring (K-Group Reversal, Rotation & Multi-Level Flattening)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nYou are given a 2D linked list where each top-level node has **two pointers**:\n1. **`next`**: Points horizontally to the next top-level head node (`N` horizontal heads total).\n2. **`child` (or `bottom`)**: Points vertically downward to a **sorted vertical sub-linked-list** of average length `M`.\nYour task is to **flatten** the entire structure into **one single sorted vertical linked list** connected via **`child` pointers** (with every node's `next` pointer set to `nullptr`) in-place!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Step 1: Adapt "Merge Two Sorted Lists" to Use `child` Pointers (`mergeTwoVerticalLists(list1, list2)`):**\n  - Instead of linking `res->next`, link **`res->child = list1` (or `list2`)**, advance via **`list1 = list1->child`**, and explicitly set **`res->next = nullptr`** on every merged node so horizontal links are cleared!\n- **Step 2: Recursive Backtracking Across Horizontal Heads (`flattenLinkedList(head)`):**\n  - **Base Case:** `if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr) return head;` (a single vertical list is already sorted and flattened!).\n  - **Recursive Call:** Flatten everything to the right first:\n    **`Node* mergedHead = flattenLinkedList(head->next);`**\n  - **Backtracking Merge:** Merge the current vertical list `head` with `mergedHead`:\n    **`return mergeTwoVerticalLists(head, mergedHead);`**!\n- **Complexity:** **`O(N * 2M) = O(2 * N * M)`** Time and **`O(N)`** Recursion Stack Space (`O(1)` auxiliary heap space since all nodes are reused in-place!).\n\n### 💻 Flatten a Multi-Level Sorted Linked List Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nNode* mergeTwoVerticalLists(Node* list1, Node* list2) {\n    Node* dummyNode = new Node(-1);\n    Node* res = dummyNode;\n\n    while (list1 != nullptr && list2 != nullptr) {\n        if (list1->data < list2->data) {\n            res->child = list1;\n            res = list1;\n            list1 = list1->child;\n        } else {\n            res->child = list2;\n            res = list2;\n            list2 = list2->child;\n        }\n        res->next = nullptr; // Clear horizontal next pointer!\n    }\n\n    if (list1 != nullptr) res->child = list1;\n    else res->child = list2;\n\n    if (dummyNode->child != nullptr) {\n        dummyNode->child->next = nullptr;\n    }\n\n    Node* newHead = dummyNode->child;\n    delete dummyNode;\n    return newHead;\n}\n\nNode* flattenLinkedList(Node* head) {\n    // Base case: 0 or 1 horizontal list is already flattened\n    if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr) {\n        return head;\n    }\n\n    // Recursively flatten the right side, then merge with current vertical list\n    Node* mergedHead = flattenLinkedList(head->next);\n    head = mergeTwoVerticalLists(head, mergedHead);\n    return head;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Advancing via `list1->next` or Returning `dummyNode->next`:** In the flattened list, all nodes must be chained vertically via **`child` (or `bottom`)** pointers, while every `next` pointer must be `nullptr`!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
7015	Infinite-Supply Combinatorial Counting: Coin Change II (Ways to Make Coin Change)	dsa-coin-change-2-infinite-supply-ways	Count total combinations to make change for an amount with infinite coin supplies using single-array left-to-right tabulation.	2026-10-03 11:31:13.734428+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Infinite-Supply Combinatorial Counting: Coin Change II (Ways to Make Coin Change)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HgyouUi11zk", "title": "Lecture 203: Coin Change II (Ways to Make Coin Change - DP 22)", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/coin-change-ii/", "title": "Coin Change II (LC 518)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the number of combinations that make up amount from an infinite supply of given coin denominations."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/coin-change2448/1", "title": "Coin Change (Count Ways)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the number of ways to make change for sum N using an infinite supply of coins."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combination-sum-iv/", "title": "Combination Sum IV (LC 377)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Contrast unordered combinations (outer loop over coins) with ordered permutations (outer loop over target)."}]	Module 30: Dynamic Programming on Subsequences, Subset Sums & 0/1 Knapsack	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array `coins` of `N` distinct denominations (each available in **infinite supply**) and a total `amount`, count the **total number of distinct combinations** of coins that sum up to `amount`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Difference Between Coin Change I (DP 20) and Coin Change II (DP 22):**\n  1. **Base Case (`ind == 0`):**\n     - In Coin Change I (min coins), if `T % coins[0] == 0`, we returned `T / coins[0]`.\n     - In **Coin Change II (count ways)**, if `T % coins[0] == 0`, using coin `0` repeatedly forms **1 single valid combination**—so we return **`1`** (and `0` otherwise)!\n  2. **Transition:**\n     - `notTake = f(ind - 1, T)`\n     - `take = 0; if (coins[ind] <= T) take = f(ind, T - coins[ind]);` (no `+1` added, just the count of ways!)\n     - Return **`take + notTake`**.\n- **Single-Array Left-to-Right Space Optimization (`O(amount)` Space):**\n  - Because `cur[T]` needs `prev[T]` (`notTake`) and **`cur[T - coins[ind]]`** (`take` from the **same row**), iterating `T` **from Left to Right (`0` to `amount`)** on a single 1D array `dp` updates `dp[T]` in-place!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * amount)` Time and `O(amount)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Coin Change II (1D Space Optimized) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nlong countWaysToMakeChange(vector<int> &coins, int n, int value) {\n    vector<long> prev(value + 1, 0), cur(value + 1, 0);\n\n    // 1. Base case at index 0: 1 way if divisible by coins[0], else 0\n    for (int T = 0; T <= value; T++) {\n        prev[T] = (T % coins[0] == 0) ? 1 : 0;\n    }\n\n    // 2. Iterate over remaining coins 1 to n - 1\n    for (int ind = 1; ind < n; ind++) {\n        for (int T = 0; T <= value; T++) {\n            long notTake = prev[T];\n            long take = 0;\n            if (coins[ind] <= T) {\n                take = cur[T - coins[ind]]; // Stay at ind -> read from 'cur'!\n            }\n            cur[T] = take + notTake;\n        }\n        prev = cur;\n    }\n    return prev[value];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Returning `T / coins[0]` in the Base Case:** In Coin Change II, we are counting **ways**, not the number of coins! Even if it takes five `2`-rupee coins to make `10`, that is still **1 way** (`return 1`), not `5` ways!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
