22208	Event Sourcing	hld-event-sourcing	Understand how Event Sourcing replaces traditional CRUD by treating append-only event logs as the ultimate source of truth for distributed state management.	2026-10-07 12:57:03.07802+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Event Sourcing\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/JTmgi0vO5Ug", "title": "System Design - Event Sourcing", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "hld-v3-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: CRUD vs Event Sourcing Race Conditions", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The CRUD Problem:** Imagine a video moves through states: `Uploaded` → `Processing` → `Success`. Three async workers push these state updates to the database. If the system is under heavy load, the worker responsible for updating the DB to `Success` might execute faster than the bogged-down worker trying to update it to `Processing`. The database row is overwritten to `Success`, but seconds later, the delayed worker overwrites it with `Processing`. The video is permanently stuck in `Processing` despite actually being complete. Traditional DBs overwrite data blindly, losing the sequence.\\n\\n**The Event Sourcing Solution:** Event Sourcing never overwrites data. Workers emit immutable events: `VideoUploaded`, `ProcessStarted`, `ProcessSucceeded`. These events are written to an append-only log. Even if events arrive slightly delayed, they are timestamped. To find the current state, the system replays the events in chronological order, inherently resolving the sequence and preventing older delays from destroying the final state.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "In a microservices architecture handling video processing, why is a traditional CRUD database update highly vulnerable to race conditions if background workers process tasks asynchronously? How does Event Sourcing fix this?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v3-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Understanding Hydration", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn Event Sourcing, the database does not contain a simple table column storing `Current_Balance = $500`. Instead, it contains a ledger of immutable events.\\n\\n**Hydration** is the process of reconstructing an entity's current state by fetching all its historical events and applying them sequentially.\\n\\n**Banking Example:**\\nWhen the user opens their app, the system queries the Event Store for their Account ID and pulls all events:\\n1. `AccountCreated` (Balance: 0)\\n2. `Deposited($500)` (+500)\\n3. `Deposited($300)` (+300)\\n4. `Withdrew($100)` (-100)\\n\\nThe system runs a reducer function over these events in memory (0 + 500 + 300 - 100 = 700). The hydration process concludes that the current state is $700 and serves that to the user.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Explain the concept of \\"Hydration\\" (or Event Replay) in an Event Sourced system. Using a banking application as an example, how is a user's current account balance determined?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v3-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Optimizing Hydration with Snapshots", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe standard mitigation strategy is **Snapshotting** combined with Command Query Responsibility Segregation (CQRS).\\n\\n**Snapshotting:** Instead of replaying the entire history from the beginning of time, a background job periodically calculates the state and saves it as a \\"Snapshot\\". For example, the bank system calculates the user's balance at Midnight every day and stores `Balance: $4,500 at Timestamp X`.\\n\\nWhen a read request comes in at 3:00 PM the next day, the hydration process fetches the latest Snapshot ($4,500) and only replays the handful of transaction events that occurred *after* Timestamp X. This bounds the read latency to a guaranteed O(1) time complexity, regardless of how old the account is.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "A major drawback of Event Sourcing is read latency; replaying a bank account's 15,000 transaction events to calculate the current balance takes too much CPU and time. Architecturally, how is this performance bottleneck mitigated?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v3-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Guaranteeing Event Ordering with Kafka", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf you use a standard queue (like SQS), multiple workers pulling concurrently will inevitably process messages out of order.\\n\\nTo guarantee strict ordering, systems utilize an event streaming platform like **Apache Kafka** with **Topic Partitions and Consumer Groups**.\\n1. **Partitioning Key:** When the API publishes an event, it assigns the `Order_ID` as the routing Key. Kafka hashes this key, guaranteeing that *every* event belonging to that specific Order_ID is routed to the exact same Partition within the topic.\\n2. **Consumer Group Rules:** Kafka enforces a strict rule: a single partition can only be consumed by exactly ONE worker instance within a consumer group.\\n\\nBecause all events for a specific order sit in a single partition queue, and that partition is read sequentially by a single worker thread, strict chronological ordering is mathematically guaranteed. Race conditions across multiple workers are entirely eliminated.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "When dealing with Event Sourcing, processing events out of sequence corrupts the state. If you have a cluster of 10 worker nodes reading from a Message Broker, how do you guarantee that all events related to a specific `Order_ID` are processed in strict chronological order?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v3-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Auditability and Time-Travel", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn a traditional CRUD database, once a row is `UPDATE`d or `DELETE`d, the previous data is permanently destroyed. If a bug corrupted user data yesterday, there is no way to know what the data looked like before the bug occurred without restoring massive database backups.\\n\\nBecause Event Sourcing uses an **append-only log**, data is never altered or deleted. Every event carries a precise timestamp.\\n\\n**Time-Travel:** If a developer needs to know the exact state of an order 30 days ago, they simply execute the Hydration process with a filter: *reconstruct the state by replaying all events up to, and strictly stopping at, the timestamp of 30 days ago.* The system will ignore all recent events and perfectly materialize the historical state, making debugging and financial auditing trivial.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "One of the largest benefits of Event Sourcing is \\"Time-Travel\\" and Auditability. How does the append-only nature of Event Sourcing allow developers to view the state of the system exactly as it was 30 days ago?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	High Level Design	### 📌 1. The Limitations of Traditional CRUD\n* In traditional architectures, databases store the **Current State** of an entity (e.g., changing an order status from `PENDING` to `SHIPPED`).\n* **The Problem:** \n  1. **Lost History:** Overwriting rows destroys the historical context of how the entity reached that state.\n  2. **Database Locking:** High-frequency updates on the same row create lock contention, bottlenecking the database.\n  3. **Race Conditions:** In microservices, async worker delays can cause older state updates to accidentally overwrite newer ones.\n\n### 💡 2. Event Sourcing Architecture\n* Instead of storing current state, Event Sourcing stores every state-changing action as an **immutable, append-only Event Log** (e.g., `ItemAdded`, `AddressUpdated`, `OrderPlaced`).\n* **The Source of Truth:** The database of events (Event Store) is the absolute source of truth. Data is never deleted or updated, only appended.\n* **Hydration / Replay:** To find the current state of an entity, the system queries all events for that ID and replays them in chronological sequence to calculate the final state.\n\n### 💡 3. Advanced Concepts & Optimizations\n* **Audit Trails & Time Travel:** Because every action is permanently logged, you gain perfect auditability and the ability to query the system's exact state at any historical point in time.\n* **Snapshotting:** Replaying 10,000 events to get a bank balance is slow. Systems create periodic "Snapshots" (e.g., balance at midnight). Hydration then only replays events that occurred *after* the latest snapshot.\n* **Ordering via Message Queues (Kafka):** Distributed queues risk processing events out of order. Using Kafka's **Topic Partitions**, all events for a specific entity ID are routed to the exact same partition. A partition is consumed by only one worker, guaranteeing strict chronological processing.	Piyush Garg	https://www.youtube.com/@piyushgarg1982	28	Event Sourcing	Understand how Event Sourcing replaces traditional CRUD by treating append-only event logs as the ultimate source of truth for distributed state management.	33	Hard	High Level Design	3	[{"id": "hld-v3-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: CRUD vs Event Sourcing Race Conditions", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The CRUD Problem:** Imagine a video moves through states: `Uploaded` → `Processing` → `Success`. Three async workers push these state updates to the database. If the system is under heavy load, the worker responsible for updating the DB to `Success` might execute faster than the bogged-down worker trying to update it to `Processing`. The database row is overwritten to `Success`, but seconds later, the delayed worker overwrites it with `Processing`. The video is permanently stuck in `Processing` despite actually being complete. Traditional DBs overwrite data blindly, losing the sequence.\\n\\n**The Event Sourcing Solution:** Event Sourcing never overwrites data. Workers emit immutable events: `VideoUploaded`, `ProcessStarted`, `ProcessSucceeded`. These events are written to an append-only log. Even if events arrive slightly delayed, they are timestamped. To find the current state, the system replays the events in chronological order, inherently resolving the sequence and preventing older delays from destroying the final state.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "In a microservices architecture handling video processing, why is a traditional CRUD database update highly vulnerable to race conditions if background workers process tasks asynchronously? How does Event Sourcing fix this?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v3-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Understanding Hydration", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn Event Sourcing, the database does not contain a simple table column storing `Current_Balance = $500`. Instead, it contains a ledger of immutable events.\\n\\n**Hydration** is the process of reconstructing an entity's current state by fetching all its historical events and applying them sequentially.\\n\\n**Banking Example:**\\nWhen the user opens their app, the system queries the Event Store for their Account ID and pulls all events:\\n1. `AccountCreated` (Balance: 0)\\n2. `Deposited($500)` (+500)\\n3. `Deposited($300)` (+300)\\n4. `Withdrew($100)` (-100)\\n\\nThe system runs a reducer function over these events in memory (0 + 500 + 300 - 100 = 700). The hydration process concludes that the current state is $700 and serves that to the user.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Explain the concept of \\"Hydration\\" (or Event Replay) in an Event Sourced system. Using a banking application as an example, how is a user's current account balance determined?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v3-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Optimizing Hydration with Snapshots", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe standard mitigation strategy is **Snapshotting** combined with Command Query Responsibility Segregation (CQRS).\\n\\n**Snapshotting:** Instead of replaying the entire history from the beginning of time, a background job periodically calculates the state and saves it as a \\"Snapshot\\". For example, the bank system calculates the user's balance at Midnight every day and stores `Balance: $4,500 at Timestamp X`.\\n\\nWhen a read request comes in at 3:00 PM the next day, the hydration process fetches the latest Snapshot ($4,500) and only replays the handful of transaction events that occurred *after* Timestamp X. This bounds the read latency to a guaranteed O(1) time complexity, regardless of how old the account is.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "A major drawback of Event Sourcing is read latency; replaying a bank account's 15,000 transaction events to calculate the current balance takes too much CPU and time. Architecturally, how is this performance bottleneck mitigated?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v3-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Guaranteeing Event Ordering with Kafka", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf you use a standard queue (like SQS), multiple workers pulling concurrently will inevitably process messages out of order.\\n\\nTo guarantee strict ordering, systems utilize an event streaming platform like **Apache Kafka** with **Topic Partitions and Consumer Groups**.\\n1. **Partitioning Key:** When the API publishes an event, it assigns the `Order_ID` as the routing Key. Kafka hashes this key, guaranteeing that *every* event belonging to that specific Order_ID is routed to the exact same Partition within the topic.\\n2. **Consumer Group Rules:** Kafka enforces a strict rule: a single partition can only be consumed by exactly ONE worker instance within a consumer group.\\n\\nBecause all events for a specific order sit in a single partition queue, and that partition is read sequentially by a single worker thread, strict chronological ordering is mathematically guaranteed. Race conditions across multiple workers are entirely eliminated.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "When dealing with Event Sourcing, processing events out of sequence corrupts the state. If you have a cluster of 10 worker nodes reading from a Message Broker, how do you guarantee that all events related to a specific `Order_ID` are processed in strict chronological order?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v3-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Auditability and Time-Travel", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn a traditional CRUD database, once a row is `UPDATE`d or `DELETE`d, the previous data is permanently destroyed. If a bug corrupted user data yesterday, there is no way to know what the data looked like before the bug occurred without restoring massive database backups.\\n\\nBecause Event Sourcing uses an **append-only log**, data is never altered or deleted. Every event carries a precise timestamp.\\n\\n**Time-Travel:** If a developer needs to know the exact state of an order 30 days ago, they simply execute the Hydration process with a filter: *reconstruct the state by replaying all events up to, and strictly stopping at, the timestamp of 30 days ago.* The system will ignore all recent events and perfectly materialize the historical state, making debugging and financial auditing trivial.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "One of the largest benefits of Event Sourcing is \\"Time-Travel\\" and Auditability. How does the append-only nature of Event Sourcing allow developers to view the state of the system exactly as it was 30 days ago?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
446	Combinatorial Invariants & Row Generation: Pascal's Triangle (Element, Row & Full Grid)	dsa-pascals-triangle-combinatorics-row-generation	Master all 3 variations of Pascal's Triangle: single element lookup using nCr formulas, full row generation in O(N) time, and complete grid synthesis in O(N²).	2026-10-02 23:12:19.363896+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Combinatorial Invariants & Row Generation: Pascal's Triangle (Element, Row & Full Grid)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bR7mQgwQ_o8", "title": "Lecture 34: Pascal's Triangle — Element, Row & Full Grid Generation", "duration": "32m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/pascals-triangle-ii/", "title": "Pascal's Triangle II", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return only the kth row of Pascal's Triangle using O(K) extra space."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-triangular-sum-of-an-array/", "title": "Find Triangular Sum of an Array", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Simulate adjacent triangular reductions modulo 10."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/pascal-triangle0652/1", "title": "Pascal Triangle", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Generate the Nth row of Pascal's Triangle modulo 10^9 + 7."}]	Module 11: Advanced Array Patterns & Hard Interview Problems	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn Pascal's Triangle, the first and last element of each row is `1`, and every other number is the sum of the two numbers directly above it. Interviewers test 3 distinct variations of this problem:\n1. **Variation 1:** Find the single element at row `R`, column `C`.\n2. **Variation 2:** Print the entire `Nth` row.\n3. **Variation 3:** Generate the full triangle of `N` rows.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Variation 1 (Element at Row `R`, Column `C`):**\n  - Uses the combinatorics formula: `Element = (R - 1) C (C - 1)` (nCr).\n  - Compute `nCr` in `O(R)` time without full factorials by multiplying and dividing on the fly:\n    `res = res * (n - i) / (i + 1)`.\n- **Variation 2 (Generate an Entire Row `N` in O(N) Time):**\n  - Start with `ans = 1`.\n  - For column `col` from `1` to `row - 1`, compute the next value iteratively from the previous one:\n    `ans = ans * (row - col) / col`.\n- **Variation 3 (Generate Full Triangle in O(N²) Time):**\n  - Call the `O(row)` row-generator for every row from `1` to `N`. This avoids `O(N³)` brute-force combinations.\n\n### 💻 Optimal Row Generator Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<long long> generateRow(int row) {\n    long long ans = 1;\n    vector<long long> rowList;\n    rowList.push_back(1); // 0th column is always 1\n    for (int col = 1; col < row; col++) {\n        ans = ans * (row - col);\n        ans = ans / col;\n        rowList.push_back(ans);\n    }\n    return rowList;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22224	SOLID Principles: SRP, OCP, and LSP	lld-solid-principles-part-1	Master clean architecture: Single Responsibility Principle (SRP), Open/Closed Principle (OCP), and Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP).	2026-10-07 16:56:30.769208+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: SOLID Principles: SRP, OCP, and LSP\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/UsNl8kcU4UA", "title": "SOLID Design Principles | Complete Guide with Code Examples", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v5-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) in Shopping Cart", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Violation:**\\nThe class has three distinct actors and three reasons to change:\\n1. If discount calculation logic changes $\\\\rightarrow$ modify `ShoppingCart`.\\n2. If invoice layout/branding changes $\\\\rightarrow$ modify `ShoppingCart`.\\n3. If database schema or ORM changes $\\\\rightarrow$ modify `ShoppingCart`.\\n\\n**The Refactored Architecture:**\\n1. `ShoppingCart`: Solely manages adding/removing items and calculating item totals.\\n2. `InvoicePrinter`: Accepts a `ShoppingCart` reference and formats the invoice for print/PDF output.\\n3. `CartRepository`: Accepts a `ShoppingCart` reference and handles SQL/NoSQL database persistence.\\nEach class now has exactly one reason to change.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Suppose a ShoppingCart class contains calculateTotal(), printInvoice(), and saveToDatabase(). Explain why this violates SRP and refactor it into compliant classes.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v5-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Open/Closed Principle (OCP) without If-Else Chains", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Breaking OCP:**\\nTo add Cassandra, you must open and modify the existing `PersistenceService` class, edit the `switch` statement, recompile, and re-test all existing SQL and Mongo logic. Existing code was modified rather than extended.\\n\\n**The OCP Solution:**\\n1. Define an abstract interface:\\n```cpp\\nclass IPersistence {\\npublic:\\n    virtual void save(const ShoppingCart& cart) = 0;\\n};\\n```\\n2. Implement concrete subclasses: `SqlPersistence : public IPersistence`, `MongoPersistence : public IPersistence`.\\n3. When Cassandra is needed, simply create `CassandraPersistence : public IPersistence` in a brand-new file.\\nExisting code is **closed for modification**, yet the system is **open for extension**.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "A persistence service uses a switch-case statement: if (type == \\"SQL\\") saveSql(); else if (type == \\"MONGO\\") saveMongo();. How does adding Cassandra break OCP, and how does Polymorphism solve it?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v5-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) Bank Account Violation", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Fatal Violation:**\\nClient code expects polymorphic safety:\\n```cpp\\nvoid processMonthlyFees(std::vector<BankAccount*> accounts) {\\n    for (auto acc : accounts) acc->withdraw(10.0); // Crashes on FixedDeposit!\\n}\\n```\\n`FixedDepositAccount` cannot be substituted for `BankAccount` because it breaks the behavioral contract established by the base class. The client is forced to introduce type-checking hacks (`if (dynamic_cast<FixedDeposit*>(acc)) ...`), creating tight coupling.\\n\\n**The LSP Solution:**\\nSegregate base interfaces based on actual capabilities:\\n1. Base class: `DepositOnlyAccount` (exposing only `deposit()`).\\n2. Derived class: `WithdrawableAccount : public DepositOnlyAccount` (adding `withdraw()`).\\n`SavingsAccount` inherits from `WithdrawableAccount`; `FixedDepositAccount` inherits directly from `DepositOnlyAccount`.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "A BankAccount base class defines deposit() and withdraw(). A FixedDepositAccount subclass throws an exception in withdraw() because withdrawals are locked. Why is this a fatal LSP violation?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v5-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Does SRP Mean Every Class Must Have Exactly One Method?", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Debunking the Myth:**\\nSRP states that a class should have **one reason to change (one responsibility)**, NOT a single method.\\n\\nA single responsibility often requires multiple complementary methods to fulfill its domain contract. For example, a `UserAuthenticationService` class has one responsibility (managing authentication), but legitimately contains multiple methods:\\n* `login(username, password)`\\n* `logout(sessionToken)`\\n* `validateToken(token)`\\n* `refreshToken(token)`\\nAll four methods serve the exact same cohesive responsibility (Authentication). Splitting this into four one-method classes (`LoginService`, `LogoutService`, etc.) creates an anti-pattern called **Class Proliferation** and destroys domain cohesion.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "A common junior developer misconception is that the Single Responsibility Principle dictates that a class can only have a single method. Debunk this misconception.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v5-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: The Classic Rectangle-Square LSP Problem", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Mathematical vs Object-Oriented Modeling:**\\nIn mathematics, a square is a rectangle. In object-oriented design, behavior dictates subtyping, not real-world taxonomy.\\n\\n**The Breakdown:**\\n1. Base class `Rectangle` defines setters:\\n```cpp\\nvirtual void setWidth(int w) { width = w; }\\nvirtual void setHeight(int h) { height = h; }\\n```\\n2. `Square` inherits and overrides them to keep sides equal:\\n```cpp\\nvoid setWidth(int w) override { width = height = w; }\\nvoid setHeight(int h) override { width = height = h; }\\n```\\n3. Client verification function:\\n```cpp\\nvoid testArea(Rectangle& r) {\\n    r.setWidth(5);\\n    r.setHeight(4);\\n    assert(r.getArea() == 20); // FAILS for Square! Area is 16.\\n}\\n```\\n`Square` breaks `Rectangle`'s behavioral contract (that setting height leaves width unchanged). Therefore, `Square` is NOT substitutable for `Rectangle`.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain why inheriting class Square from class Rectangle violates the Liskov Substitution Principle, even though mathematically a square is a rectangle.", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Cost of Fragile Code\n* Unprincipled code creates high fragility: changing one module breaks unrelated modules, unit testing becomes impossible, and new feature velocity grinds to zero.\n\n### 💡 2. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)\n* **Rule:** "A class should have one, and only one, reason to change."\n* **Violation Example:** A `ShoppingCart` class that calculates totals, formats & prints PDF invoices, AND executes SQL inserts to save orders to the database.\n* **Resolution:** Break into discrete classes: `ShoppingCart` (domain calculations), `InvoicePrinter` (presentation), and `CartRepository` (persistence).\n\n### 💡 3. Open/Closed Principle (OCP)\n* **Rule:** "Software entities should be open for extension, but closed for modification."\n* **Implementation:** Instead of adding `if (type == MONGO) ... else if (type == SQL)` branches inside a persistence class, define an abstract interface `IPersistence` and implement `SqlPersistence` and `MongoPersistence` as independent subclasses.\n\n### 💡 4. Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)\n* **Rule:** "Subclasses should be substitutable for their base classes without altering program correctness."\n* **Violation Example:** Creating a `FixedDepositAccount` subclass inheriting from `BankAccount`, where `withdraw()` throws a runtime exception because withdrawals are illegal.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	SOLID Principles: SRP, OCP, and LSP	Master clean architecture: Single Responsibility Principle (SRP), Open/Closed Principle (OCP), and Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP).	68	Hard	Low Level Design	19	[{"id": "lld-v5-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) in Shopping Cart", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Violation:**\\nThe class has three distinct actors and three reasons to change:\\n1. If discount calculation logic changes $\\\\rightarrow$ modify `ShoppingCart`.\\n2. If invoice layout/branding changes $\\\\rightarrow$ modify `ShoppingCart`.\\n3. If database schema or ORM changes $\\\\rightarrow$ modify `ShoppingCart`.\\n\\n**The Refactored Architecture:**\\n1. `ShoppingCart`: Solely manages adding/removing items and calculating item totals.\\n2. `InvoicePrinter`: Accepts a `ShoppingCart` reference and formats the invoice for print/PDF output.\\n3. `CartRepository`: Accepts a `ShoppingCart` reference and handles SQL/NoSQL database persistence.\\nEach class now has exactly one reason to change.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Suppose a ShoppingCart class contains calculateTotal(), printInvoice(), and saveToDatabase(). Explain why this violates SRP and refactor it into compliant classes.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v5-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Open/Closed Principle (OCP) without If-Else Chains", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Breaking OCP:**\\nTo add Cassandra, you must open and modify the existing `PersistenceService` class, edit the `switch` statement, recompile, and re-test all existing SQL and Mongo logic. Existing code was modified rather than extended.\\n\\n**The OCP Solution:**\\n1. Define an abstract interface:\\n```cpp\\nclass IPersistence {\\npublic:\\n    virtual void save(const ShoppingCart& cart) = 0;\\n};\\n```\\n2. Implement concrete subclasses: `SqlPersistence : public IPersistence`, `MongoPersistence : public IPersistence`.\\n3. When Cassandra is needed, simply create `CassandraPersistence : public IPersistence` in a brand-new file.\\nExisting code is **closed for modification**, yet the system is **open for extension**.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "A persistence service uses a switch-case statement: if (type == \\"SQL\\") saveSql(); else if (type == \\"MONGO\\") saveMongo();. How does adding Cassandra break OCP, and how does Polymorphism solve it?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v5-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) Bank Account Violation", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Fatal Violation:**\\nClient code expects polymorphic safety:\\n```cpp\\nvoid processMonthlyFees(std::vector<BankAccount*> accounts) {\\n    for (auto acc : accounts) acc->withdraw(10.0); // Crashes on FixedDeposit!\\n}\\n```\\n`FixedDepositAccount` cannot be substituted for `BankAccount` because it breaks the behavioral contract established by the base class. The client is forced to introduce type-checking hacks (`if (dynamic_cast<FixedDeposit*>(acc)) ...`), creating tight coupling.\\n\\n**The LSP Solution:**\\nSegregate base interfaces based on actual capabilities:\\n1. Base class: `DepositOnlyAccount` (exposing only `deposit()`).\\n2. Derived class: `WithdrawableAccount : public DepositOnlyAccount` (adding `withdraw()`).\\n`SavingsAccount` inherits from `WithdrawableAccount`; `FixedDepositAccount` inherits directly from `DepositOnlyAccount`.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "A BankAccount base class defines deposit() and withdraw(). A FixedDepositAccount subclass throws an exception in withdraw() because withdrawals are locked. Why is this a fatal LSP violation?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v5-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Does SRP Mean Every Class Must Have Exactly One Method?", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Debunking the Myth:**\\nSRP states that a class should have **one reason to change (one responsibility)**, NOT a single method.\\n\\nA single responsibility often requires multiple complementary methods to fulfill its domain contract. For example, a `UserAuthenticationService` class has one responsibility (managing authentication), but legitimately contains multiple methods:\\n* `login(username, password)`\\n* `logout(sessionToken)`\\n* `validateToken(token)`\\n* `refreshToken(token)`\\nAll four methods serve the exact same cohesive responsibility (Authentication). Splitting this into four one-method classes (`LoginService`, `LogoutService`, etc.) creates an anti-pattern called **Class Proliferation** and destroys domain cohesion.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "A common junior developer misconception is that the Single Responsibility Principle dictates that a class can only have a single method. Debunk this misconception.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v5-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: The Classic Rectangle-Square LSP Problem", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Mathematical vs Object-Oriented Modeling:**\\nIn mathematics, a square is a rectangle. In object-oriented design, behavior dictates subtyping, not real-world taxonomy.\\n\\n**The Breakdown:**\\n1. Base class `Rectangle` defines setters:\\n```cpp\\nvirtual void setWidth(int w) { width = w; }\\nvirtual void setHeight(int h) { height = h; }\\n```\\n2. `Square` inherits and overrides them to keep sides equal:\\n```cpp\\nvoid setWidth(int w) override { width = height = w; }\\nvoid setHeight(int h) override { width = height = h; }\\n```\\n3. Client verification function:\\n```cpp\\nvoid testArea(Rectangle& r) {\\n    r.setWidth(5);\\n    r.setHeight(4);\\n    assert(r.getArea() == 20); // FAILS for Square! Area is 16.\\n}\\n```\\n`Square` breaks `Rectangle`'s behavioral contract (that setting height leaves width unchanged). Therefore, `Square` is NOT substitutable for `Rectangle`.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain why inheriting class Square from class Rectangle violates the Liskov Substitution Principle, even though mathematically a square is a rectangle.", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
14167	Value-Per-Unit-Weight Density Sorting: Fractional Knapsack Algorithm	dsa-fractional-knapsack-algorithm-greedy	Master Value-Per-Unit-Weight Density Sorting: Fractional Knapsack Algorithm with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:24:23.649111+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Value-Per-Unit-Weight Density Sorting: Fractional Knapsack Algorithm\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1ibsQrnuEEg", "title": "Value-Per-Unit-Weight Density Sorting: Fractional Knapsack Algorithm - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/fractional-knapsack-1587115620/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Fractional Knapsack", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given weights and values of n items, put these items in a knapsack of capacity w to get the maximum total value in the knapsack (breaking items is allowed)."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-units-on-a-truck/", "title": "LeetCode 1710: Maximum Units on a Truck", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Sort box types by units per box descending and greedily load the truck up to truckSize."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-bags-with-full-capacity-of-rocks/", "title": "LeetCode 2279: Maximum Bags With Full Capacity of Rocks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Greedily fill bags with the smallest remaining rock deficit first."}]	Module 41: Greedy Algorithms: Sorting Heuristics, Reachability Horizons & Interval Scheduling	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven `N` items where item `i` has `value` and `weight`, and a knapsack of maximum capacity `W`, find the **maximum total value** you can carry. Unlike 0/1 Knapsack, in **Fractional Knapsack** you are allowed to **break items and take a fraction** of an item proportional to its weight!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Greedy Works for Fractional Knapsack (Whereas 0/1 Knapsack Needs DP):**\n  - Because we can slice any item into arbitrary fractions, the optimal strategy is to always greedily pick the item that yields the **highest value per `1` unit of weight (`(double)value / weight`)**!\n- **Algorithm Steps (`O(N log N + N)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  1. Sort all `N` items in **descending order of `(double)item.value / (double)item.weight`**.\n  2. Initialize `double totalVal = 0.0;`.\n  3. Iterate through each sorted item `i`:\n     - **If `arr[i].weight <= W` (Fits Completely):**\n       - Take the whole item: **`totalVal += arr[i].value;`** and reduce remaining capacity: **`W -= arr[i].weight;`**.\n     - **Else (`arr[i].weight > W`, Only a Fraction Fits):**\n       - Take the exact remaining capacity `W` at unit rate `arr[i].value / arr[i].weight`:\n         **`totalVal += ((double)arr[i].value / (double)arr[i].weight) * (double)W;`**\n       - Now the bag is 100% full (`W = 0`) → **`break`** immediately!\n  4. Return `totalVal`.\n\n### 💻 Fractional Knapsack Blueprint\n```cpp\nstruct Item {\n    int value;\n    int weight;\n};\n\nstatic bool comp(Item a, Item b) {\n    double r1 = (double)a.value / (double)a.weight;\n    double r2 = (double)b.value / (double)b.weight;\n    return r1 > r2; // Sort by value-per-weight ratio descending!\n}\n\ndouble fractionalKnapsack(int W, Item arr[], int n) {\n    // 1. Sort items by descending (value / weight) ratio\n    sort(arr, arr + n, comp);\n\n    double totalVal = 0.0;\n\n    // 2. Greedily take full items while possible, then a fraction of the last item\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        if (arr[i].weight <= W) {\n            totalVal += arr[i].value;\n            W -= arr[i].weight;\n        } else {\n            totalVal += ((double)arr[i].value / (double)arr[i].weight) * (double)W;\n            break; // Knapsack is now completely full!\n        }\n    }\n    return totalVal;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting `(double)` Typecasting Before Division:** In C++, `a.value / a.weight` performs **integer division** (truncating `100 / 60 = 1` instead of `1.6667`) unless you cast at least one operand to `double` (`(double)a.value / (double)a.weight`)!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
