ksforgeeks.org/problems/power-of-numbers-1587115620/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Power Of Numbers", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Compute N raised to the power R modulo 10^9 + 7 using binary exponentiation."}]	Module 39: Mathematical & Number Theory Algorithms: Divisors, Primes, Binary Exponentiation & Sieve Precomputation	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a floating-point or integer base `x` and an integer exponent `n` (which may be positive, zero, or **negative**), compute **`x^n`** in **`O(log2 N)` time** and **`O(1)` space**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Naive Multiplication (`O(N)`) Fails:**\n  - When `n = 2 * 10^9`, multiplying `x` one by one `n` times takes billions of operations and causes Time Limit Exceeded.\n- **Binary Exponentiation Rules (`while (nn > 0)`):**\n  - Initialize `double ans = 1.0;` and copy `long long nn = abs((long long)n);`.\n  1. **If `nn` is Odd (`nn % 2 == 1`):**\n     - Pull one factor of `x` out into `ans`: **`ans = ans * x;`** and decrement **`nn = nn - 1;`**.\n  2. **If `nn` is Even (`nn % 2 == 0`):**\n     - Since `x^nn = (x²)^(nn / 2)`, **square the base (`x = x * x;`)** and **halve the exponent (`nn = nn / 2;`)**!\n  - **Handling Negative Exponents (`n < 0`):**\n    - Since `x^(-n) = 1 / (x^n)`, if the original `n < 0`, set **`ans = 1.0 / ans;`** at the very end!\n- **Complexity:** `O(log2 N)` Time and `O(1)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Binary Exponentiation (Pow(x, n)) Blueprint\n```cpp\ndouble myPow(double x, int n) {\n    double ans = 1.0;\n    // Use long long before taking abs() so n = INT_MIN (-2147483648) never overflows!\n    long long nn = n;\n    if (nn < 0) nn = -1 * nn;\n\n    while (nn > 0) {\n        if (nn % 2 == 1) {\n            ans = ans * x;\n            nn = nn - 1;\n        } else {\n            x = x * x;\n            nn = nn / 2;\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (n < 0) {\n        ans = (double)(1.0) / (double)(ans);\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Negating `int n` Before Casting to `long long`:** In 32-bit signed integers, `INT_MIN` is `-2147483648`, while `INT_MAX` is `+2147483647`. Writing `n = -n` on a 32-bit `int` when `n == INT_MIN` causes signed integer overflow! Always copy `long long nn = n;` first and negate `nn`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
6395	Variable Start to Variable Destination DP: Minimum / Maximum Falling Path Sum	dsa-minimum-maximum-falling-path-sum-grid-dp	Compute falling path optimization from any first-row cell to any last-row cell using 3-directional transitions and O(M) space optimization.	2026-10-03 11:28:16.947746+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Variable Start to Variable Destination DP: Minimum / Maximum Falling Path Sum\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N_aJ5qQbYA0", "title": "Lecture 193: Minimum/Maximum Falling Path Sum (DP 12)", "duration": "22m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-falling-path-sum/", "title": "Minimum Falling Path Sum (LC 931)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the minimum sum of any falling path through an n x n matrix."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-falling-path-sum-ii/", "title": "Minimum Falling Path Sum II (LC 1289)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find the minimum falling path sum where no two elements chosen in adjacent rows are in the same column."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/path-in-matrix3805/1", "title": "Maximum path sum in matrix", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the maximum sum of any path starting from any column in row 0 and ending at any column in row N - 1."}]	Module 29: 2D Dynamic Programming on Multi-Choice States & Grids	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an `N x M` matrix, a **Falling Path** starts at **any cell in the first row (`0`)** and ends at **any cell in the last row (`N - 1`)**. From `(i, j)`, you can move to **`(i + 1, j - 1)` (Down-Left)**, **`(i + 1, j)` (Down-Straight)**, or **`(i + 1, j + 1)` (Down-Right)**. Find the **Maximum** (or **Minimum**) Falling Path Sum.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Handling Variable Starting AND Variable Ending Points:**\n  - Define `f(i, j)` as the max path sum to reach `(i, j)` from **any cell in row `0`**.\n  - **Base Case 1 (Out of Column Bounds `j < 0 || j >= M`):** Return **`-1e8`** for Max Path Sum (or **`+1e8`** for Min Path Sum).\n  - **Base Case 2 (First Row `i == 0`):** Return `matrix[0][j]`.\n  - **3-Way Transition:**\n    - `up = matrix[i][j] + f(i - 1, j)`\n    - `leftDiag = matrix[i][j] + f(i - 1, j - 1)`\n    - `rightDiag = matrix[i][j] + f(i - 1, j + 1)`\n    - Return `max({up, leftDiag, rightDiag})`.\n  - Finally, take the maximum of `dp[N - 1][j]` across all columns `j` from `0` to `M - 1`!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * M)` Time and `O(M)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Maximum Falling Path Sum (O(M) Space Optimized) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint getMaxPathSum(vector<vector<int>> &matrix) {\n    int n = matrix.size(), m = matrix[0].size();\n    vector<int> prev(m, 0), cur(m, 0);\n\n    // 1. Base case: Row 0\n    for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {\n        prev[j] = matrix[0][j];\n    }\n\n    // 2. Fill rows 1 to n - 1\n    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {\n            int up = matrix[i][j] + prev[j];\n            int leftDiag = matrix[i][j] + ((j - 1 >= 0) ? prev[j - 1] : -1e8);\n            int rightDiag = matrix[i][j] + ((j + 1 < m) ? prev[j + 1] : -1e8);\n\n            cur[j] = max({up, leftDiag, rightDiag});\n        }\n        prev = cur;\n    }\n\n    // 3. Answer is the maximum across all columns in the last row\n    int maxi = -1e8;\n    for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {\n        maxi = max(maxi, prev[j]);\n    }\n    return maxi;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Using `INT_MIN` (`-2e9`) as the Out-of-Bounds Sentinel:** If `matrix[i][j]` is negative (e.g., `-10`), computing `matrix[i][j] + INT_MIN` underflows a 32-bit signed integer into a huge positive number! Always use **`-1e8` (or `-1e9`)** instead of `INT_MIN`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
1363	Explicit Call-Stack Simulation: Iterative Preorder Traversal of a Binary Tree	dsa-iterative-preorder-traversal-binary-tree	Simulate recursive call stack mechanics iteratively using an explicit LIFO stack to achieve Root-Left-Right preorder traversal in O(N) time.	2026-10-03 00:50:37.254077+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Explicit Call-Stack Simulation: Iterative Preorder Traversal of a Binary Tree\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bfqd8BsPVuw", "title": "Lecture 81: Iterative Preorder Traversal of Binary Tree", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/n-ary-tree-preorder-traversal/", "title": "N-ary Tree Preorder Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Simulate iterative preorder on trees with arbitrary child counts using a stack."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-preorder-traversal/", "title": "Binary Tree Preorder Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Implement stack-based preorder traversal without recursion."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/iterative-preorder-traversal/1", "title": "Iterative Preorder", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Traverse a binary tree iteratively in Root-Left-Right order."}]	Module 15: Binary Trees: Hierarchical Representation & Fundamental Traversals	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn an interview, you may be forbidden from using recursion to traverse a tree. To execute **Preorder Traversal (`Root → Left → Right`)** iteratively, we manually simulate the operating system's call stack using a standard **LIFO `stack<TreeNode*>`**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Reverse Child Push Rule (`Right First, Then Left`):**\n  - Because a stack is Last-In-First-Out (LIFO), whichever node is pushed **last** will be popped and processed **first**!\n  - Therefore, whenever you pop `node`, you must push **`node->right` first**, and then push **`node->left` second** so that the left child sits on top of the stack ready to be processed next!\n- **Algorithm Steps:**\n  1. If `root == nullptr`, return an empty vector `{}`.\n  2. Push `root` onto `stack<TreeNode*> st`.\n  3. While `!st.empty()`:\n     - Pop `curr = st.top()`.\n     - Record `curr->val` into `ans`.\n     - If `curr->right != nullptr`, push `curr->right`.\n     - If `curr->left != nullptr`, push `curr->left`.\n- **Complexity:** `O(N)` Time (every node pushed and popped once) and `O(H)` Space (where `H` is tree height).\n\n### 💻 Iterative Preorder Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> iterativePreorder(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<int> ans;\n    if (root == nullptr) return ans;\n\n    stack<TreeNode*> st;\n    st.push(root);\n\n    while (!st.empty()) {\n        TreeNode* curr = st.top();\n        st.pop();\n        ans.push_back(curr->val);\n\n        // Push right first so left is popped first (LIFO)\n        if (curr->right != nullptr) st.push(curr->right);\n        if (curr->left != nullptr) st.push(curr->left);\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy