: "Search a node in BST", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Basic", "description": "Search for a key in a binary search tree and return 1 if found."}]	Module 19: Binary Search Trees: Concepts, Validations & Structural Mutations	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the root of a Binary Search Tree and a value `val`, locate the node with `val` and return its subtree pointer, or return `nullptr` if it does not exist.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Branch Elimination Algorithm (`O(H)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Start at `curr = root`.\n  - While `curr != nullptr && curr->val != val`:\n    - If `val < curr->val`: The target can only exist in the left subtree—move `curr = curr->left`.\n    - If `val > curr->val`: The target can only exist in the right subtree—move `curr = curr->right`.\n  - If loop finishes and `curr == nullptr`, the value is not present in the tree.\n- **Complexity:**\n  - `O(H)` Time (where `H` is `O(log2 N)` for balanced trees, up to `O(N)` for skewed trees).\n  - `O(1)` Space when implemented iteratively using a while loop.\n\n### 💻 Search in BST Blueprint\n```cpp\nTreeNode* searchBST(TreeNode* root, int val) {\n    TreeNode* curr = root;\n    while (curr != nullptr && curr->val != val) {\n        if (val < curr->val) {\n            curr = curr->left;\n        } else {\n            curr = curr->right;\n        }\n    }\n    return curr;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Using DFS to Scan Both Subtrees:** Do not search both `curr->left` and `curr->right`! Using the BST property means you explore strictly one branch at each step.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
762	Prefix Missing-Count Derivation: Finding the Kth Missing Positive Number in O(log N) Time	dsa-kth-missing-positive-number-binary-search	Find the Kth missing positive integer in an increasing array in O(log N) time using binary search on missing = arr[mid] - (mid + 1) and low + k derivation.	2026-10-02 23:53:37.123146+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Prefix Missing-Count Derivation: Finding the Kth Missing Positive Number in O(log N) Time\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uZ0N_hZpyps", "title": "Lecture 60: Kth Missing Positive Number", "duration": "26m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/missing-element-in-sorted-array/", "title": "Missing Element in Sorted Array", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the kth missing number starting from the leftmost element of a sorted array."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/first-missing-positive/", "title": "First Missing Positive", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find the smallest missing positive integer in an unsorted array in O(N) time and O(1) space."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/k-th-missing-element3635/1", "title": "K-th missing element", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Basic", "description": "Find the K-th missing element in an increasing sequence of integers."}]	Module 13: Binary Search on Answer Space & Monotonic Predicate Functions	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a strictly increasing array of positive integers (e.g., `[2, 3, 4, 7, 11]`) and an integer `k = 5`, find the **`k`-th positive integer that is missing** from this array (here the missing positives are `[1, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10...]`, so the 5th missing number is `9`).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Optimal)\n- **1. Brute Force Shift (O(N) Time):**\n  - Start with `ans = k`. Loop through each `arr[i]`: whenever `arr[i] <= k`, that element occupies one of the spots before `k`, so increment `k++`; otherwise `break` and return `k`.\n- **2. Optimal Binary Search + Mathematical Formula (O(log2 N) Time, O(1) Space):**\n  - **How Many Numbers Are Missing Up to Index `i`?**\n    - If no numbers were missing, index `i` (0-based) would hold the number `i + 1`.\n    - Since it actually holds `arr[i]`, the count of missing numbers up to index `i` is:\n      **`missing = arr[i] - (i + 1)`**!\n  - **Binary Search for the Bounding Indices:**\n    - Run Binary Search on `[0 ... n - 1]`:\n    - If `missing < k`, we need more missing numbers—move right (`low = mid + 1`).\n    - Otherwise (`missing >= k`), move left (`high = mid - 1`).\n  - **The Clean Algebraic Derivation (`low + k` or `high + 1 + k`):**\n    - When the loop ends, `high` and `low` cross (`low == high + 1`) right around where the `k`-th missing number belongs.\n    - Instead of computing `arr[high] + more` (which crashes when `high == -1` if the missing number is smaller than `arr[0]`), substitute `more = k - (arr[high] - high - 1)`:\n      `Answer = arr[high] + k - arr[high] + high + 1` → **`high + 1 + k`** (which equals **`low + k`**)!\n\n### 💻 Kth Missing Positive Number Blueprint\n```cpp\nint findKthPositive(vector<int> &arr, int k) {\n    int low = 0, high = (int)arr.size() - 1;\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        int missing = arr[mid] - (mid + 1);\n        if (missing < k) {\n            low = mid + 1;\n        } else {\n            high = mid - 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return k + high + 1; // Or equivalently: return low + k;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
1647	Last-Vertical Projection: Bottom View of a Binary Tree	dsa-bottom-view-of-binary-tree	Capture the bottommost node on each vertical line by unconditionally overwriting map entries during BFS level traversal.	2026-10-03 01:02:00.063534+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Last-Vertical Projection: Bottom View of a Binary Tree\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0FtVY6I4pB8", "title": "Lecture 95: Bottom View of Binary Tree", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/bottom-view-of-binary-tree/1", "title": "Bottom View of Binary Tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Print the nodes visible when looking at the tree from the bottom."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-level-order-traversal/", "title": "Binary Tree Level Order Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use FIFO queue processing to maintain vertical and level updates."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/top-view-of-binary-tree/1", "title": "Top View of Binary Tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Contrast bottom-view overwriting with top-view first-seen guards."}]	Module 17: Binary Trees: Views, Projections & Ancestry Analysis	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhen looking at a binary tree from the bottom, you see the **bottommost node** on each vertical line `x`. If two nodes overlap at the exact same depth on a line, the rightmost node obscures the left.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The 1-Line Change From Top View:**\n  - In Top View, we only wrote to `mpp[line]` if the key didn't exist yet.\n  - In **Bottom View**, we unconditionally overwrite: **`mpp[line] = node->val`** on every visit!\n  - Because BFS visits from top to bottom, the last node to overwrite `mpp[line]` is guaranteed to be the lowest node on that vertical line.\n- **Complexity:** `O(N log N)` Time and `O(N)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Bottom View Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> bottomView(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<int> ans;\n    if (root == nullptr) return ans;\n\n    map<int, int> mpp; // {line -> last node val}\n    queue<pair<TreeNode*, int>> q;\n    q.push({root, 0});\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        auto it = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        TreeNode* node = it.first;\n        int line = it.second;\n\n        // Unconditionally overwrite so the bottommost node remains\n        mpp[line] = node->val;\n\n        if (node->left != nullptr) q.push({node->left, line - 1});\n        if (node->right != nullptr) q.push({node->right, line + 1});\n    }\n\n    for (auto it : mpp) ans.push_back(it.second);\n    return ans;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
55	Core Programming Constructs: Data Types, Control Flow & Memory Passing	programming-constructs-data-types-memory-passing	Primitive ranges (int vs long long), control flow with if-else and switch, loops, arrays, and pass-by-value vs pass-by-reference.	2026-10-02 17:05:20.010203+05:30	### 🎯 Computer Science Fundamentals: Core Programming Constructs: Data Types, Control Flow & Memory Passing\nCore systems concepts frequently tested in technical screening rounds and engineering interviews.\n\n- **System Architecture**: How modern operating systems, networks, and processors coordinate resources.\n- **Key Concepts to Master**: Concurrency primitives, resource isolation, latency vs throughput tradeoffs, and fault tolerance.\n- **Interview Key Takeaways**:\n  - Be prepared to draw diagrams explaining data flow and component lifecycles.\n  - Explain practical real-world applications (e.g. why databases use WAL, why HTTP/2 uses multiplexing).	[{"url": "https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/", "title": "OS Three Easy Pieces Guide"}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/operating-systems/", "title": "GeeksforGeeks CS Fundamentals"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EAR7De6Goz4", "title": "Lecture 3: C++ Basics — User Input/Output, Data Types, If-Else, Loops, Functions & Arrays", "duration": "45m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/richest-customer-wealth/", "title": "Richest Customer Wealth", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1672", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "2D grid traversal computing row-wise sums testing multidimensional indexing and accumulator types."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/step-by-step-condition/1", "title": "Step by Step Condition", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "problemId": "gfg-step-cond", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Practice cleanly structured if-else ladder evaluation without redundant boolean clauses."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/the-else-if-statement/1", "title": "The Else if Statement", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "problemId": "gfg-else-if", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Evaluate multi-branch conditional flows with strict boundary guarantees."}]	Module 1: Algorithmic Foundations & Environment	### 📌 Topic Overview\nBefore diving into data structures, you need a solid grip on the building blocks of your programming language. This lecture covers standard input/output, choosing the right data types, writing clean `if-else` conditions and loops, working with arrays and strings, and understanding how functions pass data in memory.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Choosing Data Types Safely:**\n  - `int`: Stores whole numbers up to roughly 10^9 (1 billion).\n  - `long long`: Stores large whole numbers up to roughly 10^18—use this whenever multiplication or large sums might overflow `int`.\n  - `float` / `double`: Used for decimal numbers.\n  - `string` & `getline`: `cin >> s` stops reading at spaces, whereas `getline(cin, s)` reads an entire line of text.\n- **Control Flow (`if-else` & `switch`):** Use `else if` chains instead of separate `if` statements so the program stops checking once a match is found.\n- **1D & 2D Arrays:** Arrays store elements of the same type in consecutive memory slots starting at index `0`.\n- **Loops (`for`, `while`, `do-while`):** Use loops to repeat actions cleanly. A `do-while` loop is guaranteed to run at least once even if the condition is false initially.\n- **Pass by Value vs. Pass by Reference:**\n  - **Pass by Value (`void func(int x)`):** Sends a copy of the variable. Changes inside the function do not affect the original variable.\n  - **Pass by Reference (`void func(int &x)`):** Sends the actual variable using `&`. Changes inside the function directly update the original variable. (Note: C++ arrays are always passed by reference automatically).\n\n### 💻 Quick Syntax Example\n```cpp\n#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\n\nvoid addFiveByValue(int a) {\n    a = a + 5; // Only modifies the local copy\n}\n\nvoid addFiveByRef(int &b) {\n    b = b + 5; // Modifies the original variable\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int x = 10, y = 10;\n    addFiveByValue(x);\n    addFiveByRef(y);\n    cout << x << " " << y << "\n"; // Prints: 10 15\n    return 0;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22243	LLD Project: Discount Coupon Engine	lld-project-discount-coupon-engine	Architect a highly extensible rule-engine for applying dynamic discounts. Combine the Strategy pattern for math calculations with Chain of Responsibility for coupon stacking.	2026-10-07 17:16:35.515424+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: LLD Project: Discount Coupon Engine\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/jbVevoGN_pM", "title": "Build Discount Coupon Engine | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v24-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Applying Strategy Pattern to Discount Math", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf the `Coupon` class evaluated math internally, it would look like:\\n`if (type == FLAT) total -= amount; else if (type == PERCENT_CAP) total -= min(total * percent, cap);`\\n\\nBy injecting an `IDiscountStrategy` into the Coupon constructor via dependency injection, the Coupon simply delegates: `amountToDeduct = strategy->calculate(cartTotal);`.\\nIf marketing invents a \\"Buy One Get One\\" math strategy, the Coupon class logic is untouched; we simply author a new `BogoStrategy` class.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does extracting discount calculations into a Strategy Pattern prevent the core Coupon objects from becoming bloated with if-else math statements?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v24-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Product-Level vs Cart-Level Discounts", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe Coupon cannot blindly apply the strategy to the Cart's `finalTotal`. \\nInstead, inside its `getDiscount()` override, the `ElectronicsCoupon` iterates through the Cart's `CartItem` list.\\nIt inspects each `product->getCategory()`. If it matches \\"Electronics\\", it adds `item.getPrice() * item.getQuantity()` to a local `eligibleSubtotal` variable.\\nFinally, it passes this filtered `eligibleSubtotal` to the `IDiscountStrategy::calculate()` method, ensuring the 20% reduction is mathematically restricted to valid items.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "A coupon provides 20% off exclusively on \\"Electronics\\". How does the getDiscount() method securely isolate and sum only the eligible product prices from the Cart?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v24-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Coupon Stacking via Chain of Responsibility", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe applied coupons are wired together in a recursive chain (`head -> SeasonalCoupon -> BankCoupon -> null`).\\n\\nThe Cart's `applyDiscounts()` method triggers the `HeadCoupon`.\\n1. `SeasonalCoupon` executes `isApplicable()`. If true, it calculates the discount and updates the Cart's `finalTotal`.\\n2. Crucially, it then calls `if (this->isCombinable() && next != nullptr) next->applyDiscount(cart);`.\\n3. The updated Cart is passed to `BankCoupon`, which calculates its discount based on the newly reduced `finalTotal`, ensuring sequential, layered discount application without hardcoding the permutation order.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain how the Chain of Responsibility pattern facilitates \\"Coupon Stacking\\" (e.g., applying a Seasonal Promo code followed by a Bank Promo code).", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v24-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Preventing Negative Cart Totals", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThis is a classic Class Invariant that must be guarded.\\n1. Inside the `FlatDiscountStrategy::calculate(baseAmount)`, the logic strictly uses `return min(discountAmount, baseAmount);`.\\n2. At the `Cart` boundary, the `applyDiscount(amount)` setter verifies the operation:\\n```cpp\\nvoid Cart::applyDiscount(double amount) {\\n    finalTotal -= amount;\\n    if (finalTotal < 0) finalTotal = 0.0;\\n}\\n```\\nThis dual-layer protection guarantees the business rule that a checkout cart can be free, but never owes the customer money.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "If a user applies a ₹500 Flat Discount coupon to a Cart totaling ₹300, how does the architecture guarantee the final total does not become -₹200?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v24-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Thread Safety in Coupon Application (Mutex)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWithout thread safety, a Race Condition occurs: Thread A and Thread B both read `claims = 99`. Both evaluate `claims < 100` as true, and both apply the coupon, resulting in 101 claims.\\n\\n**The Mutex Implementation:**\\nInside the `CouponManager::applyCoupon()` method, a `std::mutex` is locked before checking availability.\\n```cpp\\nmutex.lock();\\nif (coupon->getClaims() < coupon->getMaxClaims()) {\\n    coupon->incrementClaim();\\n    // ... apply to cart ...\\n}\\nmutex.unlock();\\n```\\nThis creates a critical section, ensuring that out of 5,000 threads, only one thread at a time can read and increment the claim counter, strictly enforcing the mathematical cap.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "If a \\"First 100 Users\\" flash-sale coupon is being applied by 5,000 concurrent threads, how does a Mutex prevent 105 users from claiming the discount?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The E-Commerce Cart Complexity\n* A User's `Cart` aggregates multiple `CartItem`s (holding a `Product` and `Quantity`).\n* During checkout, complex promotion rules apply: 10% off Electronics (Product-level), ₹100 off orders over ₹1000 (Cart-level), plus a Bank Coupon (Flat ₹50).\n\n### 💡 2. Decoupling the Math: Strategy Pattern\n* **The Problem:** Calculating a percentage discount vs a flat discount requires vastly different mathematical bounds (e.g., checking caps).\n* **The Solution:** Abstract the math into `IDiscountStrategy`.\n* Implement `FlatDiscountStrategy`, `PercentDiscountStrategy`, and `PercentWithCapStrategy`. Coupons simply invoke `strategy->calculate(amount)` without caring about the underlying formula.\n\n### 💡 3. Coupon Stacking: Chain of Responsibility\n* Can a user apply a 10% Zomato coupon AND a ₹50 HDFC Bank coupon?\n* **The Solution:** The applied coupons form a Chain of Responsibility Linked List.\n* The `Cart` total is passed to the `HeadCoupon`. It applies its strategy, reduces the Cart's `finalTotal`, and forwards the Cart to `NextCoupon`.\n* If a coupon has an `isCombinable()` flag set to false (e.g., Bank Coupons cannot be stacked), the Chain halts execution immediately.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	LLD Project: Discount Coupon Engine	Architect a highly extensible rule-engine for applying dynamic discounts. Combine the Strategy pattern for math calculations with Chain of Responsibility for coupon stacking.	83	Hard	Low Level Design	38	[{"id": "lld-v24-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Applying Strategy Pattern to Discount Math", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf the `Coupon` class evaluated math internally, it would look like:\\n`if (type == FLAT) total -= amount; else if (type == PERCENT_CAP) total -= min(total * percent, cap);`\\n\\nBy injecting an `IDiscountStrategy` into the Coupon constructor via dependency injection, the Coupon simply delegates: `amountToDeduct = strategy->calculate(cartTotal);`.\\nIf marketing invents a \\"Buy One Get One\\" math strategy, the Coupon class logic is untouched; we simply author a new `BogoStrategy` class.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does extracting discount calculations into a Strategy Pattern prevent the core Coupon objects from becoming bloated with if-else math statements?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v24-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Product-Level vs Cart-Level Discounts", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe Coupon cannot blindly apply the strategy to the Cart's `finalTotal`. \\nInstead, inside its `getDiscount()` override, the `ElectronicsCoupon` iterates through the Cart's `CartItem` list.\\nIt inspects each `product->getCategory()`. If it matches \\"Electronics\\", it adds `item.getPrice() * item.getQuantity()` to a local `eligibleSubtotal` variable.\\nFinally, it passes this filtered `eligibleSubtotal` to the `IDiscountStrategy::calculate()` method, ensuring the 20% reduction is mathematically restricted to valid items.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "A coupon provides 20% off exclusively on \\"Electronics\\". How does the getDiscount() method securely isolate and sum only the eligible product prices from the Cart?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v24-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Coupon Stacking via Chain of Responsibility", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe applied coupons are wired together in a recursive chain (`head -> SeasonalCoupon -> BankCoupon -> null`).\\n\\nThe Cart's `applyDiscounts()` method triggers the `HeadCoupon`.\\n1. `SeasonalCoupon` executes `isApplicable()`. If true, it calculates the discount and updates the Cart's `finalTotal`.\\n2. Crucially, it then calls `if (this->isCombinable() && next != nullptr) next->applyDiscount(cart);`.\\n3. The updated Cart is passed to `BankCoupon`, which calculates its discount based on the newly reduced `finalTotal`, ensuring sequential, layered discount application without hardcoding the permutation order.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain how the Chain of Responsibility pattern facilitates \\"Coupon Stacking\\" (e.g., applying a Seasonal Promo code followed by a Bank Promo code).", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v24-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Preventing Negative Cart Totals", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThis is a classic Class Invariant that must be guarded.\\n1. Inside the `FlatDiscountStrategy::calculate(baseAmount)`, the logic strictly uses `return min(discountAmount, baseAmount);`.\\n2. At the `Cart` boundary, the `applyDiscount(amount)` setter verifies the operation:\\n```cpp\\nvoid Cart::applyDiscount(double amount) {\\n    finalTotal -= amount;\\n    if (finalTotal < 0) finalTotal = 0.0;\\n}\\n```\\nThis dual-layer protection guarantees the business rule that a checkout cart can be free, but never owes the customer money.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "If a user applies a ₹500 Flat Discount coupon to a Cart totaling ₹300, how does the architecture guarantee the final total does not become -₹200?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v24-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Thread Safety in Coupon Application (Mutex)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWithout thread safety, a Race Condition occurs: Thread A and Thread B both read `claims = 99`. Both evaluate `claims < 100` as true, and both apply the coupon, resulting in 101 claims.\\n\\n**The Mutex Implementation:**\\nInside the `CouponManager::applyCoupon()` method, a `std::mutex` is locked before checking availability.\\n```cpp\\nmutex.lock();\\nif (coupon->getClaims() < coupon->getMaxClaims()) {\\n    coupon->incrementClaim();\\n    // ... apply to cart ...\\n}\\nmutex.unlock();\\n```\\nThis creates a critical section, ensuring that out of 5,000 threads, only one thread at a time can read and increment the claim counter, strictly enforcing the mathematical cap.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "If a \\"First 100 Users\\" flash-sale coupon is being applied by 5,000 concurrent threads, how does a Mutex prevent 105 users from claiming the discount?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22245	LLD Project: Zepto / Quick Commerce	lld-project-zepto-quick-commerce	Architect a hyperscale 10-minute delivery engine. Map inventory to localized Dark Stores, calculate spatial distances, and orchestrate split-order fulfillments.	2026-10-07 17:16:35.515424+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: LLD Project: Zepto / Quick Commerce\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/FcbsppIX0bg", "title": "Build Zepto | 10-Mins Quick Commerce | Inventory Management | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v26-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Data Structures for Inventory Tracking", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA Dark Store manages thousands of unique SKUs.\\nDuring a highly concurrent checkout, the system must constantly execute `checkStock(SKU)` and `decrementStock(SKU)`.\\nIf a `std::vector` was used, verifying the stock of a toothbrush requires an $O(N)$ linear scan across thousands of products. \\nBy mapping `std::unordered_map<int, int> stocks` (SKU $\\\\rightarrow$ Quantity), stock validations and decrements resolve in $O(1)$ constant time, preventing massive latency bottlenecks during flash sales.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is an unordered_map (Hash Map) strictly superior to a std::vector (List) for tracking SKU quantities inside a Dark Store's InventoryStore?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v26-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Calculating Proximity (Spatial Distance)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Location Request:** The UI passes the user's coordinates (Lat/Lon) to the backend.\\n2. **Proximity Filtering:** The `DarkStoreManager` loops through its master registry of all Dark Stores. It calls `store->distanceTo(userLat, userLon)` (typically utilizing the Haversine formula for spherical distance).\\n3. **SLA Boundary:** If the distance is `< 5.0 km`, the store is added to a `nearbyStores` list.\\n4. **Inventory Aggregation:** The system then aggregates all products available inside the `InventoryManagers` of those specific `nearbyStores`, returning that localized product list to the frontend UI.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the DarkStoreManager dynamically resolve which items a User can see on their frontend app based on their geographic location?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v26-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Complex Split-Order Fulfillment Algorithm", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **First Pass:** `OrderManager` queries the closest Dark Store (Store A). Store A reports `checkStock(SKU) = 3`.\\n2. **Partial Deduction:** The manager takes the minimum of (Need, Available) $\\\\rightarrow \\\\min(5, 3) = 3$. It invokes `StoreA.removeStock(SKU, 3)`. The remaining unfulfilled deficit is `5 - 3 = 2`.\\n3. **Agent Assignment:** Because items were pulled from Store A, `DeliveryAgent 1` is assigned to Store A.\\n4. **Second Pass:** The manager moves to the next closest store (Store B). Store B reports `checkStock(SKU) = 10`. \\n5. **Completion:** The manager takes the remaining deficit $\\\\min(2, 10) = 2$. It invokes `StoreB.removeStock(SKU, 2)`. The deficit hits `0`.\\n6. **Split Assignment:** `DeliveryAgent 2` is assigned to Store B. The user's app displays: \\"Your order is arriving in 2 separate packages.\\"", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Walk through the mathematical logic inside OrderManager when a user orders 5 units of an item, but the closest Dark Store only contains 3 units.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v26-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Strategy Pattern for Inventory Replenishment", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nRestocking logic varies: highly perishable items (Milk) require threshold-based restocking, while dry goods (Paper Towels) use weekly batch restocking.\\n\\n1. Extract the logic into an `IReplenishStrategy` interface with an `execute(InventoryManager*)` method.\\n2. **Threshold Strategy:** Iterates over the inventory map. `if (quantity < threshold) manager->addStock(SKU, refillAmount);`\\n3. **Weekly Strategy:** Blindly executes a bulk `addStock` from a predefined warehouse manifest.\\nThe `DarkStore` simply holds an `IReplenishStrategy*`. A chron job calls `store->runReplenishment()`, delegating the logic flawlessly without modifying the store's internal mechanics.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain how the Strategy Pattern handles automated inventory replenishment (e.g., Threshold-based vs Weekly Restocking) without hardcoding business rules in the Dark Store.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v26-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Non-Singleton Dark Store Managers", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf the `InventoryManager` was a global Singleton, it would mix the inventory of Store A (Mumbai) and Store B (Delhi) into a single master map.\\nTo verify stock, you would have to query `manager.getStock(StoreID, SKU)`, adding severe data modeling complexity and creating a massive bottleneck lock point for the entire country's checkout traffic.\\nBy utilizing Composition (`DarkStore has-a InventoryManager`), each micro-warehouse maintains its own isolated, in-memory inventory state. This localized Sharding drastically improves lookup latency and perfectly isolates localized geographic traffic.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is the InventoryManager tied locally to a single DarkStore via Composition, rather than implemented as a global Singleton?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Quick Commerce Domain\n* Unlike traditional E-Commerce where goods ship from massive central warehouses over 3 days, Quick Commerce relies on highly localized micro-warehouses (**Dark Stores**) mapped to specific geographic grids.\n* **The Goal:** A 10-minute delivery SLA.\n\n### 💡 2. Inventory & Dark Store Architecture\n* **`Product` & `InventoryStore`:** Tracks SKU and quantities. Stored via an `unordered_map<SKU, Quantity>` for $O(1)$ fast lookups.\n* **`DarkStore`:** An entity aggregating an `InventoryManager` and geographic coordinates (latitude, longitude).\n* **`DarkStoreManager`:** A Singleton orchestrator. Exposes `getNearbyStores(lat, lon, maxDistance)` utilizing the Haversine distance formula to return only stores within a 5km radius of the user.\n\n### 💡 3. Split-Order Fulfillment Logic\n* If a User's Cart contains 4 Apples and 2 Bananas, the primary Dark Store might only have 3 Apples.\n* **The Allocation Algorithm:**\n  1. The `OrderManager` attempts to fulfill the entire cart from the closest Dark Store.\n  2. If stock is insufficient, it decrements available stock from Store 1, and routes the remainder to Store 2.\n  3. The Order assigns multiple `DeliveryAgent`s, orchestrating a split-delivery to hit the SLA.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	LLD Project: Zepto / Quick Commerce	Architect a hyperscale 10-minute delivery engine. Map inventory to localized Dark Stores, calculate spatial distances, and orchestrate split-order fulfillments.	96	Hard	Low Level Design	40	[{"id": "lld-v26-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Data Structures for Inventory Tracking", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA Dark Store manages thousands of unique SKUs.\\nDuring a highly concurrent checkout, the system must constantly execute `checkStock(SKU)` and `decrementStock(SKU)`.\\nIf a `std::vector` was used, verifying the stock of a toothbrush requires an $O(N)$ linear scan across thousands of products. \\nBy mapping `std::unordered_map<int, int> stocks` (SKU $\\\\rightarrow$ Quantity), stock validations and decrements resolve in $O(1)$ constant time, preventing massive latency bottlenecks during flash sales.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is an unordered_map (Hash Map) strictly superior to a std::vector (List) for tracking SKU quantities inside a Dark Store's InventoryStore?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v26-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Calculating Proximity (Spatial Distance)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Location Request:** The UI passes the user's coordinates (Lat/Lon) to the backend.\\n2. **Proximity Filtering:** The `DarkStoreManager` loops through its master registry of all Dark Stores. It calls `store->distanceTo(userLat, userLon)` (typically utilizing the Haversine formula for spherical distance).\\n3. **SLA Boundary:** If the distance is `< 5.0 km`, the store is added to a `nearbyStores` list.\\n4. **Inventory Aggregation:** The system then aggregates all products available inside the `InventoryManagers` of those specific `nearbyStores`, returning that localized product list to the frontend UI.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the DarkStoreManager dynamically resolve which items a User can see on their frontend app based on their geographic location?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v26-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Complex Split-Order Fulfillment Algorithm", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **First Pass:** `OrderManager` queries the closest Dark Store (Store A). Store A reports `checkStock(SKU) = 3`.\\n2. **Partial Deduction:** The manager takes the minimum of (Need, Available) $\\\\rightarrow \\\\min(5, 3) = 3$. It invokes `StoreA.removeStock(SKU, 3)`. The remaining unfulfilled deficit is `5 - 3 = 2`.\\n3. **Agent Assignment:** Because items were pulled from Store A, `DeliveryAgent 1` is assigned to Store A.\\n4. **Second Pass:** The manager moves to the next closest store (Store B). Store B reports `checkStock(SKU) = 10`. \\n5. **Completion:** The manager takes the remaining deficit $\\\\min(2, 10) = 2$. It invokes `StoreB.removeStock(SKU, 2)`. The deficit hits `0`.\\n6. **Split Assignment:** `DeliveryAgent 2` is assigned to Store B. The user's app displays: \\"Your order is arriving in 2 separate packages.\\"", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Walk through the mathematical logic inside OrderManager when a user orders 5 units of an item, but the closest Dark Store only contains 3 units.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v26-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Strategy Pattern for Inventory Replenishment", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nRestocking logic varies: highly perishable items (Milk) require threshold-based restocking, while dry goods (Paper Towels) use weekly batch restocking.\\n\\n1. Extract the logic into an `IReplenishStrategy` interface with an `execute(InventoryManager*)` method.\\n2. **Threshold Strategy:** Iterates over the inventory map. `if (quantity < threshold) manager->addStock(SKU, refillAmount);`\\n3. **Weekly Strategy:** Blindly executes a bulk `addStock` from a predefined warehouse manifest.\\nThe `DarkStore` simply holds an `IReplenishStrategy*`. A chron job calls `store->runReplenishment()`, delegating the logic flawlessly without modifying the store's internal mechanics.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain how the Strategy Pattern handles automated inventory replenishment (e.g., Threshold-based vs Weekly Restocking) without hardcoding business rules in the Dark Store.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v26-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Non-Singleton Dark Store Managers", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf the `InventoryManager` was a global Singleton, it would mix the inventory of Store A (Mumbai) and Store B (Delhi) into a single master map.\\nTo verify stock, you would have to query `manager.getStock(StoreID, SKU)`, adding severe data modeling complexity and creating a massive bottleneck lock point for the entire country's checkout traffic.\\nBy utilizing Composition (`DarkStore has-a InventoryManager`), each micro-warehouse maintains its own isolated, in-memory inventory state. This localized Sharding drastically improves lookup latency and perfectly isolates localized geographic traffic.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is the InventoryManager tied locally to a single DarkStore via Composition, rather than implemented as a global Singleton?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
4721	Spanning Trees & Minimum Cost Connectivity: Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) Theory	dsa-minimum-spanning-tree-theory	Understand spanning tree structural invariants (N vertices, N - 1 edges, acyclic connectivity) and MST definitions.	2026-10-03 11:07:15.31323+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Spanning Trees & Minimum Cost Connectivity: Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) Theory\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZSPjZuZWCME", "title": "Lecture 169: Minimum Spanning Tree - Theory", "duration": "16m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/min-cost-to-connect-all-points/", "title": "Min Cost to Connect All Points (LC 1584)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the minimum Manhattan distance cost to connect all 2D points into a single spanning tree."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/connecting-cities-with-minimum-cost/", "title": "Connecting Cities With Minimum Cost (LC 1135)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the minimum cost to connect all N cities so that there is a path between every pair of cities."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/minimum-spanning-tree/1", "title": "Minimum Spanning Tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Calculate the sum of weights of the edges of the Minimum Spanning Tree."}]	Module 25: Negative-Weight Shortest Paths, All-Pairs Shortest Paths & Minimum Spanning Trees	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an undirected, connected, weighted graph with `N` vertices and `M` edges:\n- A **Spanning Tree** is a subgraph that connects **all `N` vertices** using **exactly `N - 1` edges** (which guarantees **zero cycles** and a single connected component).\n- A **Minimum Spanning Tree (MST)** is the spanning tree that has the **minimum possible total sum of edge weights** among all valid spanning trees of the graph.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Core Properties of Every Spanning Tree:**\n  1. Contains **all `N` vertices** of the original graph.\n  2. Contains **exactly `N - 1` edges**.\n  3. Is **100% connected** (every vertex can reach every other vertex) and **acyclic** (adding even 1 more edge creates a cycle; removing even 1 edge disconnects the tree).\n- **The Two Classical Algorithms to Find an MST:**\n  1. **Prim's Algorithm (`O(E log E)`):** Greedy node-based expansion starting from a single vertex using a Min-Heap Priority Queue.\n  2. **Kruskal's Algorithm (`O(E log E)`):** Greedy edge-based sorting combined with the **Disjoint Set (Union-Find)** data structure.\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Confusing Shortest Path Tree (Dijkstra) With Minimum Spanning Tree (Prim/Kruskal):** Dijkstra minimizes the path distance from a specific source `S` to each individual node, whereas an MST minimizes the **total sum of all `N - 1` edge weights** needed to wire the whole network together!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
3026	Inorder Inversion Detection: Recover a BST With Two Swapped Nodes	dsa-recover-bst-two-nodes-swapped	Detect and correct two swapped nodes in a Binary Search Tree by tracking adjacent and non-adjacent inorder traversal inversions.	2026-10-03 01:35:42.032696+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Inorder Inversion Detection: Recover a BST With Two Swapped Nodes\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZWGW7FminDM", "title": "Lecture 124: Recover BST | Correct BST with two nodes swapped", "duration": "24m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recover-binary-search-tree/", "title": "Recover Binary Search Tree (LC 99)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Recover a BST where the values of exactly two nodes were swapped by mistake."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/fixed-two-nodes-of-a-bst/1", "title": "Fixing Two nodes of a BST", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Correct a BST in-place without changing its structure."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/validate-binary-search-tree/", "title": "Validate Binary Search Tree (LC 98)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Detect ordering violations during tree traversal."}]	Module 20: Advanced Binary Search Tree Operations & Validation	### 📌 Topic Overview\nYou are given the root of a Binary Search Tree in which the values of **exactly two nodes were swapped by mistake**. Recover the tree by swapping their values back without changing its structure.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Sorted Array Inversion Analogy:**\n  - Since the Inorder Traversal of a valid BST is strictly increasing, swapping two nodes creates inversions where **`root->val < prev->val`**.\n  - There are **2 possible cases** when two elements are swapped in a sorted sequence:\n    1. **Swapped Nodes Are Non-Adjacent** (e.g., `[3, 25, 7, 8, 10, 15, 20, 5]` where `25` and `5` are swapped):\n       - Produces **2 violations**: First at `(25, 7)` (`first = 25`, `middle = 7`), and second at `(20, 5)` (`last = 5`).\n       - Fix: **`swap(first->val, last->val)`**.\n    2. **Swapped Nodes Are Adjacent** (e.g., `[3, 5, 8, 7, 10, 15, 20, 25]` where `8` and `7` are swapped):\n       - Produces **only 1 violation**: At `(8, 7)` (`first = 8`, `middle = 7`), and `last` remains `nullptr`.\n       - Fix: **`swap(first->val, middle->val)`**.\n- **Complexity:** `O(N)` Time and `O(H)` Recursion Space (or `O(1)` Space using Morris Inorder Traversal).\n\n### 💻 Recover BST Blueprint\n```cpp\nclass Solution {\n    TreeNode *first, *prev, *middle, *last;\n\n    void inorder(TreeNode* root) {\n        if (root == nullptr) return;\n        inorder(root->left);\n\n        if (prev != nullptr && (root->val < prev->val)) {\n            // First violation: record both prev (first) and root (middle)\n            if (first == nullptr) {\n                first = prev;\n                middle = root;\n            }\n            // Second violation: record root (last)\n            else {\n                last = root;\n            }\n        }\n        prev = root;\n        inorder(root->right);\n    }\n\npublic:\n    void recoverTree(TreeNode* root) {\n        first = middle = last = nullptr;\n        prev = new TreeNode(INT_MIN);\n        inorder(root);\n        if (first && last) swap(first->val, last->val);\n        else if (first && middle) swap(first->val, middle->val);\n    }\n};\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting the `middle` Pointer:** If you only track `first` and `last`, your code will fail whenever the two swapped nodes happen to be adjacent in the inorder sequence (because a second violation never occurs).	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
