matrix", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Determine whether a target integer exists in a sorted 2D matrix."}]	Module 14: Multi-Array Partitioning & 2D Matrix Binary Search	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an `N x M` 2D matrix where every row is sorted and the first integer of each row is strictly greater than the last integer of the previous row, search for `target` in **O(log2(N * M)) time**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The 1D Coordinate Flattening Invariant:**\n  - The entire matrix can be treated as a single virtual 1D sorted array of length `N * M` (indices `0` to `N * M - 1`).\n  - **Conversion Formula:**\n    - `row = mid / m`\n    - `col = mid % m`\n- **Pure Binary Search on Virtual Array:**\n  - `low = 0`, `high = n * m - 1`.\n  - Calculate `val = matrix[mid / m][mid % m]`.\n  - If `val == target`: return `true`.\n  - If `val < target`: `low = mid + 1`.\n  - If `val > target`: `high = mid - 1`.\n\n### 💻 Flattened 2D Binary Search Blueprint\n```cpp\nbool searchMatrix(vector<vector<int>> &matrix, int target) {\n    int n = matrix.size(), m = matrix[0].size();\n    int low = 0, high = n * m - 1;\n\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        int row = mid / m;\n        int col = mid % m;\n        if (matrix[row][col] == target) return true;\n        else if (matrix[row][col] < target) low = mid + 1;\n        else high = mid - 1;\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
13275	Pre-Inserted Frequency Deficit Contraction: Minimum Window Substring	dsa-minimum-window-substring-sliding-window	Master Pre-Inserted Frequency Deficit Contraction: Minimum Window Substring with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:19:12.166596+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Pre-Inserted Frequency Deficit Contraction: Minimum Window Substring\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WJaij9ffOIY", "title": "Pre-Inserted Frequency Deficit Contraction: Minimum Window Substring - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-window-substring/", "title": "LeetCode 76: Minimum Window Substring", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Given two strings s and t of lengths m and n respectively, return the minimum window substring of s such that every character in t (including duplicates) is included in the window."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/permutation-in-string/", "title": "LeetCode 567: Permutation in String", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return true if s2 contains a substring of length s1.size() that is a permutation of s1."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/smallest-window-in-a-string-containing-all-the-characters-of-another-string-1587115621/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Smallest window in a string containing all the characters of another string", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find the smallest window in string S having all characters of pattern P."}]	Module 40: Sliding Window & Two-Pointer Algorithms: Fixed Windows, Variable Windows & Exact-Count Reductions	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven two strings `s` (length `N`) and `t` (length `M`), find and return the **minimum length substring in `s`** that contains **all characters of `t` (including their duplicate counts)**. If no such substring exists, return `""`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Pattern 4: Expand to Satisfy, Shrink to Minimize!)\n- **The Pre-Inserted `hash[256]` + `cnt` Trick (`O(1)` Validity Check):**\n  1. **Pre-insert `t`:** Create `int hash[256] = {0};` and for each character `c` in `t`, increment **`hash[c]++`**.\n     - Now, a **positive value (`hash[c] > 0`)** means we **still need** character `c` from `t`!\n  2. **Expand `r` (`0` to `N - 1`):**\n     - Before decrementing `hash[s[r]]`, check: **`if (hash[s[r]] > 0) cnt++;`** (meaning `s[r]` was a required character from `t`!).\n     - Then decrement **`hash[s[r]]--`** (extra occurrences or characters not in `t` go negative in `hash[]`).\n  3. **Shrink `l` While Window Is Valid (`while (cnt == m)`):**\n     - Whenever **`cnt == m`**, the window `[l ... r]` contains all `m` characters of `t`!\n     - If `r - l + 1 < minLen`, update `minLen = r - l + 1` and record **`sIndex = l`**.\n     - Now try to shrink from the left to see if we can get an even smaller valid window:\n       - Put `s[l]` back into `hash`: **`hash[s[l]]++`**.\n       - If **`hash[s[l]] > 0`** after incrementing, we just lost a required character of `t`—so decrement **`cnt--`**!\n       - Advance `l++`.\n  4. Return `(sIndex == -1) ? "" : s.substr(sIndex, minLen)`.\n- **Complexity:** **`O(2N + M)`** Time and **`O(256) = O(1)`** Space.\n\n### 💻 Minimum Window Substring Blueprint\n```cpp\nstring minWindow(string s, string t) {\n    int n = s.size(), m = t.size();\n    if (n == 0 || m == 0 || n < m) return "";\n\n    int hash[256] = {0};\n    // 1. Pre-insert all characters of t into the frequency table\n    for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {\n        hash[t[i]]++;\n    }\n\n    int l = 0, r = 0, cnt = 0;\n    int minLen = 1e9, sIndex = -1;\n\n    while (r < n) {\n        // 2. If s[r] has a positive count, it fulfills one needed character from t\n        if (hash[s[r]] > 0) {\n            cnt++;\n        }\n        hash[s[r]]--;\n\n        // 3. Once all m characters of t are matched, shrink from left to minimize window!\n        while (cnt == m) {\n            if (r - l + 1 < minLen) {\n                minLen = r - l + 1;\n                sIndex = l;\n            }\n\n            hash[s[l]]++;\n            // If hash[s[l]] becomes strictly positive (> 0), we lost a needed character of t!\n            if (hash[s[l]] > 0) {\n                cnt--;\n            }\n            l++;\n        }\n        r++;\n    }\n\n    return (sIndex == -1) ? "" : s.substr(sIndex, minLen);\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Checking `if (hash[s[l]] == 0)` Instead of `if (hash[s[l]] > 0)` When Shrinking:** Unneeded characters (or extra duplicates of needed characters) have negative values in `hash[]` (e.g., `-2 -> -1 -> 0`). A character's removal only breaks the `t` requirement when its deficit in `hash[s[l]]` becomes **strictly greater than `0` (`> 0`)**!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
9279	Heap Node Architecture & Pointer Traversal: Introduction to Singly Linked List	dsa-introduction-to-singly-linked-list	Understand non-contiguous heap node architecture, head reference preservation, array-to-linked-list conversion, length calculation, and element searching.	2026-10-03 14:56:36.91518+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Heap Node Architecture & Pointer Traversal: Introduction to Singly Linked List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nq7ok-OyEpg", "title": "Lecture 237: Introduction to Singly Linked List | Traversal & Search", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/introduction-to-linked-list/1", "title": "Array to Linked List", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Construct a singly linked list from a given 1D integer array and return its head."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/count-nodes-of-linked-list/1", "title": "Count Linked List Nodes", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Basic", "description": "Traverse a singly linked list and return its total number of nodes."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/search-in-linked-list-1664434326/1", "title": "Search in Linked List", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Check whether a given key exists in a singly linked list."}]	Module 36: Singly & Doubly Linked Lists: Memory Architecture, Pointer Mutations & Reversal	### 📌 Topic Overview\nUnlike arrays (which are stored in **contiguous** memory locations of fixed size), a **Linked List** is a linear data structure stored in **non-contiguous heap memory** that can dynamically grow or shrink at runtime. Each **Node** stores two things:\n1. **`data`**: The value stored in the node.\n2. **`next` (`Node*`)**: A pointer storing the memory address of the next node in the list (or `nullptr` at the tail).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Golden Rule of Linked Lists — NEVER Tamper With `head`!**\n  - If you move `head = head->next` during a traversal, you permanently lose the starting address of the linked list!\n  - Always copy `Node* temp = head;` and advance `temp = temp->next`!\n- **Converting an Array `arr` to a Linked List (`O(N)` Time):**\n  1. Create `Node* head = new Node(arr[0]);` and set `Node* mover = head;`.\n  2. Loop `i` from `1` to `n - 1`:\n     - Allocate `Node* temp = new Node(arr[i]);`.\n     - Link `mover->next = temp;`.\n     - Advance `mover = temp;` (or `mover = mover->next;`).\n  3. Return `head`.\n\n### 💻 Singly Linked List Struct, Conversion, Length & Search Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nclass Node {\npublic:\n    int data;\n    Node* next;\n\n    Node(int data1, Node* next1 = nullptr) {\n        data = data1;\n        next = next1;\n    }\n};\n\n// 1. Convert Array to Linked List: O(N) Time\nNode* convertArr2LL(const vector<int> &arr) {\n    if (arr.empty()) return nullptr;\n    Node* head = new Node(arr[0]);\n    Node* mover = head;\n    for (int i = 1; i < arr.size(); i++) {\n        Node* temp = new Node(arr[i]);\n        mover->next = temp;\n        mover = temp;\n    }\n    return head;\n}\n\n// 2. Length of Linked List: O(N) Time, O(1) Space\nint lengthOfLL(Node* head) {\n    int cnt = 0;\n    Node* temp = head; // Never move head directly!\n    while (temp != nullptr) {\n        cnt++;\n        temp = temp->next;\n    }\n    return cnt;\n}\n\n// 3. Search an Element in Linked List: O(N) Time\nbool checkIfPresent(Node* head, int val) {\n    Node* temp = head;\n    while (temp != nullptr) {\n        if (temp->data == val) return true;\n        temp = temp->next;\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Confusing `Node` Object With `Node*` Pointer:** `Node y = Node(2, nullptr)` creates a stack object, whereas `Node* y = new Node(2, nullptr)` allocates a node on the heap and returns its memory address pointer!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
