1364	Leftward Diving & In-Order Backtracking: Iterative Inorder Traversal of a Binary Tree	dsa-iterative-inorder-traversal-binary-tree	Traverse a binary tree in Left-Root-Right order iteratively by diving down left subtrees and backtracking up the stack.	2026-10-03 00:50:37.336383+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Leftward Diving & In-Order Backtracking: Iterative Inorder Traversal of a Binary Tree\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lxTGsVXjwvM", "title": "Lecture 82: Iterative Inorder Traversal of Binary Tree", "duration": "22m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/kth-smallest-element-in-a-bst/", "title": "Kth Smallest Element in a BST", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Stop the iterative inorder stack traversal early once k elements have been popped."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-search-tree-iterator/", "title": "Binary Search Tree Iterator", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Implement an iterator class using controlled iterative inorder stack progression."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/inorder-traversal-iterative/1", "title": "Inorder Traversal (Iterative)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Execute Left-Root-Right traversal using an explicit stack."}]	Module 15: Binary Trees: Hierarchical Representation & Fundamental Traversals	### 📌 Topic Overview\n**Iterative Inorder Traversal (`Left → Root → Right`)** requires visiting the leftmost leaf before printing any parent. We achieve this iteratively using an auxiliary stack and a current pointer `node = root`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Two-Phase State Loop:**\n  - **Phase 1 (Diving Left):** While `node != nullptr`, push `node` onto the stack and dive deeper left: `node = node->left`.\n  - **Phase 2 (Processing Root & Pivoting Right):** When `node == nullptr` (we have hit the bottom left), pop the top node from the stack, record its value, and pivot to its right subtree: `node = topNode->right`.\n- **Termination Condition:** Run while `node != nullptr || !st.empty()`. When both are false, the entire tree has been traversed.\n- **Complexity:** `O(N)` Time and `O(H)` Space (where `H` is tree height).\n\n### 💻 Iterative Inorder Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> iterativeInorder(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<int> ans;\n    stack<TreeNode*> st;\n    TreeNode* node = root;\n\n    while (true) {\n        if (node != nullptr) {\n            st.push(node);\n            node = node->left; // Keep diving left\n        } else {\n            if (st.empty()) break;\n            node = st.top();\n            st.pop();\n            ans.push_back(node->val); // Process root\n            node = node->right;       // Move right\n        }\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22231	Observer Design Pattern	lld-observer-design-pattern	Establish one-to-many event-driven architectures. Replace inefficient polling with Push notifications using Subjects (Observables) and Observers.	2026-10-07 17:07:23.574728+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Observer Design Pattern\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/Jpmp4GY8r3Q", "title": "Observer Design Pattern Explained | Real-Life Use Case + Code Example", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v12-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Polling vs Push Architectures", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Polling (Client-Driven):** The client runs a continuous timer, asking the server every 5 seconds, \\"Is there a new notification?\\" \\n*Inefficiency:* If a user receives 1 notification per day, the client makes ~17,000 useless API calls returning empty data, wasting massive mobile battery and server thread capacity.\\n\\n**Push / Observer (Server-Driven):** The client registers its interest once. It does nothing until the server explicitly \\"pushes\\" data downward when a state change occurs.\\n*Efficiency:* Zero wasted network calls. Resources are utilized exclusively when legitimate business events occur.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Contrast the \\"Polling\\" approach with the \\"Push\\" approach in event-driven systems. Why is Polling architecturally inefficient for a notification system?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v12-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Roles of Observable and Observer", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Observable (Subject):** The source of truth holding the state.\\n   * *Responsibility:* Maintains a private list/registry of interested observers and pushes alerts when state changes.\\n   * *Core Methods:* `addObserver(IObserver)`, `removeObserver(IObserver)`, and `notifyObservers()`.\\n\\n2. **Observer:** The dependent entity reacting to state changes.\\n   * *Responsibility:* Listens for triggers and executes localized business logic upon receiving an alert.\\n   * *Core Method:* `update()` (sometimes accepts the new state payload as a parameter: `update(Object payload)`).", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Define the primary responsibilities and the core interface methods required for the Observable (Subject) and Observer interfaces in the Observer Design Pattern.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v12-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Achieving Loose Coupling via Observer", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe Subject only depends on the abstract `IObserver` interface; it has absolutely zero knowledge of the concrete implementations (e.g., `EmailSubscriber`, `AppPushSubscriber`, `AnalyticsLogger`).\\n\\nWhen the `YouTubeChannel` class calls `notifyObservers()`, it blindly iterates through a `std::vector<IObserver*>` and calls `->update()`. \\nIf the engineering team adds a new `DiscordWebhookSubscriber`, the `YouTubeChannel` class is not modified or recompiled. The Subject is completely decoupled from the *side-effects* of its state changes.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain how the Observer Pattern achieves loose coupling between the Subject and its dependents. Why doesn't a YouTube Channel class need to know how emails are sent?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v12-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: State Fetching inside the Update Method", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThere are two primary paradigms:\\n1. **Push Paradigm:** The Subject passes the updated state payload directly into the method: `observer->update(newVideoData)`. This is highly decoupled but forces all Observers to accept the same payload structure.\\n2. **Pull Paradigm (Preferred in Complex LLD):** The Subject simply notifies that *something* changed: `observer->update()`. The Observer holds a reference back to the concrete Subject. Inside `update()`, the Observer proactively queries the Subject via getters: `videoTitle = channelRef->getLatestVideo()`. This allows different observers to fetch only the specific data they care about.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "When notifyObservers() triggers update() on an Observer, how does the Observer safely fetch the newly updated data from the Subject?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v12-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) Trade-offs", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The SRP Violation:**\\nA concrete `YouTubeChannel` class is now juggling two completely unrelated responsibilities:\\n1. Core Domain Logic: Uploading videos, managing metadata, deleting content.\\n2. Infrastructure Logic: Managing a subscription array, adding/removing observers, and firing network notifications.\\n\\n**The Trade-off:**\\nStrictly separating these into a `ChannelData` class and a `ChannelEventPublisher` class often creates unnecessary over-engineering and boilerplate for simple domains. In practical LLD, embedding the Observable mechanics directly into the Domain class is considered an acceptable compromise to keep the event-driven architecture lightweight and readable.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does a standard implementation of an Observable violate the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP), and what is the accepted architectural trade-off?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Polling Problem\n* **Polling:** An architecture where clients continuously ask a server, "Has your state changed? Has your state changed?"\n* **The Flaw:** Extremely inefficient. It wastes massive amounts of CPU cycles and network bandwidth responding with "No" 99% of the time.\n\n### 💡 2. The Observer Pattern (Push Mechanism)\n* **Definition:** Defines a one-to-many dependency between objects so that when one object changes state, all its dependents are notified and updated automatically.\n* **The Actors:**\n  * **Observable (Subject):** The entity generating data (e.g., a YouTube Channel). Maintains a list of subscribers.\n  * **Observer (Subscriber):** The entities waiting for data (e.g., YouTube Users).\n\n### 💡 3. Structural Mechanics\n* **Observable Interface:** Must implement `addObserver(IObserver o)`, `removeObserver(IObserver o)`, and `notifyObservers()`.\n* **Observer Interface:** Must implement `update()`.\n* **Execution Flow:** The Subject changes state $\\rightarrow$ Subject calls `notifyObservers()` $\\rightarrow$ Loop iterates through the internal list of Observers $\\rightarrow$ Calls `update()` on each Observer $\\rightarrow$ Observers autonomously fetch the new data.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	Observer Design Pattern	Establish one-to-many event-driven architectures. Replace inefficient polling with Push notifications using Subjects (Observables) and Observers.	28	Hard	Low Level Design	26	[{"id": "lld-v12-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Polling vs Push Architectures", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Polling (Client-Driven):** The client runs a continuous timer, asking the server every 5 seconds, \\"Is there a new notification?\\" \\n*Inefficiency:* If a user receives 1 notification per day, the client makes ~17,000 useless API calls returning empty data, wasting massive mobile battery and server thread capacity.\\n\\n**Push / Observer (Server-Driven):** The client registers its interest once. It does nothing until the server explicitly \\"pushes\\" data downward when a state change occurs.\\n*Efficiency:* Zero wasted network calls. Resources are utilized exclusively when legitimate business events occur.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Contrast the \\"Polling\\" approach with the \\"Push\\" approach in event-driven systems. Why is Polling architecturally inefficient for a notification system?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v12-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Roles of Observable and Observer", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Observable (Subject):** The source of truth holding the state.\\n   * *Responsibility:* Maintains a private list/registry of interested observers and pushes alerts when state changes.\\n   * *Core Methods:* `addObserver(IObserver)`, `removeObserver(IObserver)`, and `notifyObservers()`.\\n\\n2. **Observer:** The dependent entity reacting to state changes.\\n   * *Responsibility:* Listens for triggers and executes localized business logic upon receiving an alert.\\n   * *Core Method:* `update()` (sometimes accepts the new state payload as a parameter: `update(Object payload)`).", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Define the primary responsibilities and the core interface methods required for the Observable (Subject) and Observer interfaces in the Observer Design Pattern.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v12-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Achieving Loose Coupling via Observer", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe Subject only depends on the abstract `IObserver` interface; it has absolutely zero knowledge of the concrete implementations (e.g., `EmailSubscriber`, `AppPushSubscriber`, `AnalyticsLogger`).\\n\\nWhen the `YouTubeChannel` class calls `notifyObservers()`, it blindly iterates through a `std::vector<IObserver*>` and calls `->update()`. \\nIf the engineering team adds a new `DiscordWebhookSubscriber`, the `YouTubeChannel` class is not modified or recompiled. The Subject is completely decoupled from the *side-effects* of its state changes.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain how the Observer Pattern achieves loose coupling between the Subject and its dependents. Why doesn't a YouTube Channel class need to know how emails are sent?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v12-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: State Fetching inside the Update Method", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThere are two primary paradigms:\\n1. **Push Paradigm:** The Subject passes the updated state payload directly into the method: `observer->update(newVideoData)`. This is highly decoupled but forces all Observers to accept the same payload structure.\\n2. **Pull Paradigm (Preferred in Complex LLD):** The Subject simply notifies that *something* changed: `observer->update()`. The Observer holds a reference back to the concrete Subject. Inside `update()`, the Observer proactively queries the Subject via getters: `videoTitle = channelRef->getLatestVideo()`. This allows different observers to fetch only the specific data they care about.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "When notifyObservers() triggers update() on an Observer, how does the Observer safely fetch the newly updated data from the Subject?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v12-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) Trade-offs", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The SRP Violation:**\\nA concrete `YouTubeChannel` class is now juggling two completely unrelated responsibilities:\\n1. Core Domain Logic: Uploading videos, managing metadata, deleting content.\\n2. Infrastructure Logic: Managing a subscription array, adding/removing observers, and firing network notifications.\\n\\n**The Trade-off:**\\nStrictly separating these into a `ChannelData` class and a `ChannelEventPublisher` class often creates unnecessary over-engineering and boilerplate for simple domains. In practical LLD, embedding the Observable mechanics directly into the Domain class is considered an acceptable compromise to keep the event-driven architecture lightweight and readable.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does a standard implementation of an Observable violate the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP), and what is the accepted architectural trade-off?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
15096	Hash Map + Doubly Linked List Recency Queue: Implement LRU Cache in O(1)	dsa-implement-lru-cache-doubly-linked-list-hashmap	Master Hash Map + Doubly Linked List Recency Queue: Implement LRU Cache in O(1) with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:30:52.976675+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Hash Map + Doubly Linked List Recency Queue: Implement LRU Cache in O(1)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=z9bJUPxzFOw", "title": "Hash Map + Doubly Linked List Recency Queue: Implement LRU Cache in O(1) - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/lru-cache/", "title": "LeetCode 146: LRU Cache", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Design a data structure that follows the constraints of a Least Recently Used (LRU) cache where get and put run in O(1) average time."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/lfu-cache/", "title": "LeetCode 460: LFU Cache", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Extend LRU Cache to Least Frequently Used (LFU) eviction with LRU tie-breaking."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/lru-cache/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: LRU Cache", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Implement an LRU Cache supporting O(1) get(key) and set(key, value) operations."}]	Module 44: Advanced Stack, Monotonic Deque & Cache Design: Stock Span, Sliding Window Max, Celebrity & LRU/LFU Caches	### 📌 Topic Overview\nDesign a **Least Recently Used (LRU) Cache** of fixed `capacity` supporting:\n1. **`get(key)` (`O(1)`):** Return the value of `key` if present (and mark `key` as **most recently used**), else return `-1`.\n2. **`put(key, value)` (`O(1)`):** Update `key`'s value if present (and mark as most recently used), or insert `(key, value)`. If the cache is at full `capacity`, **evict the Least Recently Used key** first!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (The `unordered_map<int, Node*>` + Dummy-Head/Tail DLL Architecture)\n- **Why We Combine a Doubly Linked List With a Hash Map:**\n  - A **Hash Map (`mpp[key] = Node*`)** gives `O(1)` lookup directly to a node's memory address.\n  - A **Doubly Linked List (DLL)** with a **dummy `head`** and **dummy `tail`** lets us **`deleteNode(node)`** from anywhere and **`insertAfterHead(node)`** in **`O(1)` pointer operations**!\n  - **Ordering Invariant:**\n    - Nodes **right after `head` (`head->next`)** are **Most Recently Used (MRU)**.\n    - Nodes **right before `tail` (`tail->prev`)** are **Least Recently Used (LRU)**!\n- **Two Reusable Helper Functions (`deleteNode` and `insertAfterHead`):**\n  - On **`get(key)`**: If in `mpp`, grab `node = mpp[key]`, call `deleteNode(node)` and `insertAfterHead(node)` (reusing the exact same `Node*` without changing `mpp`!), and return `node->val`.\n  - On **`put(key, value)`**:\n    - If `key` already exists: update `node->val = value`, `deleteNode(node)`, and `insertAfterHead(node)`.\n    - If `key` is new and `mpp.size() == cap`: evict **`lruNode = tail->prev`** via `mpp.erase(lruNode->key)` and `deleteNode(lruNode)`, then insert the new node right after `head`!\n\n### 💻 Implement LRU Cache Blueprint\n```cpp\nclass LRUCache {\npublic:\n    class Node {\n    public:\n        int key;\n        int val;\n        Node* next;\n        Node* prev;\n        Node(int _key, int _val) : key(_key), val(_val), next(nullptr), prev(nullptr) {}\n    };\n\n    Node* head = new Node(-1, -1);\n    Node* tail = new Node(-1, -1);\n    int cap;\n    unordered_map<int, Node*> mpp;\n\n    LRUCache(int capacity) {\n        cap = capacity;\n        head->next = tail;\n        tail->prev = head;\n    }\n\n    void insertAfterHead(Node* currNode) {\n        Node* curAfterHead = head->next;\n        head->next = currNode;\n        currNode->next = curAfterHead;\n        currNode->prev = head;\n        curAfterHead->prev = currNode;\n    }\n\n    void deleteNode(Node* delNode) {\n        Node* delPrev = delNode->prev;\n        Node* delNext = delNode->next;\n        delPrev->next = delNext;\n        delNext->prev = delPrev;\n    }\n\n    int get(int key) {\n        if (mpp.find(key) == mpp.end()) return -1;\n\n        Node* resNode = mpp[key];\n        int res = resNode->val;\n        // Move accessed node to Most Recently Used position (right after head)\n        deleteNode(resNode);\n        insertAfterHead(resNode);\n        return res;\n    }\n\n    void put(int key, int value) {\n        if (mpp.find(key) != mpp.end()) {\n            Node* existingNode = mpp[key];\n            existingNode->val = value;\n            deleteNode(existingNode);\n            insertAfterHead(existingNode);\n            return;\n        }\n\n        if (mpp.size() == cap) {\n            // Evict Least Recently Used node (sitting right before dummy tail)\n            Node* lruNode = tail->prev;\n            mpp.erase(lruNode->key);\n            deleteNode(lruNode);\n        }\n\n        Node* newNode = new Node(key, value);\n        insertAfterHead(newNode);\n        mpp[key] = newNode;\n    }\n};\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Storing Only `val` (Not `key`) Inside `Node`:** When the cache is full and we evict `lruNode = tail->prev`, we must also delete its entry from the hash map via **`mpp.erase(lruNode->key)`**! Without storing `key` inside `Node`, you wouldn't know which key to erase from `mpp`!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22235	Adapter Design Pattern	lld-adapter-design-pattern	Connect incompatible interfaces without modifying their source code. Understand Target, Adapter, and Adaptee roles in Object and Class adapters.	2026-10-07 17:12:04.258699+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Adapter Design Pattern\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/FV3x69rpwm0", "title": "Adapter Design Pattern | Real-world use case + Code", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v16-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Core Intent of the Adapter Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe primary goal is to resolve interface incompatibility between two existing, independent systems without altering the source code of either system.\\n\\nIt is most frequently utilized when integrating **third-party libraries, vendor SDKs, or legacy systems**. If your application expects a specific interface (e.g., `ILogger`), but the new vendor SDK provides `VendorLogService` with different method signatures, the Adapter acts as a translation layer, keeping your core application immune to the vendor's specific API structure.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is the primary architectural goal of the Adapter Pattern, and in what common software scenario is it most frequently utilized?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v16-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Object Adapter vs Class Adapter", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Object Adapter (Composition):** The Adapter implements the Target interface and *contains* an instance of the Adaptee as a class member (Has-A). It delegates calls to this internal object.\\n2. **Class Adapter (Inheritance):** The Adapter achieves translation using Multiple Inheritance (in C++). It inherits *both* the Target interface AND the concrete Adaptee class.\\n\\n**Why Object Adapter is Preferred:**\\nClass Adapters tightly couple the adapter to a specific concrete Adaptee class, violating the principle of \\"Favor Composition over Inheritance\\". Furthermore, languages like Java and C# do not support multiple class inheritance, making Class Adapters impossible to implement. Object Adapters are highly flexible, as they can adapt not just the Adaptee, but any of the Adaptee's subclasses.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Compare \\"Object Adapter\\" and \\"Class Adapter\\". Why is the Object Adapter strictly preferred in modern object-oriented design?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v16-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Adapter vs Decorator", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Adapter (Translation):** Wraps an object to *change its interface*. The goal is compatibility. A square peg is wrapped to fit a round hole; no new behavior is added, only translation occurs.\\n* **Decorator (Enhancement):** Wraps an object to *add new behaviors or responsibilities*, but strictly *preserves the original interface*. If you decorate a `Coffee` object with `Milk`, it is still accessed via the exact same `ICoffee` interface.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Both the Adapter and Decorator patterns \\"wrap\\" an existing object. What is the fundamental difference in their design intent?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v16-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Payment Gateway Adapter Implementation", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. Create a wrapper class: `StripeAdapter : public IPaymentProcessor`.\\n2. Inject the Adaptee: Pass an instance of `StripeApi` into the `StripeAdapter` constructor via composition.\\n3. Implement the Target interface:\\n```cpp\\nvoid StripeAdapter::pay(double amount) override {\\n    // Translate app domain logic to Stripe-specific logic\\n    std::string token = fetchUserToken(); \\n    \\n    // Delegate to the Adaptee\\n    stripeApiInstance->chargeCard(token, amount);\\n}\\n```\\n4. The application orchestrator uses `IPaymentProcessor* p = new StripeAdapter(new StripeApi())`, shielding the checkout code from Stripe's specific implementation.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Your app uses an internal IPaymentProcessor interface with a method `pay(amount)`. A new Stripe SDK provides `StripeApi::chargeCard(token, amount)`. How do you implement the Adapter?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v16-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Two-Way Adapters", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA Two-Way Adapter implements *both* the Target interface and the Adaptee interface simultaneously.\\n\\nThis is used when an object needs to seamlessly participate in two entirely different systems. For example, a legacy system expects `OldLogger` objects, while a modern system expects `NewILogger` interfaces. A Two-Way Adapter implements both, translating calls in either direction, allowing the exact same adapter instance to be passed freely between the legacy codebase and the modern codebase.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is a \\"Two-Way Adapter\\" and in what rare architectural scenario would it be necessary?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Incompatibility Problem\n* You are building a modern analytics dashboard that expects data in JSON format (`IJSONReport`).\n* You purchase a legacy 3rd-party library that generates beautiful analytics, but its API only outputs XML format (`XMLDataProvider`).\n* Because it is a compiled 3rd-party library, you cannot open its source code to force it to return JSON.\n\n### 💡 2. The Adapter Solution\n* **Definition:** Converts the interface of a class into another interface that clients expect. Adapters let classes work together that couldn't otherwise because of incompatible interfaces.\n* **The Real-World Analogy:** You travel to Europe with a US laptop charger. You plug a Travel Adapter into the European wall socket (Adaptee), and plug your US charger (Client) into the Adapter (Target Interface).\n\n### 💡 3. Structural Roles\n* **Target:** The domain-specific interface that the client expects (`IJSONReport`).\n* **Adaptee:** The existing incompatible interface that needs adapting (`XMLDataProvider`).\n* **Adapter:** A wrapper class that implements the `Target` interface and holds a reference to the `Adaptee`. When the client calls `adapter.getJSON()`, the adapter intercepts it, calls `adaptee.getXML()`, translates the XML to JSON internally, and returns the JSON.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	Adapter Design Pattern	Connect incompatible interfaces without modifying their source code. Understand Target, Adapter, and Adaptee roles in Object and Class adapters.	22	Hard	Low Level Design	30	[{"id": "lld-v16-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Core Intent of the Adapter Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe primary goal is to resolve interface incompatibility between two existing, independent systems without altering the source code of either system.\\n\\nIt is most frequently utilized when integrating **third-party libraries, vendor SDKs, or legacy systems**. If your application expects a specific interface (e.g., `ILogger`), but the new vendor SDK provides `VendorLogService` with different method signatures, the Adapter acts as a translation layer, keeping your core application immune to the vendor's specific API structure.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is the primary architectural goal of the Adapter Pattern, and in what common software scenario is it most frequently utilized?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v16-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Object Adapter vs Class Adapter", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Object Adapter (Composition):** The Adapter implements the Target interface and *contains* an instance of the Adaptee as a class member (Has-A). It delegates calls to this internal object.\\n2. **Class Adapter (Inheritance):** The Adapter achieves translation using Multiple Inheritance (in C++). It inherits *both* the Target interface AND the concrete Adaptee class.\\n\\n**Why Object Adapter is Preferred:**\\nClass Adapters tightly couple the adapter to a specific concrete Adaptee class, violating the principle of \\"Favor Composition over Inheritance\\". Furthermore, languages like Java and C# do not support multiple class inheritance, making Class Adapters impossible to implement. Object Adapters are highly flexible, as they can adapt not just the Adaptee, but any of the Adaptee's subclasses.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Compare \\"Object Adapter\\" and \\"Class Adapter\\". Why is the Object Adapter strictly preferred in modern object-oriented design?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v16-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Adapter vs Decorator", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Adapter (Translation):** Wraps an object to *change its interface*. The goal is compatibility. A square peg is wrapped to fit a round hole; no new behavior is added, only translation occurs.\\n* **Decorator (Enhancement):** Wraps an object to *add new behaviors or responsibilities*, but strictly *preserves the original interface*. If you decorate a `Coffee` object with `Milk`, it is still accessed via the exact same `ICoffee` interface.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Both the Adapter and Decorator patterns \\"wrap\\" an existing object. What is the fundamental difference in their design intent?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v16-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Payment Gateway Adapter Implementation", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. Create a wrapper class: `StripeAdapter : public IPaymentProcessor`.\\n2. Inject the Adaptee: Pass an instance of `StripeApi` into the `StripeAdapter` constructor via composition.\\n3. Implement the Target interface:\\n```cpp\\nvoid StripeAdapter::pay(double amount) override {\\n    // Translate app domain logic to Stripe-specific logic\\n    std::string token = fetchUserToken(); \\n    \\n    // Delegate to the Adaptee\\n    stripeApiInstance->chargeCard(token, amount);\\n}\\n```\\n4. The application orchestrator uses `IPaymentProcessor* p = new StripeAdapter(new StripeApi())`, shielding the checkout code from Stripe's specific implementation.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Your app uses an internal IPaymentProcessor interface with a method `pay(amount)`. A new Stripe SDK provides `StripeApi::chargeCard(token, amount)`. How do you implement the Adapter?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v16-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Two-Way Adapters", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA Two-Way Adapter implements *both* the Target interface and the Adaptee interface simultaneously.\\n\\nThis is used when an object needs to seamlessly participate in two entirely different systems. For example, a legacy system expects `OldLogger` objects, while a modern system expects `NewILogger` interfaces. A Two-Way Adapter implements both, translating calls in either direction, allowing the exact same adapter instance to be passed freely between the legacy codebase and the modern codebase.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is a \\"Two-Way Adapter\\" and in what rare architectural scenario would it be necessary?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
