: Semantic Developer Portfolio & Resume	Production-grade single-page resume built using pure HTML structures, data tables, and internal bookmark navigation.	34	Easy	HTML Projects	7	[]	\N	34	https://youtu.be/uNcMKFkAKuw	Capstone Project: Semantic Developer Portfolio & Resume
12422	Exact-Count Subtraction Trick (f(goal) - f(goal - 1)): Binary Subarrays With Sum	dsa-binary-subarrays-with-sum-sliding-window	Master Exact-Count Subtraction Trick (f(goal) - f(goal - 1)): Binary Subarrays With Sum with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:15:39.087859+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Exact-Count Subtraction Trick (f(goal) - f(goal - 1)): Binary Subarrays With Sum\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XnMdNUkX6VM", "title": "Exact-Count Subtraction Trick (f(goal) - f(goal - 1)): Binary Subarrays With Sum - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-subarrays-with-sum/", "title": "LeetCode 930: Binary Subarrays With Sum", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given a binary array nums and an integer goal, return the number of non-empty subarrays with a sum equal to goal in O(1) extra space."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/count-number-of-nice-subarrays/", "title": "LeetCode 1248: Count Number of Nice Subarrays", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Treat odd numbers as 1 and even numbers as 0 to reduce Nice Subarrays directly to Binary Subarrays With Sum!"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/subarrays-with-k-different-integers/", "title": "LeetCode 992: Subarrays with K Different Integers", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Apply the exact same atMost(k) - atMost(k - 1) subtraction pattern to count subarrays with k distinct integers."}]	Module 40: Sliding Window & Two-Pointer Algorithms: Fixed Windows, Variable Windows & Exact-Count Reductions	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a **binary array `nums`** (`0`s and `1`s) and an integer `goal`, return the **number of non-empty subarrays** with a sum equal to `goal` in **`O(N)` time and `O(1)` extra space** (eliminating the `O(N)` hash map used in general Prefix Sum).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Pattern 3: atMost(goal) - atMost(goal - 1))\n- **Why Standard Sliding Window Struggles With `sum == goal` Directly:**\n  - Because `nums` contains `0`s, shrinking `l++` or expanding `r++` across `0`s does not change `sum`, creating ambiguity about whether to move `l` or `r` first!\n- **The Exact-to-At-Most Reduction (`f(goal) - f(goal - 1)`):**\n  - While counting `sum == goal` directly is tricky, counting subarrays where **`sum <= goal`** (`atMost(nums, goal)`) is super easy!\n  - In `atMost(nums, goal)`:\n    1. **Crucial Base Case:** **`if (goal < 0) return 0;`** (when `goal == 0`, calling `atMost(nums, goal - 1)` passes `-1`; since a binary array cannot have a negative sum, it must immediately return `0`!).\n    2. Expand `r`: `sum += nums[r]`.\n    3. Shrink `while (sum > goal)`: `sum -= nums[l]; l++;`.\n    4. Every subarray ending at `r` and starting anywhere in `[l ... r]` now has `sum <= goal`—so add **`cnt += (r - l + 1)`**!\n  - Finally, subtract subarrays with `sum <= goal - 1` from subarrays with `sum <= goal`:\n    **`return atMost(nums, goal) - atMost(nums, goal - 1);`**!\n- **Complexity:** **`O(2 * 2N) = O(N)`** Time and **`O(1)`** Space!\n\n### 💻 Binary Subarrays With Sum (O(1) Space Sliding Window) Blueprint\n```cpp\nint countAtMost(const vector<int> &nums, int goal) {\n    // Essential edge case when goal == 0 and we call countAtMost(nums, goal - 1 = -1)!\n    if (goal < 0) return 0;\n\n    int l = 0, r = 0, sum = 0, cnt = 0;\n    while (r < nums.size()) {\n        sum += nums[r];\n\n        while (sum > goal) {\n            sum -= nums[l];\n            l++;\n        }\n\n        // All (r - l + 1) subarrays ending at r within [l .. r] have sum <= goal!\n        cnt += (r - l + 1);\n        r++;\n    }\n    return cnt;\n}\n\nint numSubarraysWithSum(vector<int> &nums, int goal) {\n    return countAtMost(nums, goal) - countAtMost(nums, goal - 1);\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Omitting `if (goal < 0) return 0;` at the Top of `countAtMost`:** When `goal = 0`, `countAtMost(nums, goal - 1)` passes `goal = -1`. Without `if (goal < 0) return 0;`, `while (sum > -1)` will push `l` past `r` (`l = r + 1`) and corrupt the count!\n- **Applying This Trick to Arrays With Negative Numbers:** The `atMost(goal)` sliding window relies on monotonicity (moving `r++` never decreases `sum`, and moving `l++` never increases `sum`). It works for non-negative arrays (`>= 0`), whereas arrays with **negative numbers** require the Prefix Sum + Hash Map (`O(N)` space) approach!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22238	Composite Design Pattern	lld-composite-design-pattern	Compose objects into tree structures to represent part-whole hierarchies. Treat individual objects (Leaves) and groupings (Composites) uniformly.	2026-10-07 17:12:04.258699+05:30	### 🎯 Computer Science Fundamentals: Composite Design Pattern\nCore systems concepts frequently tested in technical screening rounds and engineering interviews.\n\n- **System Architecture**: How modern operating systems, networks, and processors coordinate resources.\n- **Key Concepts to Master**: Concurrency primitives, resource isolation, latency vs throughput tradeoffs, and fault tolerance.\n- **Interview Key Takeaways**:\n  - Be prepared to draw diagrams explaining data flow and component lifecycles.\n  - Explain practical real-world applications (e.g. why databases use WAL, why HTTP/2 uses multiplexing).	[{"url": "https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/", "title": "OS Three Easy Pieces Guide"}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/operating-systems/", "title": "GeeksforGeeks CS Fundamentals"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/xaaiMGmyDJk", "title": "Composite Design Pattern | Build File System (UML & Code) | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v19-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Part-Whole Hierarchies and Uniformity", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA part-whole hierarchy is a tree structure where complex objects (Composites/Wholes) are constructed by grouping smaller objects (Leaves/Parts). Examples include File Systems, UI DOM Trees, or Organization Charts.\\n\\nThe Composite Pattern achieves uniformity by forcing both the Leaf and the Composite to implement the exact same base interface (e.g., `IComponent`). \\nWhen a client calls `component->render()`, it doesn't need `if-else` type-checking. If the object is a single UI Button (Leaf), it draws itself. If it is a massive UI Panel (Composite), it draws its borders and blindly forwards the `render()` call to all its children.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is a \\"part-whole hierarchy\\", and how does the Composite Pattern allow clients to treat both the \\"part\\" (Leaf) and the \\"whole\\" (Composite) uniformly?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v19-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Recursive Delegation in Composite Operations", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. Client calls `root->getSize()`.\\n2. `Root` (Composite) initializes `total = 0`. It iterates over its two children (File1, SubFolder).\\n3. `Root` calls `getSize()` on File1 (Leaf). File1 immediately returns `5`. `total` becomes `5`.\\n4. `Root` calls `getSize()` on SubFolder (Composite).\\n5. `SubFolder` initializes `sub_total = 0`. It iterates over its two children (File2, File3).\\n6. `SubFolder` calls `getSize()` on File2. It returns `2`. `sub_total = 2`.\\n7. `SubFolder` calls `getSize()` on File3. It returns `2`. `sub_total = 4`.\\n8. `SubFolder` returns its `sub_total` (`4`) back up to `Root`.\\n9. `Root` adds `4` to its `total`. `Root` returns `9MB` to the Client.\\nThe recursion handled the tree traversal seamlessly without type inspection.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Walk through the recursive execution stack when getSize() is called on a Root Folder containing one 5MB File and one Sub-Folder containing two 2MB Files.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v19-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Interface Segregation vs Uniformity Trade-off", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Trade-off (Transparency vs Safety):**\\n1. **Uniformity/Transparency (Placing `add()` in the base interface):** The client treats all components identically. However, a `File` (Leaf) cannot logically hold children. It is forced to implement `add()` by throwing an `UnsupportedOperationException`. This violates ISP, as Leaves are forced to implement methods they cannot use.\\n2. **Safety (Placing `add()` only in Composite):** ISP is preserved. However, uniformity is destroyed. The client must now downcast and type-check: `if (dynamic_cast<Folder*>(comp)) folder->add(...)` before calling the method.\\nMost standard Composite implementations favor Transparency, accepting the ISP violation to maintain clean, polymorphic recursive trees.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the Composite Pattern, placing add(child) and remove(child) methods in the base interface violates the Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) for Leaf nodes. Explain this trade-off.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v19-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Validating Directory Change (cd) Operations", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nYou cannot `cd` into a text file. \\nBecause the Composite pattern treats Files and Folders uniformly via `IFileSystemComponent*`, a client executing `cd(\\"target\\")` iterates through the current directory's children to find a matching name.\\nIf it finds a match, it must verify the node is traversable before returning it as the new working directory. Exposing a simple `virtual bool isFolder() const = 0;` in the base interface (returning `false` for File, `true` for Folder) provides a clean, polymorphic way to validate structural traversal operations without resorting to brittle RTTI/dynamic casting.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "When implementing a change directory \\"cd\\" command in the Composite File System, why is an \\"isFolder()\\" boolean flag necessary in the base interface?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v19-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Real-World Use Cases for Composite Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **UI Component Frameworks (DOM / React):** A web page consists of primitive UI elements (Text, Button - Leaves) and layout containers (Div, Form - Composites). Calling `render()` on the root `Div` recursively renders the entire nested HTML DOM tree.\\n2. **Corporate Organizational Charts & Salary Calculators:** An `IEmployee` interface. A standard Engineer is a Leaf. A Manager is a Composite holding a list of subordinates. Calling `calculateBonusBudget()` on the CEO recursively cascades down the hierarchy, summing the budget for every branch and leaf in the corporate structure.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Identify two real-world enterprise software scenarios (excluding File Systems) where the Composite Pattern is the optimal architectural choice.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Hierarchical Tree Problem\n* Operating Systems possess File Systems where Folders contain Files, and Folders contain sub-Folders, recursively.\n* If a `Folder` treats a `File` and a `SubFolder` as two distinct data types, operations like calculating the total size of a `Folder` require massive, ugly `if-else` type-checking blocks to recursively traverse the children.\n\n### 💡 2. The Composite Solution\n* **Definition:** Compose objects into tree structures to represent part-whole hierarchies. Composite lets clients treat individual objects and compositions of objects uniformly.\n* Both `File` (Leaf) and `Folder` (Composite) inherit from a common `IFileSystemComponent` interface.\n\n### 💡 3. Uniform Execution\n* The `IFileSystemComponent` enforces a `getSize()` method.\n* **Leaf (File):** `getSize()` simply returns its own static file size (e.g., 5 MB).\n* **Composite (Folder):** `getSize()` iterates over its internal `std::vector<IFileSystemComponent*>` and calls `child->getSize()` on every item, summing the results.\n* The calling client (or parent folder) simply triggers `getSize()` blindly. It doesn't need to know if the child is a Leaf or a massive nested directory. The tree resolves itself recursively.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	Composite Design Pattern	Compose objects into tree structures to represent part-whole hierarchies. Treat individual objects (Leaves) and groupings (Composites) uniformly.	38	Hard	Low Level Design	33	[{"id": "lld-v19-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Part-Whole Hierarchies and Uniformity", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA part-whole hierarchy is a tree structure where complex objects (Composites/Wholes) are constructed by grouping smaller objects (Leaves/Parts). Examples include File Systems, UI DOM Trees, or Organization Charts.\\n\\nThe Composite Pattern achieves uniformity by forcing both the Leaf and the Composite to implement the exact same base interface (e.g., `IComponent`). \\nWhen a client calls `component->render()`, it doesn't need `if-else` type-checking. If the object is a single UI Button (Leaf), it draws itself. If it is a massive UI Panel (Composite), it draws its borders and blindly forwards the `render()` call to all its children.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is a \\"part-whole hierarchy\\", and how does the Composite Pattern allow clients to treat both the \\"part\\" (Leaf) and the \\"whole\\" (Composite) uniformly?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v19-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Recursive Delegation in Composite Operations", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. Client calls `root->getSize()`.\\n2. `Root` (Composite) initializes `total = 0`. It iterates over its two children (File1, SubFolder).\\n3. `Root` calls `getSize()` on File1 (Leaf). File1 immediately returns `5`. `total` becomes `5`.\\n4. `Root` calls `getSize()` on SubFolder (Composite).\\n5. `SubFolder` initializes `sub_total = 0`. It iterates over its two children (File2, File3).\\n6. `SubFolder` calls `getSize()` on File2. It returns `2`. `sub_total = 2`.\\n7. `SubFolder` calls `getSize()` on File3. It returns `2`. `sub_total = 4`.\\n8. `SubFolder` returns its `sub_total` (`4`) back up to `Root`.\\n9. `Root` adds `4` to its `total`. `Root` returns `9MB` to the Client.\\nThe recursion handled the tree traversal seamlessly without type inspection.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Walk through the recursive execution stack when getSize() is called on a Root Folder containing one 5MB File and one Sub-Folder containing two 2MB Files.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v19-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Interface Segregation vs Uniformity Trade-off", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Trade-off (Transparency vs Safety):**\\n1. **Uniformity/Transparency (Placing `add()` in the base interface):** The client treats all components identically. However, a `File` (Leaf) cannot logically hold children. It is forced to implement `add()` by throwing an `UnsupportedOperationException`. This violates ISP, as Leaves are forced to implement methods they cannot use.\\n2. **Safety (Placing `add()` only in Composite):** ISP is preserved. However, uniformity is destroyed. The client must now downcast and type-check: `if (dynamic_cast<Folder*>(comp)) folder->add(...)` before calling the method.\\nMost standard Composite implementations favor Transparency, accepting the ISP violation to maintain clean, polymorphic recursive trees.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the Composite Pattern, placing add(child) and remove(child) methods in the base interface violates the Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) for Leaf nodes. Explain this trade-off.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v19-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Validating Directory Change (cd) Operations", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nYou cannot `cd` into a text file. \\nBecause the Composite pattern treats Files and Folders uniformly via `IFileSystemComponent*`, a client executing `cd(\\"target\\")` iterates through the current directory's children to find a matching name.\\nIf it finds a match, it must verify the node is traversable before returning it as the new working directory. Exposing a simple `virtual bool isFolder() const = 0;` in the base interface (returning `false` for File, `true` for Folder) provides a clean, polymorphic way to validate structural traversal operations without resorting to brittle RTTI/dynamic casting.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "When implementing a change directory \\"cd\\" command in the Composite File System, why is an \\"isFolder()\\" boolean flag necessary in the base interface?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v19-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Real-World Use Cases for Composite Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **UI Component Frameworks (DOM / React):** A web page consists of primitive UI elements (Text, Button - Leaves) and layout containers (Div, Form - Composites). Calling `render()` on the root `Div` recursively renders the entire nested HTML DOM tree.\\n2. **Corporate Organizational Charts & Salary Calculators:** An `IEmployee` interface. A standard Engineer is a Leaf. A Manager is a Composite holding a list of subordinates. Calling `calculateBonusBudget()` on the CEO recursively cascades down the hierarchy, summing the budget for every branch and leaf in the corporate structure.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Identify two real-world enterprise software scenarios (excluding File Systems) where the Composite Pattern is the optimal architectural choice.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
12416	Last-Seen Index Jump: Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters	dsa-longest-substring-without-repeating-characters	Master Last-Seen Index Jump: Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:15:39.079443+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Last-Seen Index Jump: Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-zSxTJkcdAo", "title": "Last-Seen Index Jump: Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-substring-without-repeating-characters/", "title": "LeetCode 3: Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given a string s, find the length of the longest substring without repeating characters."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-erasure-value/", "title": "LeetCode 1695: Maximum Erasure Value", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the contiguous subarray of unique elements with the maximum sum."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/longest-distinct-characters-in-string5848/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Longest Distinct characters in string", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Find the length of the longest substring with all distinct characters."}]	Module 40: Sliding Window & Two-Pointer Algorithms: Fixed Windows, Variable Windows & Exact-Count Reductions	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a string `s` (which may contain lowercase letters, uppercase letters, digits, symbols, or spaces—any of the `256` ASCII characters), find the length of the **longest substring without repeating characters**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute O(N²) → Optimal Direct Jump O(N))\n- **Why Storing the Last-Seen Index (`hash[256]`) Beats Incrementing `l++` One by One:**\n  - Initialize an array `hash(256, -1)` storing the **last index where each ASCII character was seen**.\n  - Maintain window `[l ... r]` starting at `l = 0, r = 0`.\n  - When we are at `s[r]`, check if `s[r]` was seen previously (`hash[s[r]] != -1`):\n    - **The Crucial Boundary Guard (`if (hash[s[r]] >= l)`):**\n      - What if `s[r]` was seen at index `1`, but our left pointer `l` has **already moved ahead to index `4`**?\n      - Then that old occurrence at index `1` is **outside our current window `[l ... r]`** and is NOT a duplicate!\n      - Only if **`hash[s[r]] >= l`** (the previous occurrence is *inside* our active window `[l ... r]`), we jump `l` directly past that duplicate: **`l = hash[s[r]] + 1`**!\n  - Update `maxLen = max(maxLen, r - l + 1)`, record **`hash[s[r]] = r`**, and advance `r++`.\n- **Complexity:** **`O(N)`** Single-Pass Time (no inner loop!) and **`O(256) = O(1)`** Space.\n\n### 💻 Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters Blueprint\n```cpp\nint lengthOfLongestSubstring(string s) {\n    int n = s.size();\n    // Stores the last seen index of each of the 256 ASCII characters (-1 if unseen)\n    vector<int> hash(256, -1);\n\n    int l = 0, r = 0, maxLen = 0;\n\n    while (r < n) {\n        // If s[r] was seen before AND its last occurrence is inside the current window [l .. r]\n        if (hash[s[r]] != -1) {\n            if (hash[s[r]] >= l) {\n                l = hash[s[r]] + 1; // Jump l directly past the previous occurrence!\n            }\n        }\n\n        int len = r - l + 1;\n        maxLen = max(maxLen, len);\n\n        // Update the last seen index of s[r] to current index r\n        hash[s[r]] = r;\n        r++;\n    }\n    return maxLen;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Jumping `l = hash[s[r]] + 1` Without Checking `if (hash[s[r]] >= l)`:** On `"abba"`, when `r = 2` (`'b'`), `l` moves to `2`. Then when `r = 3` (`'a'`), `hash['a']` is `0`. Without `if (hash[s[r]] >= l)`, `l` jumps **backward** from `2` to `0 + 1 = 1`, falsely including `"bb"` inside the window!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22247	Builder Design Pattern & Step Builder	lld-builder-design-pattern	Construct complex objects cleanly. Eliminate Constructor Telescoping, ensure immutability, and enforce mandatory step-by-step sequential construction via Interface Chaining.	2026-10-07 17:16:35.515424+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Builder Design Pattern & Step Builder\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/G4Ntl9KzIxY", "title": "Builder Design Pattern | Classic Builder | Step Builder | UML + Code | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v28-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: The Constructor Telescoping Problem", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nConstructor Telescoping occurs when a class has multiple optional fields, forcing the developer to create a cascading stack of overloaded constructors:\\n`Pizza(size)`, `Pizza(size, cheese)`, `Pizza(size, cheese, pepperoni)`, etc.\\n\\nAs fields increase, the permutations explode. Furthermore, a call like `new HttpRequest(\\"url\\", null, null, \\"body\\")` is impossible to read or maintain. \\nThe Builder Pattern solves this by offering named, fluent methods (`builder.withUrl(\\"...\\").withBody(\\"...\\").build()`), providing perfect readability and eliminating the need for overloaded constructors.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is the \\"Constructor Telescoping\\" anti-pattern, and why does building complex objects with multiple optional parameters necessitate the Builder Pattern?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v28-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Immutability and State Consistency", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Immutability:** By using a Builder, the target object (e.g., `HttpRequest`) only exposes a private constructor accepting the Builder, and provides ZERO `set()` methods. Once `build()` is executed, the resulting object is 100% immutable and thread-safe.\\n\\n**Inconsistent State:** If using raw setters, a developer might call `request.execute()` before calling `request.setUrl()`, crashing the app at runtime. The Builder aggregates all parameters internally. The terminal `build()` method acts as a massive validation gate: `if (url == null) throw Exception;`. It guarantees the object is never instantiated into RAM unless its state is perfectly legal and complete.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Builder pattern guarantee object Immutability and prevent the \\"Inconsistent State\\" runtime crash?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v28-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: The Step Builder Pattern (Interface Chaining)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe Step Builder prevents developers from skipping mandatory fields by chaining return types:\\n1. Define interfaces: `IUrlStep`, `IMethodStep`, `IBuildStep`.\\n2. `IUrlStep` defines `IMethodStep* withUrl(string)`.\\n3. `IMethodStep` defines `IBuildStep* withMethod(string)`.\\n4. `IBuildStep` defines `HttpRequest* build()`.\\n\\nWhen a developer types `Builder::start()`, the compiler returns an `IUrlStep`. The IDE auto-complete will ONLY allow `.withUrl()`. Once invoked, the compiler returns `IMethodStep`. The developer is structurally forced to provide parameters in the exact, mandatory sequential order, eliminating runtime validation errors.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the internal mechanics of the Step Builder pattern. How does it leverage sequential interfaces to force compile-time parameter ordering?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v28-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: The Director Class in Builder Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWhile the Builder defines *how* to construct the object, the Director dictates *what* to construct by orchestrating the builder's methods into predefined templates.\\n\\n*Scenario:* If your application frequently sends standard JSON POST requests, you don't want developers manually typing `withMethod(\\"POST\\").withHeader(\\"Content-Type\\", \\"application/json\\")` 500 times across the codebase.\\nThe `HttpRequestDirector` provides a static method `createJsonPostRequest(url, body)`. Internally, the Director takes the Builder, wires up the standard JSON headers and POST method, and returns the pre-configured object. It guarantees structural consistency for common object variants.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is the role of the \\"Director\\" class in conjunction with a Builder? Provide a scenario where pre-configured templates are useful.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v28-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Fluent Interfaces and \\"return this\\"", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA Fluent Interface is an object-oriented API that relies on extensive method chaining to create highly readable, prose-like code (`object.doA().doB().doC()`).\\n\\nTo achieve this, every intermediate setup method in a Builder (like `withUrl()` or `withBody()`) must execute its logic and then end with `return this;` (in C++/Java). By returning a pointer/reference to the exact same Builder instance in memory, the caller can instantly chain the next method invocation onto the result, accumulating state inside the Builder seamlessly until `build()` is finally called.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is a \\"Fluent Interface\\", and why is the statement \\"return this;\\" (or returning a self-reference) structurally mandatory for a classic Builder class?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Anti-Patterns: Telescoping & Mutability\n* **Constructor Telescoping:** An `HttpRequest` needs a URL, Method, Headers, Body, and Timeout. Creating constructors for every permutation (`Http(url)`, `Http(url, method)`, `Http(url, method, headers)`) creates an ugly, unmaintainable mess.\n* **The Setter Flaw:** Using an empty constructor and `setBody()` creates a **Mutable Object** (prone to thread-safety bugs) and risks an **Inconsistent State** (a developer might call `execute()` before remembering to set the mandatory URL).\n\n### 💡 2. The Classic Builder\n* Extracts object construction into a dedicated `HttpRequestBuilder` class.\n* Provides fluent methods (`withUrl()`, `withMethod()`) that return `this` for chaining.\n* The terminal `build()` method executes final state validation and returns the fully constructed, immutable `HttpRequest` object.\n\n### 💡 3. The Step Builder (Sequential Enforcement)\n* **The Problem:** A classic builder allows you to call `withBody()` before `withUrl()`, or forget `withUrl()` entirely.\n* **The Solution:** Use Interface Chaining. `withUrl()` returns an `IMethodStep` interface. `withMethod()` returns an `IOptionalStep` interface.\n* **The Result:** The IDE compiler physically forces the developer to declare the URL first, then the Method, completely eliminating runtime state errors by elevating them to compile-time guarantees.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	Builder Design Pattern & Step Builder	Construct complex objects cleanly. Eliminate Constructor Telescoping, ensure immutability, and enforce mandatory step-by-step sequential construction via Interface Chaining.	109	Hard	Low Level Design	42	[{"id": "lld-v28-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: The Constructor Telescoping Problem", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nConstructor Telescoping occurs when a class has multiple optional fields, forcing the developer to create a cascading stack of overloaded constructors:\\n`Pizza(size)`, `Pizza(size, cheese)`, `Pizza(size, cheese, pepperoni)`, etc.\\n\\nAs fields increase, the permutations explode. Furthermore, a call like `new HttpRequest(\\"url\\", null, null, \\"body\\")` is impossible to read or maintain. \\nThe Builder Pattern solves this by offering named, fluent methods (`builder.withUrl(\\"...\\").withBody(\\"...\\").build()`), providing perfect readability and eliminating the need for overloaded constructors.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is the \\"Constructor Telescoping\\" anti-pattern, and why does building complex objects with multiple optional parameters necessitate the Builder Pattern?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v28-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Immutability and State Consistency", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Immutability:** By using a Builder, the target object (e.g., `HttpRequest`) only exposes a private constructor accepting the Builder, and provides ZERO `set()` methods. Once `build()` is executed, the resulting object is 100% immutable and thread-safe.\\n\\n**Inconsistent State:** If using raw setters, a developer might call `request.execute()` before calling `request.setUrl()`, crashing the app at runtime. The Builder aggregates all parameters internally. The terminal `build()` method acts as a massive validation gate: `if (url == null) throw Exception;`. It guarantees the object is never instantiated into RAM unless its state is perfectly legal and complete.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Builder pattern guarantee object Immutability and prevent the \\"Inconsistent State\\" runtime crash?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v28-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: The Step Builder Pattern (Interface Chaining)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe Step Builder prevents developers from skipping mandatory fields by chaining return types:\\n1. Define interfaces: `IUrlStep`, `IMethodStep`, `IBuildStep`.\\n2. `IUrlStep` defines `IMethodStep* withUrl(string)`.\\n3. `IMethodStep` defines `IBuildStep* withMethod(string)`.\\n4. `IBuildStep` defines `HttpRequest* build()`.\\n\\nWhen a developer types `Builder::start()`, the compiler returns an `IUrlStep`. The IDE auto-complete will ONLY allow `.withUrl()`. Once invoked, the compiler returns `IMethodStep`. The developer is structurally forced to provide parameters in the exact, mandatory sequential order, eliminating runtime validation errors.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the internal mechanics of the Step Builder pattern. How does it leverage sequential interfaces to force compile-time parameter ordering?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v28-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: The Director Class in Builder Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWhile the Builder defines *how* to construct the object, the Director dictates *what* to construct by orchestrating the builder's methods into predefined templates.\\n\\n*Scenario:* If your application frequently sends standard JSON POST requests, you don't want developers manually typing `withMethod(\\"POST\\").withHeader(\\"Content-Type\\", \\"application/json\\")` 500 times across the codebase.\\nThe `HttpRequestDirector` provides a static method `createJsonPostRequest(url, body)`. Internally, the Director takes the Builder, wires up the standard JSON headers and POST method, and returns the pre-configured object. It guarantees structural consistency for common object variants.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is the role of the \\"Director\\" class in conjunction with a Builder? Provide a scenario where pre-configured templates are useful.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v28-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Fluent Interfaces and \\"return this\\"", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA Fluent Interface is an object-oriented API that relies on extensive method chaining to create highly readable, prose-like code (`object.doA().doB().doC()`).\\n\\nTo achieve this, every intermediate setup method in a Builder (like `withUrl()` or `withBody()`) must execute its logic and then end with `return this;` (in C++/Java). By returning a pointer/reference to the exact same Builder instance in memory, the caller can instantly chain the next method invocation onto the result, accumulating state inside the Builder seamlessly until `build()` is finally called.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is a \\"Fluent Interface\\", and why is the statement \\"return this;\\" (or returning a self-reference) structurally mandatory for a classic Builder class?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
5249	Component-Size Aggregation: Making a Large Island Using Disjoint Set	dsa-making-a-large-island-dsu	Aggregate component sizes using DSU unionBySize and evaluate all zero-cells by summing unique neighboring island sizes.	2026-10-03 11:10:35.798828+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Component-Size Aggregation: Making a Large Island Using Disjoint Set\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lgiz0Oup6gM", "title": "Lecture 177: Making a Large Island Using Disjoint Set", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/making-a-large-island/", "title": "Making A Large Island (LC 827)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "You are allowed to change at most one 0 to be 1. Return the size of the largest island in grid after applying this operation."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/maximum-connected-group/1", "title": "Maximum Connected group", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find the maximum size of a connected group of 1s after flipping at most one 0 to 1."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/max-area-of-island/", "title": "Max Area of Island (LC 695)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Compute the maximum island size when no 0-to-1 flip is performed."}]	Module 26: Disjoint Set Union (DSU), Kruskal's MST & Dynamic Connectivity	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an `n x n` binary grid, you are allowed to flip **at most one `0` into a `1`**. Return the size of the **largest island** of `1`s possible after this operation.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why `unionBySize` Shines Here:**\n  - `DisjointSet` with `unionBySize` automatically stores the exact cell count of every island component at its ultimate parent `ds.size[ds.findUPar(node)]`!\n- **Two-Step Algorithm (`O(N²)` Time, `O(N²)` Space):**\n  1. **Step 1 (Connect Existing `1`s):**\n     - Traverse the `n x n` grid. For every cell `(r, c)` where `grid[r][c] == 1`, union `r * n + c` with all valid 4-directional neighbors that are also `1`.\n  2. **Step 2 (Try Flipping Each `0` to `1`):**\n     - For every cell `(r, c)` where `grid[r][c] == 0`:\n       - Create a `set<int> components` and insert the **ultimate parent** `ds.findUPar(adjR * n + adjC)` of all valid 4-directional neighbors that are `1`.\n       - Why use a `set` of ultimate parents? Because two neighbors (e.g., Up and Right) might belong to the **exact same island**, and we must not double-count that island's size!\n       - Sum `sizeTotal = 1` (for the flipped cell itself) `+ sum(ds.size[it])` for each unique parent `it` in `components`, and update `mx = max(mx, sizeTotal)`.\n  3. **All-1s Edge Case:** Also check `mx = max(mx, ds.size[ds.findUPar(i)])` across all cells in case the grid contains zero `0`s!\n\n### 💻 Making a Large Island (DSU) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint largestIsland(vector<vector<int>> &grid) {\n    int n = grid.size();\n    DisjointSet ds(n * n);\n    int dr[] = {-1, 0, 1, 0}, dc[] = {0, 1, 0, -1};\n\n    // Step 1: Connect all existing 1s in DSU\n    for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {\n            if (grid[r][c] == 0) continue;\n            for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n                int nr = r + dr[i], nc = c + dc[i];\n                if (nr >= 0 && nr < n && nc >= 0 && nc < n && grid[nr][nc] == 1) {\n                    ds.unionBySize(r * n + c, nr * n + nc);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Step 2: Try converting each 0 to 1 and sum unique neighboring component sizes\n    int mx = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {\n            if (grid[r][c] == 1) continue;\n            set<int> uniqueParents;\n            for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n                int nr = r + dr[i], nc = c + dc[i];\n                if (nr >= 0 && nr < n && nc >= 0 && nc < n && grid[nr][nc] == 1) {\n                    uniqueParents.insert(ds.findUPar(nr * n + nc));\n                }\n            }\n            int sizeTotal = 1; // +1 for flipping current (r, c) from 0 to 1\n            for (int ulp : uniqueParents) {\n                sizeTotal += ds.getSize(ulp); // ds.size[ulp]\n            }\n            mx = max(mx, sizeTotal);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Step 3: Handle edge case where the entire grid is already 1s\n    for (int cellNo = 0; cellNo < n * n; cellNo++) {\n        mx = max(mx, ds.getSize(ds.findUPar(cellNo)));\n    }\n    return mx;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Summing Neighbor Sizes Without Deduplicating Ultimate Parents:** If a `0` touches the same C-shaped island on its top, left, and bottom sides, adding sizes without a `set<int> uniqueParents` will triple-count that island!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
