ing and caching (GET requests), while `builder.mutation()` handles data modifications (POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE) and invalidates specific cache tags to automatically refetch stale queries.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "A query is used to request and cache server data, while a mutation sends updates (POST/PUT/DELETE) and triggers cache invalidation."}, {"id": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["It listens to keyboard and mouse events globally across all components.", "It enables automated background refetching features like `refetchOnFocus` and `refetchOnReconnect` by subscribing to window events.", "It acts as a replacement for the Redux DevTools extension.", "It prevents React components from unmounting while an active network call is pending."], "question": "What is the primary role of `setupListeners` in the RTK Query store configuration?", "explanation": "`setupListeners(store.dispatch)` is a utility function used in `store.js`. It subscribes to browser `window.focus` and `online` events, allowing RTK Query to automatically refresh stale data when a user returns to the tab or regains internet connectivity.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "It enables automated background refetching features like `refetchOnFocus` and `refetchOnReconnect` by subscribing to window events."}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["It throws an error because hooks cannot be mounted more than once.", "It fires two separate network requests to guarantee independent data copies.", "It deduplicates the request: fires only a single network fetch, caches the response, and shares that cached data with both components.", "It pauses the second component until the first component completely finishes rendering."], "question": "How does RTK Query optimize network requests when two separate components mount and call the same `useGetPostsQuery()` hook simultaneously?", "explanation": "RTK Query features built-in request deduplication. If multiple components request identical endpoints with matching arguments concurrently, RTK Query initiates only one HTTP request and distributes the cached response to all subscribers.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "It deduplicates the request: fires only a single network fetch, caches the response, and shares that cached data with both components."}, {"id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["The duration data stays persisted in the backend database before expiring.", "How many seconds cached data remains in the Redux store after all subscribed components have unmounted.", "The request timeout threshold before an HTTP call throws a timeout error.", "The maximum memory size allowed for local storage caching."], "question": "In an RTK Query API slice, what does the `keepUnusedDataFor` configuration setting control?", "explanation": "When no active component is subscribed to a query's cached data, the data becomes 'unused'. `keepUnusedDataFor` (defaults to 60 seconds) determines the countdown before RTK Query garbage-collects and clears that unused data from the Redux cache.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "How many seconds cached data remains in the Redux store after all subscribed components have unmounted."}, {"id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "A", "options": ["It is a lightweight wrapper around the native `fetch` API that automatically handles baseUrl configuration, headers, and JSON response parsing.", "It is an alternative to `createSlice` for synchronous client state.", "It translates GraphQL operations directly into SQL queries.", "It is a WebSocket client for real-time bidirectional streaming."], "question": "What is the purpose of `fetchBaseQuery` when creating an API slice with `createApi`?", "explanation": "`fetchBaseQuery` is RTK Query's built-in request utility. It encapsulates standard boilerplate like baseUrl attachment, setting authorization headers, and parsing JSON payloads out of the box.", "correctAnswer": "A", "correct_index": 0, "correct_answer": "It is a lightweight wrapper around the native `fetch` API that automatically handles baseUrl configuration, headers, and JSON response parsing."}]	\N	> **Module Overview:** RTK Query eliminates manual `useEffect` data-fetching boilerplate. It provides an automated, centralized layer for server-state caching, request deduplication, polling, and cache invalidation.\n\n### 📌 Core Technical Highlights\n- **API Slice Architecture:** Configuring `createApi` with `reducerPath` and `fetchBaseQuery` to establish centralized API definitions.\n- **Queries vs Mutations:** Implementing `builder.query` for cached GET requests and `builder.mutation` for server updates (POST/PUT/DELETE).\n- **Auto-Generated React Hooks:** Exporting convention-based hooks (`useGetPostsQuery`, `useCreatePostMutation`) with built-in `data`, `isLoading`, and `isError` states.\n- **Cache Lifecycle Management:** Request deduplication, automatic garbage collection via `keepUnusedDataFor`, manual refetching, and window focus tracking via `setupListeners`.\n\n*Complete the 5-question verified quiz below to evaluate your understanding of RTK Query.*	PedroTech	https://www.youtube.com/@PedroTechnologies	\N	RTK Query Data Fetching & Caching Architecture	Accelerated masterclass on RTK Query: configuring API slices with createApi, fetchBaseQuery, queries vs mutations, auto-generated hooks, and caching mechanics.	34	Easy	Data Fetching & Caching	2	[]	\N	34	https://youtu.be/sKJU5_3Vx0A	Learn RTK Query in 30 Minutes - React Redux Toolkit RTK Query Tutorial For Beginners
