7666	3-Way String Mutation Recurrence: Edit Distance (Levenshtein Distance)	dsa-edit-distance-dp-on-strings	Find the minimum insertions, deletions, and replacements to convert s1 into s2 using 2-row space-optimized Levenshtein dynamic programming.	2026-10-03 11:39:06.533966+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: 3-Way String Mutation Recurrence: Edit Distance (Levenshtein Distance)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fJaKO8FbDdo", "title": "Lecture 214: Edit Distance (DP on Strings - DP 33)", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/edit-distance/", "title": "Edit Distance (LC 72)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given two strings word1 and word2, return the minimum number of operations (insert, delete, replace) required to convert word1 to word2."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/edit-distance3702/1", "title": "Edit Distance", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the minimum number of edits (insert, remove, replace) required to convert string s into string t."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/one-edit-distance/", "title": "One Edit Distance (LC 161)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Determine if two strings are exactly one edit distance apart in O(N) time."}]	Module 31: Dynamic Programming on Strings: LCS, Substrings & Palindromic Subsequences	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven two strings `s1` (length `N`) and `s2` (length `M`), find the **minimum number of operations** required to convert `s1` into `s2` using three allowed operations on `s1`: **Insert** a character, **Delete** a character, or **Replace** a character.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Base Cases (1-Based Indexing `i` in `0..N`, `j` in `0..M`):**\n  - **`i == 0` (`s1` is empty):** To turn an empty string into `s2[0..j-1]` of length `j`, we need **`j` insertions** (`dp[0][j] = j`).\n  - **`j == 0` (`s2` is empty):** To turn `s1[0..i-1]` of length `i` into an empty string, we need **`i` deletions** (`dp[i][0] = i`, or **`cur[0] = i`** at the start of every row in space optimization!).\n- **Transitions at `(i, j)`:**\n  - **If `s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]` (Characters Match):** `0` operations needed! Move both pointers: **`dp[i - 1][j - 1]`**.\n  - **Else (`s1[i - 1] != s2[j - 1]`):** Try all 3 operations (each costs `1` operation) and take the minimum:\n    1. **Insert `s2[j - 1]` after `i`:** Matches `j`, `i` stays at `i` → **`1 + dp[i][j - 1]`**\n    2. **Delete `s1[i - 1]`:** Moves `i` back, `j` stays at `j` → **`1 + dp[i - 1][j]`**\n    3. **Replace `s1[i - 1]` with `s2[j - 1]`:** Both match and move back → **`1 + dp[i - 1][j - 1]`**\n- **Why Edit Distance Cannot Be Reduced to `1` Single Array Easily:**\n  - Because `cur[j]` depends on `prev[j - 1]`, `prev[j]`, **AND `cur[j - 1]`**, overwriting in-place from left-to-right destroys `prev[j - 1]`, and right-to-left lacks `cur[j - 1]`. Two 1D arrays (`prev` and `cur`) of size `M + 1` are ideal!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * M)` Time and `O(M)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Edit Distance (2-Row Space Optimized) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint editDistance(string s1, string s2) {\n    int n = s1.size(), m = s2.size();\n    vector<int> prev(m + 1, 0), cur(m + 1, 0);\n\n    // Base Case 1: i == 0 requires j insertions\n    for (int j = 0; j <= m; j++) prev[j] = j;\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        cur[0] = i; // Base Case 2: j == 0 requires i deletions!\n        for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {\n            if (s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]) {\n                cur[j] = prev[j - 1];\n            } else {\n                int insertOp  = 1 + cur[j - 1];\n                int deleteOp  = 1 + prev[j];\n                int replaceOp = 1 + prev[j - 1];\n                cur[j] = min({insertOp, deleteOp, replaceOp});\n            }\n        }\n        prev = cur;\n    }\n    return prev[m];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting `cur[0] = i;` Inside the Outer Loop:** In space-optimized Edit Distance, column `0` is **not `0`**—converting a prefix of length `i` to an empty string takes `i` deletions, so you **must set `cur[0] = i;`** at the start of every row `i`!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
