1650	Backtracking Path Reconstruction: Print Root-to-Node Path in a Binary Tree	dsa-root-to-node-path-binary-tree	Reconstruct the exact path of node values from root to any target node using boolean-guarded DFS backtracking.	2026-10-03 01:02:00.075949+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Backtracking Path Reconstruction: Print Root-to-Node Path in a Binary Tree\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fmflMqVOC7k", "title": "Lecture 98: Print Root to Node Path in Binary Tree", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-paths/", "title": "Binary Tree Paths", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return all root-to-leaf paths formatted as strings."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/path-sum-ii/", "title": "Path Sum II", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find all root-to-leaf paths where the sum of values equals targetSum."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/root-to-leaf-paths/1", "title": "Root to Leaf Paths", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find all full paths from the root node to all leaf nodes."}]	Module 17: Binary Trees: Views, Projections & Ancestry Analysis	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the root of a binary tree and a target node value `B`, return the complete path of node values from the `root` down to node `B` in order.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **DFS Backtracking With Boolean Return:**\n  - Maintain a running path vector `arr`.\n  - At node `curr`:\n    1. If `curr == nullptr`, return `false`.\n    2. Add `curr->val` to `arr`.\n    3. If `curr->val == B`, target found! Return `true` without popping.\n    4. Explore `getPath(curr->left, arr, B)` or `getPath(curr->right, arr, B)`. If either returns `true`, return `true` immediately!\n    5. If neither child branch reaches `B`, backtrack: pop `arr.pop_back()` and return `false`.\n- **Complexity:** `O(N)` Time and `O(H)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Root-to-Node Path Blueprint\n```cpp\nbool getPath(TreeNode* root, vector<int> &arr, int x) {\n    if (root == nullptr) return false;\n\n    arr.push_back(root->val);\n    if (root->val == x) return true;\n\n    if (getPath(root->left, arr, x) || getPath(root->right, arr, x)) {\n        return true;\n    }\n    arr.pop_back(); // Backtrack if x is not in this subtree\n    return false;\n}\n\nvector<int> solve(TreeNode* root, int b) {\n    vector<int> arr;\n    if (root == nullptr) return arr;\n    getPath(root, arr, b);\n    return arr;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
