6394	Fixed Start to Variable Destination DP: Minimum Path Sum in a Triangle Grid	dsa-triangle-minimum-path-sum-variable-ending-point	Solve fixed-origin to variable-destination triangular grid path minimization by starting recursion from (0, 0) and tabulating bottom-up.	2026-10-03 11:28:16.94463+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Fixed Start to Variable Destination DP: Minimum Path Sum in a Triangle Grid\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SrP-PiLSYC0", "title": "Lecture 192: Minimum Path Sum in Triangular Grid (DP 11)", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/triangle/", "title": "Triangle (LC 120)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given a triangle array, return the minimum path sum from top to bottom moving to index i or i + 1 on the next row."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/triangle-path-sum/1", "title": "Triangle Path Sum", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the minimum path sum from the top of a triangular grid to any cell in the bottom row."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/pascals-triangle/", "title": "Pascal's Triangle (LC 118)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Practice indexing and row-by-row transitions on triangular arrays."}]	Module 29: 2D Dynamic Programming on Multi-Choice States & Grids	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a triangular array `triangle` with `N` rows (where row `i` has `i + 1` elements), start at the single top element `(0, 0)` and reach **any cell in the bottom row `N - 1`**. From `(i, j)`, you can move **Down** to `(i + 1, j)` or **Diagonally Down-Right** to `(i + 1, j + 1)`. Find the minimum path sum.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Start Recursion From `(0, 0)` Instead of `(N - 1, j)`?**\n  - In previous grid problems, both the start `(0, 0)` and destination `(N - 1, M - 1)` were fixed.\n  - Here, the starting point `(0, 0)` is **fixed**, while the ending point can be **any of the `N` cells in row `N - 1`**!\n  - If we started recursion from the bottom row, we would have to run `N` separate recursive calls. Starting from **`(0, 0)` downward** requires only **1 call**, with the base case at **`i == N - 1`**!\n- **Recurrence & Bottom-Up Tabulation Direction:**\n  - **Base Case (`i == N - 1`):** For any column `j` in the last row, `f(N - 1, j) = triangle[N - 1][j]`.\n  - **Transition:** `f(i, j) = triangle[i][j] + min(f(i + 1, j), f(i + 1, j + 1))`.\n  - **Tabulation:** Since recursion goes from `0` down to `N - 1`, **Tabulation goes in the exact opposite direction: from row `N - 2` up to `0`**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N²)` Time and `O(N)` Space using a single `front` row vector.\n\n### 💻 Triangle Minimum Path Sum (O(N) Space Optimized) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint minimumTotal(vector<vector<int>> &triangle) {\n    int n = triangle.size();\n    vector<int> front(n, 0), cur(n, 0);\n\n    // 1. Base case: initialize 'front' with the last row (row n - 1)\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n        front[j] = triangle[n - 1][j];\n    }\n\n    // 2. Move bottom-up from row n - 2 up to row 0\n    for (int i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--) {\n        for (int j = i; j >= 0; j--) {\n            int down = triangle[i][j] + front[j];\n            int diagonal = triangle[i][j] + front[j + 1];\n            cur[j] = min(down, diagonal);\n        }\n        front = cur;\n    }\n    return front[0];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Adding Out-of-Bounds Checks in Triangle DP:** Because row `i` has `i + 1` columns (`j` from `0` to `i`) and row `i + 1` has `i + 2` columns (`0` to `i + 1`), both `(i + 1, j)` and `(i + 1, j + 1)` are **always valid in bounds** until you hit row `N - 1`!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
1955	Radial Infection Simulation: Minimum Time to Burn a Binary Tree from a Target Node	dsa-minimum-time-to-burn-binary-tree	Simulate radial fire propagation in a binary tree to determine minimum burn time in O(N) time and space.	2026-10-03 01:18:07.159575+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Radial Infection Simulation: Minimum Time to Burn a Binary Tree from a Target Node\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2r5wLmQfD6g", "title": "Lecture 103: Minimum time taken to BURN the Binary Tree from a Node", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/amount-of-time-for-binary-tree-to-be-infected/", "title": "Amount of Time for Binary Tree to Be Infected", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the total minutes required to infect an entire binary tree starting from start node."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/burning-tree/1", "title": "Burning Tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Calculate minimum time to burn all nodes starting from target in a binary tree."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/all-nodes-distance-k-in-binary-tree/", "title": "All Nodes Distance K in Binary Tree", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Review radial BFS traversal to contrast fixed-distance collection with maximum time propagation."}]	Module 18: Binary Trees: Node Distances, Tree Modifications & Reconstruction	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a binary tree and a `target` node that catches fire at time `t = 0`, fire spreads to all adjacent connected nodes (left child, right child, and parent) in 1 second. Find the **minimum time required to completely burn the entire tree**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Radial Propagation Mechanics:**\n  - Fire spreads outward in concentric circles like an undirected graph BFS.\n  - The minimum time to burn the whole tree is the **maximum distance from `target` to any node in the tree**.\n- **Algorithm Steps (`O(N)` Time, `O(N)` Space):**\n  - **Step 1:** Build parent pointers via BFS/DFS while locating the `target` node pointer if given only its integer value.\n  - **Step 2:** Run radial BFS from `target` with a `queue<TreeNode*> q` and `visited` map.\n  - Maintain `int time = 0`. For each second, pop all nodes currently on fire, ignite unburned neighbors (`left`, `right`, and `parent`), and push them to `q`.\n  - Maintain a boolean `flag = false`. Only increment `time++` if at least one new neighbor caught fire during that second.\n  - Return `time` when the queue becomes empty.\n\n### 💻 Minimum Time to Burn Binary Tree Blueprint\n```cpp\nint minTimeToBurn(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* target) {\n    unordered_map<TreeNode*, TreeNode*> parentTrack;\n    queue<TreeNode*> q;\n    q.push(root);\n\n    // 1. Map parent pointers\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        TreeNode* curr = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        if (curr->left) { parentTrack[curr->left] = curr; q.push(curr->left); }\n        if (curr->right) { parentTrack[curr->right] = curr; q.push(curr->right); }\n    }\n\n    // 2. Radial BFS burn simulation\n    unordered_map<TreeNode*, bool> visited;\n    q.push(target);\n    visited[target] = true;\n    int time = 0;\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int size = q.size();\n        bool burnedAny = false;\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n            TreeNode* curr = q.front();\n            q.pop();\n\n            if (curr->left && !visited[curr->left]) {\n                visited[curr->left] = true;\n                q.push(curr->left);\n                burnedAny = true;\n            }\n            if (curr->right && !visited[curr->right]) {\n                visited[curr->right] = true;\n                q.push(curr->right);\n                burnedAny = true;\n            }\n            if (parentTrack[curr] && !visited[parentTrack[curr]]) {\n                visited[parentTrack[curr]] = true;\n                q.push(parentTrack[curr]);\n                burnedAny = true;\n            }\n        }\n        if (burnedAny) time++;\n    }\n    return time;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
