11592	Sieve + Prefix Sum Precomputation: Count Primes in a Range [L, R] for Multiple Queries	dsa-count-primes-in-range-l-to-r-sieve-prefix-sum	Master Sieve + Prefix Sum Precomputation: Count Primes in a Range [L, R] for Multiple Queries with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:12:24.310723+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Sieve + Prefix Sum Precomputation: Count Primes in a Range [L, R] for Multiple Queries\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=os82DjD2-MA", "title": "Sieve + Prefix Sum Precomputation: Count Primes in a Range [L, R] for Multiple Queries - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/find-prime-numbers-in-a-range4718/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Count Primes In Range", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count or list prime numbers in a given range [L, R]."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/count-primes/", "title": "LeetCode 204: Count Primes", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count the number of primes less than n using Sieve precomputation."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/range-sum-query-immutable/", "title": "LeetCode 303: Range Sum Query - Immutable", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Review how 1D prefix sums answer [L, R] range queries in O(1) time via prefix[R] - prefix[L - 1]."}]	Module 39: Mathematical & Number Theory Algorithms: Divisors, Primes, Binary Exponentiation & Sieve Precomputation	### 📌 Topic Overview\nYou are given `Q` queries (`Q <= 10^5`), where each query provides a range **`[L, R]`** (`1 <= L <= R <= 10^6`). For each query, return the **count of prime numbers between `L` and `R` inclusive**.\n\n### 💡 Progression of 3 Approaches (Brute → Better → Optimal O(1) per Query)\n- **1. Brute Force (`O(Q * (R - L + 1) * sqrt(N))`):**\n  - For each query, loop `j` from `L` to `R` and call `isPrime(j)`. Easily exceeds `10^{11}` operations (TLE).\n- **2. Better — Sieve Without Prefix Sum (`O(N log(log N) + Q * (R - L + 1))`):**\n  - Precompute the Sieve array `prime` once, then loop from `L` to `R` per query summing `prime[j]`. Still `O(Q * N) ≈ 10^{11}` operations in the worst case!\n- **3. Optimal — Sieve + Prefix Sum Array (`O(N log(log N) + N + Q)` Time — `O(1)` per Query!):**\n  - **Step 1 (Build Sieve):** Compute the `prime[0 .. 10^6]` array using the Sieve of Eratosthenes in `O(N log(log N))`.\n  - **Step 2 (Convert `prime` into a Cumulative Prefix Count Array):**\n    - Maintain `cnt = 0`. Loop `i` from `2` to `10^6`:\n      **`cnt += prime[i]; prime[i] = cnt;`**\n    - Now `prime[x]` stores the **total number of primes in `[1 .. x]`**!\n  - **Step 3 (Answer Every Query `[L, R]` in `O(1)` Time):**\n    - The number of primes in `[L .. R]` is simply all primes up to `R` minus all primes strictly before `L` (`[1 .. L - 1]`):\n      **`ans = prime[R] - prime[L - 1]`**!\n\n### 💻 Count Primes in Range [L, R] (Sieve + Prefix Sum) Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> countPrimesInRanges(vector<pair<int, int>> &queries) {\n    int maxVal = 1000000;\n    vector<int> prime(maxVal + 1, 1);\n    prime[0] = prime[1] = 0;\n\n    // 1. Precompute Sieve of Eratosthenes up to 10^6: O(N log(log N))\n    for (int i = 2; i * i <= maxVal; i++) {\n        if (prime[i] == 1) {\n            for (int j = i * i; j <= maxVal; j += i) {\n                prime[j] = 0;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // 2. Convert Sieve into a Prefix-Count Array in-place: O(N)\n    int cnt = 0;\n    for (int i = 2; i <= maxVal; i++) {\n        cnt += prime[i];\n        prime[i] = cnt;\n    }\n\n    // 3. Answer each [L, R] query in O(1) time: O(Q)\n    vector<int> ans;\n    for (const auto &q : queries) {\n        int L = q.first;\n        int R = q.second;\n        int primesInRange = prime[R] - (L > 0 ? prime[L - 1] : 0);\n        ans.push_back(primesInRange);\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Writing `prime[R] - prime[L]` Instead of `prime[R] - prime[L - 1]`:** Because the range `[L, R]` is **inclusive of `L`**, subtracting `prime[L]` would wrongly exclude `L` if `L` itself is a prime number! Always subtract **`prime[L - 1]`**.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
9274	3D Truth-Value Partition DP: Evaluate Boolean Expression to True	dsa-evaluate-boolean-expression-to-true-partition-dp	Count ways to parenthesize a boolean expression to True using 3D partition DP (i, j, isTrue) stepping by 2 across operator boundaries.	2026-10-03 14:56:36.883636+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: 3D Truth-Value Partition DP: Evaluate Boolean Expression to True\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MM7fXopgyjw", "title": "Lecture 232: Evaluate Boolean Expression to True | Partition DP (DP 52)", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/boolean-parenthesization5610/1", "title": "Boolean Parenthesization", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Count the number of ways we can parenthesize a boolean expression of T, F, &, |, and ^ so that the value of expression evaluates to true."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/parsing-a-boolean-expression/", "title": "Parsing A Boolean Expression (LC 1106)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Evaluate complex nested boolean expressions with AND, OR, and NOT operators."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/different-ways-to-add-parentheses/", "title": "Different Ways to Add Parentheses (LC 241)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Split an arithmetic expression at every operator index and combine all left and right sub-results."}]	Module 34: Partition Dynamic Programming (MCM Pattern & Interval Splitting)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a boolean expression string `exp` containing operands `'T'` (True) and `'F'` (False) separated by operators `'&'` (AND), `'|'` (OR), and `'^'` (XOR), count the **number of ways to parenthesize** `exp` so that it evaluates to **True**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why We Need a 3rd Parameter `isTrue` (`f(i, j, isTrue)`):**\n  - Look at the XOR operator `'^'`: `True ^ False = True` and `False ^ True = True`!\n  - Similarly, for OR `'|'`: `True | False = True`.\n  - To know how many ways `left op right` evaluates to **True**, we need to know **both** how many ways the left/right subexpressions evaluate to **True (`lT, rT`)** AND how many ways they evaluate to **False (`lF, rF`)**!\n- **Stepping by `ind += 2` Over Operators:**\n  - Operands sit at even indices (`i, i + 2, ..., j`) and operators sit at `ind = i + 1, i + 3, ..., j - 1`.\n  - **Base Cases:**\n    - `if (i > j) return 0;`\n    - `if (i == j)`: If `isTrue == 1`, return `exp[i] == 'T'`; else return `exp[i] == 'F'`.\n  - **Combinatorial Rules at Operator `exp[ind]`:**\n    - **If `exp[ind] == '&'`:**\n      - Ways for True: `lT * rT`\n      - Ways for False: `(lT * rF) + (lF * rT) + (lF * rF)`\n    - **If `exp[ind] == '|'`:**\n      - Ways for True: `(lT * rT) + (lT * rF) + (lF * rT)`\n      - Ways for False: `lF * rF`\n    - **If `exp[ind] == '^'`:**\n      - Ways for True: `(lT * rF) + (lF * rT)`\n      - Ways for False: `(lT * rT) + (lF * rF)`\n- **Complexity:** `O(N³)` Time and `O(2 * N²)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Evaluate Boolean Expression to True Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nlong long solveBool(int i, int j, int isTrue, const string &exp,\n                    vector<vector<vector<long long>>> &dp) {\n    if (i > j) return 0;\n    if (i == j) {\n        if (isTrue == 1) return exp[i] == 'T';\n        else return exp[i] == 'F';\n    }\n    if (dp[i][j][isTrue] != -1) return dp[i][j][isTrue];\n\n    long long ways = 0;\n    long long mod = 1000000007;\n\n    // Operators are at every 2nd index: i + 1, i + 3, ..., j - 1\n    for (int ind = i + 1; ind <= j - 1; ind += 2) {\n        long long lT = solveBool(i, ind - 1, 1, exp, dp);\n        long long lF = solveBool(i, ind - 1, 0, exp, dp);\n        long long rT = solveBool(ind + 1, j, 1, exp, dp);\n        long long rF = solveBool(ind + 1, j, 0, exp, dp);\n\n        if (exp[ind] == '&') {\n            if (isTrue) ways = (ways + (lT * rT) % mod) % mod;\n            else ways = (ways + (lT * rF) % mod + (lF * rT) % mod + (lF * rF) % mod) % mod;\n        } else if (exp[ind] == '|') {\n            if (isTrue) ways = (ways + (lT * rT) % mod + (lT * rF) % mod + (lF * rT) % mod) % mod;\n            else ways = (ways + (lF * rF) % mod) % mod;\n        } else if (exp[ind] == '^') {\n            if (isTrue) ways = (ways + (lT * rF) % mod + (lF * rT) % mod) % mod;\n            else ways = (ways + (lT * rT) % mod + (lF * rF) % mod) % mod;\n        }\n    }\n    return dp[i][j][isTrue] = ways;\n}\n\nint evaluateExp(string &exp) {\n    int n = exp.size();\n    vector<vector<vector<long long>>> dp(n, vector<vector<long long>>(n, vector<long long>(2, -1)));\n    return solveBool(0, n - 1, 1, exp, dp);\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Incrementing `ind++` Instead of `ind += 2`:** Every operator is separated by an operand (`T` or `F`), so the partition loop must start at `ind = i + 1` and step by **`ind += 2`**!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
3438	Parent-Tracked Wave Collision: Detect a Cycle in an Undirected Graph Using BFS	dsa-detect-cycle-in-undirected-graph-bfs	Detect cycles in undirected graphs using breadth-first wave collision with explicit parent tracking.	2026-10-03 01:51:47.355086+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Parent-Tracked Wave Collision: Detect a Cycle in an Undirected Graph Using BFS\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BPlrALf1LDU", "title": "Lecture 136: Detect a Cycle in an Undirected Graph using BFS", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/detect-cycle-in-an-undirected-graph/1", "title": "Undirected Graph Cycle", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Detect whether an undirected graph with V vertices and E edges contains a cycle."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/redundant-connection/", "title": "Redundant Connection (LC 684)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Identify the extra edge that creates a cycle in an undirected tree graph."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/graph-valid-tree/", "title": "Graph Valid Tree (LC 261)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Verify that an undirected graph is connected and contains zero cycles."}]	Module 22: Graph Cycle Detection, Multi-Source BFS & Boundary Flood Algorithms	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn an **Undirected Graph**, how do we detect if a cycle exists using **Breadth-First Search (BFS)**? If two separate BFS paths diverge from a starting node and later collide at the **same already-visited vertex**, the graph contains a cycle!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why We Must Track `parent` in the Queue (`pair<int, int>`):**\n  - Because edges are bidirectional (`u — v`), when you move from `u` to `v`, `u` is still in `v`'s adjacency list and is already marked visited!\n  - Seeing the immediate `parent` you just came from is **not** a cycle.\n  - A cycle exists **if and only if** a neighbor `nextNode` is **already visited AND `nextNode != parent`**!\n- **Handling Disconnected Components:**\n  - Always loop `i` from `0` to `V - 1` and run `hasCycleBFS(i)` for every unvisited component.\n- **Complexity:** `O(V + 2E)` Time and `O(V)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Cycle Detection in Undirected Graph (BFS) Blueprint\n```cpp\nbool hasCycleBFS(int startNode, const vector<int> adjList[], vector<int> &seen) {\n    queue<pair<int, int>> q; // Stores {currentNode, parentNode}\n    q.push({startNode, -1});\n    seen[startNode] = 1;\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        auto [curr, parent] = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n\n        for (int neighbor : adjList[curr]) {\n            if (!seen[neighbor]) {\n                seen[neighbor] = 1;\n                q.push({neighbor, curr});\n            } else if (neighbor != parent) {\n                // Visited node that is NOT the parent we just came from -> Cycle!\n                return true;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n\nbool isCycle(int V, vector<int> adjList[]) {\n    vector<int> seen(V, 0);\n    for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {\n        if (!seen[i] && hasCycleBFS(i, adjList, seen)) {\n            return true;\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Flagging neighbor == parent as a Cycle:** Every undirected edge u — v would immediately trigger a false-positive cycle if you don't check neighbor != parent!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
