ink is shared, intercepted, or leaked, anyone on the internet can access the private file indefinitely."}, {"id": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["It adds a CAPTCHA to the image.", "It generates a dynamically crafted URL appended with a cryptographic signature and a strict expiration timestamp, granting access only for a temporary window.", "It encrypts the image data so it can only be viewed in Safari.", "It hides the domain name."], "question": "How does a 'Signed URL' resolve the vulnerability of unauthorized data access?", "explanation": "When your backend generates a Signed URL, it sets a timeout (e.g., 5 minutes). After 5 minutes, AWS S3 will reject any requests using that specific URL, preventing long-term link sharing.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "It generates a dynamically crafted URL appended with a cryptographic signature and a strict expiration timestamp, granting access only for a temporary window."}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "A", "options": ["The backend uses a secret private key to sign the URL, and the object storage service (like AWS S3) uses the corresponding public key to verify the signature's authenticity.", "Both use the exact same symmetric password.", "The object storage signs it, and the backend verifies it.", "They rely on third-party OAuth providers."], "question": "During the Signed URL authorization flow, what specific cryptographic roles do the backend server and the object storage service play?", "explanation": "This is asymmetric cryptography. The backend holds the private secret so only it can issue tickets. S3 holds the public key so it can quickly verify the ticket's math without talking to the backend.", "correctAnswer": "A", "correct_index": 0, "correct_answer": "The backend uses a secret private key to sign the URL, and the object storage service (like AWS S3) uses the corresponding public key to verify the signature's authenticity."}, {"id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["Cookies are for mobile; URLs are for desktop.", "A Signed URL grants access to one single specific object file; a Signed Cookie can grant broad access to an entire directory, prefix, or collection of files simultaneously.", "URLs are faster.", "Cookies cannot expire."], "question": "What is the primary architectural difference between a Signed URL and a Signed Cookie?", "explanation": "While a Signed URL is scoped explicitly to one asset, a Signed Cookie is evaluated globally for requests on a specific domain path, granting access to multiple files based on the cookie's policy.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "A Signed URL grants access to one single specific object file; a Signed Cookie can grant broad access to an entire directory, prefix, or collection of files simultaneously."}, {"id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["When allowing a user to download a single invoice PDF.", "When streaming a single profile picture.", "When authorizing a premium user to access an HLS video stream that constantly requests hundreds of small fragmented `.ts` video chunks.", "When serving an open-source library."], "question": "In which of the following scenarios would an architect strongly prefer Signed Cookies over Signed URLs?", "explanation": "HLS video streams consist of a playlist and hundreds of video segments. Generating a unique signed URL for every single segment is a massive overhead. A single Signed Cookie authorizes the entire stream seamlessly.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "When authorizing a premium user to access an HLS video stream that constantly requests hundreds of small fragmented `.ts` video chunks."}]	\N	> **Architecture Overview:** Essential technical concepts and network principles vital for robust, scalable web architectures. Focus on the core mechanics and trade-offs of the system discussed.	Hayk Simonyan	https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan	\N	How Signed URLs & Cookies Protect Your Cloud Data	Deep dive into How Signed URLs & Cookies Protect Your Cloud Data covering architectural implementations, networking, and system resilience.	4	Easy	Advanced System Concepts	20	[]	\N	4	https://youtu.be/Bbp6peXp0WU	How Signed URLs & Cookies Protect Your Cloud Data
1961	Threaded Binary Trees: Morris Inorder & Preorder Traversal in O(1) Auxiliary Space	dsa-morris-traversal-inorder-preorder	Traverse binary trees in O(N) time and O(1) auxiliary space without recursion or stack by temporarily threading predecessor pointers.	2026-10-03 01:18:07.178536+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Threaded Binary Trees: Morris Inorder & Preorder Traversal in O(1) Auxiliary Space\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=80Zug6D1_r4", "title": "Lecture 109: Morris Traversal | Preorder | Inorder", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-inorder-traversal/", "title": "Binary Tree Inorder Traversal (Morris O(1) Space)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Implement Inorder traversal in O(1) extra space using temporary threaded pointers."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-preorder-traversal/", "title": "Binary Tree Preorder Traversal (Morris O(1) Space)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Implement Preorder traversal in O(1) extra space using Morris threading."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/recover-binary-search-tree/", "title": "Recover Binary Search Tree", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Fix swapped BST nodes in O(1) space using Morris inorder traversal."}]	Module 19: Binary Trees: Threaded Binary Trees & Morris Traversal	### 📌 Topic Overview\nStandard recursive and stack-based traversals require `O(H)` auxiliary stack space. **Morris Traversal** uses the concept of **Threaded Binary Trees** to traverse in **`O(N)` Time** using strictly **`O(1)` Extra Space** without modifying the tree structure permanently!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Core Problem:**\n  - After finishing a left subtree, how do we jump back up to the root without a call stack?\n  - The last node visited in a left subtree during Inorder is its **inorder predecessor** (the rightmost node of the left subtree).\n- **The Temporary Thread Trick:**\n  - Before descending into `curr->left`, find its predecessor: `prev = curr->left; while (prev->right && prev->right != curr) prev = prev->right;`.\n  - **Case 1 (Thread Doesn't Exist):** Create a temporary bridge `prev->right = curr`, and move `curr = curr->left`. (For *Preorder*, record `curr->val` right here before diving left!).\n  - **Case 2 (Thread Already Exists):** We have returned from the left subtree! Cut the thread `prev->right = nullptr` to restore the tree. (For *Inorder*, record `curr->val` here!). Then move `curr = curr->right`.\n- **Case 3 (No Left Child):** Record `curr->val` and move `curr = curr->right`.\n\n### 💻 Morris Inorder Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> getInorderMorris(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<int> inorder;\n    TreeNode* curr = root;\n\n    while (curr != nullptr) {\n        if (curr->left == nullptr) {\n            inorder.push_back(curr->val);\n            curr = curr->right;\n        } else {\n            TreeNode* prev = curr->left;\n            while (prev->right && prev->right != curr) {\n                prev = prev->right;\n            }\n            if (prev->right == nullptr) {\n                prev->right = curr; // Create thread\n                curr = curr->left;\n            } else {\n                prev->right = nullptr; // Sever thread (restore tree)\n                inorder.push_back(curr->val);\n                curr = curr->right;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return inorder;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22258	Memento Design Pattern	lld-memento-design-pattern	Capture and restore an object's internal state without violating encapsulation. Implement database rollbacks, text-editor undo stacks, and save-game features.	2026-10-07 22:41:36.377264+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Memento Design Pattern\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/p8-ile_nWnY", "title": "Memento Design Pattern | UML + Code | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v39-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Protecting Encapsulation with Memento", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf the `Caretaker` explicitly reads state (e.g., `caretaker.savedScore = game.getScore(); caretaker.savedLevel = game.getLevel();`), the `Game` class is forced to expose all its internal details through public getters. If the `Game` developers rename or change data structures, the `Caretaker` breaks.\\n\\nThe Memento Pattern fixes this by keeping state extraction internal. The `Caretaker` simply asks the `Game` to execute `createMemento()`. The `Game` packages its own private state into a black-box `Memento` object and hands it to the `Caretaker`. The `Caretaker` holds the object without ever inspecting its contents.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is it an architectural violation for the Caretaker to manually read and copy an object's state variables to achieve an \\"Undo\\" feature? How does the Memento Pattern fix this?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v39-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Roles of Originator, Memento, and Caretaker", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Originator (The Text Editor):** Holds the actual string content. Exposes `save()` (returns a Memento containing the current string) and `restore(Memento)` (overwrites the current string with the Memento's contents).\\n2. **Memento (The Snapshot):** An immutable object storing the exact string payload. It has no business logic.\\n3. **Caretaker (The History Manager):** Listens to keystrokes. Before a user deletes a paragraph, it calls `editor.save()` and pushes the Memento onto a `std::stack<Memento>`. When the user presses `Ctrl+Z`, it pops the Memento and passes it to `editor.restore()`.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Define the specific responsibilities of the Originator, Memento, and Caretaker objects in implementing a Text Editor \\"Ctrl+Z\\" stack.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v39-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Memento vs Command Pattern for Undo", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Command Pattern (Delta Undo):** Stores only the *delta* or *action* performed. If you type \\"A\\", the Command stores \\"User added character A at index 5\\". Undo executes reverse logic: \\"Delete character at index 5\\". It is highly memory efficient but requires complex reverse-logic algorithms.\\n* **Memento Pattern (State Snapshot):** Stores the *absolute state* of the object. If you type \\"A\\" into a 10MB document, the Memento stores a 10MB copy of the entire document. Undo simply overwrites the RAM. It requires zero complex reverse logic, but is massively memory intensive. (Real systems often hybridize them).", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Both Memento and Command patterns are used to implement \\"Undo\\" features. Contrast their approaches regarding memory consumption and execution logic.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v39-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Database ACID Rollbacks and Memento", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **BEGIN TRANSACTION:** The Database Engine (Originator) generates a snapshot of the modified data rows (Memento) and passes it to the Transaction Manager (Caretaker).\\n2. **Mutations:** The client application executes `UPDATE` or `DELETE` statements, altering the live database state.\\n3. **COMMIT:** The transaction is successful. The Caretaker destroys the Memento, finalizing the new state.\\n4. **ROLLBACK:** An error or exception occurs (e.g., connection drop). The Caretaker passes the saved Memento back to the Originator, which restores the database rows exactly to their pre-transaction values, ensuring ACID atomicity.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain how the Memento Pattern conceptually maps to the execution of SQL transactions (BEGIN, COMMIT, ROLLBACK) in database systems.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v39-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Immutability of the Memento Object", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe Memento serves as the definitive historical record. If the Memento exposed setter methods, the Caretaker (or malicious/buggy external code) could intercept the Memento and execute `memento.setScore(9999)`.\\nWhen the Originator later restores from that corrupted Memento, the game's internal state invariants are destroyed. By making the Memento strictly immutable (assigning values exclusively via its constructor and providing zero setter methods), the historical snapshot is mathematically guaranteed to remain pristine.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is it structurally vital that the Memento object remains strictly immutable once instantiated by the Originator?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Snapshot Problem\n* To implement an "Undo" feature, an application must save an object's state before modifying it.\n* If a `Caretaker` object manually copies the variables of the `Originator` object to save them, the `Originator` is forced to declare all its private state variables as public. This destroys encapsulation and data security.\n\n### 💡 2. The Memento Solution\n* **Definition:** Without violating encapsulation, capture and externalize an object's internal state so that the object can be restored to this state later.\n* **The Originator:** The object whose state needs saving. It contains a `createMemento()` method that packages its private state into a Memento object, and a `restore(Memento)` method.\n* **The Memento:** A dumb state-container. It is tightly coupled to the Originator but opaque to the rest of the system.\n* **The Caretaker:** Manages the history. It holds a `std::stack<Memento>`. It requests a Memento from the Originator before executing a command, and passes it back to the Originator when the user hits "Undo".\n\n### 💡 3. Real-World Equivalents\n* **Database Rollbacks:** When an SQL transaction hits `BEGIN`, the engine generates a Memento. If `COMMIT` fires, the Memento is discarded. If `ROLLBACK` fires, the database restores the Memento.\n* **Git Version Control:** Commits are essentially heavy Mementos capturing the snapshot of a file tree.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	Memento Design Pattern	Capture and restore an object's internal state without violating encapsulation. Implement database rollbacks, text-editor undo stacks, and save-game features.	34	Hard	Low Level Design	53	[{"id": "lld-v39-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Protecting Encapsulation with Memento", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf the `Caretaker` explicitly reads state (e.g., `caretaker.savedScore = game.getScore(); caretaker.savedLevel = game.getLevel();`), the `Game` class is forced to expose all its internal details through public getters. If the `Game` developers rename or change data structures, the `Caretaker` breaks.\\n\\nThe Memento Pattern fixes this by keeping state extraction internal. The `Caretaker` simply asks the `Game` to execute `createMemento()`. The `Game` packages its own private state into a black-box `Memento` object and hands it to the `Caretaker`. The `Caretaker` holds the object without ever inspecting its contents.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is it an architectural violation for the Caretaker to manually read and copy an object's state variables to achieve an \\"Undo\\" feature? How does the Memento Pattern fix this?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v39-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Roles of Originator, Memento, and Caretaker", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Originator (The Text Editor):** Holds the actual string content. Exposes `save()` (returns a Memento containing the current string) and `restore(Memento)` (overwrites the current string with the Memento's contents).\\n2. **Memento (The Snapshot):** An immutable object storing the exact string payload. It has no business logic.\\n3. **Caretaker (The History Manager):** Listens to keystrokes. Before a user deletes a paragraph, it calls `editor.save()` and pushes the Memento onto a `std::stack<Memento>`. When the user presses `Ctrl+Z`, it pops the Memento and passes it to `editor.restore()`.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Define the specific responsibilities of the Originator, Memento, and Caretaker objects in implementing a Text Editor \\"Ctrl+Z\\" stack.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v39-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Memento vs Command Pattern for Undo", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Command Pattern (Delta Undo):** Stores only the *delta* or *action* performed. If you type \\"A\\", the Command stores \\"User added character A at index 5\\". Undo executes reverse logic: \\"Delete character at index 5\\". It is highly memory efficient but requires complex reverse-logic algorithms.\\n* **Memento Pattern (State Snapshot):** Stores the *absolute state* of the object. If you type \\"A\\" into a 10MB document, the Memento stores a 10MB copy of the entire document. Undo simply overwrites the RAM. It requires zero complex reverse logic, but is massively memory intensive. (Real systems often hybridize them).", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Both Memento and Command patterns are used to implement \\"Undo\\" features. Contrast their approaches regarding memory consumption and execution logic.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v39-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Database ACID Rollbacks and Memento", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **BEGIN TRANSACTION:** The Database Engine (Originator) generates a snapshot of the modified data rows (Memento) and passes it to the Transaction Manager (Caretaker).\\n2. **Mutations:** The client application executes `UPDATE` or `DELETE` statements, altering the live database state.\\n3. **COMMIT:** The transaction is successful. The Caretaker destroys the Memento, finalizing the new state.\\n4. **ROLLBACK:** An error or exception occurs (e.g., connection drop). The Caretaker passes the saved Memento back to the Originator, which restores the database rows exactly to their pre-transaction values, ensuring ACID atomicity.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain how the Memento Pattern conceptually maps to the execution of SQL transactions (BEGIN, COMMIT, ROLLBACK) in database systems.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v39-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Immutability of the Memento Object", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe Memento serves as the definitive historical record. If the Memento exposed setter methods, the Caretaker (or malicious/buggy external code) could intercept the Memento and execute `memento.setScore(9999)`.\\nWhen the Originator later restores from that corrupted Memento, the game's internal state invariants are destroyed. By making the Memento strictly immutable (assigning values exclusively via its constructor and providing zero setter methods), the historical snapshot is mathematically guaranteed to remain pristine.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is it structurally vital that the Memento object remains strictly immutable once instantiated by the Originator?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22248	Iterator Design Pattern	lld-iterator-design-pattern	Provide a uniform way to access elements of aggregate objects sequentially without exposing their underlying representation.	2026-10-07 22:38:19.438928+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Iterator Design Pattern\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/30fveBRLqMw", "title": "Iterator Design Pattern | UML + Code | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v29-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Exposing Internal Representations", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf a `Playlist` class provides a getter that returns a raw `std::vector<Song>`, the client code is forced to traverse it using vector-specific logic. If the `Playlist` developers optimize their storage layer by switching to a HashMap or a Linked List, the client's iteration code immediately breaks.\\n\\nThe Iterator Pattern solves this by returning an abstract `Iterator` interface. The client runs a `while(iterator.hasNext()) { iterator.next(); }` loop. The client is completely blind to whether the underlying data structure is a tree, map, or array, maintaining perfect encapsulation.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why does exposing a raw std::vector or LinkedList from a domain object to a client violate encapsulation, and how does the Iterator Pattern solve this?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v29-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) in Iterators", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA `BinarySearchTree` class should only be responsible for managing tree invariants (inserting nodes, rotating, deleting nodes).\\n\\nIf you place traversal logic (In-Order, Pre-Order, Post-Order algorithms) directly inside the BST class, you bloat the class with multiple responsibilities.\\nBy extracting traversal algorithms into independent iterator classes (`InOrderIterator`, `PreOrderIterator`), the BST remains lean. The client can instantiate multiple iterators concurrently over the same tree, as the state of the traversal (the stack or current pointer) is housed entirely inside the Iterator object, not the Tree.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain how the Iterator Pattern enforces the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) for aggregate objects like a Binary Search Tree.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v29-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Implementing next() vs hasNext()", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **`hasNext()`:** A boolean inspection method. It verifies if the pointer can advance safely without actually advancing it or mutating the iterator's state.\\n2. **`next()`:** A mutation method. It returns the current element and advances the internal pointer to the subsequent element.\\n\\n**Why separated?** If `next()` just returned `null` at the end, it would be impossible to iterate over a collection that legitimately stores `null` values as valid data points. Separating the bounds-check (`hasNext`) from the payload extraction (`next`) ensures complete type safety and unambiguous termination.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What are the distinct roles of the hasNext() and next() methods? Why are they separated instead of simply letting next() return null at the end?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v29-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Binary Tree Iterator Execution via Stack", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA recursive approach executes entirely inside a single method stack frame, which contradicts the step-by-step nature of an Iterator. Therefore, an explicit `std::stack` is used.\\n\\n1. **Constructor:** Initializes the iterator with the Root node. A helper method pushes the Root and all its continuous Left children onto the `stack`.\\n2. **`hasNext()`:** Simply returns `!stack.empty()`.\\n3. **`next()`:** \\n   * Pops the top node from the stack (this is the value to be returned).\\n   * Checks if the popped node has a Right child.\\n   * If it does, it pushes the Right child and all of its continuous Left children onto the stack.\\n   * Returns the popped node's value.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Describe the algorithmic approach to implementing an iterative In-Order Iterator for a Binary Tree using only hasNext() and next().", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v29-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Fail-Fast Iterators and Concurrent Modification", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA Fail-Fast iterator instantly throws a `ConcurrentModificationException` if the underlying collection is modified (items added or deleted) by another thread or directly via the collection's API while the iterator is actively looping.\\n\\n**The Mechanism:**\\nThe collection maintains an internal integer called `modCount` (modification count), incremented every time the list is altered.\\nWhen the Iterator is instantiated, it saves the current `modCount` into a local `expectedModCount` variable.\\nInside every call to `next()` and `hasNext()`, the Iterator checks `if (collection.modCount != expectedModCount)`. If they mismatch, it immediately crashes, preventing undefined behavior or memory segmentation faults.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is a \\"Fail-Fast\\" iterator in modern programming languages (like Java Collections)? How does the iterator detect that the underlying list was modified mid-traversal?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Traversal Problem\n* If a `Playlist` class internally stores songs in a `std::vector`, clients traversing the playlist must use a standard `for` loop. If the class switches to a `LinkedList` or a `Tree`, every single client traversing that playlist breaks because the internal storage structure was exposed.\n* This tightly couples the business logic of the client to the raw storage mechanics of the domain object.\n\n### 💡 2. The Iterator Solution\n* **Definition:** Provides a way to access the elements of an aggregate object sequentially without exposing its underlying representation.\n* **The Structure:**\n  * **`Iterable` Interface:** The domain aggregate (e.g., `Playlist`) implements an `Iterable` interface requiring a `createIterator()` method.\n  * **`Iterator` Interface:** Defines standard traversal methods: `hasNext()` and `next()`.\n\n### 💡 3. Achieving Single Responsibility\n* The Domain Class (`Playlist`) is solely responsible for managing its collection (adding, removing).\n* The `Iterator` Class (`PlaylistIterator`) is solely responsible for remembering the current traversal index and fetching the next logical item. This strictly enforces the Single Responsibility Principle.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	Iterator Design Pattern	Provide a uniform way to access elements of aggregate objects sequentially without exposing their underlying representation.	40	Hard	Low Level Design	43	[{"id": "lld-v29-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Exposing Internal Representations", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf a `Playlist` class provides a getter that returns a raw `std::vector<Song>`, the client code is forced to traverse it using vector-specific logic. If the `Playlist` developers optimize their storage layer by switching to a HashMap or a Linked List, the client's iteration code immediately breaks.\\n\\nThe Iterator Pattern solves this by returning an abstract `Iterator` interface. The client runs a `while(iterator.hasNext()) { iterator.next(); }` loop. The client is completely blind to whether the underlying data structure is a tree, map, or array, maintaining perfect encapsulation.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why does exposing a raw std::vector or LinkedList from a domain object to a client violate encapsulation, and how does the Iterator Pattern solve this?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v29-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) in Iterators", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA `BinarySearchTree` class should only be responsible for managing tree invariants (inserting nodes, rotating, deleting nodes).\\n\\nIf you place traversal logic (In-Order, Pre-Order, Post-Order algorithms) directly inside the BST class, you bloat the class with multiple responsibilities.\\nBy extracting traversal algorithms into independent iterator classes (`InOrderIterator`, `PreOrderIterator`), the BST remains lean. The client can instantiate multiple iterators concurrently over the same tree, as the state of the traversal (the stack or current pointer) is housed entirely inside the Iterator object, not the Tree.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain how the Iterator Pattern enforces the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) for aggregate objects like a Binary Search Tree.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v29-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Implementing next() vs hasNext()", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **`hasNext()`:** A boolean inspection method. It verifies if the pointer can advance safely without actually advancing it or mutating the iterator's state.\\n2. **`next()`:** A mutation method. It returns the current element and advances the internal pointer to the subsequent element.\\n\\n**Why separated?** If `next()` just returned `null` at the end, it would be impossible to iterate over a collection that legitimately stores `null` values as valid data points. Separating the bounds-check (`hasNext`) from the payload extraction (`next`) ensures complete type safety and unambiguous termination.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What are the distinct roles of the hasNext() and next() methods? Why are they separated instead of simply letting next() return null at the end?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v29-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Binary Tree Iterator Execution via Stack", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA recursive approach executes entirely inside a single method stack frame, which contradicts the step-by-step nature of an Iterator. Therefore, an explicit `std::stack` is used.\\n\\n1. **Constructor:** Initializes the iterator with the Root node. A helper method pushes the Root and all its continuous Left children onto the `stack`.\\n2. **`hasNext()`:** Simply returns `!stack.empty()`.\\n3. **`next()`:** \\n   * Pops the top node from the stack (this is the value to be returned).\\n   * Checks if the popped node has a Right child.\\n   * If it does, it pushes the Right child and all of its continuous Left children onto the stack.\\n   * Returns the popped node's value.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Describe the algorithmic approach to implementing an iterative In-Order Iterator for a Binary Tree using only hasNext() and next().", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v29-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Fail-Fast Iterators and Concurrent Modification", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA Fail-Fast iterator instantly throws a `ConcurrentModificationException` if the underlying collection is modified (items added or deleted) by another thread or directly via the collection's API while the iterator is actively looping.\\n\\n**The Mechanism:**\\nThe collection maintains an internal integer called `modCount` (modification count), incremented every time the list is altered.\\nWhen the Iterator is instantiated, it saves the current `modCount` into a local `expectedModCount` variable.\\nInside every call to `next()` and `hasNext()`, the Iterator checks `if (collection.modCount != expectedModCount)`. If they mismatch, it immediately crashes, preventing undefined behavior or memory segmentation faults.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is a \\"Fail-Fast\\" iterator in modern programming languages (like Java Collections)? How does the iterator detect that the underlying list was modified mid-traversal?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22257	Visitor Design Pattern	lld-visitor-design-pattern	Add new operations to existing class structures without modifying them. Utilize Double Dispatch to process Trees, Document Elements, and ASTs cleanly.	2026-10-07 22:41:36.377264+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Visitor Design Pattern\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/DnmsxnlCyl0", "title": "Visitor Design Pattern | UML + Code | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v38-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Strategy Pattern vs Visitor Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Strategy Pattern:** The number of operations remains constant (e.g., a Robot has a `fly()` method). The Strategy pattern allows you to inject different *implementations* of that single operation (e.g., `FlyWithWings`, `FlyWithJet`).\\n* **Visitor Pattern:** The classes remain constant, but the number of entirely new *operations* changes rapidly. A `Document` doesn't just change *how* it compresses; it suddenly needs to `scanForVirus()`, `exportToPdf()`, and `calculateSize()`. Visitor extracts these entirely new operations into independent classes without polluting the `Document` object.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Both the Strategy and Visitor patterns allow behaviors to be injected into objects. Explain the critical difference in their architectural intent regarding \\"Operations\\" vs \\"Implementations\\".", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v38-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: The Mechanics of Double Dispatch", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nSingle Dispatch means the method executed is determined solely by the runtime type of the calling object (e.g., `animal->makeSound()`).\\n\\nDouble Dispatch means the method executed is determined by the runtime type of TWO objects simultaneously: the Receiver and the Parameter.\\nWhen `file->accept(visitor)` is called:\\n1. The runtime type of `file` determines which `accept()` method fires (First Dispatch).\\n2. Inside `accept()`, the code calls `visitor->visit(this)`. Because `this` is strongly typed (e.g., `ImageFile*`), the compiler dynamically routes the call to the overloaded `visit(ImageFile* f)` method specifically inside the runtime type of the `visitor` object (Second Dispatch).", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is \\"Double Dispatch\\" in object-oriented programming? How does the statement \\"v->visit(this)\\" achieve it inside the Visitor pattern?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v38-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: The Accept Method Boilerplate", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf placed only in the abstract base class `IElement`, the keyword `this` resolves to the static type `IElement*`.\\nWhen `v->visit(this)` triggers, the compiler searches the Visitor for a method matching `visit(IElement*)`, destroying the pattern's ability to route to specific types.\\n\\nBy forcing every concrete subclass (e.g., `ImageFile`) to explicitly implement the code block, the keyword `this` is bound at compile-time to the specific concrete type `ImageFile*`. This allows the C++ compiler to trigger the correct overloaded `visit(ImageFile* img)` method inside the Visitor class.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the Visitor Pattern, why must every single concrete element class implement the identical code block \\"void accept(IVisitor* v) { v->visit(this); }\\"? Why can't this be placed once in the base class?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v38-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Single Responsibility Principle Enforcement", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWithout Visitor, a `UserNode` class would contain `toJson()`, `toXml()`, and `saveToDb()`. The class becomes an unmaintainable \\"God Object\\", modified anytime a database schema or JSON library changes.\\n\\nWith Visitor, the `UserNode` only contains data getters and `accept()`. \\nThe logic is extracted into three isolated classes: `JsonVisitor`, `XmlVisitor`, and `DatabaseVisitor`. If the XML parsing library updates, you only open and modify the `XmlVisitor` class file. The domain objects and the other visitors remain completely untouched, achieving perfect SRP.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Visitor Pattern drastically improve the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) for a system executing JSON Serialization, XML Serialization, and Database Persistence on a data tree?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v38-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: The Fatal Flaw of Visitor", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf you have `TextFile` and `ImageFile`, the `IVisitor` interface defines exactly two methods: `visit(TextFile)` and `visit(ImageFile)`.\\nIf the business requires a brand new element, `PdfFile`, you are forced to add `visit(PdfFile)` to the core `IVisitor` interface.\\nBecause you modified the master interface, EVERY SINGLE concrete visitor (`SizeVisitor`, `CompressionVisitor`, `ScanVisitor`) will instantly break at compile-time until you manually open them and implement the new `visit(PdfFile)` method.\\nThe Visitor pattern is optimal ONLY when the element hierarchy is permanently stable, but the operations change frequently.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "The Visitor Pattern adheres beautifully to the Open/Closed Principle when adding new Operations. Explain why it completely breaks OCP when adding new Element Types.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Open/Closed Violation\n* You have an object structure with `TextFile`, `ImageFile`, and `VideoFile`. \n* You are tasked with adding a `calculateSize()` method to all of them. Next week, you must add `compress()`. Next month, `scanForViruses()`.\n* Continually modifying the core entity classes violates the Open/Closed Principle and bloats the objects with unrelated operational logic.\n\n### 💡 2. The Visitor Solution\n* **Definition:** Represents an operation to be performed on the elements of an object structure. It lets you define a new operation without changing the classes of the elements on which it operates.\n* The domain classes (`TextFile`, `ImageFile`) are locked down. They only receive a single new method: `accept(IVisitor v)`.\n* Inside the `accept` method, the element simply calls `v.visit(this)`. \n\n### 💡 3. Double Dispatch Mechanics\n* The magic of Visitor is **Double Dispatch**. \n* The exact code executed depends on two factors: the runtime type of the Visitor (e.g., `CompressionVisitor`) AND the runtime type of the Element (e.g., `ImageFile`).\n* By organizing logic into `CompressionVisitor`, all compression algorithms for every file type are neatly encapsulated in one single file, perfectly preserving the Single Responsibility Principle.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	Visitor Design Pattern	Add new operations to existing class structures without modifying them. Utilize Double Dispatch to process Trees, Document Elements, and ASTs cleanly.	40	Hard	Low Level Design	52	[{"id": "lld-v38-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Strategy Pattern vs Visitor Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Strategy Pattern:** The number of operations remains constant (e.g., a Robot has a `fly()` method). The Strategy pattern allows you to inject different *implementations* of that single operation (e.g., `FlyWithWings`, `FlyWithJet`).\\n* **Visitor Pattern:** The classes remain constant, but the number of entirely new *operations* changes rapidly. A `Document` doesn't just change *how* it compresses; it suddenly needs to `scanForVirus()`, `exportToPdf()`, and `calculateSize()`. Visitor extracts these entirely new operations into independent classes without polluting the `Document` object.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Both the Strategy and Visitor patterns allow behaviors to be injected into objects. Explain the critical difference in their architectural intent regarding \\"Operations\\" vs \\"Implementations\\".", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v38-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: The Mechanics of Double Dispatch", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nSingle Dispatch means the method executed is determined solely by the runtime type of the calling object (e.g., `animal->makeSound()`).\\n\\nDouble Dispatch means the method executed is determined by the runtime type of TWO objects simultaneously: the Receiver and the Parameter.\\nWhen `file->accept(visitor)` is called:\\n1. The runtime type of `file` determines which `accept()` method fires (First Dispatch).\\n2. Inside `accept()`, the code calls `visitor->visit(this)`. Because `this` is strongly typed (e.g., `ImageFile*`), the compiler dynamically routes the call to the overloaded `visit(ImageFile* f)` method specifically inside the runtime type of the `visitor` object (Second Dispatch).", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is \\"Double Dispatch\\" in object-oriented programming? How does the statement \\"v->visit(this)\\" achieve it inside the Visitor pattern?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v38-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: The Accept Method Boilerplate", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf placed only in the abstract base class `IElement`, the keyword `this` resolves to the static type `IElement*`.\\nWhen `v->visit(this)` triggers, the compiler searches the Visitor for a method matching `visit(IElement*)`, destroying the pattern's ability to route to specific types.\\n\\nBy forcing every concrete subclass (e.g., `ImageFile`) to explicitly implement the code block, the keyword `this` is bound at compile-time to the specific concrete type `ImageFile*`. This allows the C++ compiler to trigger the correct overloaded `visit(ImageFile* img)` method inside the Visitor class.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the Visitor Pattern, why must every single concrete element class implement the identical code block \\"void accept(IVisitor* v) { v->visit(this); }\\"? Why can't this be placed once in the base class?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v38-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Single Responsibility Principle Enforcement", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWithout Visitor, a `UserNode` class would contain `toJson()`, `toXml()`, and `saveToDb()`. The class becomes an unmaintainable \\"God Object\\", modified anytime a database schema or JSON library changes.\\n\\nWith Visitor, the `UserNode` only contains data getters and `accept()`. \\nThe logic is extracted into three isolated classes: `JsonVisitor`, `XmlVisitor`, and `DatabaseVisitor`. If the XML parsing library updates, you only open and modify the `XmlVisitor` class file. The domain objects and the other visitors remain completely untouched, achieving perfect SRP.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Visitor Pattern drastically improve the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) for a system executing JSON Serialization, XML Serialization, and Database Persistence on a data tree?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v38-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: The Fatal Flaw of Visitor", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf you have `TextFile` and `ImageFile`, the `IVisitor` interface defines exactly two methods: `visit(TextFile)` and `visit(ImageFile)`.\\nIf the business requires a brand new element, `PdfFile`, you are forced to add `visit(PdfFile)` to the core `IVisitor` interface.\\nBecause you modified the master interface, EVERY SINGLE concrete visitor (`SizeVisitor`, `CompressionVisitor`, `ScanVisitor`) will instantly break at compile-time until you manually open them and implement the new `visit(PdfFile)` method.\\nThe Visitor pattern is optimal ONLY when the element hierarchy is permanently stable, but the operations change frequently.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "The Visitor Pattern adheres beautifully to the Open/Closed Principle when adding new Operations. Explain why it completely breaks OCP when adding new Element Types.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22251	State Design Pattern	lld-state-design-pattern	Allow an object to alter its behavior when its internal state changes. Eliminate sprawling switch statements by encapsulating states into discrete classes.	2026-10-07 22:38:19.438928+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: State Design Pattern\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/bJPmvie_p4w", "title": "State Design Pattern | Build Vending Machine (UML & Code) | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v32-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Core Intent of the State Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe State Pattern eliminates the \\"Sprawling Switch/If-Else Anti-Pattern.\\"\\n\\nWithout it, every public method in a class (like `insertCoin()`, `selectItem()`) must begin with a massive `switch(currentState)` block to determine what logic to execute. This creates fragile, deeply nested code that violates the Open/Closed Principle; adding a new state (e.g., `MaintenanceState`) requires modifying every single method in the class.\\nThe State Pattern delegates this to independent State classes, keeping the core Context class completely free of conditional state-checking logic.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What architectural anti-pattern does the State Design Pattern eliminate when an object's behavior depends heavily on its current lifecycle phase?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v32-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Decentralized State Transitions", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWhile there is debate, standard LLD practice heavily favors **Decentralized Transitions** (the Concrete State classes handling it).\\n\\nIf the Context handles transitions, it must contain a router determining \\"If State A finishes action X, switch to State B.\\" This violates OCP because adding State C forces you to rewrite the Context's routing logic.\\nBy placing transitions inside the Concrete States, `NoCoinState::insertCoin()` simply concludes by returning `return new HasCoinState();` (or returning a pre-initialized reference). The Context mindlessly assigns its pointer to whatever the State returns. State flow logic remains fully encapsulated within the states themselves.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the State Pattern, who should be responsible for executing the transition from State A to State B: the Context class or the Concrete State classes? Defend your answer.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v32-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Context Delegation Mechanics", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. The Context `VendingMachine` maintains a private pointer: `IState* currentState;`.\\n2. When the client calls `machine.insertCoin(coin)`, the Context performs ZERO business logic.\\n3. The Context blindly delegates the call to the state: `currentState = currentState->insertCoin(this, coin);`\\n4. The active state (e.g., `NoCoinState`) processes the coin, registers it in the Context's inventory via the `this` pointer, and executes the transition by returning a pointer to the next state (`HasCoinState`).\\n5. The Context overwrites its `currentState` pointer with the newly returned pointer.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the internal delegation mechanics of the Context class (e.g., the VendingMachine). When a client calls machine.insertCoin(), what exact pointer hand-offs occur?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v32-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Handling Invalid Actions in States", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `IState` interface requires all concrete states to implement `dispenseItem()`. \\nInside the `NoCoinState` class, the `dispenseItem()` implementation simply prints an error (\\"Please insert a coin first\\") or throws an `InvalidOperationException`.\\nCrucially, it returns `this` (a pointer to itself), refusing to transition the Context to the `DispenseState`. The invalid logic is perfectly isolated to `NoCoinState`, ensuring that `HasCoinState` never has to worry about whether a coin was actually inserted.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "If a user attempts to call dispenseItem() while the Vending Machine is currently in the NoCoinState, how does the State Pattern elegantly reject this action?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v32-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: State vs Strategy Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Strategy Pattern:** The client dynamically injects the Strategy (e.g., `setSortStrategy(new QuickSort())`). The Strategy represents a *single algorithmic approach* to solving a problem. Strategies are completely unaware of each other and never trigger transitions between themselves.\\n* **State Pattern:** The Context cycles through States autonomously based on internal rules. States represent a *phase of a lifecycle*. States are intimately aware of each other, as they actively contain the business logic to terminate themselves and transition the Context into a different specific State.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Structurally, the State Pattern and Strategy Pattern are virtually identical (Context delegating to an Interface pointer). What is the critical difference in their behavioral intent?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Finite State Machine Problem\n* A Vending Machine operates fundamentally as a Finite State Machine with limited modes: `NoCoinState`, `HasCoinState`, `DispenseState`, and `SoldOutState`.\n* If implemented inside a monolithic `VendingMachine` class, every user action (`insertCoin()`, `selectItem()`) requires a sprawling, error-prone `switch(state)` block.\n\n### 💡 2. The State Pattern Solution\n* **Definition:** Allows an object to alter its behavior when its internal state changes. The object will appear to change its class entirely.\n* **The Structure:**\n  * **Context (`VendingMachine`):** Exposes public actions (`insertCoin()`). It holds a pointer to an abstract `IState` interface. It simply delegates the action to the current state: `currentState->insertCoin()`.\n  * **State Interface (`IState`):** Defines all possible actions (`insertCoin`, `selectItem`, `dispense`).\n  * **Concrete States (`NoCoinState`, `HasCoinState`):** Implement the specific behavior for that exact state.\n\n### 💡 3. Decentralized Transitions\n* When `NoCoinState` successfully executes `insertCoin()`, it forces the Context to transition. It returns a reference to the `HasCoinState` object, commanding the Vending Machine to overwrite its internal `currentState` pointer.\n* If a user clicks `selectItem()` while in `NoCoinState`, the state class simply throws an error or returns `this` (itself), refusing to transition and perfectly containing the invalid action logic.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	State Design Pattern	Allow an object to alter its behavior when its internal state changes. Eliminate sprawling switch statements by encapsulating states into discrete classes.	74	Hard	Low Level Design	46	[{"id": "lld-v32-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Core Intent of the State Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe State Pattern eliminates the \\"Sprawling Switch/If-Else Anti-Pattern.\\"\\n\\nWithout it, every public method in a class (like `insertCoin()`, `selectItem()`) must begin with a massive `switch(currentState)` block to determine what logic to execute. This creates fragile, deeply nested code that violates the Open/Closed Principle; adding a new state (e.g., `MaintenanceState`) requires modifying every single method in the class.\\nThe State Pattern delegates this to independent State classes, keeping the core Context class completely free of conditional state-checking logic.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What architectural anti-pattern does the State Design Pattern eliminate when an object's behavior depends heavily on its current lifecycle phase?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v32-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Decentralized State Transitions", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWhile there is debate, standard LLD practice heavily favors **Decentralized Transitions** (the Concrete State classes handling it).\\n\\nIf the Context handles transitions, it must contain a router determining \\"If State A finishes action X, switch to State B.\\" This violates OCP because adding State C forces you to rewrite the Context's routing logic.\\nBy placing transitions inside the Concrete States, `NoCoinState::insertCoin()` simply concludes by returning `return new HasCoinState();` (or returning a pre-initialized reference). The Context mindlessly assigns its pointer to whatever the State returns. State flow logic remains fully encapsulated within the states themselves.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the State Pattern, who should be responsible for executing the transition from State A to State B: the Context class or the Concrete State classes? Defend your answer.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v32-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Context Delegation Mechanics", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. The Context `VendingMachine` maintains a private pointer: `IState* currentState;`.\\n2. When the client calls `machine.insertCoin(coin)`, the Context performs ZERO business logic.\\n3. The Context blindly delegates the call to the state: `currentState = currentState->insertCoin(this, coin);`\\n4. The active state (e.g., `NoCoinState`) processes the coin, registers it in the Context's inventory via the `this` pointer, and executes the transition by returning a pointer to the next state (`HasCoinState`).\\n5. The Context overwrites its `currentState` pointer with the newly returned pointer.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the internal delegation mechanics of the Context class (e.g., the VendingMachine). When a client calls machine.insertCoin(), what exact pointer hand-offs occur?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v32-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Handling Invalid Actions in States", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `IState` interface requires all concrete states to implement `dispenseItem()`. \\nInside the `NoCoinState` class, the `dispenseItem()` implementation simply prints an error (\\"Please insert a coin first\\") or throws an `InvalidOperationException`.\\nCrucially, it returns `this` (a pointer to itself), refusing to transition the Context to the `DispenseState`. The invalid logic is perfectly isolated to `NoCoinState`, ensuring that `HasCoinState` never has to worry about whether a coin was actually inserted.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "If a user attempts to call dispenseItem() while the Vending Machine is currently in the NoCoinState, how does the State Pattern elegantly reject this action?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v32-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: State vs Strategy Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Strategy Pattern:** The client dynamically injects the Strategy (e.g., `setSortStrategy(new QuickSort())`). The Strategy represents a *single algorithmic approach* to solving a problem. Strategies are completely unaware of each other and never trigger transitions between themselves.\\n* **State Pattern:** The Context cycles through States autonomously based on internal rules. States represent a *phase of a lifecycle*. States are intimately aware of each other, as they actively contain the business logic to terminate themselves and transition the Context into a different specific State.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Structurally, the State Pattern and Strategy Pattern are virtually identical (Context delegating to an Interface pointer). What is the critical difference in their behavioral intent?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
13279	Farthest-Reachable Horizon Tracking: Jump Game I	dsa-jump-game-i-greedy-reachability	Master Farthest-Reachable Horizon Tracking: Jump Game I with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:19:12.170786+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Farthest-Reachable Horizon Tracking: Jump Game I\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tZAa_jJ3SwQ", "title": "Farthest-Reachable Horizon Tracking: Jump Game I - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/jump-game/", "title": "LeetCode 55: Jump Game", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "You are initially positioned at the array's first index, and each element represents your maximum jump length at that position. Return true if you can reach the last index."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/jump-game-iii/", "title": "LeetCode 1306: Jump Game III", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Check if you can reach any index with value 0 when jumping i + arr[i] or i - arr[i]."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/minimum-number-of-jumps-1587115620/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Minimum Jumps (Reachability)", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Determine if the end of the array is reachable and find the minimum jumps."}]	Module 41: Greedy Algorithms: Sorting Heuristics, Reachability Horizons & Interval Scheduling	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an integer array `nums` of size `N`, you start at index `0`, and `nums[i]` represents the **maximum jump length** from index `i`. Determine if you can reach the last index `N - 1`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Observation 1: If There Are No `0`s in `nums`, You Can Always Reach the End!**\n  - Every positive integer `>= 1` lets you step forward at least 1 index. The only thing that can ever trap you is landing on a **`0` that no earlier jump can leap over**!\n- **Observation 2: Track `maxIndex` (The Farthest Index Reachable So Far):**\n  - Initialize `maxIndex = 0`.\n  - Loop `i` from `0` to `N - 1`:\n    1. **Unreachable Check (`if (i > maxIndex) return false;`):**\n       - If our current index `i` is **strictly greater than `maxIndex`**, it means **no jump from any earlier index `0 .. i - 1` was able to reach `i`**! Immediately return `false`.\n    2. **Horizon Update:** Otherwise, index `i` is reachable, and from `i` we can reach up to `i + nums[i]`—so update **`maxIndex = max(maxIndex, i + nums[i])`**.\n  - If the loop completes (or as soon as `maxIndex >= N - 1`), return `true`!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N)` Time and `O(1)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Jump Game I Blueprint\n```cpp\nbool canJump(vector<int> &nums) {\n    int maxIndex = 0;\n    int n = nums.size();\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        // If current index i is beyond the farthest reachable horizon, we are stuck!\n        if (i > maxIndex) {\n            return false;\n        }\n        maxIndex = max(maxIndex, i + nums[i]);\n\n        // Optional early exit once the last index is within reach\n        if (maxIndex >= n - 1) return true;\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Updating `maxIndex` Before Checking `if (i > maxIndex)`:** You can only jump from index `i` **if you were able to reach index `i` in the first place**! Always check `if (i > maxIndex) return false;` at the very top of the loop.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
12421	Majority-Frequency Minority Replacement: Longest Repeating Character Replacement	dsa-longest-repeating-character-replacement-sliding-window	Master Majority-Frequency Minority Replacement: Longest Repeating Character Replacement with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:15:39.086635+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Majority-Frequency Minority Replacement: Longest Repeating Character Replacement\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_eNhaDCr6P0", "title": "Majority-Frequency Minority Replacement: Longest Repeating Character Replacement - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-repeating-character-replacement/", "title": "LeetCode 424: Longest Repeating Character Replacement", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "You are given a string s and an integer k. You can choose any character of the string and change it to any other uppercase English character at most k times. Return the length of the longest substring containing the same letter."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximize-the-confusion-of-an-exam/", "title": "LeetCode 2024: Maximize the Confusion of an Exam", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Special 2-character case of Longest Repeating Character Replacement."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/longest-repeating-character-replacement/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Longest Repeating Character Replacement", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the maximum length of a substring with identical characters after at most K replacements."}]	Module 40: Sliding Window & Two-Pointer Algorithms: Fixed Windows, Variable Windows & Exact-Count Reductions	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an uppercase string `s` and an integer `k`, you can replace **at most `k` characters** in a substring with any uppercase letter so that all characters in that substring become identical. Return the **maximum length** of such a substring.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute O(N²) → Better O(2N * 26) → Optimal O(N))\n- **The Replacement Formula (`(r - l + 1) - maxf <= k`):**\n  - In any window `[l ... r]` of length `len = r - l + 1`, which character should we turn all other characters into?\n  - To minimize replacements, keep the **most frequent character (`maxf`)** untouched and replace all remaining characters!\n  - Therefore, the number of replacements required is:\n    **`changes = (r - l + 1) - maxf`**.\n  - A window is valid if and only if **`(r - l + 1) - maxf <= k`**!\n- **Why We NEVER Need to Decrement `maxf` (Or Rescan `hash[26]`) When Shrinking!**\n  - Suppose we found a valid window of length `5` with `maxf = 3` (`5 - 3 <= 2`).\n  - To beat `maxLen = 5` and find a valid window of length `6`, what must `maxf` be? Since `6 - maxf <= 2`, we **must find a future window where `maxf >= 4`**!\n  - Reducing `maxf` from `3` down to `2` when shrinking `l` can **never** help us achieve a larger `maxLen`—only a **strictly larger `maxf`** can ever grow `maxLen`!\n  - Combining **no `maxf` rescan** with our **single-step `if` shrink** reduces complexity from `O(2N * 26)` to pure **`O(N)`**!\n\n### 💻 Longest Repeating Character Replacement (Optimal O(N)) Blueprint\n```cpp\nint characterReplacement(string s, int k) {\n    int l = 0, r = 0, maxLen = 0, maxf = 0;\n    int hash[26] = {0};\n\n    while (r < s.size()) {\n        hash[s[r] - 'A']++;\n        maxf = max(maxf, hash[s[r] - 'A']);\n\n        // Optimal O(N): single-step 'if' shrink without rescanning hash[26] for maxf!\n        if ((r - l + 1) - maxf > k) {\n            hash[s[l] - 'A']--;\n            l++;\n        }\n\n        if ((r - l + 1) - maxf <= k) {\n            maxLen = max(maxLen, r - l + 1);\n        }\n        r++;\n    }\n    return maxLen;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Rescanning All 26 Buckets on Every Shrink:** While rescanning `hash[0..25]` inside a `while` loop is still accepted (`O(26 * 2N)`), explaining in an interview *why* `maxf` never needs to be decremented is what distinguishes a top-tier `O(N)` solution!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
11591	Harmonic Multiple Elimination: Sieve of Eratosthenes (All Primes up to N)	dsa-sieve-of-eratosthenes-all-primes-up-to-n	Master Harmonic Multiple Elimination: Sieve of Eratosthenes (All Primes up to N) with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:12:24.309173+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Harmonic Multiple Elimination: Sieve of Eratosthenes (All Primes up to N)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g5Fuxn_AvSk", "title": "Harmonic Multiple Elimination: Sieve of Eratosthenes (All Primes up to N) - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/count-primes/", "title": "LeetCode 204: Count Primes", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given an integer n, return the number of prime numbers that are strictly less than n using the Sieve of Eratosthenes."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/sieve-of-eratosthenes5242/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Sieve of Eratosthenes", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Find all prime numbers less than or equal to a given integer N."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/closest-prime-numbers-in-range/", "title": "LeetCode 2523: Closest Prime Numbers in Range", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Precompute primes up to right using the Sieve of Eratosthenes and find the adjacent prime pair with minimum gap."}]	Module 39: Mathematical & Number Theory Algorithms: Divisors, Primes, Binary Exponentiation & Sieve Precomputation	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an integer `N`, find all prime numbers from `2` up to `N`. Instead of testing each number individually in `O(N * sqrt(N))` time, the **Sieve of Eratosthenes** precomputes primality for **all numbers from `2` to `N` in `O(N log(log N))` time**, turning every subsequent primality check into an **`O(1)` lookup**!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways & The Two Optimizations\n- **Core Idea:**\n  - Create an array `prime(N + 1, 1)` initialized to `1` (meaning prime), with `prime[0] = prime[1] = 0`.\n  - When we visit `i` (starting from `i = 2`) and see `prime[i] == 1`, `i` is prime—so we mark all multiples of `i` as `0` (composite)!\n- **Optimization 1: Start the Inner Loop at `j = i * i` (Not `2 * i`!):**\n  - Why don't we need to mark `2 * i, 3 * i, ..., (i - 1) * i` when we are at prime `i`?\n  - Because every smaller multiple `k * i` (where `k < i`) has a smaller factor `k` and **was already marked as `0` when the outer loop processed `k`**!\n  - For example, when `i = 5`, `5 * 2 = 10`, `5 * 3 = 15`, and `5 * 4 = 20` were already marked by `2` and `3`. The very first unmarked multiple of `5` is **`5 * 5 = 25` (`i * i`)**!\n- **Optimization 2: Stop the Outer Loop at `i * i <= N`:**\n  - Since the inner loop starts at `j = i * i`, once `i * i > N`, the inner loop won't execute at all! Thus the outer loop only needs to run while **`i * i <= N`**.\n- **Complexity:** **`O(N log(log N))`** Time (Prime Harmonic Series) and **`O(N)`** Space.\n\n### 💻 Sieve of Eratosthenes Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> sieveOfEratosthenes(int N) {\n    vector<int> prime(N + 1, 1);\n    if (N >= 0) prime[0] = 0;\n    if (N >= 1) prime[1] = 0;\n\n    // Optimization 2: Outer loop only needs to run up to i * i <= N\n    for (int i = 2; i * i <= N; i++) {\n        if (prime[i] == 1) {\n            // Optimization 1: Start marking multiples from i * i with step +i\n            for (int j = i * i; j <= N; j += i) {\n                prime[j] = 0;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Collect all primes up to N\n    vector<int> primesList;\n    for (int i = 2; i <= N; i++) {\n        if (prime[i] == 1) {\n            primesList.push_back(i);\n        }\n    }\n    return primesList;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Incrementing the Inner Loop by `j++` Instead of `j += i`:** We want to mark **multiples of `i`** (`i * i, i * i + i, i * i + 2i...`), so the inner loop step must be **`j += i`**!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
5244	Greedy Edge-Sorted MST Construction: Kruskal's Algorithm Using Disjoint Set	dsa-kruskals-algorithm-minimum-spanning-tree	Sort all edges by weight and greedily link disconnected components using Disjoint Set Union to build the Minimum Spanning Tree.	2026-10-03 11:10:35.768301+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Greedy Edge-Sorted MST Construction: Kruskal's Algorithm Using Disjoint Set\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DMnDM_sxVig", "title": "Lecture 172: Kruskal's Algorithm (Minimum Spanning Tree)", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/minimum-spanning-tree/1", "title": "Minimum Spanning Tree (Kruskal's Algorithm)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the sum of weights of the Minimum Spanning Tree using Kruskal's Algorithm and Disjoint Set."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/min-cost-to-connect-all-points/", "title": "Min Cost to Connect All Points (LC 1584)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Generate all pairwise edges, sort by Manhattan distance, and build the MST using Kruskal's algorithm."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-critical-and-pseudo-critical-edges-in-minimum-spanning-tree/", "title": "Find Critical and Pseudo-Critical Edges in Minimum Spanning Tree (LC 1489)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Run Kruskal's MST with forced edge inclusion and exclusion to classify critical MST edges."}]	Module 26: Disjoint Set Union (DSU), Kruskal's MST & Dynamic Connectivity	### 📌 Topic Overview\n**Kruskal's Algorithm** finds the **Minimum Spanning Tree (MST)** of a weighted undirected graph by sorting all edges in ascending order of weight and greedily adding each edge `(u, v, wt)` to the MST **if and only if `u` and `v` do not already belong to the same connected component** (checked in `O(1)` via Disjoint Set).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Algorithm Steps:**\n  1. Extract all edges as tuples `{wt, {u, v}}` and **sort them in ascending order of weight `wt`** (`O(M log M)`).\n  2. Initialize a `DisjointSet ds(V)` where every vertex starts in its own component.\n  3. Iterate through the sorted edges:\n     - If **`ds.findUPar(u) != ds.findUPar(v)`**:\n       - Add `mstWt += wt`.\n       - Merge their components via `ds.unionBySize(u, v)`.\n     - Else: skip the edge (adding it would create a cycle!).\n- **Complexity:** `O(V + E + E log E + E * 4 * alpha)` Time and `O(V + E)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Kruskal's Algorithm Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint spanningTreeKruskal(int V, vector<vector<int>> adj[]) {\n    vector<pair<int, pair<int, int>>> edges;\n    for (int u = 0; u < V; u++) {\n        for (const auto &it : adj[u]) {\n            int v = it[0], wt = it[1];\n            edges.push_back({wt, {u, v}});\n        }\n    }\n\n    // 1. Sort all edges by weight ascending\n    sort(edges.begin(), edges.end());\n\n    // 2. Greedily union disconnected components\n    DisjointSet ds(V);\n    int mstWt = 0;\n\n    for (const auto &e : edges) {\n        int wt = e.first;\n        int u = e.second.first;\n        int v = e.second.second;\n\n        if (ds.findUPar(u) != ds.findUPar(v)) {\n            mstWt += wt;\n            ds.unionBySize(u, v);\n        }\n    }\n    return mstWt;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Worrying About Bidirectional Duplicate Edges in `edges`:** Even if an undirected edge `(u, v)` is added twice into `edges` (once from `adj[u]` and once from `adj[v]`), the first occurrence unions `u` and `v`, so the second occurrence automatically fails `ds.findUPar(u) != ds.findUPar(v)` and is harmlessly ignored!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
182	Subarray Sum Patterns: Prefix Sum + Hash Map vs. Variable-Size Sliding Window	dsa-longest-subarray-with-sum-k-prefix-sum-sliding-window	Master Prefix Sum + Hash Map for arrays with positives, zeroes, and negatives, contrasted with two-pointer sliding window for positive-only arrays.	2026-10-02 22:22:31.485465+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Subarray Sum Patterns: Prefix Sum + Hash Map vs. Variable-Size Sliding Window\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=frf7qxiN2qU", "title": "Lecture 20: Longest Subarray with Sum K — Prefix Sum Map vs 2-Pointer Sliding Window", "duration": "50m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/subarray-sum-equals-k/", "title": "Subarray Sum Equals K", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "560", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use Prefix Sum + Hash Map to count the total number of contiguous subarrays summing to K."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-size-subarray-sum/", "title": "Minimum Size Subarray Sum", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "209", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use the two-pointer shrinking window technique on positive numbers to find the shortest subarray with sum >= target."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/subarray-with-0-sum-1587115621/1", "title": "Subarray with 0 sum", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "problemId": "gfg-subarray-0-sum", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Detect whether any contiguous subarray sums to zero using a prefix sum set."}]	Module 8: Array Manipulation, Two-Pointer Patterns & Prefix Sums	### 📌 Topic Overview\nA **subarray** is a contiguous slice of an array. Finding the **Longest Subarray with Sum `K`** is one of the most frequently asked interview patterns because its optimal solution depends on whether the array contains **negative numbers** or **only positives and zeros**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Approach 1: Prefix Sum + Hash Map (Optimal when array has Positives, Zeros & Negatives):**\n  - As you iterate to index `i`, maintain the running `sum` from index `0` to `i`.\n  - If the total sum at `i` is `sum`, and we want a subarray ending at `i` with sum `K`, we check if a prefix sum of `rem = sum - K` appeared at some earlier index `j`. If it did, the slice from `j + 1` to `i` has sum `K` and length `i - j`!\n  - **Zero Edge Case Rule:** Only store `preSumMap[sum] = i` if `sum` is **not already in the map**. Keeping the earliest (leftmost) index guarantees we get the **longest** subarray.\n  - **Complexity:** `O(N)` or `O(N log N)` Time, `O(N)` Space.\n- **Approach 2: Two-Pointer Sliding Window (Optimal when array has ONLY Positives & Zeros):**\n  - Keep `left = 0` and `right = 0`. Expand the window by moving `right` and adding `arr[right]` to `sum`.\n  - Whenever `sum > K`, shrink the window from the left (`sum -= arr[left]; left++`).\n  - Whenever `sum == K`, update `maxLen = max(maxLen, right - left + 1)`.\n  - **Complexity:** `O(2N) → O(N)` Time and `O(1)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Two-Pointer Sliding Window (Positives & Zeros)\n```cpp\nint longestSubarrayWithSumK(vector<int> &a, long long k) {\n    int left = 0, right = 0, maxLen = 0, n = a.size();\n    long long sum = a[0];\n    while (right < n) {\n        while (left <= right && sum > k) {\n            sum -= a[left];\n            left++;\n        }\n        if (sum == k) maxLen = max(maxLen, right - left + 1);\n        right++;\n        if (right < n) sum += a[right];\n    }\n    return maxLen;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22250	LLD Project: Splitwise Clone	lld-project-splitwise-clone	Architect an expense-sharing application. Model Users, Groups, and Expenses. Integrate Strategy patterns for splits and a Greedy Algorithm to simplify transactions.	2026-10-07 22:38:19.438928+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: LLD Project: Splitwise Clone\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/cWtBZUAQpcc", "title": "Build Splitwise Clone | Expense Splitting App | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v31-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Strategy Pattern for Expense Splitting", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `Expense` creation flow is decoupled from mathematical execution.\\nWe define an `ISplitStrategy` interface with a `calculateSplits(amount, users, parameters)` method.\\n1. `EqualSplitStrategy` ignores parameters, calculates `amount / users.size()`, and returns the split array.\\n2. `ExactSplitStrategy` iterates over user-provided parameters, ensures `sum(exact_values) == amount`, and assigns the splits.\\n3. `PercentSplitStrategy` ensures `sum(percentages) == 100`, calculates the fractional amounts, and returns the splits.\\nThe `ExpenseManager` dynamically invokes the correct strategy without resorting to bloated switch-case mathematics.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How is the Strategy Pattern utilized in the Splitwise LLD to handle varying logic for Equal, Exact, and Percentage-based expense splitting?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v31-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Designing the Group Ledger Data Structure", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `Group` maintains the balance sheet using: `std::unordered_map<std::string, std::unordered_map<std::string, double>> groupBalances;`\\n\\n**The Optimal Choice:**\\n1. **Fast Retrieval:** To find out exactly how much User A owes User B, the system executes `groupBalances[\\"UserA\\"][\\"UserB\\"]`, resolving in $O(1)$ constant time.\\n2. **Symmetric Adjustments:** When User A pays User B ₹100, the system must symmetrically adjust records: it increments User A's ledger against User B, and decrements User B's ledger against User A. A Map of Maps allows instant dual updates without searching arrays or instantiating redundant transaction objects.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the internal data structure used inside the Group class to maintain the master balance sheet mapping who owes whom. Why is a double HashMap (Map of Maps) the optimal choice?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v31-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Transaction Simplification: Net Balance Phase", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nInstead of evaluating every specific directed edge (A owes B ₹50, A owes C ₹20), the algorithm simplifies the graph into net flow.\\n\\n1. Initialize a flat map: `std::unordered_map<std::string, double> netBalances;` initialized to 0 for all users.\\n2. Iterate through every edge in the $O(N^2)$ ledger.\\n3. If `A owes B ₹50`:\\n   * `netBalances[\\"A\\"] -= 50;` (Debtor loses net worth)\\n   * `netBalances[\\"B\\"] += 50;` (Creditor gains net worth)\\n4. After fully traversing the ledger, the system ignores who owes whom specifically. It simply produces a flattened list where positive numbers indicate global Creditors (need to be paid) and negative numbers indicate global Debtors (need to pay). The sum of all values in this array is mathematically guaranteed to be 0.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the Splitwise Debt Simplification algorithm, step 1 is calculating the Net Balance for every user. Walk through how an $O(N^2)$ directed graph of individual debts is flattened into a 1D array of Net Balances.", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v31-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Transaction Simplification: The Greedy Clearing Phase", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. Split the net balances into two Max-Heaps (or sorted arrays): `Creditors` (highest positive balance at the top) and `Debtors` (highest absolute negative balance at the top).\\n2. **Greedy Matching:** Pop the largest Creditor (e.g., A needs ₹1000) and the largest Debtor (e.g., B owes ₹800).\\n3. Settle the maximum possible overlap. B pays A ₹800.\\n4. Update states: B's debt is cleared (₹0). A still needs ₹200. A is pushed back into the Creditor heap with ₹200.\\nBy aggressively matching the largest debts with the largest credits, the algorithm clears massive obligations in single transactions, guaranteeing the overall ledger is settled in $O(N)$ transactions, entirely bypassing the original chaotic spaghetti graph.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Once the Creditor and Debtor queues are established in the Debt Simplification algorithm, how does the greedy sorting logic mathematically minimize the total number of transactions required?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v31-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Enforcing Domain Rules (Group Exiting)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThis validation must be placed at the core domain level, specifically inside the `Group::removeMember(userId)` method.\\n\\nBefore executing the list removal, the method invokes a private helper `hasUnsettledBalances(userId)`.\\nThis helper queries the `groupBalances` map. It iterates through all ledger entries for that user. If ANY entry evaluates to a non-zero value (`std::abs(balance) > 0.01`), the method aborts the removal, returns false, and throws an `IllegalStateException`. Placing this at the lowest domain level guarantees that no UI bug or API orchestration flaw can ever bypass the financial security constraint.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "A domain rule states: \\"A user cannot leave a group if they have unsettled balances.\\" Architecturally, where is this validation placed to ensure data integrity is never compromised?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. Modeling the Domain\n* **Entities:** `User`, `Group`, `Expense`, and `Split`.\n* **State Management:** A `User` maintains a personal balance map of what they owe others outside of groups. A `Group` maintains a master ledger: a double map `Map<String, Double Map<String,>>` mapping `User A -> User B -> Debt Amount`.\n\n### 💡 2. The Strategy for Splitting Mathematics\n* An Expense of ₹1000 can be split in multiple ways. We utilize the **Strategy Pattern** to handle the permutations:\n  * **`EqualSplitStrategy`**: Divides the total by the number of participants.\n  * **`ExactSplitStrategy`**: Validates that the sum of exact input amounts equals the total expense.\n  * **`PercentSplitStrategy`**: Validates that percentages sum to 100 and maps the calculations.\n\n### 💡 3. Transaction Simplification Algorithm\n* **The Problem:** If A owes B ₹100, B owes C ₹100, and C owes A ₹100, executing three bank transfers is inefficient when the net debt is zero.\n* **The Greedy Solution:**\n  1. Calculate the **Net Balance** for every user (Total Credits - Total Debits).\n  2. Separate users into two lists: **Debtors** (negative balance) and **Creditors** (positive balance).\n  3. Sort both lists by absolute amount descending.\n  4. Take the largest Debtor and largest Creditor. Have the Debtor pay the Creditor the minimum of their two balances. Update their net values.\n  5. Repeat until all balances reach zero. This mathematically minimizes the total number of transactions across the network.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	LLD Project: Splitwise Clone	Architect an expense-sharing application. Model Users, Groups, and Expenses. Integrate Strategy patterns for splits and a Greedy Algorithm to simplify transactions.	96	Hard	Low Level Design	45	[{"id": "lld-v31-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Strategy Pattern for Expense Splitting", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `Expense` creation flow is decoupled from mathematical execution.\\nWe define an `ISplitStrategy` interface with a `calculateSplits(amount, users, parameters)` method.\\n1. `EqualSplitStrategy` ignores parameters, calculates `amount / users.size()`, and returns the split array.\\n2. `ExactSplitStrategy` iterates over user-provided parameters, ensures `sum(exact_values) == amount`, and assigns the splits.\\n3. `PercentSplitStrategy` ensures `sum(percentages) == 100`, calculates the fractional amounts, and returns the splits.\\nThe `ExpenseManager` dynamically invokes the correct strategy without resorting to bloated switch-case mathematics.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How is the Strategy Pattern utilized in the Splitwise LLD to handle varying logic for Equal, Exact, and Percentage-based expense splitting?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v31-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Designing the Group Ledger Data Structure", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `Group` maintains the balance sheet using: `std::unordered_map<std::string, std::unordered_map<std::string, double>> groupBalances;`\\n\\n**The Optimal Choice:**\\n1. **Fast Retrieval:** To find out exactly how much User A owes User B, the system executes `groupBalances[\\"UserA\\"][\\"UserB\\"]`, resolving in $O(1)$ constant time.\\n2. **Symmetric Adjustments:** When User A pays User B ₹100, the system must symmetrically adjust records: it increments User A's ledger against User B, and decrements User B's ledger against User A. A Map of Maps allows instant dual updates without searching arrays or instantiating redundant transaction objects.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the internal data structure used inside the Group class to maintain the master balance sheet mapping who owes whom. Why is a double HashMap (Map of Maps) the optimal choice?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v31-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Transaction Simplification: Net Balance Phase", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nInstead of evaluating every specific directed edge (A owes B ₹50, A owes C ₹20), the algorithm simplifies the graph into net flow.\\n\\n1. Initialize a flat map: `std::unordered_map<std::string, double> netBalances;` initialized to 0 for all users.\\n2. Iterate through every edge in the $O(N^2)$ ledger.\\n3. If `A owes B ₹50`:\\n   * `netBalances[\\"A\\"] -= 50;` (Debtor loses net worth)\\n   * `netBalances[\\"B\\"] += 50;` (Creditor gains net worth)\\n4. After fully traversing the ledger, the system ignores who owes whom specifically. It simply produces a flattened list where positive numbers indicate global Creditors (need to be paid) and negative numbers indicate global Debtors (need to pay). The sum of all values in this array is mathematically guaranteed to be 0.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In the Splitwise Debt Simplification algorithm, step 1 is calculating the Net Balance for every user. Walk through how an $O(N^2)$ directed graph of individual debts is flattened into a 1D array of Net Balances.", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v31-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Transaction Simplification: The Greedy Clearing Phase", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. Split the net balances into two Max-Heaps (or sorted arrays): `Creditors` (highest positive balance at the top) and `Debtors` (highest absolute negative balance at the top).\\n2. **Greedy Matching:** Pop the largest Creditor (e.g., A needs ₹1000) and the largest Debtor (e.g., B owes ₹800).\\n3. Settle the maximum possible overlap. B pays A ₹800.\\n4. Update states: B's debt is cleared (₹0). A still needs ₹200. A is pushed back into the Creditor heap with ₹200.\\nBy aggressively matching the largest debts with the largest credits, the algorithm clears massive obligations in single transactions, guaranteeing the overall ledger is settled in $O(N)$ transactions, entirely bypassing the original chaotic spaghetti graph.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Once the Creditor and Debtor queues are established in the Debt Simplification algorithm, how does the greedy sorting logic mathematically minimize the total number of transactions required?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v31-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Enforcing Domain Rules (Group Exiting)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThis validation must be placed at the core domain level, specifically inside the `Group::removeMember(userId)` method.\\n\\nBefore executing the list removal, the method invokes a private helper `hasUnsettledBalances(userId)`.\\nThis helper queries the `groupBalances` map. It iterates through all ledger entries for that user. If ANY entry evaluates to a non-zero value (`std::abs(balance) > 0.01`), the method aborts the removal, returns false, and throws an `IllegalStateException`. Placing this at the lowest domain level guarantees that no UI bug or API orchestration flaw can ever bypass the financial security constraint.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "A domain rule states: \\"A user cannot leave a group if they have unsettled balances.\\" Architecturally, where is this validation placed to ensure data integrity is never compromised?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
303	Pair Sum & Complement Lookups: Two Sum (Boolean Check & Index Retrieval)	dsa-two-sum-complement-hashing-and-two-pointers	Master the classic Two Sum pattern using Brute Force, Hash Map complement lookups for index retrieval, and two-pointer greedy convergence on sorted arrays.	2026-10-02 22:33:52.47545+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Pair Sum & Complement Lookups: Two Sum (Boolean Check & Index Retrieval)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UXDSeD9mN-k", "title": "Lecture 21: Two Sum — Check if a pair with given sum exists in Array", "duration": "35m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/two-sum-ii-input-array-is-sorted/", "title": "Two Sum II - Input Array Is Sorted", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use converging left and right pointers to find two numbers that add up to the target in O(1) extra space."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/max-number-of-k-sum-pairs/", "title": "Max Number of K-Sum Pairs", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Apply sorting + two pointers or frequency hashing to count disjoint pairs summing to K."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/count-pairs-with-given-sum5022/1", "title": "Count pairs with given sum", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Use a hash map to count all index pairs whose values add up to the target sum."}]	Module 9: Medium Array Patterns & Classic Named Algorithms	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array of integers and a `target` sum, the **Two Sum** problem asks two common variations in interviews:\n1. **Variety 1 (Yes / No):** Return `YES` if any two elements add up to `target`, or `NO` otherwise.\n2. **Variety 2 (Return Indices):** Return the exact indices `[i, j]` of the two elements that add up to `target`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Better → Optimal)\n- **1. Brute Force (O(N²) Time, O(1) Space):**\n  - Use two nested loops (`i` from `0` to `n - 1`, and `j` from `i + 1` to `n - 1`) to check every pair `arr[i] + arr[j] == target`.\n- **2. Hash Map Complement Lookup (Optimal for Variety 2 — O(N) or O(N log N) Time, O(N) Space):**\n  - As you iterate at element `arr[i]`, calculate how much more you need: `more = target - arr[i]`.\n  - Check if `more` is already stored inside your hash map (`mpp`). If it is, return `{mpp[more], i}` (or `"YES"`).\n  - Otherwise, store the current element and its index in the map (`mpp[arr[i]] = i`) and move on.\n- **3. Sorting + Two Pointers (Optimal for Variety 1 without Extra Space — O(N log N) Time, O(1) Space):**\n  - Sort the array first, then place `left = 0` at the start and `right = n - 1` at the end.\n  - If `arr[left] + arr[right] < target`, move `left++` to increase the sum.\n  - If `arr[left] + arr[right] > target`, move `right--` to decrease the sum.\n\n### 💻 Hash Map Two Sum Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> twoSumIndices(vector<int> &nums, int target) {\n    unordered_map<int, int> mpp; // Stores {element -> index}\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {\n        int more = target - nums[i];\n        if (mpp.find(more) != mpp.end()) {\n            return {mpp[more], i};\n        }\n        mpp[nums[i]] = i;\n    }\n    return {-1, -1};\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22210	Back of Envelope Calculation	hld-back-of-envelope-calculation	Learn how to estimate server capacity, QPS (Queries Per Second), and storage requirements for massive scalable systems using rough math approximations.	2026-10-07 16:33:42.32487+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Back of Envelope Calculation\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/DwqTon7ZS_s", "title": "Back of Envelope Calculation - System Design Concept", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "hld-v5-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Purpose of Back of Envelope Estimations", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nEstimations reveal the true scale of the problem. Without numbers, architecture choices are arbitrary guesses.\\n\\nThey mitigate two primary engineering risks:\\n1. **Under-provisioning (Outages):** If you design for 100 QPS but calculations reveal a peak of 8,000 QPS, a single SQL database will collapse under connection limits and lock contention. The calculation demonstrates the immediate need for caching, connection pooling, and sharding.\\n2. **Over-provisioning (Wasted Spend):** If a service expects 10 QPS, designing a multi-region distributed cluster is massive over-engineering. Calculations justify simple, maintainable architectures.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why must Back of Envelope calculations precede architecture diagrams in a System Design interview? What engineering risks do they mitigate?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v5-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Calculating QPS and Peak QPS", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**1. Calculate DAU:**\\n* MAU = 300 Million\\n* DAU = 50% of 300M = 150 Million Daily Active Users.\\n\\n**2. Total Daily Requests:**\\n* Total Requests = 150M DAU × 2 requests/day = 300 Million requests/day.\\n\\n**3. Average QPS:**\\n* Seconds in a day: 86,400 seconds ≈ 100,000 (10^5) seconds.\\n* Average QPS = 300,000,000 / 100,000 = **3,000 QPS**.\\n\\n**4. Peak QPS:**\\n* Peak traffic multiplier is typically 2×.\\n* Peak QPS = 3,000 × 2 = **6,000 QPS**.\\n\\nThe system must comfortably handle 6,000 concurrent requests per second during peak hours.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "A Twitter-like application has 300M MAU with 50% daily engagement. Each daily active user generates 2 requests/day. Estimate the Average QPS and Peak QPS using standard approximation rules.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v5-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: 5-Year Storage Estimation", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**1. Daily Media Volume:**\\n* Total daily requests = 300 Million.\\n* Media uploads = 10% of 300M = 30 Million uploads/day.\\n\\n**2. Daily Storage:**\\n* 30 Million × 1 MB = 30,000,000 MB/day = 30,000 GB/day = **30 TB/day**.\\n\\n**3. 5-Year Total:**\\n* Annual Storage = 30 TB/day × 365 days ≈ 11,000 TB/year (11 Petabytes/year).\\n* 5-Year Storage = 11 PB/year × 5 years = **55 Petabytes (PB)**.\\n\\nBlock-storage relational databases cannot handle this. The system strictly requires an object storage service like AWS S3 designed for petabyte-scale unstructured data.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Using the previous app scale (150M DAU, 300M daily requests), assume 10% of requests involve uploading a 1 MB image. Calculate the estimated storage capacity required to retain media for 5 years.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v5-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Latency Numbers: Memory vs Disk", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Main Memory (RAM):** Reads execute in ~100 nanoseconds.\\n* **Physical Disk (SSD/HDD):** Reads execute in ~1 to 10 milliseconds.\\n\\nRAM is **10,000× to 100,000× faster** than disk. If thousands of read requests hit a disk-bound relational database every second, accumulated I/O wait times create server queue backpressure. Placing an in-memory cache (Redis/Memcached) in front of the database intercepts hot read queries in nanoseconds, eliminating disk seeks and keeping API latency under 10ms.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why is the physical latency difference between RAM and Disk crucial when justifying the introduction of an in-memory caching tier (e.g., Redis)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v5-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Network Latency vs CDN Justification", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nLight through trans-oceanic fiber optic cables incurs a mandatory physical round-trip latency of ~150–200ms between India and California. Transferring a 5 MB payload across that distance takes multiple TCP round-trips, producing severe buffering.\\n\\n**CDN Justification:**\\n1. **Latency Reduction:** By caching the video at a local edge server in Mumbai, round-trip latency drops from 200ms to ~15ms.\\n2. **Bandwidth Savings:** Serving 1M requests for 5 MB files from the California origin server incurs 5 Petabytes of costly cloud egress bandwidth and risks crashing the origin. The CDN absorbs ~95% of traffic at edge nodes.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "If a user in India requests a 5 MB video from a California origin server, how do Back of Envelope principles justify deploying a CDN?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	High Level Design	### 📌 1. What is Back of Envelope Estimation?\n* Using rough mathematical approximations to size hardware requirements (Servers, CPU, RAM, Storage, Bandwidth) before writing architecture code.\n* **Rule of Thumb:** Round numbers up to clean powers of 10 (e.g., treat 86,400 seconds in a day as 100,000 seconds for easy mental math).\n\n### 💡 2. Core Estimation Formulas\n* **Daily Active Users (DAU):** `Monthly Active Users (MAU) × Daily Engagement %`.\n* **Queries Per Second (QPS):** `(DAU × Requests per user per day) / 100,000 seconds`.\n* **Peak QPS:** Estimated as `2 × Average QPS`. Systems must be provisioned to absorb Peak QPS without degrading.\n* **Storage Sizing:** `Daily Writes × Average Payload Size × 365 days × Retention Years (e.g., 5 years)`.\n\n### 💡 3. Critical Latency Numbers\n* **RAM vs Disk:** Reading from Main Memory (RAM / Redis) takes ~100 nanoseconds. Reading from a physical disk (HDD/SSD seek) takes ~1–10 milliseconds. **Memory is ~100,000× faster than disk.**\n* **Network Delay:** Cross-continental round-trips take ~150ms. Serving content via CDNs at the edge drops latency to ~10–20ms.	Piyush Garg	https://www.youtube.com/@piyushgarg195	28	Back of Envelope Calculation	Learn how to estimate server capacity, QPS (Queries Per Second), and storage requirements for massive scalable systems using rough math approximations.	17	Hard	High Level Design	5	[{"id": "hld-v5-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Purpose of Back of Envelope Estimations", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nEstimations reveal the true scale of the problem. Without numbers, architecture choices are arbitrary guesses.\\n\\nThey mitigate two primary engineering risks:\\n1. **Under-provisioning (Outages):** If you design for 100 QPS but calculations reveal a peak of 8,000 QPS, a single SQL database will collapse under connection limits and lock contention. The calculation demonstrates the immediate need for caching, connection pooling, and sharding.\\n2. **Over-provisioning (Wasted Spend):** If a service expects 10 QPS, designing a multi-region distributed cluster is massive over-engineering. Calculations justify simple, maintainable architectures.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why must Back of Envelope calculations precede architecture diagrams in a System Design interview? What engineering risks do they mitigate?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v5-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Calculating QPS and Peak QPS", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**1. Calculate DAU:**\\n* MAU = 300 Million\\n* DAU = 50% of 300M = 150 Million Daily Active Users.\\n\\n**2. Total Daily Requests:**\\n* Total Requests = 150M DAU × 2 requests/day = 300 Million requests/day.\\n\\n**3. Average QPS:**\\n* Seconds in a day: 86,400 seconds ≈ 100,000 (10^5) seconds.\\n* Average QPS = 300,000,000 / 100,000 = **3,000 QPS**.\\n\\n**4. Peak QPS:**\\n* Peak traffic multiplier is typically 2×.\\n* Peak QPS = 3,000 × 2 = **6,000 QPS**.\\n\\nThe system must comfortably handle 6,000 concurrent requests per second during peak hours.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "A Twitter-like application has 300M MAU with 50% daily engagement. Each daily active user generates 2 requests/day. Estimate the Average QPS and Peak QPS using standard approximation rules.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v5-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: 5-Year Storage Estimation", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**1. Daily Media Volume:**\\n* Total daily requests = 300 Million.\\n* Media uploads = 10% of 300M = 30 Million uploads/day.\\n\\n**2. Daily Storage:**\\n* 30 Million × 1 MB = 30,000,000 MB/day = 30,000 GB/day = **30 TB/day**.\\n\\n**3. 5-Year Total:**\\n* Annual Storage = 30 TB/day × 365 days ≈ 11,000 TB/year (11 Petabytes/year).\\n* 5-Year Storage = 11 PB/year × 5 years = **55 Petabytes (PB)**.\\n\\nBlock-storage relational databases cannot handle this. The system strictly requires an object storage service like AWS S3 designed for petabyte-scale unstructured data.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Using the previous app scale (150M DAU, 300M daily requests), assume 10% of requests involve uploading a 1 MB image. Calculate the estimated storage capacity required to retain media for 5 years.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v5-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Latency Numbers: Memory vs Disk", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n* **Main Memory (RAM):** Reads execute in ~100 nanoseconds.\\n* **Physical Disk (SSD/HDD):** Reads execute in ~1 to 10 milliseconds.\\n\\nRAM is **10,000× to 100,000× faster** than disk. If thousands of read requests hit a disk-bound relational database every second, accumulated I/O wait times create server queue backpressure. Placing an in-memory cache (Redis/Memcached) in front of the database intercepts hot read queries in nanoseconds, eliminating disk seeks and keeping API latency under 10ms.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why is the physical latency difference between RAM and Disk crucial when justifying the introduction of an in-memory caching tier (e.g., Redis)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v5-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Network Latency vs CDN Justification", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nLight through trans-oceanic fiber optic cables incurs a mandatory physical round-trip latency of ~150–200ms between India and California. Transferring a 5 MB payload across that distance takes multiple TCP round-trips, producing severe buffering.\\n\\n**CDN Justification:**\\n1. **Latency Reduction:** By caching the video at a local edge server in Mumbai, round-trip latency drops from 200ms to ~15ms.\\n2. **Bandwidth Savings:** Serving 1M requests for 5 MB files from the California origin server incurs 5 Petabytes of costly cloud egress bandwidth and risks crashing the origin. The CDN absorbs ~95% of traffic at edge nodes.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "If a user in India requests a 5 MB video from a California origin server, how do Back of Envelope principles justify deploying a CDN?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
310	Right-to-Left Suffix Maximum Scan: Finding Leaders in an Array	dsa-leaders-in-an-array-suffix-max	Find all leader elements that are strictly greater than all elements to their right using an optimal right-to-left suffix maximum scan in O(N) time.	2026-10-02 22:33:52.499812+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Right-to-Left Suffix Maximum Scan: Finding Leaders in an Array\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cHrH9CQ8pmY", "title": "Lecture 28: Leaders in an Array", "duration": "24m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/replace-elements-with-greatest-element-on-right-side/", "title": "Replace Elements with Greatest Element on Right Side", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Scan backward from right to left while maintaining the running suffix maximum."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/next-greater-element-i/", "title": "Next Greater Element I", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Identify the next greater element to the right for each target query."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/elements-before-which-no-element-is-bigger0632/1", "title": "Elements before which no element is bigger", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Count elements that are strictly greater than all elements preceding them."}]	Module 10: Array Permutations, Sequence Hashing & In-Place Matrix Manipulation	### 📌 Topic Overview\nAn element in an array is called a **Leader** if it is greater than all the elements to its right. Note that the rightmost element of the array is always a leader because there are no elements to its right.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Brute Force (O(N²) Time):** For every element `arr[i]`, scan all elements to its right (`j = i + 1` to `n - 1`) to check if anything is larger.\n- **Optimal Suffix Maximum Scan (O(N) Time, O(1) Extra Space):**\n  - If `arr[i]` is greater than the **maximum element to its right**, then it is automatically greater than *every* element to its right!\n  - Start iterating from the back of the array (`i = n - 1` down to `0`) while maintaining `maxi = INT_MIN`.\n  - Whenever `arr[i] > maxi`, add `arr[i]` to your leaders list and update `maxi = arr[i]`.\n  - Finally, reverse (or sort, depending on what the question asks) the collected leaders list.\n\n### 💻 Right-to-Left Leader Scan Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> findLeaders(vector<int> &a) {\n    vector<int> ans;\n    int maxi = INT_MIN;\n    int n = a.size();\n\n    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n        if (a[i] > maxi) {\n            ans.push_back(a[i]);\n        }\n        maxi = max(maxi, a[i]);\n    }\n    reverse(ans.begin(), ans.end()); // Restore original left-to-right order\n    return ans;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
