7020	Contiguous Match Reset: Longest Common Substring	dsa-longest-common-substring-dp-on-strings	Find the longest contiguous common substring by maintaining match streaks and resetting cells to zero upon character mismatches.	2026-10-03 11:31:13.752397+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Contiguous Match Reset: Longest Common Substring\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_wP9mWNPL5w", "title": "Lecture 208: Longest Common Substring (DP 27)", "duration": "16m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/longest-common-substring1452/1", "title": "Longest Common Substring", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given two strings S1 and S2, find the length of the longest common contiguous substring."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-length-of-repeated-subarray/", "title": "Maximum Length of Repeated Subarray (LC 718)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the maximum length of a contiguous subarray that appears in both integer arrays (identical to Longest Common Substring)."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-palindromic-substring/", "title": "Longest Palindromic Substring (LC 5)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Explore contiguous substring matching constraints."}]	Module 31: Dynamic Programming on Strings: LCS, Substrings & Palindromic Subsequences	### 📌 Topic Overview\nUnlike a *subsequence*, a **Substring** must be **contiguous** (consecutive characters with zero gaps). Given two strings `s1` and `s2`, find the length of their **Longest Common Substring**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The 1-Line Modification From LCS (`Reset to 0 on Mismatch!`):**\n  - In LCS, when `s1[i - 1] != s2[j - 1]`, we carried over previous matches via `max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])`.\n  - In **Longest Common Substring**, characters must be consecutive! So what does `dp[i][j]` represent?\n    - `dp[i][j]` = length of the common substring **ending at index `i - 1` in `s1` and index `j - 1` in `s2`**.\n    - **If `s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]`:** Extend the consecutive streak from `(i - 1, j - 1)`:\n      **`dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i - 1][j - 1]`**, and update **`ans = max(ans, dp[i][j])`**!\n    - **Else (`s1[i - 1] != s2[j - 1]`):** The contiguous streak is broken! Reset **`dp[i][j] = 0`**!\n- **Why the Answer Is the Global Max Across the Whole Table (Not `dp[n][m]`):**\n  - The longest common substring can end anywhere in the middle of `s1` and `s2`, so we track `ans = max(ans, cur[j])` across every cell `(i, j)`!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * M)` Time and `O(M)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Longest Common Substring (Space Optimized) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint longestCommonSubstring(string &s1, string &s2) {\n    int n = s1.size(), m = s2.size();\n    vector<int> prev(m + 1, 0), cur(m + 1, 0);\n    int ans = 0;\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {\n            if (s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]) {\n                cur[j] = 1 + prev[j - 1];\n                ans = max(ans, cur[j]); // Track global maximum across all cells!\n            } else {\n                cur[j] = 0; // Contiguity broken -> reset to 0!\n            }\n        }\n        prev = cur;\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Returning `prev[m]` (`dp[n][m]`) Instead of `ans`:** `dp[n][m]` only gives the length of the common suffix ending at the very last characters of `s1` and `s2`. Always return the running maximum `ans`!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
