on": "Construct a BST using upper bound validation on preorder sequence."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/tree-from-postorder-and-inorder/1", "title": "Tree from Postorder and Inorder", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Reconstruct a tree using its inorder and postorder traversals."}]	Module 18: Binary Trees: Node Distances, Tree Modifications & Reconstruction	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven two integer arrays `inorder` and `postorder`, reconstruct and return the unique binary tree.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Root is at the End of Postorder:**\n  - Because postorder is `Left → Right → Root`, the root of the current subtree is always **`postorder[postEnd]`**.\n- **Calculating Window Offsets:**\n  - Find root in inorder: `inRoot = inMap[postorder[postEnd]]`.\n  - Count left subtree size: `numsLeft = inRoot - inStart`.\n  - **Left Subtree Windows:**\n    - Postorder: `[postStart ... postStart + numsLeft - 1]`\n    - Inorder: `[inStart ... inRoot - 1]`\n  - **Right Subtree Windows:**\n    - Postorder: `[postStart + numsLeft ... postEnd - 1]` (skipping the last element, which is the current root!)\n    - Inorder: `[inRoot + 1 ... inEnd]`\n\n### 💻 Construct from Postorder and Inorder Blueprint\n```cpp\nTreeNode* build(vector<int> &inorder, int is, int ie,\n                vector<int> &postorder, int ps, int pe,\n                unordered_map<int, int> &inMap) {\n    if (ps > pe || is > ie) return nullptr;\n\n    TreeNode* root = new TreeNode(postorder[pe]);\n    int inRoot = inMap[postorder[pe]];\n    int numsLeft = inRoot - is;\n\n    root->left = build(inorder, is, inRoot - 1,\n                       postorder, ps, ps + numsLeft - 1, inMap);\n    root->right = build(inorder, inRoot + 1, ie,\n                        postorder, ps + numsLeft, pe - 1, inMap);\n    return root;\n}\n\nTreeNode* buildTree(vector<int> &inorder, vector<int> &postorder) {\n    unordered_map<int, int> inMap;\n    for (int i = 0; i < inorder.size(); i++) inMap[inorder[i]] = i;\n    return build(inorder, 0, inorder.size() - 1,\n                 postorder, 0, postorder.size() - 1, inMap);\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
15094	Monotonic Decreasing Deque: Sliding Window Maximum in O(N)	dsa-sliding-window-maximum-monotonic-deque	Master Monotonic Decreasing Deque: Sliding Window Maximum in O(N) with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:30:52.974458+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Monotonic Decreasing Deque: Sliding Window Maximum in O(N)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NwBvene4Imo", "title": "Monotonic Decreasing Deque: Sliding Window Maximum in O(N) - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sliding-window-maximum/", "title": "LeetCode 239: Sliding Window Maximum", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "You are given an array of integers nums and a sliding window of size k moving from left to right. Return the max sliding window."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-continuous-subarray-with-absolute-diff-less-than-or-equal-to-limit/", "title": "LeetCode 1438: Longest Continuous Subarray With Absolute Diff Less Than or Equal to Limit", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Maintain both a max-deque and a min-deque simultaneously to track the window's max - min in O(1) time."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/maximum-of-all-subarrays-of-size-k3101/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Maximum of all subarrays of size k", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the maximum element in every contiguous subarray of size k in O(N) time."}]	Module 44: Advanced Stack, Monotonic Deque & Cache Design: Stock Span, Sliding Window Max, Celebrity & LRU/LFU Caches	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an integer array `arr` of size `N` and a sliding window of size `k`, return a list containing the **maximum element in each window of size `k`** as it slides from left to right (`N - k + 1` windows total).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute O((N - K) * K) → Optimal Monotonic Deque O(2N))\n- **Why We Need a Double-Ended Queue (`std::deque<int> dq`):**\n  - We need **two operations simultaneously**:\n    1. **Evict Out-of-Window Elements from the FRONT (`dq.pop_front()`):** When we are at index `i`, the current window covers `[i - k + 1 ... i]`. Any index **`<= i - k`** sitting at `dq.front()` has fallen out of the window!\n    2. **Maintain Monotonic Decreasing Order from the BACK (`dq.pop_back()`):** Before pushing index `i` at the back, any smaller or equal elements **`arr[dq.back()] <= arr[i]`** inside the deque can never be the maximum of this or any future window—so pop them from the back!\n  - Because `dq` is kept in **strictly decreasing order of values from front to back**, the **maximum element of the current window is ALWAYS sitting at `arr[dq.front()]`** in `O(1)` time!\n- **Complexity:** **`O(2N)`** Time (each index is pushed once and popped at most once) and **`O(K)`** Auxiliary Space!\n\n### 💻 Sliding Window Maximum (Monotonic Deque) Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> maxSlidingWindow(vector<int> &arr, int k) {\n    int n = arr.size();\n    deque<int> dq; // Stores indices in decreasing order of their array values\n    vector<int> ans;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        // 1. Remove front index if it has fallen outside the current window [i - k + 1 .. i]\n        if (!dq.empty() && dq.front() <= i - k) {\n            dq.pop_front();\n        }\n\n        // 2. Maintain monotonic decreasing order: pop smaller/equal elements from the back\n        while (!dq.empty() && arr[dq.back()] <= arr[i]) {\n            dq.pop_back();\n        }\n\n        // 3. Push current index i into the back of the deque\n        dq.push_back(i);\n\n        // 4. Once the first full window of size k is formed (i >= k - 1), record the front max!\n        if (i >= k - 1) {\n            ans.push_back(arr[dq.front()]);\n        }\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Storing Values `arr[i]` Instead of Indices `i` in `dq`:** If you only store values in `dq`, you cannot tell when `dq.front()` has stepped outside the `k`-sized window (`dq.front() <= i - k`)! Always store **indices `i`** in `dq`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
