ms/optimize-water-distribution-in-a-village/", "title": "Optimize Water Distribution in a Village (LC 1168)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Add a virtual well node 0 and find the Minimum Spanning Tree using Prim's or Kruskal's algorithm."}]	Module 25: Negative-Weight Shortest Paths, All-Pairs Shortest Paths & Minimum Spanning Trees	### 📌 Topic Overview\n**Prim's Algorithm** builds a **Minimum Spanning Tree (MST)** greedily by starting at an arbitrary node (`0`) and repeatedly picking the **minimum-weight edge** that connects a visited MST node to an unvisited node using a **Min-Heap Priority Queue**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Critical Difference From BFS — Mark Visited ONLY When POPPING:**\n  - In standard BFS, we mark a node visited as soon as we *push* it into the queue.\n  - In **Prim's Algorithm**, multiple edges of different weights (`10`, `4`, `2`) can point to the same unvisited node `v`. If you mark `v` visited when pushing `{10, v}`, you will ignore the cheaper `{2, v}` edge later!\n  - **Rule:** Never mark `seen[v] = 1` when pushing! Only mark **`seen[u] = 1` at the exact moment `u` is POPPED from `pq.top()`** (after checking `if (seen[u] == 1) continue;`).\n- **Algorithm Steps (`O(E log E)` Time, `O(V + E)` Space):**\n  1. Initialize `seen[V] = {0}`, `mstSum = 0`, and push `{0, 0}` (`{weight, node}`) into the min-heap `pq`. (If you also need the MST edges, store `{weight, {node, parent}}`).\n  2. While `!pq.empty()`:\n     - Pop `{wt, u} = pq.top()`.\n     - If `seen[u] == 1`, `continue` (already part of the MST).\n     - Mark **`seen[u] = 1`** and add **`mstSum += wt`**.\n     - Loop through all neighbors `{v, edW}` in `adj[u]`: if `!seen[v]`, push `{edW, v}` into `pq`.\n\n### 💻 Prim's Algorithm (MST Weight Sum) Blueprint\n```cpp\nint spanningTreePrims(int V, vector<vector<int>> adj[]) {\n    // Min-heap storing {edgeWeight, node}\n    priority_queue<pair<int, int>, vector<pair<int, int>>, greater<pair<int, int>>> pq;\n    vector<int> seen(V, 0);\n\n    pq.push({0, 0}); // Start at node 0 with weight 0\n    int mstSum = 0;\n\n    while (!pq.empty()) {\n        auto [wt, u] = pq.top();\n        pq.pop();\n\n        // Skip if node u is already included in the MST\n        if (seen[u] == 1) continue;\n\n        // Include node u in the MST now!\n        seen[u] = 1;\n        mstSum += wt;\n\n        for (const auto &edge : adj[u]) {\n            int v = edge[0];\n            int edW = edge[1];\n            if (!seen[v]) {\n                pq.push({edW, v});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return mstSum;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Marking `seen[v] = 1` Inside the `for` Loop:** Marking `seen[v] = 1` when pushing into the priority queue locks in the *first* edge seen to `v` rather than the *minimum-weight* edge to `v`! Always mark `seen[u] = 1` **only after popping `u` from `pq.top()`**.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
15086	Prefix/Suffix Envelopes vs. Converging Two Pointers: Trapping Rainwater	dsa-trapping-rainwater-two-pointers	Master Prefix/Suffix Envelopes vs. Converging Two Pointers: Trapping Rainwater with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:30:52.96862+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Prefix/Suffix Envelopes vs. Converging Two Pointers: Trapping Rainwater\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1_5VuquLbXg", "title": "Prefix/Suffix Envelopes vs. Converging Two Pointers: Trapping Rainwater - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/trapping-rain-water/", "title": "LeetCode 42: Trapping Rain Water", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Given n non-negative integers representing an elevation map where the width of each bar is 1, compute how much water it can trap after raining."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/container-with-most-water/", "title": "LeetCode 11: Container With Most Water", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use converging two pointers moving the shorter boundary inward to maximize water area between two lines."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/trapping-rain-water-1587115621/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Trapping Rain Water", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Compute the total volume of trapped rainwater across N elevation bars in O(N) time and O(1) space."}]	Module 43: Monotonic Stack & Contribution Algorithms: Next/Previous Greater/Smaller, Rainwater & Subarray Aggregations	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array `arr` of `N` non-negative integers representing building heights of width `1`, compute the **total units of rainwater trapped** between the buildings after it rains.\n\n### 💡 Progression (Prefix/Suffix Max O(2N) Space → Optimal Two-Pointer O(1) Space)\n- **The Per-Building Water Formula:**\n  - Water can sit on top of building `i` **if and only if** there is a taller building somewhere on its left (`leftMax`) AND a taller building somewhere on its right (`rightMax`).\n  - The water level at `i` is bottlenecked by the **smaller** of those two tallest walls:\n    **`water[i] = min(leftMax[i], rightMax[i]) - arr[i]`**!\n- **1. Approach 1 — Prefix Max & Suffix Max Arrays (`O(3N)` Time, `O(2N)` Space):**\n  - Precompute `prefixMax[i] = max(prefixMax[i - 1], arr[i])` and `suffixMax[i] = max(suffixMax[i + 1], arr[i])`, then sum `min(prefixMax[i], suffixMax[i]) - arr[i]`.\n- **2. Approach 2 — Optimal Converging Two Pointers (`O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Space!):**\n  - Why do we need *both* `leftMax` and `rightMax` if only the **smaller** one determines the water height?\n  - Maintain `l = 0, r = n - 1`, `lMax = 0, rMax = 0`, and `total = 0`.\n  - Loop **`while (l < r)`** and always process the **shorter building (`if (arr[l] <= arr[r])`)**:\n    - **If `arr[l] <= arr[r]`:** We are 100% certain there is a building on the right (`arr[r]`) at least as tall as `arr[l]` (and also `>= lMax`, because we only ever advance the smaller pointer!). So water at `l` depends **solely on `lMax`**:\n      - If `lMax > arr[l]`, add **`total += lMax - arr[l]`**; else update **`lMax = arr[l]`**.\n      - Advance **`l++`**.\n    - **Else (`arr[r] < arr[l]`):** Water at `r` depends **solely on `rMax`**:\n      - If `rMax > arr[r]`, add **`total += rMax - arr[r]`**; else update **`rMax = arr[r]`**.\n      - Advance **`r--`**.\n\n### 💻 Trapping Rainwater (Optimal O(N) Time, O(1) Space) Blueprint\n```cpp\nint trap(vector<int> &arr) {\n    int n = arr.size();\n    int l = 0, r = n - 1;\n    int lMax = 0, rMax = 0, total = 0;\n\n    while (l < r) {\n        // Always process the smaller boundary first!\n        if (arr[l] <= arr[r]) {\n            if (lMax > arr[l]) {\n                total += lMax - arr[l];\n            } else {\n                lMax = arr[l];\n            }\n            l++;\n        } else {\n            if (rMax > arr[r]) {\n                total += rMax - arr[r];\n            } else {\n                rMax = arr[r];\n            }\n            r--;\n        }\n    }\n    return total;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Comparing `if (lMax <= rMax)` without advancing the smaller bar `arr[l] <= arr[r]`:** Comparing `arr[l] <= arr[r]` guarantees that the opposite pointer's current bar (`arr[r]`) is a real physical wall at least as tall as `lMax`!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
