9275	1D Front Partitioning: Palindrome Partitioning II (Minimum Cuts)	dsa-palindrome-partitioning-ii-front-partition-dp	Reduce interval partition to 1D front partition DP f(i) = 1 + f(j+1) for each palindromic prefix s[i..j], subtracting 1 to find minimum cuts.	2026-10-03 14:56:36.887204+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: 1D Front Partitioning: Palindrome Partitioning II (Minimum Cuts)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_H8V5hJUGd0", "title": "Lecture 233: Palindrome Partitioning - II | Front Partition (DP 53)", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/palindrome-partitioning-ii/", "title": "Palindrome Partitioning II (LC 132)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Given a string s, partition s such that every substring of the partition is a palindrome. Return the minimum cuts needed."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/palindromic-patitioning4845/1", "title": "Palindromic Partitioning", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find the fewest cuts needed for palindrome partitioning of a given string."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/palindrome-partitioning/", "title": "Palindrome Partitioning (LC 131)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Contrast enumerating all palindrome partitions via backtracking with finding the minimum cuts via 1D Front Partition DP."}]	Module 34: Partition Dynamic Programming (MCM Pattern & Interval Splitting)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a string `s` of length `N`, find the **minimum number of cuts (partitions)** needed such that **every resulting substring is a palindrome**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **What Is "Front Partition DP" (`1D` State `f(i)` Instead of `2D` `f(i, j)`)?**\n  - Instead of partitioning in the middle like MCM, we only need to check prefixes starting from index `i`:\n  - If the substring **`s[i ... j]` is a palindrome**, we can make **1 cut right after `j`** and recursively solve the remaining suffix **`f(j + 1)`**!\n  - Because the right boundary is always `N - 1`, our state only needs **1 parameter `i`** (`O(N)` states instead of `O(N²)`!).\n- **Why We Subtract `1` at the End (`return dp[0] - 1`):**\n  - When `j == N - 1` (the very end of the string), our code counts `1 + f(N) = 1 + 0 = 1`, which places a partition cut **after the last character** of the string (e.g., `"a | b | c |"` counts 3 cuts instead of 2).\n  - Subtracting `1` at the very end (`dp[0] - 1`) removes that trailing cut after `N - 1`!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N²)` Time and `O(N)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Palindrome Partitioning II (Bottom-Up Tabulation) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nbool isPalindrome(int i, int j, const string &s) {\n    while (i < j) {\n        if (s[i] != s[j]) return false;\n        i++;\n        j--;\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n\nint minCut(string s) {\n    int n = s.size();\n    vector<int> dp(n + 1, 0);\n    dp[n] = 0; // Base case: empty suffix at index n requires 0 cuts\n\n    // Move i backwards from n - 1 down to 0\n    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n        int minCost = INT_MAX;\n        // Try every valid palindromic prefix s[i ... j]\n        for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n            if (isPalindrome(i, j, s)) {\n                int cost = 1 + dp[j + 1];\n                minCost = min(minCost, cost);\n            }\n        }\n        dp[i] = minCost;\n    }\n    // Subtract 1 to exclude the cut counted after the last character (index n - 1)\n    return dp[0] - 1;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting `- 1` on Return (`return dp[0] - 1`):** A string that is already a palindrome (like `"aba"`) takes `1` step in our recurrence (`"aba|"`)—subtracting `1` properly returns `0` cuts!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
