er": "IPv4 uses 32-bit addresses (running out of space); IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses, exponentially expanding the number of mathematically possible unique IPs."}, {"id": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["It identifies the physical ethernet cable.", "While the IP address routes traffic to the correct computer, the Port identifies the specific application, process, or service running on that computer.", "It translates domain names into IP addresses.", "It acts as a physical firewall switch."], "question": "What is the specific role of a 'Port' in network communication?", "explanation": "A server has one IP address, but runs many services. Traffic arriving on Port 80 goes to the web server, traffic on Port 22 goes to the SSH terminal, and Port 5432 goes to PostgreSQL.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "While the IP address routes traffic to the correct computer, the Port identifies the specific application, process, or service running on that computer."}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["Private IPs are encrypted; public IPs are plain text.", "Public IPs uniquely identify a network node globally on the open internet; Private IPs are only unique internally within a local area network (LAN) and cannot be routed on the open web.", "Public IPs are free; private IPs are paid.", "Private IPs are strictly for government use."], "question": "How does a public IP address differ from a private IP address?", "explanation": "Your home router has one Public IP visible to the world. Your laptop and phone use Private IPs (like 192.168.1.5) that only the router understands, utilizing NAT to talk to the outside world.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "Public IPs uniquely identify a network node globally on the open internet; Private IPs are only unique internally within a local area network (LAN) and cannot be routed on the open web."}, {"id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["A DNS record", "A Domain Name", "A MAC (Media Access Control) Address burned into the hardware's network interface card.", "An SSL Certificate"], "question": "At the Link Layer of network communication, what physical identifier is used to direct traffic between devices connected to the exact same local switch?", "explanation": "While IP addresses change dynamically as you move networks, the MAC address is a permanent hardware footprint used by local switches to beam data frames specifically to your exact device.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "A MAC (Media Access Control) Address burned into the hardware's network interface card."}, {"id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["To accelerate download speeds.", "To compress HTTP responses.", "To inspect, monitor, and strictly control incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predefined security rules, blocking unauthorized ports and protocols.", "To resolve IP collisions automatically."], "question": "What is the primary function of a Network Firewall?", "explanation": "A firewall acts as a bouncer at the door of your server, ensuring that only expected traffic (like HTTP on port 443) is allowed in, while silently dropping hacker scans on sensitive database ports.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "To inspect, monitor, and strictly control incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predefined security rules, blocking unauthorized ports and protocols."}]	\N	> **Architecture Overview:** Essential technical concepts and structural principles vital for robust, scalable web architectures. Focus deeply on the mechanical trade-offs discussed in this module.	Hayk Simonyan	https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan	\N	System Design Concepts: Networking Essentials	Comprehensive overview of System Design Concepts covering low-level transport mechanisms, architectural scaling, and system persistence.	6	Easy	Advanced Syste