590	Rotated Array Binary Search I: Identifying the Sorted Half in Distinct Elements	dsa-search-in-rotated-sorted-array-unique	Search for a target key in a pivoted rotated sorted array of unique integers in O(log N) time using the sorted half identification invariant.	2026-10-02 23:24:14.979036+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Rotated Array Binary Search I: Identifying the Sorted Half in Distinct Elements\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5qGrJbHhqFs", "title": "Lecture 48: Search in Rotated Sorted Array I", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/peak-index-in-a-mountain-array/", "title": "Peak Index in a Mountain Array", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use binary search slope comparison to locate the peak index in O(log N) time."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-minimum-in-rotated-sorted-array/", "title": "Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Identify the sorted half to find the pivot minimum in a rotated array."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/search-in-a-rotated-array4618/1", "title": "Search in a Rotated Array", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Locate the index of a target key in a rotated sorted array of distinct elements."}]	Module 12: 1D Binary Search, Bounds & Rotated Array Invariants	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a sorted array of **distinct** integers that has been rotated at some unknown pivot (e.g., `[7, 8, 9, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]`), find the index of `target` in **O(log2 N) time**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Standard Binary Search Fails:**\n  - In a rotated array, comparing `target` with `arr[mid]` alone is not enough to know whether `target` is on the left or right, because one of the halves contains the rotation drop.\n- **The Core Invariant ("Identify the Sorted Half"):**\n  - Whenever you split a rotated sorted array at `mid`, **at least one half (either `[low ... mid]` or `[mid ... high]`) is guaranteed to be completely sorted**!\n  - **Step 1:** Check if `arr[mid] == target`. If so, return `mid`.\n  - **Step 2:** Check if the **left half is sorted** (`arr[low] <= arr[mid]`):\n    - If `target` lies within the sorted left half (`arr[low] <= target && target <= arr[mid]`), eliminate the right half (`high = mid - 1`).\n    - Otherwise, eliminate the left half (`low = mid + 1`).\n  - **Step 3:** Otherwise, the **right half must be sorted** (`arr[mid] <= arr[high]`):\n    - If `target` lies within the sorted right half (`arr[mid] <= target && target <= arr[high]`), eliminate the left half (`low = mid + 1`).\n    - Otherwise, eliminate the right half (`high = mid - 1`).\n\n### 💻 Search in Rotated Sorted Array I Blueprint\n```cpp\nint searchRotated(vector<int> &arr, int target) {\n    int low = 0, high = (int)arr.size() - 1;\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;\n\n        // 1. If Left Half is sorted\n        if (arr[low] <= arr[mid]) {\n            if (arr[low] <= target && target <= arr[mid]) high = mid - 1;\n            else low = mid + 1;\n        }\n        // 2. Otherwise Right Half is sorted\n        else {\n            if (arr[mid] <= target && target <= arr[high]) low = mid + 1;\n            else high = mid - 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return -1;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
3	Event Loop, Libuv & Asynchronous Concurrency	event-loop-libuv-concurrency	Understanding microtasks, macrotasks, timers, and the libuv thread pool.	2026-09-27 22:28:22.294502+05:30	### 🎯 Core Concepts & Architecture Breakdown\nNode.js uses an event-driven, non-blocking I/O model that makes it lightweight and efficient. Although JavaScript is single-threaded in the V8 engine, Node.js achieves high concurrency via the **Libuv** C library.\n\n- **The Libuv Thread Pool**: By default, Libuv maintains a thread pool of 4 worker threads (`UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE=4`) used for heavy operations like file system I/O, DNS lookups, compression (zlib), and cryptographic operations (crypto).\n- **Phases of the Event Loop**:\n  1. **Timers**: Executes callbacks scheduled by `setTimeout()` and `setInterval()`.\n  2. **Pending Callbacks**: Executes I/O callbacks deferred to the next loop iteration.\n  3. **Idle, Prepare**: Used internally by Node.\n  4. **Poll**: Retrieves new I/O events; executes I/O-related callbacks.\n  5. **Check**: Executes `setImmediate()` callbacks.\n  6. **Close Callbacks**: Handles close events (e.g. `socket.on('close', ...)`).\n\n### ⚡ Microtasks: process.nextTick vs Promise.then\n- **`process.nextTick()` Queue**: Has the highest priority. Executes immediately after the currently running operation, before any other phase.\n- **Promise Microtask Queue**: Runs immediately after `process.nextTick()`, before the event loop advances to the next phase.\n\n### 💡 Placement & Interview Key Takeaways\n1. **Never Block the Event Loop**: Heavy CPU-bound computations (like calculating primes or complex image processing) in the main thread freeze all incoming client requests. Offload CPU-bound tasks to Worker Threads (`worker_threads`) or a microservice.\n2. **Difference between `setImmediate()` and `setTimeout(fn, 0)`**: `setImmediate` is designed to execute a script once the current poll phase completes. `setTimeout(fn, 0)` executes after a minimum threshold (typically 1ms) in the timers phase.	[{"url": "https://nodejs.org/en/learn/asynchronous-work/event-loop-timers-and-nexttick", "title": "Node.js Official Event Loop Guide"}, {"url": "https://docs.libuv.org/en/v1.x/design.html", "title": "Libuv Design & Architecture Documentation"}, {"url": "https://nodejs.org/en/learn/asynchronous-work/dont-block-the-event-loop", "title": "Don't Block the Event Loop (Node.js Docs)"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8aGhZQkoFbQ", "title": "What the heck is the event loop anyway? - Philip Roberts", "duration": "26m"}, {"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PNa9OM6494M", "title": "Node.js Event Loop and Libuv Thread Pool Explained", "duration": "19m"}]	[{"type": "quiz", "title": "Predict Event Loop Execution Order Output", "checklist": ["Identify synchronous code executed first", "Evaluate process.nextTick queue", "Evaluate Promise microtask queue", "Trace timers vs check phase execution"], "description": "Determine the exact console output order for a snippet mixing console.log, setTimeout(0), setImmediate, Promise.resolve().then, and process.nextTick.", "starterCodeUrl": ""}, {"type": "assignment", "title": "Benchmark CPU Starvation vs Worker Threads", "checklist": ["Simulate 100 concurrent requests while heavy task runs", "Demonstrate event loop recovery using worker_threads"], "description": "Create an Express endpoint that performs heavy PBKDF2 hashing synchronously vs asynchronously vs inside a Worker Thread, and measure latency degradation with an Apache Bench / Autocannon load test.", "starterCodeUrl": "https://nodejs.org/api/worker_threads.html"}]	\N	\N	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	5	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
