305	Frequency Cancellation: Moore's Voting Algorithm (Majority Element > N/2)	dsa-moores-voting-algorithm-majority-element	Find the majority element appearing strictly more than N/2 times using Moore's Voting vote cancellation principle in O(N) time and O(1) space.	2026-10-02 22:33:52.481529+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Frequency Cancellation: Moore's Voting Algorithm (Majority Element > N/2)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nP_ns3uSh80", "title": "Lecture 23: Find the Majority Element that occurs more than N/2 times", "duration": "28m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/majority-element-ii/", "title": "Majority Element II", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Extend Moore's Voting Algorithm to find all elements appearing more than N/3 times."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/most-frequent-even-element/", "title": "Most Frequent Even Element", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Track frequency counts with tie-breaking rules to find the dominant even integer."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/count-element-occurences/1", "title": "More than n/k Occurrences", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Count how many elements appear strictly more than N/k times in an array."}]	Module 9: Medium Array Patterns & Classic Named Algorithms	### 📌 Topic Overview\nA **Majority Element** is an element that appears **strictly more than `N / 2` times** in an array of size `N`. While a hash map solves this in `O(N)` space, **Moore's Voting Algorithm** finds the majority candidate in **O(N) time** and **O(1) extra space**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Core Intuition (Vote Cancellation):** If an element appears more than half the time (`> N / 2`), all other elements combined cannot completely cancel out its occurrences.\n- **Step 1: Apply Moore's Voting Algorithm:**\n  - Maintain two variables: `cnt = 0` and `el` (current candidate).\n  - Loop through the array:\n    - If `cnt == 0`, pick the current number as the new candidate (`el = arr[i]`, `cnt = 1`).\n    - Else if `arr[i] == el`, increment `cnt++`.\n    - Else, decrement `cnt--` (canceling out one vote).\n- **Step 2: Verify the Candidate (When Majority is Not Guaranteed):**\n  - If the problem guarantees a majority element exists, `el` is automatically the answer.\n  - If a majority element might not exist, run a quick second pass to count how many times `el` actually appears and check if `count > N / 2`.\n\n### 💻 Moore's Voting Algorithm Blueprint\n```cpp\nint majorityElement(vector<int> &v) {\n    int cnt = 0, el = 0;\n    for (int x : v) {\n        if (cnt == 0) {\n            cnt = 1;\n            el = x;\n        } else if (x == el) {\n            cnt++;\n        } else {\n            cnt--;\n        }\n    }\n    // Optional verification pass if majority element is not guaranteed\n    int actualCount = 0;\n    for (int x : v) if (x == el) actualCount++;\n    return (actualCount > v.size() / 2) ? el : -1;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22216	Multi-Conference Video Calls (WebRTC vs SFU vs MCU)	hld-video-conferencing-webrtc-sfu-mcu	Architecture of group video calling applications: WebRTC peer-to-peer limits, Mesh bottlenecks, MCU mixing overhead, and SFU selective routing.	2026-10-07 16:41:22.814808+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Multi-Conference Video Calls (WebRTC vs SFU vs MCU)\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/Zaz6hYVm-WE", "title": "System Design Behind Multi-Conference Video Calls - WebRTC vs SFU vs MCU", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "hld-v11-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Why Mesh WebRTC Fails for Group Video Calls", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn a full Mesh WebRTC architecture, there is no central media server. Every participant must establish an independent P2P connection to every other participant.\\n\\n**The Bandwidth Equation:**\\nFor $N$ participants in a call:\\n* Each client must maintain $N - 1$ uplink (upload) video streams.\\n* Each client must maintain $N - 1$ downlink (download) video streams.\\n* Total peer connections in the room $= \\\\frac{N(N - 1)}{2}$.\\n\\n**The Failure:**\\nMost residential and mobile connections have asymmetric bandwidth (high download, low upload—often capped at 2–5 Mbps upload). If each HD stream requires 1.5 Mbps, a call with 6 participants requires each client to upload $5 \\\\times 1.5 = 7.5\\\\text{ Mbps}$ simultaneously. Client uplink bandwidth saturates, hardware encoders overload, and frame rates drop to zero.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why does a pure Peer-to-Peer Mesh WebRTC architecture fail to scale beyond 4 to 5 participants in a group video call? Analyze the bandwidth equation.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v11-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: MCU (Multipoint Control Unit) Architecture & Bottlenecks", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**How MCU Solves Bandwidth:**\\nEvery participant uploads exactly ONE stream to the central MCU server. The MCU decodes all incoming video streams, mixes/composites them into a single video frame (a 2x2 or 3x3 video grid), mixes the audio tracks into a single stereo channel, re-encodes the composite video, and transmits a single video stream back to each participant. Client upload and download bandwidth is capped at $O(1)$.\\n\\n**Server-Side Bottlenecks:**\\n1. **Severe CPU/GPU Load:** Real-time decoding and re-encoding of high-definition video is one of the most computationally expensive tasks in computing. An MCU server can only support a small number of concurrent rooms before maxing out CPU cores.\\n2. **High Latency:** Decoding, resizing, compositing, and re-encoding introduces 100–300ms of processing delay, destroying real-time conversational interactivity.\\n3. **Loss of Client Flexibility:** Because the server hard-codes the grid layout into the pixels, individual clients cannot locally resize a speaker's window, pin a screen share, or mute one annoying participant locally.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "How does an MCU (Multipoint Control Unit) solve the client upload bandwidth problem, and what massive server-side bottlenecks does it introduce?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v11-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: SFU (Selective Forwarding Unit) Mechanism", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nAn SFU strikes the optimal architectural balance between client bandwidth and server compute:\\n\\n1. **Single Uplink:** Each participant encodes and transmits exactly **ONE** audio/video stream to the SFU server ($O(1)$ client upload).\\n2. **Zero Transcoding on Server:** The SFU server does NOT decode, render, or re-encode video packets. It acts as an intelligent, high-speed router. It inspects RTP packet headers and forwards individual video streams to the other participants ($N-1$ downlink streams).\\n3. **Negligible Server CPU:** Because it routes raw network packets without decoding video pixels, an SFU server can handle thousands of concurrent video streams on modest CPU hardware.\\n4. **Client-Side Rendering Independence:** Because each client receives discrete video streams for each participant, the client app can freely decide how to render them—pinning a speaker in full-screen, showing active speakers in high-res, muting individuals, or rendering thumbnails in low-res.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Explain why the Selective Forwarding Unit (SFU) architecture has become the undisputed industry standard for platforms like Zoom and Google Meet.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v11-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Simulcast and Scalable Video Coding (SVC) in SFUs", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Problem:** While client upload is fixed at 1 stream, downloading 19 separate 1080p video streams requires ~30 Mbps download bandwidth and heavy client CPU decoding power.\\n\\n**The Solution: Simulcast**\\n1. Instead of publishing one 1080p stream, the publishing client simultaneously encodes and uploads **three distinct quality layers** of its own camera stream to the SFU: Low (180p @ 100kbps), Medium (360p @ 350kbps), and High (720p @ 1.5Mbps).\\n2. **Selective Forwarding Intelligence:**\\n   * The SFU forwards the **High** layer of the *Active Speaker* to all participants (since they occupy the large display area).\\n   * For all other participants rendered as tiny grid thumbnails, the SFU forwards only their **Low** or **Medium** streams.\\n   * If a client is on a poor mobile connection, the SFU drops them down to receiving lower layers across the board.\\nSimulcast keeps total client downlink bandwidth under 2–3 Mbps regardless of how many people join the conference room.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "In an SFU architecture with 20 participants, sending high-definition video of all 20 participants to every client will overload client downlink bandwidth. How does Simulcast resolve this?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v11-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Signaling vs Media Planes in WebRTC", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **The Signaling Plane:**\\n   * The control coordination channel used before media can flow.\\n   * Used to exchange session descriptions (SDP: codecs, encryption keys) and network candidates (ICE candidates: public IP/port mappings discovered via STUN/TURN servers).\\n   * Operates over standard TCP/WebSocket connections via standard web servers.\\n\\n2. **The Media Plane:**\\n   * The actual real-time pipeline carrying encrypted audio/video payloads (SRTP/SRTCP) directly between peers or to an SFU.\\n   * Operates over low-latency **UDP** to prioritize speed over guaranteed packet delivery.\\n\\n**Why WebRTC Doesn't Define Signaling:**\\nWebRTC deliberately omitted a standardized signaling protocol to give developers complete architectural freedom. Applications can use WebSockets, SIP, MQTT, or HTTP polling depending on their existing infrastructure, while WebRTC strictly focuses on high-performance media transmission.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "What is the distinction between the \\"Signaling Plane\\" and the \\"Media Plane\\" in a video conferencing system? Why does WebRTC not define a signaling protocol?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	High Level Design	### 📌 1. Peer-to-Peer WebRTC (1:1 Calls)\n* **WebRTC:** Enables browser-to-browser real-time audio/video transmission over UDP without an intermediate media server.\n* **Limitation:** Ideal and practically free for 1-on-1 calls, but breaks down when three or more participants join.\n\n### 💡 2. Mesh Peer-to-Peer Architecture\n* Every participant opens direct P2P connections to every other participant.\n* **The Fatal Flaw:** For $N$ peers, each client must upload $N-1$ video streams and download $N-1$ video streams ($O(N)$ uplink bandwidth). At 5–6 participants, consumer residential upload bandwidth collapses and devices overheat.\n\n### 💡 3. Centralized Media Architectures: MCU vs. SFU\n* **MCU (Multipoint Control Unit):**\n  * Clients send 1 stream to the server. The server decodes, mixes all audio/video into a single composite mosaic grid video stream, encodes it, and sends 1 stream back to each user.\n  * *Pros:* Client needs minimal download bandwidth.\n  * *Cons:* Massive server CPU cost, high latency lag, and client cannot independently pin, resize, or mute individual speakers.\n* **SFU (Selective Forwarding Unit - e.g., Zoom, Google Meet, Mediasoup):**\n  * Clients send 1 uplink stream to the server.\n  * The server **does NOT decode or composite** media; it acts as a lightweight router/switch, forwarding individual streams to other participants.\n  * *Pros:* Extremely low server CPU, low latency, and full client layout flexibility. This is the industry standard for scalable video conferencing.	Piyush Garg	https://www.youtube.com/@piyushgarg195	28	Multi-Conference Video Calls (WebRTC vs SFU vs MCU)	Architecture of group video calling applications: WebRTC peer-to-peer limits, Mesh bottlenecks, MCU mixing overhead, and SFU selective routing.	24	Hard	High Level Design	11	[{"id": "hld-v11-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Why Mesh WebRTC Fails for Group Video Calls", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn a 