6398	Half-Sum Reduction: Partition Equal Subset Sum	dsa-partition-equal-subset-sum	Reduce the two-subset equal partition problem to single-subset sum checking with target = totalSum / 2.	2026-10-03 11:28:16.959712+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Half-Sum Reduction: Partition Equal Subset Sum\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7win3dcgo3k", "title": "Lecture 196: Partition Equal Subset Sum (DP 15)", "duration": "16m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/partition-equal-subset-sum/", "title": "Partition Equal Subset Sum (LC 416)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return true if you can partition the array into two subsets such that the sum of the elements in both subsets is equal."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/subset-sum-problem2014/1", "title": "Partition Equal Subset Sum", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Determine if an array can be divided into two subsets with equal sum."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/partition-to-k-equal-sum-subsets/", "title": "Partition to K Equal Sum Subsets (LC 698)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Generalize 2-subset equal partitioning to k equal-sum subsets."}]	Module 30: Dynamic Programming on Subsequences, Subset Sums & 0/1 Knapsack	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an integer array `nums` of size `N`, determine if the array can be partitioned into **two subsets `S1` and `S2`** such that the sum of elements in `S1` equals the sum of elements in `S2`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Reducing Two-Subset Partition to Single-Subset Target Sum:**\n  - Let `totSum` be the sum of all elements in `nums`.\n  - **1. Odd Sum Check:** If `totSum` is **odd** (`totSum % 2 != 0`), it is mathematically impossible to split an odd integer into two equal integer halves—immediately return **`false`**!\n  - **2. Even Sum Reduction:** If `totSum` is **even**, and we find **just one subset `S1`** whose sum equals **`target = totSum / 2`**, the remaining elements `S2` are automatically guaranteed to sum to `totSum - (totSum / 2) = totSum / 2`!\n  - Therefore, simply call **`subsetSumToK(n, totSum / 2, nums)`** from DP 14!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * (totSum / 2))` Time and `O(totSum / 2)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Partition Equal Subset Sum Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nbool canPartition(vector<int> &nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    int totSum = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) totSum += x;\n\n    // Odd total sum can never be split into two equal integer halves\n    if (totSum % 2 != 0) return false;\n\n    int target = totSum / 2;\n    vector<bool> prev(target + 1, false), cur(target + 1, false);\n    prev[0] = cur[0] = true;\n    if (nums[0] <= target) prev[nums[0]] = true;\n\n    for (int ind = 1; ind < n; ind++) {\n        for (int t = 1; t <= target; t++) {\n            bool notTake = prev[t];\n            bool take = false;\n            if (nums[ind] <= t) take = prev[t - nums[ind]];\n            cur[t] = take || notTake;\n        }\n        prev = cur;\n    }\n    return prev[target];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting the `totSum % 2 != 0` Check:** Integer division `totSum / 2` truncates odd numbers (e.g., `15 / 2 = 7`), which would falsely return `true` if a subset sums to `7` while the other sums to `8`!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
3436	Connected Region Recoloring: Flood Fill Algorithm via 4-Directional DFS/BFS	dsa-flood-fill-algorithm	Recolor connected regions of matching pixel values using 4-directional DFS with zero redundant visited memory.	2026-10-03 01:51:47.347859+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Connected Region Recoloring: Flood Fill Algorithm via 4-Directional DFS/BFS\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=C-2_uSRli8o", "title": "Lecture 134: Flood Fill Algorithm", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/flood-fill/", "title": "Flood Fill (LC 733)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Recolor all 4-directionally connected pixels matching the starting pixel's color."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/coloring-a-border/", "title": "Coloring A Border (LC 1034)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use flood-fill traversal to identify and recolor only the boundary cells of a connected component."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/flood-fill-algorithm1856/1", "title": "Flood fill Algorithm", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Replace the color of the starting cell and all 4-directionally connected cells of the same initial color."}]	Module 21: Graph Theory Foundations, Representations & Traversals	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a 2D `image` grid, a starting pixel `(sr, sc)`, and a `targetColor`, the **Flood Fill Algorithm** recolors `(sr, sc)` and all 4-directionally connected pixels that share the **exact same `startColor`** into `targetColor`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **4-Directional Delta Arrays:**\n  - Represent the 4 cardinal moves (Up, Right, Down, Left) using two 4-element arrays:\n    `rowOffsets = {-1, 0, 1, 0}` and `colOffsets = {0, 1, 0, -1}`.\n- **Avoiding Separate Visited Memory:**\n  - Work on a copy of the grid `result = image`.\n  - Before recursing into neighbor `(nr, nc)`, verify two things:\n    1. It originally had the starting color (`image[nr][nc] == startColor`).\n    2. It has not already been painted with `targetColor` (`result[nr][nc] != targetColor`)!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * M)` Time and `O(N * M)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Flood Fill DFS Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid paintRegion(int r, int c, vector<vector<int>> &result, const vector<vector<int>> &image,\n                 int startColor, int targetColor, const int dr[], const int dc[]) {\n    result[r][c] = targetColor;\n    int rows = image.size(), cols = image[0].size();\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n        int nr = r + dr[i], nc = c + dc[i];\n        if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols &&\n            image[nr][nc] == startColor && result[nr][nc] != targetColor) {\n            paintRegion(nr, nc, result, image, startColor, targetColor, dr, dc);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nvector<vector<int>> floodFill(vector<vector<int>> &image, int sr, int sc, int newColor) {\n    int startColor = image[sr][sc];\n    vector<vector<int>> result = image;\n    if (startColor == newColor) return result;\n\n    int dr[] = {-1, 0, 1, 0};\n    int dc[] = {0, 1, 0, -1};\n    paintRegion(sr, sc, result, image, startColor, newColor, dr, dc);\n    return result;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Infinite Recursion When startColor == newColor:** If the new color is identical to the starting color, checking result[nr][nc] != targetColor (or returning early when startColor == newColor) is essential to prevent bouncing back and forth forever between two adjacent cells!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
