form": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count land cells that cannot reach the grid boundary via 4-directional land moves."}]	Module 22: Graph Cycle Detection, Multi-Source BFS & Boundary Flood Algorithms	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an `N x M` binary matrix where `0` is sea and `1` is land, count the **Number of Enclaves**: the total number of land cells (`1`s) from which it is **impossible to walk off the boundary** of the grid using 4-directional moves.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Direct Application of Boundary Flood (Multi-Source BFS):**\n  - Any land cell (`1`) on the outer perimeter (row `0`, row `N - 1`, col `0`, or col `M - 1`), along with any land cell 4-directionally connected to it, can walk off the grid.\n- **Algorithm Steps (`O(N * M)` Time, `O(N * M)` Space):**\n  1. Scan the grid and push **all boundary `1`s** (`r == 0 || r == rows - 1 || c == 0 || c == cols - 1`) into a BFS queue, marking them visited in `seen`.\n  2. Run Multi-Source BFS to mark all land cells reachable from those boundary cells.\n  3. Count and return the number of remaining land cells where `grid[r][c] == 1 && !seen[r][c]`!\n\n### 💻 Number of Enclaves (Multi-Source BFS) Blueprint\n```cpp\nint numEnclaves(vector<vector<int>> &grid) {\n    int rows = grid.size(), cols = grid[0].size();\n    vector<vector<int>> seen(rows, vector<int>(cols, 0));\n    queue<pair<int, int>> q;\n\n    // 1. Push all boundary land cells into the queue\n    for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {\n            if (r == 0 || r == rows - 1 || c == 0 || c == cols - 1) {\n                if (grid[r][c] == 1) {\n                    q.push({r, c});\n                    seen[r][c] = 1;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    int dr[] = {-1, 0, 1, 0}, dc[] = {0, 1, 0, -1};\n\n    // 2. Mark all land cells connected to the boundary\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        auto [r, c] = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n            int nr = r + dr[i], nc = c + dc[i];\n            if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols &&\n                !seen[nr][nc] && grid[nr][nc] == 1) {\n                seen[nr][nc] = 1;\n                q.push({nr, nc});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // 3. Count unvisited interior land cells (enclaves)\n    int enclaves = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {\n            if (grid[r][c] == 1 && !seen[r][c]) {\n                enclaves++;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return enclaves;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting to Mark Boundary Cells as Visited When Pushing:** Always set seen[r][c] = 1 at the moment you push boundary cells into the queue so corner/edge neighbors aren't pushed multiple times.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
3875	Recursive Alternating 2-Coloring: Bipartite Graph Check Using DFS	dsa-bipartite-graph-check-dfs	Verify bipartite graph properties using recursive DFS alternating color propagation with conflict detection.	2026-10-03 02:09:36.723626+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Recursive Alternating 2-Coloring: Bipartite Graph Check Using DFS\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KG5YFfR0j8A", "title": "Lecture 143: Bipartite Graph Check using DFS", "duration": "16m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/possible-bipartition/", "title": "Possible Bipartition (LC 886)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use recursive DFS 2-coloring to determine if N people can be partitioned into two conflict-free groups."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/is-graph-bipartite/", "title": "Is Graph Bipartite? (LC 785)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Implement recursive DFS alternating color assignment across all graph components."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/flower-planting-with-no-adjacent/", "title": "Flower Planting With No Adjacent (LC 1042)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Generalize graph coloring to assign 4 flower types so no two adjacent gardens share a type."}]	Module 23: Graph Coloring, Directed Cycle Detection & Topological Sort (DAGs)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWe can also verify whether a graph is **Bipartite** using **Depth-First Search (DFS)** by recursively passing the alternating color (`!col`) to each uncolored neighbor and checking for same-color conflicts.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Recursive State `(curr, col)`:**\n  1. Set `color[curr] = col`.\n  2. Loop through every `neighbor` in `adjList[curr]`:\n     - If `color[neighbor] == -1`: call `checkDFS(neighbor, !col)`. If that recursive call returns `false`, immediately return `false`!\n     - Else if `color[neighbor] == col`: the neighbor is already colored with the **same color** as `curr`—return `false`!\n  3. If all neighbors succeed, return `true`.\n- **Complexity:** `O(V + 2E)` Time and `O(V)` Space (color array + recursion stack).\n\n### 💻 Bipartite Graph Check (DFS) Blueprint\n```cpp\nbool checkComponentDFS(int curr, int col, const vector<vector<int>> &adjList, vector<int> &color) {\n    color[curr] = col;\n\n    for (int neighbor : adjList[curr]) {\n        if (color[neighbor] == -1) {\n            if (!checkComponentDFS(neighbor, !col, adjList, color)) {\n                return false;\n            }\n        } else if (color[neighbor] == col) {\n            return false; // Neighbor already has the same color\n        }\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n\nbool isBipartite(vector<vector<int>> &graph) {\n    int V = graph.size();\n    vector<int> color(V, -1);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {\n        if (color[i] == -1) {\n            if (!checkComponentDFS(i, 0, graph, color)) return false;\n        }\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Initializing `color` With `0` Instead of `-1`:** Since `0` and `1` are our two active colors, unvisited nodes must be initialized to `-1` so they aren't mistaken for color `0`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
585	Asymmetric Merge-Sort Counting: Reverse Pairs (A[i] > 2 * A[j])	dsa-reverse-pairs-merge-sort	Count all reverse pairs (i < j and arr[i] > 2 * arr[j]) in O(N log N) time by decoupling the two-pointer counting step immediately before each merge step.	2026-10-02 23:24:14.961793+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Asymmetric Merge-Sort Counting: Reverse Pairs (A[i] > 2 * A[j])\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0e4bZaP3MDI", "title": "Lecture 43: Count Reverse Pairs", "duration": "34m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-pairs-satisfying-inequality/", "title": "Number of Pairs Satisfying Inequality", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Use divide-and-conquer merge sort to count index pairs satisfying a shifted inequality."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/count-of-range-sum/", "title": "Count of Range Sum", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Apply two-pointer counting before the merge step on prefix sums."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/number-of-pairs-1587115620/1", "title": "Number of pairs", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count pairs across two arrays satisfying an exponential inequality condition."}]	Module 11: Advanced Array Patterns & Hard Interview Problems	### 📌 Topic Overview\nA **Reverse Pair** is a pair of indices `(i, j)` such that `i < j` and **`arr[i] > 2 * arr[j]`**. While similar to Count Inversions, the `2 * arr[j]` condition means we **cannot** count pairs inside the standard merge loop—we must count them in a dedicated pass right **before** merging!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Standard Inversion Counting Fails Here:**\n  - In Count Inversions, if `arr[left] > arr[right]`, we both counted the pair and merged `arr[right]`.\n  - Here, an element `arr[left]` (like `6`) might be greater than `arr[right]` (like `3`), so `3` gets merged first—even though `6` is **not** greater than `2 * 3` (`6`)! Meanwhile, a larger element later in the left half (like `13`) *is* greater than `2 * 3`.\n- **Optimal Two-Pointer Pass Before Merging (O(2N log N) Time, O(N) Space):**\n  - Before calling `merge(arr, low, mid, high)`, run `countPairs(arr, low, mid, high)`:\n    - Keep `right = mid + 1`.\n    - Loop `i` through the left half (`low` to `mid`).\n    - Advance `right` while `right <= high && arr[i] > 2LL * arr[right]`.\n    - Because both halves are sorted, all elements from `mid + 1` to `right - 1` form valid reverse pairs with `arr[i]`, so add **`cnt += (right - (mid + 1))`**.\n    - Notice `right` **never resets** back to `mid + 1` when `i` increments, because `arr[i + 1] >= arr[i]`—so the entire `countPairs` function runs in `O(N1 + N2)` linear time!\n\n### 💻 Count Reverse Pairs Helper (Called Before Merge)\n```cpp\nint countPairs(vector<int> &arr, int low, int mid, int high) {\n    int right = mid + 1, cnt = 0;\n    for (int i = low; i <= mid; i++) {\n        while (right <= high && (long long)arr[i] > 2LL * arr[right]) {\n            right++;\n        }\n        cnt += (right - (mid + 1));\n    }\n    return cnt;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
