ssets on geographically distributed 'edge servers' closer to the user"}, {"id": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["The server that hosts the main SQL database", "The server located at the edge of the network, closest to the end user, responsible for delivering cached content", "A server that only handles security firewalls", "The user's local browser cache"], "question": "In CDN terminology, what is an 'Edge Server'?", "explanation": "Edge servers sit at the Point of Presence (PoP) locations worldwide to physically bridge the gap between the user and the origin server.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "The server located at the edge of the network, closest to the end user, responsible for delivering cached content"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["The user's computer making the request", "The CDN provider's main billing server", "The primary web server where the original, definitive version of your application's files reside", "The DNS root server"], "question": "What is an 'Origin Server' in relation to a CDN?", "explanation": "If a CDN's edge server doesn't have a file in its cache (a Cache Miss), it must request the file from the Origin Server to serve the user and update its cache.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "The primary web server where the original, definitive version of your application's files reside"}, {"id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["Push sends data to the database; Pull gets data from the database.", "In Push, you manually upload content to the CDN. In Pull, the CDN automatically fetches content from the Origin when a user requests it.", "Push is for mobile, Pull is for desktop.", "Push is used for React, Pull is used for Angular."], "question": "What is a 'Push CDN' vs a 'Pull CDN'?", "explanation": "Pull CDNs automatically fetch missing files from the origin upon request. Push CDNs require the developer/CI pipeline to upload assets to the CDN ahead of time.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "In Push, you manually upload content to the CDN. In Pull, the CDN automatically fetches content from the Origin when a user requests it."}, {"id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["By requiring users to submit their passport to view content", "By encrypting database passwords", "By hiding the Origin server's IP address and absorbing Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attacks", "By installing antivirus software on the client's browser"], "question": "How does a CDN improve website security?", "explanation": "Because DNS points to the CDN rather than the Origin, the CDN absorbs massive traffic spikes and DDoS attacks, keeping the origin server safe and online.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "By hiding the Origin server's IP address and absorbing Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attacks"}]	\N	> **Architecture Overview:** Essential systems architecture principles governing how modern web applications communicate, route data, balance loads, and scale effectively.	Hayk Simonyan	https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan	\N	CDNs Explained (Content Delivery Networks)	Core system design concepts focusing on cdns  (content delivery networks) and distributed network architecture.	10	Easy	Web Architecture	5	[]	\N	10	https://youtu.be/lcPcyNAEZgo	CDNs Explained (Content Delivery Networks)
3878	Post-Order Dependency Stacking: Topological Sort Algorithm Using DFS	dsa-topological-sort-algorithm-dfs	Compute linear topological orderings in Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs) using post-order DFS stack reversal.	2026-10-03 02:09:36.732246+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Post-Order Dependency Stacking: Topological Sort Algorithm Using DFS\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5lZ0iJMrUMk", "title": "Lecture 146: Topological Sort Algorithm | DFS", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/topological-sort/1", "title": "Topological sort", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return a valid topological linear ordering of vertices in a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG)."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/course-schedule-ii/", "title": "Course Schedule II (LC 210)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use post-order DFS stack ordering to find a valid sequence to finish all courses."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/loud-and-rich/", "title": "Loud and Rich (LC 851)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Propagate minimum quietness values across a DAG using DFS post-order traversal."}]	Module 23: Graph Coloring, Directed Cycle Detection & Topological Sort (DAGs)	### 📌 Topic Overview\n**Topological Sorting** of a **Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG)** is a linear ordering of its vertices such that for every directed edge `u → v`, vertex **`u` always appears before `v`** in the ordering.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Topological Sort Only Exists for DAGs:**\n  - **Undirected graphs** have bidirectional edges (`u — v` implies both `u → v` and `v → u`), so neither can come first.\n  - **Cyclic graphs** have circular dependencies (`1 → 2 → 3 → 1`), making linear ordering impossible.\n- **Post-Order DFS + Stack Intuition (`O(V + E)` Time, `O(V)` Space):**\n  - Run DFS from every unvisited node `u`.\n  - First recursively visit **all** outgoing descendants of `u`.\n  - Only **after** the DFS for `u` is 100% finished, push `u` onto a `stack<int> st`!\n  - Because all dependents `v` finish their DFS and get pushed onto the stack *before* `u` finishes, `u` sits above `v` in the stack—so popping the stack from top to bottom outputs `u` before `v`!\n\n### 💻 Topological Sort (DFS) Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid topoSortDFS(int curr, const vector<int> adjList[], vector<int> &seen, stack<int> &st) {\n    seen[curr] = 1;\n\n    for (int neighbor : adjList[curr]) {\n        if (!seen[neighbor]) {\n            topoSortDFS(neighbor, adjList, seen, st);\n        }\n    }\n    // Push curr ONLY after all of its outgoing dependencies have finished\n    st.push(curr);\n}\n\nvector<int> topoSort(int V, vector<int> adjList[]) {\n    vector<int> seen(V, 0);\n    stack<int> st;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {\n        if (!seen[i]) {\n            topoSortDFS(i, adjList, seen, st);\n        }\n    }\n\n    vector<int> order;\n    while (!st.empty()) {\n        order.push_back(st.top());\n        st.pop();\n    }\n    return order;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Pushing `curr` Onto the Stack Before the Neighbor Loop:** Pushing on entry produces a Preorder traversal, which violates topological ordering when branches reconverge. Always push **at the very end** of the DFS function!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
