15090	Single-Pass On-The-Fly PSE/NSE Resolution: Largest Rectangle in Histogram	dsa-largest-rectangle-in-histogram-monotonic-stack	Master Single-Pass On-The-Fly PSE/NSE Resolution: Largest Rectangle in Histogram with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:30:52.971394+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Single-Pass On-The-Fly PSE/NSE Resolution: Largest Rectangle in Histogram\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bzat9vgD0fs", "title": "Single-Pass On-The-Fly PSE/NSE Resolution: Largest Rectangle in Histogram - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/largest-rectangle-in-histogram/", "title": "LeetCode 84: Largest Rectangle in Histogram", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Given an array of integers heights representing the histogram's bar height where the width of each bar is 1, return the area of the largest rectangle in the histogram."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximal-rectangle/", "title": "LeetCode 85: Maximal Rectangle", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Apply Largest Rectangle in Histogram across each row of a 2D binary matrix."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-score-of-a-good-subarray/", "title": "LeetCode 1793: Maximum Score of a Good Subarray", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Maximize min(subarray) * width containing index k using histogram-style expansion."}]	Module 43: Monotonic Stack & Contribution Algorithms: Next/Previous Greater/Smaller, Rainwater & Subarray Aggregations	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array `heights` of `N` bars (each of width `1`), find the **area of the largest rectangle** that can be formed within the histogram.\n\n### 💡 Progression (2-Pass Precomputed PSE/NSE O(5N) → Optimal 1-Pass On-the-Fly O(2N))\n- **1. Approach 1 — Precomputing `PSE[]` and `NSE[]` Arrays (`O(5N)` Time, `O(4N)` Space):**\n  - A bar of height `arr[i]` can expand left until `pse[i]` and right until `nse[i]`.\n  - Its width is **`nse[i] - pse[i] - 1`**, giving area **`arr[i] * (nse[i] - pse[i] - 1)`**.\n- **2. Approach 2 — Optimal Single-Pass On-the-Fly Stack (`O(2N)` Time, `O(N)` Space!):**\n  - How do we know **both** `pse` and `nse` in a single pass without precomputing arrays?\n  - Maintain a **monotonic increasing stack** of indices.\n  - When we are at index `i` and see **`arr[st.top()] > arr[i]`**, we have to **pop `element = st.top()`**:\n    1. **Who knocked `element` out of the stack?** The current index **`i`**! That means **`nse = i`** is the **Next Smaller Element** of `element`!\n    2. **Who is sitting directly below `element` in the stack after `element` is popped?** Its **Previous Smaller Element** (`pse = st.empty() ? -1 : st.top()`)!\n    3. Compute area for `element` immediately while popping:\n       **`maxArea = max(maxArea, arr[element] * (nse - pse - 1))`**!\n  - **Post-Loop Cleanup (`while (!st.empty())`):**\n    - Any indices still left in `st` after the loop ends had **no smaller element on the right**—so their **`nse = n`** and their `pse = st.empty() ? -1 : st.top()`!\n\n### 💻 Largest Rectangle in Histogram (Optimal 1-Pass) Blueprint\n```cpp\nint largestRectangleArea(vector<int> &arr) {\n    int n = arr.size();\n    stack<int> st;\n    int maxArea = 0;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        // When arr[i] is smaller than arr[st.top()], arr[i] is the NSE of st.top()!\n        while (!st.empty() && arr[st.top()] > arr[i]) {\n            int element = st.top();\n            st.pop();\n\n            int nse = i;\n            int pse = st.empty() ? -1 : st.top();\n\n            maxArea = max(maxArea, arr[element] * (nse - pse - 1));\n        }\n        st.push(i);\n    }\n\n    // Cleanup remaining bars in stack (they have no NSE, so nse = n!)\n    while (!st.empty()) {\n        int nse = n;\n        int element = st.top();\n        st.pop();\n        int pse = st.empty() ? -1 : st.top();\n\n        maxArea = max(maxArea, arr[element] * (nse - pse - 1));\n    }\n\n    return maxArea;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Reading `pse = st.top()` BEFORE Calling `st.pop()`:** The element's `pse` is the index sitting **below** `element` in the stack! Always save `int element = st.top();`, call **`st.pop()` first**, and **then** read `int pse = st.empty() ? -1 : st.top();`!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
10022	Relative-Velocity Gap Reduction: Detect a Loop or Cycle in a Linked List (Floyd's Cycle Detection)	dsa-detect-loop-or-cycle-in-linked-list	Detect cycles in a linked list using Floyd's Tortoise and Hare algorithm, proven by relative velocity reducing the gap by 1 each step in O(N) time and O(1) space.	2026-10-03 15:04:07.06062+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Relative-Velocity Gap Reduction: Detect a Loop or Cycle in a Linked List (Floyd's Cycle Detection)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wiOo4DC5GGA", "title": "Lecture 250: Detect a Loop in Linked List | Floyd's Cycle Detection", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/linked-list-cycle/", "title": "Linked List Cycle (LC 141)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Given head, determine if the linked list has a cycle in it in O(1) memory."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/happy-number/", "title": "Happy Number (LC 202)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Apply Floyd's Tortoise and Hare cycle detection to a sequence of sum-of-squared-digits transitions."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/detect-loop-in-linked-list/1", "title": "Detect Loop in linked list", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Check if a given singly linked list contains a loop."}]	Module 37: Linked List Medium Problems: Dummy Nodes, In-Place Re-Linking & Tortoise-Hare Pointers	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the `head` of a singly linked list, determine whether the list contains a **cycle (loop)**—a path where starting from a node and following `next` pointers eventually brings you back to that exact same node.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Optimal + Mathematical Proof)\n- **1. Brute Force Hashing (`O(N)` Time, `O(N)` Space):**\n  - Store visited `Node*` pointers in an `unordered_map<Node*, int>`. If a `Node*` is seen again, return `true`.\n- **2. Optimal Tortoise & Hare (`O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Start `slow = head` (`+1` step) and `fast = head` (`+2` steps).\n  - Inside `while (fast != nullptr && fast->next != nullptr)`, advance `slow = slow->next;` and `fast = fast->next->next;`.\n  - If **`slow == fast`** at any step, a cycle exists → **`return true`**!\n- **Mathematical Proof — Why Are `slow` and `fast` Guaranteed to Collide (And Never Jump Over Each Other)?**\n  - Once both `slow` and `fast` are inside the loop, suppose the distance from `fast` behind `slow` is **`d`**.\n  - In every single iteration, `fast` moves `+2` steps toward `slow` while `slow` moves `+1` step away from `fast`.\n  - Therefore, the gap `d` between them shrinks by **exactly `2 - 1 = 1` per step**:\n    **`d → d - 1 → d - 2 → ... → 2 → 1 → 0`**!\n  - Because `d` decreases by **`1` (not `2` or `3`)**, `d` can **never skip over `0`**—they are 100% mathematically guaranteed to land on the exact same node (`d == 0`)!\n\n### 💻 Detect Cycle in Linked List (Floyd's Tortoise & Hare) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nbool detectCycle(Node* head) {\n    Node* slow = head;\n    Node* fast = head;\n\n    while (fast != nullptr && fast->next != nullptr) {\n        slow = slow->next;\n        fast = fast->next->next;\n\n        // Check collision AFTER advancing pointers (since both start at head!)\n        if (slow == fast) {\n            return true;\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Checking `if (slow == fast)` Before Moving `slow` and `fast`:** Since both `slow` and `fast` are initialized to `head`, checking `slow == fast` at the top of the `while` loop before advancing them would immediately return `true` on the very first iteration even for a loop-free list!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
