22246	LLD Project: Tinder / Dating App	lld-project-tinder-dating-app	Design a dating platform architecture. Implement Top-Down aggregation, Swiping mechanics, Chat Rooms, and Matchmaking using a Chain of Responsibility scoring algorithm.	2026-10-07 17:16:35.515424+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: LLD Project: Tinder / Dating App\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/IcgLSo6--ok", "title": "Build Tinder | Dating Site | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v27-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Top-Down vs Bottom-Up LLD Architecture", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nBottom-up works well when atomic entities are clearly understood (e.g., creating a `Product` before a `Cart`).\\n\\nIn a Dating App, the atomic objects are highly granular and abstract (`Interest`, `Location`, `GenderEnum`, `PreferenceLimits`). If you start by coding an `Interest` class, it lacks context.\\nBy starting Top-Down with the core `User` object, the interviewer and developer immediately see the required dependencies (`User` -> needs `Profile` -> needs `Location` -> needs `Coordinates`). It naturally maps the massive dependency graph driven by the central entity's behavioral requirements.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why was the \\"Top-Down\\" architectural approach chosen for the Dating App LLD over the standard \\"Bottom-Up\\" approach?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v27-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Algorithmic Match Scoring via Chain of Responsibility", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nInstead of a handler stopping the chain when a task is fulfilled, the dating app chain accumulates a score.\\n\\n1. **Hard Gates (BasicMatcher):** Evaluates Gender and Age bounds. If they fail, it short-circuits and returns a `0.0` score (Hard stop).\\n2. **Soft Multipliers (LocationMatcher):** If the basic gate passes, it calls `nextMatcher->calculate()`. It then calculates spatial proximity. If users are 2km apart, it adds `+20` bonus points to the running score.\\n3. **InterestMatcher:** Calculates array intersection of hobbies (`Shared Interests / Total Interests`). Adds `+30` bonus points.\\nThe recursion unwinds, summing the values, providing a rich, layered gradient score (e.g., 85% match) allowing the app to sort profiles by highest affinity.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain how the Chain of Responsibility pattern is mathematically adapted to calculate a progressive \\"Match Score\\" rather than simply resolving a boolean request.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v27-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Swipe History Data Structure", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe optimal structure is a Hash Map: `std::unordered_map<std::string, SwipeAction> swipeHistory;` mapping `TargetUserID -> ActionEnum (LEFT, RIGHT)`.\\n\\nWhen User A swipes Right on User B, the system must verify if User B already swiped Right on User A. By storing User B's history in an unordered map, verifying `UserB.swipeHistory.get(\\"UserA\\") == RIGHT` executes in $O(1)$ constant time. This guarantees instant UI responsiveness (the \\"It's a Match\\" popup) without executing heavy linear database scans.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is the optimal data structure to store a User's swipe history (Left/Right) to ensure instant match resolution without lag?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v27-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Chat Room Instantiation & Security", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `ChatRoom` entity cannot be created manually by a client or user. \\n\\n1. **Gated Instantiation:** The `ChatRoom` is strictly instantiated by the `DatingAppFacade` only inside the `if (isMatch)` block evaluating a bidirectional Right Swipe.\\n2. **Composite ID:** The `ChatRoom` is assigned a deterministic ID: `hash(min(UserA, UserB) + \\"_\\" + max(UserA, UserB))`. \\n3. **Validation:** When `sendMessage(sender, receiver, text)` is invoked, the Facade generates the deterministic hash and queries the `ChatRoomManager`. If the room does not exist, the message is dropped, structurally preventing un-matched messaging and spam.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the architecture safely manage Chat Room creation to prevent users from messaging profiles they haven't matched with?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v27-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Observer Pattern for Real-Time Match Alerts", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe core swiping logic should not contain heavy networking code for sending Apple APNS / Firebase push notifications.\\n\\nWe utilize the Observer Pattern:\\n1. The `NotificationService` acts as the Observable Subject.\\n2. The `User` object (or device client) registers itself as an `IObserver` upon login.\\n3. The moment a bidirectional match evaluates to true in the Facade, it calls `NotificationService::getInstance()->notifyUser(UserA, \\"Match!\\")`.\\n4. The service fires the update downward. The UI reacts instantly, decoupling the core matching algorithm from the external networking SDKs.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How is the Observer Design Pattern utilized to trigger the \\"It's a Match!\\" push notification securely?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. Top-Down Design Approach\n* Unlike bottom-up designs, hyper-complex domains with dozens of small entities are easier to model **Top-Down**.\n* Define the central `User` class first, then flesh out its massive dependency graph: `UserProfile` (photos, bio, gender), `UserPreferences` (desired age, distance), `Location`, and `SwipeHistory`.\n\n### 💡 2. Matchmaking: Chain of Responsibility\n* Matching isn't a boolean yes/no; it is an aggregated percentage score based on layered criteria.\n* We implement an algorithmic pipeline using the CoR pattern:\n  1. **Basic Matcher:** Validates hard constraints (Gender preference matching, Age ranges). If failed, halts chain and returns 0%.\n  2. **Location Matcher:** Calculates proximity via Haversine formula. Adds bonus points for geographic closeness.\n  3. **Interest Matcher:** Calculates intersection of arrays (shared hobbies). Yields final dynamic Match Score.\n\n### 💡 3. Swiping & Observer Callbacks\n* **Swiping Logic:** When User A swipes right on User B, it is logged in User A's `Map<String, SwipeType>`.\n* **Match Evaluation:** The system immediately checks User B's map. If User B previously swiped right on User A, it evaluates to `IsMatch = true`.\n* **Observer Execution:** The system triggers an event. The `NotificationService` pushes alerts ("It's a match!"), and a new `ChatRoom` entity is instantiated linking the two users.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	LLD Project: Tinder / Dating App	Design a dating platform architecture. Implement Top-Down aggregation, Swiping mechanics, Chat Rooms, and Matchmaking using a Chain of Responsibility scoring algorithm.	82	Hard	Low Level Design	41	[{"id": "lld-v27-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Top-Down vs Bottom-Up LLD Architecture", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nBottom-up works well when atomic entities are clearly understood (e.g., creating a `Product` before a `Cart`).\\n\\nIn a Dating App, the atomic objects are highly granular and abstract (`Interest`, `Location`, `GenderEnum`, `PreferenceLimits`). If you start by coding an `Interest` class, it lacks context.\\nBy starting Top-Down with the core `User` object, the interviewer and developer immediately see the required dependencies (`User` -> needs `Profile` -> needs `Location` -> needs `Coordinates`). It naturally maps the massive dependency graph driven by the central entity's behavioral requirements.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why was the \\"Top-Down\\" architectural approach chosen for the Dating App LLD over the standard \\"Bottom-Up\\" approach?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v27-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Algorithmic Match Scoring via Chain of Responsibility", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nInstead of a handler stopping the chain when a task is fulfilled, the dating app chain accumulates a score.\\n\\n1. **Hard Gates (BasicMatcher):** Evaluates Gender and Age bounds. If they fail, it short-circuits and returns a `0.0` score (Hard stop).\\n2. **Soft Multipliers (LocationMatcher):** If the basic gate passes, it calls `nextMatcher->calculate()`. It then calculates spatial proximity. If users are 2km apart, it adds `+20` bonus points to the running score.\\n3. **InterestMatcher:** Calculates array intersection of hobbies (`Shared Interests / Total Interests`). Adds `+30` bonus points.\\nThe recursion unwinds, summing the values, providing a rich, layered gradient score (e.g., 85% match) allowing the app to sort profiles by highest affinity.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain how the Chain of Responsibility pattern is mathematically adapted to calculate a progressive \\"Match Score\\" rather than simply resolving a boolean request.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v27-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Swipe History Data Structure", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe optimal structure is a Hash Map: `std::unordered_map<std::string, SwipeAction> swipeHistory;` mapping `TargetUserID -> ActionEnum (LEFT, RIGHT)`.\\n\\nWhen User A swipes Right on User B, the system must verify if User B already swiped Right on User A. By storing User B's history in an unordered map, verifying `UserB.swipeHistory.get(\\"UserA\\") == RIGHT` executes in $O(1)$ constant time. This guarantees instant UI responsiveness (the \\"It's a Match\\" popup) without executing heavy linear database scans.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is the optimal data structure to store a User's swipe history (Left/Right) to ensure instant match resolution without lag?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v27-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Chat Room Instantiation & Security", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `ChatRoom` entity cannot be created manually by a client or user. \\n\\n1. **Gated Instantiation:** The `ChatRoom` is strictly instantiated by the `DatingAppFacade` only inside the `if (isMatch)` block evaluating a bidirectional Right Swipe.\\n2. **Composite ID:** The `ChatRoom` is assigned a deterministic ID: `hash(min(UserA, UserB) + \\"_\\" + max(UserA, UserB))`. \\n3. **Validation:** When `sendMessage(sender, receiver, text)` is invoked, the Facade generates the deterministic hash and queries the `ChatRoomManager`. If the room does not exist, the message is dropped, structurally preventing un-matched messaging and spam.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the architecture safely manage Chat Room creation to prevent users from messaging profiles they haven't matched with?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v27-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Observer Pattern for Real-Time Match Alerts", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe core swiping logic should not contain heavy networking code for sending Apple APNS / Firebase push notifications.\\n\\nWe utilize the Observer Pattern:\\n1. The `NotificationService` acts as the Observable Subject.\\n2. The `User` object (or device client) registers itself as an `IObserver` upon login.\\n3. The moment a bidirectional match evaluates to true in the Facade, it calls `NotificationService::getInstance()->notifyUser(UserA, \\"Match!\\")`.\\n4. The service fires the update downward. The UI reacts instantly, decoupling the core matching algorithm from the external networking SDKs.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How is the Observer Design Pattern utilized to trigger the \\"It's a Match!\\" push notification securely?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
1116	Pointer-Based Node Allocation: Binary Tree Representation in C++	dsa-binary-tree-representation-cpp	Implement self-referential pointer node structs in C++ to dynamically allocate and link binary tree nodes in memory.	2026-10-03 00:35:47.114005+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Pointer-Based Node Allocation: Binary Tree Representation in C++\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ctCpP0RFDFc", "title": "Lecture 74: Binary Tree Representation in C++", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/binary-tree-representation/1", "title": "Binary Tree Representation", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Construct a binary tree using pointer nodes from a level-order array."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/root-equals-sum-of-children/", "title": "Root Equals Sum of Children", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Access root, root->left, and root->right pointers on a 3-node binary tree."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/count-leaves-in-binary-tree/1", "title": "Count Leaves in Binary Tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Basic", "description": "Identify leaf nodes where both left and right pointers are NULL."}]	Module 15: Binary Trees: Hierarchical Representation & Fundamental Traversals	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn C++, a Binary Tree is represented in memory by defining a custom `struct Node` (or `class Node`) that stores an integer `data` value along with two self-referential pointers: `left` and `right`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Structure of a Tree Node:**\n  - `int data`: Holds the node's value.\n  - `Node* left`: Points to the left child node (or `NULL` / `nullptr` if none exists).\n  - `Node* right`: Points to the right child node (or `NULL` / `nullptr` if none exists).\n- **Constructor Initialization:**\n  - When a new node is created (`new Node(val)`), its constructor sets `data = val` and initializes both `left` and `right` pointers to `NULL`.\n- **Linking Nodes Together:**\n  - After creating `Node* root = new Node(1);`, you attach children via `root->left = new Node(2);` and `root->right = new Node(3);`.\n\n### 💻 C++ Binary Tree Node Blueprint\n```cpp\nstruct Node {\n    int data;\n    Node* left;\n    Node* right;\n\n    Node(int val) {\n        data = val;\n        left = nullptr;\n        right = nullptr;\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Node* root = new Node(1);\n    root->left = new Node(2);\n    root->right = new Node(3);\n    root->left->right = new Node(5);\n    return 0;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
