es/"}, {"id": "pg-12-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/", "name": "Customers Who Never Order", "title": "Customers Who Never Order", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "12_7", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Customers Who Never Order\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-never-order/"}, {"id": "pg-12-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/", "name": "Duplicate Emails", "title": "Duplicate Emails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "12_8", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Duplicate Emails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/duplicate-emails/"}, {"id": "pg-12-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/", "name": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "title": "Employees Earning More Than Their Managers", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "12_9", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Employees Earning More Than Their Managers\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/employees-earning-more-than-their-managers/"}, {"id": "pg-12-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/", "name": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "title": "Delete Duplicate Emails", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "12_10", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Delete Duplicate Emails\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-duplicate-emails/"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nChoosing the right **Primary Key** strategy impacts both application security and B-Tree index performance. Exposing sequential IDs (`/payments/1`, `/payments/2`) in public URLs creates **IDOR (Insecure Direct Object Reference)** enumeration vulnerabilities, while random `UUIDv4` keys cause B-Tree index fragmentation. **PostgreSQL 18's native `uuidv7()`** solves both problems.\n\n### ⚖️ Identifier Architecture Comparison\n\n* **`SERIAL`:** 32-bit (4 Bytes) — Yes (`1, 2, 3...`) — ❌ No (Easy to guess) — Small internal tables / learning projects.\n* **`BIGSERIAL`:** 64-bit (8 Bytes) — Yes (`1, 2, 3...`) — ❌ No (Easy to guess) — **Internal Foreign Keys & fast Joins** in large systems.\n* **`UUIDv4` (`gen_random_uuid()`):** 128-bit (16 Bytes) — ❌ Completely Random (Breaks B-Tree locality) — ✅ Yes — Legacy distributed IDs.\n* **`UUIDv7` (`uuidv7()`):** 128-bit (16 Bytes) — ✅ **Time-Ordered Prefix** + Random Suffix — ✅ Yes — **Modern Production Public IDs** (Index-friendly + unguessable).\n\n### 🏗️ Production Dual-ID Pattern (Internal `BIGSERIAL` + External `UUIDv7`)\nIn enterprise architectures, tables use a compact `BIGSERIAL` `id` for fast internal joins and a time-ordered `UUIDv7` `public_id` for external APIs, receipts, and shareable links:\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Dual-ID Production Table Pattern (PostgreSQL 18+)\nCREATE TABLE payments (\n    id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,                  -- Fast 8-byte internal ID for Joins\n    public_id UUID DEFAULT uuidv7() UNIQUE,    -- Safe 16-byte external ID for URLs/APIs\n    amount NUMERIC(10, 2) NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT NOW()\n);\n\n-- 2. Standalone UUID Primary Key Table (Digital Certificates)\nCREATE TABLE certificates (\n    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT uuidv7(),      -- Or gen_random_uuid() on PG 14-17\n    student_id INT NOT NULL,\n    course_id INT NOT NULL,\n    issued_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\n);\n\n-- Insert without specifying ID (Auto-generated by DEFAULT)\nINSERT INTO certificates (student_id, course_id)\nVALUES (9, 3)\nRETURNING *;\n```	Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur 