179	Sorted Array Invariants: Monotonicity Check & In-Place Two-Pointer Deduplication	dsa-arrays-sorted-check-and-inplace-deduplication	Verify sorted order in O(N) and master the slow-and-fast two-pointer pattern to remove duplicates from a sorted array in-place in O(1) space.	2026-10-02 22:22:31.475215+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Sorted Array Invariants: Monotonicity Check & In-Place Two-Pointer Deduplication\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=37E9ckMDdTk", "title": "Lecture 17: Check if Array is Sorted & Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array In-Place", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/check-if-array-is-sorted-and-rotated/", "title": "Check if Array Is Sorted and Rotated", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1752", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Check adjacent order drops to determine if an array is sorted and rotated."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/remove-element/", "title": "Remove Element", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "27", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Use the slow-and-fast two-pointer pattern to filter out target values in-place."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/remove-duplicates-from-sorted-array-ii/", "title": "Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array II", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "80", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Extend in-place two-pointer deduplication so each unique element appears at most twice."}]	Module 8: Array Manipulation, Two-Pointer Patterns & Prefix Sums	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThis topic focuses on two classic sorted-array problems: verifying whether an array is sorted in non-decreasing order, and **Removing Duplicates In-Place from a Sorted Array** in `O(N)` time and `O(1)` extra space using the **Slow & Fast Two-Pointer** pattern.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **1. Checking if an Array is Sorted (`O(N)`):**\n  - Loop from `i = 1` to `n - 1` and check if every element is greater than or equal to its previous neighbor (`arr[i] >= arr[i - 1]`). If any element is smaller than the previous one, immediately return `false`.\n- **2. Removing Duplicates from a Sorted Array:**\n  - *Brute Force (`O(N log N)` Time, `O(N)` Space):* Insert all elements into a `set<int>` (which keeps unique sorted values), then copy them back into the front of the array.\n  - *Optimal Two-Pointer (`O(N)` Time, `O(1)` Space):*\n    - Keep pointer `i = 0` marking the last unique element placed.\n    - Scan pointer `j` from `1` to `n - 1`.\n    - Whenever `arr[j] != arr[i]`, we have found a new unique number! Place it at `arr[i + 1] = arr[j]` and increment `i++`.\n    - Return `i + 1` as the count of unique elements.\n\n### 💻 Two-Pointer In-Place Deduplication\n```cpp\nint removeDuplicates(vector<int> &arr) {\n    int i = 0; // Points to the last unique element\n    for (int j = 1; j < arr.size(); j++) {\n        if (arr[j] != arr[i]) {\n            arr[i + 1] = arr[j];\n            i++;\n        }\n    }\n    return i + 1; // Total count of unique elements\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
307	Single-Pass Prefix Minimum Tracking: Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock	dsa-best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-single-pass	Maximize stock profit by tracking running prefix minimum prices in a single O(N) pass with O(1) space.	2026-10-02 22:33:52.489949+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Single-Pass Prefix Minimum Tracking: Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=excAOvwF_Wk", "title": "Lecture 25: Stock Buy and Sell — Maximize Profit with Single Transaction", "duration": "22m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-difference-between-increasing-elements/", "title": "Maximum Difference Between Increasing Elements", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Track the minimum element seen so far to maximize nums[j] - nums[i] for i < j."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-ii/", "title": "Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock II", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Extend stock profit logic when you are allowed to buy and sell multiple times."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/stock-buy-and-sell-1587115621/1", "title": "Stock buy and sell", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Identify optimal buy and sell days across a price series."}]	Module 9: Medium Array Patterns & Classic Named Algorithms	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array `prices` where `prices[i]` is the price of a stock on day `i`, you want to maximize your profit by choosing **one day to buy** and a **later day to sell**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Buy Before You Sell:** You cannot sell on day `1` and buy on day `2`—the buy day must strictly come before the sell day.\n- **Core Intuition ("Remember the Minimum on the Left"):**\n  - If you decide to sell the stock on day `i`, when should you have bought it to get the maximum profit?\n  - On whichever previous day (`0` to `i - 1`) had the **lowest price**!\n- **Single-Pass Algorithm (O(N) Time, O(1) Space):**\n  - Initialize `mini = prices[0]` (cheapest buy price seen so far) and `maxProfit = 0`.\n  - Loop `i` from `1` to `n - 1`:\n    1. Calculate the profit if sold today: `cost = prices[i] - mini`.\n    2. Update `maxProfit = max(maxProfit, cost)`.\n    3. Update the lowest buy price for future days: `mini = min(mini, prices[i])`.\n\n### 💻 Best Time to Buy & Sell Stock Blueprint\n```cpp\nint maxProfit(vector<int> &prices) {\n    int mini = prices[0], maxProfit = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i < prices.size(); i++) {\n        int cost = prices[i] - mini;\n        maxProfit = max(maxProfit, cost);\n        mini = min(mini, prices[i]); // Remember minimum price seen so far\n    }\n    return maxProfit;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
304	Three-Way Partitioning: Dutch National Flag Algorithm (Sort 0s, 1s, and 2s)	dsa-dutch-national-flag-three-way-partitioning	Sort an array containing only 0s, 1s, and 2s in a single pass using Dijkstra's Dutch National Flag 3-pointer partition invariant (low, mid, high).	2026-10-02 22:33:52.478695+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Three-Way Partitioning: Dutch National Flag Algorithm (Sort 0s, 1s, and 2s)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tp8JIuCXBaU", "title": "Lecture 22: Sort an array of 0's 1's and 2's — Dutch National Flag Algorithm", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/partition-array-according-to-given-pivot/", "title": "Partition Array According to Given Pivot", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Partition elements into three groups (< pivot, == pivot, > pivot)."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sort-array-by-parity-ii/", "title": "Sort Array By Parity II", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Use multi-pointer swapping to place even and odd integers at matching parity indices."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/three-way-partitioning/1", "title": "Three way partitioning", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Apply the Dutch National Flag 3-pointer partitioning around a given range [a, b]."}]	Module 9: Medium Array Patterns & Classic Named Algorithms	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhen an array contains only `0`s, `1`s, and `2`s, standard sorting takes `O(N log N)` time and counting frequencies takes two passes (`O(2N)`). The **Dutch National Flag Algorithm** sorts the array in a **single pass (O(N) time)** and **O(1) space** using three pointers: `low`, `mid`, and `high`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (The 4-Region Invariant)\nThe algorithm divides the array into four sections:\n- `0` to `low - 1` → All `0`s (extreme left)\n- `low` to `mid - 1` → All `1`s (middle sorted section)\n- `mid` to `high` → **Unsorted elements** we are currently processing\n- `high + 1` to `n - 1` → All `2`s (extreme right)\n\n### ⚡ The 3 Pointer Rules (While `mid <= high`)\nStart with `low = 0`, `mid = 0`, and `high = n - 1`:\n1. **If `arr[mid] == 0`:** Swap `arr[low]` and `arr[mid]`, then increment both `low++` and `mid++`.\n2. **If `arr[mid] == 1`:** It is already in the right place; simply move `mid++`.\n3. **If `arr[mid] == 2`:** Swap `arr[mid]` and `arr[high]`, then decrement **only** `high--` (do not move `mid` yet, because the new element swapped from `high` is still uninspected).\n\n### 💻 Dutch National Flag Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid sortZeroOneTwo(vector<int> &arr) {\n    int low = 0, mid = 0, high = arr.size() - 1;\n    while (mid <= high) {\n        if (arr[mid] == 0) {\n            swap(arr[low], arr[mid]);\n            low++;\n            mid++;\n        } else if (arr[mid] == 1) {\n            mid++;\n        } else {\n            swap(arr[mid], arr[high]);\n            high--;\n        }\n    }\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
