given Preorder and Inorder arrays."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/construct-binary-tree-from-inorder-and-postorder-traversal/", "title": "Construct Binary Tree from Inorder and Postorder Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Construct a unique binary tree given Inorder and Postorder arrays."}]	Module 18: Binary Trees: Node Distances, Tree Modifications & Reconstruction	### 📌 Topic Overview\nCan we always reconstruct a unique binary tree given any two traversal sequences? This theoretical lecture covers the mathematical requirements needed to guarantee a single unique tree.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Inorder is Mandatory:**\n  - **Preorder (`Root → Left → Right`)** and **Postorder (`Left → Right → Root`)** tell you which node is the parent, but they **cannot distinguish** whether a single child belongs to the left subtree or the right subtree!\n  - **Inorder (`Left → Root → Right`)** is the only traversal that provides spatial separation: once you identify the root, everything to its left belongs to the left subtree, and everything to its right belongs to the right subtree.\n- **Valid Traversal Pairs:**\n  - **`Preorder + Inorder`:** Always constructs a unique binary tree.\n  - **`Postorder + Inorder`:** Always constructs a unique binary tree.\n  - **`Preorder + Postorder`:** Does **NOT** construct a unique binary tree in general (it only works if the tree is guaranteed to be a *Full* Binary Tree where every node has 0 or 2 children).	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22215	What are Bloom Filters?	hld-bloom-filters	Master probabilistic data structures, bit arrays, hash functions, and false-positive trade-offs for high-speed membership verification.	2026-10-07 16:41:22.814808+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: What are Bloom Filters?\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/vz0QUa4CS3o", "title": "What are Bloom Filters? | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "hld-v10-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: The Fundamental Trade-off of Bloom Filters", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nBloom Filters are probabilistic because they trade deterministic accuracy for extreme memory compression.\\n\\n**The Core Guarantee:**\\n1. **False Negatives are IMPOSSIBLE (0%):** If the Bloom Filter reports that an element does not exist, it is **100% mathematically certain that the element is NOT in the set**. If it had been inserted, all $k$ corresponding hash bits would have been toggled to 1.\\n2. **False Positives are POSSIBLE (>0%):** If the filter reports that an element exists, it means the element is **probably in the set**. Because other previously inserted elements may have independently set those same bit coordinates to 1, the filter can report a collision match for an item that was never inserted.\\n\\n*Summary:* \\"Definitely No, or Maybe Yes.\\"", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why are Bloom Filters described as \\"Probabilistic\\" data structures? What is the core mathematical guarantee regarding False Negatives vs False Positives?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v10-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Username Availability Architecture with Bloom Filters", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Problem:** Users typing in sign-up forms fire hundreds of queries checking if variations (`john`, `john1`, `john_doe`) are taken. Querying PostgreSQL directly creates disk I/O bottlenecks.\\n\\n**The Architecture:**\\n1. Maintain an in-memory Bloom Filter in Redis or application memory representing all registered usernames.\\n2. When a user checks `desired_username`, pass it to the Bloom Filter first:\\n   * **Case A: Bloom Filter returns FALSE:** Because there are zero false negatives, the username is **guaranteed available**. The system immediately returns \\"Available\\" with zero database queries.\\n   * **Case B: Bloom Filter returns TRUE:** The username is *probably taken*. Only in this minority case does the server query PostgreSQL (`SELECT 1 FROM users WHERE username = ...`) to confirm whether it is genuinely taken or a rare false positive.\\n3. **Result:** >95% of unavailable searches and available queries are resolved in nanoseconds from RAM, offloading 90%+ of read traffic from the primary database.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Design a high-throughput username registration system for a social network with 100M users using a Bloom Filter to protect the primary PostgreSQL database from query exhaustion.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v10-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Why Standard Bloom Filters Cannot Support Deletions", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Why Deletions Fail in Standard Filters:**\\nIn a standard Bloom filter, inserting an element sets $k$ bits to `1`. If you attempt to delete an element by setting its $k$ bits back to `0`, you will inadvertently destroy the bits belonging to *other* previously inserted elements that happen to share those same bit positions. This would introduce False Negatives, violating the data structure's fundamental invariant.\\n\\n**The Solution: Counting Bloom Filter**\\nInstead of a single-bit array of 0s and 1s, an array of small integer counters (e.g., 4-bit integers) is used.\\n* **Insertion:** Increment the counter at each of the $k$ hashed positions by 1.\\n* **Deletion:** Decrement the counter at each of the $k$ hashed positions by 1.\\nAs long as counters do not overflow, elements can be safely deleted without corrupting shared entries, at the cost of 3–4× more memory consumption.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why is it impossible to delete an item from a standard Bloom Filter? How does a Counting Bloom Filter solve this limitation?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v10-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Bloom Filters in Storage Engines (LSM-Trees in Cassandra / RocksDB)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn Log-Structured Merge (LSM) tree databases like Cassandra, data is initially written to an in-memory Memtable and periodically flushed to immutable on-disk files called **SSTables (Sorted String Tables)**. Over time, dozens of SSTables accumulate on disk for a single partition.\\n\\n**The Bottleneck:** To read a key that doesn't exist, or to find a key written days ago, the database would have to perform physical disk seeks across every single SSTable file on disk.\\n\\n**The Bloom Filter Optimization:**\\nEvery SSTable on disk has a small corresponding Bloom Filter held in RAM.\\nBefore reading an SSTable from disk, the engine checks its in-memory Bloom filter:\\n* If the filter returns `0`, the key is guaranteed not in that SSTable. The engine skips the file completely without touching the disk.\\n* Only if the filter returns `1` does the engine execute a disk I/O seek.\\nThis eliminates over 90% of redundant disk read operations, keeping point-lookup read latency predictable.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "How do database engines like Apache Cassandra, Google Bigtable, and RocksDB utilize Bloom Filters to prevent expensive disk seeks on SSTables?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v10-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Tuning Parameters: Array Size (m) vs Hash Functions (k)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe False Positive probability $p$ is governed by the saturation of the bit array:\\n\\n$$p \\\\approx \\\\left(1 - e^{-kn/m}\\\\right)^k$$\\n\\n1. **Array Size ($m$):** Increasing the bit array size $m$ spreads bits across more coordinates, reducing bit collisions and lowering the false positive rate.\\n2. **Number of Elements ($n$):** As more elements are inserted, the ratio of 1s in the array increases. If $n$ grows too large, the array fills with 1s, and the false positive rate approaches 100%.\\n3. **Optimal Number of Hashes ($k$):** For a given array size $m$ and item count $n$, the optimal number of hash functions that minimizes false positives is mathematically given by:\\n$$k = \\\\frac{m}{n} \\\\ln(2) \\\\approx 0.7 \\\\times \\\\frac{m}{n}$$\\nIf $k$ is too low, differentiation is poor; if $k$ is too high, each insertion sets too many bits, rapidly saturating the array.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "What is the relationship between the bit array size (m), the number of hash functions (k), the number of inserted elements (n), and the False Positive probability (p)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	High Level Design	### 📌 1. The Membership Problem at Scale\n* Systems like Twitter/X (username availability), Google Chrome (malicious URL checking), and database engines (LSM-tree disk seek prevention) must check if an item exists among hundreds of millions of records.\n* **Standard Hash Maps:** $O(1)$ lookups, but memory consumption is $O(N)$—storing 100M string usernames in RAM consumes gigabytes of memory.\n* **Database Queries:** Running `SELECT WHERE username = '...' ` across disk-bound B-Trees causes excessive I/O bottlenecks.\n\n### 💡 2. The Bloom Filter Solution\n* A **Bloom Filter** is a space-efficient, probabilistic data structure designed to test whether an element is a member of a set.\n* **Component Anatomy:** Consists of a **Bit Array of size $m$** initialized to all $0$s and **$k$ independent uniform hash functions**.\n* **Insertion:** Pass the element through all $k$ hash functions to get $k$ array indices; set the bits at those positions to $1$.\n* **Query:** Hash the search key through all $k$ hash functions. If **ANY** bit is $0$, the element is **definitely NOT in the set**. If **ALL** bits are $1$, the element is **PROBABLY in the set**.\n\n### 💡 3. The Core Guarantee & Trade-offs\n* **Zero False Negatives:** If a Bloom Filter says "Not Present", it is 100% guaranteed to be absent.\n* **Possible False Positives:** Due to overlapping bit collisions from other elements, it may claim an item is present when it isn't.\n* **No Deletion:** Standard Bloom Filters do not support deleting elements because resetting a bit to $0$ might accidentally delete bits shared by other elements.	Piyush Garg	https://www.youtube.com/@piyushgarg195	28	What are Bloom Filters?	Master probabilistic data structures, bit arrays, hash functions, and false-positive trade-offs for high-speed membership verification.	21	Hard	High Level Design	10	[{"id": "hld-v10-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: The Fundamental Trade-off of Bloom Filters", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nBloom Filters are probabilistic because they trade deterministic accuracy for extreme memory compression.\\n\\n**The Core Guarantee:**\\n1. **False Negatives are IMPOSSIBLE (0%):** If the Bloom Filter reports that an element does not exist, it is **100% mathematically certain that the element is NOT in the set**. If it had been inserted, all $k$ corresponding hash bits would have been toggled to 1.\\n2. **False Positives are POSSIBLE (>0%):** If the filter reports that an element exists, it means the element is **probably in the set**. Because other previously inserted elements may have independently set those same bit coordinates to 1, the filter can report a collision match for an item that was never inserted.\\n\\n*Summary:* \\"Definitely No, or Maybe Yes.\\"", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why are Bloom Filters described as \\"Probabilistic\\" data structures? What is the core mathematical guarantee regarding False Negatives vs False Positives?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v10-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Username Availability Architecture with Bloom Filters", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Problem:** Users typing in sign-up forms fire hundreds of queries checking if variations (`john`, `john1`, `john_doe`) are taken. Querying PostgreSQL directly creates disk I/O bottlenecks.\\n\\n**The Architecture:**\\n1. Maintain an in-memory Bloom Filter in Redis or application memory representing all registered usernames.\\n2. When a user checks `desired_username`, pass it to the Bloom Filter first:\\n   * **Case A: Bloom Filter returns FALSE:** Because there are zero false negatives, the username is **guaranteed available**. The system immediately returns \\"Available\\" with zero database queries.\\n   * **Case B: Bloom Filter returns TRUE:** The username is *probably taken*. Only in this minority case does the server query PostgreSQL (`SELECT 1 FROM users WHERE username = ...`) to confirm whether it is genuinely taken or a rare false positive.\\n3. **Result:** >95% of unavailable searches and available queries are resolved in nanoseconds from RAM, offloading 90%+ of read traffic from the primary database.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Design a high-throughput username registration system for a social network with 100M users using a Bloom Filter to protect the primary PostgreSQL database from query exhaustion.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v10-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Why Standard Bloom Filters Cannot Support Deletions", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Why Deletions Fail in Standard Filters:**\\nIn a standard Bloom filter, inserting an element sets $k$ bits to `1`. If you attempt to delete an element by setting its $k$ bits back to `0`, you will inadvertently destroy the bits belonging to *other* previously inserted elements that happen to share those same bit positions. This would introduce False Negatives, violating the data structure's fundamental invariant.\\n\\n**The Solution: Counting Bloom Filter**\\nInstead of a single-bit array of 0s and 1s, an array of small integer counters (e.g., 4-bit integers) is used.\\n* **Insertion:** Increment the counter at each of the $k$ hashed positions by 1.\\n* **Deletion:** Decrement the counter at each of the $k$ hashed positions by 1.\\nAs long as counters do not overflow, elements can be safely deleted without corrupting shared entries, at the cost of 3–4× more memory consumption.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why is it impossible to delete an item from a standard Bloom Filter? How does a Counting Bloom Filter solve this limitation?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v10-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Bloom Filters in Storage Engines (LSM-Trees in Cassandra / RocksDB)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn Log-Structured Merge (LSM) tree databases like Cassandra, data is initially written to an in-memory Memtable and periodically flushed to immutable on-disk files called **SSTables (Sorted String Tables)**. Over time, dozens of SSTables accumulate on disk for a single partition.\\n\\n**The Bottleneck:** To read a key that doesn't exist, or to find a key written days ago, the database would have to perform physical disk seeks across every single SSTable file on disk.\\n\\n**The Bloom Filter Optimization:**\\nEvery SSTable on disk has a small corresponding Bloom Filter held in RAM.\\nBefore reading an SSTable from disk, the engine checks its in-memory Bloom filter:\\n* If the filter returns `0`, the key is guaranteed not in that SSTable. The engine skips the file completely without touching the disk.\\n* Only if the filter returns `1` does the engine execute a disk I/O seek.\\nThis eliminates over 90% of redundant disk read operations, keeping point-lookup read latency predictable.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "How do database engines like Apache Cassandra, Google Bigtable, and RocksDB utilize Bloom Filters to prevent expensive disk seeks on SSTables?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v10-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Tuning Parameters: Array Size (m) vs Hash Functions (k)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe False Positive probability $p$ is governed by the saturation of the bit array:\\n\\n$$p \\\\approx \\\\left(1 - e^{-kn/m}\\\\right)^k$$\\n\\n1. **Array Size ($m$):** Increasing the bit array size $m$ spreads bits across more coordinates, reducing bit collisions and lowering the false positive rate.\\n2. **Number of Elements ($n$):** As more elements are inserted, the ratio of 1s in the array increases. If $n$ grows too large, the array fills with 1s, and the false positive rate approaches 100%.\\n3. **Optimal Number of Hashes ($k$):** For a given array size $m$ and item count $n$, the optimal number of hash functions that minimizes false positives is mathematically given by:\\n$$k = \\\\frac{m}{n} \\\\ln(2) \\\\approx 0.7 \\\\times \\\\frac{m}{n}$$\\nIf $k$ is too low, differentiation is poor; if $k$ is too high, each insertion sets too many bits, rapidly saturating the array.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "What is the relationship between the bit array size (m), the number of hash functions (k), the number of inserted elements (n), and the False Positive probability (p)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22079	Lecture 21: Advanced SQL Interview Practice (Part 1) — Combining INNER JOIN, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING & ORDER BY	pg-lecture-21-advanced-sql-practice-joins-group-by-having-part-1	Solve multi-constraint business intelligence queries by combining table joins, row filtering, grouping, aggregate filtering, and sorting in a single pipeline.	2026-10-05 19:34:44.247876+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Lecture 21: Advanced SQL Interview Practice (Part 1) — Combining INNER JOIN, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING & ORDER BY\nPart of the **PostgreSQL & Relational SQL Mastery** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OC1R2JROmsY", "title": "Advanced PostgreSQL Practice Session | Real SQL Interview Questions & Solutions", "duration": "18m", "creator_name": "Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)", "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical"}]	[{"id": "pg-21-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/bank-account-summary-ii/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/bank-account-summary-ii/", "name": "Bank Account Summary II (INNER JOIN + GROUP BY + HAVING SUM)", "title": "Bank Account Summary II (INNER JOIN + GROUP BY + HAVING SUM)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Bank Account Summary II (INNER JOIN + GROUP BY + HAVING SUM)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/bank-account-summary-ii/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/managers-with-at-least-5-direct-reports/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/managers-with-at-least-5-direct-reports/", "name": "Managers with at Least 5 Direct Reports (JOIN + GROUP BY + HAVING)", "title": "Managers with at Least 5 Direct Reports (JOIN + GROUP BY + HAVING)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Managers with at Least 5 Direct Reports (JOIN + GROUP BY + HAVING)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/managers-with-at-least-5-direct-reports/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/", "name": "Customers Who Bought All Products", "title": "Customers Who Bought All Products", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Customers Who Bought All Products\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/movie-rating/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/movie-rating/", "name": "Movie Rating (JOIN + WHERE + GROUP BY + ORDER BY)", "title": "Movie Rating (JOIN + WHERE + GROUP BY + ORDER BY)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Movie Rating (JOIN + WHERE + GROUP BY + ORDER BY)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/movie-rating/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/", "name": "Monthly Transactions I", "title": "Monthly Transactions I", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Monthly Transactions I\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/capital-gainloss/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/capital-gainloss/", "name": "Capital Gain/Loss", "title": "Capital Gain/Loss", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Capital Gain/Loss\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/capital-gainloss/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/", "name": "Market Analysis I", "title": "Market Analysis I", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_7", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Market Analysis I\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/product-sales-analysis-iii/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/product-sales-analysis-iii/", "name": "Product Sales Analysis III", "title": "Product Sales Analysis III", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_8", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Product Sales Analysis III\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/product-sales-analysis-iii/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/friend-requests-ii-who-has-the-most-friends/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/friend-requests-ii-who-has-the-most-friends/", "name": "Friend Requests II: Who Has the Most Friends", "title": "Friend Requests II: Who Has the Most Friends", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_9", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Friend Requests II: Who Has the Most Friends\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/friend-requests-ii-who-has-the-most-friends/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/trips-and-users/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/trips-and-users/", "name": "Trips and Users", "title": "Trips and Users", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_10", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Solve \\"Trips and Users\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/trips-and-users/"}]	\N	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn real backend engineering and technical interviews, you are rarely asked to write an isolated `JOIN` or an isolated `GROUP BY`. Instead, you are given a **business problem** that requires chaining `SELECT`, `FROM`, `INNER JOIN`, `WHERE`, `GROUP BY`, `HAVING`, and `ORDER BY` in exact syntactic order.\n\n### 🏆 Case Study 1: The VIP Top-Spending Active Students Query\n\n#### Business Requirement:\nThe college wants to send a VIP thank-you gift to top-spending active students. Write a query that:\n1. Calculates the **total revenue spent per student** across all their payments.\n2. **Filter 1 (Row-level):** Only include students who are currently active (`is_active = true`).\n3. **Filter 2 (Group-level):** Only show students whose total spent amount is strictly greater than `5000`.\n4. **Sorting:** Sort the list so the highest spender appears at the very top.\n\n#### Step-by-Step Solution:\n```sql\nSELECT \n    u.name AS student_name,\n    SUM(p.amount) AS total_spent\nFROM users u\nINNER JOIN payments p\n    ON u.id = p.student_id\nWHERE u.is_active = true\nGROUP BY u.name\nHAVING SUM(p.amount) > 5000\nORDER BY total_spent DESC;\n```\n\n### 🧠 Why Every Clause Is Positioned Where It Is\n1. **`INNER JOIN payments p ON u.id = p.student_id`** comes immediately after `FROM users u` — **before** `WHERE`.\n2. **`WHERE u.is_active = true`** filters out inactive students **before** PostgreSQL spends CPU cycles grouping their payments.\n3. **`GROUP BY u.name`** collapses multiple payment rows per student into a single summary bucket per student.\n4. **`HAVING SUM(p.amount) > 5000`** filters the aggregated buckets after `SUM(p.amount)` has been computed.\n5. **`ORDER BY total_spent DESC`** runs after `SELECT`, which is why it can reference the output alias `total_spent` directly.	Thapa Technical (Vinod Bahadur Thapa)	https://www.youtube.com/@ThapaTechnical	654	Lecture 21: Advanced SQL Interview Practice (Part 1) — Combining INNER JOIN, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING & ORDER BY	Solve multi-constraint business intelligence queries by combining table joins, row filtering, grouping, aggregate filtering, and sorting in a single pipeline.	18	Medium	Module 6: Aggregations, Grouping & Analytical Queries	21	[{"id": "pg-21-prob-1", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/bank-account-summary-ii/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/bank-account-summary-ii/", "name": "Bank Account Summary II (INNER JOIN + GROUP BY + HAVING SUM)", "title": "Bank Account Summary II (INNER JOIN + GROUP BY + HAVING SUM)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_1", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Solve \\"Bank Account Summary II (INNER JOIN + GROUP BY + HAVING SUM)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/bank-account-summary-ii/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-2", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/managers-with-at-least-5-direct-reports/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/managers-with-at-least-5-direct-reports/", "name": "Managers with at Least 5 Direct Reports (JOIN + GROUP BY + HAVING)", "title": "Managers with at Least 5 Direct Reports (JOIN + GROUP BY + HAVING)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_2", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Managers with at Least 5 Direct Reports (JOIN + GROUP BY + HAVING)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/managers-with-at-least-5-direct-reports/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-3", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/", "name": "Customers Who Bought All Products", "title": "Customers Who Bought All Products", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_3", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Customers Who Bought All Products\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/customers-who-bought-all-products/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-4", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/movie-rating/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/movie-rating/", "name": "Movie Rating (JOIN + WHERE + GROUP BY + ORDER BY)", "title": "Movie Rating (JOIN + WHERE + GROUP BY + ORDER BY)", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_4", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Movie Rating (JOIN + WHERE + GROUP BY + ORDER BY)\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/movie-rating/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-5", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/", "name": "Monthly Transactions I", "title": "Monthly Transactions I", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_5", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Monthly Transactions I\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/monthly-transactions-i/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-6", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/capital-gainloss/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/capital-gainloss/", "name": "Capital Gain/Loss", "title": "Capital Gain/Loss", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_6", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Capital Gain/Loss\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/capital-gainloss/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-7", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/", "name": "Market Analysis I", "title": "Market Analysis I", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_7", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Market Analysis I\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/market-analysis-i/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-8", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/product-sales-analysis-iii/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/product-sales-analysis-iii/", "name": "Product Sales Analysis III", "title": "Product Sales Analysis III", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_8", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Product Sales Analysis III\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/product-sales-analysis-iii/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-9", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/friend-requests-ii-who-has-the-most-friends/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/friend-requests-ii-who-has-the-most-friends/", "name": "Friend Requests II: Who Has the Most Friends", "title": "Friend Requests II: Who Has the Most Friends", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_9", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve \\"Friend Requests II: Who Has the Most Friends\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/friend-requests-ii-who-has-the-most-friends/"}, {"id": "pg-21-prob-10", "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/trips-and-users/", "link": "https://leetcode.com/problems/trips-and-users/", "name": "Trips and Users", "title": "Trips and Users", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "21_10", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Solve \\"Trips and Users\\" on LeetCode using PostgreSQL.", "external_url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/trips-and-users/"}]	https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-agg.html	\N	\N	\N
4718	Sequential Edge Relaxation & Negative Cycle Detection: Bellman-Ford Algorithm	dsa-bellman-ford-algorithm-negative-cycles	Compute shortest paths in graphs with negative edge weights and detect negative weight cycles via V - 1 edge relaxations.	2026-10-03 11:07:15.304347+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Sequential Edge Relaxation & Negative Cycle Detection: Bellman-Ford Algorithm\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0vVofAhAYjc", "title": "Lecture 166: Bellman Ford Algorithm", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/distance-from-the-source-bellman-ford-algorithm/1", "title": "Distance from the Source (Bellman-Ford Algorithm)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Compute shortest paths from source S to all vertices and detect negative weight cycles."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/cheapest-flights-within-k-stops/", "title": "Cheapest Flights Within K Stops (LC 787)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Solve K-stop shortest paths using K + 1 iterations of Bellman-Ford edge relaxation."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/negative-weight-cycle3504/1", "title": "Negative weight cycle", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Determine whether a directed weighted graph contains a negative weight cycle."}]	Module 25: Negative-Weight Shortest Paths, All-Pairs Shortest Paths & Minimum Spanning Trees	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhile Dijkstra's Algorithm fails on graphs with negative edge weights, the **Bellman-Ford Algorithm** computes single-source shortest paths in directed graphs **even with negative weights** and detects **negative weight cycles** (cycles whose total edge weight sum is `< 0`).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Relax All Edges `V - 1` Times?**\n  - Any simple shortest path in a graph of `V` vertices can contain at most **`V - 1` edges**.\n  - In iteration 1, we are guaranteed to relax the 1st edge of any shortest path; in iteration 2, the 2nd edge; and after **`V - 1` full iterations** over all `E` edges, all shortest distances are guaranteed to be finalized!\n- **How to Detect a Negative Cycle (The `V`-th Iteration):**\n  - After completing `V - 1` iterations, run **one more (`V`-th) iteration** over all edges.\n  - If **`dist[u] + wt < dist[v]`** still holds for any edge on the `V`-th pass, a reachable **negative weight cycle** exists—return `{-1}`!\n- **Complexity:** `O(V * E)` Time and `O(V)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Bellman-Ford Algorithm Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> bellmanFord(int V, vector<vector<int>> &edges, int S) {\n    vector<int> dist(V, 1e8);\n    dist[S] = 0;\n\n    // 1. Relax all E edges exactly V - 1 times\n    for (int i = 0; i < V - 1; i++) {\n        for (const auto &it : edges) {\n            int u = it[0], v = it[1], wt = it[2];\n            if (dist[u] != 1e8 && dist[u] + wt < dist[v]) {\n                dist[v] = dist[u] + wt;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // 2. N-th relaxation to check for negative weight cycles\n    for (const auto &it : edges) {\n        int u = it[0], v = it[1], wt = it[2];\n        if (dist[u] != 1e8 && dist[u] + wt < dist[v]) {\n            return {-1}; // Negative cycle detected!\n        }\n    }\n\n    return dist;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Relaxing From Unreachable Nodes (`dist[u] == 1e8`):** With negative weights (e.g., `wt = -5`), if you don't check `dist[u] != 1e8`, an unreachable node's distance will become `1e8 - 5`, corrupting `dist[v]`!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
