22253	LLD Project: Snake and Ladder Game	lld-project-snake-and-ladder	Design a dynamic grid game. Abstract Snakes and Ladders as BoardEntities, utilize Top-Down LLD for gameplay orchestration, and generate boards via Builder Strategies.	2026-10-07 22:38:19.438928+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: LLD Project: Snake and Ladder Game\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/1NJB54UB8nE", "title": "Build Snake and Ladder Game | UML + Code | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v34-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Abstracting Snakes and Ladders", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf the Board holds `vector<Snake>` and `vector<Ladder>` separately, the game loop must perform two distinct lookups every time a player moves: `if (isSnake(...)) moveDown(); else if (isLadder(...)) moveUp();`. This breaks the Open/Closed Principle (e.g., what if you add a \\"Teleport Portal\\" entity?).\\n\\nBy inheriting both from `BoardEntity(start, end)`, the abstraction captures the core behavior: moving a player from point A to point B. The game loop simply queries `entity = map.get(currentPos); newPos = entity.getEnd();`. The logic is perfectly unified and extensible to infinite entity types.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is it an architectural best practice to inherit both Snake and Ladder from a common BoardEntity abstract class rather than treating them as separate arrays in the Board?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v34-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Memory Optimization via Hash Maps", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn a 10,000-cell Snake & Ladder board, 9,900 of those cells are completely empty and perform no action. Allocating a massive 10,000-element Array containing mostly `null` pointers is a massive waste of RAM.\\n\\nA Hash Map leverages sparse data structures. It only allocates memory for the active trigger cells (e.g., cell 25 has a ladder). When checking a cell, `map.find(25)` returns the entity in $O(1)$ time, while `map.find(26)` instantly returns `null`, keeping the memory footprint strictly bounded to the number of entities, not the size of the board.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain why using a std::unordered_map<int, BoardEntity> for the Board is significantly more efficient than initializing a 100-element or 10,000-element Array.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v34-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Infinite Loop Prevention Validation", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThis configuration creates an **Infinite Teleportation Cycle**. If a player lands on 50, they drop to 20, which instantly triggers the ladder back to 50, causing a runtime infinite recursion crash.\\n\\n**The Validation Shield:**\\nInside the `Board::addEntity(BoardEntity e)` method, the system must enforce strict graph cycle detection.\\n1. Check that `e.start != e.end`.\\n2. Before adding, check if an entity already exists at `e.start` (prevents overlapping heads).\\n3. Check if an entity already exists at `e.end`. If adding a Snake dropping to 20, and 20 is already the head of a Ladder, the system must reject the insertion (`throw CycleDetectionException`) to guarantee deterministic game state resolution.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "During Board setup using a CustomStrategy, a user places a Snake from 50 to 20, and a Ladder from 20 to 50. How must the Board architecture validate and prevent this catastrophic flaw?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v34-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Strategy Pattern for Board Generation", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `Board` class is a data container. It shouldn't contain complex procedural generation algorithms.\\n\\nWe define an `IBoardSetupStrategy` interface with a `setup(Board* b)` method.\\n1. `StandardSetup`: Hardcodes the classic 1980s Milton Bradley snake/ladder coordinates via `b->addEntity(...)`.\\n2. `RandomSetup`: Uses RNG to generate non-overlapping pairs. If `difficulty == HARD`, it generates 80% snakes and 20% ladders.\\n3. `CustomSetup`: Parses a JSON payload provided by the client and inserts entities directly.\\nThe Factory passes the chosen Strategy to the Board during initialization, executing `strategy->setup(this);`, keeping the board generation logic perfectly isolated.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How is the Strategy Pattern utilized to support \\"Standard\\", \\"Randomized\\", and \\"Custom\\" board setups without polluting the Board class?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v34-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Bounding Logic (End Game Resolution)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThis logic belongs strictly inside the `GameRules::calculateNewPosition(currentPos, diceValue, boardSize)` method.\\n\\n1. It calculates `target = currentPos + diceValue` (98 + 5 = 103).\\n2. It evaluates `if (target > boardSize)`.\\n3. Because 103 > 100, the move is invalid under standard rules.\\n4. The method rejects the move and returns the unmodified `currentPos` (98).\\nThe orchestration loop recognizes that the position hasn't changed, notifies the user \\"You need exactly a 2 to win!\\", and advances the deque to the next player's turn.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "A player is on cell 98. They roll a 5 on the dice, pushing their calculated target to 103 on a 100-cell board. Where is this validated, and what is the outcome?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Abstraction of Board Entities\n* A Snake (Start=99, End=10) and a Ladder (Start=15, End=85) perform the exact same mathematical function: they intercept a player landing on `Start` and teleport them to `End`.\n* To prevent duplicate code and ugly `if (isSnake) else if (isLadder)` blocks, both inherit from an abstract `BoardEntity` class.\n* The Board simply queries: `if (BoardEntity entity = getEntityAt(position)) { return entity.getEnd(); }`.\n\n### 💡 2. Scalable Board Mapping\n* Unlike Tic Tac Toe, a 100-cell board does not require a 100-element array.\n* Only the active jump nodes matter. The Board uses a `std::unordered_map<int, BoardEntity>` mapping `StartIndex -> Entity`.\n* This yields $O(1)$ instant lookup. If a player lands on 25, the game checks the hash map. If cell 25 is not a key in the map, it is an empty cell; the player stays at 25.\n\n### 💡 3. Game Orchestration via Top-Down Design\n* The `Game` orchestrator holds the `Board`, a `Dice` (with configurable faces), and a `Deque<Player>`.\n* The game loop rolls the dice, calculates the target position, checks bounds, checks for entity interception, updates the player's position, checks for victory (Position == 100), and cycles the deque.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	LLD Project: Snake and Ladder Game	Design a dynamic grid game. Abstract Snakes and Ladders as BoardEntities, utilize Top-Down LLD for gameplay orchestration, and generate boards via Builder Strategies.	68	Hard	Low Level Design	48	[{"id": "lld-v34-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Abstracting Snakes and Ladders", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf the Board holds `vector<Snake>` and `vector<Ladder>` separately, the game loop must perform two distinct lookups every time a player moves: `if (isSnake(...)) moveDown(); else if (isLadder(...)) moveUp();`. This breaks the Open/Closed Principle (e.g., what if you add a \\"Teleport Portal\\" entity?).\\n\\nBy inheriting both from `BoardEntity(start, end)`, the abstraction captures the core behavior: moving a player from point A to point B. The game loop simply queries `entity = map.get(currentPos); newPos = entity.getEnd();`. The logic is perfectly unified and extensible to infinite entity types.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why is it an architectural best practice to inherit both Snake and Ladder from a common BoardEntity abstract class rather than treating them as separate arrays in the Board?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v34-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Memory Optimization via Hash Maps", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn a 10,000-cell Snake & Ladder board, 9,900 of those cells are completely empty and perform no action. Allocating a massive 10,000-element Array containing mostly `null` pointers is a massive waste of RAM.\\n\\nA Hash Map leverages sparse data structures. It only allocates memory for the active trigger cells (e.g., cell 25 has a ladder). When checking a cell, `map.find(25)` returns the entity in $O(1)$ time, while `map.find(26)` instantly returns `null`, keeping the memory footprint strictly bounded to the number of entities, not the size of the board.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain why using a std::unordered_map<int, BoardEntity> for the Board is significantly more efficient than initializing a 100-element or 10,000-element Array.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v34-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Infinite Loop Prevention Validation", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThis configuration creates an **Infinite Teleportation Cycle**. If a player lands on 50, they drop to 20, which instantly triggers the ladder back to 50, causing a runtime infinite recursion crash.\\n\\n**The Validation Shield:**\\nInside the `Board::addEntity(BoardEntity e)` method, the system must enforce strict graph cycle detection.\\n1. Check that `e.start != e.end`.\\n2. Before adding, check if an entity already exists at `e.start` (prevents overlapping heads).\\n3. Check if an entity already exists at `e.end`. If adding a Snake dropping to 20, and 20 is already the head of a Ladder, the system must reject the insertion (`throw CycleDetectionException`) to guarantee deterministic game state resolution.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "During Board setup using a CustomStrategy, a user places a Snake from 50 to 20, and a Ladder from 20 to 50. How must the Board architecture validate and prevent this catastrophic flaw?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v34-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Strategy Pattern for Board Generation", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe `Board` class is a data container. It shouldn't contain complex procedural generation algorithms.\\n\\nWe define an `IBoardSetupStrategy` interface with a `setup(Board* b)` method.\\n1. `StandardSetup`: Hardcodes the classic 1980s Milton Bradley snake/ladder coordinates via `b->addEntity(...)`.\\n2. `RandomSetup`: Uses RNG to generate non-overlapping pairs. If `difficulty == HARD`, it generates 80% snakes and 20% ladders.\\n3. `CustomSetup`: Parses a JSON payload provided by the client and inserts entities directly.\\nThe Factory passes the chosen Strategy to the Board during initialization, executing `strategy->setup(this);`, keeping the board generation logic perfectly isolated.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How is the Strategy Pattern utilized to support \\"Standard\\", \\"Randomized\\", and \\"Custom\\" board setups without polluting the Board class?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v34-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Bounding Logic (End Game Resolution)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThis logic belongs strictly inside the `GameRules::calculateNewPosition(currentPos, diceValue, boardSize)` method.\\n\\n1. It calculates `target = currentPos + diceValue` (98 + 5 = 103).\\n2. It evaluates `if (target > boardSize)`.\\n3. Because 103 > 100, the move is invalid under standard rules.\\n4. The method rejects the move and returns the unmodified `currentPos` (98).\\nThe orchestration loop recognizes that the position hasn't changed, notifies the user \\"You need exactly a 2 to win!\\", and advances the deque to the next player's turn.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "A player is on cell 98. They roll a 5 on the dice, pushing their calculated target to 103 on a 100-cell board. Where is this validated, and what is the outcome?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
4221	Algorithmic Analysis: Why Priority Queue Over Queue & Time Complexity Derivation of Dijkstra (Part 3)	dsa-dijkstras-algorithm-intuition-and-time-complexity	Analyze why min-heap priority queues prevent redundant edge propagations and derive Dijkstra's O(E log V) complexity.	2026-10-03 11:01:59.936425+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Algorithmic Analysis: Why Priority Queue Over Queue & Time Complexity Derivation of Dijkstra (Part 3)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3dINsjyfooY", "title": "Lecture 159: Why Priority Queue is used in Dijkstra's Algorithm | Time Complexity Derivation - Part 3", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/network-delay-time/", "title": "Network Delay Time (LC 743)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Add an early-skip check (if d > dist[u] continue) to optimize priority queue Dijkstra."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-time-to-visit-disappearing-nodes/", "title": "Minimum Time to Visit Disappearing Nodes (LC 3112)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Apply Dijkstra's algorithm where skipping stale heap entries is mandatory to avoid TLE."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/cheapest-flights-within-k-stops/", "title": "Cheapest Flights Within K Stops (LC 787)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Understand when a plain Queue sorted by stops is used vs. a Priority Queue sorted by cost."}]	Module 24: Shortest Path Algorithms in Graphs: DAG Relaxation, BFS & Dijkstra's Algorithm	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhy do we use a **Priority Queue (`O(E log V)`)** instead of a plain **FIFO Queue** in Dijkstra's Algorithm, and how do we mathematically derive **`O(E log V)`** from first principles?\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **1. Why a Priority Queue Instead of a Plain Queue?**\n  - A plain `queue` will still eventually find the correct shortest distances, **but** it processes paths in arbitrary weight order.\n  - If you explore a costly edge `(0 → 1, weight = 100)` first using a plain queue, you will relax all of `1`'s descendants with `100 + ...`, and then later when you discover `(0 → 2 → 1, weight = 3)`, you have to **re-relax** the entire subgraph all over again (degrading toward `O(V * E)`)!\n  - A **Min-Heap** greedily pops the **minimal distance first**, guaranteeing we never waste time propagating suboptimal heavy paths.\n- **2. Step-by-Step Derivation of `O(E log V)`:**\n  - In the worst-case dense graph, each vertex has `V - 1` edges, so the heap can hold at most `V²` pairs (`heapSize <= V²`).\n  - The outer loop runs at most `V` times, and for each vertex we iterate over its `ne` outgoing edges, doing a heap push (`log(heapSize)`):\n    - `Time = V * (popTime + ne * pushTime)`\n    - `Time = V * (log(heapSize) + (V - 1) * log(heapSize))`\n    - `Time = V * V * log(V²)`\n    - Since `V² = E` in a dense graph and `log(V²) = 2 log V`:\n    - **`Time = E * 2 log V = O(E log V)`**!\n\n### 💻 Optimized Dijkstra With Stale-Entry Skip Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> dijkstraOptimized(int V, vector<vector<pair<int, int>>> &adj, int src) {\n    priority_queue<pair<int, int>, vector<pair<int, int>>, greater<pair<int, int>>> pq;\n    vector<int> dist(V, 1e9);\n\n    dist[src] = 0;\n    pq.push({0, src});\n\n    while (!pq.empty()) {\n        auto [d, u] = pq.top();\n        pq.pop();\n\n        // Crucial Optimization: Skip stale heap entries immediately!\n        if (d > dist[u]) continue;\n\n        for (auto [v, wt] : adj[u]) {\n            if (d + wt < dist[v]) {\n                dist[v] = d + wt;\n                pq.push({dist[v], v});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return dist;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Stating Dijkstra's Complexity as `O(V log V)`:** Remember that in connected graphs `E >= V - 1` (and up to `V²`), so edge relaxations dominate—always state the complexity as **`O(E log V)`**!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22255	Prototype Design Pattern	lld-prototype-design-pattern	Avoid expensive database calls and complex initializations. Clone existing objects seamlessly using Copy Constructors and the Prototype Interface.	2026-10-07 22:41:36.377264+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Prototype Design Pattern\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/KMQFNV8LFec", "title": "Prototype Design Pattern | UML + Code | System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v36-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: The Expensive Instantiation Problem", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe Prototype pattern shines when an object's initialization state is computationally expensive or highly time-consuming to create from scratch.\\n\\nFor example, if an object requires hitting a remote REST API, authenticating, downloading a 50MB payload, and parsing a JSON document to initialize its state. If you need 5 identical instances of this object, calling `new` 5 times repeats the 50MB download 5 times. By using the Prototype pattern, you endure the heavy initialization exactly once, and then rapidly clone the resulting state in RAM 4 times, bypassing the network overhead entirely.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the specific architectural scenario where using the Prototype pattern is vastly superior to using the \\"new\\" keyword for object creation.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v36-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Shallow Copy vs Deep Copy Memory Leaks", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf the developer implements a shallow copy, the clone simply receives a copy of the pointer address. Now, both the Original object and the Clone object point to the exact same dynamic array in heap memory.\\n\\n**The Catastrophe:**\\nWhen the Original object goes out of scope, its destructor fires and `delete[] array` is called, freeing the heap memory. Later, when the Clone object goes out of scope, its destructor ALSO fires and attempts to `delete[] array`. Because the memory was already freed, this triggers a **Double Free** memory corruption fault, instantly crashing the program with a segmentation fault. A Deep Copy avoids this by allocating a brand new array for the clone.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What catastrophic runtime error occurs if a Prototype object contains a pointer to a dynamically allocated array, but the developer only implements a \\"Shallow Copy\\" in the clone method?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v36-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: The Prototype Interface Marker", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA Marker Interface is an interface that contains zero methods. It exists purely as metadata.\\n\\nIn Java, implementing `Cloneable` tells the JVM runtime that it is legally safe to perform a field-by-field memory copy of the object using `Object.clone()`. If a client attempts to call `clone()` on an object that does not implement the marker interface, the JVM throws a `CloneNotSupportedException`. It acts as a safety contract, ensuring that developers explicitly opt-in to allowing their objects to be duplicated.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why do languages like Java use a \\"Marker Interface\\" (like Cloneable) to implement the Prototype pattern? What does it signal to the client?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v36-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Prototype Registry / Factory", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA \\"Prototype Registry\\" (or Factory) stores pre-configured objects in a Hash Map.\\n\\nInstead of hardcoding initializations, an admin can create a customized `Monster` object at runtime (e.g., green skin, axe weapon, 500 HP). This instance is saved into the Registry map with the key `\\"ForestOrc\\"`.\\nLater, when the game spawner needs a monster, it asks the Factory: `factory.spawn(\\"ForestOrc\\")`. The Factory looks up the key, finds the pre-configured object, and calls `return prototypeMap[\\"ForestOrc\\"]->clone();`. This allows entirely new object configurations to be created, stored, and replicated at runtime without altering source code.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How is the Prototype pattern often combined with the Factory pattern to manage dynamic object templates at runtime?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v36-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Modifying Cloned State", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe Prototype pattern handles the heavy lifting of copying the massive, shared state (e.g., the 3D meshes, the texture pointers, the complex AI behavior trees).\\n\\nOnce the clone is created and returned, the Client simply uses standard setter methods to tweak the minor variations. `NPC* orc2 = orcPrototype->clone(); orc2->setHealth(random(80, 120));`. This achieves the perfect balance: the 99% expensive shared state is cloned instantly, and the 1% unique state is mutated cheaply post-cloning.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "If Prototype is used to clone objects, how does the architecture allow for minor variations between clones (e.g., creating 10 Orcs, but each has a slightly different health pool)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. The Cost of Initialization\n* Instantiating objects via the `new` keyword frequently triggers expensive backend processes: opening database connections, parsing huge files, or resolving complex algorithms.\n* If a game needs 100 identical "Alien" NPC objects, running that expensive constructor 100 times will freeze the application.\n\n### 💡 2. The Prototype Solution\n* **Definition:** Allows you to copy existing objects without making your code dependent on their classes.\n* We define a `Cloneable` (or `IPrototype`) interface with a single pure virtual method: `clone()`.\n* A concrete `NPC` class implements `clone()` by invoking its own **Copy Constructor**, returning a fresh object that is a perfect clone of itself.\n\n### 💡 3. Shallow vs Deep Copy\n* A **Shallow Copy** duplicates raw primitive values. If the object holds a pointer, only the memory address is copied, resulting in two objects sharing the exact same heap reference.\n* A **Deep Copy** resolves this by manually allocating new heap memory inside the copy constructor (`this->obj = new InnerObj(*other.obj);`), guaranteeing complete independence of the cloned object.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	Prototype Design Pattern	Avoid expensive database calls and complex initializations. Clone existing objects seamlessly using Copy Constructors and the Prototype Interface.	31	Hard	Low Level Design	50	[{"id": "lld-v36-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: The Expensive Instantiation Problem", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe Prototype pattern shines when an object's initialization state is computationally expensive or highly time-consuming to create from scratch.\\n\\nFor example, if an object requires hitting a remote REST API, authenticating, downloading a 50MB payload, and parsing a JSON document to initialize its state. If you need 5 identical instances of this object, calling `new` 5 times repeats the 50MB download 5 times. By using the Prototype pattern, you endure the heavy initialization exactly once, and then rapidly clone the resulting state in RAM 4 times, bypassing the network overhead entirely.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the specific architectural scenario where using the Prototype pattern is vastly superior to using the \\"new\\" keyword for object creation.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v36-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Shallow Copy vs Deep Copy Memory Leaks", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIf the developer implements a shallow copy, the clone simply receives a copy of the pointer address. Now, both the Original object and the Clone object point to the exact same dynamic array in heap memory.\\n\\n**The Catastrophe:**\\nWhen the Original object goes out of scope, its destructor fires and `delete[] array` is called, freeing the heap memory. Later, when the Clone object goes out of scope, its destructor ALSO fires and attempts to `delete[] array`. Because the memory was already freed, this triggers a **Double Free** memory corruption fault, instantly crashing the program with a segmentation fault. A Deep Copy avoids this by allocating a brand new array for the clone.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What catastrophic runtime error occurs if a Prototype object contains a pointer to a dynamically allocated array, but the developer only implements a \\"Shallow Copy\\" in the clone method?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v36-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: The Prototype Interface Marker", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA Marker Interface is an interface that contains zero methods. It exists purely as metadata.\\n\\nIn Java, implementing `Cloneable` tells the JVM runtime that it is legally safe to perform a field-by-field memory copy of the object using `Object.clone()`. If a client attempts to call `clone()` on an object that does not implement the marker interface, the JVM throws a `CloneNotSupportedException`. It acts as a safety contract, ensuring that developers explicitly opt-in to allowing their objects to be duplicated.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Why do languages like Java use a \\"Marker Interface\\" (like Cloneable) to implement the Prototype pattern? What does it signal to the client?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v36-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Prototype Registry / Factory", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA \\"Prototype Registry\\" (or Factory) stores pre-configured objects in a Hash Map.\\n\\nInstead of hardcoding initializations, an admin can create a customized `Monster` object at runtime (e.g., green skin, axe weapon, 500 HP). This instance is saved into the Registry map with the key `\\"ForestOrc\\"`.\\nLater, when the game spawner needs a monster, it asks the Factory: `factory.spawn(\\"ForestOrc\\")`. The Factory looks up the key, finds the pre-configured object, and calls `return prototypeMap[\\"ForestOrc\\"]->clone();`. This allows entirely new object configurations to be created, stored, and replicated at runtime without altering source code.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How is the Prototype pattern often combined with the Factory pattern to manage dynamic object templates at runtime?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v36-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Modifying Cloned State", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nThe Prototype pattern handles the heavy lifting of copying the massive, shared state (e.g., the 3D meshes, the texture pointers, the complex AI behavior trees).\\n\\nOnce the clone is created and returned, the Client simply uses standard setter methods to tweak the minor variations. `NPC* orc2 = orcPrototype->clone(); orc2->setHealth(random(80, 120));`. This achieves the perfect balance: the 99% expensive shared state is cloned instantly, and the 1% unique state is mutated cheaply post-cloning.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "If Prototype is used to clone objects, how does the architecture allow for minor variations between clones (e.g., creating 10 Orcs, but each has a slightly different health pool)?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
10784	One-Step-Delayed Tortoise & Hare: Delete the Middle Node of a Linked List	dsa-delete-middle-node-of-linked-list	Delete the middle node in a single pass by starting fast at head->next->next, stopping slow exactly one node before the middle in O(1) space.	2026-10-03 15:07:44.948204+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: One-Step-Delayed Tortoise & Hare: Delete the Middle Node of a Linked List\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ePpV-_pfOeI", "title": "Lecture 252: Delete Middle Node of Linked List", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-the-middle-node-of-a-linked-list/", "title": "Delete the Middle Node of a Linked List (LC 2095)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given the head of a linked list, delete the middle node (floor(n / 2)-th 0-indexed node) and return the head of the modified linked list."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/delete-middle-of-linked-list/1", "title": "Delete Middle of Linked List", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Delete the middle node of a singly linked list (or the second middle node if length is even)."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/middle-of-the-linked-list/", "title": "Middle of the Linked List (LC 876)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Compare landing on the middle node vs. landing one node before the middle node."}]	Module 37: Linked List Medium Problems: Dummy Nodes, In-Place Re-Linking & Tortoise-Hare Pointers	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the `head` of a singly linked list, delete its **middle node** (for an even-length list with two middles `m1` and `m2`, delete the **second middle `m2`**) and return the updated head. If the list has only `1` node, return `nullptr`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Optimal)\n- **Why Landing ON the Middle Node Is Not Enough:**\n  - To delete the middle node in a singly linked list, we must re-wire the `next` pointer of the node **immediately BEFORE the middle node**!\n- **1. Brute Force Two-Pass (`O(N + N/2)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Count total nodes `N`, then walk `res = N / 2` steps from `head` to stop right before the middle node.\n- **2. Optimal 1-Step-Skip Tortoise & Hare (`O(N / 2)` Single Pass, `O(1)` Space — No Extra `prev` Pointer Needed!):**\n  - In standard Tortoise & Hare, both `slow` and `fast` start at `head` and move together, causing `slow` to land **on** the middle node.\n  - How do we make `slow` stop **1 node before** the middle node? Simply **skip `slow`'s very first move**!\n  - Start `slow = head` and `fast = head->next->next;` (giving `fast` a 2-step head start while `slow` stays at `head`).\n  - Then run the standard loop `while (fast != nullptr && fast->next != nullptr)` moving `slow` by `1` and `fast` by `2`.\n  - When the loop finishes, `slow` is guaranteed to sit **1 node before the middle node**! Simply re-link `slow->next = slow->next->next` and `delete middle`.\n\n### 💻 Delete the Middle Node (One-Step-Skip Tortoise & Hare) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nNode* deleteMiddle(Node* head) {\n    // Edge case: 0 or 1 node -> deleting middle leaves an empty list\n    if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr) {\n        return nullptr;\n    }\n\n    Node* slow = head;\n    // Skip slow's first step by giving fast a 2-step head start!\n    Node* fast = head->next->next;\n\n    while (fast != nullptr && fast->next != nullptr) {\n        slow = slow->next;\n        fast = fast->next->next;\n    }\n\n    // 'slow' now sits immediately before the middle node\n    Node* middle = slow->next;\n    slow->next = slow->next->next;\n    delete middle;\n\n    return head;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting the Single-Node Edge Case (`head->next == nullptr`):** If the list has only `1` node, `head->next` is `nullptr`, so `head->next->next` will cause an immediate null-pointer crash! Always check `if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr) return nullptr;` first.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
1652	Level-Indexed Coordinate Normalization: Maximum Width of a Binary Tree	dsa-maximum-width-of-binary-tree	Compute the maximum level width in a binary tree including virtual null nodes using 0-based coordinate normalization to prevent integer overflow.	2026-10-03 01:02:00.082484+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Level-Indexed Coordinate Normalization: Maximum Width of a Binary Tree\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZbybYvcVLks", "title": "Lecture 100: Maximum Width of Binary Tree", "duration": "26m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-width-of-binary-tree/", "title": "Maximum Width of Binary Tree", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the maximum width of a binary tree including null nodes between end nodes."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-level-order-traversal/", "title": "Binary Tree Level Order Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Track queue level snapshots to frame width calculation."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/maximum-width-of-tree/1", "title": "Maximum Width of Tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Compute the maximum number of nodes present across any single level."}]	Module 17: Binary Trees: Views, Projections & Ancestry Analysis	### 📌 Topic Overview\nThe **Maximum Width** of a binary tree is the maximum width among all levels. The width of a level is defined as the length between the leftmost and rightmost non-null nodes, **including any null nodes** that would lie between them in a complete binary tree.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Zero-Based Segment Tree Indexing:**\n  - If a parent node at a level has index `i`:\n    - Its left child has index `2 * i + 1`.\n    - Its right child has index `2 * i + 2`.\n  - The width of any level is: `lastIndex - firstIndex + 1`.\n- **The Critical Integer Overflow Fix (Level-Normalization):**\n  - In a deep skewed tree of height 1000, `2 * i` doubles at each level and quickly overflows 64-bit integers (`2^1000`).\n  - **The Fix:** At the start of each level, capture `minId = q.front().second` (the index of the level's first node).\n  - Subtract `minId` from every node's index at that level: **`curr_id = q.front().second - minId`**!\n  - Now indices reset to `0` at the start of each level, completely preventing overflow!\n\n### 💻 Maximum Width Blueprint\n```cpp\nint widthOfBinaryTree(TreeNode* root) {\n    if (root == nullptr) return 0;\n    long long maxWidth = 0;\n    queue<pair<TreeNode*, long long>> q;\n    q.push({root, 0});\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int size = q.size();\n        long long minId = q.front().second; // First index of current level\n        long long first = 0, last = 0;\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n            long long curr_id = q.front().second - minId; // Normalize to avoid overflow\n            TreeNode* node = q.front().first;\n            q.pop();\n\n            if (i == 0) first = curr_id;\n            if (i == size - 1) last = curr_id;\n\n            if (node->left) q.push({node->left, curr_id * 2 + 1});\n            if (node->right) q.push({node->right, curr_id * 2 + 2});\n        }\n        maxWidth = max(maxWidth, last - first + 1);\n    }\n    return maxWidth;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
56	Asymptotic Analysis: Big-O Time & Auxiliary Space Complexity	asymptotic-analysis-big-o-space-complexity	Understand Big-O notation, the 3 rules of complexity analysis, input vs auxiliary space, and the 10^8 operations per second rule.	2026-10-02 17:05:20.014635+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Asymptotic Analysis: Big-O Time & Auxiliary Space Complexity\nPart of the **Problem Solving: DSA & SQL** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FPu9Uld7W-E", "title": "Lecture 4: Time and Space Complexity — Big O, Omega, Theta, Worst Case & Best Case", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-good-pairs/", "title": "Number of Good Pairs", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1512", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Compare the O(N²) pairwise brute force against the optimal O(N) frequency counting method."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/kids-with-the-greatest-number-of-candies/", "title": "Kids With the Greatest Number of Candies", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1431", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Analyze O(N) linear scan bounds checking against peak element reference."}]	Module 1: Algorithmic Foundations & Environment	### 📌 Topic Overview\nHow do interviewers judge whether your code is fast and efficient? Since different computers run at different speeds, we don't measure code in seconds. Instead, we use **Time Complexity** (how the number of steps grows as input size `N` grows) and **Space Complexity** (how much extra memory your code uses).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **What is Time Complexity?** It measures the rate at which the number of operations increases as the input size `N` gets larger, written using **Big-O Notation** (`O(...)`).\n- **The 3 Simple Rules of Big-O:**\n  1. **Always Calculate for the Worst Case:** Plan for the toughest input your program might face.\n  2. **Ignore Constants:** `O(3N)` or `O(N / 2)` is simply written as `O(N)`.\n  3. **Drop Smaller Terms:** In `O(N² + N + 5)`, when `N` is large, `N²` dominates everything else, so we write `O(N²)`.\n- **Space Complexity = Input Space + Auxiliary Space:**\n  - *Input Space:* Memory used to store the given problem inputs.\n  - *Auxiliary Space:* Extra variables or arrays you create to solve the problem.\n- **The 1-Second Online Judge Rule:** Coding platforms (like LeetCode and GFG) execute roughly **10^8 (100 million) operations per second**. If `N = 10^5`, an `O(N²)` solution takes `10^10` steps (which causes **Time Limit Exceeded / TLE**), so you need an `O(N)` or `O(N log N)` approach.\n\n### 💻 Common Loop Complexities\n```cpp\n// 1. Single Loop -> O(N) Time\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    // Runs N times\n}\n\n// 2. Nested Loop -> O(N²) Time\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    for (int j = 0; j <= i; j++) {\n        // Runs 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + N = N*(N+1)/2 times -> O(N²)\n    }\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
953	Greedy Stall Allocation: Aggressive Cows (Maximize Minimum Distance)	dsa-aggressive-cows-maximize-minimum-distance	Assign K cows to stalls such that the minimum distance between any two cows is maximized using binary search on answer space.	2026-10-03 00:19:12.421295+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Greedy Stall Allocation: Aggressive Cows (Maximize Minimum Distance)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R_Mfw4ew-Vo", "title": "Lecture 61: Aggressive Cows", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/magnetic-force-between-two-balls/", "title": "Magnetic Force Between Two Balls", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Maximize the minimum magnetic force between m balls placed in baskets."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-tastiness-of-candy-basket/", "title": "Maximum Tastiness of Candy Basket", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the maximum tastiness of a candy basket using binary search on minimum absolute difference."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/aggressive-cows/1", "title": "Aggressive Cows", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Assign K cows to stalls such that the minimum distance between any two cows is maximized."}]	Module 13: Binary Search on Answer Space & Monotonic Predicate Functions	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven `n` stall coordinates and `k` aggressive cows, place all `k` cows in stalls such that the **minimum distance between any two cows is as large as possible** (Max-of-Min pattern).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Sort the Stalls First:**\n  - Sorting the coordinates allows us to measure distances strictly between consecutive cows.\n- **Answer Space Range (`[1, max(stalls) - min(stalls)]`):**\n  - Minimum possible distance between two cows is `1`.\n  - Maximum possible distance is placing cows at the two extreme ends: `stalls[n - 1] - stalls[0]`.\n- **Greedy Feasibility Check (`canWePlace(stalls, dist, k)`):**\n  - Always place the first cow at `stalls[0]`.\n  - Iterate through subsequent stalls: if `stalls[i] - lastStall >= dist`, place the next cow here and update `lastStall = stalls[i]`.\n  - If we can place all `k` cows, this distance is feasible.\n- **Binary Search Elimination:**\n  - If `canWePlace` returns true: Distance `mid` is feasible. Since we want to **maximize** distance, search right (`low = mid + 1`).\n  - Otherwise: Cows are spaced too far apart—search left (`high = mid - 1`).\n  - `high` ends at the maximum feasible distance!\n\n### 💻 Aggressive Cows Blueprint\n```cpp\nbool canWePlace(vector<int> &stalls, int dist, int cows) {\n    int cntCows = 1, last = stalls[0];\n    for (int i = 1; i < stalls.size(); i++) {\n        if (stalls[i] - last >= dist) {\n            cntCows++;\n            last = stalls[i];\n        }\n        if (cntCows >= cows) return true;\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n\nint aggressiveCows(vector<int> &stalls, int k) {\n    sort(stalls.begin(), stalls.end());\n    int low = 1, high = stalls.back() - stalls.front();\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (canWePlace(stalls, mid, k)) {\n            low = mid + 1;  // Try a larger minimum distance\n        } else {\n            high = mid - 1; // Try a smaller distance\n        }\n    }\n    return high;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
958	Cross-Partition Symmetry: Median of Two Sorted Arrays in O(log(min(N1, N2))) Time	dsa-median-of-two-sorted-arrays-optimal-binary-search	Find the median of two sorted arrays in O(log(min(N1, N2))) time and O(1) space using binary search cross-partition symmetry.	2026-10-03 00:19:12.450595+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Cross-Partition Symmetry: Median of Two Sorted Arrays in O(log(min(N1, N2))) Time\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F9c7LpRZWVQ", "title": "Lecture 66: Median of Two Sorted Arrays — Optimal Binary Search", "duration": "35m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/median-of-two-sorted-arrays/", "title": "Median of Two Sorted Arrays", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find the median of two sorted arrays in O(log(min(m, n))) runtime."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-subsequence-with-limited-sum/", "title": "Longest Subsequence With Limited Sum", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Use prefix sums and binary search upper_bound to answer multiple range queries."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/median-in-a-row-wise-sorted-matrix1527/1", "title": "Median in a row-wise sorted Matrix", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Locate the median of a row-wise sorted matrix using binary search on answer value."}]	Module 14: Multi-Array Partitioning & 2D Matrix Binary Search	### 📌 Topic Overview\nFind the median of two sorted arrays in **O(log(min(N1, N2))) time** and **O(1) space** using symmetry partitioning.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Always Binary Search on the Shorter Array:**\n  - If `a.size() > b.size()`, swap the call `return findMedian(b, a)` so the search range is minimized.\n- **Partition Invariant (`leftHalfSize = (n1 + n2 + 1) / 2`):**\n  - Divide the combined elements into a left half and right half.\n  - Pick `mid1` elements from array `a`, and the remaining `mid2 = leftHalfSize - mid1` elements from array `b`.\n  - Boundary elements:\n    - `l1 = a[mid1 - 1]`, `r1 = a[mid1]`\n    - `l2 = b[mid2 - 1]`, `r2 = b[mid2]`\n    - Use `INT_MIN` if a left boundary has no elements, and `INT_MAX` if a right boundary has none.\n- **Validity Condition:**\n  - The partition is correct if **`l1 <= r2 && l2 <= r1`**!\n  - If `l1 > r2`: Too many elements taken from `a`—search left (`high = mid1 - 1`).\n  - If `l2 > r1`: Too few elements taken from `a`—search right (`low = mid1 + 1`).\n\n### 💻 Optimal Binary Search Partition Blueprint\n```cpp\ndouble findMedianSortedArrays(vector<int> &a, vector<int> &b) {\n    int n1 = a.size(), n2 = b.size();\n    if (n1 > n2) return findMedianSortedArrays(b, a);\n\n    int low = 0, high = n1;\n    int left = (n1 + n2 + 1) / 2;\n    int n = n1 + n2;\n\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid1 = (low + high) >> 1;\n        int mid2 = left - mid1;\n        int l1 = (mid1 > 0) ? a[mid1 - 1] : INT_MIN;\n        int l2 = (mid2 > 0) ? b[mid2 - 1] : INT_MIN;\n        int r1 = (mid1 < n1) ? a[mid1] : INT_MAX;\n        int r2 = (mid2 < n2) ? b[mid2] : INT_MAX;\n\n        if (l1 <= r2 && l2 <= r1) {\n            if (n % 2 == 1) return max(l1, l2);\n            return ((double)(max(l1, l2) + min(r1, r2))) / 2.0;\n        } else if (l1 > r2) {\n            high = mid1 - 1;\n        } else {\n            low = mid1 + 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return 0.0;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
1646	First-Vertical Projection: Top View of a Binary Tree	dsa-top-view-of-binary-tree	Project nodes vertically to capture the topmost visible node on each vertical line using BFS level order and hash mapping.	2026-10-03 01:02:00.059975+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: First-Vertical Projection: Top View of a Binary Tree\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Et9OCDNvJ78", "title": "Lecture 94: Top View of Binary Tree", "duration": "22m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/top-view-of-binary-tree/1", "title": "Top View of Binary Tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the nodes visible when looking at the tree from the top."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-vertical-order-traversal/", "title": "Binary Tree Vertical Order Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Group nodes by column offset using BFS level order."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/bottom-view-of-binary-tree/1", "title": "Bottom View of Binary Tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Contrast first-seen vertical projection with last-seen vertical projection."}]	Module 17: Binary Trees: Views, Projections & Ancestry Analysis	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhen looking at a binary tree from the top, you only see the **very first node** that appears on each vertical line `x`. Nodes directly underneath on the same vertical line are hidden from sight.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why BFS Level Order Must Be Used (Not DFS):**\n  - BFS visits nodes strictly from higher levels to lower levels.\n  - The first time a vertical line `line` is visited during BFS, the current node is guaranteed to be the topmost node on that line!\n- **Data Structures:**\n  - `map<int, int> mpp`: Stores `{line -> nodeValue}` (sorted automatically from leftmost line to rightmost line).\n  - `queue<pair<TreeNode*, int>> q`: Holds `{node, line}` pairs.\n- **Top View Invariant:**\n  - When popping `{node, line}`, only insert into `mpp` if `mpp.find(line) == mpp.end()`!\n  - If `line` is already present, ignore it because a higher node on that vertical line was already recorded.\n\n### 💻 Top View Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> topView(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<int> ans;\n    if (root == nullptr) return ans;\n\n    map<int, int> mpp; // {line -> first node val}\n    queue<pair<TreeNode*, int>> q;\n    q.push({root, 0});\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        auto it = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        TreeNode* node = it.first;\n        int line = it.second;\n\n        if (mpp.find(line) == mpp.end()) {\n            mpp[line] = node->val; // Record only the first node seen on this line\n        }\n        if (node->left != nullptr) q.push({node->left, line - 1});\n        if (node->right != nullptr) q.push({node->right, line + 1});\n    }\n\n    for (auto it : mpp) ans.push_back(it.second);\n    return ans;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
