rogramming style"], "question": "In the context of the CAP Theorem, what does 'Consistency' mean?", "explanation": "Consistency guarantees that once data is updated on one node, any subsequent read from any other node will return that updated data.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "Every read receives the most recent write or an error; all nodes see the exact same data at the exact same time"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["The system is available for purchase", "Every request receives a (non-error) response, but without the guarantee that it contains the most recent write", "The server uses open-source software", "The database is not password protected"], "question": "What does 'Availability' mean in the CAP Theorem?", "explanation": "Availability guarantees that the system is always 'up' and responds to requests, even if nodes are failing or disconnected, though the data returned might be slightly stale.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "Every request receives a (non-error) response, but without the guarantee that it contains the most recent write"}, {"id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["The system's ability to partition hard drives quickly", "The system continues to operate despite an arbitrary number of messages being dropped or delayed by the network between nodes", "The ability to split frontend and backend teams", "The database's ability to store partitioned data tables"], "question": "What is 'Partition Tolerance'?", "explanation": "Partition Tolerance means the system survives network failures (partitions) where servers can no longer communicate with each other. In distributed internet systems, P is mandatory.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "The system continues to operate despite an arbitrary number of messages being dropped or delayed by the network between nodes"}, {"id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["They must choose between Cost and Performance", "They must choose between SQL and NoSQL", "During a network partition, they must choose between guaranteeing Consistency (CP) or guaranteeing Availability (AP)", "They must choose between TCP and UDP"], "question": "Because network partitions (P) are unavoidable in distributed web systems, what trade-off must engineers make according to the CAP Theorem?", "explanation": "When a network link breaks between nodes, the system must either stop serving requests to maintain perfect Consistency (CP), or continue serving requests using potentially stale data to maintain Availability (AP).", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "During a network partition, they must choose between guaranteeing Consistency (CP) or guaranteeing Availability (AP)"}]	\N	> **Architecture Overview:** Essential systems architecture principles governing how modern web applications communicate, route data, balance loads, and scale effectively.	Hayk Simonyan	https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan	\N	CAP Theorem Explained	Core system design concepts focusing on cap theorem and distributed network architecture.	10	Easy	Web Architecture	10	[]	\N	10	https://youtu.be/DYFocSiPOl8	CAP Theorem Explained
1956	Subtree Completeness Invariant: Count Total Nodes in a Complete Binary Tree in O((log N)^2)	dsa-count-nodes-complete-binary-tree	Count all nodes in a complete binary tree in O((log N)^2) time by detecting full subtrees with height equality.	2026-10-03 01:18:07.163326+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Subtree Completeness Invariant: Count Total Nodes in a Complete Binary Tree in O((log N)^2)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u-yWemKGWO0", "title": "Lecture 104: Count total Nodes in a COMPLETE Binary Tree", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/count-complete-tree-nodes/", "title": "Count Complete Tree Nodes", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Count the total nodes of a complete binary tree in strictly less than O(N) runtime."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/balanced-binary-tree/", "title": "Balanced Binary Tree", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Compare subtree depth calculations with complete tree node counts."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/complete-binary-tree/1", "title": "Complete Binary Tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Verify whether a given binary tree satisfies the complete tree invariant."}]	Module 18: Binary Trees: Node Distances, Tree Modifications & Reconstruction	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the root of a **Complete Binary Tree**, count its total number of nodes in **`O((log2 N)^2)` time**—strictly faster than `O(N)` linear traversal.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Perfect Binary Tree Shortcut:**\n  - If a tree of height `h` is completely full, its total node count is **`2^h - 1`** (computed via bitshift `(1 << h) - 1`).\n- **Optimal Subtree Pruning (`O((log2 N)^2)` Time, `O(log2 N)` Space):**\n  - At any node `curr`:\n    - Compute `leftHeight`: traverse strictly down left edges (`curr = curr->left`).\n    - Compute `rightHeight`: traverse strictly down right edges (`curr = curr->right`).\n    - **If `leftHeight == rightHeight`:** The subtree is a perfect binary tree! Immediately return **`(1 << leftHeight) - 1`** without traversing internal nodes!\n    - **If `leftHeight != rightHeight`:** The bottom level is partially filled on the right. Return **`1 + countNodes(curr->left) + countNodes(curr->right)`**.\n- **Why Time is `O((log2 N)^2)`:**\n  - Finding heights takes `O(log2 N)` time.\n  - At each branching step, at least one of the two subtrees is guaranteed to be a perfect binary tree and finishes in `O(1)`! We only dive deeper into the single irregular branch, so height comparisons run at most `O(log2 N)` times: `O(log N * log N) = O((log2 N)^2)`.\n\n### 💻 Count Nodes in Complete Tree Blueprint\n```cpp\nint findHeightLeft(TreeNode* node) {\n    int h = 0;\n    while (node) { h++; node = node->left; }\n    return h;\n}\n\nint findHeightRight(TreeNode* node) {\n    int h = 0;\n    while (node) { h++; node = node->right; }\n    return h;\n}\n\nint countNodes(TreeNode* root) {\n    if (root == nullptr) return 0;\n\n    int lh = findHeightLeft(root);\n    int rh = findHeightRight(root);\n\n    if (lh == rh) return (1 << lh) - 1; // Formula shortcut: 2^h - 1\n\n    return 1 + countNodes(root->left) + countNodes(root->right);\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
