7016	Single-Array Left-to-Right Space Optimization: Unbounded Knapsack	dsa-unbounded-knapsack-1d-space-optimized	Maximize profit with infinite item supplies by iterating capacity left-to-right on a single 1D array in O(N * W) time and O(W) space.	2026-10-03 11:31:13.738028+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Single-Array Left-to-Right Space Optimization: Unbounded Knapsack\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OgvOZ6OrJoY", "title": "Lecture 204: Unbounded Knapsack (DP 23)", "duration": "21m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/knapsack-with-duplicate-items4201/1", "title": "Knapsack with Duplicate Items (Unbounded Knapsack)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given N items with weights and values and a knapsack limit W, maximize profit when each item can be picked any number of times."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/coin-change/", "title": "Coin Change (LC 322)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Compare minimizing coins in infinite supply with maximizing value in Unbounded Knapsack."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/rod-cutting0840/1", "title": "Rod Cutting", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Apply Unbounded Knapsack directly to maximize profit from cutting a rod of length N."}]	Module 30: Dynamic Programming on Subsequences, Subset Sums & 0/1 Knapsack	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn **Unbounded Knapsack**, you are given `N` items with weights `wt[i]` and values `val[i]`, and a bag of capacity `W`. Unlike 0/1 Knapsack, you have an **infinite supply of each item**! Maximize the total value inside capacity `W`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Base Case at `ind == 0` (`(W / wt[0]) * val[0]`):**\n  - If we are at item `0` with remaining capacity `W`, how many copies of item `0` should we steal?\n  - As many as fit! We can fit **`(int)(W / wt[0])` copies**, earning a total value of **`(W / wt[0]) * val[0]`**!\n- **Why Single-Array Optimization Iterates LEFT-TO-RIGHT (`0` to `W`):**\n  - In **0/1 Knapsack**, `cur[w]` needed `prev[w - wt[ind]]` (from the *previous* row), so we had to iterate `w` from **Right to Left** (`W → 0`) to avoid overwriting `prev`.\n  - In **Unbounded Knapsack**, `cur[w]` needs `prev[w]` (right above it) and **`cur[w - wt[ind]]`** (from the **current row** to the left!).\n  - Therefore, iterating `w` from **Left to Right (`0 → W`)** on a **single array `cur`** means `cur[w - wt[ind]]` has *already* been updated for the current row `ind`—which is **exactly what we need**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * W)` Time and **`O(W)` Single-Array Space**.\n\n### 💻 Unbounded Knapsack (1-Array Space Optimized) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint unboundedKnapsack(int n, int w, vector<int> &profit, vector<int> &weight) {\n    // Single 1D array of size w + 1!\n    vector<int> cur(w + 1, 0);\n\n    // 1. Base case at index 0: take as many copies of item 0 as fit in W\n    for (int W = 0; W <= w; W++) {\n        cur[W] = (W / weight[0]) * profit[0];\n    }\n\n    // 2. Iterate W from LEFT TO RIGHT (0 to w) on the single array!\n    for (int ind = 1; ind < n; ind++) {\n        for (int W = 0; W <= w; W++) {\n            int notTake = 0 + cur[W]; // cur[W] currently holds prev[W]\n            int take = 0;\n            if (weight[ind] <= W) {\n                take = profit[ind] + cur[W - weight[ind]]; // cur[W - wt] holds updated current row!\n            }\n            cur[W] = max(take, notTake);\n        }\n    }\n    return cur[w];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Writing `(W / weight[0])` Outside Parentheses Without Integer Division:** In C++, `(W / weight[0]) * profit[0]` performs integer floor division first (`10 / 3 = 3`, `3 * profit[0]`). Never write `(W * profit[0]) / weight[0]`, which would award fractional item values!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
5248	Dynamic 2D Grid Flattening: Number of Islands II (Online Queries Using DSU)	dsa-number-of-islands-ii-online-queries-dsu	Flatten 2D grid coordinates into 1D integers and track the online island count as land cells are dynamically activated.	2026-10-03 11:10:35.793304+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Dynamic 2D Grid Flattening: Number of Islands II (Online Queries Using DSU)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rn6B-Q4SNyA", "title": "Lecture 176: Number of Islands II (Online Queries Using DSU)", "duration": "21m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/number-of-islands/1", "title": "Number Of Islands", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Process online land-addition queries on an N x M sea grid and return the island count after each query."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-islands-ii/", "title": "Number of Islands II (LC 305)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Return an array of the number of islands after each addLand(row, col) operation."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/last-day-where-you-can-still-cross/", "title": "Last Day Where You Can Still Cross (LC 1970)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Use 2D-to-1D DSU flattening in reverse across daily grid updates to detect top-to-bottom connectivity."}]	Module 26: Disjoint Set Union (DSU), Kruskal's MST & Dynamic Connectivity	### 📌 Topic Overview\nYou are given an `n x m` grid initially filled with `0`s (water) and a list of `Q` online queries `(r, c)` that turn cell `(r, c)` into `1` (land) one by one. Return the **total number of islands after each individual query**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **1D Coordinate Flattening Formula (`nodeNo = r * m + c`):**\n  - To use a 1D `DisjointSet ds(n * m)` on an `n x m` 2D grid, map every cell `(r, c)` to a unique 1D integer ID:\n    **`nodeNo = r * m + c`** (where `m` is the number of columns).\n- **Dynamic Island Counter Maintenance (`cnt`):**\n  - When query `(r, c)` arrives:\n    1. **Duplicate Query Guard:** If `vis[r][c] == 1`, `(r, c)` is already land—append the current `cnt` to `ans` and `continue`!\n    2. Mark `vis[r][c] = 1` and increment **`cnt++`** (assuming the new cell forms a standalone island of size 1).\n    3. Check all 4 neighbors `(adjR, adjC)`:\n       - If `(adjR, adjC)` is valid AND is already land (`vis[adjR][adjC] == 1`):\n         - If **`ds.findUPar(nodeNo) != ds.findUPar(adjNodeNo)`**, merge them via `ds.unionBySize(nodeNo, adjNodeNo)` and decrement **`cnt--`**!\n    4. Append `cnt` to `ans`.\n- **Complexity:** `O(Q * 4 * alpha) ≈ O(Q)` Time and `O(N * M)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Number of Islands II (Online Queries DSU) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nvector<int> numOfIslandsOnline(int n, int m, vector<vector<int>> &operators) {\n    DisjointSet ds(n * m);\n    vector<vector<int>> vis(n, vector<int>(m, 0));\n    int cnt = 0;\n    vector<int> ans;\n\n    int dr[] = {-1, 0, 1, 0}, dc[] = {0, 1, 0, -1};\n\n    for (const auto &it : operators) {\n        int r = it[0], c = it[1];\n        if (vis[r][c] == 1) {\n            ans.push_back(cnt);\n            continue;\n        }\n        vis[r][c] = 1;\n        cnt++; // New land cell initially adds 1 island\n\n        int nodeNo = r * m + c;\n        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n            int adjR = r + dr[i], adjC = c + dc[i];\n            if (adjR >= 0 && adjR < n && adjC >= 0 && adjC < m && vis[adjR][adjC] == 1) {\n                int adjNodeNo = adjR * m + adjC;\n                if (ds.findUPar(nodeNo) != ds.findUPar(adjNodeNo)) {\n                    cnt--; // Two separate islands merged into one!\n                    ds.unionBySize(nodeNo, adjNodeNo);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        ans.push_back(cnt);\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting to Check `if (vis[r][c] == 1)` First:** Online query lists often contain duplicate coordinates `(r, c)`. Without the duplicate guard, `cnt++` will falsely inflate the island count!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
1121	Recursive Postorder Traversal: Left-Right-Root Bottom-Up Aggregation	dsa-postorder-traversal-binary-tree	Execute recursive postorder traversal (Left -> Right -> Root) for bottom-up calculation and tree cleanup.	2026-10-03 00:35:47.131699+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Recursive Postorder Traversal: Left-Right-Root Bottom-Up Aggregation\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=COQOU6klsBg", "title": "Lecture 79: Postorder Traversal of Binary Tree", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-postorder-traversal/", "title": "Binary Tree Postorder Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the postorder traversal of a binary tree's node values."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/invert-binary-tree/", "title": "Invert Binary Tree", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Recursively swap left and right subtrees across all nodes."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/postorder-traversal/1", "title": "Postorder Traversal", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Basic", "description": "Return a list containing the postorder traversal of a binary tree."}]	Module 15: Binary Trees: Hierarchical Representation & Fundamental Traversals	### 📌 Topic Overview\n**Postorder Traversal** visits nodes in the order **`Left → Right → Root`**. A node is only processed *after* both its left subtree and right subtree have completely finished executing.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Base Case:** If `node == nullptr`, return immediately.\n- **Recursive Steps:**\n  1. Call `postorder(node->left)`.\n  2. Call `postorder(node->right)`.\n  3. Process/record `node->data`.\n- **Why Postorder Matters:** Anytime a parent node's calculation depends on answers from both of its children (such as tree height, diameter, subtree sum, or deleting a tree), you are using Postorder logic!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N)` Time and `O(H)` Auxiliary Call Stack Space.\n\n### 💻 Recursive Postorder Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid dfsPostorder(TreeNode* node, vector<int> &ans) {\n    if (node == nullptr) return;\n    dfsPostorder(node->left, ans);   // 1. Left\n    dfsPostorder(node->right, ans);  // 2. Right\n    ans.push_back(node->val);        // 3. Root\n}\n\nvector<int> postorderTraversal(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<int> ans;\n    dfsPostorder(root, ans);\n    return ans;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
1365	Dual-Stack Inversion Pipeline: Iterative Postorder Traversal Using 2 Stacks	dsa-iterative-postorder-traversal-two-stacks	Leverage dual stacks to exploit reversal symmetry (Root-Right-Left reverses into Left-Right-Root) for clean postorder traversal.	2026-10-03 00:50:37.34071+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Dual-Stack Inversion Pipeline: Iterative Postorder Traversal Using 2 Stacks\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2YBhNLodD8Q", "title": "Lecture 83: Post-order Traversal of Binary Tree using 2 Stacks", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-postorder-traversal/", "title": "Binary Tree Postorder Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Implement postorder traversal using an iterative stack approach."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/n-ary-tree-postorder-traversal/", "title": "N-ary Tree Postorder Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Generalize two-stack postorder reversal to N-ary trees."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/postorder-traversal-iterative/1", "title": "Postorder Traversal (Iterative)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Traverse a tree in Left-Right-Root order using iterative methods."}]	Module 15: Binary Trees: Hierarchical Representation & Fundamental Traversals	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhile **Postorder Traversal (`Left → Right → Root`)** is tricky to do iteratively with one stack, it becomes trivial using **two stacks** by exploiting reversal symmetry!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Reversal Intuition (`Root-Right-Left Reverses to Left-Right-Root`):**\n  - Recall that standard preorder is `Root → Left → Right`.\n  - If we instead traverse in `Root → Right → Left` order and push every visited node into a second stack `st2`, popping `st2` at the end outputs the exact reverse: **`Left → Right → Root` (Postorder)**!\n- **Algorithm Steps:**\n  1. Push `root` into `st1`.\n  2. While `!st1.empty()`:\n     - Pop `curr = st1.top()`.\n     - Push `curr` into `st2`.\n     - If `curr->left != nullptr`, push `curr->left` into `st1`.\n     - If `curr->right != nullptr`, push `curr->right` into `st1`.\n  3. Pop all elements from `st2` into `ans`.\n- **Complexity:** `O(N)` Time and `O(2N)` Space across both stacks.\n\n### 💻 Iterative Postorder (2 Stacks) Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> postorderTwoStacks(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<int> ans;\n    if (root == nullptr) return ans;\n\n    stack<TreeNode*> st1, st2;\n    st1.push(root);\n\n    while (!st1.empty()) {\n        TreeNode* curr = st1.top();\n        st1.pop();\n        st2.push(curr);\n\n        if (curr->left != nullptr) st1.push(curr->left);\n        if (curr->right != nullptr) st1.push(curr->right);\n    }\n\n    while (!st2.empty()) {\n        ans.push_back(st2.top()->val);\n        st2.pop();\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
