ww.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/path-with-minimum-effort/1", "title": "Path With Minimum Effort", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Compute the minimum effort required to travel from the top-left cell to the bottom-right cell."}]	Module 24: Shortest Path Algorithms in Graphs: DAG Relaxation, BFS & Dijkstra's Algorithm	### 📌 Topic Overview\nYou are given a 2D `heights` grid of size `rows x cols`. A path's **Effort** is defined as the **maximum absolute difference in heights** between any two consecutive cells along the path. Find the **minimum effort** required to travel from `(0, 0)` to `(rows - 1, cols - 1)`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Minimax Dijkstra Relaxation Rule:**\n  - Instead of summing edge weights (`d + wt`), the effort of extending a path to `(nr, nc)` is the **maximum** of the effort experienced so far (`diff`) and the current step's height jump:\n    **`newEffort = max(diff, abs(heights[r][c] - heights[nr][nc]))`**.\n  - If `newEffort < dist[nr][nc]`, update `dist[nr][nc] = newEffort` and push `{newEffort, {nr, nc}}` into the min-heap!\n- **Why Popping the Destination Guarantees Optimality:**\n  - Because the min-heap always pops the globally smallest effort across all active frontier cells, the **moment `(rows - 1, cols - 1)` is popped from `pq.top()`**, its effort `diff` cannot possibly be improved by any other path in the queue!\n- **Complexity:** `O(4 * N * M * log(N * M))` Time and `O(N * M)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Path With Minimum Effort Blueprint\n```cpp\nint minimumEffortPath(vector<vector<int>> &heights) {\n    int rows = heights.size(), cols = heights[0].size();\n    priority_queue<pair<int, pair<int, int>>,\n                   vector<pair<int, pair<int, int>>>,\n                   greater<pair<int, pair<int, int>>>> pq;\n\n    vector<vector<int>> dist(rows, vector<int>(cols, 1e9));\n    dist[0][0] = 0;\n    pq.push({0, {0, 0}});\n\n    int dr[] = {-1, 0, 1, 0}, dc[] = {0, 1, 0, -1};\n\n    while (!pq.empty()) {\n        auto [diff, coords] = pq.top();\n        pq.pop();\n        int r = coords.first, c = coords.second;\n\n        // Optimal early return when destination is popped from min-heap!\n        if (r == rows - 1 && c == cols - 1) return diff;\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n            int nr = r + dr[i], nc = c + dc[i];\n            if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols) {\n                int newEffort = max(abs(heights[r][c] - heights[nr][nc]), diff);\n                if (newEffort < dist[nr][nc]) {\n                    dist[nr][nc] = newEffort;\n                    pq.push({newEffort, {nr, nc}});\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Returning When Pushing Instead of When Popping:** Unlike unit-weight BFS, edge jumps here vary wildly! Do **not** return `newEffort` inside the `for` loop when pushing `(rows - 1, cols - 1)`—wait until `(rows - 1, cols - 1)` is **popped** from `pq.top()`!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
3873	Relative Coordinate Normalization: Number of Distinct Islands via Shape Hashing	dsa-number-of-distinct-islands-shape-hashing	Count distinct island shapes in a 2D binary grid using base-coordinate relative offset normalization and set hashing.	2026-10-03 02:09:36.718907+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Relative Coordinate Normalization: Number of Distinct Islands via Shape Hashing\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7zmgQSJghpo", "title": "Lecture 141: Number of Distinct Islands", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/number-of-distinct-islands/1", "title": "Number of Distinct Islands", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count the number of distinct island shapes in a 2D binary grid using relative coordinate normalization."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-distinct-islands/", "title": "Number of Distinct Islands (LC 694)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the number of distinct 4-directionally connected island shapes under translation."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/count-sub-islands/", "title": "Count Sub Islands (LC 1905)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use DFS across two binary grids to count islands in grid2 that are completely contained within islands of grid1."}]	Module 22: Graph Cycle Detection, Multi-Source BFS & Boundary Flood Algorithms	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an N x M binary matrix where 1 represents land and 0 represents water, count the number of **distinct islands**. Two islands are considered identical if one can be translated (shifted without rotation or reflection) to match the other's exact shape.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Base-Coordinate Subtraction Trick:**\n  - How do we store an island's shape in a `set` so that two identical shapes at different locations produce the exact same representation?\n  - Record the starting cell where you initiate the island's DFS as the **base coordinate `(baseR, baseC)`**.\n  - For every cell `(r, c)` visited in that island, subtract the base coordinate and append the relative offset **`(r - baseR, c - baseC)`** into a `vector<pair<int, int>> shape`.\n  - If two islands have the same shape and you traverse neighbors in the **exact same fixed direction order** (Up, Right, Down, Left), their relative offset vectors will be 100% identical!\n- **Inserting Shapes into a Set:**\n  - Insert each island's `shape` vector into a `set<vector<pair<int, int>>> uniqueShapes`.\n  - The total number of distinct islands is simply `uniqueShapes.size()`.\n- **Time & Space Complexity:**\n  - **Time Complexity:** `O(N * M * log(N * M)) + O(N * M * 4)` (grid scan + 4-directional DFS + set insertion).\n  - **Space Complexity:** `O(N * M)` for the `seen` matrix and `uniqueShapes` set.\n\n### 💻 Number of Distinct Islands Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid recordShapeDFS(int r, int c, int baseR, int baseC, const vector<vector<int>> &grid,\n                    vector<vector<int>> &seen, vector<pair<int, int>> &shape) {\n    seen[r][c] = 1;\n    shape.push_back({r - baseR, c - baseC}); // Store relative offset from base\n\n    int rows = grid.size(), cols = grid[0].size();\n    int dr[] = {-1, 0, 1, 0}, dc[] = {0, 1, 0, -1};\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n        int nr = r + dr[i], nc = c + dc[i];\n        if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols &&\n            !seen[nr][nc] && grid[nr][nc] == 1) {\n            recordShapeDFS(nr, nc, baseR, baseC, grid, seen, shape);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nint countDistinctIslands(vector<vector<int>> &grid) {\n    int rows = grid.size(), cols = grid[0].size();\n    vector<vector<int>> seen(rows, vector<int>(cols, 0));\n    set<vector<pair<int, int>>> uniqueShapes;\n\n    for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {\n            if (!seen[r][c] && grid[r][c] == 1) {\n                vector<pair<int, int>> shape;\n                recordShapeDFS(r, c, r, c, grid, seen, shape);\n                uniqueShapes.insert(shape);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return uniqueShapes.size();\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Subtracting the Previous Cell Instead of the Base Cell:** Always subtract the **original starting cell `(baseR, baseC)`** of the island, not the immediate parent cell in the DFS tree!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
