3031	Multi-Component Graph Architecture: Visited Array Invariant & Connected Components	dsa-connected-components-in-graphs	Master the connected components loop pattern with a visited array to guarantee complete traversal across disconnected graph components.	2026-10-03 01:35:42.045364+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Multi-Component Graph Architecture: Visited Array Invariant & Connected Components\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lea-Wl_uWXY", "title": "Lecture 129: Connected Components in Graph", "duration": "16m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-provinces/", "title": "Number of Provinces (LC 547)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count the total number of connected components in an undirected graph."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-connected-components-in-an-undirected-graph/", "title": "Number of Connected Components in an Undirected Graph (LC 323)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use a visited array loop over all vertices to count disconnected graph components."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/count-the-number-of-complete-components/", "title": "Count the Number of Complete Components (LC 2685)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Traverse each connected component and verify if every pair of nodes in it has an edge."}]	Module 21: Graph Theory Foundations, Representations & Traversals	### 📌 Topic Overview\nA single graph does not have to be one connected piece—it can consist of **multiple disconnected islands (Connected Components)**. If you only start a graph traversal from node `1`, you will completely miss all other disconnected components!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Starting Only at Node `1` Fails:**\n  - A traversal (BFS or DFS) starting at node `1` can only visit nodes reachable from `1` (Component 1).\n- **The Universal Connected-Components Loop Pattern:**\n  - Always maintain a `vis[N + 1] = {0}` array.\n  - Loop `i` from `1` to `N` across every vertex in the graph:\n    - If `!vis[i]`, then vertex `i` belongs to a **brand-new unvisited component**!\n    - Increment `componentCount++` and launch `traverse(i, adj, vis)`, which will visit and mark every node inside that component.\n\n### 💻 Universal Connected Components Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid traverseComponent(int startNode, vector<int> adj[], vector<int> &vis) {\n    // Standard BFS or DFS marks all reachable nodes in this component as vis = 1\n    queue<int> q;\n    q.push(startNode);\n    vis[startNode] = 1;\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int node = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        for (int neighbor : adj[node]) {\n            if (!vis[neighbor]) {\n                vis[neighbor] = 1;\n                q.push(neighbor);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nint countComponents(int n, vector<int> adj[]) {\n    vector<int> vis(n + 1, 0);\n    int components = 0;\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        if (!vis[i]) {\n            components++; // Found a new disconnected component!\n            traverseComponent(i, adj, vis);\n        }\n    }\n    return components;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting the Outer 1..N Loop in Graph Problems:** In interviews, unless the problem explicitly guarantees the graph is fully connected, always wrap your BFS/DFS inside a `for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) if (!vis[i])` loop!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
15089	Directional Collision Simulation: Asteroid Collision	dsa-asteroid-collision-stack-simulation	Master Directional Collision Simulation: Asteroid Collision with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:30:52.970724+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Directional Collision Simulation: Asteroid Collision\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_eYGqw_VDR4", "title": "Directional Collision Simulation: Asteroid Collision - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/asteroid-collision/", "title": "LeetCode 735: Asteroid Collision", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find out the state of the asteroids after all collisions. Positive means moving right, negative means moving left."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/count-collisions-on-a-road/", "title": "LeetCode 2211: Count Collisions on a Road", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Simulate directional L, R, and S car collisions along a 1D road."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/asteroid-collision/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Asteroid Collision", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Simulate all asteroid collisions and return the surviving asteroids in order."}]	Module 43: Monotonic Stack & Contribution Algorithms: Next/Previous Greater/Smaller, Rainwater & Subarray Aggregations	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array `asteroids` where `abs(asteroids[i])` is the size and the sign (`+` = moving right, `-` = moving left) is the direction, simulate all collisions:\n- When two asteroids collide, the **smaller one explodes**.\n- If both are the **exact same size, BOTH explode**.\n- Two asteroids moving in the same direction (or a left-moving asteroid on the left and a right-moving asteroid on the right, `<-` and `->`) **never collide**!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Use a `vector<int> st` Instead of `std::stack<int>`:**\n  - `vector<int>` supports `.push_back()`, `.pop_back()`, and `.back()` in `O(1)` time just like a stack, **and** can be returned directly at the end without needing to pop and reverse!\n- **The 3 Cases for an Incoming Asteroid `arr[i]`:**\n  - **If `arr[i] > 0` (Moving Right):** It can never collide with anything currently on its left → **`st.push_back(arr[i])`**.\n  - **Else (`arr[i] < 0`, Moving Left):**\n    1. **Destroy Smaller Right-Moving Asteroids:**\n       `while (!st.empty() && st.back() > 0 && st.back() < abs(arr[i])) st.pop_back();`\n    2. **Equal Size Mutual Destruction:**\n       `if (!st.empty() && st.back() == abs(arr[i])) st.pop_back();` (both `st.back()` and `arr[i]` are destroyed!).\n    3. **Surviving Negative Asteroid:**\n       `else if (st.empty() || st.back() < 0) st.push_back(arr[i]);` (if the stack is now empty OR its top is also negative, `arr[i]` survives and joins `st`!).\n- **Complexity:** `O(2N)` Time and `O(N)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Asteroid Collision Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> asteroidCollision(vector<int> &arr) {\n    vector<int> st; // Using vector as a stack to return directly in left-to-right order\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {\n        if (arr[i] > 0) {\n            st.push_back(arr[i]);\n        } else {\n            // 1. Destroy all smaller right-moving (positive) asteroids on top of stack\n            while (!st.empty() && st.back() > 0 && st.back() < abs(arr[i])) {\n                st.pop_back();\n            }\n\n            // 2. If equal-sized right-moving asteroid is on top, both explode!\n            if (!st.empty() && st.back() == abs(arr[i])) {\n                st.pop_back();\n            }\n            // 3. Negative asteroid only survives if stack is empty or top is also negative!\n            else if (st.empty() || st.back() < 0) {\n                st.push_back(arr[i]);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return st;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Colliding When `st.back() < 0` and `arr[i] > 0`:** If `st.back()` is `-5` (moving **left** `<-`) and `arr[i]` is `+10` (moving **right** `->`), they are moving **away from each other** and NEVER collide! Collisions ONLY happen when `st.back() > 0` (`->`) and `arr[i] < 0` (`<-`).	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
4716	Bounded Modulo State-Space BFS: Minimum Multiplications to Reach End	dsa-minimum-multiplications-to-reach-end	Calculate minimum multiplication steps to reach target modulo 100,000 using bounded state-space queue BFS.	2026-10-03 11:07:15.295779+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Bounded Modulo State-Space BFS: Minimum Multiplications to Reach End\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_BvEJ3VIDWw", "title": "Lecture 164: Minimum Multiplications to Reach End", "duration": "16m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/minimum-multiplications-to-reach-end/1", "title": "Minimum Multiplications to reach End", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the minimum steps to multiply start by array elements modulo 100000 to reach end."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-operations-to-convert-number/", "title": "Minimum Operations to Convert Number (LC 2059)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Run bounded state-space BFS over [0, 1000] using +, -, and ^ operations with array elements."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/broken-calculator/", "title": "Broken Calculator (LC 991)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Explore state transitions to transform startValue into targetValue in minimum operations."}]	Module 24: Shortest Path Algorithms in Graphs: DAG Relaxation, BFS & Dijkstra's Algorithm	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an integer `start`, an integer `end`, and an array `arr`, in each step you can multiply `start` by any element in `arr` and take the result **modulo `100,000` (`10^5`)**. Find the **minimum number of multiplications** needed to reach `end`, or `-1` if impossible.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Recognizing the Implicit State-Space Graph (`100,000` Nodes):**\n  - Because every multiplication is taken `% 100000`, any state number `num` is strictly bounded in the range **`[0, 99999]`**!\n  - Therefore, there are at most `100,000` possible nodes in this graph, and every multiplication step costs `+1` operation.\n- **Unit-Weight BFS With `dist[100000]`:**\n  - Initialize `vector<int> dist(100000, 1e9)` and set `dist[start] = 0`.\n  - Push `{start, 0}` into a FIFO `queue<pair<int, int>> q`.\n  - For each popped `{node, steps}`, loop through every multiplier `it` in `arr`:\n    - Compute `nextNum = (node * it) % 100000`.\n    - If `steps + 1 < dist[nextNum]`, update `dist[nextNum] = steps + 1` and push `{nextNum, steps + 1}` (returning `steps + 1` immediately if `nextNum == end`).\n- **Complexity:** `O(100000 * N)` Time and `O(100000)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Minimum Multiplications to Reach End Blueprint\n```cpp\nint minimumMultiplications(vector<int> &arr, int start, int end) {\n    if (start == end) return 0;\n\n    queue<pair<int, int>> q;\n    q.push({start, 0});\n    vector<int> dist(100000, 1e9);\n    dist[start] = 0;\n    int mod = 100000;\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        auto [node, steps] = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n\n        for (int factor : arr) {\n            int num = (node * factor) % mod;\n            if (steps + 1 < dist[num]) {\n                dist[num] = steps + 1;\n                if (num == end) return steps + 1;\n                q.push({num, steps + 1});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return -1;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Thinking the State Space Is Infinite:** The `% 100000` operation caps the total distinct states at `100,000`, which is why a fixed-size array `dist(100000, 1e9)` works without hashing!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
1118	Tree Traversal Paradigms: Depth-First Search (Inorder, Preorder, Postorder) vs. Breadth-First Search	dsa-binary-tree-traversals-dfs-vs-bfs	Differentiate Depth-First Search (Preorder, Inorder, Postorder) from Breadth-First Search (Level-Order) on binary trees.	2026-10-03 00:35:47.121015+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Tree Traversal Paradigms: Depth-First Search (Inorder, Preorder, Postorder) vs. Breadth-First Search\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jmy0LaGET1I", "title": "Lecture 76: Binary Tree Traversals in Binary Tree", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/preorder-traversal/1", "title": "Tree Traversal (All 3 DFS in One Pass)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Practice tracing recursive tree traversal orders."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/n-ary-tree-preorder-traversal/", "title": "N-ary Tree Preorder Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Generalize root-first depth-first traversal to N-ary trees."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/n-ary-tree-postorder-traversal/", "title": "N-ary Tree Postorder Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Generalize children-before-root traversal to N-ary trees."}]	Module 15: Binary Trees: Hierarchical Representation & Fundamental Traversals	### 📌 Topic Overview\nBecause a binary tree is non-linear, there are two main ways to visit every node systematically: **Depth-First Search (DFS)** (diving deep down subtrees first) and **Breadth-First Search (BFS)** (visiting nodes level by level from top to bottom).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **1. Depth-First Search (DFS) — 3 Variants:**\n  - Easy mnemonic: The name tells you **where the `Root` is placed** relative to `Left` and `Right`!\n  - **Preorder (`Root → Left → Right`):** `Pre` means Root comes *first*, followed by the left subtree, then the right subtree.\n  - **Inorder (`Left → Root → Right`):** `In` means Root sits *in between* the left subtree and the right subtree.\n  - **Postorder (`Left → Right → Root`):** `Post` means Root comes *last*, after both left and right subtrees finish.\n- **2. Breadth-First Search (BFS / Level-Order Traversal):**\n  - Visits nodes **level by level** from top to bottom, and from left to right within each level.\n\n### 💻 Quick Example Trace\nFor a simple tree where `1` has left child `2` (with children `4, 5`) and right child `3` (with children `6, 7`):\n- **Inorder (`Left → Root → Right`):** `4, 2, 5, 1, 6, 3, 7`\n- **Preorder (`Root → Left → Right`):** `1, 2, 4, 5, 3, 6, 7`\n- **Postorder (`Left → Right → Root`):** `4, 5, 2, 6, 7, 3, 1`\n- **Level-Order (BFS):** `1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7`\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Applying Order Only at the Top Root:** Remember that the traversal rule (`Left → Root → Right`, etc.) must be applied **recursively to every single subtree**, not just the top root!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22212	Consistent Hashing	hld-consistent-hashing	Solve distributed cache reshuffling and database sharding bottlenecks using Hash Rings, Virtual Nodes, and bounded key redistributions.	2026-10-07 16:41:22.814808+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Consistent Hashing\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/IC5Y1EE-aj4", "title": "Consistent Hashing - System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "hld-v7-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Failure of Modulo Hashing in Distributed Caching", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nUnder standard modulo hashing, if you have 4 cache servers ($N = 4$), a key with hash 14 maps to server $14 \\\\% 4 = 2$.\\n\\nIf one server crashes or you scale out to 5 servers ($N = 5$), the denominator changes for all future lookups. The same key now evaluates to $14 \\\\% 5 = 4$. \\n\\n**The Catastrophe:** Nearly **100% of all cached keys across the entire cluster instantly miss**, because their modulo hash resolves to the wrong physical node. This causes a massive \\"Cache Stampede\\" (Thundering Herd) where millions of client queries bypass the cache and hit the primary database simultaneously, leading to catastrophic database outages.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why does standard modulo hashing (hash(key) % N) fail catastrophically in a distributed caching tier like Memcached or Redis when servers are dynamically scaled up or down?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v7-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Mechanism of the Consistent Hashing Ring", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Mechanism:**\\n1. A fixed 360-degree circular integer range (e.g., $0$ to $2^{32}-1$) forms the Hash Ring.\\n2. Server IP addresses or identifiers are hashed using a uniform function (e.g., MurmurHash) and placed at coordinates on the ring.\\n3. Incoming request keys are hashed using the exact same function to determine their ring coordinates.\\n4. To route a key, the algorithm traverses the ring **clockwise** from the key coordinate until it reaches the first server node.\\n\\n**Fraction of Keys Moved:**\\nWhen a new server joins, it only assumes responsibility for keys situated between itself and its immediate counter-clockwise neighbor. On average, only **$K/N$ keys** (where $K$ is total keys and $N$ is total servers) need to be reassigned, leaving the remaining $(N-1)/N$ keys completely intact.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Explain how Consistent Hashing maps keys to servers on a conceptual ring. What fraction of keys must be moved when a new server joins a cluster of N nodes?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v7-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Non-Uniform Distribution & Virtual Nodes", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Hot Spot Problem:** With a small number of physical nodes (e.g., 3 servers), random hash outputs often place nodes close together on the ring. One server may end up responsible for 70% of the ring circumference while another covers only 5%, leading to CPU exhaustion and out-of-memory crashes on the overburdened node.\\n\\n**Virtual Nodes Solution:**\\nInstead of placing each physical server on the ring once, each physical server is mapped to multiple logical points (e.g., 100 to 256 \\"virtual nodes\\" created by hashing `server_ip#1`, `server_ip#2`, etc.).\\n\\nBecause hundreds of virtual nodes per server are interweaved randomly across the ring, the law of large numbers guarantees an evenly distributed circumference for each physical machine (standard deviation drops drastically). If a physical machine fails, its load is evenly inherited by all remaining nodes rather than overwhelming a single neighbor.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "What is the \\"Hot Spot\\" problem in basic Consistent Hashing, and how do Virtual Nodes (Vnodes) mathematically resolve data skewness?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v7-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Consistent Hashing with Replication for High Availability", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nTo achieve fault tolerance, storage engines do not store data on only one node on the ring:\\n1. **Replication Walk:** When a key is hashed, it routes clockwise to the first node (the \\"Coordinator\\" or Primary replica).\\n2. **Preference List:** The coordinator automatically routes copies of the write to the next $N-1$ physically distinct nodes appearing immediately clockwise on the ring.\\n3. **Physical Node Diversity:** The ring topology aware router ensures that these next clockwise virtual nodes map to distinct physical server racks or availability zones to prevent co-located power failure loss.\\n4. **Quorum Consistency:** Reads and writes use quorum formulas ($R + W > N$) over these clockwise replicas. If one replica node crashes, the remaining nodes on the ring handle the read/write seamlessly.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "In distributed storage engines like Apache Cassandra or Amazon DynamoDB, how is Consistent Hashing adapted to maintain replication factors (e.g., RF=3) while surviving node failures?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v7-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Identifying Affected Key Ranges During Rebalancing", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nOn a clockwise consistent hash ring:\\n1. Prior to insertion, all keys falling in the arc between `S_prev` and `S_next` routed clockwise into `S_next`.\\n2. When `S_new` is inserted between `S_prev` and `S_next`, the ring partition between `(S_prev, S_new]` now routes directly to `S_new`.\\n3. Therefore, the only affected key range is `(S_prev, S_new]`.\\n4. **Data Offloading:** `S_next` is the only node that held those keys. `S_next` streams that specific subset of keys over to `S_new` and evicts them from its own storage. No other nodes on the ring participate in the migration.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "When a new node S_new is inserted onto a consistent hash ring between existing nodes S_prev and S_next, describe the exact key range that must be migrated and which node offloads the data.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	High Level Design	### 📌 1. The Flaw of Traditional Modulo Hashing\n* In naive sharding, keys are mapped to servers using `hash(key) % N` where `N` is the number of servers.\n* **The Problem:** Adding or removing a single server changes `N` to `N+1` or `N-1`. This causes almost **all existing keys to map to new nodes**, triggering massive cache invalidation storms and database connection saturation.\n\n### 💡 2. The Consistent Hashing Ring\n* Both servers and data keys are mapped onto an abstract circular address space (**Hash Ring**, typically 0 to $2^{32}-1$).\n* **Mapping Logic:** To locate the server for a given key, hash the key to a position on the ring and walk **clockwise** until encountering the first available server node.\n* **Minimal Disruption:** Adding or removing a node only redistributes keys located between the affected node and its immediate predecessor (on average only $K/N$ keys move), leaving all other servers completely untouched.\n\n### 💡 3. Virtual Nodes (Vnodes)\n* **Hot Spot Problem:** With few physical nodes, random hashing positions can leave servers unevenly spaced, causing one node to hold 80% of data while others sit idle.\n* **Solution:** Assign multiple **Virtual Nodes** (e.g., 100–300 tokens per physical node) spread throughout the ring. This balances load uniformly and ensures failed node capacity is shared equally across all survivors.	Piyush Garg	https://www.youtube.com/@piyushgarg195	28	Consistent Hashing	Solve distributed cache reshuffling and database sharding bottlenecks using Hash Rings, Virtual Nodes, and bounded key redistributions.	31	Hard	High Level Design	7	[{"id": "hld-v7-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Failure of Modulo Hashing in Distributed Caching", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nUnder standard modulo hashing, if you have 4 cache servers ($N = 4$), a key with hash 14 maps to server $14 \\\\% 4 = 2$.\\n\\nIf one server crashes or you scale out to 5 servers ($N = 5$), the denominator changes for all future lookups. The same key now evaluates to $14 \\\\% 5 = 4$. \\n\\n**The Catastrophe:** Nearly **100% of all cached keys across the entire cluster instantly miss**, because their modulo hash resolves to the wrong physical node. This causes a massive \\"Cache Stampede\\" (Thundering Herd) where millions of client queries bypass the cache and hit the primary database simultaneously, leading to catastrophic database outages.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why does standard modulo hashing (hash(key) % N) fail catastrophically in a distributed caching tier like Memcached or Redis when servers are dynamically scaled up or down?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v7-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Mechanism of the Consistent Hashing Ring", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Mechanism:**\\n1. A fixed 360-degree circular integer range (e.g., $0$ to $2^{32}-1$) forms the Hash Ring.\\n2. Server IP addresses or identifiers are hashed using a uniform function (e.g., MurmurHash) and placed at coordinates on the ring.\\n3. Incoming request keys are hashed using the exact same function to determine their ring coordinates.\\n4. To route a key, the algorithm traverses the ring **clockwise** from the key coordinate until it reaches the first server node.\\n\\n**Fraction of Keys Moved:**\\nWhen a new server joins, it only assumes responsibility for keys situated between itself and its immediate counter-clockwise neighbor. On average, only **$K/N$ keys** (where $K$ is total keys and $N$ is total servers) need to be reassigned, leaving the remaining $(N-1)/N$ keys completely intact.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Explain how Consistent Hashing maps keys to servers on a conceptual ring. What fraction of keys must be moved when a new server joins a cluster of N nodes?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v7-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Non-Uniform Distribution & Virtual Nodes", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Hot Spot Problem:** With a small number of physical nodes (e.g., 3 servers), random hash outputs often place nodes close together on the ring. One server may end up responsible for 70% of the ring circumference while another covers only 5%, leading to CPU exhaustion and out-of-memory crashes on the overburdened node.\\n\\n**Virtual Nodes Solution:**\\nInstead of placing each physical server on the ring once, each physical server is mapped to multiple logical points (e.g., 100 to 256 \\"virtual nodes\\" created by hashing `server_ip#1`, `server_ip#2`, etc.).\\n\\nBecause hundreds of virtual nodes per server are interweaved randomly across the ring, the law of large numbers guarantees an evenly distributed circumference for each physical machine (standard deviation drops drastically). If a physical machine fails, its load is evenly inherited by all remaining nodes rather than overwhelming a single neighbor.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "What is the \\"Hot Spot\\" problem in basic Consistent Hashing, and how do Virtual Nodes (Vnodes) mathematically resolve data skewness?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v7-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Consistent Hashing with Replication for High Availability", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nTo achieve fault tolerance, storage engines do not store data on only one node on the ring:\\n1. **Replication Walk:** When a key is hashed, it routes clockwise to the first node (the \\"Coordinator\\" or Primary replica).\\n2. **Preference List:** The coordinator automatically routes copies of the write to the next $N-1$ physically distinct nodes appearing immediately clockwise on the ring.\\n3. **Physical Node Diversity:** The ring topology aware router ensures that these next clockwise virtual nodes map to distinct physical server racks or availability zones to prevent co-located power failure loss.\\n4. **Quorum Consistency:** Reads and writes use quorum formulas ($R + W > N$) over these clockwise replicas. If one replica node crashes, the remaining nodes on the ring handle the read/write seamlessly.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "In distributed storage engines like Apache Cassandra or Amazon DynamoDB, how is Consistent Hashing adapted to maintain replication factors (e.g., RF=3) while surviving node failures?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v7-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Identifying Affected Key Ranges During Rebalancing", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nOn a clockwise consistent hash ring:\\n1. Prior to insertion, all keys falling in the arc between `S_prev` and `S_next` routed clockwise into `S_next`.\\n2. When `S_new` is inserted between `S_prev` and `S_next`, the ring partition between `(S_prev, S_new]` now routes directly to `S_new`.\\n3. Therefore, the only affected key range is `(S_prev, S_new]`.\\n4. **Data Offloading:** `S_next` is the only node that held those keys. `S_next` streams that specific subset of keys over to `S_new` and evicts them from its own storage. No other nodes on the ring participate in the migration.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "When a new node S_new is inserted onto a consistent hash ring between existing nodes S_prev and S_next, describe the exact key range that must be migrated and which node offloads the data.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
