5250	Bipartite Row-Column Coordinate Union: Most Stones Removed With Same Row or Column (DSU)	dsa-most-stones-removed-with-same-row-or-column-dsu	Model row and column coordinates as distinct bipartite nodes in DSU to determine maximum stone removals via n - components.	2026-10-03 11:10:35.802768+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Bipartite Row-Column Coordinate Union: Most Stones Removed With Same Row or Column (DSU)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OwMNX8SPavM", "title": "Lecture 178: Most Stones Removed With Same Row or Column", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/most-stones-removed-with-same-row-or-column/", "title": "Most Stones Removed with Same Row or Column (LC 947)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the largest possible number of stones that can be removed if a stone can be removed when it shares a row or column with another stone."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/maximum-stone-removal-1662179442/1", "title": "Maximum Stone Removal", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the maximum number of stones that can be removed from a 2D plane using row-column DSU."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/remove-max-number-of-edges-to-keep-graph-fully-traversable/", "title": "Remove Max Number of Edges to Keep Graph Fully Traversable (LC 1579)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Practice component-level removal formulas where each connected component leaves 1 surviving element."}]	Module 26: Disjoint Set Union (DSU), Kruskal's MST & Dynamic Connectivity	### 📌 Topic Overview\nOn a 2D plane, we place `n` stones at integer coordinates `[r, c]`. A stone can be removed if there is at least one other stone left in the **same row or the same column**. Find the **maximum number of stones** that can be removed.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Mathematical Formula (`ans = n - numberOfConnectedComponents`):**\n  - If `k` stones are connected via shared rows or columns into a single component, we can remove **`k - 1` stones** one by one, leaving **1 final stone** per component!\n  - Summing across all `C` components: `(k1 - 1) + (k2 - 1) + ... + (kC - 1) = (k1 + k2 + ... + kC) - C =` **`n - C`**!\n- **Row-Column Node Mapping Trick in DSU:**\n  - Instead of treating the `n` stones as nodes (`O(N²)` pairwise comparisons), treat **each Row `0 .. maxRow`** and **each Column `0 .. maxCol`** as nodes in DSU!\n  - Shift column indices by `+ maxRow + 1` so row and column IDs never collide:\n    - `nodeRow = r`\n    - **`nodeCol = c + maxRow + 1`**\n  - For every stone `[r, c]`, simply call **`ds.unionBySize(nodeRow, nodeCol)`** and record `nodeRow` and `nodeCol` in `stoneNodes`!\n  - Count how many active `stoneNodes` satisfy `ds.findUPar(it) == it` to get `C`, and return `n - C`!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * 4 * alpha)` Time and `O(maxRow + maxCol)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Most Stones Removed (Row-Column DSU) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint removeStones(vector<vector<int>> &stones) {\n    int n = stones.size();\n    int maxRow = 0, maxCol = 0;\n    for (const auto &it : stones) {\n        maxRow = max(maxRow, it[0]);\n        maxCol = max(maxCol, it[1]);\n    }\n\n    DisjointSet ds(maxRow + maxCol + 1);\n    unordered_map<int, int> stoneNodes;\n\n    for (const auto &it : stones) {\n        int nodeRow = it[0];\n        int nodeCol = it[1] + maxRow + 1; // Offset column index to avoid collision with rows\n        ds.unionBySize(nodeRow, nodeCol);\n        stoneNodes[nodeRow] = 1;\n        stoneNodes[nodeCol] = 1;\n    }\n\n    int cntComponents = 0;\n    for (const auto &it : stoneNodes) {\n        if (ds.findUPar(it.first) == it.first) {\n            cntComponents++;\n        }\n    }\n    return n - cntComponents;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Counting Empty Rows/Columns as Components:** A row or column with zero stones is initialized in `DisjointSet` as its own parent, but it is **not** a stone component! Only count roots among rows/columns that actually contain at least one stone (`stoneNodes`).	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
