1367	State-Tagged Stack Unification: Preorder, Inorder & Postorder in a Single Traversal	dsa-all-traversals-in-one-pass	Unify preorder, inorder, and postorder traversals in a single pass using state-tagged stack pairs tracking node visit counts.	2026-10-03 00:50:37.348902+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: State-Tagged Stack Unification: Preorder, Inorder & Postorder in a Single Traversal\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ySp2epYvgTE", "title": "Lecture 85: Preorder, Inorder, and Postorder in One Traversal", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/tree-traversal--170646/1", "title": "Tree Traversals", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return inorder, preorder, and postorder traversals in a single combined pass."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-level-order-traversal/", "title": "Binary Tree Level Order Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Compare single-stack DFS state transitions with single-queue BFS level snapshots."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-inorder-traversal/", "title": "Binary Tree Inorder Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Contrast state-tagged stack iteration with classical two-phase inorder."}]	Module 15: Binary Trees: Hierarchical Representation & Fundamental Traversals	### 📌 Topic Overview\nInstead of writing three separate algorithms, we can generate **Preorder, Inorder, and Postorder simultaneously in a single pass** using a stack of pairs: **`stack<pair<TreeNode*, int>> st`**, where `int num` tracks how many times a node has been visited (1, 2, or 3).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The 3-State Rule:**\n  - **State 1 (First visit → Preorder):** Append `node->val` to `preorder`, increment `num = 2`, and push `node->left` with state `1`.\n  - **State 2 (Second visit → Inorder):** Append `node->val` to `inorder`, increment `num = 3`, and push `node->right` with state `1`.\n  - **State 3 (Third visit → Postorder):** Append `node->val` to `postorder` and **pop** the node permanently from the stack!\n- **Complexity:** `O(3N) = O(N)` Time and `O(4N)` Space (holding the 3 result lists plus stack).\n\n### 💻 Preorder, Inorder & Postorder in One Pass Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid allTraversals(TreeNode* root, vector<int> &pre, vector<int> &in, vector<int> &post) {\n    if (root == nullptr) return;\n    stack<pair<TreeNode*, int>> st;\n    st.push({root, 1});\n\n    while (!st.empty()) {\n        auto p = st.top();\n        st.pop();\n\n        if (p.second == 1) {\n            pre.push_back(p.first->val);\n            p.second++;\n            st.push(p);\n            if (p.first->left != nullptr) st.push({p.first->left, 1});\n        } else if (p.second == 2) {\n            in.push_back(p.first->val);\n            p.second++;\n            st.push(p);\n            if (p.first->right != nullptr) st.push({p.first->right, 1});\n        } else {\n            post.push_back(p.first->val);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
14163	Complement of Maximum Interval Packing: Non-Overlapping Intervals	dsa-non-overlapping-intervals-greedy	Master Complement of Maximum Interval Packing: Non-Overlapping Intervals with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:24:23.644277+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Complement of Maximum Interval Packing: Non-Overlapping Intervals\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HDHQ8lAWakY", "title": "Complement of Maximum Interval Packing: Non-Overlapping Intervals - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/non-overlapping-intervals/", "title": "LeetCode 435: Non-overlapping Intervals", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given an array of intervals, return the minimum number of intervals you need to remove to make the rest of the intervals non-overlapping."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-number-of-arrows-to-burst-balloons/", "title": "LeetCode 452: Minimum Number of Arrows to Burst Balloons", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Sort balloon horizontal intervals by end coordinate to find the minimum arrows needed to burst all balloons."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/non-overlapping-intervals/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Non Overlapping Intervals", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the minimum number of intervals to remove so that no two remaining intervals overlap."}]	Module 41: Greedy Algorithms: Sorting Heuristics, Reachability Horizons & Interval Scheduling	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array of `N` intervals where `intervals[i] = [start_i, end_i]`, return the **minimum number of intervals you need to remove** to make the rest of the intervals non-overlapping. Note that intervals touching only at their boundary (such as `[1, 2]` and `[2, 3]`) are **considered non-overlapping**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Exact Inverse of "N Meetings in One Room" (Topic 290!):**\n  - Instead of asking *"Which intervals should we remove?"*, flip the question:\n    **"What is the MAXIMUM number of non-overlapping intervals (`cnt`) we can keep?"**\n  - Once we find the maximum number of compatible intervals `cnt` using the exact *N Meetings in One Room* greedy algorithm, the minimum number of removals is simply:\n    **`Minimum Removals = N - cnt`**!\n- **Boundary Difference From N Meetings (`>=` Instead of `>`):**\n  - Sort `intervals` in **ascending order of `end` time** (`a[1] < b[1]`).\n  - Pick the first interval: `cnt = 1`, `lastEndTime = intervals[0][1]`.\n  - Loop `i` from `1` to `N - 1`:\n    - Because touching endpoints like `[1, 2]` and `[2, 3]` are allowed here, check **`if (intervals[i][0] >= lastEndTime)`**:\n      - Increment `cnt++` and update `lastEndTime = intervals[i][1]`.\n  - Return **`N - cnt`**.\n- **Complexity:** `O(N log N + N)` Time and `O(1)` Auxiliary Space.\n\n### 💻 Non-Overlapping Intervals Blueprint\n```cpp\nstatic bool comp(const vector<int> &a, const vector<int> &b) {\n    return a[1] < b[1]; // Sort by end time ascending!\n}\n\nint eraseOverlapIntervals(vector<vector<int>> &intervals) {\n    int n = intervals.size();\n    if (n == 0) return 0;\n\n    // 1. Sort intervals by earliest ending time\n    sort(intervals.begin(), intervals.end(), comp);\n\n    // 2. Count the maximum number of non-overlapping intervals we can keep\n    int cnt = 1;\n    int lastEndTime = intervals[0][1];\n\n    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n        // Touching endpoints (e.g., [1, 2] and [2, 3]) do NOT overlap -> use >=\n        if (intervals[i][0] >= lastEndTime) {\n            cnt++;\n            lastEndTime = intervals[i][1];\n        }\n    }\n\n    // 3. Minimum removals = Total intervals - Maximum kept intervals\n    return n - cnt;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Sorting by Start Time (`a[0] < b[0]`) Without Adjusting `lastEndTime` on Overlap:** Sorting by **end time (`a[1] < b[1]`)** is much cleaner and directly reuses the *N Meetings in One Room* logic.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
4219	Greedy Min-Heap Relaxation: Dijkstra's Shortest Path Algorithm Using Priority Queue (Part 1)	dsa-dijkstras-algorithm-using-priority-queue	Implement Dijkstra's single-source shortest path algorithm using a min-heap priority queue in O(E log V) time.	2026-10-03 11:01:59.928219+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Greedy Min-Heap Relaxation: Dijkstra's Shortest Path Algorithm Using Priority Queue (Part 1)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V6H1qAeB-l4", "title": "Lecture 157: Dijkstra's Algorithm - Using Priority Queue - Part 1", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/implementing-dijkstra-set-1-adjacency-matrix/1", "title": "Implementing Dijkstra Algorithm", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the shortest distance from source vertex S to all vertices in a weighted undirected graph."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/network-delay-time/", "title": "Network Delay Time (LC 743)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use Dijkstra's min-heap algorithm to find the time it takes for all N nodes to receive a signal."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/path-with-maximum-probability/", "title": "Path with Maximum Probability (LC 1514)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Adapt Dijkstra's priority queue relaxation to maximize product probabilities along a path."}]	Module 24: Shortest Path Algorithms in Graphs: DAG Relaxation, BFS & Dijkstra's Algorithm	### 📌 Topic Overview\n**Dijkstra's Algorithm** finds the shortest path from a starting source node `S` to all other vertices in a directed or undirected weighted graph with **non-negative edge weights**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Min-Heap Priority Queue (`priority_queue<pair<int, int>, vector<pair<int, int>>, greater<pair<int, int>>>`):**\n  - Stores pairs of **`{distance, node}`** ordered so the smallest distance is always at the top.\n  - Initialize `dist[V]` with `1e9`, set `dist[S] = 0`, and push `{0, S}` into the min-heap.\n  - While `!pq.empty()`:\n    - Pop `{d, u} = pq.top()`.\n    - For each neighbor `{v, edgeWeight}` in `adj[u]`:\n      - If **`d + edgeWeight < dist[v]`**:\n        - Update `dist[v] = d + edgeWeight`.\n        - Push `{dist[v], v}` into `pq`.\n- **Why Dijkstra Fails on Negative Weights:**\n  - A negative edge weight (e.g., `-2`) creates a negative cycle where traversing the edge back and forth reduces distance infinitely (`-2, -4, -6...`), trapping the priority queue in an infinite loop!\n- **Complexity:** `O(E log V)` Time and `O(V + E)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Dijkstra's Algorithm (Min-Heap) Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> dijkstraPriorityQueue(int V, vector<vector<int>> adj[], int S) {\n    // Min-heap storing {distance, node}\n    priority_queue<pair<int, int>, vector<pair<int, int>>, greater<pair<int, int>>> pq;\n    vector<int> dist(V, 1e9);\n\n    dist[S] = 0;\n    pq.push({0, S});\n\n    while (!pq.empty()) {\n        auto [d, u] = pq.top();\n        pq.pop();\n\n        for (const auto &edge : adj[u]) {\n            int v = edge[0];\n            int wt = edge[1];\n\n            if (d + wt < dist[v]) {\n                dist[v] = d + wt;\n                pq.push({dist[v], v});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return dist;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Putting `{node, distance}` Instead of `{distance, node}` in the Pair:** C++ `pair` sorts by `.first` first! You **must** put `distance` as `.first` (`{dist[v], v}`) so the min-heap orders elements by shortest distance!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
14168	Mountain-Slope Peak Reconciliation: Candy Distribution Problem in O(N) Time & O(1) Space	dsa-candy-distribution-slope-approach-greedy	Master Mountain-Slope Peak Reconciliation: Candy Distribution Problem in O(N) Time & O(1) Space with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:24:23.650492+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Mountain-Slope Peak Reconciliation: Candy Distribution Problem in O(N) Time & O(1) Space\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IIqVFvKE6RY", "title": "Mountain-Slope Peak Reconciliation: Candy Distribution Problem in O(N) Time & O(1) Space - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/candy/", "title": "LeetCode 135: Candy", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "There are n children standing in a line with ratings. Give each child at least 1 candy such that children with a higher rating get more candies than their neighbors. Return the minimum candies needed."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/candy/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Candy", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find the minimum number of candies required to distribute among N children based on adjacent rating constraints."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-mountain-in-array/", "title": "LeetCode 845: Longest Mountain in Array", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Practice single-pass upward slope and downward slope traversal in O(1) space."}]	Module 41: Greedy Algorithms: Sorting Heuristics, Reachability Horizons & Interval Scheduling	### 📌 Topic Overview\n`N` children stand in a line with `ratings[i]`. You must give candies to these children subject to two rules:\n1. Every child must have **at least `1` candy**.\n2. Children with a **strictly higher rating** get **strictly more candies** than their immediate left and right neighbors.\nReturn the **minimum total number of candies** you need to distribute.\n\n### 💡 Progression (Brute O(2N) Space → Better O(N) Space → Optimal O(1) Space Slope Approach)\n- **1. Brute Force (`left[]` and `right[]` Arrays — `O(3N)` Time, `O(2N)` Space):**\n  - Pass 1 (Left-to-Right): Satisfy left neighbors (`if (ratings[i] > ratings[i - 1]) left[i] = left[i - 1] + 1; else left[i] = 1;`).\n  - Pass 2 (Right-to-Left): Satisfy right neighbors (`if (ratings[i] > ratings[i + 1]) right[i] = right[i + 1] + 1; else right[i] = 1;`).\n  - Pass 3: Sum `max(left[i], right[i])` for all `i`.\n- **2. Optimal Slope Approach (`O(N)` Single Pass, `O(1)` Space!):**\n  - Instead of storing arrays, view `ratings` as a sequence of **Flat Plateaus (`==`)**, **Rising Slopes (`>`)**, and **Falling Slopes (`<`)**!\n  - Initialize `sum = 1` (for index `0`) and `i = 1`:\n    1. **Flat Surface (`ratings[i] == ratings[i - 1]`):** Equal neighbor needs only `1` candy → `sum += 1; i++; continue;`.\n    2. **Increasing Slope (`ratings[i] > ratings[i - 1]`):** Start `peak = 1`. While rising, increment `peak++`, add **`sum += peak`**, and `i++`.\n    3. **Decreasing Slope (`ratings[i] < ratings[i - 1]`):** Start `down = 1`. While falling, add **`sum += down`**, increment `down++`, and `i++`.\n       - *(Why can we add `1 + 2 + 3...` forward instead of `3 + 2 + 1` backward? Because the sum `1 + 2 + ... + k` is identical in both directions!)*\n    4. **Peak Adjustment (`if (down > peak) sum += (down - peak)`):**\n       - If the downward slope was longer than the upward slope (`down > peak`), the mountain peak child needs `down` candies instead of `peak` candies—so add the difference **`sum += (down - peak)`**!\n\n### 💻 Candy Distribution (Optimal O(N) Time, O(1) Space Slope Method) Blueprint\n```cpp\nint candy(vector<int> &ratings) {\n    int n = ratings.size();\n    int sum = 1; // Index 0 starts with 1 candy\n    int i = 1;\n\n    while (i < n) {\n        // Case 1: Flat plateau (equal ratings)\n        if (ratings[i] == ratings[i - 1]) {\n            sum += 1;\n            i++;\n            continue;\n        }\n\n        // Case 2: Strictly increasing slope\n        int peak = 1;\n        while (i < n && ratings[i] > ratings[i - 1]) {\n            peak += 1;\n            sum += peak;\n            i++;\n        }\n\n        // Case 3: Strictly decreasing slope\n        int down = 1;\n        while (i < n && ratings[i] < ratings[i - 1]) {\n            sum += down;\n            i++;\n            down += 1;\n        }\n\n        // Reconcile the mountain peak if the downward slope was taller than the upward peak!\n        if (down > peak) {\n            sum += (down - peak);\n        }\n    }\n    return sum;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Incrementing `peak += 1` *After* `sum += peak` vs. `down += 1` *Before* `sum += down`:**\n  - On the **rising** slope, index `0` (or the valley bottom) *already* got `1` candy, so the first rising step needs `2` candies (`peak += 1` **before** `sum += peak`).\n  - On the **falling** slope, the peak was *already* counted in the rising loop, and the bottom of the falling slope needs `1` candy—so add `sum += down` (starting at `1`) **before** incrementing `down += 1`!	take U forward (Striver)