448	Tri-Pointer Converging Sums: 3 Sum Problem (Unique Triplets Summing to Zero)	dsa-3-sum-unique-triplets-two-pointers	Find all unique zero-sum triplets using sorted two-pointer convergence in O(N²) time and O(1) extra space while gracefully skipping duplicates.	2026-10-02 23:12:19.371237+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Tri-Pointer Converging Sums: 3 Sum Problem (Unique Triplets Summing to Zero)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DhFh8Kw7ymk", "title": "Lecture 36: 3 Sum — Find all unique triplets that sums up to zero", "duration": "38m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/3sum-closest/", "title": "3Sum Closest", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use sorted tri-pointer convergence to find the three integers whose sum is closest to target."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/3sum-smaller/", "title": "3Sum Smaller", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count the number of triplets whose sum is strictly less than target."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/triplet-sum-in-array-1587115621/1", "title": "Triplet Sum in Array", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Determine if there exists a triplet in the array whose sum equals a given target."}]	Module 11: Advanced Array Patterns & Hard Interview Problems	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array of integers, find all **unique triplets** `[nums[i], nums[j], nums[k]]` such that `i != j != k` and `nums[i] + nums[j] + nums[k] == 0`. The result must not contain duplicate triplets.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Better → Optimal)\n- **1. Brute Force (O(N³) Time):** Three nested loops with a `set<vector<int>>` to filter duplicates.\n- **2. Better Approach via Hashing (O(N²) Time, O(N) Space):** Fix `i` and `j`, then check if `-(nums[i] + nums[j])` exists in a hash set. Still requires a set of vectors for deduplication.\n- **3. Optimal Sorted Two-Pointer Approach (O(N log N + N²) Time, O(1) Extra Space):**\n  - Sort the array first.\n  - Fix the first element using loop `i` from `0` to `n - 1`. If `i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]`, skip it to avoid duplicates.\n  - Place `j = i + 1` (left pointer) and `k = n - 1` (right pointer).\n  - While `j < k`:\n    - If `sum < 0`: Increment `j++` to increase the sum.\n    - If `sum > 0`: Decrement `k--` to decrease the sum.\n    - If `sum == 0`: Found a valid triplet! Push to result, increment `j++`, decrement `k--`, and **skip all identical neighboring duplicates** with while loops.\n\n### 💻 Optimal 3Sum Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<vector<int>> threeSum(vector<int> &nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    vector<vector<int>> ans;\n    sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        if (i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) continue; // Skip duplicate i\n        int j = i + 1, k = n - 1;\n        while (j < k) {\n            int sum = nums[i] + nums[j] + nums[k];\n            if (sum < 0) {\n                j++;\n            } else if (sum > 0) {\n                k--;\n            } else {\n                ans.push_back({nums[i], nums[j], nums[k]});\n                j++; k--;\n                while (j < k && nums[j] == nums[j - 1]) j++; // Skip duplicate j\n                while (j < k && nums[k] == nums[k + 1]) k--; // Skip duplicate k\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
