586	Bidirectional Prefix-Suffix Accumulation: Maximum Product Subarray	dsa-maximum-product-subarray-prefix-suffix	Find the contiguous subarray with the maximum product in O(N) time and O(1) space using simultaneous forward prefix and backward suffix accumulations.	2026-10-02 23:24:14.964818+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Bidirectional Prefix-Suffix Accumulation: Maximum Product Subarray\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hnswaLJvr6g", "title": "Lecture 44: Maximum Product Subarray in an Array", "duration": "26m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/product-of-array-except-self/", "title": "Product of Array Except Self", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use prefix and suffix running products to compute array products without division."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/subarray-product-less-than-k/", "title": "Subarray Product Less Than K", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count contiguous subarrays whose product is strictly less than K."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/count-the-subarrays-having-product-less-than-k1708/1", "title": "Count the subarrays having product less than k", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Maintain a running product window to count valid subarrays in O(N) time."}]	Module 11: Advanced Array Patterns & Hard Interview Problems	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an integer array containing positives, negatives, and zeros, find the contiguous subarray that has the **largest product** and return that product.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (The 4 Observations)\nInstead of memorizing non-intuitive DP formulas, solve this problem in **O(N) time** and **O(1) space** using four simple observations:\n1. **All Positives:** The answer is simply the product of the entire array.\n2. **Even Number of Negatives:** Multiplying an even number of negatives gives a positive number—so the answer is still the product of the entire array!\n3. **Odd Number of Negatives:** To get a positive product, we must **exclude one negative number**. Excluding any single negative splits the array into a **prefix** (everything before that negative) and a **suffix** (everything after that negative). Thus, the maximum product will always be either a prefix product from the left or a suffix product from the right!\n4. **Zeros in the Array:** Multiplying by `0` turns any running product into `0`. Whenever `pre == 0` or `suff == 0`, simply **reset it to `1`** to start a fresh subarray after the zero!\n\n### 💻 Prefix & Suffix Single-Pass Blueprint\n```cpp\nint maxProductSubarray(vector<int> &arr) {\n    int n = arr.size();\n    int pre = 1, suff = 1, ans = INT_MIN;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        if (pre == 0) pre = 1;   // Reset after encountering a 0\n        if (suff == 0) suff = 1;\n\n        pre = pre * arr[i];\n        suff = suff * arr[n - i - 1];\n        ans = max(ans, max(pre, suff));\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
