4713	Unit-Weight Grid Dijkstra: Shortest Distance in a Binary Maze	dsa-shortest-distance-in-a-binary-maze	Find the shortest distance between source and destination cells in a binary maze using unit-weight queue BFS.	2026-10-03 11:07:15.285076+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Unit-Weight Grid Dijkstra: Shortest Distance in a Binary Maze\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U5Mw4eyUmw4", "title": "Lecture 161: Shortest Distance in a Binary Maze", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/shortest-path-in-binary-matrix/", "title": "Shortest Path in Binary Matrix (LC 1091)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the shortest 8-directional clear path from top-left (0, 0) to bottom-right (n - 1, n - 1) in a binary matrix."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/shortest-path-in-a-binary-maze-1655453161/1", "title": "Shortest Distance in a Binary Maze", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the shortest 4-directional path from a given source cell to a destination cell in a binary grid."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/nearest-exit-from-entrance-in-maze/", "title": "Nearest Exit from Entrance in Maze (LC 1926)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use unit-weight grid BFS to find the minimum steps from entrance to the nearest border exit."}]	Module 24: Shortest Path Algorithms in Graphs: DAG Relaxation, BFS & Dijkstra's Algorithm	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an `N x M` binary grid where `1` represents an open cell and `0` represents a blocked cell, find the **shortest distance** from a `source` coordinate `{sr, sc}` to a `destination` coordinate `{dr, dc}` moving in 4 cardinal directions (or 8 directions on LeetCode 1091). Return `-1` if unreachable.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Plain Queue Replaces Priority Queue Here:**\n  - Every move to an adjacent cell adds an identical step cost of `+1`.\n  - Because edge weights are uniform (`1`), elements pushed into a standard FIFO `queue<pair<int, pair<int, int>>>` are **already naturally sorted by distance**!\n  - Using a plain `queue` instead of a `priority_queue` saves the `O(log(N * M))` heap overhead per push, reducing runtime to pure **`O(N * M)`**!\n- **Early Termination Optimization:**\n  - As soon as a step transition reaches `nr == destination.first && nc == destination.second` (or if `source == destination` initially), return `d + 1` (or `0`) immediately!\n\n### 💻 Shortest Distance in a Binary Maze Blueprint\n```cpp\nint shortestPathBinaryMaze(vector<vector<int>> &grid, pair<int, int> source, pair<int, int> destination) {\n    if (source.first == destination.first && source.second == destination.second) return 0;\n\n    int rows = grid.size(), cols = grid[0].size();\n    vector<vector<int>> dist(rows, vector<int>(cols, 1e9));\n    queue<pair<int, pair<int, int>>> q; // Stores {distance, {r, c}}\n\n    dist[source.first][source.second] = 0;\n    q.push({0, {source.first, source.second}});\n\n    int dr[] = {-1, 0, 1, 0}, dc[] = {0, 1, 0, -1};\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        auto [d, coords] = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        int r = coords.first, c = coords.second;\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n            int nr = r + dr[i], nc = c + dc[i];\n            if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols &&\n                grid[nr][nc] == 1 && d + 1 < dist[nr][nc]) {\n                dist[nr][nc] = d + 1;\n                if (nr == destination.first && nc == destination.second) {\n                    return d + 1;\n                }\n                q.push({d + 1, {nr, nc}});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return -1;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Missing the `source == destination` Edge Case:** If the starting cell is already the destination cell, the inner neighbor loop won't trigger for it—always check `if (source == destination) return 0;` at the very top!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
