outs	18	[]	\N	25	https://youtu.be/Bq58-o8mji0	CSS Specificity, Inheritance & The Cascade Engine
5806	Minimum-Cost State Transitions: Frog Jump (1D DP)	dsa-frog-jump-1d-dynamic-programming	Compute the minimum energy required to reach the N-th stair allowing 1-step and 2-step jumps using 1D DP with O(1) space optimization.	2026-10-03 11:17:17.092327+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Minimum-Cost State Transitions: Frog Jump (1D DP)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EgG3jsGoPvQ", "title": "Lecture 184: Frog Jump (1D DP)", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/geek-jump/1", "title": "Geek Jump (Frog Jump)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Find the minimum total energy used by Geek to jump from stair 0 to stair N - 1 taking 1 or 2 steps."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/min-cost-climbing-stairs/", "title": "Min Cost Climbing Stairs (LC 746)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Compute the minimum cost to reach the top of a staircase with per-step costs."}, {"url": "https://atcoder.jp/contests/dp/tasks/dp_a", "title": "Frog 1 (DP Contest A)", "platform": "AtCoder", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Find the minimum possible total cost incurred before the frog reaches Stone N."}]	Module 28: 1D Dynamic Programming: Recurrences, Memoization, Tabulation & Space Optimization	### 📌 Topic Overview\nA frog wants to climb from stair `0` to stair `N - 1`. In one jump, it can jump to `i + 1` or `i + 2`. The energy lost in a jump from `i` to `j` is **`abs(heights[i] - heights[j])`**. Find the **minimum total energy** required to reach stair `N - 1`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Greedy Fails Here:**\n  - Taking the locally smaller jump from stair `0` might trap the frog on a stone that forces a massive height jump on the next step! We must try **all possible paths** using DP.\n- **1D Recurrence Relation (`f(ind)` = min energy to reach `ind` from `0`):**\n  - Base case: `f(0) = 0` (cost to stand at `0` is `0`).\n  - `jumpOne = f(ind - 1) + abs(heights[ind] - heights[ind - 1])`\n  - `jumpTwo = INT_MAX`\n  - If `ind > 1`: `jumpTwo = f(ind - 2) + abs(heights[ind] - heights[ind - 2])`\n  - Return **`min(jumpOne, jumpTwo)`**.\n- **Progression:** Recursion (`O(2^N)`) → Memoization (`O(N)` Time, `O(2N)` Space) → Tabulation (`O(N)` Time, `O(N)` Space) → Space Optimization (`O(N)` Time, **`O(1)` Space** using `prev` and `prev2`).\n\n### 💻 Frog Jump (Memoization, Tabulation & O(1) Space) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\n// 1. Top-Down Memoization: O(N) Time, O(N) Space\nint solveMemo(int ind, const vector<int> &heights, vector<int> &dp) {\n    if (ind == 0) return 0;\n    if (dp[ind] != -1) return dp[ind];\n\n    int left = solveMemo(ind - 1, heights, dp) + abs(heights[ind] - heights[ind - 1]);\n    int right = INT_MAX;\n    if (ind > 1) {\n        right = solveMemo(ind - 2, heights, dp) + abs(heights[ind] - heights[ind - 2]);\n    }\n    return dp[ind] = min(left, right);\n}\n\n// 2. Space-Optimized Bottom-Up: O(N) Time, O(1) Space\nint frogJumpSpaceOptimized(int n, vector<int> &heights) {\n    int prev = 0, prev2 = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n        int fs = prev + abs(heights[i] - heights[i - 1]);\n        int ss = INT_MAX;\n        if (i > 1) {\n            ss = prev2 + abs(heights[i] - heights[i - 2]);\n        }\n        int curi = min(fs, ss);\n        prev2 = prev;\n        prev = curi;\n    }\n    return prev;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting `if (ind > 1)` Before Computing the 2-Step Jump:** When `ind == 1`, `ind - 2` evaluates to `-1` (out of bounds)! Always guard the 2-step jump with `if (ind > 1)` and initialize `right = INT_MAX`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
