aming.	45	Easy	CSS3 & UI Layouts	20	[]	\N	45	https://youtu.be/DrWjRwC1kYw	UI Construction Project: Component Wireframing (Part 1)
22259	Anti-Patterns & Null Object Pattern	lld-anti-patterns-null-object	Avoid God Objects, Spaghetti Code, and Premature Optimization. Eliminate repetitive null pointer checks using the Null Object Pattern to execute do-nothing polymorphic logic.	2026-10-07 22:41:36.377264+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Anti-Patterns & Null Object Pattern\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/iXHlc_S3Ae4", "title": "Null Object Pattern | Anti-Patterns in System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v40-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: The \\"God Object\\" Anti-Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA God Object is a monolithic class that knows too much and does too much, violently breaking the Single Responsibility Principle. \\n\\n**The Trap:** Facade classes (like an `AppOrchestrator`) start innocently to delegate calls. Over time, developers lazily add direct business logic (e.g., password validation or database retries) directly into the Facade rather than passing it to independent services. The Facade absorbs everything.\\n\\n**Mitigation:** The Orchestrator must strictly remain a \\"dumb router\\". It should instantiate services and blindly delegate requests to them, holding absolutely zero business logic or state manipulation of its own.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Define the \\"God Object\\" anti-pattern. Why do centralized Facade or Orchestrator classes frequently degenerate into God Objects, and how can this be mitigated?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v40-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Null Object Pattern vs Defensive Programming", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nDefensive programming forces the client to explicitly check boundaries: \\n```cpp\\nif (logger != null) {\\n    logger->log(\\"Error\\");\\n}\\n```\\nIf a method has 5 nullable dependencies, the cyclomatic complexity multiplies due to endless branching paths, heavily cluttering unit tests.\\n\\nThe Null Object Pattern eliminates branching. A `NullLogger : public ILogger` is injected containing an empty `void log(string) {}` block.\\nThe client code reduces to `logger->log(\\"Error\\");`. The branch is eliminated, dropping cyclomatic complexity to its lowest possible denominator, and the application is mathematically immune to Null Pointer Dereference crashes.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Null Object pattern mathematically reduce cyclomatic complexity in client code compared to traditional Defensive Programming?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v40-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Applying Null Object to the Command Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA remote has 10 buttons. The user only assigns commands to buttons 1 and 2.\\nInstead of buttons 3-10 holding `nullptr`, causing a crash if pressed, the remote initializes every slot in its `Command` array with a `NoCommand` object.\\n```cpp\\nclass NoCommand : public ICommand {\\npublic:\\n    void execute() override { /* Do absolutely nothing */ }\\n};\\n```\\nWhen the user accidentally presses button 5, the remote blindly fires `array[5]->execute()`. The `NoCommand` triggers an empty block, handling the misclick seamlessly without crashing the firmware.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In a Smart Home Remote Control utilizing the Command Pattern, how does the Null Object pattern gracefully handle unassigned buttons?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v40-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Premature Optimization Anti-Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nDevelopers often waste weeks implementing highly complex architectures—such as deploying a Redis distributed caching cluster, utilizing multi-threaded thread pools, or drafting Custom Assembly optimizations—before proving the core feature works.\\n\\nIn LLD, this manifests as building massively abstracted Strategy and Factory ecosystems for a feature that only ever has one use-case.\\nThe proper engineering lifecycle is: \\"Make it work, make it right, make it fast.\\" Prove the MVP business logic first, apply SOLID principles to make the architecture maintainable, and *only* introduce caching or algorithmic optimization when profiling tools definitively prove a bottleneck exists.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the famous Donald Knuth quote: \\"Premature optimization is the root of all evil.\\" How does this relate to LLD architectural planning?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v40-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Constructor Telescoping as an Anti-Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nConstructor Telescoping occurs when an object requires multiple optional parameters. The developer creates a cascading pyramid of constructors to handle combinations:\\n`User(name)`, `User(name, age)`, `User(name, age, email)`, etc. This rapidly becomes unmaintainable and impossible to read.\\n\\nThe **Builder Design Pattern** eradicates this. Instead of a constructor, you instantiate a Builder object and utilize fluent methods: `UserBuilder().withName(\\"John\\").withEmail(\\"...\\").build()`. It provides infinite configuration flexibility without requiring a single overloaded constructor.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Identify the \\"Constructor Telescoping\\" anti-pattern. Which specific Design Pattern from the Gang of GoF is deployed to eradicate it?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. Engineering Anti-Patterns\n* **God Object:** A class that handles too many responsibilities (violating SRP). It becomes the central hub of the application, rendering modular testing and parallel development impossible.\n* **Spaghetti Code:** Code with an unintelligible control flow, completely devoid of distinct entry/exit points, resulting in infinite coupling.\n* **Premature Optimization:** "Make it work, then make it fast." Wasting weeks engineering an $O(\\log N)$ structure before the system even successfully executes the business logic.\n\n### 💡 2. The Null Pointer Epidemic\n* A frequent source of application crashes (Null Pointer Exceptions) stems from uninitialized objects.\n* Developers combat this with defensive programming: sprinkling `if (obj != null) { obj->execute(); }` constantly throughout the codebase.\n\n### 💡 3. The Null Object Pattern\n* **Definition:** Provide an object as a surrogate for the lack of an object of a given type. The Null Object provides intelligent "do nothing" behavior, hiding the details from its collaborators.\n* Instead of leaving a pointer `null`, you inject a `NoCommand` or `EmptyLogger` class that implements the target interface.\n* When the client invokes `obj->execute()`, the Null Object simply fires an empty method block. The client code is cleaned of all `if-null` statements and polymorphic flow is preserved.	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	Anti-Patterns & Null Object Pattern	Avoid God Objects, Spaghetti Code, and Premature Optimization. Eliminate repetitive null pointer checks using the Null Object Pattern to execute do-nothing polymorphic logic.	25	Hard	Low Level Design	54	[{"id": "lld-v40-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: The \\"God Object\\" Anti-Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA God Object is a monolithic class that knows too much and does too much, violently breaking the Single Responsibility Principle. \\n\\n**The Trap:** Facade classes (like an `AppOrchestrator`) start innocently to delegate calls. Over time, developers lazily add direct business logic (e.g., password validation or database retries) directly into the Facade rather than passing it to independent services. The Facade absorbs everything.\\n\\n**Mitigation:** The Orchestrator must strictly remain a \\"dumb router\\". It should instantiate services and blindly delegate requests to them, holding absolutely zero business logic or state manipulation of its own.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Define the \\"God Object\\" anti-pattern. Why do centralized Facade or Orchestrator classes frequently degenerate into God Objects, and how can this be mitigated?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v40-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Null Object Pattern vs Defensive Programming", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nDefensive programming forces the client to explicitly check boundaries: \\n```cpp\\nif (logger != null) {\\n    logger->log(\\"Error\\");\\n}\\n```\\nIf a method has 5 nullable dependencies, the cyclomatic complexity multiplies due to endless branching paths, heavily cluttering unit tests.\\n\\nThe Null Object Pattern eliminates branching. A `NullLogger : public ILogger` is injected containing an empty `void log(string) {}` block.\\nThe client code reduces to `logger->log(\\"Error\\");`. The branch is eliminated, dropping cyclomatic complexity to its lowest possible denominator, and the application is mathematically immune to Null Pointer Dereference crashes.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "How does the Null Object pattern mathematically reduce cyclomatic complexity in client code compared to traditional Defensive Programming?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v40-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Applying Null Object to the Command Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA remote has 10 buttons. The user only assigns commands to buttons 1 and 2.\\nInstead of buttons 3-10 holding `nullptr`, causing a crash if pressed, the remote initializes every slot in its `Command` array with a `NoCommand` object.\\n```cpp\\nclass NoCommand : public ICommand {\\npublic:\\n    void execute() override { /* Do absolutely nothing */ }\\n};\\n```\\nWhen the user accidentally presses button 5, the remote blindly fires `array[5]->execute()`. The `NoCommand` triggers an empty block, handling the misclick seamlessly without crashing the firmware.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "In a Smart Home Remote Control utilizing the Command Pattern, how does the Null Object pattern gracefully handle unassigned buttons?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v40-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Premature Optimization Anti-Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nDevelopers often waste weeks implementing highly complex architectures—such as deploying a Redis distributed caching cluster, utilizing multi-threaded thread pools, or drafting Custom Assembly optimizations—before proving the core feature works.\\n\\nIn LLD, this manifests as building massively abstracted Strategy and Factory ecosystems for a feature that only ever has one use-case.\\nThe proper engineering lifecycle is: \\"Make it work, make it right, make it fast.\\" Prove the MVP business logic first, apply SOLID principles to make the architecture maintainable, and *only* introduce caching or algorithmic optimization when profiling tools definitively prove a bottleneck exists.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain the famous Donald Knuth quote: \\"Premature optimization is the root of all evil.\\" How does this relate to LLD architectural planning?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v40-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Constructor Telescoping as an Anti-Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nConstructor Telescoping occurs when an object requires multiple optional parameters. The developer creates a cascading pyramid of constructors to handle combinations:\\n`User(name)`, `User(name, age)`, `User(name, age, email)`, etc. This rapidly becomes unmaintainable and impossible to read.\\n\\nThe **Builder Design Pattern** eradicates this. Instead of a constructor, you instantiate a Builder object and utilize fluent methods: `UserBuilder().withName(\\"John\\").withEmail(\\"...\\").build()`. It provides infinite configuration flexibility without requiring a single overloaded constructor.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Identify the \\"Constructor Telescoping\\" anti-pattern. Which specific Design Pattern from the Gang of GoF is deployed to eradicate it?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
