2302	Upper-Bound Prefix Validation: Construct a BST from Preorder Traversal	dsa-construct-bst-from-preorder-traversal	Reconstruct the unique BST from its preorder sequence in O(N) time and O(H) space using single upper-bound propagation.	2026-10-03 01:26:48.314104+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Upper-Bound Prefix Validation: Construct a BST from Preorder Traversal\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UmJT3j26t1I", "title": "Lecture 120: Construct a BST from a preorder traversal", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/construct-binary-search-tree-from-preorder-traversal/", "title": "Construct Binary Search Tree from Preorder Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Construct and return the root of the BST matching the preorder sequence in O(N) time."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/construct-binary-tree-from-preorder-and-inorder-traversal/", "title": "Construct Binary Tree from Preorder and Inorder Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Compare general tree inorder construction with BST upper-bound construction."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/preorder-to-postorder4423/1", "title": "Preorder to BST", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Build a BST from its preorder sequence and return the root."}]	Module 19: Binary Search Trees: Concepts, Validations & Structural Mutations	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array of integers representing the **Preorder Traversal** of a BST, reconstruct and return the root of the unique BST in **`O(N)` time**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Better → Optimal)\n- **1. Brute Force (`O(N²)` Time):** Insert elements one by one into an initially empty BST using standard BST insertion.\n- **2. Better Approach via Sorting (`O(N log N)` Time):** Sort `preorder` to obtain the `inorder` traversal, then use the `Preorder + Inorder` tree reconstruction algorithm.\n- **3. Optimal Upper-Bound Technique (`O(N)` Time, `O(H)` Space):**\n  - Maintain a global index pointer `i = 0` into `preorder`.\n  - Pass an `upperBound` into the recursive function (initially `INT_MAX`).\n  - At each step:\n    - If `i == preorder.size()` or `preorder[i] > upperBound`, return `nullptr`.\n    - Allocate `root = new TreeNode(preorder[i++])`.\n    - For `root->left`: upper bound becomes `root->val`.\n    - For `root->right`: upper bound remains the parent's `upperBound`.\n  - Notice you do **not** need a lower bound! Any element that fails the left upper bound is automatically redirected to the right child.\n\n### 💻 Construct BST from Preorder Blueprint\n```cpp\nTreeNode* build(vector<int> &A, int &i, int bound) {\n    if (i == A.size() || A[i] > bound) return nullptr;\n\n    TreeNode* root = new TreeNode(A[i++]);\n    root->left = build(A, i, root->val); // Upper bound for left is root->val\n    root->right = build(A, i, bound);    // Upper bound for right remains bound\n    return root;\n}\n\nTreeNode* bstFromPreorder(vector<int> &preorder) {\n    int i = 0;\n    return build(preorder, i, INT_MAX);\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Passing Index i by Value:** In `build(A, int &i, int bound)`, `i` must be passed by reference (`int &i`) so that index advances across all recursive calls!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
1366	Single-Stack Boundary Tracking: Iterative Postorder Traversal Using 1 Stack	dsa-iterative-postorder-traversal-one-stack	Execute Left-Right-Root postorder traversal iteratively with strictly one stack by tracking right child completion and unwinding ancestors.	2026-10-03 00:50:37.344875+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Single-Stack Boundary Tracking: Iterative Postorder Traversal Using 1 Stack\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NzIGLLwZBS8", "title": "Lecture 84: Post-order Traversal of Binary Tree using 1 Stack", "duration": "32m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-postorder-traversal/", "title": "Binary Tree Postorder Traversal (Constant Space / 1 Stack)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Perform postorder traversal using strictly one stack."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/vertical-order-traversal-of-a-binary-tree/", "title": "Vertical Order Traversal of a Binary Tree", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Coordinate postorder and vertical tree coordinates using custom stack/map tracking."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/postorder-traversal-from-given-inorder-and-preorder-traversals/1", "title": "Postorder Traversal from given Inorder and Preorder", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Construct postorder sequence directly without full tree instantiation."}]	Module 15: Binary Trees: Hierarchical Representation & Fundamental Traversals	### 📌 Topic Overview\nImplementing iterative Postorder using **only 1 stack** is a classic FAANG interview question. Because a parent node must wait for its right child to complete before it can be processed, we dive left, check the right child, and backtrack up the stack when returning from right subtrees.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Single-Stack State Invariant:**\n  - Maintain pointer `curr = root` and an auxiliary stack.\n  - While `curr != nullptr || !st.empty()`:\n    1. If `curr != nullptr`: Push `curr` to stack and continue diving left (`curr = curr->left`).\n    2. Else: Check `temp = st.top()->right`.\n       - If `temp != nullptr`: A right child exists and hasn't been visited—set `curr = temp` to explore it.\n       - If `temp == nullptr`: No right child exists! Pop `temp = st.top()`, append to `ans`, and **unwind all completed ancestors** whose right child equals `temp` using an inner while loop!\n- **Complexity:** `O(2N) = O(N)` Time (every node is visited at most twice) and `O(H)` Space (strictly one stack of height `H`).\n\n### 💻 Iterative Postorder (1 Stack) Blueprint\n```cpp\nvector<int> postorderOneStack(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<int> ans;\n    stack<TreeNode*> st;\n    TreeNode* curr = root;\n\n    while (curr != nullptr || !st.empty()) {\n        if (curr != nullptr) {\n            st.push(curr);\n            curr = curr->left;\n        } else {\n            TreeNode* temp = st.top()->right;\n            if (temp == nullptr) {\n                temp = st.top();\n                st.pop();\n                ans.push_back(temp->val);\n                // Unwind completed parent nodes\n                while (!st.empty() && temp == st.top()->right) {\n                    temp = st.top();\n                    st.pop();\n                    ans.push_back(temp->val);\n                }\n            } else {\n                curr = temp;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22209	CQRS System Design Pattern	hld-cqrs-pattern	Master Command Query Responsibility Segregation (CQRS) to decouple heavy read/write workloads, prevent database lock contention, and optimize microservices.	2026-10-07 16:33:42.32487+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: CQRS System Design Pattern\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/vNplj9LwQSw", "title": "CQRS System Design Pattern", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "hld-v4-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Architectural Need for CQRS", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Bottleneck:** In a standard CRUD application using a single normalized relational DB, viewing a product page (Read) requires joins across Products, Reviews, Sellers, and Inventory tables. Concurrently, inventory and price updates (Writes) acquire row/table locks. At 100:1 read-heavy ratios, write locks stall user read queries, and complex join queries exhaust DB compute.\\n\\n**The CQRS Solution:** CQRS separates these concerns:\\n1. Writes target a normalized relational DB optimized strictly for transactional integrity.\\n2. Reads target a separate, denormalized read store (e.g., MongoDB, Elasticsearch) where the entire product page is pre-computed as a flat JSON document.\\nThis eliminates multi-table joins, serves reads in sub-milliseconds, and removes write-lock contention from user traffic.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Explain the fundamental bottleneck of using a single relational database for an e-commerce platform with a 100:1 read-to-write ratio. How does CQRS resolve this?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v4-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Eventual Consistency Trade-offs", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Eventual Consistency in CQRS:** Because the Command and Query databases are physically separate, synchronization occurs asynchronously over a message bus. A delay of milliseconds to seconds exists before a write appears in the read database.\\n\\n* **Acceptable Scenario (Product Catalog / Social Media):** If a seller updates a product description or a user edits a bio, a 2-second delay before all global viewers see the new text is harmless. High Availability takes priority.\\n* **Unacceptable Scenario (Banking / Stock Trading):** If a user transfers funds (Write) and immediately attempts another transaction (Read balance), eventual consistency could display the old balance and permit an overdraft. Financial ledgers require strict ACID strong consistency.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "A major trade-off of implementing CQRS with separate databases is Eventual Consistency. Define it in this context and compare an acceptable vs unacceptable use case.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v4-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Routing Traffic in a CQRS Architecture", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nRouting is performed at the network edge by the API Gateway acting as a Reverse Proxy:\\n1. **Query Routing:** Incoming `GET` requests (e.g., `GET /api/v1/products`) are recognized as Query actions and routed to the Load Balancer fronting the Read Microservices cluster (reading from the denormalized Read DB/Cache).\\n2. **Command Routing:** Incoming `POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, and `DELETE` requests are identified as Commands and forwarded to the Load Balancer fronting the Write Microservices cluster (handling business validation and writing to the transactional Write DB).\\nThis prevents heavy read surges from starving the CPU and thread pools dedicated to processing order transactions.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "In a cloud-deployed CQRS system, how does the API Gateway route Command requests and Query requests to their respective backend services?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v4-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Database Synchronization via Change Data Capture (CDC)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nDual-writing in application code is an anti-pattern because network failure on the second write causes permanent database desynchronization.\\n\\n**The Solution: Outbox Pattern & CDC**\\n1. **Change Data Capture (CDC):** A tool like Debezium continuously tails the Write DB's transaction log (PostgreSQL WAL).\\n2. **Message Broker (Kafka):** As soon as a transaction commits, CDC streams an event (e.g., `ProductPriceUpdated`) into a persistent Kafka topic.\\n3. **Consumer Workers:** Dedicated read-model worker pools consume the event and update the target document in MongoDB/Elasticsearch.\\n4. **Fault Tolerance:** If a worker crashes, Kafka retains the committed offset. Upon restart, the worker resumes exactly where it stopped, guaranteeing zero lost updates.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "How do you reliably synchronize the Relational Write Database with the NoSQL Read Database without dual-write race conditions or data loss if a worker crashes?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v4-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Combining CQRS with Event Sourcing", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Flaw of Event Sourcing:** In Event Sourcing, the database is an append-only log of events. Querying data requires \\"hydrating\\" (replaying) the full history of an entity. Running filters like `SELECT * WHERE price < 50` across raw event streams is prohibitively slow.\\n\\n**The CQRS Solution:** CQRS uses the Event Store strictly as the **Command (Write) Database**. As events are appended, workers listen to the stream, calculate the current state, and update a materialized view inside the **Query (Read) Database**. Event Sourcing handles high-speed, audit-proof writes, while CQRS provides indexed, sub-millisecond read access.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why are CQRS and Event Sourcing frequently combined? What fundamental weakness of Event Sourcing does CQRS solve?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	High Level Design	### 📌 1. The Bottleneck of Traditional CRUD\n* In traditional systems, a single database handles both Reads and Writes.\n* **Lock Contention:** Heavy write operations acquire row locks, blocking read operations and stalling latency.\n* **Sub-optimal Schemas:** Normalized relational schemas ensure ACID compliance for writes, but perform poorly for complex multi-table `JOIN` read queries.\n\n### 💡 2. Command Query Responsibility Segregation (CQRS)\n* **Command Side (Writes):** Handles `POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, `DELETE`. Performs validation and writes to a normalized, write-optimized database (e.g., PostgreSQL).\n* **Query Side (Reads):** Handles `GET` requests. Reads from a denormalized, read-optimized database (e.g., MongoDB, Elasticsearch, or Redis) where data is pre-joined into flat JSON documents.\n\n### 💡 3. Synchronization & Event Sourcing\n* **Eventual Consistency:** Write DB updates propagate asynchronously to Read DB views via an Event Bus (Kafka / SQS), introducing minor replication latency.\n* **Event Sourcing Synergy:** Write DB acts as an immutable append-only event log; the Read DB stores materialized projections of those events.	Piyush Garg	https://www.youtube.com/@piyushgarg195	28	CQRS System Design Pattern	Master Command Query Responsibility Segregation (CQRS) to decouple heavy read/write workloads, prevent database lock contention, and optimize microservices.	33	Hard	High Level Design	4	[{"id": "hld-v4-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Architectural Need for CQRS", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Bottleneck:** In a standard CRUD application using a single normalized relational DB, viewing a product page (Read) requires joins across Products, Reviews, Sellers, and Inventory tables. Concurrently, inventory and price updates (Writes) acquire row/table locks. At 100:1 read-heavy ratios, write locks stall user read queries, and complex join queries exhaust DB compute.\\n\\n**The CQRS Solution:** CQRS separates these concerns:\\n1. Writes target a normalized relational DB optimized strictly for transactional integrity.\\n2. Reads target a separate, denormalized read store (e.g., MongoDB, Elasticsearch) where the entire product page is pre-computed as a flat JSON document.\\nThis eliminates multi-table joins, serves reads in sub-milliseconds, and removes write-lock contention from user traffic.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Explain the fundamental bottleneck of using a single relational database for an e-commerce platform with a 100:1 read-to-write ratio. How does CQRS resolve this?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v4-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Eventual Consistency Trade-offs", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Eventual Consistency in CQRS:** Because the Command and Query databases are physically separate, synchronization occurs asynchronously over a message bus. A delay of milliseconds to seconds exists before a write appears in the read database.\\n\\n* **Acceptable Scenario (Product Catalog / Social Media):** If a seller updates a product description or a user edits a bio, a 2-second delay before all global viewers see the new text is harmless. High Availability takes priority.\\n* **Unacceptable Scenario (Banking / Stock Trading):** If a user transfers funds (Write) and immediately attempts another transaction (Read balance), eventual consistency could display the old balance and permit an overdraft. Financial ledgers require strict ACID strong consistency.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "A major trade-off of implementing CQRS with separate databases is Eventual Consistency. Define it in this context and compare an acceptable vs unacceptable use case.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v4-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Routing Traffic in a CQRS Architecture", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nRouting is performed at the network edge by the API Gateway acting as a Reverse Proxy:\\n1. **Query Routing:** Incoming `GET` requests (e.g., `GET /api/v1/products`) are recognized as Query actions and routed to the Load Balancer fronting the Read Microservices cluster (reading from the denormalized Read DB/Cache).\\n2. **Command Routing:** Incoming `POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, and `DELETE` requests are identified as Commands and forwarded to the Load Balancer fronting the Write Microservices cluster (handling business validation and writing to the transactional Write DB).\\nThis prevents heavy read surges from starving the CPU and thread pools dedicated to processing order transactions.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "In a cloud-deployed CQRS system, how does the API Gateway route Command requests and Query requests to their respective backend services?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v4-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Database Synchronization via Change Data Capture (CDC)", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nDual-writing in application code is an anti-pattern because network failure on the second write causes permanent database desynchronization.\\n\\n**The Solution: Outbox Pattern & CDC**\\n1. **Change Data Capture (CDC):** A tool like Debezium continuously tails the Write DB's transaction log (PostgreSQL WAL).\\n2. **Message Broker (Kafka):** As soon as a transaction commits, CDC streams an event (e.g., `ProductPriceUpdated`) into a persistent Kafka topic.\\n3. **Consumer Workers:** Dedicated read-model worker pools consume the event and update the target document in MongoDB/Elasticsearch.\\n4. **Fault Tolerance:** If a worker crashes, Kafka retains the committed offset. Upon restart, the worker resumes exactly where it stopped, guaranteeing zero lost updates.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "How do you reliably synchronize the Relational Write Database with the NoSQL Read Database without dual-write race conditions or data loss if a worker crashes?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v4-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Combining CQRS with Event Sourcing", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Flaw of Event Sourcing:** In Event Sourcing, the database is an append-only log of events. Querying data requires \\"hydrating\\" (replaying) the full history of an entity. Running filters like `SELECT * WHERE price < 50` across raw event streams is prohibitively slow.\\n\\n**The CQRS Solution:** CQRS uses the Event Store strictly as the **Command (Write) Database**. As events are appended, workers listen to the stream, calculate the current state, and update a materialized view inside the **Query (Read) Database**. Event Sourcing handles high-speed, audit-proof writes, while CQRS provides indexed, sub-millisecond read access.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why are CQRS and Event Sourcing frequently combined? What fundamental weakness of Event Sourcing does CQRS solve?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
