ways to reach the n'th stair", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count distinct ways to reach the N-th stair using 1 or 2 steps."}]	Module 28: 1D Dynamic Programming: Recurrences, Memoization, Tabulation & Space Optimization	### 📌 Topic Overview\nYou are standing at the `0`-th stair and want to reach the `n`-th stair. Each time you can climb either **`1` step or `2` steps**. In how many distinct ways can you climb to the top?\n\n### 💡 Striver's 3-Step Shortcut to Write Any 1D Recurrence Relation\nWhenever you face a DP problem, follow these 3 universal rules to write the recurrence first:\n1. **Represent the problem in terms of an index `ind`** (here, `ind` is the current stair `0 .. n`).\n2. **Do all possible stuffs on that index** according to the problem statement (jump `1` step to `ind - 1`, or jump `2` steps to `ind - 2`).\n3. **Aggregate the results:**\n   - If the problem asks to **"Count all ways"** → **Sum up** all stuffs (`left + right`).\n   - If the problem asks for **"Minimum / Maximum"** → Take **`min(left, right)` or `max(left, right)`**!\n- **Base Cases for Climbing Stairs:**\n  - `ind == 0`: `1` way (we are already at stair 0).\n  - `ind == 1`: `1` way (only a single 1-step jump from 0 to 1 is valid).\n  - Recurrence: **`f(ind) = f(ind - 1) + f(ind - 2)`** (identical to Fibonacci with `f(0) = 1, f(1) = 1`!).\n\n### 💻 Climbing Stairs (O(1) Space Optimized) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint climbStairs(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;\n    int prev2 = 1; // Base case: 0-th stair = 1 way\n    int prev = 1;  // Base case: 1-st stair = 1 way\n\n    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {\n        int curi = prev + prev2;\n        prev2 = prev;\n        prev = curi;\n    }\n    return prev;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Setting `dp[0] = 0` Like Standard Fibonacci:** In Climbing Stairs, reaching stair `0` from stair `2` via a 2-step jump is a valid completed path! Therefore `dp[0]` must be **`1`**, not `0`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
7017	Piece-Collection Framing: Rod Cutting Problem (1D Space Optimized)	dsa-rod-cutting-problem-unbounded-knapsack	Reframe cutting a rod of length N into picking rod pieces of length ind + 1 under an Unbounded Knapsack formulation in O(N^2) time.	2026-10-03 11:31:13.741111+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Piece-Collection Framing: Rod Cutting Problem (1D Space Optimized)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mO8XpGoJwuo", "title": "Lecture 205: Rod Cutting Problem (DP 24)", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/rod-cutting0840/1", "title": "Rod Cutting", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given a rod of length N inches and an array of prices for pieces of size 1 to N, determine the maximum value obtainable by cutting up the rod."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/integer-break/", "title": "Integer Break (LC 343)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Break an integer n into the sum of k >= 2 positive integers to maximize the product of those integers."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-cost-to-cut-a-stick/", "title": "Minimum Cost to Cut a Stick (LC 1547)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Contrast Unbounded Knapsack rod cutting with partition-DP stick cutting."}]	Module 30: Dynamic Programming on Subsequences, Subset Sums & 0/1 Knapsack	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a rod of length `N` and an array `price` of size `N` where `price[i]` represents the price of a rod piece of length **`i + 1`**, find the **maximum value** obtainable by cutting the rod into pieces and selling them.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Reframing "Cutting a Rod" as "Collecting Rod Pieces to Sum to `N`" (Unbounded Knapsack!):**\n  - Instead of thinking about where to slice the rod, flip the perspective:\n    - Total Knapsack Capacity **`W = N`** (the target rod length).\n    - At index `ind` (`0` to `N - 1`), the piece has **`rodLength = ind + 1`** and value **`price[ind]`**.\n    - We can pick a piece of `rodLength` **as many times as we want** (infinite supply) until the sum of piece lengths equals `N`!\n- **Base Case (`ind == 0`):**\n  - At `ind == 0`, `rodLength = 1`. To form remaining length `N`, we must pick `N` pieces of length `1`, giving total value **`N * price[0]`**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * N)` Time and **`O(N)` Single-Array Space**.\n\n### 💻 Rod Cutting Problem (1-Array Space Optimized) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint cutRod(vector<int> &price, int n) {\n    vector<int> cur(n + 1, 0);\n\n    // 1. Base case at ind == 0 (rodLength == 1): N pieces of length 1 cost N * price[0]\n    for (int N = 0; N <= n; N++) {\n        cur[N] = N * price[0];\n    }\n\n    // 2. Process piece indices 1 to n - 1 using single-array left-to-right iteration\n    for (int ind = 1; ind < n; ind++) {\n        int rodLength = ind + 1;\n        for (int N = 0; N <= n; N++) {\n            int notTake = 0 + cur[N];\n            int take = INT_MIN;\n            if (rodLength <= N) {\n                take = price[ind] + cur[N - rodLength];\n            }\n            cur[N] = max(take, notTake);\n        }\n    }\n    return cur[n];\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Using `ind` Instead of `ind + 1` as the Piece Length:** Because arrays are 0-indexed, index `ind` corresponds to a piece of length **`rodLength = ind + 1`**! Using `ind` directly would treat index `0` as a piece of length `0` and loop infinitely.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
7022	Palindromic Core Preservation: Minimum Insertions to Make a String Palindrome	dsa-minimum-insertions-to-make-string-palindrome	Minimize character insertions or deletions to form a palindrome by keeping the Longest Palindromic Subsequence intact via N - LPS(s).	2026-10-03 11:31:13.759967+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Palindromic Core Preservation: Minimum Insertions to Make a String Palindrome\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xPBLEj41rFU", "title": "Lecture 210: Minimum Insertions to Make String Palindrome (DP 29)", "duration": "15m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-insertion-steps-to-make-a-string-palindrome/", "title": "Minimum Insertion Steps to Make a String Palindrome (LC 1312)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Return the minimum number of character insertions needed to make string s a palindrome."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/form-a-palindrome1455/1", "title": "Form a palindrome", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the minimum number of insertions required to convert a given string into a palindrome."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/minimum-deletions1648/1", "title": "Minimum Deletions", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the minimum number of deletions required to make a string palindrome (uses the exact same N - LPS(s) formula!)."}]	Module 31: Dynamic Programming on Strings: LCS, Substrings & Palindromic Subsequences	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a string `s` of length `N`, you can insert any character at any position in `s`. Find the **minimum number of insertions** needed to make `s` a palindrome.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Maximum Possible Operations:**\n  - You can always make any string of length `N` a palindrome in `N` insertions by concatenating `reverse(s)` to `s`!\n- **How to Minimize Insertions (`Keep the Longest Palindromic Subsequence Intact!`):**\n  - To insert as **few** characters as possible, we should keep the **longest already-palindromic portion** of `s` untouched, and only insert mirror copies for the remaining characters that don't match!\n  - What is the longest already-palindromic portion of `s`? The **Longest Palindromic Subsequence (`LPS(s)`)**!\n  - Every character **not** in `LPS(s)` needs exactly `1` matching character inserted on the opposite side.\n  - Therefore:\n    **`Minimum Insertions = N - LPS(s)`**!\n  - *(Bonus Note: The **Minimum Deletions** to make a string palindrome is also `N - LPS(s)`!)*\n- **Complexity:** `O(N²)` Time and `O(N)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Minimum Insertions to Make String Palindrome Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint longestCommonSubsequence(string &s1, string &s2) {\n    int n = s1.size(), m = s2.size();\n    vector<int> prev(m + 1, 0), cur(m + 1, 0);\n\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {\n            if (s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]) {\n                cur[j] = 1 + prev[j - 1];\n            } else {\n                cur[j] = max(prev[j], cur[j - 1]);\n            }\n        }\n        prev = cur;\n    }\n    return prev[m];\n}\n\nint minInsertions(string s) {\n    string t = s;\n    reverse(t.begin(), t.end());\n    int lpsLength = longestCommonSubsequence(s, t);\n    return s.size() - lpsLength;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Writing a Separate Interval DP From Scratch When `s.size() - LPS(s)` Solves Both Insertions and Deletions:** Recognizing that `minInsertions(s) == minDeletions(s) == s.size() - LCS(s, reverse(s))` lets you reuse your space-optimized LCS code directly in interviews!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22213	How Video Streaming Works on Scale	hld-video-streaming-architecture	Architecting high-scale video delivery pipelines using Adaptive Bitrate Streaming (ABR), HLS, MPEG-DASH, and chunked media transcoding.	2026-10-07 16:41:22.814808+05:30	### 🎯 Computer Science Fundamentals: How Video Streaming Works on Scale\nCore systems concepts frequently tested in technical screening rounds and engineering interviews.\n\n- **System Architecture**: How modern operating systems, networks, and processors coordinate resources.\n- **Key Concepts to Master**: Concurrency primitives, resource isolation, latency vs throughput tradeoffs, and fault tolerance.\n- **Interview Key Takeaways**:\n  - Be prepared to draw diagrams explaining data flow and component lifecycles.\n  - Explain practical real-world applications (e.g. why databases use WAL, why HTTP/2 uses multiplexing).	[{"url": "https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/", "title": "OS Three Easy Pieces Guide"}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/operating-systems/", "title": "GeeksforGeeks CS Fundamentals"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/-JtjQ-OA7XE", "title": "How Video Streaming Works on Scale - System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "hld-v8-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Progressive Download vs Streaming Bandwidth Wastage", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn Progressive Download, when a user clicks play, the client requests the whole file or aggressively buffers hundreds of megabytes ahead via HTTP byte-range requests.\\n\\nIf a user opens a 2-hour 1080p video (~2.5 GB) and clicks away after 15 seconds, the progressive player may have already downloaded 300 MB to 1 GB of data. Across hundreds of millions of users, paying cloud egress fees (e.g., AWS CloudFront / S3 data transfer out at $0.08/GB) on unwatched buffered data burns millions of dollars.\\n\\nChunk-based streaming (HLS/DASH) fetches data in small 2-to-6-second chunks strictly on-demand. If the user leaves after 15 seconds, only ~15 seconds of media data was downloaded, reducing wasted egress bandwidth to nearly zero.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why does hosting raw .mp4 files with Progressive Download cause massive financial losses in cloud egress fees for platforms like YouTube or Netflix when users abandon videos early?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v8-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: How Adaptive Bitrate Streaming (ABR) Prevents Playback Freezes", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Pre-Transcoding:** During video ingestion, FFmpeg processes raw media into multiple discrete bitrate/resolution profiles (e.g., 360p at 500kbps, 720p at 2.5Mbps, 1080p at 5Mbps). Each stream is sliced into identical time-aligned chunks (e.g., exactly 4.00 seconds).\\n2. **Manifest Files:** A master manifest file (`master.m3u8`) describes these streams to the client player.\\n3. **Client Heuristics:** The client video player runs an ABR algorithm measuring two metrics: *Download Throughput* (how fast chunk $N$ downloaded) and *Buffer Length* (how many seconds of decoded video remain in memory).\\n4. **Seamless Switching:** If buffer health declines because network throughput dipped below 5Mbps, the player finishes decoding chunk $K$ in 1080p and requests chunk $K+1$ from the 480p playlist. Because chunk keyframes (I-frames) are strictly time-synchronized across all encoded bitrates, the player transitions instantly with zero audio crackle, visual freeze, or buffering spinners.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Explain the internal algorithmic mechanism of Adaptive Bitrate Streaming (ABR). How does a video player shift quality mid-playback without audio/video de-sync?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v8-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Master Manifest vs Media Manifest in HLS", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nHLS utilizes a two-tier manifest hierarchy:\\n\\n1. **Master Manifest (`master.m3u8`):** This is the entry point URL loaded by the client player. It contains metadata about all available video and audio streams (codecs, resolutions, aspect ratios, and target bitrates). It does NOT contain video segment chunk links. Instead, it links to child playlist URLs.\\n\\n2. **Variant / Media Manifest (e.g., `720p.m3u8`, `1080p.m3u8`):** Each media manifest corresponds to one specific encoding profile. It contains the ordered list of actual media segment file URLs (e.g., `segment_001.ts`, `segment_002.ts`) along with their exact durations (`#EXTINF:4.0`).\\n\\nThe client first fetches the Master Manifest once, selects an initial profile, fetches that profile's Media Manifest, and starts downloading chunk files sequentially.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Describe the hierarchical structure of HLS playlists. What is the difference between a Master Manifest (.m3u8) and a Variant/Media Manifest (.m3u8)?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v8-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Asynchronous Video Transcoding Ingestion Pipeline", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Pre-signed S3 Upload:** The client requests an upload URL from an API Gateway. The server returns a pre-signed S3 PUT URL, allowing the client to upload the 5GB raw video directly to an S3 Ingestion Bucket, bypassing application server memory.\\n2. **S3 Event Notification:** Upon upload completion, S3 triggers an event notification to an AWS SNS topic or SQS queue.\\n3. **Orchestration & Fan-Out:** An orchestrator service pulls the task and submits parallel jobs to a cluster of worker instances (e.g., AWS Batch / ECS running FFmpeg or AWS Elemental MediaConvert).\\n4. **Chunking & Transcoding:** Workers slice and encode the video into chunks (240p, 480p, 720p, 1080p, 4K) and generate the `.m3u8` playlist files.\\n5. **Destination Bucket & CDN:** The resulting segments and manifests are written to an S3 Origin Bucket fronted by Amazon CloudFront (CDN) with aggressive edge-caching configured for immutable `.ts` segment files.\\n6. **Callback:** The worker writes a completion status to the database, notifying the user via WebSockets/Push notification that processing is finished.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Design the backend architecture for a video upload and ingestion pipeline that converts a 4K raw video upload into HLS multi-bitrate streams without blocking web servers.", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v8-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Caching Video Segments at CDN Edge Locations", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Standard HTTP/HTTPS Delivery:** HLS and DASH run over standard HTTP port 80/443 using standard `GET` requests. They bypass strict enterprise firewalls and require no proprietary streaming server software on edge routers.\\n2. **Cacheability of Immutable Segments:** Every media segment (e.g., `segment_105.ts`) represents an immutable static file that never changes. CDNs can cache segments indefinitely at edge PoPs with high Cache-Hit Ratios (>98%). Once one viewer in a city watches a viral video chunk, subsequent viewers in that region receive it directly from the local edge cache with zero origin server traffic.\\n3. **RTMP Limitations:** Legacy RTMP relies on persistent, stateful TCP socket connections over non-standard port 1935. Standard edge caching proxies cannot cache RTMP streams, requiring expensive dedicated media streaming servers that fail to scale elastically.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why are chunked streaming protocols (HLS/DASH) inherently ideal for Content Delivery Networks (CDNs), while legacy protocols like RTMP are not?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	High Level Design	### 📌 1. Progressive Download vs. Streaming\n* **Progressive Download:** The browser downloads an entire monolithic `.mp4` file over HTTP.\n  * *Flaws:* High initial buffering latency, wasted server bandwidth if user stops watching after 10 seconds, and no adaptation to changing network bandwidth.\n* **True Streaming:** Media is chopped into short, discrete chunks (2–6 seconds each) downloaded dynamically as playback progresses.\n\n### 💡 2. Adaptive Bitrate Streaming (ABR)\n* **The Core Problem:** Mobile users move between fast Wi-Fi and slow cellular networks, while screens range from smartwatches to 4K TVs.\n* **ABR Solution:** Video files are pre-transcoded into multiple resolution and bitrate tiers (e.g., 240p, 360p, 720p, 1080p, 4K).\n* **Dynamic Player Adaptation:** The client video player continuously monitors buffer occupancy and download throughput. If bandwidth drops, it requests the next 3-second chunk in 480p instead of 1080p, preventing playback stalls and buffering spinners.\n\n### 💡 3. Protocols: HLS & MPEG-DASH\n* **HLS (HTTP Live Streaming by Apple):** Uses `.m3u8` playlist files and `.ts` / `.mp4` segment chunks. A Master Manifest lists available qualities; child manifests list chronological chunk URLs.\n* **MPEG-DASH:** Open international standard using XML-based Media Presentation Description (`.mpd`) manifests.	Piyush Garg	https://www.youtube.com/@piyushgarg195	28	How Video Streaming Works on Scale	Architecting high-scale video delivery pipelines using Adaptive Bitrate Streaming (ABR), HLS, MPEG-DASH, and chunked media transcoding.	31	Hard	High Level Design	8	[{"id": "hld-v8-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Progressive Download vs Streaming Bandwidth Wastage", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nIn Progressive Download, when a user clicks play, the client requests the whole file or aggressively buffers hundreds of megabytes ahead via HTTP byte-range requests.\\n\\nIf a user opens a 2-hour 1080p video (~2.5 GB) and clicks away after 15 seconds, the progressive player may have already downloaded 300 MB to 1 GB of data. Across hundreds of millions of users, paying cloud egress fees (e.g., AWS CloudFront / S3 data transfer out at $0.08/GB) on unwatched buffered da