e `.m3u8` playlist files.\\n5. **Destination Bucket & CDN:** The resulting segments and manifests are written to an S3 Origin Bucket fronted by Amazon CloudFront (CDN) with aggressive edge-caching configured for immutable `.ts` segment files.\\n6. **Callback:** The worker writes a completion status to the database, notifying the user via WebSockets/Push notification that processing is finished.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Design the backend architecture for a video upload and ingestion pipeline that converts a 4K raw video upload into HLS multi-bitrate streams without blocking web servers.", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v8-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Caching Video Segments at CDN Edge Locations", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Standard HTTP/HTTPS Delivery:** HLS and DASH run over standard HTTP port 80/443 using standard `GET` requests. They bypass strict enterprise firewalls and require no proprietary streaming server software on edge routers.\\n2. **Cacheability of Immutable Segments:** Every media segment (e.g., `segment_105.ts`) represents an immutable static file that never changes. CDNs can cache segments indefinitely at edge PoPs with high Cache-Hit Ratios (>98%). Once one viewer in a city watches a viral video chunk, subsequent viewers in that region receive it directly from the local edge cache with zero origin server traffic.\\n3. **RTMP Limitations:** Legacy RTMP relies on persistent, stateful TCP socket connections over non-standard port 1935. Standard edge caching proxies cannot cache RTMP streams, requiring expensive dedicated media streaming servers that fail to scale elastically.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why are chunked streaming protocols (HLS/DASH) inherently ideal for Content Delivery Networks (CDNs), while legacy protocols like RTMP are not?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
7672	Per-Transaction Cost Deduction: Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock With Transaction Fee	dsa-best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-with-transaction-fee	Deduct a fixed fee once per round-trip trade during sell state transitions in O(N) time and O(1) space.	2026-10-03 11:39:06.557726+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Per-Transaction Cost Deduction: Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock With Transaction Fee\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k4eK-vEmnKg", "title": "Lecture 220: Buy and Sell Stock With Transaction Fee (DP 40)", "duration": "15m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-with-transaction-fee/", "title": "Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock with Transaction Fee (LC 714)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return the maximum profit you can achieve with unlimited transactions where you pay 'fee' for each transaction."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/buy-stock-with-transaction-fee/1", "title": "Buy Stock with Transaction Fee", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Calculate maximum profit when every completed stock transaction incurs a fixed transaction fee."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-alternating-subsequence-sum/", "title": "Maximum Alternating Subsequence Sum (LC 1911)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Apply the exact same Buy/Sell state-machine DP to maximize alternating subsequence sums."}]	Module 32: Dynamic Programming on Stocks: State Machines, Transaction Caps, Cooldowns & Fees	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array `prices` of length `N` and a fixed integer `fee`, you may complete unlimited transactions, but you must pay **`fee` once for every completed transaction (Buy + Sell pair)**. Maximize your total profit.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The 1-Line Modification From Stock II (`Deduct fee Once per Transaction`):**\n  - Take the exact **Buy and Sell Stock II (DP 36)** code and subtract `fee` **either** when you **Sell** (`prices[ind] - fee + aheadBuy`) **or** when you **Buy** (`-prices[ind] - fee + aheadNotBuy`)!\n  - Never subtract `fee` on *both* buy and sell—a transaction fee is charged **once per round-trip trade**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N)` Time and `O(1)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Buy and Sell Stock With Transaction Fee (O(1) Space) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint maxProfitWithFee(vector<int> &prices, int fee) {\n    int n = prices.size();\n    int aheadNotBuy = 0, aheadBuy = 0;\n    int curNotBuy = 0, curBuy = 0;\n\n    for (int ind = n - 1; ind >= 0; ind--) {\n        // State 1: buy == 1 (allowed to buy)\n        curBuy = max(-prices[ind] + aheadNotBuy, 0 + aheadBuy);\n\n        // State 0: buy == 0 (deduct 'fee' upon completing the sale!)\n        curNotBuy = max(prices[ind] - fee + aheadBuy, 0 + aheadNotBuy);\n\n        aheadBuy = curBuy;\n        aheadNotBuy = curNotBuy;\n    }\n    return aheadBuy;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Subtracting `fee` on Buy When Using Top-Down Memoization With `INT_MIN`:** Subtracting `fee` when selling (`prices[ind] - fee + aheadBuy`) is safer against integer underflow than subtracting `fee` when buying (`-prices[ind] - fee`).	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
13277	Cumulative Waiting-Time Minimization: Shortest Job First (SJF) CPU Scheduling	dsa-shortest-job-first-sjf-cpu-scheduling-greedy	Master Cumulative Waiting-Time Minimization: Shortest Job First (SJF) CPU Scheduling with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:19:12.168715+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Cumulative Waiting-Time Minimization: Shortest Job First (SJF) CPU Scheduling\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3-QbX1iDbXs", "title": "Cumulative Waiting-Time Minimization: Shortest Job First (SJF) CPU Scheduling - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/shortest-job-first/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Shortest Job first", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given an array of burst times of N processes all arriving at time 0, calculate the average waiting time using Shortest Job First scheduling."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/single-threaded-cpu/", "title": "LeetCode 1834: Single-Threaded CPU", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Extend Shortest Job First scheduling to tasks with variable arrival times using a Min-Heap."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/average-waiting-time/", "title": "LeetCode 1701: Average Waiting Time", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Simulate cumulative timer and waiting time calculations across sequential tasks."}]	Module 41: Greedy Algorithms: Sorting Heuristics, Reachability Horizons & Interval Scheduling	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an array `bt` of `N` process execution (burst) times where **all processes arrive at time `0`**, compute the **floor of the average waiting time** across all `N` processes under the **Shortest Job First (SJF)** scheduling policy.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Executing the Shortest Jobs First Minimizes Total Waiting Time:**\n  - The execution time of the `1st` job gets added to the waiting time of **all `N - 1` subsequent jobs**!\n  - Therefore, to make the cumulative waiting time as small as possible, we must **sort `bt` in ascending order** and run the shortest jobs first!\n- **Tracking `t` (Current Clock Time) and `wtTime` (Total Waiting Time):**\n  1. Sort `bt` in ascending order (`O(N log N)`).\n  2. Initialize `t = 0` and `wtTime = 0`.\n  3. For each process `i` from `0` to `N - 1`:\n     - Process `i` starts at time `t`, so it waited `t` units of time: **`wtTime += t;`**\n     - Process `i` runs for `bt[i]` units, advancing the clock: **`t += bt[i];`**\n  4. Return **`wtTime / N`**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N log N + N)` Time and `O(1)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Shortest Job First (SJF) Blueprint\n```cpp\nlong long solveSJF(vector<int> &bt) {\n    int n = bt.size();\n    // 1. Sort burst times in ascending order so shortest jobs execute first\n    sort(bt.begin(), bt.end());\n\n    long long t = 0;      // Tracks current elapsed CPU clock time\n    long long wtTime = 0; // Tracks sum of waiting times of all processes\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        wtTime += t;  // Process i waited 't' units before starting\n        t += bt[i];   // Advance CPU clock by process i's burst time\n    }\n\n    return wtTime / n;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Swapping the Order of `wtTime += t` and `t += bt[i]`:** The very first job (`i = 0`) starts immediately at `t = 0` (`0` waiting time). If you add `t += bt[i]` *before* `wtTime += t`, you compute **turnaround time** (completion time) instead of **waiting time**!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
753	Rotation Count via Pivot Index: Finding How Many Times a Sorted Array Has Been Rotated	dsa-find-how-many-times-array-rotated	Find the rotation count of a rotated sorted array in O(log N) time by locating the index of the minimum element using sorted-half elimination.	2026-10-02 23:53:37.075624+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Rotation Count via Pivot Index: Finding How Many Times a Sorted Array Has Been Rotated\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jtSiWTPLwd0", "title": "Lecture 51: Find out how many times the array has been rotated", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/check-if-array-is-sorted-and-rotated/", "title": "Check if Array Is Sorted and Rotated", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Verify whether an array was originally sorted in non-decreasing order and then rotated."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-minimum-in-rotated-sorted-array/", "title": "Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the pivot minimum value in a rotated sorted array in O(log N) time."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/search-in-a-rotated-array4618/1", "title": "Search in a Rotated Array", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use sorted-half identification to locate a target index in a rotated array."}]	Module 12: 1D Binary Search, Bounds & Rotated Array Invariants	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a rotated sorted array of distinct numbers (for example, `[3, 4, 5, 1, 2]`), how do we find out **how many times** the array has been rotated in `O(log2 N)` time?\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Key Observation (Rotation Count == Index of Minimum Element):**\n  - In an unrotated sorted array `[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]`, the minimum element `1` sits at index `0` (rotated `0` times).\n  - In `[3, 4, 5, 1, 2]`, the minimum element `1` sits at index `3`—which means the array was rotated **3 times**!\n  - Therefore, finding how many times an array has been rotated is **identical to finding the index of its minimum element**.\n- **Optimal Binary Search (O(log2 N) Time, O(1) Space):**\n  - Maintain both `ans = INT_MAX` (smallest value seen) and `index = -1` (index of that smallest value).\n  - If the entire current range `[low ... high]` is already sorted (`arr[low] <= arr[high]`), check if `arr[low] < ans`. If so, update `ans = arr[low]`, `index = low`, and `break`.\n  - If the left half is sorted (`arr[low] <= arr[mid]`), compare `arr[low]` against `ans` (updating `ans` and `index = low` if smaller), then eliminate the left half (`low = mid + 1`).\n  - Otherwise, the right half is sorted: compare `arr[mid]` against `ans` (updating `ans` and `index = mid` if smaller), then eliminate the right half (`high = mid - 1`).\n\n### 💻 Rotation Count Blueprint\n```cpp\nint findKRotation(vector<int> &arr) {\n    int low = 0, high = (int)arr.size() - 1;\n    int ans = INT_MAX, index = -1;\n\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (arr[low] <= arr[high]) {\n            if (arr[low] < ans) {\n                ans = arr[low];\n                index = low;\n            }\n            break;\n        }\n        if (arr[low] <= arr[mid]) {\n            if (arr[low] < ans) {\n                ans = arr[low];\n                index = low;\n            }\n            low = mid + 1;\n        } else {\n            if (arr[mid] < ans) {\n                ans = arr[mid];\n                index = mid;\n            }\n            high = mid - 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return index;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
3435	2D Grid Component Traversal: Number of Islands (8-Directional & 4-Directional)	dsa-number-of-islands-connected-components-in-grid	Count distinct land components in 2D binary grids across 4-directional and 8-directional neighbor connection models.	2026-10-03 01:51:47.345399+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: 2D Grid Component Traversal: Number of Islands (8-Directional & 4-Directional)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=muncqlKJrH0", "title": "Lecture 133: Number of Islands | Number of Connected Components in Grid", "duration": "24m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-islands/", "title": "Number of Islands (LC 200)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count the number of 4-directionally connected land islands in an M x N binary grid."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/max-area-of-island/", "title": "Max Area of Island (LC 695)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Traverse each island component in a 2D grid and track the maximum cell count."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/find-the-number-of-islands/1", "title": "Find the number of islands", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count islands where land cells are connected horizontally, vertically, and diagonally (8 directions)."}]	Module 21: Graph Theory Foundations, Representations & Traversals	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an `N x M` grid of `'1'`s (land) and `'0'`s (water), count the total **Number of Islands**. Treat every `'1'` cell as a graph vertex connected to its neighboring `'1'` cells—either in **8 directions** (horizontal, vertical, and diagonal, as on GeeksforGeeks) or **4 directions** (as on LeetCode).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Grid as an Implicit Graph:**\n  - Scan every cell `(r, c)` in the `N x M` grid.\n  - Whenever you find an unvisited land cell (`grid[r][c] == '1' && !seen[r][c]`), increment `islands++` and launch a BFS/DFS from `(r, c)` to mark all connected land cells in that island.\n- **8-Directional Offset Shortcut:**\n  - Instead of writing 8 separate `if` statements, loop `dr` from `-1` to `+1` and `dc` from `-1` to `+1` to generate all 8 neighbors `(r + dr, c + dc)` cleanly!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N * M)` Time and `O(N * M)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Number of Islands (8-Directional BFS) Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid exploreIslandBFS(int startR, int startC, const vector<vector<char>> &grid, vector<vector<int>> &seen) {\n    int rows = grid.size(), cols = grid[0].size();\n    queue<pair<int, int>> q;\n    q.push({startR, startC});\n    seen[startR][startC] = 1;\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        auto [r, c] = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n\n        // Traverse all 8 surrounding directions\n        for (int dr = -1; dr <= 1; dr++) {\n            for (int dc = -1; dc <= 1; dc++) {\n                int nr = r + dr, nc = c + dc;\n                if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols &&\n                    grid[nr][nc] == '1' && !seen[nr][nc]) {\n                    seen[nr][nc] = 1;\n                    q.push({nr, nc});\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nint numIslands(vector<vector<char>> &grid) {\n    int rows = grid.size(), cols = grid[0].size(), islands = 0;\n    vector<vector<int>> seen(rows, vector<int>(cols, 0));\n\n    for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {\n            if (grid[r][c] == '1' && !seen[r][c]) {\n                islands++;\n                exploreIslandBFS(r, c, grid, seen);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return islands;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Confusing 4-Directional vs. 8-Directional Rules:** Always read the problem statement carefully—LeetCode 200 only connects 4 cardinal neighbors (dr = {-1, 0, 1, 0}, dc = {0, 1, 0, -1}), whereas GFG connects all 8 neighbors.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
