"platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Verify whether a binary tree satisfies the height-balanced invariant."}]	Module 15: Binary Trees: Hierarchical Representation & Fundamental Traversals	### 📌 Topic Overview\nUnlike linear data structures (arrays, stacks, queues, linked lists), a **Tree** is a hierarchical data structure. A **Binary Tree** is a tree in which every node has **at most 2 children** (0, 1, or 2 children).\n\n### 💡 Core Terminology\n- **Root:** The topmost starting node of the tree.\n- **Children & Parent:** Nodes directly connected beneath a node are its children; the node above is their parent.\n- **Leaf Node:** A node at the bottom that has **0 children**.\n- **Subtree:** Any node along with all of its descendants beneath it.\n- **Ancestors:** A node's parent, grandparent, and all nodes along the path up to the root.\n\n### 🌲 The 5 Structural Types of Binary Trees\n1. **Full Binary Tree:** Every node has either **0 or 2 children** (never a single child).\n2. **Complete Binary Tree:** All levels are completely filled except possibly the last level, and all nodes in the last level are placed **as far left as possible**.\n3. **Perfect Binary Tree:** All internal nodes have 2 children and **all leaf nodes are at the exact same level**.\n4. **Balanced Binary Tree:** The height of the tree is at most **`O(log2 N)`** for `N` nodes.\n5. **Degenerate (Skewed) Tree:** Every internal node has only 1 child, reducing the tree to a straight line like a Linked List (`O(N)` height).\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Confusing Complete vs. Full Trees:** A tree with a single left child on the last level is a *Complete* Binary Tree, but it is **not** a *Full* Binary Tree (since that parent has 1 child instead of 0 or 2).	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
1960	Delimiter Tokenization & Stream Deserialization: Serialize and Deserialize a Binary Tree	dsa-serialize-and-deserialize-binary-tree	Design level-order serialization and stream-based deserialization algorithms to encode and decode binary trees in O(N) time.	2026-10-03 01:18:07.175485+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Delimiter Tokenization & Stream Deserialization: Serialize and Deserialize a Binary Tree\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-YbXySKJsX8", "title": "Lecture 108: Serialize and De-serialize Binary Tree", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/serialize-and-deserialize-binary-tree/", "title": "Serialize and Deserialize Binary Tree", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Design an algorithm to serialize a binary tree to a string and deserialize it back."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/serialize-and-deserialize-bst/", "title": "Serialize and Deserialize BST", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Optimize tree serialization using BST key boundary properties."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/serialize-and-deserialize-a-binary-tree/1", "title": "Serialize and deserialize a binary tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Convert a tree into an array of integers and reconstruct it back."}]	Module 19: Binary Trees: Threaded Binary Trees & Morris Traversal	### 📌 Topic Overview\nDesign an algorithm to **serialize** a binary tree into a single string, and **deserialize** that string back into the identical original binary tree.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **1. Serialization via Level-Order BFS:**\n  - Run standard BFS queue traversal, appending node values separated by commas (`,`).\n  - When encountering a `nullptr` child, append a special null symbol (e.g., `'#'` or `'null'`).\n  - Example string: `"1,2,3,#,#,4,5,#,#,#,#,"`.\n- **2. Deserialization via `stringstream` + `getline`:**\n  - Wrap the string in `stringstream s(data)`.\n  - Read the first token using `getline(s, str, ',')` and allocate `root = new TreeNode(stoi(str))`.\n  - Push `root` to queue `q`.\n  - While `!q.empty()`:\n    - Pop `curr = q.front()`.\n    - Read next token for left child: if `str != "#"`, create `curr->left` and push to `q`.\n    - Read next token for right child: if `str != "#"`, create `curr->right` and push to `q`.\n- **Complexity:** `O(N)` Time and `O(N)` Space for both operations.\n\n### 💻 Serialize & Deserialize Blueprint\n```cpp\nclass Codec {\npublic:\n    string serialize(TreeNode* root) {\n        if (!root) return "";\n        string s = "";\n        queue<TreeNode*> q;\n        q.push(root);\n\n        while (!q.empty()) {\n            TreeNode* curr = q.front();\n            q.pop();\n            if (curr == nullptr) {\n                s += "#,";\n            } else {\n                s += to_string(curr->val) + ",";\n                q.push(curr->left);\n                q.push(curr->right);\n            }\n        }\n        return s;\n    }\n\n    TreeNode* deserialize(string data) {\n        if (data.empty()) return nullptr;\n        stringstream s(data);\n        string str;\n        getline(s, str, ',');\n        TreeNode* root = new TreeNode(stoi(str));\n        queue<TreeNode*> q;\n        q.push(root);\n\n        while (!q.empty()) {\n            TreeNode* curr = q.front();\n            q.pop();\n\n            // Left child\n            getline(s, str, ',');\n            if (str != "#") {\n                curr->left = new TreeNode(stoi(str));\n                q.push(curr->left);\n            }\n\n            // Right child\n            getline(s, str, ',');\n            if (str != "#") {\n                curr->right = new TreeNode(stoi(str));\n                q.push(curr->right);\n            }\n        }\n        return root;\n    }\n};\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
