rg/problems/boolean-matrix-problem-1587115620/1", "title": "Boolean Matrix", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Modify a matrix in-place so that if a cell is 1, its entire row and column become 1."}]	Module 10: Array Permutations, Sequence Hashing & In-Place Matrix Manipulation	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an `N x M` matrix, whenever you find an original `0` at cell `(i, j)`, you must set its **entire row `i`** and **entire column `j`** to `0` in-place.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Better → Optimal)\n- **1. Brute Force (O((N*M)*(N+M)) Time, O(1) Space):**\n  - You cannot immediately turn neighbors into `0` while scanning, or those new `0`s will wipe out the whole matrix! Instead, mark affected non-zero cells as `-1` first, then convert all `-1`s to `0` in a second pass.\n- **2. Better Approach (O(2 * N * M) Time, O(N + M) Space):**\n  - Create two marker arrays: `row[N] = {0}` and `col[M] = {0}`.\n  - Pass 1: Whenever `matrix[i][j] == 0`, mark `row[i] = 1` and `col[j] = 1`.\n  - Pass 2: Set `matrix[i][j] = 0` if `row[i] == 1` or `col[j] == 1`.\n- **3. Optimal In-Place Marker Approach (O(2 * N * M) Time, O(1) Extra Space):**\n  - Instead of external `row` and `col` arrays, use the **first column** (`matrix[i][0]`) as your row marker and the **first row** (`matrix[0][j]`) as your column marker!\n  - Because cell `matrix[0][0]` overlaps both, use `matrix[0][0]` for row `0` and keep one separate integer variable `col0 = 1` to track column `0`.\n  - **Crucial Update Order:** First update the inner submatrix (`i = 1..n-1`, `j = 1..m-1`), then update the first row (`if matrix[0][0] == 0`), and finally update the first column (`if col0 == 0`) so you don't overwrite your markers too early!\n\n### 💻 Optimal O(1) Space Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid setZeroes(vector<vector<int>> &matrix) {\n    int n = matrix.size(), m = matrix[0].size();\n    int col0 = 1;\n\n    // 1. Mark first row and first column\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {\n            if (matrix[i][j] == 0) {\n                matrix[i][0] = 0;\n                if (j != 0) matrix[0][j] = 0;\n                else col0 = 0;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    // 2. Update inner cells (1 to n-1, 1 to m-1)\n    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n        for (int j = 1; j < m; j++) {\n            if (matrix[i][0] == 0 || matrix[0][j] == 0) {\n                matrix[i][j] = 0;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    // 3. Update first row, then first column\n    if (matrix[0][0] == 0) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) matrix[0][j] = 0;\n    }\n    if (col0 == 0) {\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) matrix[i][0] = 0;\n    }\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
14169	LIFO & FIFO Architecture: Implement Stack and Queue Using Arrays, Linked Lists, and Cross-Structures	dsa-introduction-to-stack-and-queue-implementations	Master LIFO & FIFO Architecture: Implement Stack and Queue Using Arrays, Linked Lists, and Cross-Structures with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:24:23.651729+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: LIFO & FIFO Architecture: Implement Stack and Queue Using Arrays, Linked Lists, and Cross-Structures\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tqQ5fTamIN4", "title": "LIFO & FIFO Architecture: Implement Stack and Queue Using Arrays, Linked Lists, and Cross-Structures - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/implement-stack-using-queues/", "title": "LeetCode 225: Implement Stack using Queues", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Implement a last-in-first-out (LIFO) stack using only a single queue by rotating existing elements on push."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/implement-queue-using-stacks/", "title": "LeetCode 232: Implement Queue using Stacks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Implement a first-in-first-out (FIFO) queue using two stacks in amortized O(1) time per operation."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/implement-queue-using-linked-list/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Implement Queue using Linked List", "difficulty": "Basic", "description": "Implement a Queue using a singly linked list with start and end pointers in O(1) time."}]	Module 42: Stack & Queue Foundations: Custom Data Structure Implementations, Expression Conversions & Min Stack	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMaster the core abstractions and all **6 classic interview implementations** of **Stack (LIFO: Last-In-First-Out)** and **Queue (FIFO: First-In-First-Out)**:\n1. **Stack using Array:** Maintain `top = -1`. `push(x)` increments `arr[++top] = x`; `pop()` decrements `top--`. (`O(1)` time, fixed capacity).\n2. **Queue using Circular Array:** Maintain `start = -1, end = -1, currSize = 0`. Advance pointers modulo `maxSize`: **`end = (end + 1) % maxSize`** and **`start = (start + 1) % maxSize`**. (`O(1)` time, fixed capacity).\n3. **Stack using Linked List:** Insert and delete at the **Head (`top`)** (`newNode->next = top; top = newNode;`) so both `push` and `pop` run in `O(1)` time with dynamic sizing!\n4. **Queue using Linked List:** Maintain both **`start` (head)** for `O(1)` `pop()` and **`end` (tail)** for `O(1)` `push()`.\n5. **Stack using 1 Single Queue (`O(N)` Push, `O(1)` Pop/Top):**\n   - Grab `s = q.size()`, push `x` into `q`, and then **rotate the previous `s` elements to the back** (`for (int i = 0; i < s; i++) { q.push(q.front()); q.pop(); }`).\n6. **Queue using 2 Stacks (Amortized `O(1)` Push/Pop/Top):**\n   - Maintain an **input stack `s1`** and an **output stack `s2`**.\n   - `push(x)`: Always push onto `s1` (`O(1)`).\n   - `pop()` / `top()`: If `s2` is non-empty, pop/read directly from `s2.top()` (`O(1)`). Only when **`s2` is empty**, pour all elements from `s1` into `s2`!\n\n### 💻 Stack Using 1 Queue & Queue Using 2 Stacks (Amortized O(1)) Blueprint\n```cpp\n// 1. Implement Stack Using a Single Queue (LeetCode 225)\nclass MyStack {\n    queue<int> q;\npublic:\n    void push(int x) {\n        int s = q.size();\n        q.push(x);\n        // Rotate the previous 's' elements behind the newly pushed element 'x'\n        for (int i = 0; i < s; i++) {\n            q.push(q.front());\n            q.pop();\n        }\n    }\n    int pop() {\n        int n = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        return n;\n    }\n    int top() { return q.front(); }\n    bool empty() { return q.empty(); }\n};\n\n// 2. Implement Queue Using Two Stacks in Amortized O(1) Time (LeetCode 232)\nclass MyQueue {\n    stack<int> s1, s2;\npublic:\n    void push(int x) {\n        s1.push(x); // O(1) push into input stack\n    }\n    int pop() {\n        if (s2.empty()) {\n            while (!s1.empty()) {\n                s2.push(s1.top());\n                s1.pop();\n            }\n        }\n        int val = s2.top();\n        s2.pop();\n        return val;\n    }\n    int peek() {\n        if (s2.empty()) {\n            while (!s1.empty()) {\n                s2.push(s1.top());\n                s1.pop();\n            }\n        }\n        return s2.top();\n    }\n    bool empty() { return s1.empty() && s2.empty(); }\n};\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Pouring `s1` Into `s2` When `s2` Is NOT Empty:** In the 2-stack Queue, if `s2` still has elements from an earlier transfer, pouring `s1` on top of `s2` buries the older elements underneath the newer ones! Always transfer `s1 -> s2` **only when `s2.empty()` is true**.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
2299	In-Order Sorted Count Extraction: K-th Smallest and Largest Element in a BST	dsa-kth-smallest-and-largest-element-in-bst	Extract the k-th smallest and largest values in a BST in O(min(K, N)) time using in-order and reverse in-order early-exit counters.	2026-10-03 01:26:48.305637+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: In-Order Sorted Count Extraction: K-th Smallest and Largest Element in a BST\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9TJYWh0adfk", "title": "Lecture 117: Find K-th Smallest/Largest element in BST", "duration": "22m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/kth-smallest-element-in-a-bst/", "title": "Kth Smallest Element in a BST", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the kth smallest value in a BST using in-order traversal."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/kth-largest-element-in-bst/1", "title": "Kth largest element in BST", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Find the kth largest element in a BST using reverse in-order (Right-Root-Left)."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/convert-bst-to-greater-tree/", "title": "Convert BST to Greater Tree", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use reverse in-order traversal to accumulate suffixes into nodes."}]	Module 19: Binary Search Trees: Concepts, Validations & Structural Mutations	### 📌 Topic Overview\nFind the **k-th smallest** (and **k-th largest**) element in a Binary Search Tree without copying all elements into an array.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Inorder Traversal Invariant:**\n  - Standard Inorder (`Left → Root → Right`) visits elements in **strictly ascending order**.\n  - The `k`-th node visited during Inorder is guaranteed to be the **k-th smallest element**!\n- **Early-Exit Counter:**\n  - Maintain `cnt = 0` and `ans = -1`.\n  - Traverse left.\n  - Increment `cnt++`. If `cnt == k`, record `ans = node->val` and return immediately.\n  - Traverse right.\n- **K-th Largest via Reverse Inorder (`Right → Root → Left`):**\n  - Visiting `Right → Root → Left` traverses the BST in **descending order**.\n  - The `k`-th node visited is the **k-th largest element**! (Alternatively, find the `(N - k + 1)`-th smallest element).\n- **Complexity:** `O(min(K, N))` Time and `O(H)` Space (or `O(1)` space using Morris Inorder).\n\n### 💻 K-th Smallest in BST Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid inorder(TreeNode* root, int &cnt, int k, int &ans) {\n    if (root == nullptr || cnt >= k) return;\n\n    inorder(root->left, cnt, k, ans);\n\n    cnt++;\n    if (cnt == k) {\n        ans = root->val;\n        return;\n    }\n\n    inorder(root->right, cnt, k, ans);\n}\n\nint kthSmallest(TreeNode* root, int k) {\n    int cnt = 0, ans = -1;\n    inorder(root, cnt, k, ans);\n    return ans;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Passing cnt by Value:** Pass `cnt` by reference (`int &cnt`), otherwise recursive branches retain stale counts.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
14171	Operator Precedence & Expression Parsing: Infix, Prefix, and Postfix Conversions	dsa-prefix-infix-and-postfix-conversions-stack	Master Operator Precedence & Expression Parsing: Infix, Prefix, and Postfix Conversions with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:24:23.655044+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Operator Precedence & Expression Parsing: Infix, Prefix, and Postfix Conversions\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4pIc9UBHJtk", "title": "Operator Precedence & Expression Parsing: Infix, Prefix, and Postfix Conversions - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/infix-to-postfix-1587115620/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Infix to Postfix", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given an infix expression in the form of string s, convert this infix expression to a postfix expression."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/evaluate-reverse-polish-notation/", "title": "LeetCode 150: Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Evaluate the value of an arithmetic expression in Reverse Polish Notation (Postfix) using a stack."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/postfix-to-infix-conversion/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Postfix to Infix Conversion", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Convert a valid postfix expression back into a fully parenthesized infix expression using a stack of strings."}]	Module 42: Stack & Queue Foundations: Custom Data Structure Implementations, Expression Conversions & Min Stack	### 📌 Topic Overview\nUnderstand **Operator Priority** (`'^'` = `3`, `'*'` and `'/'` = `2`, `'+'` and `'-'` = `1`, others = `-1`) and master all **6 classic expression conversions** between **Infix** (`a + b`), **Prefix** (`+ab`), and **Postfix / Reverse Polish Notation** (`ab+`):\n\n### 💡 Summary of All 6 Conversion Algorithms (O(N) Time, O(N) Space)\n1. **Infix → Postfix:**\n   - Scan **Left to Right**:\n     - **Operand (`a-z, A-Z, 0-9`):** Append directly to `ans`.\n     - **`'('`:** Push onto `st`.\n     - **`')'`:** Pop and append to `ans` until `'('` is at `st.top()`, then pop `'('`.\n     - **Operator `s[i]`:** While `!st.empty() && priority(s[i]) <= priority(st.top())`, pop and append `st.top()` to `ans`; then push `s[i]`.\n   - Finally, pop all remaining operators from `st` into `ans`.\n2. **Infix → Prefix (3-Step Trick):**\n   - **Step 1:** Reverse the infix string AND swap every `'('` with `')'`.\n   - **Step 2:** Run *Controlled Infix-to-Postfix* on the reversed string: for `'^'`, pop while `priority(s[i]) <= priority(st.top())`; for all other operators (`*, /, +, -`), pop only while **strictly smaller** (`priority(s[i]) < priority(st.top())`)!\n   - **Step 3:** Reverse the resulting string to get the Prefix expression!\n3. **Postfix → Infix:**\n   - Scan **Left to Right** with `stack<string> st`. Push operands. On operator `op`, pop `t1 = st.top()`, then `t2 = st.top()`, and push **`"(" + t2 + op + t1 + ")"`** (`t2` goes **first**!).\n4. **Prefix → Infix:**\n   - Scan **Right to Left** (`N - 1` down to `0`) with `stack<string> st`. Push operands. On operator `op`, pop `t1 = st.top()`, then `t2 = st.top()`, and push **`"(" + t1 + op + t2 + ")"`** (`t1` goes **first**!).\n5. **Postfix → Prefix:**\n   - Scan **Left to Right** with `stack<string> st`. Push operands. On operator `op`, pop `t1`, then `t2`, and push **`op + t2 + t1`**.\n6. **Prefix → Postfix:**\n   - Scan **Right to Left** (`N - 1` down to `0`) with `stack<string> st`. Push operands. On operator `op`, pop `t1`, then `t2`, and push **`t1 + t2 + op`**.\n\n### 💻 Infix to Postfix & Postfix to Infix Blueprint\n```cpp\nint priority(char c) {\n    if (c == '^') return 3;\n    if (c == '*' || c == '/') return 2;\n    if (c == '+' || c == '-') return 1;\n    return -1;\n}\n\n// 1. Infix to Postfix Conversion: O(N) Time, O(N) Space\nstring infixToPostfix(string s) {\n    stack<char> st;\n    string ans = "";\n\n    for (char c : s) {\n        if ((c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') || (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z') || (c >= '0' && c <= '9')) {\n            ans += c;\n        } else if (c == '(') {\n            st.push('(');\n        } else if (c == ')') {\n            while (!st.empty() && st.top() != '(') {\n                ans += st.top();\n                st.pop();\n            }\n            if (!st.empty()) st.pop(); // Pop the '('\n        } else {\n            while (!st.empty() && priority(c) <= priority(st.top())) {\n                ans += st.top();\n                st.pop();\n            }\n            st.push(c);\n        }\n    }\n    while (!st.empty()) {\n        ans += st.top();\n        st.pop();\n    }\n    return ans;\n}\n\n// 2. Postfix to Infix Conversion: O(N) Time, O(N) Space\nstring postfixToInfix(string s) {\n    stack<string> st;\n    for (char c : s) {\n        if (isalnum(c)) {\n            st.push(string(1, c));\n        } else {\n            string t1 = st.top(); st.pop();\n            string t2 = st.top(); st.pop();\n            // In Postfix (Left-to-Right), second popped element t2 is the LEFT operand!\n            st.push("(" + t2 + c + t1 + ")");\n        }\n    }\n    return st.top();\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Confusing `t1` and `t2` Operand Order in Postfix vs. Prefix:**\n  - When scanning **Left to Right (Postfix)**, the deeper element `t2` was pushed *earlier*, so **`t2` is the left operand** (`t2 op t1`).\n  - When scanning **Right to Left (Prefix)**, the top element `t1` was pushed *most recently* from the left, so **`t1` is the left operand** (`t1 op t2`)!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22211	Master Rate Limiting	hld-rate-limiting-algorithms	Deep dive into API security and traffic shaping using Token Bucket, Leaky Bucket, Fixed Window, and Sliding Window Rate Limiting algorithms.	2026-10-07 16:33:42.32487+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Master Rate Limiting\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/CVItTb_jdkE", "title": "Master Rate Limiting - System Design", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "hld-v6-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Purpose of Rate Limiting", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA Rate Limiter regulates traffic ingress at the API Gateway layer:\\n1. **DDoS & Brute-Force Prevention:** Stops bots from spamming authentication endpoints or scraping data.\\n2. **Resource Starvation Protection:** Prevents buggy client apps from exhausting thread pools and DB connections.\\n3. **Monetization & Quotas:** Enforces tiered commercial API contracts (e.g., Free Tier: 60 req/min; Enterprise: 10,000 req/min).\\n4. **Noisy Neighbor Isolation:** Prevents one tenant from degrading performance for all other tenants.\\n\\nWhen a quota is breached, the gateway drops the request and returns **HTTP 429: Too Many Requests**.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Explain the primary functions of a Rate Limiter in a distributed architecture. What HTTP status code is universally returned upon limit violation?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v6-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Token Bucket Algorithm", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Mechanism:** Each user has a logical bucket holding up to capacity $C$ tokens. A background process continuously adds tokens at a fixed rate of $r$ tokens/second. When an API call arrives:\\n* If tokens $\\\\ge 1$, one token is consumed and the request passes.\\n* If tokens $= 0$, the request is dropped with HTTP 429.\\n\\n**Why Stripe/Amazon Use It:** It naturally accommodates burst traffic. If a user is idle, their bucket fills to max capacity (e.g., 10 tokens). If they suddenly fire 10 simultaneous requests (a legitimate app burst), all 10 succeed instantly without artificial delay. Once drained, they are capped at the replenishment rate $r$, offering high responsiveness with safety.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "How does the Token Bucket algorithm function? Why do platforms like Amazon and Stripe prefer it for API traffic shaping?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v6-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Leaky Bucket vs Token Bucket", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWhile **Token Bucket** limits the input volume while permitting bursts, **Leaky Bucket** strictly enforces a smooth, constant output processing rate.\\n\\n**Leaky Bucket Mechanism:** Requests enter a fixed-size FIFO queue. A worker pulls and processes requests from the bottom of the queue at an unvarying rate (e.g., exactly 5 requests/sec). Any request arriving when the queue is full overflows and is dropped.\\n\\n**When to Choose Leaky Bucket:** Choose it when downstream systems (such as legacy databases or third-party payment gateways) cannot tolerate traffic spikes and crash under sudden concurrency bursts. Leaky Bucket smooths erratic bursts into a predictable steady-state stream.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Contrast Leaky Bucket with Token Bucket. In what specific architectural scenario is Leaky Bucket explicitly preferred?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v6-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: The Fixed Window Edge-Case Bug", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Fixed Window Mechanism:** Time is partitioned into rigid slots (e.g., 12:00:00–12:00:59). A counter tracks calls within the slot and resets to 0 at the slot boundary.\\n\\n**The Edge-Case Exploit:**\\nIf the limit is 100 requests per minute:\\n1. A client sends 100 requests at `12:00:59` (end of Window 1). All 100 are permitted.\\n2. At `12:01:00`, the window resets to 0.\\n3. The client immediately sends another 100 requests at `12:01:01` (start of Window 2). All 100 are permitted.\\n\\n**The Result:** The backend processed 200 requests in a 2-second rolling window (`12:00:59 to 12:01:01`), delivering 2× the allowed throughput and risking an overload.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Explain the mathematical edge-case flaw of the Fixed Window Counter algorithm that allows users to bypass configured limits.", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v6-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Sliding Window Log vs Sliding Window Counter", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**1. Sliding Window Log:** Records the exact UNIX timestamp of every request in a Redis Sorted Set. Upon a new request, it evicts timestamps older than 1 minute and counts the remaining elements.\\n* *Flaw:* Massive memory overhead. Storing thousands of timestamps per user across millions of users exhausts Redis RAM.\\n\\n**2. Sliding Window Counter (Hybrid):** Tracks only two numbers: previous window request count and current window request count. It estimates rolling traffic via time-weighted interpolation:\\n`Estimated Requests = (Previous Window Count × Overlap %) + Current Window Count`.\\n\\n*Why Counter is Preferred:* It prevents the fixed-window boundary burst exploit with 99%+ accuracy while requiring only two numeric variables in memory per user, making it orders of magnitude more scalable than timestamp logging.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Compare Sliding Window Log and Sliding Window Counter (Hybrid). Why is the Counter preferred in high-scale distributed systems?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	High Level Design	### 📌 1. What is Rate Limiting?\n* A defensive layer (typically deployed at the API Gateway) restricting the number of requests a client/IP can make in a given time window.\n* **Goals:** Mitigate DDoS/brute-force attacks, protect internal resources from cascading failure, and enforce API tier billing.\n* **HTTP Response:** Exceeded quotas return **HTTP 429: Too Many Requests**.\n\n### 💡 2. Core Rate Limiting Algorithms\n* **Token Bucket:** Tokens are refilled into a bucket of size `C` at rate `r`. Requests consume a token. Empty bucket → HTTP 429.\n  * *Pros:* Allows short bursts of traffic up to bucket capacity. Highly memory efficient.\n* **Leaky Bucket:** Requests enter a FIFO queue and leak out to the server at a constant rate. Excess requests spill over and are dropped.\n  * *Pros:* Completely smooths bursty traffic into a predictable outflow rate. Great for fragile legacy databases.\n* **Fixed Window Counter:** Counts requests within fixed time blocks (e.g., 1:00:00 to 1:00:59).\n  * *Flaw:* Boundary burst exploit: 100 requests at 1:00:59 and 100 at 1:01:00 allows 200 requests in a 2-second window.\n\n### 💡 3. Advanced Algorithms\n* **Sliding Window Log:** Stores exact request timestamps in Redis Sorted Sets. Evicts timestamps older than the window window before counting.\n  * *Pros:* 100% accurate. *Cons:* High memory footprint.\n* **Sliding Window Counter (Hybrid):** Approximates request density by combining the previous window's count with the current window's count using fractional time overlap. Memory-efficient and eliminates boundary burst bugs.	Piyush Garg	https://www.youtube.com/@piyushgarg195	28	Master Rate Limiting	Deep dive into API security and traffic shaping using Token Bucket, Leaky Bucket, Fixed Window, and Sliding Window Rate Limiting algorithms.	41	Hard	High Level Design	6	[{"id": "hld-v6-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Purpose of Rate Limiting", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nA Rate Limiter regulates traffic ingress at the API Gateway layer:\\n1. **DDoS & Brute-Force Prevention:** Stops bots from spamming authentication endpoints or scraping data.\\n2. **Resource Starvation Protection:** Prevents buggy client apps from exhausting thread pools and DB connections.\\n3. **Monetization & Quotas:** Enforces tiered commercial API contracts (e.g., Free Tier: 60 req/min; Enterprise: 10,000 req/min).\\n4. **Noisy Neighbor Isolation:** Prevents one tenant from degrading performance for all other tenants.\\n\\nWhen a quota is breached, the gateway drops the request and returns **HTTP 429: Too Many Requests**.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Explain the primary functions of a Rate Limiter in a distributed architecture. What HTTP status code is universally returned upon limit violation?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v6-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Token Bucket Algorithm", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Mechanism:** Each user has a logical bucket holding up to capacity $C$ tokens. A background process continuously adds tokens at a fixed rate of $r$ tokens/second. When an API call arrives:\\n* If tokens $\\\\ge 1$, one token is consumed and the request passes.\\n* If tokens $= 0$, the request is dropped with HTTP 429.\\n\\n**Why Stripe/Amazon Use It:** It naturally accommodates burst traffic. If a user is idle, their bucket fills to max capacity (e.g., 10 tokens). If they suddenly fire 10 simultaneous requests (a legitimate app burst), all 10 succeed instantly without artificial delay. Once drained, they are capped at the replenishment rate $r$, offering high responsiveness with safety.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "How does the Token Bucket algorithm function? Why do platforms like Amazon and Stripe prefer it for API traffic shaping?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v6-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Leaky Bucket vs Token Bucket", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nWhile **Token Bucket** limits the input volume while permitting bursts, **Leaky Bucket** strictly enforces a smooth, constant output processing rate.\\n\\n**Leaky Bucket Mechanism:** Requests enter a fixed-size FIFO queue. A worker pulls and processes requests from the bottom of the queue at an unvarying rate (e.g., exactly 5 requests/sec). Any request arriving when the queue is full overflows and is dropped.\\n\\n**When to Choose Leaky Bucket:** Choose it when downstream systems (such as legacy databases or third-party payment gateways) cannot tolerate traffic spikes and crash under sudden concurrency bursts. Leaky Bucket smooths erratic bursts into a predictable steady-state stream.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Contrast Leaky Bucket with Token Bucket. In what specific architectural scenario is Leaky Bucket explicitly preferred?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v6-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: The Fixed Window Edge-Case Bug", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Fixed Window Mechanism:** Time is partitioned into rigid slots (e.g., 12:00:00–12:00:59). A counter tracks calls within the slot and resets to 0 at the slot boundary.\\n\\n**The Edge-Case Exploit:**\\nIf the limit is 100 requests per minute:\\n1. A client sends 100 requests at `12:00:59` (end of Window 1). All 100 are permitted.\\n2. At `12:01:00`, the window resets to 0.\\n3. The client immediately sends another 100 requests at `12:01:01` (start of Window 2). All 100 are permitted.\\n\\n**The Result:** The backend processed 200 requests in a 2-second rolling window (`12:00:59 to 12:01:01`), delivering 2× the allowed throughput and risking an overload.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Explain the mathematical edge-case flaw of the Fixed Window Counter algorithm that allows users to bypass configured limits.", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v6-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Sliding Window Log vs Sliding Window Counter", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**1. Sliding Window Log:** Records the exact UNIX timestamp of every request in a Redis Sorted Set. Upon a new request, it evicts timestamps older than 1 minute and counts the remaining elements.\\n* *Flaw:* Massive memory overhead. Storing thousands of timestamps per user across millions of users exhausts Redis RAM.\\n\\n**2. Sliding Window Counter (Hybrid):** Tracks only two numbers: previous window request count and current window request count. It estimates rolling traffic via time-weighted interpolation:\\n`Estimated Requests = (Previous Window Count × Overlap %) + Current Window Count`.\\n\\n*Why Counter is Preferred:* It prevents the fixed-window boundary burst exploit with 99%+ accuracy while requiring only two numeric variables in memory per user, making it orders of magnitude more scalable than timestamp logging.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Compare Sliding Window Log and Sliding Window Counter (Hybrid). Why is the Counter preferred in high-scale distributed systems?", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22225	SOLID Principles: Rules of LSP, ISP, and DIP	lld-solid-principles-part-2	Deep dive into formal contract rules: LSP Signature/Property/Method rules (Pre/Post conditions), Interface Segregation (ISP), and Dependency Inversion (DIP).	2026-10-07 16:56:30.769208+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: SOLID Principles: Rules of LSP, ISP, and DIP\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/hU9koy6A2I0", "title": "SOLID Design Principles | part 2", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "lld-v6-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Pre-conditions and Post-conditions in LSP", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Pre-conditions (Input Requirements):**\\n   * Base class `User::setPassword(string pwd)` requires `pwd.length >= 8`.\\n   * If derived class `AdminUser` strengthens pre-conditions to require `pwd.length >= 12` plus special symbols, client code that successfully passed 8-character passwords to `User` will crash when passed an `AdminUser`.\\n   * *Rule:* Derived classes can weaken pre-conditions (accept more inputs), but can NEVER strengthen them.\\n\\n2. **Post-conditions (Output Guarantees):**\\n   * Base class `Car::brake()` guarantees that vehicle speed decreases.\\n   * If derived class `HybridCar::brake()` decreases speed AND regenerates battery, it strengthens post-conditions (valid!). But if it fails to decrease speed, it weakens post-conditions, breaking client safety expectations.\\n   * *Rule:* Derived classes can strengthen post-conditions (guarantee more), but can NEVER weaken them.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain why a derived class cannot strengthen pre-conditions or weaken post-conditions under the Liskov Substitution Principle using an authentication example.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v6-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Return Type Co-variance in Derived Methods", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Return Type Co-variance:**\\nWhen overriding a virtual function, the derived class method may return a pointer or reference to a class derived from the base method's return type:\\n```cpp\\nclass Animal {};\\nclass Dog : public Animal {};\\n\\nclass Breeder {\\n    virtual Animal* breed() { return new Animal(); }\\n};\\nclass DogBreeder : public Breeder {\\n    Dog* breed() override { return new Dog(); } // Co-variant return!\\n};\\n```\\n**Why it Preserves LSP:**\\nA client expecting an `Animal*` from `Breeder::breed()` receives a `Dog*`. Because `Dog` is an `Animal`, any operation valid on `Animal` is 100% valid on `Dog`. The contract is fully honored without type casting.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is Return Type Co-variance in C++? Why does allowing a derived method to return a more specialized (derived) pointer preserve LSP?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v6-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) and Fat Interfaces", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**A Fat Interface:** An interface with too many methods attempting to serve multiple distinct client use cases.\\n\\n**The Violation:**\\n```cpp\\nclass IMachine {\\n    virtual void print() = 0;\\n    virtual void scan() = 0;\\n    virtual void fax() = 0;\\n};\\n```\\nIf you build a budget `BasicPrinter`, it only has print hardware. Under `IMachine`, it is forced to implement `scan()` and `fax()` by throwing `UnsupportedOperationException`.\\n\\n**Ripple Effect:** If the office updates the `fax()` protocol in `IMachine`, `BasicPrinter` is forced to recompile, re-deploy, and risk bugs despite having zero fax hardware.\\n\\n**The ISP Fix:** Split into role interfaces: `IPrinter`, `IScanner`, `IFax`. Basic printers implement `IPrinter`; multi-function office printers implement all three via multiple interface inheritance.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is a \\"Fat Interface\\"? How does a Fat Interface violating ISP in a printer system (Print, Scan, Fax) cause ripple-effect maintenance issues for basic printers?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v6-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) in High-Level Modules", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Flaw of Direct Instantiation:**\\nWhen `UserService` calls `new MySqlDatabase()`, high-level policy is held hostage by low-level database mechanics. If the database schema changes, connection strings change, or the company migrates to PostgreSQL, core user business logic must be edited.\\n\\n**The Inverted Control Flow:**\\n1. High-level module defines the abstraction it needs: `IDatabaseRepository`.\\n2. Low-level module implements this abstraction: `MySqlRepository : public IDatabaseRepository`.\\n3. The dependency arrow is inverted: instead of high-level depending down on low-level, **both modules depend upon the abstract interface**.\\n4. `UserService` receives `IDatabaseRepository` via Dependency Injection at runtime. It remains 100% immune to database infrastructure changes.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain why high-level business logic modules (e.g., UserService) must never directly instantiate low-level infrastructure modules (e.g., MySqlDatabase). Detail the inverted control flow.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v6-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: History Constraint Violation in LSP", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The History Constraint:**\\nA derived class cannot introduce state mutations that would violate the immutable history rules established by the base class. If a base class specifies that a property is immutable once created, a derived class cannot make it mutable.\\n\\n**Violation Scenario:**\\n```cpp\\nclass ImmutablePoint {\\nprotected:\\n    const int x, y;\\npublic:\\n    ImmutablePoint(int x, int y) : x(x), y(y) {}\\n    int getX() const { return x; }\\n    int getY() const { return y; }\\n};\\n\\nclass MutablePoint : public ImmutablePoint {\\npublic:\\n    // Violates History Constraint by introducing mutation methods!\\n    void setX(int newX) { const_cast<int&>(x) = newX; }\\n};\\n```\\nClient code relying on `ImmutablePoint` caches coordinates assuming they can never change over time. If a `MutablePoint` is substituted, coordinates mutate underneath the client, corrupting spatial hash maps and thread-safety invariants.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Define the \\"History Constraint\\" in Liskov Substitution Principle. Provide a code scenario where a subclass violates the history constraint without modifying method signatures.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	Low Level Design	### 📌 1. Formal Rules of Liskov Substitution (LSP)\n* **Signature Rules:**\n  * **Method Arguments:** Contravariant or equal in derived class.\n  * **Return Types (Co-variance):** Derived methods can return a narrower (derived) type, but never a broader (ancestor) type.\n  * **Exceptions:** Derived methods can throw narrower/sub-type exceptions, but never broader unexpected exceptions.\n* **Property Rules:** Derived classes must preserve **Class Invariants** (e.g., balance cannot be negative) and **History Constraints** (cannot make immutable states mutable).\n* **Method Rules:**\n  * **Pre-conditions cannot be strengthened** in derived classes (cannot demand stricter inputs).\n  * **Post-conditions cannot be weakened** in derived classes (cannot deliver fewer guarantees).\n\n### 💡 2. Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)\n* **Rule:** "Clients should not be forced to depend upon interfaces that they do not use."\n* **The Fat Interface Flaw:** A single `Shape` interface containing `area()` and `volume()`. `Square` and `Circle` are 2D shapes forced to dummy-implement or throw errors on `volume()`.\n* **Solution:** Segregate into `2DShape` and `3DShape` interfaces.\n\n### 💡 3. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)\n* **Rule:** High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions.\n* **The Core Formula:** "If OCP is the goal, DIP is the solution."	Coder Army	https://www.youtube.com/@CoderArmy	28	SOLID Principles: Rules of LSP, ISP, and DIP	Deep dive into formal contract rules: LSP Signature/Property/Method rules (Pre/Post conditions), Interface Segregation (ISP), and Dependency Inversion (DIP).	77	Hard	Low Level Design	20	[{"id": "lld-v6-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Pre-conditions and Post-conditions in LSP", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Pre-conditions (Input Requirements):**\\n   * Base class `User::setPassword(string pwd)` requires `pwd.length >= 8`.\\n   * If derived class `AdminUser` strengthens pre-conditions to require `pwd.length >= 12` plus special symbols, client code that successfully passed 8-character passwords to `User` will crash when passed an `AdminUser`.\\n   * *Rule:* Derived classes can weaken pre-conditions (accept more inputs), but can NEVER strengthen them.\\n\\n2. **Post-conditions (Output Guarantees):**\\n   * Base class `Car::brake()` guarantees that vehicle speed decreases.\\n   * If derived class `HybridCar::brake()` decreases speed AND regenerates battery, it strengthens post-conditions (valid!). But if it fails to decrease speed, it weakens post-conditions, breaking client safety expectations.\\n   * *Rule:* Derived classes can strengthen post-conditions (guarantee more), but can NEVER weaken them.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain why a derived class cannot strengthen pre-conditions or weaken post-conditions under the Liskov Substitution Principle using an authentication example.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "lld-v6-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: Return Type Co-variance in Derived Methods", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Return Type Co-variance:**\\nWhen overriding a virtual function, the derived class method may return a pointer or reference to a class derived from the base method's return type:\\n```cpp\\nclass Animal {};\\nclass Dog : public Animal {};\\n\\nclass Breeder {\\n    virtual Animal* breed() { return new Animal(); }\\n};\\nclass DogBreeder : public Breeder {\\n    Dog* breed() override { return new Dog(); } // Co-variant return!\\n};\\n```\\n**Why it Preserves LSP:**\\nA client expecting an `Animal*` from `Breeder::breed()` receives a `Dog*`. Because `Dog` is an `Animal`, any operation valid on `Animal` is 100% valid on `Dog`. The contract is fully honored without type casting.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is Return Type Co-variance in C++? Why does allowing a derived method to return a more specialized (derived) pointer preserve LSP?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "lld-v6-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) and Fat Interfaces", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**A Fat Interface:** An interface with too many methods attempting to serve multiple distinct client use cases.\\n\\n**The Violation:**\\n```cpp\\nclass IMachine {\\n    virtual void print() = 0;\\n    virtual void scan() = 0;\\n    virtual void fax() = 0;\\n};\\n```\\nIf you build a budget `BasicPrinter`, it only has print hardware. Under `IMachine`, it is forced to implement `scan()` and `fax()` by throwing `UnsupportedOperationException`.\\n\\n**Ripple Effect:** If the office updates the `fax()` protocol in `IMachine`, `BasicPrinter` is forced to recompile, re-deploy, and risk bugs despite having zero fax hardware.\\n\\n**The ISP Fix:** Split into role interfaces: `IPrinter`, `IScanner`, `IFax`. Basic printers implement `IPrinter`; multi-function office printers implement all three via multiple interface inheritance.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "What is a \\"Fat Interface\\"? How does a Fat Interface violating ISP in a printer system (Print, Scan, Fax) cause ripple-effect maintenance issues for basic printers?", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "lld-v6-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) in High-Level Modules", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Flaw of Direct Instantiation:**\\nWhen `UserService` calls `new MySqlDatabase()`, high-level policy is held hostage by low-level database mechanics. If the database schema changes, connection strings change, or the company migrates to PostgreSQL, core user business logic must be edited.\\n\\n**The Inverted Control Flow:**\\n1. High-level module defines the abstraction it needs: `IDatabaseRepository`.\\n2. Low-level module implements this abstraction: `MySqlRepository : public IDatabaseRepository`.\\n3. The dependency arrow is inverted: instead of high-level depending down on low-level, **both modules depend upon the abstract interface**.\\n4. `UserService` receives `IDatabaseRepository` via Dependency Injection at runtime. It remains 100% immune to database infrastructure changes.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Explain why high-level business logic modules (e.g., UserService) must never directly instantiate low-level infrastructure modules (e.g., MySqlDatabase). Detail the inverted control flow.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "lld-v6-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: History Constraint Violation in LSP", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The History Constraint:**\\nA derived class cannot introduce state mutations that would violate the immutable history rules established by the base class. If a base class specifies that a property is immutable once created, a derived class cannot make it mutable.\\n\\n**Violation Scenario:**\\n```cpp\\nclass ImmutablePoint {\\nprotected:\\n    const int x, y;\\npublic:\\n    ImmutablePoint(int x, int y) : x(x), y(y) {}\\n    int getX() const { return x; }\\n    int getY() const { return y; }\\n};\\n\\nclass MutablePoint : public ImmutablePoint {\\npublic:\\n    // Violates History Constraint by introducing mutation methods!\\n    void setX(int newX) { const_cast<int&>(x) = newX; }\\n};\\n```\\nClient code relying on `ImmutablePoint` caches coordinates assuming they can never change over time. If a `MutablePoint` is substituted, coordinates mutate underneath the client, corrupting spatial hash maps and thread-safety invariants.", "category": "Low Level Design", "question": "Define the \\"History Constraint\\" in Liskov Substitution Principle. Provide a code scenario where a subclass violates the history constraint without modifying method signatures.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
