3874	2-Coloring Wavefront Validation: Bipartite Graph Check Using BFS	dsa-bipartite-graph-check-bfs	Determine if an undirected graph is bipartite by validating alternating 2-color BFS waves across odd/even cycle parities.	2026-10-03 02:09:36.721393+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: 2-Coloring Wavefront Validation: Bipartite Graph Check Using BFS\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-vu34sct1g8", "title": "Lecture 142: Bipartite Graph Check using BFS", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/is-graph-bipartite/", "title": "Is Graph Bipartite? (LC 785)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Return true if and only if an undirected graph can be partitioned into two independent sets."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/possible-bipartition/", "title": "Possible Bipartition (LC 886)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Model dislike pairs as edges and check if people can be split into two groups using 2-coloring."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/bipartite-graph/1", "title": "Bipartite Graph", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Check whether a given undirected graph is bipartite using BFS 2-coloring."}]	Module 23: Graph Coloring, Directed Cycle Detection & Topological Sort (DAGs)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nA **Bipartite Graph** is a graph whose vertices can be colored using **at most 2 colors** (say `0` and `1`) such that **no two adjacent vertices share the same color**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Cycle Parity Rule (The Core Theorem):**\n  - Any **acyclic (linear/tree) graph** is always bipartite.\n  - Any graph with only **even-length cycles** is always bipartite.\n  - Any graph containing an **odd-length cycle** can **NEVER** be bipartite!\n- **BFS 2-Coloring Algorithm (`O(V + 2E)` Time, `O(V)` Space):**\n  1. Initialize a `color` array of size `V` with `-1` (meaning uncolored).\n  2. Loop `i` from `0` to `V - 1` to cover all disconnected components.\n  3. If `color[i] == -1`, assign `color[i] = 0` and push `i` into a BFS queue.\n  4. When popping `curr`, inspect each `neighbor`:\n     - If `color[neighbor] == -1`: color it with the **opposite color** (`!color[curr]`) and push to queue.\n     - Else if `color[neighbor] == color[curr]`: an adjacent node already has the exact same color—return `false`!\n\n### 💻 Bipartite Graph Check (BFS) Blueprint\n```cpp\nbool checkComponentBFS(int startNode, const vector<vector<int>> &adjList, vector<int> &color) {\n    queue<int> q;\n    q.push(startNode);\n    color[startNode] = 0;\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int curr = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n\n        for (int neighbor : adjList[curr]) {\n            if (color[neighbor] == -1) {\n                color[neighbor] = !color[curr]; // Assign opposite color (0 -> 1, 1 -> 0)\n                q.push(neighbor);\n            } else if (color[neighbor] == color[curr]) {\n                return false; // Conflict: two adjacent nodes have the same color\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n\nbool isBipartite(vector<vector<int>> &graph) {\n    int V = graph.size();\n    vector<int> color(V, -1);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {\n        if (color[i] == -1) {\n            if (!checkComponentBFS(i, graph, color)) return false;\n        }\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Only Checking From Node 0:** A disconnected graph may have an even cycle in Component 1 and an odd cycle in Component 2. Always wrap your check in a `0` to `V - 1` loop!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
