geeks.org/problems/nodes-at-given-distance-in-binary-tree/1", "title": "Nodes at given distance in binary tree", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find all nodes at distance K from a target node in a binary tree."}]	Module 18: Binary Trees: Node Distances, Tree Modifications & Reconstruction	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven the root of a binary tree, a `target` node, and an integer `k`, return an array of all node values located **exactly distance `k`** away from the target node.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Core Problem with Binary Trees:**\n  - Finding nodes distance `k` *downward* is trivial, but nodes can also lie *upward* through ancestors and across sister subtrees.\n  - Since standard binary tree nodes only point to `left` and `right`, we cannot traverse upward without a parent link.\n- **Two-Step Solution (`O(N)` Time, `O(N)` Space):**\n  - **Step 1: Map Parent Pointers:**\n    - Run a BFS or DFS starting from `root`. Store each node's parent in `unordered_map<TreeNode*, TreeNode*> parentTrack`.\n  - **Step 2: Radial BFS from Target:**\n    - Initialize `queue<TreeNode*> q` with `target` and mark `target` in `unordered_map<TreeNode*, bool> visited`.\n    - Run BFS in level-by-level radial bursts (`currDist = 0` to `k`).\n    - At each node, explore in 3 directions: `left`, `right`, and `parentTrack[curr]`.\n    - Push unvisited neighbors to `q` and mark them visited.\n    - When `currDist == k`, stop! All nodes remaining in the queue are at distance `k`.\n\n### 💻 Nodes at Distance K Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid markParents(TreeNode* root, unordered_map<TreeNode*, TreeNode*> &parent_track) {\n    queue<TreeNode*> q;\n    q.push(root);\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        TreeNode* current = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        if (current->left) {\n            parent_track[current->left] = current;\n            q.push(current->left);\n        }\n        if (current->right) {\n            parent_track[current->right] = current;\n            q.push(current->right);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nvector<int> distanceK(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* target, int k) {\n    unordered_map<TreeNode*, TreeNode*> parent_track;\n    markParents(root, parent_track);\n\n    unordered_map<TreeNode*, bool> visited;\n    queue<TreeNode*> q;\n    q.push(target);\n    visited[target] = true;\n    int curr_level = 0;\n\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int size = q.size();\n        if (curr_level == k) break;\n        curr_level++;\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n            TreeNode* current = q.front();\n            q.pop();\n\n            if (current->left && !visited[current->left]) {\n                visited[current->left] = true;\n                q.push(current->left);\n            }\n            if (current->right && !visited[current->right]) {\n                visited[current->right] = true;\n                q.push(current->right);\n            }\n            if (parent_track[current] && !visited[parent_track[current]]) {\n                visited[parent_track[current]] = true;\n                q.push(parent_track[current]);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    vector<int> result;\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        result.push_back(q.front()->val);\n        q.pop();\n    }\n    return result;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
5251	Finish-Time Ordering & Edge Reversal: Strongly Connected Components (Kosaraju's Algorithm)	dsa-strongly-connected-components-kosarajus-algorithm	Find all Strongly Connected Components in directed graphs using post-order finish time sorting, graph transposing, and stack-ordered DFS.	2026-10-03 11:10:35.806658+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Finish-Time Ordering & Edge Reversal: Strongly Connected Components (Kosaraju's Algorithm)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R6uoSjZ2imo", "title": "Lecture 179: Strongly Connected Components (Kosaraju's Algorithm)", "duration": "23m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/strongly-connected-components-kosarajus-algo/1", "title": "Strongly Connected Components (Kosaraju's Algo)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the number of Strongly Connected Components in a directed graph using Kosaraju's Algorithm."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-employees-to-be-invited-to-a-meeting/", "title": "Maximum Employees to Be Invited to a Meeting (LC 2127)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Analyze directed cycles (strongly connected components) and chains in a directed graph."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-number-of-days-to-disconnect-island/", "title": "Minimum Number of Days to Disconnect Island (LC 1568)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Explore graph connectivity and component separation properties."}]	Module 27: Strongly Connected Components (Kosaraju's) & Tarjan's Bridge Detection	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn a **Directed Graph**, a **Strongly Connected Component (SCC)** is a maximal subset of vertices where **every vertex in the subset is reachable from every other vertex in the subset** (`u` can reach `v` AND `v` can reach `u`). **Kosaraju's Algorithm** finds all SCCs in **`O(V + E)`** time!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Reversing All Edges Isolates SCCs:**\n  - If you condense each SCC into a single super-node, a directed graph looks like a chain of SCCs: `SCC1 → SCC2 → SCC3`.\n  - If you run a normal DFS starting in `SCC1`, it leaks across the bridge edge `SCC1 → SCC2` and visits the whole graph at once!\n  - **The Genius of Kosaraju's Algorithm:** If we **reverse all edges** (`SCC1 ← SCC2 ← SCC3`), every internal cycle inside `SCC1` remains a valid cycle, **but** the outgoing bridge edge from `SCC1` to `SCC2` is flipped backward (`SCC1 ← SCC2`), **trapping the DFS strictly inside `SCC1`**!\n- **The 3 Steps of Kosaraju's Algorithm (`O(V + E)` Time, `O(V + E)` Space):**\n  1. **Step 1 (Sort Nodes by Finishing Time):** Run a standard post-order DFS across all vertices `0 .. V - 1` and push each node onto `stack<int> st` when its DFS finishes (so `SCC1` nodes sit at the top of the stack).\n  2. **Step 2 (Reverse the Graph):** Build `adjT` where every directed edge `u → v` becomes `v → u`.\n  3. **Step 3 (DFS in Stack Order on `adjT`):** Pop nodes from `st` one by one. Whenever a popped node `u` is unvisited, increment `sccCount++` and run `dfs3(u, adjT, vis)` to mark its entire isolated SCC!\n\n### 💻 Kosaraju's Algorithm (SCC) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nvoid dfsFinishOrder(int u, const vector<vector<int>> &adj, vector<int> &vis, stack<int> &st) {\n    vis[u] = 1;\n    for (int v : adj[u]) {\n        if (!vis[v]) dfsFinishOrder(v, adj, vis, st);\n    }\n    st.push(u);\n}\n\nvoid dfsReversed(int u, const vector<vector<int>> &adjT, vector<int> &vis) {\n    vis[u] = 1;\n    for (int v : adjT[u]) {\n        if (!vis[v]) dfsReversed(v, adjT, vis);\n    }\n}\n\nint kosarajuSCC(int V, vector<vector<int>> &adj) {\n    // Step 1: Order vertices by finish time in stack\n    vector<int> vis(V, 0);\n    stack<int> st;\n    for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {\n        if (!vis[i]) dfsFinishOrder(i, adj, vis, st);\n    }\n\n    // Step 2: Reverse all directed edges (Transpose Graph)\n    vector<vector<int>> adjT(V);\n    for (int u = 0; u < V; u++) {\n        vis[u] = 0; // Reset vis array for Step 3\n        for (int v : adj[u]) {\n            adjT[v].push_back(u);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Step 3: Process vertices in stack order on the reversed graph\n    int sccCount = 0;\n    while (!st.empty()) {\n        int u = st.top();\n        st.pop();\n        if (!vis[u]) {\n            sccCount++;\n            dfsReversed(u, adjT, vis);\n        }\n    }\n    return sccCount;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Skipping Step 1 (Running DFS on `adjT` in Arbitrary Order `0 .. V - 1`):** If you start DFS on the reversed graph from `SCC3` instead of `SCC1`, the reversed edge `SCC1 ← SCC2 ← SCC3` will allow `SCC3` to leak backward into `SCC2` and `SCC1`! You **must** process `adjT` in the exact finishing-time stack order from Step 1.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
