l": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-the-city-with-the-smallest-number-of-neighbors-at-a-threshold-distance/", "title": "Find the City With the Smallest Number of Neighbors at a Threshold Distance (LC 1334)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Practice shortest path distance calculations across weighted graphs."}]	Module 24: Shortest Path Algorithms in Graphs: DAG Relaxation, BFS & Dijkstra's Algorithm	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a weighted **Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG)** with `N` vertices and `M` weighted edges `[u, v, wt]`, find the **shortest distance** from source vertex `0` to every other vertex (or `-1` if a vertex is unreachable).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Topological Sort Beats Dijkstra on a DAG (`O(N + M)` vs `O(M log N)`):**\n  - Because a DAG has **no cycles**, if we process vertices in **Topological Order**, by the time we arrive at vertex `u`, **every possible incoming path to `u` has already been relaxed**!\n  - That means `dist[u]` is already finalized, and we only need to relax `u`'s outgoing edges once!\n- **Two-Step Algorithm (`O(N + M)` Time, `O(N + M)` Space):**\n  1. **Step 1 (Topological Sort):** Build the weighted adjacency list `adj[u].push_back({v, wt})` and perform DFS Topological Sort to populate `stack<int> st`.\n  2. **Step 2 (Relax Edges in Stack Order):**\n     - Initialize `dist[N]` with `1e9` (`infinity`), and set `dist[src] = 0`.\n     - Pop vertices `u` one by one from `st`. If `dist[u] != 1e9`, relax all outgoing edges `(v, wt)`:\n       **`if (dist[u] + wt < dist[v]) dist[v] = dist[u] + wt;`**.\n     - Replace any remaining `1e9` values with `-1`.\n\n### 💻 Shortest Path in DAG Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid topoSortDFS(int u, const vector<pair<int, int>> adj[], vector<int> &seen, stack<int> &st) {\n    seen[u] = 1;\n    for (auto [v, wt] : adj[u]) {\n        if (!seen[v]) {\n            topoSortDFS(v, adj, seen, st);\n        }\n    }\n    st.push(u);\n}\n\nvector<int> shortestPathInDAG(int N, int M, vector<vector<int>> &edges) {\n    vector<pair<int, int>> adj[N];\n    for (const auto &e : edges) {\n        adj[e[0]].push_back({e[1], e[2]});\n    }\n\n    vector<int> seen(N, 0);\n    stack<int> st;\n    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {\n        if (!seen[i]) topoSortDFS(i, adj, seen, st);\n    }\n\n    vector<int> dist(N, 1e9);\n    dist[0] = 0; // Source node is 0\n\n    while (!st.empty()) {\n        int u = st.top();\n        st.pop();\n\n        if (dist[u] != 1e9) {\n            for (auto [v, wt] : adj[u]) {\n                if (dist[u] + wt < dist[v]) {\n                    dist[v] = dist[u] + wt;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {\n        if (dist[i] == 1e9) dist[i] = -1;\n    }\n    return dist;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Using `INT_MAX` Without Checking Reachability:** If `dist[u] == INT_MAX`, computing `dist[u] + wt` overflows a 32-bit signed integer into a negative number! Use `1e9` as infinity and only relax outgoing edges when `dist[u] != 1e9`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
5245	Ultimate-Parent Counting: Number of Provinces Using Disjoint Set	dsa-number-of-provinces-disjoint-set	Count total connected graph components by linking adjacent vertices in DSU and counting nodes that serve as their own ultimate parent.	2026-10-03 11:10:35.771976+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Ultimate-Parent Counting: Number of Provinces Using Disjoint Set\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZGr5nX-Gi6Y", "title": "Lecture 173: Number of Provinces Using Disjoint Set", "duration": "16m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-provinces/", "title": "Number of Provinces (LC 547)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count the total number of provinces using Disjoint Set Union and ultimate-parent counting."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/number-of-provinces/1", "title": "Number of Provinces", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Find the number of connected components in an adjacency matrix using DSU."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-connected-components-in-an-undirected-graph/", "title": "Number of Connected Components in an Undirected Graph (LC 323)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Union all given edges and count the remaining distinct root parents."}]	Module 26: Disjoint Set Union (DSU), Kruskal's MST & Dynamic Connectivity	### 📌 Topic Overview\nHow do we count the **Number of Provinces (Connected Components)** using a **Disjoint Set** instead of BFS/DFS? After performing `unionBySize(i, j)` for every edge in the graph, each connected component has **exactly one unique ultimate parent (boss node)** where **`ds.parent[i] == i`**!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Counting Components via Boss Nodes:**\n  1. Initialize `DisjointSet ds(V)`.\n  2. Loop over the adjacency matrix: whenever `adj[i][j] == 1`, call `ds.unionBySize(i, j)`.\n  3. Loop `i` from `0` to `V - 1`: if **`ds.findUPar(i) == i`** (or `ds.parent[i] == i`), increment `cnt++`!\n- **Complexity:** `O(V²)` Time (to scan the `V x V` input matrix) and `O(V)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Number of Provinces (DSU) Blueprint\n~~~cpp\nint numProvincesDSU(vector<vector<int>> &adj, int V) {\n    DisjointSet ds(V);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < V; j++) {\n            if (adj[i][j] == 1) {\n                ds.unionBySize(i, j);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    int provinces = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {\n        // Count how many nodes are their own ultimate parent (component root)\n        if (ds.findUPar(i) == i) {\n            provinces++;\n        }\n    }\n    return provinces;\n}\n~~~\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Counting Distinct Values in `parent[i]` Without Calling `findUPar(i)`:** Unless you check `ds.findUPar(i) == i` (which simply checks if `i` is a root node where `parent[i] == i`), un-compressed intermediate parents in `parent[]` could mislead you if you try inserting `parent[i]` into a set! Checking `ds.findUPar(i) == i` is always safe and `O(1)`.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/