13274	Exact-Cardinality Subtraction (atMost(k) - atMost(k - 1)): Subarrays With K Different Integers	dsa-subarrays-with-k-different-integers-sliding-window	Master Exact-Cardinality Subtraction (atMost(k) - atMost(k - 1)): Subarrays With K Different Integers with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:19:12.165308+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Exact-Cardinality Subtraction (atMost(k) - atMost(k - 1)): Subarrays With K Different Integers\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7wYGbV_LsX4", "title": "Exact-Cardinality Subtraction (atMost(k) - atMost(k - 1)): Subarrays With K Different Integers - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/subarrays-with-k-different-integers/", "title": "LeetCode 992: Subarrays with K Different Integers", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Given an integer array nums and an integer k, return the number of good subarrays of nums that contain exactly k different integers."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-sum-of-distinct-subarrays-with-length-k/", "title": "LeetCode 2461: Maximum Sum of Distinct Subarrays With Length K", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Maintain a frequency map over a sliding window to enforce distinct element counts."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/subarrays-with-k-different-integers/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Subarrays with K Different Integers", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Find the number of subarrays with exactly K distinct elements using the two-pointer subtraction trick."}]	Module 40: Sliding Window & Two-Pointer Algorithms: Fixed Windows, Variable Windows & Exact-Count Reductions	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an integer array `nums` and an integer `k`, return the **number of contiguous subarrays** where the number of **different (distinct) integers** in the subarray is **exactly `k`**.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute O(N²) → Optimal O(2N) Subtraction)\n- **Why Standard Single-Pass Sliding Window Misses Valid Subarrays for `== k`:**\n  - When `[l ... r]` has `k` distinct integers and `r` moves right to another duplicate of an existing number, shrinking `l` would miss subarrays with the new `r`, while advancing `r` without shrinking `l` misses shorter valid subarrays inside `[l ... r]`!\n- **The Monotonic Reduction (`atMostK(nums, k) - atMostK(nums, k - 1)`):**\n  - If a window `[l ... r]` has **at most `k` distinct integers** (`mpp.size() <= k`), then **every single sub-window ending at `r` (`[l ... r], [l + 1 ... r], ..., [r ... r]`) ALSO has at most `k` distinct integers**!\n  - That gives us **`r - l + 1` valid subarrays ending at `r`** in one step!\n  - Subtracting the count of subarrays with `<= k - 1` distinct integers from the count of subarrays with `<= k` distinct integers leaves **only subarrays with EXACTLY `k` distinct integers**:\n    **`ans = atMostK(nums, k) - atMostK(nums, k - 1)`**!\n- **Complexity:** **`O(2 * 2N) = O(N)`** Time and **`O(N)`** Space for the frequency hash map.\n\n### 💻 Subarrays With K Different Integers Blueprint\n```cpp\nint countAtMostKDistinct(const vector<int> &nums, int k) {\n    if (k <= 0) return 0;\n\n    int l = 0, r = 0, cnt = 0;\n    unordered_map<int, int> mpp;\n\n    while (r < nums.size()) {\n        mpp[nums[r]]++;\n\n        // Shrink from left until distinct count is <= k\n        while (mpp.size() > k) {\n            mpp[nums[l]]--;\n            if (mpp[nums[l]] == 0) {\n                mpp.erase(nums[l]);\n            }\n            l++;\n        }\n\n        // All (r - l + 1) subarrays ending at r within [l .. r] have <= k distinct elements!\n        cnt += (r - l + 1);\n        r++;\n    }\n    return cnt;\n}\n\nint subarraysWithKDistinct(vector<int> &nums, int k) {\n    return countAtMostKDistinct(nums, k) - countAtMostKDistinct(nums, k - 1);\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Using `if (mpp.size() > k)` Instead of `while (mpp.size() > k)` in Counting Problems:** The single-step `if` trick is **only for Longest Subarray (`maxLen`)** problems! When **counting** subarrays via `cnt += (r - l + 1)`, the window `[l ... r]` must be 100% valid on every iteration, so you **must use `while (mpp.size() > k)`**!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
