or or output.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Functions used to fetch data from an API.", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "What are Props in React?", "explanation": "Props (short for properties) are how components talk to each other. A parent component passes data to a child component as props. Props are immutable within the child component.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "Read-only inputs passed from a parent component to a child component to configure its behavior or output."}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": [{"text": "Bottom-up (from child to parent)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Unidirectional, top-down (from parent to child)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bidirectional (parent to child and child to parent simultaneously)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Randomly, based on component rendering order", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "How does data flow in a standard React application?", "explanation": "React enforces a unidirectional data flow. State is often lifted up to a common ancestor, which then passes it down to child components via props.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "Unidirectional, top-down (from parent to child)"}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": [{"text": "To provide a unique CSS class name for styling.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To help React identify which items have changed, are added, or are removed, optimizing rendering performance.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "To encrypt the data being rendered.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To define the index of the array element.", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "What is the purpose of the `key` prop when rendering a list of elements in React using `.map()`?", "explanation": "Keys give elements a stable identity. React uses keys to match children in the original tree with children in the subsequent tree, making DOM updates efficient.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "To help React identify which items have changed, are added, or are removed, optimizing rendering performance."}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": [{"text": "A method to fetch data from an external API.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A hook that allows functional components to have state variables.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A component lifecycle method.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A CSS framework for managing UI states.", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "What is `useState` in React?", "explanation": "`useState` is a React Hook that lets you add a state variable to your component. It returns an array with two values: the current state and a function to update it.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "A hook that allows functional components to have state variables."}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": [{"text": "It instantly updates the variable, but the UI updates only on page reload.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It schedules a state update and triggers a re-render of the component to reflect the new state in the UI.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It directly modifies the real DOM.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It updates the state but does not cause a re-render.", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "How does the `useState` setter function behave regarding UI updates?", "explanation": "Calling the setter function returned by `useState` tells React that the component's state has changed, which queues a re-render of that component and its children.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "It schedules a state update and triggers a re-render of the component to reflect the new state in the UI."}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": [{"text": "Because `console.log` is broken in React.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Because state updates in React are asynchronous; the state variable is updated on the next render cycle.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Because the new value was rejected by React.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Because you must use `await` before `console.log`.", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "Why might `console.log(stateVariable)` immediately after calling `setStateVariable(newValue)` print the old value?", "explanation": "React batches state updates for performance. The setter function schedules an update, but the value of the state variable in the current execution context remains unchanged until the next render.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "Because state updates in React are asynchronous; the state variable is updated on the next render cycle."}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": [{"text": "The input updates the DOM directly without React's knowledge.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "React state dictates the input's `value`, and the input's `onChange` event updates the React state.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Data flows from the backend to the frontend and vice-versa simultaneously.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Using two different state variables for one input.", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "What is 'Two-Way Data Binding' in the context of React forms (Controlled Components)?", "explanation": "In controlled components, the form data is handled by the React component. The input's `value` is tied to a state variable, and an `onChange` handler updates that state, keeping them in sync.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "React state dictates the input's `value`, and the input's `onChange` event updates the React state."}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": [{"text": "By returning `false` from the component.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "By calling `event.preventDefault()` inside the `onSubmit` handler.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "By using the `<PreventReload>` tag.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "React automatically prevents default form submissions.", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "How do you prevent the default page reload behavior when a form is submitted in React?", "explanation": "Forms in HTML default to navigating to a new page on submit. In React, you use the standard DOM method `event.preventDefault()` within the event handler to stop this behavior.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "By calling `event.preventDefault()` inside the `onSubmit` handler."}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": [{"text": "To declare CSS styles for a component.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To perform side effects in functional components, such as data fetching, subscriptions, or manually changing the DOM.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "To manage complex state logic with multiple sub-values.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To create reusable UI components.", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "What is the primary use case for the `useEffect` hook?", "explanation": "`useEffect` lets you synchronize a component with an external system. It serves the same purpose as `componentDidMount`, `componentDidUpdate`, and `componentWillUnmount` in React class components.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "To perform side effects in functional components, such as data fetching, subscriptions, or manually changing the DOM."}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": [{"text": "It causes the effect to run after every single render.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It causes the effect to run only once, immediately after the initial render (component mount).", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It prevents the effect from ever running.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It tells React to run the effect only when the component unmounts.", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "What does the dependency array `[]` as the second argument in `useEffect` do?", "explanation": "An empty dependency array means the effect does not depend on any state or props, so it only needs to execute once when the component mounts.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "It causes the effect to run only once, immediately after the initial render (component mount)."}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": [{"text": "Only when the component mounts.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Every time the component re-renders, regardless of what changed.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "After the initial render, and subsequently every time the value of `count` changes.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only when the component unmounts.", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "If a state variable `count` is included in the `useEffect` dependency array `[count]`, when will the effect run?", "explanation": "React compares the current value of dependencies with their value on the previous render. If any value in the array has changed (like `count`), the effect is re-run.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "After the initial render, and subsequently every time the value of `count` changes."}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": [{"text": "react-navigation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "react-router-dom", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "express.js", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "react-routes", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "What library is commonly used for performing routing in a React application?", "explanation": "`react-router-dom` is the standard routing library for React web applications, enabling navigation between different components without full page reloads, creating a Single Page Application (SPA) experience.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "react-router-dom"}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": [{"text": "To open external links in a new browser tab.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To wrap the application and enable client-side routing using the HTML5 history API.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "To manage HTTP requests to the backend server.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To render components conditionally based on browser type.", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "What is the purpose of the `<BrowserRouter>` component in `react-router-dom`?", "explanation": "`BrowserRouter` is a router implementation that uses the HTML5 history API (`pushState`, `replaceState` and the `popstate` event) to keep your UI in sync with the URL.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "To wrap the application and enable client-side routing using the HTML5 history API."}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": [{"text": "<Href>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<Navigate>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<Link> (or <NavLink>)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "<Route>", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "In `react-router-dom`, what component should you use to create navigation links instead of the standard HTML `<a>` tag?", "explanation": "Using an `<a>` tag causes a full page reload. The `<Link>` component updates the URL and allows React Router to render the appropriate component client-side, maintaining the SPA behavior.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "<Link> (or <NavLink>)"}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": [{"text": "<Route path=\\"/user/?id\\"/>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<Route path=\\"/user/:id\\"/>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "<Route path=\\"/user/{id}\\"/>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<Route path=\\"/user/[id]\\"/>", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "How do you define a dynamic route segment (e.g., a specific user ID) in `react-router-dom`?", "explanation": "The colon `:` syntax denotes a dynamic segment in a route path. The value matched by this segment can be accessed inside the component.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "<Route path=\\"/user/:id\\"/>"}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": [{"text": "useUrl()", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "useParams()", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "useRouteMatch()", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "useLocation()", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "Which hook allows you to access dynamic parameters from the URL in `react-router-dom`?", "explanation": "`useParams` returns an object of key/value pairs of the dynamic params from the current URL that were matched by the `<Route path>`.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "useParams()"}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": [{"text": "It automatically fetches data for the next route.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It returns a function that lets you navigate programmatically (e.g., after a form submission or button click).", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It provides information about the current browser's navigation history.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It renders a navigation bar component.", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "What does the `useNavigate` hook do?", "explanation": "`useNavigate` provides a `navigate` function. You can call it with a path (e.g., `navigate('/home')`) or a delta to go back in history (e.g., `navigate(-1)`).", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "It returns a function that lets you navigate programmatically (e.g., after a form submission or button click)."}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": [{"text": "To manage database connections.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To replace the need for CSS styling.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To solve the problem of 'prop drilling' by allowing data to be shared globally or across deeply nested component trees without passing props manually at every level.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "To handle asynchronous API requests.", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "What is the Context API used for in React?", "explanation": "Context provides a way to pass data through the component tree without having to pass props down manually at every level. It is ideal for global data like themes, authenticated user info, or preferred language.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "To solve the problem of 'prop drilling' by allowing data to be shared globally or across deeply nested component trees without passing props manually at every level."}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": [{"text": "fetch(), store(), display()", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "createContext(), Context.Provider, useContext()", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "useState(), useEffect(), useReducer()", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ComponentDidMount, ComponentDidUpdate, ComponentWillUnmount", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "What are the three main steps to use the Context API?", "explanation": "First, you create the context using `createContext()`. Second, you wrap the parts of your app that need the data with a `<Context.Provider value=\\"{...}\\">`. Third, you consume the data in child components using the `useContext(Context)` hook.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "createContext(), Context.Provider, useContext()"}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": [{"text": "A security vulnerability where malicious code is passed through props.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A performance optimization technique for rendering lists.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The process of passing data from a parent component down to a deeply nested child component through multiple intermediate components that don't need the data themselves.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The automatic extraction of data from API responses.", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "What is 'Prop Drilling'?", "explanation": "Prop drilling is the term used when you pass props down through several layers of components just to get data to a deeply nested component. Context API or state management libraries (like Redux) are used to avoid this.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "The process of passing data from a parent component down to a deeply nested child component through multiple intermediate components that don't need the data themselves."}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "A", "options": [{"text": "Because `useEffect` itself cannot be an `async` function directly, so you define an `async` function inside it and call it.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Because API calls are synchronous by nature.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Because `async` functions are required by Vite.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Because `fetch` only works inside `async` functions.", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "When fetching data from an API inside `useEffect`, why is it common practice to use an asynchronous function?", "explanation": "The `useEffect` hook expects its callback to return either nothing or a cleanup function. An `async` function always returns a Promise. Therefore, you must define an `async` function *inside* the `useEffect` callback and invoke it there.", "correctAnswer": "A", "correct_index": 0, "correct_answer": "Because `useEffect` itself cannot be an `async` function directly, so you define an `async` function inside it and call it."}, {"type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": [{"text": "To render an external website inside an iframe.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To define where child routes (nested routes) should be rendered within a parent layout component.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "To export data to local storage.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To catch all undefined routes (404 page).", "isCorrect": false}], "question": "In `react-router-dom`, what is the `<Outlet/>` component used for?", "explanation": "When you have nested routes, the parent route component acts as a layout. The `<Outlet/>` acts as a placeholder telling React Router where to render the matching child route's component.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "To define where child routes (nested routes) should be rendered within a parent layout component."}]	\N	> **Masterclass Overview:** This 11-hour comprehensive tutorial is designed to take you from React basics to an industry-ready Frontend Developer. It covers the core philosophy of React, state management, routing, and real-world project building.\n\n### 📌 Core Technical Highlights\n- **React Fundamentals:** Understanding the Virtual DOM vs. Real DOM, JSX syntax, and Vite initialization.\n- **Component Architecture:** Building reusable UI elements, atomic design vs. feature-based structures, and Prop Drilling.\n- **State Management (Hooks):** Deep dive into `useState`, handling two-way data binding for forms, and managing object/array state immutability.\n- **Side Effects & APIs:** Mastering `useEffect` for side-effects, `useRef` for DOM manipulation, and fetching data using `axios`.\n- **Advanced Routing:** Implementing SPAs with `react-router-dom`, nested routes, dynamic routing (`useParams`), and programmatic navigation (`useNavigate`).\n- **Global State:** Solving prop drilling by implementing the Context API (`createContext`, `useContext`).\n\n*Practice your understanding of React.js architecture by completing the 30-question verified quiz below.*	Sheryians Coding School	https://www.youtube.com/@SheryiansCodingSchool	\N	React.js Complete Masterclass: UI, Hooks & Routing	An exhaustive 11-hour deep dive into React.js. Covers Virtual DOM, component architecture, hooks (useState, useEffect, useContext), API integration, and React Router DOM.	676	Easy	Frontend Framework	1	[]	\N	676	https://youtu.be/3LRZRSIh_KE	ReactJS Full Course | ReactJS - Learn Everything | Sheryians Coding School
59	Number Theory Essentials: Digit Extraction, Sqrt(N) Factorization & Euclidean GCD	number-theory-digit-extraction-sqrt-euclidean-gcd	Master digit extraction via modulo division, number reversal, O(sqrt(N)) paired divisor counting, and the Euclidean GCD algorithm.	2026-10-02 17:05:20.024875+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Number Theory Essentials: Digit Extraction, Sqrt(N) Factorization & Euclidean GCD\nPart of the **Problem Solving: DSA & SQL** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1xNbjMdbjug", "title": "Lecture 7: Basic Math for DSA — Count Digits, Reverse Number, Palindrome, GCD, Divisors, Prime", "duration": "40m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/subtract-the-product-and-sum-of-digits-of-an-integer/", "title": "Subtract the Product and Sum of Digits of an Integer", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1281", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Extract digits via modulo-10 division while maintaining dual running product and sum accumulators."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-greatest-common-divisor-of-array/", "title": "Find Greatest Common Divisor of Array", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1979", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Find extremal elements in O(N) and compute their GCD using the Euclidean algorithm."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/three-divisors/", "title": "Three Divisors", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "1952", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Leverage sqrt(N) factor pairing: a number has exactly 3 divisors iff it is the square of a prime."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/number-of-factors1435/1", "title": "Number of Factors", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "problemId": "gfg-num-factors", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Enumerate all paired divisors i and n/i up to sqrt(N) guarding against perfect square duplicates."}]	Module 4: Number Theory & Mathematical Foundations	### 📌 Topic Overview\nMany coding problems revolve around breaking numbers into digits, finding factors, checking if a number is prime, or finding the Greatest Common Divisor (GCD). This lecture covers the essential math tricks that turn slow `O(N)` loops into fast `O(log N)` and `O(sqrt(N))` solutions.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Extracting Digits (`% 10` and `/ 10`):**\n  - `n % 10` gives you the **last digit** of `n`.\n  - `n = n / 10` removes the last digit. Repeating this in a loop processes all digits in `O(log10(N))` steps.\n- **Reversing a Number & Palindromes:** Build the reversed number step by step using `rev = (rev * 10) + lastDigit`. A number is a palindrome if its reverse equals the original number.\n- **Finding Divisors & Checking Primes in `O(sqrt(N))`:**\n  - Divisors always come in pairs: if `i` divides `n`, then `n / i` also divides `n`.\n  - You only need to loop from `i = 1` while `i * i <= n`.\n  - A **Prime Number** is a number greater than 1 that has **exactly two factors** (1 and itself).\n- **Euclidean Algorithm for GCD:** Instead of looping to find the greatest common factor of `a` and `b`, repeatedly take `a = a % b` (or `b = b % a`) until one number becomes `0`. The remaining non-zero number is the GCD!\n\n### 💻 Fast Prime Check & Euclidean GCD\n```cpp\n// Euclidean GCD in logarithmic time\nint findGCD(int a, int b) {\n    while (a > 0 && b > 0) {\n        if (a > b) a = a % b;\n        else b = b % a;\n    }\n    return (a == 0) ? b : a;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
