62	Parameterized Accumulators vs. Functional Recurrence Relations	parameterized-vs-functional-recursion-recurrence	Master parameter-carried state vs value-returning functional recursion, laying the foundation for recurrence relations.	2026-10-02 17:05:20.035481+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Parameterized Accumulators vs. Functional Recurrence Relations\nPart of the **Problem Solving: DSA & SQL** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=69ZCDFy-OUo", "title": "Lecture 10: Parameterised and Functional Recursion — Sum of First N Numbers & Factorial", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/fibonacci-number/", "title": "Fibonacci Number", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "509", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Model mathematical functional recurrence f(n) = f(n-1) + f(n-2) with dual base cases."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/add-digits/", "title": "Add Digits", "platform": "LeetCode", "problemId": "258", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Implement functional recursive digital root aggregation until a single digit remains."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/factorials-of-large-numbers2508/1", "title": "Factorials of Large Numbers", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "problemId": "gfg-fact-large", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Bridge functional recurrence with array-based multiplication to prevent 64-bit integer overflow."}]	Module 5: Recursion & Call Stack Mechanics	### 📌 Topic Overview\nWhen solving a problem recursively (like finding the sum or factorial of `1` to `N`), there are two ways to get the answer: carry the running answer inside a parameter (**Parameterized Recursion**) or have the function return the answer directly (**Functional Recursion**).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **1. Parameterized Recursion (`void` function):**\n  - You pass an extra variable (like `sum`) into the function parameters: `solve(i - 1, sum + i)`.\n  - Once you hit the base case (`i < 1`), `sum` holds the final answer and you print it.\n- **2. Functional Recursion (Return-based function):**\n  - Instead of printing inside the base case, the function **returns** the result of smaller subproblems: `return n + sum(n - 1)`.\n  - For example, `sum(3)` returns `3 + sum(2)`, which returns `2 + sum(1)`, building the final result cleanly.\n  - **Why Functional Matters:** Functional recursion is the exact pattern you will use later in Trees and Dynamic Programming!\n\n### 💻 Parameterized vs. Functional Examples\n```cpp\n// 1. Functional Sum of First N Numbers\nint sumN(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) return 0;      // Base case for addition is 0\n    return n + sumN(n - 1);\n}\n\n// 2. Functional Factorial of N (1 * 2 * ... * N)\nint factorial(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) return 1;      // Base case for multiplication must be 1!\n    return n * factorial(n - 1);\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
