13273	Odd-Parity Modulo Reduction (nums[i] % 2): Count Number of Nice Subarrays	dsa-count-number-of-nice-subarrays-sliding-window	Master Odd-Parity Modulo Reduction (nums[i] % 2): Count Number of Nice Subarrays with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:19:12.164098+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Odd-Parity Modulo Reduction (nums[i] % 2): Count Number of Nice Subarrays\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=j_QOv9OT9Og", "title": "Odd-Parity Modulo Reduction (nums[i] % 2): Count Number of Nice Subarrays - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/count-number-of-nice-subarrays/", "title": "LeetCode 1248: Count Number of Nice Subarrays", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given an array of integers nums and an integer k, return the number of continuous subarrays that contain exactly k odd numbers."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-subarrays-with-sum/", "title": "LeetCode 930: Binary Subarrays With Sum", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Review the underlying Binary Subarray Sum engine that powers Nice Subarrays via modulo-2 transformation."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/count-subarray-with-k-odds/1", "title": "GeeksforGeeks: Count Subarray with k odds", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Count the number of contiguous subarrays having exactly k odd numbers in O(N) time and O(1) space."}]	Module 40: Sliding Window & Two-Pointer Algorithms: Fixed Windows, Variable Windows & Exact-Count Reductions	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an integer array `nums` and an integer `k`, a continuous subarray is called **Nice** if there are **exactly `k` odd numbers** in it. Return the total number of Nice subarrays.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Reducing "Count k Odd Numbers" to "Binary Subarrays With Sum k" (Topic 280!):**\n  - Why write a brand-new algorithm when we can map every element `nums[i]` to **`nums[i] % 2`**?\n    - Every **odd number** satisfies `nums[i] % 2 == 1`.\n    - Every **even number** satisfies `nums[i] % 2 == 0`.\n  - Under this `0/1` mapping, a subarray has **exactly `k` odd numbers** if and only if the **sum of `(nums[i] % 2)` in that subarray equals `k`**!\n- **Applying the `atMost(k) - atMost(k - 1)` Two-Pointer Engine:**\n  - Instead of allocating an extra array or `O(N)` hash map, use our `countAtMost(nums, k)` function from *Binary Subarrays With Sum*:\n    - When expanding `r`: add **`sum += (nums[r] % 2)`**.\n    - When shrinking `l` (`while (sum > goal)`): subtract **`sum -= (nums[l] % 2)`** and advance `l++`.\n    - Add `cnt += (r - l + 1)`.\n  - Return **`countAtMost(nums, k) - countAtMost(nums, k - 1)`**!\n- **Complexity:** **`O(2 * 2N) = O(N)`** Time and **`O(1)`** Space!\n\n### 💻 Count Number of Nice Subarrays Blueprint\n```cpp\nint countAtMostOdds(const vector<int> &nums, int goal) {\n    if (goal < 0) return 0;\n\n    int l = 0, r = 0, sum = 0, cnt = 0;\n    while (r < nums.size()) {\n        // Odd numbers contribute 1, even numbers contribute 0\n        sum += (nums[r] % 2);\n\n        while (sum > goal) {\n            sum -= (nums[l] % 2);\n            l++;\n        }\n\n        cnt += (r - l + 1);\n        r++;\n    }\n    return cnt;\n}\n\nint numberOfSubarrays(vector<int> &nums, int k) {\n    return countAtMostOdds(nums, k) - countAtMostOdds(nums, k - 1);\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Mutating the Input Array `nums[i] = nums[i] % 2` Unnecessarily:** You don't need to modify the caller's array! Simply evaluate `(nums[r] % 2)` and `(nums[l] % 2)` on the fly inside the sliding window loop.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
3880	Incomplete Topological Ordering: Detect a Cycle in a Directed Graph Using Kahn's BFS	dsa-detect-cycle-in-directed-graph-kahns-bfs	Detect directed cycles by verifying whether Kahn's BFS algorithm can process all V vertices.	2026-10-03 02:09:36.736331+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Incomplete Topological Ordering: Detect a Cycle in a Directed Graph Using Kahn's BFS\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iTBaI90lpDQ", "title": "Lecture 148: Detect a Cycle in Directed Graph | Topo Sort | Kahn's Algorithm | BFS", "duration": "16m"}]	[{"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/detect-cycle-in-a-directed-graph/1", "title": "Directed Graph Cycle (BFS)", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Detect whether a directed graph contains a cycle using Kahn's BFS algorithm."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/course-schedule/", "title": "Course Schedule (LC 207)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Check if all courses can be completed by verifying that Kahn's BFS visits all V nodes."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/largest-color-value-in-a-directed-graph/", "title": "Largest Color Value in a Directed Graph (LC 1857)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Use Kahn's BFS to simultaneously detect cycles and propagate DP color counts across a DAG."}]	Module 23: Graph Coloring, Directed Cycle Detection & Topological Sort (DAGs)	### 📌 Topic Overview\nHow can we detect a cycle in a **Directed Graph** using **BFS**? Since Topological Sort is only valid for Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs), we simply run **Kahn's Algorithm** and check how many vertices get processed!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Why Nodes in a Cycle Never Enter the Queue:**\n  - Every vertex inside a directed cycle has an `indegree >= 1` from the previous vertex in the cycle.\n  - Because no vertex in the cycle ever reaches `indegree == 0`, the cycle can never be unlocked or pushed into the queue!\n- **The Count Check (`visitedCount < V`):**\n  - Run Kahn's BFS and increment `visitedCount++` every time a vertex is popped from the queue.\n  - If **`visitedCount == V`**, a full topological ordering was produced, so the graph is **acyclic** (`return false`).\n  - If **`visitedCount < V`**, the queue became empty prematurely because a cyclic dependency blocked the remaining nodes—**a cycle exists (`return true`)**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(V + E)` Time and `O(V)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Directed Cycle Detection (Kahn's BFS) Blueprint\n```cpp\nbool isCyclicKahnsBFS(int V, vector<int> adjList[]) {\n    vector<int> indegree(V, 0);\n    for (int u = 0; u < V; u++) {\n        for (int v : adjList[u]) {\n            indegree[v]++;\n        }\n    }\n\n    queue<int> q;\n    for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {\n        if (indegree[i] == 0) q.push(i);\n    }\n\n    int visitedCount = 0;\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int curr = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        visitedCount++;\n\n        for (int neighbor : adjList[curr]) {\n            indegree[neighbor]--;\n            if (indegree[neighbor] == 0) {\n                q.push(neighbor);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // If we couldn't visit all V vertices, the graph contains a cycle!\n    return visitedCount != V;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Inverting the Return Boolean:** Remember that `visitedCount == V` means **NO cycle** (`false`), whereas `visitedCount < V` means **YES cycle** (`true`)!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
15097	Frequency-Bucketed Doubly Linked Lists: Implement LFU Cache in O(1)	dsa-implement-lfu-cache-frequency-lists-hashmap	Master Frequency-Bucketed Doubly Linked Lists: Implement LFU Cache in O(1) with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:30:52.978291+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Frequency-Bucketed Doubly Linked Lists: Implement LFU Cache in O(1)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzqHlAW7jeE", "title": "Frequency-Bucketed Doubly Linked Lists: Implement LFU Cache in O(1) - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/lfu-cache/", "title": "LeetCode 460: LFU Cache", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Design and implement a data structure for a Least Frequently Used (LFU) cache where get and put run in O(1) average time complexity."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/all-oone-data-structure/", "title": "LeetCode 432: All O`one Data Structure", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Maintain strings organized by frequency buckets in a Doubly Linked List to support O(1) inc, dec, getMaxKey, and getMinKey."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-frequency-stack/", "title": "LeetCode 895: Maximum Frequency Stack", "difficulty": "Hard", "description": "Use frequency-bucketed stacks (freqMap + groupStackMap + maxFreq) to pop the most frequent element in O(1) time."}]	Module 44: Advanced Stack, Monotonic Deque & Cache Design: Stock Span, Sliding Window Max, Celebrity & LRU/LFU Caches	### 📌 Topic Overview\nDesign a **Least Frequently Used (LFU) Cache** of fixed `capacity` where both `get(key)` and `put(key, value)` run in **`O(1)` time**:\n- Each time a key is accessed (`get` or `put`), its **use counter (`cnt`) increments by `+1`**.\n- When the cache is full and a new key must be inserted, evict the key with the **lowest frequency (`minFreq`)**.\n- **Tie-Breaker Rule:** If multiple keys share the same lowest frequency `minFreq`, evict the **Least Recently Used (LRU)** key among them!\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Two Hash Maps + Per-Frequency Doubly Linked Lists)\n- **Data Structures Required:**\n  1. **`Node`**: Stores `(key, value, cnt, prev, next)`.\n  2. **`List` (DLL with `size` counter)**: Each frequency bucket (`cnt = 1, 2, 3...`) has its own LRU Doubly Linked List (`addFront(node)` and `removeNode(node)`), automatically handling LRU tie-breaking within that frequency!\n  3. **`keyNode` (`unordered_map<int, Node*>`)**: Maps `key -> Node*` for `O(1)` node lookup.\n  4. **`freqListMap` (`unordered_map<int, List*>`)**: Maps `frequency -> List*` (the DLL of all nodes currently having that frequency).\n  5. **`minFreq`**: Tracks the current minimum frequency across the cache.\n- **How `updateFreqListMap(node)` Promotes a Node in O(1):**\n  1. Remove `node` from its current frequency list `freqListMap[node->cnt]->removeNode(node)`.\n  2. **Crucial `minFreq` Increment Check:** If `node->cnt == minFreq` AND **`freqListMap[node->cnt]->size == 0`** (that was the last node at `minFreq`!), increment **`minFreq++`**!\n  3. Increment `node->cnt++` and insert `node` at the front of `freqListMap[node->cnt]`!\n- **How `put(key, value)` Handles New Insertions in O(1):**\n  - If `curSize == maxSizeCache`, look up `List* list = freqListMap[minFreq]`, evict its LRU tail **`list->tail->prev`**, and erase it from `keyNode`.\n  - Since a brand-new key always starts with frequency `1`, reset **`minFreq = 1`** and insert the new node at the front of `freqListMap[1]`!\n\n### 💻 Implement LFU Cache Blueprint\n```cpp\nstruct LFUNode {\n    int key, value, cnt;\n    LFUNode *next, *prev;\n    LFUNode(int _key, int _value) : key(_key), value(_value), cnt(1), next(nullptr), prev(nullptr) {}\n};\n\nstruct LFUList {\n    int size;\n    LFUNode *head, *tail;\n    LFUList() {\n        head = new LFUNode(0, 0);\n        tail = new LFUNode(0, 0);\n        head->next = tail;\n        tail->prev = head;\n        size = 0;\n    }\n\n    void addFront(LFUNode *node) {\n        LFUNode *temp = head->next;\n        node->next = temp;\n        node->prev = head;\n        head->next = node;\n        temp->prev = node;\n        size++;\n    }\n\n    void removeNode(LFUNode *delnode) {\n        LFUNode *delprev = delnode->prev;\n        LFUNode *delnext = delnode->next;\n        delprev->next = delnext;\n        delnext->prev = delprev;\n        size--;\n    }\n};\n\nclass LFUCache {\n    unordered_map<int, LFUNode*> keyNode;\n    unordered_map<int, LFUList*> freqListMap;\n    int maxSizeCache;\n    int minFreq;\n    int curSize;\n\npublic:\n    LFUCache(int capacity) {\n        maxSizeCache = capacity;\n        minFreq = 0;\n        curSize = 0;\n    }\n\n    void updateFreqListMap(LFUNode *node) {\n        freqListMap[node->cnt]->removeNode(node);\n\n        // If this was the only node at minFreq, minFreq increases by 1!\n        if (node->cnt == minFreq && freqListMap[node->cnt]->size == 0) {\n            minFreq++;\n        }\n\n        LFUList *nextHigherFreqList = new LFUList();\n        if (freqListMap.find(node->cnt + 1) != freqListMap.end()) {\n            nextHigherFreqList = freqListMap[node->cnt + 1];\n        }\n        node->cnt += 1;\n        nextHigherFreqList->addFront(node);\n        freqListMap[node->cnt] = nextHigherFreqList;\n    }\n\n    int get(int key) {\n        if (keyNode.find(key) != keyNode.end()) {\n            LFUNode *node = keyNode[key];\n            int val = node->value;\n            updateFreqListMap(node);\n            return val;\n        }\n        return -1;\n    }\n\n    void put(int key, int value) {\n        if (maxSizeCache == 0) return;\n\n        if (keyNode.find(key) != keyNode.end()) {\n            LFUNode *node = keyNode[key];\n            node->value = value;\n            updateFreqListMap(node);\n        } else {\n            if (curSize == maxSizeCache) {\n                LFUList *list = freqListMap[minFreq];\n                keyNode.erase(list->tail->prev->key);\n                list->removeNode(list->tail->prev);\n                curSize--;\n            }\n            curSize++;\n            // Brand-new element always has frequency 1!\n            minFreq = 1;\n            LFUList *listFreq = new LFUList();\n            if (freqListMap.find(minFreq) != freqListMap.end()) {\n                listFreq = freqListMap[minFreq];\n            }\n            LFUNode *node = new LFUNode(key, value);\n            listFreq->addFront(node);\n            keyNode[key] = node;\n            freqListMap[minFreq] = listFreq;\n        }\n    }\n};\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Forgetting to Reset `minFreq = 1` When Inserting a Brand-New Key:** Even if `minFreq` had climbed to `5` from earlier `get()` calls, any newly inserted key starts with frequency `1`—so `minFreq` **must** be reset to `1` on every new key insertion!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
