rect_answer": "A Three-Way Handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK)"}, {"id": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["It ignores them and moves on.", "It uses a timeout mechanism to detect the loss and automatically requests the missing packets to be retransmitted, guaranteeing complete delivery.", "It terminates the entire connection immediately.", "It replaces the missing data with empty strings."], "question": "How does TCP handle data packets that are dropped or lost during transmission across the internet?", "explanation": "This is why TCP is 'reliable'. If packet 4 of 10 gets lost at a router, TCP halts assembly and demands the sender transmit packet 4 again until it arrives safely.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "It uses a timeout mechanism to detect the loss and automatically requests the missing packets to be retransmitted, guaranteeing complete delivery."}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["It has terrible encryption.", "It only works on local networks.", "It fires packets at the destination without a handshake, performs no ordering, and never retransmits dropped packets.", "It frequently corrupts file headers."], "question": "Why is UDP (User Datagram Protocol) considered 'unreliable'?", "explanation": "UDP is a 'fire-and-forget' protocol. It strips away all the heavy tracking logic of TCP, making it incredibly fast but completely unforgiving of network packet loss.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "It fires packets at the destination without a handshake, performs no ordering, and never retransmits dropped packets."}, {"id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "D", "options": ["Processing a credit card payment via a REST API.", "Sending a text-based email via SMTP.", "Downloading a critical software update file.", "Streaming live multiplayer game data or a real-time Zoom video call."], "question": "In which of the following scenarios is UDP strictly preferred over TCP?", "explanation": "In a live video call, if a frame drops, you don't want the protocol to pause the call to re-download an old frame from 2 seconds ago. You want it to drop the frame and keep moving forward instantly.", "correctAnswer": "D", "correct_index": 3, "correct_answer": "Streaming live multiplayer game data or a real-time Zoom video call."}, {"id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["The file is corrupted permanently.", "TCP discards the out-of-order packets and requests them again.", "TCP utilizes the sequence numbers attached to each packet to hold and flawlessly reassemble them in their exact original order before handing them to the application.", "The browser throws an HTTP 500 error."], "question": "What happens in TCP if data packets arrive at the destination completely out of order?", "explanation": "Because different packets might take different physical routes across the globe, they arrive out of order. TCP buffers them and uses sequence numbers to stitch them back together seamlessly.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "TCP utilizes the sequence numbers attached to each packet to hold and flawlessly reassemble them in their exact original order before handing them to the application."}]	\N	> **Architecture Overview:** Essential technical concepts and structural principles vital for robust, scalable web architectures. Focus deeply on the mechanical trade-offs discussed in this module.	Hayk Simonyan	https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan	\N	Transport Layer Explained: TCP vs UDP	Comprehensive overview of Transport Layer covering low-level transport mechanisms, architectural scaling, and system persistence.	5	Easy	Advanced System Concepts	29	[]	\N	5	https://youtu.be/QyrHol2Ylfo	Transport Layer Explained: TCP vs UDP
15092	Lexicographical Prefix Minimization: Remove K Digits	dsa-remove-k-digits-monotonic-stack	Master Lexicographical Prefix Minimization: Remove K Digits with step-by-step intuition, code blueprints, and verified LeetCode/GFG practice.	2026-10-03 15:30:52.972875+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Lexicographical Prefix Minimization: Remove K Digits\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jmbuRzYPGrg", "title": "Lexicographical Prefix Minimization: Remove K Digits - Full Lecture"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/remove-k-digits/", "title": "LeetCode 402: Remove K Digits", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Given string num representing a non-negative integer num, and an integer k, return the smallest possible integer after removing k digits from num."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/remove-duplicate-letters/", "title": "LeetCode 316: Remove Duplicate Letters", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Use a monotonic increasing stack with remaining-count checks to produce the lexicographically smallest subsequence of unique characters."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-the-most-competitive-subsequence/", "title": "LeetCode 1673: Find the Most Competitive Subsequence", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Keep the smallest digits at the front using the exact same Remove (N - K) Digits monotonic stack!"}]	Module 43: Monotonic Stack & Contribution Algorithms: Next/Previous Greater/Smaller, Rainwater & Subarray Aggregations	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven a string `num` representing a non-negative integer of length `N` and an integer `k`, remove **`k` digits** from `num` so that the new number is the **smallest possible** integer.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways & The 3 Crucial Edge Cases\n- **Greedy Intuition (Keep Smaller Digits at the Most Significant Left Positions!):**\n  - In `"1432219"`, why is removing `'4'`, `'3'`, `'2'` (`"1219"`) optimal? Because replacing a larger digit (`'4'`) on the left with a smaller digit (`'3'`, then `'2'`) immediately reduces the most significant place values!\n  - Traverse `i` from `0` to `N - 1` maintaining a **monotonic non-decreasing stack**:\n    - **`while (!st.empty() && k > 0 && st.top() > num[i]) { st.pop(); k--; }`**\n    - Push `st.push(num[i])`.\n- **The 3 Mandatory Edge Cases to Handle:**\n  1. **Edge Case 1 (`k > 0` Remaining After the Loop, e.g., `"123456"`):**\n     - If the digits were already in increasing order, nothing got popped! Pop the remaining `k` largest digits from the top of the stack: **`while (k > 0 && !st.empty()) { st.pop(); k--; }`**.\n  2. **Edge Case 2 (Empty Stack When `k == N`):**\n     - If `st.empty()`, return **`"0"`**.\n  3. **Edge Case 3 (Leading Zeros, e.g., `"00100"` → `"100"`, or `"000"` → `"0"`):**\n     - When popping characters from `st` into `res` (in reverse order), strip all leading zeros (which sit at the **back of `res` before reversing**):\n       `while (!res.empty() && res.back() == '0') res.pop_back();`\n     - Reverse `res`, and if `res.empty()`, return `"0"`!\n- **Complexity:** `O(3N + K)` Time and `O(2N)` Space.\n\n### 💻 Remove K Digits Blueprint\n```cpp\nstring removeKdigits(string num, int k) {\n    stack<char> st;\n\n    for (char digit : num) {\n        // Greedily pop larger preceding digits while we still have k removals left\n        while (!st.empty() && k > 0 && (st.top() - '0') > (digit - '0')) {\n            st.pop();\n            k--;\n        }\n        st.push(digit);\n    }\n\n    // Edge Case 1: If k > 0 remains (e.g., "123456"), remove largest digits from the end\n    while (k > 0 && !st.empty()) {\n        st.pop();\n        k--;\n    }\n\n    // Edge Case 2: All digits removed\n    if (st.empty()) return "0";\n\n    // Extract characters from stack (currently in reverse order)\n    string res = "";\n    while (!st.empty()) {\n        res.push_back(st.top());\n        st.pop();\n    }\n\n    // Edge Case 3: Strip leading zeros (which are at the back of 'res' before reversing!)\n    while (!res.empty() && res.back() == '0') {\n        res.pop_back();\n    }\n\n    reverse(res.begin(), res.end());\n    if (res.empty()) return "0";\n    return res;\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Concatenating `res = res + st.top()` Inside a Loop on Large Strings:** On LeetCode 402 (`N = 10^5`), writing `res = res + st.top()` copies the entire string `res` on every iteration, causing `O(N²)` Time Limit Exceeded or Memory Limit Exceeded! Always use **`res.push_back(st.top())`** (`O(1)` amortized)!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
22219	Core System Design Patterns	hld-core-system-design-patterns	Architectural patterns: Microservices vs Monoliths, Database-per-Service trade-offs, Circuit Breaker resilience, Event Sourcing, and CQRS.	2026-10-07 16:41:22.814808+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Core System Design Patterns\nPart of the **System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/OdNpY3WQniQ", "title": "System Design Patterns you should Master Right Now", "platform": "YouTube"}]	[{"id": "hld-v14-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Monolith vs Microservices Blast Radius", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Monolithic Blast Radius (100% System Failure):**\\nIn a monolith, all business domains (Authentication, Orders, Payments, Notifications, PDF Export) share a single runtime process, memory heap, and thread pool.\\nIf a memory leak occurs in a low-priority background feature like \\"PDF Export\\", the memory exhaustion crashes the entire Node.js/Java process. As a result, critical core features like \\"User Login\\" and \\"Checkout\\" are dragged down completely.\\n\\n**Microservices Blast Radius (Fault Isolation):**\\nEach domain runs in an independent container with dedicated CPU/RAM allocations.\\nIf the \\"PDF Export Service\\" crashes or runs out of memory, its container restarts in isolation. The \\"Payment Service\\" and \\"Auth Service\\" continue serving users with zero downtime, bounding the blast radius strictly to the offending feature.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Compare the \\"Blast Radius\\" of an unhandled runtime exception or memory leak in a Monolithic architecture versus a Microservices architecture.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v14-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: The Problem with Shared Databases in Microservices", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Anti-Pattern (Shared Database):**\\nWhen multiple microservices share one database, the decoupling is an illusion:\\n1. **Hidden Coupling:** Service A modifying a table schema breaks Service B without warning.\\n2. **Lock Contention:** Heavy batch updates from an analytics service lock tables needed by low-latency customer checkout services.\\n3. **Security Vulnerability:** A bug in an auxiliary service can accidentally wipe or leak sensitive user password records.\\n\\n**The Solution (Database-per-Service):**\\nEach microservice strictly possesses private data storage. No other service can connect directly to that database; access is strictly gated behind published REST/gRPC APIs.\\nThis enables polyglot persistence (e.g., Auth uses MongoDB, Payments uses PostgreSQL, Analytics uses ClickHouse) and ensures complete data schema autonomy.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why is sharing a single relational database across multiple microservices considered an architectural anti-pattern? How does \\"Database-per-Service\\" solve this?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v14-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Solving Distributed Joins in Database-per-Service", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nTwo primary architectural patterns resolve this:\\n\\n1. **API Composition (Application-Level Join):**\\n   * An API Gateway or aggregator service issues concurrent requests to `GET /orders/123`, `GET /users/456`, and `GET /payments/789`.\\n   * The aggregator combines the three JSON payloads in memory and returns a composite object to the client.\\n   * *Best for:* Low-to-medium throughput queries with simple relationships.\\n\\n2. **CQRS with Materialized Views (Event-Driven Aggregation):**\\n   * Whenever User or Payment updates occur, they publish domain events (`UserUpdated`, `PaymentCaptured`) to a Kafka event bus.\\n   * An Order Read Model service consumes these events and updates a denormalized read database (e.g., Elasticsearch or MongoDB).\\n   * Client queries read the pre-joined, materialized view in a single fast lookup with zero cross-service network calls.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "In a Database-per-Service architecture, an order management UI needs to display Order details alongside User profile data and Payment status. Because cross-database SQL JOINs are impossible, how do you architect this read operation?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v14-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: The Circuit Breaker Pattern States and Cascading Failure", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Cascading Failure Prevention:**\\nIf Service C becomes sluggish and times out, Service B keeps calling it, blocking its own thread pool. In turn, Service A blocks waiting on Service B. Thread pool exhaustion cascades upstream until the entire application goes offline.\\n\\n**Circuit Breaker State Machine:**\\n1. **CLOSED (Normal Operation):** All requests pass through to the downstream service. The circuit breaker tracks failure rates.\\n2. **OPEN (Fail Fast):** If the failure rate exceeds a threshold (e.g., >50% errors over 10 seconds), the circuit trips to OPEN. For the next 30 seconds, **all incoming calls are failed immediately locally** (or served via fallback cache) without attempting to reach the downstream service. This prevents upstream thread starvation and gives the downstream service breathing room to recover.\\n3. **HALF-OPEN (Trial Probe):** After the cooldown period, the circuit allows a small number of trial requests through. If they succeed, it assumes the downstream service has recovered and transitions back to **CLOSED**. If any trial request fails, it immediately resets to **OPEN**.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Explain how the Circuit Breaker pattern prevents Cascading Failures in microservices. Detail the state transitions between CLOSED, OPEN, and HALF-OPEN.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v14-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Distributed Transactions: 2PC vs Saga Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Two-Phase Commit (2PC):**\\n   * Relies on a centralized coordinator that executes in two phases: *Prepare* (all databases lock rows and vote commit readiness) and *Commit* (coordinator executes the commit).\\n   * *Flaws:* Highly blocking. Database row locks are held across slow network hops. If the coordinator or any database network blips, locks remain held, causing severe latency and deadlock bottlenecks. It is fundamentally unscalable in cloud microservices.\\n\\n2. **Saga Pattern (Compensating Transactions):**\\n   * The transaction is broken into a series of local transactions in each microservice.\\n   * Each service updates its own database and emits an event triggering the next step (e.g., Order Created -> Payment Deducted -> Inventory Reserved).\\n   * **Compensating Action on Failure:** If step 3 (Inventory) fails because stock is depleted, the Saga issues reverse compensating transactions backward (Refund Payment -> Cancel Order).\\n   * *Advantage:* Highly scalable, completely non-blocking, and embraces eventual consistency without holding distributed database locks.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "When a business transaction spans multiple microservices with separate databases, traditional ACID transactions fail. Compare Two-Phase Commit (2PC) with the Saga Pattern.", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	High Level Design	### 📌 1. Monolith vs. Microservices\n* **Monolith:** Single unified codebase and deployment pipeline. Simple to develop, debug, and query, but scaling requires replicating the entire application, and a single uncaught exception can bring down the entire system.\n* **Microservices:** Decoupled independent domain services communicating over network APIs. Enables polyglot tech stacks, independent scaling, and failure isolation, at the cost of distributed networking complexity.\n\n### 💡 2. Database-per-Service Pattern\n* Each microservice strictly owns its own dedicated database.\n* **The Benefit:** True domain isolation; no foreign service can corrupt another service's tables.\n* **The Trade-off:** Cross-service SQL `JOIN`s are strictly impossible. Data must be joined at the application level, or aggregated via event-driven materialized views.\n\n### 💡 3. The Circuit Breaker Pattern\n* Prevents **Cascading Failures** across distributed microservices.\n* **States:**\n  * **CLOSED:** Normal state. Calls flow through to the downstream service.\n  * **OPEN:** If error thresholds are exceeded (e.g., 50% failure rate over 10 seconds), the circuit trips open. Calls fail fast immediately without hitting the broken downstream service, allowing it breathing room to recover.\n  * **HALF-OPEN:** After a timeout, trial requests are allowed through. If they succeed, the circuit resets to CLOSED; if they fail, it re-opens.	Piyush Garg	https://www.youtube.com/@piyushgarg195	28	Core System Design Patterns	Architectural patterns: Microservices vs Monoliths, Database-per-Service trade-offs, Circuit Breaker resilience, Event Sourcing, and CQRS.	20	Hard	High Level Design	14	[{"id": "hld-v14-q1", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q1: Monolith vs Microservices Blast Radius", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Monolithic Blast Radius (100% System Failure):**\\nIn a monolith, all business domains (Authentication, Orders, Payments, Notifications, PDF Export) share a single runtime process, memory heap, and thread pool.\\nIf a memory leak occurs in a low-priority background feature like \\"PDF Export\\", the memory exhaustion crashes the entire Node.js/Java process. As a result, critical core features like \\"User Login\\" and \\"Checkout\\" are dragged down completely.\\n\\n**Microservices Blast Radius (Fault Isolation):**\\nEach domain runs in an independent container with dedicated CPU/RAM allocations.\\nIf the \\"PDF Export Service\\" crashes or runs out of memory, its container restarts in isolation. The \\"Payment Service\\" and \\"Auth Service\\" continue serving users with zero downtime, bounding the blast radius strictly to the offending feature.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Compare the \\"Blast Radius\\" of an unhandled runtime exception or memory leak in a Monolithic architecture versus a Microservices architecture.", "difficulty": "Moderate", "questionNumber": 1}, {"id": "hld-v14-q2", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q2: The Problem with Shared Databases in Microservices", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**The Anti-Pattern (Shared Database):**\\nWhen multiple microservices share one database, the decoupling is an illusion:\\n1. **Hidden Coupling:** Service A modifying a table schema breaks Service B without warning.\\n2. **Lock Contention:** Heavy batch updates from an analytics service lock tables needed by low-latency customer checkout services.\\n3. **Security Vulnerability:** A bug in an auxiliary service can accidentally wipe or leak sensitive user password records.\\n\\n**The Solution (Database-per-Service):**\\nEach microservice strictly possesses private data storage. No other service can connect directly to that database; access is strictly gated behind published REST/gRPC APIs.\\nThis enables polyglot persistence (e.g., Auth uses MongoDB, Payments uses PostgreSQL, Analytics uses ClickHouse) and ensures complete data schema autonomy.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Why is sharing a single relational database across multiple microservices considered an architectural anti-pattern? How does \\"Database-per-Service\\" solve this?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 2}, {"id": "hld-v14-q3", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q3: Solving Distributed Joins in Database-per-Service", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\nTwo primary architectural patterns resolve this:\\n\\n1. **API Composition (Application-Level Join):**\\n   * An API Gateway or aggregator service issues concurrent requests to `GET /orders/123`, `GET /users/456`, and `GET /payments/789`.\\n   * The aggregator combines the three JSON payloads in memory and returns a composite object to the client.\\n   * *Best for:* Low-to-medium throughput queries with simple relationships.\\n\\n2. **CQRS with Materialized Views (Event-Driven Aggregation):**\\n   * Whenever User or Payment updates occur, they publish domain events (`UserUpdated`, `PaymentCaptured`) to a Kafka event bus.\\n   * An Order Read Model service consumes these events and updates a denormalized read database (e.g., Elasticsearch or MongoDB).\\n   * Client queries read the pre-joined, materialized view in a single fast lookup with zero cross-service network calls.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "In a Database-per-Service architecture, an order management UI needs to display Order details alongside User profile data and Payment status. Because cross-database SQL JOINs are impossible, how do you architect this read operation?", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 3}, {"id": "hld-v14-q4", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q4: The Circuit Breaker Pattern States and Cascading Failure", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n**Cascading Failure Prevention:**\\nIf Service C becomes sluggish and times out, Service B keeps calling it, blocking its own thread pool. In turn, Service A blocks waiting on Service B. Thread pool exhaustion cascades upstream until the entire application goes offline.\\n\\n**Circuit Breaker State Machine:**\\n1. **CLOSED (Normal Operation):** All requests pass through to the downstream service. The circuit breaker tracks failure rates.\\n2. **OPEN (Fail Fast):** If the failure rate exceeds a threshold (e.g., >50% errors over 10 seconds), the circuit trips to OPEN. For the next 30 seconds, **all incoming calls are failed immediately locally** (or served via fallback cache) without attempting to reach the downstream service. This prevents upstream thread starvation and gives the downstream service breathing room to recover.\\n3. **HALF-OPEN (Trial Probe):** After the cooldown period, the circuit allows a small number of trial requests through. If they succeed, it assumes the downstream service has recovered and transitions back to **CLOSED**. If any trial request fails, it immediately resets to **OPEN**.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "Explain how the Circuit Breaker pattern prevents Cascading Failures in microservices. Detail the state transitions between CLOSED, OPEN, and HALF-OPEN.", "difficulty": "Hard", "questionNumber": 4}, {"id": "hld-v14-q5", "type": "subjective", "title": "Q5: Distributed Transactions: 2PC vs Saga Pattern", "answer": "**Detailed Answer:**\\n\\n1. **Two-Phase Commit (2PC):**\\n   * Relies on a centralized coordinator that executes in two phases: *Prepare* (all databases lock rows and vote commit readiness) and *Commit* (coordinator executes the commit).\\n   * *Flaws:* Highly blocking. Database row locks are held across slow network hops. If the coordinator or any database network blips, locks remain held, causing severe latency and deadlock bottlenecks. It is fundamentally unscalable in cloud microservices.\\n\\n2. **Saga Pattern (Compensating Transactions):**\\n   * The transaction is broken into a series of local transactions in each microservice.\\n   * Each service updates its own database and emits an event triggering the next step (e.g., Order Created -> Payment Deducted -> Inventory Reserved).\\n   * **Compensating Action on Failure:** If step 3 (Inventory) fails because stock is depleted, the Saga issues reverse compensating transactions backward (Refund Payment -> Cancel Order).\\n   * *Advantage:* Highly scalable, completely non-blocking, and embraces eventual consistency without holding distributed database locks.", "category": "High Level Design", "question": "When a business transaction spans multiple microservices with separate databases, traditional ACID transactions fail. Compare Two-Phase Commit (2PC) with the Saga Pattern.", "difficulty": "Very Hard", "questionNumber": 5}]	\N	\N	\N	\N
3024	Controlled Call-Stack Streaming: Binary Search Tree Iterator in O(H) Space	dsa-binary-search-tree-iterator	Implement a memory-efficient BST iterator streaming elements in ascending order with amortized O(1) next() and strictly O(H) auxiliary space.	2026-10-03 01:35:42.028371+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Controlled Call-Stack Streaming: Binary Search Tree Iterator in O(H) Space\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D2jMcmxU4bs", "title": "Lecture 122: BST Iterator", "duration": "25m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-search-tree-iterator/", "title": "Binary Search Tree Iterator (LC 173)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Implement an iterator over a binary search tree with amortized O(1) next() and O(H) memory."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-search-tree-iterator-ii/", "title": "Binary Search Tree Iterator II (LC 1586)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Extend the BST iterator to support both next() and prev() bidirectional operations."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/peeking-iterator/", "title": "Peeking Iterator (LC 284)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Design an iterator that supports peeking at the next element without advancing."}]	Module 20: Advanced Binary Search Tree Operations & Validation	### 📌 Topic Overview\nDesign a `BSTIterator` class that iterates over the **Inorder Traversal** of a BST:\n- `next()` returns the next smallest number in the BST.\n- `hasNext()` returns `true` if there is a next number in the traversal.\n- Both operations must run in **average `O(1)` time** and use strictly **`O(H)` memory** (where `H` is tree height), forbidding `O(N)` array pre-storage.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Lazy Left-Chain Stack Pushing (`pushAll`):**\n  - Instead of storing the whole inorder sequence in `O(N)` space, maintain a `stack<TreeNode*> myStack` that stores only the left spine of the current subtree (`O(H)` nodes).\n  - **Constructor:** Call `pushAll(root)`, which pushes `root` and all of its left descendants (`node = node->left`) onto the stack.\n  - **`hasNext()`:** Returns `!myStack.empty()`.\n  - **`next()`:**\n    1. Pop the top node `tmpNode = myStack.top()`.\n    2. Before returning `tmpNode->val`, call `pushAll(tmpNode->right)` to load the leftmost chain of its right subtree!\n- **Why Average Time Complexity is `O(1)`:**\n  - Across `N` calls to `next()`, every node in the tree is pushed and popped **exactly once**. Total work for `N` calls is `O(N)`, so the amortized cost per `next()` call is `O(N) / N = O(1)`!\n\n### 💻 BST Iterator Blueprint\n```cpp\nclass BSTIterator {\nprivate:\n    stack<TreeNode*> myStack;\n\n    void pushAll(TreeNode* node) {\n        while (node != nullptr) {\n            myStack.push(node);\n            node = node->left;\n        }\n    }\n\npublic:\n    BSTIterator(TreeNode* root) {\n        pushAll(root);\n    }\n\n    bool hasNext() {\n        return !myStack.empty();\n    }\n\n    int next() {\n        TreeNode* tmpNode = myStack.top();\n        myStack.pop();\n        pushAll(tmpNode->right); // Load left chain of right child\n        return tmpNode->val;\n    }\n};\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Precomputing the Full Inorder Vector:** Storing all N elements in a vector inside the constructor violates the O(H) memory constraint required by interviewers.	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
3441	Boundary-Inward Flood Protection: Surrounded Regions (Replace O's With X's)	dsa-surrounded-regions-replace-os-with-xs	Capture interior surrounded regions by preserving boundary-connected components using inward flood traversal.	2026-10-03 01:51:47.362026+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Boundary-Inward Flood Protection: Surrounded Regions (Replace O's With X's)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BtdgAys4yMk", "title": "Lecture 139: Surrounded Regions | Replace O's with X's", "duration": "20m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/surrounded-regions/", "title": "Surrounded Regions (LC 130)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Capture all regions of 'O's that are 4-directionally surrounded by 'X's and flip them to 'X'."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-closed-islands/", "title": "Number of Closed Islands (LC 1254)", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Eliminate boundary-connected land components first, then count remaining closed interior islands."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/replace-os-with-xs0052/1", "title": "Replace O's with X's", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Replace all 'O's or groups of 'O's that are completely surrounded by 'X's."}]	Module 22: Graph Cycle Detection, Multi-Source BFS & Boundary Flood Algorithms	### 📌 Topic Overview\nGiven an `N x M` board of `'X'` and `'O'` characters, capture all regions of `'O'`s that are completely surrounded on all 4 sides by `'X'`s by flipping them to `'X'`.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **The Boundary Escape Observation:**\n  - When can a group of `'O'`s **never** be surrounded by `'X'`s? Whenever at least one `'O'` in that connected group touches the **outer boundary** of the board (first row, last row, first column, or last column)!\n- **Boundary-Inward DFS Algorithm (`O(N * M)` Time, `O(N * M)` Space):**\n  1. Traverse the **4 outer borders** of the grid.\n  2. Whenever you see a boundary `'O'` that is not yet visited, launch a DFS/BFS to mark it and all `'O'`s connected to it in `seen` as **safe** (cannot be flipped).\n  3. Scan the entire grid: any `'O'` that was **not** marked in `seen` is completely enclosed inside the grid—flip it to `'X'`!\n\n### 💻 Surrounded Regions Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid markSafeDFS(int r, int c, const vector<vector<char>> &board, vector<vector<int>> &seen) {\n    seen[r][c] = 1;\n    int rows = board.size(), cols = board[0].size();\n    int dr[] = {-1, 0, 1, 0}, dc[] = {0, 1, 0, -1};\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n        int nr = r + dr[i], nc = c + dc[i];\n        if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols &&\n            !seen[nr][nc] && board[nr][nc] == 'O') {\n            markSafeDFS(nr, nc, board, seen);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nvoid solveSurroundedRegions(vector<vector<char>> &board) {\n    int rows = board.size(), cols = board[0].size();\n    vector<vector<int>> seen(rows, vector<int>(cols, 0));\n\n    // 1. Check first and last rows\n    for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {\n        if (!seen[0][c] && board[0][c] == 'O') markSafeDFS(0, c, board, seen);\n        if (!seen[rows - 1][c] && board[rows - 1][c] == 'O') markSafeDFS(rows - 1, c, board, seen);\n    }\n    // 2. Check first and last columns\n    for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {\n        if (!seen[r][0] && board[r][0] == 'O') markSafeDFS(r, 0, board, seen);\n        if (!seen[r][cols - 1] && board[r][cols - 1] == 'O') markSafeDFS(r, cols - 1, board, seen);\n    }\n    // 3. Flip all unvisited interior 'O's to 'X'\n    for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {\n            if (!seen[r][c] && board[r][c] == 'O') {\n                board[r][c] = 'X';\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid\n- **Trying to Check "Is Surrounded" From the Inside Out:** Starting from interior 'O's makes it hard to tell whether a component eventually leaks to the border without backtracking. Starting from the border inward solves the problem in one clean pass!	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
