 the SERVER to hide and protect the backend servers"}, {"id": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["To speed up compiling code", "To monitor, filter, and control outbound internet usage, block malicious sites, and cache frequently visited pages to save bandwidth", "To generate SSL certificates", "To route database queries"], "question": "Why do corporations often use internal Forward Proxies for employee networks?", "explanation": "A corporate forward proxy intercepts an employee's request to a website, scans it for malware, checks if it violates policy, and caches the result for the next employee.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "To monitor, filter, and control outbound internet usage, block malicious sites, and cache frequently visited pages to save bandwidth"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["A proxy that uses Tor routing exclusively", "A proxy that identifies itself as a proxy but strips the user's original IP address from the forwarded request headers", "A proxy that drops 50% of packets", "A proxy that only works on the dark web"], "question": "What is an 'Anonymous Proxy'?", "explanation": "It provides basic anonymity by hiding the client's actual IP from the destination server, though the server still knows it is talking to a proxy.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "A proxy that identifies itself as a proxy but strips the user's original IP address from the forwarded request headers"}, {"id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["A standard home WiFi router", "A Local Area Network (LAN)", "Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) and Load Balancers", "A desktop VPN client"], "question": "Which of the following relies heavily on Reverse Proxy architecture?", "explanation": "CDNs and Load Balancers sit in front of the origin servers. The client talks to the CDN (the reverse proxy), which fetches data from the hidden backend.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) and Load Balancers"}, {"id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["Forcing users to use unencrypted HTTP", "Decrypting incoming HTTPS traffic at the proxy level and forwarding it as plain HTTP to the internal backend servers to save them CPU processing power", "Generating fake SSL certificates", "Encrypting database hard drives"], "question": "What is 'SSL Offloading' (SSL Termination) performed by a Reverse Proxy?", "explanation": "Decrypting TLS/SSL is computationally heavy. The reverse proxy handles the math, allowing the internal backend servers to focus purely on application logic.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "Decrypting incoming HTTPS traffic at the proxy level and forwarding it as plain HTTP to the internal backend servers to save them CPU processing power"}]	\N	> **Architecture Overview:** Essential technical concepts and structural principles vital for robust, scalable web architectures. Focus deeply on the mechanical trade-offs discussed in this module.	Hayk Simonyan	https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan	\N	Proxy Servers: How Proxies Work	Comprehensive overview of Proxy Servers covering low-level transport mechanisms, architectural scaling, and system persistence.	7	Easy	Advanced System Concepts	33	[]	\N	7	https://youtu.be/sYhKiPzGPYc	Proxy Servers: How Proxies Work
