the atomic level", "That a transaction is all-or-nothing; if any single statement fails, the entire transaction is rolled back, leaving the database unchanged", "That the server will never crash", "That passwords are encrypted"], "question": "What does the 'Atomicity' property guarantee in an ACID-compliant database?", "explanation": "If a transfer deducts $100 from Account A but crashes before adding it to Account B, Atomicity guarantees the $100 is returned to Account A automatically via a rollback.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "That a transaction is all-or-nothing; if any single statement fails, the entire transaction is rolled back, leaving the database unchanged"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["That databases are disconnected from the internet", "That concurrent transactions execute as if they were running serially, preventing them from interfering with each other's intermediate uncommitted states", "That primary keys are hidden", "That data is isolated into shards"], "question": "What does the 'Isolation' property of ACID ensure?", "explanation": "If two users try to buy the last concert ticket at the exact same millisecond, Isolation locks the row so that Transaction A finishes before Transaction B can read the available ticket count.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "That concurrent transactions execute as if they were running serially, preventing them from interfering with each other's intermediate uncommitted states"}, {"id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["Reading a corrupted disk sector", "When a transaction reads data that has been updated by another concurrent transaction but has NOT yet been committed", "Reading data from a deleted table", "Reading data that is older than 24 hours"], "question": "What is a 'Dirty Read' anomaly in a database?", "explanation": "If Transaction B reads a bank balance modified by Transaction A, and then Transaction A rolls back, Transaction B has made a decision based on phantom 'dirty' data that technically never existed.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "When a transaction reads data that has been updated by another concurrent transaction but has NOT yet been committed"}, {"id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "D", "options": ["Read Uncommitted", "Read Committed", "Repeatable Read", "Serializable"], "question": "Which SQL isolation level provides the strictest protection, eliminating dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, and phantom reads entirely?", "explanation": "Serializable is the highest isolation level. It forces transactions to execute sequentially, guaranteeing perfect data accuracy at the cost of significantly reduced concurrency and performance bottlenecks.", "correctAnswer": "D", "correct_index": 3, "correct_answer": "Serializable"}]	\N	> **Architecture Overview:** Essential technical concepts and structural principles vital for robust, scalable web architectures. Focus deeply on the mechanical trade-offs discussed in this module.	Hayk Simonyan	https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan	\N	SQL Transactions and ACID Properties	Comprehensive overview of SQL Transactions and ACID Properties covering low-level transport mechanisms, architectural scaling, and system persistence.	7	Easy	Advanced System Concepts	36	[]	\N	7	https://youtu.be/LoxO-P7hhUQ	SQL Transactions and ACID Properties
