 in API security?", "explanation": "Logging in verifies your identity (Authentication). Attempting to delete a database table checks if you have 'Admin' rights to perform that action (Authorization).", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "Authentication confirms WHO the user is (Identity); Authorization determines WHAT the user is allowed to do (Permissions)"}, {"id": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["Access tokens are stored in local storage; Refresh tokens are stored in Redux", "Access tokens are short-lived (e.g., 15 mins) and sent with every API request; Refresh tokens are long-lived and securely stored to silently fetch new access tokens when they expire", "Access tokens are for REST; Refresh tokens are for GraphQL", "There is no functional distinction"], "question": "What is the key functional distinction between an Access Token and a Refresh Token?", "explanation": "If an Access Token is stolen, the damage is limited to 15 minutes. The Refresh Token is kept securely in an HTTP-Only cookie, protecting the user's permanent session.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "Access tokens are short-lived (e.g., 15 mins) and sent with every API request; Refresh tokens are long-lived and securely stored to silently fetch new access tokens when they expire"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["Because it uses JSON", "Because it proves identity natively", "Because it does not tell the app WHO you are; it merely issues an access token proving the user granted the app permission to access specific resources on their behalf", "Because it only works with Google"], "question": "Why is OAuth 2.0 classified as an 'Authorization Framework' rather than an 'Authentication Method'?", "explanation": "OAuth 2.0 (like 'Connect to Google Drive') gives the app a token to read your files. It doesn't authenticate your identity to the app itself. OpenID Connect (OIDC) layers identity on t