1113	Column-Guided 2D Binary Search: Find a Peak Element in a 2D Grid (Peak Element II)	dsa-find-peak-element-ii-2d-grid	Locate a 2D peak cell strictly greater than all four neighbors in O(N log M) time using column-maximum binary search.	2026-10-03 00:35:47.038713+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Column-Guided 2D Binary Search: Find a Peak Element in a 2D Grid (Peak Element II)\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nGGp5XBzC4g", "title": "Lecture 71: Find Peak Element II (2D Matrix)", "duration": "30m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-a-peak-element-ii/", "title": "Find a Peak Element II", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Locate a 2D peak cell strictly greater than its top, bottom, left, and right neighbors in O(N log M) time."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-peak-element/", "title": "Find Peak Element", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Medium", "description": "Review 1D slope elimination to contrast with 2D column-maximum elimination."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/peak-element/1", "title": "Peak element", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Basic", "description": "Find the index of any peak element in an array."}]	Module 14: Multi-Array Partitioning & 2D Matrix Binary Search	### 📌 Topic Overview\nIn a 2D grid where no two adjacent cells are equal and the outer perimeter is surrounded by `-1`, a **2D Peak Element** is a cell strictly greater than all four of its neighbors (top, bottom, left, and right).\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Brute → Optimal)\n- **1. Brute Force (`O(N * M)` Time):**\n  - Scan the entire matrix to find the global maximum element (which is guaranteed to be larger than all neighbors).\n- **2. Optimal Column Binary Search (`O(N * log2 M)` Time, `O(1)` Space):**\n  - Perform binary search on the columns from `low = 0` to `high = m - 1`.\n  - For the middle column `mid`, scan its `N` rows to find the row index `maxRow` holding the **maximum value in column `mid`**.\n  - Because `mat[maxRow][mid]` is the largest value in its column, it is **already guaranteed to be greater than its top and bottom neighbors**!\n  - Now only compare it against its `left` (`mid - 1`) and `right` (`mid + 1`) neighbors:\n    - If `mat[maxRow][mid] > left && mat[maxRow][mid] > right`, return `{maxRow, mid}`.\n    - If `mat[maxRow][mid] < left`, a higher slope lies to the left (`high = mid - 1`).\n    - Otherwise, move right (`low = mid + 1`).\n\n### 💻 2D Peak Element Blueprint\n```cpp\nint findMaxRow(vector<vector<int>> &mat, int col) {\n    int maxVal = -1, rowIdx = -1;\n    for (int r = 0; r < mat.size(); r++) {\n        if (mat[r][col] > maxVal) {\n            maxVal = mat[r][col];\n            rowIdx = r;\n        }\n    }\n    return rowIdx;\n}\n\nvector<int> findPeakGrid(vector<vector<int>> &mat) {\n    int n = mat.size(), m = mat[0].size();\n    int low = 0, high = m - 1;\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        int r = findMaxRow(mat, mid);\n        int left = (mid - 1 >= 0) ? mat[r][mid - 1] : -1;\n        int right = (mid + 1 < m) ? mat[r][mid + 1] : -1;\n\n        if (mat[r][mid] > left && mat[r][mid] > right) return {r, mid};\n        else if (mat[r][mid] < left) high = mid - 1;\n        else low = mid + 1;\n    }\n    return {-1, -1};\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
