fully found in the cache, avoiding a trip to the primary database or origin server", "When the cache is forcefully cleared by an admin", "When a user likes a cached post"], "question": "What does a 'Cache Hit' mean?", "explanation": "A Cache Hit means the system found the data in the fast storage layer. A Cache Miss means the data wasn't there, and must be fetched from the slower, primary source.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "When a requested piece of data is successfully found in the cache, avoiding a trip to the primary database or origin server"}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["Local Route Update", "Least Recently Used - discards the items that haven't been accessed for the longest time when the cache is full", "Latest Request Uncached - drops the newest items", "Logical Resource Unification"], "question": "What is the LRU cache eviction policy?", "explanation": "LRU is an algorithm that frees up memory in a full cache by evicting the data that was accessed the furthest back in time.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "Least Recently Used - discards the items that haven't been accessed for the longest time when the cache is full"}, {"id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["To prevent SQL injection", "To compress HTML responses", "To instruct the browser and intermediate caches on how long they are allowed to store a response (e.g., max-age)", "To define which CSS files should be ignored"], "question": "What is the purpose of the HTTP `Cache-Control` header?", "explanation": "Headers like Cache-Control: max-age=3600 tell the browser it can safely use the cached version of a file for the next hour without asking the server again.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "To instruct the browser and intermediate caches on how long they are allowed to store a response (e.g., max-age)"}, {"id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["A unique HTML tag used for SEO", "A tracking pixel used by advertisers", "A unique identifier assigned by a web server to a specific version of a resource, used to check if the browser's cached version is still valid", "A new JavaScript variable type"], "question": "What is an ETag (Entity Tag)?", "explanation": "The browser sends the ETag back to the server. If the file hasn't changed, the server responds with a '304 Not Modified', saving bandwidth by telling the browser to just use its cached copy.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "A unique identifier assigned by a web server to a specific version of a resource, used to check if the browser's cached version is still valid"}]	\N	> **Architecture Overview:** Essential systems architecture principles governing how modern web applications communicate, route data, balance loads, and scale effectively.	Hayk Simonyan	https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan	\N	Caching Explained	Core system design concepts focusing on caching and distributed network architecture.	10	Easy	Web Architecture	6	[]	\N	10	https://youtu.be/7QfswaV0re4	Caching Explained
1120	Recursive Inorder Traversal: Left-Root-Right Call Stack Unwinding	dsa-inorder-traversal-binary-tree	Execute recursive inorder traversal (Left -> Root -> Right) and understand its relationship to sorted BST ordering.	2026-10-03 00:35:47.128017+05:30	### 🎯 Algorithmic Pattern Breakdown: Recursive Inorder Traversal: Left-Root-Right Call Stack Unwinding\nMastering this module is critical for technical coding rounds and LeetCode algorithmic interviews.\n\n- **Core Problem-Solving Pattern**: Break down problem constraints, identify edge cases (empty inputs, negative values, integer overflow), and establish time/space complexity bounds.\n- **Optimal Complexity Target**: Aim for O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity with O(1) or O(N) auxiliary space.\n- **Interview Walkthrough Strategy**:\n  1. Clarify inputs, outputs, and constraints before coding.\n  2. State brute force approach and explain why it is sub-optimal.\n  3. Walk the interviewer through the optimized algorithm with a small test case.\n  4. Write clean, modular code with descriptive variable names.	[{"url": "https://neetcode.io/roadmap", "title": "NeetCode Algorithmic Roadmap"}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problemset/all/", "title": "LeetCode Problem Set"}]	[{"url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z_NEgBgbRVI", "title": "Lecture 78: Inorder Traversal of Binary Tree", "duration": "18m"}]	[{"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-inorder-traversal/", "title": "Binary Tree Inorder Traversal", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Return the inorder traversal of a binary tree's node values."}, {"url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/sum-of-left-leaves/", "title": "Sum of Left Leaves", "platform": "LeetCode", "difficulty": "Easy", "description": "Traverse a binary tree recursively to accumulate values of all left leaf nodes."}, {"url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/inorder-traversal/1", "title": "Inorder Traversal", "platform": "GeeksforGeeks", "difficulty": "Basic", "description": "Return a list containing the inorder traversal of a given binary tree."}]	Module 15: Binary Trees: Hierarchical Representation & Fundamental Traversals	### 📌 Topic Overview\n**Inorder Traversal** visits nodes in the order **`Left → Root → Right`**. You recursively dive as far left as possible first, process the current node when returning from the left branch, and then explore the right branch.\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n- **Base Case:** If `node == nullptr`, return immediately.\n- **Recursive Steps:**\n  1. Call `inorder(node->left)`.\n  2. Process/record `node->data`.\n  3. Call `inorder(node->right)`.\n- **Why Inorder Matters:** In a Binary Search Tree (BST), an Inorder Traversal visits all elements in **sorted ascending order**!\n- **Complexity:** `O(N)` Time and `O(H)` Auxiliary Call Stack Space (where `H` is tree height, `O(N)` worst case).\n\n### 💻 Recursive Inorder Blueprint\n```cpp\nvoid dfsInorder(TreeNode* node, vector<int> &ans) {\n    if (node == nullptr) return;\n    dfsInorder(node->left, ans);    // 1. Left\n    ans.push_back(node->val);       // 2. Root\n    dfsInorder(node->right, ans);   // 3. Right\n}\n\nvector<int> inorderTraversal(TreeNode* root) {\n    vector<int> ans;\n    dfsInorder(root, ans);\n    return ans;\n}\n```	take U forward (Striver)	https://www.youtube.com/@takeUforward	25	\N	\N	20	Easy	\N	1	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N
