nks of data map correctly to specific shards."}, {"id": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["It compresses database logs.", "It acts as a load-balancing interface that directs application queries to the correct underlying shard containing the requested data.", "It performs automated daily backups.", "It compiles JSON into SQL syntax."], "question": "What purpose does the 'mongos' query router serve in a sharded cluster?", "explanation": "Applications connect to the `mongos` router just like a normal database. The router abstracts the complex shard logic, making the distributed cluster invisible to the frontend.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "It acts as a load-balancing interface that directs application queries to the correct underlying shard containing the requested data."}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["It is deleted automatically.", "It is evenly distributed across all shards randomly.", "It resides entirely on the cluster's designated 'Primary Shard'.", "It is rejected by the query router."], "question": "If sharding is enabled for a cluster, but not explicitly enabled for a specific database/collection, where is that collection's data stored?", "explanation": "Sharding is applied at the collection level. Unsharded collections within a sharded cluster fall back to living 100% on the default primary shard.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "It resides entirely on the cluster's designated 'Primary Shard'."}, {"id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["It has only two possible values (e.g., true/false).", "It changes constantly (e.g., timestamp).", "It has high cardinality (many unique values) to prevent duplicate bottlenecks, and is frequently used in query filters.", "It is fully encrypted."], "question": "What characteristic makes a field an excellent candidate for a Shard Key?", "explanation": "A good shard key spreads data evenly. If you shard by a low-cardinality field like 'Country', one shard (e.g., USA) might become massively overloaded while others sit empty.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "It has high cardinality (many unique values) to prevent duplicate bottlenecks, and is frequently used in query filters."}, {"id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["It stores hashed passwords securely.", "It runs an MD5 hash over every query to authenticate the user.", "It applies a hash function to the chosen shard key to enforce a mathematically even, random-looking distribution of documents across all shards.", "It creates a blockchain ledger of transactions."], "question": "What does 'Hashed Sharding' achieve in a distributed database?", "explanation": "Hashed sharding guarantees that even if you insert sequential IDs (1, 2, 3, 4), they will be scattered equally across all available machines rather than piling up on a single server.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "It applies a hash function to the chosen shard key to enforce a mathematically even, random-looking distribution of documents across all shards."}]	\N	> **Architecture Overview:** Essential technical concepts and structural principles vital for robust, scalable web architectures. Focus deeply on the mechanical trade-offs discussed in this module.	Hayk Simonyan	https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan	\N	How to Configure and Provision Database Sharding	Comprehensive overview of How to Configure and Provision Database Sharding covering low-level transport mechanisms, architectural scaling, and system persistence.	12	Easy	Advanced System Concepts	22	[]	\N	12	https://youtu.be/98_OJ1Eoibw	How to Configure and Provision Database Sharding
22359	Single Point of Failure (SPOF) in System Design	single-point-of-failure-spof	Comprehensive overview of Single Point of Failure (SPOF) in System Design covering low-level transport mechanisms, architectural scaling, and system persistence.	2026-10-12 02:59:57.98668+05:30	### 🎯 Engineering Guide: Single Point of Failure (SPOF) in System Design\nPart of the **Web Architecture & Frontend System Design** curriculum designed for modern software development.\n\n- **Core Concepts**: Architectural patterns, modular design principles, and production best practices.\n- **Hands-on Objective**: Understand the lifecycle, error boundaries, and integration points with the rest of the application stack.\n- **Production Checklist**:\n  - Clean separation of concerns (controller, service, repository layers).\n  - Robust error handling and structured logging.\n  - Unit and integration testing considerations.	[{"url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/", "title": "MDN Web Docs"}, {"url": "https://devdocs.io/", "title": "Official Documentation & API Reference"}]	[{"url": "https://youtu.be/Iy2YqgjXtRM", "title": "Single Point of Failure (SPOF) in System Design", "creator_name": "Hayk Simonyan", "duration_minutes": 3, "creator_channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan"}]	[{"id": 1, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["A bug in a specific line of JavaScript.", "One specific component or node within a system that, if it fails or goes offline, causes the entire system to crash or become totally unavailable.", "A database that only stores one type of record.", "A user forgetting their master password."], "question": "What is the strict definition of a Single Point of Failure (SPOF)?", "explanation": "A SPOF represents an architectural bottleneck with zero redundancy. If your app has 100 web servers but only one database server, that database is a SPOF.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "One specific component or node within a system that, if it fails or goes offline, causes the entire system to crash or become totally unavailable."}, {"id": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["By compressing the database files.", "By moving the database to a faster hard drive.", "By introducing redundancy: deploying database replicas so if the primary master fails, a standby replica immediately takes over.", "By converting it to a NoSQL format."], "question": "How is a database SPOF typically resolved in enterprise architecture?", "explanation": "Redundancy eliminates SPOFs. Having multiple identical copies of a critical component running simultaneously ensures high availability during hardware failures.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "By introducing redundancy: deploying database replicas so if the primary master fails, a standby replica immediately takes over."}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["Because it blocks IP addresses.", "Because it routes traffic slowly.", "Because if all incoming internet traffic funnels through one load balancer and it crashes, users cannot reach any of the healthy backend servers.", "Because it decrypts SSL certificates."], "question": "Why can a Load Balancer act as a dangerous Single Point of Failure?", "explanation": "A load balancer distributes traffic, but it sits at the very edge of the network. Redundant load balancers (Active-Passive or Active-Active setups) are required to fix this SPOF.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "Because if all incoming internet traffic funnels through one load balancer and it crashes, users cannot reach any of the healthy backend servers."}, {"id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["By routing all users to the closest server.", "By ensuring that if a localized disaster (like a data center power outage or natural disaster) destroys a facility, servers in completely different regions keep the app online.", "By translating the app into different languages.", "By forcing databases to use UTC time."], "question": "How does 'Geographic Distribution' mitigate catastrophic single points of failure?", "explanation": "Hosting an application exclusively in 'us-east-1' makes that entire data center a SPOF. Multi-region deployments ensure continental-scale redundancy.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "By ensuring that if a localized disaster (like a data center power outage or natural disaster) destroys a facility, servers in completely different regions keep the app online."}, {"id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["To automatically write code fixes for the developer.", "To detect degrading components or failing nodes instantly, alerting engineers to mitigate the issue before it cascades into a total system failure.", "To ban users causing too much traffic.", "To restart the browser cache."], "question": "What is the primary role of continuous monitoring and alerting systems regarding SPOFs?", "explanation": "You cannot fix what you cannot see. Automated monitoring (like Datadog/Prometheus) tracks health metrics, catching potential SPOF failures early.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "To detect degrading components or failing nodes instantly, alerting engineers to mitigate the issue before it cascades into a total system failure."}]	\N	> **Architecture Overview:** Essential technical concepts and structural principles vital for robust, scalable web architectures. Focus deeply on the mechanical trade-offs discussed in this module.	Hayk Simonyan	https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan	\N	Single Point of Failure (SPOF) in System Design	Comprehensive overview of Single Point of Failure (SPOF) in System Design covering low-level transport mechanisms, architectural scaling, and system persistence.	3	Easy	Advanced System Concepts	24	[]	\N	3	https://youtu.be/Iy2YqgjXtRM	Single Point of Failure (SPOF) in System Design
