ysical resources (like RAM, CPU, or faster SSDs) to handle increased load."}, {"id": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "B", "options": ["A massively upgraded CPU.", "A load balancer must be introduced to stand in front of the server pool and distribute incoming traffic across the newly added machines.", "A completely schema-less database.", "A GraphQL API gateway."], "question": "What is the fundamental architectural requirement to implement Horizontal Scaling (Scaling Out)?", "explanation": "When you add a second or third server, the client doesn't know which one to talk to. The load balancer receives the single request and intelligently routes it to an available worker node.", "correctAnswer": "B", "correct_index": 1, "correct_answer": "A load balancer must be introduced to stand in front of the server pool and distribute incoming traffic across the newly added machines."}, {"id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["Because horizontal servers have better antivirus software.", "Because it relies on a single massive machine.", "Because duplicating the application across multiple separate servers eliminates the Single Point of Failure; if one crashes, the others continue serving users.", "Because horizontal scaling encrypts all network traffic."], "question": "Why does Horizontal Scaling inherently provide higher Fault Tolerance than Vertical Scaling?", "explanation": "A vertically scaled supercomputer is still just one computer. If its power supply blows, the entire app dies. A horizontally scaled fleet can lose multiple nodes and survive.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "Because duplicating the application across multiple separate servers eliminates the Single Point of Failure; if one crashes, the others continue serving users."}, {"id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["Vertical Scaling", "Database Indexing", "Horizontal Scaling", "Code Minification"], "question": "Which scaling strategy generally provides near-infinite scalability for massive global web applications?", "explanation": "Tech giants scale horizontally. Instead of building an impossibly large supercomputer, they string together millions of standard commodity servers, adding more on demand as traffic grows.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "Horizontal Scaling"}, {"id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "answer": "C", "options": ["It makes the UI look worse.", "Servers become physically too heavy.", "State management and data consistency become highly complex, requiring distributed caching, stateless APIs, and database replication/sharding.", "It restricts the application to a single programming language."], "question": "What is a major engineering challenge introduced by Horizontal Scaling?", "explanation": "If a user uploads a profile picture to Server A, and their next request hits Server B, Server B needs to know about that picture. Distributed systems require complex shared data architecture.", "correctAnswer": "C", "correct_index": 2, "correct_answer": "State management and data consistency become highly complex, requiring distributed caching, stateless APIs, and database replication/sharding."}]	\N	> **Architecture Overview:** Essential technical concepts and structural principles vital for robust, scalable web architectures. Focus deeply on the mechanical trade-offs discussed in this module.	Hayk Simonyan	https://www.youtube.com/@HaykSimonyan	\N	Vertical vs Horizontal Scaling in System Design	Comprehensive overview of Vertical vs Horizontal Scaling in System Design covering low-level transport mechanisms, architectural scaling, and system persistence.	4	Easy	Advanced System Concepts	27	[]	\N	4	https://youtu.be/1v687z1rhNA	Vertical vs Horizontal Scaling in System Design
